32	kneeling_cable_crunch	Kneeling Cable Crunch	Kneeling Rope Cable Crunch	Core	Core	Isolation	Cable	60.00	45.59	{"obliques": 26.67, "rectus_abdominis": 18.92}	{"grip": "Hold the rope with a neutral or semi-neutral grip, keeping the hands beside the head rather than pulling the rope in front of the torso. Grip only firmly enough to secure the attachment.", "tldr": {"focus": "Make the movement come from spinal flexion rather than sitting the hips back or pulling the rope with the arms.", "setup": "Kneel facing the cable stack with the rope held beside the head and the pulley set above you.", "execution": "Flex the trunk by bringing the ribcage toward the pelvis while keeping the hips relatively fixed and the rope position unchanged.", "stop_when": "Stop when another repetition would require hip hinging, arm pulling, loss of spinal flexion, or a materially shorter trunk range."}, "stance": "Use a stable bilateral kneeling position with the knees approximately hip-width apart or slightly wider as needed for balance. The lower legs may rest naturally on the floor behind the knees.", "bracing": "Maintain enough abdominal and pelvic control to prevent the movement from becoming a hip hinge. Keep the hips relatively stationary over the knees and avoid using posterior hip displacement to create cable travel.", "overview": "A kneeling rope cable crunch performed facing a high pulley. The defining movement is loaded trunk flexion produced by approximating the ribcage toward the pelvis while the hips remain relatively stable. The rope is held as a passive connection to the cable rather than actively pulled downward with the arms, distinguishing the exercise from a kneeling pulldown or hip-dominant bowing movement.", "eccentric": "Reverse the spinal flexion under control, allowing the trunk to lengthen and return toward the starting position while keeping the hips, hands, and elbow angle substantially unchanged. Stop the return before the cable loses tension or the spine is forced into excessive extension.", "concentric": "Flex the spine by drawing the lower ribcage toward the pelvis while keeping the rope beside the head and the elbows at an approximately constant angle. Allow the upper and mid-back to round progressively as the trunk shortens anteriorly. Keep the hips relatively fixed and avoid pulling the cable with shoulder extension or elbow motion.", "tempo_notes": "Avoid jerking the cable or rebounding out of the extended position. The eccentric should remain controlled enough that the next repetition begins from a stable trunk position rather than elastic or momentum-assisted motion.", "end_position": "Finish the concentric phase when maximal controlled trunk flexion is reached with the ribcage substantially approximated toward the pelvis, without sitting the hips backward or pulling the rope farther down with the arms.", "safety_notes": ["Use padding under the knees if needed to maintain a stable kneeling position.", "Do not force the spine into painful flexion or extension beyond the individual's comfortable range.", "Ensure the rope attachment and selector pin are securely engaged before beginning."], "external_cues": "Curl the torso down around the front of the abdomen while keeping the rope parked beside the head.", "internal_cues": "Bring the ribs toward the pelvis; keep the hips quiet; let the spine round instead of pulling with the arms.", "common_mistakes": ["Sitting the hips backward during the concentric phase and turning the movement into a hip hinge.", "Pulling the rope downward with shoulder extension or elbow movement instead of flexing the trunk.", "Keeping the spine rigid and merely bowing forward from the hips.", "Allowing the hands to drift away from the head and using the rope as a lever.", "Overextending the lumbar spine at the top to create additional range.", "Using momentum to rebound out of the top position."], "equipment_setup": "Attach a rope to a high pulley and set the cable high enough that tension is maintained through the full trunk-flexion range. Position yourself far enough from the stack that the cable can track freely without the weight stack contacting at the top.", "range_of_motion": "Use the largest spinal-flexion range that can be produced without hip-dominant movement, painful spinal positioning, or loss of cable tension. The top position should allow meaningful trunk lengthening, while the bottom should be defined by controlled spinal flexion rather than by how close the elbows or hands come to the floor.", "rir_0_definition": "RIR 0 is reached when no additional repetition can be completed through the defined spinal-flexion range while keeping the hips relatively fixed and the arms passive, even if the cable could still be moved by sitting backward, hinging at the hips, or pulling with the upper limbs.", "rir_1_indicators": "Approximately one technically valid repetition may remain when trunk-flexion speed slows markedly and the final portion becomes difficult, but the lifter can still maintain stable hips, fixed rope position, controlled spinal flexion, and the established range.", "individualization": "Knee width, distance from the pulley, degree of hip flexion, hand position beside the head, and exact spinal-flexion range may vary with torso length, limb proportions, mobility, and cable geometry. These variations are acceptable provided the hips remain relatively stable, the rope does not become an active pulling implement, and the movement remains primarily loaded spinal flexion.", "plane_of_movement": "Primarily sagittal-plane spinal flexion, with the hips and upper limbs functioning mainly as stabilizers.", "starting_position": "Kneel facing the cable stack with the knees comfortably spaced and the hips positioned above or slightly behind the knees. Hold the rope ends beside the temples or upper sides of the head, with the elbows flexed and relatively fixed. Begin with the trunk upright to slightly flexed, the spine controlled, and the cable already under tension.", "technical_failure": "Technical failure occurs when another repetition cannot be completed through the established trunk-flexion range without materially changing the mechanics. Observable signs include sitting the hips backward, hinging primarily at the hips, pulling the rope downward with the arms, losing the established spinal-flexion pattern, or substantially shortening the range.", "primary_joint_actions": "During the concentric phase, the thoracic and lumbar spine flex while the pelvis may posteriorly tilt slightly as the ribcage approaches the pelvis. The hips remain relatively fixed in flexion, and the shoulders and elbows remain mostly static while the hands maintain the rope position."}	{}	{https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b9FJ4hIK3pI,https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AV5PmZJIrrw,https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aBd6T01PBqw}	Bilateral	{"slug": "kneeling_cable_crunch", "notes": ["The contribution vector generates approximately 59 Nm of thoracolumbar flexion capacity using representative effective internal moment arms of about 0.055 m for rectus abdominis and 0.050 m for the oblique aggregate.", "Iliopsoas is excluded because a strict cable crunch holds the hip angle relatively constant; meaningful hip-flexion contribution would indicate that the exercise had become a kneeling hip hinge.", "Latissimus dorsi and triceps are excluded because holding the rope and maintaining arm position do not generate a meaningful share of the required net thoracolumbar flexion moment.", "Transverse abdominis is excluded because its primary mechanical role is abdominal compression and segmental stabilization rather than production of a large net spinal-flexion moment.", "The estimate is sensitive to pulley ratio, kneeling distance from the stack, rope position, spinal range of motion, and the extent to which the hips remain fixed."], "limiting_phase": "Early-to-mid concentric spinal flexion from the extended or neutral starting position, before the cable line moves closer to the thoracolumbar flexion axis and the external moment declines.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 45.59, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.7800, "E_ND": 0.6200, "E_LoA": 0.7500, "E_mech": 0.3264, "muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "The bilateral oblique aggregate contributes substantial sagittal-plane trunk-flexion torque, although part of its anatomical capacity is oriented toward axial rotation and lateral flexion rather than pure flexion.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 26.67}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.9000, "E_ND": 0.9300, "E_LoA": 0.9800, "E_mech": 0.7793, "muscle": "rectus_abdominis", "rationale": "Acts as the most directly aligned thoracolumbar flexor and operates at favorable length and leverage near the initiation of the spinal-flexion phase.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.29, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 18.92}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["A representative selectorized cable station with an approximately 0.90 effective stack-to-rope force ratio is assumed.", "The rope is held beside the head or upper chest, and the cable force acts with an effective perpendicular moment arm of approximately 0.112 m about the thoracolumbar flexion axis.", "The pelvis and hips remain substantially fixed while the thoracic and lumbar spine flex against the cable.", "The athlete avoids converting the movement into a kneeling hip hinge or forceful shoulder-extension exercise.", "The estimated cable force excludes meaningful momentum and assumes a controlled concentric repetition."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "thoracolumbar_flexion", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 59.33}], "nominal_load_kg": 60.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 529.74}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 45.59, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 45.59}
30	seated_dumbbell_arnold_press	Arnold Press	Seated Dumbbell Arnold Press	Upper	Chest_Clav_AD	Secondary_Compound	Dumbbell	44.00	124.67	{"deltoid_lateral": 18.42, "trapezius_lower": 3.83, "trapezius_upper": 7.38, "deltoid_anterior": 23.75, "serratus_anterior": 15.83, "triceps_long_head": 16.53, "triceps_medial_head": 9.52, "triceps_lateral_head": 23.09, "pectoralis_major_clavicular": 6.32}	{"grip": "Grip on the dumbbells remains fixed while the forearms rotate through the movement, transitioning from palms facing the body at the bottom to palms facing forward at the top.", "tldr": {"focus": "Coordinate the shoulder rotation with the press so the forearms rotate continuously throughout the ascent rather than rotating early or late.", "setup": "Sit upright on a bench with back support, holding two dumbbells at shoulder height with palms facing the body.", "execution": "Press the dumbbells overhead while simultaneously rotating the palms outward, ending with palms facing forward at lockout.", "stop_when": "The dumbbells can no longer be pressed to full overhead extension or the rotation stalls partway through the range."}, "stance": "Seated with feet flat on the floor for overall stability, not contributing to the pressing action.", "bracing": "Torso braced against the bench back throughout, avoiding excessive lumbar arching to assist the press; the ribcage stays stacked over the pelvis as the arms rise.", "overview": "This is the seated dumbbell Arnold press, a shoulder press variant in which the forearms rotate from a pronated-facing-inward position at the bottom to a fully pronated, forward-facing position at the top, performed concurrently with the pressing motion. This combined rotation-and-press pattern distinguishes it from a standard dumbbell shoulder press, which uses a fixed hand orientation throughout, and increases the relative time under tension for the anterior and lateral deltoid across a broader arc of shoulder rotation.", "eccentric": "The dumbbells lower under control back toward shoulder height, with the forearms rotating in reverse from palms-forward back to palms-facing-body as the elbows bend and the arms descend.", "concentric": "The dumbbells are pressed overhead through combined glenohumeral flexion, abduction, and elbow extension, with the forearms rotating continuously from palms-facing-body to palms-facing-forward as the arms rise.", "tempo_notes": "The rotational component should occur smoothly and continuously throughout the press rather than being completed early or delayed until near lockout.", "end_position": "Arms extended overhead with the palms facing forward and the dumbbells roughly in line with or slightly in front of the ears.", "safety_notes": ["Use a bench with back support to limit excessive lumbar arching during the press.", "Control the rotational component to avoid unnecessary shoulder strain from rushing the transition between hand positions."], "external_cues": "Press the dumbbells up and rotate them to finish with palms facing forward overhead.", "internal_cues": "Rotate the palms outward as the arms rise, as if turning a door handle while pressing; keep the rotation and the press moving together rather than as separate steps.", "common_mistakes": ["Rushing or omitting the rotational component and turning the movement into a standard shoulder press.", "Arching the lower back excessively off the bench to assist the press.", "Shrugging the shoulders upward toward the ears at the top of the press.", "Pressing the dumbbells forward in front of the face instead of straight overhead.", "Using momentum from the hips or torso to initiate the press when seated on an unsupported bench."], "equipment_setup": "Bench with upright back support set to a vertical or near-vertical angle, providing a stable base for the torso during the seated press.", "range_of_motion": "From the dumbbells at shoulder height with palms facing the body at the bottom to full overhead extension with palms facing forward at the top, limited by the individual's shoulder flexion and rotation mobility.", "rir_0_definition": "Inability to press the dumbbells to full overhead extension with the accompanying forearm rotation while keeping the torso against the bench, as distinct from continuing to move the dumbbells by arching the lower back.", "rir_1_indicators": "Slower press speed through the mid-range, incomplete or rushed rotation of the forearms, and increasing effort to reach full overhead extension, while the general press-and-rotate pattern remains intact.", "individualization": "The exact starting elbow position, degree of forward elbow placement at the bottom, and pressing width may vary moderately with shoulder structure and mobility, provided the rotational component from palms-facing-body to palms-facing-forward is preserved throughout the press.", "plane_of_movement": "Combined frontal-plane shoulder abduction/elevation with a transverse-plane rotational component of the humerus as the forearms rotate through the press.", "starting_position": "Seated with the back against the bench, dumbbells held at approximately shoulder height with the elbows bent, palms facing the body, elbows positioned in front of or alongside the torso.", "technical_failure": "The point at which the dumbbells can no longer be pressed to full overhead extension with the accompanying rotation, or the torso arches significantly to assist the press.", "primary_joint_actions": "Glenohumeral flexion and abduction combined with internal-to-neutral rotation of the humerus as the arms rise; elbow extension occurring concurrently; scapular upward rotation as the arms approach overhead; the wrists and forearms rotate from a palms-facing-body position toward a pronated, forward-facing position through the ascent."}	{}	{https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ris9tKqMwgU}	Bilateral	{"slug": "seated_dumbbell_arnold_press", "notes": ["The load appears consistent with a demanding 8-12 repetition working set for the reference athlete when interpreted as two 22 kg dumbbells.", "Compared with a conventional seated dumbbell overhead press, the Arnold path shifts mechanical demand toward the anterior deltoid and clavicular pectoralis while reducing the lateral-deltoid share.", "The rotator-cuff aggregate is excluded because its principal function is humeral-head compression and rotational control rather than production of a large net elevation moment.", "Pronators and supinators are excluded because rotating the forearms reorients the dumbbells but does not generate a meaningful share of the external shoulder or elbow moments."], "limiting_phase": "Early-to-mid concentric transition as the elbows move from an anterior flexed position into humeral elevation, with the dumbbells still anterior and lateral to the shoulder axes and the elbows remaining substantially flexed.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 124.67, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9400, "E_MA": 0.9100, "E_ND": 0.9300, "E_LoA": 0.9800, "E_mech": 0.7796, "muscle": "deltoid_anterior", "rationale": "The anterior starting position and forward pressing arc strongly align the anterior deltoid with the required humeral-flexion and elevation moment.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 30.46, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 23.75}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8700, "E_ND": 0.8600, "E_LoA": 0.9500, "E_mech": 0.6397, "muscle": "triceps_lateral_head", "rationale": "Provides direct elbow-extension torque with favorable monoarticular force availability and strong alignment as the dumbbells accelerate upward.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 36.10, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 23.09}, {"E_LT": 0.8600, "E_MA": 0.7800, "E_ND": 0.7700, "E_LoA": 0.7800, "E_mech": 0.4029, "muscle": "deltoid_lateral", "rationale": "Contributes to humeral elevation after the elbows begin moving outward, but the predominantly anterior pressing path reduces its alignment relative to a conventional overhead press.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 45.73, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 18.42}, {"E_LT": 0.7900, 