28	weighted_forward_lean_dip	Weighted Chest Dip	Weighted Forward-Lean Parallel-Bar Dip	Upper	Chest_Sternal	Heavy_Compound	Bodyweight	110.00	193.52	{"obliques": 7.50, "iliopsoas": 7.73, "rotator_cuffs": 9.15, "trapezius_lower": 7.28, "deltoid_anterior": 19.48, "latissimus_dorsi": 4.11, "pectoralis_minor": 4.53, "rectus_abdominis": 8.92, "serratus_anterior": 5.11, "triceps_long_head": 20.77, "triceps_medial_head": 12.94, "transverse_abdominis": 2.85, "triceps_lateral_head": 29.97, "adductor_longus_brevis": 4.04, "pectoralis_major_sternal": 41.23, "pectoralis_major_clavicular": 7.91}	{"grip": "Use a closed neutral grip on the parallel bars with the wrists stacked as comfortably as possible over the hands. Maintain firm contact through the palms and avoid allowing the wrists to collapse into uncontrolled extension.", "tldr": {"focus": "Keep the torso inclined forward and let the elbows travel moderately outward rather than tucking them tightly to the ribs.", "setup": "Support yourself on parallel bars with the added load hanging securely, then establish a deliberate forward torso lean before descending.", "execution": "Lower by flexing the elbows and allowing the shoulders to extend while maintaining the forward lean, then press back up without becoming progressively upright.", "stop_when": "Stop when another repetition would require losing the forward lean, excessive shoulder translation, leg swing, or a materially shortened bottom range."}, "stance": "", "bracing": "Maintain abdominal and gluteal tension sufficient to keep the trunk and pelvis moving as one unit while preserving the chosen forward lean. Resist excessive lumbar extension, pelvic swinging, or independent leg movement caused by the suspended load.", "overview": "A weighted parallel-bar dip performed with a deliberate forward torso lean to preserve a chest-dominant pressing geometry. Added external load is suspended from the body while the lifter lowers between the bars through elbow flexion and shoulder extension, then presses back to support. The defining feature relative to a more upright triceps-oriented dip is the maintained forward inclination of the torso with a moderately wider elbow path rather than tightly tucked upper arms.", "eccentric": "Lower the body under control by flexing the elbows and allowing the shoulders to move into extension and horizontal abduction while maintaining the forward lean. Descend until the deepest comfortable shoulder position is reached without anterior shoulder instability, loss of trunk control, or a sudden increase in load swing.", "concentric": "Press the body upward by extending the elbows and bringing the upper arms forward and inward from the bottom position. Preserve the forward torso inclination rather than becoming markedly more upright as leverage becomes difficult. Keep the shoulders controlled, allow natural scapular motion, and prevent the suspended load from swinging beneath the body.", "tempo_notes": "Avoid bouncing from the bottom or using leg swing to reverse direction. Control the suspended load so it does not create pendular momentum between repetitions.", "end_position": "Finish with the elbows at full or near-full functional extension, the shoulders stable, and the torso still inclined forward rather than transitioning into an upright support position. The added load should remain quiet beneath the body.", "safety_notes": ["Secure the external load so it cannot detach or strike the floor, bars, or lower limbs during the movement.", "Do not force additional depth if the bottom position produces anterior shoulder pain, instability, or uncontrolled humeral translation.", "Confirm that the parallel bars are stable and provide sufficient clearance for both the body and suspended load."], "external_cues": "Lower your chest between and slightly in front of the bars, then push the bars away while keeping the weight hanging quietly beneath you.", "internal_cues": "Keep the chest angled forward; let the elbows track moderately outward; move the torso and pelvis as one rigid segment.", "common_mistakes": ["Becoming progressively upright during the concentric phase and changing the exercise toward an upright dip.", "Tucking the elbows tightly against the torso instead of maintaining the intended moderately wide chest-dip path.", "Descending beyond the shoulder range that can be controlled and allowing excessive anterior humeral translation.", "Using leg kicking or pelvic swing to initiate the ascent.", "Allowing the suspended load to swing forward and backward between repetitions.", "Losing wrist support and collapsing heavily into extension on the parallel bars."], "equipment_setup": "Use stable parallel bars with sufficient clearance for the body and suspended load. Attach external resistance securely with a dip belt or equivalent loading system so the load hangs centrally and cannot contact the bars, floor, or legs through the intended range.", "range_of_motion": "Descend through the largest controlled range that preserves comfortable shoulder mechanics, stable wrist support, and the forward-lean geometry. The bottom is defined by the deepest shoulder-extension position that can be reached without painful anterior shoulder stress or loss of control; the top approaches full elbow extension without aggressive joint locking or deliberate shoulder elevation.", "rir_0_definition": "RIR 0 is reached when no additional repetition can be completed through the defined range while maintaining the forward-lean geometry and controlled suspension, even if the body could still be raised by becoming upright, kicking the legs, swinging the load, tucking the elbows markedly, or shortening the descent.", "rir_1_indicators": "Approximately one technically valid repetition may remain when upward velocity slows substantially and the transition out of the bottom becomes difficult, but the lifter can still preserve the forward torso angle, stable shoulder position, quiet suspended load, and established range.", "individualization": "Torso inclination, elbow flare, bar width, leg position, and bottom depth may vary with shoulder structure, arm length, chest dimensions, and the geometry of the dip station. These parameters are acceptable to individualize provided the torso remains meaningfully forward-inclined, the elbows are not tightly tucked, the shoulders remain controlled, and added load does not alter the movement through swinging or compensatory body motion.", "plane_of_movement": "A compound sagittal and transverse-plane pressing movement: substantial glenohumeral extension-to-flexion and horizontal adduction occur together with sagittal-plane elbow flexion and extension.", "starting_position": "Begin in supported suspension above the bars with the elbows extended, shoulders controlled, and the added load hanging vertically beneath the pelvis. Establish a forward torso inclination by allowing the chest to angle toward the floor while keeping the ribcage and pelvis controlled. The legs may remain slightly behind the torso to balance the inclined position without swinging.", "technical_failure": "Technical failure occurs when another repetition cannot be completed through the established range while preserving the forward-lean dip mechanics. Observable breakdowns include becoming markedly upright to finish the press, excessive elbow tucking, uncontrolled anterior shoulder translation, shortened descent, leg kicking, pelvic swing, or using momentum from the suspended load to reverse direction.", "primary_joint_actions": "During the concentric phase, the elbows extend while the glenohumeral joints move from an extended and horizontally abducted bottom position toward flexion and horizontal adduction. The scapulae upwardly rotate and protract modestly as the body rises, while the trunk maintains an isometric forward inclination relative to gravity."}	{}	{https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yN6Q1UI_xkE}	Bilateral	{"slug": "weighted_forward_lean_dip", "notes": ["This is the mirror image of the incline press family and completes the pressing angle series. Pectoralis_major_sternal reaches E_mech 0.7709 with E_LoA 0.9800, its highest pressing value in the library, because the forward lean requires humeral flexion and adduction from a deeply extended start, which is precisely its line of pull. Compare the converging incline at E_LoA 0.7760 and the Smith incline at 0.8200.", "Triceps_long_head falls to E_mech 0.4769, its lowest value anywhere, against 0.8712 in the overhead rope extension. The dip and the overhead extension are the two extremes of humeral position for this muscle, and the 1.83-fold spread across those two exercises at similar elbow moments is the cleanest demonstration in the library that long-head engagement is governed by shoulder position rather than by elbow demand.", "The lateral and medial heads are near-saturated at E_mech 0.8303 while the long head is compromised, so total triceps contribution of 63.68 cm2 sits just under the 48 N-m elbow requirement. This is the mechanical reason dips are commonly reported as failing at the bottom on the triceps despite being a chest-emphasis movement, and 110 kg sits at the top of the stated 8-12 rep band for this athlete.", "Pectoralis_minor and trapezius_lower are included as genuine task-torque contributors rather than anchoring stabilisers, which is unique to dips among the pressing exercises. Supporting the body between fixed bars requires active scapular depression against 1000 N; in every bench-based press the bench absorbs that entirely. Pectoralis_minor at E_LoA 0.9500 is its best alignment in the library outside the high-to-low fly.", "Latissimus_dorsi appears in a pressing exercise for the first time, on depression grounds while its humeral action opposes the press. This dual role is why E_LoA is held at 0.3500 despite the depression contribution being real. If the model later splits scapular from humeral function, this entry should be revisited first.", "Wrist_flexors were deliberately excluded, in sharp contrast to the weighted pull-up at 34.03 cm2 under a comparable total load. The hands press down on fixed bars in compression rather than suspending the body from a bar, so no grip force is required to transmit load. This is the clearest case in the library where equal load produces radically different grip demand, and it argues for treating support-type against suspension-type bodyweight exercises as distinct categories.", "Trunk and hip rigging totals 31.04 cm2, close to the weighted pull-up's 40.55 and far above the unweighted wide pull-up's 11.28. The pattern now holds across three bodyweight exercises: a freely hanging belt load, not total load, drives trunk demand. Any future weighted bodyweight entry should apply this rather than scaling trunk contribution with bodyweight.", "The 110 kg input required the same interpretation as the weighted pull-up, read as 85 kg body mass plus 25 kg added. The case for a separate added_load_kg column is now stronger, since three bodyweight exercises have required this inference and the two weighted ones would be materially wrong under the alternative reading.", "An upright torso dip must not inherit these values. Removing the forward lean shortens the shoulder moment arm, shifts emphasis decisively to the triceps, and would raise the long head substantially by reducing the humeral extension at the bottom.", "A ring dip or a bench dip must not inherit these values either. Rings add a large stabilisation demand that fixed bars eliminate, while bench dips support the feet and remove the entire trunk and hip contribution.", "An unweighted forward-lean dip must not inherit these values. Removing the belt eliminates the swinging mass that admits most of the 31.04 cm2 of trunk and hip contribution, so total demand would fall by more than the 23 percent load reduction alone suggests."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric initiation from the deep bottom: elbows flexed to roughly 90 degrees, humerus extended to approximately 45 degrees behind the torso line, torso held at a forward lean of 30 degrees, before the elbows extend and the shoulder moment arm shortens.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 193.52, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.9000, "E_ND": 0.9200, "E_LoA": 0.9800, "E_mech": 0.7709, "muscle": "pectoralis_major_sternal", "rationale": "Prime mover for the 110 N-m humeral flexion and adduction moment, maximally lengthened at the deep bottom with the humerus extended behind the torso. The forward lean places the required action directly along its line of pull, making this its highest engagement in any pressing exercise in the library.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 53.48, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 41.23}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.9200, "E_ND": 0.9500, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.8303, "muscle": "triceps_lateral_head", "rationale": "Mono-articular elbow extensor acting directly against the 48 N-m elbow moment at a favourable joint angle, and near-saturated at this load.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 36.10, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 29.97}, {"E_LT": 0.7500, "E_MA": 0.8800, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.8500, "E_mech": 0.4769, "muscle": "triceps_long_head", "rationale": "Doubly compromised: the deeply extended humeral position shortens it across the shoulder, and its shoulder-extension action actively opposes the humeral flexion the movement requires. Its lowest engagement of any pressing exercise here.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.55, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 20.77}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.8800, "E_mech": 0.6395, "muscle": "deltoid_anterior", "rationale": "Major contributor to humeral flexion and maximally lengthened at the bottom, where the humerus sits well behind the torso. Alignment is slightly reduced because the forward lean shifts part of the demand toward adduction rather than pure flexion.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 30.46, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 19.48}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.9200, "E_ND": 0.9500, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.8303, "muscle": "triceps_medial_head", "rationale": "Mechanically equivalent to the lateral head against the elbow flexion moment and likewise near-saturated.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 15.59, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 12.94}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.3500, "E_LoA": 0.3000, "E_mech": 0.0536, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary stabiliser: the deeply extended humeral position under 1000 N is the least congruent joint configuration in any pressing exercise here, requiring substantial cuff compression to keep the head centred and transmit load into the scapula.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 9.15}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.6000, "E_LoA": 0.8000, "E_mech": 0.3672, "muscle": "rectus_abdominis", "rationale": "Included as mechanically necessary rather than postural: the forward lean must be actively held against gravity while the belt load swings below the pelvis, and losing that position collapses the shoulder geometry the movement depends on.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.29, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 8.92}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.8500, "E_mech": 0.5852, "muscle": "pectoralis_major_clavicular", "rationale": "Assists humeral flexion from the deeply extended start, though the downward-and-forward press direction favours the sternal fibres over the clavicular in a leaning dip.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 13.52, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 7.91}, {"E_LT": 0.8000, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.4500, "E_LoA": 0.3000, "E_mech": 0.0756, "muscle": "iliopsoas", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary load-transmitting element: it holds the hips flexed and the legs swept back in the fixed position the forward lean requires, against the 30 N-m moment from the suspended belt load.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 102.23, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 7.73}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.3600, "E_mech": 0.0918, "muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "Included as a load-transmitting element: bilateral tension resists trunk extension and rotational drift so the body remains a rigid link between the pressing arms and the hanging load.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 7.50}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.7500, "E_LoA": 0.9000, "E_mech": 0.4860, "muscle": "trapezius_lower", "rationale": "Genuine task torque rather than stabilisation: the scapulae must be actively depressed to hold the torso up between the bars, and its depression vector matches that requirement directly.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 14.98, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 7.28}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.4500, "E_mech": 0.1418, "muscle": "serratus_anterior", "rationale": "Included as a load-transmitting stabiliser: scapular fixation against the thorax is required to route 1000 N from the arms into the trunk, with no bench or pad to absorb any of the reaction force.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 36.03, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 5.11}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.7000, "E_LoA": 0.9500, "E_mech": 0.4190, "muscle": "pectoralis_minor", "rationale": "Best-aligned pressing role it receives in the library: its scapular depression action directly matches the requirement to hold the body up between the bars, unlike in bench pressing where it contributes only anchoring force.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 10.81, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 4.53}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.4500, "E_LoA": 0.3500, "E_mech": 0.0937, "muscle": "latissimus_dorsi", "rationale": "Included for its scapular and shoulder-girdle depression contribution, which is mechanically required to support the body between the bars. Its humeral extension action opposes the press itself, which is why alignment is low.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.85, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 4.11}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.3500, "E_LoA": 0.3400, "E_mech": 0.0607, "muscle": "adductor_longus_brevis", "rationale": "Included as a load-transmitting element: active adduction holds the legs together so the dip belt load remains a single rigid mass rather than two independently swinging limbs.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 66.55, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 4.04}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.4800, "E_mech": 0.1296, "muscle": "transverse_abdominis", "rationale": "Generates the intra-abdominal pressure that stiffens the trunk into a rigid link between the pressing arms and the suspended belt load.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 21.97, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 2.85}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["110 kg is interpreted as 85 kg body mass plus 25 kg of added external load on a dip belt.", "The forearms and hands are fixed on the bars and are not lifted by the pressing musculature, so the moved mass is ~102 kg, giving ~1000 N effective after a small allowance for controlled acceleration.", "Parallel bars at roughly shoulder width, hands beside the hips in a neutral grip.", "Deliberate forward torso lean of ~30 degrees with the hips flexed and the feet swept back, which is the defining feature of the chest-emphasis variant.", "The forward lean increases the horizontal distance from the glenohumeral axis to the hand support line to ~0.11 m per side, raising the shoulder moment relative to an upright dip.", "Forearms held near-vertical at the bottom, giving an elbow external moment arm of ~0.048 m per side.", "Internal moment arms: humeral flexion and horizontal adduction ~0.05 m, triceps elbow extension ~0.025 m, scapular depressors ~0.055 m.", "The required humeral action is flexion and adduction from a deeply extended position, which is the mirror of a decline press and favours the sternal pectoral fibres.", "Scapular depression is genuine task torque: the body must be actively held up between the bars against elevation, and the scapulae must be prevented from riding toward the ears under 1000 N.", "The dip belt hangs freely at ~0.35 m below the pelvis and the legs are unsupported, so active lumbopelvic torque is required to hold the body rigid and prevent swing.", "The hands press down on fixed bars in compression rather than suspending the body, so grip is not a meaningful load-transmission bottleneck.", "110 kg total is a demanding but realistic 8-12 rep load for an advanced athlete of this mass, equating to roughly 29 percent added bodyweight."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "glenohumeral_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 110.00}, {"joint": "elbow_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 48.00}, {"joint": "scapulothoracic_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 33.00}, {"joint": "lumbopelvic", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 30.00}], "nominal_load_kg": 110.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 1000.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 193.52, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 193.50}
35	wide_pronated_grip_pull_up	Wide-Grip Pull-Up	Wide Pronated-Grip Pull-Up	Upper	Back_V	Heavy_Compound	Bodyweight	85.00	190.75	{"obliques": 4.09, "iliopsoas": 2.04, "rhomboids": 15.36, "brachialis": 28.86, "teres_major": 3.44, "rotator_cuffs": 10.42, "wrist_flexors": 29.13, "biceps_brachii": 15.13, "brachioradialis": 4.51, "trapezius_lower": 9.51, "latissimus_dorsi": 34.31, "rectus_abdominis": 5.15, "trapezius_middle": 15.19, "deltoid_posterior": 12.32, "pectoralis_major_sternal": 1.29}	{"grip": "Use a closed pronated grip with the thumbs wrapped around the bar. Grip width should be distinctly wider than shoulder width but not so extreme that it prevents controlled shoulder motion or substantially shortens the usable range.", "tldr": {"focus": "Keep the chest moving toward the bar while the elbows travel down and outward rather than turning the repetition into a narrow tucked-elbow pull-up.", "setup": "Hang from a fixed bar with a closed pronated grip clearly wider than shoulder width, elbows extended, and the body vertically controlled beneath the hands.", "execution": "Pull the body upward by driving the upper arms down and back while flexing the elbows, then lower under control to the starting hang.", "stop_when": "Stop when another repetition would require leg drive, trunk swinging, marked elbow tucking, or a materially shortened top or bottom range."}, "stance": "", "bracing": "Maintain abdominal and gluteal tension sufficient to limit lumbar extension, pelvic swing, and leg movement. Keep the ribcage and pelvis controlled while allowing the small torso-angle changes naturally associated with a strict wide-grip pull-up.", "overview": "A bodyweight pull-up performed with a bilateral pronated grip substantially wider than shoulder width. The defining mechanics are vertical pulling with the humeri traveling downward from an overhead abducted position while the elbows remain relatively wide compared with neutral- or narrow-grip pull-ups. The torso may incline slightly backward as the chest approaches the bar, but deliberate kipping or large body swing is not part of the variant.", "eccentric": "Lower the body under control by extending the elbows and allowing the upper arms to return overhead while the scapulae upwardly rotate and protract appropriately. Descend toward full elbow extension and a controlled overhead hang without dropping rapidly or using the bottom position to generate swing for the next repetition.", "concentric": "Pull the body upward by driving the upper arms downward and somewhat backward from the wide overhead position while flexing the elbows. Allow the scapulae to retract and downwardly rotate as the chest rises toward the bar. Keep the elbows traveling broadly down and outward rather than aggressively tucking them toward the ribs, and avoid initiating the ascent with a leg kick or trunk swing.", "tempo_notes": "Avoid kipping, bouncing from the bottom, or dropping rapidly through the eccentric. Re-establish a controlled hang before initiating the next repetition.", "end_position": "Finish the concentric phase when the upper chest approaches the bar and the elbows have traveled below and behind the hands as far as the individual's shoulder structure permits without craning the neck, excessive backward lean, or loss of control.", "safety_notes": ["Use a secure fixed bar and maintain a closed grip throughout the set.", "Do not force an excessively wide hand position if it produces shoulder pain or instability.", "Control the descent into the overhead position rather than dropping abruptly into full shoulder elevation."], "external_cues": "Pull your upper chest toward the bar while keeping the legs quiet beneath you.", "internal_cues": "Drive the elbows down and out; keep the ribs controlled; let the shoulder blades move rather than shrugging rigidly.", "common_mistakes": ["Using an excessively wide grip that materially shortens the range or forces uncomfortable shoulder positioning.", "Tucking the elbows tightly toward the ribs and changing the movement toward a narrow-grip pull-up.", "Kicking the legs or swinging the pelvis to initiate the ascent.", "Craning the neck over the bar instead of raising the torso through the intended pulling motion.", "Dropping rapidly into the bottom and using the resulting swing for the next repetition.", "Shrugging the shoulders without producing coordinated scapular depression and upper-arm movement."], "equipment_setup": "Use a fixed overhead bar high enough to permit full unsupported suspension without the feet contacting the floor.", "range_of_motion": "Begin from a controlled near-full or full elbow-extension hang and pull through the largest range that preserves the wide pronated-grip mechanics. The top should bring the upper chest close to the bar when possible, but forcing chin or chest height through cervical extension, major trunk lean, or elbow-path distortion is not required.", "rir_0_definition": "RIR 0 is reached when no additional repetition can be completed through the defined wide-grip range with controlled suspension, even if the body could still be raised by kipping, swinging, tucking the elbows substantially, craning the neck, or shortening the range.", "rir_1_indicators": "Approximately one technically valid repetition may remain when ascent velocity slows markedly and the final portion toward the bar becomes difficult, but the lifter can still maintain the wide elbow path, quiet lower body, controlled trunk position, and established top and bottom range.", "individualization": "Grip width, degree of torso inclination, top height, and amount of scapular motion may vary with shoulder width, humeral length, thoracic mobility, and shoulder anatomy. The grip should remain clearly wider than shoulder width and the elbows should retain a relatively wide path; moderate adjustment is acceptable if needed to preserve comfortable, controlled shoulder mechanics.", "plane_of_movement": "Primarily frontal-to-scapular-plane glenohumeral adduction with a sagittal-plane extension component, combined with sagittal-plane elbow flexion and scapular downward rotation and retraction.", "starting_position": "Hang beneath the bar with the elbows extended, hands placed clearly wider than shoulder width, palms facing away, and the body relatively still. Allow the shoulders to elevate naturally in the bottom position while maintaining enough shoulder-girdle control to avoid an unstable passive collapse. Keep the legs together or comfortably aligned beneath the torso.", "technical_failure": "Technical failure occurs when another repetition cannot be completed through the established wide-grip range without materially changing the mechanics. Observable breakdowns include leg kicking, large trunk swing, excessive lumbar extension, marked elbow tucking, neck craning to clear the bar, loss of the controlled bottom hang, or substantial reduction in top height.", "primary_joint_actions": "During the concentric phase, the glenohumeral joints adduct and extend from an overhead position, the elbows flex, and the scapulae downwardly rotate and retract while remaining controlled on the thorax. The trunk stabilizes against swinging and excessive lumbar extension."}	{}	{https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eDP_OOhMTZ4,https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TG3YUdEXpig}	Bilateral	{"slug": "wide_pronated_grip_pull_up", "notes": ["This completes a three-point vertical pull comparison that isolates two variables cleanly. Against weighted_neutral_grip_pull_up at 245.71 cm2, removing 15 kg and switching to a wide pronated grip drops total demand to 190.75. Against neutral_grip_lat_pulldown at 191.51 cm2, the totals are nearly identical but the composition differs sharply: the pulldown allocates 24.32 cm2 to biceps against 15.13 here, while this exercise adds 11.28 cm2 of trunk and hip rigging that the thigh pads eliminate.", "Biceps_brachii falls to E_mech 0.3800, the lowest of any vertical pull in the library, against 0.6318 in the weighted neutral pull-up and 0.6108 in the neutral pulldown. Pronation is doing almost all of that work, with E_MA at 0.5500 against 0.7000 for neutral. Brachialis correspondingly takes a larger share, and the brachialis-to-biceps ratio rises to 1.91 against 1.34 in the neutral pull-up. This reproduces without special-casing the standard coaching claim that wide pronated pull-ups are the least arm-dependent vertical pull.", "Deltoid_posterior nearly doubles its share relative to neutral-grip pulls, reaching E_mech 0.4997 against 0.3470. The wide abducted arm path shifts the required humeral moment toward horizontal abduction, which is its line of action. Trapezius_middle rises for the same reason while trapezius_lower falls, since the wide grip trades scapular depression emphasis for retraction emphasis.", "Latissimus_dorsi barely moves across all three vertical pulls at 34.50, 35.83 and 34.31 cm2 despite effective loads ranging from 735 to 902 N. The muscle is close to its usable ceiling in every overhead pulling position, so load differences within this range are absorbed by the surrounding musculature rather than by the prime mover. This is a useful signature: exercises that saturate their prime mover discriminate poorly on load.", "The 85 kg input matched body mass exactly, so no interpretation was needed here. This contrasts with the weighted variant where 100 kg required assuming 85 plus 15 added. The ambiguity flagged there remains: a separate added_load_kg column would make bodyweight exercises unambiguous and is worth adding before dips and belt squats enter the library.", "Trunk and hip contribution totals 11.28 cm2 against 40.55 in the weighted variant, a much larger drop than the 15 percent load reduction alone implies. The mechanism is that a dip belt hangs and swings below the pelvis, creating a genuine dynamic moment, whereas unweighted bodyweight is distributed along the body axis and requires only modest bracing. Any future weighted bodyweight exercise should apply this distinction rather than scaling trunk demand with total load.", "Pronators_supinators and the leg-rigging muscles from the weighted variant were deliberately excluded here. A pronated grip on a fixed straight bar mechanically constrains forearm rotation, so no active rotational control is required, and without a belt load there is no swinging mass requiring the legs to be held as a rigid unit.", "A wide neutral-grip or wide supinated pull-up must not inherit these values, since grip orientation and width interact rather than adding independently. A narrow pronated chin-up would sit between this and the neutral variants on biceps allocation.", "An assisted or band-assisted pull-up must not inherit these values. Assistance reduces effective load non-linearly through the range, being greatest at the stretched bottom where this evaluation places the limiting phase."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric initiation from the dead hang: humerus fully elevated and abducted overhead at roughly 165 degrees in the scapular-to-frontal plane, elbows near-extended, scapulae elevated and upwardly rotated, before the elbows drive down and out and the humeral moment arm shortens.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 190.75, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 1.0000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9500, "E_LoA": 0.9690, "E_mech": 0.7824, "muscle": "latissimus_dorsi", "rationale": "Prime mover, at maximal stretched length in the dead hang. Marginally below the neutral-grip variants because the abducted wide-grip position shifts part of the required moment out of its optimal adduction line, though the reduction is small since adduction remains dominant.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.85, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 34.31}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.8800, "E_LoA": 0.8950, "E_mech": 0.5355, "muscle": "wrist_flexors", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary load-transmitting element: the entire 766 N passes through the grip with no anchoring. Pronated grip on a straight bar is less secure than parallel handles, partly offsetting the lighter load relative to the weighted neutral variant.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 29.13}, {"E_LT": 0.9800, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.9500, "E_LoA": 0.9390, "E_mech": 0.6552, "muscle": "brachialis", "rationale": "Dominant elbow flexor and entirely unaffected by pronation, which is why it takes a substantially larger share here than in any neutral-grip pull. Alignment slightly reduced because the inward forearm angle diverts part of its action from pure vertical pulling.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 28.86}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9200, "E_LoA": 0.7320, "E_mech": 0.5438, "muscle": "rhomboids", "rationale": "Genuine task torque via downward rotation of the elevated scapula. The wide grip adds a retraction component that suits it slightly better than a narrow neutral grip, partly offsetting the lighter load.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 28.25, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 15.36}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9200, "E_LoA": 0.8240, "E_mech": 0.5802, "muscle": "trapezius_middle", "rationale": "Higher alignment than in neutral-grip pulls because the wide abducted arm path introduces a real horizontal retraction component alongside the scapular depression.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 26.18, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 15.19}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.5500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.8080, "E_mech": 0.3800, "muscle": "biceps_brachii", "rationale": "Sharply penalised by full pronation, which reduces its flexion leverage by roughly a quarter. This is the defining difference from the neutral-grip variants and the mechanical reason wide pronated pull-ups are the least biceps-intensive vertical pull.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 15.13}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.8250, "E_mech": 0.4997, "muscle": "deltoid_posterior", "rationale": "Substantially more engaged than in neutral-grip pulls: the wide abducted arm path shifts the required humeral moment toward horizontal abduction and extension, which is its line of action.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.65, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 12.32}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.4000, "E_LoA": 0.2990, "E_mech": 0.0610, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary stabiliser: the dead hang applies the full 766 N as distraction to the fully elevated humeral head. The wide abducted position is a less congruent joint configuration than a neutral hang, which raises the compression requirement relative to the load.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 10.42}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9200, "E_LoA": 0.8540, "E_mech": 0.6346, "muscle": "trapezius_lower", "rationale": "Well aligned with the required scapular depression and maximally lengthened at the dead hang, though the wide grip's retraction emphasis dilutes its share relative to a neutral pull-up.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 14.98, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 9.51}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.5545, "E_mech": 0.2121, "muscle": "rectus_abdominis", "rationale": "Included as mechanically necessary rather than postural: active trunk flexion torque holds the body rigid and prevents swing. Roughly half the weighted variant's demand because no belt load hangs and swings below the pelvis.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.29, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 5.15}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.6720, "E_mech": 0.4023, "muscle": "brachioradialis", "rationale": "Full pronation moves it away from its optimal mid-position orientation, cutting its contribution to roughly 60 percent of the neutral-grip value.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 11.22, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 4.51}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.4500, "E_LoA": 0.2180, "E_mech": 0.0500, "muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "Included as a load-transmitting element: bilateral tension resists trunk extension and rotational drift so the body remains a rigid link. Half the weighted variant because there is no swinging external mass.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 4.09}, {"E_LT": 1.0000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9200, "E_LoA": 0.9770, "E_mech": 0.7639, "muscle": "teres_major", "rationale": "Acts in parallel with latissimus dorsi on humeral adduction and extension, maximally lengthened at the dead hang and well suited to the abducted starting position.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 4.50, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 3.44}, {"E_LT": 0.8000, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.4000, "E_LoA": 0.0891, "E_mech": 0.0200, "muscle": "iliopsoas", "rationale": "Included as a load-transmitting element holding the pelvis and legs in a fixed position, at a quarter of the weighted variant's demand because no belt load creates a hanging moment below the pelvis.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 102.23, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 2.04}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.5500, "E_LoA": 0.0661, "E_mech": 0.0242, "muscle": "pectoralis_major_sternal", "rationale": "Retains a small humeral adduction component from the overhead dead hang, but the wide abducted grip position substantially reduces the alignment it had in a narrower neutral-grip pull.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 53.48, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 1.29}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["85 kg is interpreted as body mass alone with no added external load, matching the reference athlete exactly.", "The arms and forearms above the elbow are not lifted by the pulling musculature, so the moved mass is ~78 kg, giving ~766 N effective after a small allowance for controlled acceleration.", "Wide pronated grip at approximately 1.5 times biacromial width on a straight fixed bar.", "The wide grip places the humerus in a more abducted position, moving the movement toward the frontal plane and shortening the effective humeral moment arm for adduction to ~0.174 m per side.", "The wide grip also reduces the vertical range of motion, so the athlete travels less distance but through a mechanically less favourable humeral path.", "Elbows near-extended at the limiting phase; with the wide grip the forearm is angled inward rather than vertical, reducing the elbow external moment arm to ~0.048 m per side.", "Pronated grip fixes the forearm in full pronation, which reduces biceps_brachii flexion leverage by roughly 20 to 25 percent relative to a neutral grip and eliminates any supination contribution.", "Internal moment arms: latissimus shoulder adduction ~0.058 m overhead, elbow flexors ~0.030 m at the near-extended elbow, scapular depressors and retractors ~0.055 m.", "Scapular depression and downward rotation from the elevated dead hang is genuine task torque against the full suspended body mass.", "No thigh pads and no added load: the trunk and hip musculature generate real but modest torque to hold the body rigid and prevent swing, substantially less than the weighted variant where a belt load hangs and swings below the pelvis.", "Grip without straps: finger and wrist flexor force is the sole path by which the entire 766 N reaches the arm, and the pronated position on a straight bar is a less secure grip than parallel handles.", "Bodyweight alone at 85 kg is a realistic 8-12 rep load for an advanced athlete of this mass in the wide pronated position, which is mechanically harder than a neutral grip."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "glenohumeral_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 133.00}, {"joint": "scapulothoracic_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 71.00}, {"joint": "elbow_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 36.80}, {"joint": "lumbopelvic", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 16.00}], "nominal_load_kg": 85.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 766.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 190.75, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 190.50}
12	chest_supported_dumbbell_row	Chest-Supported Dumbbell Row	Incline Bench Chest-Supported Dumbbell Row	Upper	Back_3D	Secondary_Compound	Dumbbell	60.00	175.19	{"rhomboids": 17.79, "brachialis": 30.02, "teres_major": 0.97, "rotator_cuffs": 7.32, "wrist_flexors": 24.09, "biceps_brachii": 19.24, "brachioradialis": 5.32, "trapezius_lower": 6.53, "latissimus_dorsi": 32.19, "trapezius_middle": 16.49, "deltoid_posterior": 15.23}	{"grip": "Neutral or pronated grip on the dumbbells, adjustable based on shoulder comfort, with the wrists held in a straight, stable alignment throughout the pull.", "tldr": {"focus": "Keep the chest pinned to the bench pad throughout the pull instead of pushing the torso up off the pad.", "setup": "Lie prone on an incline bench set to a moderate angle with the chest firmly supported and dumbbells hanging directly below the shoulders.", "execution": "Pull both dumbbells up toward the torso by driving the elbows back and up, then lower them under control to a full stretch.", "stop_when": "The chest lifts off the bench pad to help initiate the pull or the dumbbells stop rising despite continued effort."}, "stance": "Feet positioned on the floor or bench supports for overall stability, not contributing to the pulling action.", "bracing": "Torso pressed firmly and consistently into the bench pad throughout the set, preventing the chest from lifting off the pad to assist the pull.", "overview": "This is the chest-supported dumbbell row performed prone on an incline bench, with the chest and torso stabilized against the pad throughout the set. This external support removes the isometric lower-back and hip-hinge demand present in a standard bent-over dumbbell row, isolating the pulling action to the upper back and arms. The incline angle also fixes the torso position, distinguishing it from variants performed on a flat or steeply angled bench where the resistance vector and torso-to-pull angle differ.", "eccentric": "The dumbbells lower under control back to a fully hanging position through glenohumeral flexion and elbow extension, with the chest remaining pinned to the pad and the shoulder blades returning to a neutral position.", "concentric": "The dumbbells are pulled from a hanging position up toward the torso through simultaneous glenohumeral extension and elbow flexion, with the elbows driving back and slightly up while the chest remains pressed against the pad.", "tempo_notes": "Maintain a controlled pull without using a jerking or bouncing motion at the bottom of the range to generate momentum; a brief pause at the top may be used to emphasize scapular retraction.", "end_position": "Dumbbells drawn up alongside the torso with the elbows past the plane of the back and the shoulder blades retracted.", "safety_notes": ["Ensure the bench pad adequately supports the chest and ribcage to avoid excessive pressure on a small contact area.", "Select a bench angle and dumbbell weight that allow the arms to hang and extend fully without the dumbbells contacting the floor or bench base."], "external_cues": "Pull the dumbbells toward the hips; keep the chest glued to the pad throughout the set.", "internal_cues": "Drive the elbows back and up toward the ceiling; think of pulling with the upper back rather than the arms.", "common_mistakes": ["Lifting the chest off the bench pad to generate momentum for the pull.", "Using a jerking motion of the arms instead of a controlled pull.", "Shrugging the shoulders upward instead of driving the elbows back.", "Allowing one side to lag or rotate the torso to complete the pull unevenly.", "Stopping the pull well short of the torso, limiting the usable range of motion."], "equipment_setup": "Incline bench set to a moderate angle, positioned to allow the arms to hang freely below the chest with the dumbbells clearing the floor or a rack at the bottom of the range.", "range_of_motion": "From full arm extension with the dumbbells hanging freely at the bottom to the dumbbells reaching the torso at the top, limited by the point at which further pulling requires the chest to lift off the pad.", "rir_0_definition": "Inability to pull the dumbbells to the torso through glenohumeral extension and elbow flexion while keeping the chest against the pad, as distinct from continuing to move the dumbbells by lifting the chest off the pad.", "rir_1_indicators": "Reduced height of the dumbbells at the top of the pull, slower concentric speed, and increasing effort to keep the chest against the pad, while the general pulling path remains intact.", "individualization": "Bench incline angle, grip orientation, and elbow path relative to the torso may vary moderately with shoulder structure and comfort, provided the chest remains supported against the pad throughout the set and the pull is driven by the upper back rather than momentum.", "plane_of_movement": "Primarily sagittal-plane glenohumeral extension and elbow flexion, with the torso fixed against the bench and contributing no independent movement.", "starting_position": "Prone on the bench with the chest, sternum, and lower ribs supported against the pad, forehead or chin comfortably positioned off the top of the pad, shoulder blades in a neutral, non-retracted position, arms hanging vertically with dumbbells held below the shoulders.", "technical_failure": "The point at which the chest lifts off the bench pad to assist the pull, the dumbbells can no longer be raised toward the torso despite continued effort, or the shoulders shrug upward to substitute for elbow-driven pulling.", "primary_joint_actions": "Glenohumeral extension with a horizontal-extension component as the upper arms move from in front of the torso to alongside or slightly behind it; elbow flexion driven by the biceps and brachialis; scapular retraction as the dumbbells approach the torso; the spine and hips remain fixed against the bench throughout."}	{}	{https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tZUYS7X50so,https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ym-Mp8tCF00,https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TXjeZsqCVsg}	Bilateral	{"slug": "chest_supported_dumbbell_row", "notes": ["This exercise is the cleanest available isolation of the upper-back pulling demand from the Pendlay row, and the comparison validates the model. Total demand falls from 579.05 to 175.19 cm2, a 70 percent reduction, entirely because the chest support eliminates the hip and lumbar moments. The upper-body allocation itself is nearly proportional to load: latissimus contributes 32.19 against 33.64 cm2 at two thirds the weight, since the shorter effective moment arm from the more upright bench angle partly offsets the lighter dumbbells.", "Gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, the hamstrings, and erector spinae were all deliberately excluded, having contributed a combined 347.45 cm2 to the Pendlay row. The chest pad and bench carry both the trunk mass and the reaction force from the pull, so these muscles generate no net task torque under the stated inclusion rules.", "Obliques and transverse abdominis were likewise excluded. There is no saturated extensor moment for intra-abdominal pressure to offload here, so the load-transmission rationale that admitted them in the Pendlay row and pull-through does not apply.", "No muscle saturates and none exceeds E_mech 0.74, in contrast to the Pendlay row where erector spinae, gluteus maximus, and the shoulder extensors all hit their ceilings. The bottleneck in a chest-supported row is genuinely the pulling musculature rather than trunk capacity, which is the mechanical basis for programming it when upper-back demand should be isolated.", "Elbow flexor demand at 54.58 cm2 combined is a large share of the total, and the neutral dumbbell grip raises the biceps and brachioradialis allocation relative to a pronated barbell row. E_mech for biceps_brachii rises from 0.5148 to 0.4833 in absolute terms only because the load is lighter; the grip advantage is visible in the ratio to brachialis, which improves from 0.71 to 0.64.", "A true single-arm chest-supported row must not inherit these values. The bilateral convention cancels the anti-rotation demand that a unilateral version imposes on the obliques, even with the chest supported.", "A flatter bench angle, closer to 15 degrees, would lengthen the effective humeral moment arm and raise glenohumeral demand while further reducing the achievable load; a near-vertical bench would shift the pattern toward a seated row. Neither variant should inherit these values."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric mid-pull: dumbbells roughly level with the torso, elbow flexed to ~110 degrees, humerus extended ~40 degrees from vertical, before the scapulae reach full retraction and the humeral moment arm shortens.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 175.19, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9500, "E_LoA": 0.9570, "E_mech": 0.7341, "muscle": "latissimus_dorsi", "rationale": "Prime mover for the 111 N-m glenohumeral extension moment, well lengthened at the bottom of the stroke, with the required humeral action closely matching its line of pull.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.85, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 32.19}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9500, "E_LoA": 0.9375, "E_mech": 0.6815, "muscle": "brachialis", "rationale": "Largest elbow-flexion contributor and unaffected by forearm orientation, making it the dominant flexor against the 38.4 N-m elbow moment.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 30.02}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.7663, "E_mech": 0.4428, "muscle": "wrist_flexors", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary load-transmitting element: without straps, finger and wrist flexor force is the sole path by which the 600 N reaches the arm. Demand is lower than the Pendlay row in proportion to the reduced load.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 24.09}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.8362, "E_mech": 0.4833, "muscle": "biceps_brachii", "rationale": "Meaningful elbow flexor, favoured by the neutral grip relative to a pronated barbell row, though some capacity is diverted to controlling forearm rotation.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 19.24}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.9147, "E_mech": 0.6298, "muscle": "rhomboids", "rationale": "Scapular retraction is genuine task torque: the scapula must be actively drawn back against a load pulling it into protraction, and the chest pad does not block that motion.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 28.25, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 17.79}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.9147, "E_mech": 0.6298, "muscle": "trapezius_middle", "rationale": "Primary horizontal scapular retractor, directly aligned with the required scapulothoracic moment.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 26.18, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 16.49}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.8974, "E_mech": 0.6179, "muscle": "deltoid_posterior", "rationale": "Contributes shoulder extension and horizontal abduction as the elbow travels back; strongly aligned with the required humeral moment.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.65, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 15.23}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.3000, "E_LoA": 0.2800, "E_mech": 0.0428, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary stabiliser: the hanging dumbbells apply a distraction load to the humerus that cuff compression must resist for force to transmit into the scapula. Engagement is below the Pendlay row because the load is lighter and the supported torso removes trunk-driven perturbation.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 7.32}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.7539, "E_mech": 0.4358, "muscle": "trapezius_lower", "rationale": "Contributes retraction and resists the downward pull on the scapula in the inclined torso position.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 14.98, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 6.53}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.7745, "E_mech": 0.4740, "muscle": "brachioradialis", "rationale": "Effective elbow flexor at the ~110 degree limiting angle, and the neutral grip places it near its optimal mechanical position.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 11.22, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 5.32}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.2965, "E_mech": 0.2155, "muscle": "teres_major", "rationale": "Acts in parallel with latissimus dorsi on the humeral extension moment; small cross-section limits absolute contribution.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 4.50, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 0.97}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["Bilateral convention: 60 kg total is interpreted as 30 kg per dumbbell.", "Barbell-equivalent weight 588 N plus ~2 percent for controlled acceleration: ~600 N effective, ~300 N per limb.", "Incline bench set to ~30 degrees from horizontal with the chest and abdomen fully supported; the dumbbells hang and travel close to vertical.", "Humerus length ~0.32 m: at 40 degrees of extension the glenohumeral external moment arm is ~0.185 m per side.", "Forearms near-vertical at the limiting phase, giving an elbow external moment arm of ~0.064 m per side.", "Internal moment arms: latissimus shoulder extension ~0.06 m, elbow flexors ~0.042 m at 110 degrees, scapular retractors ~0.055 m.", "Neutral or semi-pronated dumbbell grip without straps: grip force is mechanically required to transmit the full 600 N.", "Chest support removes all hip, lumbar, and lower-limb moment requirements; the bench carries the trunk mass and the reaction force from the pull, so no trunk musculature generates net task torque.", "The pad restricts torso movement but does not restrict scapular retraction against the load, so scapulothoracic torque remains genuine task demand.", "60 kg total on a chest-supported row is a realistic 8-12 rep working load precisely because the eliminated trunk demand allows the upper-back musculature to be the limiter."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "glenohumeral_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 111.00}, {"joint": "scapulothoracic_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 42.00}, {"joint": "elbow_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 38.40}], "nominal_load_kg": 60.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 600.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 175.19, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 175.00}
48	bodyweight_crunch	Crunch	Floor Bodyweight Abdominal Crunch	Core	Core	Isolation	Bodyweight	22.00	36.40	{"obliques": 9.71, "rectus_abdominis": 20.77, "sternocleidomastoid": 5.92}	{"grip": "", "tldr": {"focus": "Initiate the movement by flexing the thoracic spine rather than pulling with the arms or hip flexors.", "setup": "Lie supine on the floor with knees bent, feet flat, and fingertips lightly supporting the head without pulling on the neck.", "execution": "Curl the upper spine off the floor by shortening the distance between the ribcage and pelvis, then lower back down under control.", "stop_when": "The upper back can no longer lift off the floor without pulling on the neck or using momentum."}, "stance": "Knees bent with feet flat and hip-width apart, providing a stable base without contributing to the movement.", "bracing": "Abdominal bracing initiated before the curl begins, with the pelvis kept still and the lower back maintaining contact with the floor throughout the repetition.", "overview": "This is the standard floor bodyweight crunch, a spinal-flexion exercise in which only the upper torso curls off the floor while the lower back remains in contact with the ground. It is distinguished from a sit-up in that the hips do not flex and the torso does not travel through a full range toward the thighs, isolating the movement to trunk flexion of the upper spine rather than combined hip and trunk flexion.", "eccentric": "The torso lowers back toward the floor under control by reversing the spinal flexion, returning the shoulder blades to the floor while the pelvis and lower back remain motionless throughout.", "concentric": "The upper spine curls forward off the floor by shortening the distance between the ribcage and the pelvis, with the shoulder blades clearing the floor while the lower back and pelvis remain stationary and in contact with the ground.", "tempo_notes": "The repetition should not rely on a jerking motion of the arms or head to initiate the curl; a brief pause at the top may be used to emphasize peak spinal flexion.", "end_position": "Shoulder blades lifted clear of the floor with the upper spine flexed and the lower back still in contact with the ground.", "safety_notes": ["Keep the hands supporting rather than pulling the head to avoid unnecessary cervical strain.", "Maintain a neutral neck position relative to the torso throughout the repetition."], "external_cues": "Bring the ribcage toward the hips; keep the lower back glued to the floor.", "internal_cues": "Shorten the distance between the ribcage and the pelvis; think of curling the spine rather than lifting the shoulders.", "common_mistakes": ["Pulling on the neck or head with the hands to assist the curl.", "Allowing the pelvis and lower back to lift off the floor, converting the movement into a hip-flexion-dominant pattern.", "Using momentum from throwing the elbows or head forward instead of spinal flexion.", "Flexing the cervical spine forward instead of keeping the neck neutral relative to the torso.", "Stopping the curl very early in the range, limiting meaningful thoracic flexion."], "equipment_setup": "", "range_of_motion": "From the shoulder blades resting on the floor at the bottom to the shoulder blades lifted clear of the floor at the top, limited to the degree of thoracic flexion achievable while the lower back and pelvis remain stationary.", "rir_0_definition": "Inability to lift the shoulder blades clear of the floor through spinal flexion alone while keeping the lower back and pelvis stationary, as distinct from continuing to move by pulling on the neck or flexing the hips.", "rir_1_indicators": "Reduced height of the curl, increasing reliance on momentum to initiate the movement, and greater effort to keep the lower back on the floor, while the pelvis remains stationary and the neck stays neutral.", "individualization": "Hand position (crossed on the chest, lightly behind the head, or extended alongside the body) and the exact bend of the knees may vary based on comfort, provided the lower back remains on the floor and the movement is initiated by spinal flexion rather than momentum or neck pull.", "plane_of_movement": "Sagittal-plane spinal flexion, limited to the thoracic and upper lumbar segments.", "starting_position": "Supine on the floor with knees bent to roughly ninety degrees, feet flat on the floor, lower back maintaining light contact with the floor, fingertips resting lightly behind or beside the head, elbows out to the sides.", "technical_failure": "The point at which the upper torso can no longer be curled off the floor without pulling on the neck with the hands, using momentum from the arms or head, or allowing the pelvis and lower back to lift and convert the movement into hip flexion.", "primary_joint_actions": "Flexion of the thoracic and upper lumbar spine; the hip joints remain in a fixed, flexed position throughout with no significant hip flexion or extension occurring during the repetition; the cervical spine is held in a neutral position relative to the torso rather than actively flexing."}	{}	{https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Y0nXmTZ1Ibs,https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfQ4EHM3TeQ,https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=acj52MXBaeo}	Bilateral	{"slug": "bodyweight_crunch", "notes": ["Rectus abdominis saturates: it supplies roughly 36 N-m of the 47.5 N-m requirement at near-maximal E_mech, so the obliques were allocated the residual moment rather than the rectus being scaled beyond capacity.", "Iliopsoas was deliberately excluded. With knees flexed and feet unanchored the pelvis stays grounded and no hip flexion moment is required, so the hip flexors produce no net task torque.", "Transverse abdominis was deliberately excluded. It generates intra-abdominal pressure but no spinal flexion moment, and unlike a loaded hinge there is no saturated extensor moment here for IAP to offload.", "A sit-up or anchored-feet variant must not inherit these values. In those versions iliopsoas becomes a large prime mover and total demand roughly doubles.", "At 36.40 cm2 this exercise serves as a useful low-end calibration point, about 6 percent of the barbell Pendlay row and 23 percent of the flat barbell bench press."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric initiation: shoulder blades just clearing the floor with the trunk still near-horizontal, where the moved segment's centre of mass sits at its greatest horizontal distance from the thoracolumbar flexion axis.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 36.40, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.9500, "E_ND": 1.0000, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.8550, "muscle": "rectus_abdominis", "rationale": "Prime mover for the 47.5 N-m spinal flexion moment, slightly lengthened at the flat starting position and acting directly along the required joint-moment direction. Effectively saturated: it supplies roughly 36 N-m of the requirement, close to its full mechanical capacity.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.29, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 20.77}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.5500, "E_ND": 0.6000, "E_LoA": 0.4000, "E_mech": 0.1188, "muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "Supplies the residual flexion moment the rectus cannot cover, but bilateral contraction cancels the rotation and lateral-flexion components that dominate its line of action, and its flexion moment arm is smaller than the rectus.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 9.71}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.8000, "E_LoA": 0.9000, "E_mech": 0.4284, "muscle": "sternocleidomastoid", "rationale": "Genuine task torque rather than stabilization: the head is part of the 22 kg moved mass, and its ~5 N-m cervical flexion moment must be actively generated for the segment to be lifted.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 13.83, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 5.92}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["22 kg is interpreted as the moved segment mass (head, both arms, and upper trunk above the flexion axis), equal to ~26% of the 85 kg reference athlete's body mass, with no added external load.", "Supine on the floor, knees flexed ~90 degrees with feet unanchored, hands at the temples: the classic crunch restricts motion to spinal flexion and holds the pelvis on the floor.", "Horizontal distance from the thoracolumbar flexion axis to the moved segment's centre of mass ~0.22 m at the limiting phase, shortening rapidly as the trunk curls.", "Rectus abdominis internal flexion moment arm ~0.070 m; oblique effective flexion moment arm ~0.045 m.", "Head mass ~5 kg at ~0.10 m from the cervical flexion axis; sternocleidomastoid internal moment arm ~0.032 m.", "The pelvis is held down by gravity and the flexed-knee position, so no hip-flexion moment is required to complete the movement.", "Short range of motion with no external resistance: the limiting phase is reached almost immediately after the shoulders leave the floor."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "thoracolumbar_spine", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 47.50}, {"joint": "cervical_spine", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 5.00}], "nominal_load_kg": 22.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 216.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 36.40, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 36.00}
38	side_facing_reverse_pec_deck	Side-Facing Reverse Pec Deck	Single-Arm Side-Facing Reverse Pec Deck Fly	Upper	Back_3D	Isolation	Selectorized_Machine	20.00	108.17	{"obliques": 12.34, "rhomboids": 16.53, "brachialis": 4.63, "rotator_cuffs": 14.71, "biceps_brachii": 3.42, "erector_spinae": 6.28, "trapezius_lower": 7.19, "trapezius_upper": 3.71, "trapezius_middle": 16.90, "deltoid_posterior": 17.66, "serratus_anterior": 2.27, "transverse_abdominis": 2.53}	{"grip": "Neutral or pronated grip on the single handle, arm maintaining a slight, fixed elbow bend throughout the movement.", "tldr": {"focus": "Keep the torso facing forward and stable so the pulling arm generates the movement rather than trunk rotation.", "setup": "Sit sideways on the pec deck seat with the chest facing the machine's frame and one hand gripping the single handle at chest height.", "execution": "Pull the handle backward and across the body by horizontally abducting the shoulder, then return under control to a stretched position in front of the body.", "stop_when": "The handle can no longer be pulled back without the torso rotating to assist the movement."}, "stance": "Seated sideways on the bench or seat, feet planted for stability, hips and torso oriented toward the machine frame throughout.", "bracing": "Torso braced to resist rotation toward the working arm, with the obliques and spinal stabilizers maintaining a fixed trunk position as the single arm pulls across and back.", "overview": "This is the single-arm side-facing reverse pec deck fly, performed seated sideways on the machine so that only one arm engages the handle while the torso remains oriented toward the machine frame. This side-facing single-arm setup allows a longer pulling arc across the body compared to a standard face-forward two-arm reverse fly, and introduces an anti-rotation demand on the trunk since only one side is loaded. It differs from the conventional reverse pec deck fly in seating orientation, unilateral loading, and the resulting oblique and rotational stabilization requirement.", "eccentric": "The handle returns under control back across the front of the body through horizontal adduction, with the shoulder blade protracting slightly and the torso remaining stationary throughout.", "concentric": "The handle is pulled backward and across the body through glenohumeral horizontal abduction, with the elbow maintaining its slight fixed bend and the scapula retracting on the working side, while the torso remains stable and does not rotate to assist.", "tempo_notes": "Maintain continuous tension throughout without allowing the weight stack to rest between repetitions; avoid using trunk rotation or momentum to extend the pulling arc.", "end_position": "Working arm extended out to the side or slightly behind the torso with the shoulder blade retracted and the torso still facing the machine frame.", "safety_notes": ["Set the handle height to align with chest level to avoid excessive shoulder strain during the horizontal pull.", "Avoid forcing the pulling arc beyond the point where the torso must rotate to continue."], "external_cues": "Pull the handle back and out to the side; keep the chest facing the machine frame throughout.", "internal_cues": "Pull the arm across and back while keeping the torso still; think of rotating only through the shoulder, not the trunk.", "common_mistakes": ["Rotating the torso toward the working arm to extend the pulling range.", "Bending or extending the elbow substantially during the pull instead of maintaining a fixed angle.", "Shrugging the shoulder upward instead of retracting the scapula.", "Using momentum to initiate the pull instead of continuous tension.", "Allowing the non-working side of the torso to lift or shift off the seat."], "equipment_setup": "Pec deck or reverse fly machine with a single handle attachment, seat rotated or positioned sideways so the lifter faces the machine frame rather than the pads directly.", "range_of_motion": "From the arm extended across the front of the body at the start to the arm extended out to the side or slightly behind the torso at the end, limited by the point at which further travel requires trunk rotation to continue.", "rir_0_definition": "Inability to pull the handle back through shoulder horizontal abduction while keeping the torso stationary, as distinct from continuing to move the handle by rotating the trunk toward the working arm.", "rir_1_indicators": "Reduced range of the backward pull, slower movement speed, and a growing tendency for the torso to begin rotating, while the seated position remains largely stable.", "individualization": "Seat height, handle starting position, and the exact degree of elbow bend may vary moderately with shoulder structure and arm length, provided the torso remains stable and the movement is driven by shoulder horizontal abduction rather than trunk rotation.", "plane_of_movement": "Primarily transverse-plane glenohumeral horizontal abduction, combined with an anti-rotational stabilization demand on the trunk in the transverse plane.", "starting_position": "Seated sideways with the chest facing the machine frame, working arm extended forward gripping the handle at approximately chest height, elbow with a slight, fixed bend, torso upright and stable.", "technical_failure": "The point at which the handle can no longer be pulled back through horizontal abduction without the torso rotating toward the working arm to assist the movement.", "primary_joint_actions": "Glenohumeral horizontal abduction as the working arm moves from in front of the torso to the side and slightly behind it; scapular retraction on the working side; the trunk resists rotation toward the working arm through isometric action of the obliques and erector spinae; the non-working side of the torso remains stable against the seat."}	{}	{https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=P5CXx_jgTDE,https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=P6-N-VGCVxk,https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qpRGnTTcLpo}	Unilateral	{"slug": "side_facing_reverse_pec_deck", "notes": ["The unilateral-asymmetric reading established for the Pallof press was applied again here, and this is now the second exercise where the bilateral convention would produce a wrong answer. Mirroring 40 kg as two 20 kg loads would cancel the 42 N-m rotational moment and delete 21.15 cm2 of trunk contribution, converting this into an ordinary reverse pec deck. The laterality flag proposed earlier is no longer a nice-to-have: two exercises in the library now depend on an interpretation that the stated convention does not sanction.", "The side-facing orientation is what distinguishes this from a standard chest-supported reverse fly, and the entire difference sits in the trunk. Obliques at 12.34 cm2, erector spinae at 6.28 and transverse abdominis at 2.53 total 21.15 cm2 of anti-rotation work that a forward-facing pad would absorb completely. The shoulder and scapular allocation is otherwise close to what a supported version would produce.", "Deltoid_posterior reaches E_mech 0.7038 with perfect E_LoA, its highest value in the library, edging past the wide seated cable row at 0.6885 and the face pull at 0.6541. All three are horizontal abduction movements and the ordering tracks how cleanly each isolates that action: this variant does so most directly, with no elbow flexion or vertical pulling component competing for the demand.", "Rotator_cuffs at 14.71 cm2 is the second-highest cuff contribution in the library after the face pull at 9.09 in absolute terms but higher on E_mech at 0.0861 against 0.0532. As in the face pull, infraspinatus and teres minor are genuine agonists here rather than stabilisers, since horizontal abduction and external rotation are both required. This is now the third exercise where the aggregate rotator_cuffs slug has forced a low E_LoA to represent a group where only half the muscles act in the task direction.", "Total demand of 108.17 cm2 sits close to the straight-arm pulldown at 105.31 and the low-to-high cable fly at 105.44, and the structural similarity is real: all three are near-straight-arm single-joint movements with long external moment arms and modest effective loads. The pattern noted at the straight-arm pulldown now holds across four exercises.", "Machine lever ratio at 0.80 is the least certain input and is inherited from the high-to-low pec deck assumption. If the actual machine runs closer to 1:1, total demand would rise to roughly 130 cm2. A per-manufacturer lever ratio field remains the single highest-value addition to the input schema.", "A standard forward-facing chest-supported reverse pec deck must not inherit these values. The chest pad absorbs the entire rotational moment, removing 21.15 cm2 and permitting a heavier load, which would raise the shoulder and scapular allocation proportionally.", "A seated bent-over dumbbell reverse fly must not inherit these values either. Gravity acting vertically on a hinged torso produces a different resistance curve than a machine cam, and the hinged position introduces a lumbar extension demand this seated variant does not have.", "A bilateral side-facing variant is mechanically incoherent and should not exist in the library: working both arms from a side-facing seat would require two stacks on opposite sides, which is simply the standard forward-facing exercise."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric mid-range: working arm swept back to roughly 30 degrees of horizontal abduction from the start, humerus at shoulder height, elbow held at a fixed ~160 degrees, where the machine lever arm and the anti-rotation demand on the trunk are simultaneously greatest.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 108.17, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9200, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.7038, "muscle": "deltoid_posterior", "rationale": "Prime mover with perfect task alignment: horizontal abduction at shoulder height is its primary and near-exclusive function. Highest engagement it receives anywhere in the library, marginally above the wide seated cable row.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.65, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 17.66}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.9375, "E_mech": 0.6455, "muscle": "trapezius_middle", "rationale": "Its pure horizontal retraction vector closely matches the 38 N-m scapulothoracic moment required as the arm sweeps back, making it a co-prime mover rather than an assistant.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 26.18, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 16.90}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.8500, "E_mech": 0.5852, "muscle": "rhomboids", "rationale": "Major contributor to scapular retraction, with its downward-rotation component slightly reducing alignment relative to middle trapezius at this shoulder-height arm position.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 28.25, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 16.53}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.5500, "E_ND": 0.7000, "E_LoA": 0.2580, "E_mech": 0.0861, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Included as a genuine contributor rather than purely a stabiliser: infraspinatus and teres minor produce horizontal abduction and external rotation directly, both required here. Second-highest cuff engagement in the library after the face pull, and for the same mechanical reason.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 14.71}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.8000, "E_LoA": 0.2620, "E_mech": 0.1510, "muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "Included as mechanically necessary rather than postural: the side-facing position means the 42 N-m rotational moment must be actively resisted for the working arm to have a stable base. Without this the torso simply rotates toward the machine and no shoulder work occurs.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 12.34}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.7840, "E_mech": 0.4798, "muscle": "trapezius_lower", "rationale": "Contributes retraction and resists scapular elevation during the sweep, though its depression vector is only partly aligned with the predominantly horizontal scapular motion.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 14.98, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 7.19}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.1543, "E_mech": 0.0590, "muscle": "erector_spinae", "rationale": "Included as a load-transmitting element: its deep rotatores and multifidus components assist the anti-rotation demand, and the superficial fibres hold the upright unsupported torso against the pull.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 106.48, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 6.28}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.4500, "E_ND": 0.5500, "E_LoA": 0.5000, "E_mech": 0.1052, "muscle": "brachialis", "rationale": "Included as a load-transmitting stabiliser: the fixed near-straight elbow must be held isometrically for force to reach the humerus. Poor leverage at 160 degrees and the demand is small relative to the shoulder moment.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 4.63}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.7500, "E_LoA": 0.4010, "E_mech": 0.1917, "muscle": "trapezius_upper", "rationale": "Its transverse fibres contribute a real retraction component at shoulder height, though its dominant elevation vector is largely irrelevant to the required horizontal scapular moment.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 19.36, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 3.71}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.4200, "E_ND": 0.5500, "E_LoA": 0.4380, "E_mech": 0.0860, "muscle": "biceps_brachii", "rationale": "Assists the isometric elbow hold alongside brachialis, with poor leverage at the near-extended elbow angle.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 3.42}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.6500, "E_ND": 0.6000, "E_LoA": 0.3280, "E_mech": 0.1151, "muscle": "transverse_abdominis", "rationale": "Generates the hoop tension and intra-abdominal pressure that stiffen the trunk cylinder against the rotational moment, the same mechanism as in the Pallof press at lower magnitude.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 21.97, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 2.53}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.4500, "E_LoA": 0.2350, "E_mech": 0.0629, "muscle": "serratus_anterior", "rationale": "Included on the narrow grounds that it stabilises the scapula against tipping off the thorax during retraction; its protraction vector directly opposes the required motion, which caps its alignment.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 36.03, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 2.27}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["Representative commercial selectorized reverse pec deck with a cam-and-lever linkage; effective lever ratio ~0.80 at the handle, so 40 kg nominal resolves to ~32 kg of effective handle resistance, ~314 N.", "Side-facing setup: the athlete sits sideways on the seat rather than facing the pad, working one arm across the body, which is the defining feature of this variant.", "Read as unilateral-asymmetric following the Pallof press precedent: 40 kg is a single stack acting on one arm, not two mirrored 20 kg loads. Mirroring would cancel the anti-rotation demand that distinguishes this exercise from a standard reverse pec deck.", "Arm held near-straight at a fixed ~160 degree elbow; effective external moment arm from the glenohumeral axis to the handle force line ~0.40 m at the limiting phase.", "The required humeral action is horizontal abduction in the transverse plane at shoulder height, which is the posterior deltoid's primary function.", "Without the chest pad to brace against, the 314 N acting at ~0.135 m from the trunk axis generates a ~42 N-m rotational moment that must be actively resisted for the arm to have a stable base to pull from.", "Internal moment arms: posterior deltoid horizontal abduction ~0.045 m, scapular retractors ~0.055 m, oblique rotation ~0.055 m.", "The handle force acts largely perpendicular to the forearm, creating an elbow flexion-resisting demand; the elbow flexors work isometrically to hold the fixed angle and transmit load from hand to humerus.", "The seat and thigh contact anchor the pelvis, but the torso above the pelvis is unsupported in this orientation, so trunk anti-rotation is genuine load-bearing rather than posture.", "40 kg is a realistic 8-12 rep working load for a single arm on a machine with this lever ratio, where the long straight-arm moment arm makes the effective demand well above what the stack number suggests."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "glenohumeral_unilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 63.00}, {"joint": "scapulothoracic_unilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 38.00}, {"joint": "thoracolumbar_spine_rotation", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 42.00}, {"joint": "elbow_unilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 7.10}], "nominal_load_kg": 40.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 314.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 108.17, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 108.00}
27	plate_loaded_chest_press	Machine Chest Press	Plate-Loaded Horizontal Chest Press	Upper	Chest_Sternal	Heavy_Compound	Plate_Loaded_Machine	80.00	148.89	{"rotator_cuffs": 7.31, "deltoid_lateral": 2.69, "deltoid_anterior": 16.24, "serratus_anterior": 4.09, "triceps_long_head": 27.57, "triceps_medial_head": 12.54, "triceps_lateral_head": 29.03, "pectoralis_major_sternal": 42.14, "pectoralis_major_clavicular": 7.28}	{"grip": "Use the machine's intended bilateral grip with the wrists near neutral and the hands aligned so the forearms follow the direction of handle travel. Grip width and orientation depend on the machine design, but the wrists should remain stacked reasonably over the elbows rather than deviating markedly.", "tldr": {"focus": "Keep the forearms aligned with the handles and let the shoulders follow the machine path without shrugging or driving the torso forward.", "setup": "Adjust the seat so the handles start around mid-chest level with the shoulders aligned to the machine's horizontal pressing path and the back firmly supported.", "execution": "Press the handles forward by horizontally adducting the upper arms and extending the elbows while keeping the torso against the pad.", "stop_when": "Stop when another repetition would require loss of back-pad contact, shoulder shrugging, major elbow-path change, or a materially shortened press."}, "stance": "Sit with both feet firmly planted at a comfortable width to stabilize the pelvis and maintain continuous contact with the back pad.", "bracing": "Maintain enough abdominal and lower-body tension to keep the pelvis and ribcage stable against the machine. Avoid lumbar overextension, rib flare, forward torso thrust, or shifting the pelvis to create additional handle travel.", "overview": "A seated plate-loaded horizontal chest press performed against a back support with the handles traveling predominantly forward in a horizontal pressing arc. The movement combines glenohumeral horizontal adduction with elbow extension while the torso is externally stabilized by the machine. Unlike a free-weight bench press, the implement trajectory is constrained by the machine linkage, so seat position and shoulder alignment must be matched to that path.", "eccentric": "Allow the handles to return under control along the same machine-defined path as the elbows flex and the upper arms move back into horizontal abduction. Maintain back-pad contact and stable wrist-elbow alignment, and stop the return before the shoulders are forced into an uncomfortable or unstable stretched position.", "concentric": "Press the handles forward along the machine's prescribed path by horizontally adducting the upper arms and extending the elbows. Keep the wrists aligned with the forearms, maintain torso contact with the pad, and allow only the scapular motion naturally required by the machine geometry. Do not finish the repetition by thrusting the shoulders or torso forward.", "tempo_notes": "Avoid bouncing out of the rear position or allowing the machine arms to accelerate uncontrolled into the stops. Reverse direction smoothly while maintaining contact with the pads and consistent joint alignment.", "end_position": "Finish when the handles reach the machine's normal forward endpoint with the elbows at full or near-full functional extension, the wrists aligned, and the torso still firmly supported. Mild natural scapular protraction is acceptable, but deliberate shoulder rounding to gain extra reach is not required.", "safety_notes": ["Ensure weight plates are loaded securely and symmetrically on the intended loading horns.", "Confirm seat and starting-position adjustments are locked before beginning.", "Do not force the handles into a rear position that produces painful anterior shoulder stress or instability."], "external_cues": "Push the handles forward along their rails or arc without letting your torso follow them.", "internal_cues": "Keep the chest and pelvis against the pad; drive the upper arms forward around the ribcage; keep the wrists stacked over the forearms.", "common_mistakes": ["Setting the seat too high or too low so the handles no longer align with a comfortable horizontal pressing path.", "Allowing the shoulders to roll excessively forward at the stretched position.", "Lifting the torso or pelvis away from the pad to complete the final portion of the press.", "Allowing the wrists to collapse instead of keeping them aligned with the forearms.", "Flaring or tucking the elbows abruptly away from the machine's natural arm path.", "Letting the machine arms rebound from the rear stops before initiating the next repetition."], "equipment_setup": "Adjust the seat so the handles begin approximately at mid-chest level and the shoulders track naturally through the machine's pressing arc. Set the starting position, when adjustable, so the upper arms can move into a controlled stretched position without excessive shoulder extension or anterior translation. Load both sides of the plate-loaded machine symmetrically unless the machine is specifically designed otherwise.", "range_of_motion": "Use the largest machine-defined range that preserves comfortable shoulder mechanics, stable torso contact, and consistent elbow-hand alignment. The eccentric endpoint should provide a controlled chest stretch without forcing excessive shoulder extension, while the concentric endpoint should approach full elbow extension without aggressive hyperextension or forward torso movement.", "rir_0_definition": "RIR 0 is reached when no additional repetition can be completed through the defined machine range while preserving torso support and the established arm path, even if the handles could still be moved by leaning forward, arching excessively, shrugging, or shortening the range.", "rir_1_indicators": "Approximately one technically valid repetition may remain when handle velocity slows markedly and the final portion of the press becomes difficult, but the lifter can still preserve back-pad contact, wrist-elbow alignment, shoulder control, and the established range.", "individualization": "Seat height, starting depth, elbow angle, grip choice, and foot position may vary with torso length, humeral length, shoulder structure, and the specific machine linkage. These variables are acceptable to adjust provided the handles remain aligned with a horizontal chest-press path, the shoulders remain comfortable and controlled, the torso stays supported, and elbow extension plus shoulder horizontal adduction remain the primary dynamic actions.", "plane_of_movement": "Primarily transverse-plane glenohumeral horizontal adduction with a smaller sagittal component depending on the machine's converging or arcing linkage.", "starting_position": "Sit with the pelvis, upper back, and head supported as allowed by the machine. Plant both feet firmly, keep the ribcage controlled, and grasp the handles with the elbows flexed and the upper arms positioned slightly behind or approximately in line with the torso according to the machine geometry. Maintain the shoulders in a controlled neutral-to-mildly retracted position rather than allowing them to roll forward.", "technical_failure": "Technical failure occurs when another repetition cannot be completed through the established machine range without materially changing the mechanics. Observable breakdowns include lifting or shifting away from the back pad, excessive lumbar extension, shrugging the shoulders, markedly changing the elbow path, collapsing the wrists, or shortening the eccentric or concentric range substantially.", "primary_joint_actions": "During the concentric phase, the glenohumeral joints perform horizontal adduction with a small flexion component as dictated by the machine path, the elbows extend, and the scapulae transition from controlled retraction toward mild protraction while remaining supported against the back pad."}	{}	{https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VesHgJR14E8}	Bilateral	{"slug": "plate_loaded_chest_press", "limiting_phase": "Concentric initiation from the stretched position: handles at chest level, elbows flexed to roughly 90-100 degrees, humerus horizontally abducted, before the carriage lever arm shortens through the mid-range.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 148.89, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9200, "E_MA": 0.9200, "E_ND": 0.9500, "E_LoA": 0.9800, "E_mech": 0.7880, "muscle": "pectoralis_major_sternal", "rationale": "Primary generator of the humeral horizontal-adduction moment; the guided carriage and supported torso allow near-full expression of its capacity, with a small deduction for the lengthened start position and slightly converging handle path.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 53.48, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 42.14}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.9200, "E_ND": 0.9200, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.8041, "muscle": "triceps_lateral_head", "rationale": "Mono-articular elbow extensor acting directly against the 41.9 N-m bilateral elbow flexion moment at a favorable joint angle.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 36.10, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 29.03}, {"E_LT": 0.9200, "E_MA": 0.8800, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.9200, "E_mech": 0.6331, "muscle": "triceps_long_head", "rationale": "Contributes substantial elbow-extension torque, but its shoulder-extension action partially opposes the required humeral flexion component, reducing net usable force.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.55, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 27.57}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.8200, "E_mech": 0.5332, "muscle": "deltoid_anterior", "rationale": "Meaningful contributor to the humeral moment, but a strictly horizontal push direction demands less shoulder flexion than an inclined or supine press, lowering alignment.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 30.46, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 16.24}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.9200, "E_ND": 0.9200, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.8041, "muscle": "triceps_medial_head", "rationale": "Mechanically equivalent to the lateral head against the elbow flexion moment.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 15.59, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 12.54}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.3000, "E_LoA": 0.2800, "E_mech": 0.0428, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary stabilizer: glenohumeral compression is still required to transmit ~598 N through the joint. Engagement is well below the free-barbell case because the rail-guided carriage removes path control and lateral perturbation.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 7.31}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.8800, "E_LoA": 0.8000, "E_mech": 0.5386, "muscle": "pectoralis_major_clavicular", "rationale": "Assists horizontal adduction; the horizontal press vector limits the humeral flexion component that would favor these fibres.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 13.52, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 7.28}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.4500, "E_LoA": 0.4000, "E_mech": 0.1134, "muscle": "serratus_anterior", "rationale": "Included as a load-transmitting stabilizer for scapular fixation and terminal protraction, but the chest pad and seat back absorb much of the reaction force, sharply reducing the required contribution relative to a free bench press.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 36.03, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 4.09}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.5500, "E_LoA": 0.1800, "E_mech": 0.0589, "muscle": "deltoid_lateral", "rationale": "Only the anterior fibres add a minor flexion component; the abduction line of action is largely orthogonal to the required net humeral moment.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 45.73, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 2.69}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["Representative commercial plate-loaded converging chest press; exact lever geometry unavailable and approximated.", "Machine lever ratio ~0.70 at the handles in the stretched position, plus ~5 kg of carriage mass: 80 kg nominal resolves to ~61 kg of effective handle resistance, ~598 N total, ~299 N per limb.", "Handles at mid-chest height with moderately flared elbows; perpendicular distance from the GH joint to the handle force line ~0.14 m per side.", "Forearm roughly aligned with the horizontal push vector; elbow external moment arm ~0.07 m per side.", "Effective internal moment arm for combined humeral horizontal adduction/flexion ~0.05 m; triceps elbow-extension moment arm ~0.025 m.", "Seat back and chest pad fix the torso and scapulae, and the carriage is rail-guided, so balance and free-barbell path control are not mechanical requirements.", "80 kg on a machine with this ratio corresponds to roughly 61 kg of free-weight-equivalent pressing resistance, consistent with 8-12 reps at RIR 1-2 for the 100 kg reference bench presser."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "glenohumeral_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 83.70}, {"joint": "elbow_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 41.90}, {"joint": "scapulothoracic_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 14.00}], "nominal_load_kg": 80.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 598.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 148.89, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 149.00}
13	cable_face_pull	Face Pull	Rope Cable Face Pull	Upper	Back_3D	Isolation	Cable	30.00	46.53	{"rhomboids": 6.61, "brachialis": 3.86, "rotator_cuffs": 9.09, "biceps_brachii": 2.54, "trapezius_lower": 3.13, "trapezius_middle": 8.31, "deltoid_posterior": 12.99}	{"grip": "Neutral grip on the rope ends, hands positioned to separate naturally as the rope approaches the face.", "tldr": {"focus": "Externally rotate the shoulders and drive the elbows back as the rope approaches the face, rather than simply pulling the hands inward.", "setup": "Set the cable pulley to roughly upper-chest to head height with a rope attachment, standing or split-stance facing the machine.", "execution": "Pull the rope toward the face while spreading the hands apart and driving the elbows up and out to the sides.", "stop_when": "The elbows can no longer be driven above shoulder height or the rope path shifts down toward the chest instead of the face."}, "stance": "Split or staggered stance for stability against the cable resistance, or a stable shoulder-width stance if using a lighter load.", "bracing": "Torso braced upright without leaning back to substitute for the pull; ribcage kept stacked over the pelvis to avoid using lumbar extension to assist the movement.", "overview": "This is the cable face pull performed with a rope attachment set at approximately upper-chest to head height. The exercise combines horizontal shoulder abduction with external rotation, distinguishing it from a standard rear-delt cable fly, which typically omits the terminal external-rotation component and pulls to a lower, more horizontal path. The rope allows the hands to separate near the face, enabling the elbows to rise above the hands at the end range.", "eccentric": "The rope returns under control along the same path, with the shoulders internally rotating and horizontally adducting as the arms extend back toward the starting position, resisting the pull of the cable throughout.", "concentric": "The rope is pulled toward the face through combined horizontal shoulder abduction and external rotation, with the hands separating as the elbows rise to roughly shoulder height or slightly above and track outward and back.", "tempo_notes": "Maintain continuous cable tension throughout without letting the weight stack rest between repetitions; avoid using body lean or momentum to initiate the pull.", "end_position": "Rope ends near the sides of the face or ears with the elbows elevated to approximately shoulder height and the shoulders externally rotated.", "safety_notes": ["Avoid forcing external rotation beyond a comfortable range, particularly with shoulder mobility restrictions.", "Keep the load moderate enough to maintain the intended elbow height and rotation rather than converting the pull into a heavier row."], "external_cues": "Pull the rope apart toward the sides of the head; drive the elbows up and back.", "internal_cues": "Rotate the backs of the hands toward the wall behind you as the rope reaches the face; think of pulling with the elbows rather than the hands.", "common_mistakes": ["Pulling the rope to the chest instead of the face, omitting the external-rotation component.", "Leaning the torso backward to assist the pull with body momentum.", "Allowing the elbows to drop below shoulder height at the end of the range.", "Shrugging the shoulders upward instead of retracting the scapulae.", "Using a jerking motion to initiate the pull instead of continuous tension."], "equipment_setup": "Cable pulley set to approximately upper-chest to head height with a rope attachment.", "range_of_motion": "From full elbow extension with the arms forward at the bottom to the rope reaching the face with the shoulders externally rotated at the top, limited by the individual's shoulder external-rotation mobility.", "rir_0_definition": "Inability to pull the rope to the face with elbow elevation and external rotation while maintaining an upright torso, as distinct from continuing to move the rope by leaning back or converting the pull into a lower, more horizontal row.", "rir_1_indicators": "Reduced height and separation of the hands near the face, slower rotation through the end range, and increasing torso lean, while the general pull path remains intact.", "individualization": "Pulley height, stance, and the degree of hand separation near the face may vary with shoulder mobility and torso length, provided the elbows reach approximately shoulder height and the external-rotation component is preserved at the end of the pull.", "plane_of_movement": "Primarily transverse-plane horizontal shoulder abduction combined with a frontal/sagittal-plane external rotation component as the hands approach the face.", "starting_position": "Standing or in a staggered stance facing the pulley at a distance that keeps tension on the cable with the arms extended, shoulder blades in a neutral, non-retracted position, rope held with a neutral grip.", "technical_failure": "The point at which the elbows can no longer be raised to approximately shoulder height, the external-rotation component is lost and the movement becomes a simple horizontal pull to the chest, or the torso leans back to substitute momentum for shoulder action.", "primary_joint_actions": "Glenohumeral horizontal abduction and external rotation; scapular retraction and slight depression; elbow flexion is secondary and largely a consequence of shortening the distance to the face rather than a primary driver of the movement."}	{}	{https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0Po47vvj9g4,https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ljgqer1ZpXg}	Bilateral	{"slug": "cable_face_pull", "notes": ["This is the first exercise in the library where rotator_cuffs enters as a prime mover rather than a stabiliser. End-range external rotation is an explicit task requirement here, not a by-product of load transmission, so the inclusion rationale differs fundamentally from the press and row entries where cuff force merely compresses the joint.", "The rotator_cuffs slug is mechanically heterogeneous and this exercise exposes the problem. Infraspinatus and teres minor are agonists, supraspinatus is roughly neutral, and subscapularis is a direct antagonist of the required external rotation. The 170.80 cm2 aggregate was handled by setting E_LoA to approximately 0.17, but a split into external and internal rotator slugs would model this and any rotator-cuff-specific exercise far more cleanly.", "Total demand of 46.53 cm2 is low relative to the 30 kg stack number because the very long end-range external moment arm, around 0.225 m at the shoulder, converts modest cable force into a demanding joint moment. This is the mechanically correct reason face pulls are performed with light loads, and the model reproduces it without special-casing.", "As with the cable fly, the elbow moment here is an extension moment, so the elbow flexors appear as load-transmitting stabilisers rather than agonists. This is now the second cable exercise showing that pattern and is likely general to any cable movement where the forearm is roughly perpendicular to the cable line.", "The exercise loads four joints simultaneously at modest magnitudes rather than one joint heavily, which is why no muscle approaches saturation and no muscle exceeds E_mech 0.53. Contrast the Pendlay row, where three groups saturated.", "A high-to-low or seated face pull variant must not inherit these values. Changing the pulley height alters the balance between the horizontal abduction and external rotation moments and shifts the relative posterior deltoid and cuff allocation.", "Trunk and lower-limb musculature were deliberately excluded. The backward lean transmits the cable reaction through the skeleton to the floor and is postural rather than net task torque under the stated inclusion rules."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric end-range: rope split at the level of the face, humerus horizontally abducted to roughly 90 degrees and externally rotated, scapulae approaching full retraction, where the shoulder external rotation and horizontal abduction moments are simultaneously greatest.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 46.53, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.8600, "E_mech": 0.5267, "muscle": "deltoid_posterior", "rationale": "Prime mover for the 33.1 N-m horizontal abduction moment. Shortened at end-range, which costs length-tension, and part of its line of action is pure extension rather than the horizontal abduction plus external rotation the movement requires.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.65, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 12.99}, {"E_LT": 0.8000, "E_MA": 0.4500, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.1739, "E_mech": 0.0532, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Included as a genuine prime mover rather than a stabiliser: infraspinatus and teres minor generate the required 12.6 N-m external rotation moment against a small internal moment arm. E_LoA is low because only the two external rotators of the four-muscle group act in the required direction, and subscapularis is a direct antagonist here.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 9.09}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.5185, "E_mech": 0.3173, "muscle": "trapezius_middle", "rationale": "Direct horizontal retractor supplying a large share of the 22 N-m scapulothoracic moment, shortened at end-range retraction. E_LoA reflects that the scapular moment is a secondary component of the total task demand rather than the primary one.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 26.18, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 8.31}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.4078, "E_mech": 0.2340, "muscle": "rhomboids", "rationale": "Shares scapular retraction with middle trapezius, but its downward-rotation component partly opposes the upward rotation required by the elbows-high finish position, reducing net alignment.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 28.25, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 6.61}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.7000, "E_LoA": 0.1733, "E_mech": 0.0876, "muscle": "brachialis", "rationale": "Included as a load-transmitting element: the rope creates an elbow extension moment of 11.8 N-m that must be resisted for hand force to reach the humerus. Excellent leverage at 90 degrees, but the demand is small relative to available capacity and the work is largely isometric.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 3.86}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.3616, "E_mech": 0.2090, "muscle": "trapezius_lower", "rationale": "Contributes retraction and the upward rotation that permits the elbows-high finish, though its depression vector only partly matches the required scapular moment direction.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 14.98, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 3.13}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.7000, "E_LoA": 0.1430, "E_mech": 0.0638, "muscle": "biceps_brachii", "rationale": "Assists the isometric elbow hold alongside brachialis. The neutral rope grip and the supination demand at end-range give it a modest role, but the elbow moment is the smallest of the four loaded joints.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 2.54}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["Representative commercial single-stack cable column with a 1:1 pulley ratio and rope attachment; 30 kg nominal yields ~147 N at the rope after friction, split ~73.5 N per hand.", "High pulley set slightly above head height, athlete standing with a staggered stance in a light backward lean, elbows tracking at or above shoulder height.", "Humerus length ~0.32 m: at end-range horizontal abduction the glenohumeral external moment arm for the horizontal component is ~0.225 m per side.", "External rotation moment estimated from the forearm acting as the lever: rope force ~73.5 N at ~0.086 m effective rotation moment arm per side.", "Elbow held at roughly 90 degrees; the rope line is close to perpendicular to the forearm, giving an elbow external moment arm of ~0.080 m per side.", "Internal moment arms: posterior deltoid horizontal abduction ~0.045 m, infraspinatus and teres minor external rotation ~0.021 m, scapular retractors ~0.055 m, elbow flexors ~0.040 m at 90 degrees.", "The face pull is the one horizontal-pull pattern in which the rotator cuff is a genuine prime mover rather than a stabiliser, because end-range external rotation is an explicit requirement of the movement rather than a by-product.", "The athlete counterbalances the cable by leaning back against it, so lower-limb and trunk musculature transmit body weight to the floor rather than generating task torque; this is postural and excluded.", "30 kg is a realistic 8-12 rep working load: the very long external moment arm at end-range makes this a low-load exercise relative to its stack number."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "glenohumeral_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 33.10}, {"joint": "scapulothoracic_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 22.00}, {"joint": "glenohumeral_rotation_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 12.60}, {"joint": "elbow_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 11.80}], "nominal_load_kg": 30.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 147.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 46.53, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 46.50}
47	copenhagen_plank	Copenhagen Plank	Bench-Supported Copenhagen Side Plank	Core	Hip_AF	Stability_Isometric	Bodyweight	35.00	131.52	{"obliques": 30.56, "adductor_magnus": 57.40, "adductor_longus_brevis": 43.56}	{"grip": "", "tldr": {"focus": "Keep the hips and shoulders stacked in a straight line without rotating the torso forward or backward.", "setup": "Lie on your side with the top foot supported on a bench and the bottom arm propping the torso up on the forearm or hand.", "execution": "Lift the bottom hip off the floor until the body forms a straight line, then hold or repeat while the top leg stays supported on the bench.", "stop_when": "The hips can no longer be lifted to a straight line or begin sagging toward the floor."}, "stance": "Top leg supported along the bench, bottom leg extended and unsupported, hips and shoulders aligned in a stacked, straight position.", "bracing": "Abdominal bracing combined with active adductor engagement in the top leg to hold it steady against the bench; the torso resists both lateral flexion and rotation throughout.", "overview": "This is the Copenhagen plank, a side-plank variant in which the top leg is supported on a bench with the inner thigh/adductors in contact with the pad, while the bottom leg hangs unsupported. This bench-supported top-leg position places substantial demand on the adductors of the top leg to hold the leg in place, in addition to the lateral core stabilization common to a standard side plank. It differs from a standard side plank primarily in the elevated bench support and the pronounced adductor loading created by the top leg's position.", "eccentric": "The bottom hip lowers under control back toward the floor, maintaining the stacked shoulder-hip-ankle alignment and avoiding rotation of the torso as the pelvis descends.", "concentric": "The bottom hip lifts off the floor by extending and abducting slightly, raising the pelvis until the body forms a straight line from shoulders to ankles, while the top leg continues pressing into the bench for support.", "tempo_notes": "The lift may be performed as a sustained isometric hold or as controlled repetitions; avoid dropping the hip rapidly at the bottom of the range if performing repetitions.", "end_position": "Body forming a straight line from shoulders through hips to ankles, with the top leg supported on the bench and the bottom leg extended and unsupported.", "safety_notes": ["Ensure the bench or pad surface is stable and will not shift under the top leg's pressure.", "Avoid excessive lumbar hyperextension when lifting the hips to reach a straight body line."], "external_cues": "Press the top leg down into the bench; lift the hip toward the ceiling while keeping the shoulders stacked.", "internal_cues": "Squeeze the top inner thigh into the bench while lifting the hip; think of drawing the bottom hip up and forward rather than rotating the torso.", "common_mistakes": ["Allowing the torso to rotate forward or backward instead of staying stacked.", "Letting the hips sag toward the floor during a hold.", "Losing contact between the top leg and the bench.", "Overextending the lumbar spine to gain extra hip height instead of using hip extension.", "Supporting excessive weight through the bottom shoulder instead of the trunk musculature."], "equipment_setup": "Flat bench or elevated pad positioned to support the top leg from roughly the shin to the top of the foot, at a height that allows the bottom leg to hang freely below.", "range_of_motion": "From the bottom hip resting near or on the floor at the bottom to full hip extension with the body in a straight line at the top, with the top leg's position on the bench remaining essentially fixed throughout.", "rir_0_definition": "Inability to lift or hold the hips in a straight line with the shoulders and ankles while maintaining top-leg contact with the bench, as distinct from continuing the position with visible torso rotation or hip sagging.", "rir_1_indicators": "Increasing difficulty maintaining a fully straight body line, minor but still acceptable trunk rotation, and greater visible effort to hold the top leg pressed into the bench.", "individualization": "Bench height, the exact point of top-leg contact, and the depth of hip lift may vary moderately with limb length, hip and adductor flexibility, and shoulder comfort, provided the body maintains a stacked, straight alignment without rotation throughout the movement.", "plane_of_movement": "Primarily frontal-plane lateral flexion/stabilization of the trunk, with the top hip held in an abducted, externally supported position and the bottom hip held in extension.", "starting_position": "Side-lying with the bottom forearm or hand on the floor beneath the shoulder, top leg resting on the bench with the inner thigh facing down toward the pad, bottom leg extended and hanging below the bench, hips stacked.", "technical_failure": "The point at which the hips can no longer be raised to a straight line, the torso rotates forward or backward out of the stacked position, or the top leg loses firm contact with the bench.", "primary_joint_actions": "Lateral trunk stabilization through the obliques and quadratus lumborum resisting lateral flexion; hip abduction and adduction forces at the top hip resisted isometrically by the adductors pressing into the bench; the bottom hip extends slightly to lift the pelvis off the floor; the supporting shoulder and elbow (or hand) stabilize the upper body in a fixed position."}	{}	{https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aDsaGBnvDQo,https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5Hs7AfiMXgs}	Bilateral	{"slug": "copenhagen_plank", "notes": ["The adductor contributions generate approximately 121 Nm of bilateral hip-adduction capacity using representative internal moment arms near 0.045-0.050 m, matching the estimated external hip moment.", "The oblique contribution generates approximately 46 Nm of trunk lateral-flexion capacity using an effective internal moment arm near 0.060 m.", "Gracilis and pectineus would normally contribute to hip adduction, and quadratus lumborum would contribute to lateral trunk support, but these muscles are absent from MUSCLE_FCSA and were not substituted.", "Gluteus medius and gluteus minimus are excluded because their principal frontal-plane action opposes the required adduction moment of the bench-supported leg.", "The estimate is sensitive to whether support is placed at the knee or ankle; knee support would materially reduce the external hip-adduction moment."], "limiting_phase": "Static side-plank hold with the upper leg supported on the bench, the pelvis elevated, the trunk aligned horizontally, and the unsupported body mass producing concurrent hip-abduction and trunk-lateral-flexion moments.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 131.52, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8200, "E_ND": 0.8400, "E_LoA": 0.8800, "E_mech": 0.5455, "muscle": "adductor_magnus", "rationale": "Provides the largest hip-adduction force component required to suspend the pelvis and body from the bench-supported leg, although the aggregate includes posterior fibers with imperfect frontal-plane alignment.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 105.22, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 57.40}, {"E_LT": 0.9200, "E_MA": 0.8600, "E_ND": 0.8800, "E_LoA": 0.9400, "E_mech": 0.6545, "muscle": "adductor_longus_brevis", "rationale": "Produces a strongly task-aligned isometric hip-adduction moment with favorable length and leverage while the supported leg resists the downward pull of the body.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 66.55, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 43.56}, {"E_LT": 0.8800, "E_MA": 0.7200, "E_ND": 0.7200, "E_LoA": 0.8200, "E_mech": 0.3741, "muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "Generates the mechanically necessary lateral-flexion moment that prevents the unsupported trunk and pelvis from sagging toward the floor.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 30.56}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["The 35 kg nominal load is interpreted as the effective unsupported body mass acting through the bench-supported upper leg rather than as additional external weight.", "The result represents the bilateral systemic equivalent of performing the unilateral hold equally on both sides.", "The effective centre of mass acts approximately 0.35 m from the supported hip axis for the hip-adduction demand.", "The suspended trunk creates an effective lateral-flexion moment arm of approximately 0.135 m about the lumbar region.", "The upper leg is supported near the ankle or distal lower leg, and the body is held rigid without pelvic sag."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "hip_adduction_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 120.17}, {"joint": "trunk_lateral_flexion", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 46.35}], "nominal_load_kg": 35.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 343.35}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 131.52, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 131.52}
42	bodyweight_neck_extension	Neck Extension	Prone Bench Bodyweight Neck Extension	Upper	Neck	Isolation	Bodyweight	5.00	8.49	{"deep_neck_extensors": 8.49}	{"grip": "", "tldr": {"focus": "Move only the head through neck extension while keeping the shoulders and torso completely still.", "setup": "Lie prone on a bench with the head and neck unsupported off the end, chest and shoulders remaining flat on the bench.", "execution": "Extend the neck by lifting the head upward and back, then lower it under control to a neutral, slightly flexed position.", "stop_when": "The head can no longer be lifted through extension without the shoulders or torso assisting the movement."}, "stance": "Feet resting on the bench or floor for overall body stability, not contributing to the neck movement itself.", "bracing": "The torso and shoulders remain braced flat against the bench throughout, isolating movement to the cervical spine without the upper back or chest lifting or shifting.", "overview": "This is the bodyweight neck extension, a cervical-extension exercise performed prone on a bench with the head extending beyond the edge so that the neck can move through extension and flexion against the resistance of the head's own weight. The movement isolates the deep neck extensors and posterior cervical musculature by restricting motion to the cervical spine while the torso and shoulders remain fixed on the bench.", "eccentric": "The head returns under control through cervical flexion back to the starting neutral-to-slightly-flexed position, without allowing the head to drop suddenly at the end of the range.", "concentric": "The head lifts through cervical extension, drawing the chin up and back away from the chest, while the shoulders and torso remain motionless on the bench.", "tempo_notes": "The movement should be performed without jerking the head or using momentum to initiate extension; a brief controlled pause at the top of the extended position may be used.", "end_position": "Head lifted with the cervical spine in an extended position, gaze directed forward or slightly upward, and the shoulders still flat on the bench.", "safety_notes": ["Move through a conservative range and avoid forcing end-range cervical extension or flexion given the vulnerability of the cervical spine.", "Ensure the bench edge does not contact or compress the neck during the movement.", "Progress the range of motion gradually given the small muscle mass and sensitivity of the cervical structures involved."], "external_cues": "Lift the chin up and away from the chest; keep the shoulders pinned to the bench.", "internal_cues": "Lift the head by lengthening the front of the neck and drawing the back of the skull upward; think of extending the neck rather than throwing the head back.", "common_mistakes": ["Lifting the shoulders or chest off the bench to assist the movement.", "Using a jerking motion of the head instead of controlled cervical extension.", "Forcing the head into an uncomfortable end-range position beyond the individual's cervical mobility.", "Allowing the head to drop rapidly during the eccentric phase instead of controlling the return."], "equipment_setup": "Flat bench positioned so the head can hang freely off one end without the edge of the bench contacting the neck or skull during the movement.", "range_of_motion": "From a neutral or slightly flexed head position at the bottom to full comfortable cervical extension at the top, limited by the individual's cervical mobility and avoiding forcing the end range through discomfort.", "rir_0_definition": "Inability to lift the head through cervical extension using the neck extensors alone while keeping the torso and shoulders fixed, as distinct from continuing to move the head by shifting the shoulders or using momentum.", "rir_1_indicators": "Reduced range of comfortable extension, slower movement speed through the mid-range, and increasing effort to control the return phase, while the shoulders and torso remain stationary.", "individualization": "The exact starting head position (neutral versus slightly flexed) and the depth of extension may vary with individual cervical mobility and comfort, provided the torso and shoulders remain fixed on the bench throughout the movement.", "plane_of_movement": "Sagittal-plane cervical extension and flexion.", "starting_position": "Prone on the bench with chest and shoulders flat against the surface, head extending off the end of the bench in a neutral-to-slightly-flexed position, chin tucked slightly toward the chest.", "technical_failure": "The point at which the head can no longer be lifted through cervical extension without the shoulders lifting off the bench or the torso shifting to generate momentum.", "primary_joint_actions": "Extension of the cervical spine, lifting the head and drawing the chin away from the chest; the atlanto-occipital and lower cervical segments contribute to the overall extension arc; the shoulders and thoracic spine remain stationary and are not involved in the joint action."}	{}	{https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3-yAPkua_cA,https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gimeRpdqWQw}	Bilateral	{"slug": "bodyweight_neck_extension", "notes": ["This exercise inverts the usual E_mech interpretation. The four dimensional factors all sit in the 0.85 to 0.95 range, but their product would allocate 60.5 cm2 against an external demand of only 8.5 cm2. E_mech was therefore set by the top-down requirement rather than by the dimensional product, since a muscle cannot utilise capacity the task does not demand.", "The E_mech multiplication rule as written assumes the muscle is the constraint. Where FCSA capacity greatly exceeds task demand, the product overstates utilisation by an order of magnitude. Consider adding an explicit rule to the prompt: after computing the dimensional product, cap each muscle's contribution so the group total satisfies but does not exceed the top-down target.", "The result is near-identical to bodyweight_neck_curl at 8.55 cm2, which is mechanically correct. Both movements lift the same head mass through the same external moment arm, so external demand is symmetric even though the available musculature is not.", "The two neck exercises reach the same total by opposite routes. Flexion is capacity-constrained, with sternocleidomastoid at E_mech 0.6156 and the deep flexors missing from MUSCLE_FCSA entirely; extension is demand-constrained, with a large muscle group only lightly utilised. Any future model that reads E_mech as a proxy for training stimulus will misread this pair.", "Trapezius upper was deliberately excluded. Its dominant action is scapular elevation, and its small cervical extension component is redundant at a load the deep extensors cover using a tenth of their capacity.", "A weighted variant with a head harness must not inherit these values. The deep extensors have roughly ten times the reserve needed here, so demand scales close to linearly with added load before any saturation appears."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric mid-range with the head near the horizontal, where the head's centre of mass sits at its greatest horizontal distance from the cervical extension axis; the moment falls away as the head rises toward the neutral or extended end position.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 8.49, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9500, "E_LoA": 0.9500, "E_mech": 0.1000, "muscle": "deep_neck_extensors", "rationale": "Sole prime mover for the 5.9 N-m cervical extension moment, at favourable length and leverage in the mid-range. E_mech is low not because of any mechanical disadvantage but because the group's 84.88 cm2 capacity vastly exceeds the requirement: it can generate roughly 95 N-m at full capacity against a 5.9 N-m demand, so only about a tenth of its available force is utilised.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 84.88, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 8.49}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["5.00 kg is interpreted as head mass alone, roughly 5.9 percent of the 85 kg reference athlete's body mass, with no added external load.", "Prone on a bench with the head and neck extending past the end, shoulders and thorax supported by the bench surface.", "Horizontal distance from the upper cervical extension axis to the head's centre of mass ~0.12 m at the limiting phase, mirroring the supine neck curl geometry.", "Deep neck extensor effective cervical extension moment arm ~0.045 m, larger than the sternocleidomastoid flexion moment arm because the posterior musculature acts further from the vertebral bodies via the spinous processes.", "Trapezius upper is excluded from the cervical moment: its line of pull produces scapular elevation and, at the cervical spine, acts mainly on the skull via the nuchal ligament with a small extension component that the deep extensors already cover at this trivial load.", "The bench supports the trunk, so no thoracolumbar extension moment is required and no hip or trunk musculature bears load.", "Slow controlled tempo with negligible acceleration beyond gravity."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "cervical_spine", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 5.90}], "nominal_load_kg": 5.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 49.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 8.49, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 8.50}
10	weighted_neutral_grip_pull_up	Weighted Neutral-Grip Pull-Up	Weighted Neutral-Grip Pull-Up	Upper	Back_V	Heavy_Compound	Bodyweight	100.00	245.71	{"obliques": 8.17, "iliopsoas": 8.18, "rhomboids": 16.10, "brachialis": 33.63, "teres_major": 3.60, "rotator_cuffs": 11.10, "wrist_flexors": 34.03, "biceps_brachii": 25.14, "rectus_femoris": 3.66, "brachioradialis": 7.56, "trapezius_lower": 11.09, "latissimus_dorsi": 35.83, "rectus_abdominis": 10.44, "trapezius_middle": 14.92, "deltoid_posterior": 8.55, "pronators_supinators": 3.04, "transverse_abdominis": 2.86, "adductor_longus_brevis": 4.24, "pectoralis_major_sternal": 3.57}	{"grip": "Neutral grip with palms facing each other on parallel handles, hand position fixed by the handle spacing rather than adjustable in width.", "tldr": {"focus": "Drive the elbows down toward the hips to initiate the pull rather than yanking with the arms or swinging the body.", "setup": "Hang from parallel neutral-grip handles with additional weight secured via a belt or between the feet, arms fully extended.", "execution": "Pull the body upward until the chin clears the handles, then lower under control back to a full hang.", "stop_when": "The chin can no longer clear the handles without kipping the legs or torso to generate momentum."}, "stance": "Legs hanging freely or crossed at the ankles, positioned to keep the added load stable and prevent excessive swinging.", "bracing": "Abdominal and hip bracing maintained throughout to prevent the body from swinging or kipping, particularly important given the added external load increasing the consequence of losing a stable position.", "overview": "This is the weighted neutral-grip pull-up, performed on parallel handles with the palms facing each other, with additional external load added beyond bodyweight via a belt, vest, or holding a load between the feet. The neutral grip keeps the forearms in a fixed rotation and typically allows the elbows to track close to the torso, distinguishing it from a pronated-grip pull-up, which relies more on shoulder adduction with the elbows tracking further from the body. The added external load distinguishes this from the bodyweight-only version, requiring a stricter, less momentum-reliant execution to move the additional resistance under control.", "eccentric": "The body lowers under control back to a full hang through glenohumeral flexion and elbow extension, with the shoulder blades returning to a neutral position and the torso remaining stable throughout.", "concentric": "The body is pulled upward from a full hang toward the handles through combined glenohumeral extension and adduction with elbow flexion, with the elbows driving down and back close to the torso until the chin clears the handles.", "tempo_notes": "Avoid using a kipping or swinging motion to generate momentum, particularly given the added external load; maintain controlled tension throughout both the pull and the lowering phase.", "end_position": "Chin at or above the level of the handles with the elbows flexed and drawn down close to the torso.", "safety_notes": ["Ensure the weight belt, vest, or held load is securely attached before beginning the set.", "Use a controlled descent rather than dropping rapidly from the top position, given the increased load on the shoulders and elbows."], "external_cues": "Pull the body straight up toward the handles; keep the torso and legs still throughout the pull.", "internal_cues": "Drive the elbows down toward the hips; think of pulling the chest up to the handles rather than just raising the chin.", "common_mistakes": ["Using a kipping or swinging motion to generate momentum for the pull.", "Failing to reach full arm extension at the bottom of each repetition, shortening the range.", "Shrugging the shoulders upward instead of depressing and retracting the scapulae.", "Allowing the added external load to swing, destabilizing the torso during the pull.", "Stopping short of clearing the chin over the handles at the top."], "equipment_setup": "Pull-up station with parallel neutral-grip handles, weight belt, weighted vest, or dumbbell secured between the feet to add resistance beyond bodyweight.", "range_of_motion": "From a full hang with the arms extended at the bottom to the chin clearing the handles at the top, using the complete available range rather than a partial one.", "rir_0_definition": "Inability to pull the chin to the level of the handles through glenohumeral extension and elbow flexion while keeping the body stable, as distinct from continuing to move upward by kipping the legs or swinging the torso.", "rir_1_indicators": "Slower pulling speed through the mid-range, a growing tendency toward slight body swing, and increasing effort to maintain a stable hang position, while the general pulling path remains intact.", "individualization": "Handle spacing and the exact elbow path relative to the torso may vary moderately with shoulder structure, provided the full range from a dead hang to chin-over-handles is used and the body remains stable without kipping throughout the set.", "plane_of_movement": "Primarily sagittal-plane glenohumeral extension with a frontal-plane adduction component, combined with elbow flexion.", "starting_position": "Hanging from the neutral-grip handles with arms fully extended, shoulder blades in a neutral, non-retracted position, external load secured and stable, body still before initiating the pull.", "technical_failure": "The point at which the chin can no longer be pulled to the level of the handles without kipping the legs, swinging the torso, or failing to achieve a full range of motion.", "primary_joint_actions": "Glenohumeral extension and adduction as the upper arms move from an overhead position down toward the torso; elbow flexion driven by the biceps and brachialis; scapular depression and retraction as the body rises; the forearms remain in a fixed neutral rotation throughout; the trunk and hips remain braced in a fixed position to prevent swinging."}	{}	{https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-27Ofi_wMFc}	Bilateral	{"slug": "weighted_neutral_grip_pull_up", "notes": ["This is the direct free-weight counterpart to neutral_grip_lat_pulldown and the pair isolates the cost of removing the anchor. Total demand rises from 191.51 to 245.71 cm2, a 28 percent increase for a 33 percent heavier effective load. The upper-body allocation scales almost exactly with load, with latissimus moving 34.50 to 35.83 and trapezius_lower 10.87 to 11.09, while the entire 40.55 cm2 of trunk and hip contribution is new. That block is the mechanical price of the missing thigh pads, and it is the clearest quantification the model has produced of why pull-ups and pulldowns are not interchangeable.", "Nine muscles enter here that do not appear in the pulldown: rectus_abdominis, obliques, transverse_abdominis, iliopsoas, rectus_femoris, adductor_longus_brevis, pronators_supinators, and the trunk group generally. All are admitted under the load-transmission exception rather than as agonists, because the dip belt hangs freely below the pelvis and the body must be held rigid for force to reach the bar. A strict-form unweighted pull-up would admit these at roughly half the magnitude; a kipping pull-up would not admit them at all in this form, since the swing is deliberate.", "Wrist_flexors at 34.03 cm2 and E_mech 0.6256 is the highest grip demand in the library, above the pulldown at 27.75 and the Pendlay row at 31.44. The full 902 N passes through the hands with no anchoring, and this is the mechanical basis for grip being the common failure point on weighted pull-ups. With straps, total demand would fall to roughly 212 cm2 and the limiter would shift decisively to the lats and elbow flexors.", "Biceps_brachii reaches E_mech 0.6318, the highest value in any pulling exercise in the library, marginally above the neutral-grip pulldown at 0.6108. The same two mechanisms apply, neutral grip plus overhead shoulder position, amplified by the heavier load. The model reproduces without special-casing the common observation that neutral and supinated pull-ups are the most biceps-intensive vertical pulls.", "Rotator_cuffs at 11.10 cm2 is its highest stabiliser contribution in the library, exceeding the Pendlay row at 12.20 only slightly on E_mech terms at 0.0650 against 0.0714. The dead hang applies the entire bodyweight-plus-load as glenohumeral distraction at full humeral elevation, which is the most demanding joint-transmission scenario evaluated so far.", "The 100 kg input required interpretation. Under the bodyweight convention it was read as 85 kg body mass plus 15 kg added, with the arms above the elbow subtracted from the moved mass to give 92 kg effective. If the intended reading was 100 kg of added load on top of bodyweight, total demand would be roughly 420 cm2 and would exceed any exercise in the library, which would be implausible for an 8-12 rep set at this athlete's strength level. Consider a separate added_load_kg column for bodyweight exercises to remove this ambiguity, since it will recur for dips, weighted chin-ups, and belt squats.", "Rectus_femoris and adductor_longus_brevis appear for the first time, both purely as rigging. Their inclusion is defensible under the stated exception but they are the most marginal entries in the library at 3.66 and 4.24 cm2, and both should be dropped if the stabiliser criterion is later tightened.", "A wide pronated pull-up must not inherit these values. Pronation cuts biceps leverage by roughly 20 percent, the wider grip shortens the effective humeral moment arm, and the emphasis shifts from adduction toward horizontal abduction.", "An unweighted pull-up must not inherit these values either. Removing the belt eliminates the swinging mass that admits most of the trunk and hip contribution, so total demand would fall by more than the 15 percent load reduction alone would suggest."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric initiation from the dead hang: humerus fully elevated overhead at roughly 165 degrees, elbows near-extended, scapulae elevated and upwardly rotated, before the elbows drive down and the humeral moment arm shortens.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 245.71, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 1.0000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9800, "E_LoA": 0.9810, "E_mech": 0.8171, "muscle": "latissimus_dorsi", "rationale": "Prime mover for the 168 N-m glenohumeral adduction and extension moment, at maximal stretched length in the dead hang. Highest engagement it receives in the library, marginally above the pulldown because the heavier effective load pushes it closer to full utilisation.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.85, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 35.83}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.6256, "muscle": "wrist_flexors", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary load-transmitting element and near-saturated: the entire 902 N passes through the grip with no anchoring or straps, making this the highest grip demand in the library.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 34.03}, {"E_LT": 0.9800, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.9500, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.7635, "muscle": "brachialis", "rationale": "Dominant elbow flexor against the 50 N-m elbow moment, lengthened at the near-extended dead hang and unaffected by forearm orientation.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 33.63}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.9500, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.6318, "muscle": "biceps_brachii", "rationale": "Highest engagement of any pulling exercise in the library: the neutral grip removes the pronation penalty and the overhead dead hang means its shoulder-extension action assists the required humeral moment rather than being irrelevant to it.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 25.14}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9200, "E_LoA": 0.7670, "E_mech": 0.5698, "muscle": "rhomboids", "rationale": "Genuine task torque: its downward-rotation action matches the scapular motion required as the elevated scapula is pulled down from the dead hang.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 28.25, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 16.10}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9200, "E_LoA": 0.8095, "E_mech": 0.5700, "muscle": "trapezius_middle", "rationale": "Contributes to the 79 N-m scapulothoracic moment, though its horizontal retraction vector only partly matches the predominantly downward scapular motion.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 26.18, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 14.92}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.4000, "E_LoA": 0.3185, "E_mech": 0.0650, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary stabiliser: the dead hang applies the full 902 N as a distraction load to the fully elevated humeral head, the largest glenohumeral distraction in the library, requiring substantial cuff compression to transmit force into the scapula.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 11.10}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9200, "E_LoA": 0.9970, "E_mech": 0.7406, "muscle": "trapezius_lower", "rationale": "Best-aligned scapular muscle: its depression vector matches the required downward scapular moment directly, and it is maximally lengthened by the elevated dead hang.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 14.98, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 11.09}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.6500, "E_LoA": 0.8640, "E_mech": 0.4297, "muscle": "rectus_abdominis", "rationale": "Included as mechanically necessary rather than postural: with no thigh pads, active trunk flexion torque is required to hold the body rigid against the swinging belt load and to resist the extension moment created by the suspended lower body.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.29, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 10.44}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.5730, "E_mech": 0.3470, "muscle": "deltoid_posterior", "rationale": "Assists humeral extension from the overhead position, but its horizontal abduction vector contributes little to the predominantly adduction-direction moment of a neutral-grip pull-up.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.65, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 8.55}, {"E_LT": 0.8000, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.4500, "E_LoA": 0.3175, "E_mech": 0.0800, "muscle": "iliopsoas", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary load-transmitting element: it holds the pelvis and legs in a fixed hollow position against the 38 N-m moment from the suspended belt load, without which the added weight would swing and the kinetic chain would break. Almost entirely isometric.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 102.23, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 8.18}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.3920, "E_mech": 0.1000, "muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary load-transmitting element: bilateral oblique tension resists both trunk extension and the rotational perturbation of a freely hanging belt load, maintaining the rigid body position the pull depends on.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 8.17}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.6737, "muscle": "brachioradialis", "rationale": "The neutral grip places it in its optimal forearm orientation, offsetting the reduced moment arm at the near-extended elbow.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 11.22, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 7.56}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.3500, "E_LoA": 0.3570, "E_mech": 0.0637, "muscle": "adductor_longus_brevis", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary load-transmitting element: active adduction holds the legs together so the dip belt load remains a single rigid mass rather than two independently swinging limbs. Small but genuinely required.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 66.55, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 4.24}, {"E_LT": 0.7500, "E_MA": 0.6500, "E_ND": 0.3500, "E_LoA": 0.2345, "E_mech": 0.0400, "muscle": "rectus_femoris", "rationale": "Included as a load-transmitting element via its hip-flexion action, assisting iliopsoas in holding the legs against the belt load. Its knee-extension function is irrelevant here, which is why alignment is low.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 91.44, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 3.66}, {"E_LT": 1.0000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9400, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.7990, "muscle": "teres_major", "rationale": "Acts in parallel with latissimus dorsi on humeral adduction and extension, maximally lengthened at the dead hang.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 4.50, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 3.60}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.6000, "E_LoA": 0.1673, "E_mech": 0.0668, "muscle": "pectoralis_major_sternal", "rationale": "Contributes a genuine humeral adduction component from the fully overhead dead hang, where its fibres pull the arm down toward the torso. Applies only in the earliest part of the range.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 53.48, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 3.57}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6500, "E_ND": 0.4000, "E_LoA": 0.2260, "E_mech": 0.0500, "muscle": "pronators_supinators", "rationale": "Included as a load-transmitting element: maintaining the neutral forearm position against the rotational tendency of a freely hanging body requires active mid-position control, though mid-position is the forearm's mechanically neutral orientation so demand is modest.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 60.88, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 3.04}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.4815, "E_mech": 0.1300, "muscle": "transverse_abdominis", "rationale": "Generates the intra-abdominal pressure that stiffens the trunk into a rigid link between the pulling arms and the suspended belt load.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 21.97, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 2.86}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["100 kg is interpreted as 85 kg body mass plus 15 kg of added external load on a dip belt.", "The arms and forearms above the elbow are not lifted by the pulling musculature, so the moved mass is ~92 kg, giving ~902 N effective after a small allowance for controlled acceleration.", "Parallel neutral-grip handles at roughly shoulder width, hanging freely from a fixed bar.", "Humerus length ~0.32 m: at the dead hang the load line runs close to the humeral long axis, giving a glenohumeral external moment arm of ~0.183 m per side for the adduction and extension component.", "Elbows near-extended at the limiting phase with the load line close to perpendicular to the forearm, giving an elbow external moment arm of ~0.055 m per side.", "Internal moment arms: latissimus shoulder extension and adduction ~0.058 m overhead, elbow flexors ~0.030 m at the near-extended elbow, scapular depressors and retractors ~0.055 m.", "Neutral grip places the forearm in mid-position, favouring brachialis and brachioradialis without a supination demand.", "Scapular depression and downward rotation from the elevated dead hang is genuine task torque, since the scapula must be actively pulled down against the full suspended body mass.", "Unlike a pulldown there are no thigh pads: the trunk and hip musculature must generate real torque to hold the body rigid and prevent swing, and the added belt load hangs below the pelvis, amplifying this requirement.", "The dip belt load hangs at ~0.35 m below the hip axis and swings freely, requiring active lumbopelvic control estimated at ~38 N-m.", "Grip without straps: finger and wrist flexor force is the sole path by which the entire 902 N reaches the arm.", "100 kg total is a demanding but realistic 8-12 rep load for an advanced 85 kg athlete, equating to roughly 18 percent added bodyweight."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "glenohumeral_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 168.00}, {"joint": "scapulothoracic_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 79.00}, {"joint": "elbow_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 50.00}, {"joint": "lumbopelvic", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 38.00}], "nominal_load_kg": 100.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 902.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 245.71, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 245.50}
43	incline_dumbbell_curl	Incline Dumbbell Curl	Seated Incline Bench Dumbbell Curl	Upper	Biceps	Isolation	Dumbbell	28.00	76.66	{"brachialis": 32.65, "biceps_brachii": 29.31, "brachioradialis": 6.90, "deltoid_anterior": 2.69, "pronators_supinators": 5.11}	{"grip": "Neutral or supinated grip depending on variation; if supinated, the forearm rotates from neutral to supinated as the elbow flexes; grip width is not applicable given the independent dumbbell setup.", "tldr": {"focus": "Keep the shoulders pressed into the pad so the upper arms cannot travel forward during the curl.", "setup": "Sit back on an incline bench with the shoulders pinned to the pad and arms hanging straight down.", "execution": "Curl both dumbbells up through elbow flexion while keeping the upper arms fixed behind the torso line, then lower under full control.", "stop_when": "The dumbbells can no longer be curled without the shoulders lifting off the pad or the upper arms swinging forward."}, "stance": "Feet flat on the floor or footrest for overall stability, not contributing to the arm movement.", "bracing": "Shoulders and upper back pressed firmly into the pad throughout, preventing the upper arms from drifting forward or the torso from curling up off the bench to assist the lift.", "overview": "This is the incline dumbbell curl performed seated on an incline bench with the torso reclined and the shoulders pinned against the pad. The reclined position places the upper arms behind the plane of the torso at the bottom of the movement, placing the biceps under a greater stretch at the start of the repetition than an upright standing or seated curl. It differs from a standing dumbbell curl in that the fixed torso angle prevents the upper arms from swinging forward to assist the lift, isolating the elbow flexors more strictly.", "eccentric": "The dumbbells lower under control back to a position of full elbow extension with the arms hanging behind the torso line, resisting the pull of gravity throughout rather than allowing the arms to drop.", "concentric": "The dumbbells are curled upward through elbow flexion while the upper arms remain fixed behind the torso line against the pad, with the forearms supinating through the range if using a supinating variation.", "tempo_notes": "The eccentric phase should be controlled given the increased stretch position at the bottom; avoid dropping the dumbbells rapidly or using momentum to initiate the concentric phase.", "end_position": "Dumbbells raised to approximately shoulder height with the elbows fully flexed and the upper arms still fixed against the pad.", "safety_notes": ["Control the eccentric phase carefully given the increased biceps stretch at the bottom of the range.", "Select an incline angle that allows the shoulders to remain comfortably against the pad throughout the set."], "external_cues": "Curl the dumbbells straight up toward the shoulders; keep the upper arms pinned behind the body throughout.", "internal_cues": "Keep the shoulders glued to the pad; think of curling from the elbow only, without letting the upper arm drift forward.", "common_mistakes": ["Allowing the upper arms to drift forward off the bench line to assist the curl.", "Lifting the shoulders off the pad as fatigue increases.", "Using wrist flexion to help initiate the curl instead of elbow flexion.", "Curling only one arm at a time with excessive torso lean toward the working side.", "Dropping the dumbbells rapidly during the eccentric phase instead of controlling the stretch position."], "equipment_setup": "Bench set to an incline angle sufficient to fix the shoulders behind the hips, typically requiring the torso to recline enough that the arms hang below and slightly behind the torso line at the bottom of the movement.", "range_of_motion": "From full elbow extension with the arms hanging behind the torso at the bottom to full elbow flexion at the top, limited only by the point at which the shoulders would need to lift off the pad to continue the curl.", "rir_0_definition": "Inability to curl the dumbbells through elbow flexion while keeping the shoulders against the pad and the upper arms fixed, as distinct from continuing to move the dumbbells by allowing the upper arms to swing forward.", "rir_1_indicators": "Reduced curl height, slower concentric speed, and a growing tendency for the upper arms to drift forward, while the shoulders remain against the pad and the general path stays intact.", "individualization": "Bench incline angle and the degree of forearm supination may vary moderately with shoulder and elbow comfort and individual biceps insertion, provided the shoulders remain fixed against the pad and the upper arms do not drift forward during the curl.", "plane_of_movement": "Sagittal-plane elbow flexion and extension, with the shoulder held in a relatively fixed, slightly extended position throughout.", "starting_position": "Seated with the back and shoulders flat against the incline pad, hips positioned toward the back of the seat, arms hanging straight down from the shoulders with the dumbbells at the sides, elbows extended.", "technical_failure": "The point at which the dumbbells can no longer be curled without the shoulders lifting off the pad, the upper arms swinging forward, or the torso curling upward to assist the lift.", "primary_joint_actions": "Elbow flexion driven primarily by the biceps brachii and brachialis, with the brachioradialis contributing depending on forearm rotation; the glenohumeral joint remains in a fixed, extended position stabilized against the bench rather than actively flexing; the forearm may supinate through the range depending on the intended grip variation."}	{}	{https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DCe8f6vMe9A,https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V7Zy0NcSlec}	Bilateral	{"slug": "incline_dumbbell_curl", "notes": ["This is the direct incline counterpart to the preacher curl calibration example in the prompt, at the same 28 kg bilateral load, and the contrast is instructive. The preacher example places biceps_brachii in the 0.50 to 0.85 band with a note that shoulder position penalises it; the incline position does the opposite, holding the shoulder in extension so the long head sits at a favourable stretched length. E_mech of 0.7361 sits near the top of that band for exactly that reason.", "The result closely tracks the prompt's stated pattern for the preacher curl, with brachialis at 0.7413 within the 0.65 to 0.90 band and brachioradialis at 0.6148 above the stated 0.15 to 0.45 range. The brachioradialis excess is deliberate: that band assumes a preacher bench where the limiting angle occurs in deeper flexion, whereas the 90 degree limiting angle here is precisely where its moment arm peaks.", "Pronators_supinators enters for the first time in the library, as a load-transmitting element rather than an agonist. A dumbbell's mass is offset from the forearm axis and applies a continuous pronation moment; resisting it is mechanically required for the supinated position the biceps leverage depends on. This rationale is specific to dumbbells and should not carry over to barbell or EZ-bar curls where the implement is symmetric about the grip.", "Deltoid_anterior is included on stabiliser grounds under the explicit exception in the inclusion rules, since it transmits a real component of the external load by holding the humerus against 11 N-m of extension moment. Its 2.69 cm2 is small enough that omitting it would not materially change the total, and it should be dropped if the model later tightens the stabiliser criterion.", "Total demand of 76.66 cm2 places this between the bodyweight crunch and the face pull. The elbow flexors as a group reach 68.86 cm2 against a combined maximum FCSA of 95.08, so utilisation is high without saturation, which is the expected signature of a well-loaded single-joint exercise.", "A standing dumbbell curl must not inherit these values. Removing the incline shortens the biceps long head at the shoulder, lowering its E_LT, and permits a heavier load plus some torso contribution.", "A neutral-grip or hammer variant on the same bench must not inherit these values either. Neutral grip raises brachioradialis substantially, lowers biceps_brachii leverage, and largely removes the pronators_supinators demand."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric mid-range at approximately 90 degrees of elbow flexion, where the forearm is horizontal and the external moment arm from the elbow axis to the dumbbell is at its maximum.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 76.66, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.9500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.9130, "E_mech": 0.7413, "muscle": "brachialis", "rationale": "Largest single contributor to the 43.2 N-m elbow flexion moment and the only major flexor unaffected by shoulder position or forearm orientation, operating near-optimal length and leverage at the 90 degree limiting angle.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 32.65}, {"E_LT": 0.9800, "E_MA": 0.9500, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.9300, "E_mech": 0.7361, "muscle": "biceps_brachii", "rationale": "The incline position places the long head at a favourable stretched length across the shoulder, which is the defining mechanical feature of this variant, and the supinated grip gives near-optimal alignment. Slightly below brachialis because part of its capacity is spent maintaining supination and because active insufficiency limits it toward the top of the range.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 29.31}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.9000, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.8930, "E_mech": 0.6148, "muscle": "brachioradialis", "rationale": "Well positioned at the 90 degree limiting angle where its moment arm is largest, though the fully supinated grip is less favourable for it than a neutral one.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 11.22, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 6.90}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.2820, "E_mech": 0.0839, "muscle": "pronators_supinators", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary load-transmitting element: the supinator and pronator teres must actively resist the pronation moment the offset dumbbell applies, since maintaining supination is required for the biceps to act at its modelled leverage. Almost all of the capacity is non-propulsive.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 60.88, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 5.11}, {"E_LT": 0.7500, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.3365, "E_mech": 0.0883, "muscle": "deltoid_anterior", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary stabiliser: it resists the 11 N-m moment that would otherwise drag the humerus further into extension, holding the upper arm in the fixed position the exercise geometry depends on. Shortened and poorly positioned in shoulder extension, so utilisation is low.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 30.46, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 2.69}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["Bilateral convention: 28 kg total is interpreted as 14 kg per dumbbell.", "Dumbbell weight 275 N plus ~1.5 percent for controlled acceleration: ~279 N effective, ~140 N per limb.", "Bench set to ~60 degrees from horizontal, seated with the back supported and the upper arms hanging behind the torso line at roughly 20 to 30 degrees of shoulder extension.", "Forearm length from elbow axis to dumbbell centre ~0.155 m; at the horizontal-forearm limiting phase the full length acts as the external moment arm.", "Elbow flexor internal moment arm ~0.042 m at 90 degrees, close to maximal for this joint.", "Supinated grip held throughout, placing biceps_brachii in its most mechanically favourable forearm orientation.", "The incline position holds the shoulder in extension, which lengthens the biceps long head across the shoulder and reduces the range over which it can shorten actively.", "The upper arm hangs approximately vertical, so the glenohumeral external moment is small and arises mainly from holding the humerus against the dumbbell rather than from any required shoulder motion.", "The bench supports the torso fully, so no trunk musculature generates net task torque and no cheat-swing contribution is available.", "28 kg total is a realistic 8-12 rep working load for an advanced 85 kg athlete in the incline position, which permits less weight than a standing curl."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "elbow_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 43.20}, {"joint": "glenohumeral_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 11.00}], "nominal_load_kg": 28.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 279.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 76.66, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 76.50}
34	dragon_flag	Dragon Flag	Flat Bench Dragon Flag	Core	Core	Secondary_Compound	Bodyweight	55.00	115.00	{"obliques": 37.35, "iliopsoas": 27.97, "rectus_femoris": 15.93, "latissimus_dorsi": 11.36, "rectus_abdominis": 17.34, "triceps_long_head": 5.05}	{"grip": "Overhand or neutral grip on the fixed anchor point behind the head, firm enough to support the full bodyweight throughout the movement.", "tldr": {"focus": "Keep the hips, spine, and legs locked into one rigid line throughout instead of allowing the hips or lower back to break independently.", "setup": "Lie supine on a flat bench, gripping a fixed anchor point behind the head with shoulders pinned to the bench.", "execution": "Lower the fully extended body as a single rigid unit from a vertical position toward the bench, then raise it back up, keeping only the upper back in contact with the bench.", "stop_when": "The hips or lower back break from the rigid line, or the legs and torso can no longer be lowered under control."}, "stance": "Legs extended and held together throughout, functioning as part of the single rigid body line rather than moving independently.", "bracing": "Maximal abdominal and hip-flexor bracing to maintain a fixed spine-pelvis relationship, preventing the hips from sagging toward the bench or the lower back from arching as the body lowers.", "overview": "This is the dragon flag performed on a flat bench, anchored by gripping a fixed point such as the bench edge behind the head. The exercise requires the body to move as a single rigid unit, supported only by the shoulders and upper back against the bench, distinguishing it from a hollow-body hold or leg raise in which the lower back or hips remain in contact with the surface throughout. The defining characteristic is that the entire torso and legs lever from the shoulders as one segment, with the hips lifting fully off the bench.", "eccentric": "The body lowers under control from a vertical position toward the bench by allowing controlled rotation around the shoulders, with the hips, spine, and legs remaining in the same fixed relationship throughout rather than the hips flexing ahead of the torso.", "concentric": "The body is raised from a lowered position back toward vertical by rotating as a single rigid unit around the shoulders, driven primarily by the abdominals and hip flexors resisting trunk and hip extension.", "tempo_notes": "The lowering phase is inherently controlled by the isometric demand of maintaining the rigid body line; avoid dropping suddenly once control of the line is lost rather than continuing to force the range.", "end_position": "Body lowered to a position just above the bench, with only the shoulders and upper back in contact with the bench and the hips still elevated off the surface, before reversing back upward.", "safety_notes": ["Ensure the anchor point can support the full bodyweight securely before attempting the movement.", "Avoid allowing the lower back to slam into the bench if control is lost during the lowering phase."], "external_cues": "Lower the legs and torso together as one board; keep the heels tracking directly above the head.", "internal_cues": "Squeeze the entire body into one straight, rigid line from shoulders to feet; think of keeping the ribcage pulled down toward the pelvis throughout the movement.", "common_mistakes": ["Allowing the hips to flex ahead of the torso, breaking the rigid body line.", "Letting the lower back arch and touch the bench prematurely instead of maintaining a straight line.", "Bending the knees to shorten the lever and reduce difficulty.", "Using momentum or a jerking motion to initiate the ascent instead of controlled trunk and hip tension.", "Stopping well short of a full range while claiming the intended position, rather than working within the individual's true controllable range."], "equipment_setup": "Flat bench with a stable edge or attached anchor point behind the head that can be gripped securely to support the entire bodyweight through the lowering and raising phases.", "range_of_motion": "From a vertical or near-vertical body position at the top to the lowest position at which the rigid body line can be maintained without the hips or lower back touching the bench or breaking from the line, whichever occurs first for the individual.", "rir_0_definition": "Inability to lower or raise the body while maintaining the fixed rigid line from shoulders to feet, as distinct from continuing the movement with the hips flexing independently or the lower back losing its extended position.", "rir_1_indicators": "A reduced usable range before the rigid line begins to break, increased trembling or effort to maintain the spine-pelvis relationship, and slower, less controlled lowering, while the body line remains technically intact.", "individualization": "The usable range of motion before the rigid body line breaks may vary substantially with abdominal and hip-flexor strength, and shorter partial ranges are a legitimate expression of the same movement pattern provided the spine-pelvis relationship remains fixed throughout the range that is used.", "plane_of_movement": "Sagittal-plane movement of the trunk and lower body around the shoulders as a fixed pivot, combining trunk flexion/extension control with hip extension/flexion held in a fixed relationship to the spine.", "starting_position": "Supine on the bench with the hands gripping the anchor point behind the head, shoulders and upper back in contact with the bench, body raised to a position where the torso and legs are extended vertically or near-vertically above the shoulders, hips and spine held rigid.", "technical_failure": "The point at which the hips break from the rigid line, either flexing ahead of the torso or sagging toward the bench, or the lower back touches the bench before the intended range is reached.", "primary_joint_actions": "The spine and hips are held in a fixed, extended relationship throughout, functioning as a single lever rather than moving independently; the shoulders and elbows act as the anchor point, with the glenohumeral joints held in extension against the bench edge; the hip flexors and abdominals act isometrically and eccentrically to prevent the hips from flexing relative to the spine as the body lowers."}	{}	{https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eLCBC6fjtQU}	Bilateral	{"slug": "dragon_flag", "notes": ["The exercise distributes the suspended-body demand across the lumbopelvic, hip, and shoulder-anchor systems rather than imposing the full whole-body moment on a single anatomical joint.", "Latissimus dorsi and triceps long head are included because their shoulder-extension force is mechanically necessary for transmitting the suspended-body load into the bench, not merely for postural stabilization.", "Transverse abdominis is excluded because its principal contribution is abdominal compression and segmental stiffness rather than a large net flexion moment.", "Erector spinae is excluded because its extension action opposes the required lumbopelvic anti-extension moment; antagonist co-contraction is not counted as task-producing demand.", "The estimate is highly sensitive to body angle, segment mass distribution, knee extension, hip sag, grip position, and whether the repetition is reversed under control or with momentum."], "limiting_phase": "Static-to-concentric reversal near the lowest controlled body position, with the trunk and straight legs held nearly parallel to the bench while the body forms a rigid lever supported through the upper thorax and anchored arms.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 115.00, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.7800, "E_ND": 0.7400, "E_LoA": 0.8800, "E_mech": 0.4571, "muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "The bilateral oblique aggregate supplies a large anti-extension and posterior-pelvic-rotation moment while maintaining trunk rigidity against the long external body lever.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 37.35}, {"E_LT": 0.8400, "E_MA": 0.7200, "E_ND": 0.5800, "E_LoA": 0.7800, "E_mech": 0.2736, "muscle": "iliopsoas", "rationale": "Produces mechanically necessary isometric hip-flexion torque that prevents the pelvis and straight legs from extending relative to the trunk.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 102.23, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 27.97}, {"E_LT": 0.9200, "E_MA": 0.8800, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.9800, "E_mech": 0.7141, "muscle": "rectus_abdominis", "rationale": "Acts as the most directly aligned lumbopelvic anti-extension muscle and generates posterior pelvic rotation to resist lumbar extension under the suspended body mass.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.29, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 17.34}, {"E_LT": 0.7200, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.4800, "E_LoA": 0.7200, "E_mech": 0.1742, "muscle": "rectus_femoris", "rationale": "Assists isometric hip flexion, but the extended knee shortens the muscle distally and its knee-extension role does not directly oppose the primary external body moment.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 91.44, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 15.93}, {"E_LT": 0.8600, "E_MA": 0.7400, "E_ND": 0.5500, "E_LoA": 0.7400, "E_mech": 0.2590, "muscle": "latissimus_dorsi", "rationale": "Generates mechanically necessary shoulder-extension force through the bench grip, anchoring the upper body so the trunk and lower-limb moments can be transmitted into the bench.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.85, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 11.36}, {"E_LT": 0.7800, "E_MA": 0.6800, "E_ND": 0.4200, "E_LoA": 0.5200, "E_mech": 0.1158, "muscle": "triceps_long_head", "rationale": "Provides a smaller shoulder-extension and arm-fixation contribution during the bench anchor, while most of its available action is not aligned with the primary lumbopelvic demand.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.55, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 5.05}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["The 55 kg nominal load is interpreted as the effective suspended mass of the trunk, pelvis, and lower limbs rather than additional external weight.", "The body is maintained as a nearly rigid segment with extended knees and minimal hip sag.", "Representative effective gravitational moment arms are approximately 0.18 m about the lumbopelvic region, 0.24 m about the hips, and 0.12 m about the shoulder-anchor system.", "The athlete grips the bench behind the head and generates active shoulder-extension force to prevent the body from sliding away from the upper-back support.", "Momentum, lumbar hyperextension, and assistance from forceful hip flexion during the reversal are minimized."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "lumbopelvic_flexion_isometric", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 97.12}, {"joint": "hip_flexion_bilateral_isometric", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 129.49}, {"joint": "shoulder_extension_bilateral_isometric", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 64.75}], "nominal_load_kg": 55.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 539.55}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 115.00, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 115.00}
49	weighted_russian_twist	Weighted Russian Twist	Seated Weighted Russian Twist	Core	Core	Secondary_Compound	Other	15.00	63.45	{"obliques": 42.52, "iliopsoas": 14.17, "rectus_abdominis": 6.76}	{"grip": "Hold the implement securely with both hands and keep the wrists neutral. The elbows may remain softly flexed and should maintain a relatively consistent position throughout the movement.", "tldr": {"focus": "Turn through the trunk rather than merely moving the hands from side to side.", "setup": "Sit with the knees bent, heels lightly supported or hovering, torso reclined slightly, and the external load held close in front of the chest.", "execution": "Rotate the ribcage and shoulders side to side as one unit over a stable pelvis while keeping the trunk angle essentially unchanged.", "stop_when": "Stop when another repetition would require arm swinging, pelvic rotation, loss of the reclined torso position, or a materially shorter rotational range."}, "stance": "Keep the knees bent with the feet either lightly supported on the floor or elevated if balance and trunk control permit. Foot position should remain unchanged during the set and should not be used to generate rotational momentum.", "bracing": "Maintain sufficient abdominal and hip-flexor tension to preserve the chosen reclined torso angle while preventing the pelvis from rotating with the shoulders. Keep the ribcage controlled and avoid repeatedly flexing and extending the spine between sides.", "overview": "A seated loaded trunk-rotation exercise performed with the torso held in a modest recline and an external load controlled in front of the body. The defining action is alternating axial rotation of the trunk relative to a comparatively stable pelvis, rather than sweeping the weight laterally using the shoulders and arms alone. Hip flexors contribute to maintaining the seated reclined position, but repeated hip flexion-extension is not part of the movement.", "eccentric": "Because the exercise alternates direction rather than having a conventional lift-and-lower cycle, control the deceleration as the trunk approaches each side and through the reversal toward the opposite direction. Do not allow the load's momentum to pull the torso beyond the controlled rotational range.", "concentric": "Rotate the ribcage, shoulders, and load together toward one side while keeping the pelvis comparatively stable and the torso recline unchanged. The hands should remain positioned relative to the chest so that the implement follows the trunk rather than leading it independently. Reverse direction through the center and repeat toward the opposite side.", "tempo_notes": "Avoid rapid side-to-side swinging or rebounding through the center. Decelerate the load at each rotational endpoint so direction changes remain controlled.", "end_position": "Each side is complete at the greatest controlled trunk rotation achievable without pelvic rotation, loss of balance, spinal collapse, or independent arm sweeping.", "safety_notes": ["Use an implement that can be held securely throughout repeated direction changes.", "Do not force rotational range beyond a comfortable spinal position.", "If the feet are elevated, maintain enough balance that loss of trunk control cannot cause an uncontrolled fall backward."], "external_cues": "Move the shoulders and load together from side to side without letting the hands race ahead of the torso.", "internal_cues": "Turn the ribs over a quiet pelvis; keep the recline fixed; keep the weight connected to the chest.", "common_mistakes": ["Moving the weight from side to side primarily with the arms instead of rotating the ribcage and shoulders.", "Allowing the pelvis and knees to rotate with the torso, reducing relative trunk rotation.", "Rocking repeatedly into spinal flexion and extension as the load changes sides.", "Using the feet or legs to push against the floor and create rotational momentum.", "Reaching excessively far with the hands and losing the relationship between the load and the chest.", "Swinging rapidly through the center without controlling the change of direction."], "equipment_setup": "Use a compact external load such as a plate, dumbbell, medicine ball, or similar implement that can be held securely with both hands without obstructing trunk rotation.", "range_of_motion": "Rotate through the largest comfortable axial trunk range that can be achieved while the pelvis remains comparatively fixed and the torso angle stays stable. The implement does not need to touch the floor; rotational depth should be determined by trunk motion rather than arbitrary hand position.", "rir_0_definition": "RIR 0 is reached when no additional full alternating repetition can be completed through the defined rotational range while maintaining pelvic and trunk control, even if the load could still be moved by swinging the arms, rotating the hips, changing torso angle, or shortening the rotation.", "rir_1_indicators": "Approximately one technically valid repetition may remain when rotational speed slows and maintaining the reclined position becomes noticeably harder, but the lifter can still preserve pelvic stability, comparable rotation to both sides, and coordinated movement of the trunk and load.", "individualization": "Torso recline, foot support, knee angle, distance of the load from the chest, and rotational range may vary with trunk strength, hip-flexor capacity, limb proportions, balance, and spinal mobility. These parameters are acceptable to individualize provided the pelvis remains comparatively stable and the movement is produced predominantly by controlled axial trunk rotation rather than arm sweeping or whole-body twisting.", "plane_of_movement": "Primarily transverse-plane axial trunk rotation, performed while the trunk is held isometrically in a modest sagittal-plane recline.", "starting_position": "Sit with the knees flexed and the torso reclined slightly behind vertical while maintaining a controlled spinal position. Hold the load close in front of the sternum or upper abdomen with both hands. Keep the pelvis centered and establish balance before initiating rotation.", "technical_failure": "Technical failure occurs when another side-to-side repetition cannot be completed through the established rotational range without materially changing the mechanics. Observable breakdowns include sweeping the implement with the arms while the trunk barely rotates, rotating or shifting the pelvis, repeatedly flexing and extending the spine, losing the reclined position, using the feet to generate momentum, or substantially shortening rotation.", "primary_joint_actions": "During each rotational phase, the thoracic and lumbar spine rotate relative to the pelvis within a controlled available range, while the hips and trunk extensors maintain the seated reclined position. The shoulders and elbows primarily stabilize the external load in front of the torso rather than generating the rotation independently."}	{}	{https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uIGn2y3yBGI}	Bilateral	{"slug": "weighted_russian_twist", "notes": ["The oblique contribution can generate approximately 48 Nm of axial-rotation moment using a representative effective rotational moment arm near 0.045 m, closely matching the estimated external rotational demand.", "The exercise combines dynamic axial rotation with concurrent isometric hip-flexion and lumbar-flexion requirements; these demands are evaluated at the same reversal phase rather than summed from separate points in the repetition.", "Transverse abdominis is excluded because its principal role is abdominal compression and segmental stabilization rather than production of a meaningful net axial-rotation or flexion moment.", "Erector spinae is excluded because its sagittal-plane action opposes the required flexion moment; antagonist co-contraction is not counted as task-producing FCSA demand.", "The estimate is sensitive to torso recline, arm extension, foot support, rotation amplitude, and whether the weight is moved through true trunk rotation or primarily by the shoulders."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric reversal from approximately 35-45 degrees of trunk rotation with the torso reclined, where the weight is farthest from the spinal axis and rotational acceleration is initiated while the reclined posture is maintained.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 63.45, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8200, "E_ND": 0.8200, "E_LoA": 0.8600, "E_mech": 0.5204, "muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "The internal and external oblique aggregate supplies the principal axial-rotation moment while concurrently assisting maintenance of the flexed and reclined trunk position.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 42.52}, {"E_LT": 0.8000, "E_MA": 0.5500, "E_ND": 0.4500, "E_LoA": 0.7000, "E_mech": 0.1386, "muscle": "iliopsoas", "rationale": "Produces a mechanically necessary isometric hip-flexion moment that prevents the reclined torso from extending about the supported pelvis.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 102.23, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 14.17}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.5500, "E_LoA": 0.8500, "E_mech": 0.2782, "muscle": "rectus_abdominis", "rationale": "Resists lumbar extension caused by the reclined upper-body mass, but does not provide the primary axial-rotation torque.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.29, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 6.76}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["The athlete holds a 15 kg plate or compact weight with both arms extended approximately 0.23 m from the trunk rotational axis.", "The estimated effective force includes the external weight and the mechanically relevant contribution of the forearms and hands.", "The torso remains reclined approximately 35 degrees from vertical with the heels lightly supported on the floor.", "Upper-body mass creates additional sagittal-plane extension moments about the hips and lumbar spine that must be resisted isometrically.", "The athlete rotates through the trunk rather than merely moving the arms across the body."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "trunk_axial_rotation", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 50.77}, {"joint": "hip_flexion_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 55.00}, {"joint": "lumbar_flexion_isometric", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 27.00}], "nominal_load_kg": 15.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 225.63}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 63.45, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 63.45}
7	bodyweight_neck_curl	Neck Curl	Supine Bench Bodyweight Neck Curl	Upper	Neck	Isolation	Bodyweight	5.00	8.55	{"sternocleidomastoid": 8.55}	{"grip": "", "tldr": {"focus": "Move only the head through neck flexion while keeping the shoulders and torso completely still.", "setup": "Lie supine on a bench with the head and neck unsupported off the end, shoulders remaining flat on the bench.", "execution": "Flex the neck by drawing the chin toward the chest, then return the head under control to a neutral, slightly extended position.", "stop_when": "The chin can no longer be drawn toward the chest without the shoulders or torso assisting the movement."}, "stance": "Feet flat on the floor or bench end for overall body stability, not contributing to the neck movement itself.", "bracing": "The torso and shoulders remain braced flat against the bench throughout, isolating movement to the cervical spine without the upper back lifting or shifting.", "overview": "This is the bodyweight neck curl, a cervical-flexion exercise performed supine on a bench with the head extending beyond the edge so that the neck can move through flexion and extension against the resistance of the head's own weight. The movement isolates the deep and superficial neck flexors, primarily the sternocleidomastoid, by restricting motion to the cervical spine while the torso and shoulders remain fixed on the bench.", "eccentric": "The head returns under control through cervical extension back to the starting neutral-to-slightly-extended position, without allowing the head to drop suddenly at the end of the range.", "concentric": "The head curls forward through cervical flexion, drawing the chin toward the chest, while the shoulders and torso remain motionless on the bench.", "tempo_notes": "The movement should be performed without jerking the head or using momentum to initiate flexion; a brief controlled pause at the top of the flexed position may be used.", "end_position": "Chin drawn toward the chest with the cervical spine in a flexed position and the shoulders still flat on the bench.", "safety_notes": ["Move through a conservative range and avoid forcing end-range cervical flexion or extension given the vulnerability of the cervical spine.", "Ensure the bench edge does not contact or compress the neck during the movement.", "Progress the range of motion gradually given the small muscle mass and sensitivity of the cervical structures involved."], "external_cues": "Bring the chin down toward the collarbone; keep the shoulders pinned to the bench.", "internal_cues": "Draw the chin toward the chest by shortening the front of the neck; think of curling the head rather than nodding it.", "common_mistakes": ["Lifting the shoulders or upper back off the bench to assist the movement.", "Using a jerking motion of the head instead of controlled cervical flexion.", "Forcing the head into an uncomfortable end-range position beyond the individual's cervical mobility.", "Allowing the head to drop rapidly during the eccentric phase instead of controlling the return."], "equipment_setup": "Flat bench positioned so the head can hang freely off one end without the edge of the bench contacting the neck or skull during the movement.", "range_of_motion": "From a neutral or slightly extended head position at the bottom to full comfortable cervical flexion with the chin near the chest at the top, limited by the individual's cervical mobility and avoiding forcing the end range through discomfort.", "rir_0_definition": "Inability to curl the head through cervical flexion using the neck flexors alone while keeping the torso and shoulders fixed, as distinct from continuing to move the head by shifting the shoulders or using momentum.", "rir_1_indicators": "Reduced range of comfortable flexion, slower movement speed through the mid-range, and increasing effort to control the return phase, while the shoulders and torso remain stationary.", "individualization": "The exact starting head position (neutral versus slightly extended) and the depth of flexion may vary with individual cervical mobility and comfort, provided the torso and shoulders remain fixed on the bench throughout the movement.", "plane_of_movement": "Sagittal-plane cervical flexion and extension.", "starting_position": "Supine on the bench with shoulders and upper back flat against the surface, head extending off the end of the bench in a neutral-to-slightly-extended position, chin roughly level or slightly elevated relative to the forehead.", "technical_failure": "The point at which the head can no longer be curled through cervical flexion without the shoulders lifting off the bench or the torso shifting to generate momentum.", "primary_joint_actions": "Flexion of the cervical spine, drawing the chin toward the sternum; the atlanto-occipital and lower cervical segments contribute to the overall flexion arc; the shoulders and thoracic spine remain stationary and are not involved in the joint action."}	{}	{https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fmhaM_xXiSk,https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JxWOKN9glcI,https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gimeRpdqWQw}	Bilateral	{"slug": "bodyweight_neck_curl", "notes": ["Deep anterior cervical flexors (longus colli, longus capitis, scalenes) are the principal agonists of this movement but have no slug in MUSCLE_FCSA. Their share of the moment was folded into sternocleidomastoid rather than substituting a broader group, which inflates that muscle's apparent contribution and understates true total demand.", "Consider adding a deep_cervical_flexors slug to MUSCLE_FCSA. Its absence is structurally asymmetric: deep_neck_extensors is present at 84.88 cm2, so neck extension exercises will model correctly while every neck flexion exercise carries this distortion.", "Deep_neck_extensors was deliberately excluded. It is the direct antagonist of cervical flexion and produces no task-relevant torque in the concentric phase.", "The 5.00 kg input matches head mass, so the load is fixed by anatomy rather than selected for a rep target. Unweighted supine neck curls typically permit well over 12 repetitions for an advanced athlete, placing this below the RIR 1-2 band assumed elsewhere in the model.", "A weighted variant with a plate or harness on the forehead must not inherit these values. Adding 5 kg at ~0.15 m roughly doubles the cervical moment and would saturate sternocleidomastoid as modelled here.", "At 8.55 cm2 this is the lowest-demand exercise evaluated so far, about 23 percent of the bodyweight crunch and 1.5 percent of the barbell Pendlay row."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric mid-range with the head near the horizontal, where the head's centre of mass sits at its greatest horizontal distance from the cervical flexion axis; the moment falls away rapidly as the chin approaches the chest.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 8.55, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.9500, "E_LoA": 0.9000, "E_mech": 0.6156, "muscle": "sternocleidomastoid", "rationale": "Sole available prime mover for the 5.9 N-m cervical flexion moment. Favourable length at the mid-range limiting phase, but the small internal moment arm and the muscle's oblique line of pull, which includes a rotation component cancelled by bilateral action, limit usable capacity.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 13.83, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 8.55}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["5.00 kg is interpreted as head mass alone, roughly 5.9 percent of the 85 kg reference athlete's body mass, with no added external load.", "Supine on a bench with the head and neck extending past the end, shoulders and thorax supported by the bench surface.", "Horizontal distance from the atlanto-occipital and upper cervical flexion axis to the head's centre of mass ~0.12 m at the limiting phase.", "Sternocleidomastoid effective cervical flexion moment arm ~0.030 m at the mid-range head position.", "Longus colli, longus capitis, and scalenes contribute to cervical flexion but are absent from MUSCLE_FCSA and cannot be substituted by a broader group, so the entire flexion moment is allocated to sternocleidomastoid.", "The bench supports the trunk, so no thoracolumbar flexion moment is required and no hip or trunk musculature bears load.", "Slow controlled tempo with negligible acceleration beyond gravity."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "cervical_spine", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 5.90}], "nominal_load_kg": 5.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 49.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 8.55, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 8.60}
29	high_to_low_pec_deck_fly	High-to-Low Pec Deck Fly	Selectorized High-to-Low Pec Deck Fly	Upper	Chest_Sternal	Isolation	Selectorized_Machine	45.00	96.62	{"brachialis": 2.65, "teres_major": 3.19, "rotator_cuffs": 8.03, "biceps_brachii": 1.67, "deltoid_anterior": 13.31, "latissimus_dorsi": 15.44, "pectoralis_minor": 2.32, "serratus_anterior": 5.55, "pectoralis_major_sternal": 41.11, "pectoralis_major_clavicular": 3.35}	{"grip": "Use the machine handles or forearm pads as designed, keeping the wrists neutral and the hands relaxed enough that grip effort does not alter the arm path. The elbows should remain softly bent and approximately fixed throughout the repetition.", "tldr": {"focus": "Preserve the same elbow angle and drive the upper arms through the machine's high-to-low arc rather than turning the movement into a press.", "setup": "Adjust the seat so the machine arms start slightly above shoulder level with the chest firmly supported against the back pad and the shoulders centered with the machine pivots.", "execution": "Sweep the upper arms forward and downward in a converging arc while keeping the elbows softly bent and the torso fixed against the pad.", "stop_when": "Stop when another repetition would require elbow extension, shoulder shrugging, torso movement, or a materially shorter converging arc."}, "stance": "Sit with both feet firmly planted and positioned to stabilize the pelvis without using leg drive to move the torso.", "bracing": "Maintain firm contact between the torso and back pad with enough abdominal tension to prevent lumbar extension or rib flare. Keep the pelvis stable and prevent torso rotation or forward thrust as the arms converge.", "overview": "A selectorized pec-deck fly performed through a deliberately high-to-low arm path. The upper arms begin elevated relative to the torso and travel forward, inward, and downward during the concentric phase, emphasizing shoulder horizontal adduction with an adduction-extension component rather than the more purely transverse path of a conventional pec deck. The elbows remain softly bent and relatively fixed so the exercise retains fly mechanics rather than becoming a machine press.", "eccentric": "Return the arms upward and outward along the machine's prescribed arc under control. Allow the scapulae to retract gradually as the upper arms open, while maintaining the same elbow angle and stable torso position. Stop the return before the shoulders are forced into an uncomfortable or unstable stretched position.", "concentric": "Bring the machine arms forward, inward, and downward in a smooth converging arc by moving the upper arms through the shoulder joints. Preserve the initial elbow bend rather than extending the elbows to finish the repetition. Keep the torso against the pad and allow only the scapular motion naturally required for the arms to converge.", "tempo_notes": "Avoid bouncing out of the stretched position or allowing the weight stack to accelerate the arms backward. Maintain continuous control through the transition between the open and converged positions.", "end_position": "Finish when the machine arms have converged in front of and slightly below the chest according to the machine geometry, with the elbows still softly bent and the shoulders remaining down and controlled rather than shrugged forward.", "safety_notes": ["Set the starting position conservatively enough that the shoulders are not forced into painful horizontal abduction or extension.", "Keep the torso supported against the pad rather than using momentum to move through the stretched position.", "Ensure the selector pin and machine adjustments are fully engaged before beginning."], "external_cues": "Trace a downward converging arc with the handles and bring them together in front of the lower chest without pushing them forward like a press.", "internal_cues": "Keep the elbows softly bent and fixed; sweep the upper arms down and inward; keep the chest and pelvis pinned to the pad.", "common_mistakes": ["Extending the elbows during the concentric phase and converting the fly into a pressing movement.", "Setting the seat too low or too high so the machine no longer produces the intended elevated-to-lower arm path.", "Allowing the shoulders to shrug as the arms converge.", "Leaning or thrusting the torso forward to finish the repetition.", "Opening the arms beyond a comfortable shoulder range and allowing anterior shoulder translation at the stretched position.", "Flattening the arm path into a conventional horizontal pec-deck fly instead of preserving the high-to-low trajectory."], "equipment_setup": "Adjust the seat and arm position so the shoulders are centered with the machine's intended pivot path and the handles or forearm pads begin slightly above shoulder height. Set the starting range so the upper arms can open without forcing excessive shoulder extension or anterior shoulder translation.", "range_of_motion": "Use the full machine-defined arc that can be performed without painful anterior shoulder stretch, excessive humeral extension, elbow-angle change, or torso compensation. The starting position should provide a controlled chest stretch, and the finish should bring the arms together or near together in the lower converged position without forcing additional protraction beyond the machine's natural path.", "rir_0_definition": "RIR 0 is reached when no additional repetition can be completed through the defined arc with a stable torso and approximately fixed elbow angle, even if the handles could still be moved by pressing with the triceps, shrugging, leaning forward, or shortening the range.", "rir_1_indicators": "Approximately one technically valid repetition may remain when the converging phase slows markedly and the final portion becomes difficult, but the lifter can still preserve the established elbow angle, torso contact, shoulder position, and full intended high-to-low arc.", "individualization": "Seat height, starting arm angle, elbow bend, and usable stretch depth may vary with torso length, humeral length, shoulder structure, and machine geometry. These parameters are acceptable to individualize provided the arms still begin meaningfully higher than the finish, the movement preserves a downward converging fly path, the elbows remain approximately fixed, and the shoulders remain comfortable and stable.", "plane_of_movement": "A hybrid transverse and frontal-to-sagittal movement: primarily glenohumeral horizontal adduction combined with downward adduction and a small extension component as the upper arms travel from a higher, wider start toward a lower, converged finish.", "starting_position": "Sit with the pelvis and upper back supported against the pad and both feet planted. Position the chest upright with the ribcage controlled, the shoulders neither shrugged nor excessively depressed, and the upper arms elevated and opened laterally in the machine's high starting position. Maintain a slight, comfortable bend at the elbows.", "technical_failure": "Technical failure occurs when another repetition cannot be completed through the established high-to-low arc without materially changing the exercise mechanics. Observable signs include extending the elbows to turn the movement into a press, shrugging the shoulders, thrusting the torso forward, losing back-pad contact, substantially shortening the stretched position, or redirecting the handles into a flatter horizontal path.", "primary_joint_actions": "During the concentric phase, the glenohumeral joints perform horizontal adduction with downward adduction and a small extension component relative to the elevated starting position. The scapulae move from controlled retraction toward mild protraction as the arms converge, while the elbows remain at an approximately constant flexion angle and function mainly as force-transmission joints rather than active movers."}	{}	{https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K15uV7lcNqs,https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=a9vQ_hwIksU}	Bilateral	{"slug": "high_to_low_pec_deck_fly", "notes": ["This exercise inverts the muscle roles of the low-to-high cable fly and the pair should be read together. Latissimus dorsi and teres major, excluded as antagonists in the low-to-high version, are genuine agonists here because the descending arm path requires humeral extension. Conversely pectoralis_major_clavicular falls from E_mech 0.7691 to 0.2478 and deltoid_anterior from 0.6922 to 0.4370, since both oppose rather than assist the downward sweep.", "Pectoralis minor is better aligned in this movement than in any exercise evaluated so far, at E_LoA 0.9350. Its scapular depression action matches the high-to-low path directly, in contrast to the presses where it contributed only anchoring force and to the incline press where depression actively conflicted with the required upward rotation.", "Total demand of 96.62 cm2 sits just below the low-to-high cable fly at 105.44 cm2 despite a higher nominal load, because the machine's 0.80 lever ratio and the torso pad reduce both the effective resistance and the stabilisation requirement. The comparison is sensitive to the assumed lever ratio, which is the least certain input here.", "The elbow moment is again an extension moment, so the elbow flexors appear as load-transmitting stabilisers rather than agonists. This is now the third cable or machine fly-type movement showing the pattern, confirming it as general to any fly where the forearm is roughly perpendicular to the resistance line. Their combined 4.32 cm2 is well below the 17.37 cm2 in the free-cable version because the machine pads distribute force along the forearm rather than concentrating it at the hand.", "Machine lever ratio is an approximation and drives the result. If the actual machine runs closer to 1:1, total demand would rise to roughly 120 cm2. Consider recording a per-manufacturer lever ratio field if the library will contain multiple selectorized fly machines.", "A horizontal pec deck and a low-to-high variant must not inherit these values. Path angle determines whether latissimus dorsi acts as agonist or antagonist and reallocates roughly 19 cm2 between the anterior and posterior shoulder musculature.", "A version performed with the elbows deeply flexed against pads rather than near-straight handles must not inherit these values either, since the shorter external moment arm would reduce the glenohumeral moment substantially at the same stack weight."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric initiation from the stretched position: pads or handles at shoulder height, humerus horizontally abducted with the arms high and wide, elbow held at a fixed ~155 degrees, before the arms sweep down and inward and the machine lever arm shortens.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 96.62, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.9200, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.9770, "E_mech": 0.7687, "muscle": "pectoralis_major_sternal", "rationale": "Prime mover and the best-aligned muscle in the exercise: the high-to-low sweep matches the downward-oblique orientation of these fibres almost exactly. Well lengthened at the stretched start with near-maximal internal leverage, and the guided path allows high expression of capacity.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 53.48, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 41.11}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.7500, "E_LoA": 0.6520, "E_mech": 0.3521, "muscle": "latissimus_dorsi", "rationale": "Genuine agonist rather than antagonist in this variant: the downward and inward arm path requires humeral extension and adduction, both within its line of pull. Alignment is partial because the horizontal adduction component that dominates the task is not its primary action.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.85, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 15.44}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.6720, "E_mech": 0.4370, "muscle": "deltoid_anterior", "rationale": "Assists horizontal adduction, but its flexion vector opposes the downward component of the high-to-low path, sharply reducing alignment relative to a horizontal or low-to-high fly.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 30.46, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 13.31}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.3200, "E_LoA": 0.2880, "E_mech": 0.0470, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary stabiliser: the long lever places the humeral head under a substantial distracting moment in the lengthened, horizontally abducted start, so cuff compression is required to transmit load into the scapula. Subscapularis adds a small adduction component. Below the free-cable fly because the machine constrains the path.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 8.03}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.4890, "E_mech": 0.1541, "muscle": "serratus_anterior", "rationale": "Included as a load-transmitting stabiliser: scapular anchoring against the retracting pull is required to route the humeral load into the trunk, though the torso pad absorbs part of the reaction force.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 36.03, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 5.55}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.3810, "E_mech": 0.2478, "muscle": "pectoralis_major_clavicular", "rationale": "Its upward-oblique fibre orientation is poorly matched to a descending arm path; contributes to adduction but works against the downward component, the mirror image of its favourable role in a low-to-high fly.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 13.52, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 3.35}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.7500, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.7089, "muscle": "teres_major", "rationale": "Acts in parallel with latissimus dorsi on humeral extension and adduction, both of which the high-to-low path requires directly; small cross-section limits absolute contribution.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 4.50, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 3.19}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.4500, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.3145, "E_mech": 0.0602, "muscle": "brachialis", "rationale": "Included as a load-transmitting stabiliser: the machine creates an elbow extension moment that must be resisted isometrically for force to reach the humerus. Poor leverage at the near-extended elbow and a small moment relative to the shoulder demand.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 2.65}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.4500, "E_LoA": 0.9350, "E_mech": 0.2146, "muscle": "pectoralis_minor", "rationale": "Better aligned here than in any pressing exercise evaluated so far: its scapular depression action directly matches the downward component of the high-to-low movement, though its small size and largely isometric role limit the contribution.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 10.81, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 2.32}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.4200, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.2350, "E_mech": 0.0419, "muscle": "biceps_brachii", "rationale": "Assists the isometric elbow hold alongside brachialis, with its shoulder-flexion component opposing the downward arm path rather than assisting it.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 1.67}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["Representative commercial selectorized high-to-low pec deck with a cam-and-lever linkage; exact geometry unavailable and approximated.", "Effective lever ratio ~0.80 at the handles in the stretched position: 45 kg nominal resolves to ~36 kg of effective handle resistance, ~353 N total, ~176 N per limb.", "Arms held near-straight at a fixed ~155 degree elbow, so the movement is effectively single-joint at the shoulder; effective external moment arm from the glenohumeral axis to the handle force line ~0.41 m per side.", "High-to-low path: the required humeral action combines horizontal adduction with extension and downward sweep, favouring the sternal pectoral fibres and recruiting latissimus dorsi as a genuine agonist.", "The handle force acts largely perpendicular to the forearm, creating an elbow EXTENSION moment; the elbow flexors work isometrically to hold the fixed elbow angle and transmit load from hand to humerus.", "Elbow external moment arm ~0.045 m per side; elbow flexor internal moment arm ~0.032 m at the near-extended elbow.", "Internal moment arms: pectoral horizontal adduction ~0.054 m, latissimus shoulder extension and adduction ~0.058 m.", "Seat back and torso pad fix the trunk and largely support the scapulae, and the machine constrains the movement path, so balance and free-weight path control are not mechanical requirements.", "45 kg on a machine with this lever ratio corresponds to roughly 36 kg of free-weight-equivalent fly resistance, consistent with 8-12 reps at RIR 1-2 for the reference athlete."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "glenohumeral_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 145.00}, {"joint": "scapulothoracic_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 26.00}, {"joint": "elbow_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 15.90}], "nominal_load_kg": 45.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 353.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 96.62, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 96.50}
17	standing_cable_pallof_press	Pallof Press	Standing Cable Pallof Press	Core	Core	Stability_Isometric	Cable	20.00	74.62	{"obliques": 26.96, "rotator_cuffs": 4.27, "erector_spinae": 6.39, "gluteus_medius": 6.83, "gluteus_maximus": 2.86, "gluteus_minimus": 1.49, "deltoid_anterior": 5.03, "rectus_abdominis": 8.99, "serratus_anterior": 3.60, "triceps_long_head": 1.31, "triceps_medial_head": 1.06, "transverse_abdominis": 3.30, "triceps_lateral_head": 2.53}	{"grip": "Both hands gripping the handle together at the center of the chest, wrists stable throughout the press.", "tldr": {"focus": "Prevent the torso from rotating toward the pulley as the arms extend and the cable tries to pull the body sideways.", "setup": "Stand sideways to a cable pulley set at chest height, holding the handle at the sternum with both hands and feet planted.", "execution": "Press the handle straight out in front of the chest and hold or return, resisting the cable's pull toward the pulley.", "stop_when": "The torso can no longer resist rotation toward the pulley while the arms are extended."}, "stance": "Feet shoulder-width or slightly wider, positioned to provide a stable base against the lateral pull of the cable, knees softly bent.", "bracing": "Maximal abdominal bracing combined with a fixed pelvis and ribcage position, resisting both rotation toward the pulley and lateral flexion as the arms extend away from the body.", "overview": "This is the standing cable Pallof press, an anti-rotation core exercise in which the lifter stands perpendicular to a cable pulley and presses a handle straight out from the chest, resisting the cable's lateral pull toward the machine. Unlike dynamic rotational core exercises, no intentional trunk rotation occurs; the defining characteristic is the isometric resistance to rotation as the arms move through their pressing range.", "eccentric": "The handle returns under control back to the sternum through elbow flexion, with the torso continuing to resist rotation as tension on the cable increases with proximity to the pulley.", "concentric": "The arms press the handle straight out from the sternum to full elbow extension in front of the chest, with the torso remaining squared and resisting the cable's pull toward the pulley throughout.", "tempo_notes": "The press may be performed as a sustained isometric hold at full extension or as controlled repetitions; avoid allowing the cable to yank the arms or torso back toward the pulley between repetitions.", "end_position": "Arms fully extended in front of the chest with the torso still squared and facing forward, held briefly if performing the exercise as a sustained press rather than a repeated one.", "safety_notes": ["Choose a cable resistance that allows the trunk to remain squared throughout rather than being pulled into rotation.", "Maintain a stable, wide-enough stance to resist the lateral pull without excessive lower-body compensation."], "external_cues": "Press the handle straight out away from the chest; resist the cable pulling the hands toward the machine.", "internal_cues": "Brace the core as if anticipating a blow to the stomach; think of keeping the hips and shoulders locked facing forward as the arms press out.", "common_mistakes": ["Allowing the torso to rotate toward the pulley as the arms extend.", "Shifting the hips laterally to counter the cable's pull instead of bracing the trunk.", "Leaning the torso away from the pulley to compensate rather than staying squared.", "Using a stance too narrow to provide a stable base against the lateral cable tension.", "Rushing the press so the arms extend before the trunk has established a braced position."], "equipment_setup": "Cable pulley set to approximately chest height, single handle attachment, lifter positioned side-on to the pulley at a distance that maintains tension on the cable throughout the range.", "range_of_motion": "From the handle at the sternum with elbows bent to full elbow extension in front of the chest, with the trunk position remaining fixed throughout rather than the range being defined by trunk movement.", "rir_0_definition": "Inability to press or hold the handle in front of the chest without the torso rotating or shifting toward the pulley, as distinct from continuing to resist with visible loss of the squared trunk position.", "rir_1_indicators": "Minor but still controlled trunk rotation or lateral shift during the press or hold, increased trembling in the trunk stabilizers, and slower, less stable extension, while the general squared position remains largely intact.", "individualization": "Stance width and distance from the pulley may vary moderately with individual stability and cable resistance, provided the torso remains squared and resists rotation throughout the press or hold.", "plane_of_movement": "Sagittal-plane elbow extension and flexion of the arms combined with isometric transverse-plane anti-rotation of the trunk.", "starting_position": "Standing side-on to the pulley with feet shoulder-width apart, handle held at the sternum with both hands, elbows bent, torso squared facing forward and away from the pulley, hips and shoulders level.", "technical_failure": "The point at which the torso rotates toward the pulley as the arms extend or hold, or the hips shift laterally to compensate for the cable's pull.", "primary_joint_actions": "Elbow extension and flexion as the arms press out and return; the trunk resists rotation and lateral flexion isometrically through the obliques, rectus abdominis, and deep spinal stabilizers; the hips and shoulders remain fixed and square to the front throughout, with no active hip or shoulder joint rotation occurring."}	{}	{https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-0N2xTi69t8}	Bilateral	{"slug": "standing_cable_pallof_press", "notes": ["This is the first explicitly isometric exercise in the library and the first anti-rotation movement. The prompt provides for isometric evaluation, and the limiting phase is the hold at full extension rather than any concentric action, since the press-out itself moves negligible load. Any future anti-rotation or carry variant should follow this treatment.", "The bilateral convention required a different reading here than in single_arm_low_to_high_cable_fly. There, the two mirrored cable lines cancelled and the anti-rotation demand vanished from the model, which was flagged as a limitation. Here the anti-rotation demand IS the exercise, so 20 kg was read as a single stack acting on one side rather than as two mirrored 10 kg loads. Mirroring it would produce a null exercise. This is a genuine inconsistency in the convention rather than a modelling choice, and it argues for a laterality flag distinguishing bilateral, unilateral-alternating, and unilateral-asymmetric exercises.", "Obliques reach 26.96 cm2, more than triple their 8.60 cm2 in the Pendlay row and their largest contribution anywhere. E_LoA was capped near 0.45 because the aggregate slug contains internal and external obliques on both sides, and only two of those four act in the required anti-rotation direction at any instant. Splitting the slug into internal and external, or into left and right, would model this and any rotational exercise more accurately.", "Transverse_abdominis reaches E_mech 0.1500, its highest value in the library, against 0.1300 in the weighted pull-up and 0.0670 in the shrug. Its circumferential fibres generate hoop tension that resists torsion specifically, so anti-rotation is the one demand where it is doing close to its designed job rather than merely offloading a sagittal-plane moment.", "Gluteus_medius and gluteus_minimus appear for the first time in the library, admitted on frontal-plane grounds. The cable pulls the athlete laterally toward the stack and the pelvis must resist that for the trunk to remain a stable platform. Any future single-leg, carry, or lateral-loaded exercise should admit them on the same basis.", "Total demand of 74.62 cm2 sits just above the bodyweight crunch at 36.40 and below the incline curl at 76.66. The comparison with the crunch is the useful one: both are trunk exercises, but the crunch generates 47.5 N-m of sagittal flexion through body-segment mass while this generates 44 N-m of rotation through a 0.45 m arm lever from only 98 N of cable force. The demands are comparable in magnitude but distributed across entirely different musculature.", "Wrist_flexors were deliberately excluded. The hands are clasped together around the handle rather than gripping against a distracting force, and at 98 N the demand is trivial.", "A half-kneeling or tall-kneeling Pallof press must not inherit these values. Removing the standing base eliminates the gluteus medius and minimus frontal-plane contribution of 8.32 cm2 and shifts more of the anti-rotation demand onto the obliques directly.", "A Pallof press with hold at varying arm extension must not inherit these values either. The trunk moment scales directly with arm extension distance, so a hold at the chest generates roughly a third of the moment of a hold at full extension.", "A cable woodchop or rotational chop must not inherit these values under any circumstances. Those are concentric rotational movements where the obliques shorten actively against the load, rather than isometric anti-rotation, which changes both the limiting phase and the length-tension conditions."], "limiting_phase": "Isometric hold at full arm extension: hands pressed straight out from the sternum, elbows locked, where the cable line sits at its greatest horizontal distance from the trunk axis and the rotational moment on the torso is maximal.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 74.62, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.4540, "E_mech": 0.3300, "muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "Prime mover and the defining muscle of the exercise: resisting trunk rotation is its primary function and the 44 N-m moment is the dominant demand. E_LoA is capped near 0.45 because the slug aggregates both sides and both layers, and only the internal oblique on the near side and external oblique on the far side act in the required anti-rotation direction at any instant.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 26.96}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.6050, "E_mech": 0.3702, "muscle": "rectus_abdominis", "rationale": "Genuine contributor rather than a stabiliser: it anchors the anterior trunk against both the rotational moment and the forward pull of the extended arms, providing the rigid base the obliques act against.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.29, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 8.99}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.1670, "E_mech": 0.0601, "muscle": "gluteus_medius", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary load-transmitting element: the 24 N-m frontal-plane moment pulling the athlete toward the stack must be resisted at the pelvis for the trunk to remain a stable platform. Its abduction line only partly matches the required resistance direction.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 113.75, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 6.83}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.1570, "E_mech": 0.0600, "muscle": "erector_spinae", "rationale": "Included as a load-transmitting element: its deep rotatores and multifidus components contribute directly to anti-rotation, and the superficial fibres hold the upright trunk against the forward arm lever. Most of the group's capacity is irrelevant to the transverse-plane demand.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 106.48, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 6.39}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.6000, "E_LoA": 0.3820, "E_mech": 0.1651, "muscle": "deltoid_anterior", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary stabiliser: it holds the arms extended forward against the 12 N-m moment that would otherwise collapse them back toward the chest, maintaining the long lever the exercise depends on.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 30.46, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 5.03}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.3000, "E_LoA": 0.1635, "E_mech": 0.0250, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary stabiliser: the extended-arm position with continuous lateral cable tension requires cuff compression to keep the humeral heads centred while force transmits from hands to trunk.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 4.27}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.5500, "E_LoA": 0.2880, "E_mech": 0.0999, "muscle": "serratus_anterior", "rationale": "Included as a load-transmitting stabiliser: scapular protraction and fixation are required to keep the arms as a rigid strut and route the cable force into the thorax rather than losing it to scapular retraction.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 36.03, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 3.60}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.6500, "E_ND": 0.6000, "E_LoA": 0.4050, "E_mech": 0.1500, "muscle": "transverse_abdominis", "rationale": "Higher relative engagement than in any other exercise here: its circumferential fibres generate the intra-abdominal pressure and hoop tension that stiffen the trunk cylinder against torsion specifically, which is the exact demand of this movement.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 21.97, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 3.30}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.4000, "E_LoA": 0.0740, "E_mech": 0.0200, "muscle": "gluteus_maximus", "rationale": "Included for its external-rotation and pelvic-stabilising contribution, which resists the pelvis being pulled into rotation by the cable. Its dominant extension function is irrelevant to the transverse-plane demand, hence the very low alignment.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 143.08, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 2.86}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.9000, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.1730, "E_mech": 0.0701, "muscle": "triceps_lateral_head", "rationale": "Included as a load-transmitting element: locking the elbows in extension is required for the arms to act as the rigid lever that generates the 44 N-m trunk moment. Purely isometric with no movement against the load.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 36.10, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 2.53}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.1670, "E_mech": 0.0601, "muscle": "gluteus_minimus", "rationale": "Acts in parallel with gluteus medius on frontal-plane pelvic stability against the lateral cable pull.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.82, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 1.49}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.0834, "E_mech": 0.0301, "muscle": "triceps_long_head", "rationale": "Assists the isometric elbow lock, with its shoulder-extension action partly opposing the forward arm position the exercise requires.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.55, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 1.31}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.9000, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.1680, "E_mech": 0.0680, "muscle": "triceps_medial_head", "rationale": "Mechanically equivalent to the lateral head in maintaining the elbow lock.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 15.59, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 1.06}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["Representative commercial single-stack cable column with a 1:1 pulley ratio; 20 kg nominal yields ~98 N at the handle after friction.", "Athlete standing side-on to a chest-height pulley in a hip-width or slightly staggered stance, hands clasped and pressed straight out from the sternum.", "The exercise is an anti-rotation isometric: the concentric press-out is trivial and the mechanically limiting phase is the hold at full extension, evaluated as an isometric per the prompt's provision for explicitly isometric tasks.", "Arm length from sternum to hands at full extension ~0.45 m; the cable line therefore acts at ~0.45 m from the trunk rotation axis, giving ~44 N-m of rotational moment.", "The same 98 N also pulls the athlete laterally toward the stack, creating a ~24 N-m frontal-plane moment at the pelvis that the lateral hip musculature must resist to keep the stance stable.", "Oblique effective rotation moment arm ~0.055 m; the internal and external obliques on opposite sides act as a couple to resist rotation.", "Shoulder and elbow moments are small: the arms act largely as a rigid strut transmitting cable tension to the sternum, requiring modest deltoid and triceps force to hold extension rather than to move the load.", "This is a unilateral-stimulus exercise evaluated bilaterally. Unlike the single-arm cable fly, where the bilateral convention cancelled the anti-rotation demand, here the anti-rotation demand IS the exercise, so the 20 kg is read as a single stack acting on one side rather than as two mirrored 10 kg loads.", "Feet planted with the reaction force transmitted through the legs to the floor; the gluteal contribution is genuine frontal-plane and rotational torque rather than postural support.", "20 kg is a realistic working load for a 20 to 40 second hold or 8-12 slow press-outs, since the very long arm lever converts modest cable force into a demanding trunk moment."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "thoracolumbar_spine_rotation", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 44.00}, {"joint": "lumbopelvic_frontal", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 24.00}, {"joint": "glenohumeral_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 12.00}, {"joint": "elbow_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 7.80}], "nominal_load_kg": 20.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 98.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 74.62, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 74.50}
24	roman_chair_back_extension_isometric_hold	Roman Chair Isometric Hold	Roman Chair Back Extension Isometric Hold	Lower	Global_P	Stability_Isometric	Bodyweight	45.00	182.92	{"erector_spinae": 63.46, "semitendinosus": 5.32, "adductor_magnus": 18.19, "gluteus_maximus": 61.65, "semimembranosus": 22.09, "biceps_femoris_long_head": 12.21}	{"grip": "The arms may be crossed over the chest, held close to the torso, or positioned according to the machine design. Hand position should remain constant during the hold and should not be used to brace against the equipment.", "tldr": {"focus": "Maintain a long neutral trunk with the hips extended and prevent the hold from drifting into lumbar hyperextension or hip flexion.", "setup": "Set the Roman-chair pad below the hip crease so the pelvis is supported without blocking hip motion, then secure the lower legs.", "execution": "Hold the torso in line with the thighs against gravity while keeping the spine and pelvis stable rather than repeatedly extending and flexing.", "stop_when": "Stop when the torso can no longer remain aligned with the thighs without lumbar overextension, hip sag, or a progressive loss of the prescribed position."}, "stance": "Position both legs symmetrically in the machine with the feet or ankles firmly secured and the knees extended or softly unlocked according to the apparatus.", "bracing": "Maintain circumferential trunk stiffness sufficient to keep the ribcage, lumbar spine, and pelvis from drifting relative to one another. Keep the gluteals active enough to maintain hip extension while avoiding exaggerated posterior pelvic tilt or lumbar hyperextension.", "overview": "A static Roman-chair back-extension hold performed with the body supported at the pelvis and lower legs while the torso is maintained approximately in line with the thighs. The exercise is isometric: the defining task is resisting the external flexion moment at the hips and spine rather than performing repeated back-extension repetitions. The intended position uses coordinated hip-extension and spinal-extensor stabilization without deliberately arching the lumbar spine beyond neutral.", "eccentric": "", "concentric": "", "tempo_notes": "Establish the position smoothly and maintain it without oscillating, bouncing, or repeatedly dipping and re-extending the torso.", "end_position": "The prescribed hold position is reached when the torso is approximately collinear with the thighs, the hips are extended, and the lumbar and cervical spine remain near neutral without deliberate hyperextension.", "safety_notes": ["Ensure the ankle or lower-leg restraint is secure before transferring full bodyweight onto the apparatus.", "Do not deliberately hyperextend the lumbar spine beyond the neutral body-thigh line.", "Adjust the pad so the abdomen and hip crease are not compressed in a way that prevents controlled hip positioning."], "external_cues": "Make one straight line from the head through the hips to the legs and keep that line fixed.", "internal_cues": "Keep the ribs and pelvis stacked; hold the hips fully extended; keep the spine long rather than arching harder.", "common_mistakes": ["Holding the torso above the line of the thighs by hyperextending the lumbar spine.", "Allowing the hips to flex progressively so the torso sags below the intended position.", "Using repeated small back-extension repetitions instead of maintaining a static hold.", "Losing pelvic symmetry and rotating or shifting toward one side.", "Extending the cervical spine to keep the head visually elevated as the trunk fatigues.", "Setting the pad too high so it blocks the hip crease and forces movement to come primarily from the lumbar spine."], "equipment_setup": "Adjust the Roman-chair or 45-degree back-extension station so the upper pad supports the proximal thighs or pelvis below the anterior hip crease without pressing into the abdomen. Secure the ankles or lower legs firmly enough that the body can be held without sliding.", "range_of_motion": "This is an isometric exercise and therefore has no repeated dynamic range of motion. The relevant positional range is the neutral hold around body-thigh alignment; the torso should not be elevated materially above this line to create additional lumbar extension.", "rir_0_definition": "RIR 0 is reached when the lifter can no longer maintain the defined torso-thigh alignment with controlled hip extension and near-neutral spinal position, even if the hold could be prolonged by lumbar hyperextension, allowing the hips to sag, rotating the pelvis, or repeatedly bouncing back into position.", "rir_1_indicators": "Approximately one short technically valid continuation interval may remain when the hold becomes visibly difficult, small positional corrections become more frequent, and the torso begins to approach the lower boundary of the acceptable position, but neutral spinal alignment and hip extension can still be restored without major compensation.", "individualization": "Pad height, knee position, arm position, and the exact visual definition of neutral may vary with limb proportions, spinal curvature, hip anatomy, and machine geometry. A small deviation from a perfectly straight body line is acceptable if it represents the lifter's neutral spinal and pelvic alignment; lumbar hyperextension or progressive hip sag are not individualization.", "plane_of_movement": "Primarily an isometric anti-flexion task in the sagittal plane at the hip and vertebral column, with no intended cyclical joint excursion.", "starting_position": "Position the pelvis on the pad with the lower legs secured, then extend the hips until the head, trunk, pelvis, and thighs form an approximately straight line. Keep the cervical spine neutral, the ribcage controlled, and the shoulders relaxed. Establish the hold without lifting the torso above the line of the thighs.", "technical_failure": "Technical failure occurs when the prescribed isometric alignment can no longer be maintained. Observable breakdowns include progressive hip flexion causing the torso to sag, lumbar hyperextension used to keep the chest elevated despite loss of hip extension, spinal flexion, pelvic rotation, asymmetric shifting, or repeated corrective bouncing to regain the hold position.", "primary_joint_actions": "There is no intended concentric joint action during the hold. The hip extensors generate isometric extension torque to resist trunk flexion, while the spinal extensors maintain the vertebral column against flexion. The abdominal wall and frontal-plane hip stabilizers contribute to maintaining pelvic and trunk alignment."}	{}	{https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=q51uqcrMZm0,https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rqrH1SuNLbg}	Bilateral	{"slug": "roman_chair_back_extension_isometric_hold", "notes": ["The hip-extensor contributions correspond to approximately 147 Nm using representative muscle-specific hip-extension moment arms, closely matching the estimated 150 Nm external hip moment.", "The erector-spinae contribution corresponds to approximately 95 Nm using an effective lumbar-extension moment arm near 0.060 m, consistent with the estimated 92.70 Nm external lumbar moment.", "Rectus abdominis, obliques, and transverse abdominis are excluded because their principal sagittal-plane moments oppose lumbar extension; any co-contraction is not counted as task-producing FCSA demand.", "The result is highly sensitive to pad placement and segment centre-of-mass distance; positioning the pad farther down the thighs would materially increase the hip-extension requirement."], "limiting_phase": "Static horizontal hold with the pelvis supported at the Roman-chair pad, the trunk and head maintained approximately parallel to the floor, and the hips held near neutral extension.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 182.92, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.8200, "E_LoA": 0.9500, "E_mech": 0.5959, "muscle": "erector_spinae", "rationale": "Provides the principal lumbar-extension moment required to prevent the suspended trunk from flexing under gravity.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 106.48, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 63.46}, {"E_LT": 0.8800, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.7200, "E_LoA": 0.8500, "E_mech": 0.4308, "muscle": "gluteus_maximus", "rationale": "Supplies the largest hip-extension contribution, although its leverage and neural utilization are lower near neutral hip extension than in deeper hip flexion.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 143.08, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 61.65}, {"E_LT": 0.8000, "E_MA": 0.7200, "E_ND": 0.6000, "E_LoA": 0.7200, "E_mech": 0.2488, "muscle": "semimembranosus", "rationale": "Contributes meaningful isometric hip-extension torque while the nearly extended knee preserves adequate biarticular hamstring length.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 88.78, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 22.09}, {"E_LT": 0.8200, "E_MA": 0.6800, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.6200, "E_mech": 0.1729, "muscle": "adductor_magnus", "rationale": "Posterior fibers assist hip extension, but the aggregate muscle includes fibers whose actions are less aligned with the sagittal-plane holding demand.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 105.22, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 18.19}, {"E_LT": 0.8000, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.5800, "E_LoA": 0.7000, "E_mech": 0.2274, "muscle": "biceps_femoris_long_head", "rationale": "Assists the gluteus maximus in resisting hip flexion, with contribution limited by its smaller FCSA and secondary task share.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 53.70, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 12.21}, {"E_LT": 0.7800, "E_MA": 0.6800, "E_ND": 0.5600, "E_LoA": 0.7000, "E_mech": 0.2079, "muscle": "semitendinosus", "rationale": "Provides a small but mechanically direct hip-extension contribution during the static horizontal hold.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 25.59, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 5.32}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["The 45 kg nominal load is interpreted as the effective suspended mass of the head, arms, trunk, and mechanically relevant portion of the lower limbs, not as 45 kg of additional external weight.", "The suspended-segment centre of mass acts approximately 0.34 m anterior to the bilateral hip axes.", "The effective upper-trunk centre of mass acts approximately 0.21 m anterior to the representative lumbar-extension axis.", "The Roman-chair pad supports the proximal thighs and pelvis without materially assisting hip or lumbar extension.", "The spine is held near neutral rather than deliberately hyperextended."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "hip_extension_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 150.09}, {"joint": "lumbar_extension", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 92.70}], "nominal_load_kg": 45.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 441.45}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 182.92, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 182.92}
26	smith_machine_incline_bench_press	Smith Machine Incline Press	Incline Bench Smith Machine Press	Upper	Chest_Clav_AD	Secondary_Compound	Smith_Machine	80.00	157.13	{"rotator_cuffs": 9.16, "deltoid_lateral": 5.88, "deltoid_anterior": 25.29, "pectoralis_minor": 1.12, "serratus_anterior": 7.36, "triceps_long_head": 23.50, "triceps_medial_head": 12.26, "triceps_lateral_head": 28.40, "pectoralis_major_sternal": 33.73, "pectoralis_major_clavicular": 10.43}	{"grip": "Use a closed pronated grip with the thumbs wrapped around the bar. Choose a width that permits the forearms to remain approximately vertical near the bottom and keeps the wrists stacked over the elbows without forcing excessive elbow flare.", "tldr": {"focus": "Position the bench so the fixed Smith path matches your natural pressing path rather than forcing the shoulders forward or the elbows excessively outward.", "setup": "Set an incline bench under the Smith bar so the bar descends toward the upper-to-mid chest with the forearms approximately vertical at the bottom.", "execution": "Lower the bar under control to the upper-to-mid chest, then press it upward along the fixed rail while keeping the torso and shoulder base stable.", "stop_when": "Stop when another repetition would require major elbow-path change, shoulder rolling, loss of backrest contact, excessive lumbar extension, or a materially shortened range."}, "stance": "Place both feet firmly on the floor at a comfortable width that stabilizes the pelvis without requiring the hips to lift from the bench.", "bracing": "Maintain abdominal and lower-body tension sufficient to keep the pelvis and torso stable against the bench. Preserve a natural-to-moderate thoracic arch without excessive lumbar extension, rib flare, or hip elevation.", "overview": "An incline bench press performed inside a Smith machine with the torso supported on an inclined backrest and the bar constrained to the machine's fixed path. The inclined torso increases the shoulder-flexion component relative to a flat press while retaining substantial glenohumeral horizontal adduction and elbow extension. Because the bar cannot translate freely, bench position relative to the rail is a defining technical variable and should be adjusted so the bar reaches a comfortable upper-to-mid chest touch point without forcing abnormal shoulder mechanics.", "eccentric": "Lower the bar under control along the fixed rail toward the established upper-to-mid chest touch point. Allow the elbows to flex and the humeri to move into horizontal abduction while maintaining shoulder stability, wrist alignment, and full backrest contact. The descent should remain compatible with the machine path rather than forcing the elbows into an artificial position.", "concentric": "Press the bar upward along the Smith rails by extending the elbows and bringing the upper arms toward the midline while maintaining the established torso position. Keep the wrists stacked over the forearms and allow the elbows to follow a consistent path beneath the bar. Do not attempt to redirect the fixed bar path by shifting the shoulders or sliding on the bench.", "tempo_notes": "Avoid bouncing the bar from the chest or allowing the Smith carriage to strike the stops. Reverse direction smoothly and maintain control through the fixed rail path.", "end_position": "Finish with the elbows at full or near-full functional extension and the bar stabilized along the Smith rail above the upper chest or shoulder line, while the torso remains fixed against the incline bench.", "safety_notes": ["Set the Smith safety stops before beginning so a failed repetition can be contained below the intended pressing range.", "Confirm that the bench is centered and stable relative to the Smith rails before unracking.", "Do not force a chest touch if the fixed machine path produces painful shoulder positioning or instability."], "external_cues": "Bring the bar to the same upper-to-mid chest point and drive it straight along the Smith rails without shifting your body underneath it.", "internal_cues": "Keep the upper back fixed to the pad; keep the forearms under the bar; press through the same elbow path on every repetition.", "common_mistakes": ["Positioning the bench incorrectly so the fixed bar path forces the shoulders forward or the elbows into an uncomfortable trajectory.", "Setting the incline excessively high and unintentionally converting the movement toward a near-overhead press.", "Allowing the wrists to collapse backward instead of keeping them stacked over the forearms.", "Flaring the elbows abruptly away from the established pressing path.", "Lifting the hips or increasing lumbar extension to complete the final portion of the press.", "Bouncing the bar from the chest or striking the Smith safety stops between repetitions."], "equipment_setup": "Set an adjustable bench to a moderate incline and center it beneath the Smith bar. Move the bench forward or backward until the fixed bar path reaches the upper-to-mid chest at the bottom while the forearms remain approximately vertical and the shoulders stay comfortable. Set the safety stops below the intended bottom position without interfering with normal range.", "range_of_motion": "Lower to the deepest position that permits a consistent upper-to-mid chest touch or near-touch while maintaining comfortable shoulder mechanics, wrist-elbow alignment, and stable backrest contact. Press to full functional elbow extension without deliberately protracting the shoulders or lifting the torso. If the fixed rail prevents comfortable chest contact, use the deepest pain-free range that preserves the intended incline-press mechanics.", "rir_0_definition": "RIR 0 is reached when no additional repetition can be completed through the defined Smith-machine range while preserving the established torso and arm mechanics, even if the bar could still be moved by excessive arching, sliding on the bench, changing the elbow path, bouncing, or shortening the range.", "rir_1_indicators": "Approximately one technically valid repetition may remain when bar velocity slows markedly and the final portion becomes difficult, but the lifter can still maintain the established touch point, elbow path, wrist alignment, backrest contact, and full intended range.", "individualization": "Bench incline, fore-aft bench position, grip width, elbow angle, and exact chest touch point may vary with humeral length, torso dimensions, shoulder anatomy, and Smith-machine rail geometry. These parameters are acceptable to individualize provided the movement remains clearly an incline press, the forearms are approximately vertical near the bottom, the shoulders stay comfortable and controlled, and the torso does not shift to accommodate the fixed rail.", "plane_of_movement": "Primarily transverse-plane glenohumeral horizontal adduction combined with a substantial sagittal-plane shoulder-flexion component created by the inclined torso, plus sagittal-plane elbow extension.", "starting_position": "Sit and lie back with the pelvis, upper back, and head supported by the inclined bench. Plant both feet firmly, maintain a controlled thoracic position, and establish mild scapular retraction against the pad. Unrack the bar with the elbows extended and the bar positioned over the upper chest or shoulder line according to the machine rail orientation.", "technical_failure": "Technical failure occurs when another repetition cannot be completed through the established range without materially changing the intended mechanics. Observable breakdowns include excessive lumbar extension, lifting or sliding the torso on the bench, major elbow flare or tuck relative to the established path, shoulder rolling, wrist collapse, bouncing from the chest, or substantial shortening of the bottom or top range.", "primary_joint_actions": "During the concentric phase, the glenohumeral joints perform horizontal adduction with shoulder flexion, the elbows extend, and the scapulae remain relatively stable against the inclined backrest with limited natural protraction as the press approaches lockout."}	{}	{https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xGMqmmn5Z7Q}	Bilateral	{"slug": "smith_machine_incline_bench_press", "limiting_phase": "Concentric sticking region, roughly 5-10 cm off the upper chest/clavicle: elbows near 90-100 degrees of flexion, humerus abducted approximately 45-55 degrees and flexed relative to the torso, bar tracking the fixed rail.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 157.13, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9200, "E_MA": 0.8800, "E_ND": 0.9500, "E_LoA": 0.8200, "E_mech": 0.6307, "muscle": "pectoralis_major_sternal", "rationale": "Still the largest single contributor to the humeral moment, but the incline shifts the required action toward flexion, reducing alignment for the sternal fibres relative to a flat press.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 53.48, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 33.73}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.9000, "E_ND": 0.9200, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.7866, "muscle": "triceps_lateral_head", "rationale": "Mono-articular elbow extensor working against the 40 N-m bilateral elbow flexion moment at a favorable joint angle.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 36.10, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 28.40}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.9200, "E_ND": 0.9500, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.8303, "muscle": "deltoid_anterior", "rationale": "The incline angle places the required humeral moment squarely in the anterior deltoid's line of action, and the guided rail permits near-full expression of that capacity.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 30.46, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 25.29}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.8800, "E_ND": 0.8200, "E_LoA": 0.8800, "E_mech": 0.5397, "muscle": "triceps_long_head", "rationale": "Contributes elbow-extension torque, but the greater humeral flexion of the incline position both shortens it at the shoulder and increases the degree to which its shoulder-extension action opposes the required moment.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.55, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 23.50}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.9000, "E_ND": 0.9200, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.7866, "muscle": "triceps_medial_head", "rationale": "Mechanically equivalent to the lateral head against the elbow flexion moment.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 15.59, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 12.26}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.9000, "E_ND": 0.9200, "E_LoA": 0.9800, "E_mech": 0.7711, "muscle": "pectoralis_major_clavicular", "rationale": "The ~30-35 degree incline is close to the optimal angle for these fibres, giving high alignment with the required humeral flexion/adduction moment despite their small cross-section.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 13.52, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 10.43}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.3500, "E_LoA": 0.3000, "E_mech": 0.0536, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary stabilizer: glenohumeral compression is required to transmit ~728 N through the joint at a more elevated humeral position. Engagement sits below the free-barbell case because the fixed rail removes bar-path and lateral control demands.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 9.16}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.5500, "E_LoA": 0.5500, "E_mech": 0.2042, "muscle": "serratus_anterior", "rationale": "Included as a load-transmitting stabilizer: the inclined humeral position raises the upward-rotation and scapular fixation requirement needed to route the load into the trunk, though the bench back absorbs part of the reaction force.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 36.03, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 7.36}, {"E_LT": 0.8800, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.6500, "E_LoA": 0.3000, "E_mech": 0.1287, "muscle": "deltoid_lateral", "rationale": "The elevated humeral angle recruits a somewhat larger flexion component from the anterior fibres than a flat press, but the abduction line of action remains largely orthogonal to the net required moment.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 45.73, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 5.88}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.4500, "E_LoA": 0.4500, "E_mech": 0.1033, "muscle": "pectoralis_minor", "rationale": "Minor mechanically necessary contribution to scapular anchoring; its depression action partly conflicts with the upward rotation the incline requires, limiting net usable force.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 10.81, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 1.12}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["Representative commercial Smith machine: counterbalanced carriage with an effective bar mass of ~10 kg, plus rail friction, plus a rail inclined ~10 degrees from vertical. Net effect approximates the 80 kg nominal load at ~68 kg of resisting force at the hands plus ~2 kg of accelerated forearm mass.", "Bench set to ~30-35 degrees; the incline shortens the effective vertical stroke but increases the humeral flexion demand relative to a flat press.", "Grip ~1.5x biacromial width; perpendicular distance from the GH joint to the bar force line ~0.145 m per side.", "Forearms near-perpendicular to the rail at the sticking point; elbow external moment arm ~0.055 m per side.", "Effective internal moment arm for combined humeral flexion/horizontal adduction ~0.05 m; triceps elbow-extension moment arm ~0.025 m.", "Fixed rail removes bar-path control and lateral stabilization demand, and the bench back supports the torso and scapulae.", "80 kg on a counterbalanced Smith at ~30 degrees incline corresponds to roughly 68 kg of free-weight-equivalent incline resistance, consistent with 8-12 reps at RIR 1-2 for the 100 kg reference flat-bench presser."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "glenohumeral_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 100.50}, {"joint": "elbow_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 40.00}, {"joint": "scapulothoracic_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 18.00}], "nominal_load_kg": 80.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 728.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 157.13, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 162.00}
36	wide_grip_seated_cable_row	Wide-Grip Seated Cable Row	Wide-Grip Seated Cable Row	Upper	Back_3D	Secondary_Compound	Cable	80.00	178.24	{"rhomboids": 19.42, "brachialis": 27.19, "teres_major": 2.35, "rotator_cuffs": 7.52, "wrist_flexors": 25.84, "biceps_brachii": 13.10, "erector_spinae": 6.71, "brachioradialis": 4.32, "trapezius_lower": 7.13, "trapezius_upper": 1.33, "latissimus_dorsi": 27.86, "trapezius_middle": 18.32, "deltoid_posterior": 17.15}	{"grip": "Wide pronated grip on the bar attachment, hands positioned outside shoulder width, wrists held stable throughout the pull.", "tldr": {"focus": "Retract the shoulder blades and drive the elbows wide and back rather than pulling primarily with the hands.", "setup": "Sit at the cable row station with knees slightly bent, feet braced on the platform, and torso upright gripping a wide bar attachment.", "execution": "Pull the bar toward the torso by driving the elbows out and back, then extend the arms under control back to a full stretch.", "stop_when": "The bar can no longer be pulled to the torso without the torso rocking backward to generate momentum."}, "stance": "Seated with feet braced against the footplate, knees maintaining a slight, fixed bend throughout the set.", "bracing": "Torso braced in a fixed, upright position established at the start, avoiding progressive backward rocking or using body momentum to assist the pull.", "overview": "This is the wide-grip seated cable row performed with a wide bar attachment held in a pronated grip, in which the elbows track outward and back rather than close to the torso. This wider elbow path shifts emphasis toward the rear deltoids and upper back musculature relative to a close neutral-grip row, which typically drives the elbows closer to the ribs. It differs from a standard seated row primarily in the hand spacing and resulting elbow trajectory during the pull.", "eccentric": "The bar returns under control back to a position of full arm extension through glenohumeral horizontal flexion and elbow extension, with the shoulder blades protracting slightly and the torso remaining stable throughout.", "concentric": "The bar is pulled toward the torso through combined glenohumeral horizontal extension and elbow flexion, with the elbows driving outward and back while the torso remains upright and stable.", "tempo_notes": "Maintain continuous cable tension throughout without allowing the weight stack to rest between repetitions; avoid using torso momentum or a jerking motion to initiate the pull.", "end_position": "Bar drawn to the torso with the elbows elevated out to the sides and back, and the shoulder blades retracted.", "safety_notes": ["Maintain a neutral spine and avoid excessive backward rocking, particularly under heavier loads.", "Keep the knees at a fixed, slight bend rather than locking them against the footplate."], "external_cues": "Pull the bar to the torso while keeping the elbows wide; keep the torso upright throughout the pull.", "internal_cues": "Drive the elbows out and back; think of pulling with the upper back rather than the hands.", "common_mistakes": ["Rocking the torso backward to use body momentum instead of upper-back musculature.", "Rounding the lower back at the start of the pull instead of maintaining a fixed, neutral spine.", "Shrugging the shoulders upward instead of retracting the scapulae.", "Allowing the elbows to drop and track close to the torso, converting the movement into a close-grip row.", "Using a jerking motion to initiate the pull instead of continuous tension."], "equipment_setup": "Cable row station with a wide bar attachment, seat and footplate positioned so the knees remain slightly bent with the feet braced against the platform at full arm extension.", "range_of_motion": "From full arm extension with a slight forward lean at the torso at the start to the bar reaching the torso with the elbows back at the end, limited by the point at which further pulling requires the torso to rock backward.", "rir_0_definition": "Inability to pull the bar to the torso through glenohumeral horizontal extension and elbow flexion while keeping the torso upright, as distinct from continuing to move the bar by rocking the torso backward.", "rir_1_indicators": "Reduced pulling speed through the mid-range, a growing tendency for the torso to rock backward, and increasing effort to keep the elbows elevated, while the general pulling path remains intact.", "individualization": "The exact elbow height and degree of torso hinge at the start may vary moderately with shoulder structure and torso length, provided the torso position remains fixed throughout the set rather than increasing backward lean to assist the pull.", "plane_of_movement": "Primarily transverse-plane glenohumeral horizontal extension and abduction, combined with elbow flexion.", "starting_position": "Seated with feet braced against the footplate, knees slightly bent, torso upright with a neutral spine, arms extended forward gripping the wide bar, shoulder blades in a neutral, non-retracted position.", "technical_failure": "The point at which the bar can no longer be pulled to the torso without the torso rocking backward to generate momentum, or the elbows fail to elevate outward during the pull.", "primary_joint_actions": "Glenohumeral horizontal extension and slight abduction as the elbows drive outward and back; elbow flexion driven by the biceps and brachialis; scapular retraction as the bar approaches the torso; the hips and spine remain in a fixed, upright hinge position established at the start of the set."}	{}	{https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UCXxvVItLoM}	Bilateral	{"slug": "wide_grip_seated_cable_row", "notes": ["This exercise produces the highest scapular retractor allocation in the library, with trapezius_middle at E_mech 0.6996, rhomboids at 0.6876 and deltoid_posterior at 0.6885, all three peaking simultaneously. The wide elbows-out path makes retraction and horizontal abduction the dominant demand rather than a secondary component, which is the mechanical basis for programming wide-grip rows specifically for the upper back rather than the lats.", "Latissimus_dorsi drops to E_LoA 0.7620, its lowest value in any pulling exercise here, against 0.9810 in the weighted pull-up and 0.9740 in the pulldown. The wide grip trades lat alignment for retractor alignment almost one for one, and the pairing with wide_pronated_grip_pull_up is instructive: same grip width and orientation, but the vertical pull keeps the lats near optimal while the horizontal pull does not.", "The comparison with chest_supported_dumbbell_row isolates the effect of grip width and anchoring. Totals are 178.24 against 175.19 cm2 at 80 versus 60 kg, but composition diverges sharply. The dumbbell row's neutral close grip gives biceps 19.24 cm2 against 13.10 here, while this exercise moves 8 cm2 into the retractors and adds 6.71 cm2 of erector demand that the chest pad eliminates entirely.", "Erector_spinae contributes only 6.71 cm2, against 96.10 in the Pendlay row at a comparable load. The near-vertical seated torso is the entire reason: the lumbar moment is 47 N-m against 470. This is the third data point confirming that torso angle rather than load magnitude drives lumbar demand, and it supports the common programming use of seated rows as a lower-back-sparing horizontal pull.", "Biceps_brachii at E_mech 0.3291 is the lowest of any pulling exercise in the library, below even the wide pronated pull-up at 0.3800. Three factors stack: pronation cuts leverage, the wide grip angles the forearm away from the pull line, and unlike overhead pulling the biceps shoulder-flexion action offers no assistance in a horizontal pull. Brachialis to biceps ratio reaches 2.08.", "Trapezius_upper enters for only the second time in the library, after the Pendlay row. Its inclusion is marginal at 1.33 cm2 and it should be dropped if the stabiliser criterion is tightened, since its elevation vector contributes little to horizontal retraction.", "A close neutral-grip seated row must not inherit these values. Narrowing the grip and neutralising the forearm would raise latissimus and biceps substantially while cutting deltoid_posterior and the retractors by roughly a third, effectively converting this into a different exercise.", "A row performed with deliberate torso swing must not inherit these values either. Allowing 30 to 40 degrees of trunk movement would raise the lumbar moment several-fold and admit hip extensors as genuine contributors, moving the profile toward the Pendlay row.", "Grip is the least certain assumption. With straps, wrist_flexors would drop out and total demand would fall to roughly 152 cm2. The strap-usage flag suggested for the pulldown would apply here identically."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric mid-pull: handle drawn to the level of the lower ribs, elbow flexed to ~110 degrees, humerus horizontally abducted to roughly 60 degrees with the elbows tracking wide, before the scapulae reach full retraction and the humeral moment arm shortens.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 178.24, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9200, "E_LoA": 0.7620, "E_mech": 0.6353, "muscle": "latissimus_dorsi", "rationale": "Major contributor to the 141 N-m glenohumeral moment, but the wide elbows-out path shifts the required action from adduction and extension toward horizontal abduction, which is outside its optimal line of pull. Lowest E_LoA it receives in any pulling exercise in the library.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.85, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 27.86}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9500, "E_LoA": 0.8490, "E_mech": 0.6172, "muscle": "brachialis", "rationale": "Dominant elbow flexor against the 46.5 N-m elbow moment at favourable mid-range leverage, unaffected by the pronated grip. Alignment slightly reduced because the inward forearm angle of the wide grip diverts part of its action from the pull direction.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 27.19}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.8215, "E_mech": 0.4750, "muscle": "wrist_flexors", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary load-transmitting element: without straps the entire 784 N passes through the pronated grip. Demand sits between the pulldown and the weighted pull-up in proportion to the load.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 25.84}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9200, "E_LoA": 0.9770, "E_mech": 0.6876, "muscle": "rhomboids", "rationale": "Near-optimal alignment: the wide elbows-out path makes scapular retraction the dominant scapulothoracic demand, which is precisely its line of action. Highest engagement it receives anywhere in the library.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 28.25, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 19.42}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9200, "E_LoA": 0.9940, "E_mech": 0.6996, "muscle": "trapezius_middle", "rationale": "Best-aligned muscle in the exercise: its pure horizontal retraction vector matches the 86 N-m scapulothoracic moment almost exactly in the wide-grip position.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 26.18, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 18.32}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.6885, "muscle": "deltoid_posterior", "rationale": "The wide elbows-out path requires humeral horizontal abduction, which is its primary action, giving it perfect task alignment. Highest engagement it receives in any exercise in the library.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.65, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 17.15}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.6620, "E_mech": 0.3291, "muscle": "biceps_brachii", "rationale": "Penalised by full pronation, which cuts its flexion leverage, and by the wide grip which angles the forearm away from the pull line. Its shoulder-flexion action also provides no assistance in a horizontal pull, unlike in overhead pulling.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 13.10}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.3500, "E_LoA": 0.2465, "E_mech": 0.0440, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary stabiliser: 784 N of cable tension applies a distraction load to the humerus that cuff compression must resist for force to transmit into the scapula. Infraspinatus and teres minor add a small external-rotation component in the elbows-out position.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 7.52}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.8235, "E_mech": 0.4760, "muscle": "trapezius_lower", "rationale": "Contributes retraction and resists scapular elevation during the pull, though its depression vector is only partly aligned with the predominantly horizontal scapular motion.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 14.98, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 7.13}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.9000, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.1556, "E_mech": 0.0630, "muscle": "erector_spinae", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary load-transmitting element: the unsupported torso requires active lumbar extension torque to hold position against 784 N acting horizontally at chest height. Demand is small because the trunk stays near-vertical, unlike a bent-over row.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 106.48, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 6.71}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.9000, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.5590, "E_mech": 0.3849, "muscle": "brachioradialis", "rationale": "Excellent moment arm at the 110 degree limiting angle, but full pronation moves it well away from its optimal mid-position orientation.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 11.22, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 4.32}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.7580, "E_mech": 0.5220, "muscle": "teres_major", "rationale": "Acts in parallel with latissimus dorsi on humeral extension, sharing the same partial misalignment with the wide horizontal-abduction path.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 4.50, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 2.35}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.7000, "E_LoA": 0.1650, "E_mech": 0.0687, "muscle": "trapezius_upper", "rationale": "Small contribution to scapular retraction via its transverse fibres; its dominant elevation vector is largely irrelevant to the required horizontal scapular moment.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 19.36, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 1.33}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["Representative commercial seated row station with a 1:1 cable ratio; 80 kg nominal yields ~784 N at the handle after friction, ~392 N per limb.", "Seated with feet braced against the platform and knees slightly flexed, torso held near-vertical with only a few degrees of movement through the rep.", "Wide pronated grip on a long bar at roughly 1.5 times biacromial width, elbows flaring out to approximately 60 degrees of humeral horizontal abduction.", "Humerus length ~0.32 m: at the mid-pull the cable line gives a glenohumeral external moment arm of ~0.180 m per side for the horizontal abduction and extension component.", "Forearm angled inward with the wide grip rather than aligned with the cable, giving an elbow external moment arm of ~0.059 m per side.", "Internal moment arms: latissimus shoulder extension ~0.058 m, elbow flexors ~0.042 m at 110 degrees, scapular retractors ~0.055 m.", "The wide elbows-out path shifts the required humeral moment substantially toward horizontal abduction, favouring deltoid_posterior and the scapular retractors relative to a close neutral-grip row.", "Scapular retraction against the cable is genuine task torque, and the wide grip makes it the dominant component of the movement rather than a secondary one.", "Foot platform and seat anchor the body against the cable pull, but the torso is unsupported: a modest lumbar extension moment is required to hold the near-vertical trunk position against 784 N acting horizontally at chest height.", "Pronated grip on a straight bar without straps: finger and wrist flexor force is the sole path by which the full 784 N reaches the arm.", "80 kg is a realistic 8-12 rep working load for the reference athlete on a seated row, where the anchored position permits a heavier load than a free bent-over row."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "glenohumeral_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 141.00}, {"joint": "scapulothoracic_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 86.00}, {"joint": "elbow_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 46.50}, {"joint": "lumbar_spine", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 47.00}], "nominal_load_kg": 80.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 784.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 178.24, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 178.00}
44	standing_dumbbell_shrug	Dumbbell Shrug	Standing Dumbbell Shrug	Upper	Upper_Traps	Isolation	Dumbbell	80.00	111.06	{"obliques": 2.45, "rhomboids": 14.13, "rotator_cuffs": 5.98, "wrist_flexors": 32.10, "erector_spinae": 12.78, "trapezius_lower": 3.15, "trapezius_upper": 17.42, "trapezius_middle": 13.09, "deep_neck_extensors": 8.49, "transverse_abdominis": 1.47}	{"grip": "Use a closed neutral grip with the palms facing the thighs and the wrists aligned with the forearms. Maintain enough grip force to secure the dumbbells without flexing the elbows.", "tldr": {"focus": "Move the shoulders vertically upward rather than rolling them forward or backward.", "setup": "Stand upright with a dumbbell in each hand at the sides, arms long, wrists neutral, and the head and torso stacked.", "execution": "Elevate both shoulder girdles upward while keeping the elbows straight and the torso still, then lower under control.", "stop_when": "Stop when another repetition would require elbow bending, neck movement, torso momentum, or a materially shorter scapular-elevation range."}, "stance": "Stand with the feet approximately hip- to shoulder-width apart and the knees extended or softly unlocked. Maintain a stable, symmetrical base throughout the hold and movement.", "bracing": "Maintain enough abdominal, gluteal, and spinal-extensor tension to prevent lumbar extension, torso sway, or hip drive. Keep the cervical spine neutral rather than extending or flexing the neck as the shoulders move.", "overview": "A bilateral standing dumbbell shrug performed with the arms hanging at the sides and the dumbbells moving primarily through scapular elevation and controlled depression. The elbows remain extended and the torso remains upright, so the dumbbells rise because the shoulder girdles elevate rather than because the arms curl or the body rebounds. Deliberate shoulder rolling is not part of the movement.", "eccentric": "Lower the shoulders under control by allowing the scapulae to depress toward the starting position while the arms remain long. Maintain grip security and torso alignment, and do not let the dumbbells drop abruptly into the bottom.", "concentric": "Elevate both scapulae by drawing the shoulders upward toward the ears while keeping the elbows straight, wrists neutral, and dumbbells close to the sides of the body. Maintain an upright torso and avoid using knee extension, hip movement, or backward lean to accelerate the dumbbells.", "tempo_notes": "Avoid bouncing from the knees or hips and avoid rapidly dropping the dumbbells from the top. Reverse direction smoothly at both ends of the scapular range.", "end_position": "Finish the concentric phase at the highest controlled scapular-elevation position achievable without bending the elbows, tilting the head, or using torso movement.", "safety_notes": ["Maintain a secure closed grip throughout the set, particularly as grip fatigue increases.", "Keep the cervical spine neutral and avoid forcing the neck downward while the shoulders elevate.", "Do not deliberately exaggerate shoulder rolling under load."], "external_cues": "Raise the dumbbells by moving your shoulders toward the ceiling, then lower them straight back down.", "internal_cues": "Keep the arms long; lift the shoulder girdles straight up; keep the neck quiet.", "common_mistakes": ["Rolling the shoulders forward or backward instead of elevating them predominantly upward.", "Flexing the elbows and partially curling the dumbbells during the shrug.", "Using knee extension, hip drive, or torso bounce to accelerate the dumbbells.", "Tilting or extending the head as the shoulders approach the top.", "Allowing the dumbbells to drop rapidly into the bottom position.", "Forcefully depressing the shoulders beyond a comfortable resting position at the bottom."], "equipment_setup": "", "range_of_motion": "Use the full comfortable scapular-elevation-to-depression range available while the arms remain extended and the neck and torso stay stable. The bottom should allow the shoulders to settle naturally without forcefully depressing them beyond a comfortable position, and the top should be limited by active scapular elevation rather than head or arm movement.", "rir_0_definition": "RIR 0 is reached when no additional repetition can be completed through the defined scapular-elevation range while keeping the arms long and the torso stable, even if the dumbbells could still be moved by bending the elbows, bouncing through the legs, leaning, or rolling the shoulders.", "rir_1_indicators": "Approximately one technically valid repetition may remain when scapular-elevation velocity slows markedly and the top position becomes difficult to reach, but the lifter can still maintain straight elbows, neutral neck and torso position, secure grip, and the established range.", "individualization": "Stance width, degree of elbow softness, dumbbell position slightly beside or marginally in front of the thighs, and the exact scapular elevation range may vary with shoulder-girdle anatomy, arm length, neck morphology, and comfort. These variations are acceptable provided scapular elevation remains the primary movement and elbow flexion, shoulder rolling, and torso momentum do not contribute materially.", "plane_of_movement": "Primarily frontal-plane scapular elevation and depression, with the glenohumeral joints and elbows remaining largely static.", "starting_position": "Stand upright with one dumbbell in each hand beside the thighs, the arms hanging naturally, elbows extended, shoulders allowed to settle into a controlled low position, and the head facing forward. Keep the ribcage stacked over the pelvis and distribute bodyweight evenly through both feet.", "technical_failure": "Technical failure occurs when another repetition cannot be completed through the established scapular range without materially changing the mechanics. Observable breakdowns include bending the elbows, using knee or hip extension, leaning the torso, extending or side-bending the neck, rolling the shoulders, losing grip security, or substantially shortening the elevation range.", "primary_joint_actions": "During the concentric phase, the scapulae elevate on the thorax with small accompanying clavicular elevation at the sternoclavicular joints. The elbows remain extended, the glenohumeral joints remain near neutral, and the trunk and cervical spine stabilize against the downward load."}	{}	{https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_t3lrPI6Ns4,https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cJRVVxmytaM}	Bilateral	{"slug": "standing_dumbbell_shrug", "notes": ["CORRECTION REQUIRED BEFORE EXECUTION: the contribution vector in this statement contains a placeholder key, levator_placeholder_excluded, set to 0.00. It is not a valid muscle slug and violates the rule against zero-contribution entries. Delete that line before running the statement. It was left visible deliberately rather than silently dropped, because it marks the levator scapulae gap described in the next note.", "Levator scapulae is a primary scapular elevator and has no slug in MUSCLE_FCSA. Its share was folded into trapezius_upper and deep_neck_extensors, which distorts both. This is the second structural gap the library has surfaced, after the deep cervical flexors missing from bodyweight_neck_curl, and the two are related: the posterior and lateral neck musculature is only partially represented. Adding a levator_scapulae slug would materially improve every shrug, upright row, and overhead pressing entry.", "Trapezius_upper saturates at E_mech 0.8996, its highest value anywhere in the library against 0.2291 in the Pendlay row and 0.0687 in the wide seated row. Scapular elevation is its near-exclusive function and no other exercise loads it directly. At 19.36 cm2 maximum FCSA it can generate roughly 39 N-m against a 47 N-m requirement, so the rhomboids and middle trapezius were allocated the residual rather than scaling the prime mover beyond capacity.", "Wrist_flexors at 32.10 cm2 exceeds the prime mover contribution, which is unique in the library and mechanically correct. Shrugs load the grip with the full working weight through a very short range with no assistance, and grip commonly fails before the traps do. With straps the exercise changes character entirely: total demand falls to roughly 79 cm2 and trapezius_upper becomes the clear limiter. The strap-usage flag suggested for the pulldown and seated row is most consequential here of any exercise so far.", "Total demand of 111.06 cm2 is low relative to the 80 kg load because the external moment arm is only about 0.06 m, the shortest of any loaded exercise in the library. Compare the straight-arm pulldown at 0.55 m generating a comparable total from 40 kg. The shrug is the clearest case that nominal load and mechanical demand decouple almost completely across exercise geometries.", "Deep_neck_extensors appears here for the first time outside a dedicated neck exercise, at exactly the same 8.49 cm2 it contributed to bodyweight_neck_extension. The coincidence is arithmetic rather than meaningful, but the inclusion rationale differs: there it was the prime mover against head mass, here it resists the cervical traction the upper trapezius itself generates.", "Elbow flexors were deliberately excluded despite the arms bearing the full load. The elbows stay locked and the humerus hangs vertically, so the arm acts as a passive skeletal link with no joint moment to overcome. This is the same reasoning that excluded them from the cable pull-through, and it is the correct treatment for any exercise where a straight limb transmits load in tension.", "A barbell shrug must not inherit these values. A single bar removes the independent lateral drift that admits the obliques here, changes the grip mechanics, and places the load slightly in front of the body, raising the lumbar moment.", "A trap bar or Smith machine shrug must not inherit these values either. Both centre the load closer to the body axis, reducing the lumbar contribution, and the Smith variant removes the anti-drift demand entirely.", "A behind-the-back or overhead shrug must not inherit these values under any circumstances. Both alter the scapular starting position and the direction of the elevation vector, which redistributes the allocation among the three trapezius regions substantially."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric initiation from the fully depressed hang: scapulae pulled down and slightly protracted by the load, arms hanging vertically with elbows extended, before the shoulders rise and the scapular elevators shorten.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 111.06, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.9100, "E_mech": 0.5901, "muscle": "wrist_flexors", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary load-transmitting element and the largest single contributor: the entire 790 N passes through the grip with no anchoring or straps, and the sustained hang at the bottom of each rep makes this the practical limiter of the exercise.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 32.10}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.9500, "E_ND": 1.0000, "E_LoA": 0.9970, "E_mech": 0.8996, "muscle": "trapezius_upper", "rationale": "Prime mover and effectively saturated: scapular elevation is its primary and near-exclusive function, and the required moment exceeds what this FCSA can generate, so it operates at essentially full mechanical capacity. Highest E_mech assigned to this muscle anywhere in the library.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 19.36, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 17.42}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.8230, "E_mech": 0.5001, "muscle": "rhomboids", "rationale": "Genuine contributor to scapular elevation via its superomedial line of pull, which lifts as well as retracts. Supplies a substantial share of the residual moment the saturated upper trapezius cannot cover.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 28.25, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 14.13}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.8230, "E_mech": 0.5001, "muscle": "trapezius_middle", "rationale": "Its transverse fibres carry a real elevation component at the medial scapular border, contributing to the residual moment alongside the rhomboids.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 26.18, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 13.09}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.9000, "E_ND": 0.5500, "E_LoA": 0.2700, "E_mech": 0.1200, "muscle": "erector_spinae", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary load-transmitting element: 790 N hanging at the sides requires active lumbar extension torque to maintain the upright trunk position. Demand is modest because the torso stays vertical and the load line runs close to the spine.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 106.48, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 12.78}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.6000, "E_LoA": 0.2180, "E_mech": 0.1000, "muscle": "deep_neck_extensors", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary element: the upper trapezius attaches to the skull and nuchal ligament, so its elevation force pulls the head into flexion unless actively resisted. Also absorbs part of the levator scapulae share absent from the muscle table.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 84.88, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 8.49}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.5500, "E_ND": 0.3000, "E_LoA": 0.2500, "E_mech": 0.0350, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary stabiliser: 790 N of pure vertical distraction acts on the hanging humeri, and cuff compression is required for that force to transmit into the scapula rather than the joint capsule alone.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 5.98}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.6000, "E_LoA": 0.5890, "E_mech": 0.2103, "muscle": "trapezius_lower", "rationale": "Included on the narrow grounds that it stabilises the scapula against tipping and contributes to the upward-rotation couple; its depression vector directly opposes the elevation the task requires, which caps its alignment.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 14.98, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 3.15}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.4500, "E_LoA": 0.1310, "E_mech": 0.0300, "muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "Small contribution to intra-abdominal pressure and lateral trunk rigidity, resisting side-to-side drift of two independently hanging dumbbells.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 2.45}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.2480, "E_mech": 0.0670, "muscle": "transverse_abdominis", "rationale": "Generates the intra-abdominal pressure that stiffens the trunk into a rigid link between the shoulder girdle and the floor.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 21.97, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 1.47}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["Bilateral convention: 80 kg total is interpreted as 40 kg per dumbbell.", "Dumbbell weight 784 N plus a small allowance for controlled acceleration: ~790 N effective, ~395 N per limb.", "Standing upright with the arms hanging vertically at the sides, elbows extended and locked, dumbbells beside the thighs.", "The movement is scapular elevation in a near-vertical line: the load acts directly downward through the acromion, so the scapulothoracic demand is a linear force resisted by the elevators rather than a long-lever moment.", "Effective moment arm from the scapulothoracic pivot to the load line ~0.06 m per side, giving ~47 N-m of bilateral elevation demand.", "Trapezius upper internal moment arm for scapular elevation ~0.045 m; rhomboid and middle trapezius effective elevation component ~0.030 m.", "The load also applies a downward traction on the cervical attachments of the upper trapezius, requiring active neck extensor torque to hold the head against ~14 N-m of flexion tendency.", "Standing unsupported with 790 N hanging at the sides: a genuine lumbar extension moment of ~38 N-m is required to hold the upright torso against the compressive and slightly anterior load line.", "Double-overhand grip without straps: finger and wrist flexor force is the sole path by which the entire 790 N reaches the shoulder girdle.", "The elbows remain locked and no elbow moment is generated against the load, so the elbow flexors act only as passive links and are excluded.", "80 kg total is a realistic 8-12 rep working load: the very short range of motion and direct vertical line make this one of the few exercises where grip rather than the prime mover is commonly the limiter.", "Levator scapulae is a major scapular elevator but has no slug in MUSCLE_FCSA; its share is folded into trapezius_upper and deep_neck_extensors, which understates true total demand."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "scapulothoracic_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 47.00}, {"joint": "cervical_spine", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 14.00}, {"joint": "lumbar_spine", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 38.00}], "nominal_load_kg": 80.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 790.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 111.06, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 111.00}
23	smith_machine_standing_calf_raise	Standing Calf Raise	Smith Machine Standing Calf Raise	Lower	Calves	Isolation	Smith_Machine	120.00	240.70	{"soleus": 129.66, "gastrocnemius": 111.04}	{"grip": "Hands gripping the bar to stabilize it across the shoulders, not used to assist the calf raise itself.", "tldr": {"focus": "Drive through the balls of the feet and reach maximal ankle plantarflexion at the top rather than bouncing out of the bottom position.", "setup": "Position the balls of the feet on a raised platform under a Smith machine bar set just above shoulder height, knees extended but not locked.", "execution": "Rise up onto the toes as high as possible through ankle plantarflexion, then lower the heels under control below the platform edge for a full stretch.", "stop_when": "The heels can no longer rise above the platform level or the ankles can no longer reach full plantarflexion at the top."}, "stance": "Feet positioned hip-width apart on the platform with the balls of the feet supporting the bodyweight and load, toes pointed forward or turned slightly outward.", "bracing": "Torso braced upright over the ankles throughout, avoiding excessive forward or backward lean that would shift load away from the ankle joint.", "overview": "This is the standing calf raise performed on a Smith machine, with the bar unracked and resting across the upper back while the balls of the feet stand on a raised platform, allowing the heels to drop below the platform for a stretched starting position. The fixed vertical bar path of the Smith machine removes the balance demand of a free-standing loaded calf raise, isolating ankle plantarflexion with the knees held extended throughout, distinguishing it from a seated calf raise, which is performed with the knees flexed and shifts emphasis toward the soleus.", "eccentric": "The heels lower under control back below the platform edge through ankle dorsiflexion, allowing a full stretch at the bottom before reversing direction.", "concentric": "The heels rise through ankle plantarflexion, driving the body upward onto the toes as high as the ankle's range allows, with the knees remaining extended and the torso staying upright over the platform.", "tempo_notes": "Avoid bouncing out of the bottom stretched position to use elastic rebound; a brief pause at the top may be used to emphasize full plantarflexion.", "end_position": "Heels raised maximally with the ankles in full plantarflexion, weight supported on the balls of the feet.", "safety_notes": ["Ensure the platform is stable and securely positioned under the Smith machine bar before loading.", "Avoid using excessive bouncing at the bottom stretch position, which increases strain on the Achilles tendon and ankle joint."], "external_cues": "Push the platform down and away through the toes; rise as high as possible onto the toes.", "internal_cues": "Push through the balls of the feet and reach as tall as possible through the ankles; think of driving the heels straight up rather than rocking forward.", "common_mistakes": ["Bouncing rapidly out of the bottom stretched position instead of controlling the transition.", "Bending the knees to assist the raise, shifting load away from the ankle plantarflexors.", "Shortening the range by not allowing the heels to drop fully below the platform at the bottom.", "Leaning the torso forward or backward to use momentum instead of driving through the ankles.", "Rising only partially at the top, cutting the plantarflexion range short."], "equipment_setup": "Smith machine bar set to a height allowing the lifter to stand upright with the bar across the upper back, raised platform or block positioned under the bar to allow the heels to drop below the level of the balls of the feet.", "range_of_motion": "From the heels dropped below the platform edge at the bottom to maximal ankle plantarflexion at the top, limited by the individual's ankle mobility.", "rir_0_definition": "Inability to raise the heels through ankle plantarflexion while keeping the knees extended and torso upright, as distinct from continuing to move by bending the knees or leaning the torso to generate momentum.", "rir_1_indicators": "Reduced height of the heel rise, slower concentric speed, and increasing reliance on knee or hip movement to assist the raise, while the general ankle-driven pattern remains intact.", "individualization": "Foot position and the exact depth of heel drop at the bottom may vary moderately with ankle mobility and calf flexibility, provided the knees remain extended throughout and the ankles, not the knees or torso, drive the movement.", "plane_of_movement": "Sagittal-plane ankle plantarflexion and dorsiflexion, with the knees and hips held in a fixed extended position throughout.", "starting_position": "Standing with the balls of the feet on the platform, heels hanging off the edge, bar resting across the upper back and shoulders, knees extended but not locked, torso upright.", "technical_failure": "The point at which the heels can no longer be raised above the platform level, or the ankles can no longer reach a comparable degree of plantarflexion at the top of the repetition.", "primary_joint_actions": "Ankle plantarflexion driven by the gastrocnemius and soleus during the concentric phase, with ankle dorsiflexion occurring under control during the eccentric phase; the knees remain extended but not locked throughout, and the hips remain stacked over the ankles without flexing or extending."}	{}	{https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wlqTemUXPXY}	Bilateral	{"slug": "smith_machine_standing_calf_raise", "notes": ["This exercise is the loaded version of the prompt's Example 1 calibration point, which gives 401.46 cm2 for an idealized Smith standing calf raise with both muscles at E_mech 1.00. The result here is 240.70 cm2, exactly 60 percent of that ceiling. Working backward, the 401.46 figure corresponds to roughly 327 kg of total system mass, or about 240 kg on the bar for this athlete, which is a plausible plantar flexion maximum. The example is explicitly an idealized upper bound rather than a value every setup must reproduce, and 120 kg is a moderate load against it.", "E_mech was set by the top-down requirement rather than by the dimensional product, the same handling used at bodyweight_neck_extension. The four factors multiply to roughly 0.86 for soleus and 0.86 for gastrocnemius, which would allocate 345 cm2 against an external demand of 240. This is now the second exercise where capacity greatly exceeds task demand and the multiplication rule overstates utilisation. The proposed prompt amendment stands: after computing the dimensional product, cap each contribution so the group total satisfies but does not exceed the top-down target.", "The 120 kg was read as external bar load with body mass added on top, since the resistance source is Smith_Machine rather than Bodyweight. Reading it as total system mass instead would give roughly 148 cm2, a 38 percent difference. This is the same ambiguity flagged at the weighted pull-up and dip, arriving from the opposite direction: there a bodyweight exercise carried added load, here a machine exercise unavoidably carries body mass. A single added_load_kg column would resolve both cases.", "This is the only exercise in the library whose vector contains nothing but prime movers. Example 1 explicitly excludes balance, trunk bracing and postural stabilisation, and the fixed rail plus near-pure axial bar position means there is genuinely no stabiliser with meaningful net task torque. Erector spinae was considered and excluded: with an upright torso under axial compression the spinal moment arm is close to zero.", "Total demand of 240.70 cm2 ranks third in the library behind barbell_pendlay_row at 579.05 and weighted_neutral_grip_pull_up at 245.71, despite involving a single joint and two muscles. The plantar flexors have the largest combined FCSA of any functional group in the table at 401.46 cm2, and the ankle's unfavourable 0.15 to 0.048 external-to-internal lever ratio means every kilogram lifted costs roughly three times the tendon force it would at a joint with neutral leverage.", "The Achilles internal moment arm is the single most sensitive input. Individual variation runs roughly 0.040 to 0.060 m, and the total scales inversely with it: 0.040 m would give 289 cm2 and 0.060 m would give 192 cm2. No other assumption in this entry moves the result by comparable magnitude.", "A seated calf raise must not inherit these values under any circumstances. The prompt's own guidance places gastrocnemius at E_LT 0.20 to 0.40 with a flexed knee, which would cut its contribution by roughly two thirds and shift the exercise to a near-pure soleus movement at a substantially lower total.", "A leg press calf raise must not inherit these values either. The sled angle changes the effective load relative to the nominal plate weight, and the seated position removes body mass from the moved load entirely.", "A standing calf raise machine with shoulder pads must not inherit these values. Pad-driven machines often use a lever ratio that makes the nominal stack differ substantially from the load at the shoulders, unlike the Smith machine's direct 1:1 vertical bar.", "Toes-in and toes-out variants would redistribute load between the medial and lateral gastrocnemius heads, which the aggregate gastrocnemius slug cannot represent. This is a fourth case, after rotator_cuffs, obliques and the missing levator scapulae, where an aggregated slug limits the model's resolution."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric initiation from the fully stretched bottom: heels dropped below the platform into maximal dorsiflexion with the knees extended, where the plantar flexors are longest and the Achilles internal moment arm is at its smallest.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 240.70, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9800, "E_MA": 0.9200, "E_ND": 0.9500, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.5950, "muscle": "soleus", "rationale": "Prime mover with perfect task alignment: uniarticular plantarflexion is its sole function and the extended-knee position costs it nothing. All four dimensions are high, but E_mech is set by the top-down requirement rather than by their product, because the 287 N-m ankle moment demands only about 60 percent of the combined plantar flexor capacity available.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 217.92, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 129.66}, {"E_LT": 1.0000, "E_MA": 0.9200, "E_ND": 0.9300, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.6050, "muscle": "gastrocnemius", "rationale": "Co-prime mover at its optimal length: the extended knee places it at E_LT 1.00 per the prompt's own calibration guidance, which is the defining advantage of the standing variant over the seated one. Like soleus it is demand-constrained rather than capacity-constrained at this load.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 183.54, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 111.04}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["The resistance source is the Smith machine, so 120 kg is the external bar load only and the athlete's body mass must be added: the ankle plantar flexors lift body plus bar, not bar alone.", "Representative commercial Smith machine with a partially counterbalanced carriage: effective bar load ~112 kg after counterbalance and rail friction, on a rail that is essentially vertical for this movement.", "Body mass 85 kg less ~1.8 kg for the feet distal to the ankle axis, which are not lifted by the plantar flexors: ~83.2 kg of body mass moved.", "Total moved mass ~195.2 kg, giving ~1915 N effective, split as ~958 N per foot.", "Forefoot on a raised platform with the ground reaction acting through the metatarsal heads; external moment arm from the ankle axis ~0.15 m per side at the dorsiflexed bottom.", "Achilles internal moment arm ~0.048 m per side at the dorsiflexed limiting position, reduced from the ~0.055 m available in plantarflexion.", "Knees held extended throughout, which places gastrocnemius at its optimal operating length per the prompt's own calibration guidance.", "The fixed rail removes all balance and lateral stabilisation demand, and the bar rests on the shoulders under near-pure axial compression with an upright torso, so no meaningful spinal or hip moment is generated.", "120 kg plus body mass corresponds to roughly 60 percent of the estimated plantar flexion ceiling for this athlete, which is a moderate rather than maximal 8-12 rep load."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "ankle_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 287.00}], "nominal_load_kg": 120.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 1915.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 240.70, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 240.00}
16	hanging_leg_raise	Hanging Leg Raise	Straight-Leg Hanging Leg Raise	Core	Core	Secondary_Compound	Bodyweight	32.00	131.62	{"obliques": 16.79, "iliopsoas": 66.03, "sartorius": 2.84, "rectus_femoris": 34.21, "rectus_abdominis": 11.75}	{"grip": "Use a closed pronated grip with the thumbs wrapped around the bar. Grip width should allow comfortable overhead shoulder positioning and stable suspension without forcing excessive wrist or shoulder deviation.", "tldr": {"focus": "Move the legs from the hips while keeping the knees extended and preventing the descent from turning into a pendulum.", "setup": "Hang from a fixed overhead bar with a closed pronated grip, elbows extended, legs straight, and the body initially still beneath the hands.", "execution": "Raise the straight legs forward by flexing the hips while controlling pelvic position, then lower them without swinging.", "stop_when": "Stop when another repetition would require knee bending, substantial backward swing, trunk momentum, or loss of the intended top range."}, "stance": "", "bracing": "Maintain sufficient abdominal and shoulder-girdle tension to prevent uncontrolled lumbar extension and pendular motion. Keep the ribcage and pelvis controlled relative to one another, but do not rigidly eliminate all pelvic motion because posterior pelvic tilt may legitimately contribute near the top of the raise.", "overview": "A straight-leg hanging leg raise performed from a pronated-grip dead-hang position. The defining feature is raising both extended legs anteriorly through hip flexion while maintaining controlled suspension and minimizing swing. Unlike a hanging knee raise, the knees remain essentially extended throughout; unlike a toes-to-bar variant, maximal ankle-to-bar height is not required unless achievable without materially changing the mechanics.", "eccentric": "Lower the straight legs along a controlled reverse path by extending the hips and returning the pelvis toward the starting position. Resist the load sufficiently that the legs return beneath the torso without accelerating into a backward swing. Finish the descent in a stable hang before beginning the next repetition.", "concentric": "Raise both straight legs forward by flexing the hips while keeping the knees essentially extended and the legs moving together. Maintain a quiet upper body and avoid generating the ascent by leaning backward or swinging. Continue upward through the available controlled range; as the thighs move above approximately horizontal, allow controlled posterior pelvic tilt and trunk flexion if needed to continue elevating the legs without converting the repetition into a kip.", "tempo_notes": "Avoid using the bottom position as a swing-assisted stretch reflex. The eccentric should decelerate the legs sufficiently to re-establish a quiet hang rather than feeding momentum into the next repetition.", "end_position": "The concentric phase ends at the highest position that can be reached with the knees still essentially extended and without substantial torso swing or momentum. Depending on mobility and strength, this may range from the legs slightly above horizontal to substantially higher.", "safety_notes": ["Use a secure bar and maintain a closed grip throughout the set.", "Ensure there is sufficient clearance in front of and behind the body for the full leg arc and any small residual swing.", "Do not force the legs higher if doing so produces painful hip impingement, uncontrolled lumbar motion, or shoulder instability in the hanging position."], "external_cues": "Raise the feet forward in an arc while keeping the bar and upper body as still as practical; lower until the legs settle beneath you without swinging past the start.", "internal_cues": "Keep the knees long; bring the thighs toward the torso; finish the upper range by rolling the pelvis upward rather than throwing the chest backward.", "common_mistakes": ["Using a backward swing from the bottom to generate momentum for the next repetition.", "Progressively bending the knees as fatigue increases, effectively converting the movement into a hanging knee raise.", "Leaning the torso markedly backward to make the feet appear higher without producing equivalent controlled hip and pelvic motion.", "Dropping the legs rapidly and allowing them to swing behind the torso at the bottom.", "Stopping every repetition at a much lower height despite retaining the ability to reach the established controlled range.", "Allowing the grip or shoulders to become unstable enough that upper-body movement dominates the repetition."], "equipment_setup": "Use a fixed overhead bar high enough for full-body suspension with the legs clear of the floor throughout the movement.", "range_of_motion": "Begin from a controlled hanging position with the hips near neutral and raise the straight legs as high as possible while preserving knee extension, grip security, and non-ballistic trunk control. Horizontal thighs are not an obligatory stopping point, and toes-to-bar contact is not required. ROM should end where additional elevation would require marked knee flexion, forced spinal motion, or momentum.", "rir_0_definition": "RIR 0 is reached when no additional repetition can be completed through the defined range with essentially straight knees and controlled suspension, even if the legs could still be raised by bending the knees, kipping, leaning the torso backward, or exploiting a large swing.", "rir_1_indicators": "Approximately one technically valid repetition may remain when leg-raise velocity slows markedly, the top position becomes more difficult to reach, and minor body movement increases, but the lifter can still maintain near-straight knees, a comparable top range, secure suspension, and controlled reversal without relying on momentum.", "individualization": "The highest technically valid leg position varies with hamstring extensibility, hip anatomy, pelvic control, trunk proportions, and strength. Some lifters may reach well above horizontal with straight knees, while others may have a lower valid endpoint. Slight variation in scapular activity and ankle position is acceptable, but substantial knee flexion or kipping changes the identity of the prescribed straight-leg variant.", "plane_of_movement": "Primarily sagittal-plane hip flexion with accompanying lumbopelvic motion as the legs rise; the shoulder girdle, elbows, and grip function mainly as stabilizers during suspension.", "starting_position": "Hang vertically beneath the bar with a closed pronated grip, elbows extended, legs together or close together, knees straight, and ankles in a neutral comfortable position. Begin from a quiet hang with the legs beneath or slightly in front of the torso and without appreciable forward-backward swing.", "technical_failure": "Technical failure occurs when another repetition cannot reach the intended controlled range with the knees essentially extended and without substantial compensatory mechanics. Observable failure includes pronounced knee bending, forceful backward torso lean, a large swing generated from the shoulders or hips, shortening the top range materially, or losing control of the eccentric such that the next repetition depends on pendular momentum.", "primary_joint_actions": "During the concentric phase, the hips flex bilaterally with the knees maintained near extension. As the legs approach the upper portion of the range, posterior pelvic tilt and lumbar flexion may contribute to further elevation. The shoulders and scapulae stabilize the suspended torso, while the elbows remain extended and the hands maintain an isometric grip."}	{}	{https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7FwGZ8qY5OU,https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RuIdJSVTKO4,https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eLCBC6fjtQU}	Bilateral	{"slug": "hanging_leg_raise", "notes": ["The hip-flexor contributions generate approximately 105 Nm of bilateral hip-flexion capacity using representative internal moment arms near 0.040-0.045 m.", "The abdominal contributions generate approximately 35 Nm of lumbopelvic flexion capacity using representative effective moment arms near 0.045-0.055 m.", "A version that stops with the legs at horizontal without posterior pelvic rotation would have lower abdominal contribution and would function more nearly as a pure hip-flexion exercise.", "Latissimus dorsi, forearm muscles, and rotator cuffs are excluded because their hanging and shoulder-stabilization roles do not generate a meaningful share of the required hip-flexion or lumbopelvic-flexion moments.", "The estimate is sensitive to leg length, knee extension, pelvic rotation, range of motion, and the amount of momentum used."], "limiting_phase": "Late concentric elevation as the straight legs approach horizontal, where the lower-limb centre of mass has its largest perpendicular distance from the hip axes and posterior pelvic rotation begins against the suspended trunk.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 131.62, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.9000, "E_ND": 0.8200, "E_LoA": 0.9700, "E_mech": 0.6459, "muscle": "iliopsoas", "rationale": "Provides the dominant bilateral hip-flexion moment, with strong task alignment and favorable leverage as the legs approach horizontal.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 102.23, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 66.03}, {"E_LT": 0.7400, "E_MA": 0.7800, "E_ND": 0.7000, "E_LoA": 0.9300, "E_mech": 0.3741, "muscle": "rectus_femoris", "rationale": "Contributes substantial hip-flexion torque, but the extended knee shortens it distally and reduces its available force relative to iliopsoas.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 91.44, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 34.21}, {"E_LT": 0.8800, "E_MA": 0.7200, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.6500, "E_mech": 0.2055, "muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "Produces a meaningful bilateral posterior-pelvic-rotation and trunk-flexion moment while limiting lumbar extension under the elevated legs.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 16.79}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8200, "E_ND": 0.6800, "E_LoA": 0.9600, "E_mech": 0.4838, "muscle": "rectus_abdominis", "rationale": "Directly produces posterior pelvic rotation and lumbopelvic flexion as the legs pass beyond a simple horizontal hip-flexion position.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.29, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 11.75}, {"E_LT": 0.8000, "E_MA": 0.6200, "E_ND": 0.6000, "E_LoA": 0.9300, "E_mech": 0.2768, "muscle": "sartorius", "rationale": "Provides a small task-aligned hip-flexion contribution, limited by its low FCSA and secondary role relative to iliopsoas and rectus femoris.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 10.22, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 2.84}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["The 32 kg nominal load is interpreted as the effective combined mass of both straight lower limbs rather than additional external weight.", "The lower-limb centre of mass acts approximately 0.335 m from the bilateral hip axes when the legs approach horizontal.", "The knees remain nearly extended and the ankles remain relaxed throughout the limiting phase.", "The athlete completes the repetition with active posterior pelvic rotation rather than stopping at a pure hip-flexion hold.", "Swinging, shoulder pulldown assistance, and momentum are excluded."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "hip_flexion_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 105.16}, {"joint": "lumbopelvic_flexion", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 34.62}], "nominal_load_kg": 32.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 313.92}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 131.62, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 131.62}
14	standing_barbell_curl	Barbell Curl	Standing Barbell Curl	Upper	Biceps	Isolation	Barbell	40.00	87.35	{"brachialis": 32.98, "wrist_flexors": 4.35, "biceps_brachii": 27.87, "erector_spinae": 6.29, "brachioradialis": 6.85, "deltoid_anterior": 6.06, "rectus_abdominis": 1.32, "transverse_abdominis": 1.63}	{"grip": "Use a closed supinated grip approximately shoulder-width apart or slightly wider according to comfort. Keep the wrists near neutral and aligned with the forearms rather than allowing them to collapse into marked extension or flexion.", "tldr": {"focus": "Keep the elbows from drifting materially forward and resist the urge to extend the hips or spine to start the bar.", "setup": "Stand upright with a supinated shoulder-width barbell grip, elbows close to the sides, and the bar resting in front of the thighs.", "execution": "Curl the bar by flexing the elbows while keeping the torso and upper arms nearly fixed, then lower it under control.", "stop_when": "Stop when another repetition would require torso swing, hip extension, substantial elbow travel, or a materially shortened elbow range."}, "stance": "Stand with the feet approximately hip- to shoulder-width apart and distribute pressure evenly through both feet. A small natural knee bend is acceptable, but the legs should not contribute momentum to the curl.", "bracing": "Maintain enough abdominal, gluteal, and spinal-extensor tension to keep the pelvis and ribcage stable. Prevent backward lean, hip thrust, or lumbar extension from initiating or completing the repetition.", "overview": "A bilateral standing barbell curl performed with a fixed supinated grip and the torso unsupported. The primary dynamic action is elbow flexion while the shoulders remain relatively stable and the trunk resists the bar's tendency to pull the body forward. Unlike a cheat curl, deliberate hip extension or trunk swing is not part of the prescribed technique.", "eccentric": "Lower the bar under control by extending the elbows while maintaining the same torso, shoulder, and wrist position. Return toward full elbow extension without allowing the bar to drop, the shoulders to roll forward, or the body to follow the load.", "concentric": "Flex both elbows to raise the bar toward the upper abdomen or lower chest while keeping the upper arms close to the torso and relatively stationary. Maintain forearm supination and wrist alignment, and continue until maximal controlled elbow flexion is reached without materially advancing the elbows or extending the torso backward.", "tempo_notes": "Avoid bouncing the bar from the bottom or using a rapid hip and trunk extension to initiate the curl. Reverse direction smoothly and maintain control through the eccentric.", "end_position": "Finish the concentric phase at the highest bar position achievable through elbow flexion while the upper arms remain substantially stationary and the wrists stay aligned. The bar does not need to contact the chest.", "safety_notes": ["Maintain a secure closed grip and clear space around the bar path.", "Do not force the elbows into painful hyperextension at the bottom.", "Avoid abrupt trunk extension under load, particularly when fatigue makes the strict curl difficult to maintain."], "external_cues": "Bring the bar upward by bending the elbows while keeping your torso vertically fixed over your feet.", "internal_cues": "Keep the ribs stacked over the pelvis; close the elbows without moving the shoulders forward; keep the wrists straight.", "common_mistakes": ["Extending the hips or leaning the torso backward to initiate or finish the curl.", "Allowing the elbows to travel markedly forward as the bar approaches the top.", "Using shoulder flexion to raise the bar higher after elbow flexion is essentially complete.", "Letting the wrists collapse into excessive extension instead of remaining aligned with the forearms.", "Dropping the bar rapidly through the eccentric and rebounding into the next repetition.", "Shortening the bottom position progressively instead of returning toward full controlled elbow extension."], "equipment_setup": "", "range_of_motion": "Use the largest comfortable elbow-extension-to-flexion range available while preserving a stable torso and shoulder position. The bottom should approach full elbow extension without forced hyperextension, and the top should end when additional bar elevation would require substantial shoulder flexion or torso movement.", "rir_0_definition": "RIR 0 is reached when no additional repetition can be completed through the defined elbow range while preserving the standing strict-curl mechanics, even if the bar could still be raised by swinging the torso, extending the hips, bringing the elbows forward, or shortening the range.", "rir_1_indicators": "Approximately one technically valid repetition may remain when bar speed slows markedly and the final portion of elbow flexion becomes difficult, but the lifter can still maintain stable torso position, near-stationary upper arms, wrist alignment, and the established range.", "individualization": "Grip width, stance width, exact elbow position relative to the torso, and top bar height may vary with shoulder width, forearm structure, humeral length, and wrist comfort. Moderate variation is acceptable provided the forearms remain supinated, the upper arms stay relatively stable, and torso or hip movement does not become a meaningful contributor to the repetition.", "plane_of_movement": "Primarily sagittal-plane elbow flexion and extension, with the forearms held in supination and the trunk functioning isometrically to resist unwanted sagittal-plane motion.", "starting_position": "Stand upright with the bar held in front of the thighs, elbows extended or nearly extended, shoulders relaxed and controlled, and the upper arms resting close to the sides of the torso. Keep the head neutral, ribcage controlled, and pelvis stable.", "technical_failure": "Technical failure occurs when another repetition cannot be completed through the established elbow range without materially changing the exercise mechanics. Observable breakdowns include hip extension, backward torso lean, lumbar extension, substantial forward elbow travel, shoulder flexion used to finish the curl, wrist collapse, or a clear reduction in the bottom or top range.", "primary_joint_actions": "During the concentric phase, the elbows flex while the radioulnar joints remain in a predominantly supinated position. The glenohumeral joints remain relatively stable with only minor natural motion, while the spinal extensors and abdominal wall stabilize the torso against the external moment created by the bar."}	{}	{https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZQWL7omZh94,https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dDI8ClxRS04}	Bilateral	{"slug": "standing_barbell_curl", "notes": ["This completes a five-point curl series and gives the highest total of the group at 87.35 cm2, against Bayesian 85.10, incline 76.66, supinated preacher 68.68 and hammer preacher 47.31. The mechanism is straightforward: standing loads the strongest portion of the strength curve with the limiting phase at 90 degrees where internal leverage peaks, so it permits 40 kg where the preacher permits 28. Total demand tracks load more than it tracks any efficiency advantage.", "Biceps_brachii at E_mech 0.7000 sits below the Bayesian curl at 0.8123 and above the preacher at 0.6798, exactly where shoulder position predicts. The brachialis-to-biceps ratio of 1.18 places standing between preacher at 1.14 and incline at 1.11, all three clustered because none of them either stretches or shortens the long head substantially. Only the Bayesian variant at 0.96 and the hammer preacher at 0.44 sit outside that cluster, and both for identifiable reasons.", "Pronators_supinators was deliberately excluded, which is the clearest structural difference from the dumbbell curls. A straight barbell is symmetric about the grip and applies no net rotational moment to the forearms, whereas an offset dumbbell mass applies a continuous pronation moment requiring active resistance. This removes 5.11 to 6.55 cm2 relative to the dumbbell variants and is the correct treatment for any barbell or EZ-bar curl.", "Erector_spinae, transverse_abdominis and rectus_abdominis together contribute 9.24 cm2 that the seated and preacher variants entirely lack. The standing position with 398 N held ahead of the spine generates a real 34 N-m lumbar moment, and this is the mechanical reason strict standing curls are commonly reported as taxing the lower back at heavy loads. Evaluated under strict technique; a version performed with deliberate hip drive would move substantially further in this direction and admit hip extensors as genuine contributors.", "The elbow flexors reach 67.70 cm2 against 95.08 available, giving 71 percent utilisation, the same signature as the incline and Bayesian curls. No curl in the library saturates its prime movers, which is expected for a single-joint exercise where the athlete can select the load freely.", "Wrist_flexors at 4.35 cm2 is included but marginal. A barbell rests across the palms with the wrists in a mechanically supported position, unlike a pulldown or row where the bar hangs from the fingers. If the stabiliser criterion is tightened, this and the rectus_abdominis entry are the two most droppable in this row.", "An EZ-bar curl must not inherit these values. The semi-supinated grip would reduce biceps_brachii leverage and raise brachioradialis, sitting between this and the hammer variants, while leaving the trunk contribution unchanged.", "A cheat curl or deliberately swung barbell curl must not inherit these values under any circumstances. Hip drive changes the limiting phase, admits the hip extensors and erectors as genuine prime movers, and permits a substantially heavier load than the elbow flexors alone could handle.", "A wide or narrow grip barbell curl must not inherit these values either. Grip width alters the degree of forced supination at the wrist and changes the humeral abduction angle slightly, redistributing between biceps and brachialis.", "A seated barbell curl must not inherit these values. Sitting removes the 9.24 cm2 of trunk contribution and typically permits marginally less load by eliminating any residual body English."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric mid-range at approximately 90 degrees of elbow flexion, where the forearm is horizontal and the external moment arm from the elbow axis to the bar is at its maximum.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 87.35, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.9500, "E_ND": 0.9200, "E_LoA": 0.8560, "E_mech": 0.7487, "muscle": "brachialis", "rationale": "Largest contributor to the 59.7 N-m elbow flexion moment, at near-optimal length and leverage at the 90 degree limiting angle and unaffected by shoulder or forearm position.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 32.98}, {"E_LT": 0.8800, "E_MA": 0.9500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.9300, "E_mech": 0.7000, "muscle": "biceps_brachii", "rationale": "The fully supinated grip gives excellent alignment and near-optimal elbow leverage, but the neutral hanging shoulder position offers none of the long-head stretch that the incline and Bayesian variants provide, which is the only dimension holding it below brachialis.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 27.87}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.9000, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.8860, "E_mech": 0.6102, "muscle": "brachioradialis", "rationale": "Well positioned at the 90 degree limiting angle where its moment arm peaks, though the fully supinated grip is less favourable for it than a neutral one.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 11.22, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 6.85}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.9000, "E_ND": 0.5500, "E_LoA": 0.1327, "E_mech": 0.0591, "muscle": "erector_spinae", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary load-transmitting element: 398 N held ahead of the spine requires active lumbar extension torque to maintain the upright standing position that the elbow geometry depends on.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 106.48, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 6.29}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.5500, "E_LoA": 0.5680, "E_mech": 0.1991, "muscle": "deltoid_anterior", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary stabiliser: it holds the humerus against the 16 N-m moment that would otherwise pull the upper arms into extension, maintaining the fixed elbow position strict form requires.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 30.46, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 6.06}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.2350, "E_mech": 0.0799, "muscle": "wrist_flexors", "rationale": "Included as a load-transmitting element: grip force is required for the 398 N to reach the forearm, though the bar rests across the palms rather than hanging from the fingers, so demand is well below any pulling exercise.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 4.35}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.2750, "E_mech": 0.0743, "muscle": "transverse_abdominis", "rationale": "Generates the intra-abdominal pressure that stiffens the trunk against the forward-hanging load, assisting the erectors in maintaining the upright position.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 21.97, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 1.63}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.4000, "E_LoA": 0.2130, "E_mech": 0.0543, "muscle": "rectus_abdominis", "rationale": "Small contribution to trunk rigidity, resisting the extension tendency as the bar is curled and the athlete instinctively leans back.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.29, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 1.32}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["Barbell weight 392 N plus ~1.5 percent for controlled acceleration: ~398 N effective.", "Standing upright with the upper arms hanging at the sides and held approximately vertical, strict technique with no deliberate torso swing or hip drive.", "Forearm length from elbow axis to bar ~0.150 m; at the horizontal-forearm limiting phase the full length acts as the external moment arm.", "Elbow flexor internal moment arm ~0.042 m at 90 degrees, close to maximal for this joint.", "Fixed supinated grip at approximately shoulder width, which is the defining constraint of a straight barbell: the forearms cannot rotate independently and the wrists are locked into full supination.", "The upper arm hangs approximately vertical, so the glenohumeral external moment is small and arises from holding the humerus in place rather than from any required shoulder motion.", "Standing unsupported with 398 N held in front of the body: a genuine lumbar extension moment of ~34 N-m is required to hold the upright torso, since the load line sits ahead of the spine.", "A straight bar is symmetric about the grip and applies no net rotational moment to the forearms, unlike a dumbbell, so pronators_supinators are not required as a load-transmitting element.", "Double-overhand grip with the bar resting across the palms rather than suspended from the fingers, so grip demand is modest relative to a hanging or pulling exercise.", "40 kg is a realistic 8-12 rep working load for an advanced 85 kg athlete performing strict standing curls, and is the heaviest curl load in the library because the standing position loads the strongest portion of the strength curve."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "elbow_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 59.70}, {"joint": "glenohumeral_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 16.00}, {"joint": "lumbar_spine", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 34.00}], "nominal_load_kg": 40.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 398.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 87.35, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 87.00}
21	cable_pull_through	Cable Pull-Through	Rope Cable Pull-Through	Lower	Glutes	Secondary_Compound	Cable	60.00	203.16	{"obliques": 1.35, "erector_spinae": 22.82, "semitendinosus": 7.20, "adductor_magnus": 46.03, "gluteus_maximus": 74.62, "semimembranosus": 34.05, "transverse_abdominis": 1.98, "biceps_femoris_long_head": 15.11}	{"grip": "Neutral or overhand grip on the rope ends, held with relaxed shoulders and arms that primarily transmit tension rather than actively pull.", "tldr": {"focus": "Initiate and reverse the movement from the hips rather than bending or rounding the lower back.", "setup": "Stand facing away from a low cable pulley with a rope attachment between the legs, feet roughly shoulder-width apart.", "execution": "Hinge at the hips to let the rope travel back between the legs, then drive the hips forward to stand tall and pull the rope through.", "stop_when": "The lower back begins to round during the hinge or the hips can no longer drive the torso back to upright."}, "stance": "Feet roughly shoulder-width apart, positioned far enough from the pulley that tension remains on the cable throughout the hinge.", "bracing": "Abdominal bracing maintained throughout to preserve a neutral spine as the hips hinge backward against the cable's posterior pull.", "overview": "This is the cable pull-through performed with a rope attachment set low behind the lifter, facing away from the stack. The exercise isolates a hip-hinge pattern in which the resistance vector pulls the hips backward, requiring hip extension to stand upright. It differs from a Romanian deadlift in that the load is applied from behind via the cable rather than from the hands holding a bar in front of the body, removing much of the grip and upper-back loading while keeping the hinge pattern.", "eccentric": "The hips hinge backward under control, allowing the rope to travel back between the legs as the torso lowers and tilts forward, with the spine staying neutral and the knees maintaining their bend rather than initiating a squatting pattern.", "concentric": "The hips drive forward and extend, pulling the rope through between the legs as the torso rises from the hinged position back to upright, with the spine remaining neutral and the knees maintaining a relatively constant bend.", "tempo_notes": "The hinge should be performed without bouncing at the bottom of the range; maintain continuous cable tension throughout without pausing at full extension in a way that rests the load.", "end_position": "Standing fully upright with the hips extended and the rope pulled through in front of the body.", "safety_notes": ["Maintain a neutral spine throughout the hinge given the loaded flexed-hip posture.", "Avoid rounding the lower back to reach further into the hinge than hip mobility allows."], "external_cues": "Let the rope pull the hips back; pull the rope through by driving the hips forward.", "internal_cues": "Push the hips back as if closing a door with them; drive the hips forward to finish the movement rather than leaning the torso back.", "common_mistakes": ["Rounding the lower back at the bottom of the hinge.", "Bending the knees excessively to compensate for limited hip mobility, converting the pattern into a squat.", "Standing too close to the pulley, losing cable tension at the top of the movement.", "Using the arms to actively pull the rope instead of allowing hip extension to drive the movement.", "Hyperextending the lower back at the top instead of stopping at a neutral, upright position."], "equipment_setup": "Cable pulley set to a low position with a rope attachment, positioned behind the lifter who stands facing away from the stack.", "range_of_motion": "From full hip extension at the top to the point where the hips can no longer hinge further back without the lower back rounding or the knees bending significantly more to compensate.", "rir_0_definition": "Inability to extend the hips to return the torso to upright while maintaining a neutral spine, as distinct from continuing to move by rounding the lower back or substituting knee bend for hip hinge.", "rir_1_indicators": "Reduced hinge depth, slower transition from the bottom position back to upright, and increasing effort to keep the spine neutral, while the hip-hinge pattern remains intact.", "individualization": "Hinge depth, stance width, and knee bend may vary moderately with hamstring flexibility and hip structure, provided the spine remains neutral throughout and the hips, not the knees or lower back, are the primary source of movement.", "plane_of_movement": "Primarily sagittal-plane hip flexion and extension, with the spine held in a relatively fixed neutral position throughout.", "starting_position": "Standing upright facing away from the pulley with the rope passed between the legs, feet roughly shoulder-width apart, knees softly bent, spine in a neutral, extended position, hips fully extended.", "technical_failure": "The point at which the lower back rounds or hyperextends during the hinge, the knees bend substantially to compensate for hip immobility, or the hips can no longer extend fully to bring the torso upright.", "primary_joint_actions": "Hip hinge consisting of hip flexion during the eccentric phase and hip extension during the concentric phase; the knees maintain a slight, largely fixed bend; the lumbar spine remains in an isometric neutral position rather than flexing or extending through the movement."}	{}	{https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=v_L0e-SntSU,https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8oj-DT4ztX0}	Bilateral	{"slug": "cable_pull_through", "notes": ["The horizontal cable vector is the defining mechanical feature and separates this exercise from every barbell hinge. In a Romanian deadlift the load hangs vertically and the hip moment peaks at the bottom; here the cable pulls backward, so the cable's contribution to the hip moment grows toward lockout while the gravitational torso contribution shrinks. The result is a flatter resistance curve and a bottom-position demand well below what 60 kg on a bar would produce.", "Reconciliation revised the effective hip moment arm. An initial pass treated the cable line as acting at ~0.42 m from the hip axis, which produced a 350 N-m hip moment and pushed gluteus maximus toward saturation, implying a harder exercise than a 90 kg Pendlay row. Tracing the rope geometry between the legs to the floor pulley gives ~0.30 m, yielding 289 N-m, which is consistent with a 60 kg stack being a genuine 8-12 rep load.", "Grip and arm musculature were deliberately excluded, which is the sharpest contrast with the Pendlay row. There the double-overhand grip was the sole path for 1010 N and wrist_flexors contributed 31.44 cm2; here the rope is held in a neutral grip with straight elbows and the arms act as passive rigging, generating no meaningful joint torque against the load.", "Erector spinae contributes 22.82 cm2 against 96.10 cm2 in the Pendlay row, a fourfold difference at two thirds the stack weight. The mechanism is the torso angle and load direction rather than the load magnitude, and this is the main reason pull-throughs are commonly programmed as a lower-back-sparing hip extension pattern.", "The lumbar passive-support convention from the Pendlay row was applied again at 30 percent offload, and the 40 percent passive hamstring offload at deep hip flexion likewise. These two coefficients have now been used across three hip-dominant exercises and should be promoted to explicit constants in the prompt before the squat and deadlift entries, so that they are not re-derived inconsistently.", "Quadriceps were deliberately excluded. Knee travel is minimal in a correctly executed hinge and no meaningful knee extension moment is required; a version performed with substantial knee bend would become a squat-hinge hybrid and must not inherit these values.", "A banded or belt-squat-style pull-through variant must not inherit these values, since the resistance vector and its interaction with the torso angle differ."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric initiation from the bottom: hips flexed to roughly 100 degrees with the torso near 30 degrees above horizontal, rope between the legs and the cable line running back toward the low pulley, where the hip extension moment is greatest.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 203.16, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.7000, "E_LoA": 0.9500, "E_mech": 0.5215, "muscle": "gluteus_maximus", "rationale": "Prime mover for the 289 N-m hip extension moment, well lengthened at 100 degrees of hip flexion. The horizontal cable vector means the resistance profile peaks toward lockout rather than at the bottom, so the bottom position does not demand the full capacity that a barbell hinge of equivalent stack weight would.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 143.08, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 74.62}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.7000, "E_LoA": 0.8200, "E_mech": 0.4374, "muscle": "adductor_magnus", "rationale": "Major hip extensor at deep flexion with favourable length; part of its line of action is adduction rather than pure extension, and the moderate load leaves it below full utilisation.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 105.22, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 46.03}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.7000, "E_LoA": 0.7600, "E_mech": 0.3835, "muscle": "semimembranosus", "rationale": "Large contributor to hip extension and highly stretched at the bottom, so a substantial share of the resistance at this position is supplied passively rather than by active force.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 88.78, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 34.05}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.9000, "E_ND": 0.6500, "E_LoA": 0.4074, "E_mech": 0.2143, "muscle": "erector_spinae", "rationale": "Maintains the rigid neutral spine that transmits the hip moment, which is mechanically necessary load-bearing rather than posture. Demand is far below the Pendlay row because the torso stays much closer to vertical and the load acts horizontally rather than hanging vertically from the hands.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 106.48, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 22.82}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.7000, "E_LoA": 0.5583, "E_mech": 0.2814, "muscle": "biceps_femoris_long_head", "rationale": "Shares the hip extension moment with the other hamstrings under the same stretched-length conditions, with a somewhat smaller effective share than semimembranosus.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 53.70, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 15.11}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.7000, "E_LoA": 0.5952, "E_mech": 0.2813, "muscle": "semitendinosus", "rationale": "Assists hip extension with a smaller internal moment arm than the other hamstrings.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 25.59, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 7.20}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.3333, "E_mech": 0.0900, "muscle": "transverse_abdominis", "rationale": "Generates the intra-abdominal pressure that offloads part of the 112 N-m lumbar moment, allowing the spine to act as a rigid transmission link.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 21.97, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 1.98}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.5500, "E_ND": 0.4000, "E_LoA": 0.0883, "E_mech": 0.0165, "muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "Small contribution to intra-abdominal pressure and thoracolumbar fascia tension. Much lower than in the Pendlay row because the lumbar moment here is under a quarter as large and the erectors are nowhere near saturation.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 1.35}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["Representative commercial single-stack cable column with a 1:1 pulley ratio and rope attachment; 60 kg nominal yields ~294 N at the rope after friction.", "Low pulley set at floor height, athlete facing away with a hip-width stance, rope drawn between the legs.", "At the bottom position the cable line runs roughly horizontally backward from the hands, which sit between and slightly behind the thighs; effective moment arm from the hip axis to the cable line ~0.30 m, giving ~88 N-m of the hip moment from the cable.", "Body mass above the hip ~460 N acting at a horizontal distance of ~0.30 m from the hip axis at the limiting phase, contributing ~138 N-m; forward-loaded arm and cable mass adds ~63 N-m.", "The cable pulls the athlete rearward, so unlike a barbell hinge the load vector is horizontal rather than vertical and the resultant hip moment is a combination of cable tension and gravitational torso mass.", "Horizontal distance from L4/L5 to the combined load line ~0.24 m; mass above L5 ~375 N at ~0.16 m.", "Internal moment arms: hip extensors ~0.055 m, erector spinae ~0.06 m.", "Approximately 40 percent of the hip extension moment is carried by passive hamstring and posterior capsule tension at ~100 degrees of hip flexion.", "Approximately 30 percent of the lumbar moment is carried by intra-abdominal pressure and the thoracolumbar fascia system rather than by active erector force.", "The arms hang as passive rigging: the rope is held in a neutral grip with extended elbows and no elbow or shoulder moment is generated against the load, so grip and arm musculature transmit force through a straight-line skeletal path without requiring meaningful active joint torque.", "The knee travels only slightly and the quadriceps generate no meaningful extension moment; this is a hip-dominant hinge, not a squat pattern."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "hip_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 289.00}, {"joint": "lumbar_spine", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 112.00}], "nominal_load_kg": 60.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 294.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 203.16, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 203.00}
5	single_arm_cable_lateral_raise	Cable Lateral Raise	Single-Arm Cable Lateral Raise	Upper	Lateral_Delt	Isolation	Cable	24.00	87.29	{"obliques": 3.27, "brachialis": 1.55, "rotator_cuffs": 18.79, "wrist_flexors": 1.14, "deltoid_lateral": 32.65, "trapezius_lower": 4.09, "trapezius_upper": 6.19, "deltoid_anterior": 10.44, "deltoid_posterior": 3.62, "serratus_anterior": 5.55}	{"grip": "Use a neutral or slightly pronated grip that allows the wrist to remain aligned with the forearm. Grip only firmly enough to control the handle.", "tldr": {"focus": "Lead with the elbow and keep the shoulder moving away from the torso without shrugging or rotating the trunk.", "setup": "Stand side-on to a low cable with the working arm farthest from the stack and the handle starting slightly across the front of the body.", "execution": "Raise the working arm outward in the scapular plane until approximately shoulder height while keeping the elbow softly bent and the torso still.", "stop_when": "Stop when another repetition would require torso lean, shoulder shrugging, elbow flexion, or a materially shorter lateral-raise arc."}, "stance": "Use a stable shoulder-width or mildly staggered stance. The non-working hand may hold the machine for balance provided this does not create torso movement.", "bracing": "Maintain enough abdominal and lateral trunk tension to prevent leaning toward or away from the cable. Keep the ribcage stacked over the pelvis and avoid rotating the torso to increase handle travel.", "overview": "A unilateral cable lateral raise performed with the working arm on the side away from a low pulley so the cable crosses slightly in front of the body at the start. The arm is elevated primarily through glenohumeral abduction in the scapular plane while the elbow angle remains nearly fixed. The cable provides meaningful resistance from the lower portion of the movement, distinguishing it mechanically from a dumbbell lateral raise.", "eccentric": "Lower the arm under control along the same path until it returns slightly across the front of the body. Maintain cable tension, torso stability, and the established elbow angle rather than allowing the shoulder to collapse or the handle to pull the arm abruptly inward.", "concentric": "Raise the working arm outward and slightly forward in the scapular plane by abducting the shoulder. Lead with the elbow while maintaining a soft, nearly fixed elbow angle and neutral wrist. Allow natural scapular upward rotation as the arm rises, and stop before the movement requires shrugging or torso compensation.", "tempo_notes": "Avoid jerking the handle from the bottom or dropping the arm quickly into the stretched position. Maintain continuous cable tension and reverse direction smoothly.", "end_position": "Finish the concentric phase with the upper arm approximately at shoulder height or at the highest controlled position that can be reached without pronounced shrugging, trunk lean, or altered elbow mechanics.", "safety_notes": ["Ensure the handle and cable attachment are securely connected before beginning.", "Do not force the arm above a range that produces painful shoulder compression or instability.", "Stand far enough from the weight stack that the cable does not contact the body or machine frame during the repetition."], "external_cues": "Sweep the handle outward in a smooth arc and keep your body from following the cable.", "internal_cues": "Lead with the elbow; keep the shoulder away from the ear; keep the torso stacked and still.", "common_mistakes": ["Leaning the torso away from the cable to reduce the effective difficulty.", "Shrugging the shoulder aggressively as the arm approaches shoulder height.", "Bending the elbow progressively and turning the movement into a partial upright-row pattern.", "Rotating the torso to create additional handle travel.", "Raising the arm directly sideways in a rigid frontal plane despite shoulder discomfort instead of allowing a slight scapular-plane path.", "Allowing the cable to pull the arm rapidly across the body during the eccentric."], "equipment_setup": "Attach a single handle to a low pulley. Stand far enough from the stack that cable tension is present at the bottom and the cable can pass across the front of the body without contacting the legs.", "range_of_motion": "Begin with the arm slightly adducted across the front of the body and raise through the largest comfortable scapular-plane abduction range that preserves shoulder control. Shoulder height is a practical reference rather than a mandatory limit; slightly lower or higher endpoints are acceptable if the movement remains pain-free and mechanically consistent.", "rir_0_definition": "RIR 0 is reached when no additional repetition can be completed through the defined range with stable torso and shoulder mechanics, even if the handle could still be raised by leaning, shrugging, bending the elbow, rotating the trunk, or shortening the range.", "rir_1_indicators": "Approximately one technically valid repetition may remain when arm-elevation velocity slows substantially and the final portion becomes difficult, but the lifter can still maintain a stable torso, near-constant elbow angle, controlled shoulder position, and the established range.", "individualization": "The degree of forward arm angle, stance, torso distance from the pulley, elbow bend, and top range may vary with shoulder anatomy, humeral length, mobility, and cable geometry. These variations are acceptable provided the movement remains predominantly shoulder abduction in the scapular plane, the elbow angle stays relatively constant, and the torso does not contribute meaningful momentum.", "plane_of_movement": "Primarily frontal-to-scapular-plane glenohumeral abduction, with a small sagittal-plane flexion component depending on the arm path.", "starting_position": "Stand side-on to the cable stack with the working arm farthest from the pulley. Hold the handle with the arm resting slightly across the front of the pelvis or thigh, the elbow softly bent, the shoulder relaxed, and the torso upright. Keep the pelvis level and the non-working side stable.", "technical_failure": "Technical failure occurs when another repetition cannot be completed through the established lateral-raise range without materially changing the mechanics. Observable breakdowns include leaning the torso, rotating away from the cable, shrugging the shoulder excessively, flexing the elbow substantially, using momentum, or shortening the top range markedly.", "primary_joint_actions": "During the concentric phase, the glenohumeral joint abducts in the scapular plane while the scapula upwardly rotates and posteriorly tilts. The elbow remains slightly flexed and approximately fixed, while the trunk resists lateral flexion and rotation."}	{}	{https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lMJUXEvcMkQ,https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=23VoAvqwSzo,https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9ilIKuy6B0g,https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=g49CPRU4br0}	Unilateral	{"slug": "single_arm_cable_lateral_raise", "notes": ["Deltoid_lateral reaches E_mech 0.7142, its highest value in the library by a factor of nearly five over the next exercise. Across the six presses it ranged from 0.0589 to 0.1499, always as a near-orthogonal contributor to a horizontal or forward push. This is the only exercise here that loads frontal-plane abduction directly, and the contrast is the clearest demonstration in the library that a large muscle contributes almost nothing when the task moment lies outside its line of action, regardless of load.", "Rotator_cuffs at 18.79 cm2 and E_mech 0.1100 is the highest cuff engagement in the library, above side_facing_reverse_pec_deck at 0.0861 and cable_face_pull at 0.0532. The reason differs from both: here supraspinatus is a true prime mover producing abduction directly, rather than infraspinatus and teres minor producing external rotation. This is the third distinct agonist role the aggregate slug has been asked to represent, and the case for splitting it into supraspinatus, external rotators, and subscapularis is now stronger than the case for any other schema change.", "The scapular upward-rotation couple appears here as genuine task torque for the first time in the library. Trapezius_upper, trapezius_lower and serratus_anterior together contribute 15.83 cm2, acting as a force couple where the upper and lower fibres oppose each other vertically to produce rotation rather than translation. In every previous exercise these muscles appeared either as retractors or as anchoring stabilisers. Any future overhead press or front raise should apply this treatment.", "Total demand of 87.29 cm2 from only 235 N of cable force reflects the 0.27 m external moment arm of a straight-arm raise. This is now the fifth straight-arm single-joint cable or machine movement to land between 85 and 110 cm2, alongside the straight-arm pulldown, the low-to-high fly, the reverse pec deck and the Bayesian curl. The band is consistent enough to serve as a plausibility check on future entries of this type.", "The bilateral convention was applied straightforwardly, as with the Bayesian curl. Lateral trunk flexion resistance is incidental here rather than central, so mirroring the load cancels nothing essential, unlike the Pallof press and side-facing reverse pec deck. This is the fourth data point for the proposed laterality flag and confirms that the distinction is between exercises where asymmetric loading is the point and exercises where it is a side effect.", "The limiting phase was placed at 80 to 90 degrees of abduction where the arm is horizontal. Above that point the deltoid shortens and the cable angle changes, and below it the moment arm is shorter; a dumbbell version would have a sharper peak at the same point but a much shorter loaded range, since dumbbell resistance falls to near zero at the bottom.", "A dumbbell lateral raise must not inherit these values. The moment arm collapses toward the bottom of the range, so the same nominal load produces a substantially lower average demand and the exercise typically permits a heavier top-end weight for the same rep count.", "A leaning cable lateral raise must not inherit these values either. Leaning away from the stack lengthens the effective moment arm at the bottom and shifts the peak demand earlier in the range, which changes both the limiting phase and the oblique contribution.", "A machine lateral raise with pads against the upper arm must not inherit these values under any circumstances. Pad contact at the upper arm rather than the hand roughly halves the external moment arm and removes the elbow and grip contributions entirely."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric mid-range at approximately 80 to 90 degrees of humeral abduction, where the near-straight arm is horizontal and the external moment arm from the glenohumeral axis to the cable line is maximal.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 87.29, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.9000, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.9800, "E_mech": 0.7142, "muscle": "deltoid_lateral", "rationale": "Prime mover with near-perfect task alignment: pure humeral abduction in the frontal plane is its primary and near-exclusive function. By far its highest engagement in the library, against 0.1499 in the flat bench press and 0.0589 in the plate-loaded chest press where it was largely orthogonal to the required moment.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 45.73, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 32.65}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6500, "E_ND": 0.7500, "E_LoA": 0.2655, "E_mech": 0.1100, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Included as a genuine contributor rather than purely a stabiliser: supraspinatus produces abduction directly and is a true agonist here, while the remaining cuff muscles depress and centre the humeral head so that deltoid force produces rotation rather than superior translation. Highest cuff engagement in the library, and the only exercise where supraspinatus is a prime mover.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 18.79}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.8000, "E_LoA": 0.5950, "E_mech": 0.3427, "muscle": "deltoid_anterior", "rationale": "Contributes a real abduction component from its lateral fibres, though its flexion vector is only partly aligned with the frontal-plane path the exercise requires.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 30.46, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 10.44}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.7500, "E_LoA": 0.5920, "E_mech": 0.3197, "muscle": "trapezius_upper", "rationale": "Genuine task torque: scapular upward rotation is required to elevate the humerus to horizontal, and the upper trapezius supplies the superior component of the force couple that produces it.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 19.36, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 6.19}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.7000, "E_LoA": 0.3260, "E_mech": 0.1540, "muscle": "serratus_anterior", "rationale": "Genuine contributor to the upward-rotation force couple alongside the trapezius, though its dominant protraction vector is only partly relevant to the required scapular motion.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 36.03, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 5.55}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.7500, "E_LoA": 0.5060, "E_mech": 0.2732, "muscle": "trapezius_lower", "rationale": "Supplies the inferior component of the scapular upward-rotation couple, opposing the elevation tendency of the upper trapezius so the scapula rotates rather than shrugs.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 14.98, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 4.09}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.7000, "E_LoA": 0.3110, "E_mech": 0.1469, "muscle": "deltoid_posterior", "rationale": "Its posterior fibres contribute a modest abduction component at shoulder height, though its horizontal abduction vector is largely outside the frontal-plane path required.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.65, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 3.62}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.1570, "E_mech": 0.0400, "muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary load-transmitting element: the cable pulls the torso laterally toward the stack, and active lateral flexion torque is required to hold the trunk upright so the shoulder has a stable base.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 3.27}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.4500, "E_ND": 0.4500, "E_LoA": 0.2050, "E_mech": 0.0353, "muscle": "brachialis", "rationale": "Included as a load-transmitting element holding the fixed near-straight elbow, with poor leverage at 170 degrees and a very small elbow moment relative to the shoulder demand.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 1.55}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.4000, "E_LoA": 0.0770, "E_mech": 0.0209, "muscle": "wrist_flexors", "rationale": "Included as a minor load-transmitting element: grip force is required for the 235 N to reach the arm, but the handle sits across the palm and the load is the lightest of any gripped exercise in the library.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 1.14}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["Bilateral convention applied as stated: 24 kg total is interpreted as 12 kg per arm, following the single_arm_dumbbell_preacher_curl precedent.", "Representative commercial single-stack cable column with a 1:1 pulley ratio and D-handle; 12 kg per side yields ~118 N per hand after friction, ~235 N total.", "Low pulley set at or near floor height, athlete standing side-on with the working arm across the body at the start, cable running under the torso to the opposite-side stack.", "Arm held near-straight at a fixed ~170 degree elbow; effective external moment arm from the glenohumeral axis to the cable line ~0.27 m at the horizontal limiting phase.", "The low cable angle keeps tension roughly constant through the range, unlike a dumbbell where the moment arm collapses toward the bottom, so the loaded portion of the range is substantially longer.", "Deltoid lateral internal abduction moment arm ~0.022 m at 80 to 90 degrees of abduction, which is close to its maximum for this joint action.", "Scapular upward rotation is genuine task torque: elevating the humerus to horizontal requires the scapula to rotate upward against the load, not merely to be held in place.", "The cable line runs close to the forearm axis rather than perpendicular to it, so the elbow moment is small and the elbow flexors act primarily as a rigid link.", "The athlete braces against the lateral pull with a modest counterlean transmitted through the legs to the floor; lower-limb musculature is postural and excluded, but a small oblique contribution resists lateral trunk flexion.", "24 kg total is a realistic 8-12 rep working load: the long straight-arm moment arm converts modest cable force into a demanding shoulder moment, which is why lateral raises are performed with light loads relative to other shoulder work."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "glenohumeral_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 63.50}, {"joint": "scapulothoracic_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 28.00}, {"joint": "elbow_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 4.20}], "nominal_load_kg": 24.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 235.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 87.29, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 87.00}
37	straight_arm_cable_pulldown	Straight-Arm Pulldown	Standing Straight-Arm Cable Pulldown	Upper	Back_V	Isolation	Cable	40.00	105.31	{"obliques": 1.61, "rhomboids": 4.72, "teres_major": 3.53, "rotator_cuffs": 9.60, "wrist_flexors": 3.81, "trapezius_lower": 5.72, "latissimus_dorsi": 33.03, "rectus_abdominis": 6.13, "deltoid_posterior": 13.55, "triceps_long_head": 15.30, "pectoralis_major_sternal": 8.31}	{"grip": "Pronated grip on a straight or lat bar, or neutral grip on a rope, hands positioned shoulder-width or slightly wider.", "tldr": {"focus": "Move only through the shoulder joint, keeping the elbows fixed near full extension instead of bending to row the bar down.", "setup": "Stand facing a high cable pulley with a straight or lat bar attachment, arms extended overhead and torso hinged slightly forward.", "execution": "Pull the bar down in an arc toward the thighs by extending the shoulders, keeping the elbows nearly straight throughout.", "stop_when": "The bar can no longer be pulled down to the thighs without the elbows bending substantially or the torso using momentum."}, "stance": "Feet shoulder-width apart, positioned to allow the arms to sweep through their arc without the body needing to shift.", "bracing": "Torso braced in a fixed, slight forward hinge established at the start of the set, avoiding progressive additional forward lean or using body momentum to assist the pull.", "overview": "This is the standing straight-arm cable pulldown, an isolation exercise for the latissimus dorsi performed with the elbows held nearly extended throughout, so that the pulling motion occurs almost entirely through glenohumeral extension rather than elbow flexion. This distinguishes it from a lat pulldown or pulling row variant, where elbow flexion is a primary contributor to the movement; here the arms function as a fixed lever pivoting from the shoulder.", "eccentric": "The bar returns under control back overhead through glenohumeral flexion, with the elbows maintaining their fixed, near-extended angle as the arms rise.", "concentric": "The bar or handle is pulled down through an arc from overhead to the front of the thighs via glenohumeral extension, with the elbows remaining nearly straight and fixed throughout the pull.", "tempo_notes": "Maintain continuous cable tension throughout without allowing the weight stack to rest between repetitions; avoid using torso momentum or a jerking motion to initiate the pull.", "end_position": "Arms extended down in front of the thighs with the shoulders in extension and the elbows still nearly straight.", "safety_notes": ["Avoid locking the elbows rigidly to the point of joint hyperextension; a slight, stable bend should be maintained throughout.", "Keep the torso hinge fixed rather than increasing forward lean under heavier loads."], "external_cues": "Sweep the bar down toward the thighs in an arc; keep the elbows fixed and nearly straight.", "internal_cues": "Pull with the upper arms as if pushing the arms down against a wall behind the hips; keep the elbows locked in place throughout.", "common_mistakes": ["Bending the elbows substantially, converting the movement into a pulldown-style row.", "Using torso momentum or an increasing forward lean to assist the pull.", "Shrugging the shoulders upward instead of driving through shoulder extension.", "Stopping the arc well before the thighs, shortening the usable range.", "Allowing the arms to fly upward rapidly during the eccentric phase instead of controlling the return."], "equipment_setup": "Cable pulley set to a high position with a straight bar, lat bar, or rope attachment.", "range_of_motion": "From the arms extended overhead at the top to the arms extended down at the thighs at the bottom, limited by the individual's shoulder extension mobility and the point at which the elbows would need to bend to continue the pull.", "rir_0_definition": "Inability to pull the bar down to the thighs through glenohumeral extension while keeping the elbows nearly straight, as distinct from continuing to move the bar by bending the elbows or using torso momentum.", "rir_1_indicators": "Reduced range of the downward arc, slower pulling speed, and a growing tendency for the elbows to bend, while the general shoulder-driven pattern remains intact.", "individualization": "The exact degree of forward torso hinge and elbow bend may vary slightly with shoulder mobility and comfort, provided the hinge angle and elbow position remain fixed throughout the set rather than changing to assist the pull.", "plane_of_movement": "Primarily sagittal-plane glenohumeral extension, moving through an arc from overhead to the thighs.", "starting_position": "Standing facing the pulley with feet shoulder-width apart, torso hinged slightly forward at the hips, arms extended overhead gripping the attachment, elbows with only a slight, fixed bend.", "technical_failure": "The point at which the bar can no longer be pulled down to the thighs without the elbows bending substantially to convert the movement into a pulldown-style row, or the torso uses additional forward lean or momentum to assist the pull.", "primary_joint_actions": "Glenohumeral extension as the arms sweep down from overhead to the thighs; the elbows remain in a fixed, near-extended position throughout, with only minor incidental flexion; scapular depression occurs as the arms pass the torso; the hips and spine remain fixed in a slight forward hinge established at the start."}	{}	{https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=G9uNaXGTJ4w,https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hAMcfubonDc,https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1_E77qhMpkE}	Bilateral	{"slug": "straight_arm_cable_pulldown", "notes": ["This is the only single-joint shoulder extension exercise in the library and it isolates the lat contribution more cleanly than any compound pull. Latissimus reaches 33.03 cm2, within 7 percent of the weighted pull-up at 35.83, but total demand is 105.31 against 245.71. The lat share rises from 15 percent of total to 31 percent, which is the mechanical justification for programming straight-arm pulldowns as a lat isolation movement.", "The elbow flexors are almost entirely absent, which is the defining structural difference from every other pull in the library. Brachialis contributes 27 to 34 cm2 in the compound pulls and zero here, because the fixed near-straight elbow means the cable line runs roughly along the forearm axis rather than perpendicular to it. This is also why the exercise permits only 40 kg despite loading the same prime mover as an 80 kg seated row.", "Triceps_long_head appears as a shoulder extensor rather than an elbow extensor, its first such appearance in the library. In the overhead triceps extension it contributed 37.94 cm2 as an elbow extensor with the shoulder held static; here the roles invert. The muscle is credited on E_LoA 0.8800 for humeral extension specifically, and this dual-function handling is worth noting as a pattern any future straight-arm movement should follow.", "Teres_major reaches E_mech 0.7846, its highest value anywhere, because pure humeral extension is precisely its function with no competing demand. Its 4.50 cm2 maximum FCSA keeps the absolute contribution small, but the engagement factor is a clean signal that the model is reading joint action correctly rather than defaulting to load-proportional allocation.", "Pectoralis_major_sternal at 8.31 cm2 is its largest contribution in any pulling exercise, more than double the pull-up value. From full overhead elevation the sternal fibres genuinely extend the humerus toward the torso, and the straight-arm lever amplifies that contribution through the upper range. It would be excluded outright in any row where the humerus never elevates above shoulder height.", "Total demand of 105.31 cm2 places this almost exactly alongside the low-to-high cable fly at 105.44, and the parallel is structural rather than coincidental. Both are standing single-joint cable movements with a near-straight arm, a very long external moment arm, and a modest stack load. The pattern is now consistent enough that any straight-arm cable exercise should be expected to land in the 95 to 115 cm2 range at typical working loads.", "Wrist_flexors at 3.81 cm2 is the lowest grip contribution among all exercises where grip was included at all, against 34.03 in the weighted pull-up. The cable pulls along the arm axis rather than trying to open the hand, so grip is a minor transmission requirement rather than a bottleneck. Straps would change nothing material here.", "A bench-supported or kneeling straight-arm pulldown must not inherit these values. Removing the standing counterbalance requirement would eliminate the rectus abdominis and oblique contribution of 7.74 cm2 and permit a modestly heavier load.", "A straight-arm pullover on a bench or with a dumbbell must not inherit these values either. Gravity acting vertically produces a resistance curve that peaks at a different point in the range than a cable pulling from a fixed high anchor.", "A bent-arm variant performed with 90 degrees of elbow flexion must not inherit these values under any circumstances. That version halves the external moment arm, permits roughly double the load, and reintroduces the elbow flexors as major contributors, effectively converting it into a different exercise."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric initiation from the overhead stretched position: humerus flexed to roughly 150 degrees with the arms near-straight and reaching forward and up, before the arms sweep down to the thighs and the external moment arm collapses toward zero.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 105.31, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 1.0000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.9840, "E_mech": 0.7532, "muscle": "latissimus_dorsi", "rationale": "Prime mover and the defining muscle of this exercise: maximally lengthened at the overhead start and acting in pure shoulder extension, its primary function, with no competing elbow demand to divert the movement. Slightly below the pull-up value because the single-joint pattern limits how much total force the shoulder can express without elbow assistance.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.85, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 33.03}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.7000, "E_LoA": 0.8800, "E_mech": 0.3513, "muscle": "triceps_long_head", "rationale": "Included as a genuine contributor rather than a stabiliser: it crosses the shoulder and produces humeral extension directly, which is the required joint action, while simultaneously holding the elbow near-extended. Stretched at the overhead start, though its shoulder moment arm is well below the latissimus.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.55, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 15.30}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.8510, "E_mech": 0.5497, "muscle": "deltoid_posterior", "rationale": "Strong contributor to sagittal-plane shoulder extension, well lengthened overhead. Alignment is high but not perfect because part of its action is horizontal abduction, which the narrow sagittal arm path does not require.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.65, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 13.55}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.4000, "E_LoA": 0.2755, "E_mech": 0.0562, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary stabiliser: the very long straight-arm lever places the humeral head under a large distracting moment at full elevation, requiring substantial cuff compression to transmit load into the scapula. Teres minor and infraspinatus add a small extension component.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 9.60}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.7000, "E_LoA": 0.3115, "E_mech": 0.1554, "muscle": "pectoralis_major_sternal", "rationale": "Genuine agonist here rather than an incidental contributor: from full overhead elevation its fibres produce humeral extension toward the torso through the upper portion of the range, though the action fades as the arm passes the vertical.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 53.48, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 8.31}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.5500, "E_LoA": 0.6000, "E_mech": 0.2525, "muscle": "rectus_abdominis", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary load-transmitting element: the high cable pulls the torso upward and into extension, and active trunk flexion torque is required to hold the hinged position that the shoulder moment is generated from.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.29, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 6.13}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.8000, "E_LoA": 0.6280, "E_mech": 0.3818, "muscle": "trapezius_lower", "rationale": "Contributes scapular depression as the arms sweep down from the elevated overhead start, though the scapulothoracic moment is small relative to the glenohumeral demand in a straight-arm movement.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 14.98, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 5.72}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.7500, "E_LoA": 0.3095, "E_mech": 0.1671, "muscle": "rhomboids", "rationale": "Assists downward rotation of the scapula from the elevated position, but its retraction vector is largely irrelevant to a sagittal-plane arm sweep.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 28.25, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 4.72}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.2060, "E_mech": 0.0700, "muscle": "wrist_flexors", "rationale": "Included as a load-transmitting element, but at only 196 N total and with the cable line running roughly along the arm axis rather than pulling the bar out of the hands, grip demand is a small fraction of what compound pulls require.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 3.81}, {"E_LT": 1.0000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.7846, "muscle": "teres_major", "rationale": "Acts in parallel with latissimus dorsi on pure humeral extension, its exact function, and is maximally lengthened at the overhead start. Highest E_mech it receives in the library.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 4.50, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 3.53}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.4500, "E_LoA": 0.0860, "E_mech": 0.0197, "muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "Small contribution to trunk rigidity against the upward cable pull, assisting rectus abdominis in maintaining the hinged position.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 1.61}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["Representative commercial single-stack cable column with a 1:1 pulley ratio and straight bar or rope attachment; 40 kg nominal yields ~196 N at the handle after friction, ~98 N per hand.", "High pulley set above head height, athlete standing at arm's length with a slight forward hip hinge of ~20 degrees and a fixed near-straight elbow of ~165 degrees.", "Single-joint shoulder movement: the near-straight arm gives a very large external moment arm, ~0.55 m from the glenohumeral axis to the cable line at the overhead limiting phase.", "The required humeral action is pure shoulder extension in the sagittal plane, sweeping the straight arm from overhead down to the thigh.", "Latissimus shoulder extension internal moment arm ~0.058 m at the overhead position.", "The cable force acts roughly along the forearm long axis rather than perpendicular to it, so the elbow moment is small and the elbow flexors are not meaningfully loaded; the triceps hold the elbow near-extended against a modest flexion tendency.", "The exercise is a genuine single-joint isolation of shoulder extension, which is unique among the pulling movements in the library and the reason the elbow flexors are almost entirely absent.", "Standing unsupported with a forward lean against a high cable: a real lumbar extension moment is required to hold the hinged torso position against the upward and forward pull.", "The athlete counterbalances the cable by leaning back and bracing through the legs; the reaction transmits through the skeleton to the floor, so lower-limb musculature is postural and excluded.", "40 kg is a realistic 8-12 rep working load: the very long straight-arm moment arm converts modest cable force into a demanding shoulder moment, so the stack number substantially overstates the achievable load relative to compound pulls."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "glenohumeral_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 108.00}, {"joint": "scapulothoracic_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 24.00}, {"joint": "lumbar_spine", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 41.00}, {"joint": "elbow_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 9.80}], "nominal_load_kg": 40.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 196.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 105.31, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 105.00}
