height, slower concentric speed, and a growing tendency for the upper arm to lift slightly off the pad, while the general path remains intact.", "individualization": "Pad height and torso position relative to the bench may vary moderately with limb length and shoulder comfort, provided the upper arm remains against the pad throughout the curl and the forearm stays in a neutral rotation.", "plane_of_movement": "Sagittal-plane elbow flexion and extension, with the shoulder fixed in a supported flexed position throughout.", "starting_position": "Seated or standing at the preacher bench with the working upper arm flat against the pad, elbow extended, dumbbell held with a neutral grip, opposite hand may brace on the bench or opposite knee for stability.", "technical_failure": "The point at which the dumbbell can no longer be curled without the upper arm lifting off the pad or the shoulder elevating to assist the lift.", "primary_joint_actions": "Elbow flexion driven primarily by the brachialis and brachioradialis, with the biceps brachii contributing in its non-supinated, neutral-forearm role; the glenohumeral joint remains fixed against the pad rather than actively moving; the forearm stays in a neutral rotation throughout, without supinating."}	{}	{https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oHbSWPo3swM}	Unilateral	{"slug": "preacher_dumbbell_hammer_curl", "notes": ["This exercise produces the sharpest brachialis to biceps divergence in the library, at a 2.27 ratio against 1.11 in the incline dumbbell curl. Two independent factors stack against the biceps: the neutral grip removes its supination leverage and the preacher pad holds the shoulder in flexion, shortening the long head. The incline curl is the exact mechanical opposite on the second factor, which makes the pair a clean demonstration that shoulder position, not just grip, determines biceps allocation.", "Brachioradialis reaches E_mech 0.6413, its highest value anywhere, exceeding the neutral-grip pulldown at 0.6543 only marginally and the supinated incline curl at 0.6148 more clearly. The neutral grip is its optimal orientation and the model reproduces the common observation that hammer curls emphasise it without any special-casing.", "The limiting phase was placed at the stretched bottom rather than the mid-range, which departs from the incline curl where 90 degrees was limiting. Preacher geometry is the reason: the 45 degree pad puts the forearm near horizontal at full extension, so the maximal external moment arm coincides with the worst internal leverage. This is the mechanical basis for preacher curls being perceived as hardest at the bottom, and it is why E_MA sits at 0.70 for brachialis here against 0.95 in the incline curl.", "Total demand of 47.31 cm2 against 76.66 for the incline dumbbell curl comes from a 43 percent lighter load, which is itself the mechanically correct consequence of loading the weakest point of the strength curve with no momentum available. The prompt's own preacher calibration example uses 28 kg; at that load this variant would demand roughly 82 cm2, which suggests 28 kg is closer to a supinated preacher curl than a hammer preacher curl for the reference athlete.", "The result sits within the prompt's stated preacher curl bands for brachialis at 0.5818 against 0.65 to 0.90, marginally below, and above the stated brachioradialis band of 0.15 to 0.45. Both deviations are attributable to the hammer grip rather than to the preacher position, since those bands were specified for a supinated dumbbell preacher curl.", "Deltoid_anterior was deliberately excluded, in contrast to the incline dumbbell curl where it contributed 2.69 cm2 holding the humerus against 11 N-m of extension moment. The preacher pad absorbs the entire shoulder moment mechanically, so no active shoulder torque is required at all.", "Wrist_flexors were deliberately excluded. At 159 N total the grip demand is trivial and the dumbbell handle sits in the palm rather than being suspended from a hook grip, so grip is not a meaningful transmission bottleneck.", "A supinated preacher curl at the same load must not inherit these values. Supination would roughly double the biceps allocation, cut brachioradialis by about a third, and raise pronators_supinators demand.", "An EZ-bar or barbell preacher curl must not inherit these values either. A symmetric implement removes the rotational moment that admits pronators_supinators here, and the fixed bar prevents independent forearm positioning."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric initiation from the deeply extended bottom: elbow near 160 degrees of extension with the upper arm fixed against the preacher pad, where the pad angle places the forearm close to horizontal and the external moment arm is greatest.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 47.31, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9800, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.9420, "E_mech": 0.5818, "muscle": "brachialis", "rationale": "Dominant flexor in this variant: maximally lengthened at the extended bottom and entirely unaffected by the neutral forearm position that penalises the biceps. Moment arm is poor at 160 degrees of extension, which is the binding constraint rather than any alignment issue.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 25.63}, {"E_LT": 0.8000, "E_MA": 0.6500, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.6400, "E_mech": 0.2830, "muscle": "biceps_brachii", "rationale": "Doubly penalised: the neutral grip removes its supination advantage and reduces its effective flexion leverage, while the preacher pad holds the shoulder in flexion, shortening the long head across that joint. This is its weakest position of any elbow-flexion exercise in the library.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 11.27}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.6413, "muscle": "brachioradialis", "rationale": "Highest engagement it receives anywhere in the library: the neutral hammer grip is its optimal forearm orientation and it is well lengthened at the extended bottom, partly offsetting the poor moment arm at that joint angle.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 11.22, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 7.55}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6500, "E_ND": 0.4000, "E_LoA": 0.2125, "E_mech": 0.0470, "muscle": "pronators_supinators", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary load-transmitting element: the offset dumbbell mass applies a rotational moment that must be actively resisted to hold the neutral position the exercise geometry depends on. Demand is roughly half that of a supinated dumbbell curl because mid-position is the forearm's mechanically neutral resting orientation.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 60.88, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 2.86}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["Bilateral convention: 16 kg total is interpreted as 8 kg per arm.", "Dumbbell weight 157 N plus ~1.5 percent for controlled acceleration: ~159 N effective, ~80 N per limb.", "Standard preacher bench with the pad inclined at ~45 degrees; the upper arm is fully supported and immobilised against the pad.", "Forearm length from elbow axis to dumbbell centre ~0.155 m; at the extended bottom position the forearm approaches horizontal and close to the full length acts as the external moment arm, giving ~0.150 m effective.", "Elbow flexor internal moment arm ~0.028 m at 160 degrees of extension, substantially reduced from the ~0.042 m available at 90 degrees.", "Neutral hammer grip held throughout: the forearm is in mid-position, which is the optimal orientation for brachioradialis and removes any supination demand from biceps_brachii.", "The preacher pad immobilises the humerus and absorbs all shoulder moment, so no glenohumeral or scapular torque is required and no deltoid contribution is needed.", "The bench and pad support the torso and upper arm fully, so no trunk musculature generates net task torque and no cheat-swing contribution is available.", "The limiting phase is the stretched bottom rather than the mid-range, because the preacher geometry combines a near-maximal external moment arm with the worst internal leverage of the range.", "16 kg total is a realistic 8-12 rep working load: the preacher position eliminates momentum and loads the weakest portion of the strength curve, so it permits substantially less weight than a standing or incline curl."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "elbow_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 23.90}], "nominal_load_kg": 16.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 159.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 47.31, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 47.30}
