mall range and joint involved; avoid using forearm or elbow movement to add momentum.", "end_position": "Wrists fully flexed with the dumbbells raised as high as the wrist flexors allow, forearms still resting on the support.", "safety_notes": ["Use a moderate load given the small muscle mass and joint structures involved in wrist flexion.", "Avoid forcing the wrist into an uncomfortable end-range position at either end of the movement."], "external_cues": "Curl the dumbbells up toward the forearms; keep the forearms pressed flat against the thighs or bench.", "internal_cues": "Curl the dumbbells using only the wrists; think of closing the hand and rolling the wrist upward without moving the forearm.", "common_mistakes": ["Lifting the forearms off the support to assist the curl.", "Using a jerking motion instead of controlled wrist flexion.", "Shortening the range by not allowing the wrist to extend fully at the bottom.", "Gripping too tightly throughout, limiting the ability of the dumbbell to roll into the fingers at the bottom of the range."], "equipment_setup": "Flat bench or the lifter's own thighs used to support the forearms in a supinated position, with the wrists positioned just past the edge of the support to allow full flexion and extension.", "range_of_motion": "From full wrist extension at the bottom, with the dumbbells allowed to roll slightly into the fingers, to full wrist flexion at the top, limited by the individual's wrist mobility.", "rir_0_definition": "Inability to curl the dumbbells through wrist flexion while keeping the forearms supported and still, as distinct from continuing to move the dumbbells by lifting the forearms or using momentum.", "rir_1_indicators": "Reduced height of the wrist curl, slower concentric speed, and increasing grip fatigue, while the forearms remain supported and stationary.", "individualization": "The exact wrist position at the bottom of the range and the degree to which the dumbbell rolls into the fingers may vary with individual wrist mobility and hand size, provided the forearms remain supported and stationary throughout the set.", "plane_of_movement": "Sagittal-plane wrist flexion and extension.", "starting_position": "Seated with forearms resting on the thighs or bench, palms facing up, wrists extended slightly off the edge of the support, dumbbells held with the fingers curled around the handles, wrists in a neutral-to-extended position.", "technical_failure": "The point at which the dumbbells can no longer be curled through wrist flexion without the forearms lifting off the support or the grip loosening substantially to compensate.", "primary_joint_actions": "Flexion of the wrist driven by the wrist flexor group; the elbow and forearm remain fixed and supported throughout, with no meaningful elbow or shoulder joint action occurring during the repetition."}	{}	{https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3VLTzIrnb5g,https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2wPpcJBe03o}	Unilateral	{"slug": "seated_dumbbell_wrist_curl", "notes": ["The wrist-flexor aggregate approaches its modeled maximum capacity; E_mech is therefore near the upper limit rather than representing moderate utilization.", "The estimated contribution generates approximately 15.3 Nm of bilateral wrist-flexion torque using a representative internal wrist-flexor moment arm near 0.0121 m.", "The supplied 24 kg bilateral load is at the high end of a plausible strict 8-12 repetition working load for the reference athlete and requires full forearm support and controlled technique.", "Wrist extensors and pronators-supinators are excluded because any co-contraction or rotational stabilization does not produce a meaningful share of the required net wrist-flexion moment."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric initiation from moderate wrist extension, with the forearms fully supported on the thighs or bench and the dumbbells held near the distal fingers before the external wrist moment arm shortens.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 5