25	plate_loaded_seated_calf_raise	Seated Calf Raise	Plate-Loaded Seated Calf Raise	Lower	Calves	Isolation	Plate_Loaded_Machine	80.00	162.70	{"soleus": 155.82, "gastrocnemius": 6.88}	{"grip": "Use the machine handles or pad supports as needed to stabilize the torso. The hands should not assist the movement by lifting the thigh pads.", "tldr": {"focus": "Move through the ankles while keeping the knees, thighs, and forefoot contact points fixed.", "setup": "Sit with the knee pads secured just above the knees, the balls of the feet supported on the platform, and the heels free to move vertically.", "execution": "Plantarflex the ankles to raise the heels as high as controlled, then lower into dorsiflexion without bouncing or changing knee position.", "stop_when": "Stop when another repetition would require bouncing, shortening the ankle range, shifting the feet, or lifting the thighs into the pads."}, "stance": "Place the feet approximately parallel and at a comfortable width that allows the ankles to track naturally through plantarflexion and dorsiflexion. Maintain consistent forefoot contact throughout the repetition.", "bracing": "Keep the pelvis and trunk stable against the seat and maintain enough tension through the legs to prevent the knees or thighs from shifting under the pads. Avoid using hip movement or upper-body force to unload the ankle musculature.", "overview": "A plate-loaded seated calf raise performed with the knees flexed and the forefeet supported on a platform while the thighs are restrained by pads. The defining movement is loaded ankle plantarflexion and dorsiflexion with minimal movement at the knees or hips. The flexed-knee position reduces the mechanical contribution of the gastrocnemius relative to a straight-knee calf raise and makes the movement mechanically distinct from standing variants.", "eccentric": "Lower the heels under control by allowing the ankles to dorsiflex while maintaining forefoot contact and stable knee position. Descend to