taining pressure through the metatarsal heads."], "equipment_setup": "Adjust the seat or thigh-pad height so the pads contact the distal thighs just above the knees and can be secured firmly without pressing directly onto the patellae. Position the forefeet on the platform so the metatarsal heads are supported while the heels remain free to descend below the platform level if the machine permits.", "range_of_motion": "Use the largest controlled ankle range available with secure forefoot contact and stable knees. Lower into dorsiflexion until a comfortable calf and Achilles stretch is reached or machine geometry limits further descent, then rise into maximal controlled plantarflexion. Do not force additional range through painful ankle or Achilles loading.", "rir_0_definition": "RIR 0 is reached when no additional repetition can be completed through the defined ankle range with stable forefoot contact and knee position, even if the lever could still be moved by bouncing, shortening the range, shifting the feet, or assisting through the thighs or torso.", "rir_1_indicators": "Approximately one technically valid repetition may remain when heel-rise velocity slows markedly and the top position becomes difficult to reach, but the lifter can still preserve stable knees, full forefoot contact, the established ankle range, and controlled reversal at the bottom.", "individualization": "Foot width, degree of toe-out, forefoot position, and usable dorsiflexion depth may vary with ankle structure, foot anatomy, mobility, and machine geometry. Mild variation is acceptable provided the metatarsal heads remain securely supported, the knees stay stable, and the movement remains predominantly ankle plantarflexion and dorsiflexion rather than foot rolling or leg movement.", "plane_of_movement": "Primarily sagittal-plane ankle plantarflexion and dorsiflexion.", "starting_position": "Sit upright with the pelvis supported, knees flexed according to the machine geometry, and the thighs secured beneath the pads. Place the balls of both feet firmly on the platform with the heels unsupported. Begin from a controlled dorsiflexed ankle position without allowing the feet to roll markedly inward or outward.", "technical_failure": "Technical failure occurs when another repetition cannot be completed through the established ankle range without materially changing the mechanics. Observable breakdowns include bouncing out of the bottom, markedly shortening dorsiflexion or plantarflexion, shifting the forefeet on the platform, allowing the ankles to collapse inward or outward, or lifting the thighs against the pads to assist the movement.", "primary_joint_actions": "During the concentric phase, the talocrural joints plantarflex while the knees remain flexed and essentially stationary. The forefeet remain supported on the platform, and the hips and trunk function mainly as stabilizers."}	{}	{https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2Q-HQ3mnePg}	Bilateral	{"slug": "plate_loaded_seated_calf_raise", "notes": ["The contribution vector corresponds to approximately 203 Nm of bilateral plantar-flexion capacity using a representative capacity-weighted internal Achilles-tendon moment arm near 0.050 m.", "The gastrocnemius is retained only as a small contributor because it remains anatomically capable of plantar flexion despite severe shortening across the flexed knee.", "Tibialis anterior is excluded because its dorsiflexion action opposes the required net ankle moment.", "The estimate is sensitive to the machine pivot location, thigh-pad lever ratio, carriage mass, forefoot position, and the amount of dorsiflexion used."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric initiation from the dorsiflexed bottom position, with the knees flexed approximately 85-95 degrees and the forefeet fixed on the platform while the heel begins to rise.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 162.70, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9200, "E_MA": 0.8800, "E_ND": 0.9200, "E_LoA": 0.9600, "E_mech": 0.7150, "m