46	seated_dumbbell_wrist_extension	Dumbbell Wrist Extension	Seated Palms-Down Dumbbell Wrist Extension	Upper	Forearms	Isolation	Dumbbell	12.00	38.37	{"wrist_extensors": 38.37}	{"grip": "Use a closed pronated grip around the dumbbells with enough finger tension to prevent slipping. Keep the grip secure but avoid deliberately crushing the handles if that restricts smooth wrist motion.", "tldr": {"focus": "Move only at the wrists and keep the forearms fully supported so the repetition does not become an elbow or shoulder movement.", "setup": "Sit with the forearms supported on the thighs or a bench, palms facing down, and the wrists extending just beyond the support edge.", "execution": "Extend the wrists to raise the dumbbells while keeping the forearms fixed, then lower into controlled wrist flexion.", "stop_when": "Stop when another repetition would require lifting the forearms, changing elbow position, using momentum, or materially shortening the wrist range."}, "stance": "Sit with both feet planted and the legs or bench positioned to provide stable forearm support.", "bracing": "Maintain a stable seated posture and keep the forearms pressed against their support. Prevent the elbows, shoulders, or trunk from lifting or shifting to assist the dumbbells.", "overview": "A seated bilateral dumbbell wrist extension performed with the forearms supported and the palms facing downward. The defining movement is isolated wrist extension against gravity while the elbows, forearms, and upper arms remain essentially stationary. Unlike a forearm curl with active elbow movement, the dumbbells move primarily because the hands rotate at the radiocarpal and midcarpal joints.", "eccentric": "Lower the dumbbells under control by allowing the wrists to flex while the forearms remain fixed on the support. Descend through the available comfortable range without letting the dumbbells drop or using finger opening to create artificial additional motion.", "concentric": "Extend both wrists to raise the dumbbells by moving the backs of the hands toward the forearms. Keep the forearms and elbows stationary and maintain the palms-down orientation throughout the movement. Continue until the highest controlled wrist-extension position is reached without radial or ulnar deviation dominating the motion.", "tempo_notes": "Avoid bouncing at the bottom or flicking the dumbbells upward. Reverse direction smoothly and maintain control through the relatively short wrist arc.", "end_position": "Finish the concentric phase at the highest controlled wrist-extension position achievable without lifting the forearms, deviating markedly toward either side, or changing elbow position.", "safety_notes": ["Keep the wrists clear of the support edge so the dumbbells can move without contacting the bench or thighs.", "Do not force painful end-range wrist extension or flexion.", "Maintain a secure grip so the dumbbells cannot roll or fall from the hands."], "external_cues": "Raise and lower the dumbbells only by pivoting the hands over the edge of the support.", "internal_cues": "Keep the forearms heavy on the support; lift the backs of the hands; keep the knuckles traveling straight rather than drifting sideways.", "common_mistakes": ["Lifting the forearms or elbows from the support to assist the dumbbells upward.", "Using rapid hand flicking instead of controlled wrist extension.", "Allowing excessive radial or ulnar deviation instead of predominantly straight wrist extension.", "Opening the fingers excessively at the bottom and turning the movement into a grip-repositioning drill.", "Placing too much of the forearm beyond the support edge, reducing stability.", "Shortening the motion to a small top-range pulse as fatigue increases."], "equipment_setup": "Use dumbbells light enough to permit full controlled wrist motion. Support the forearms on the thighs or a flat bench so the wrists and hands extend just beyond the edge and can move freely without the dumbbells contacting the support.", "range_of_motion": "Use the largest comfortable wrist flexion-to-extension range available while maintaining secure grip and fixed forearm support. The exact endpoints vary with wrist anatomy and mobility; neither forced end-range extension nor exaggerated flexion is required.", "rir_0_definition": "RIR 0 is reached when no additional repetition can be completed through the defined wrist range with the forearms stationary and the movement isolated to the wrists, even if the dumbbells could still be moved by lifting the elbows, using shoulder motion, flicking the hands, or shortening the range.", "rir_1_indicators": "Approximately one technically valid repetition may remain when wrist-extension velocity slows markedly and the top position becomes difficult to reach, but the lifter can still keep the forearms fixed, maintain a secure pronated grip, and reproduce the established wrist range.", "individualization": "Forearm support position, elbow spacing, wrist starting angle, and usable flexion-extension range may vary with forearm length, wrist structure, mobility, and the chosen support surface. These variations are acceptable provided the forearms remain stable, the palms stay predominantly down, and wrist extension remains the primary dynamic action.", "plane_of_movement": "Primarily sagittal-plane wrist extension and flexion with the forearms maintained in pronation.", "starting_position": "Sit with the torso stable and the forearms fully supported, palms facing the floor. Position the wrists beyond the edge of the thighs or bench, hold the dumbbells securely, and begin from a controlled flexed or neutral-to-flexed wrist position according to available range.", "technical_failure": "Technical failure occurs when another repetition cannot be completed through the established wrist range without materially changing the mechanics. Observable breakdowns include lifting the forearms from the support, moving the elbows or shoulders, using trunk momentum, excessive radial or ulnar deviation, dropping the dumbbells through the eccentric, or substantially shortening the wrist excursion.", "primary_joint_actions": "During the concentric phase, the wrists extend while the forearms remain pronated and the elbows remain essentially fixed. The finger flexors maintain enough grip to secure the dumbbells, while the forearms and upper limbs stabilize against unwanted motion."}	{}	{https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jtQslxR3f0A,https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UcwgCTkVvIQ}	Unilateral	{"slug": "seated_dumbbell_wrist_extension", "notes": ["The contribution corresponds to approximately 7.67 Nm of bilateral wrist-extension capacity using a representative internal wrist-extensor moment arm near 0.0080 m.", "The 12 kg bilateral load is plausible for a strict 8-12 repetition set but requires substantially less absolute resistance than the palms-up wrist curl because the wrist extensors have lower practical force capacity.", "Wrist flexors are excluded because their net wrist-flexion action opposes the required movement; antagonist co-contraction is not counted as task-producing FCSA demand.", "Pronators and supinators are excluded because maintaining the palms-down orientation does not require them to produce a meaningful share of the external wrist-extension moment."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric initiation from moderate wrist flexion, with the forearms fully supported and the dumbbells producing their largest perpendicular moment about the wrist axes.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 38.37, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9300, "E_MA": 0.8800, "E_ND": 0.9100, "E_LoA": 0.9600, "E_mech": 0.7150, "muscle": "wrist_extensors", "rationale": "The wrist-extensor aggregate directly generates the required net wrist-extension moment, with favorable starting length but a relatively small internal tendon moment arm.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 53.67, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 38.37}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["The 12 kg nominal load represents two 6 kg dumbbells.", "The forearms remain fully supported on the thighs or a bench, minimizing meaningful elbow and shoulder loading.", "The combined perpendicular distance from the wrist axes to the dumbbell centres of mass is approximately 0.065 m at the limiting phase.", "The wrists begin in controlled flexion and extend without forearm lifting, elbow movement, or momentum.", "The wrist_extensors aggregate represents the principal muscles producing wrist-extension torque while maintaining the palms-down grip."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "wrist_extension_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 7.65}], "nominal_load_kg": 12.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 117.72}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 38.37, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 38.37}
