30	gluteus_maximus	Musculus gluteus maximus	Lower	Glutes	2555.00	2419.51	65.00	Coarse Multipennate	0.440	0.560	extreme_high	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\nThe **gluteus maximus** is the largest and most superficial of the major gluteal muscles and one of the principal force-producing muscles of the hip.\r\n\r\nIts dominant mechanical roles include:\r\n\r\n- **hip extension**;\r\n- **external rotation of the femur**;\r\n- region-dependent contribution to **hip abduction or adduction**;\r\n- stabilization of the pelvis and femur under high force;\r\n- transmission of force into the iliotibial tract and femur.\r\n\r\nIt is not mechanically homogeneous.\r\n\r\nThe superior and inferior portions differ in:\r\n\r\n- fiber orientation;\r\n- insertion;\r\n- moment arms;\r\n- secondary joint actions;\r\n- regional recruitment.\r\n\r\nThis means `gluteus_maximus` is best treated as one large aggregate actuator with a dominant hip-extension identity, while recognizing substantial internal regional specialization.\r\n\r\nFor resistance training, the most important practical distinction is between exercises that load hip extension primarily:\r\n\r\n- at **large hip flexion**, such as deep squats and leg presses;\r\n- near **hip neutral / terminal extension**, such as hip thrusts.\r\n\r\nBoth can hypertrophy gluteus maximus, but they challenge different parts of the force-length and external-torque profile.\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\nGluteus maximus is a thick quadrilateral muscle forming much of the superficial buttock.\r\n\r\n## Origin\r\n\r\nIts broad proximal origin includes regions of the:\r\n\r\n- posterior ilium, behind the posterior gluteal line;\r\n- dorsal sacrum;\r\n- coccyx;\r\n- sacrotuberous ligament;\r\n- thoracolumbar and gluteal fascial structures.\r\n\r\nThis exceptionally broad origin contributes to the muscle's large mass and highly varied fiber trajectories.\r\n\r\nSuperior fibers tend to run more inferolaterally.\r\n\r\nInferior fibers tend to run more laterally and distally.\r\n\r\nBecause of this variation, different regions do not produce identical joint moments.\r\n\r\n## Insertion\r\n\r\nGluteus maximus has two major distal insertion systems.\r\n\r\n### Iliotibial Tract\r\n\r\nA substantial proportion of superior and superficial fibers insert into the **iliotibial tract**.\r\n\r\nForce can therefore be transmitted from the gluteus maximus into the lateral fascial system of the thigh.\r\n\r\n### Gluteal Tuberosity\r\n\r\nDeeper and more inferior fibers insert onto the **gluteal tuberosity of the femur**.\r\n\r\nThis direct femoral insertion is particularly relevant to hip extension and external rotation.\r\n\r\nThe dual insertion pattern helps explain why superior and inferior gluteus maximus fibers differ functionally.\r\n\r\n## Architecture\r\n\r\nGluteus maximus has complex fascicular architecture with considerable regional variation.\r\n\r\nIts large PCSA supports high force production.\r\n\r\nThe fibers are not arranged as one uniform parallel sheet.\r\n\r\nDifferent regions possess distinct:\r\n\r\n- fiber angles;\r\n- attachment geometry;\r\n- moment arms.\r\n\r\nTherefore a single whole-muscle:\r\n\r\n- pennation angle;\r\n- optimal fiber length;\r\n- PCSA\r\n\r\nshould be interpreted as an effective aggregate approximation.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nGluteus maximus is innervated by the **inferior gluteal nerve**, primarily from spinal roots:\r\n\r\n- L5;\r\n- S1;\r\n- S2.\r\n\r\nThis distinguishes it from gluteus medius and minimus, which are innervated by the superior gluteal nerve.\r\n\r\nThe inferior gluteal nerve enters the deep surface of the muscle and distributes across its large regional architecture.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Hip Extension\r\n\r\nHip extension is the principal gross function of gluteus maximus.\r\n\r\nIt generates posteriorly directed force on the femur and can:\r\n\r\n- extend the femur relative to the pelvis;\r\n- extend the pelvis/trunk relative to a fixed femur.\r\n\r\nThis makes it important in both open- and closed-chain tasks.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- rising from a squat;\r\n- climbing stairs;\r\n- sprint acceleration;\r\n- deadlifting;\r\n- hip thrusting;\r\n- standing up from a seated position.\r\n\r\n## Extension Moment Depends on Hip Angle\r\n\r\nGluteus maximus extension leverage is not constant across ROM.\r\n\r\nClassic in vivo measurements show that its hip-extension moment arm **decreases as hip flexion increases**. :contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n- near neutral hip position, its internal extension leverage is generally very favorable;\r\n- in deeper flexion, the muscle is longer but its extension moment arm becomes smaller.\r\n\r\nThis creates an important distinction between:\r\n\r\n- **muscle length**;\r\n- **internal leverage**.\r\n\r\nA deeply flexed hip can place gluteus maximus at a long length while simultaneously reducing its moment arm.\r\n\r\nHigh external hip-extension torque can still make the muscle work extremely hard in that position.\r\n\r\n## External Rotation\r\n\r\nGluteus maximus is a strong **external rotator of the femur**.\r\n\r\nMost of the muscle maintains an external-rotation moment arm across a broad range of hip flexion. :contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}\r\n\r\nThis role becomes important in:\r\n\r\n- squatting;\r\n- sprinting;\r\n- cutting;\r\n- stabilization against femoral internal rotation.\r\n\r\nExternal rotation should generally be treated as a secondary but significant function.\r\n\r\n## Regional Abduction and Adduction\r\n\r\nThe superior and inferior gluteus maximus regions have different frontal-plane actions.\r\n\r\n### Superior Fibers\r\n\r\nTend to have greater **abduction** capacity.\r\n\r\n### Inferior Fibers\r\n\r\nCan contribute relatively more toward **adduction**, depending on hip position.\r\n\r\nThis is one reason the whole muscle should not be reduced to a single three-axis action vector.\r\n\r\nFor most exercise-level modelling, however, extension should dominate.\r\n\r\n## Pelvic Stabilization\r\n\r\nIn closed-chain tasks, gluteus maximus contributes to stabilization of the pelvis relative to the femur.\r\n\r\nIt is especially important when large sagittal-plane moments occur.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- unilateral stance under load;\r\n- sprint acceleration;\r\n- split squats;\r\n- step-ups;\r\n- lunges.\r\n\r\nIt works with:\r\n\r\n- gluteus medius;\r\n- adductor magnus;\r\n- hamstrings;\r\n- trunk musculature\r\n\r\nto control pelvis-femur orientation.\r\n\r\n## Force Transmission Through the Iliotibial Tract\r\n\r\nThe iliotibial insertion means gluteus maximus can transmit force into the lateral thigh fascial system.\r\n\r\nThis contributes to lateral stabilization of the hip and knee complex.\r\n\r\nIt should not be interpreted as direct knee extension or flexion.\r\n\r\nRather, it is part of a broader force-transmission and stabilization system.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nGluteus maximus has a strong anatomical and mechanical rationale for hypertrophy under substantial hip flexion.\r\n\r\nHip flexion lengthens the muscle.\r\n\r\nTherefore exercises that combine:\r\n\r\n- large hip flexion;\r\n- high external hip-extension torque\r\n\r\ncan create high force at long muscle lengths.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- deep squat;\r\n- deep leg press;\r\n- deep split squat;\r\n- lunge variants.\r\n\r\nHowever, the relationship is not as simple as:\r\n\r\n> deeper hip flexion = automatically greater glute hypertrophy.\r\n\r\nInternal leverage changes with hip angle, and exercise-specific external torque also matters.\r\n\r\n## Squats\r\n\r\nDeep squats expose gluteus maximus to substantial hip flexion.\r\n\r\nThis increases muscle length and can create large hip-extension demands during ascent.\r\n\r\nResistance-training studies show that squat training can substantially hypertrophy the gluteus maximus. :contentReference[oaicite:3]{index=3}\r\n\r\nGreater squat depth is generally more compelling for glute hypertrophy than shallow squatting because it increases:\r\n\r\n- hip ROM;\r\n- lengthened-position exposure;\r\n- hip-extension work.\r\n\r\nHowever, useful depth remains individual.\r\n\r\nThe goal is not maximal anatomical flexion at all costs.\r\n\r\n## Hip Thrust\r\n\r\nHip thrusts load hip extension much more strongly near:\r\n\r\n- neutral hip position;\r\n- terminal extension.\r\n\r\nThe gluteus maximus has favorable internal leverage in this region.\r\n\r\nTherefore hip thrusts can generate very high gluteal force despite operating at shorter muscle lengths than deep squats.\r\n\r\nThis is a classic example of:\r\n\r\n> shorter muscle length but favorable leverage and high external torque.\r\n\r\n## Squat vs Hip Thrust\r\n\r\nA 2023 longitudinal comparison found that back squat and hip thrust training produced **similar overall gluteal hypertrophy** over nine weeks, despite very different mechanics and EMG profiles. :contentReference[oaicite:4]{index=4}\r\n\r\nThis strongly argues against treating either exercise as universally superior.\r\n\r\nThey represent different loading solutions:\r\n\r\n### Squat\r\n\r\n- longer gluteal lengths;\r\n- larger knee-extensor demand;\r\n- substantial adductor magnus involvement;\r\n- higher systemic cost.\r\n\r\n### Hip Thrust\r\n\r\n- shorter gluteal operating lengths;\r\n- very high hip-extension specificity;\r\n- low knee-extensor demand;\r\n- lower axial/systemic cost.\r\n\r\nBoth are valid hypertrophy tools.\r\n\r\n## Lengthened Loading Interpretation\r\n\r\nThe current evidence supports the value of glute training through large hip-flexion ranges, but it does not imply that shortening-biased exercises are ineffective.\r\n\r\nA more accurate model is:\r\n\r\n> gluteus maximus responds to high mechanical tension across multiple muscle lengths, with deep hip-flexion exercises and near-neutral hip-extension exercises providing complementary stimuli.\r\n\r\n## SMH Confidence\r\n\r\nFor gluteus maximus, a moderate-to-high `smh_factor` is defensible.\r\n\r\nThe evidence is stronger than for:\r\n\r\n- lateral deltoid;\r\n- SCM;\r\n- deep neck extensors.\r\n\r\nHowever, it is less cleanly isolated than gastrocnemius, where knee position creates a very direct natural experiment.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Squat\r\n\r\nSquats can provide substantial gluteus maximus stimulus when sufficient depth and hip-extension demand are present.\r\n\r\nThe key variables are:\r\n\r\n- hip flexion depth;\r\n- torso angle;\r\n- stance;\r\n- load;\r\n- bar position;\r\n- anthropometry.\r\n\r\nA squat that remains very shallow may provide much less gluteal stimulus than one involving substantial hip flexion.\r\n\r\n## Deep Leg Press\r\n\r\nA deep leg press can be an excellent glute exercise because it combines:\r\n\r\n- high stability;\r\n- large hip flexion;\r\n- large external load;\r\n- low balance demand.\r\n\r\nIt can provide a high local glute stimulus with lower spinal stabilization demands than free squatting.\r\n\r\nPelvic position matters.\r\n\r\nIf additional depth is achieved primarily by posterior pelvic rotation rather than hip flexion, the intended gluteal lengthening is reduced and lumbar flexion increases.\r\n\r\n## Hip Thrust\r\n\r\nHip thrusts provide a highly specific hip-extension stimulus.\r\n\r\nAdvantages include:\r\n\r\n- high glute specificity;\r\n- favorable loading near terminal extension;\r\n- low quadriceps demand;\r\n- low spinal erector demand relative to many hinges;\r\n- easy progressive overload.\r\n\r\nTheir main mechanical limitation is reduced loading at very long glute lengths.\r\n\r\nThus hip thrusts are best viewed as complementary to deep-flexion exercises.\r\n\r\n## Romanian Deadlift\r\n\r\nRDLs train gluteus maximus through hip extension while also strongly loading:\r\n\r\n- hamstrings;\r\n- adductor magnus;\r\n- erector spinae.\r\n\r\nGluteus maximus becomes longer as the hip flexes.\r\n\r\nHowever, relative force sharing depends on:\r\n\r\n- knee angle;\r\n- hip angle;\r\n- external moment;\r\n- individual hamstring and adductor leverage.\r\n\r\nRDLs can provide meaningful glute stimulus but are generally less glute-specific than hip thrusts.\r\n\r\n## Conventional Deadlift\r\n\r\nConventional deadlifting also produces substantial hip-extension demand.\r\n\r\nIts glute stimulus comes with high systemic cost because the exercise simultaneously loads:\r\n\r\n- quadriceps;\r\n- hamstrings;\r\n- adductors;\r\n- spinal extensors;\r\n- grip;\r\n- upper back.\r\n\r\nIt should therefore not automatically be considered the most efficient hypertrophy exercise merely because external loads are high.\r\n\r\n## Split Squat\r\n\r\nA long-stride or hip-flexion-rich split squat can strongly load gluteus maximus.\r\n\r\nAdvantages include:\r\n\r\n- deep hip flexion;\r\n- unilateral loading;\r\n- reduced absolute load requirement;\r\n- frontal-plane stabilization demand.\r\n\r\nA modest forward torso lean can increase the external hip-flexion moment and therefore hip-extensor demand.\r\n\r\n## Step-Up\r\n\r\nStep-ups can generate substantial gluteus maximus demand when:\r\n\r\n- step height is sufficient;\r\n- the working leg performs most of the work;\r\n- push-off from the trailing leg is minimized.\r\n\r\nHigh step heights increase hip flexion but also increase balance and mobility demands.\r\n\r\n## Forward Lunge\r\n\r\nForward lunges can produce meaningful gluteal loading through:\r\n\r\n- hip flexion;\r\n- eccentric deceleration;\r\n- subsequent hip extension.\r\n\r\nStride length and torso orientation strongly alter muscle distribution.\r\n\r\n## Cable Pull-Through\r\n\r\nCable pull-throughs are hip-extension exercises with relatively low axial loading.\r\n\r\nThey can provide gluteal and hamstring demand.\r\n\r\nTheir limitations include:\r\n\r\n- relatively low absolute loading;\r\n- stability constraints;\r\n- cable geometry.\r\n\r\nThey are useful as low-fatigue accessory work but are rarely the strongest primary glute hypertrophy exercise.\r\n\r\n## Back Extension\r\n\r\nBack extension can be glute-dominant if performed primarily through the hip.\r\n\r\nA useful glute-focused variant keeps the spine relatively stable and moves through:\r\n\r\n- hip flexion;\r\n- hip extension.\r\n\r\nIf the exercise instead involves large spinal extension, more demand shifts toward `erector_spinae`.\r\n\r\n## Abduction Exercises\r\n\r\nBecause superior gluteus maximus fibers can contribute to abduction, glute max activity can occur during hip-abduction exercises.\r\n\r\nHowever, gluteus medius and minimus should usually remain the primary allocation.\r\n\r\nAbduction should not be treated as the main hypertrophy route for the whole gluteus maximus.\r\n\r\n## External Rotation\r\n\r\nDedicated external-rotation exercises can recruit gluteus maximus.\r\n\r\nHowever, hip-extension exercises generally provide far greater total force and hypertrophic potential.\r\n\r\nExternal rotation is better treated as part of the muscle's functional profile than as a mandatory bodybuilding movement.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Count Compound Lower-Body Volume\r\n\r\nGluteus maximus receives substantial volume from many exercises:\r\n\r\n- squats;\r\n- leg presses;\r\n- deadlifts;\r\n- RDLs;\r\n- split squats;\r\n- lunges;\r\n- hip thrusts;\r\n- step-ups.\r\n\r\nTherefore direct glute programming should begin by evaluating total hip-extension exposure.\r\n\r\nA lifter can accumulate very high glute volume without performing any exercise labeled "glute isolation."\r\n\r\n## Weekly Volume\r\n\r\nA practical hypertrophy range may begin around:\r\n\r\n- **6-12 effective glute-focused sets per week**\r\n\r\ndepending on how much contribution comes from multi-joint exercises.\r\n\r\nThis should not be interpreted as:\r\n\r\n- 12 hip-thrust sets;\r\nor\r\n- 12 direct-isolation sets.\r\n\r\nA deep squat set and hip thrust set can both count strongly.\r\n\r\nA shallow leg press set may count less.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nTwo to three weekly exposures are practical.\r\n\r\nBecause gluteus maximus is large and frequently trained through compounds, recovery is affected by:\r\n\r\n- local muscular fatigue;\r\n- spinal fatigue;\r\n- hamstring fatigue;\r\n- adductor fatigue.\r\n\r\nExercise distribution is therefore more important than simply increasing frequency.\r\n\r\n## Rep Ranges\r\n\r\nGluteus maximus can hypertrophy across broad repetition ranges.\r\n\r\n### Compounds\r\n\r\nUseful ranges include:\r\n\r\n- 5-10 reps for heavy squats and hinges;\r\n- 8-15 reps for hypertrophy-oriented leg presses and split squats.\r\n\r\n### Hip Thrust / Stable Machine Work\r\n\r\nUseful ranges include:\r\n\r\n- 6-12 reps for heavier loading;\r\n- 8-20 reps for general hypertrophy.\r\n\r\nThere is no special glute-specific repetition zone.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nStable exercises can be trained close to failure.\r\n\r\nExamples:\r\n\r\n- hip thrust machine;\r\n- leg press;\r\n- machine hip extension.\r\n\r\nFree-weight compounds generally benefit from more technical reserve.\r\n\r\nExamples:\r\n\r\n- squat;\r\n- RDL;\r\n- deadlift.\r\n\r\nThe target should be high glute tension, not simply maximal systemic exhaustion.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Complementarity\r\n\r\nA mechanically complete glute program often benefits from combining:\r\n\r\n1. a **deep hip-flexion exercise**;\r\n2. a **near-neutral hip-extension exercise**.\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n\r\n- deep squat or leg press;\r\n- hip thrust.\r\n\r\nThis provides high tension across substantially different parts of the hip-extension ROM.\r\n\r\nA hip hinge can optionally add another long-length posterior-chain stimulus.\r\n\r\n## Stimulus-to-Fatigue Ratio\r\n\r\nExercises differ substantially in systemic cost.\r\n\r\n### High Systemic Cost\r\n\r\n- deadlift;\r\n- heavy squat;\r\n- RDL.\r\n\r\n### Moderate\r\n\r\n- split squat;\r\n- deep leg press.\r\n\r\n### Lower Systemic Cost\r\n\r\n- hip thrust machine;\r\n- cable pull-through;\r\n- machine hip extension.\r\n\r\nTherefore more glute volume does not necessarily require more axial loading.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Order\r\n\r\nIf glute hypertrophy is a priority, place at least one high-value glute exercise early enough to preserve performance.\r\n\r\nPossible examples:\r\n\r\n- squat first;\r\n- hip thrust first;\r\n- deep leg press first.\r\n\r\nThere is no universal requirement that squats must precede hip thrusts.\r\n\r\nExercise order should reflect priority.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## It Is One of the Largest Muscles in the Human Body\r\n\r\nGluteus maximus has enormous muscle volume and force-generating capacity.\r\n\r\nIts large size reflects the substantial hip-extension demands associated with upright human locomotion.\r\n\r\n## It Is More Important in Powerful Tasks Than Quiet Standing\r\n\r\nGluteus maximus activity is relatively modest during low-demand upright stance and walking compared with high-force tasks.\r\n\r\nIts role becomes much larger during:\r\n\r\n- sprinting;\r\n- stair climbing;\r\n- rising;\r\n- jumping;\r\n- heavy resistance exercise.\r\n\r\nThis fits its identity as a powerful rather than continuously active postural extensor.\r\n\r\n## It Does Not Have One Single Action in the Frontal Plane\r\n\r\nSuperior and inferior fibers can possess different abduction/adduction moment arms.\r\n\r\nThis makes gluteus maximus another clear example of why large fan-shaped muscles should not be assigned one fixed action vector.\r\n\r\n## Hip Thrust EMG Does Not Predict Hypertrophy Superiority\r\n\r\nHip thrusts often produce very high acute gluteus maximus EMG.\r\n\r\nHowever, longitudinal data show that back squats can produce similar gluteal hypertrophy despite lower acute EMG in some comparisons. :contentReference[oaicite:5]{index=5}\r\n\r\nThis is an important warning against using EMG as a direct hypertrophy score.\r\n\r\n## Longer Muscle Length Can Coexist With Worse Internal Leverage\r\n\r\nAs hip flexion increases:\r\n\r\n- gluteus maximus length increases;\r\n- its extension moment arm can decrease. :contentReference[oaicite:6]{index=6}\r\n\r\nThis means long-length training does not simply correspond to optimal leverage.\r\n\r\nThe muscle may be longer but require greater force to meet a given joint torque because its moment arm is smaller.\r\n\r\nThat interaction is central to realistic muscle-force modelling.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\n`gluteus_maximus` should primarily receive demand from:\r\n\r\n1. **hip-extension torque**;\r\n2. secondary external-rotation torque;\r\n3. smaller region-dependent abduction/adduction components;\r\n4. pelvic stabilization.\r\n\r\nHip extension should dominate the vector.\r\n\r\n## Length-Tension Model\r\n\r\nA simplified model can use hip flexion angle:\r\n\r\n- deep hip flexion → long;\r\n- moderate flexion → intermediate-long;\r\n- near neutral → intermediate;\r\n- terminal extension → short.\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_glute_max = f(hip_flexion_angle)`\r\n\r\nHowever, this must remain separate from **moment-arm efficiency**.\r\n\r\n## Internal Leverage\r\n\r\nClassic in vivo data show that gluteus maximus extension moment arm decreases with increasing hip flexion. :contentReference[oaicite:7]{index=7}\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`muscle_force_required ≈ joint_extension_torque / internal_moment_arm`\r\n\r\nall else equal.\r\n\r\nAt deeper hip flexion, the glute can be:\r\n\r\n- longer;\r\n- mechanically less advantaged;\r\n- still highly loaded because external joint torque is large.\r\n\r\nThis makes `E_LT` and `E_MA` genuinely distinct dimensions in your model.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Allocation\r\n\r\n### 45° Leg Press\r\n\r\nHigh `gluteus_maximus` demand when deep hip flexion is achieved.\r\n\r\nAlso high:\r\n\r\n- quadriceps;\r\n- adductor magnus.\r\n\r\nGlute allocation should increase with hip depth.\r\n\r\n### Deep Squat\r\n\r\nHigh gluteus maximus demand.\r\n\r\nThe exact amount depends on:\r\n\r\n- depth;\r\n- torso angle;\r\n- stance;\r\n- load;\r\n- anthropometry.\r\n\r\n### Hip Thrust\r\n\r\nVery high gluteus maximus specificity near neutral hip extension.\r\n\r\nLower long-length loading than deep squats.\r\n\r\n### Romanian Deadlift\r\n\r\nHigh glute demand through hip extension.\r\n\r\nAlso substantial:\r\n\r\n- hamstrings;\r\n- adductor magnus;\r\n- erector spinae.\r\n\r\n### Cable Pull-Through\r\n\r\nModerate-to-high gluteus maximus demand with low axial cost.\r\n\r\n### Roman-Chair Back Extension\r\n\r\nGlute allocation depends strongly on whether movement occurs through:\r\n\r\n- hip extension;\r\nor\r\n- spinal extension.\r\n\r\n### Copenhagen Plank\r\n\r\nLow gluteus maximus demand compared with adductors and lateral pelvic stabilizers.\r\n\r\n### Pallof Press\r\n\r\nLow-to-moderate gluteus maximus stabilization demand depending on stance.\r\n\r\nIt should not receive a large dynamic hip-extension allocation.\r\n\r\n## Squat vs Hip Thrust in Agonez\r\n\r\nThese exercises should not receive the same muscle-state modifiers.\r\n\r\n### Squat\r\n\r\n- high `E_LT`;\r\n- smaller glute extension moment arm at deep flexion;\r\n- high external hip torque;\r\n- high systemic cost.\r\n\r\n### Hip Thrust\r\n\r\n- lower `E_LT`;\r\n- favorable extension leverage near neutral;\r\n- very high glute specificity;\r\n- lower systemic cost.\r\n\r\nThe fact that both can produce similar hypertrophy is exactly why Agonez should keep these dimensions separate instead of collapsing them into one “glute activation” coefficient. :contentReference[oaicite:8]{index=8}\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Adductor Magnus\r\n\r\nDeep hip-extension exercises require careful force sharing between:\r\n\r\n- `gluteus_maximus`;\r\n- `adductor_magnus`;\r\n- hamstrings.\r\n\r\nAs discussed in the `adductor_magnus` record, adductor magnus becomes a major hip extensor in flexed positions.\r\n\r\nTherefore deep squat or leg-press demand should not be assigned overwhelmingly to gluteus maximus merely because it is commonly called the primary hip extensor.\r\n\r\nThis is particularly relevant at deep hip flexion.\r\n\r\nNear neutral extension, gluteus maximus becomes relatively more mechanically favorable.\r\n\r\n## Regional Simplification\r\n\r\nThe current record aggregates superior and inferior gluteus-maximus regions.\r\n\r\nThis is adequate for most exercise-level modelling, but it loses:\r\n\r\n- superior-region abduction bias;\r\n- inferior-region adduction bias;\r\n- region-specific hypertrophy.\r\n\r\nIf Agonez eventually models regional musculature, gluteus maximus would be one of the strongest candidates for subdivision.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nA relatively high `smh_factor` is defensible because:\r\n\r\n- hip flexion clearly lengthens the muscle;\r\n- deep-flexion exercises generate substantial hypertrophy;\r\n- long-length loading is mechanically relevant.\r\n\r\nHowever, the evidence also shows that shorter-length-loaded hip thrusts can produce comparable hypertrophy to squats in some contexts. :contentReference[oaicite:9]{index=9}\r\n\r\nTherefore `smh_factor` should not imply that terminal-extension loading is unimportant.\r\n\r\nThe more accurate interpretation is:\r\n\r\n> **gluteus maximus benefits from high tension across a broad length range, with deep-flexion and near-neutral-extension exercises providing complementary rather than mutually exclusive stimuli.**\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **broad pelvic origin and IT-band/femoral insertion:** high;\r\n- **inferior gluteal nerve innervation:** high;\r\n- **hip-extension function:** high;\r\n- **external-rotation function:** high;\r\n- **regional abduction/adduction differences:** well supported biomechanically;\r\n- **moment arm decreasing with hip flexion:** directly measured in vivo;\r\n- **hypertrophy from squats:** directly supported;\r\n- **hypertrophy from hip thrusts:** directly supported;\r\n- **similar squat vs hip-thrust hypertrophy in direct comparison:** directly supported;\r\n- **exercise-specific regional hypertrophy:** emerging but less complete;\r\n- **precise long-length hypertrophy advantage:** plausible and partially supported, but not uniquely dominant.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **gluteus maximus = a massive, regionally heterogeneous hip extensor and external rotator whose training demand depends on both hip-extension torque and hip angle, with deep-flexion and near-neutral-extension exercises loading different but complementary mechanical states.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/GluteusMaximus}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	15.69	21.90	0.928	154.21	143.08
31	gluteus_medius	Musculus gluteus medius	Lower	Glutes	940.00	890.15	65.00	Fan-shaped/Radiate	0.550	0.450	high	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\nThe **gluteus medius** is a broad, fan-shaped muscle located on the lateral surface of the ilium, deep to the upper portion of gluteus maximus.\r\n\r\nIts primary mechanical identity is as a:\r\n\r\n- **hip abductor**;\r\n- **frontal-plane pelvic stabilizer**;\r\n- regionally differentiated hip rotator;\r\n- contributor to control of femoral and pelvic position during unilateral loading.\r\n\r\nIt is particularly important during:\r\n\r\n- walking;\r\n- running;\r\n- single-leg stance;\r\n- lunges;\r\n- split squats;\r\n- step-ups;\r\n- cutting and lateral movement.\r\n\r\nThe muscle should not be understood as one homogeneous "side glute."\r\n\r\nIts broad fan-shaped architecture produces substantially different fiber orientations across its anterior, middle and posterior regions.\r\n\r\nA useful simplification is:\r\n\r\n- **anterior fibers** → abduction + internal-rotation/flexion bias;\r\n- **middle fibers** → strong abduction bias;\r\n- **posterior fibers** → abduction + external-rotation/extension bias.\r\n\r\nThese functions are not absolute categories.\r\n\r\nMoment arms change with:\r\n\r\n- hip flexion;\r\n- abduction/adduction;\r\n- axial rotation;\r\n- pelvic position.\r\n\r\nFor exercise modelling, gluteus medius is therefore best represented as a powerful **abductor/stabilizer aggregate with regional secondary actions**.\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\n## Origin\r\n\r\nGluteus medius originates broadly from the **external surface of the ilium** between approximately the:\r\n\r\n- anterior gluteal line;\r\n- posterior gluteal line;\r\n\r\nand from adjacent portions of the iliac crest and gluteal fascia.\r\n\r\nIts broad proximal attachment produces a fan-shaped muscle.\r\n\r\nAnterior fibers originate more anteriorly and descend posterolaterally.\r\n\r\nMiddle fibers descend more vertically.\r\n\r\nPosterior fibers run more anteriorly and inferiorly toward the greater trochanter.\r\n\r\nThis architecture creates substantially different lines of action within the same muscle.\r\n\r\n## Insertion\r\n\r\nGluteus medius inserts through a strong tendon onto the **greater trochanter of the femur**.\r\n\r\nThe attachment spans primarily the lateral and superoposterior facets of the greater trochanter, with regional tendon organization corresponding to different muscle subdivisions.\r\n\r\nThe insertion is mechanically favorable for producing frontal-plane torque because it lies lateral to the hip joint center.\r\n\r\nHowever, the exact rotational and sagittal-plane moment arms depend strongly on hip position.\r\n\r\n## Regional Architecture\r\n\r\nGluteus medius is commonly divided functionally into:\r\n\r\n- anterior;\r\n- middle;\r\n- posterior regions.\r\n\r\nThis division is more than a visual convenience.\r\n\r\nAnatomical studies demonstrate distinct:\r\n\r\n- fiber orientations;\r\n- tendinous attachments;\r\n- neural organization;\r\n- mechanical functions.\r\n\r\nThe middle region possesses the most intuitive abduction line of pull.\r\n\r\nAnterior and posterior regions retain substantial abduction capability while also possessing different rotational and sagittal-plane actions.\r\n\r\nFor the Agonez database, a single whole-muscle record remains practical, but the regional heterogeneity should be remembered whenever interpreting recruitment values.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nGluteus medius is innervated by the **superior gluteal nerve**, primarily from spinal roots:\r\n\r\n- L4;\r\n- L5;\r\n- S1.\r\n\r\nThe superior gluteal nerve also supplies:\r\n\r\n- gluteus minimus;\r\n- tensor fasciae latae.\r\n\r\nRegional branching within gluteus medius allows some functional differentiation between portions of the muscle.\r\n\r\nThis is consistent with evidence showing region-specific recruitment during different hip tasks.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Hip Abduction\r\n\r\nThe principal gross function of gluteus medius is **hip abduction**.\r\n\r\nIn open-chain movement, contraction moves the femur laterally away from the body's midline.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- standing cable hip abduction;\r\n- side-lying hip abduction;\r\n- machine hip abduction.\r\n\r\nHowever, much of the muscle's real-world importance occurs in closed-chain tasks rather than obvious open-chain abduction.\r\n\r\n## Pelvic Stabilization\r\n\r\nDuring single-leg stance, body weight creates an external moment tending to drop the unsupported side of the pelvis.\r\n\r\nThe stance-side hip abductors must generate an opposing moment.\r\n\r\nGluteus medius is a major contributor to this internal abduction torque.\r\n\r\nFor example, during right-leg single-leg stance:\r\n\r\n- gravity tends to lower the left side of the pelvis;\r\n- the right gluteus medius generates abduction torque to maintain pelvic position.\r\n\r\nThis function is critical during walking because every step contains a period of unilateral support.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n> gluteus medius can generate high force without visible hip abduction.\r\n\r\nThis is analogous to erector spinae generating extension torque isometrically without spinal extension.\r\n\r\n## Frontal-Plane Force Balance\r\n\r\nThe hip abductors oppose the external adduction moment produced by body weight.\r\n\r\nThe approximate mechanical requirement can be conceptualized as:\r\n\r\n`M_abductor ≈ body_weight_force × body_COM_moment_arm`\r\n\r\nThe internal muscular force required is then influenced by the relatively short abductor moment arm.\r\n\r\nTherefore gluteus medius force during single-leg stance can be substantial even though external load appears modest.\r\n\r\nAdding external load or moving it farther from the stance hip can increase this requirement substantially.\r\n\r\n## Anterior Fibers\r\n\r\nAnterior gluteus medius fibers generally possess greater ability to contribute to:\r\n\r\n- **hip internal rotation**;\r\n- hip flexion depending on position;\r\n- abduction.\r\n\r\nTheir exact rotational moment changes with hip angle.\r\n\r\nInternal rotation should therefore be interpreted as a regional bias rather than a universal whole-muscle action.\r\n\r\n## Posterior Fibers\r\n\r\nPosterior fibers can contribute relatively more to:\r\n\r\n- **external rotation**;\r\n- hip extension;\r\n- abduction.\r\n\r\nThis produces an apparent paradox:\r\n\r\n> different portions of the same muscle can generate opposite axial-rotation moments.\r\n\r\nThe phenomenon follows directly from the fan-shaped architecture and different lines of pull.\r\n\r\n## Middle Fibers\r\n\r\nThe middle region has a relatively direct lateral line of action and is particularly suited to generating hip-abduction torque.\r\n\r\nFor a simplified whole-muscle model, this is the dominant mechanical identity of gluteus medius.\r\n\r\n## Rotation Depends on Hip Angle\r\n\r\nHip rotation moment arms are strongly position-dependent.\r\n\r\nA fiber that functions as an external rotator in one hip configuration may become mechanically weaker or even change rotational role in another.\r\n\r\nTherefore static textbook labels such as:\r\n\r\n- anterior = internal rotator;\r\n- posterior = external rotator\r\n\r\nare useful but incomplete.\r\n\r\nThe primary invariant function across the muscle is **abduction**, while secondary rotational behavior is more variable.\r\n\r\n## Single-Leg Control\r\n\r\nDuring unilateral tasks, gluteus medius contributes to control of:\r\n\r\n- pelvic drop;\r\n- femoral adduction;\r\n- femoral rotation;\r\n- trunk-pelvis alignment.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- walking;\r\n- running;\r\n- single-leg squat;\r\n- split squat;\r\n- step-down;\r\n- lateral cutting.\r\n\r\nThis is why the muscle is frequently studied in relation to lower-limb mechanics.\r\n\r\nHowever, poor knee or hip mechanics should not automatically be attributed to "weak glute medius."\r\n\r\nWhole-body movement emerges from:\r\n\r\n- strength;\r\n- technique;\r\n- morphology;\r\n- motor strategy;\r\n- ankle mechanics;\r\n- trunk position;\r\n- task constraints.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nDirect evidence specifically examining **stretch-mediated hypertrophy of gluteus medius** is sparse.\r\n\r\nBiomechanically, gluteus medius length changes with several hip motions.\r\n\r\nThe most intuitive lengthening occurs through **hip adduction**, because the muscle spans the lateral side of the hip.\r\n\r\nHowever, the fan-shaped architecture means muscle length also depends on:\r\n\r\n- hip flexion;\r\n- axial rotation;\r\n- regional fiber orientation.\r\n\r\nRecent anatomical work has demonstrated substantial excursion of the gluteus medius musculotendinous unit with hip flexion, confirming that muscle length cannot be inferred from abduction/adduction alone.\r\n\r\n## Hip Adduction\r\n\r\nMoving the femur across the midline increases the distance between iliac origin and greater-trochanter insertion for much of the muscle.\r\n\r\nThis can create a relatively lengthened gluteus medius state.\r\n\r\nTherefore cable or machine abduction performed from some degree of adduction provides a plausible long-length hypertrophy stimulus.\r\n\r\n## Cross-Body Cable Abduction\r\n\r\nA cable configuration can allow the working leg to begin slightly across the body's midline.\r\n\r\nThis creates:\r\n\r\n- hip adduction;\r\n- high gluteus medius length;\r\n- external adduction torque;\r\n- active abduction force.\r\n\r\nThis is mechanically attractive for hypertrophy.\r\n\r\nHowever, excessive cross-body ROM is unnecessary.\r\n\r\nLarge pelvic movement or lumbar lateral flexion can create apparent additional ROM without meaningful gluteus medius lengthening.\r\n\r\n## Hip Flexion\r\n\r\nGluteus medius length also changes with hip flexion.\r\n\r\nExperimental cadaveric measurements demonstrate meaningful musculotendinous excursion with increasing hip flexion.\r\n\r\nThis further reinforces that:\r\n\r\n`L_gluteus_medius ≠ f(abduction_angle only)`\r\n\r\nA more complete model would incorporate multiple hip-plane variables.\r\n\r\n## Long-Length Evidence\r\n\r\nUnlike gastrocnemius, there is currently no equivalent high-quality longitudinal experiment showing that one gluteus-medius exercise configuration produces greater hypertrophy specifically because it operates at longer muscle lengths.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n- long-length loading is mechanically plausible;\r\n- hip adduction clearly lengthens much of the muscle;\r\n- hip flexion also affects length;\r\n- direct SMH magnitude remains uncertain.\r\n\r\nA high `smh_factor` should therefore be interpreted as a mechanistic hypothesis rather than a directly quantified effect.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Hip-Abduction Machine\r\n\r\nA hip-abduction machine is one of the most direct ways to load gluteus medius.\r\n\r\nAdvantages include:\r\n\r\n- high stability;\r\n- simple progression;\r\n- low balance demand;\r\n- easy proximity to failure;\r\n- direct frontal-plane resistance.\r\n\r\nMachine geometry matters.\r\n\r\nA useful machine should permit:\r\n\r\n- meaningful starting adduction or at least neutral position;\r\n- substantial loaded abduction ROM;\r\n- minimal pelvic compensation.\r\n\r\n## Standing Cable Hip Abduction\r\n\r\nStanding cable abduction provides direct gluteus medius demand.\r\n\r\nAdvantages include:\r\n\r\n- configurable resistance direction;\r\n- unilateral loading;\r\n- potential loading in mild hip adduction;\r\n- relatively natural movement path.\r\n\r\nLimitations include:\r\n\r\n- balance demands;\r\n- pelvic stabilization;\r\n- reduced absolute load relative to machines.\r\n\r\nHolding onto a stable support is generally advantageous for hypertrophy because it removes unnecessary balance limitation.\r\n\r\n## Side-Lying Hip Abduction\r\n\r\nSide-lying hip abduction produces strong gluteus medius activation and is widely used in rehabilitation and introductory strengthening.\r\n\r\nAdvantages include:\r\n\r\n- minimal equipment;\r\n- simple movement;\r\n- low systemic fatigue.\r\n\r\nIts primary limitation for advanced hypertrophy is load progression.\r\n\r\nOnce meaningful external resistance becomes difficult to apply comfortably, machine or cable variants become easier to progress.\r\n\r\n## Single-Leg Squat\r\n\r\nSingle-leg squats can produce substantial gluteus medius activation because the muscle must resist the external hip-adduction moment and stabilize the pelvis.\r\n\r\nExperimental studies commonly identify single-leg squat variants as high-activation gluteus medius exercises.\r\n\r\nHowever, the exercise is not gluteus-medius-specific.\r\n\r\nDemand is shared with:\r\n\r\n- gluteus maximus;\r\n- quadriceps;\r\n- adductors;\r\n- plantarflexors;\r\n- trunk stabilizers.\r\n\r\nBalance and technique can also limit performance before gluteus medius reaches local muscular failure.\r\n\r\nTherefore single-leg compounds are valuable functional loading but less efficient isolation hypertrophy exercises.\r\n\r\n## Split Squat\r\n\r\nSplit squats also require frontal-plane pelvic control.\r\n\r\nGluteus medius contributes isometrically to:\r\n\r\n- stabilize the stance hip;\r\n- control femoral adduction;\r\n- maintain pelvic position.\r\n\r\nHowever, most external work occurs through:\r\n\r\n- knee extension;\r\n- hip extension.\r\n\r\nThus gluteus medius should receive meaningful stabilization demand but should not be treated as a prime mover equivalent to direct hip abduction.\r\n\r\n## Step-Up\r\n\r\nStep-ups create similar demands.\r\n\r\nAs the body is supported primarily by one leg, the stance-side abductors help maintain pelvic orientation.\r\n\r\nHigher step heights and external loading can increase hip demands, but the exact gluteus medius stimulus depends strongly on:\r\n\r\n- trunk lean;\r\n- pelvis control;\r\n- step width;\r\n- external load location.\r\n\r\n## Lateral Step-Up\r\n\r\nLateral step-ups can increase frontal-plane hip demand compared with some sagittal-plane variants.\r\n\r\nThey remain compound exercises involving:\r\n\r\n- quadriceps;\r\n- gluteus maximus;\r\n- gluteus medius.\r\n\r\nThey can provide useful functional overload but are not as standardized for gluteus medius hypertrophy as direct machine abduction.\r\n\r\n## Single-Leg Standing\r\n\r\nEven simple single-leg stance recruits gluteus medius because body weight creates an external adduction moment.\r\n\r\nAdding load can increase this requirement.\r\n\r\nHowever, an isometric stance is usually more valuable for:\r\n\r\n- stability;\r\n- endurance;\r\n- rehabilitation;\r\n\r\nthan for maximizing hypertrophic mechanical tension.\r\n\r\n## Lateral Band Walk\r\n\r\nLateral band walks can produce substantial gluteus medius activation.\r\n\r\nThey are useful for:\r\n\r\n- warm-up;\r\n- motor-control work;\r\n- moderate strengthening;\r\n- athletic preparation.\r\n\r\nFor advanced hypertrophy, their disadvantages include:\r\n\r\n- difficult external-load quantification;\r\n- limited progressive overload;\r\n- fatigue distribution across both limbs;\r\n- changing band tension across each step.\r\n\r\nTherefore high EMG should not automatically make them the preferred hypertrophy exercise.\r\n\r\n## Hip Rotation Cues\r\n\r\nAttempts to "isolate posterior glute medius" by aggressively rotating the femur should be treated cautiously.\r\n\r\nRegional recruitment can change with hip position, but strong rotational manipulation also changes:\r\n\r\n- moment arms;\r\n- comfort;\r\n- contribution of gluteus maximus/minimus;\r\n- tensor fasciae latae.\r\n\r\nFor whole-muscle hypertrophy, maximizing stable abduction torque is generally more important than chasing one regional orientation.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Direct Abduction Has High Value\r\n\r\nUnlike gluteus maximus, gluteus medius is not necessarily exposed to a large dynamic hypertrophic stimulus from ordinary bilateral squats and leg presses.\r\n\r\nThose exercises require pelvic stabilization, but bilateral stance greatly reduces the frontal-plane body-weight moment compared with single-leg stance.\r\n\r\nTherefore direct hip-abduction work often provides useful non-redundant stimulus.\r\n\r\n## Weekly Volume\r\n\r\nA practical starting range when gluteus medius hypertrophy is a meaningful priority is approximately:\r\n\r\n- **4-8 direct hard hip-abduction sets per week**.\r\n\r\nAdditional stabilization exposure can come from:\r\n\r\n- split squats;\r\n- lunges;\r\n- step-ups;\r\n- single-leg tasks.\r\n\r\nThese should generally receive partial rather than full direct-set credit.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nTwo to three weekly exposures are practical.\r\n\r\nDirect abduction exercises have:\r\n\r\n- low systemic fatigue;\r\n- little axial loading;\r\n- relatively low interference with major compounds.\r\n\r\nThis makes them easy to distribute across the week.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\nHip-abduction exercises generally work well in moderate and high repetition ranges:\r\n\r\n- 8-15 reps for heavier machine work;\r\n- 12-20 reps as a practical default;\r\n- 15-30+ reps for lighter cable or machine work.\r\n\r\nThere is no special high-repetition requirement for gluteus medius.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nStable machine and cable abduction can generally be trained close to failure:\r\n\r\n- **0-2 RIR** is practical.\r\n\r\nFailure should occur through loss of productive hip abduction rather than progressively greater:\r\n\r\n- pelvic hiking;\r\n- trunk lean;\r\n- torso rotation.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Order\r\n\r\nWhen gluteus medius is a secondary target, direct abduction works well late in a lower-body session.\r\n\r\nWhen it is a priority, placing direct work earlier can improve:\r\n\r\n- load;\r\n- ROM;\r\n- technique;\r\n- proximity to true local failure.\r\n\r\nThere is no physiological requirement to perform abduction exercises only as a warm-up.\r\n\r\n## Stability vs Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nA major programming distinction is required between:\r\n\r\n### Stability-Oriented Exercises\r\n\r\nExamples:\r\n\r\n- single-leg balance;\r\n- lateral band walks;\r\n- low-load step-downs.\r\n\r\nThese emphasize:\r\n\r\n- motor control;\r\n- endurance;\r\n- coordinated pelvic stabilization.\r\n\r\n### Hypertrophy-Oriented Exercises\r\n\r\nExamples:\r\n\r\n- loaded abduction machine;\r\n- supported cable abduction.\r\n\r\nThese emphasize:\r\n\r\n- high mechanical tension;\r\n- progression;\r\n- local muscular failure.\r\n\r\nHigh functional value does not necessarily imply high hypertrophic efficiency.\r\n\r\n## Avoid Redundant "Activation" Volume\r\n\r\nBecause gluteus medius is commonly targeted through activation drills, programs can accumulate many low-load exercises:\r\n\r\n- clamshells;\r\n- band walks;\r\n- side steps;\r\n- fire hydrants;\r\n- bodyweight abductions.\r\n\r\nFor an advanced trainee, replacing some of this redundancy with one progressively overloaded abduction exercise is often more coherent.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## It Works Hardest When the Other Leg Leaves the Ground\r\n\r\nOne of the clearest demonstrations of gluteus medius function is ordinary walking.\r\n\r\nDuring single-leg support, the stance-side gluteus medius prevents the unsupported side of the pelvis from dropping excessively.\r\n\r\nThus the muscle can become mechanically critical precisely when the leg itself is not visibly abducting.\r\n\r\n## Trendelenburg Sign Reflects Its Mechanical Role\r\n\r\nInsufficient stance-side hip-abductor function can produce a **Trendelenburg pattern**, where the contralateral side of the pelvis drops during unilateral stance.\r\n\r\nThis is a direct demonstration of the abductor system's role in pelvic stabilization.\r\n\r\nHowever, the sign can arise from multiple causes and should not automatically be interpreted as isolated gluteus medius weakness.\r\n\r\n## Different Regions Can Rotate the Hip in Opposite Directions\r\n\r\nAnterior gluteus medius fibers can contribute to internal rotation, while posterior fibers can contribute to external rotation.\r\n\r\nThis apparent contradiction results from the fan-shaped architecture.\r\n\r\nIt is an excellent example of why large muscles should not always be represented by one fixed secondary action.\r\n\r\n## The Muscle Is Not Simply a Smaller Gluteus Maximus\r\n\r\nGluteus medius differs from gluteus maximus in:\r\n\r\n- position;\r\n- insertion;\r\n- innervation;\r\n- dominant joint action;\r\n- role during gait.\r\n\r\nIts defining mechanical task is frontal-plane control rather than high-force sagittal-plane extension.\r\n\r\n## High EMG Does Not Equal Best Hypertrophy Exercise\r\n\r\nExercises such as:\r\n\r\n- side planks;\r\n- single-leg squats;\r\n- lateral band tasks\r\n\r\ncan generate high gluteus medius EMG.\r\n\r\nThis is useful evidence for recruitment but does not prove they produce the greatest hypertrophy.\r\n\r\nA stable loaded machine may permit substantially greater progressive mechanical tension despite appearing less "functional."\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\n`gluteus_medius` should primarily receive demand from:\r\n\r\n1. **hip-abduction torque**;\r\n2. **anti-adduction / pelvic-stabilization torque**;\r\n3. secondary regional rotational demands.\r\n\r\nThis distinction between dynamic abduction and isometric anti-adduction is important.\r\n\r\n## Single-Leg Mechanical Model\r\n\r\nDuring single-leg stance:\r\n\r\n`M_external_adduction ≈ BW_component × horizontal_COM_distance_from_hip`\r\n\r\nThe stance-side abductor system must generate an opposing moment.\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`F_abductors ≈ M_external_adduction / internal_abductor_moment_arm`\r\n\r\nBecause the internal hip-abductor moment arm is relatively short, muscle force can become substantial.\r\n\r\nTherefore single-leg tasks deserve meaningful gluteus medius demand even when:\r\n\r\n`hip_abduction_ROM ≈ 0`.\r\n\r\n## Bilateral vs Unilateral Exercises\r\n\r\n### Bilateral Squat\r\n\r\nLow-to-moderate gluteus medius stabilization demand.\r\n\r\nPrimary work is performed by:\r\n\r\n- quadriceps;\r\n- gluteus maximus;\r\n- adductor magnus.\r\n\r\n### 45° Leg Press\r\n\r\nGenerally low gluteus medius demand because the pelvis and trunk are externally supported and frontal-plane stabilization requirements are small.\r\n\r\n### Split Squat\r\n\r\nModerate-to-high stance-side gluteus medius stabilization demand.\r\n\r\n### Single-Leg Squat\r\n\r\nHigh gluteus medius anti-adduction demand.\r\n\r\n### Step-Up\r\n\r\nModerate-to-high demand depending on geometry and load.\r\n\r\n### Hip-Abduction Machine\r\n\r\nVery high direct gluteus medius demand.\r\n\r\n### Standing Cable Hip Abduction\r\n\r\nVery high direct demand if balance does not become limiting.\r\n\r\n### Copenhagen Plank\r\n\r\nGluteus medius of the supporting/stabilizing side can contribute, but the principal local target is the adductor system on the working side depending on execution.\r\n\r\nIt should not receive an automatically high dynamic abduction allocation.\r\n\r\n### Pallof Press\r\n\r\nLow-to-moderate gluteus medius stabilization demand depending on stance width and unilateral loading.\r\n\r\n## Force Sharing With Gluteus Minimus and TFL\r\n\r\nHip abduction torque is not produced by gluteus medius alone.\r\n\r\nImportant synergists include:\r\n\r\n- `gluteus_minimus`;\r\n- tensor fasciae latae;\r\n- superior fibers of gluteus maximus.\r\n\r\nTherefore direct hip-abduction demand should be distributed across an abductor system rather than assigned exclusively to gluteus medius.\r\n\r\nGluteus medius will usually receive the largest share because of its size and favorable architecture.\r\n\r\n## Regional Rotation\r\n\r\nA simplified model can treat whole-muscle rotational demand conservatively.\r\n\r\nIf future regional modelling is introduced:\r\n\r\n### Anterior Region\r\n\r\nGreater weighting toward:\r\n- internal rotation;\r\n- flexion.\r\n\r\n### Posterior Region\r\n\r\nGreater weighting toward:\r\n- external rotation;\r\n- extension.\r\n\r\nBut a whole-muscle `gluteus_medius` scalar should not receive a very large net rotation coefficient because opposing regional moments can partially offset.\r\n\r\n## Length-Tension Model\r\n\r\nA simple first approximation is:\r\n\r\n- hip adduction → longer;\r\n- neutral → intermediate;\r\n- hip abduction → shorter.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_gluteus_medius ≈ f(hip_abduction_adduction_angle)`\r\n\r\nHowever, this is incomplete.\r\n\r\nExperimental anatomy shows meaningful excursion with hip flexion, and rotation can also change fiber-specific length.\r\n\r\nA more complete model would therefore be:\r\n\r\n`E_LT = f(abduction/adduction, flexion/extension, rotation)`\r\n\r\nwith the frontal-plane component receiving the largest weight.\r\n\r\n## Pelvic Position\r\n\r\nApparent hip-abduction ROM can be contaminated by pelvic motion.\r\n\r\nDuring a standing cable abduction:\r\n\r\n- true femoral abduction relative to the pelvis lengthens/shortens the target appropriately;\r\n- pelvic hiking can move the foot without equivalent glenohumeral-style — here femoroacetabular — joint excursion.\r\n\r\nTherefore exercise modelling should ideally consider **femur relative to pelvis**, not foot displacement alone.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nA high `smh_factor` should be interpreted more cautiously here than for gastrocnemius.\r\n\r\nCurrent evidence supports:\r\n\r\n- substantial length change with hip position;\r\n- plausible long-length loading through adduction;\r\n- meaningful excursion with hip flexion.\r\n\r\nBut direct evidence demonstrating superior gluteus-medius hypertrophy specifically from long-length training remains limited.\r\n\r\nThe strongest recommendation is therefore:\r\n\r\n> ensure high mechanical tension through a broad abduction ROM, including meaningful loading near neutral or modest adduction, rather than assigning a large unvalidated stretch multiplier.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **iliac origin and greater-trochanter insertion:** high;\r\n- **superior-gluteal-nerve innervation:** high;\r\n- **primary hip-abduction function:** high;\r\n- **pelvic stabilization during unilateral stance:** high;\r\n- **regional anterior/posterior functional differences:** well supported anatomically and biomechanically;\r\n- **substantial recruitment during side-lying abduction and single-leg tasks:** supported by EMG literature;\r\n- **hip-flexion-dependent musculotendinous excursion:** directly demonstrated anatomically;\r\n- **direct resistance-training hypertrophy evidence:** sparse;\r\n- **stretch-mediated hypertrophy magnitude:** poorly established;\r\n- **precise regional recruitment coefficients:** strongly dependent on hip position.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **gluteus medius = a fan-shaped, regionally heterogeneous hip abductor whose major functional role is generation of frontal-plane torque, particularly isometric anti-adduction torque that stabilizes the pelvis during unilateral support.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/GluteusMedius}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	7.33	20.50	0.937	121.44	113.75
20	triceps_lateral_head	Musculus triceps brachii caput laterale	Upper	Triceps	440.00	416.67	22.00	Pennate	0.250	0.750	low	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\nThe **lateral head of triceps brachii** is one of the three heads of the triceps brachii complex:\r\n\r\n- `triceps_lateral_head`;\r\n- `triceps_medial_head`;\r\n- `triceps_long_head`.\r\n\r\nIts dominant function is:\r\n\r\n- **elbow extension**.\r\n\r\nUnlike the long head, the lateral head does **not** cross the shoulder joint.\r\n\r\nTherefore its muscle length and force capacity are governed primarily by:\r\n\r\n- elbow angle;\r\n- local triceps tendon geometry;\r\n\r\nand are not directly altered by shoulder flexion or extension.\r\n\r\nThis makes it mechanically closer to the medial head than to the long head.\r\n\r\nThe lateral head is also visually important because it forms much of the characteristic lateral contour of the posterior upper arm.\r\n\r\nFor Agonez, the most useful abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **triceps lateral head = a monoarticular, relatively high-force elbow extensor whose recruitment rises with elbow-extension demand and high-force tasks, while shoulder position affects it mainly indirectly through redistribution of force among the three triceps heads rather than by changing its own muscle length.**\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\n## Origin\r\n\r\nThe lateral head originates from the posterior surface of the humerus, primarily:\r\n\r\n- superior to the radial groove;\r\n- lateral/posterior humeral shaft;\r\n- adjacent intermuscular septal structures.\r\n\r\nIts origin lies distal to the shoulder joint.\r\n\r\nTherefore it does not cross the glenohumeral joint.\r\n\r\n## Insertion\r\n\r\nThe lateral head joins the common triceps tendon inserting onto the:\r\n\r\n- **olecranon process of the ulna**.\r\n\r\nForce generated by the lateral head is therefore transmitted through the common triceps tendon to produce elbow-extension torque.\r\n\r\n## Relationship With the Other Heads\r\n\r\n### Lateral Head\r\n\r\n- monoarticular;\r\n- elbow extension only;\r\n- relatively superficial;\r\n- large visible contribution to lateral posterior upper arm.\r\n\r\n### Medial Head\r\n\r\n- monoarticular;\r\n- elbow extension;\r\n- deeper;\r\n- often considered a highly consistent contributor across force levels and joint configurations.\r\n\r\n### Long Head\r\n\r\n- biarticular;\r\n- elbow extension;\r\n- also crosses the shoulder;\r\n- shoulder extension/adduction contribution;\r\n- muscle length changes substantially with shoulder angle.\r\n\r\nThis distinction is central to exercise modelling.\r\n\r\nShoulder position directly changes `E_LT` for the long head but should have little direct effect on `E_LT` for the lateral head.\r\n\r\n## Architecture\r\n\r\nThe lateral head has a pennate architecture that supports substantial force production within a relatively compact muscle volume.\r\n\r\nIts architecture differs from the long head and medial head, reinforcing the idea that triceps brachii is not one homogeneous muscle.\r\n\r\nClassic architectural studies of the elbow musculature demonstrate meaningful differences among elbow extensors in:\r\n\r\n- fascicle length;\r\n- pennation;\r\n- PCSA;\r\n- moment-generating capacity. :contentReference[oaicite:0]{index=0}\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nThe lateral head is innervated by the **radial nerve**, primarily from approximately:\r\n\r\n- C6;\r\n- C7;\r\n- C8.\r\n\r\nThe radial nerve supplies all three triceps heads, though individual motor branches enter them separately.\r\n\r\nThis permits different recruitment patterns among:\r\n\r\n- lateral;\r\n- medial;\r\n- long heads\r\n\r\nduring different elbow and shoulder configurations.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Elbow Extension\r\n\r\nThe primary and essentially exclusive major joint action of the lateral head is **elbow extension**.\r\n\r\nIt generates force through the common triceps tendon to rotate the ulna relative to the humerus.\r\n\r\nIt acts synergistically with:\r\n\r\n- medial head;\r\n- long head;\r\n- anconeus.\r\n\r\nBecause the lateral head does not cross the shoulder, its direct mechanical contribution can be represented approximately as:\r\n\r\n`F_lateral = f(elbow_angle, activation, muscle_length, moment_arm)`\r\n\r\nwithout a direct shoulder-length term.\r\n\r\n## High-Force Elbow Extension\r\n\r\nThe lateral head is particularly important during substantial elbow-extension tasks.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- pressing;\r\n- cable pushdowns;\r\n- heavy triceps extensions;\r\n- dips.\r\n\r\nIt should not be described simplistically as a head that activates only under "heavy loads," because all three heads can contribute across a broad range of forces.\r\n\r\nHowever, experimental work shows that the force and activation distribution among the three heads changes with joint configuration and task intensity rather than remaining fixed. :contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}\r\n\r\n## Elbow-Angle Dependence\r\n\r\nThe total triceps moment arm changes substantially with elbow angle.\r\n\r\nMRI-based human measurements found that the triceps tendon moment arm was larger near more extended elbow positions and smaller at deeper flexion angles. :contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`T_elbow = F_triceps × r_triceps(elbow_angle)`\r\n\r\nrather than:\r\n\r\n`T_elbow = F × constant_moment_arm`.\r\n\r\nThis matters for the lateral head even though it is monoarticular.\r\n\r\n## No Direct Shoulder Torque\r\n\r\nBecause the lateral head originates on the humerus rather than the scapula:\r\n\r\n- shoulder flexion does not lengthen it proximally;\r\n- shoulder extension does not shorten it proximally;\r\n- it does not generate shoulder-extension torque.\r\n\r\nThis sharply distinguishes it from the long head.\r\n\r\nThus in an overhead triceps extension:\r\n\r\n- lateral head still extends the elbow;\r\n- long head additionally changes length because the shoulder is elevated.\r\n\r\n# Force Sharing Among Triceps Heads\r\n\r\nThe three heads do not contribute fixed proportions of elbow-extension force.\r\n\r\nExperimental measurements comparing the heads across shoulder elevation show changing recruitment and estimated force distribution. :contentReference[oaicite:3]{index=3}\r\n\r\nThis means:\r\n\r\n`triceps_force_share != constant`\r\n\r\nand instead depends on:\r\n\r\n- shoulder angle;\r\n- elbow angle;\r\n- force level;\r\n- task.\r\n\r\n## Shoulder Position\r\n\r\nAlthough shoulder position does not directly change lateral-head length, it changes the mechanical state of the long head.\r\n\r\nAs the shoulder elevates:\r\n\r\n- long-head operating length changes;\r\n- its elbow-extension contribution can change;\r\n- medial/lateral heads may compensate.\r\n\r\nTherefore shoulder angle can affect **relative lateral-head force share indirectly**.\r\n\r\nThis distinction is important:\r\n\r\n> shoulder angle changes lateral-head recruitment through system-level force sharing, not because the lateral head itself crosses the shoulder.\r\n\r\n## Elbow Position\r\n\r\nElbow position directly changes:\r\n\r\n- lateral-head muscle-tendon length;\r\n- triceps tendon leverage;\r\n- force-angle relationship.\r\n\r\nTherefore elbow angle should be the dominant `E_LT` variable for this head.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nThe lateral head is monoarticular.\r\n\r\nIts length is therefore manipulated primarily through **elbow flexion and extension**.\r\n\r\n## Elbow Flexion Lengthens the Lateral Head\r\n\r\nBecause the lateral head extends the elbow:\r\n\r\n- elbow flexion lengthens it;\r\n- elbow extension shortens it.\r\n\r\nTherefore the bottom position of an elbow-extension exercise places it at greater length.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- cable pushdown with deeply flexed elbow;\r\n- skull crusher;\r\n- overhead extension.\r\n\r\nFor the lateral head, these exercises differ much less in proximal muscle length than they do for the long head.\r\n\r\n## Shoulder Elevation Does Not Directly Lengthen It\r\n\r\nThis point is crucial.\r\n\r\nOverhead elbow extension dramatically lengthens the **long head** because it crosses the shoulder.\r\n\r\nThe lateral head does not.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_lateral_overhead ≈ E_LT_lateral_neutral`\r\n\r\nat the same elbow angle, ignoring smaller indirect effects from humeral/tendon geometry.\r\n\r\n## Overhead vs Neutral Extension Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nA direct 12-week within-subject study found substantially greater overall triceps hypertrophy after cable elbow extension performed overhead than with the arm in a neutral position, with the strongest mechanistic rationale and pronounced effect involving the biarticular long head. :contentReference[oaicite:4]{index=4}\r\n\r\nThis result should **not** be interpreted as evidence that shoulder flexion specifically lengthens the lateral head.\r\n\r\nFor the lateral head, any difference between overhead and neutral-arm training may instead arise from:\r\n\r\n- altered force sharing;\r\n- exercise resistance profile;\r\n- systemic triceps recruitment;\r\n- regional interactions.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n> the overhead long-length advantage is highly specific mechanically to the long head and should not simply be copied onto the lateral head's `E_LT`.\r\n\r\n## Deep Elbow Flexion\r\n\r\nFor the lateral head, the most relevant long-length state is simply substantial elbow flexion.\r\n\r\nAn exercise that maintains high external extension torque near deep elbow flexion can therefore provide long-length tension.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nThe defensible model is:\r\n\r\n- elbow flexion lengthens lateral head;\r\n- shoulder elevation does not directly lengthen it;\r\n- long-length elbow-extension loading is mechanically plausible;\r\n- direct lateral-head-specific evidence for a special SMH advantage is much weaker than for the long head.\r\n\r\nTherefore a moderate `smh_factor` may be justified, but not a special overhead bonus based on shoulder position alone.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Cable Pushdown\r\n\r\nCable pushdowns are highly effective for the lateral head because they directly generate elbow-extension torque.\r\n\r\nAdvantages include:\r\n\r\n- high stability;\r\n- low shoulder involvement;\r\n- easy progressive overload;\r\n- straightforward proximity to failure.\r\n\r\nThe lateral and medial heads can contribute strongly because their force production is not compromised by shoulder position.\r\n\r\n## Straight-Bar Pushdown\r\n\r\nA straight bar provides a fixed pronated grip.\r\n\r\nForearm orientation has little direct effect on triceps head function because the triceps inserts on the ulna and does not generate forearm pronation/supination.\r\n\r\nTherefore pronated versus neutral grip should not be treated as a strong lateral-head selector.\r\n\r\nDifferences usually arise from:\r\n\r\n- comfort;\r\n- elbow position;\r\n- stability;\r\n- achievable loading.\r\n\r\n## Rope Pushdown\r\n\r\nA rope attachment allows more freedom at:\r\n\r\n- wrist;\r\n- forearm;\r\n- shoulder.\r\n\r\nThis can improve comfort and allow a natural terminal elbow-extension position.\r\n\r\nHowever:\r\n\r\n> rope pushdown is not inherently a lateral-head isolation exercise.\r\n\r\nAll three triceps heads can contribute.\r\n\r\n## Overhead Rope Extension\r\n\r\nOverhead extension strongly changes the long-head length state.\r\n\r\nThe lateral head still contributes to elbow extension, but shoulder elevation provides no unique direct stretch to it.\r\n\r\nTherefore compared with pushdown:\r\n\r\n- long-head relative stimulus may increase substantially;\r\n- lateral-head stimulus remains high but does not receive the same proximal long-length benefit.\r\n\r\n## Skull Crusher\r\n\r\nA lying triceps extension generates high elbow-extension demand and can load the lateral head through substantial elbow flexion.\r\n\r\nThe exact resistance profile depends on:\r\n\r\n- upper-arm angle;\r\n- bar path;\r\n- forearm orientation.\r\n\r\nAllowing the upper arm to angle somewhat backward can increase long-head length and alter elbow torque, but the lateral head remains primarily dependent on elbow flexion.\r\n\r\n## California Press\r\n\r\nThe California press combines:\r\n\r\n- elbow extension;\r\n- shoulder motion;\r\n- pressing mechanics.\r\n\r\nThe lateral head should receive high demand because large elbow-extension torque is required.\r\n\r\nHowever, compared with isolated extensions, force is distributed across:\r\n\r\n- triceps;\r\n- pectoralis major;\r\n- anterior deltoid.\r\n\r\nIt remains an excellent heavy triceps-dominant compound movement.\r\n\r\n## Bench Press\r\n\r\nBench pressing requires substantial elbow extension.\r\n\r\nTherefore the lateral head contributes meaningfully.\r\n\r\nThe degree depends on:\r\n\r\n- grip width;\r\n- elbow path;\r\n- load;\r\n- sticking-region mechanics.\r\n\r\nNarrower grip widths tend to increase triceps activity relative to wider grips, although changes are not equivalent to isolation of any particular triceps head. :contentReference[oaicite:5]{index=5}\r\n\r\n## Incline Press\r\n\r\nIncline pressing still imposes substantial elbow-extension demand.\r\n\r\nThus the lateral head remains active.\r\n\r\nShoulder elevation changes the long-head force-sharing context but not the lateral head's direct length.\r\n\r\n## Overhead Press\r\n\r\nOverhead press requires high elbow-extension torque, especially during substantial portions of the ascent.\r\n\r\nThe lateral head contributes alongside:\r\n\r\n- medial head;\r\n- long head.\r\n\r\nAt high shoulder elevations, experimental data indicate that relative force sharing among the triceps heads can shift, with the medial head becoming particularly important. :contentReference[oaicite:6]{index=6}\r\n\r\nThus overhead pressing should not automatically receive an exaggerated lateral-head coefficient.\r\n\r\n## Dips\r\n\r\nDips impose large elbow-extension torque.\r\n\r\nThe lateral head can therefore contribute strongly.\r\n\r\nDemand is shared with:\r\n\r\n- triceps medial head;\r\n- long head;\r\n- pectoralis major;\r\n- anterior deltoid.\r\n\r\nForward lean affects chest-versus-triceps distribution at the shoulder but does not remove elbow-extension demand.\r\n\r\n## Lockout\r\n\r\nNear terminal elbow extension, the external moment may become small in some exercises depending on resistance geometry.\r\n\r\nTherefore the subjective "hard squeeze" at lockout does not necessarily imply maximum lateral-head force.\r\n\r\nExercise torque profile remains more important than perceived contraction.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Lateral Head Usually Receives Plenty of Compound Volume\r\n\r\nBecause it is active during nearly all loaded elbow extension, substantial lateral-head training can come from:\r\n\r\n- presses;\r\n- dips;\r\n- direct triceps extensions.\r\n\r\nDedicated "lateral head isolation" is generally unnecessary.\r\n\r\n## Direct Extension Is Still Useful\r\n\r\nPressing can provide high triceps force, but direct elbow extension allows:\r\n\r\n- greater local specificity;\r\n- lower pectoral/deltoid limitation;\r\n- easier accumulation of triceps volume.\r\n\r\nA pushdown is therefore a very efficient lateral/medial-head accessory.\r\n\r\n## Weekly Volume\r\n\r\nA practical triceps-wide starting point is approximately:\r\n\r\n- **6-12 hard effective sets per week**,\r\n\r\ncounting:\r\n\r\n- direct extensions strongly;\r\n- compound presses as partial-to-substantial triceps work depending on execution.\r\n\r\nThere is generally no need for a separate lateral-head volume quota.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nTwo to three exposures per week are practical.\r\n\r\nRecovery overlaps with:\r\n\r\n- chest pressing;\r\n- overhead pressing;\r\n- dips.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\n### Heavy Compound Pressing\r\n\r\nUseful:\r\n- 4-8;\r\n- 6-10 reps.\r\n\r\n### Direct Elbow Extension\r\n\r\nUseful:\r\n- 8-15;\r\n- 10-20;\r\n- 15-25 reps.\r\n\r\nThere is no evidence that the lateral head uniquely requires one repetition range.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nStable cable pushdowns can be trained very close to failure:\r\n\r\n- approximately 0-2 RIR.\r\n\r\nHeavy compound pressing generally benefits from slightly more technical reserve.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Selection\r\n\r\nA comprehensive triceps program does not need a separate exercise for each head.\r\n\r\nA useful combination might include:\r\n\r\n1. heavy press or California press;\r\n2. overhead extension;\r\n3. pushdown if additional triceps volume is needed.\r\n\r\nThis covers:\r\n\r\n- high-force elbow extension;\r\n- long-head-specific shoulder-position exposure;\r\n- stable monoarticular elbow extension.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## The Lateral Head Does Not Cross the Shoulder\r\n\r\nThis is the most important distinction from the long head.\r\n\r\nChanging shoulder position does not directly stretch the lateral head.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n> "overhead extensions stretch all triceps heads more"\r\n\r\nis anatomically incorrect.\r\n\r\nThey specifically increase proximal length of the **long head**.\r\n\r\n## Shoulder Angle Can Still Change Its Recruitment\r\n\r\nAlthough the lateral head does not cross the shoulder, shoulder elevation can alter overall triceps force sharing because the long head's mechanical state changes.\r\n\r\nHuman experiments demonstrate different patterns of head-specific triceps force and activation across shoulder elevations. :contentReference[oaicite:7]{index=7}\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n`shoulder angle → indirect lateral-head force-share effect`\r\n\r\nbut not:\r\n\r\n`shoulder angle → direct lateral-head length effect`.\r\n\r\n## The Triceps Moment Arm Changes With Elbow Angle\r\n\r\nHuman MRI measurements found larger triceps moment arms in more extended elbow positions and smaller moment arms with deeper flexion. :contentReference[oaicite:8]{index=8}\r\n\r\nThis means the same muscle force does not create the same elbow torque across ROM.\r\n\r\n## Grip Orientation Does Not Anatomically Select the Lateral Head\r\n\r\nThe triceps inserts onto the ulna.\r\n\r\nForearm rotation occurs mainly through the radius rotating around the ulna.\r\n\r\nTherefore changing:\r\n\r\n- pronated;\r\n- neutral;\r\n- supinated\r\n\r\ngrip does not fundamentally change the lateral head's anatomical action.\r\n\r\nGrip changes may still alter:\r\n\r\n- comfort;\r\n- external lever geometry;\r\n- usable load.\r\n\r\nBut "pronated grip = lateral head" should not be treated as a robust anatomical rule.\r\n\r\n## The Three Heads Are Not Fixed-Percentage Synergists\r\n\r\nExperimental work shows that relative activation/force changes with shoulder position and elbow angle. :contentReference[oaicite:9]{index=9}\r\n\r\nTherefore assigning something like:\r\n\r\n`lateral 40% + medial 30% + long 30%`\r\n\r\nto every elbow-extension exercise would be overly simplistic.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\n`triceps_lateral_head` should receive demand almost entirely from:\r\n\r\n1. **elbow-extension torque**;\r\n2. secondary elbow-joint stabilization.\r\n\r\nIt should receive approximately zero direct:\r\n\r\n- shoulder-extension torque;\r\n- shoulder-adduction torque.\r\n\r\nThose shoulder actions belong only to `triceps_long_head` within the triceps complex.\r\n\r\n## Length-Tension Model\r\n\r\nA first-order model is straightforward:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_lateral = f(elbow_flexion)`\r\n\r\n### Long\r\n\r\n- elbow flexed.\r\n\r\n### Intermediate\r\n\r\n- mid-range elbow angle.\r\n\r\n### Short\r\n\r\n- elbow near full extension.\r\n\r\nShoulder angle should not directly enter the muscle-length function.\r\n\r\nThis is a major difference from:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_long_head = f(elbow_angle, shoulder_angle)`.\r\n\r\n## Shoulder-Angle Force-Sharing Modifier\r\n\r\nShoulder angle may still influence recruitment indirectly.\r\n\r\nA better architecture is:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_lateral = f(elbow_angle)`\r\n\r\nbut:\r\n\r\n`force_share_lateral =\r\n    f(elbow_angle,\r\n      shoulder_angle,\r\n      task,\r\n      force_level)`\r\n\r\nbecause shoulder position changes the mechanical status of the long head and therefore how total elbow-extension torque is distributed.\r\n\r\nThis cleanly separates:\r\n\r\n- muscle length;\r\n- neural/mechanical force sharing.\r\n\r\n## Elbow Moment Arm\r\n\r\nHuman MRI measurements show that the triceps tendon moment arm varies with elbow angle, increasing from deeper flexion toward more extended configurations. :contentReference[oaicite:10]{index=10}\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n`E_MA_lateral ≈ f(elbow_angle)`.\r\n\r\nSince all heads converge into the distal extensor mechanism, a shared first-order triceps tendon moment-arm curve is a reasonable KISS approximation.\r\n\r\n## Force Sharing During Elbow Extension\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`T_elbow_extension =\r\n    F_lateral × r_lateral\r\n  + F_medial × r_medial\r\n  + F_long × r_long\r\n  + F_anconeus × r_anconeus`\r\n\r\nThe three triceps forces should not be assumed equal.\r\n\r\nRelative allocation can vary by joint configuration. :contentReference[oaicite:11]{index=11}\r\n\r\n## Exercise Allocation\r\n\r\n### Standing Cable Pushdown\r\n\r\nVery high:\r\n- `triceps_lateral_head`;\r\n- `triceps_medial_head`.\r\n\r\nHigh:\r\n- `triceps_long_head`.\r\n\r\nExact sharing depends on arm position and force level.\r\n\r\n### Standing Cable Overhead Rope Triceps Extension\r\n\r\nHigh lateral-head demand.\r\n\r\nHigh medial-head demand.\r\n\r\nVery high long-head length exposure.\r\n\r\nDo **not** give the lateral head the same overhead `E_LT` bonus as the long head.\r\n\r\n### Flat Bench California Press\r\n\r\nVery high lateral-head demand through large elbow-extension torque.\r\n\r\nAlso very high:\r\n- long and medial triceps heads.\r\n\r\nMeaningful:\r\n- pectoralis major;\r\n- anterior deltoid.\r\n\r\n### Flat Bench Press\r\n\r\nModerate-to-high lateral-head demand.\r\n\r\nHigher with greater elbow-extension requirement, such as relatively narrower pressing configurations.\r\n\r\n### Smith Incline Bench Press\r\n\r\nModerate-to-high lateral-head demand.\r\n\r\nShoulder angle primarily changes shoulder-muscle and long-head mechanics; lateral-head direct action remains elbow extension.\r\n\r\n### Seated Dumbbell Overhead Press\r\n\r\nHigh elbow-extension contribution.\r\n\r\nLateral head meaningful-to-high, but relative head sharing changes at high shoulder elevation. Experimental work found distinct head-specific force patterns as shoulder elevation increased. :contentReference[oaicite:12]{index=12}\r\n\r\n### Weighted Forward-Lean Dip\r\n\r\nVery high total triceps demand.\r\n\r\nLateral head high.\r\n\r\nAlso high:\r\n- medial;\r\n- long head.\r\n\r\nForward lean raises pectoralis involvement without removing the large elbow-extension requirement.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Triceps Medial Head\r\n\r\nBoth lateral and medial heads are monoarticular elbow extensors.\r\n\r\nTherefore they share a relatively simple length model.\r\n\r\nTheir force distribution, however, is not identical.\r\n\r\nThe medial head appears to remain an important contributor across a broad range of shoulder elevations and can become especially important at high shoulder elevation. :contentReference[oaicite:13]{index=13}\r\n\r\nLateral-head allocation should therefore not simply absorb all monoarticular triceps demand.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Triceps Long Head\r\n\r\nThe long head is the main source of shoulder-position sensitivity.\r\n\r\n### Arm at Side\r\n\r\nLong head:\r\n- shorter proximally than overhead.\r\n\r\nLateral:\r\n- essentially unchanged proximally.\r\n\r\n### Arm Overhead\r\n\r\nLong head:\r\n- substantially lengthened.\r\n\r\nLateral:\r\n- no corresponding proximal lengthening.\r\n\r\nThis is the critical rationale for maintaining separate head rows in the database.\r\n\r\n## Overhead Extension Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nMaeo et al. directly compared overhead versus neutral-arm cable elbow extension for 12 weeks and found substantially greater triceps hypertrophy in the overhead condition, particularly relevant to the long head because shoulder elevation lengthens it. :contentReference[oaicite:14]{index=14}\r\n\r\nFor Agonez this should produce:\r\n\r\n`SMH/E_LT_long_head_overhead ↑↑`\r\n\r\nwhile:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_lateral_overhead ≈ driven by elbow angle only`.\r\n\r\nDo not propagate the long-head-specific biarticular mechanism to the lateral head.\r\n\r\n## Grip Modifier\r\n\r\nI would assign essentially **no direct grip-orientation modifier** to lateral-head recruitment.\r\n\r\nPronated, neutral and supinated attachments can alter comfort and execution, but anatomically the lateral head:\r\n\r\n- does not cross the radioulnar joints;\r\n- does not rotate the forearm.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`grip → lateral_head`\r\n\r\nshould be a second-order or zero modifier unless it changes elbow/shoulder geometry or achievable force.\r\n\r\n## FCSA Interpretation\r\n\r\nThe lateral head's FCSA can be treated relatively cleanly as elbow-extension force capacity.\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`F_max_lateral ≈ σ × FCSA_lateral`\r\n\r\nand:\r\n\r\n`T_lateral = F_lateral × r_elbow(angle)`.\r\n\r\nThis is substantially easier to interpret than antagonist-containing aggregates such as:\r\n\r\n- `rotator_cuffs`;\r\n- `pronators_supinators`.\r\n\r\n## Shared Tendon Caveat\r\n\r\nAlthough each head has distinct architecture and activation, their forces converge toward a common distal triceps tendon system.\r\n\r\nTherefore independent per-head moment arms are more difficult to define precisely than their separate database rows might imply.\r\n\r\nFor a KISS implementation, a common elbow-angle-dependent triceps moment arm combined with head-specific:\r\n\r\n- FCSA;\r\n- `E_LT`;\r\n- recruitment share\r\n\r\nis defensible.\r\n\r\n## ETU Interpretation\r\n\r\nLateral-head ETU should derive mainly from:\r\n\r\n- active elbow-extension force;\r\n- contraction duration;\r\n- ROM;\r\n- proximity to failure.\r\n\r\nUnlike long head, shoulder movement should contribute little direct lateral-head work.\r\n\r\nThus an exercise involving shoulder extension but little elbow extension should produce very little lateral-head ETU.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nFor the lateral head:\r\n\r\n`long muscle length ≈ flexed elbow`.\r\n\r\nThe overhead arm position should not itself raise `E_LT`.\r\n\r\nDirect evidence that long-length elbow positioning specifically enhances lateral-head hypertrophy remains limited.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n- recognize loaded elbow-flexion positions;\r\n- preserve substantial ROM;\r\n- use conservative SMH weighting;\r\n- reserve the large shoulder-position long-length bonus for `triceps_long_head`.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **posterior-humeral origin and olecranon insertion:** high;\r\n- **radial-nerve innervation:** high;\r\n- **monoarticular elbow-extension function:** very high;\r\n- **no direct glenohumeral action:** very high;\r\n- **elbow-angle-dependent triceps moment arm:** directly demonstrated with MRI; :contentReference[oaicite:15]{index=15}\r\n- **head-specific recruitment changes across joint configurations:** directly supported experimentally; :contentReference[oaicite:16]{index=16}\r\n- **greater total triceps hypertrophy from overhead versus neutral-arm extensions:** directly demonstrated longitudinally; :contentReference[oaicite:17]{index=17}\r\n- **specific large lateral-head benefit from shoulder-elevation-induced stretch:** not mechanically supported because the lateral head does not cross the shoulder;\r\n- **precise lateral-vs-medial force sharing:** task dependent;\r\n- **lateral-head-specific stretch-mediated hypertrophy magnitude:** insufficiently established.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **triceps lateral head = a monoarticular, force-oriented elbow extensor whose length and leverage depend primarily on elbow position; shoulder elevation may alter its relative recruitment through triceps force sharing, but unlike the long head it receives no direct proximal stretch or shoulder torque from changing arm position.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/TricepsBrachii}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	11.40	9.00	0.988	36.55	36.10
9	rotator_cuffs	Musculus: Teres Minor, Infraspinatus, Sucscapularis, Supraspinatus	Upper	Shoulder	1065.00	1008.52	60.00	Undefined	0.750	0.250	zero	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\n`rotator_cuffs` is a functional aggregate representing the four muscles of the **rotator cuff**:\r\n\r\n- **supraspinatus**;\r\n- **infraspinatus**;\r\n- **teres minor**;\r\n- **subscapularis**.\r\n\r\nThese muscles surround the glenohumeral joint and share one defining mechanical role:\r\n\r\n> **dynamic stabilization and centering of the humeral head within the glenoid during shoulder movement and force production.**\r\n\r\nThey also produce distinct rotational moments.\r\n\r\n### External rotators\r\n\r\nPrimarily:\r\n- infraspinatus;\r\n- teres minor.\r\n\r\n### Internal rotator\r\n\r\nPrimarily:\r\n- subscapularis.\r\n\r\n### Abduction contributor\r\n\r\nPrimarily:\r\n- supraspinatus.\r\n\r\nThe cuff therefore should not be treated as a single-direction prime mover.\r\n\r\nIt is an aggregate of muscles with partly opposing actions that cooperate to stabilize the glenohumeral joint.\r\n\r\nThis is especially important during exercises involving large forces from:\r\n\r\n- deltoid;\r\n- pectoralis major;\r\n- latissimus dorsi;\r\n- posterior deltoid.\r\n\r\nThose large muscles can generate substantial translational components at the humeral head.\r\n\r\nThe cuff contributes compressive and directional forces that keep the joint mechanically centered while permitting rotation.\r\n\r\nFor Agonez, the most useful abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **rotator cuff = the glenohumeral stabilizing and axial-rotation system whose demand rises with shoulder torque, humeral elevation and the need to counteract translation produced by larger prime movers.**\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\nThe four cuff muscles arise from the scapula and converge toward the proximal humerus, forming a tendinous cuff around the glenohumeral joint.\r\n\r\n## Supraspinatus\r\n\r\n### Origin\r\n\r\nSupraspinatus originates from the:\r\n\r\n- supraspinous fossa of the scapula.\r\n\r\n### Insertion\r\n\r\nIt inserts onto the superior facet of the:\r\n\r\n- greater tubercle of the humerus.\r\n\r\nIts tendon passes beneath the acromial region before inserting on the humerus.\r\n\r\nThis close relationship with the superior joint structures is important both mechanically and clinically.\r\n\r\n## Infraspinatus\r\n\r\n### Origin\r\n\r\nInfraspinatus originates from the:\r\n\r\n- infraspinous fossa of the scapula.\r\n\r\n### Insertion\r\n\r\nIt inserts onto the middle facet of the:\r\n\r\n- greater tubercle of the humerus.\r\n\r\nIts posterior line of pull gives it strong external-rotation leverage.\r\n\r\n## Teres Minor\r\n\r\n### Origin\r\n\r\nTeres minor originates from the:\r\n\r\n- lateral border of the scapula.\r\n\r\n### Insertion\r\n\r\nIt inserts onto the inferior facet of the:\r\n\r\n- greater tubercle of the humerus.\r\n\r\nIt lies inferior to infraspinatus and contributes to:\r\n\r\n- external rotation;\r\n- posterior joint stabilization.\r\n\r\n## Subscapularis\r\n\r\n### Origin\r\n\r\nSubscapularis originates broadly from the:\r\n\r\n- subscapular fossa on the anterior surface of the scapula.\r\n\r\n### Insertion\r\n\r\nIt inserts primarily onto the:\r\n\r\n- lesser tubercle of the humerus.\r\n\r\nIts anterior position gives it strong internal-rotation leverage.\r\n\r\nIt is the largest of the cuff muscles.\r\n\r\n## Tendinous Cuff\r\n\r\nThe tendons of the four muscles blend with the glenohumeral capsule and surround the humeral head.\r\n\r\nThe cuff is incomplete inferiorly compared with its:\r\n\r\n- superior;\r\n- anterior;\r\n- posterior\r\n\r\ncoverage.\r\n\r\nThis structural integration allows muscle force to influence both:\r\n\r\n- humeral rotation;\r\n- joint compression and centering.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\n## Supraspinatus\r\n\r\nInnervated by the **suprascapular nerve**, primarily:\r\n\r\n- C5;\r\n- C6.\r\n\r\n## Infraspinatus\r\n\r\nAlso innervated by the **suprascapular nerve**, primarily:\r\n\r\n- C5;\r\n- C6.\r\n\r\n## Teres Minor\r\n\r\nInnervated by the **axillary nerve**, primarily:\r\n\r\n- C5;\r\n- C6.\r\n\r\n## Subscapularis\r\n\r\nInnervated by the:\r\n\r\n- upper subscapular nerve;\r\n- lower subscapular nerve,\r\n\r\nprimarily from approximately:\r\n\r\n- C5;\r\n- C6.\r\n\r\nThe aggregate therefore combines muscles supplied by several branches of the brachial plexus rather than one unified motor nerve.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Dynamic Glenohumeral Stabilization\r\n\r\nThe most important aggregate function of the rotator cuff is **dynamic stabilization of the humeral head**.\r\n\r\nLarge shoulder muscles often generate both:\r\n\r\n- rotational torque;\r\n- translational force.\r\n\r\nFor example, deltoid force during early abduction has a substantial superiorly directed component.\r\n\r\nWithout opposing stabilization, this would tend to translate the humeral head superiorly.\r\n\r\nThe cuff helps maintain joint congruence through:\r\n\r\n- compression of the humeral head into the glenoid;\r\n- directional force balancing;\r\n- co-contraction across the joint.\r\n\r\nThis stabilizing role persists throughout many shoulder movements.\r\n\r\n## Concavity Compression\r\n\r\nThe glenoid is relatively shallow.\r\n\r\nThe cuff contributes to a mechanism often described as **concavity compression**:\r\n\r\n- cuff force compresses the humeral head into the glenoid concavity;\r\n- this increases resistance to translational displacement.\r\n\r\nThus the cuff is not simply rotating the humerus.\r\n\r\nIt helps create the stable joint platform from which larger muscles can produce torque.\r\n\r\n## Force Couples\r\n\r\nShoulder motion depends on coordinated force couples.\r\n\r\nA classic example occurs during elevation:\r\n\r\n- deltoid produces elevation torque but also superior translation;\r\n- cuff muscles generate compression and opposing components.\r\n\r\nThe net result can be:\r\n\r\n- rotation;\r\n- minimal unwanted translation.\r\n\r\nThis is why cuff demand often scales with prime-mover force.\r\n\r\n## Supraspinatus: Abduction\r\n\r\nSupraspinatus contributes to **glenohumeral abduction**.\r\n\r\nIt is especially mechanically relevant in lower and middle elevation ranges, but the classic claim that it alone initiates the first 15 degrees and then becomes irrelevant is too simplistic.\r\n\r\nSupraspinatus and deltoid cooperate throughout a substantial portion of elevation.\r\n\r\nIts role also includes humeral-head compression and stabilization.\r\n\r\n## Infraspinatus: External Rotation\r\n\r\nInfraspinatus is one of the principal **external rotators of the humerus**.\r\n\r\nIt also provides important posterior stabilizing force.\r\n\r\nIt is highly relevant during:\r\n\r\n- face pulls;\r\n- external-rotation exercises;\r\n- pressing stabilization;\r\n- pulling stabilization.\r\n\r\n## Teres Minor: External Rotation\r\n\r\nTeres minor also produces **external rotation**, particularly depending on arm elevation.\r\n\r\nIts relative contribution differs from infraspinatus as shoulder angle changes.\r\n\r\nIt also contributes to posterior stabilization of the humeral head.\r\n\r\n## Subscapularis: Internal Rotation\r\n\r\nSubscapularis is the major cuff **internal rotator**.\r\n\r\nIt also provides strong anterior joint stabilization.\r\n\r\nIts force often balances posterior cuff activity during high-force shoulder tasks.\r\n\r\n## Co-Contraction\r\n\r\nOpposing cuff muscles can contract simultaneously.\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n\r\n- internal rotator subscapularis;\r\n- external rotators infraspinatus/teres minor\r\n\r\nmay co-contract during pressing or pulling.\r\n\r\nThis may create little net rotational torque but substantial:\r\n\r\n- joint compression;\r\n- stiffness;\r\n- stabilization.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`net_rotation_torque ≈ 0`\r\n\r\ndoes not imply:\r\n\r\n`rotator_cuff_force ≈ 0`.\r\n\r\nThis is critical for Agonez.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nDirect evidence for **stretch-mediated hypertrophy of individual rotator-cuff muscles** is limited.\r\n\r\nThe length state of each cuff muscle depends on:\r\n\r\n- humeral elevation;\r\n- axial rotation;\r\n- horizontal position;\r\n- scapular orientation.\r\n\r\nBecause the aggregate contains muscles with opposing rotational actions, there is no single cuff-wide length state.\r\n\r\n## External Rotators\r\n\r\nInfraspinatus and teres minor are generally lengthened by **internal rotation**.\r\n\r\nThus external-rotation training beginning from a controlled internally rotated position can load them at relatively longer lengths.\r\n\r\n## Subscapularis\r\n\r\nSubscapularis is generally lengthened by **external rotation**.\r\n\r\nResisted internal rotation beginning from external rotation can therefore produce long-length active loading.\r\n\r\n## Supraspinatus\r\n\r\nSupraspinatus length changes with humeral elevation and rotation in a more complex way.\r\n\r\nIts tendon and muscle operate across a relatively small excursion compared with large limb muscles.\r\n\r\nDirect long-length hypertrophy optimization is much less established.\r\n\r\n## Practical Interpretation\r\n\r\nFor cuff training:\r\n\r\n- use meaningful controlled rotational ROM;\r\n- load the muscles from positions that remain comfortable;\r\n- avoid forcing extreme end-range rotation solely to maximize stretch;\r\n- prioritize high-quality tension and joint control.\r\n\r\nThe shoulder's passive structures become increasingly important near end range.\r\n\r\nTherefore aggressive long-length loading is less straightforward than for muscles such as gastrocnemius or biceps femoris.\r\n\r\n## SMH Confidence\r\n\r\nA high aggregate `smh_factor` would be difficult to justify directly.\r\n\r\nThe more defensible position is:\r\n\r\n- individual cuff muscles clearly change length with rotation;\r\n- active long-length loading is possible;\r\n- direct hypertrophy evidence is sparse;\r\n- extreme rotational ROM introduces joint-specific constraints.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## External Rotation\r\n\r\nDedicated external-rotation exercises primarily target:\r\n\r\n- infraspinatus;\r\n- teres minor.\r\n\r\nCommon variants include:\r\n\r\n- cable external rotation;\r\n- band external rotation;\r\n- side-lying external rotation;\r\n- machine external rotation.\r\n\r\nThese are among the most direct cuff exercises.\r\n\r\n## Side-Lying External Rotation\r\n\r\nSide-lying external rotation can provide high specificity because:\r\n\r\n- the humerus can remain relatively stable;\r\n- external rotation occurs against gravity;\r\n- deltoid demand is modest.\r\n\r\nIts limitation is relatively low absolute loading.\r\n\r\n## Cable External Rotation\r\n\r\nCable resistance allows:\r\n\r\n- adjustable resistance direction;\r\n- more continuous loading;\r\n- easy progression.\r\n\r\nElbow position matters substantially.\r\n\r\nExternal rotation with the arm:\r\n\r\n- at the side;\r\n- abducted approximately 90 degrees\r\n\r\ncreates different cuff recruitment and joint mechanics.\r\n\r\n## Internal Rotation\r\n\r\nDirect resisted internal rotation strongly recruits:\r\n\r\n- subscapularis.\r\n\r\nHowever, larger internal rotators such as:\r\n\r\n- pectoralis major;\r\n- latissimus dorsi;\r\n- teres major\r\n\r\ncan contribute depending on arm position.\r\n\r\nA setup designed for cuff-specific work should minimize their mechanical advantage where possible.\r\n\r\n## Face Pull\r\n\r\nFace pulls combine:\r\n\r\n- external rotation;\r\n- horizontal abduction;\r\n- scapular retraction.\r\n\r\nTherefore they can strongly involve:\r\n\r\n- infraspinatus;\r\n- teres minor;\r\n- posterior deltoid;\r\n- rhomboids;\r\n- middle/lower trapezius.\r\n\r\nThey should not be treated as pure cuff isolation.\r\n\r\n## Lateral Raise\r\n\r\nLateral raises require substantial cuff stabilization.\r\n\r\nSupraspinatus contributes to elevation, while the remaining cuff helps center the humeral head.\r\n\r\nTherefore lateral raises should receive non-zero cuff demand even though `deltoid_lateral` is the major prime mover.\r\n\r\n## Overhead Press\r\n\r\nOverhead pressing creates large glenohumeral forces.\r\n\r\nCuff demand can be substantial because it must stabilize the humeral head while:\r\n\r\n- anterior deltoid;\r\n- lateral deltoid;\r\n- triceps;\r\n- clavicular pec\r\n\r\ngenerate high force.\r\n\r\nThis is largely a stabilization demand rather than direct external-rotation exercise.\r\n\r\n## Bench Press\r\n\r\nBench pressing also requires cuff stabilization.\r\n\r\nThe degree depends on:\r\n\r\n- humeral abduction;\r\n- shoulder rotation;\r\n- load;\r\n- technique.\r\n\r\nSubscapularis can contribute significantly to anterior stabilization/internal rotation, while posterior cuff muscles provide balancing force.\r\n\r\nThe cuff should receive meaningful but secondary demand.\r\n\r\n## Rows\r\n\r\nRows can involve cuff activity through:\r\n\r\n- humeral stabilization;\r\n- posterior shoulder control;\r\n- external rotation depending on path.\r\n\r\nHigh-elbow rows and face-pull-like patterns generally increase posterior cuff involvement.\r\n\r\n## Pull-Ups and Pulldowns\r\n\r\nVertical pulling requires glenohumeral stabilization under large shoulder torque.\r\n\r\nCuff demand is meaningful, though typically secondary to:\r\n\r\n- latissimus;\r\n- elbow flexors.\r\n\r\nGrip and humeral path influence rotation requirements.\r\n\r\n## Heavy vs Light Cuff Work\r\n\r\nDedicated cuff exercises typically use much lighter external loads than major pressing exercises.\r\n\r\nThis does not mean cuff force during heavy compounds is trivial.\r\n\r\nThe cuff can generate large internal forces during compound movements because stabilization demands scale with total joint loading.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Direct Cuff Training Is Not Equivalent to Compound Stabilization\r\n\r\nPresses, rows and pull-ups provide substantial stabilizing exposure.\r\n\r\nHowever, they may not provide sufficient targeted:\r\n\r\n- external rotation;\r\n- internal rotation;\r\n- isolated cuff strength through ROM.\r\n\r\nTherefore direct cuff work can remain useful even in a compound-heavy program.\r\n\r\n## Weekly Volume\r\n\r\nFor general cuff strength and hypertrophy, a practical starting point can be approximately:\r\n\r\n- **4-8 direct sets per week**\r\n\r\ndistributed across:\r\n\r\n- external rotation;\r\n- optional internal rotation;\r\n- scapular/shoulder control work.\r\n\r\nThis should be adjusted downward if face pulls and similar exercises already provide large posterior-cuff volume.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nTwo to four low-volume weekly exposures are practical.\r\n\r\nThe cuff muscles are relatively small and often tolerate frequent submaximal loading.\r\n\r\nHowever, tendon irritation can accumulate if:\r\n\r\n- frequency;\r\n- range;\r\n- load\r\n\r\nare increased too aggressively.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\nDedicated cuff work is usually most practical in:\r\n\r\n- 10-20 reps;\r\n- 15-30 reps.\r\n\r\nModerate loads allow better control of:\r\n\r\n- humeral position;\r\n- rotation;\r\n- scapular motion.\r\n\r\nVery heavy low-repetition cuff isolation is generally unnecessary.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nDirect cuff isolation usually does not need repeated absolute failure.\r\n\r\nApproximately:\r\n\r\n- 1-3 RIR\r\n\r\nis often sufficient.\r\n\r\nCloser failure can be used selectively on stable exercises, but quality of rotation and joint position should remain the limiting criteria.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Order\r\n\r\nCuff isolation can be placed:\r\n\r\n- after major upper-body training;\r\n- in separate low-fatigue sessions;\r\n- before training when used as low-load preparation.\r\n\r\nHeavy fatigue-inducing cuff work immediately before maximal pressing may reduce shoulder stability.\r\n\r\n## Avoid Treating Every Cuff Exercise as Rehabilitation\r\n\r\nHealthy lifters can progressively overload cuff muscles like other skeletal muscles.\r\n\r\nThe key differences are:\r\n\r\n- smaller absolute loads;\r\n- greater sensitivity to technique;\r\n- high stabilization overlap.\r\n\r\nDirect cuff work can therefore be genuine strength/hypertrophy training rather than only "prehab."\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## The Cuff Contains Antagonists\r\n\r\nThis aggregate combines:\r\n\r\n- external rotators;\r\n- an internal rotator;\r\n- an abduction contributor.\r\n\r\nYet they often contract together.\r\n\r\nThis is a good example of why muscle function cannot be inferred solely from net joint movement.\r\n\r\n## The Cuff Stabilizes by Compression, Not by "Holding the Shoulder Down"\r\n\r\nA common oversimplification is that the cuff merely pulls the humeral head downward.\r\n\r\nIts function is better understood as a three-dimensional combination of:\r\n\r\n- compression;\r\n- rotation;\r\n- translation control.\r\n\r\nDifferent cuff components produce different directional forces depending on joint position.\r\n\r\n## Supraspinatus Does Not Simply Work for the First 15 Degrees\r\n\r\nThe classic teaching that supraspinatus initiates abduction and then hands the task to deltoid is too discrete.\r\n\r\nBoth muscles contribute through a broad portion of elevation, while their leverage and relative force share change continuously.\r\n\r\n## Subscapularis Is the Largest Cuff Muscle\r\n\r\nIt has substantially greater mass and PCSA than teres minor and usually than the individual posterior cuff muscles.\r\n\r\nTherefore equal force allocation among the four cuff muscles would be anatomically implausible.\r\n\r\n## Cuff Demand Can Be High Without Rotation\r\n\r\nDuring a heavy bench press or lateral raise, little external/internal rotation may occur.\r\n\r\nThe cuff can nevertheless generate large co-contraction forces to maintain glenohumeral stability.\r\n\r\nThis is analogous to:\r\n\r\n- obliques during Pallof press;\r\n- erector spinae during RDL.\r\n\r\n## Rotator Cuff Tears Affect More Than Strength\r\n\r\nBecause cuff muscles are stabilizers, dysfunction can alter:\r\n\r\n- humeral-head translation;\r\n- scapulohumeral mechanics;\r\n- force transmission.\r\n\r\nThe result is not simply reduced rotational strength.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Definition of This Aggregate\r\n\r\n`rotator_cuffs` should represent:\r\n\r\n- `supraspinatus`;\r\n- `infraspinatus`;\r\n- `teres_minor`;\r\n- `subscapularis`.\r\n\r\nIt should not include:\r\n\r\n- teres major.\r\n\r\nTeres major is not part of the rotator cuff because its tendon does not blend into the glenohumeral capsular cuff in the same functional arrangement and it acts more like a large humeral adductor/extensor/internal rotator.\r\n\r\n## Opposing-Action Aggregate Caveat\r\n\r\nLike `pronators_supinators`, this record combines muscles with opposing rotational functions.\r\n\r\nTherefore one scalar:\r\n\r\n`rotator_cuffs = X`\r\n\r\nworks well as:\r\n\r\n- total local cuff exposure;\r\n- aggregate stabilization demand.\r\n\r\nIt works less well for:\r\n\r\n- exact `E_LT`;\r\n- direction-specific rotational force.\r\n\r\nIf future precision is required, this aggregate is a strong candidate for subdivision into at least:\r\n\r\n- `supraspinatus`;\r\n- `external_rotator_cuff` = infraspinatus + teres minor;\r\n- `subscapularis`.\r\n\r\n## Primary Demand Dimensions\r\n\r\nThe cuff should receive demand from two conceptually separate sources:\r\n\r\n### Rotational Demand\r\n\r\n- external rotation;\r\n- internal rotation;\r\n- abduction contribution through supraspinatus.\r\n\r\n### Stabilization Demand\r\n\r\n- humeral-head compression;\r\n- translation control;\r\n- co-contraction opposing large prime-mover forces.\r\n\r\nThis second component is essential.\r\n\r\n## Stabilization Demand Model\r\n\r\nA useful first-order approximation is:\r\n\r\n`cuff_stabilization_demand ∝ glenohumeral_joint_force + translational_component_of_prime_mover_force`\r\n\r\nThus high cuff demand should be expected when:\r\n\r\n- shoulder torque is high;\r\n- prime-mover force is high;\r\n- the humeral head experiences a large destabilizing component.\r\n\r\nThis is more defensible than assigning a fixed percentage of every upper-body exercise to the cuff.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Lateral Deltoid\r\n\r\nDuring lateral raise:\r\n\r\n### Deltoid\r\n\r\nProduces:\r\n- large abduction torque;\r\n- superior translational component.\r\n\r\n### Cuff\r\n\r\nProduces:\r\n- compression;\r\n- opposing translation;\r\n- supraspinatus abduction contribution.\r\n\r\nTherefore your existing `single_arm_cable_lateral_raise` vector with substantial `rotator_cuffs` demand is biomechanically sensible.\r\n\r\nThe cuff share can be particularly important near lower-to-middle elevation angles.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Anterior Deltoid\r\n\r\nOverhead and incline pressing create strong anterior/elevation forces.\r\n\r\nCuff co-contraction stabilizes the humeral head.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n`high deltoid_anterior demand`\r\n\r\nshould usually imply:\r\n\r\n`non-zero rotator_cuff demand`.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Pectoralis Major\r\n\r\nHeavy horizontal pressing creates large humeral forces.\r\n\r\nSubscapularis and posterior cuff contribute to balancing and centering the joint.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n- bench press;\r\n- chest press;\r\n- weighted dip\r\n\r\nshould all receive meaningful cuff stabilization demand.\r\n\r\nBut the cuff should remain secondary to the major pressing muscles.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Latissimus Dorsi\r\n\r\nLatissimus generates large:\r\n\r\n- adduction;\r\n- extension;\r\n- internal-rotation moments.\r\n\r\nThe cuff must stabilize the humeral head during these forces.\r\n\r\nThus vertical and horizontal pulling should receive non-zero cuff demand even when there is little visible rotation.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Allocation\r\n\r\n### Single-Arm Cable Lateral Raise\r\n\r\nHigh cuff demand relative to many isolation exercises.\r\n\r\nComponents include:\r\n- supraspinatus abduction;\r\n- humeral-head stabilization.\r\n\r\n### Seated Dumbbell Overhead Press\r\n\r\nModerate-to-high stabilization demand.\r\n\r\nPrime movers remain:\r\n- anterior/lateral deltoid;\r\n- triceps.\r\n\r\n### Smith Incline Bench Press\r\n\r\nModerate cuff stabilization demand.\r\n\r\n### Plate-Loaded Horizontal Chest Press\r\n\r\nModerate stabilization demand.\r\n\r\n### Cable Face Pull\r\n\r\nVery high posterior cuff demand through:\r\n- external rotation;\r\n- stabilization.\r\n\r\n### Reverse Pec Deck\r\n\r\nModerate posterior cuff stabilization demand.\r\n\r\nHigher if the exercise includes active external rotation.\r\n\r\n### Weighted Neutral-Grip Pull-Up\r\n\r\nModerate cuff demand from high glenohumeral force.\r\n\r\n### Wide Pronated-Grip Pull-Up\r\n\r\nModerate cuff demand, with humeral geometry potentially increasing stabilization requirements.\r\n\r\n### Straight-Arm Cable Pulldown\r\n\r\nModerate cuff stabilization demand.\r\n\r\nLarge lat torque still requires humeral-head control.\r\n\r\n### Dead Hang\r\n\r\nLow-to-moderate cuff stabilization demand depending on whether the shoulder is passive or actively stabilized.\r\n\r\nAn active hang should receive more cuff demand than a fully passive hang.\r\n\r\n## Length-Tension Model\r\n\r\nA single aggregate `E_LT_rotator_cuffs` is intrinsically problematic.\r\n\r\n### External Rotators\r\n\r\nLonger in:\r\n- internal rotation.\r\n\r\n### Subscapularis\r\n\r\nLonger in:\r\n- external rotation.\r\n\r\n### Supraspinatus\r\n\r\nLength depends more strongly on:\r\n- elevation;\r\n- rotation;\r\n- scapular orientation.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n> one scalar cuff length state can hide opposing conditions.\r\n\r\nFor KISS modelling, it may be better to use:\r\n\r\n- neutral aggregate `E_LT`;\r\n- exercise-specific rotational modifiers;\r\n- separate stabilization demand.\r\n\r\nDo not over-interpret a single aggregate length score.\r\n\r\n## Force-Sharing Model\r\n\r\nEqual force allocation among four cuff muscles would be incorrect.\r\n\r\nSubscapularis possesses much larger force capacity than teres minor.\r\n\r\nSupraspinatus contributes differently from the posterior cuff.\r\n\r\nA better aggregate model is to avoid estimating individual force unless needed.\r\n\r\nIf subdivision is introduced later:\r\n\r\n- allocate according to PCSA;\r\n- moment arm;\r\n- rotation direction;\r\n- shoulder angle.\r\n\r\n## FCSA Interpretation\r\n\r\nBecause the aggregate combines four muscles with different directions of action, total projected FCSA is useful mainly as:\r\n\r\n- aggregate force capacity;\r\n- stabilization capacity proxy.\r\n\r\nIt should not be treated as if all FCSA can contribute simultaneously to one external-rotation or abduction direction.\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n\r\n`FCSA_total_rotator_cuffs`\r\n\r\ncannot be multiplied directly by specific tension to estimate maximum external-rotation force because subscapularis generates the opposite rotational moment.\r\n\r\nThis is a major modelling caveat.\r\n\r\n## ETU Interpretation\r\n\r\nFor the cuff, ETU should include both:\r\n\r\n- prime-mover rotational work;\r\n- high-force stabilization.\r\n\r\nA heavy lateral raise or press can therefore generate meaningful cuff ETU despite low direct cuff ROM.\r\n\r\nAs with other stabilizers:\r\n\r\n`ROM = 0`\r\n\r\ndoes not mean:\r\n\r\n`ETU = 0`.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nThe aggregate should receive conservative SMH treatment.\r\n\r\nIndividual muscles clearly experience length changes during shoulder rotation and elevation, but:\r\n\r\n- the aggregate contains antagonists;\r\n- direct long-length hypertrophy data are sparse;\r\n- shoulder end-range loading has structural constraints.\r\n\r\nThe most defensible approach is to base training value primarily on:\r\n\r\n- force;\r\n- direction-specific recruitment;\r\n- stabilization;\r\n- controlled ROM\r\n\r\nrather than a large generic stretch multiplier.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **four-muscle cuff definition:** very high;\r\n- **scapular origins and humeral insertions:** high;\r\n- **supraspinatus abduction role:** high;\r\n- **infraspinatus/teres minor external rotation:** high;\r\n- **subscapularis internal rotation:** high;\r\n- **dynamic humeral-head stabilization:** very high;\r\n- **concavity-compression mechanism:** well established;\r\n- **co-contraction during loaded shoulder tasks:** well supported;\r\n- **large cuff demand during compound pressing/pulling despite low rotation ROM:** biomechanically strong;\r\n- **precise exercise-specific cuff force:** difficult to quantify non-invasively;\r\n- **direct cuff-specific hypertrophy literature:** limited;\r\n- **stretch-mediated hypertrophy magnitude:** poorly established;\r\n- **single aggregate `E_LT` validity:** low because opposing muscles are combined.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **rotator cuffs = a four-muscle glenohumeral stabilization and rotation system that combines supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor and subscapularis; its most important training demand is often not visible shoulder rotation but the compressive and directional force required to keep the humeral head centered while larger prime movers generate high torque.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/Subscapularis,https://exrx.net/Muscles/Supraspinatus,https://exrx.net/Muscles/TeresMajor,https://exrx.net/Muscles/Infraspinatus}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	5.90	2.31	0.999	170.94	170.80
43	adductor_longus_brevis	Musculus adductor longus, Musculus adductor brevis	Lower	Hip_FA	750.00	710.23	30.00	Fan-shaped/Triangular	0.495	0.505	extreme_high	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\n`adductor_longus_brevis` is an aggregate representing two anatomically distinct muscles of the medial thigh:\r\n\r\n- **adductor longus** — larger and more superficial;\r\n- **adductor brevis** — shorter, deeper and positioned largely posterior to adductor longus.\r\n\r\nBoth belong to the hip-adductor system and share a broadly similar mechanical role, but they are not redundant copies of one another. Their different origins, insertions, depth and line of pull create somewhat different moment arms and recruitment patterns across hip positions.\r\n\r\nIn the context of this database, the aggregate is most useful as a representation of the **anterior/superficial pubofemoral adductor compartment**, distinct from the much larger and mechanically more complex `adductor_magnus`.\r\n\r\nThe primary task of this pair is production and control of **hip adduction**. Functionally, however, they should not be thought of merely as muscles that "close the legs". They also participate in control of the femur relative to the pelvis during locomotion, unilateral stance, cutting and other tasks involving frontal-plane stabilization.\r\n\r\nTheir sagittal-plane action is position-dependent. Because the line of action changes relative to the hip joint centre as the femur moves, an adductor can assist hip flexion in one joint configuration while having little flexion moment or potentially contributing differently in another. Thus, assigning a fixed secondary label such as "hip flexor" is an oversimplification.\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\n## Adductor Longus\r\n\r\n### Origin\r\n\r\nAdductor longus originates from the **body of the pubis**, close to the pubic symphysis and inferior to the pubic crest.\r\n\r\nIts relatively proximal and medial origin gives the muscle a line of pull directed inferiorly and laterally toward the femur.\r\n\r\n### Insertion\r\n\r\nIt inserts through a broad, relatively flat attachment onto the **middle portion of the linea aspera of the femur**, primarily along its medial lip.\r\n\r\nUnlike gracilis, it does **not** cross the knee joint.\r\n\r\nThe muscle therefore acts exclusively through the hip and contributes force directly between the pelvis and femur.\r\n\r\n## Adductor Brevis\r\n\r\n### Origin\r\n\r\nAdductor brevis originates from the **body and inferior ramus of the pubis**.\r\n\r\nIts origin is located inferior to that of adductor longus.\r\n\r\n### Insertion\r\n\r\nIt inserts on the proximal femur, including the region of the **pectineal line and proximal linea aspera**.\r\n\r\nThe muscle lies deep to adductor longus and superficial to portions of adductor magnus.\r\n\r\nBecause brevis has a shorter proximodistal course than longus, its mechanical behaviour should not automatically be assumed to be identical despite the shared gross action of hip adduction.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nBoth muscles are innervated primarily by the **obturator nerve**, arising predominantly from spinal roots **L2-L4**.\r\n\r\nAdductor longus is classically supplied by the **anterior division of the obturator nerve**.\r\n\r\nAdductor brevis is also supplied by the obturator nerve; branching patterns can vary anatomically, so attempting to encode an overly rigid anterior-versus-posterior division distinction for brevis is usually unnecessary for a training-oriented model.\r\n\r\nThe common obturator innervation reflects their membership in the medial compartment of the thigh.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Hip Adduction\r\n\r\nThe dominant function of both muscles is **femoral adduction at the hip**.\r\n\r\nIn an open-chain movement, they pull the femur toward the body's midline.\r\n\r\nIn a closed-chain context, the same force can instead act on the pelvis, contributing to control of pelvic position relative to the stance femur.\r\n\r\nThe functional distinction is important: during athletic movement, the adductors often operate less as isolated "leg-closing" muscles and more as force-transmitting stabilizers between the pelvis and femur.\r\n\r\n## Frontal-Plane Control\r\n\r\nDuring unilateral stance, running and direction changes, the adductor complex participates in controlling frontal-plane motion of the hip.\r\n\r\nIt functions together with the abductors rather than simply opposing them. Co-contraction can increase control of the femoral head and limit uncontrolled lateral motion of the pelvis or femur.\r\n\r\nThis makes the adductor system relevant not only to direct adduction strength but also to:\r\n\r\n- change-of-direction mechanics;\r\n- cutting;\r\n- acceleration;\r\n- deceleration;\r\n- single-leg stability;\r\n- pelvic control.\r\n\r\n## Sagittal-Plane Contribution\r\n\r\nAdductor longus and brevis can contribute to **hip flexion**, particularly from relatively extended or neutral hip positions.\r\n\r\nTheir sagittal-plane contribution is nevertheless highly joint-angle dependent.\r\n\r\nAs hip flexion changes, the muscle line of action changes relative to the transverse axis of the joint. Consequently, the magnitude and sometimes even functional interpretation of the sagittal moment changes.\r\n\r\nFor programming purposes:\r\n\r\n- treat **adduction** as their primary action;\r\n- treat flexion/extension contribution as a secondary, position-dependent function;\r\n- do not model them as conventional hip flexors in the same sense as iliopsoas.\r\n\r\n## Rotation\r\n\r\nThe adductor group can also contribute small internal- or external-rotation moments depending on hip angle and individual anatomy.\r\n\r\nThese rotational effects are substantially less reliable as a defining function than adduction because the relevant moment arms change with joint position.\r\n\r\nFor this aggregate, rotation is therefore best treated as a **context-dependent secondary contribution**, not a primary programming target.\r\n\r\n## Pelvic and Hip Stability\r\n\r\nBecause both muscles connect the pubis directly to the femur, force production also affects the pelvis.\r\n\r\nTheir stabilizing role becomes particularly relevant during:\r\n\r\n- unilateral loading;\r\n- rapid changes in direction;\r\n- high-force running;\r\n- wide-stance movements;\r\n- lateral lunging patterns;\r\n- transitions between hip abduction and adduction.\r\n\r\nThe proximal attachment near the pubic region also explains why excessive or rapidly increased adductor loading can produce clinically relevant groin symptoms.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nThe adductors are mechanically well suited to receiving high tension at long muscle lengths because **hip abduction lengthens the medial-thigh musculature**.\r\n\r\nFor adductor longus in particular, large hip-abduction positions can substantially increase muscle-tendon length.\r\n\r\nThis creates a plausible basis for emphasizing exercises that load adduction from a meaningfully abducted starting position.\r\n\r\nHowever, the distinction between **mechanical plausibility** and **direct hypertrophy evidence** is important.\r\n\r\nDirect longitudinal hypertrophy research specifically comparing long- versus short-muscle-length resistance training for adductor longus and adductor brevis is limited. Therefore, a high database-level `smh_factor` should be interpreted as a biomechanical modelling assumption rather than as proof of a precisely quantified hypertrophic advantage.\r\n\r\nPractical implications:\r\n\r\n- favour exercises that maintain meaningful resistance as the hip enters abduction;\r\n- avoid using only short-range squeezing movements if hypertrophy is the goal;\r\n- progressively expose the muscle to deeper abduction rather than abruptly forcing maximal range;\r\n- distinguish productive loaded stretch from uncontrolled end-range stress around the groin.\r\n\r\nA seated hip-adduction machine can be particularly useful if the starting position allows substantial hip abduction and the resistance remains meaningful near that position.\r\n\r\nCable adduction can also create a strong lengthened stimulus if the setup permits the working leg to move sufficiently away from the midline.\r\n\r\nCopenhagen variations load the adductors strongly but should not automatically be considered optimal hypertrophy movements merely because activation is high. Their stimulus is strongly influenced by lever length, stabilization requirements and isometric/eccentric demands.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Exercise Selection\r\n\r\nFor direct hypertrophy, the most straightforward option is usually a **hip-adduction machine**.\r\n\r\nAdvantages include:\r\n\r\n- easy stabilization;\r\n- simple progressive overload;\r\n- low technical complexity;\r\n- relatively direct loading of the adductor complex;\r\n- convenient training close to muscular failure;\r\n- controllable exposure to hip abduction.\r\n\r\nCable hip adduction is also useful, especially when equipment geometry allows a large active range of motion.\r\n\r\nCompared with the machine, cable variants generally require more balance and pelvic control, which may reduce the amount of local adductor loading achievable before stabilization becomes limiting.\r\n\r\n## Copenhagen Adduction\r\n\r\nThe Copenhagen adduction exercise is a high-demand adductor exercise with strong evidence for increasing adductor strength and extensive use in groin-strengthening programmes.\r\n\r\nIts value is particularly high for:\r\n\r\n- athletic preparation;\r\n- eccentric/isometric adductor strength;\r\n- groin robustness;\r\n- frontal-plane trunk and pelvic control.\r\n\r\nHowever, it is not necessarily the most efficient sole hypertrophy exercise.\r\n\r\nIn long-lever variants, performance may be limited by:\r\n\r\n- trunk stabilization;\r\n- shoulder support;\r\n- discomfort at the support leg;\r\n- lever-arm tolerance;\r\n- general coordination.\r\n\r\nFor bodybuilding-style local loading, a machine or cable variation can therefore complement Copenhagen work rather than being replaced by it.\r\n\r\n## Multi-Joint Exercises\r\n\r\nThe adductors are substantially loaded in many compound lower-body movements.\r\n\r\nThey can contribute meaningfully during:\r\n\r\n- deep squats;\r\n- leg presses;\r\n- wide-stance squatting;\r\n- split squats;\r\n- lateral lunges;\r\n- sumo-style deadlifting;\r\n- acceleration and cutting tasks.\r\n\r\nHowever, compound exercise exposure should not automatically be equated with maximal direct stimulus to `adductor_longus_brevis`.\r\n\r\nIn deep hip-flexion movements, **adductor magnus** can assume a particularly important extensor role. Thus, high "adductor" demand in a squat does not mean that longus and brevis receive the same proportional stimulus.\r\n\r\nThis distinction is important in the Agonez model: total hip-adductor loading and loading of this specific aggregate are not synonymous.\r\n\r\n## Range of Motion\r\n\r\nFor direct adduction work, a useful hypertrophy-oriented ROM generally begins with the hip clearly abducted and finishes around neutral or modest adduction.\r\n\r\nGoing aggressively beyond comfortable end-range abduction is unnecessary.\r\n\r\nThe limiting factor should be the ability to maintain:\r\n\r\n- controlled pelvic position;\r\n- smooth eccentric motion;\r\n- high local adductor tension;\r\n- absence of sharp pubic or groin pain.\r\n\r\nIncreasing ROM should be treated as progressive overload in its own right.\r\n\r\n## Technique\r\n\r\nOn a hip-adduction machine:\r\n\r\n1. establish a stable pelvis against the seat;\r\n2. allow controlled abduction during the eccentric phase;\r\n3. avoid bouncing out of the stretched position;\r\n4. adduct by moving the femurs rather than compensating through pelvic rotation;\r\n5. use a controlled eccentric;\r\n6. do not sacrifice ROM simply to increase stack weight.\r\n\r\nA brief squeeze near the shortened position is acceptable but generally should not replace loading through the eccentric and lengthened half of the movement.\r\n\r\n## Fatigue Characteristics\r\n\r\nDirect adductor exercises can produce unusually pronounced delayed soreness when first introduced, especially when performed through large abduction ROM.\r\n\r\nThis is particularly relevant because athletes may already accumulate substantial adductor load from running, sprinting, cutting or lower-body compound exercises.\r\n\r\nInitial direct volume should therefore be conservative.\r\n\r\nHigh soreness around the medial thigh or groin can interfere with:\r\n\r\n- squatting;\r\n- running;\r\n- lateral movement;\r\n- hip mobility;\r\n- sports involving rapid direction changes.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\nFor a lifter who already performs substantial lower-body compound work, direct adductor training usually requires relatively little additional volume.\r\n\r\nA reasonable starting point is approximately **2-4 direct hard sets per week**, followed by adjustment according to:\r\n\r\n- hypertrophy priority;\r\n- recovery;\r\n- existing squat/leg-press volume;\r\n- sporting activity;\r\n- groin tolerance.\r\n\r\nIf the muscle is a specific hypertrophy priority, volume can be increased progressively rather than assuming that a high number of sets is required from the outset.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nTraining the adductors **1-3 times per week** can all be viable.\r\n\r\nTwo exposures per week are often convenient because they distribute soreness and lengthened-position loading more evenly.\r\n\r\nFor athletes performing sprinting or cutting, the placement of hard adductor sessions should account for sport-specific workload.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\nMachine and cable adduction are well suited to moderate and high repetition ranges.\r\n\r\nTypical productive ranges include:\r\n\r\n- approximately 6-12 repetitions for heavier controlled work;\r\n- approximately 10-20 repetitions as a general default;\r\n- 15-30 repetitions where joint comfort and local muscular fatigue are preferable to high external load.\r\n\r\nThere is no unique hypertrophy-specific repetition range for the adductors. Sets should instead provide sufficiently high effort and mechanical tension while maintaining controlled hip position.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nStable machine adduction can generally be trained relatively close to failure.\r\n\r\nFor highly demanding Copenhagen or unstable cable variants, absolute failure is less useful because technique or stabilization may fail before the target tissue reaches the same level of local fatigue.\r\n\r\nExercise-specific failure criteria should therefore differ:\r\n\r\n- **machine:** local concentric failure is a useful endpoint;\r\n- **cable:** stop when pelvic compensation becomes substantial;\r\n- **Copenhagen:** stop when hip height or lever position can no longer be maintained.\r\n\r\n## Progression\r\n\r\nProgression can come from:\r\n\r\n- additional load;\r\n- additional repetitions;\r\n- greater controlled abduction ROM;\r\n- improved eccentric control;\r\n- longer lever arms in Copenhagen variations;\r\n- additional weekly sets when recovery remains good.\r\n\r\nDo not progress load and maximal stretch simultaneously at an aggressive rate.\r\n\r\nFor the adductors, rapid increases in both variables can create a disproportionately large jump in tissue demand.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Lower-Body Training\r\n\r\nPlace direct adductor training according to its priority.\r\n\r\nIf hypertrophy is the priority, direct adduction should not always be relegated to the end of a session after the adductors are already heavily fatigued.\r\n\r\nIf adductor development is secondary, it works well after major lower-body compounds.\r\n\r\nFor an athlete with substantial sprinting or change-of-direction work, direct adductor loading should be coordinated with the weekly high-intensity running schedule rather than viewed in isolation.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\nAdductor longus is one of the muscles most frequently discussed in the context of **adductor-related groin pain**, particularly in field sports involving sprinting and rapid direction changes.\r\n\r\nThe term "adductor" can be biomechanically misleading if interpreted too literally. These muscles do considerably more than produce isolated frontal-plane adduction: their moment arms allow them to participate in sagittal and transverse-plane mechanics as hip position changes.\r\n\r\nAdductor brevis is anatomically hidden beneath adductor longus, which makes it difficult to study selectively using conventional surface EMG. Consequently, a large proportion of exercise literature focuses specifically on **adductor longus**, while conclusions about brevis are often inferred from anatomy, modelling or deeper measurement techniques.\r\n\r\nThe pubic origin of the adductors means that their forces converge anatomically near a region that also receives substantial loading from the abdominal wall and contralateral adductor system. This helps explain why groin biomechanics are often better understood as a regional force-transfer problem than as an isolated single-muscle problem.\r\n\r\nAdductor strength is especially relevant in sports such as football because the muscles must tolerate rapid transitions between eccentric lengthening and forceful concentric action.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Interpretation of This Aggregate\r\n\r\nThis database entry intentionally combines two muscles.\r\n\r\nWhen using it computationally, remember that:\r\n\r\n- `mass_g`, `mv_cm3`, PCSA-related variables and fiber-type estimates represent the aggregate;\r\n- the two constituent muscles do not necessarily share identical architecture or moment arms;\r\n- exercise recruitment vectors should therefore represent the combined expected demand rather than imply perfectly equal recruitment.\r\n\r\nFor exercises whose biomechanics strongly favour another adductor — particularly `adductor_magnus` — the allocation should not be forced into this aggregate simply because the movement contains hip adduction.\r\n\r\nFor direct machine or cable hip adduction, however, `adductor_longus_brevis` can reasonably receive a substantial share of the modeled adductor demand.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\nEvidence confidence varies by topic:\r\n\r\n- **gross anatomy and innervation:** high;\r\n- **primary adduction function:** high;\r\n- **exercise activation of adductor longus:** moderate to high;\r\n- **specific recruitment of adductor brevis:** lower because it is difficult to isolate experimentally;\r\n- **position-dependent secondary hip actions:** supported biomechanically, but sensitive to modelling assumptions and individual geometry;\r\n- **stretch-mediated hypertrophy specifically in longus/brevis:** plausible but currently much less directly established than the general long-muscle-length hypertrophy literature.\r\n\r\nThe model should preserve these distinctions rather than converting every biomechanically plausible mechanism into an equally strong empirical claim.\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/Adductors}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	10.60	6.68	0.993	67.00	66.55
42	adductor_magnus	Musculus adductor magnus	Lower	Hip_FA	1665.00	1576.70	88.00	Multipennate/Triangular	0.425	0.575	extreme_high	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\nThe **adductor magnus** is a very large, broad muscle occupying much of the medial and posteromedial thigh.\r\n\r\nDespite its name, it should not be conceptualized simply as a larger version of the other hip adductors. Its anatomy creates two major functional identities:\r\n\r\n- a large **adductor portion**, connecting the pubis and ischial ramus to the posterior femur;\r\n- a distinct **hamstring portion**, connecting the ischial tuberosity to the adductor tubercle.\r\n\r\nThese regions differ in fiber direction, insertion and innervation.\r\n\r\nFunctionally, adductor magnus lies at the intersection of the hip-adductor and hip-extensor systems. Its enormous size and favorable extension leverage in flexed hip positions make it an important contributor to movements traditionally classified as glute-dominant or posterior-chain exercises.\r\n\r\nFor training purposes, the most important implication is:\r\n\r\n> **Adductor magnus is both an adductor and a major hip extensor, with its extensor contribution becoming particularly important when the hip starts in substantial flexion.**\r\n\r\nThis distinguishes it from `adductor_longus_brevis`, whose primary training identity remains more strongly centered on hip adduction.\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\nAdductor magnus is a broad triangular muscle extending from the pelvis to a very long attachment along the posterior femur.\r\n\r\nIt is commonly divided into two major anatomical components:\r\n\r\n1. **Adductor portion**\r\n2. **Hamstring portion**\r\n\r\nSome anatomical descriptions further subdivide the adductor region into multiple fascicular compartments. Therefore, the muscle should not be assumed to behave as one mechanically homogeneous actuator.\r\n\r\n## Origin\r\n\r\n### Adductor Portion\r\n\r\nThe adductor portion arises broadly from the:\r\n\r\n- inferior ramus of the pubis;\r\n- ramus of the ischium.\r\n\r\nThe broad proximal attachment produces fibers with substantially different orientations across the muscle.\r\n\r\nMore superior fibers tend to run relatively horizontally toward the proximal femur, while progressively inferior fibers follow increasingly vertical or inferolateral trajectories.\r\n\r\n### Hamstring Portion\r\n\r\nThe hamstring portion originates primarily from the **ischial tuberosity**.\r\n\r\nThis origin resembles that of the hamstrings and creates a longitudinal fiber orientation particularly suited to producing hip extension.\r\n\r\n## Insertion\r\n\r\n### Adductor Portion\r\n\r\nThe adductor portion inserts across a long region of the posterior femur, including:\r\n\r\n- the gluteal tuberosity;\r\n- linea aspera;\r\n- medial supracondylar line.\r\n\r\nIts insertion therefore spans a substantial proportion of femoral length.\r\n\r\nThis broad attachment is one reason why different regions of adductor magnus can have markedly different lines of action.\r\n\r\n### Hamstring Portion\r\n\r\nThe hamstring portion descends to a strong tendinous insertion at the **adductor tubercle of the medial femoral condyle**.\r\n\r\nBetween the distal attachments are openings allowing passage of perforating vessels.\r\n\r\nThe most distal opening forms the **adductor hiatus**, through which the femoral vessels pass from the adductor canal into the popliteal fossa.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nAdductor magnus has an unusual **dual innervation** corresponding to its functional subdivisions.\r\n\r\n## Adductor Portion\r\n\r\nThe adductor portion is supplied primarily by the **posterior division of the obturator nerve**, predominantly from spinal roots **L2-L4**.\r\n\r\n## Hamstring Portion\r\n\r\nThe hamstring portion is supplied by the **tibial division of the sciatic nerve**, predominantly from **L4-S1**.\r\n\r\nThis dual innervation is more than anatomical trivia.\r\n\r\nIt reflects the muscle's mixed developmental and functional identity:\r\n\r\n- its adductor portion belongs functionally to the medial thigh compartment;\r\n- its hamstring portion behaves more like a posterior-chain hip extensor.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Hip Adduction\r\n\r\nAdductor magnus produces strong **hip adduction**.\r\n\r\nBecause of its large physiological cross-sectional area and broad insertion, it can generate substantial force pulling the femur toward the body's midline.\r\n\r\nIn open-chain movement, this means femoral adduction.\r\n\r\nIn closed-chain movement, the same force contributes to stabilization of the pelvis over the femur.\r\n\r\nHowever, describing the entire muscle merely as a hip adductor substantially understates its role.\r\n\r\n## Hip Extension\r\n\r\nAdductor magnus is an important **hip extensor**.\r\n\r\nThis is especially true when the hip begins in moderate-to-deep flexion.\r\n\r\nAs hip flexion increases, the geometry of the muscle can create favorable extension leverage. Consequently, adductor magnus can contribute strongly to the ascent from:\r\n\r\n- deep squats;\r\n- leg presses;\r\n- lunges;\r\n- split squats;\r\n- deep hip-hinge positions.\r\n\r\nIts extension moment arm is not constant across ROM.\r\n\r\nThe muscle becomes particularly mechanically relevant when substantial hip-extension torque must be produced from a flexed starting position.\r\n\r\nThis creates an important distinction from gluteus maximus.\r\n\r\nThe two muscles overlap strongly in function but their leverage profiles across hip angles are not identical.\r\n\r\n## Regional Function\r\n\r\nAdductor magnus should not be treated as a uniform muscle.\r\n\r\nDifferent fascicular regions have different orientations and moment arms.\r\n\r\nBroadly:\r\n\r\n### Superior / Adductor Fibers\r\n\r\nThese fibers have stronger adduction roles and can contribute to other hip motions depending on joint position.\r\n\r\n### Inferior / Hamstring Fibers\r\n\r\nThese fibers are strongly oriented toward hip extension.\r\n\r\nThey act mechanically more like a large monoarticular hip extensor than a conventional short adductor.\r\n\r\nThis regionalization means an exercise can load one functional component of adductor magnus substantially more than another.\r\n\r\n## Role in Deep Hip Flexion\r\n\r\nAdductor magnus becomes especially relevant when the hip is deeply flexed.\r\n\r\nIn this position:\r\n\r\n- substantial external hip-flexion torque exists;\r\n- the muscle possesses favorable geometry for opposing that torque;\r\n- large forces can therefore be required during the transition from deep flexion toward extension.\r\n\r\nThis explains why deep squatting patterns can provide a significant hypertrophic stimulus to adductor magnus even though no obvious visible "adduction" occurs.\r\n\r\nThe muscle is functioning primarily as part of the hip-extension system.\r\n\r\n## Pelvic Stabilization\r\n\r\nAs with the other adductors, adductor magnus contributes to frontal-plane stabilization.\r\n\r\nDuring unilateral tasks it can:\r\n\r\n- control femoral position;\r\n- assist stabilization of the pelvis;\r\n- cooperate with abductors through co-contraction;\r\n- contribute to transfer of force between the pelvis and lower limb.\r\n\r\nIts large size gives it substantial potential to contribute to joint stiffness and force control even when visible movement occurs mainly in another plane.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nAdductor magnus has a strong theoretical case for receiving hypertrophic stimulus at long muscle lengths, but the relevant "stretch" depends on which portion of the muscle is considered.\r\n\r\nFor its hip-extensor regions, **hip flexion** is especially important.\r\n\r\nDeep hip flexion places these fibers in a relatively lengthened configuration while simultaneously creating large external hip-flexion moments that must be overcome during exercises such as the squat.\r\n\r\nThis combination is attractive from a hypertrophy perspective:\r\n\r\n- long muscle length;\r\n- substantial active force;\r\n- high external joint torque;\r\n- large loaded ROM.\r\n\r\nThis may partly explain why full-depth squat training can produce substantial adductor magnus hypertrophy.\r\n\r\nImportantly, this is not exactly the same mechanism as direct hip-adduction training.\r\n\r\nFor `adductor_longus_brevis`, hip abduction is the most intuitive method of creating substantial lengthening.\r\n\r\nFor `adductor_magnus`, particularly its posterior/inferior regions, **deep hip flexion under extension load** can be at least as important.\r\n\r\nTherefore two distinct long-length loading strategies exist:\r\n\r\n### 1. Abduction-Based Lengthening\r\n\r\nDirect hip-adduction exercises begin with the legs apart and load the muscle while the hip is abducted.\r\n\r\nExamples:\r\n\r\n- seated hip-adduction machine;\r\n- cable hip adduction.\r\n\r\n### 2. Flexion-Based Lengthening\r\n\r\nHip-extension exercises load adductor magnus while the hip is deeply flexed.\r\n\r\nExamples:\r\n\r\n- deep squat;\r\n- deep leg press;\r\n- deficit or deep split squat;\r\n- certain hip-hinge patterns.\r\n\r\nThe second mechanism is particularly important for differentiating adductor magnus from the more anterior adductors.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Interpretation\r\n\r\nThere is direct evidence that adductor magnus can hypertrophy substantially in response to squat training.\r\n\r\nHowever, this should not be interpreted as definitive proof that all observed hypertrophy is caused specifically by stretch-mediated mechanisms.\r\n\r\nMultiple factors coexist:\r\n\r\n- high mechanical tension;\r\n- large hip-extension demand;\r\n- substantial muscle length;\r\n- regional recruitment;\r\n- progressive overload.\r\n\r\nThe database `smh_factor` should therefore describe the expected importance of lengthened loading rather than imply a precisely isolated biological mechanism.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Deep Squatting Is Direct Adductor Magnus Training\r\n\r\nA major practical point is that adductor magnus does **not** require an explicit adduction movement to receive a strong training stimulus.\r\n\r\nDeep squats can load it heavily as a hip extensor.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n> A lifter performing substantial deep squat or leg-press volume may already be performing considerable adductor magnus training.\r\n\r\nThis is particularly important when estimating muscle-specific volume.\r\n\r\nCounting only hip-adduction-machine sets would substantially underestimate total adductor magnus exposure.\r\n\r\n## Squat Depth\r\n\r\nGreater squat depth generally increases hip flexion.\r\n\r\nGreater hip flexion increases the hip-extension demand required to rise from the bottom position.\r\n\r\nThis creates conditions that favor substantial adductor magnus involvement.\r\n\r\nConsequently:\r\n\r\n- shallow squats are less compelling as a dedicated adductor magnus stimulus;\r\n- parallel squats provide meaningful loading;\r\n- deep squats can provide particularly strong loading where individual mobility permits.\r\n\r\nThis does not mean that maximum anatomical depth must always be pursued.\r\n\r\nUseful depth is the deepest position that preserves:\r\n\r\n- stable foot pressure;\r\n- controlled pelvic position;\r\n- tolerable hip mechanics;\r\n- appropriate lumbar control;\r\n- productive force generation.\r\n\r\n## Leg Press\r\n\r\nA deep leg press can also provide substantial adductor magnus loading.\r\n\r\nIts advantages include:\r\n\r\n- high external loading;\r\n- strong stabilization;\r\n- easy proximity to failure;\r\n- substantial hip flexion;\r\n- lower balance requirements than free-weight squatting.\r\n\r\nMachine geometry matters substantially.\r\n\r\nA leg press that allows large hip flexion while maintaining pelvic control will generally offer a stronger theoretical adductor magnus stimulus than one with a very restricted ROM.\r\n\r\n## Stance Width\r\n\r\nA somewhat wider stance can increase the relevance of the hip adductors, but stance width should not be treated as a simple linear "more width = more adductor magnus" relationship.\r\n\r\nChanging stance affects:\r\n\r\n- hip abduction;\r\n- hip rotation;\r\n- achievable depth;\r\n- knee trajectory;\r\n- pelvic mechanics;\r\n- moment arms.\r\n\r\nA moderate individualized stance that allows deep comfortable hip flexion is generally more useful than forcing an artificially extreme sumo position solely to target the muscle.\r\n\r\n## Hip Hinges\r\n\r\nHip-extension exercises such as Romanian deadlifts can involve adductor magnus.\r\n\r\nHowever, their muscle-distribution profile differs from deep squatting.\r\n\r\nRDLs strongly load the hamstrings because the knees remain relatively extended, whereas deep squatting shortens the hamstrings at the knee and changes the relative distribution of hip-extension demand.\r\n\r\nThus, if the goal is specifically adductor magnus development, deep squat/leg-press patterns may provide a particularly distinctive stimulus.\r\n\r\nHip hinges still remain valid supplementary exposure.\r\n\r\n## Direct Hip Adduction\r\n\r\nMachine hip adduction trains adductor magnus together with the other hip adductors.\r\n\r\nThis may be useful when:\r\n\r\n- adductor hypertrophy is a specific priority;\r\n- compound lower-body volume is low;\r\n- greater frontal-plane strength is desired;\r\n- additional volume is needed without further axial loading.\r\n\r\nDirect adduction should therefore be considered **complementary**, not mandatory.\r\n\r\nA program containing deep squats and deep leg presses may already provide substantial adductor magnus stimulus even without isolation work.\r\n\r\n## Single-Leg Exercises\r\n\r\nDeep unilateral exercises can also provide meaningful loading:\r\n\r\n- Bulgarian split squats;\r\n- lunges;\r\n- step-ups;\r\n- deep split squats.\r\n\r\nAdductor magnus contributes both to hip extension and stabilization.\r\n\r\nSingle-leg variants also increase frontal-plane stabilization requirements, creating a slightly different functional demand than bilateral squats.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Count Compound Volume\r\n\r\nUnlike smaller adductors, adductor magnus exposure should not be estimated solely from isolation exercises.\r\n\r\nWhen estimating weekly stimulus, account for hard sets of:\r\n\r\n- deep squats;\r\n- deep leg presses;\r\n- split squats;\r\n- lunges;\r\n- selected hip hinges;\r\n- direct hip adduction.\r\n\r\nA lifter completing substantial lower-body compound volume may need **zero additional direct sets** specifically for adductor magnus hypertrophy.\r\n\r\n## Hypertrophy Priority\r\n\r\nIf adductor magnus is not a specific priority, compound training is usually sufficient.\r\n\r\nIf development is specifically desired, direct adduction can be added after accounting for existing compound exposure.\r\n\r\nA reasonable progression might be:\r\n\r\n- first maximize productive ROM in existing compound exercises;\r\n- then add a small amount of direct adduction;\r\n- increase direct volume only if recovery and performance remain good.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\nBecause the muscle can be trained through several very different exercise classes, useful repetition ranges are broad.\r\n\r\n### Compound Exercises\r\n\r\nApproximately:\r\n\r\n- 5-10 reps for heavy squats or presses;\r\n- 8-15 reps for moderate hypertrophy-oriented work;\r\n- higher repetitions where machine stability permits.\r\n\r\n### Direct Adduction\r\n\r\nApproximately:\r\n\r\n- 8-15 reps for heavier machine work;\r\n- 10-20 reps as a practical default;\r\n- 15-30 reps for lighter joint-friendly isolation work.\r\n\r\nNo special repetition range is physiologically unique to adductor magnus.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nThe muscle can receive meaningful loading across multiple lower-body sessions.\r\n\r\nFor most lifters, **2 weekly exposures** arise naturally through lower-body programming.\r\n\r\nThree or more exposures may also be viable, but total load should include indirect work rather than counting only designated adductor exercises.\r\n\r\n## Fatigue Management\r\n\r\nAdductor magnus is a large muscle capable of tolerating substantial training loads, but it also participates in many common lower-body movements.\r\n\r\nExcessive direct work can therefore create hidden interference with:\r\n\r\n- squatting;\r\n- deadlifting;\r\n- leg pressing;\r\n- sprinting;\r\n- running;\r\n- lateral movement.\r\n\r\nLarge increases in squat depth or direct adduction ROM can also produce substantial initial soreness.\r\n\r\nProgressive exposure is preferable to abrupt addition of large volumes.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Order\r\n\r\nWhen adductor magnus is merely a secondary target, compounds can remain the primary stimulus and no special ordering is required.\r\n\r\nIf direct adductor development is a priority:\r\n\r\n1. perform the primary compound lower-body exercise;\r\n2. perform additional hip-extension work if programmed;\r\n3. finish with stable hip-adduction work.\r\n\r\nIsolation before heavy squatting is generally unnecessary unless adductor activation or rehabilitation is itself the objective.\r\n\r\n## Failure\r\n\r\nDeep squats and other free-weight compounds should not be taken to failure solely to maximize adductor magnus stimulus.\r\n\r\nThe systemic and technical cost becomes disproportionately high.\r\n\r\nStable machine exercises allow much closer proximity to local failure:\r\n\r\n- leg press: close to failure with appropriate safety;\r\n- hip-adduction machine: very close to local failure;\r\n- free-weight squat: usually leave more technical reserve.\r\n\r\nThis creates a useful combination:\r\n\r\n- high-load, long-length stimulus from compounds;\r\n- low-systemic-cost additional volume from isolation.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## The Name Understates the Muscle\r\n\r\nAdductor magnus literally means **"large adductor"**, yet hip extension may be one of its most important functional roles in human movement.\r\n\r\nThis makes it a useful example of why anatomical muscle names should not be treated as complete biomechanical descriptions.\r\n\r\n## It Is One of the Largest Muscles of the Thigh\r\n\r\nAdductor magnus occupies a very large fraction of the medial and posterior thigh.\r\n\r\nVisually, much of its mass is hidden beneath other muscles, so its contribution to overall thigh musculature is easy to underestimate.\r\n\r\n## Dual Innervation\r\n\r\nIts two major portions receive innervation from completely different major nerves:\r\n\r\n- obturator nerve;\r\n- tibial division of the sciatic nerve.\r\n\r\nFew major muscles display such an obvious anatomical split between neural supply and functional specialization.\r\n\r\n## The Adductor Hiatus\r\n\r\nThe distal tendon forms the **adductor hiatus**, an anatomical passage through which the femoral artery and vein transition toward the posterior knee.\r\n\r\nAfter traversing this opening, the vessels are referred to as the popliteal artery and vein.\r\n\r\nThus, adductor magnus is also an important anatomical landmark for the transition between the anterior/medial thigh and popliteal region.\r\n\r\n## Squats Train More Than the Visible Prime Movers\r\n\r\nAdductor magnus is one of the clearest examples of why exercise classification based only on visible joint movement can be misleading.\r\n\r\nA squat contains essentially no obvious dynamic hip adduction.\r\n\r\nNevertheless, adductor magnus can receive substantial training stimulus because its fibers generate **hip-extension torque**.\r\n\r\nThis is an important principle for muscle-recruitment modelling:\r\n\r\n> muscle function should be derived from moment arms and force requirements, not merely from the anatomical name of the muscle or the visually dominant plane of motion.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\n`adductor_magnus` should be modelled independently from `adductor_longus_brevis`.\r\n\r\nThe distinction is biomechanically meaningful.\r\n\r\n### Direct Hip Adduction\r\n\r\nBoth records should receive substantial demand.\r\n\r\n### Deep Squat / Deep Leg Press\r\n\r\n`adductor_magnus` should receive a much larger allocation than would be predicted by a simplistic "adduction-only" model because it acts strongly as a hip extensor.\r\n\r\n### Hip Hinge\r\n\r\nAdductor magnus should receive non-zero and potentially meaningful extension demand, although the relative distribution versus:\r\n\r\n- gluteus maximus;\r\n- semimembranosus;\r\n- semitendinosus;\r\n- biceps femoris long head\r\n\r\ndepends strongly on hip and knee geometry.\r\n\r\n### Shallow Knee-Dominant Movements\r\n\r\nDemand should generally be lower than in deep hip-flexion variants because the exercise imposes less hip-extension work and typically places the muscle in a less advantageous context for substantial extension loading.\r\n\r\n## Regional Simplification\r\n\r\nThe current database represents adductor magnus as a single muscle vector.\r\n\r\nThat is a reasonable abstraction for exercise-level modelling, but biologically the muscle contains regions with different:\r\n\r\n- fiber trajectories;\r\n- moment arms;\r\n- insertion sites;\r\n- neural supply;\r\n- functional roles.\r\n\r\nTherefore a single recruitment scalar for `adductor_magnus` represents **aggregate mechanical demand**, not uniform activation of every fascicle.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **Anatomy and dual innervation:** high.\r\n- **Hip adduction function:** high.\r\n- **Important hip-extension function:** high.\r\n- **Greater extension relevance in flexed hip positions:** high.\r\n- **Substantial involvement in deep squatting:** high.\r\n- **Hypertrophy from squat training:** directly supported.\r\n- **Exact regional recruitment during individual resistance exercises:** moderate.\r\n- **Specific magnitude of stretch-mediated hypertrophy:** plausible but not independently quantifiable from current evidence.\r\n\r\nFor Agonez, the key modelling correction is therefore not simply "adductor magnus = strong adductor."\r\n\r\nIt is:\r\n\r\n> **adductor magnus = large, regionally heterogeneous hip muscle with major adduction and hip-extension functions, particularly important for producing extension torque from flexed hip positions.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/Adductors}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	14.44	15.50	0.964	109.19	105.22
41	iliopsoas	Musculus iliopsoas	Lower	Hip_FA	1230.00	1164.77	77.00	Fusiform/Parallel	0.475	0.525	high	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\n`iliopsoas` is a functional aggregate representing the deep anterior hip-flexor complex formed primarily by:\r\n\r\n- **psoas major**;\r\n- **iliacus**.\r\n\r\nThese muscles have different proximal origins but converge distally toward a common insertion on the lesser trochanter of the femur.\r\n\r\nFor exercise-level modelling, combining them is practical because both are major contributors to **hip flexion**.\r\n\r\nHowever, they are not mechanically identical.\r\n\r\nThe key distinction is:\r\n\r\n- `iliacus` originates from the pelvis;\r\n- `psoas_major` originates from the lumbar spine and adjacent vertebral structures.\r\n\r\nTherefore psoas major can influence both:\r\n\r\n- the femur relative to the pelvis;\r\n- the lumbar spine relative to the pelvis/femur.\r\n\r\nThis gives iliopsoas a more complex role than a simple monoarticular limb muscle.\r\n\r\nIts dominant mechanical identity remains:\r\n\r\n> **generation of hip-flexion torque, especially during open-chain elevation of the femur.**\r\n\r\nIt is heavily involved in:\r\n\r\n- straight-leg raising;\r\n- hanging leg raises;\r\n- high-knee actions;\r\n- sprint recovery mechanics;\r\n- climbing;\r\n- resisted hip flexion.\r\n\r\nIt should be distinguished from other hip flexors such as:\r\n\r\n- rectus femoris;\r\n- sartorius;\r\n- tensor fasciae latae;\r\n- pectineus.\r\n\r\nAmong these, iliopsoas is particularly important because it retains strong hip-flexion leverage without crossing the knee.\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\n## Psoas Major\r\n\r\n### Origin\r\n\r\nPsoas major originates broadly from the lumbar spine.\r\n\r\nIts attachments include regions of the:\r\n\r\n- bodies of the lower thoracic and lumbar vertebrae;\r\n- intervertebral discs;\r\n- transverse processes of the lumbar vertebrae.\r\n\r\nThe muscle contains superficial and deeper fascicular components rather than one simple longitudinal bundle.\r\n\r\nIts proximal attachments place it directly adjacent to the lumbar vertebral column.\r\n\r\nThis is the anatomical basis for its ability to influence both:\r\n\r\n- hip motion;\r\n- lumbar mechanics.\r\n\r\n### Insertion\r\n\r\nPsoas major descends through the pelvis and joins the iliacus distally.\r\n\r\nThe combined tendon inserts primarily onto the **lesser trochanter of the femur**.\r\n\r\nThe tendon passes anterior to the hip joint.\r\n\r\nThis anterior line of action produces a strong hip-flexion moment.\r\n\r\n## Iliacus\r\n\r\n### Origin\r\n\r\nIliacus originates broadly from the **iliac fossa** on the internal surface of the ilium.\r\n\r\nAdditional attachments may include adjacent iliac and sacral regions.\r\n\r\nIts broad fan-shaped origin converges toward the anterior hip.\r\n\r\n### Insertion\r\n\r\nMost iliacus fibers merge into the iliopsoas tendon and insert onto or near the **lesser trochanter**.\r\n\r\nSome iliacus fibers can have more independent femoral attachments near the lesser-trochanter region.\r\n\r\n## Iliopsoas Tendon\r\n\r\nThe psoas major and iliacus converge into a strong tendon as they pass anterior to the hip.\r\n\r\nThe tendon travels close to:\r\n\r\n- the anterior hip capsule;\r\n- the iliopectineal region;\r\n- the iliopsoas bursa.\r\n\r\nThis close relationship is clinically relevant because snapping, irritation or tendinopathy can occur around the anterior hip.\r\n\r\n## Psoas Minor\r\n\r\nPsoas minor is anatomically variable and absent in a substantial proportion of people.\r\n\r\nIt does not insert on the femur and is not a major hip flexor.\r\n\r\nTherefore, for the Agonez `iliopsoas` record:\r\n\r\n> **psoas minor should not contribute materially to the modeled hip-flexion PCSA or recruitment vector.**\r\n\r\nThe functional aggregate should primarily represent psoas major + iliacus.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nThe two major components have different neural supply.\r\n\r\n## Psoas Major\r\n\r\nPsoas major is supplied directly by branches from the **lumbar plexus / anterior rami**, primarily around:\r\n\r\n- L1;\r\n- L2;\r\n- L3.\r\n\r\n## Iliacus\r\n\r\nIliacus is supplied primarily by the **femoral nerve**, predominantly from:\r\n\r\n- L2;\r\n- L3.\r\n\r\nThis difference reinforces the fact that iliopsoas is an anatomical complex rather than one indivisible muscle.\r\n\r\nFor exercise-level modelling, however, separate innervation is usually less important than their shared hip-flexion function.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Hip Flexion\r\n\r\nThe dominant function of iliopsoas is **hip flexion**.\r\n\r\nIn open-chain movement, it moves the femur toward the torso.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- leg raise;\r\n- knee raise;\r\n- resisted march;\r\n- sprint recovery.\r\n\r\nIn closed-chain movement, the same force can move the trunk/pelvis toward the femur.\r\n\r\nTherefore hip-flexor torque can manifest as either:\r\n\r\n- femoral flexion;\r\n- trunk movement relative to a fixed thigh.\r\n\r\nIliopsoas activation rises substantially during open-chain hip-flexion tasks, particularly through moderate-to-high hip-flexion angles. :contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}\r\n\r\n## Hip Flexion Across Joint Angles\r\n\r\nIliopsoas does not possess a constant flexion moment arm throughout ROM.\r\n\r\nIts leverage changes as the femur moves relative to the pelvis.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n- force contribution;\r\n- muscle length;\r\n- torque capacity\r\n\r\nshould be considered angle-dependent.\r\n\r\nThe muscle remains particularly relevant through substantial hip-flexion ROM and is strongly recruited during tasks such as straight-leg raising. :contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}\r\n\r\n## Open-Chain Hip Flexion\r\n\r\nOpen-chain hip flexion is one of the cleanest ways to generate direct iliopsoas demand.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- straight-leg raise;\r\n- hanging knee raise;\r\n- hanging leg raise;\r\n- cable hip flexion;\r\n- resisted knee drive.\r\n\r\nThese exercises require active femoral flexion against gravity or external resistance.\r\n\r\nThis is mechanically different from merely holding the pelvis stable while other muscles move the lower limb.\r\n\r\n## Lumbar-Spine Interaction\r\n\r\nPsoas major originates from the lumbar spine.\r\n\r\nTherefore it can influence lumbar mechanics depending on which end is relatively fixed.\r\n\r\nThis does **not** mean it should be simplistically labeled a "lumbar flexor."\r\n\r\nIts actual spinal effect depends on:\r\n\r\n- segment;\r\n- posture;\r\n- bilateral vs unilateral contraction;\r\n- pelvic position;\r\n- external loading.\r\n\r\nDepending on configuration, psoas force can contribute to:\r\n\r\n- lumbar compression;\r\n- segmental stabilization;\r\n- lateral flexion;\r\n- altered lumbar lordotic mechanics.\r\n\r\nFor resistance-training modelling, its dominant function should still remain hip flexion.\r\n\r\nLumbar effects are secondary and context-dependent.\r\n\r\n## Pelvic Control\r\n\r\nIliopsoas can influence pelvic position indirectly through its relationship with the femur and lumbar spine.\r\n\r\nDuring high-force hip flexion, insufficient trunk stabilization can allow:\r\n\r\n- anterior pelvic rotation;\r\n- lumbar extension;\r\n- torso movement.\r\n\r\nTherefore many apparent "hip-flexor exercises" are actually coupled tasks involving:\r\n\r\n- iliopsoas;\r\n- abdominal musculature;\r\n- spinal stabilizers.\r\n\r\nThis distinction is highly relevant to hanging leg raises.\r\n\r\n## External Rotation\r\n\r\nIliopsoas can contribute to **external rotation of the femur**, although this is secondary to hip flexion.\r\n\r\nThe magnitude of this rotational action depends on hip angle and individual geometry.\r\n\r\nHip rotational moment arms are strongly angle-dependent across the musculature. :contentReference[oaicite:3]{index=3}\r\n\r\nTherefore external rotation should be treated as a secondary modifier rather than a defining training function.\r\n\r\n## Dynamic Hip Stabilization\r\n\r\nBecause the iliopsoas tendon crosses close to the anterior hip joint, contraction can also contribute to dynamic stabilization of the femoral head.\r\n\r\nThis becomes particularly relevant during:\r\n\r\n- hip flexion under load;\r\n- locomotion;\r\n- rapid changes between extension and flexion.\r\n\r\nAgain, stabilization is secondary to its large hip-flexion role.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nDirect evidence specifically examining **stretch-mediated hypertrophy of iliopsoas** is limited.\r\n\r\nThe anatomy nevertheless gives a clear first-order prediction:\r\n\r\n> **hip extension lengthens iliopsoas.**\r\n\r\nThis is especially evident for iliacus and psoas major as the femur moves posteriorly relative to the pelvis.\r\n\r\nStatic hip-extension stretching measurably affects iliacus mechanical properties, confirming that hip extension meaningfully loads the muscle in a lengthened state. :contentReference[oaicite:4]{index=4}\r\n\r\n## Long-Length Position\r\n\r\nThe most intuitive long-length iliopsoas position combines:\r\n\r\n- hip extension;\r\n- relatively stable pelvis.\r\n\r\nExamples include the trailing leg of:\r\n\r\n- a split stance;\r\n- a lunge;\r\n- a dedicated hip-flexor stretch.\r\n\r\nHowever, passive stretching is not equivalent to hypertrophic loading.\r\n\r\nFor stretch-mediated hypertrophy, the relevant condition would be:\r\n\r\n- long muscle length;\r\n- meaningful active hip-flexion force.\r\n\r\n## Long-Length Resistance Challenge\r\n\r\nThis is more difficult to create than for muscles such as gastrocnemius.\r\n\r\nMany common hip-flexion exercises become hardest after the femur has already moved into flexion.\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n\r\n- hanging leg raises;\r\n- straight-leg raises\r\n\r\noften provide little direct external hip-flexion torque when the leg is vertically aligned with gravity and more torque as the leg becomes horizontal.\r\n\r\nThus the exercise can load iliopsoas strongly while it is already shortening.\r\n\r\n## Cable Hip Flexion From Extension\r\n\r\nA cable setup can create more explicit long-length loading.\r\n\r\nIf the working leg begins behind the torso in hip extension and the cable provides a posteriorly directed force, the iliopsoas can generate active hip-flexion torque from a relatively lengthened position.\r\n\r\nThis is mechanically attractive for hypertrophy.\r\n\r\nHowever, direct longitudinal evidence showing superiority of this strategy is currently limited.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Interpretation\r\n\r\nThe strongest defensible conclusions are:\r\n\r\n- hip extension lengthens iliopsoas;\r\n- active resistance can theoretically be applied in that position;\r\n- direct long-length hypertrophy evidence is sparse;\r\n- `smh_factor` should therefore be conservative unless it represents a model-level biomechanical estimate.\r\n\r\nIliopsoas should not receive a high-confidence SMH classification comparable with gastrocnemius.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Straight-Leg Raise\r\n\r\nStraight-leg raising is a strong hip-flexor task.\r\n\r\nThe long lever created by an extended knee increases external torque around the hip.\r\n\r\nMajor contributors include:\r\n\r\n- iliopsoas;\r\n- rectus femoris;\r\n- tensor fasciae latae;\r\n- sartorius.\r\n\r\nIliopsoas activation can become high through mid-to-high ranges of hip flexion. :contentReference[oaicite:5]{index=5}\r\n\r\nHowever, the movement is not isolated.\r\n\r\nThe trunk and pelvis must be stabilized by the abdominal system.\r\n\r\n## Hanging Leg Raise\r\n\r\nHanging leg raises create large hip-flexion demands, particularly during the initial femoral elevation phase.\r\n\r\nThe movement can be decomposed conceptually into:\r\n\r\n### Hip Flexion\r\n\r\nDriven substantially by:\r\n- iliopsoas;\r\n- rectus femoris;\r\n- other hip flexors.\r\n\r\n### Posterior Pelvic Rotation / Trunk Flexion\r\n\r\nDriven substantially by:\r\n- rectus abdominis;\r\n- obliques;\r\n- deeper abdominal musculature.\r\n\r\nThis distinction is crucial.\r\n\r\nA hanging leg raise is not purely an abdominal exercise.\r\n\r\nDuring much of the movement, iliopsoas can be one of the major prime movers.\r\n\r\n## Hanging Knee Raise\r\n\r\nFlexing the knees shortens the external leg lever.\r\n\r\nThis reduces the external hip-flexion moment compared with a straight-leg raise at equivalent hip angles.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n> straight-leg hanging raise generally imposes greater hip-flexor torque than bent-knee raising, all else equal.\r\n\r\nKnee flexion also shortens rectus femoris distally, potentially changing force sharing toward iliopsoas.\r\n\r\nThis can make the interpretation more complex than simple load reduction.\r\n\r\n## Cable Hip Flexion\r\n\r\nCable hip flexion is one of the most direct ways to train iliopsoas.\r\n\r\nAdvantages include:\r\n\r\n- clear progressive overload;\r\n- adjustable resistance direction;\r\n- unilateral loading;\r\n- possibility of training from hip extension.\r\n\r\nA cuff around the ankle or thigh can be used depending on desired lever length.\r\n\r\nA thigh attachment reduces knee and lower-leg stabilization demands.\r\n\r\n## Resisted March\r\n\r\nStanding resisted marches can train hip flexion while incorporating:\r\n\r\n- pelvic stabilization;\r\n- contralateral stance control;\r\n- trunk anti-extension.\r\n\r\nThey are useful for athletic transfer but less locally standardized than machine or cable hip flexion.\r\n\r\n## Sit-Ups\r\n\r\nTraditional sit-ups involve significant hip-flexor activity after the trunk begins moving substantially relative to the pelvis.\r\n\r\nThis is one reason they differ mechanically from isolated spinal-flexion exercises.\r\n\r\nAs the torso rises, iliopsoas can contribute to closing the hip angle.\r\n\r\nTherefore sit-ups should not be modeled as pure abdominal work.\r\n\r\n## Kneeling Cable Crunch\r\n\r\nA well-executed kneeling cable crunch should primarily involve spinal flexion.\r\n\r\nHip angle may change somewhat, but iliopsoas should receive much less dynamic demand than during leg raises or sit-ups.\r\n\r\nThis distinction is important in exercise classification.\r\n\r\n## Deep Squat\r\n\r\nDeep squatting places iliopsoas in a shortened hip-flexed state.\r\n\r\nHowever, the exercise does not require active hip-flexion torque during ascent.\r\n\r\nHip extensors dominate.\r\n\r\nTherefore high hip flexion does not imply high iliopsoas recruitment.\r\n\r\nThis is a key modelling rule:\r\n\r\n> **muscle length state and active torque demand must remain separate.**\r\n\r\n## Running and Sprinting\r\n\r\nIliopsoas is important during swing-phase hip flexion, particularly when rapidly advancing the thigh.\r\n\r\nThis makes it relevant to:\r\n\r\n- sprint mechanics;\r\n- high-knee actions;\r\n- rapid leg recovery.\r\n\r\nHigh-speed hip-flexion demands differ substantially from slow hypertrophy training because of:\r\n\r\n- contraction velocity;\r\n- force-time constraints;\r\n- elastic and inertial effects.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Direct Training Is Often Absent\r\n\r\nMany resistance-training programs contain no dedicated hip-flexion exercise.\r\n\r\nIliopsoas may nevertheless receive some stimulus from:\r\n\r\n- hanging leg raises;\r\n- leg raises;\r\n- sit-ups;\r\n- running.\r\n\r\nIf hip-flexor hypertrophy or strength is a specific goal, direct resistance is usually required.\r\n\r\n## Weekly Volume\r\n\r\nFor direct hypertrophy, a practical starting point is approximately:\r\n\r\n- **2-6 hard hip-flexion sets per week**.\r\n\r\nThis is usually sufficient because many trainees have little prior direct hip-flexor resistance exposure.\r\n\r\nVolume can be increased progressively if:\r\n\r\n- recovery is good;\r\n- local anterior-hip tolerance remains good;\r\n- the muscle is a priority.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nTwo weekly exposures are a useful default.\r\n\r\nHigher frequency can be appropriate for athletic hip-flexor training if individual sessions remain low volume.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\nDirect cable or machine hip flexion works well in:\r\n\r\n- 8-15 reps;\r\n- 12-20 reps;\r\n- 15-25 reps.\r\n\r\nVery heavy low-repetition hip flexion is often limited by:\r\n\r\n- pelvic compensation;\r\n- trunk movement;\r\n- discomfort around the anterior hip.\r\n\r\nModerate loading is usually easier to standardize.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nStable direct hip-flexion exercises can be trained close to failure:\r\n\r\n- approximately **0-2 RIR**.\r\n\r\nHowever, the set should end when the intended hip-flexion ROM can no longer be maintained without:\r\n\r\n- pelvic rotation;\r\n- lumbar extension;\r\n- trunk momentum.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Order\r\n\r\nIf iliopsoas is a hypertrophy or strength priority, direct hip flexion should occur before excessive abdominal or lower-body fatigue.\r\n\r\nIf it is secondary, it can be placed:\r\n\r\n- after lower-body compounds;\r\n- alongside abdominal training.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Ab Training\r\n\r\nHip-flexion exercises can create substantial abdominal fatigue because the trunk must resist pelvic movement.\r\n\r\nConversely, pre-fatiguing the abdominals can reduce performance in hip-flexion exercises.\r\n\r\nThus hanging leg raises are not locally isolated and should be counted in both systems.\r\n\r\n## Athletic Programming\r\n\r\nFor sprint-oriented training, iliopsoas work can include:\r\n\r\n- resisted marches;\r\n- rapid cable hip flexion;\r\n- high-knee drills;\r\n- sprinting itself.\r\n\r\nThese emphasize:\r\n\r\n- rate of force development;\r\n- coordination;\r\n- high contraction velocity.\r\n\r\nThat programming goal differs from hypertrophy-oriented slow cable flexion.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## It Connects the Spine Directly to the Femur\r\n\r\nPsoas major is unusual because it connects the **axial skeleton** directly to the lower limb.\r\n\r\nIts path runs from the lumbar vertebral region to the femur through the iliopsoas tendon.\r\n\r\nThis gives it a unique mechanical role at the junction between:\r\n\r\n- spine;\r\n- pelvis;\r\n- hip.\r\n\r\n## Iliopsoas Is Not One Anatomical Muscle\r\n\r\nThe name refers primarily to the functional union of:\r\n\r\n- psoas major;\r\n- iliacus.\r\n\r\nThey have:\r\n\r\n- different origins;\r\n- different innervation;\r\n- partly different mechanical roles.\r\n\r\nThey converge because they share a major distal tendon and hip-flexion action.\r\n\r\n## Psoas Minor Should Not Be Treated as a Major Hip Flexor\r\n\r\nPsoas minor is frequently absent and does not insert on the femur.\r\n\r\nIt cannot generate the same hip-flexion torque as psoas major and iliacus.\r\n\r\nTherefore including it substantially in PCSA or hip-flexion demand would distort the aggregate.\r\n\r\n## Straight-Leg Raises Are Strong Hip-Flexor Exercises\r\n\r\nLeg raises are frequently described primarily as abdominal exercises.\r\n\r\nBiomechanically, the initial and middle portions require substantial hip-flexor force, and iliopsoas activation rises markedly during these tasks. :contentReference[oaicite:6]{index=6}\r\n\r\nThe abdominal muscles are crucial largely because they stabilize and reposition the pelvis.\r\n\r\n## A "Tight Psoas" Is Not a Complete Diagnosis\r\n\r\nPopular fitness discourse frequently attributes:\r\n\r\n- anterior pelvic tilt;\r\n- back pain;\r\n- poor posture\r\n\r\nto a supposedly "tight psoas."\r\n\r\nThis is usually an oversimplification.\r\n\r\nLumbar and pelvic posture reflect interacting contributions from:\r\n\r\n- skeletal morphology;\r\n- passive tissues;\r\n- abdominal muscles;\r\n- hip extensors;\r\n- spinal extensors;\r\n- neurological control.\r\n\r\nPsoas length or resting tone alone cannot explain complex posture.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\n`iliopsoas` should primarily receive demand from **hip-flexion torque**.\r\n\r\nIts major exercise-state variables are:\r\n\r\n1. required hip-flexion moment;\r\n2. hip angle;\r\n3. external lever length;\r\n4. pelvic/trunk stabilization requirements.\r\n\r\n## Aggregate Definition\r\n\r\nThe record should principally represent:\r\n\r\n- `psoas_major`;\r\n- `iliacus`.\r\n\r\nIt should not materially represent psoas minor.\r\n\r\nBecause psoas major and iliacus possess different architecture and innervation, aggregate:\r\n\r\n- PCSA;\r\n- fiber length;\r\n- pennation;\r\n- fiber-type bias\r\n\r\nshould be interpreted as effective combined parameters.\r\n\r\n## Length-Tension Model\r\n\r\nA simple first approximation is:\r\n\r\n- hip extension → long;\r\n- neutral → intermediate;\r\n- hip flexion → progressively shorter.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_iliopsoas ≈ f(hip_flexion_extension_angle)`\r\n\r\nThis is more defensible for iliacus than as a complete description of psoas major because psoas length can also be affected by lumbar position.\r\n\r\nA more complete conceptual model would be:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_psoas = f(hip_angle, lumbar_position)`\r\n\r\nwhile:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_iliacus ≈ f(hip_angle)`.\r\n\r\nThe aggregate necessarily simplifies this distinction.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Allocation\r\n\r\n### Hanging Leg Raise\r\n\r\nVery high `iliopsoas` demand during the hip-flexion portion.\r\n\r\nAlso substantial:\r\n- abdominal demand;\r\n- grip;\r\n- shoulder/scapular stabilization.\r\n\r\nThe exercise should not be modeled as predominantly abdominal across its entire ROM.\r\n\r\n### Alternating Hanging Oblique Knee Raise\r\n\r\nHigh hip-flexor demand with additional:\r\n- oblique;\r\n- rotational;\r\n- pelvic-control requirements.\r\n\r\n### Dragon Flag\r\n\r\nMeaningful iliopsoas demand can occur because the lower body creates a large external moment and the hip must be controlled.\r\n\r\nHowever, trunk anti-extension and abdominal demand should remain dominant depending on execution.\r\n\r\n### Kneeling Cable Crunch\r\n\r\nLow iliopsoas demand if the movement is true spinal flexion with relatively stable hip angle.\r\n\r\n### Bodyweight Crunch\r\n\r\nVery low iliopsoas dynamic demand if hip angle changes minimally.\r\n\r\n### Straight-Leg Raise\r\n\r\nVery high iliopsoas demand.\r\n\r\n### Bent-Knee Raise\r\n\r\nHigh demand but lower external hip moment than an otherwise comparable straight-leg raise.\r\n\r\n### Sprinting\r\n\r\nHigh functional demand during swing-phase hip flexion.\r\n\r\nDifferent ETU profile from slow resistance exercise because of high velocity.\r\n\r\n## Lever-Length Effect\r\n\r\nFor leg raises:\r\n\r\n`M_hip ≈ segment_weight × horizontal_distance_from_hip`\r\n\r\nStraight knees place more distal limb mass farther from the hip.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n> straight-leg raise should generally require substantially more hip-flexor torque than bent-knee raise at the same hip angle.\r\n\r\nThis is a mechanically clean modifier for Agonez.\r\n\r\n## Rectus Femoris Force Sharing\r\n\r\nHip-flexion demand should not be assigned entirely to iliopsoas.\r\n\r\nImportant synergists include:\r\n\r\n- rectus femoris;\r\n- tensor fasciae latae;\r\n- sartorius;\r\n- pectineus.\r\n\r\nHowever, knee angle modifies this distribution.\r\n\r\nWhen the knee is flexed, rectus femoris becomes shorter distally.\r\n\r\nThis can shift relative hip-flexion demand toward iliopsoas.\r\n\r\nTherefore hanging knee raise and straight-leg raise differ not only in external lever arm but also in synergist length states.\r\n\r\n## Core Interaction\r\n\r\nHip-flexor force can create pelvic and lumbar moments.\r\n\r\nTherefore high iliopsoas force often implies meaningful demand on:\r\n\r\n- rectus abdominis;\r\n- obliques;\r\n- transverse abdominal system;\r\n- spinal stabilizers.\r\n\r\nFor example, in a hanging leg raise:\r\n\r\n`iliopsoas hip-flexion torque`\r\n\r\nmust coexist with sufficient:\r\n\r\n`abdominal pelvic-control torque`\r\n\r\nto prevent the movement from degenerating into uncontrolled lumbar/pelvic compensation.\r\n\r\nThis explains why the exercise can simultaneously be legitimately classified as:\r\n\r\n- hip-flexor exercise;\r\n- abdominal exercise.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nThe evidence-based position is conservative:\r\n\r\n- hip extension clearly lengthens iliopsoas;\r\n- iliacus mechanical properties respond to hip-extension stretching; :contentReference[oaicite:7]{index=7}\r\n- active long-length resistance is mechanically possible;\r\n- direct iliopsoas-specific long-length hypertrophy evidence is sparse.\r\n\r\nTherefore `smh_factor` should not be assigned high confidence unless it represents theoretical mechanical potential rather than proven longitudinal hypertrophy response.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **psoas major + iliacus composition:** high; :contentReference[oaicite:8]{index=8}\r\n- **primary hip-flexion function:** high; :contentReference[oaicite:9]{index=9}\r\n- **strong recruitment during straight-leg raising and loaded hip flexion:** well supported; :contentReference[oaicite:10]{index=10}\r\n- **angle-dependent hip-flexor recruitment:** supported; :contentReference[oaicite:11]{index=11}\r\n- **hip extension lengthening iliacus/iliopsoas:** high mechanically; :contentReference[oaicite:12]{index=12}\r\n- **lumbar mechanical contribution of psoas major:** anatomically well established but highly task-dependent;\r\n- **external-rotation contribution:** plausible and angle-dependent;\r\n- **direct iliopsoas hypertrophy studies:** limited;\r\n- **stretch-mediated hypertrophy magnitude:** poorly established.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **iliopsoas = a deep psoas-major/iliacus hip-flexor complex whose primary resistance-training demand is determined by hip-flexion torque and limb lever length, while psoas major additionally couples hip force production to lumbar mechanics.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/Iliopsoas}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	11.11	12.80	0.975	104.84	102.23
32	gluteus_minimus	Musculus gluteus minimus	Lower	Glutes	270.00	255.68	15.00	Fan-shaped/Radiate	0.575	0.425	high	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\nThe **gluteus minimus** is the smallest and deepest of the three major gluteal muscles.\r\n\r\nIt lies deep to `gluteus_medius` on the external surface of the ilium and inserts onto the greater trochanter of the femur.\r\n\r\nIts principal mechanical roles are:\r\n\r\n- **hip abduction**;\r\n- **pelvic stabilization during unilateral support**;\r\n- contribution to **internal rotation**, especially through anterior fibers;\r\n- dynamic stabilization of the femoral head within the acetabulum;\r\n- control of femoral position during gait and single-leg loading.\r\n\r\nAlthough gluteus minimus and gluteus medius overlap strongly in function, they should not be treated as completely redundant.\r\n\r\nGluteus minimus is:\r\n\r\n- deeper;\r\n- smaller;\r\n- positioned closer to the hip joint;\r\n- relatively well situated for contributing to femoral-head stabilization;\r\n- generally more internally-rotating in its anterior regions.\r\n\r\nThe muscle is also regionally heterogeneous.\r\n\r\nIts anterior and posterior fibers differ in line of pull and can contribute differently to:\r\n\r\n- rotation;\r\n- flexion/extension;\r\n- abduction.\r\n\r\nFor exercise modelling, the most useful simplification is:\r\n\r\n> **gluteus minimus = deep hip abductor and stabilizer with an important internal-rotation bias, especially anteriorly.**\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\n## Origin\r\n\r\nGluteus minimus originates from the **external surface of the ilium**, primarily between the:\r\n\r\n- anterior gluteal line;\r\n- inferior gluteal line.\r\n\r\nIts origin lies inferior and deep to that of gluteus medius.\r\n\r\nThe broad iliac attachment creates a fan-shaped muscle whose fibers converge toward the greater trochanter.\r\n\r\nAnterior fibers run in a somewhat posteroinferior direction.\r\n\r\nPosterior fibers approach the trochanter from a more posterior orientation.\r\n\r\nThis regional geometry underlies differences in rotational function.\r\n\r\n## Insertion\r\n\r\nGluteus minimus inserts onto the **anterior facet of the greater trochanter** and adjacent capsular structures.\r\n\r\nSome fibers have intimate anatomical relationships with the hip joint capsule.\r\n\r\nThis proximity to the capsule supports a role in controlling capsular position and femoral-head mechanics during movement.\r\n\r\nIts insertion is more anterior than much of gluteus medius.\r\n\r\nThat difference contributes to its relatively stronger internal-rotation bias.\r\n\r\n## Relationship With Gluteus Medius\r\n\r\nGluteus minimus lies almost completely beneath gluteus medius.\r\n\r\nBoth muscles:\r\n\r\n- originate from the lateral ilium;\r\n- insert around the greater trochanter;\r\n- generate hip-abduction torque;\r\n- stabilize the pelvis during unilateral support.\r\n\r\nHowever, minimus is positioned:\r\n\r\n- deeper;\r\n- more anteriorly in parts of its insertion;\r\n- closer to the joint center.\r\n\r\nThis changes both leverage and rotational contribution.\r\n\r\n## Regional Architecture\r\n\r\nThe muscle is not mechanically homogeneous.\r\n\r\nA practical regional division is:\r\n\r\n- anterior fibers;\r\n- middle fibers;\r\n- posterior fibers.\r\n\r\nAnterior regions tend to show stronger internal-rotation and flexion tendencies.\r\n\r\nPosterior regions can shift toward more neutral or extension/external-rotation contributions depending on hip angle.\r\n\r\nThese secondary actions are highly position-dependent.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nGluteus minimus is innervated by the **superior gluteal nerve**, primarily from:\r\n\r\n- L4;\r\n- L5;\r\n- S1.\r\n\r\nThe superior gluteal nerve also supplies:\r\n\r\n- gluteus medius;\r\n- tensor fasciae latae.\r\n\r\nThis shared innervation reflects the functional relationship between these muscles as part of the lateral hip-abductor system.\r\n\r\nRegional nerve branching within the muscle supports some degree of functional differentiation across its fascicles.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Hip Abduction\r\n\r\nThe primary gross action of gluteus minimus is **hip abduction**.\r\n\r\nIn open-chain movement, it contributes to moving the femur away from the midline.\r\n\r\nIn closed-chain movement, the same torque stabilizes the pelvis over the stance femur.\r\n\r\nThis makes gluteus minimus important in:\r\n\r\n- walking;\r\n- running;\r\n- single-leg stance;\r\n- step-ups;\r\n- split squats;\r\n- lateral movement.\r\n\r\n## Pelvic Stabilization\r\n\r\nDuring unilateral support, body weight creates an external hip-adduction moment.\r\n\r\nThe stance-side abductor system must oppose that moment.\r\n\r\nGluteus minimus contributes alongside:\r\n\r\n- gluteus medius;\r\n- tensor fasciae latae;\r\n- superior fibers of gluteus maximus.\r\n\r\nTherefore substantial gluteus minimus force can exist even when the hip is not visibly abducting.\r\n\r\nThis is a high-force isometric stabilization role.\r\n\r\n## Femoral-Head Stabilization\r\n\r\nBecause gluteus minimus lies close to the hip joint and has attachments near the capsule, it contributes to dynamic control of the femoral head.\r\n\r\nIts force has both:\r\n\r\n- rotational;\r\n- compressive/stabilizing\r\n\r\ncomponents.\r\n\r\nThis function is particularly relevant during weight bearing.\r\n\r\nThe muscle can help maintain congruence between:\r\n\r\n- femoral head;\r\n- acetabulum\r\n\r\nwhile larger global muscles generate movement.\r\n\r\n## Internal Rotation\r\n\r\nGluteus minimus contributes substantially to **internal rotation of the hip**, especially through its anterior fibers.\r\n\r\nThis function becomes important in combination with:\r\n\r\n- hip flexion;\r\n- abduction;\r\n- gait-related femoral control.\r\n\r\nHowever, rotational moment arms change with hip flexion.\r\n\r\nStudies of hip rotator moment arms show that increasing hip flexion can substantially change whether individual muscles function as internal or external rotators. :contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n> gluteus minimus should not receive one fixed rotation coefficient across every hip angle.\r\n\r\n## Flexion and Extension\r\n\r\nAnterior fibers can contribute to hip flexion.\r\n\r\nMore posterior fibers can contribute relatively more toward extension depending on joint position.\r\n\r\nThese are secondary actions.\r\n\r\nThe whole muscle's dominant aggregate identity remains:\r\n\r\n- abduction;\r\n- stabilization.\r\n\r\n## Gait\r\n\r\nGluteus minimus is active during phases of gait where the stance limb must resist pelvic drop and control femoral orientation.\r\n\r\nIts role overlaps with gluteus medius but may differ regionally and temporally.\r\n\r\nThis makes it particularly relevant to:\r\n\r\n- frontal-plane control;\r\n- transverse-plane femoral control;\r\n- hip-joint stabilization.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nDirect evidence for **stretch-mediated hypertrophy specifically in gluteus minimus** is limited.\r\n\r\nHowever, the muscle is clearly trainable.\r\n\r\nA targeted resistance-training intervention in people with hip osteoarthritis produced measurable **gluteus minimus hypertrophy**, demonstrating that this relatively deep muscle can respond directly to progressive resistance training. :contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}\r\n\r\nThe long-muscle-length question is less well established.\r\n\r\n## Hip Adduction\r\n\r\nBecause gluteus minimus lies lateral to the hip, **hip adduction** generally lengthens much of the muscle.\r\n\r\nTherefore an abduction exercise beginning from:\r\n\r\n- neutral;\r\n- mild adduction;\r\n\r\ncan plausibly load the muscle at relatively long lengths.\r\n\r\nThis is similar conceptually to gluteus medius.\r\n\r\n## Hip Flexion\r\n\r\nMuscle length and rotational leverage also change with hip flexion.\r\n\r\nBecause gluteus minimus is fan-shaped and regionally heterogeneous, the effect is not uniform across fibers.\r\n\r\nAnterior fibers may behave differently from posterior fibers as hip flexion increases.\r\n\r\nTherefore a one-dimensional:\r\n\r\n`E_LT = f(abduction_angle)`\r\n\r\nmodel is useful but incomplete.\r\n\r\n## Loaded Long-Length Abduction\r\n\r\nPotential long-length-biased configurations include:\r\n\r\n- standing cable abduction beginning across the midline;\r\n- machine abduction beginning from slight adduction;\r\n- certain side-lying abduction setups.\r\n\r\nThe important combination is:\r\n\r\n- relatively adducted starting position;\r\n- meaningful external adduction torque;\r\n- active abduction force.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Interpretation\r\n\r\nUnlike gastrocnemius, there is no strong direct longitudinal evidence showing a specific hypertrophic superiority of long-length gluteus minimus training.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n- adduction-based lengthening is mechanically plausible;\r\n- targeted resistance training clearly hypertrophies gluteus minimus;\r\n- the magnitude of any special stretch-mediated effect remains uncertain.\r\n\r\n`smh_factor` should therefore be interpreted conservatively.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Hip-Abduction Machine\r\n\r\nA hip-abduction machine can strongly load gluteus minimus together with:\r\n\r\n- gluteus medius;\r\n- tensor fasciae latae.\r\n\r\nAdvantages include:\r\n\r\n- high stability;\r\n- easy progression;\r\n- large force production;\r\n- controlled ROM;\r\n- low balance demand.\r\n\r\nIt is unlikely to isolate gluteus minimus specifically.\r\n\r\nInstead, it should be modeled as a high-demand exercise for the **hip-abductor system**.\r\n\r\n## Standing Cable Hip Abduction\r\n\r\nStanding cable abduction provides direct abduction torque.\r\n\r\nIt can be particularly useful when the working leg begins slightly across the midline.\r\n\r\nThis can create:\r\n\r\n- greater muscle length;\r\n- high frontal-plane torque;\r\n- controlled unilateral loading.\r\n\r\nHolding onto support improves hypertrophy specificity by preventing balance from becoming limiting.\r\n\r\n## Side-Lying Hip Abduction\r\n\r\nSide-lying hip abduction can recruit gluteus minimus strongly.\r\n\r\nIts main advantages are:\r\n\r\n- simple mechanics;\r\n- low systemic cost;\r\n- direct abduction.\r\n\r\nIts main limitation is progressive overload.\r\n\r\nFor advanced hypertrophy, cables or machines are usually easier to standardize.\r\n\r\n## Internal-Rotation Exercises\r\n\r\nBecause gluteus minimus contributes to internal rotation, resisted internal rotation can recruit it.\r\n\r\nHowever, this should not usually be the primary hypertrophy strategy.\r\n\r\nInternal rotation is shared with:\r\n\r\n- tensor fasciae latae;\r\n- anterior gluteus medius;\r\n- other hip muscles depending on joint angle.\r\n\r\nDirect abduction permits greater total force and is generally easier to overload.\r\n\r\n## Single-Leg Squat\r\n\r\nSingle-leg squats require substantial hip-abductor stabilization.\r\n\r\nGluteus minimus contributes to resisting:\r\n\r\n- pelvic drop;\r\n- femoral adduction;\r\n- excessive femoral rotation.\r\n\r\nThis is a useful functional stimulus but less specific than machine abduction.\r\n\r\nLocal failure may be limited by:\r\n\r\n- quadriceps;\r\n- gluteus maximus;\r\n- balance;\r\n- ankle mechanics.\r\n\r\n## Split Squat\r\n\r\nSplit squats provide meaningful gluteus minimus stabilization demand on the stance side.\r\n\r\nThe muscle acts primarily isometrically in the frontal plane while larger sagittal-plane muscles perform most of the visible work.\r\n\r\nThus a split squat should receive:\r\n\r\n- non-zero gluteus minimus demand;\r\n- but less than direct hip-abduction exercises.\r\n\r\n## Step-Up\r\n\r\nStep-ups similarly require lateral hip stabilization.\r\n\r\nDemand increases when:\r\n\r\n- the task is unilateral;\r\n- the external load is large;\r\n- the body's center of mass creates a large frontal-plane moment.\r\n\r\n## Lateral Step Tasks\r\n\r\nLateral step-downs and lateral step-ups can impose substantial frontal-plane control requirements.\r\n\r\nThey are useful for:\r\n\r\n- strength;\r\n- motor control;\r\n- rehabilitation.\r\n\r\nFor pure hypertrophy, machine or cable abduction remains easier to progressively overload.\r\n\r\n## Band Walks\r\n\r\nBand walks recruit the hip-abductor system and can include gluteus minimus activity.\r\n\r\nThey are useful for:\r\n\r\n- warm-up;\r\n- motor-control work;\r\n- endurance;\r\n- low-load accessory training.\r\n\r\nTheir limitations for advanced hypertrophy include:\r\n\r\n- poor load quantification;\r\n- low absolute tension;\r\n- complex force sharing;\r\n- difficulty standardizing proximity to failure.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Direct Abduction Is the Main Hypertrophy Route\r\n\r\nMost conventional bilateral lower-body compounds do not provide a large dynamic gluteus minimus stimulus.\r\n\r\nTherefore direct hip-abduction work has relatively high marginal value if development of the lateral hip musculature is desired.\r\n\r\n## Weekly Volume\r\n\r\nBecause gluteus minimus shares almost all major exercises with gluteus medius, it usually does not require a separate volume prescription.\r\n\r\nApproximately:\r\n\r\n- **4-8 direct hard hip-abduction sets per week**\r\n\r\ncan provide a useful starting range for the combined lateral hip-abductor system.\r\n\r\nThese sets will typically train both:\r\n\r\n- gluteus medius;\r\n- gluteus minimus.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nTwo to three weekly exposures are practical.\r\n\r\nDirect hip-abduction work has low systemic fatigue and can be distributed easily across lower-body sessions.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\nStable machine and cable abduction can be trained effectively in:\r\n\r\n- 8-15 reps;\r\n- 12-20 reps;\r\n- 15-30+ reps.\r\n\r\nHigher repetitions are often practical because the exercises are stable and technically simple.\r\n\r\nThere is no known gluteus-minimus-specific repetition requirement.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nStable abduction can generally be trained close to failure:\r\n\r\n- **0-2 RIR**.\r\n\r\nFailure should be defined by loss of meaningful femoral abduction without excessive:\r\n\r\n- pelvic hiking;\r\n- trunk lean;\r\n- rotation.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Redundancy\r\n\r\nPrograms often contain many low-load "glute activation" exercises.\r\n\r\nFor hypertrophy, several nearly identical drills usually add little.\r\n\r\nA simpler approach is:\r\n\r\n1. one progressively overloaded hip-abduction exercise;\r\n2. unilateral compounds for functional stabilization exposure.\r\n\r\nThis provides both:\r\n\r\n- local hypertrophic tension;\r\n- task-specific pelvic control.\r\n\r\n## Relationship With Gluteus Medius\r\n\r\nProgramming cannot cleanly isolate gluteus minimus from gluteus medius.\r\n\r\nMost direct abduction exercises train both.\r\n\r\nTherefore the goal should usually be:\r\n\r\n> develop the hip-abductor complex,\r\n\r\nrather than attempting to isolate the deepest muscle with small exercise variations.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## It Is Completely Hidden by Gluteus Medius\r\n\r\nGluteus minimus is not externally visible under normal anatomy because it lies deep to gluteus medius.\r\n\r\nIts contribution to hip function is therefore much larger than its visual prominence suggests.\r\n\r\n## It Is Directly Trainable\r\n\r\nDespite being deep and difficult to assess with surface EMG, targeted resistance training has produced measurable hypertrophy of gluteus minimus on imaging. :contentReference[oaicite:3]{index=3}\r\n\r\nThis is useful because many exercise recommendations for deep hip muscles rely only on activation studies.\r\n\r\nHere there is at least direct longitudinal evidence that the muscle adapts structurally.\r\n\r\n## It Is Closely Related to the Hip Capsule\r\n\r\nIts deep position and capsular attachments mean that gluteus minimus is not merely a torque generator.\r\n\r\nIt also participates in controlling structures very close to the joint itself.\r\n\r\n## Rotation Changes With Joint Position\r\n\r\nHip muscle rotation moment arms can change substantially as the hip flexes.\r\n\r\nThis means a muscle described as an internal rotator in anatomical position may become more or less effective — and in some cases change rotational behavior — at other hip angles. :contentReference[oaicite:4]{index=4}\r\n\r\nGluteus minimus is a strong example of why rotational actions should be angle-dependent in a biomechanical model.\r\n\r\n## It Is Commonly Grouped With Medius Clinically\r\n\r\nGluteus medius and minimus tendons are both implicated in **greater trochanteric pain syndrome**.\r\n\r\nThis reflects their shared attachment region and mechanical role around the lateral hip.\r\n\r\nHowever, they remain anatomically distinct muscles with different insertion geometry and regional mechanics.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\n`gluteus_minimus` should primarily receive demand from:\r\n\r\n1. **hip-abduction torque**;\r\n2. **anti-adduction / pelvic-stabilization torque**;\r\n3. internal-rotation torque, especially in appropriate hip positions;\r\n4. local glenohumeral-equivalent hip-joint stabilization — femoroacetabular stabilization.\r\n\r\nThe first two should dominate most resistance-training vectors.\r\n\r\n## Relationship With Gluteus Medius\r\n\r\nHip-abduction demand should be shared between:\r\n\r\n- `gluteus_medius`;\r\n- `gluteus_minimus`;\r\n- tensor fasciae latae;\r\n- superior gluteus maximus to a smaller degree.\r\n\r\n`gluteus_medius` should usually receive the larger absolute allocation because of its greater size and force capacity.\r\n\r\nHowever, `gluteus_minimus` should remain a consistent meaningful contributor.\r\n\r\n## Single-Leg Mechanics\r\n\r\nDuring unilateral stance:\r\n\r\n`M_external_adduction ≈ BW_component × horizontal_COM_distance`\r\n\r\nThe hip-abductor system must oppose this external moment.\r\n\r\nTherefore gluteus minimus can receive substantial demand even when:\r\n\r\n`hip_abduction_ROM ≈ 0`.\r\n\r\nThis makes anti-adduction stabilization an important explicit component of its exercise vector.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Allocation\r\n\r\n### Hip-Abduction Machine\r\n\r\nHigh `gluteus_minimus` demand.\r\n\r\nUsually even higher absolute demand to `gluteus_medius`.\r\n\r\nAlso meaningful TFL contribution.\r\n\r\n### Standing Cable Hip Abduction\r\n\r\nHigh minimus demand if the movement is true femoral abduction rather than pelvic hiking.\r\n\r\n### Single-Leg Squat\r\n\r\nModerate-to-high stabilization demand.\r\n\r\nNot as locally specific as direct abduction.\r\n\r\n### Split Squat\r\n\r\nModerate stance-side stabilization demand.\r\n\r\n### 45° Leg Press\r\n\r\nLow minimus demand because:\r\n\r\n- pelvis is supported;\r\n- movement is bilateral;\r\n- frontal-plane stabilization requirements are small.\r\n\r\n### Bilateral Squat\r\n\r\nLow-to-moderate stabilization demand.\r\n\r\nHigher than a highly supported leg press, but far lower than unilateral stance tasks.\r\n\r\n### Copenhagen Plank\r\n\r\nPotential stabilization demand depending on side and execution, but adductors remain the principal target.\r\n\r\n### Pallof Press\r\n\r\nLow-to-moderate minimus demand depending on stance and anti-rotation requirements.\r\n\r\n## Rotation Modifier\r\n\r\nInternal-rotation allocation should not be fixed.\r\n\r\nA better conceptual model is:\r\n\r\n`E_rotation = f(hip_flexion_angle, regional_fiber_geometry)`\r\n\r\nThis matters because hip flexion changes rotational moment arms across multiple hip muscles. :contentReference[oaicite:5]{index=5}\r\n\r\nFor a KISS implementation:\r\n\r\n- assign gluteus minimus a baseline internal-rotation contribution;\r\n- modulate it by hip flexion;\r\n- avoid treating it as a universal strong internal rotator at every joint configuration.\r\n\r\n## Length-Tension Model\r\n\r\nA first approximation can use:\r\n\r\n`E_LT ≈ f(hip_abduction_adduction)`\r\n\r\nwhere:\r\n\r\n- adduction → longer;\r\n- neutral → intermediate;\r\n- abduction → shorter.\r\n\r\nA more complete model would include:\r\n\r\n- hip flexion;\r\n- rotation.\r\n\r\nBecause of its fan-shaped architecture, regional fibers will not share identical length changes.\r\n\r\n## Direct Hypertrophy Evidence\r\n\r\nThe targeted resistance trial demonstrating gluteus minimus hypertrophy is important but should be interpreted carefully.\r\n\r\nIt establishes:\r\n\r\n- the muscle is trainable;\r\n- targeted exercise can increase its size.\r\n\r\nIt does not establish:\r\n\r\n- an optimal exercise;\r\n- an optimal repetition range;\r\n- a unique long-length advantage;\r\n- precise isolation from medius.\r\n\r\nTherefore program design should continue to rely on anatomy and general hypertrophy principles rather than overfitting to one intervention. :contentReference[oaicite:6]{index=6}\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nEvidence for a special stretch-mediated hypertrophy effect remains limited.\r\n\r\nThe defensible interpretation is:\r\n\r\n- hip adduction generally lengthens gluteus minimus;\r\n- loaded abduction from mild adduction is mechanically attractive;\r\n- the muscle can hypertrophy from targeted resistance training;\r\n- no strong evidence currently quantifies a unique long-length benefit.\r\n\r\nTherefore `smh_factor` should be conservative unless the field represents a model-level estimate rather than direct literature evidence.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **iliac origin and greater-trochanter insertion:** high;\r\n- **superior-gluteal-nerve innervation:** high;\r\n- **hip-abduction role:** high;\r\n- **pelvic-stabilization role:** high;\r\n- **internal-rotation contribution:** high but angle-dependent;\r\n- **regional functional heterogeneity:** well supported anatomically;\r\n- **targeted resistance-training hypertrophy:** directly supported; :contentReference[oaicite:7]{index=7}\r\n- **precise exercise-specific recruitment:** harder to establish because the muscle is deep;\r\n- **stretch-mediated hypertrophy magnitude:** limited direct evidence;\r\n- **exact rotation coefficients across hip angles:** geometry-dependent.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **gluteus minimus = a deep, regionally heterogeneous hip abductor and stabilizer with an important internal-rotation bias, whose major resistance-training demand comes from direct abduction and anti-adduction during unilateral support rather than from sagittal-plane hip extension.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/GluteusMinimus}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	10.30	0.00	1.000	24.82	24.82
46	soleus	Musculus soleus	Lower	Calves	1150.00	1089.02	150.00	Multipennate	0.775	0.225	medium	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\nThe **soleus** is a large, broad, predominantly slow-twitch plantarflexor located deep to the gastrocnemius.\r\n\r\nTogether with the medial and lateral heads of gastrocnemius, it forms the major force-producing component of the **triceps surae**.\r\n\r\nIts defining anatomical distinction from gastrocnemius is simple:\r\n\r\n- `gastrocnemius` crosses the knee and ankle;\r\n- `soleus` crosses only the ankle.\r\n\r\nConsequently, soleus is a **monoarticular plantarflexor**.\r\n\r\nIts musculotendinous length is determined primarily by ankle position rather than knee angle.\r\n\r\nThis has major implications for exercise selection.\r\n\r\nKnee flexion substantially shortens and mechanically disadvantages gastrocnemius, but it does not directly shorten soleus.\r\n\r\nTherefore bent-knee plantarflexion changes the relative distribution of plantarflexion demand toward soleus.\r\n\r\nThe soleus is also exceptionally important outside resistance training.\r\n\r\nIt contributes continuously to:\r\n\r\n- quiet standing;\r\n- postural control;\r\n- walking;\r\n- running;\r\n- propulsion;\r\n- control of forward tibial progression.\r\n\r\nIts high proportion of type I fibers and extensive pennate architecture are consistent with a muscle specialized for producing substantial force repeatedly and economically over long periods.\r\n\r\nFor hypertrophy programming, however, its endurance specialization should not be misinterpreted as meaning that it requires exclusively high-repetition training.\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\n## Origin\r\n\r\nThe soleus has a broad proximal origin from the posterior proximal tibia and fibula.\r\n\r\nMajor attachment regions include:\r\n\r\n- the posterior surface of the fibular head and proximal fibula;\r\n- the **soleal line of the tibia**;\r\n- adjacent medial border of the tibia;\r\n- a fibrous arch spanning between tibial and fibular attachment regions.\r\n\r\nThis broad origin creates a large muscle mass occupying much of the posterior leg beneath gastrocnemius.\r\n\r\n## Insertion\r\n\r\nSoleus fibers converge into substantial internal and external aponeurotic structures that contribute to the **calcaneal tendon / Achilles tendon complex**.\r\n\r\nThe Achilles tendon ultimately inserts onto the posterior calcaneus.\r\n\r\nAlthough gastrocnemius and soleus share this distal force-transmission system, their force contributions and fascicle behavior are not identical.\r\n\r\nThe Achilles tendon itself has complex internal organization, with subtendons arising from different triceps-surae components rather than behaving as a perfectly homogeneous cable.\r\n\r\n## Architecture\r\n\r\nSoleus is a highly pennate and three-dimensionally complex muscle.\r\n\r\nIt cannot be accurately represented as one flat bundle of fibers running from tibia to Achilles tendon.\r\n\r\nThree-dimensional reconstruction has demonstrated substantial regional variation in:\r\n\r\n- fascicle length;\r\n- pennation angle;\r\n- fascicle curvature;\r\n- physiological cross-sectional area;\r\n- internal aponeurotic geometry.\r\n\r\nThis architecture allows a very large physiological cross-sectional area to be packed into a relatively compact anatomical region.\r\n\r\nThe tradeoff is relatively short fascicle excursion compared with long parallel-fibered muscles.\r\n\r\nThis is consistent with soleus functioning primarily as a high-force plantarflexor rather than a muscle specialized for enormous shortening velocity.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nSoleus is innervated by the **tibial nerve**, predominantly from spinal roots **S1-S2**.\r\n\r\nThe tibial nerve supplies the posterior compartment of the leg, including:\r\n\r\n- soleus;\r\n- gastrocnemius;\r\n- plantaris;\r\n- deeper posterior-compartment muscles.\r\n\r\nMotor branches enter the soleus proximally and distribute throughout its broad muscle architecture.\r\n\r\nFor exercise-level modelling, regional neural subdivisions are much less important than the distinction between soleus and the biarticular gastrocnemius.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Ankle Plantarflexion\r\n\r\nThe primary function of soleus is **plantarflexion at the ankle**.\r\n\r\nThrough the Achilles tendon, soleus pulls on the calcaneus and generates plantarflexion torque.\r\n\r\nIt acts synergistically with gastrocnemius during:\r\n\r\n- calf raises;\r\n- standing;\r\n- walking;\r\n- running;\r\n- jumping.\r\n\r\nBecause soleus does not cross the knee, its plantarflexion function remains mechanically available across a wide range of knee positions.\r\n\r\nThis makes it particularly important when the knee is flexed.\r\n\r\n## Bent-Knee Plantarflexion\r\n\r\nWhen the knee bends substantially, gastrocnemius shortens proximally.\r\n\r\nIts capacity to contribute to plantarflexion is therefore reduced.\r\n\r\nSoleus does not experience this proximal shortening.\r\n\r\nAs a result, relative plantarflexion force sharing shifts toward soleus.\r\n\r\nThis is the anatomical basis for using:\r\n\r\n- seated calf raises;\r\n- bent-knee calf raises\r\n\r\nto bias soleus relative to gastrocnemius.\r\n\r\nThe word **bias** is important.\r\n\r\nGastrocnemius does not become completely inactive, and soleus is also highly active in straight-leg plantarflexion.\r\n\r\n## Straight-Leg Plantarflexion\r\n\r\nSoleus remains a major plantarflexor when the knee is extended.\r\n\r\nStanding calf raises are therefore not exclusively gastrocnemius exercises.\r\n\r\nThey train:\r\n\r\n- gastrocnemius strongly;\r\n- soleus strongly.\r\n\r\nThe difference is that straight-knee positioning allows gastrocnemius to participate under much more favorable length conditions.\r\n\r\nThus the correct model is:\r\n\r\n> standing calf raise = gastrocnemius + soleus\r\n\r\nrather than:\r\n\r\n> standing calf raise = gastrocnemius only.\r\n\r\n## Knee Independence\r\n\r\nBecause soleus originates below the knee, changing knee angle does not directly alter the distance between its origin and insertion.\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`L_soleus ≈ f(ankle_angle)`\r\n\r\nwhereas:\r\n\r\n`L_gastrocnemius = f(ankle_angle, knee_angle)`\r\n\r\nThis is one of the cleanest monoarticular-versus-biarticular distinctions in the lower limb.\r\n\r\n## Control of Tibial Progression\r\n\r\nDuring closed-chain stance, soleus can act on the tibia rather than simply moving the foot.\r\n\r\nAs the tibia advances anteriorly over a planted foot, ankle dorsiflexion increases.\r\n\r\nSoleus can contract eccentrically to resist and control this forward tibial progression.\r\n\r\nTherefore its functional role can be described either as:\r\n\r\n- plantarflexing the foot relative to the tibia;\r\nor\r\n- resisting forward rotation of the tibia over the foot.\r\n\r\nBoth describe the same joint torque from different reference frames.\r\n\r\nThis is particularly important during:\r\n\r\n- walking;\r\n- running;\r\n- squatting;\r\n- landing.\r\n\r\n## Postural Function\r\n\r\nSoleus is active during quiet standing because the body's center of mass tends to create a forward rotational moment at the ankle.\r\n\r\nThe plantarflexors generate an opposing moment.\r\n\r\nSoleus is especially suited to this task because of:\r\n\r\n- large force capacity;\r\n- high type I fiber representation;\r\n- low fatigue rate.\r\n\r\nThis continuous postural function is one reason soleus is often described as a classic **antigravity muscle**.\r\n\r\n## Locomotion\r\n\r\nDuring gait, soleus contributes substantially to:\r\n\r\n- support of body weight;\r\n- forward propulsion;\r\n- ankle power generation;\r\n- control of tibial movement.\r\n\r\nDuring running, force requirements become much larger.\r\n\r\nHowever, the Achilles tendon allows elastic storage and return, so muscle fascicle behavior is not identical to ankle-joint motion.\r\n\r\nSubstantial ankle displacement can occur while soleus fascicles operate over a much smaller excursion.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nSoleus has a strong anatomical rationale for training at long muscle lengths, but the evidence must be interpreted differently from gastrocnemius.\r\n\r\nBecause soleus crosses only the ankle:\r\n\r\n> **ankle dorsiflexion is the primary joint movement that lengthens soleus.**\r\n\r\nKnee extension does not provide an additional proximal stretch.\r\n\r\n## Dorsiflexion\r\n\r\nDuring a calf raise, soleus is relatively lengthened when the ankle moves into dorsiflexion.\r\n\r\nIt becomes progressively shorter during plantarflexion.\r\n\r\nTherefore exercises that maintain high plantarflexion torque in dorsiflexion provide a mechanically plausible long-length stimulus.\r\n\r\nThis applies to both:\r\n\r\n- standing calf raises;\r\n- seated calf raises.\r\n\r\n## Standing vs Seated Training\r\n\r\nDirect longitudinal evidence comparing standing and seated calf raises is particularly informative.\r\n\r\nStanding training produced dramatically greater gastrocnemius hypertrophy because knee extension places gastrocnemius at longer muscle lengths.\r\n\r\nFor **soleus**, however, hypertrophy was similar between standing and seated conditions.\r\n\r\nThis is exactly what monoarticular anatomy predicts:\r\n\r\n- knee position strongly alters gastrocnemius length;\r\n- knee position does not directly alter soleus length.\r\n\r\nTherefore seated training does not possess a special soleus hypertrophy advantage merely because the knee is flexed.\r\n\r\nInstead, knee flexion improves **relative soleus specificity** by reducing gastrocnemius contribution.\r\n\r\nThat is a different statement.\r\n\r\n## Important Distinction\r\n\r\nThese concepts should not be conflated:\r\n\r\n### Relative Recruitment\r\n\r\nBent knee:\r\n- greater relative soleus contribution.\r\n\r\n### Soleus Muscle Length\r\n\r\nDetermined primarily by:\r\n- ankle dorsiflexion/plantarflexion.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n> `knee flexion → soleus bias`\r\n\r\ndoes **not** mean:\r\n\r\n> `knee flexion → longer soleus`.\r\n\r\nFor Agonez, those should be separate modifiers.\r\n\r\n## Long-Length Calf Training\r\n\r\nThe strongest general long-length principle is therefore:\r\n\r\n- train through substantial dorsiflexion;\r\n- maintain active plantarflexion torque near the bottom;\r\n- avoid making the lengthened portion mechanically trivial.\r\n\r\nThis can be achieved using:\r\n\r\n- seated calf raise;\r\n- standing calf raise;\r\n- leg-press calf raise;\r\n- suitable machine variants.\r\n\r\nThe best option depends on the desired gastrocnemius/soleus force distribution.\r\n\r\n## SMH Evidence Interpretation\r\n\r\nDirect soleus-specific evidence for stretch-mediated hypertrophy is less extensive than the gastrocnemius evidence.\r\n\r\nThe available standing-versus-seated study is nevertheless valuable because soleus hypertrophy was similar despite very different knee positions.\r\n\r\nThis strongly supports the idea that the special standing-training advantage is gastrocnemius-specific rather than a generic feature of the entire triceps surae.\r\n\r\nFor soleus, ankle ROM and resistance in dorsiflexion are more relevant variables than knee angle for `E_LT`.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Seated Calf Raise\r\n\r\nThe seated calf raise is the canonical soleus-biased resistance exercise.\r\n\r\nIts defining feature is substantial knee flexion, commonly around 90 degrees.\r\n\r\nThis mechanically disadvantages gastrocnemius while leaving soleus capable of generating plantarflexion torque.\r\n\r\nAdvantages include:\r\n\r\n- high soleus specificity;\r\n- excellent external stability;\r\n- simple progressive overload;\r\n- easy training near muscular failure;\r\n- low balance demand;\r\n- easy access to large ankle ROM.\r\n\r\nIt should be thought of as:\r\n\r\n> a plantarflexion exercise in which gastrocnemius participation is intentionally suppressed,\r\n\r\nnot:\r\n\r\n> an exercise that uniquely activates soleus.\r\n\r\n## Standing Calf Raise\r\n\r\nStanding calf raises also train soleus strongly.\r\n\r\nThey simply allow gastrocnemius to participate much more effectively.\r\n\r\nTherefore standing calf raises can still generate soleus hypertrophy.\r\n\r\nThis is directly supported by longitudinal evidence showing similar soleus growth between standing and seated calf-raise training.\r\n\r\nIf total calf development is the goal, standing raises are unusually efficient because they train:\r\n\r\n- gastrocnemius;\r\n- soleus\r\n\r\nsimultaneously.\r\n\r\nIf selective soleus work is desired, seated training offers greater specificity.\r\n\r\n## Plate-Loaded Seated Calf Raise\r\n\r\nA well-designed plate-loaded seated calf raise is particularly useful because it provides:\r\n\r\n- high stability;\r\n- direct loading over the distal thigh;\r\n- easy load progression;\r\n- minimal upper-body fatigue.\r\n\r\nImportant setup points include:\r\n\r\n- knee pads positioned securely;\r\n- forefoot supported;\r\n- heel free to descend;\r\n- controlled dorsiflexion;\r\n- minimal bouncing.\r\n\r\n## Range of Motion\r\n\r\nFor hypertrophy, special attention should be given to the **dorsiflexed region**.\r\n\r\nA useful repetition involves:\r\n\r\n1. controlled descent;\r\n2. substantial but comfortable dorsiflexion;\r\n3. active transition into plantarflexion;\r\n4. strong ascent;\r\n5. controlled return.\r\n\r\nThere is little reason to deliberately shorten the eccentric ROM simply to use more external weight.\r\n\r\n## Bottom Position\r\n\r\nThe bottom should be:\r\n\r\n- controlled;\r\n- loaded;\r\n- actively reversed.\r\n\r\nA passive hang with no muscular control is not the same as active long-length loading.\r\n\r\nLikewise, bouncing rapidly from the bottom can use Achilles-tendon elastic recoil and reduce the interpretability of muscular work.\r\n\r\nA brief controlled transition is usually useful.\r\n\r\n## Top Position\r\n\r\nAchieving substantial plantarflexion remains part of a broad-ROM repetition.\r\n\r\nHowever, a prolonged exaggerated squeeze at peak plantarflexion is not known to possess a unique hypertrophic benefit.\r\n\r\nIf a choice must be made between:\r\n\r\n- preserving loaded dorsiflexion;\r\n- exaggerating the peak contraction,\r\n\r\nthe lengthened region generally deserves priority.\r\n\r\n## Knee Angle\r\n\r\nIncreasing knee flexion changes the gastrocnemius contribution more than soleus length.\r\n\r\nTherefore the practical purpose of the bent knee is to alter **force sharing**.\r\n\r\nA stable 70-100 degree knee-flexion configuration is generally sufficient.\r\n\r\nThere is little reason to pursue maximal knee flexion solely to "stretch" soleus.\r\n\r\n## Ankle Position\r\n\r\nAnkle angle is the main direct soleus length variable.\r\n\r\nAny Agonez exercise comparison should therefore examine:\r\n\r\n- maximum dorsiflexion;\r\n- minimum plantarflexion;\r\n- torque across this ROM.\r\n\r\nA seated calf raise with poor dorsiflexion can provide less useful lengthened loading than a standing variation with excellent ROM.\r\n\r\nExercise labels alone are insufficient.\r\n\r\n## Leg-Press Calf Raise\r\n\r\nA leg-press calf raise with straight knees provides high soleus demand alongside high gastrocnemius demand.\r\n\r\nIf the knees are intentionally bent and maintained in flexion, gastrocnemius contribution can be reduced.\r\n\r\nHowever, machine geometry must permit the ankle to move independently rather than turning the exercise into a combined leg press.\r\n\r\n## Unilateral Calf Raise\r\n\r\nSoleus participates strongly in unilateral standing calf raises.\r\n\r\nThe exercise is not soleus-specific because gastrocnemius remains favorably positioned.\r\n\r\nIts value is primarily:\r\n\r\n- unilateral loading;\r\n- minimal equipment;\r\n- easy side-to-side comparison.\r\n\r\nExternal support is useful to prevent balance from limiting force production.\r\n\r\n## Running and Walking\r\n\r\nThe soleus already performs enormous amounts of low-to-moderate-intensity work through daily locomotion.\r\n\r\nThis does not make resistance training unnecessary.\r\n\r\nWalking provides:\r\n\r\n- high repetition count;\r\n- relatively low force per contraction.\r\n\r\nHypertrophy-oriented calf training provides:\r\n\r\n- much higher mechanical tension;\r\n- proximity to local fatigue;\r\n- progressive overload.\r\n\r\nThe two stimuli should not be treated as equivalent.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Seated Work Is Useful, Not Mandatory\r\n\r\nIf the program already contains high-quality standing calf raises, soleus receives meaningful hypertrophic exposure.\r\n\r\nSeated calf work becomes especially valuable when the goal is to:\r\n\r\n- prioritize soleus;\r\n- add plantarflexion volume without proportionally increasing gastrocnemius volume;\r\n- create a mechanically distinct calf stimulus.\r\n\r\nThus seated calf training is highly useful but not an absolute requirement for soleus growth.\r\n\r\n## Combined Calf Programming\r\n\r\nA particularly clean arrangement is:\r\n\r\n### Standing Calf Raise\r\n\r\nHigh:\r\n- gastrocnemius;\r\n- soleus.\r\n\r\n### Seated Calf Raise\r\n\r\nVery high relative:\r\n- soleus.\r\n\r\nReduced:\r\n- gastrocnemius.\r\n\r\nThis combination covers the triceps surae efficiently.\r\n\r\n## Weekly Volume\r\n\r\nA practical starting range for direct soleus-oriented training can be approximately:\r\n\r\n- **4-8 hard plantarflexion sets per week**,\r\n\r\nincluding both straight- and bent-knee work.\r\n\r\nIf soleus hypertrophy is a high priority, additional seated work can be added progressively.\r\n\r\nExisting running volume and total calf workload should be considered.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nTwo to four weekly exposures are practical.\r\n\r\nCalf training has low systemic fatigue, but local recovery still matters.\r\n\r\nPotential limiting tissues include:\r\n\r\n- soleus;\r\n- Achilles tendon;\r\n- foot musculature;\r\n- plantar structures.\r\n\r\nThe fact that soleus is highly fatigue-resistant does not eliminate the need for recovery from high-tension resistance exercise.\r\n\r\n## Fiber-Type Considerations\r\n\r\nHuman soleus is strongly biased toward **type I / slow-twitch fibers**.\r\n\r\nThis explains much of its exceptional postural endurance.\r\n\r\nHowever, fiber-type composition does not imply that hypertrophy requires only very high repetitions.\r\n\r\nType I fibers are still capable of hypertrophy under sufficiently high mechanical tension.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n> "soleus is slow-twitch, so use 30-50 reps only"\r\n\r\nis an unjustified programming rule.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\nA broad range can be productive:\r\n\r\n- 6-10 reps for heavy stable machine work;\r\n- 8-15 reps for general hypertrophy;\r\n- 15-30+ reps for lighter work.\r\n\r\nBecause soleus is fatigue-resistant, high-repetition sets can be practical.\r\n\r\nBut low-to-moderate repetitions with appropriate loading are also valid.\r\n\r\nThe determining factors remain:\r\n\r\n- effort;\r\n- mechanical tension;\r\n- ROM;\r\n- progression.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nStable seated calf raises can be trained close to failure.\r\n\r\nApproximately:\r\n\r\n- **0-2 RIR**\r\n\r\nis practical for hypertrophy work.\r\n\r\nBecause soleus is fatigue-resistant, subjective discomfort can become intense before true mechanical failure.\r\n\r\nThe set should not automatically stop merely because the muscle begins burning.\r\n\r\nConversely, technique should not deteriorate into:\r\n\r\n- bouncing;\r\n- shortened ROM;\r\n- shifting pressure away from the forefoot.\r\n\r\n## Rest Intervals\r\n\r\nSoleus endurance does not imply that very short rest intervals are optimal.\r\n\r\nIf the goal is hypertrophy, sufficient recovery should allow high force production across repeated sets.\r\n\r\nApproximately:\r\n\r\n- 1.5-3 minutes\r\n\r\ncan be useful depending on load and repetition range.\r\n\r\nVery short rests may reduce external performance without providing a unique soleus-specific advantage.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Order\r\n\r\nSoleus isolation usually fits late in a lower-body session because:\r\n\r\n- systemic fatigue is low;\r\n- the exercise requires little coordination;\r\n- it rarely compromises earlier large compound movements when performed afterward.\r\n\r\nIf calf development is a priority, it can be moved earlier.\r\n\r\n## Athletic Interference\r\n\r\nSoleus and Achilles loading from:\r\n\r\n- running;\r\n- sprinting;\r\n- jumping;\r\n- hill work\r\n\r\nshould be considered alongside resistance training.\r\n\r\nThe muscle may recover quickly metabolically, while the Achilles tendon can respond to cumulative loading on a different timescale.\r\n\r\nTotal plantarflexor loading therefore matters beyond bodybuilding set counts.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## Soleus Is One of the Most Slow-Twitch-Dominant Human Muscles\r\n\r\nClassical human histochemical studies report approximately **70% slow-twitch fibers** in soleus on average, substantially more than gastrocnemius.\r\n\r\nIndividual values vary considerably.\r\n\r\nIts phenotype is consistent with its role in:\r\n\r\n- standing;\r\n- posture;\r\n- walking;\r\n- repeated low-intensity force production.\r\n\r\n## It Is a Major Antigravity Muscle\r\n\r\nSoleus produces low-level plantarflexion torque almost continuously during quiet standing.\r\n\r\nWithout plantarflexor control, the body's center of mass would tend to rotate the tibia forward over the ankle.\r\n\r\nThe soleus therefore functions as part of a continuous postural control system rather than only becoming relevant during deliberate calf raises.\r\n\r\n## It Is Larger and More Architecturally Complex Than It Looks\r\n\r\nMuch of soleus lies hidden beneath gastrocnemius.\r\n\r\nThree-dimensional studies show complex fascicle and aponeurotic architecture with substantial regional variation.\r\n\r\nIts visual obscurity therefore underestimates its mechanical importance.\r\n\r\n## Sitting Does Not Lengthen Soleus\r\n\r\nThis is an important misconception.\r\n\r\nKnee flexion makes seated calf raises more **soleus-specific**, but it does not directly increase soleus length.\r\n\r\nThe origin and insertion of soleus both remain distal to the knee.\r\n\r\nIts length is governed primarily by ankle angle.\r\n\r\n## Standing Calf Raises Grow Soleus Too\r\n\r\nA direct longitudinal comparison of standing and seated calf raises found similar soleus hypertrophy between conditions.\r\n\r\nThe dramatic difference between exercises occurred in gastrocnemius, not soleus.\r\n\r\nThis provides an unusually elegant experimental demonstration of monoarticular versus biarticular anatomy.\r\n\r\n## Fiber Type Is Not a Rep-Range Prescription\r\n\r\nThe high type I content of soleus is frequently converted into the advice that soleus must be trained only with high repetitions.\r\n\r\nThat conclusion does not follow.\r\n\r\nFiber phenotype affects:\r\n\r\n- fatigue resistance;\r\n- contractile velocity;\r\n- metabolic characteristics.\r\n\r\nIt does not create a hard rep-range requirement for hypertrophy.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\n`soleus` should be modeled primarily as a **monoarticular ankle plantarflexor**.\r\n\r\nIts major contractile demand dimension is:\r\n\r\n1. ankle plantarflexion torque.\r\n\r\nIt should receive essentially no direct knee-flexion torque allocation.\r\n\r\nThis sharply contrasts with gastrocnemius.\r\n\r\n## Length-Tension Model\r\n\r\nThe appropriate simplified relationship is:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_soleus ≈ f(ankle_angle)`\r\n\r\nSpecifically:\r\n\r\n### Dorsiflexion\r\n\r\nRelatively long soleus.\r\n\r\n### Neutral\r\n\r\nIntermediate.\r\n\r\n### Plantarflexion\r\n\r\nShortened.\r\n\r\nKnee angle should not directly modify soleus anatomical length.\r\n\r\n## Force-Sharing Modifier\r\n\r\nKnee angle should nevertheless influence **relative plantarflexor recruitment**, because it changes gastrocnemius mechanics.\r\n\r\nThus Agonez should separate:\r\n\r\n### Soleus Length\r\n\r\n`f(ankle_angle)`\r\n\r\nfrom:\r\n\r\n### Soleus Relative Force Share\r\n\r\n`f(ankle_angle, knee_angle, gastrocnemius_state)`\r\n\r\nThis distinction is important.\r\n\r\nKnee flexion does not make soleus longer; it makes its major synergist less mechanically favorable.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Allocation\r\n\r\n### Plate-Loaded Seated Calf Raise\r\n\r\nVery high `soleus` allocation.\r\n\r\nReduced `gastrocnemius` allocation.\r\n\r\nThis is the most specific direct soleus exercise in the current Agonez catalogue.\r\n\r\n### Smith Machine Standing Calf Raise\r\n\r\nHigh `soleus` allocation.\r\n\r\nAlso very high `gastrocnemius` allocation.\r\n\r\nDo not reduce soleus merely because the knees are extended.\r\n\r\n### Leg-Press Calf Raise\r\n\r\nHigh soleus demand if ankle plantarflexion torque is large.\r\n\r\nGastrocnemius contribution depends strongly on knee angle.\r\n\r\n### Running\r\n\r\nVery high functional soleus demand, particularly for support and propulsion.\r\n\r\nHowever, its ETU characteristics differ substantially from resistance training because:\r\n\r\n- contraction velocity;\r\n- tendon contribution;\r\n- repetition count;\r\n- force-time characteristics\r\n\r\nare different.\r\n\r\n### Seated Leg Curl\r\n\r\nApproximately zero direct soleus demand.\r\n\r\nUnlike gastrocnemius, soleus does not cross the knee.\r\n\r\n## Straight-vs-Bent Knee Logic\r\n\r\nThe correct Agonez logic is:\r\n\r\n### Gastrocnemius\r\n\r\nKnee angle changes:\r\n- muscle length;\r\n- force-generating capacity;\r\n- plantarflexion contribution.\r\n\r\n### Soleus\r\n\r\nKnee angle changes:\r\n- relative force sharing indirectly.\r\n\r\nKnee angle does **not** directly change:\r\n- soleus length.\r\n\r\nThis is one of the cleanest cases where `E_LT` and `E_ND` / force-sharing modifiers should remain conceptually separate.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nFor soleus, a high lengthened-loading score should arise primarily from **dorsiflexion under load**.\r\n\r\nIt should not arise from bent-knee positioning itself.\r\n\r\nDirect evidence indicates similar soleus hypertrophy after seated and standing calf raises despite their very different knee positions.\r\n\r\nTherefore the appropriate interpretation is:\r\n\r\n- long ankle position matters mechanistically;\r\n- knee position determines specificity;\r\n- the magnitude of soleus-specific stretch-mediated hypertrophy remains less directly characterized than for gastrocnemius.\r\n\r\n## Architecture and PCSA\r\n\r\nSoleus possesses highly pennate, spatially heterogeneous architecture.\r\n\r\nThree-dimensional anatomical work demonstrates substantial regional differences in fascicle lengths, pennation angles, PCSA and fascicle curvature.\r\n\r\nTherefore a single database:\r\n\r\n- `optimal_fiber_length_cm`;\r\n- `pennation_angle_deg`;\r\n- `pcsa_fiber_cm2`\r\n\r\nshould be understood as a **whole-muscle effective approximation**, not a literal description of every soleus compartment.\r\n\r\nThis is especially important for soleus because its three-dimensional architecture is unusually complex.\r\n\r\n## Fiber Bias\r\n\r\nA strong `fiber_bias_type_i` for soleus is physiologically well justified.\r\n\r\nHuman histological data consistently identify soleus as one of the most type-I-rich major skeletal muscles.\r\n\r\nHowever, the exact percentage depends on:\r\n\r\n- individual;\r\n- sampling location;\r\n- age;\r\n- activity background;\r\n- histochemical classification method.\r\n\r\nTherefore the database value should be interpreted as a population-level modeling parameter rather than a deterministic individual phenotype.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **tibial/fibular origin and calcaneal insertion:** high;\r\n- **tibial-nerve innervation:** high;\r\n- **plantarflexion function:** high;\r\n- **monoarticular anatomy:** high;\r\n- **lack of direct knee-angle effect on soleus length:** high;\r\n- **increased relative importance during bent-knee plantarflexion:** high;\r\n- **similar soleus hypertrophy from seated and standing calf raises:** direct longitudinal evidence;\r\n- **strong type I fiber bias:** directly supported by human histology;\r\n- **complex three-dimensional pennate architecture:** directly demonstrated;\r\n- **specific soleus long-length hypertrophy magnitude:** less directly established than gastrocnemius;\r\n- **exact exercise-specific force-sharing coefficients:** dependent on ankle angle, knee angle, load and individual anatomy.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **soleus = a large, highly pennate, type-I-biased monoarticular plantarflexor whose anatomical length depends primarily on ankle angle, while knee flexion increases its relative contribution indirectly by mechanically disadvantaging the biarticular gastrocnemius.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/Soleus}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	4.40	28.30	0.880	247.50	217.92
47	tibialis_anterior	Musculus tibialis anterior	Lower	Shin	305.00	288.83	25.00	Circumpennate/Fusiform	0.600	0.400	extreme_high	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\nThe **tibialis anterior** is the dominant superficial muscle of the anterior compartment of the lower leg.\r\n\r\nIts principal functions are:\r\n\r\n- **ankle dorsiflexion**;\r\n- **foot inversion**;\r\n- control of plantarflexion during gait;\r\n- stabilization of the medial foot and ankle during stance.\r\n\r\nIt originates from the lateral tibia and inserts medially onto the foot, creating a line of pull that passes:\r\n\r\n- anterior to the talocrural axis;\r\n- medial to the subtalar / foot inversion axis.\r\n\r\nThis geometry explains its combined action:\r\n\r\n> **dorsiflexion + inversion.**\r\n\r\nTibialis anterior is especially important during locomotion.\r\n\r\nDuring swing phase it actively dorsiflexes the ankle to provide toe clearance.\r\n\r\nImmediately after heel strike it contracts eccentrically to control the rate at which the forefoot lowers toward the ground.\r\n\r\nIn resistance training, it is commonly undertrained compared with the plantarflexors:\r\n\r\n- soleus;\r\n- gastrocnemius.\r\n\r\nFor Agonez, the most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **tibialis anterior = the major dorsiflexor and a meaningful inverter of the foot, capable of both dynamic shortening during dorsiflexion and high eccentric/isometric force while controlling plantarflexion.**\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\n## Origin\r\n\r\nTibialis anterior originates broadly from the proximal and lateral tibial region, including:\r\n\r\n- lateral condyle of the tibia;\r\n- proximal lateral tibial surface;\r\n- adjacent interosseous membrane;\r\n- surrounding deep fascia.\r\n\r\nIts muscle belly occupies much of the proximal anterior lower leg.\r\n\r\n## Insertion\r\n\r\nThe muscle transitions into a long distal tendon that passes across the anterior ankle beneath the extensor retinacula.\r\n\r\nIt inserts primarily onto:\r\n\r\n- the medial cuneiform;\r\n- the base of the first metatarsal.\r\n\r\nThis medial insertion is important.\r\n\r\nBecause the tendon crosses the ankle anteriorly, contraction produces dorsiflexion.\r\n\r\nBecause it inserts on the medial side of the foot, it also generates an inversion/supination component.\r\n\r\n## Relationship With Other Anterior-Compartment Muscles\r\n\r\nThe anterior compartment also contains muscles such as:\r\n\r\n- extensor hallucis longus;\r\n- extensor digitorum longus;\r\n- fibularis tertius.\r\n\r\nThese muscles can assist ankle dorsiflexion.\r\n\r\nHowever, their additional roles include:\r\n\r\n- toe extension;\r\n- eversion in the case of fibularis tertius.\r\n\r\nTibialis anterior is therefore the most important dedicated large dorsiflexor.\r\n\r\n## Relationship With Tibialis Posterior\r\n\r\nDespite the similar name, tibialis anterior and tibialis posterior differ significantly.\r\n\r\nBoth can contribute to:\r\n\r\n- foot inversion.\r\n\r\nBut:\r\n\r\n### Tibialis Anterior\r\n\r\nProduces:\r\n- dorsiflexion;\r\n- inversion.\r\n\r\n### Tibialis Posterior\r\n\r\nProduces:\r\n- plantarflexion;\r\n- inversion.\r\n\r\nThus they form an interesting pair:\r\n\r\n- same frontal-plane bias;\r\n- opposite sagittal-plane ankle actions.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nTibialis anterior is innervated by the **deep fibular nerve**, primarily from spinal roots approximately:\r\n\r\n- L4;\r\n- L5.\r\n\r\nThe deep fibular nerve supplies the anterior compartment of the leg.\r\n\r\nLoss of its function can substantially impair dorsiflexion and contribute to a foot-drop pattern.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Ankle Dorsiflexion\r\n\r\nThe primary action of tibialis anterior is **dorsiflexion at the talocrural joint**.\r\n\r\nThis moves the dorsum of the foot toward the anterior lower leg.\r\n\r\nIt works synergistically with:\r\n\r\n- extensor hallucis longus;\r\n- extensor digitorum longus;\r\n- fibularis tertius.\r\n\r\nHowever, tibialis anterior generally provides the largest dedicated contribution.\r\n\r\n## Foot Inversion\r\n\r\nTibialis anterior also contributes to **inversion** of the foot.\r\n\r\nIts tendon passes medially and inserts on the medial midfoot/first metatarsal region.\r\n\r\nThis produces an inversion moment.\r\n\r\nThe action is shared with:\r\n\r\n- tibialis posterior.\r\n\r\nThe two muscles can co-contract to create inversion while partially canceling their opposing plantarflexion/dorsiflexion moments.\r\n\r\n## Gait: Swing Phase\r\n\r\nDuring swing, tibialis anterior dorsiflexes the ankle to maintain **toe clearance**.\r\n\r\nWithout sufficient dorsiflexion, the toes may contact the ground prematurely.\r\n\r\nTherefore TA is important for efficient limb advancement.\r\n\r\n## Gait: Heel Strike\r\n\r\nImmediately after heel contact, gravity and ground reaction forces tend to plantarflex the ankle.\r\n\r\nTibialis anterior contracts **eccentrically** to control the lowering of the forefoot.\r\n\r\nThis produces the smooth transition from:\r\n\r\n- heel strike;\r\nto\r\n- foot-flat.\r\n\r\nThe muscle can therefore experience substantial force while lengthening.\r\n\r\n## Ankle Stabilization\r\n\r\nTibialis anterior also contributes to controlling ankle and foot position under load.\r\n\r\nDepending on external force direction, it can resist:\r\n\r\n- plantarflexion;\r\n- excessive eversion;\r\n- unwanted foot motion.\r\n\r\nThus it can generate meaningful isometric force without visible dorsiflexion.\r\n\r\n## Medial Foot Control\r\n\r\nThrough its insertion onto the medial cuneiform and first metatarsal, tibialis anterior can influence medial-foot mechanics.\r\n\r\nIts exact role in arch behavior depends on:\r\n\r\n- foot loading;\r\n- subtalar position;\r\n- co-contraction with other intrinsic and extrinsic foot muscles.\r\n\r\nIt should not be treated as the sole controller of the medial longitudinal arch.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nDirect longitudinal evidence specifically examining **stretch-mediated hypertrophy of tibialis anterior** is limited compared with gastrocnemius and some other muscles.\r\n\r\nMechanically, however, its length state is straightforward.\r\n\r\n## Plantarflexion Lengthens Tibialis Anterior\r\n\r\nBecause tibialis anterior dorsiflexes the ankle:\r\n\r\n- dorsiflexion shortens it;\r\n- plantarflexion lengthens it.\r\n\r\nThus the most lengthened sagittal-plane state occurs with the ankle in plantarflexion.\r\n\r\n## Eversion Can Further Lengthen It\r\n\r\nBecause TA contributes to inversion, an everted foot position can further increase its length.\r\n\r\nTherefore a highly lengthened state can combine:\r\n\r\n- plantarflexion;\r\n- eversion.\r\n\r\nHowever, deliberately forcing extreme eversion under resistance is unnecessary and may introduce unwanted joint stress.\r\n\r\n## Loaded Dorsiflexion\r\n\r\nA dorsiflexion exercise beginning from plantarflexion can load TA from a relatively long muscle length.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- tibialis raises with full plantarflexion at the bottom;\r\n- cable dorsiflexion;\r\n- dedicated tibialis machines.\r\n\r\nThis is mechanically more complete than short partials performed only near neutral.\r\n\r\n## Eccentric Loading\r\n\r\nWalking itself includes repeated eccentric TA activity after heel strike.\r\n\r\nResistance training can amplify this by emphasizing:\r\n\r\n- controlled plantarflexion eccentric;\r\n- full dorsiflexion concentric.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nThe mechanically defensible position is:\r\n\r\n- plantarflexion clearly lengthens TA;\r\n- resisted dorsiflexion from plantarflexion creates active long-length loading;\r\n- direct TA-specific long-length hypertrophy evidence is limited;\r\n- a moderate theoretical SMH effect is plausible but not strongly quantified.\r\n\r\nTherefore `smh_factor` should be mechanistically informed but evidence-conservative.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Tibialis Raise\r\n\r\nThe simplest direct exercise is the **tibialis raise**.\r\n\r\nTypical execution:\r\n\r\n1. heels remain on the ground or platform;\r\n2. ankle begins in plantarflexion;\r\n3. forefoot is lifted through dorsiflexion;\r\n4. movement returns under control.\r\n\r\nThe exercise directly loads tibialis anterior.\r\n\r\n## Wall Tibialis Raise\r\n\r\nA common bodyweight variation places the back against a wall with the feet slightly forward.\r\n\r\nIncreasing the distance of the heels from the wall increases mechanical difficulty because the ankle must produce greater dorsiflexion torque.\r\n\r\nAdvantages include:\r\n\r\n- minimal equipment;\r\n- easy high-repetition loading.\r\n\r\nLimitations include:\r\n\r\n- progressive overload eventually becomes awkward;\r\n- resistance profile depends heavily on body geometry.\r\n\r\n## Weighted Tibialis Raise\r\n\r\nDedicated tibialis machines or weighted foot attachments allow more systematic progression.\r\n\r\nThese are better suited to hypertrophy programming because:\r\n\r\n- load is measurable;\r\n- ROM is reproducible;\r\n- failure is easier to standardize.\r\n\r\n## Cable Dorsiflexion\r\n\r\nA cable attached to the forefoot can provide direct dorsiflexion resistance.\r\n\r\nThe line of force should be arranged so that the cable creates a plantarflexion moment about the ankle.\r\n\r\nThis permits:\r\n\r\n- substantial ROM;\r\n- progressive overload;\r\n- unilateral training.\r\n\r\n## Full ROM\r\n\r\nFor hypertrophy, a useful repetition should include:\r\n\r\n- controlled plantarflexion at the bottom;\r\n- strong dorsiflexion at the top.\r\n\r\nThe ankle does not need to be forced into painful maximal plantarflexion.\r\n\r\nThe goal is meaningful active excursion.\r\n\r\n## Heel Walking\r\n\r\nHeel walking produces prolonged low-load dorsiflexor demand.\r\n\r\nThis can be useful for:\r\n\r\n- endurance;\r\n- motor control.\r\n\r\nIt is less efficient for hypertrophy because progressive overload is limited.\r\n\r\n## Running\r\n\r\nRunning exposes TA to repeated:\r\n\r\n- swing-phase dorsiflexion;\r\n- eccentric control after foot strike.\r\n\r\nHowever, this should not automatically be counted as equivalent to direct resistance-training volume.\r\n\r\nThe force pattern and proximity to local failure are different.\r\n\r\n## Calf Raises\r\n\r\nCalf raises primarily train plantarflexors:\r\n\r\n- soleus;\r\n- gastrocnemius.\r\n\r\nTibialis anterior acts as an antagonist/stabilizer.\r\n\r\nIts direct hypertrophic demand should generally be low.\r\n\r\nHowever, it may contribute to ankle control.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Direct Training Is Often Missing\r\n\r\nMany lower-body programs directly train:\r\n\r\n- plantarflexors;\r\n- knee extensors;\r\n- hip extensors;\r\n\r\nbut omit dorsiflexors.\r\n\r\nIf anterior lower-leg development or dorsiflexion strength matters, direct TA work is useful.\r\n\r\n## Weekly Volume\r\n\r\nA practical starting range is approximately:\r\n\r\n- **4-8 hard direct sets per week**\r\n\r\nwhen tibialis anterior development is a priority.\r\n\r\nLower volumes may be sufficient if the goal is merely maintenance or basic ankle robustness.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nTwo to four weekly exposures are practical.\r\n\r\nThe muscle is relatively small and direct training creates little systemic fatigue.\r\n\r\nHowever, sudden large increases in volume can produce substantial soreness along the anterior shin.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\nTibialis raises work well in:\r\n\r\n- 10-20 reps;\r\n- 15-30 reps;\r\n- 20-40 reps when resistance is limited.\r\n\r\nThere is no physiological requirement for exclusively high repetitions.\r\n\r\nIf enough external resistance is available, conventional hypertrophy ranges are appropriate.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nStable tibialis raises can be trained close to failure:\r\n\r\n- approximately 0-2 RIR.\r\n\r\nFailure should be defined by inability to achieve meaningful dorsiflexion without:\r\n\r\n- knee movement;\r\n- toe-extension compensation;\r\n- shortened ROM.\r\n\r\n## Progressive Overload\r\n\r\nProgression can use:\r\n\r\n- more external load;\r\n- greater body leverage;\r\n- larger controlled ROM;\r\n- more repetitions.\r\n\r\nA dedicated machine or cable setup makes load progression easier than wall raises.\r\n\r\n## Interference\r\n\r\nDirect TA training usually creates little systemic fatigue.\r\n\r\nHowever, severe local soreness can affect:\r\n\r\n- running;\r\n- hiking;\r\n- sprinting;\r\n- downhill walking.\r\n\r\nScheduling should account for locomotor demands.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## It Is an Antagonist to the Calf Complex\r\n\r\nTibialis anterior produces dorsiflexion, while:\r\n\r\n- soleus;\r\n- gastrocnemius\r\n\r\nproduce plantarflexion.\r\n\r\nYet these muscles can co-contract to increase ankle stiffness.\r\n\r\nThus dorsiflexor and plantarflexor activity are not always mutually exclusive.\r\n\r\n## It Works Eccentrically After Heel Strike\r\n\r\nOne of TA's most important everyday functions occurs while the muscle is **lengthening**.\r\n\r\nAfter heel strike, it slows plantarflexion so the forefoot does not slap into the ground.\r\n\r\nThis is a clear example of substantial muscle function without concentric shortening.\r\n\r\n## Foot Drop Reveals Its Importance\r\n\r\nLoss of effective dorsiflexion can produce difficulty clearing the foot during swing.\r\n\r\nCompensatory gait may then involve increased:\r\n\r\n- hip flexion;\r\n- knee flexion.\r\n\r\nThis demonstrates how one relatively small lower-leg muscle can strongly influence whole-limb locomotion.\r\n\r\n## It Shares Inversion With Tibialis Posterior\r\n\r\nBoth tibialis muscles invert the foot.\r\n\r\nBut their sagittal-plane actions oppose each other:\r\n\r\n`TA = dorsiflexion + inversion`\r\n\r\n`TP = plantarflexion + inversion`\r\n\r\nCo-contraction can therefore generate inversion or medial-foot stabilization with reduced net sagittal ankle torque.\r\n\r\n## Shin Appearance\r\n\r\nTibialis anterior forms much of the visible muscular contour immediately lateral to the tibial crest.\r\n\r\nHypertrophy can therefore noticeably change anterior lower-leg appearance despite its much smaller mass than the calf complex.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\n`tibialis_anterior` should primarily receive demand from:\r\n\r\n1. **ankle dorsiflexion torque**;\r\n2. secondary inversion torque;\r\n3. anti-plantarflexion eccentric/isometric stabilization;\r\n4. medial-foot stabilization.\r\n\r\nIt should receive little direct force from pure plantarflexion exercises.\r\n\r\n## Dorsiflexion Force Sharing\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`T_dorsiflexion =\r\n    tibialis_anterior\r\n  + extensor_hallucis_longus\r\n  + extensor_digitorum_longus\r\n  + fibularis_tertius`\r\n\r\nTA should generally receive the largest single share.\r\n\r\nIf those smaller muscles are not represented separately in the database, `tibialis_anterior` may reasonably absorb much of the aggregate dorsiflexor demand, but should not be interpreted as literally generating 100% of it.\r\n\r\n## Inversion Force Sharing\r\n\r\nFor inversion:\r\n\r\n`T_inversion =\r\n    tibialis_anterior\r\n  + tibialis_posterior\r\n  + smaller contributors`\r\n\r\nThe relative share depends on simultaneous sagittal-plane requirements.\r\n\r\n### Inversion + Dorsiflexion\r\n\r\nFavor:\r\n- tibialis anterior.\r\n\r\n### Inversion + Plantarflexion\r\n\r\nFavor:\r\n- tibialis posterior.\r\n\r\nThis is useful for multi-axis ankle modelling.\r\n\r\n## Length-Tension Model\r\n\r\nA first-order model can use:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_TA = f(ankle_plantarflexion, foot_eversion)`\r\n\r\nQualitatively:\r\n\r\n### Long\r\n\r\n- plantarflexed;\r\n- somewhat everted.\r\n\r\n### Intermediate\r\n\r\n- neutral ankle.\r\n\r\n### Short\r\n\r\n- dorsiflexed;\r\n- inverted.\r\n\r\nFor a KISS implementation, ankle plantarflexion/dorsiflexion should carry most of the weight.\r\n\r\n## Tibialis Raise Allocation\r\n\r\n### Direct Tibialis Raise\r\n\r\nVery high:\r\n- `tibialis_anterior`.\r\n\r\nAlso small:\r\n- other dorsiflexors.\r\n\r\nThis should be one of the cleanest single-muscle vectors in the lower leg.\r\n\r\n## Walking / Running Allocation\r\n\r\nTA exposure should be treated as cyclical locomotor work rather than traditional ETU.\r\n\r\nRelevant phases include:\r\n\r\n### Swing\r\n\r\nConcentric/isometric dorsiflexion for toe clearance.\r\n\r\n### Initial Contact\r\n\r\nEccentric anti-plantarflexion control.\r\n\r\nThe repeated low-to-moderate force nature differs from hard resistance sets.\r\n\r\n## Calf Raise Allocation\r\n\r\n### Standing Calf Raise\r\n\r\nVery low dynamic TA demand.\r\n\r\nPossible:\r\n- ankle stabilization;\r\n- antagonist co-contraction.\r\n\r\nPrimary:\r\n- gastrocnemius;\r\n- soleus.\r\n\r\n### Seated Calf Raise\r\n\r\nAgain:\r\n- very low TA dynamic demand.\r\n\r\nPrimary:\r\n- soleus.\r\n\r\nDo not give TA meaningful hypertrophic credit just because it crosses the same ankle.\r\n\r\n## Leg Press\r\n\r\nTibialis-anterior demand is generally low during conventional leg press if the foot remains firmly supported.\r\n\r\nIt may contribute isometrically to ankle positioning, but plantarflexors and proximal lower-limb muscles dominate.\r\n\r\n## Hanging / Open-Chain Leg Exercises\r\n\r\nUnless active ankle dorsiflexion is demanded, TA should receive essentially no meaningful vector.\r\n\r\nThe fact that the lower leg moves through space does not imply TA activation.\r\n\r\n## Eccentric Demand\r\n\r\nFor Agonez, the muscle is a useful example where eccentric demand should be recognized explicitly.\r\n\r\nDuring controlled plantarflexion against resistance:\r\n\r\n`TA_length ↑`\r\n\r\nwhile:\r\n\r\n`TA_force > 0`.\r\n\r\nThat is genuine loaded eccentric work.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Soleus and Gastrocnemius\r\n\r\nThese muscles form the major sagittal-plane antagonist relationship at the ankle.\r\n\r\n### TA\r\n\r\nDorsiflexion.\r\n\r\n### Soleus/Gastrocnemius\r\n\r\nPlantarflexion.\r\n\r\nDuring high-force stance tasks, co-contraction can increase ankle stiffness.\r\n\r\nTherefore antagonist coactivation can produce non-zero TA demand even without dorsiflexion, but the magnitude should depend on actual stabilization need.\r\n\r\n## FCSA Interpretation\r\n\r\nTA FCSA should be interpreted as dorsiflexion-force capacity.\r\n\r\nUnlike antagonist-containing aggregates such as `rotator_cuffs`, this is relatively straightforward.\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`F_max_TA ≈ σ × FCSA_TA`\r\n\r\nand ankle dorsiflexion torque:\r\n\r\n`T_TA ≈ F_TA × r_TA`\r\n\r\nwith the moment arm varying somewhat with ankle position.\r\n\r\n## Moment-Arm Consideration\r\n\r\nThe dorsiflexion moment arm of TA is not perfectly constant across ROM.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`E_MA_TA = f(ankle_angle)`\r\n\r\nis more accurate than one fixed value.\r\n\r\nFor Agonez, however, a moderate angle-dependent correction is probably sufficient.\r\n\r\n## Architecture Implication\r\n\r\nTibialis anterior possesses a mixed design:\r\n\r\n- meaningful PCSA for repeated dorsiflexion force;\r\n- fascicle excursion sufficient for substantial ankle ROM.\r\n\r\nIt is neither as extreme a force-oriented muscle as soleus nor as excursion-oriented as sartorius.\r\n\r\nThis is consistent with its dual locomotor role:\r\n\r\n- repeated active shortening during swing;\r\n- repeated eccentric control after heel strike.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nThe mechanically useful rule is:\r\n\r\n`long TA = plantarflexed ankle`\r\n\r\nand:\r\n\r\n`active dorsiflexion from plantarflexion = long-length loading`.\r\n\r\nDirect longitudinal evidence for a special TA stretch-mediated hypertrophy effect remains limited.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n- preserve full controlled plantarflexion ROM;\r\n- recognize long-length active tension;\r\n- avoid assigning a large evidence-based SMH premium without direct data.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **lateral tibial origin and medial cuneiform/first-metatarsal insertion:** high;\r\n- **deep-fibular-nerve innervation:** high;\r\n- **ankle-dorsiflexion function:** very high;\r\n- **inversion function:** high;\r\n- **eccentric control after heel strike:** very high;\r\n- **swing-phase toe-clearance role:** very high;\r\n- **cooperation with other dorsiflexors:** high;\r\n- **interaction with tibialis posterior in inversion:** high;\r\n- **exercise-specific hypertrophy evidence:** limited;\r\n- **stretch-mediated hypertrophy magnitude:** poorly established;\r\n- **precise ankle-angle-dependent force coefficients:** variable with geometry.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **tibialis anterior = the principal ankle dorsiflexor and a secondary foot inverter, strongly active both concentrically for toe clearance and eccentrically to resist plantarflexion, with a straightforward length state governed primarily by ankle plantarflexion versus dorsiflexion.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/TibialisAnterior}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	6.83	9.60	0.986	42.29	41.70
5	deltoid_lateral	Musculus Deltoid lateral	Upper	Shoulder	540.00	511.36	28.00	Multipenate	0.500	0.500	zero	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\nThe **lateral deltoid**, also called the middle or acromial deltoid, forms the central portion of the deltoid muscle and is one of the primary generators of **glenohumeral abduction torque**.\r\n\r\nIt should be distinguished mechanically from:\r\n\r\n- `deltoid_anterior`;\r\n- `deltoid_posterior`.\r\n\r\nAlthough all three regions share a common distal insertion, their different origins and fiber orientations create substantially different moment arms and preferred movement planes.\r\n\r\nThe primary mechanical identity of the lateral deltoid is:\r\n\r\n> **elevation of the humerus away from the torso, particularly through abduction and scapular-plane elevation.**\r\n\r\nIts role is especially important through the middle portion of humeral elevation, where its abduction moment arm is generally favorable.\r\n\r\nThe lateral deltoid is therefore one of the clearest examples of a muscle whose hypertrophy can be targeted effectively through a relatively simple joint action:\r\n\r\n- loaded shoulder abduction.\r\n\r\nHowever, actual shoulder elevation is not produced by the deltoid alone.\r\n\r\nSuccessful elevation requires coordinated action of:\r\n\r\n- rotator cuff;\r\n- scapular upward rotators;\r\n- clavicular motion;\r\n- other deltoid regions.\r\n\r\nTherefore, even a technically simple lateral raise is biomechanically a multi-joint-complex movement.\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\n## Origin\r\n\r\nThe lateral deltoid originates primarily from the **lateral margin and superior surface of the acromion of the scapula**.\r\n\r\nThis origin lies between:\r\n\r\n- the clavicular origin of the anterior deltoid;\r\n- the scapular-spine origin of the posterior deltoid.\r\n\r\nIts fibers descend predominantly inferiorly toward the humerus.\r\n\r\nCompared with the anterior and posterior regions, this fiber orientation gives the lateral deltoid a particularly favorable line of action for humeral abduction.\r\n\r\n## Insertion\r\n\r\nThe lateral deltoid inserts, together with the anterior and posterior portions, onto the **deltoid tuberosity of the humerus**.\r\n\r\nThe common insertion does not make the three portions mechanically equivalent.\r\n\r\nBecause each region approaches the tuberosity from a different direction, contraction produces different combinations of:\r\n\r\n- elevation;\r\n- flexion/extension;\r\n- rotation.\r\n\r\n## Architecture\r\n\r\nThe deltoid is a complex multipennate muscle divided into several intramuscular segments.\r\n\r\nThe conventional three-part division into anterior, lateral and posterior regions is therefore an anatomical simplification.\r\n\r\nWithin the lateral deltoid itself, different fascicular segments may have slightly different:\r\n\r\n- fiber trajectories;\r\n- moment arms;\r\n- recruitment patterns.\r\n\r\nNevertheless, `deltoid_lateral` is a useful exercise-level aggregate because the acromial region shares a strong functional bias toward humeral elevation and abduction.\r\n\r\nPennation allows a relatively large amount of contractile tissue to be packed into the shoulder cap, supporting high force generation despite relatively short fascicle excursions.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nThe lateral deltoid is innervated by the **axillary nerve**, predominantly from spinal roots **C5-C6**.\r\n\r\nThe axillary nerve supplies:\r\n\r\n- deltoid;\r\n- teres minor.\r\n\r\nIt passes through the quadrangular space around the proximal humerus and then distributes branches to different regions of the deltoid.\r\n\r\nThis regional neural organization permits partial functional differentiation between anterior, middle and posterior deltoid segments.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Glenohumeral Abduction\r\n\r\nThe primary function of the lateral deltoid is **abduction of the humerus at the glenohumeral joint**.\r\n\r\nIts effectiveness changes substantially with shoulder angle.\r\n\r\nAt low abduction angles, deltoid contraction contains a relatively large superior translational component and its rotational leverage is less favorable.\r\n\r\nAs elevation increases, the lateral deltoid's abduction moment arm generally increases.\r\n\r\nStudies of shoulder moment arms show that the middle deltoid often reaches particularly favorable leverage around the **middle portion of abduction ROM**.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n> the lateral deltoid is not equally mechanically effective at every point in a lateral raise.\r\n\r\nThis is highly relevant to exercise resistance profiles.\r\n\r\n## Scapular-Plane Elevation\r\n\r\nPure frontal-plane abduction is not the only movement capable of strongly recruiting the lateral deltoid.\r\n\r\nThe muscle is also a major contributor during **scapular-plane elevation**, where the humerus travels somewhat anterior to the strict frontal plane.\r\n\r\nThis is often a comfortable anatomical plane because the glenoid itself is oriented somewhat anterior to the frontal plane.\r\n\r\nA slightly forward arm path in a lateral raise therefore does not automatically mean that the anterior deltoid has taken over.\r\n\r\nThe transition between:\r\n\r\n- frontal-plane abduction;\r\n- scapular-plane elevation;\r\n- sagittal-plane flexion\r\n\r\nis continuous.\r\n\r\nAs the movement becomes progressively more anterior, anterior-deltoid contribution generally increases.\r\n\r\n## Contribution at Low Angles\r\n\r\nAt the very beginning of abduction, the lateral deltoid is mechanically less efficient as a pure rotator than it becomes later.\r\n\r\nThe rotator cuff contributes importantly in this region.\r\n\r\nIn particular, cuff forces:\r\n\r\n- contribute to initial elevation;\r\n- compress and center the humeral head;\r\n- counteract superior translation caused by deltoid force.\r\n\r\nThis means that the old textbook concept of:\r\n\r\n> supraspinatus starts abduction, deltoid takes over after 15°\r\n\r\nis too discrete.\r\n\r\nBoth systems cooperate across the ROM, with their relative mechanical effectiveness changing continuously.\r\n\r\n## High-Angle Elevation\r\n\r\nAt higher elevation angles, the deltoid remains important, but total arm elevation increasingly depends on scapular movement.\r\n\r\nThus global arm elevation above approximately shoulder height cannot be interpreted as pure glenohumeral abduction.\r\n\r\nThe scapula must contribute through:\r\n\r\n- upward rotation;\r\n- posterior tilt;\r\n- external rotation.\r\n\r\nClavicular motion also contributes.\r\n\r\nTherefore, when a lateral raise continues above shoulder level, additional ROM does not represent proportional additional lateral-deltoid shortening.\r\n\r\n## Glenohumeral Stabilization\r\n\r\nThe lateral deltoid generates substantial superiorly directed force on the humerus.\r\n\r\nThe rotator cuff counteracts this tendency and helps maintain the humeral head within the glenoid.\r\n\r\nThis force couple is essential.\r\n\r\nAt low elevation angles especially, deltoid force without cuff stabilization would produce much more superior translation and less efficient rotation.\r\n\r\nTherefore high lateral-deltoid force should normally imply some degree of `rotator_cuffs` stabilization demand in the Agonez model.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nDirect evidence for a large **stretch-mediated hypertrophy effect specific to the lateral deltoid** is currently limited.\r\n\r\nThe biomechanics also differ from muscles such as the hamstrings or biceps.\r\n\r\nFor the lateral deltoid, the relatively lengthened state occurs when the humerus is closer to the torso and particularly when positioned in some degree of adduction.\r\n\r\nHowever, conventional free-weight lateral raises have a major limitation:\r\n\r\n> when the arm hangs nearly vertically, the external gravitational moment around the shoulder is very small.\r\n\r\nThus the lateral deltoid can be relatively long while external torque is close to zero.\r\n\r\nThis makes the standard dumbbell lateral raise a poor example of maximal long-length loading despite being an excellent overall lateral-deltoid exercise.\r\n\r\n## Cable Loading\r\n\r\nCable geometry can solve this problem.\r\n\r\nA properly configured cable lateral raise can maintain meaningful resistance when the arm is close to the torso.\r\n\r\nIf the cable originates across or behind the body, the exercise can even create substantial torque while the working humerus is slightly adducted.\r\n\r\nThis provides a plausible method of loading the lateral deltoid at longer muscle lengths.\r\n\r\n## Cable vs Dumbbell Evidence\r\n\r\nImportantly, mechanical theory does not automatically translate into a proven hypertrophic advantage.\r\n\r\nA 2025 longitudinal study directly compared **cable and dumbbell lateral raises** and found both produced lateral-deltoid hypertrophy, with no compelling evidence of a meaningful difference between conditions. :contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}\r\n\r\nThis is an important constraint on overly strong SMH claims.\r\n\r\nCable lateral raises may provide:\r\n\r\n- greater torque near the bottom;\r\n- more explicit long-length loading;\r\n- a different resistance profile.\r\n\r\nBut available direct hypertrophy evidence does not establish that this automatically produces superior lateral-deltoid growth.\r\n\r\n## Practical Interpretation\r\n\r\nFor lateral-deltoid hypertrophy:\r\n\r\n- meaningful loading through a broad ROM is desirable;\r\n- cables can improve resistance near low abduction angles;\r\n- dumbbells remain highly effective;\r\n- there is no need to chase extreme humeral adduction;\r\n- direct evidence for a uniquely large SMH effect remains limited.\r\n\r\nThe most defensible interpretation is therefore that long-length loading is **mechanically plausible and potentially useful**, but not clearly dominant over other resistance profiles.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Lateral Raise\r\n\r\nThe lateral raise is the most direct conventional resistance exercise for the lateral deltoid.\r\n\r\nIt can be performed with:\r\n\r\n- dumbbells;\r\n- cable;\r\n- machine;\r\n- resistance bands.\r\n\r\nIts primary action should be humeral elevation rather than shrugging.\r\n\r\nA well-executed lateral raise generally involves:\r\n\r\n1. stable torso;\r\n2. controlled humeral elevation;\r\n3. modest elbow flexion;\r\n4. minimal momentum;\r\n5. scapular motion occurring naturally as elevation increases.\r\n\r\nThe arm does not need to remain in a mathematically perfect frontal plane.\r\n\r\nA slight scapular-plane trajectory is usually natural and mechanically appropriate.\r\n\r\n## Dumbbell Lateral Raise\r\n\r\nThe dumbbell lateral raise has a strongly gravity-dependent resistance curve.\r\n\r\nExternal shoulder torque is approximately:\r\n\r\n- low near the bottom;\r\n- progressively larger as the arm approaches horizontal;\r\n- highest around the region where the arm is approximately perpendicular to gravity.\r\n\r\nThis aligns reasonably well with the favorable mid-range leverage of the lateral deltoid.\r\n\r\nThus the classic dumbbell lateral raise is not biomechanically "bad" merely because it is lightly loaded at the bottom.\r\n\r\nIts resistance curve interacts reasonably well with the muscle's leverage curve.\r\n\r\n## Cable Lateral Raise\r\n\r\nA cable can reshape the external torque profile.\r\n\r\nDepending on pulley position, it can provide:\r\n\r\n- substantial resistance near the bottom;\r\n- smoother loading through ROM;\r\n- less extreme mid-range torque concentration.\r\n\r\nThis makes cable raises especially useful when the goal is to expose the lateral deltoid to force across a broader range of lengths.\r\n\r\nHowever, pulley geometry matters enormously.\r\n\r\nTwo exercises both named "cable lateral raise" can have different resistance profiles depending on:\r\n\r\n- pulley height;\r\n- body distance from the machine;\r\n- whether the cable crosses in front or behind the body;\r\n- starting humeral position.\r\n\r\n## Behind-the-Body / Cross-Body Cable Raise\r\n\r\nAllowing the cable to pull the working arm slightly across the body can create resistance while the humerus begins in relative adduction.\r\n\r\nThis can increase long-length tension.\r\n\r\nThe position should remain comfortable.\r\n\r\nExtreme cross-body stretching is unnecessary and may simply introduce torso rotation or shoulder discomfort.\r\n\r\n## Machine Lateral Raise\r\n\r\nA well-designed lateral-raise machine offers several advantages:\r\n\r\n- high stability;\r\n- standardized movement path;\r\n- low grip limitation;\r\n- easy proximity to failure;\r\n- potentially favorable resistance profile.\r\n\r\nBecause the machine can apply force through the upper arm rather than the hand, wrist and elbow limitations can be minimized.\r\n\r\nThis can make it extremely efficient for lateral-deltoid hypertrophy.\r\n\r\nIts quality depends on:\r\n\r\n- machine axis alignment;\r\n- pad position;\r\n- resistance cam;\r\n- individual anthropometry.\r\n\r\n## Shoulder Press\r\n\r\nShoulder pressing also trains the lateral deltoid substantially.\r\n\r\nEMG comparisons show high middle-deltoid activation during shoulder pressing, often comparable with lateral raise under the specific tested conditions. :contentReference[oaicite:3]{index=3}\r\n\r\nHowever, shoulder press distributes demand among:\r\n\r\n- anterior deltoid;\r\n- lateral deltoid;\r\n- triceps;\r\n- upper chest depending on angle;\r\n- rotator cuff.\r\n\r\nTherefore it is less mechanically specific than a lateral raise.\r\n\r\nFor a lifter seeking maximal lateral-delt development, pressing should usually be viewed as valuable indirect volume rather than a complete substitute for direct abduction.\r\n\r\n## Bench Press\r\n\r\nHorizontal bench pressing provides relatively little lateral-deltoid stimulus compared with:\r\n\r\n- shoulder press;\r\n- lateral raise.\r\n\r\nExperimental EMG data support this distinction. :contentReference[oaicite:4]{index=4}\r\n\r\nTherefore, unlike anterior deltoid:\r\n\r\n> a high volume of chest pressing does not necessarily provide sufficient lateral-deltoid exposure.\r\n\r\nThis is one of the most important programming distinctions between anterior and lateral deltoid.\r\n\r\n## Arnold Press\r\n\r\nArnold-style pressing includes a highly anterior arm path during part of the movement.\r\n\r\nIt still recruits lateral deltoid through humeral elevation, but the anterior deltoid receives substantial demand.\r\n\r\nIt should therefore not be treated as uniquely lateral-delt focused.\r\n\r\nIts lateral-deltoid allocation should generally be lower proportionally than in a well-executed lateral raise.\r\n\r\n## Scaption\r\n\r\nScaption refers to humeral elevation in the scapular plane.\r\n\r\nIt strongly recruits the deltoid and generally permits comfortable shoulder mechanics.\r\n\r\nAs the arm path moves progressively anterior:\r\n\r\n- anterior-deltoid contribution increases;\r\n- the movement becomes less specific to lateral deltoid.\r\n\r\nA modest scapular-plane angle remains entirely compatible with lateral-deltoid training.\r\n\r\n## Humeral Rotation\r\n\r\nDifferent internal/external rotation positions during lateral raises alter deltoid and surrounding-muscle activation.\r\n\r\nEMG studies demonstrate that lateral-raise variants with different humeral rotations produce different recruitment patterns. :contentReference[oaicite:5]{index=5}\r\n\r\nHowever, aggressively internally rotating the shoulder — the classic "pour the pitcher" cue — is unnecessary.\r\n\r\nThere is no convincing hypertrophic reason to force the shoulder into uncomfortable internal rotation.\r\n\r\nA neutral or comfortable humeral rotation is generally preferable.\r\n\r\n## Elbow Flexion\r\n\r\nFlexing the elbow reduces the distance between the load and shoulder.\r\n\r\nFor a fixed dumbbell mass this decreases external shoulder torque.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n> a 10 kg straight-arm lateral raise and a 10 kg heavily bent-arm lateral raise are not equivalent.\r\n\r\nIf Agonez models external mechanical demand, effective moment arm to the load must matter.\r\n\r\nThis is especially relevant when comparing exercise variants.\r\n\r\n## Upper Trapezius Participation\r\n\r\nScapular upward rotation is normal during humeral elevation.\r\n\r\nTherefore upper trapezius activity during lateral raises is not automatically "cheating."\r\n\r\nThe incorrect simplification is:\r\n\r\n> traps active = lateral delt not working.\r\n\r\nThe scapula must move during normal elevation.\r\n\r\nThe practical goal is to avoid turning the exercise into predominantly shoulder girdle elevation with little glenohumeral abduction.\r\n\r\nSome upper-trapezius recruitment is physiologically appropriate.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Direct Isolation Is Usually Valuable\r\n\r\nUnlike anterior deltoid, lateral deltoid often benefits substantially from direct isolation.\r\n\r\nA press-heavy program may provide:\r\n\r\n- large anterior-deltoid volume;\r\n- substantial triceps volume;\r\n\r\nwhile still providing only moderate lateral-deltoid exposure.\r\n\r\nTherefore lateral raises frequently have high marginal value even in programs containing overhead pressing.\r\n\r\n## Weekly Volume\r\n\r\nA practical starting point for a trained lifter can be approximately:\r\n\r\n- **6-12 direct hard sets per week**\r\n\r\nwhen lateral-deltoid hypertrophy is a meaningful priority.\r\n\r\nLower volumes may be sufficient when:\r\n\r\n- shoulder pressing volume is high;\r\n- the muscle is not a priority;\r\n- recovery is constrained.\r\n\r\nHigher volumes can sometimes be tolerated because lateral raises have relatively low systemic fatigue.\r\n\r\nHowever, low systemic cost does not mean infinite local recoverability.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nThe lateral deltoid lends itself well to relatively high training frequency.\r\n\r\nUseful schedules include:\r\n\r\n- 2 sessions per week;\r\n- 3 sessions per week;\r\n- occasionally more frequent low-volume exposure.\r\n\r\nThis is possible because direct lateral raises:\r\n\r\n- require modest external loads;\r\n- create little axial fatigue;\r\n- have low cardiovascular cost;\r\n- minimally interfere with major lower-body training.\r\n\r\nFrequency should still be constrained by local shoulder recovery.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\nThe lateral deltoid works well across broad repetition ranges, but moderate-to-high repetitions are especially practical for isolation.\r\n\r\nUseful ranges include:\r\n\r\n- 8-15 reps for heavier machine/cable work;\r\n- 12-20 reps as a general default;\r\n- 15-30 reps for lighter isolation work.\r\n\r\nEven higher repetitions can work when proximity to failure is sufficient.\r\n\r\nVery heavy low-repetition lateral raises often become limited by:\r\n\r\n- momentum;\r\n- upper-trapezius compensation;\r\n- poor ROM;\r\n- difficulty controlling the eccentric.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nStable lateral raises can generally be trained close to failure.\r\n\r\nApproximately:\r\n\r\n- **0-2 RIR**\r\n\r\nis often practical.\r\n\r\nThe exercise has relatively low systemic cost and technical risk compared with heavy compound pressing.\r\n\r\nHowever, failure should refer to inability to continue productive humeral elevation, not inability to move the dumbbell through progressively larger amounts of:\r\n\r\n- torso lean;\r\n- leg drive;\r\n- shrugging.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Diversity\r\n\r\nVariation is useful when it changes the resistance profile.\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n\r\n1. cable lateral raise with greater bottom-range loading;\r\n2. machine or dumbbell lateral raise emphasizing mid-range.\r\n\r\nThis combination provides more mechanical diversity than simply performing several nearly identical dumbbell variations.\r\n\r\nExercise diversity should therefore be justified by:\r\n\r\n- resistance profile;\r\n- stability;\r\n- ROM;\r\n- comfort;\r\n\r\nrather than by cosmetic exercise naming.\r\n\r\n## Pressing Volume\r\n\r\nOverhead pressing should count toward lateral-deltoid volume, but generally not as a full equivalent of isolation work.\r\n\r\nThe exercise distributes demand among several large contributors.\r\n\r\nA simple programming model might treat:\r\n\r\n- direct lateral raise → high lateral-delt specificity;\r\n- shoulder press → substantial partial contribution;\r\n- incline press → smaller contribution;\r\n- flat bench → low contribution.\r\n\r\nThis is much more realistic than counting all pressing sets equally.\r\n\r\n## Fatigue Management\r\n\r\nBecause lateral raises are inexpensive systemically, they are often added aggressively.\r\n\r\nPotential local constraints include:\r\n\r\n- glenohumeral irritation;\r\n- rotator-cuff fatigue;\r\n- upper-trapezius soreness;\r\n- elbow/wrist discomfort from certain variations.\r\n\r\nIf weekly volume becomes high, using a mixture of:\r\n\r\n- cable;\r\n- machine;\r\n- dumbbell\r\n\r\ncan distribute repetitive loading across slightly different mechanics.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## "Lateral Deltoid" Is Really the Middle Deltoid\r\n\r\nTerminology varies.\r\n\r\nCommon names include:\r\n\r\n- lateral deltoid;\r\n- middle deltoid;\r\n- medial deltoid;\r\n- acromial deltoid.\r\n\r\n`Middle` or `acromial` is anatomically clearer, because "lateral" describes its visible position rather than a unique formal anatomical division.\r\n\r\n## It Has Excellent Mid-Range Abduction Leverage\r\n\r\nStudies measuring shoulder muscle moment arms consistently identify the middle deltoid as a highly effective abductor, with leverage often becoming particularly favorable around the middle of humeral elevation. :contentReference[oaicite:6]{index=6}\r\n\r\nThis provides a biomechanical explanation for why lateral raises feel mechanically demanding around the region where the arm approaches horizontal.\r\n\r\n## The Rotator Cuff Does Not Simply "Start" the Movement\r\n\r\nThe common teaching that supraspinatus performs the first 15 degrees and deltoid takes over afterward is overly discrete.\r\n\r\nBoth systems contribute throughout shoulder elevation.\r\n\r\nTheir relative leverage and stabilization roles change continuously with joint angle. :contentReference[oaicite:7]{index=7}\r\n\r\n## Upper Trapezius Activity Is Normal\r\n\r\nA lateral raise necessarily involves the entire shoulder complex once elevation becomes substantial.\r\n\r\nThe scapula upwardly rotates, and upper trapezius participates in that process.\r\n\r\nThus perfectly "turning off the traps" is neither realistic nor necessarily desirable.\r\n\r\n## Cable Is Not Automatically Better Than Dumbbell\r\n\r\nFrom first principles, cables can clearly provide more external torque near the bottom of a lateral raise.\r\n\r\nThis led to a plausible expectation that they might produce greater lateral-deltoid hypertrophy.\r\n\r\nA direct 2025 longitudinal comparison, however, found similar increases in lateral-deltoid thickness from cable and dumbbell lateral raises. :contentReference[oaicite:8]{index=8}\r\n\r\nThis is a useful reminder that:\r\n\r\n> a biomechanical difference does not automatically imply a practically meaningful hypertrophy difference.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\n`deltoid_lateral` should primarily receive demand from:\r\n\r\n1. **glenohumeral abduction**;\r\n2. **scapular-plane humeral elevation**;\r\n3. substantial contribution to overhead pressing;\r\n4. relatively little contribution to pure horizontal pressing.\r\n\r\nThe exercise model should strongly distinguish lateral deltoid from anterior deltoid.\r\n\r\n## Angle-Dependent Leverage\r\n\r\nA simplified internal leverage profile can reasonably be modeled as:\r\n\r\n- low abduction angle → poorer deltoid abduction leverage;\r\n- mid-range → strong / near-peak leverage;\r\n- high elevation → still substantial but increasingly affected by scapulohumeral mechanics.\r\n\r\nThis supports a:\r\n\r\n`Bell-shaped`\r\n\r\nor similar mid-range-peaking `strength_curve`.\r\n\r\nFor this muscle, `leverage_peak = Mid_Range` is biomechanically well justified.\r\n\r\n## External Torque in Lateral Raises\r\n\r\nFor a dumbbell lateral raise:\r\n\r\n`external_shoulder_torque ≈ load × horizontal_distance_from_shoulder`\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n### Bottom\r\n\r\nArm approximately vertical:\r\n\r\n- horizontal distance small;\r\n- external torque low.\r\n\r\n### Mid-Range\r\n\r\nArm approaches horizontal:\r\n\r\n- horizontal distance large;\r\n- external torque high.\r\n\r\nThis external profile interacts with the lateral deltoid's favorable mid-range internal moment arm.\r\n\r\nThus dumbbell lateral raises possess a naturally mid-range-biased loading profile.\r\n\r\n## Cable Geometry\r\n\r\nCable raises require vector-specific modelling.\r\n\r\nExternal torque depends on:\r\n\r\n- cable force;\r\n- cable direction;\r\n- arm position;\r\n- perpendicular distance from the glenohumeral axis.\r\n\r\nA cable starting across the body can produce substantial external torque near low abduction angles.\r\n\r\nTherefore Agonez should not assign identical resistance profiles to:\r\n\r\n- dumbbell lateral raise;\r\n- low-cable lateral raise;\r\n- cross-body cable lateral raise;\r\n- machine lateral raise.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Allocation\r\n\r\n### Single-Arm Cable Lateral Raise\r\n\r\nVery high `deltoid_lateral` allocation.\r\n\r\nAlso non-zero:\r\n\r\n- `rotator_cuffs`;\r\n- `serratus_anterior`;\r\n- `trapezius_upper`;\r\n- `trapezius_lower`;\r\n\r\nbecause normal humeral elevation requires both glenohumeral stabilization and scapular rotation.\r\n\r\n### Seated Dumbbell Overhead Press\r\n\r\nSubstantial lateral-deltoid allocation.\r\n\r\nAlso large:\r\n\r\n- `deltoid_anterior`;\r\n- triceps;\r\n- rotator cuff.\r\n\r\n### Arnold Press\r\n\r\nMeaningful lateral-deltoid demand, but greater anterior-deltoid bias than a pure lateral raise because of its anterior humeral path.\r\n\r\n### Incline Bench Press\r\n\r\nRelatively low-to-moderate lateral-deltoid allocation.\r\n\r\nAnterior deltoid should dominate the deltoid component.\r\n\r\n### Flat Horizontal Chest Press\r\n\r\nLow lateral-deltoid allocation.\r\n\r\nHigh values here should be treated skeptically unless unusual machine geometry produces significant abduction/elevation torque.\r\n\r\n## Scapular Upward Rotation\r\n\r\nGlobal arm elevation must be separated conceptually into:\r\n\r\n- glenohumeral elevation;\r\n- scapulothoracic upward rotation.\r\n\r\nThe lateral deltoid directly acts on the humerus.\r\n\r\nIt does **not** upwardly rotate the scapula directly.\r\n\r\nTherefore high-arm-elevation exercises should distribute some demand to:\r\n\r\n- `serratus_anterior`;\r\n- `trapezius_upper`;\r\n- `trapezius_lower`.\r\n\r\nThis becomes increasingly relevant at higher arm angles.\r\n\r\n## Rotator-Cuff Demand\r\n\r\nAt lower abduction angles, deltoid force contains a substantial superior translational component.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n> high lateral-deltoid torque without cuff stabilization is mechanically implausible.\r\n\r\n`rotator_cuffs` demand should be non-zero during loaded lateral raises and overhead presses.\r\n\r\nIts proportional importance may be especially high near lower elevation angles, even when lateral-deltoid torque itself is not yet maximal. :contentReference[oaicite:9]{index=9}\r\n\r\n## Length-Tension Model\r\n\r\nA simplified lateral-deltoid length model can use glenohumeral abduction/adduction:\r\n\r\n- slight adduction → relatively long;\r\n- arm near torso → long-to-intermediate;\r\n- increasing abduction → progressively shorter.\r\n\r\nHowever, the complete state also depends on:\r\n\r\n- plane of elevation;\r\n- humeral rotation;\r\n- scapular orientation.\r\n\r\nThus a single abduction angle is a useful approximation rather than a complete anatomical model.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nThe strongest evidence-based conclusion is conservative:\r\n\r\n- lower humeral positions place the lateral deltoid at relatively longer length;\r\n- cable geometry can produce greater external torque there than dumbbells;\r\n- this makes long-length loading mechanically plausible;\r\n- direct evidence does **not currently show a clear hypertrophy superiority of cable over dumbbell lateral raises**. :contentReference[oaicite:10]{index=10}\r\n\r\nTherefore, if this row currently has a high `smh_factor`, I would interpret that field cautiously rather than treating it as strongly experimentally established.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **acromial origin and humeral insertion:** high;\r\n- **axillary-nerve innervation:** high;\r\n- **primary abduction function:** high;\r\n- **strong mid-range abduction leverage:** directly supported biomechanically;\r\n- **substantial recruitment during lateral raise:** directly supported;\r\n- **substantial recruitment during shoulder press:** directly supported;\r\n- **low contribution during horizontal bench pressing:** supported experimentally;\r\n- **cable vs dumbbell lateral-raise hypertrophy equivalence:** supported by direct 2025 longitudinal evidence;\r\n- **specific stretch-mediated hypertrophy advantage:** currently limited;\r\n- **precise exercise-specific recruitment coefficients:** dependent on geometry and individual anatomy.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **lateral deltoid = the acromial deltoid region specialized for humeral abduction and scapular-plane elevation, with especially favorable leverage through the middle of elevation and a hypertrophic workload best generated by direct abduction rather than inferred from horizontal pressing.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/DeltoidLateral}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	10.80	15.00	0.966	47.35	45.73
45	gastrocnemius	Musculus gastrocnemius	Lower	Calves	1055.00	999.05	62.00	Pennate/Bipennate	0.425	0.575	high	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\nThe **gastrocnemius** is the large superficial biarticular muscle forming much of the visible proximal calf.\r\n\r\nIt consists of two major heads:\r\n\r\n- **medial head**;\r\n- **lateral head**.\r\n\r\nTogether with the deeper `soleus`, gastrocnemius forms the major force-producing component of the **triceps surae**.\r\n\r\nBoth gastrocnemius and soleus converge into the calcaneal/Achilles tendon system and generate ankle plantarflexion, but they are mechanically distinct.\r\n\r\nThe defining difference is:\r\n\r\n- **gastrocnemius crosses both the knee and ankle**;\r\n- **soleus crosses only the ankle**.\r\n\r\nThis distinction has major consequences for:\r\n\r\n- muscle length;\r\n- force production;\r\n- exercise selection;\r\n- hypertrophy.\r\n\r\nGastrocnemius is lengthened by:\r\n\r\n- **knee extension**;\r\n- **ankle dorsiflexion**.\r\n\r\nIt is shortened by:\r\n\r\n- knee flexion;\r\n- ankle plantarflexion.\r\n\r\nTherefore the longest practical gastrocnemius configuration combines:\r\n\r\n> **an extended knee with a dorsiflexed ankle.**\r\n\r\nThis makes straight-leg calf raises fundamentally different from seated bent-knee calf raises.\r\n\r\nThe muscle is not merely a plantarflexor. Because it originates above the knee, it also contributes to **knee flexion** and participates in dynamic transfer of force across both knee and ankle during locomotion.\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\nThe gastrocnemius contains two superficial heads surrounding the proximal posterior leg.\r\n\r\n## Medial Head\r\n\r\n### Origin\r\n\r\nThe medial head originates primarily from the posterior aspect of the femur superior to the **medial femoral condyle**, with attachment extending around the medial supracondylar region and adjacent joint capsule.\r\n\r\nIt is generally:\r\n\r\n- larger;\r\n- longer;\r\n- more distal\r\n\r\nthan the lateral head.\r\n\r\nThe medial head contributes substantially to the characteristic medial contour of the upper calf.\r\n\r\n### Insertion\r\n\r\nIts muscle fibers converge distally into a broad aponeurotic structure that contributes to the **calcaneal tendon / Achilles tendon complex**.\r\n\r\nThe tendon ultimately inserts onto the posterior calcaneus.\r\n\r\n## Lateral Head\r\n\r\n### Origin\r\n\r\nThe lateral head originates from the posterior/lateral region of the femur superior to the **lateral femoral condyle**, with associated attachment to the posterior knee capsule.\r\n\r\n### Insertion\r\n\r\nLike the medial head, its fibers converge into the distal gastrocnemius aponeurosis and ultimately into the Achilles tendon system.\r\n\r\nThe two heads therefore possess:\r\n\r\n- separate proximal origins;\r\n- regionally distinct muscle bellies;\r\n- a shared distal force-transmission pathway.\r\n\r\n## Relationship With Soleus\r\n\r\nSoleus lies deep to gastrocnemius.\r\n\r\nDistally, force from both muscles converges into the calcaneal tendon system.\r\n\r\nDespite sharing this distal pathway, they should remain separate entities in an exercise model because:\r\n\r\n- gastrocnemius crosses the knee;\r\n- soleus does not;\r\n- knee angle therefore changes their operating conditions differently.\r\n\r\n## Architecture\r\n\r\nGastrocnemius is a **pennate muscle**.\r\n\r\nIts fascicles insert obliquely into aponeurotic structures rather than running directly from femur to calcaneus.\r\n\r\nPennation permits a relatively large physiological cross-sectional area to be packed into the available volume.\r\n\r\nIts architecture is dynamic.\r\n\r\nDuring contraction:\r\n\r\n- fascicles shorten;\r\n- pennation angle changes;\r\n- the Achilles tendon stretches;\r\n- muscle-tendon-unit length change is distributed between contractile and elastic structures.\r\n\r\nThe medial and lateral heads are also not architecturally identical.\r\n\r\nThey can differ in:\r\n\r\n- muscle volume;\r\n- fascicle length;\r\n- pennation;\r\n- regional hypertrophic response.\r\n\r\nTherefore `gastrocnemius` represents an aggregate of two related but non-identical heads.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nGastrocnemius is innervated by the **tibial nerve**, predominantly from spinal roots **S1-S2**.\r\n\r\nThe tibial nerve supplies the superficial posterior compartment of the leg, including:\r\n\r\n- gastrocnemius;\r\n- soleus;\r\n- plantaris.\r\n\r\nSeparate motor branches supply the medial and lateral gastrocnemius heads.\r\n\r\nFor exercise-level modelling, the two heads can reasonably remain aggregated unless future regional modelling requires side-specific or head-specific recruitment.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Ankle Plantarflexion\r\n\r\nThe primary function of gastrocnemius is **plantarflexion at the talocrural joint**.\r\n\r\nContraction pulls through the Achilles tendon on the calcaneus, producing a plantarflexion moment.\r\n\r\nThis action is essential for:\r\n\r\n- standing calf raises;\r\n- walking;\r\n- running;\r\n- sprinting;\r\n- jumping;\r\n- acceleration.\r\n\r\nHowever, plantarflexion torque is shared substantially with soleus.\r\n\r\nThe relative contribution of gastrocnemius depends strongly on knee angle.\r\n\r\n## Knee Flexion\r\n\r\nBecause gastrocnemius originates from the femur above the knee joint, it also contributes to **knee flexion**.\r\n\r\nIts knee-flexion role is much smaller than its plantarflexion role and generally smaller than that of the major hamstrings.\r\n\r\nNevertheless, it is mechanically real.\r\n\r\nDuring loaded knee flexion, gastrocnemius can therefore receive some demand even when the ankle does not move substantially.\r\n\r\nThis should generally be modeled as a secondary contribution rather than as a primary hypertrophy stimulus.\r\n\r\n## Biarticular Mechanics\r\n\r\nGastrocnemius spans two joints.\r\n\r\nIts force-generating state is therefore determined jointly by:\r\n\r\n- knee angle;\r\n- ankle angle.\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`L_gastrocnemius = f(knee_angle, ankle_angle)`\r\n\r\nThis is analogous to other biarticular muscles such as:\r\n\r\n- biceps femoris long head;\r\n- rectus femoris;\r\n- biceps brachii.\r\n\r\nA calf exercise cannot therefore be described adequately using ankle angle alone.\r\n\r\n## Knee Extension\r\n\r\nExtending the knee increases gastrocnemius muscle-tendon length proximally.\r\n\r\nThus straight-leg plantarflexion places the muscle at greater length than bent-knee plantarflexion at the same ankle angle.\r\n\r\nThis is one of the most practically important anatomical facts for calf training.\r\n\r\n## Knee Flexion\r\n\r\nFlexing the knee shortens gastrocnemius proximally.\r\n\r\nAt sufficiently high knee flexion, gastrocnemius becomes less favorable for producing plantarflexion force.\r\n\r\nThis changes force sharing toward `soleus`, whose length is not directly altered by the knee.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n> bent-knee calf training does not remove gastrocnemius from the movement, but it mechanically disadvantages it relative to soleus.\r\n\r\n## Active Insufficiency\r\n\r\nGastrocnemius can become particularly shortened when:\r\n\r\n- the knee is flexed;\r\n- the ankle is simultaneously plantarflexed.\r\n\r\nThis represents a configuration of **active insufficiency** for the biarticular muscle.\r\n\r\nIts ability to generate additional force can decrease as it approaches very short operating lengths.\r\n\r\nThis helps explain why gastrocnemius plantarflexion contribution falls with substantial knee flexion.\r\n\r\n## Passive Tension\r\n\r\nThe opposite configuration:\r\n\r\n- knee extended;\r\n- ankle dorsiflexed\r\n\r\nplaces gastrocnemius under large lengthening.\r\n\r\nThis can be felt clearly during a straight-knee calf stretch.\r\n\r\nFlexing the knee during the same stretch substantially reduces gastrocnemius tension and shifts the stretch toward the deeper soleus complex.\r\n\r\n## Locomotion\r\n\r\nDuring walking and running, gastrocnemius does not merely "push the foot down."\r\n\r\nIts biarticular anatomy allows it to transfer mechanical energy and influence both:\r\n\r\n- knee mechanics;\r\n- ankle mechanics.\r\n\r\nDuring late stance, the triceps surae generates substantial plantarflexion force while contributing to propulsion and control of the lower limb.\r\n\r\nThe Achilles tendon also stores and returns elastic energy, making the muscle-tendon system particularly important for efficient locomotion.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nGastrocnemius has unusually strong direct evidence supporting the importance of training at longer muscle lengths.\r\n\r\nTwo independent manipulations are particularly informative:\r\n\r\n1. changing **knee angle**;\r\n2. changing the portion of **ankle ROM** being trained.\r\n\r\n## Straight-Leg vs Bent-Knee Calf Raise\r\n\r\nKnee extension lengthens gastrocnemius because the muscle crosses the knee.\r\n\r\nKnee flexion shortens it.\r\n\r\nThis provides a powerful natural experiment.\r\n\r\nTraining studies directly comparing:\r\n\r\n- standing calf raises with extended knees;\r\n- seated calf raises with knees flexed approximately 90°;\r\n\r\nshow substantially greater hypertrophy of both medial and lateral gastrocnemius when training is performed with the knee extended.\r\n\r\nThis is one of the strongest pieces of muscle-specific evidence supporting a long-length advantage.\r\n\r\nImportantly, soleus does not show the same large difference because it does not cross the knee.\r\n\r\nTherefore the result strongly matches the anatomical prediction.\r\n\r\n## Lengthened Partial ROM\r\n\r\nCalf training research has also compared different ankle-ROM regions.\r\n\r\nTraining emphasizing the **initial / dorsiflexed portion** of plantarflexion — where gastrocnemius is relatively lengthened — has produced greater gastrocnemius hypertrophy than training emphasizing the shortened plantarflexed region.\r\n\r\nThis strengthens the case beyond merely comparing standing and seated exercises.\r\n\r\nThe two findings converge:\r\n\r\n### Knee Dimension\r\n\r\nExtended knee → longer gastrocnemius → greater hypertrophic stimulus.\r\n\r\n### Ankle Dimension\r\n\r\nGreater dorsiflexion → longer gastrocnemius → greater hypertrophic stimulus.\r\n\r\nTogether they provide unusually coherent evidence for a meaningful long-muscle-length effect.\r\n\r\n## Practical Long-Length Position\r\n\r\nFor gastrocnemius:\r\n\r\n> **knee extended + ankle dorsiflexed**\r\n\r\nis the most important trainable lengthened configuration.\r\n\r\nTherefore a high-quality standing calf raise should allow:\r\n\r\n- nearly straight knees;\r\n- substantial controlled dorsiflexion;\r\n- meaningful resistance in the bottom region.\r\n\r\nThis is not merely a theoretical preference.\r\n\r\nIt has direct hypertrophy evidence.\r\n\r\n## Bottom-Range Loading\r\n\r\nThe bottom portion of a calf raise deserves particular attention.\r\n\r\nThe goal is not merely to lower the heel as far as anatomically possible.\r\n\r\nThe goal is to create:\r\n\r\n- dorsiflexion;\r\n- high gastrocnemius length;\r\n- active plantarflexion force.\r\n\r\nA machine whose resistance remains substantial in dorsiflexion is therefore mechanically attractive.\r\n\r\n## Passive Stretch vs Active Loading\r\n\r\nPassive stretching alone is not equivalent to resistance training.\r\n\r\nThe relevant hypertrophic condition is long muscle length combined with sufficiently high mechanical tension.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n- standing on a calf block and passively hanging;\r\n- performing loaded plantarflexion from dorsiflexion\r\n\r\nshould not automatically be treated as equivalent stimuli.\r\n\r\nLong-length calf training should remain **active resistance training**.\r\n\r\n## Loaded Stretch\r\n\r\nThere is some evidence that adding loaded inter-set stretching to calf training can augment adaptations under certain conditions.\r\n\r\nHowever, this should not distract from the stronger practical intervention:\r\n\r\n> simply train plantarflexion hard while the gastrocnemius is operating at long lengths.\r\n\r\nThe primary programming variable remains exercise execution and ROM rather than special stretching techniques.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nUnlike many muscles in this database, a high `smh_factor` for gastrocnemius can be defended with comparatively direct muscle-specific evidence.\r\n\r\nThe evidence supports:\r\n\r\n- greater growth with extended versus flexed knee training;\r\n- favorable hypertrophic responses to dorsiflexion-biased long-length ROM;\r\n- anatomical mechanisms consistent with both observations.\r\n\r\nGastrocnemius should therefore be considered one of the stronger examples of **length-dependent hypertrophic responsiveness** in practical resistance training.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Standing Calf Raise\r\n\r\nStraight-leg calf raises are the primary direct exercise category for gastrocnemius hypertrophy.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- Smith machine standing calf raise;\r\n- standing calf raise machine;\r\n- leg-press calf raise with knees extended;\r\n- donkey calf raise;\r\n- unilateral standing calf raise.\r\n\r\nThe important common feature is:\r\n\r\n> substantial ankle plantarflexion torque while the knee remains approximately extended.\r\n\r\n## Knee Position\r\n\r\nThe knees do not need to be forcefully hyperextended.\r\n\r\nA natural near-extended position is sufficient.\r\n\r\nThe main objective is to avoid substantial knee flexion.\r\n\r\nAs knee flexion increases:\r\n\r\n- gastrocnemius shortens;\r\n- its plantarflexion contribution decreases;\r\n- force sharing shifts toward soleus.\r\n\r\nThus even modest technique changes can alter the exercise vector.\r\n\r\n## Smith Machine Standing Calf Raise\r\n\r\nA Smith machine can be excellent for gastrocnemius because it provides:\r\n\r\n- high stability;\r\n- simple progressive overload;\r\n- low balance demand;\r\n- easy use of a calf block;\r\n- straightforward training close to failure.\r\n\r\nA useful setup includes:\r\n\r\n- forefoot securely supported;\r\n- heel free to descend;\r\n- knees nearly extended;\r\n- stable hips and trunk.\r\n\r\nThe main movement should occur at the ankle.\r\n\r\n## Leg-Press Calf Raise\r\n\r\nA leg press can also strongly load gastrocnemius when the knees remain extended or only slightly flexed.\r\n\r\nAdvantages include:\r\n\r\n- high stability;\r\n- large external loads;\r\n- no spinal axial loading;\r\n- easy long-ROM plantarflexion.\r\n\r\nHowever, excessive knee flexion during the set progressively changes the exercise toward greater soleus bias.\r\n\r\n## Seated Calf Raise\r\n\r\nSeated calf raises still involve gastrocnemius to some degree.\r\n\r\nHowever, knee flexion substantially shortens it proximally.\r\n\r\nTherefore they should be modeled as predominantly `soleus` exercises rather than equivalent gastrocnemius exercises.\r\n\r\nFor overall calf development, seated calf raises can complement standing work.\r\n\r\nFor gastrocnemius specifically, they are inferior to straight-leg plantarflexion as the primary exercise.\r\n\r\n## Range of Motion\r\n\r\nGastrocnemius training benefits from particular attention to the lengthened half of the ROM.\r\n\r\nA strong default is:\r\n\r\n1. descend under control into meaningful dorsiflexion;\r\n2. avoid bouncing from the bottom;\r\n3. initiate plantarflexion actively;\r\n4. rise through the available ROM;\r\n5. return under control.\r\n\r\nThe bottom position should be loaded, not merely visited.\r\n\r\n## Stretch Reflex and Bouncing\r\n\r\nRapid bouncing at the bottom can store and return elastic energy through the Achilles tendon.\r\n\r\nThis may allow more external movement without proportional contractile work.\r\n\r\nFor hypertrophy-oriented calf raises, a controlled transition near dorsiflexion can reduce excessive reliance on rebound.\r\n\r\nA mandatory long pause is not required, but uncontrolled ballistic bouncing makes the muscular stimulus harder to standardize.\r\n\r\n## Top Position\r\n\r\nReaching substantial plantarflexion is useful for training through a broad ROM.\r\n\r\nHowever, available evidence gives particular importance to the **lengthened region** rather than suggesting that maximal shortened-position squeezing is uniquely hypertrophic.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n> sacrificing dorsiflexion and bottom-range loading merely to achieve an exaggerated peak contraction is usually a poor trade.\r\n\r\n## Foot Rotation\r\n\r\nTurning the toes inward or outward is sometimes proposed as a method of selectively targeting the medial or lateral gastrocnemius head.\r\n\r\nSome acute studies suggest foot position may alter regional activation to a degree.\r\n\r\nHowever, the effect is not equivalent to clean isolation.\r\n\r\nBoth heads remain active during plantarflexion.\r\n\r\nExtreme foot rotation is unnecessary.\r\n\r\nA natural foot position allowing:\r\n\r\n- stable pressure through the forefoot;\r\n- comfortable ankle motion;\r\n- high force production\r\n\r\nis generally preferable.\r\n\r\n## Unilateral Training\r\n\r\nSingle-leg calf raises can be useful because they:\r\n\r\n- increase relative resistance without very large external loads;\r\n- expose side-to-side differences;\r\n- reduce bilateral compensation.\r\n\r\nTheir main limitation is balance.\r\n\r\nExternal support should be used when balance otherwise becomes the limiting factor.\r\n\r\n## Achilles Tendon Contribution\r\n\r\nThe Achilles tendon is mechanically inseparable from calf exercise.\r\n\r\nDuring plantarflexion:\r\n\r\n- gastrocnemius fascicles generate force;\r\n- tendon stores and transmits elastic energy;\r\n- force reaches the calcaneus.\r\n\r\nFor hypertrophy programming, this means very fast repetitions can exploit elastic recoil.\r\n\r\nControlled repetitions make local muscular loading easier to standardize.\r\n\r\nFor athletic performance, faster stretch-shortening-cycle work serves a different and valuable purpose.\r\n\r\nThese objectives should not be conflated.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Straight-Leg Work Is Essential for Gastrocnemius Priority\r\n\r\nIf gastrocnemius hypertrophy is the goal, the program should contain direct plantarflexion with an approximately extended knee.\r\n\r\nA calf program consisting exclusively of seated calf raises is mechanically incomplete.\r\n\r\nThis is not merely an exercise-preference argument.\r\n\r\nDirect longitudinal evidence indicates substantially greater gastrocnemius growth from straight-leg than bent-knee calf-raise training.\r\n\r\n## Overall Calf Programming\r\n\r\nA mechanically complete calf program can pair:\r\n\r\n### Straight-Leg Calf Raise\r\n\r\nBias:\r\n\r\n- gastrocnemius;\r\n- soleus also contributes.\r\n\r\n### Bent-Knee Calf Raise\r\n\r\nBias:\r\n\r\n- soleus;\r\n- reduced gastrocnemius contribution.\r\n\r\nThis creates a useful division of labor based on anatomy rather than exercise folklore.\r\n\r\n## Weekly Volume\r\n\r\nA practical starting point for gastrocnemius hypertrophy can be approximately:\r\n\r\n- **4-8 direct hard straight-leg sets per week**.\r\n\r\nMore may be appropriate when calf hypertrophy is a major priority.\r\n\r\nVolume should be adjusted according to:\r\n\r\n- training status;\r\n- proximity to failure;\r\n- ROM;\r\n- frequency;\r\n- running/jumping workload;\r\n- Achilles tolerance.\r\n\r\nHigh-frequency locomotor use does not mean the muscle is immune to resistance-training fatigue.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nTwo to four weekly exposures can be practical.\r\n\r\nCalf exercises have relatively low systemic fatigue, which permits frequent training.\r\n\r\nHowever, local recovery can still be limited by:\r\n\r\n- gastrocnemius soreness;\r\n- Achilles tendon load;\r\n- plantar-foot discomfort;\r\n- concurrent running.\r\n\r\nFrequency should therefore follow local tissue recovery rather than assuming calves can be trained daily simply because they are used during walking.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\nGastrocnemius can hypertrophy under both relatively heavy and relatively light loading when sets are performed with sufficient effort.\r\n\r\nUseful practical ranges include:\r\n\r\n- 6-10 reps for heavy stable calf raises;\r\n- 8-15 reps as a general default;\r\n- 15-30+ reps for lighter work.\r\n\r\nThere is no requirement to perform very high repetitions because the calf is supposedly composed exclusively of endurance fibers.\r\n\r\nLoad selection should instead allow:\r\n\r\n- high effort;\r\n- controlled ROM;\r\n- stable execution.\r\n\r\n## Load\r\n\r\nHeavy and light-load calf training can both produce substantial triceps-surae hypertrophy when effort is appropriately high.\r\n\r\nThus load is a programming variable rather than a unique determinant of growth.\r\n\r\nStable machines can make both ends of the repetition spectrum effective.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nCalf raises are well suited to training close to local muscular failure.\r\n\r\nApproximately:\r\n\r\n- **0-2 RIR**\r\n\r\nis practical on stable machine variants.\r\n\r\nFailure should be defined by inability to continue producing meaningful plantarflexion through the intended ROM.\r\n\r\nA set should not continue indefinitely using:\r\n\r\n- progressively shorter partials;\r\n- knee flexion;\r\n- bouncing;\r\n- hip movement\r\n\r\nunless such partials are deliberately programmed.\r\n\r\n## Lengthened Partials\r\n\r\nLengthened partial repetitions are unusually defensible for gastrocnemius.\r\n\r\nIf additional partial repetitions are deliberately used, the dorsiflexed-to-mid-range region has a stronger hypertrophy rationale than performing only short-range pulses near peak plantarflexion.\r\n\r\nHowever, this does not make full ROM obsolete.\r\n\r\nA useful default remains:\r\n\r\n> full productive ROM with particular attention to maintaining tension in the lengthened region.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Order\r\n\r\nCalf work can generally be placed late in a lower-body session because it produces relatively little interference with major compounds.\r\n\r\nIf calf development is a major priority, placing it earlier can improve:\r\n\r\n- load;\r\n- effort;\r\n- execution quality.\r\n\r\nHowever, fatiguing plantarflexors before exercises requiring strong ankle stability may reduce performance.\r\n\r\n## Running and Plyometrics\r\n\r\nRunning, sprinting and jumping create substantial gastrocnemius and Achilles loading.\r\n\r\nThis should be considered when programming resistance volume.\r\n\r\nHowever, sport exposure and hypertrophy sets are not equivalent.\r\n\r\nHigh-speed locomotion emphasizes:\r\n\r\n- stretch-shortening-cycle performance;\r\n- tendon energy storage;\r\n- rate of force development.\r\n\r\nSlow controlled calf raises emphasize:\r\n\r\n- local muscular tension;\r\n- fatigue;\r\n- hypertrophy.\r\n\r\nBoth contribute load to the same tissues but through different mechanical contexts.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## Gastrocnemius Means "Belly of the Leg"\r\n\r\nThe name derives from Greek roots referring to the **belly of the leg**, reflecting its prominent superficial shape.\r\n\r\nThe two heads are responsible for much of the visible upper-calf contour.\r\n\r\n## It Is a Two-Joint Calf Muscle\r\n\r\nThe soleus is often hidden beneath gastrocnemius, but the functional difference between them is profound.\r\n\r\nGastrocnemius:\r\n\r\n- crosses knee + ankle.\r\n\r\nSoleus:\r\n\r\n- crosses ankle only.\r\n\r\nThis single anatomical distinction explains much of the difference between standing and seated calf training.\r\n\r\n## Bent Knees Do Not Merely "Bias Soleus"\r\n\r\nThe effect of knee flexion is not cosmetic.\r\n\r\nFlexing the knee physically shortens gastrocnemius and changes its force-generating state.\r\n\r\nExperimental measurements show reduced gastrocnemius activation and altered fascicle behavior at strongly flexed knee positions.\r\n\r\nThus the classic training distinction has a real anatomical and mechanical basis.\r\n\r\n## Straight-Leg vs Bent-Leg Training Has Direct Hypertrophy Evidence\r\n\r\nMany exercise-selection rules are inferred from EMG or anatomy.\r\n\r\nFor gastrocnemius, the standing-versus-seated distinction has been tested longitudinally.\r\n\r\nStraight-leg training produced substantially greater gastrocnemius hypertrophy than bent-knee training.\r\n\r\nThis makes it one of the cleaner examples where:\r\n\r\n> anatomical prediction → acute mechanics → longitudinal hypertrophy\r\n\r\nall point in the same direction.\r\n\r\n## Long-Length Partials Also Work Exceptionally Well\r\n\r\nTraining emphasizing the dorsiflexed portion of calf raises has produced greater gastrocnemius hypertrophy than training emphasizing the shortened portion.\r\n\r\nThis is unusually direct evidence supporting the practical relevance of long-muscle-length loading.\r\n\r\n## The Achilles Tendon Changes the Visible Relationship Between Joint Motion and Fascicle Motion\r\n\r\nDuring movement, ankle ROM is not identical to gastrocnemius fascicle shortening.\r\n\r\nPart of the muscle-tendon-unit displacement occurs through elastic deformation of the Achilles tendon and aponeuroses.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n> joint ROM ≠ muscle fascicle ROM.\r\n\r\nThis distinction becomes especially important during running and jumping, where substantial ankle motion can coexist with relatively economical fascicle behavior.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\n`gastrocnemius` should be represented as a **biarticular plantarflexor and secondary knee flexor**.\r\n\r\nThe primary demand dimension is:\r\n\r\n1. ankle plantarflexion torque.\r\n\r\nSecondary:\r\n\r\n2. knee-flexion torque.\r\n\r\nHowever, muscle-length and force-sharing modifiers must depend on **both joints**.\r\n\r\n## Length-Tension Model\r\n\r\nA simplified model should use:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_gastrocnemius = f(knee_angle, ankle_angle)`\r\n\r\nThe important states are:\r\n\r\n### Long\r\n\r\n- knee approximately extended;\r\n- ankle dorsiflexed.\r\n\r\n### Intermediate\r\n\r\n- knee extended with ankle near neutral;\r\nor\r\n- some knee flexion with dorsiflexion.\r\n\r\n### Short\r\n\r\n- knee flexed;\r\n- ankle plantarflexed.\r\n\r\nThis should be fundamentally different from:\r\n\r\n`soleus E_LT ≈ f(ankle_angle)`\r\n\r\nbecause soleus does not cross the knee.\r\n\r\n## Knee-Angle Modifier\r\n\r\nFor plantarflexion exercises:\r\n\r\n### Knee Extended\r\n\r\nHigh gastrocnemius contribution.\r\n\r\n### Moderate Knee Flexion\r\n\r\nReduced gastrocnemius contribution.\r\n\r\n### Deep Knee Flexion\r\n\r\nSubstantially reduced gastrocnemius contribution, with greater relative force sharing toward soleus.\r\n\r\nThis should be a continuous modifier, not a binary switch.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Allocation\r\n\r\n### Smith Machine Standing Calf Raise\r\n\r\nVery high `gastrocnemius` demand.\r\n\r\nAlso high `soleus` demand.\r\n\r\nThe extended knee makes this substantially more gastrocnemius-favorable than seated calf work.\r\n\r\n### Plate-Loaded Standing Calf Raise\r\n\r\nSame general mechanics.\r\n\r\nHigh gastrocnemius allocation if knees remain nearly extended and substantial dorsiflexion is used.\r\n\r\n### Leg-Press Calf Raise With Extended Knees\r\n\r\nHigh gastrocnemius demand.\r\n\r\nMachine stabilization can permit very large plantarflexion torque with low balance requirements.\r\n\r\n### Plate-Loaded Seated Calf Raise\r\n\r\nLow-to-moderate gastrocnemius allocation.\r\n\r\nVery high `soleus` allocation.\r\n\r\nThe gastrocnemius should not be assigned zero, but knee flexion should strongly suppress its share.\r\n\r\n### Seated Leg Curl\r\n\r\nNon-zero gastrocnemius demand through its knee-flexion function.\r\n\r\nHowever, hamstrings should dominate knee-flexion force allocation.\r\n\r\nGastrocnemius should be treated as a secondary synergist.\r\n\r\n### Squat\r\n\r\nGenerally low direct gastrocnemius hypertrophy allocation.\r\n\r\nThe muscle participates in lower-limb control but does not undergo a simple high-torque plantarflexion pattern equivalent to calf raises.\r\n\r\n### Running / Sprinting\r\n\r\nHigh functional gastrocnemius demand.\r\n\r\nNot directly comparable to conventional hypertrophy exercise because force, velocity and tendon behavior differ dramatically.\r\n\r\n## Long-Length Modifier\r\n\r\nFor this muscle, `E_LT` deserves unusually high weight.\r\n\r\nTwo separate dimensions support this:\r\n\r\n### Knee\r\n\r\nExtension increases gastrocnemius length.\r\n\r\n### Ankle\r\n\r\nDorsiflexion increases gastrocnemius length.\r\n\r\nConsequently, a straight-leg calf raise performed through substantial dorsiflexion should receive a strong lengthened-position modifier.\r\n\r\nA seated calf raise should not.\r\n\r\n## Resistance Profile\r\n\r\nCalf machines should ideally be evaluated by ankle torque rather than stack weight alone.\r\n\r\nA useful approximation is:\r\n\r\n`M_ankle = F_external × perpendicular moment arm`\r\n\r\nMachine leverage can differ substantially.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n- 120 kg Smith standing calf raise;\r\n- 120 kg plate-loaded calf machine;\r\n- 120 kg leg-press calf raise\r\n\r\nare not mechanically equivalent solely because external mass is identical.\r\n\r\n## Achilles-Tendon Contribution\r\n\r\nAgonez should distinguish muscle demand from tendon demand.\r\n\r\nHigh gastrocnemius force implies high Achilles-tendon loading, but tendon strain also depends on:\r\n\r\n- rate of loading;\r\n- ankle angle;\r\n- contraction velocity;\r\n- elastic recoil;\r\n- exercise technique.\r\n\r\nSlow calf raises and plyometric contacts can therefore have similar peak force but very different connective-tissue loading profiles.\r\n\r\n## Implication for Your Existing `smh_factor`\r\n\r\nFor gastrocnemius, a relatively high `smh_factor` is much easier to defend empirically than for muscles such as:\r\n\r\n- lateral deltoid;\r\n- SCM;\r\n- erector spinae.\r\n\r\nDirect evidence supports both:\r\n\r\n- greater gastrocnemius growth with extended-knee versus flexed-knee training;\r\n- greater growth from ROM emphasizing longer muscle lengths.\r\n\r\nThis should therefore be considered a **high-confidence SMH case**.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **two-headed femoral origin:** high;\r\n- **Achilles/calcaneal insertion:** high;\r\n- **tibial-nerve innervation:** high;\r\n- **plantarflexion function:** high;\r\n- **secondary knee-flexion function:** high;\r\n- **knee-angle effect on gastrocnemius mechanics:** high and directly demonstrated;\r\n- **greater hypertrophy from straight-leg versus bent-knee calf raises:** direct longitudinal evidence;\r\n- **greater hypertrophy from long-length-biased partial ROM:** direct longitudinal evidence;\r\n- **broad-load-range hypertrophy:** supported experimentally;\r\n- **exact medial-vs-lateral-head recruitment under individual foot positions:** lower confidence;\r\n- **precise exercise-specific recruitment coefficients:** dependent on knee angle, ankle angle and machine geometry.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **gastrocnemius = a pennate biarticular plantarflexor and secondary knee flexor whose force capacity and hypertrophic stimulus depend strongly on the interaction of knee extension and ankle dorsiflexion, making straight-leg long-length plantarflexion its defining resistance-training condition.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/Gastrocnemius}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	5.35	10.62	0.983	186.74	183.54
6	deltoid_posterior	Musculus Deltoid posterior	Upper	Shoulder	375.00	355.11	8.00	Fusiform/Parallel-like	0.650	0.350	high	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\nThe **posterior deltoid** is the scapular portion of the deltoid muscle and forms the posterior component of the shoulder cap.\r\n\r\nIts primary mechanical roles are:\r\n\r\n- **shoulder extension**;\r\n- **horizontal abduction / horizontal extension**;\r\n- contribution to **external rotation**;\r\n- stabilization of the humeral head during pulling and arm-elevation tasks.\r\n\r\nIt should be distinguished clearly from:\r\n\r\n- `deltoid_anterior`;\r\n- `deltoid_lateral`.\r\n\r\nAlthough all three regions insert onto the deltoid tuberosity, their origins and fiber orientations give them substantially different moment arms and preferred movement planes.\r\n\r\nFor training purposes, the posterior deltoid is important because it receives meaningful stimulus from many rows and rear-delt isolation exercises, but the degree of recruitment depends strongly on:\r\n\r\n- humeral elevation angle;\r\n- elbow path;\r\n- shoulder extension versus scapular retraction;\r\n- resistance direction.\r\n\r\nA row is therefore not automatically a strong posterior-deltoid exercise.\r\n\r\nThe posterior deltoid is best understood as a **glenohumeral extensor and horizontal abductor**, not as a scapular retractor.\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\n## Origin\r\n\r\nThe posterior deltoid originates primarily from the **inferior border of the spine of the scapula**.\r\n\r\nIts origin is posterior to that of the acromial/lateral deltoid.\r\n\r\nThe fibers run laterally and inferiorly toward the humerus.\r\n\r\nThis orientation gives the posterior region favorable leverage for moving the humerus posteriorly relative to the torso.\r\n\r\n## Insertion\r\n\r\nPosterior deltoid fibers converge with the rest of the deltoid onto the **deltoid tuberosity of the humerus**.\r\n\r\nThe common distal insertion means that different deltoid regions generate different moments around the glenohumeral joint because they approach the humerus from different directions.\r\n\r\n## Architecture\r\n\r\nThe posterior deltoid is not a perfectly homogeneous bundle.\r\n\r\nLike the rest of the deltoid, it contains multiple intramuscular segments with somewhat different:\r\n\r\n- fiber orientations;\r\n- moment arms;\r\n- recruitment patterns.\r\n\r\nSome posterior fibers are more effective at extension, while others contribute more to horizontal abduction or rotation depending on humeral position.\r\n\r\nFor exercise-level modelling, however, a single `deltoid_posterior` aggregate is appropriate.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nThe posterior deltoid is innervated by the **axillary nerve**, predominantly from spinal roots **C5-C6**.\r\n\r\nMotor branches to the posterior deltoid are regionally distributed.\r\n\r\nThe axillary nerve also supplies teres minor, which is relevant because both muscles can contribute to posterior shoulder function but have different primary roles.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Shoulder Extension\r\n\r\nThe posterior deltoid contributes to **extension of the humerus at the glenohumeral joint**.\r\n\r\nThis is especially relevant when the arm begins in flexion or elevation and moves posteriorly.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- rearward arm movement;\r\n- some rowing patterns;\r\n- reverse fly variants;\r\n- pull-through motion of the humerus during certain pulling exercises.\r\n\r\nIts extension contribution overlaps with:\r\n\r\n- latissimus dorsi;\r\n- teres major;\r\n- long head of triceps.\r\n\r\nThe relative contribution depends heavily on humeral elevation.\r\n\r\n## Horizontal Abduction\r\n\r\nOne of the posterior deltoid's defining functions is **horizontal abduction**, sometimes described as horizontal extension.\r\n\r\nThis occurs when the humerus moves posteriorly in the transverse plane.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- reverse pec deck;\r\n- reverse cable fly;\r\n- high-elbow row;\r\n- face-pull-like movements when substantial humeral horizontal abduction occurs.\r\n\r\nThis function distinguishes posterior deltoid from latissimus dorsi.\r\n\r\nA row performed with the elbows tucked close to the torso may produce large shoulder-extension torque but comparatively less posterior-deltoid horizontal-abduction demand.\r\n\r\nA row performed with the elbows elevated and moving outward can shift considerably more demand toward posterior deltoid.\r\n\r\n## External Rotation\r\n\r\nPosterior deltoid can contribute to **external rotation of the humerus**.\r\n\r\nThis role is secondary to its extension and horizontal-abduction functions.\r\n\r\nExternal rotation is also generated strongly by:\r\n\r\n- infraspinatus;\r\n- teres minor.\r\n\r\nTherefore posterior-deltoid allocation during external-rotation exercises should usually remain smaller than rotator-cuff allocation unless the exercise simultaneously includes substantial horizontal abduction.\r\n\r\n## Abduction\r\n\r\nSome posterior-deltoid fibers can contribute to humeral abduction depending on arm position.\r\n\r\nThe deltoid regions do not act as perfectly isolated sagittal, frontal and transverse-plane motors.\r\n\r\nTheir actions vary continuously with joint angle.\r\n\r\nHowever, lateral deltoid remains the principal deltoid region for pure abduction.\r\n\r\n## Scapular Retraction: Important Distinction\r\n\r\nPosterior deltoid does **not** retract the scapula directly.\r\n\r\nIt originates from the scapula and inserts on the humerus.\r\n\r\nTherefore it acts at the **glenohumeral joint**, not directly at the scapulothoracic articulation.\r\n\r\nScapular retraction is produced primarily by:\r\n\r\n- middle trapezius;\r\n- rhomboids.\r\n\r\nThis distinction matters greatly in rowing exercises.\r\n\r\nA row can contain both:\r\n\r\n- glenohumeral horizontal extension;\r\n- scapular retraction.\r\n\r\nPosterior deltoid contributes to the former, not the latter.\r\n\r\n## Glenohumeral Stabilization\r\n\r\nThe posterior deltoid contributes to dynamic shoulder stability during loaded pulling.\r\n\r\nIts humeral force must be balanced by:\r\n\r\n- rotator cuff;\r\n- scapular stabilizers;\r\n- other shoulder musculature.\r\n\r\nThus high posterior-deltoid demand should usually coexist with non-zero cuff and scapular-stabilizer demand.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nDirect evidence specifically examining **stretch-mediated hypertrophy of the posterior deltoid** is limited.\r\n\r\nMechanically, the posterior deltoid is lengthened when the humerus moves anteriorly relative to the torso.\r\n\r\nRelevant positions include:\r\n\r\n- shoulder flexion;\r\n- horizontal adduction;\r\n- combinations of flexion and internal positioning depending on fiber region.\r\n\r\nThis means the posterior deltoid can potentially be loaded at long muscle lengths during exercises that begin with the arm in front of the torso.\r\n\r\n## Lengthened Position\r\n\r\nA reverse fly or rear-delt row can place the posterior deltoid at relatively long length when the humerus begins in:\r\n\r\n- flexion;\r\n- horizontal adduction.\r\n\r\nHowever, the hypertrophic value of this position depends on whether meaningful external torque exists there.\r\n\r\nA free-weight rear-delt raise may provide relatively little torque at certain starting positions depending on torso orientation.\r\n\r\nCable and machine geometry can maintain resistance more effectively through the lengthened region.\r\n\r\n## Reverse Pec Deck\r\n\r\nA reverse pec deck can provide a useful long-length stimulus if:\r\n\r\n- the handles begin sufficiently forward;\r\n- resistance is meaningful near the start;\r\n- the torso remains fixed;\r\n- the movement occurs through the shoulder rather than being dominated by scapular retraction.\r\n\r\nThis is one of the most mechanically straightforward rear-delt hypertrophy exercises.\r\n\r\n## Cable Rear-Delt Fly\r\n\r\nCable geometry can create high tension while the arm begins across or in front of the torso.\r\n\r\nThis allows meaningful loading at relatively long posterior-deltoid lengths.\r\n\r\nThe exact profile depends on:\r\n\r\n- cable height;\r\n- body position;\r\n- arm path;\r\n- degree of horizontal adduction at the start.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Interpretation\r\n\r\nThe current evidence base supports:\r\n\r\n- posterior deltoid hypertrophy from resistance training;\r\n- regional differences in deltoid recruitment;\r\n- strong exercise-specific recruitment differences.\r\n\r\nHowever, there is insufficient direct evidence to claim a precisely quantified posterior-deltoid-specific stretch-mediated hypertrophy advantage.\r\n\r\nTherefore the conservative model is:\r\n\r\n- anterior humeral positioning lengthens the posterior deltoid;\r\n- meaningful torque in that state is mechanically plausible and likely useful;\r\n- direct long-length hypertrophy evidence remains limited.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Reverse Pec Deck\r\n\r\nThe reverse pec deck is one of the most stable and specific posterior-deltoid exercises.\r\n\r\nAdvantages include:\r\n\r\n- low balance demand;\r\n- fixed torso;\r\n- easy progressive overload;\r\n- strong horizontal-abduction demand;\r\n- low grip limitation;\r\n- easy proximity to failure.\r\n\r\nThe key technical goal is to move the **humerus**, not merely squeeze the shoulder blades together.\r\n\r\nIf the scapulae retract excessively while humeral motion remains small, more of the load shifts toward:\r\n\r\n- middle trapezius;\r\n- rhomboids.\r\n\r\nSome scapular motion is normal, but posterior-deltoid hypertrophy depends on substantial glenohumeral motion.\r\n\r\n## Reverse Cable Fly\r\n\r\nCable reverse fly variants are excellent because resistance can be maintained throughout much of the ROM.\r\n\r\nUseful setup characteristics include:\r\n\r\n- cables crossing in front of the torso;\r\n- slight humeral flexion at the start;\r\n- controlled horizontal abduction;\r\n- minimal trunk rotation.\r\n\r\nThe exercise can be performed:\r\n\r\n- bilaterally;\r\n- unilaterally;\r\n- standing;\r\n- chest-supported.\r\n\r\nChest support improves standardization by reducing trunk compensation.\r\n\r\n## Rear-Delt Row\r\n\r\nA row can be biased toward posterior deltoid when the humerus is more abducted.\r\n\r\nTypical characteristics include:\r\n\r\n- elbows elevated away from the torso;\r\n- upper arm travelling laterally/posteriorly;\r\n- less emphasis on pure shoulder extension with elbows tucked;\r\n- relatively high transverse-plane shoulder demand.\r\n\r\nThis contrasts with a lat-biased row, where:\r\n\r\n- elbows remain closer to the torso;\r\n- humeral extension dominates;\r\n- latissimus contribution increases.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n> elbow path is a major determinant of posterior-deltoid recruitment.\r\n\r\n## Wide-Grip Row\r\n\r\nA wide-grip row often increases humeral abduction and horizontal-extension demand.\r\n\r\nThis can increase posterior-deltoid involvement.\r\n\r\nHowever, it also increases:\r\n\r\n- middle trapezius;\r\n- rhomboid;\r\n- scapular-retraction demand.\r\n\r\nTherefore wide rows are effective but less isolated than reverse-fly variations.\r\n\r\n## Face Pull\r\n\r\nFace pulls can provide meaningful posterior-deltoid stimulus when they include:\r\n\r\n- humeral horizontal abduction;\r\n- external rotation.\r\n\r\nHowever, they are highly multifunctional.\r\n\r\nThey can also strongly involve:\r\n\r\n- posterior rotator cuff;\r\n- middle/lower trapezius;\r\n- rhomboids;\r\n- elbow flexors.\r\n\r\nTherefore face pull should not be treated as a pure posterior-deltoid isolation exercise.\r\n\r\nIts recruitment vector should be relatively distributed.\r\n\r\n## Chest-Supported Row\r\n\r\nChest support reduces:\r\n\r\n- spinal erector demand;\r\n- hip-extension stabilization;\r\n- cheating.\r\n\r\nThis can make posterior-shoulder recruitment easier to standardize.\r\n\r\nWhether the posterior deltoid is highly involved still depends on elbow path.\r\n\r\nA chest-supported row with tucked elbows can remain lat-dominant.\r\n\r\n## Pull-Ups and Pulldowns\r\n\r\nVertical pulling typically provides relatively little posterior-deltoid stimulus compared with rows and reverse-fly patterns.\r\n\r\nThe dominant glenohumeral actions are more strongly:\r\n\r\n- adduction;\r\n- extension.\r\n\r\nPosterior deltoid may contribute, but usually not enough to consider vertical pulling a primary hypertrophy exercise for this muscle.\r\n\r\n## Shoulder Extension Exercises\r\n\r\nMovements involving pure humeral extension from flexion can recruit posterior deltoid.\r\n\r\nHowever, latissimus dorsi and teres major often have strong leverage in these tasks.\r\n\r\nTo bias posterior deltoid, more humeral abduction / transverse-plane movement is generally useful.\r\n\r\n## Range of Motion\r\n\r\nA productive rear-delt ROM typically includes:\r\n\r\n- meaningful anterior starting position;\r\n- controlled posterior movement;\r\n- avoidance of excessive end-range scapular squeezing as the main source of motion.\r\n\r\nThe exact endpoint does not need to involve maximal posterior excursion.\r\n\r\nOnce the humerus has moved through productive horizontal abduction, forcing additional range through scapular retraction adds little posterior-deltoid stimulus.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Direct Training Is Usually Valuable\r\n\r\nPosterior deltoid receives some indirect volume from rows.\r\n\r\nHowever, many back exercises distribute tension across larger muscles and may not provide enough posterior-deltoid-specific stimulus.\r\n\r\nTherefore direct work often has high marginal value.\r\n\r\nUseful direct exercises include:\r\n\r\n- reverse pec deck;\r\n- cable reverse fly;\r\n- rear-delt row.\r\n\r\n## Weekly Volume\r\n\r\nA practical starting point for a trained lifter can be approximately:\r\n\r\n- **4-10 direct hard sets per week**\r\n\r\nwhen posterior deltoid development is a meaningful priority.\r\n\r\nExisting rowing volume should be counted partially.\r\n\r\nA program with extensive high-elbow rowing may require less isolation work than one dominated by:\r\n\r\n- pulldowns;\r\n- pull-ups;\r\n- tucked-elbow rows.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nTwo to three weekly exposures are practical.\r\n\r\nPosterior-deltoid isolation has relatively low systemic fatigue.\r\n\r\nFrequency is usually constrained more by:\r\n\r\n- local shoulder fatigue;\r\n- rotator-cuff fatigue;\r\n- overlap with back sessions.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\nPosterior-deltoid isolation works particularly well in moderate-to-high repetition ranges.\r\n\r\nUseful ranges include:\r\n\r\n- 8-15 reps for heavier machine/cable work;\r\n- 12-20 reps as a general default;\r\n- 15-30 reps for lighter isolation work.\r\n\r\nHeavy low-repetition rear-delt work often becomes limited by:\r\n\r\n- scapular retraction;\r\n- upper-back momentum;\r\n- shortened ROM.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nStable rear-delt isolation can usually be trained close to failure.\r\n\r\nApproximately:\r\n\r\n- **0-2 RIR**\r\n\r\nis practical.\r\n\r\nFailure should be defined by inability to continue meaningful humeral horizontal abduction without excessive:\r\n\r\n- torso rotation;\r\n- scapular substitution;\r\n- momentum.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Diversity\r\n\r\nMechanical diversity is useful.\r\n\r\nA simple combination could include:\r\n\r\n1. reverse pec deck or cable fly;\r\n2. high-elbow chest-supported row.\r\n\r\nThis combines:\r\n\r\n- highly specific horizontal abduction;\r\n- heavier compound loading.\r\n\r\nThere is little need to perform many nearly identical rear-delt fly variants.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Back Training\r\n\r\nPosterior deltoid often accumulates hidden volume from rows.\r\n\r\nThis means total weekly exposure should account for:\r\n\r\n- high-elbow rows;\r\n- wide-grip rows;\r\n- face pulls;\r\n- reverse flys.\r\n\r\nBy contrast, lat-focused pulldowns and tucked-elbow rows should receive much smaller posterior-deltoid credit.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Order\r\n\r\nIf posterior deltoid is a priority, direct work can be placed:\r\n\r\n- early in a pull session;\r\n- after primary back compounds;\r\n- on push/shoulder days to distribute fatigue.\r\n\r\nThere is no requirement that rear delts must be trained only on pull days.\r\n\r\nThey can fit wherever total shoulder recovery is best managed.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## Posterior Deltoid Is Not a Scapular Retractor\r\n\r\nThis is one of the most common functional confusions in resistance training.\r\n\r\nPosterior deltoid moves the **humerus**.\r\n\r\nMiddle trapezius and rhomboids move the **scapula**.\r\n\r\nDuring rows, both motions occur together, which makes them easy to conflate.\r\n\r\n## Elbow Path Changes the Exercise\r\n\r\nTwo rows using the same machine and same load can produce very different posterior-deltoid demand.\r\n\r\nA tucked-elbow row emphasizes:\r\n\r\n- shoulder extension;\r\n- latissimus;\r\n- teres major.\r\n\r\nA high-elbow row increases:\r\n\r\n- horizontal abduction;\r\n- posterior-deltoid demand.\r\n\r\nThus exercise classification should be geometry-based, not name-based.\r\n\r\n## The Posterior Deltoid Can Externally Rotate\r\n\r\nAlthough usually taught as an extensor/horizontal abductor, posterior deltoid also contributes to external rotation depending on humeral angle.\r\n\r\nThis is why some rear-delt exercises overlap mechanically with rotator-cuff exercises.\r\n\r\n## Rows Are Not Automatically Rear-Delt Exercises\r\n\r\nA row can contain large total pulling force while producing only moderate posterior-deltoid stimulus.\r\n\r\nThe decisive variable is how much of the shoulder motion is:\r\n\r\n- horizontal abduction;\r\nversus\r\n- extension.\r\n\r\nThis is particularly important in exercise-vector modelling.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\n`deltoid_posterior` should primarily receive demand from:\r\n\r\n1. **horizontal abduction / horizontal extension**;\r\n2. **shoulder extension**;\r\n3. secondary external rotation;\r\n4. glenohumeral stabilization.\r\n\r\nIt should receive little or no direct demand from pure scapular retraction.\r\n\r\n## Row Geometry\r\n\r\nA useful conceptual continuum is:\r\n\r\n### Elbows Tucked\r\n\r\nHigh:\r\n\r\n- `latissimus_dorsi`;\r\n- `teres_major`;\r\n- elbow flexors.\r\n\r\nLower:\r\n\r\n- `deltoid_posterior`.\r\n\r\n### Elbows Moderately Abducted\r\n\r\nMore balanced:\r\n\r\n- posterior deltoid;\r\n- lats;\r\n- scapular retractors.\r\n\r\n### Elbows High / Wide\r\n\r\nHigher:\r\n\r\n- `deltoid_posterior`;\r\n- `trapezius_middle`;\r\n- `rhomboids`.\r\n\r\nLower relative lat contribution.\r\n\r\nThis should be modeled continuously rather than as three discrete categories.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Allocation\r\n\r\n### Reverse Pec Deck\r\n\r\nVery high `deltoid_posterior` allocation.\r\n\r\nAlso meaningful:\r\n\r\n- middle trapezius;\r\n- rhomboids;\r\n- rotator cuff.\r\n\r\n### Cable Face Pull\r\n\r\nModerate-to-high posterior-deltoid allocation.\r\n\r\nAlso substantial:\r\n\r\n- external rotators;\r\n- middle/lower trapezius;\r\n- rhomboids;\r\n- elbow flexors.\r\n\r\n### Wide-Grip Seated Cable Row\r\n\r\nMeaningful posterior-deltoid demand if elbows travel laterally.\r\n\r\nScapular retractors should also receive high allocation.\r\n\r\n### Chest-Supported Dumbbell Row\r\n\r\nPosterior-deltoid allocation depends strongly on elbow path.\r\n\r\nTucked elbows:\r\n- relatively low.\r\n\r\nHigh elbows:\r\n- substantially higher.\r\n\r\n### Neutral-Grip Pulldown\r\n\r\nLow posterior-deltoid allocation.\r\n\r\n### Weighted Pull-Up\r\n\r\nLow posterior-deltoid allocation relative to lats and elbow flexors.\r\n\r\n## Length-Tension Model\r\n\r\nA simplified posterior-deltoid length model should depend primarily on humeral position:\r\n\r\n- shoulder flexion / horizontal adduction → relatively long;\r\n- neutral → intermediate;\r\n- extension / horizontal abduction → shorter.\r\n\r\nA more complete model would include:\r\n\r\n- flexion-extension angle;\r\n- horizontal abduction-adduction;\r\n- humeral rotation.\r\n\r\nFor most exercise-level Agonez calculations, the first two dimensions are the most important.\r\n\r\n## Scapular Motion Should Be Separated\r\n\r\nFor rowing exercises, total movement can be decomposed conceptually into:\r\n\r\n`row_motion = glenohumeral_motion + scapular_motion`\r\n\r\nPosterior deltoid should receive demand only from the glenohumeral component.\r\n\r\nMiddle trapezius and rhomboids should receive demand primarily from scapular retraction.\r\n\r\nThis separation is critical for avoiding inflated posterior-deltoid vectors.\r\n\r\n## Rotator-Cuff Demand\r\n\r\nRear-delt exercises require glenohumeral stabilization.\r\n\r\nFace pulls and externally rotated rear-delt variations increase cuff demand further.\r\n\r\nTherefore a high posterior-deltoid vector with zero cuff contribution would often be mechanically suspicious.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nThe evidence-based position should remain conservative:\r\n\r\n- posterior deltoid is lengthened in humeral flexion/horizontal adduction;\r\n- cables and machines can maintain tension in this region;\r\n- direct posterior-deltoid-specific long-length hypertrophy data are limited;\r\n- exercise geometry remains more certain than any precise SMH multiplier.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **scapular-spine origin and humeral insertion:** high;\r\n- **axillary-nerve innervation:** high;\r\n- **shoulder-extension role:** high;\r\n- **horizontal-abduction role:** high;\r\n- **external-rotation contribution:** high;\r\n- **high recruitment during reverse-fly and high-elbow rowing patterns:** well supported;\r\n- **row-geometry dependence:** high;\r\n- **posterior-deltoid-specific stretch-mediated hypertrophy:** limited direct evidence;\r\n- **precise exercise-specific recruitment coefficients:** strongly geometry-dependent.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **posterior deltoid = the scapular deltoid region specialized for humeral extension and horizontal abduction, with recruitment determined strongly by elbow path and glenohumeral motion rather than by scapular retraction itself.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/DeltoidPosterior}	{https://youtu.be/dKluhLck1Zs?si=bF_p0OmsqaYav8pp}	Bilateral	13.70	18.00	0.951	25.92	24.65
37	biceps_femoris_long_head	Musculus biceps femoris caput longum	Lower	Hamstrings	565.00	535.04	35.00	Fusiform/Unipennate	0.400	0.600	extreme_high	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\nThe **biceps femoris long head** is the lateral component of the hamstring group and one of the major biarticular muscles of the posterior thigh.\r\n\r\nIt should be distinguished explicitly from the **biceps femoris short head**.\r\n\r\nThe long head crosses:\r\n\r\n- the hip joint;\r\n- the knee joint.\r\n\r\nThe short head crosses only the knee.\r\n\r\nThis distinction gives the long head two major mechanical roles:\r\n\r\n1. **hip extension**;\r\n2. **knee flexion**.\r\n\r\nIt also contributes to **external rotation of the tibia** when the knee is flexed and can contribute to external rotation of the thigh at the hip.\r\n\r\nBecause it crosses both hip and knee, its muscle length is strongly dependent on the simultaneous position of both joints.\r\n\r\nThe long head is relatively lengthened when:\r\n\r\n- the hip is flexed;\r\n- the knee is extended.\r\n\r\nIt is relatively shortened when:\r\n\r\n- the hip is extended;\r\n- the knee is flexed.\r\n\r\nThis biarticular geometry is one of the most important features for understanding both its training and its injury mechanics.\r\n\r\nFor hypertrophy programming, the main implication is:\r\n\r\n> exercises that combine substantial hip flexion with meaningful knee-extension angles can expose the biceps femoris long head to particularly long operating lengths.\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\n## Origin\r\n\r\nThe long head originates primarily from the **ischial tuberosity**.\r\n\r\nIts proximal attachment is closely associated with the origins of the other hamstrings, particularly semitendinosus.\r\n\r\nThe proximal hamstring region contains shared and closely interconnected aponeurotic structures rather than completely independent muscle origins.\r\n\r\nThis is clinically relevant because high-force eccentric injuries can occur near the proximal musculotendinous or aponeurotic regions.\r\n\r\n## Insertion\r\n\r\nThe biceps femoris long head descends along the posterolateral thigh and converges with the short head distally.\r\n\r\nThe common distal tendon inserts primarily onto the **head of the fibula**.\r\n\r\nAdditional expansions can attach to surrounding structures of the lateral knee.\r\n\r\nBecause the insertion is lateral to the knee axis, the muscle is mechanically positioned not only to flex the knee but also to produce **external rotation of the tibia** when the knee is flexed.\r\n\r\nUnlike semimembranosus and semitendinosus, which insert medially, biceps femoris forms the **lateral hamstring**.\r\n\r\n## Architecture\r\n\r\nBiceps femoris long head has a pennate architecture with long fascicles arranged relative to an extensive internal aponeurotic system.\r\n\r\nIts architecture is not uniform along the entire muscle.\r\n\r\nRegional differences exist in:\r\n\r\n- fascicle length;\r\n- pennation;\r\n- aponeurosis geometry;\r\n- strain distribution.\r\n\r\nThe distal and proximal regions therefore do not necessarily experience identical mechanical strain during exercise.\r\n\r\nThis regional heterogeneity is important when interpreting both hypertrophy and injury risk.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nThe long head is innervated by the **tibial division of the sciatic nerve**, predominantly from spinal roots **L5-S2**.\r\n\r\nThis differs from the biceps femoris short head, which is typically innervated by the **common fibular division of the sciatic nerve**.\r\n\r\nThe two heads therefore share a distal tendon region but differ in both proximal anatomy and neural supply.\r\n\r\nThis reinforces the fact that "biceps femoris" is not functionally a single homogeneous muscle.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Knee Flexion\r\n\r\nThe long head is a strong **knee flexor**.\r\n\r\nDuring open-chain knee flexion, it acts together with:\r\n\r\n- biceps femoris short head;\r\n- semitendinosus;\r\n- semimembranosus;\r\n- gastrocnemius to a lesser extent.\r\n\r\nIts relative contribution changes with:\r\n\r\n- hip angle;\r\n- knee angle;\r\n- contraction velocity;\r\n- forefoot/tibial rotation;\r\n- external resistance profile.\r\n\r\nBecause the long head crosses the hip, its force-generating condition during knee flexion is strongly influenced by hip position.\r\n\r\nA seated leg curl therefore does not place it in the same mechanical state as a prone leg curl even if the knee ROM is identical.\r\n\r\n## Hip Extension\r\n\r\nThe long head also produces **hip extension**.\r\n\r\nThis function is shared with:\r\n\r\n- semitendinosus;\r\n- semimembranosus;\r\n- gluteus maximus;\r\n- adductor magnus.\r\n\r\nIt contributes particularly during hip-dominant movements such as:\r\n\r\n- Romanian deadlifts;\r\n- stiff-leg deadlifts;\r\n- good mornings;\r\n- hip hinges;\r\n- sprinting.\r\n\r\nUnlike biceps femoris short head, the long head can receive substantial training stimulus from exercises involving hip extension even when the knee does not actively flex.\r\n\r\n## External Rotation of the Tibia\r\n\r\nBecause the distal tendon inserts laterally on the fibula, contraction of biceps femoris can produce **external rotation of the tibia** relative to the femur when the knee is flexed.\r\n\r\nThis contrasts with the medial hamstrings:\r\n\r\n- semitendinosus;\r\n- semimembranosus,\r\n\r\nwhich contribute to internal rotation.\r\n\r\nThis rotational role becomes mechanically more meaningful as the knee flexes and the tibia gains greater rotational freedom.\r\n\r\n## External Rotation at the Hip\r\n\r\nThe long head can also contribute modestly to external rotation of the femur at the hip.\r\n\r\nThis is secondary to its extension role.\r\n\r\nIt should generally be treated as a minor positional contribution rather than a primary programming target.\r\n\r\n## Biarticular Coordination\r\n\r\nBecause the muscle spans hip and knee, simultaneous joint movement can either preserve or strongly alter its length.\r\n\r\nDuring sprinting, for example, hip flexion and knee extension can occur together during terminal swing.\r\n\r\nThis combination places the biceps femoris long head under substantial length and high eccentric demand.\r\n\r\nLater in the gait cycle, hip extension and knee flexion can shorten it simultaneously.\r\n\r\nThe ability to transfer force across two joints is a defining feature of hamstring function.\r\n\r\n## Active and Passive Insufficiency\r\n\r\nWhen the hip is extended and knee flexed simultaneously, the long head becomes very short.\r\n\r\nIts ability to generate force can therefore decrease through **active insufficiency**.\r\n\r\nConversely, simultaneous hip flexion and knee extension places the muscle under considerable passive tension.\r\n\r\nThis is why toe-touch-style hip flexion with straight knees produces a strong hamstring stretch.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nThe biceps femoris long head has one of the clearest biomechanical rationales for long-muscle-length training.\r\n\r\nBecause it crosses both the hip and knee, substantial lengthening can be created through:\r\n\r\n- hip flexion;\r\n- knee extension;\r\n- or both simultaneously.\r\n\r\nThis makes exercise configuration particularly important.\r\n\r\n## Seated vs Prone Leg Curl\r\n\r\nA seated leg curl places the hip in substantial flexion.\r\n\r\nA prone leg curl places the hip much closer to neutral or slight extension.\r\n\r\nAt the same knee angle, the seated position therefore places the biarticular hamstrings — including biceps femoris long head — at a longer overall muscle length.\r\n\r\nDirect training evidence supports the practical importance of this difference.\r\n\r\nMaeo et al. found greater hamstring hypertrophy after **seated leg curl training** than after prone leg curl training, consistent with the hypothesis that training at longer muscle lengths can improve hypertrophic stimulus. citeturn269737search1\r\n\r\nThe important detail is that the effect is especially relevant to the **biarticular hamstrings**.\r\n\r\nBiceps femoris short head does not cross the hip and therefore does not receive the same proximal lengthening from hip flexion.\r\n\r\n## Hip-Hinge Exercises\r\n\r\nRomanian deadlifts and similar hip hinges lengthen the long head through hip flexion while maintaining relatively extended knees.\r\n\r\nThis combination creates:\r\n\r\n- substantial muscle length;\r\n- high hip-extension torque;\r\n- strong eccentric loading.\r\n\r\nThus, an RDL represents a fundamentally different long-length stimulus from a leg curl.\r\n\r\nThe RDL loads the muscle primarily through its **hip-extension function**.\r\n\r\nThe seated leg curl loads it primarily through **knee flexion** while maintaining substantial proximal length.\r\n\r\nUsing both can therefore expose the muscle to high tension through different joint actions.\r\n\r\n## Regional Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nHamstring hypertrophy is exercise-specific.\r\n\r\nHip-extension and knee-flexion exercises do not necessarily produce uniform growth across:\r\n\r\n- biceps femoris long head;\r\n- semitendinosus;\r\n- semimembranosus;\r\n- different regions within each muscle.\r\n\r\nRecent training literature emphasizes that hamstring hypertrophy varies considerably according to exercise selection rather than every hamstring exercise enlarging all muscles equally. citeturn269737search5\r\n\r\nThis is particularly important for biceps femoris long head because some exercises that produce high global hamstring effort preferentially stimulate other hamstrings.\r\n\r\n## Practical Interpretation\r\n\r\nFor long-head hypertrophy, the strongest general strategy is to include both:\r\n\r\n1. **knee flexion at long hamstring length**;\r\n2. **hip extension at long hamstring length**.\r\n\r\nExamples:\r\n\r\n- seated leg curl;\r\n- Romanian deadlift.\r\n\r\nThis is biomechanically more complete than relying exclusively on either leg curls or hip hinges.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Seated Leg Curl\r\n\r\nThe seated leg curl is particularly valuable for the long head because hip flexion lengthens the biarticular hamstrings proximally.\r\n\r\nAdvantages include:\r\n\r\n- stable setup;\r\n- direct knee-flexion loading;\r\n- meaningful resistance near long muscle lengths;\r\n- low systemic fatigue;\r\n- easy proximity to muscular failure.\r\n\r\nFor direct hamstring hypertrophy, it is one of the strongest general-purpose exercise categories.\r\n\r\nSeat position should allow:\r\n\r\n- stable pelvis;\r\n- substantial hip flexion;\r\n- near-full knee extension;\r\n- controlled knee flexion without posterior pelvic compensation.\r\n\r\nIf the pelvis rolls backward aggressively as the knees extend, effective hamstring length may be partially reduced.\r\n\r\n## Prone Leg Curl\r\n\r\nProne leg curls remain effective knee-flexion exercises.\r\n\r\nHowever, the hip is less flexed, so the long head operates at a shorter overall length than in the seated position.\r\n\r\nThey may still be useful for:\r\n\r\n- exercise variety;\r\n- a different resistance profile;\r\n- high shortened-position tension;\r\n- situations where seated equipment is unavailable.\r\n\r\nThey should not be considered useless simply because seated leg curls show an advantage in some hypertrophy data.\r\n\r\n## Romanian Deadlift\r\n\r\nThe RDL trains biceps femoris long head as a hip extensor.\r\n\r\nA good hypertrophy-oriented execution generally involves:\r\n\r\n- substantial hip flexion;\r\n- relatively small knee flexion;\r\n- neutral or controlled spinal position;\r\n- load kept close to the body;\r\n- eccentric descent until additional depth would mainly come from spinal or pelvic compensation.\r\n\r\nThe goal is not to reach the floor.\r\n\r\nThe goal is to generate high hip-extension demand while preserving long hamstring length.\r\n\r\nExcessive knee flexion shortens the hamstrings distally and can shift more work toward:\r\n\r\n- gluteus maximus;\r\n- adductor magnus.\r\n\r\n## Stiff-Leg Deadlift\r\n\r\nA straighter knee generally increases hamstring length relative to a more flexed-knee hinge.\r\n\r\nHowever, completely locked knees are not required.\r\n\r\nA small amount of knee flexion can improve comfort while preserving a strong long-length stimulus.\r\n\r\nThe critical variable is the combination of:\r\n\r\n- hip flexion;\r\n- knee extension;\r\n- external hip-flexion moment.\r\n\r\n## Good Morning\r\n\r\nThe good morning can also strongly load the long head.\r\n\r\nIts mechanical characteristics resemble a hip hinge, but the bar position increases the external moment arm around the hip and spine.\r\n\r\nThis can produce a strong hamstring stimulus but also greater axial and spinal stabilization demands.\r\n\r\nFor hypertrophy efficiency, an RDL or machine-based hip-extension exercise may provide similar local benefits with lower systemic cost for many lifters.\r\n\r\n## Nordic Hamstring Curl\r\n\r\nThe Nordic hamstring exercise generates very high eccentric knee-flexor demand.\r\n\r\nIt has strong relevance for:\r\n\r\n- eccentric strength;\r\n- fascicle adaptation;\r\n- sprint-related hamstring preparation;\r\n- injury-risk mitigation programs.\r\n\r\nHowever, it should not automatically be considered the single best hypertrophy movement for biceps femoris long head.\r\n\r\nSeveral factors can limit it:\r\n\r\n- extremely high eccentric intensity;\r\n- difficulty scaling resistance;\r\n- large recovery cost;\r\n- relatively unusual resistance profile;\r\n- technique breakdown before conventional concentric failure.\r\n\r\nNordics are therefore highly valuable but serve a somewhat different programming role from seated leg curls.\r\n\r\n## Leg Curl Tibial Position\r\n\r\nExternally rotating the tibia is sometimes suggested as a way to "target the biceps femoris."\r\n\r\nThere is a biomechanical rationale because biceps femoris contributes to tibial external rotation.\r\n\r\nHowever, practical head isolation through foot rotation is limited.\r\n\r\nSmall changes in tibial orientation may alter recruitment but should not replace exercise selection based on:\r\n\r\n- joint action;\r\n- muscle length;\r\n- resistance profile.\r\n\r\nTrying to aggressively twist the lower leg during loaded knee flexion is unnecessary.\r\n\r\n## Squats\r\n\r\nSquats are not a strong direct knee-flexion stimulus for the hamstrings.\r\n\r\nDuring descent:\r\n\r\n- hip flexion lengthens them proximally;\r\n- knee flexion shortens them distally.\r\n\r\nThese opposing length changes partially offset each other.\r\n\r\nDuring ascent, the reverse occurs.\r\n\r\nConsequently, hamstring length may change less than expected despite substantial joint movement.\r\n\r\nThis helps explain why squats can strongly develop:\r\n\r\n- quadriceps;\r\n- gluteus maximus;\r\n- adductor magnus,\r\n\r\nwhile often producing much less hamstring hypertrophy than dedicated knee-flexion or hip-hinge exercises.\r\n\r\n## Sprinting\r\n\r\nBiceps femoris long head is heavily involved in high-speed running.\r\n\r\nDuring late swing, it experiences:\r\n\r\n- hip flexion;\r\n- rapid knee extension;\r\n- high eccentric force.\r\n\r\nThis combination produces substantial strain and is one reason the long head is commonly implicated in sprint-related hamstring injuries.\r\n\r\nFor athletes, resistance-training load should therefore be considered together with sprint exposure.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Use Both Functions\r\n\r\nFor comprehensive development, include both major functions:\r\n\r\n### Knee Flexion\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n- seated leg curl.\r\n\r\n### Hip Extension\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n- Romanian deadlift.\r\n\r\nThis avoids assuming that all hamstring exercises provide equivalent stimulation.\r\n\r\nA simple pairing of one knee-flexion and one hip-extension exercise often provides a more complete stimulus than multiple variants from only one category.\r\n\r\n## Weekly Volume\r\n\r\nWhen substantial direct hamstring work is already present, a practical starting point may be approximately:\r\n\r\n- **4-8 hard sets per week** directly involving the long head.\r\n\r\nHigher volumes can be useful when hamstrings are a priority, but exposure from:\r\n\r\n- RDLs;\r\n- leg curls;\r\n- Nordics;\r\n- sprinting\r\n\r\nshould be considered together.\r\n\r\nDo not count every compound lower-body set as a full hamstring set.\r\n\r\nA squat set and a seated leg-curl set should not receive equal long-head stimulus credit.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nTwo weekly exposures are a practical default.\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n\r\n- Session A: hip-extension dominant;\r\n- Session B: knee-flexion dominant.\r\n\r\nThis distributes both mechanical stress and soreness.\r\n\r\nHigher frequency can work with lower session volumes.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\n### Leg Curl\r\n\r\nCommon effective ranges:\r\n\r\n- 6-10 reps for heavier work;\r\n- 8-15 reps as a default;\r\n- 15-25 reps for lower-load isolation work.\r\n\r\n### Hip Hinge\r\n\r\nCommon effective ranges:\r\n\r\n- 5-8 reps for heavier strength-oriented RDLs;\r\n- 6-12 reps for hypertrophy-oriented work;\r\n- occasionally higher if technique remains stable.\r\n\r\nVery high-repetition hinges can become limited by:\r\n\r\n- grip;\r\n- spinal erectors;\r\n- cardiovascular fatigue;\r\n- general discomfort\r\n\r\nbefore the long head itself becomes the primary limiting factor.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nStable leg curls can generally be taken very close to local failure.\r\n\r\nHip hinges should usually retain more technical reserve.\r\n\r\nA useful distinction is:\r\n\r\n- **seated leg curl:** 0-2 RIR is often practical;\r\n- **RDL:** approximately 1-3 RIR is generally more interpretable;\r\n- **Nordic:** failure is often eccentric/technical and should be treated separately.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Order\r\n\r\nIf hamstring hypertrophy is a priority, performing at least one hamstring exercise before substantial fatigue from other lower-body work can be advantageous.\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n\r\n1. RDL;\r\n2. leg press;\r\n3. seated leg curl.\r\n\r\nor:\r\n\r\n1. seated leg curl;\r\n2. squat;\r\n3. secondary posterior-chain exercise.\r\n\r\nExercise order should follow the target priority rather than a fixed "compounds always first" rule.\r\n\r\n## Managing Long-Length Load\r\n\r\nThe long head can generate substantial soreness when exposed suddenly to:\r\n\r\n- deep hip flexion;\r\n- extended knee positions;\r\n- high eccentric loading;\r\n- Nordics.\r\n\r\nNew exercises should therefore be introduced progressively.\r\n\r\nA simultaneous jump in:\r\n\r\n- ROM;\r\n- eccentric load;\r\n- weekly volume\r\n\r\ncan create a disproportionate increase in muscle damage.\r\n\r\n## Sprinting Athletes\r\n\r\nFor athletes, sprinting is itself a high-intensity biceps femoris long-head stimulus.\r\n\r\nHigh-load eccentric hamstring sessions should not be programmed independently of sprint exposure.\r\n\r\nPotential high-stress inputs include:\r\n\r\n- maximal sprinting;\r\n- acceleration work;\r\n- Nordics;\r\n- heavy RDLs;\r\n- long-length leg curls.\r\n\r\nThe weekly distribution should avoid unnecessarily clustering all maximal hamstring stressors.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## Only One Head Crosses the Hip\r\n\r\nDespite the name "biceps femoris," only the **long head** crosses the hip.\r\n\r\nThe short head originates from the femur.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n- both heads flex the knee;\r\n- only the long head contributes to hip extension.\r\n\r\nThis distinction is fundamental for exercise modelling.\r\n\r\n## The Long Head Is the Lateral Hamstring\r\n\r\nBiceps femoris forms the lateral border of the popliteal region.\r\n\r\nThe medial hamstrings are:\r\n\r\n- semitendinosus;\r\n- semimembranosus.\r\n\r\nThis anatomical opposition also corresponds to rotational function:\r\n\r\n- biceps femoris → external tibial rotation;\r\n- medial hamstrings → internal tibial rotation.\r\n\r\n## It Is Frequently Injured During Sprinting\r\n\r\nBiceps femoris long head is one of the most commonly injured muscles in high-speed running.\r\n\r\nIts vulnerability is related partly to its functional state during late swing, where the muscle must produce high eccentric force while simultaneously operating at long length.\r\n\r\nThis is a useful example of why muscle strain risk cannot be inferred from force alone.\r\n\r\n**Force × muscle length × contraction velocity × architecture** all matter.\r\n\r\n## Fascicle Length Is Trainable\r\n\r\nBiceps femoris long-head fascicle length can change in response to training.\r\n\r\nEccentric training, particularly Nordic-style work, has been associated with increases in fascicle length.\r\n\r\nThis adaptation has attracted substantial interest because shorter fascicles have been associated observationally with hamstring injury risk in field athletes.\r\n\r\nHowever, fascicle length should not be interpreted as a complete injury-risk metric in isolation.\r\n\r\n## Hamstrings Do Not Behave as One Muscle\r\n\r\nExercises commonly labeled "hamstring exercises" can produce substantially different recruitment and hypertrophy patterns.\r\n\r\nResearch comparing different hamstring exercises shows clear muscle-specific and region-specific responses. citeturn269737search5\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n> "hamstring stimulus" is too coarse a variable for a detailed exercise model.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\n`biceps_femoris_long_head` should receive demand from both:\r\n\r\n1. **hip-extension torque**;\r\n2. **knee-flexion torque**.\r\n\r\nThis is fundamentally different from `biceps_femoris_short_head`, which should receive essentially no direct hip-extension allocation.\r\n\r\n## Muscle-Length Modifier\r\n\r\nA meaningful length-tension model should depend jointly on:\r\n\r\n- hip angle;\r\n- knee angle.\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n### Longest Relevant State\r\n\r\n- high hip flexion;\r\n- low knee flexion / near knee extension.\r\n\r\nExamples:\r\n\r\n- bottom of an RDL;\r\n- starting portion of a seated leg curl.\r\n\r\n### Intermediate State\r\n\r\n- neutral hip;\r\n- relatively extended knee.\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n- prone leg curl starting position.\r\n\r\n### Short State\r\n\r\n- hip extension;\r\n- deep knee flexion.\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n- peak contraction of some prone leg-curl configurations.\r\n\r\nTherefore, using knee angle alone to estimate `E_LT` would systematically misrepresent the long head.\r\n\r\n## Exercise-Specific Allocation\r\n\r\n### Seated Leg Curl\r\n\r\nHigh `biceps_femoris_long_head` demand.\r\n\r\nAdvantages:\r\n\r\n- direct knee-flexion torque;\r\n- long proximal muscle length;\r\n- low stabilization requirement.\r\n\r\n### Prone Leg Curl\r\n\r\nHigh knee-flexion demand but shorter long-head operating length relative to seated curling.\r\n\r\nStill a valid stimulus, but not mechanically identical.\r\n\r\n### Romanian Deadlift\r\n\r\nHigh long-head demand through hip extension.\r\n\r\nThe relative allocation depends on:\r\n\r\n- knee flexion;\r\n- hip ROM;\r\n- load;\r\n- torso geometry.\r\n\r\n### Squat\r\n\r\nLower long-head allocation than a superficial "posterior-chain" classification might imply.\r\n\r\nHip and knee motions partly offset hamstring length changes.\r\n\r\n### Nordic Curl\r\n\r\nVery high eccentric knee-flexor demand.\r\n\r\nParticularly relevant to force production, fascicle adaptation and athletic preparation.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Short Head\r\n\r\nIn knee-flexion exercises, demand must be distributed between:\r\n\r\n- `biceps_femoris_long_head`;\r\n- `biceps_femoris_short_head`;\r\n- `semitendinosus`;\r\n- `semimembranosus`.\r\n\r\nThe distribution should not be fixed across all knee-flexion exercises.\r\n\r\nHip position specifically changes the operating length of the biarticular muscles but not the short head.\r\n\r\nConsequently, seated versus prone leg curls should not have identical muscle vectors.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **gross anatomy:** high;\r\n- **tibial-division sciatic innervation:** high;\r\n- **knee-flexion function:** high;\r\n- **hip-extension function:** high;\r\n- **external tibial rotation:** high;\r\n- **joint-angle-dependent muscle length:** high;\r\n- **greater hypertrophy from seated vs prone leg curl:** directly supported;\r\n- **exercise-specific regional hamstring hypertrophy:** directly supported;\r\n- **exact fascicle-level demand for every exercise:** moderate;\r\n- **precise injury prediction from fascicle length or strength variables:** limited and multifactorial.\r\n\r\nThe useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **biceps femoris long head = a biarticular lateral hamstring whose mechanical state is determined jointly by hip and knee position, with substantial roles in both hip extension and knee flexion and unusually high relevance to long-muscle-length loading.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	9.76	11.60	0.980	54.82	53.70
29	transverse_abdominis	Musculus transversus abdominis	Core	Core	225.00	213.07	28.00	Broad Transverse	0.725	0.275	zero	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\nThe **transversus abdominis (TrA)** is the deepest of the three broad lateral abdominal-wall muscle layers.\r\n\r\nIts fibers run predominantly in a **transverse / circumferential direction** around the abdominal wall, distinguishing it mechanically from:\r\n\r\n- external oblique;\r\n- internal oblique;\r\n- rectus abdominis.\r\n\r\nIts primary functions are not large visible trunk movements.\r\n\r\nInstead, transversus abdominis contributes strongly to:\r\n\r\n- **compression of the abdominal contents**;\r\n- regulation of **intra-abdominal pressure**;\r\n- tensioning of the abdominal wall and thoracolumbar fascial system;\r\n- lumbopelvic stabilization;\r\n- anticipatory stabilization before rapid limb movement;\r\n- forced expiration.\r\n\r\nClassic intramuscular-EMG experiments showed that TrA activity is closely associated with changes in intra-abdominal pressure and is recruited in a task-specific manner with the other abdominal muscles. :contentReference[oaicite:0]{index=0}\r\n\r\nExperiments involving rapid limb movement also demonstrated early TrA recruitment before or around the onset of limb-muscle activation, supporting an important role in anticipatory trunk stabilization rather than a purely movement-producing role. :contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}\r\n\r\nFor Agonez, the most useful abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **transversus abdominis = a circumferential abdominal-wall compressor and trunk-stiffness generator whose main training demand arises from bracing, pressure generation and resistance to trunk deformation rather than from a large isolated joint torque.**\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\n## Origin\r\n\r\nTransversus abdominis arises broadly from structures along the lateral and posterior abdominal wall.\r\n\r\nIts proximal attachments include regions associated with:\r\n\r\n- lower ribs and costal cartilages;\r\n- thoracolumbar fascia;\r\n- iliac crest;\r\n- lateral inguinal region.\r\n\r\nThe broad attachment allows the muscle to form a deep circumferential sheet around much of the abdominal cavity.\r\n\r\n## Insertion\r\n\r\nIts fibers travel predominantly horizontally toward a broad anterior aponeurosis.\r\n\r\nThe aponeurosis contributes to structures including:\r\n\r\n- rectus sheath;\r\n- linea alba;\r\n- pubic region through inferior aponeurotic connections.\r\n\r\nThus TrA transmits force through a wide fascial network rather than through one narrow distal tendon.\r\n\r\n## Relationship With Other Abdominal Layers\r\n\r\nFrom superficial to deep, the major lateral abdominal-wall layers are:\r\n\r\n1. external oblique;\r\n2. internal oblique;\r\n3. transversus abdominis.\r\n\r\nThese layers form a laminate-like muscular system with different fiber orientations.\r\n\r\nCadaveric architectural analysis demonstrates substantial differences among these muscles in:\r\n\r\n- fascicle orientation;\r\n- fascicle length;\r\n- PCSA;\r\n- sarcomere length.\r\n\r\nTrA possesses relatively transversely oriented fibers and an architecture distinct from both oblique layers and rectus abdominis. :contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}\r\n\r\n## Architecture\r\n\r\nHuman architectural analysis found that TrA does not possess the extremely long fascicles characteristic of rectus abdominis.\r\n\r\nIts architecture is better suited to generating circumferential abdominal-wall tension over a smaller shortening excursion. :contentReference[oaicite:3]{index=3}\r\n\r\nThis is mechanically consistent with a role emphasizing:\r\n\r\n- compression;\r\n- stiffness;\r\n- tension generation\r\n\r\nrather than large-amplitude trunk motion.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nTransversus abdominis receives segmental innervation from lower thoracic and upper lumbar spinal nerves.\r\n\r\nBecause the muscle spans a large cranio-caudal region, neural supply is distributed rather than being provided by one single peripheral nerve trunk.\r\n\r\nThis arrangement is compatible with regionally modulated activation across the abdominal wall.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Abdominal Compression\r\n\r\nThe clearest primary function of TrA is **compression of the abdominal contents**.\r\n\r\nIts transverse fiber orientation allows contraction to reduce abdominal circumference.\r\n\r\nA useful mechanical analogy is a muscular corset:\r\n\r\n`circumferential tension ↑ → abdominal-wall radius/volume tendency ↓`\r\n\r\nThis increases pressure within the abdominal cavity when coordinated with:\r\n\r\n- diaphragm;\r\n- pelvic floor;\r\n- other abdominal muscles.\r\n\r\nDirect simultaneous EMG and intra-abdominal-pressure measurements found TrA activity to be particularly consistently related to changes in intra-abdominal pressure. :contentReference[oaicite:4]{index=4}\r\n\r\n## Intra-Abdominal Pressure\r\n\r\nIntra-abdominal pressure is generated by a coordinated system rather than by TrA alone.\r\n\r\nImportant contributors include:\r\n\r\n- diaphragm;\r\n- pelvic floor;\r\n- TrA;\r\n- internal/external oblique;\r\n- rectus abdominis.\r\n\r\nTrA is nevertheless particularly well positioned to increase abdominal pressure because of its circumferential fiber orientation.\r\n\r\nExperimental work shows coordinated activation between abdominal musculature and pelvic-floor musculature during tasks requiring abdominal-wall contraction. :contentReference[oaicite:5]{index=5}\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n> **TrA is an important IAP generator, but IAP should never be represented as a single-muscle phenomenon.**\r\n\r\n## Trunk Stiffness\r\n\r\nTrA contributes to **stiffening the lumbopelvic system**.\r\n\r\nUnlike rectus abdominis, which can generate substantial gross spinal-flexion torque, TrA produces relatively little obvious sagittal movement.\r\n\r\nIts contribution is instead related to:\r\n\r\n- abdominal-wall tension;\r\n- intra-abdominal pressure;\r\n- fascial tension;\r\n- co-contraction.\r\n\r\nThis means it can generate substantial force while:\r\n\r\n`trunk_ROM ≈ 0`.\r\n\r\n## Anticipatory Postural Control\r\n\r\nA classic series of intramuscular-EMG experiments found that TrA can activate before rapid voluntary movement of the upper or lower limbs. :contentReference[oaicite:6]{index=6}\r\n\r\nThis recruitment was less dependent on the direction of limb movement than that of several more direction-specific trunk muscles.\r\n\r\nThis supports the interpretation that TrA contributes to a general anticipatory stabilization strategy.\r\n\r\nHowever, this should not be turned into the simplistic claim that:\r\n\r\n> "TrA must always fire first."\r\n\r\nThe timing depends on:\r\n\r\n- movement speed;\r\n- task;\r\n- load;\r\n- experimental conditions.\r\n\r\nFor example, one study found anticipatory TrA activation before deltoid specifically in the faster limb-movement condition, showing that timing is task dependent rather than a universal fixed sequence. :contentReference[oaicite:7]{index=7}\r\n\r\n## Force Transmission Through the Abdominal Wall\r\n\r\nTrA contributes to a layered force-transmission system with:\r\n\r\n- internal oblique;\r\n- external oblique;\r\n- rectus sheath;\r\n- linea alba;\r\n- thoracolumbar fascia.\r\n\r\nIts force can therefore influence trunk mechanics without producing a simple isolated rotation around one anatomical joint.\r\n\r\nThis makes TrA unusually poorly represented by traditional single-joint muscle models.\r\n\r\n## Forced Expiration\r\n\r\nBy compressing the abdominal contents, TrA contributes to forced expiration.\r\n\r\nAbdominal compression can displace the diaphragm superiorly and assist high-pressure expiratory tasks such as:\r\n\r\n- coughing;\r\n- forceful exhalation.\r\n\r\nFor normal resistance-training modelling, the respiratory role is secondary but becomes relevant during high-force bracing.\r\n\r\n# Abdominal Hollowing vs Bracing\r\n\r\nThese two concepts should not be treated as equivalent.\r\n\r\n## Abdominal Hollowing\r\n\r\nHollowing attempts to selectively draw the abdominal wall inward with relatively low global trunk-muscle activation.\r\n\r\nIt is frequently used in:\r\n\r\n- motor-control training;\r\n- rehabilitation;\r\n- ultrasound assessment.\r\n\r\nUltrasound studies demonstrate that individuals can increase TrA thickness during abdominal-hollowing tasks, and visual ultrasound feedback can improve selective TrA contraction over short training periods. :contentReference[oaicite:8]{index=8}\r\n\r\nHowever, selective TrA activation is not the same goal as maximizing trunk stiffness under a heavy external load.\r\n\r\n## Abdominal Bracing\r\n\r\nBracing involves co-contraction of multiple trunk muscles.\r\n\r\nUnder heavy resistance training, a robust strategy generally requires coordinated force from:\r\n\r\n- TrA;\r\n- obliques;\r\n- rectus abdominis;\r\n- erector spinae;\r\n- diaphragm;\r\n- pelvic floor.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n`maximal trunk stiffness != maximal selective TrA isolation`.\r\n\r\nFor Agonez, heavy compound lifting should therefore produce distributed abdominal demand rather than a huge TrA-only vector.\r\n\r\n# Function vs Movement\r\n\r\nTrA is one of the clearest examples where traditional "muscle action" lists become insufficient.\r\n\r\nIt does not primarily produce:\r\n\r\n- obvious trunk flexion;\r\n- large axial rotation;\r\n- large lateral flexion.\r\n\r\nInstead, it frequently works to **prevent unwanted deformation**.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- resisting abdominal expansion under pressure;\r\n- stabilizing the lumbar spine during limb movement;\r\n- maintaining trunk stiffness during unilateral loading.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n`movement produced ≈ small`\r\n\r\nwhile:\r\n\r\n`muscle force ≈ meaningful/high`.\r\n\r\nThis is directly analogous to:\r\n\r\n- obliques during Pallof press;\r\n- erector spinae during RDL;\r\n- rotator cuff during pressing.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nDirect evidence for **stretch-mediated hypertrophy of transversus abdominis** is extremely limited.\r\n\r\nThe concept is also harder to apply than for long fusiform limb muscles.\r\n\r\n## Length State\r\n\r\nTrA fiber length changes with:\r\n\r\n- abdominal-wall circumference;\r\n- trunk rotation;\r\n- trunk extension/flexion;\r\n- breathing;\r\n- intra-abdominal pressure;\r\n- regional abdominal deformation.\r\n\r\nBecause its fibers wrap transversely around the abdomen, its length state cannot be described accurately from one simple spinal joint angle.\r\n\r\n## Abdominal Expansion\r\n\r\nExpansion of the abdominal wall can lengthen TrA fibers.\r\n\r\nThis can occur during:\r\n\r\n- inspiration;\r\n- increased abdominal volume;\r\n- changes in trunk posture.\r\n\r\nHowever, passive expansion is not necessarily a hypertrophic stimulus.\r\n\r\n## Trunk Extension\r\n\r\nExtension of the trunk may increase tension in portions of the abdominal wall, but this is much less straightforward for TrA than for rectus abdominis.\r\n\r\nIts fibers are predominantly transverse rather than longitudinal.\r\n\r\nTherefore sagittal trunk extension should receive less weight in `E_LT_TrA` than in `E_LT_rectus_abdominis`.\r\n\r\n## Rotation\r\n\r\nBecause the abdominal wall deforms asymmetrically during trunk rotation, left/right TrA regions may change length differently.\r\n\r\nA bilateral aggregate necessarily hides this regional information.\r\n\r\n## Long-Length Isometrics\r\n\r\nGeneral resistance-training evidence suggests that isometric training at longer muscle lengths can produce meaningful hypertrophy, but this evidence is not specific to TrA. :contentReference[oaicite:9]{index=9}\r\n\r\nIt should therefore not be used to assign a large TrA-specific SMH multiplier.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nFor Agonez:\r\n\r\n- direct TrA-specific SMH evidence is insufficient;\r\n- its geometry makes a simple long-vs-short classification difficult;\r\n- force and duration of contraction are more defensible primary variables than muscle-length bonus.\r\n\r\nTherefore `smh_factor` should be conservative.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Heavy Compound Lifting\r\n\r\nHeavy exercises can produce meaningful TrA activity through trunk stabilization and pressure generation.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- squats;\r\n- deadlifts;\r\n- unsupported rows;\r\n- overhead presses;\r\n- loaded carries.\r\n\r\nHowever, TrA should not automatically receive enormous hypertrophic demand in every compound lift.\r\n\r\nIts allocation should depend on the actual requirement for:\r\n\r\n- abdominal pressure;\r\n- anti-deformation stiffness;\r\n- asymmetric stabilization.\r\n\r\n## Squat\r\n\r\nDuring a heavy squat, TrA contributes to the abdominal-pressure and trunk-stiffness system.\r\n\r\nMajor synergists include:\r\n\r\n- obliques;\r\n- rectus abdominis;\r\n- erector spinae;\r\n- diaphragm;\r\n- pelvic floor.\r\n\r\nThe muscle is functioning primarily:\r\n\r\n- isometrically;\r\n- through pressure/stiffness generation.\r\n\r\nIt is not creating the main external hip or knee torque.\r\n\r\n## Deadlift / RDL\r\n\r\nA similar pattern occurs during hip hinges.\r\n\r\nThe external trunk-flexion moment is resisted primarily by large spinal extensor torque.\r\n\r\nTrA and the abdominal wall co-contract to stiffen the trunk and regulate pressure.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n- `erector_spinae` → high direct anti-flexion/extensor demand;\r\n- `transverse_abdominis` → meaningful co-contraction/stiffness demand.\r\n\r\nThese vectors should not be symmetric merely because both muscles are active.\r\n\r\n## Pendlay Row\r\n\r\nPendlay rows require considerable trunk stiffness because the unsupported torso is held under load.\r\n\r\nTrA demand should therefore be meaningful.\r\n\r\nHowever, the dominant sagittal-plane resisting torque remains with:\r\n\r\n- erector spinae;\r\n- hip extensors.\r\n\r\nAgain, TrA should be viewed as a pressure/stiffness contributor.\r\n\r\n## Leg Press\r\n\r\nThe back support substantially reduces the requirement for free trunk stabilization.\r\n\r\nTrA demand should therefore generally be lower than in a free squat or unsupported hinge.\r\n\r\nThis is an important exercise-specific distinction.\r\n\r\n## Plank\r\n\r\nA plank creates **anti-extension** demand across the trunk.\r\n\r\nTrA contributes alongside:\r\n\r\n- rectus abdominis;\r\n- obliques.\r\n\r\nHowever, because the main external deformation tendency is sagittal extension, rectus abdominis and obliques can generate more direct flexion torque.\r\n\r\nTrA contributes strongly to:\r\n\r\n- bracing;\r\n- abdominal-wall tension;\r\n- stiffness.\r\n\r\nThus it should receive meaningful but not exclusive allocation.\r\n\r\n## Long-Lever Plank\r\n\r\nExtending the lever increases external trunk-extension torque.\r\n\r\nThis increases demand across the abdominal system.\r\n\r\nTrA should rise with the global bracing requirement, but the large sagittal torque should still be shared with direct spinal flexors.\r\n\r\n## Pallof Press\r\n\r\nThe Pallof press creates:\r\n\r\n- anti-rotation demand;\r\n- global trunk stabilization.\r\n\r\n`obliques` should receive the dominant direction-specific anti-rotation allocation.\r\n\r\nTrA contributes through:\r\n\r\n- generalized abdominal stiffness;\r\n- co-contraction.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`obliques > TrA`\r\n\r\nfor the primary rotational component.\r\n\r\n## Dragon Flag\r\n\r\nDragon flags impose high anti-extension demand.\r\n\r\nRectus abdominis and obliques should receive very large force allocations.\r\n\r\nTrA also contributes substantially to global trunk stiffness and abdominal-wall tension.\r\n\r\nHowever, it is not the primary long-lever sagittal flexor.\r\n\r\n## Hanging Leg Raise\r\n\r\nTrA contributes to lumbopelvic stabilization during leg raising.\r\n\r\nThe major active roles remain:\r\n\r\n- iliopsoas → hip flexion;\r\n- rectus abdominis / obliques → anti-extension and posterior pelvic control.\r\n\r\nTrA should receive secondary global stabilization demand.\r\n\r\n## Cable Crunch\r\n\r\nA cable crunch is primarily a dynamic spinal-flexion task.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n- rectus abdominis should dominate;\r\n- obliques contribute;\r\n- TrA contributes abdominal compression/stabilization.\r\n\r\nThis should not be treated as a direct TrA isolation exercise.\r\n\r\n## Abdominal Hollowing\r\n\r\nHollowing can selectively increase TrA activation/thickness relative to surrounding abdominal muscles and can be useful for learning selective motor control. :contentReference[oaicite:10]{index=10}\r\n\r\nHowever:\r\n\r\n> selective activation does not necessarily make it the best hypertrophy or strength exercise.\r\n\r\nExternal loading is usually low.\r\n\r\nTherefore it should receive high **specificity** but low absolute mechanical demand.\r\n\r\n## Loaded Carry\r\n\r\nCarries create trunk stiffness demands, particularly when asymmetric.\r\n\r\n### Bilateral Farmer Carry\r\n\r\nModerate global abdominal stiffness.\r\n\r\n### Suitcase Carry\r\n\r\nHigher:\r\n- anti-lateral-flexion;\r\n- anti-rotation.\r\n\r\nObliques and quadratus-lumborum-type systems handle much of the directional torque, while TrA contributes general circumferential stiffness.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Dedicated Hypertrophy Work Is Usually Unnecessary\r\n\r\nFor most lifters, TrA does not require a separate hypertrophy program.\r\n\r\nIt receives repeated exposure through:\r\n\r\n- bracing;\r\n- compound lifts;\r\n- abdominal training;\r\n- locomotion.\r\n\r\nIf the goal is trunk strength rather than selective motor control, progressively overloadable whole-core tasks are generally more transferable than endless low-load hollowing.\r\n\r\n## Hollowing Has a Specific Use\r\n\r\nHollowing is useful when the objective is:\r\n\r\n- learning selective TrA contraction;\r\n- rehabilitation;\r\n- ultrasound-guided motor-control practice.\r\n\r\nIt should not automatically replace bracing for:\r\n\r\n- squatting;\r\n- deadlifting;\r\n- pressing.\r\n\r\nThose tasks require a coordinated multi-muscle stabilization strategy.\r\n\r\n## Weekly Volume\r\n\r\nThere is insufficient evidence to prescribe an evidence-based TrA hypertrophy set target.\r\n\r\nFor ordinary strength/hypertrophy training, no separate volume quota is necessary.\r\n\r\nThe muscle should instead receive **derived exposure** from:\r\n\r\n- compound bracing;\r\n- anti-extension work;\r\n- anti-rotation work.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nTrA is activated very frequently during normal movement and breathing-related postural tasks.\r\n\r\nLow-to-moderate direct activation can therefore occur frequently.\r\n\r\nHigh-fatigue isolated sessions are generally unnecessary.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\nThere is no meaningful TrA-specific hypertrophy repetition range established.\r\n\r\nFor training tasks:\r\n\r\n- dynamic core exercises → conventional reps;\r\n- bracing → contraction duration/load;\r\n- hollowing → controlled low-load holds.\r\n\r\n## Failure\r\n\r\nSelective TrA work does not require conventional muscular failure.\r\n\r\nFor high-force compound tasks, failure of trunk stability should terminate the relevant set before gross technique deteriorates.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## TrA Can Activate Before the Limb Moves\r\n\r\nClassic intramuscular-EMG experiments showed anticipatory TrA activity before rapid voluntary limb movement in several tasks. :contentReference[oaicite:11]{index=11}\r\n\r\nThis strongly influenced modern concepts of feedforward trunk stabilization.\r\n\r\nHowever, later task-dependent findings show that the timing is not an immutable sequence; movement speed can influence whether TrA activation actually precedes the limb prime mover. :contentReference[oaicite:12]{index=12}\r\n\r\n## It Is Closely Linked to Intra-Abdominal Pressure\r\n\r\nAmong the ventrolateral abdominal muscles measured during pressure-changing tasks, TrA activity was particularly consistently associated with intra-abdominal-pressure changes. :contentReference[oaicite:13]{index=13}\r\n\r\nThis fits its transverse fiber orientation.\r\n\r\n## It Is Not a "Magic Core Muscle"\r\n\r\nThe historical emphasis on selective TrA control sometimes produced the misconception that spinal stability depends primarily on this one muscle.\r\n\r\nThe experimental literature instead shows coordinated recruitment of multiple abdominal and respiratory muscles during stabilization and pressure generation. :contentReference[oaicite:14]{index=14}\r\n\r\nTherefore TrA is important, but trunk stability is a **system property**.\r\n\r\n## Its Architecture Differs Strongly From Rectus Abdominis\r\n\r\nHuman cadaveric measurements show substantial abdominal-wall architectural specialization.\r\n\r\nRectus abdominis has much longer fascicles, whereas TrA has shorter, transversely oriented architecture more consistent with tension generation and abdominal compression. :contentReference[oaicite:15]{index=15}\r\n\r\n## "Drawing the Belly In" Is Not the Same as Heavy Bracing\r\n\r\nHollowing can preferentially increase TrA contraction in controlled low-load conditions. :contentReference[oaicite:16]{index=16}\r\n\r\nA heavy squat requires much broader co-contraction.\r\n\r\nThus both strategies can be valid while serving different mechanical goals.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\n`transverse_abdominis` is unusual because it should **not** be modelled primarily as a joint-torque muscle.\r\n\r\nIts major demand dimensions are better represented by:\r\n\r\n1. **abdominal compression demand**;\r\n2. **intra-abdominal-pressure demand**;\r\n3. **trunk-stiffness demand**;\r\n4. generalized anti-deformation stabilization;\r\n5. secondary respiratory-pressure contribution.\r\n\r\nThis distinguishes it from:\r\n\r\n- `rectus_abdominis`;\r\n- `obliques`.\r\n\r\n## Comparison With Rectus Abdominis\r\n\r\n### Rectus Abdominis\r\n\r\nStrong direct:\r\n- spinal-flexion torque;\r\n- posterior-pelvic-tilt torque;\r\n- anti-extension torque.\r\n\r\n### Transversus Abdominis\r\n\r\nStrong:\r\n- circumferential compression;\r\n- stiffness;\r\n- IAP generation.\r\n\r\nThus a dragon flag should give both muscles demand, but for different mechanical reasons.\r\n\r\n## Comparison With Obliques\r\n\r\n### Obliques\r\n\r\nStrong:\r\n- rotation;\r\n- anti-rotation;\r\n- lateral flexion;\r\n- anti-lateral-flexion;\r\n- compression.\r\n\r\n### TrA\r\n\r\nWeak direction-specific rotational identity.\r\n\r\nStrong:\r\n- circumferential tension;\r\n- pressure/stiffness.\r\n\r\nThus in a Pallof press:\r\n\r\n`obliques directional_torque >> TrA directional_torque`\r\n\r\nwhile both contribute to stabilization.\r\n\r\n## Trunk-Stiffness Model\r\n\r\nA useful KISS abstraction could be:\r\n\r\n`D_TrA ∝ required_trunk_stiffness × abdominal_pressure_requirement`\r\n\r\nwhere `required_trunk_stiffness` rises with:\r\n\r\n- external load;\r\n- long trunk/limb lever;\r\n- unstable/asymmetric force;\r\n- need to prevent spinal deformation.\r\n\r\nThis is better than deriving TrA demand directly from spinal ROM.\r\n\r\n## Intra-Abdominal Pressure Model\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`IAP = f(diaphragm_force,\r\n         abdominal_wall_force,\r\n         pelvic_floor_response,\r\n         abdominal_volume)`\r\n\r\nTrA contributes substantially to the abdominal-wall term but does not independently determine IAP. Experimental measurements show a particularly consistent relationship between TrA activity and IAP changes. :contentReference[oaicite:17]{index=17}\r\n\r\nThus Agonez should avoid:\r\n\r\n`IAP = TrA activation`\r\n\r\nand instead use:\r\n\r\n`TrA activation ↔ one contributor to IAP`.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Allocation\r\n\r\n### Kneeling Cable Crunch\r\n\r\nModerate TrA demand.\r\n\r\nVery high:\r\n- rectus abdominis.\r\n\r\nMeaningful:\r\n- obliques.\r\n\r\n### Bodyweight Crunch\r\n\r\nLow-to-moderate TrA.\r\n\r\nHigh:\r\n- rectus abdominis.\r\n\r\n### Dragon Flag\r\n\r\nHigh TrA stabilization demand.\r\n\r\nVery high:\r\n- rectus abdominis;\r\n- obliques.\r\n\r\n### Hanging Leg Raise\r\n\r\nModerate TrA stabilization.\r\n\r\nHigh:\r\n- rectus abdominis;\r\n- iliopsoas.\r\n\r\n### Hanging Oblique Knee Raise\r\n\r\nModerate TrA stabilization.\r\n\r\nHigh:\r\n- obliques;\r\n- iliopsoas.\r\n\r\n### Pallof Press\r\n\r\nModerate-to-high TrA global bracing.\r\n\r\nVery high:\r\n- obliques through anti-rotation.\r\n\r\n### Copenhagen Plank\r\n\r\nModerate TrA stabilization.\r\n\r\nHigh:\r\n- adductors;\r\n- obliques through anti-lateral-flexion.\r\n\r\n### Pendlay Row\r\n\r\nModerate TrA bracing demand.\r\n\r\nVery high:\r\n- erector spinae through external trunk-flexion moment.\r\n\r\n### 45° Leg Press\r\n\r\nLow TrA relative to free lower-body compounds because torso support reduces free-spine stabilization demand.\r\n\r\n### Heavy Free Squat\r\n\r\nModerate-to-high TrA pressure/stiffness demand.\r\n\r\nBut:\r\n- quadriceps/glutes/adductors produce lower-limb prime-mover torque;\r\n- spinal extensors handle much of the direct sagittal trunk moment.\r\n\r\n## Isometric ETU\r\n\r\nTrA reinforces one of the central Agonez principles:\r\n\r\n`ROM ≈ 0` does not imply `ETU ≈ 0`.\r\n\r\nA heavy brace can involve:\r\n\r\n- substantial motor-unit recruitment;\r\n- prolonged force;\r\n- little fascicle excursion.\r\n\r\nTherefore TrA ETU should account for:\r\n\r\n- force magnitude;\r\n- contraction duration;\r\n- stabilization requirement\r\n\r\nrather than mechanical external work alone.\r\n\r\n## External Work Caveat\r\n\r\nFor TrA, traditional:\r\n\r\n`work = force × displacement`\r\n\r\nis particularly misleading.\r\n\r\nThe muscle may perform little external mechanical work while contributing substantially to internal joint/system stability.\r\n\r\nThus muscle ETU and whole-exercise mechanical work should remain conceptually separate.\r\n\r\n## Architecture and FCSA\r\n\r\nHuman architectural measurements show that TrA differs substantially from RA and the obliques in fascicle geometry and sarcomere operating state. :contentReference[oaicite:18]{index=18}\r\n\r\nIts FCSA can still serve as an estimate of contractile force capacity, but translating that into an external joint torque is difficult because:\r\n\r\n- force is distributed through broad aponeuroses;\r\n- there is no single tendon lever arm;\r\n- its primary action is circumferential wall tension.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`F_TrA ≈ σ × FCSA_TrA`\r\n\r\nis more interpretable than attempting:\r\n\r\n`T_spine = F_TrA × one_fixed_moment_arm`.\r\n\r\n## Pressure-Based Mechanical Interpretation\r\n\r\nA more biologically appropriate abstraction may eventually be:\r\n\r\n`F_TrA → abdominal_wall_tension → IAP/stiffness`\r\n\r\nrather than:\r\n\r\n`F_TrA → single_joint_torque`.\r\n\r\nThis makes TrA one of the few muscles in the Agonez database where an explicit **pressure/stiffness pathway** may be more accurate than the standard joint-moment pathway.\r\n\r\n## Abdominal Hollowing\r\n\r\nHollowing is useful for selectively recruiting TrA in low-load conditions, and ultrasound-guided training can measurably improve TrA contraction behavior. :contentReference[oaicite:19]{index=19}\r\n\r\nFor Agonez, however:\r\n\r\n- specificity = high;\r\n- absolute force = low;\r\n- hypertrophic ETU = probably low-to-moderate.\r\n\r\nThis prevents the common mistake:\r\n\r\n`selective activation = high training stimulus`.\r\n\r\n## Compound Bracing\r\n\r\nConversely, a heavy squat may have:\r\n\r\n- low selectivity;\r\n- much higher absolute TrA force.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n`activation_specificity`\r\n\r\nand:\r\n\r\n`absolute mechanical demand`\r\n\r\nmust remain separate.\r\n\r\nThis is the same logic used elsewhere in Agonez for smaller synergists.\r\n\r\n## Length-Tension Model\r\n\r\nA single scalar `E_LT_TrA` is difficult to justify.\r\n\r\nA more complete formulation would require:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_TrA =\r\n    f(abdominal_circumference,\r\n      trunk_rotation,\r\n      trunk_posture,\r\n      respiration,\r\n      regional_side)`\r\n\r\nFor a KISS model, it is probably better to assign a broad mid-range `E_LT` during most bracing tasks rather than introduce false precision.\r\n\r\nMuscle length should carry **less weight in TrA ETU** than:\r\n\r\n- force;\r\n- duration;\r\n- stabilization demand.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nDirect TrA-specific stretch-mediated hypertrophy evidence is essentially absent.\r\n\r\nIts morphology and function also make classical long-length limb-muscle concepts difficult to transfer directly.\r\n\r\nThe defensible model is:\r\n\r\n- active force matters;\r\n- prolonged high-tension stabilization matters;\r\n- abdominal-wall length state probably matters somewhat;\r\n- no large SMH multiplier should be assigned.\r\n\r\n## Clinical Timing Caveat\r\n\r\nStudies have reported delayed TrA recruitment in some people with low-back pain during specific rapid-limb tasks. :contentReference[oaicite:20]{index=20}\r\n\r\nThis finding is scientifically interesting but should not be generalized into:\r\n\r\n- diagnosis from TrA weakness;\r\n- mandatory hollowing for everyone;\r\n- a claim that delayed TrA activation causes all low-back pain.\r\n\r\nIndeed, ultrasound measures of TrA contraction during hollowing have shown poor ability to distinguish individual subjects with chronic low-back pain from healthy controls. :contentReference[oaicite:21]{index=21}\r\n\r\nFor Agonez, these clinical observations should not affect ordinary healthy-athlete exercise coefficients.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **deep transverse abdominal-wall architecture:** directly established anatomically; :contentReference[oaicite:22]{index=22}\r\n- **abdominal compression function:** high;\r\n- **strong relationship with intra-abdominal-pressure generation:** directly demonstrated experimentally; :contentReference[oaicite:23]{index=23}\r\n- **anticipatory recruitment during rapid limb movement:** directly demonstrated, but task dependent; :contentReference[oaicite:24]{index=24}\r\n- **role in global trunk stabilization:** strongly supported experimentally;\r\n- **coordination with pelvic-floor/other abdominal muscles:** directly demonstrated; :contentReference[oaicite:25]{index=25}\r\n- **selective recruitment during abdominal hollowing:** directly supported; :contentReference[oaicite:26]{index=26}\r\n- **TrA-specific resistance-training hypertrophy evidence:** sparse;\r\n- **stretch-mediated hypertrophy magnitude:** essentially unestablished;\r\n- **single-joint moment-arm representation:** poor biomechanical abstraction;\r\n- **pressure/stiffness-based representation:** high mechanical plausibility.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **transversus abdominis = a deep circumferential abdominal-wall muscle whose main mechanical contribution is abdominal compression, intra-abdominal-pressure regulation and multidirectional trunk stiffness, allowing substantial muscular demand during nearly isometric bracing even though it generates little obvious external trunk movement.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/TransverseAbdominis}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	9.70	0.00	1.000	21.97	21.97
28	obliques	Musculus obliquus externus abdominis, Musculus obliquus internus abdominis	Core	Core	925.00	875.95	38.50	Broad Flat/Parallel	0.475	0.525	high	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\n`obliques` is a functional aggregate representing two paired layers of the lateral abdominal wall:\r\n\r\n- **external oblique — obliquus externus abdominis**;\r\n- **internal oblique — obliquus internus abdominis**.\r\n\r\nThese muscles form a crossed, multilayered force-transmission system rather than one homogeneous abdominal muscle.\r\n\r\nTheir major functions include:\r\n\r\n- trunk rotation;\r\n- trunk lateral flexion;\r\n- trunk flexion;\r\n- anti-rotation;\r\n- anti-lateral-flexion;\r\n- abdominal-wall compression;\r\n- regulation of intra-abdominal pressure;\r\n- force transfer between the thorax, pelvis and contralateral limb.\r\n\r\nThe most important rotational distinction is:\r\n\r\n- **external oblique** → primarily contralateral trunk rotation;\r\n- **internal oblique** → primarily ipsilateral trunk rotation.\r\n\r\nTherefore trunk rotation is commonly generated through diagonal cooperation.\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n\r\n- rotation to the left → right external oblique + left internal oblique;\r\n- rotation to the right → left external oblique + right internal oblique.\r\n\r\nThis crossed architecture makes the oblique system particularly important during:\r\n\r\n- gait;\r\n- sprinting;\r\n- throwing;\r\n- cutting;\r\n- unilateral loading;\r\n- resisted rotation;\r\n- anti-rotation tasks.\r\n\r\nFor Agonez, `obliques` should therefore not be modeled simply as a trunk-flexion muscle.\r\n\r\nIts primary identity is:\r\n\r\n> **a multidirectional trunk torque and stabilization system with particularly important rotational, anti-rotational and lateral-flexion functions.**\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\nThe external and internal obliques form two of the major muscular layers of the anterolateral abdominal wall.\r\n\r\nFrom superficial to deep:\r\n\r\n1. external oblique;\r\n2. internal oblique;\r\n3. transversus abdominis.\r\n\r\nTheir differently oriented fibers create a crossed muscular lattice around the trunk.\r\n\r\n## External Oblique\r\n\r\n### Origin\r\n\r\nExternal oblique originates from the external surfaces of the lower ribs, classically ribs approximately **5-12**.\r\n\r\nIts fibers generally run inferomedially.\r\n\r\nThis orientation is often described as analogous to the direction of placing the hands into trouser pockets.\r\n\r\n### Insertion\r\n\r\nIts broad aponeurosis and muscle fibers contribute to attachments including:\r\n\r\n- linea alba;\r\n- anterior iliac crest;\r\n- pubic region;\r\n- inguinal ligament through its inferior aponeurotic border.\r\n\r\nThe aponeurotic portion contributes substantially to the anterior abdominal wall and rectus sheath.\r\n\r\n## Internal Oblique\r\n\r\n### Origin\r\n\r\nInternal oblique originates from regions including:\r\n\r\n- thoracolumbar fascia;\r\n- iliac crest;\r\n- lateral inguinal ligament.\r\n\r\n### Insertion\r\n\r\nIts fibers pass predominantly superomedially and contribute to:\r\n\r\n- lower ribs;\r\n- linea alba;\r\n- pubic and conjoint-tendon regions.\r\n\r\nThe orientation is approximately orthogonal to much of external oblique.\r\n\r\nThis crossed architecture is central to trunk rotation.\r\n\r\n## Rectus Sheath and Aponeurotic Force Transmission\r\n\r\nBoth oblique layers contribute extensively to abdominal aponeuroses.\r\n\r\nThese aponeuroses form portions of the:\r\n\r\n- rectus sheath;\r\n- linea alba;\r\n- anterior abdominal fascial system.\r\n\r\nTherefore oblique force is not transmitted only from a simple muscular origin directly to an insertion.\r\n\r\nThe system distributes tension across broad connective-tissue sheets.\r\n\r\nThis allows force transfer between:\r\n\r\n- left and right abdominal walls;\r\n- ribs;\r\n- pelvis;\r\n- thoracolumbar structures.\r\n\r\n## Regional Architecture\r\n\r\nNeither external nor internal oblique is mechanically homogeneous.\r\n\r\nDifferent superior, middle and inferior fascicles possess different:\r\n\r\n- fiber directions;\r\n- moment arms;\r\n- relationships to the pelvis and ribs.\r\n\r\nInternal oblique in particular exhibits regional functional differentiation.\r\n\r\nTherefore a single `obliques` aggregate is intentionally coarse but practical for exercise-level modelling.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nExternal and internal obliques receive segmental innervation from the lower thoracic and upper lumbar nerves.\r\n\r\nMajor contributors include:\r\n\r\n- thoracoabdominal nerves;\r\n- subcostal nerve;\r\n- upper lumbar branches, particularly for lower internal-oblique regions.\r\n\r\nThis segmental organization matches the broad longitudinal extent of the abdominal wall.\r\n\r\nUnlike a limb muscle supplied by one major peripheral nerve, the obliques can exhibit regional recruitment across different trunk levels.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Trunk Rotation\r\n\r\nRotation is one of the defining functions of the oblique system.\r\n\r\nThe two layers have opposite rotational biases.\r\n\r\n### External Oblique\r\n\r\nA unilateral external oblique tends to rotate the thorax **away from the contracting side**.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n- right external oblique → leftward rotation;\r\n- left external oblique → rightward rotation.\r\n\r\n### Internal Oblique\r\n\r\nA unilateral internal oblique tends to rotate the thorax **toward the contracting side**.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n- right internal oblique → rightward rotation;\r\n- left internal oblique → leftward rotation.\r\n\r\nTherefore active axial rotation usually involves a crossed muscular pair.\r\n\r\n### Example: Left Rotation\r\n\r\nMajor synergistic pair:\r\n\r\n- right external oblique;\r\n- left internal oblique.\r\n\r\n### Example: Right Rotation\r\n\r\nMajor synergistic pair:\r\n\r\n- left external oblique;\r\n- right internal oblique.\r\n\r\nThis diagonal organization is fundamental to rotational exercise modelling.\r\n\r\n## Anti-Rotation\r\n\r\nThe same muscles can generate high force without visible rotation.\r\n\r\nIf an external torque attempts to rotate the trunk, the obliques can contract isometrically to resist it.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- Pallof press;\r\n- unilateral cable loading;\r\n- suitcase-style carries;\r\n- asymmetrically loaded presses or rows.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n> rotation torque can be high while trunk rotation ROM remains approximately zero.\r\n\r\nThis makes the obliques another important case where muscular demand cannot be inferred from movement amplitude alone.\r\n\r\n## Lateral Flexion\r\n\r\nUnilateral contraction of both oblique layers contributes to **ipsilateral trunk lateral flexion**.\r\n\r\nRight external and internal oblique can therefore contribute to rightward side bending.\r\n\r\nThis function overlaps with:\r\n\r\n- quadratus lumborum;\r\n- erector spinae;\r\n- other abdominal musculature.\r\n\r\nUnder unilateral external load, the obliques can also work isometrically to resist lateral flexion.\r\n\r\n## Trunk Flexion\r\n\r\nBilateral contraction contributes to **flexion of the trunk**.\r\n\r\nThis action overlaps strongly with rectus abdominis.\r\n\r\nThe obliques become particularly relevant when trunk flexion includes:\r\n\r\n- rotation;\r\n- lateral flexion;\r\n- asymmetric loading.\r\n\r\nA simple symmetric crunch therefore recruits obliques, but generally provides less unique oblique demand than exercises containing transverse- or frontal-plane torque.\r\n\r\n## Abdominal Compression\r\n\r\nBoth oblique layers compress the abdominal contents.\r\n\r\nTogether with:\r\n\r\n- transversus abdominis;\r\n- rectus abdominis;\r\n- diaphragm;\r\n- pelvic floor,\r\n\r\nthey participate in regulating trunk stiffness and intra-abdominal pressure.\r\n\r\nThis is relevant during high-force lifting.\r\n\r\nThe abdominal wall does not merely create visible spinal movement.\r\n\r\nIt also acts as a pressure-generating and force-transmitting cylinder.\r\n\r\n## Lumbopelvic Stabilization\r\n\r\nThe obliques couple ribcage and pelvis mechanics.\r\n\r\nThey can resist relative movement between these segments during:\r\n\r\n- lifting;\r\n- running;\r\n- unilateral stance;\r\n- upper-limb force production.\r\n\r\nTheir diagonal orientation makes them particularly relevant to cross-body force transfer.\r\n\r\n## Gait and Running\r\n\r\nDuring gait, the thorax and pelvis rotate relative to each other.\r\n\r\nThe oblique system participates in controlling and producing this movement.\r\n\r\nMorphological studies show relationships between repeated rotational athletic activity and oblique muscle morphology, supporting their substantial role during locomotor rotation. :contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nDirect evidence for **stretch-mediated hypertrophy specifically in the internal and external obliques is very limited**.\r\n\r\nThe anatomy nevertheless suggests several positions capable of lengthening the system.\r\n\r\nBecause the fibers are oblique and multidirectional, muscle length depends simultaneously on:\r\n\r\n- trunk rotation;\r\n- lateral flexion;\r\n- spinal extension/flexion;\r\n- rib-pelvis relationship.\r\n\r\nThis makes oblique length considerably harder to represent than that of a simple hinge muscle.\r\n\r\n## Rotation-Based Lengthening\r\n\r\nA rotational muscle is generally lengthened by movement opposite to its concentric rotational action.\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n\r\n- right external oblique produces left rotation;\r\n- rightward trunk rotation therefore tends to lengthen it.\r\n\r\nConversely:\r\n\r\n- right internal oblique contributes to right rotation;\r\n- leftward rotation tends to lengthen it.\r\n\r\nThus a loaded rotational exercise alternates long and short states across diagonal pairs.\r\n\r\n## Lateral Flexion\r\n\r\nContralateral lateral flexion can also lengthen a unilateral oblique system.\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n\r\n- right oblique wall contributes to right lateral flexion;\r\n- leftward side bending lengthens portions of the right abdominal wall.\r\n\r\n## Extension\r\n\r\nTrunk extension can lengthen the anterior/lateral abdominal wall globally.\r\n\r\nHowever, the magnitude differs regionally and should not be assumed uniform across internal and external obliques.\r\n\r\n## Long-Length Resistance\r\n\r\nIn principle, loaded rotation or lateral flexion beginning from a controlled counter-rotated or contralaterally flexed position can expose the obliques to high tension at longer lengths.\r\n\r\nHowever, there is insufficient longitudinal evidence to quantify a special oblique-specific hypertrophic advantage from this strategy.\r\n\r\n## Practical Interpretation\r\n\r\nFor hypertrophy:\r\n\r\n- use a meaningful ROM when performing dynamic rotation or lateral flexion;\r\n- avoid limiting every exercise to very small isometric contractions;\r\n- do not pursue extreme spinal rotation simply to create additional stretch;\r\n- prioritize high muscular torque under controlled conditions.\r\n\r\nThe spine is a multisegment structure with passive constraints, so maximizing end-range rotation under load is not analogous to stretching a calf or biceps.\r\n\r\n## SMH Confidence\r\n\r\n`smh_factor` for the obliques should therefore be interpreted conservatively.\r\n\r\nThe evidence supports:\r\n\r\n- clear anatomical length changes;\r\n- high force production during rotation and stabilization;\r\n- trainability of abdominal musculature;\r\n\r\nbut not a precisely established oblique-specific stretch-mediated hypertrophy multiplier.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Cable Rotation\r\n\r\nCable trunk rotation directly trains the obliques through rotational torque.\r\n\r\nA good setup should allow:\r\n\r\n- rotation through the trunk;\r\n- controlled pelvic position;\r\n- meaningful resistance across ROM;\r\n- minimal uncontrolled momentum.\r\n\r\nDepending on the objective, the pelvis can either:\r\n\r\n- remain relatively fixed to emphasize trunk rotation;\r\n- participate as part of a whole-body rotational task.\r\n\r\nThese are different exercises mechanically.\r\n\r\n## Pallof Press\r\n\r\nThe Pallof press is primarily an **anti-rotation exercise**.\r\n\r\nThe cable produces an external rotational moment around the trunk.\r\n\r\nThe obliques generate opposing isometric torque.\r\n\r\nTherefore the exercise can create meaningful oblique demand despite:\r\n\r\n`trunk_rotation_ROM ≈ 0`.\r\n\r\nThis makes it particularly useful for modelling stabilization demand.\r\n\r\nThe load should not be evaluated solely by cable-stack mass.\r\n\r\nTorque increases with:\r\n\r\n- cable force;\r\n- horizontal distance between the line of force and trunk axis;\r\n- arm extension.\r\n\r\nExtending the arms increases the external moment arm and therefore rotational demand.\r\n\r\n## Cable Woodchop\r\n\r\nWoodchop variations combine:\r\n\r\n- trunk rotation;\r\n- trunk flexion/extension depending on path;\r\n- shoulder movement;\r\n- pelvic rotation.\r\n\r\nThey can provide high oblique loading but are less isolated than pure cable rotation.\r\n\r\nHigh-to-low and low-to-high variants create different force directions.\r\n\r\n## Side Bend\r\n\r\nLoaded side bends train lateral-flexion torque.\r\n\r\nThe obliques share this demand with:\r\n\r\n- quadratus lumborum;\r\n- erector spinae.\r\n\r\nTherefore side bends are useful but should not receive an exclusively oblique recruitment vector.\r\n\r\n## Side Plank\r\n\r\nThe side plank creates large **anti-lateral-flexion** demands.\r\n\r\nThe downside oblique wall generates substantial isometric torque to prevent the pelvis and trunk from collapsing.\r\n\r\nSystematic EMG evidence supports substantial oblique activity across plank and side-plank families. :contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}\r\n\r\nThe exercise also involves:\r\n\r\n- gluteus medius;\r\n- quadratus lumborum;\r\n- shoulder stabilizers.\r\n\r\nThus it is a multi-region stabilization exercise rather than isolated abdominal work.\r\n\r\n## Copenhagen Plank\r\n\r\nThe Copenhagen plank is primarily an adductor exercise but also creates significant trunk stabilization demand.\r\n\r\nThe obliques contribute to maintaining:\r\n\r\n- lateral trunk alignment;\r\n- pelvic position;\r\n- anti-rotation.\r\n\r\nHowever, the working hip adductors should remain the primary local muscular target.\r\n\r\nCopenhagen should therefore receive meaningful but secondary `obliques` demand.\r\n\r\n## Russian Twist\r\n\r\nA weighted Russian twist can produce substantial rotational torque.\r\n\r\nHowever, execution varies dramatically.\r\n\r\nUseful versions involve controlled rotation of the trunk relative to the pelvis.\r\n\r\nPoorly standardized versions can become mostly:\r\n\r\n- arm movement;\r\n- rapid momentum;\r\n- hip-flexor stabilization.\r\n\r\nFor Agonez, the actual trunk rotation angle and load position matter more than the exercise name.\r\n\r\n## Hanging Oblique Knee Raise\r\n\r\nAlternating hanging knee raises combine:\r\n\r\n- hip flexion;\r\n- pelvic rotation;\r\n- trunk stabilization.\r\n\r\nThe obliques can contribute substantially to the rotational and pelvic-control component.\r\n\r\nHowever, iliopsoas remains heavily involved in lifting the legs.\r\n\r\nThis should therefore be modeled as a mixed:\r\n\r\n- hip-flexor;\r\n- abdominal rotational;\r\n- shoulder/grip stabilization exercise.\r\n\r\n## Crunch With Rotation\r\n\r\nAdding rotation to a crunch increases oblique specificity compared with pure sagittal flexion.\r\n\r\nHowever, the load is still shared with rectus abdominis.\r\n\r\nThe exercise becomes particularly useful when rotation is produced through the trunk rather than merely moving the elbow across the body.\r\n\r\n## Heavy Compound Lifting\r\n\r\nSquats, deadlifts, rows and overhead presses can create substantial oblique stabilization demand.\r\n\r\nThe magnitude depends on asymmetry.\r\n\r\n### Symmetric Bilateral Loading\r\n\r\nMostly:\r\n- bracing;\r\n- anti-flexion;\r\n- pressure generation.\r\n\r\n### Unilateral Loading\r\n\r\nAdds:\r\n- anti-rotation;\r\n- anti-lateral-flexion.\r\n\r\nTherefore a one-arm carry or unilateral cable press can create much greater oblique-specific stabilization demand than a symmetric exercise of comparable total load.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Train Multiple Torque Directions\r\n\r\nBecause the obliques are multidirectional, a complete program does not need dozens of exercises, but it benefits from covering more than pure spinal flexion.\r\n\r\nA simple combination might include:\r\n\r\n1. **anti-rotation**;\r\n2. **dynamic rotation or anti-lateral-flexion**.\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n\r\n- Pallof press;\r\n- side plank or controlled cable rotation.\r\n\r\n## Weekly Volume\r\n\r\nA practical starting point for direct oblique hypertrophy/strength can be approximately:\r\n\r\n- **4-8 hard direct sets per week**.\r\n\r\nThis should account for substantial indirect stabilization from compound exercises.\r\n\r\nAthletes performing large amounts of:\r\n\r\n- sprinting;\r\n- throwing;\r\n- combat work;\r\n- rotational sport\r\n\r\nmay already accumulate additional oblique loading.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nTwo to three weekly exposures are practical.\r\n\r\nCore exercises generally have low systemic cost, but excessive abdominal fatigue can interfere with:\r\n\r\n- squats;\r\n- deadlifts;\r\n- unsupported rows;\r\n- overhead work.\r\n\r\nTherefore direct high-effort trunk work should be scheduled with compound performance in mind.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\nDynamic cable exercises work well in approximately:\r\n\r\n- 8-15 repetitions;\r\n- 12-20 repetitions.\r\n\r\nFor isometric exercises such as Pallof press or side plank, progression can be based on:\r\n\r\n- resistance;\r\n- lever length;\r\n- duration;\r\n- repetitions of controlled holds.\r\n\r\nThere is no special high-repetition requirement for obliques.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nStable dynamic rotation can generally be trained close to muscular failure.\r\n\r\nFor loaded spinal movements, technical quality should remain the limiting criterion.\r\n\r\nFor anti-rotation tasks, failure occurs when:\r\n\r\n- trunk rotation begins;\r\n- pelvic position can no longer be maintained;\r\n- the intended lever position collapses.\r\n\r\n## Progressive Overload\r\n\r\nProgression can use:\r\n\r\n- increased load;\r\n- larger lever arm;\r\n- larger controlled ROM;\r\n- longer isometric duration;\r\n- increased repetitions.\r\n\r\nFor Pallof press, simply extending the arms farther can meaningfully increase torque even without changing cable load.\r\n\r\n## Avoid Exercise Inflation\r\n\r\nCore programming often accumulates many nearly equivalent low-load exercises.\r\n\r\nFor hypertrophy and strength, a few progressively overloadable movements are generally more useful than a large collection of activation drills.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## External and Internal Oblique Rotate in Opposite Directions\r\n\r\nThe crossed fiber orientations create opposite unilateral rotational actions:\r\n\r\n- external oblique → contralateral rotation;\r\n- internal oblique → ipsilateral rotation.\r\n\r\nThis means trunk rotation is generated by muscles on **both sides of the body simultaneously**, but from different abdominal layers.\r\n\r\n## Left Rotation Uses a Diagonal Pair\r\n\r\nLeftward rotation is produced substantially by:\r\n\r\n- right external oblique;\r\n- left internal oblique.\r\n\r\nThis diagonal organization resembles a muscular sling connecting the ribcage and pelvis.\r\n\r\n## Rotation Changes Muscle Geometry Substantially\r\n\r\nBiomechanical imaging shows that trunk twisting alters abdominal-oblique geometry and moment arms, with some moment-arm changes approaching approximately twofold across tested positions. :contentReference[oaicite:3]{index=3}\r\n\r\nTherefore a fixed rotational leverage coefficient is only a simplification.\r\n\r\n## Obliques Can Hypertrophy Asymmetrically\r\n\r\nAthletes exposed to asymmetric rotational demands can develop side-to-side differences in oblique morphology.\r\n\r\nStudies in sprinters and hurdlers have documented measurable asymmetries, illustrating that repeated sport-specific torque can influence abdominal muscle structure. :contentReference[oaicite:4]{index=4}\r\n\r\n## Anti-Rotation Is Still Rotation Training\r\n\r\nDuring a Pallof press, no visible rotation occurs if the exercise succeeds.\r\n\r\nYet the obliques generate rotational torque continuously.\r\n\r\nThis provides another clear example of:\r\n\r\n> **joint motion is not required for high muscle force.**\r\n\r\n## The Abdominal Wall Is a Pressure System\r\n\r\nThe obliques do more than move the spine.\r\n\r\nTogether with the other trunk muscles, diaphragm and pelvic floor, they contribute to abdominal pressure and stiffness.\r\n\r\nThis allows the trunk to transmit forces between upper and lower limbs during high-force tasks.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Definition of This Aggregate\r\n\r\n`obliques` combines:\r\n\r\n- `external_oblique`;\r\n- `internal_oblique`.\r\n\r\nIt should not automatically include:\r\n\r\n- `transversus_abdominis`;\r\n- `rectus_abdominis`.\r\n\r\nThose muscles have sufficiently different architecture and primary mechanical roles to justify separate modelling when available.\r\n\r\nBecause EO and IO have opposite rotational actions, aggregate rotational demand must be interpreted carefully.\r\n\r\nThe aggregate can represent:\r\n\r\n- total oblique-system demand;\r\n\r\nbut cannot encode which diagonal pair is active without laterality.\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\nThe major dimensions should be:\r\n\r\n1. **trunk rotation torque**;\r\n2. **anti-rotation torque**;\r\n3. **lateral-flexion / anti-lateral-flexion torque**;\r\n4. secondary trunk-flexion contribution;\r\n5. trunk stabilization / abdominal compression.\r\n\r\nThis is a much richer model than:\r\n\r\n`obliques = rotation`.\r\n\r\n## Rotation Pairing\r\n\r\nIf Agonez remains bilateral and non-side-specific, rotation can simply assign high demand to the aggregate.\r\n\r\nIf laterality is introduced, the mapping should be:\r\n\r\n### Left Rotation\r\n\r\n- right EO ↑;\r\n- left IO ↑.\r\n\r\n### Right Rotation\r\n\r\n- left EO ↑;\r\n- right IO ↑.\r\n\r\nThe opposite diagonal muscles can still contribute through stabilization/co-contraction but should receive smaller primary rotational allocations.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Allocation\r\n\r\n### Standing Cable Pallof Press\r\n\r\nHigh `obliques` demand through anti-rotation.\r\n\r\nAlso meaningful:\r\n- transverse abdominis;\r\n- rectus abdominis;\r\n- gluteus medius depending on stance;\r\n- shoulder stabilizers.\r\n\r\n### Weighted Russian Twist\r\n\r\nHigh oblique demand if true trunk rotation occurs under load.\r\n\r\nAlso meaningful:\r\n- rectus abdominis;\r\n- iliopsoas depending on posture;\r\n- spinal stabilizers.\r\n\r\n### Hanging Oblique Knee Raise\r\n\r\nHigh oblique demand through pelvic/trunk rotation.\r\n\r\nAlso very high:\r\n- iliopsoas.\r\n\r\nMeaningful:\r\n- grip;\r\n- lats/scapular stabilizers.\r\n\r\n### Copenhagen Plank\r\n\r\nModerate oblique anti-lateral-flexion and stabilization demand.\r\n\r\nVery high adductor demand remains primary.\r\n\r\n### Dragon Flag\r\n\r\nHigh global trunk anti-extension demand.\r\n\r\nObliques should receive substantial allocation alongside other abdominal muscles.\r\n\r\n### Kneeling Cable Crunch\r\n\r\nModerate oblique contribution through trunk flexion.\r\n\r\nRectus abdominis should generally receive the larger sagittal-plane share.\r\n\r\n### Bilateral Leg Press\r\n\r\nLow oblique demand because external support minimizes trunk stabilization.\r\n\r\n### Pendlay Row\r\n\r\nMeaningful oblique isometric bracing, but erector spinae dominates anti-flexion.\r\n\r\n### Unilateral Cable Exercise\r\n\r\nPotentially high oblique demand if the load creates substantial rotational or lateral-flexion moment.\r\n\r\n## Pallof Press Torque\r\n\r\nFor Pallof-type exercises:\r\n\r\n`M_rotation ≈ F_cable × perpendicular_distance_to_trunk_axis`\r\n\r\nTherefore extending the arms increases the external rotational moment.\r\n\r\nThis means two sets using identical stack loads but different arm positions can produce substantially different oblique demands.\r\n\r\nFor Agonez this lever-arm effect should ideally be represented explicitly.\r\n\r\n## Russian Twist Torque\r\n\r\nFor weighted rotation:\r\n\r\n`M_rotation ≈ load × horizontal_radial_distance`\r\n\r\nHolding the weight close to the chest reduces rotational inertia and external torque.\r\n\r\nExtending the arms increases the mechanical demand.\r\n\r\nAgain:\r\n\r\n> external kilograms alone do not define trunk torque.\r\n\r\n## Rotation vs Anti-Rotation\r\n\r\nFrom the muscle-force perspective, both can generate high oblique demand.\r\n\r\nThe distinction is contraction mode:\r\n\r\n### Dynamic Rotation\r\n\r\n- concentric/eccentric changes in muscle length;\r\n- visible spinal/trunk motion.\r\n\r\n### Anti-Rotation\r\n\r\n- predominantly isometric;\r\n- high torque with minimal ROM.\r\n\r\nFor ETU, `ROM = 0` should therefore not imply a low stimulus.\r\n\r\n## Length-Tension Model\r\n\r\nA single scalar `E_LT` is particularly approximate for this aggregate because EO and IO on opposite sides can simultaneously occupy different length states.\r\n\r\nA better conceptual model is:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_obliques = f(rotation, lateral_flexion, flexion_extension, side, layer)`\r\n\r\nFor a bilateral KISS implementation:\r\n\r\n- neutral trunk → intermediate;\r\n- controlled rotation/lateral flexion → one diagonal pair lengthens while the opposite pair shortens;\r\n- sagittal extension tends to lengthen the anterior-lateral abdominal wall.\r\n\r\nTherefore the aggregate `E_LT` should avoid extreme precision.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nDirect oblique-specific stretch-mediated hypertrophy evidence is weak.\r\n\r\nThe defensible interpretation is:\r\n\r\n- rotation and lateral flexion clearly alter oblique muscle geometry; :contentReference[oaicite:5]{index=5}\r\n- loaded movement through meaningful ROM can train longer states;\r\n- direct evidence for a unique long-length hypertrophic advantage is lacking;\r\n- high-force anti-rotation remains valuable despite minimal muscle excursion.\r\n\r\nTherefore `smh_factor` should be conservative and should **not penalize isometric anti-rotation exercises excessively**.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **external/internal oblique gross anatomy:** high; :contentReference[oaicite:6]{index=6}\r\n- **EO contralateral / IO ipsilateral rotational roles:** high;\r\n- **lateral-flexion contribution:** high;\r\n- **abdominal compression/stabilization:** high;\r\n- **high activity during core stabilization exercises:** supported by systematic EMG literature; :contentReference[oaicite:7]{index=7}\r\n- **rotation-dependent geometry and moment arms:** directly demonstrated; :contentReference[oaicite:8]{index=8}\r\n- **sport-related morphological adaptation:** observationally supported; :contentReference[oaicite:9]{index=9}\r\n- **direct hypertrophy programming evidence:** limited;\r\n- **stretch-mediated hypertrophy magnitude:** poorly established;\r\n- **precise EO-vs-IO exercise allocation:** strongly task- and side-dependent.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **obliques = a crossed external/internal abdominal muscle system that generates rotation, anti-rotation and lateral-flexion torque while coupling the thorax to the pelvis, with high force possible during both dynamic movement and near-isometric trunk stabilization.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/Obliques}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	10.72	0.00	1.000	81.71	81.71
14	trapezius_lower	Musculus Trapezius lower	Upper	Back	200.00	189.39	7.00	Parallel/Convergent	0.550	0.450	very_high	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\nThe **lower trapezius** is the inferior functional region of the trapezius muscle.\r\n\r\nIts principal scapular roles are:\r\n\r\n- **scapular upward rotation**;\r\n- **scapular posterior tilt**;\r\n- contribution to scapular retraction;\r\n- depression of the medial scapular region depending on arm position;\r\n- stabilization of the scapula during humeral elevation.\r\n\r\nIt should be distinguished from:\r\n\r\n- `trapezius_middle`, which is more strongly specialized for scapular retraction;\r\n- `trapezius_upper`, which contributes strongly to clavicular/scapular elevation and upward rotation;\r\n- `serratus_anterior`, which produces upward rotation together with protraction and posterior tilt;\r\n- `rhomboids`, which retract but have a downward-rotation bias.\r\n\r\nThe lower trapezius is therefore not simply a "scapular depressor."\r\n\r\nDuring overhead movement, its more important identity is:\r\n\r\n> **a medial scapular stabilizer and upward-rotation/posterior-tilt contributor that works with serratus anterior and upper trapezius to orient the glenoid for humeral elevation.**\r\n\r\nFor Agonez, this distinction matters greatly.\r\n\r\nA movement can involve substantial lower-trapezius force even if the scapula is not visibly moving downward.\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\n## Origin\r\n\r\nThe lower fibers of trapezius arise from the lower thoracic vertebral region through attachments associated with:\r\n\r\n- lower thoracic spinous processes;\r\n- supraspinous ligaments.\r\n\r\nThe exact boundary between middle and lower trapezius is functional rather than perfectly discrete.\r\n\r\nThe fibers run superiorly and laterally toward the scapula.\r\n\r\n## Insertion\r\n\r\nLower trapezius inserts primarily near the **medial end / root of the scapular spine**.\r\n\r\nIts line of pull from the inferior thoracic spine toward this scapular attachment creates a force directed:\r\n\r\n- medially;\r\n- inferiorly.\r\n\r\nBecause this force is applied medial to the scapular rotational center, it can generate substantial **upward-rotation torque** despite its downward-directed translational component.\r\n\r\nThis is a key biomechanical point.\r\n\r\n## Regional Continuity\r\n\r\nTrapezius is one continuous muscle with:\r\n\r\n- upper;\r\n- middle;\r\n- lower\r\n\r\nregions rather than three completely independent muscles.\r\n\r\nHowever, regional fiber orientation differs enough that separating them for Agonez is mechanically justified.\r\n\r\nThe regions can differ in:\r\n\r\n- scapular moment arms;\r\n- activation;\r\n- functional role.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nTrapezius receives its primary motor innervation from the **spinal accessory nerve (cranial nerve XI)**.\r\n\r\nCervical spinal nerves, particularly approximately:\r\n\r\n- C3;\r\n- C4\r\n\r\nprovide substantial sensory/proprioceptive contributions.\r\n\r\nThis differs from many neighboring scapular muscles, which are supplied directly by branches of the brachial plexus.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Scapular Upward Rotation\r\n\r\nOne of the lower trapezius's most important functions is **upward rotation of the scapula**.\r\n\r\nDuring upward rotation:\r\n\r\n- the glenoid turns superiorly;\r\n- the inferior angle moves laterally.\r\n\r\nLower trapezius contributes through its pull on the medial scapular spine.\r\n\r\nThis action works synergistically with:\r\n\r\n- upper trapezius;\r\n- serratus anterior.\r\n\r\nTogether these muscles form the principal scapular upward-rotation force system during arm elevation.\r\n\r\n## Posterior Tilt\r\n\r\nLower trapezius also contributes to **posterior tilt of the scapula**.\r\n\r\nPosterior tilt is important during overhead elevation because it helps orient the acromion and glenoid relative to the humerus.\r\n\r\nThis function overlaps especially with:\r\n\r\n- serratus anterior.\r\n\r\nThe two muscles therefore cooperate not only in upward rotation but also in three-dimensional scapular positioning.\r\n\r\n## Scapular Retraction\r\n\r\nLower trapezius can contribute to **scapular retraction** because its fibers pull the scapula medially.\r\n\r\nHowever, pure retraction is more strongly associated with:\r\n\r\n- middle trapezius;\r\n- rhomboids.\r\n\r\nTherefore a horizontal row should generally allocate:\r\n\r\n- high `trapezius_middle`;\r\n- high `rhomboids`;\r\n- lower or moderate `trapezius_lower`\r\n\r\nunless the movement also creates substantial depression/upward-rotation demands.\r\n\r\n## Scapular Depression\r\n\r\nLower trapezius can contribute to depression of the scapula, particularly through its inferiorly directed force.\r\n\r\nHowever, treating it as a simple depressor is incomplete.\r\n\r\nThe rotational effect of its force is often more important than its vertical translation.\r\n\r\nDuring overhead elevation it can be active while the scapula as a whole is moving **upward**, because it participates in upward rotation rather than gross downward translation.\r\n\r\nThis is a major modelling distinction.\r\n\r\n## Scapular Stabilization\r\n\r\nLower trapezius stabilizes the medial scapular region during upper-limb force production.\r\n\r\nIt can therefore generate substantial force during tasks with relatively little visible scapular motion.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- overhead pressing;\r\n- loaded carries;\r\n- controlled arm elevation;\r\n- certain pulling patterns.\r\n\r\nAgain:\r\n\r\n`scapular_ROM ≈ 0`\r\n\r\ndoes not imply:\r\n\r\n`lower_trapezius_force ≈ 0`.\r\n\r\n# Relationship With Serratus Anterior\r\n\r\nLower trapezius and serratus anterior form an especially important functional partnership.\r\n\r\nBoth can contribute to:\r\n\r\n- upward rotation;\r\n- posterior tilt.\r\n\r\nBut their translational actions differ.\r\n\r\n### Lower Trapezius\r\n\r\nBias:\r\n- medial stabilization;\r\n- retraction/depression component;\r\n- upward rotation;\r\n- posterior tilt.\r\n\r\n### Serratus Anterior\r\n\r\nBias:\r\n- protraction;\r\n- upward rotation;\r\n- posterior tilt;\r\n- external rotation of scapula.\r\n\r\nTherefore these muscles can create compatible rotational effects while pulling the scapula in different linear directions.\r\n\r\nThis is a classic force-couple concept.\r\n\r\n# Relationship With Upper Trapezius\r\n\r\nUpper and lower trapezius are also mechanically complementary.\r\n\r\n### Upper Trapezius\r\n\r\nContributes strongly to:\r\n- clavicular elevation;\r\n- scapular elevation;\r\n- upward rotation.\r\n\r\n### Lower Trapezius\r\n\r\nContributes strongly to:\r\n- upward rotation;\r\n- posterior tilt;\r\n- medial/inferior stabilization.\r\n\r\nTheir opposing vertical force components help create rotation rather than simply translating the scapula upward or downward.\r\n\r\n# Relationship With Rhomboids\r\n\r\nBoth lower trapezius and rhomboids can retract the scapula.\r\n\r\nHowever:\r\n\r\n### Lower Trapezius\r\n\r\nBias:\r\n- upward rotation.\r\n\r\n### Rhomboids\r\n\r\nBias:\r\n- downward rotation.\r\n\r\nThis is one of the most important distinctions for overhead movement modelling.\r\n\r\nA model that assigns the same function to all "mid-back retractors" will become mechanically inconsistent.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nDirect longitudinal evidence for **lower-trapezius-specific stretch-mediated hypertrophy** is limited.\r\n\r\nThe muscle's length state depends on scapular position.\r\n\r\nIt is generally lengthened by combinations of:\r\n\r\n- scapular protraction;\r\n- elevation;\r\n- anterior tilt;\r\n- downward-rotation geometry depending on region.\r\n\r\n## Protraction\r\n\r\nScapular protraction moves the medial scapular attachment laterally away from the thoracic origin.\r\n\r\nThis lengthens the trapezius broadly, including the lower region.\r\n\r\nTherefore a movement beginning from controlled protraction can expose lower trapezius to greater length.\r\n\r\n## Elevation\r\n\r\nBecause lower fibers pull inferiorly toward the thorax, scapular elevation can lengthen them.\r\n\r\nThis is one reason lower trapezius may experience meaningful length during overhead arm positions.\r\n\r\n## Posterior Tilt\r\n\r\nBecause lower trapezius contributes to posterior tilt, an anteriorly tilted starting position can lengthen relevant fibers.\r\n\r\nHowever, exact regional length changes are three-dimensional and not well represented by a single angle.\r\n\r\n## Rows\r\n\r\nRows beginning from scapular protraction can provide some lower-trapezius lengthening.\r\n\r\nHowever, if the movement is primarily horizontal retraction, middle trapezius and rhomboids should still dominate.\r\n\r\n## Overhead Elevation\r\n\r\nArm elevation creates substantial scapular upward rotation and posterior tilt.\r\n\r\nLower trapezius can therefore shorten actively from an elevated/protracted starting state while generating high stabilizing force.\r\n\r\nThis provides a plausible long-to-short excursion.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nThe evidence-based position is conservative:\r\n\r\n- scapular position clearly alters lower-trapezius length;\r\n- meaningful long-length active loading is mechanically possible;\r\n- direct hypertrophy evidence is sparse;\r\n- no strong lower-trapezius-specific SMH multiplier is established.\r\n\r\nTherefore `smh_factor` should remain moderate/conservative.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Overhead Arm Elevation\r\n\r\nLower trapezius is highly relevant during overhead movement because upward rotation and posterior tilt are required.\r\n\r\nExercises include:\r\n\r\n- overhead press;\r\n- lateral raise into high elevation;\r\n- Y-raise;\r\n- overhead cable movements.\r\n\r\nThese can produce meaningful lower-trapezius demand even though the exercise is not classified as "back training."\r\n\r\n## Prone Y-Raise\r\n\r\nThe prone Y-raise is one of the most specific lower-trapezius exercises.\r\n\r\nThe arms are elevated in a high-abduction/scaption angle, typically forming a Y shape.\r\n\r\nThis configuration promotes:\r\n\r\n- scapular upward rotation;\r\n- posterior tilt;\r\n- retraction/depression components.\r\n\r\nExternal load should usually remain modest because the long arm lever creates substantial shoulder/scapular torque.\r\n\r\n## Incline Bench Y-Raise\r\n\r\nUsing an incline bench increases stability and reduces the need for trunk stabilization.\r\n\r\nThis makes it easier to focus on:\r\n\r\n- scapular movement;\r\n- lower-trapezius force.\r\n\r\nIt is often preferable to free-standing variants for hypertrophy-oriented loading.\r\n\r\n## Cable Y-Raise\r\n\r\nCable resistance can maintain tension through a larger portion of ROM.\r\n\r\nA cable Y-raise can therefore be useful for progressive overload.\r\n\r\nIts demand is shared with:\r\n\r\n- deltoid;\r\n- serratus anterior;\r\n- rotator cuff;\r\n- upper trapezius depending on arm path.\r\n\r\n## Face Pull\r\n\r\nFace pulls can recruit lower trapezius through:\r\n\r\n- scapular retraction;\r\n- posterior tilt;\r\n- stabilization.\r\n\r\nHowever, the exercise is distributed across many muscles:\r\n\r\n- posterior deltoid;\r\n- external rotator cuff;\r\n- rhomboids;\r\n- middle trapezius.\r\n\r\nLower trapezius should receive meaningful but not dominant allocation.\r\n\r\n## Row\r\n\r\nHorizontal rows recruit lower trapezius to some degree.\r\n\r\nHowever, if the scapular action is mainly pure retraction:\r\n\r\n- middle trapezius;\r\n- rhomboids\r\n\r\nshould receive larger shares.\r\n\r\nLower-trapezius demand rises if the exercise includes:\r\n\r\n- depression;\r\n- upward-rotation stabilization;\r\n- diagonal scapular movement.\r\n\r\n## Overhead Press\r\n\r\nOverhead pressing should receive meaningful lower-trapezius demand.\r\n\r\nThe scapula must progressively:\r\n\r\n- upwardly rotate;\r\n- posteriorly tilt.\r\n\r\nThe lower trapezius participates with serratus anterior and upper trapezius.\r\n\r\nThis is mostly a scapulothoracic-support role, not humeral pressing torque.\r\n\r\n## Lateral Raise\r\n\r\nAs humeral elevation increases, scapular upward rotation becomes increasingly important.\r\n\r\nTherefore lower-trapezius demand should generally rise through higher portions of a lateral raise.\r\n\r\nAt low humeral angles:\r\n\r\n- deltoid and cuff dominate.\r\n\r\nAt higher angles:\r\n\r\n- serratus/trapezius contribution becomes more important.\r\n\r\n## Cable Lateral Raise\r\n\r\nA cable lateral raise carried through substantial elevation should therefore receive meaningful lower-trapezius demand.\r\n\r\nThis is particularly true if the scapula is allowed to upwardly rotate naturally rather than being forcibly held depressed.\r\n\r\n## Pulldown\r\n\r\nPulldowns can involve lower trapezius through scapular depression and control.\r\n\r\nHowever, vertical pulling also involves substantial:\r\n\r\n- latissimus;\r\n- teres major;\r\n- elbow flexors.\r\n\r\nLower trapezius should generally remain secondary.\r\n\r\n## Pull-Up\r\n\r\nPull-ups can recruit lower trapezius during scapular control and depression.\r\n\r\nA strict active-hang-to-pull pattern can create meaningful lower-trap demand.\r\n\r\nHowever, the major prime movers remain:\r\n\r\n- lats;\r\n- teres major;\r\n- elbow flexors.\r\n\r\n## Scapular Pull-Up\r\n\r\nA scapular pull-up emphasizes movement of the scapula while keeping the elbows relatively extended.\r\n\r\nThis can recruit:\r\n\r\n- lower trapezius;\r\n- latissimus;\r\n- serratus;\r\n- other scapular stabilizers.\r\n\r\nIts exact vector depends on whether the movement is mainly:\r\n\r\n- depression;\r\n- retraction;\r\n- upward/downward rotation control.\r\n\r\nIt should not be treated as pure lower-trapezius isolation.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Dedicated Lower-Trapezius Work Can Be Useful\r\n\r\nUnlike middle trapezius, lower trapezius is not always maximally loaded by ordinary horizontal rows.\r\n\r\nA program dominated by:\r\n\r\n- bench press;\r\n- pulldown;\r\n- rows\r\n\r\nmay still benefit from some direct high-angle scapular work.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- Y-raises;\r\n- high-angle cable raises;\r\n- controlled overhead scapular work.\r\n\r\n## Weekly Volume\r\n\r\nIf lower trapezius development is a priority, a practical starting range is approximately:\r\n\r\n- **4-8 direct or strongly contributory sets per week**.\r\n\r\nIndirect exposure also comes from:\r\n\r\n- overhead presses;\r\n- high-elevation lateral raises;\r\n- face pulls;\r\n- pull-ups.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nTwo to four weekly exposures are practical.\r\n\r\nThe muscle is relatively small and direct exercises usually create little systemic fatigue.\r\n\r\nHowever, excessive pre-fatigue before heavy overhead work may reduce scapular control.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\nLower-trapezius isolation work is often practical in:\r\n\r\n- 10-20 reps;\r\n- 15-30 reps.\r\n\r\nThis is largely because long-arm exercises generate high torque with small external loads.\r\n\r\nIt does not mean the muscle requires high repetitions biologically.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nStable Y-raise and cable variants can be trained close to local failure:\r\n\r\n- approximately 0-2 RIR.\r\n\r\nTechnical failure occurs when the movement becomes dominated by:\r\n\r\n- excessive shrugging;\r\n- lumbar extension;\r\n- humeral compensation;\r\n- loss of scapular posterior tilt/upward rotation.\r\n\r\n## Heavy Rows\r\n\r\nHeavy rows can provide large absolute scapular loads but may be limited by:\r\n\r\n- lats;\r\n- elbow flexors;\r\n- spinal extensors.\r\n\r\nTherefore they are not necessarily the most specific lower-trap hypertrophy exercises.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## Lower Trapezius Can Help Move the Scapula Upward While Pulling Downward\r\n\r\nThis apparent paradox disappears when torque is considered.\r\n\r\nIts force vector pulls the medial scapula inferiorly, but because of where that force acts relative to the scapular rotational axis, it can produce **upward rotation**.\r\n\r\nThis is why:\r\n\r\n> direction of muscle pull != direction of whole-bone translation.\r\n\r\n## "Keep Your Shoulders Down" Can Be Mechanically Wrong Overhead\r\n\r\nDuring overhead elevation, the scapula normally upwardly rotates and may elevate.\r\n\r\nForcibly depressing the shoulders throughout the movement can interfere with normal scapular mechanics.\r\n\r\nLower trapezius participates in upward rotation; it is not simply a muscle that pins the scapula downward.\r\n\r\n## Lower Trap and Rhomboids Are Not Interchangeable\r\n\r\nBoth can retract.\r\n\r\nBut their rotational biases differ:\r\n\r\n`lower trapezius → upward rotation`\r\n\r\n`rhomboids → downward rotation`.\r\n\r\nThis makes them very different during overhead tasks.\r\n\r\n## Lower Trap and Serratus Can Both Be Active Despite Opposite Translation\r\n\r\nSerratus tends to protract.\r\n\r\nLower trapezius tends to retract/depress.\r\n\r\nYet both can generate:\r\n\r\n- upward rotation;\r\n- posterior tilt.\r\n\r\nThey therefore cooperate rotationally while opposing one another translationally.\r\n\r\nThis is a textbook force-couple behavior.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\n`trapezius_lower` should primarily receive demand from:\r\n\r\n1. **scapular upward-rotation torque**;\r\n2. **posterior-tilt torque**;\r\n3. medial scapular stabilization;\r\n4. secondary retraction torque;\r\n5. secondary depression torque.\r\n\r\nIt should not be modeled simply as:\r\n\r\n`lower_trapezius = scapular_depression`.\r\n\r\nThat would substantially underrepresent its function during arm elevation.\r\n\r\n## Upward-Rotation Force Sharing\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`T_upward_rotation =\r\n    serratus_anterior\r\n  + trapezius_upper\r\n  + trapezius_lower`\r\n\r\nThese muscles should not receive equal shares.\r\n\r\nForce sharing depends on:\r\n\r\n- humeral elevation;\r\n- scapular plane;\r\n- external load;\r\n- protraction/retraction requirement.\r\n\r\nA useful KISS rule is:\r\n\r\n### Upward Rotation + Protraction\r\n\r\nIncrease:\r\n- serratus anterior.\r\n\r\n### Upward Rotation + Medial Stabilization / Retraction\r\n\r\nIncrease:\r\n- lower trapezius.\r\n\r\n### Upward Rotation + Elevation\r\n\r\nIncrease:\r\n- upper trapezius.\r\n\r\n## Retraction Force Sharing\r\n\r\nFor pure scapular retraction:\r\n\r\n`T_retraction =\r\n    trapezius_middle\r\n  + rhomboids\r\n  + trapezius_lower`\r\n\r\nwith:\r\n\r\n- middle trapezius typically highest;\r\n- rhomboids high;\r\n- lower trapezius secondary.\r\n\r\nIf the movement also requires upward rotation/posterior tilt, lower-trapezius share rises.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Allocation\r\n\r\n### Cable Face Pull\r\n\r\nModerate lower-trapezius demand.\r\n\r\nHigh:\r\n- posterior deltoid;\r\n- posterior cuff;\r\n- rhomboids;\r\n- middle trapezius.\r\n\r\n### Wide-Grip Seated Cable Row\r\n\r\nLow-to-moderate lower-trapezius demand if primarily horizontal retraction.\r\n\r\nHigher:\r\n- middle trapezius;\r\n- rhomboids.\r\n\r\n### Chest-Supported Dumbbell Row\r\n\r\nModerate lower-trapezius demand depending on scapular path.\r\n\r\n### Pendlay Row\r\n\r\nModerate lower-trapezius demand plus high global stabilization cost.\r\n\r\n### Seated Dumbbell Overhead Press\r\n\r\nModerate-to-high lower-trapezius demand.\r\n\r\nAlso high:\r\n- serratus anterior;\r\n- upper trapezius;\r\n- deltoid;\r\n- rotator cuff.\r\n\r\n### Arnold Press\r\n\r\nModerate-to-high lower-trapezius demand through progressive scapular upward rotation.\r\n\r\n### Single-Arm Cable Lateral Raise\r\n\r\nMeaningful lower-trapezius demand, especially as elevation increases.\r\n\r\n### Straight-Arm Cable Pulldown\r\n\r\nLow-to-moderate lower-trap demand.\r\n\r\nLatissimus and teres major dominate glenohumeral torque.\r\n\r\n### Weighted Pull-Up\r\n\r\nModerate lower-trapezius demand through scapular stabilization/depression.\r\n\r\n### Dead Hang\r\n\r\nDepends strongly on execution.\r\n\r\n#### Passive Hang\r\n\r\nLow active lower-trap demand.\r\n\r\n#### Active Hang\r\n\r\nModerate demand through scapular positioning.\r\n\r\n## Relationship With Serratus Anterior\r\n\r\nThis is the most important functional partnership.\r\n\r\nA useful model is:\r\n\r\n`scapular_upward_rotation =\r\n    SA + UT + LT`\r\n\r\nwhile:\r\n\r\n`posterior_tilt =\r\n    SA + LT + other contributors`.\r\n\r\nTherefore high-elevation arm exercises should usually generate non-zero demand for both:\r\n\r\n- `serratus_anterior`;\r\n- `trapezius_lower`.\r\n\r\n## Relationship With Trapezius Middle\r\n\r\nA row that is mostly:\r\n\r\n- retraction\r\n\r\nshould favor:\r\n- `trapezius_middle`.\r\n\r\nA high-angle Y-raise emphasizing:\r\n\r\n- upward rotation;\r\n- posterior tilt\r\n\r\nshould favor:\r\n- `trapezius_lower`.\r\n\r\nThus exercise naming alone is insufficient; the scapular motion determines the regional trapezius vector.\r\n\r\n## Relationship With Rhomboids\r\n\r\nIf retraction occurs together with **downward rotation**, rhomboids become relatively more important.\r\n\r\nIf retraction/stabilization occurs together with **upward rotation**, lower trapezius becomes relatively more important.\r\n\r\nThis is one of the cleanest ways to differentiate these muscles computationally.\r\n\r\n## Length-Tension Model\r\n\r\nA first-order model could use:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_LT =\r\n    f(protraction_retraction,\r\n      elevation_depression,\r\n      anterior_posterior_tilt,\r\n      upward_downward_rotation)`\r\n\r\nQualitatively, lower trapezius is relatively longer with combinations of:\r\n\r\n- scapular protraction;\r\n- elevation;\r\n- anterior tilt.\r\n\r\nIt shortens with:\r\n\r\n- retraction;\r\n- depression;\r\n- posterior tilt.\r\n\r\nBecause upward rotation simultaneously changes several attachment relationships, one scalar remains approximate.\r\n\r\n## Humeral Elevation as Proxy\r\n\r\nFor KISS modelling, humeral elevation can serve as a useful indirect driver because scapular upward rotation increases with arm elevation.\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`lower_trap_demand ↑ as humeral_elevation ↑`\r\n\r\nprovided the scapula is free to move normally.\r\n\r\nThis should not be treated as direct humeral action.\r\n\r\nThe causal chain is:\r\n\r\n`humeral elevation requirement`\r\n→ `scapular upward rotation/posterior tilt requirement`\r\n→ `lower trapezius demand`.\r\n\r\n## FCSA Interpretation\r\n\r\nLower-trapezius FCSA can represent available force capacity in its regional line of pull.\r\n\r\nHowever, translating this to scapular torque requires a scapular moment arm:\r\n\r\n`T_LT = F_LT × r_LT`\r\n\r\nwhere `r_LT` depends on scapular orientation.\r\n\r\nTherefore a fixed `E_MA` is only approximate.\r\n\r\n## ETU Interpretation\r\n\r\nLower trapezius can accumulate meaningful ETU through:\r\n\r\n- dynamic scapular rotation;\r\n- isometric medial stabilization;\r\n- sustained overhead positioning.\r\n\r\nThus a heavy overhead press can load it substantially even if visible scapular motion per repetition is modest.\r\n\r\nAs with other stabilizers:\r\n\r\n`ROM = 0` should not imply `ETU = 0`.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nDirect lower-trapezius long-length hypertrophy evidence is sparse.\r\n\r\nThe mechanistic interpretation is:\r\n\r\n- protraction/elevation can lengthen the region;\r\n- loaded scapular motion can expose it to large excursion;\r\n- direct longitudinal evidence is insufficient to justify a large SMH bonus.\r\n\r\nTherefore `smh_factor` should remain conservative.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **lower thoracic origin and scapular-spine insertion:** high;\r\n- **accessory-nerve motor innervation:** high;\r\n- **upward-rotation role:** high;\r\n- **posterior-tilt contribution:** high;\r\n- **retraction contribution:** high;\r\n- **depression component:** high but often overemphasized;\r\n- **force-couple interaction with serratus anterior and upper trapezius:** high;\r\n- **meaningful activation during high-angle/Y-type movements:** strongly supported experimentally;\r\n- **precise exercise-specific force sharing:** angle- and task-dependent;\r\n- **direct hypertrophy evidence:** limited;\r\n- **stretch-mediated hypertrophy magnitude:** unestablished.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **lower trapezius = an inferior trapezius region specialized for scapular upward rotation, posterior tilt and medial stabilization, with secondary retraction/depression actions; its most important role during training is often orienting and stabilizing the scapula during arm elevation rather than simply pulling the shoulder blade downward.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/TrapeziusLower}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	12.64	0.00	1.000	14.98	14.98
21	triceps_medial_head	Musculus triceps brachii caput mediale	Upper	Triceps	190.00	179.92	20.00	Pennate	0.500	0.500	very_low	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\nThe **medial head of triceps brachii** is the deepest of the three triceps heads and a monoarticular muscle acting primarily at the elbow.\r\n\r\nIts defining function is:\r\n\r\n- **elbow extension**.\r\n\r\nUnlike `triceps_long_head`, it does **not** cross the shoulder joint.\r\n\r\nTherefore its direct muscle-length state depends predominantly on:\r\n\r\n- elbow flexion/extension;\r\n\r\nrather than shoulder position.\r\n\r\nHowever, shoulder position can still influence its **relative force share** indirectly because changing shoulder angle alters the mechanical state of the long head.\r\n\r\nThe medial head is especially important conceptually because it should not be treated merely as a small accessory head.\r\n\r\nIt contributes across a wide range of elbow-extension tasks and can assume a particularly large share of elbow-extension force in some shoulder positions, including high shoulder elevation.\r\n\r\nIts deep location also means its visual contribution to arm shape is less obvious than that of the lateral and long heads despite meaningful contractile capacity.\r\n\r\nFor Agonez, the most useful abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **triceps medial head = a deep monoarticular elbow extensor with broad participation across elbow-extension tasks, whose length depends mainly on elbow angle while its force share can change substantially with shoulder position because of redistribution among the three triceps heads.**\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\n## Origin\r\n\r\nThe medial head originates broadly from the posterior surface of the humerus, primarily:\r\n\r\n- inferior to the radial groove;\r\n- extending along much of the distal posterior humeral shaft;\r\n- with attachments related to the medial and lateral intermuscular septa.\r\n\r\nIts humeral origin means the muscle begins distal to the shoulder joint.\r\n\r\nTherefore it does not cross the glenohumeral joint.\r\n\r\n## Insertion\r\n\r\nThe medial head contributes to the common triceps extensor apparatus inserting onto the:\r\n\r\n- olecranon process of the ulna.\r\n\r\nIts force therefore generates elbow-extension torque through the triceps tendon system.\r\n\r\nBecause the insertion lies on the ulna, the medial head does not directly produce:\r\n\r\n- forearm pronation;\r\n- forearm supination.\r\n\r\n## Deep Position\r\n\r\nThe medial head lies deep to much of:\r\n\r\n- lateral head;\r\n- long head.\r\n\r\nPortions become more visible distally near the elbow.\r\n\r\nIts deep location contributes to the common misconception that it is mechanically unimportant.\r\n\r\nIn reality, depth and external visual prominence are poor proxies for force contribution.\r\n\r\n## Relationship With the Other Heads\r\n\r\n### Medial Head\r\n\r\n- monoarticular;\r\n- elbow extension;\r\n- deep;\r\n- broad participation across elbow-extension tasks.\r\n\r\n### Lateral Head\r\n\r\n- monoarticular;\r\n- elbow extension;\r\n- more superficial/lateral;\r\n- substantial high-force contribution.\r\n\r\n### Long Head\r\n\r\n- biarticular;\r\n- elbow extension;\r\n- shoulder extension/adduction contribution;\r\n- strongly affected by shoulder angle.\r\n\r\nFor modelling purposes, the medial and lateral heads share a simpler length model than the long head but should still not be assigned identical recruitment coefficients.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nThe medial head is innervated by the **radial nerve**, with contributions predominantly from lower cervical roots associated with the triceps motor pool.\r\n\r\nLike the other triceps heads, it receives a distinct motor branch despite sharing the radial nerve as the major peripheral nerve.\r\n\r\nThis permits task-dependent recruitment differences between:\r\n\r\n- medial;\r\n- lateral;\r\n- long head.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Elbow Extension\r\n\r\nThe principal function of the medial head is **extension of the elbow**.\r\n\r\nIt works synergistically with:\r\n\r\n- lateral head;\r\n- long head;\r\n- anconeus.\r\n\r\nIts force is transmitted through the common triceps tendon to the ulna.\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`T_elbow_extension =\r\n    Σ(F_head × r_head)`\r\n\r\nwhere the medial head contributes one of the major terms.\r\n\r\n## Broad Participation Across Force Levels\r\n\r\nThe medial head should not be described as a muscle that only works during:\r\n\r\n- light loads;\r\n- fine control;\r\n- final lockout.\r\n\r\nIt participates across a wide range of elbow-extension intensities.\r\n\r\nThe relative share changes with task mechanics.\r\n\r\nA useful interpretation is:\r\n\r\n> the medial head is a consistently available monoarticular elbow extensor rather than a specialized low-load head.\r\n\r\n## High Shoulder Elevation\r\n\r\nShoulder elevation does not directly alter medial-head length because the muscle originates from the humerus.\r\n\r\nHowever, changing shoulder position alters the long head's:\r\n\r\n- muscle length;\r\n- leverage;\r\n- activation;\r\n- ability to contribute to elbow-extension torque.\r\n\r\nConsequently, the medial head can assume a larger share of required elbow-extension force in elevated-arm configurations.\r\n\r\nThis distinction is essential:\r\n\r\n`shoulder angle → force-sharing modifier`\r\n\r\nbut not:\r\n\r\n`shoulder angle → direct medial-head E_LT modifier`.\r\n\r\n## Elbow Stabilization\r\n\r\nThe medial head also contributes to active elbow stability while producing extension force.\r\n\r\nThis stabilizing component is secondary to its torque-producing role.\r\n\r\n## No Direct Shoulder Function\r\n\r\nUnlike the long head, the medial head does not directly generate:\r\n\r\n- shoulder extension;\r\n- shoulder adduction.\r\n\r\nAny activity observed during shoulder-dominant movements should therefore arise from:\r\n\r\n- elbow-extension demand;\r\n- isometric stabilization;\r\n- task-related co-contraction;\r\n\r\nrather than direct glenohumeral torque.\r\n\r\n# Length-Tension Mechanics\r\n\r\nBecause the medial head is monoarticular, its length model is relatively straightforward.\r\n\r\n## Elbow Flexion\r\n\r\nLengthens the muscle.\r\n\r\n## Elbow Extension\r\n\r\nShortens the muscle.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_medial ≈ f(elbow_angle)`\r\n\r\nis a reasonable first-order approximation.\r\n\r\n## Shoulder Position\r\n\r\nShoulder position should not directly change medial-head muscle length.\r\n\r\nAt the same elbow angle:\r\n\r\n`L_medial(arm_overhead) ≈ L_medial(arm_at_side)`\r\n\r\nto first order.\r\n\r\nSmall secondary changes may occur through:\r\n\r\n- tendon geometry;\r\n- humeral positioning;\r\n- soft-tissue deformation;\r\n\r\nbut these are second-order relative to elbow angle.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nDirect medial-head-specific evidence for stretch-mediated hypertrophy is limited.\r\n\r\nThe mechanically relevant long-length variable is primarily:\r\n\r\n- **elbow flexion**.\r\n\r\n## Deep Elbow Flexion\r\n\r\nBecause the medial head extends the elbow, deeper flexion places it at a longer state.\r\n\r\nThis occurs in:\r\n\r\n- skull crushers;\r\n- overhead extensions;\r\n- deep cable extensions;\r\n- certain machine triceps extensions.\r\n\r\n## Overhead Position\r\n\r\nThe overhead position should **not** receive a direct medial-head long-length bonus based on shoulder angle alone.\r\n\r\nThat bonus belongs primarily to:\r\n\r\n- `triceps_long_head`.\r\n\r\nHowever, overhead extensions can still train the medial head extremely well because:\r\n\r\n- elbow flexion is substantial;\r\n- elbow-extension torque is high;\r\n- force sharing may favor the medial head under elevated shoulder conditions.\r\n\r\nTherefore an overhead extension may produce high medial-head hypertrophic stimulus without providing unique proximal stretch.\r\n\r\nThis is a good example of:\r\n\r\n> **high stimulus through force sharing without a corresponding increase in muscle length.**\r\n\r\n## Neutral-Arm Extension\r\n\r\nPushdowns also strongly train the medial head.\r\n\r\nAt equivalent elbow angles, its direct length is similar to that in overhead extensions.\r\n\r\nDifferences in stimulus arise more from:\r\n\r\n- resistance profile;\r\n- force sharing;\r\n- stability;\r\n- achievable force;\r\n\r\nthan from shoulder-mediated length change.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nFor Agonez:\r\n\r\n- `E_LT_medial` should depend mainly on elbow flexion;\r\n- overhead position should not directly increase `E_LT`;\r\n- long-length loading can still occur at deep elbow flexion;\r\n- medial-head-specific SMH evidence is insufficient for a large special multiplier.\r\n\r\nThus a conservative-to-moderate `smh_factor` is appropriate.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Cable Pushdown\r\n\r\nCable pushdowns are excellent medial-head exercises because they provide:\r\n\r\n- direct elbow-extension torque;\r\n- high stability;\r\n- low systemic fatigue;\r\n- easy progressive overload.\r\n\r\nThe medial head should receive very high direct demand.\r\n\r\nForce is shared with:\r\n\r\n- lateral head;\r\n- long head.\r\n\r\n## Rope Pushdown\r\n\r\nA rope does not anatomically isolate the medial head.\r\n\r\nIts practical advantages include:\r\n\r\n- comfortable wrist position;\r\n- freedom of forearm rotation;\r\n- natural elbow path.\r\n\r\nAttachment choice should primarily be considered a setup variable rather than a head-isolation mechanism.\r\n\r\n## Reverse-Grip Pushdown\r\n\r\nSupinated-grip pushdowns are sometimes claimed to selectively target the medial head.\r\n\r\nAnatomically, this claim is weak.\r\n\r\nThe triceps inserts on the ulna and does not cross the radioulnar joints.\r\n\r\nTherefore forearm rotation does not directly create a medial-head-specific mechanical action.\r\n\r\nAny perceived difference is more likely due to:\r\n\r\n- usable load;\r\n- elbow positioning;\r\n- comfort;\r\n- neural strategy.\r\n\r\nAgonez should therefore assign little or no direct `grip → medial_head` modifier.\r\n\r\n## Overhead Rope Extension\r\n\r\nThe medial head can be highly recruited during overhead elbow extension.\r\n\r\nAlthough the shoulder position does not lengthen it, force sharing can favor the medial head when the long head operates under altered mechanics at high shoulder elevation.\r\n\r\nThus an overhead extension may simultaneously provide:\r\n\r\n- very high long-head `E_LT`;\r\n- very high medial-head force demand.\r\n\r\nThe two heads can therefore be strongly stimulated for different reasons.\r\n\r\n## Skull Crusher\r\n\r\nSkull crushers impose substantial elbow-extension torque and deep elbow flexion.\r\n\r\nThe medial head should receive high demand.\r\n\r\nChanging the upper-arm angle strongly affects the long head but has much less effect on medial-head length.\r\n\r\n## California Press\r\n\r\nThe California press requires large elbow-extension torque.\r\n\r\nThe medial head should receive very high demand alongside:\r\n\r\n- lateral head;\r\n- long head.\r\n\r\nBecause the movement also includes pressing mechanics, some load is shared with:\r\n\r\n- pectoralis major;\r\n- anterior deltoid.\r\n\r\nStill, the triceps contribution is substantial.\r\n\r\n## Bench Press\r\n\r\nBench pressing requires meaningful elbow-extension torque.\r\n\r\nThe medial head contributes materially throughout.\r\n\r\nIts exact demand depends on:\r\n\r\n- grip width;\r\n- external load;\r\n- elbow angle;\r\n- sticking region;\r\n- shoulder mechanics.\r\n\r\nA narrower grip usually increases total triceps demand, but does not selectively isolate the medial head.\r\n\r\n## Incline Bench Press\r\n\r\nThe medial head remains a major elbow extensor.\r\n\r\nIncreasing shoulder elevation changes triceps head force sharing, so its relative contribution can differ from flat pressing.\r\n\r\nIts direct muscle length remains primarily elbow-dependent.\r\n\r\n## Overhead Press\r\n\r\nThe medial head can be particularly important during overhead pressing.\r\n\r\nThe shoulder is highly elevated, while substantial elbow-extension torque is required.\r\n\r\nThis is precisely the kind of configuration where:\r\n\r\n- medial-head force share can be high;\r\n- long-head length can also be high;\r\n- the two variables should not be conflated.\r\n\r\nTherefore Agonez should not assume that the long head dominates simply because the arm is overhead.\r\n\r\n## Dips\r\n\r\nDips impose high elbow-extension torque.\r\n\r\nThe medial head should receive high demand.\r\n\r\nShoulder extension affects the long head much more strongly than the medial head.\r\n\r\nTherefore dips can be a strong medial/lateral-head exercise even when the long head is in a relatively shortened proximal position.\r\n\r\n## Lockout\r\n\r\nThe medial head contributes near elbow extension, but it should not be described as a dedicated "lockout head."\r\n\r\nExternal elbow torque can become small near lockout depending on resistance geometry.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n`joint angle ≠ external demand`.\r\n\r\nA muscle can be anatomically capable of force while the exercise provides little external torque at that point.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Dedicated Medial-Head Isolation Is Usually Unnecessary\r\n\r\nAny program containing substantial:\r\n\r\n- pressing;\r\n- pushdowns;\r\n- extensions;\r\n\r\nwill train the medial head.\r\n\r\nThere is little justification for attempting highly specific medial-head isolation through exotic grip manipulations.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Complementarity\r\n\r\nA useful triceps program can include:\r\n\r\n1. overhead extension;\r\n2. pushdown;\r\n3. heavy compound press.\r\n\r\nFor the medial head:\r\n\r\n### Overhead Extension\r\n\r\nHigh force, potentially favorable force sharing.\r\n\r\n### Pushdown\r\n\r\nHigh force with simple stable mechanics.\r\n\r\n### Compound Press\r\n\r\nHigh absolute elbow-extension demand.\r\n\r\nTogether these provide robust exposure.\r\n\r\n## Weekly Volume\r\n\r\nA practical overall triceps starting range remains approximately:\r\n\r\n- **6-12 hard effective sets per week**.\r\n\r\nThe medial head receives a share of nearly all of these.\r\n\r\nNo independent medial-head set quota is normally required.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nTwo to three weekly exposures are practical.\r\n\r\nRecovery overlaps with:\r\n\r\n- chest pressing;\r\n- overhead pressing;\r\n- direct triceps training.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\nUseful ranges include:\r\n\r\n### Direct Extensions\r\n\r\n- 8-15;\r\n- 10-20;\r\n- 15-25 reps.\r\n\r\n### Compound Pressing\r\n\r\n- 4-8;\r\n- 6-12 reps.\r\n\r\nThe medial head has no established unique optimal repetition range.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nStable cable extensions can be trained close to failure:\r\n\r\n- approximately 0-2 RIR.\r\n\r\nCompound presses generally benefit from some additional technical reserve.\r\n\r\n## Grip Choice\r\n\r\nGrip should primarily be selected for:\r\n\r\n- joint comfort;\r\n- stability;\r\n- load progression;\r\n- consistent elbow mechanics.\r\n\r\nNot for supposed medial-head isolation.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## It Is Deep but Not Mechanically Minor\r\n\r\nThe medial head is partly hidden beneath the other heads.\r\n\r\nThis makes it less visually obvious, but depth does not imply low force contribution.\r\n\r\nIt is a major elbow extensor.\r\n\r\n## Shoulder Position Can Change Its Force Without Changing Its Length\r\n\r\nThis is one of the most useful biomechanical lessons from the triceps complex.\r\n\r\nFor the medial head:\r\n\r\n`shoulder angle does not directly change muscle length`\r\n\r\nbut:\r\n\r\n`shoulder angle can change force share`.\r\n\r\nThis occurs because the biarticular long head changes mechanical state.\r\n\r\n## Overhead Does Not Mean Long Head Only\r\n\r\nAn overhead extension may strongly stimulate:\r\n\r\n- long head through long muscle length;\r\n- medial head through force sharing;\r\n- lateral head through direct elbow-extension demand.\r\n\r\nThe exercise should therefore not be represented as a single-head movement.\r\n\r\n## Grip Myths Are Mechanically Weak\r\n\r\nBecause all triceps heads transmit force to the ulna, pronating or supinating the forearm does not give the medial head a special anatomical line of action.\r\n\r\nGrip choice can matter practically, but it is not a strong head-selector.\r\n\r\n## It Is Monoarticular\r\n\r\nCompared with the long head, this makes the medial head much easier to model.\r\n\r\nIts direct length state depends overwhelmingly on:\r\n\r\n- elbow angle.\r\n\r\nThis makes it similar in modelling complexity to the lateral head.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\n`triceps_medial_head` should receive demand almost entirely from:\r\n\r\n1. **elbow-extension torque**;\r\n2. secondary elbow stabilization.\r\n\r\nIt should receive approximately zero direct:\r\n\r\n- shoulder-extension torque;\r\n- shoulder-adduction torque.\r\n\r\nThose shoulder actions belong to `triceps_long_head`.\r\n\r\n## Length-Tension Model\r\n\r\nA useful first-order representation is:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_medial = f(elbow_flexion)`\r\n\r\n### Long\r\n\r\n- deeply flexed elbow.\r\n\r\n### Intermediate\r\n\r\n- midrange elbow position.\r\n\r\n### Short\r\n\r\n- near full extension.\r\n\r\nShoulder angle should not directly enter this function.\r\n\r\n## Force-Sharing Model\r\n\r\nShoulder angle should instead enter a separate function:\r\n\r\n`force_share_medial =\r\n    f(elbow_angle,\r\n      shoulder_angle,\r\n      external_torque,\r\n      task,\r\n      force_level)`\r\n\r\nThis is the cleanest way to represent the difference between:\r\n\r\n- local muscle geometry;\r\n- whole-system recruitment.\r\n\r\n## Comparison With Lateral Head\r\n\r\nBoth are monoarticular elbow extensors.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_medial ≈ f(elbow)`\r\n\r\nand:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_lateral ≈ f(elbow)`.\r\n\r\nBut this does **not** imply identical force sharing.\r\n\r\nThe two heads differ in:\r\n\r\n- architecture;\r\n- origin geometry;\r\n- motor recruitment;\r\n- regional mechanical contribution.\r\n\r\nTherefore equal fixed coefficients should be avoided.\r\n\r\n## Comparison With Long Head\r\n\r\n### Medial Head\r\n\r\nLength:\r\n- elbow dependent.\r\n\r\nShoulder torque:\r\n- none.\r\n\r\n### Long Head\r\n\r\nLength:\r\n- elbow + shoulder dependent.\r\n\r\nShoulder torque:\r\n- extension/adduction contribution.\r\n\r\nThis creates a useful division in overhead tasks:\r\n\r\n`long_head E_LT ↑↑`\r\n\r\nwhile:\r\n\r\n`medial_head force_share may ↑`\r\n\r\nwithout corresponding proximal lengthening.\r\n\r\n## Elbow Moment Arm\r\n\r\nA KISS model can reasonably apply a common or near-common triceps tendon moment-arm curve to the three heads.\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`T_medial =\r\n    F_medial × r_triceps(elbow_angle)`\r\n\r\nwith:\r\n\r\n`r_triceps`\r\n\r\nvarying across the elbow ROM.\r\n\r\nHead-specific proximal architecture can still modify force capacity and recruitment.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Allocation\r\n\r\n### Standing Cable Pushdown\r\n\r\nVery high:\r\n- `triceps_medial_head`;\r\n- `triceps_lateral_head`.\r\n\r\nHigh:\r\n- long head.\r\n\r\n### Overhead Rope Extension\r\n\r\nVery high medial-head force demand can be justified.\r\n\r\nVery high:\r\n- long-head `E_LT`.\r\n\r\nThe distinction is:\r\n\r\n`medial = force-sharing benefit`\r\n\r\nversus:\r\n\r\n`long = force + direct shoulder-mediated stretch`.\r\n\r\n### California Press\r\n\r\nVery high medial-head demand.\r\n\r\nAlso very high:\r\n- lateral;\r\n- long.\r\n\r\n### Flat Bench Press\r\n\r\nModerate-to-high medial-head demand.\r\n\r\n### Smith Incline Bench Press\r\n\r\nModerate-to-high.\r\n\r\nShoulder elevation can increase relative medial-head participation compared with some neutral-arm configurations.\r\n\r\n### Seated Dumbbell Overhead Press\r\n\r\nHigh medial-head demand.\r\n\r\nThis should not be assigned mainly to long head simply because the shoulder is overhead.\r\n\r\n### Weighted Forward-Lean Dip\r\n\r\nHigh medial-head demand due large elbow-extension torque.\r\n\r\n### Straight-Arm Cable Pulldown\r\n\r\nNear-zero direct medial-head demand if the elbow remains fixed.\r\n\r\nThe medial head does not cross the shoulder.\r\n\r\nThis cleanly distinguishes it from the long head.\r\n\r\n## Overhead Exercise Modelling\r\n\r\nFor an overhead extension, a useful conceptual decomposition is:\r\n\r\n### Long Head\r\n\r\n- high elbow-extension demand;\r\n- very high `E_LT`.\r\n\r\n### Medial Head\r\n\r\n- high/very-high elbow-extension demand;\r\n- no shoulder-induced `E_LT` bonus.\r\n\r\n### Lateral Head\r\n\r\n- high elbow-extension demand;\r\n- no shoulder-induced `E_LT` bonus.\r\n\r\nThus the same exercise can achieve different stimulus pathways across heads.\r\n\r\n## FCSA Interpretation\r\n\r\nThe medial-head FCSA can be interpreted relatively cleanly as elbow-extension force capacity:\r\n\r\n`F_max_medial ≈ σ × FCSA_medial`.\r\n\r\nThen:\r\n\r\n`T_medial =\r\n    F_medial × r_elbow`.\r\n\r\nBecause the medial head is monoarticular, its force does not need to be simultaneously allocated across multiple joints.\r\n\r\nThis makes it easier to model than the long head.\r\n\r\n## Capacity vs Recruitment\r\n\r\nA large `FCSA` does not imply that the medial head should always receive a large force vector.\r\n\r\nActual demand remains:\r\n\r\n`F_medial =\r\n    capacity\r\n    × E_LT\r\n    × E_MA\r\n    × E_ND\r\n    × E_LoA`.\r\n\r\nThe key advantage of separate heads is that `E_ND` / force sharing can vary by exercise.\r\n\r\n## Grip Modifier\r\n\r\nFor Agonez I would give:\r\n\r\n`grip_orientation_modifier_medial ≈ 0`\r\n\r\nunless grip changes:\r\n\r\n- elbow position;\r\n- shoulder position;\r\n- achievable load;\r\n- resistance direction.\r\n\r\nThe forearm orientation itself should not be treated as a direct triceps-head selector.\r\n\r\n## ETU Interpretation\r\n\r\nMedial-head ETU should be driven primarily by:\r\n\r\n- elbow-extension force;\r\n- duration;\r\n- ROM;\r\n- proximity to failure.\r\n\r\nAn exercise with high shoulder motion but little elbow extension should generate little medial-head ETU.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n### Straight-Arm Pulldown\r\n\r\nLow.\r\n\r\n### Pushdown\r\n\r\nVery high.\r\n\r\n### Overhead Press\r\n\r\nHigh if elbow-extension torque is substantial.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nThe medial head is lengthened primarily by elbow flexion.\r\n\r\nTherefore long-length loading can be achieved in:\r\n\r\n- deep extensions;\r\n- skull crushers;\r\n- overhead extensions.\r\n\r\nBut shoulder elevation itself should not increase `E_LT_medial`.\r\n\r\nDirect medial-head-specific evidence for a special long-length hypertrophy effect is limited.\r\n\r\nThe safest abstraction is:\r\n\r\n`SMH_effective_medial =\r\n    base_smh_factor\r\n    × E_LT(elbow_angle)\r\n    × active_force`.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **posterior-humeral origin:** high;\r\n- **olecranon insertion via the triceps mechanism:** high;\r\n- **radial-nerve innervation:** high;\r\n- **monoarticular elbow-extension function:** very high;\r\n- **absence of direct shoulder action:** very high;\r\n- **elbow-angle dependence of muscle length:** very high;\r\n- **meaningful contribution across elbow-extension tasks:** high;\r\n- **shoulder-position-dependent redistribution of triceps force:** high;\r\n- **particularly important medial-head contribution at high shoulder elevation:** supported by experimental head-specific recruitment/force work;\r\n- **grip-specific medial-head isolation:** poorly supported;\r\n- **direct medial-head hypertrophy evidence:** limited;\r\n- **stretch-mediated hypertrophy magnitude:** insufficiently established;\r\n- **precise medial-vs-lateral force sharing:** task dependent.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **triceps medial head = a deep monoarticular elbow extensor whose direct length state depends primarily on elbow flexion, while shoulder position alters its recruitment only indirectly through redistribution of elbow-extension force among the triceps heads; it is therefore a major contributor to both neutral-arm and overhead extensions despite receiving no shoulder-induced stretch.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/TricepsBrachii}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	11.40	9.00	0.988	15.78	15.59
13	trapezius_middle	Musculus Trapezius middle	Upper	Back	230.00	217.80	11.00	Parallel	0.650	0.350	very_high	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\nThe **middle trapezius** is the central functional region of the trapezius muscle.\r\n\r\nIts dominant mechanical role is:\r\n\r\n- **scapular retraction**.\r\n\r\nIt also contributes to:\r\n\r\n- medial stabilization of the scapula;\r\n- control of scapular protraction;\r\n- stabilization during humeral movement;\r\n- force sharing with upper and lower trapezius during complex scapular tasks.\r\n\r\nCompared with the other trapezius regions:\r\n\r\n### Upper Trapezius\r\n\r\nMore strongly associated with:\r\n- clavicular/scapular elevation;\r\n- upward rotation.\r\n\r\n### Middle Trapezius\r\n\r\nMost strongly associated with:\r\n- **scapular retraction**.\r\n\r\n### Lower Trapezius\r\n\r\nMore strongly associated with:\r\n- upward rotation;\r\n- posterior tilt;\r\n- medial/inferior stabilization.\r\n\r\nMiddle trapezius should therefore be distinguished from the `rhomboids`.\r\n\r\nBoth retract the scapula, but their rotational biases differ.\r\n\r\n### Middle Trapezius\r\n\r\nRelatively close to a pure horizontal retractor.\r\n\r\n### Rhomboids\r\n\r\nRetraction +\r\n- downward rotation;\r\n- some elevation.\r\n\r\nThis makes middle trapezius particularly important in exercises that require strong scapular retraction without a large downward-rotation component.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- wide rows;\r\n- chest-supported rows;\r\n- reverse-fly-type movements with scapular excursion;\r\n- face pulls.\r\n\r\nFor Agonez, the most useful abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **middle trapezius = the primary horizontally oriented scapular retractor and medial stabilizer, with exercise demand determined by actual scapular retraction torque rather than by humeral pulling motion alone.**\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\n## Origin\r\n\r\nThe middle region of trapezius arises primarily from the upper and middle thoracic vertebral region through attachments associated with:\r\n\r\n- spinous processes;\r\n- supraspinous ligaments.\r\n\r\nThe exact boundary between:\r\n\r\n- upper;\r\n- middle;\r\n- lower trapezius\r\n\r\nis not anatomically discrete.\r\n\r\nThese are functional regions of one continuous muscle.\r\n\r\n## Insertion\r\n\r\nMiddle trapezius fibers insert primarily along the:\r\n\r\n- acromial region;\r\n- superior lip / medial portion of the spine of the scapula.\r\n\r\nIts fibers run approximately horizontally from the vertebral column toward the scapula.\r\n\r\nThis near-horizontal fiber orientation gives the middle region particularly favorable leverage for:\r\n\r\n- scapular retraction.\r\n\r\n## Regional Continuity\r\n\r\nTrapezius should be understood as one large muscle with regionally different fiber directions.\r\n\r\nThe middle region is not a separate anatomical muscle in the same sense as:\r\n\r\n- rhomboid major;\r\n- rhomboid minor.\r\n\r\nNevertheless, separating `trapezius_middle` is highly useful for exercise modelling because the three trapezius regions differ materially in:\r\n\r\n- line of action;\r\n- moment arms;\r\n- recruitment;\r\n- functional role.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nThe trapezius receives primary motor innervation from the **spinal accessory nerve, cranial nerve XI**.\r\n\r\nCervical spinal nerves, particularly around:\r\n\r\n- C3;\r\n- C4,\r\n\r\nprovide sensory and proprioceptive contributions.\r\n\r\nThis innervation pattern is shared across the trapezius regions.\r\n\r\nRegional recruitment differences therefore arise largely from:\r\n\r\n- motor-unit organization;\r\n- fiber geometry;\r\n- mechanical task\r\n\r\nrather than separate peripheral nerves for each region.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Scapular Retraction\r\n\r\nThe defining action of middle trapezius is **scapular retraction**, also called scapular adduction.\r\n\r\nRetraction moves the scapula medially toward the vertebral column.\r\n\r\nThis occurs during the scapular component of:\r\n\r\n- rows;\r\n- reverse flys;\r\n- face pulls;\r\n- deliberate scapular-retraction exercises.\r\n\r\nMiddle trapezius is one of the strongest direct contributors to this action.\r\n\r\n## Anti-Protraction\r\n\r\nThe same muscle can generate substantial force isometrically when resisting an external protraction moment.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n`scapular_ROM ≈ 0`\r\n\r\ncan coexist with:\r\n\r\n`middle_trapezius_force = high`.\r\n\r\nThis may occur when the upper limb is loaded while the scapula must remain stable relative to the thorax.\r\n\r\n## Medial Scapular Stabilization\r\n\r\nMiddle trapezius helps maintain the scapula in a mechanically useful position while humeral muscles generate force.\r\n\r\nThis is important because large forces from:\r\n\r\n- latissimus dorsi;\r\n- posterior deltoid;\r\n- pectoralis major;\r\n- deltoid\r\n\r\ncan alter scapular loading indirectly.\r\n\r\nMiddle trapezius can counter these perturbations through medial stabilization.\r\n\r\n## Relationship With Humeral Motion\r\n\r\nMiddle trapezius does **not** directly move the humerus.\r\n\r\nIts distal attachment is on the scapula.\r\n\r\nTherefore it cannot directly generate:\r\n\r\n- shoulder extension;\r\n- horizontal abduction;\r\n- shoulder adduction.\r\n\r\nThis is a central exercise-modelling distinction.\r\n\r\nA row should be decomposed into:\r\n\r\n1. humeral motion;\r\n2. scapular motion;\r\n3. elbow motion.\r\n\r\nMiddle trapezius receives force from component 2.\r\n\r\n## Relationship With Upward Rotation\r\n\r\nMiddle trapezius is less specialized for upward rotation than upper and lower trapezius.\r\n\r\nIts approximately horizontal fiber direction means its primary mechanical identity remains retraction.\r\n\r\nDepending on scapular orientation, it can participate in stabilization during upward rotation, but it should not receive the same upward-rotation coefficient as:\r\n\r\n- lower trapezius;\r\n- upper trapezius;\r\n- serratus anterior.\r\n\r\n## Relationship With Downward Rotation\r\n\r\nMiddle trapezius also lacks the strong downward-rotation bias of the rhomboids.\r\n\r\nThis makes it useful to conceptualize it as a relatively neutral retractor in the rotational plane.\r\n\r\nThis is a simplification, but a useful one for Agonez.\r\n\r\n# Relationship With Rhomboids\r\n\r\nThis is the most important force-sharing relationship.\r\n\r\nBoth:\r\n\r\n- middle trapezius;\r\n- rhomboids\r\n\r\nretract the scapula.\r\n\r\nHowever:\r\n\r\n### Middle Trapezius\r\n\r\nPrimary:\r\n- retraction.\r\n\r\nRotational bias:\r\n- relatively neutral compared with rhomboids.\r\n\r\n### Rhomboids\r\n\r\nPrimary:\r\n- retraction.\r\n\r\nAdditional:\r\n- downward rotation;\r\n- some elevation.\r\n\r\nThus during pure horizontal retraction, middle trapezius may receive a larger relative share.\r\n\r\nDuring:\r\n\r\n- retraction + downward rotation\r\n\r\nrhomboid share should increase.\r\n\r\nDuring:\r\n\r\n- retraction/stabilization + upward rotation\r\n\r\nlower trapezius and other upward rotators become more relevant.\r\n\r\n# Relationship With Posterior Deltoid\r\n\r\nPosterior deltoid is often confused with middle trapezius because both are heavily involved in many row and reverse-fly exercises.\r\n\r\nTheir joint actions are different.\r\n\r\n### Posterior Deltoid\r\n\r\nActs on:\r\n- humerus.\r\n\r\nProduces:\r\n- horizontal abduction;\r\n- extension depending on arm angle.\r\n\r\n### Middle Trapezius\r\n\r\nActs on:\r\n- scapula.\r\n\r\nProduces:\r\n- retraction.\r\n\r\nTherefore a high-elbow row can simultaneously produce:\r\n\r\n- high posterior-deltoid demand;\r\n- high middle-trapezius demand.\r\n\r\nBut they are solving different mechanical problems.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nDirect longitudinal evidence for **middle-trapezius-specific stretch-mediated hypertrophy** is limited.\r\n\r\nMechanically, the middle trapezius is lengthened primarily by **scapular protraction**.\r\n\r\n## Scapular Protraction\r\n\r\nDuring protraction, the scapula moves laterally away from the spine.\r\n\r\nThis increases the distance between:\r\n\r\n- thoracic origin;\r\n- scapular insertion.\r\n\r\nTherefore middle trapezius length increases.\r\n\r\nA row beginning from controlled scapular protraction can consequently load the muscle from a relatively lengthened position.\r\n\r\n## Loaded Start of a Row\r\n\r\nCable and machine rows can preserve substantial resistance while the scapula is protracted.\r\n\r\nThis creates a plausible long-length stimulus.\r\n\r\nThe quality of this stimulus depends on whether the trainee actually allows:\r\n\r\n- scapular excursion\r\n\r\nrather than holding the scapula rigidly retracted throughout the set.\r\n\r\n## Chest-Supported Row\r\n\r\nChest support can make controlled protraction particularly useful because the thorax remains stable while the scapula moves around it.\r\n\r\nThis can provide:\r\n\r\n- large retraction ROM;\r\n- reduced lumbar fatigue;\r\n- clearer middle-trap loading.\r\n\r\n## Reverse Fly\r\n\r\nReverse-fly exercises can also begin with the scapula relatively protracted.\r\n\r\nHowever, whether middle trapezius receives high long-length loading depends on how much movement occurs at the scapula versus the glenohumeral joint.\r\n\r\nA reverse fly performed almost entirely through humeral horizontal abduction may become posterior-delt dominant.\r\n\r\n## Full ROM vs End-Range Squeeze\r\n\r\nFor hypertrophy, there is little reason to sacrifice the protracted starting position merely to maximize an end-range retraction squeeze.\r\n\r\nThe middle trapezius can be trained through:\r\n\r\n- long position;\r\n- mid-range;\r\n- shortened position.\r\n\r\nA large controlled ROM is therefore mechanically attractive.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nThe defensible position is:\r\n\r\n- scapular protraction clearly lengthens middle trapezius;\r\n- rows can load the muscle from this position;\r\n- direct long-length hypertrophy evidence is sparse;\r\n- no large specific SMH multiplier is established.\r\n\r\nThus `smh_factor` should remain conservative while `E_LT` can still reward loaded protraction.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Horizontal Rows\r\n\r\nHorizontal rows are the main high-load exercise family for middle trapezius.\r\n\r\nThe key determinant is:\r\n\r\n> **how much scapular retraction actually occurs.**\r\n\r\nA row can involve large arm movement but little scapular motion.\r\n\r\nIn such a case:\r\n\r\n- lats;\r\n- posterior deltoid;\r\n- elbow flexors\r\n\r\nmay work very hard while middle-trap demand is lower.\r\n\r\n## Chest-Supported Row\r\n\r\nChest-supported rows are particularly useful because the bench removes much of the need for:\r\n\r\n- lumbar extension;\r\n- hip stabilization.\r\n\r\nThis improves local stimulus-to-fatigue ratio for the upper back.\r\n\r\nAllowing the scapulae to:\r\n\r\n- protract at the beginning;\r\n- retract during the pull\r\n\r\ncreates high middle-trapezius demand.\r\n\r\n## Wide-Grip Row\r\n\r\nA wider/high-elbow row often increases the horizontal-abduction and scapular-retraction components.\r\n\r\nThis usually increases demand on:\r\n\r\n- posterior deltoid;\r\n- middle trapezius;\r\n- rhomboids.\r\n\r\nRelative latissimus contribution tends to fall as the humeral path becomes less extension-dominant.\r\n\r\n## Tucked-Elbow Row\r\n\r\nA tucked-elbow row still uses middle trapezius if the scapula retracts.\r\n\r\nHowever, the glenohumeral component becomes more:\r\n\r\n- extension/adduction oriented.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n- latissimus;\r\n- teres major\r\n\r\nreceive larger shares.\r\n\r\nMiddle trapezius remains tied to the scapular component rather than elbow path itself.\r\n\r\n## Wide-Grip Seated Cable Row\r\n\r\nThis can be an excellent middle-trapezius exercise if:\r\n\r\n- the torso remains stable;\r\n- the scapulae protract at the start;\r\n- the pull includes substantial retraction;\r\n- the elbows travel in a moderately abducted path.\r\n\r\nIt should generally receive high `trapezius_middle` demand.\r\n\r\n## Reverse Pec Deck\r\n\r\nReverse pec deck can load middle trapezius, but machine design and execution matter.\r\n\r\nIf the scapulae are allowed to:\r\n\r\n- protract;\r\n- retract,\r\n\r\nmiddle-trap demand can be substantial.\r\n\r\nIf the shoulder blades are held fixed and the movement occurs almost entirely through humeral horizontal abduction:\r\n\r\n- posterior deltoid becomes dominant;\r\n- middle-trap share decreases.\r\n\r\n## Face Pull\r\n\r\nFace pulls combine:\r\n\r\n- scapular retraction;\r\n- humeral horizontal abduction;\r\n- external rotation.\r\n\r\nTherefore they recruit:\r\n\r\n- middle trapezius;\r\n- rhomboids;\r\n- posterior deltoid;\r\n- rotator cuff;\r\n- lower trapezius to some degree.\r\n\r\nThey should receive high but distributed upper-back demand.\r\n\r\n## Pendlay Row\r\n\r\nPendlay rows can generate substantial middle-trapezius demand through powerful scapular retraction.\r\n\r\nHowever, the exercise also has high systemic cost from:\r\n\r\n- erector spinae;\r\n- gluteus maximus;\r\n- hamstrings;\r\n- adductor magnus;\r\n- grip.\r\n\r\nFor pure middle-trap hypertrophy, chest-supported rowing often offers a better local stimulus-to-fatigue ratio.\r\n\r\n## One-Arm Rows\r\n\r\nUnilateral rows can train middle trapezius effectively.\r\n\r\nHowever, trunk rotation can substitute for scapular retraction.\r\n\r\nThe intended movement should therefore distinguish:\r\n\r\n- thoracic rotation;\r\nfrom\r\n- scapular movement.\r\n\r\nA large visual ROM does not necessarily mean a large middle-trap ROM.\r\n\r\n## Scapular Row\r\n\r\nA scapular-only row minimizes:\r\n\r\n- elbow flexion;\r\n- humeral extension.\r\n\r\nThis increases mechanical specificity for:\r\n\r\n- middle trapezius;\r\n- rhomboids.\r\n\r\nAbsolute loading is usually lower, but the exercise can be useful for:\r\n\r\n- motor control;\r\n- targeted accessory work.\r\n\r\n## Shrugs\r\n\r\nConventional vertical shrugs should provide relatively little middle-trapezius dynamic demand.\r\n\r\nThey primarily involve:\r\n\r\n- upper trapezius;\r\n- levator scapulae.\r\n\r\nMiddle trapezius may stabilize the scapula but should not receive a high prime-mover vector.\r\n\r\n## Overhead Press\r\n\r\nMiddle trapezius contributes to scapular stabilization during overhead pressing.\r\n\r\nHowever, upward rotation is dominated more by:\r\n\r\n- serratus anterior;\r\n- upper trapezius;\r\n- lower trapezius.\r\n\r\nThus middle-trap demand should generally be low-to-moderate.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Rows Usually Provide Most Required Volume\r\n\r\nA program containing substantial rowing volume generally provides enough direct middle-trapezius work.\r\n\r\nDedicated isolation is often unnecessary unless:\r\n\r\n- middle-back hypertrophy is a priority;\r\n- rowing technique is extremely lat-dominant;\r\n- scapular retraction strength is specifically deficient.\r\n\r\n## Weekly Volume\r\n\r\nA practical starting range is approximately:\r\n\r\n- **6-12 hard retraction-focused sets per week**.\r\n\r\nThis can include:\r\n\r\n- chest-supported rows;\r\n- cable rows;\r\n- face pulls;\r\n- reverse-fly variations with scapular excursion.\r\n\r\nNot every pulling set should count equally.\r\n\r\n## Set Credit\r\n\r\n### Full Middle-Trap Credit\r\n\r\nExamples:\r\n- chest-supported wide row with full scapular excursion;\r\n- wide seated cable row;\r\n- scapular retraction row.\r\n\r\n### Partial Credit\r\n\r\nExamples:\r\n- tucked-elbow lat row;\r\n- pull-up;\r\n- pulldown.\r\n\r\n### Low Credit\r\n\r\nExamples:\r\n- straight-arm pulldown;\r\n- shrug;\r\n- lat-biased isolation.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nTwo to three weekly exposures are practical.\r\n\r\nMiddle-trapezius work itself has relatively low systemic cost when chest-supported.\r\n\r\nExercise-level fatigue rises sharply when the same retraction task is embedded in:\r\n\r\n- Pendlay rows;\r\n- unsupported barbell rows.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\nUseful ranges include:\r\n\r\n- 6-10 reps for heavy supported rows;\r\n- 8-15 reps for machine/cable rows;\r\n- 12-20+ reps for face pulls and reverse-fly variants.\r\n\r\nThere is no middle-trap-specific biological repetition range.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nStable rowing machines can be trained close to failure:\r\n\r\n- approximately 0-2 RIR.\r\n\r\nTechnical failure occurs when:\r\n\r\n- scapular retraction ROM collapses;\r\n- torso momentum replaces retraction;\r\n- shoulders elevate excessively;\r\n- humeral motion continues despite loss of scapular contribution.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Order\r\n\r\nIf middle-back hypertrophy is a priority, a retraction-focused row can be performed early.\r\n\r\nIf lats are the priority, lat-biased pulling can precede it.\r\n\r\nThis allows exercise order to reflect the intended force-sharing emphasis.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## Middle Trapezius Does Not Move the Arm Directly\r\n\r\nThis is the most important anatomical distinction.\r\n\r\nIts insertion is on the scapula.\r\n\r\nTherefore it cannot directly produce:\r\n\r\n- humeral extension;\r\n- horizontal abduction.\r\n\r\nA row can heavily load middle trapezius and posterior deltoid simultaneously, but for separate joint actions.\r\n\r\n## "Back Thickness" Is Not One Muscle\r\n\r\nA thick upper/mid-back appearance can involve:\r\n\r\n- middle trapezius;\r\n- rhomboids;\r\n- posterior deltoid;\r\n- erector spinae;\r\n- latissimus dorsi.\r\n\r\nThese muscles respond differently to:\r\n\r\n- elbow path;\r\n- scapular motion;\r\n- torso support.\r\n\r\nTherefore generic "row = back thickness" language hides important mechanical distinctions.\r\n\r\n## Middle Trap and Rhomboids Are Similar but Not Identical\r\n\r\nBoth retract.\r\n\r\nRhomboids add a clearer downward-rotation bias.\r\n\r\nMiddle trapezius is mechanically closer to a direct horizontal retractor.\r\n\r\nThis matters particularly when the scapula must simultaneously upwardly rotate.\r\n\r\n## Scapular Protraction Is Not a Technique Error\r\n\r\nControlled protraction at the beginning of a row:\r\n\r\n- lengthens the retractors;\r\n- increases scapular ROM.\r\n\r\nIt becomes problematic only if the movement is produced mainly through uncontrolled thoracic flexion or loss of shoulder control.\r\n\r\n## A Bigger Squeeze Is Not Automatically More Stimulus\r\n\r\nEnd-range retraction shortens middle trapezius strongly.\r\n\r\nBut hypertrophic stimulus depends on force across the whole range, not merely peak subjective contraction.\r\n\r\nFull loaded scapular excursion is generally a better mechanical target than chasing an exaggerated squeeze.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\n`trapezius_middle` should primarily receive demand from:\r\n\r\n1. **scapular-retraction torque**;\r\n2. anti-protraction stabilization;\r\n3. medial scapular stabilization.\r\n\r\nIt should receive relatively little primary:\r\n\r\n- upward-rotation;\r\n- downward-rotation;\r\n- elevation;\r\n- depression\r\n\r\nallocation compared with other scapular muscles.\r\n\r\nThis makes it one of the cleaner scapular muscles to model.\r\n\r\n## Row Decomposition\r\n\r\nFor Agonez:\r\n\r\n`row =\r\n    glenohumeral_component\r\n  + scapular_component\r\n  + elbow_component\r\n  + trunk_stabilization_component`\r\n\r\n### Glenohumeral Component\r\n\r\nPossible contributors:\r\n- latissimus dorsi;\r\n- teres major;\r\n- posterior deltoid.\r\n\r\n### Scapular Retraction Component\r\n\r\nPrimary:\r\n- middle trapezius;\r\n- rhomboids.\r\n\r\n### Elbow Flexion\r\n\r\n- brachialis;\r\n- biceps;\r\n- brachioradialis.\r\n\r\n### Trunk Stabilization\r\n\r\n- erector spinae;\r\n- abdominal stabilizers depending setup.\r\n\r\nThis decomposition should be the basis of all row vectors.\r\n\r\n## Retraction Force Sharing\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`T_retraction =\r\n    trapezius_middle\r\n  + rhomboids\r\n  + trapezius_lower\r\n  + smaller contributors`\r\n\r\nA useful baseline is:\r\n\r\n### Pure Horizontal Retraction\r\n\r\nHighest:\r\n- middle trapezius.\r\n\r\nHigh:\r\n- rhomboids.\r\n\r\nLower:\r\n- lower trapezius.\r\n\r\n### Retraction + Downward Rotation\r\n\r\nIncrease:\r\n- rhomboids.\r\n\r\n### Retraction + Upward Rotation / Posterior Tilt\r\n\r\nIncrease:\r\n- lower trapezius.\r\n\r\nThis gives Agonez a clean way to distribute scapular torque.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Allocation\r\n\r\n### Wide-Grip Seated Cable Row\r\n\r\nVery high:\r\n- `trapezius_middle`.\r\n\r\nHigh:\r\n- rhomboids.\r\n\r\nHigh:\r\n- posterior deltoid depending elbow path.\r\n\r\nModerate:\r\n- latissimus depending humeral extension component.\r\n\r\n### Chest-Supported Dumbbell Row\r\n\r\nHigh middle-trap demand if full scapular excursion is allowed.\r\n\r\nIf elbows remain very tucked:\r\n- lat share rises.\r\n\r\nIf elbows flare:\r\n- posterior-delt share rises.\r\n\r\nMiddle trap remains driven mostly by scapular retraction.\r\n\r\n### Pendlay Row\r\n\r\nHigh middle-trap demand.\r\n\r\nAlso high:\r\n- rhomboids;\r\n- lats;\r\n- elbow flexors;\r\n- erector spinae.\r\n\r\n### Cable Face Pull\r\n\r\nHigh middle-trap demand.\r\n\r\nAlso high:\r\n- posterior deltoid;\r\n- rotator cuff;\r\n- rhomboids.\r\n\r\nModerate:\r\n- lower trapezius.\r\n\r\n### Reverse Pec Deck\r\n\r\nModerate-to-high middle-trap demand if scapular excursion is allowed.\r\n\r\nPotentially much lower if scapulae remain fixed.\r\n\r\n### Neutral-Grip Pull-Up\r\n\r\nLow-to-moderate middle-trap demand.\r\n\r\nThe main task is shoulder adduction/extension rather than maximal scapular retraction.\r\n\r\n### Straight-Arm Cable Pulldown\r\n\r\nLow middle-trap demand.\r\n\r\nLatissimus and teres major dominate.\r\n\r\n### Standing Dumbbell Shrug\r\n\r\nLow middle-trap dynamic demand.\r\n\r\nUpper trapezius dominates elevation.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Rhomboids\r\n\r\nThis should not be represented as a fixed 50/50 split.\r\n\r\nThe rotational requirement modifies sharing.\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`middle_trap_share ↑`\r\nwhen:\r\n- motion approaches pure retraction.\r\n\r\n`rhomboid_share ↑`\r\nwhen:\r\n- retraction is accompanied by downward rotation.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Posterior Deltoid\r\n\r\nHigh-elbow row:\r\n\r\n`horizontal_abduction torque ↑`\r\n→ posterior deltoid ↑.\r\n\r\nIf scapular retraction also rises:\r\n\r\n→ middle trapezius ↑.\r\n\r\nThus both can be high simultaneously.\r\n\r\nThis is not double counting because they act at different articulations.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Latissimus Dorsi\r\n\r\nTucked-elbow row:\r\n\r\n`shoulder_extension torque ↑`\r\n→ latissimus ↑.\r\n\r\nIf scapular retraction ROM remains large:\r\n\r\n→ middle trapezius remains high.\r\n\r\nIf the athlete minimizes scapular motion and mostly drives the elbow backward:\r\n\r\n→ middle-trap demand falls relative to lat demand.\r\n\r\nThis makes technique a first-order modifier.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Lower Trapezius\r\n\r\nMiddle and lower trapezius share some retraction function.\r\n\r\nBut:\r\n\r\n### Middle\r\n\r\nMore horizontal line of pull:\r\n- retraction optimized.\r\n\r\n### Lower\r\n\r\nMore oblique line:\r\n- upward rotation;\r\n- posterior tilt;\r\n- medial/inferior stabilization.\r\n\r\nTherefore lower trap should not simply inherit a fixed percentage of middle-trap demand.\r\n\r\n## Length-Tension Model\r\n\r\nA first-order model is comparatively straightforward:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_middle_trap ≈ f(scapular_protraction_retraction)`\r\n\r\n### Long\r\n\r\n- scapular protraction.\r\n\r\n### Intermediate\r\n\r\n- neutral position.\r\n\r\n### Short\r\n\r\n- strong retraction.\r\n\r\nSecondary modifiers can include:\r\n\r\n- scapular rotation;\r\n- tilt.\r\n\r\nBut protraction/retraction should dominate.\r\n\r\n## E_MA Model\r\n\r\nMiddle trapezius has a relatively favorable line of action for horizontal scapular retraction.\r\n\r\nIts leverage should still vary with scapular orientation.\r\n\r\nA KISS implementation can therefore use:\r\n\r\n`E_MA_middle_trap = high`\r\n\r\nfor genuine scapular-retraction tasks,\r\n\r\nwith smaller corrections for:\r\n\r\n- scapular rotation;\r\n- humeral elevation.\r\n\r\nThis is simpler than the multi-axis models required for serratus anterior or lower trapezius.\r\n\r\n## FCSA Interpretation\r\n\r\nMiddle-trapezius FCSA can be interpreted relatively cleanly as retraction-force capacity.\r\n\r\nHowever, external scapular torque still depends on:\r\n\r\n`T_retraction = F_middle_trap × r_scapular`\r\n\r\nand the effective moment arm changes with scapular position.\r\n\r\nThus FCSA alone remains force capacity, not torque capacity.\r\n\r\n## ETU Interpretation\r\n\r\nMiddle trapezius can accumulate substantial ETU through:\r\n\r\n- concentric retraction;\r\n- eccentric control of protraction;\r\n- isometric anti-protraction.\r\n\r\nTherefore all three contraction modes should be represented.\r\n\r\nA static hold at strong external protraction torque can create meaningful middle-trap demand despite no movement.\r\n\r\n## Long-Length Row Modelling\r\n\r\nFor a row beginning in protraction:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_middle_trap ↑`\r\n\r\nwhile external resistance may remain substantial.\r\n\r\nThis creates high long-length tension.\r\n\r\nAs the scapula retracts:\r\n\r\n`muscle_length ↓`.\r\n\r\nThus cable and supported rows can provide a full long-to-short loading cycle.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nThe mechanical basis for long-length loading is clear:\r\n\r\n- protraction lengthens middle trapezius;\r\n- controlled rows can load from this position.\r\n\r\nHowever, direct middle-trap-specific hypertrophy evidence is limited.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n- reward full loaded scapular excursion;\r\n- avoid a large evidence-based SMH premium;\r\n- do not assume end-range squeeze is uniquely hypertrophic.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **thoracic origin and scapular-spine insertion:** high;\r\n- **spinal-accessory-nerve motor innervation:** high;\r\n- **primary scapular-retraction function:** very high;\r\n- **anti-protraction/stabilization role:** high;\r\n- **synergy with rhomboids:** high;\r\n- **functional distinction from lower and upper trapezius:** high;\r\n- **exercise demand dependence on scapular excursion:** mechanically strong;\r\n- **precise middle-trap vs rhomboid force sharing:** task-dependent;\r\n- **direct hypertrophy evidence:** limited;\r\n- **stretch-mediated hypertrophy magnitude:** unestablished.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **middle trapezius = the predominantly horizontal scapular-retraction region of trapezius, generating medial scapular torque and anti-protraction stability; its training demand depends on actual scapular movement rather than on how far the elbow or humerus travels during a pulling exercise.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/TrapeziusMiddle}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	8.32	0.00	1.000	26.18	26.18
48	sternocleidomastoid	Musculus sternocleidomastoideus	Upper	Neck	175.00	165.72	8.00	Parallel/Fusiform	0.650	0.350	high	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\nThe **sternocleidomastoid (SCM)** is a large, superficial paired muscle of the anterolateral neck.\r\n\r\nIts name describes its three principal attachment regions:\r\n\r\n- **sterno-** → sternum;\r\n- **cleido-** → clavicle;\r\n- **-mastoid** → mastoid process.\r\n\r\nThe muscle has two major proximal heads:\r\n\r\n- **sternal head**;\r\n- **clavicular head**.\r\n\r\nThese converge superiorly toward the mastoid region but retain partially distinct fiber trajectories.\r\n\r\nThe SCM is mechanically important because it participates in several different actions depending on whether contraction is:\r\n\r\n- unilateral;\r\n- bilateral;\r\n- performed with the head free;\r\n- performed while parts of the cervical spine are stabilized.\r\n\r\nIts most important functions are:\r\n\r\n1. **contralateral cervical/head rotation** during unilateral contraction;\r\n2. **ipsilateral lateral flexion**;\r\n3. contribution to **cervical flexion** during bilateral contraction;\r\n4. contribution to complex craniocervical flexion-extension coupling;\r\n5. stabilization of the head and cervical spine;\r\n6. accessory inspiration when respiratory demand is high.\r\n\r\nTherefore, describing SCM simply as "the neck flexor" is incomplete.\r\n\r\nFor resistance-training purposes, it is best modeled as a large **superficial anterolateral cervical actuator**, distinct from the deep cervical flexors such as:\r\n\r\n- longus colli;\r\n- longus capitis.\r\n\r\nThose deeper muscles are more strongly associated with fine segmental control, whereas SCM has substantially greater capacity for gross head and neck torque.\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\n## Sternal Head\r\n\r\n### Origin\r\n\r\nThe sternal head originates from the **anterior surface of the manubrium of the sternum**, primarily near its superior aspect.\r\n\r\nIt begins as a relatively narrow tendinous attachment.\r\n\r\nFrom this origin, the fibers travel superiorly, laterally and posteriorly.\r\n\r\n### Insertion\r\n\r\nThe sternal fibers merge into the superior SCM attachment on:\r\n\r\n- the lateral surface of the **mastoid process of the temporal bone**;\r\n- the lateral portion of the **superior nuchal line** of the occipital bone.\r\n\r\n## Clavicular Head\r\n\r\n### Origin\r\n\r\nThe clavicular head originates from the superior surface of the **medial clavicle**, classically involving approximately its medial third.\r\n\r\nIts proximal attachment is broader and more muscular than the sternal head.\r\n\r\n### Insertion\r\n\r\nThe clavicular fibers ascend superiorly and posteriorly and join the common superior attachment around:\r\n\r\n- the mastoid process;\r\n- superior nuchal line.\r\n\r\n## Relationship Between the Heads\r\n\r\nThe two heads form a visible triangular interval inferiorly before converging.\r\n\r\nTheir differing proximal attachments and fiber trajectories mean that they should not be assumed to contribute identically to every cervical movement.\r\n\r\nSome functional differentiation between the sternal and clavicular portions exists, particularly during combinations of:\r\n\r\n- rotation;\r\n- flexion;\r\n- lateral flexion.\r\n\r\nFor the present database, however, a single `sternocleidomastoid` entity is a reasonable abstraction.\r\n\r\n## Surface Anatomy\r\n\r\nSCM is one of the most visually identifiable muscles of the neck.\r\n\r\nWhen contracted, it forms a prominent oblique ridge running from the upper thorax/clavicle toward the mastoid process.\r\n\r\nIt divides each side of the neck into:\r\n\r\n- anterior cervical triangle;\r\n- posterior cervical triangle.\r\n\r\nThis makes it an important anatomical landmark for:\r\n\r\n- vascular structures;\r\n- nerves;\r\n- lymph nodes;\r\n- surgical approaches.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nMotor innervation is supplied primarily by the **spinal accessory nerve — cranial nerve XI**.\r\n\r\nCervical spinal nerves, principally from **C2-C3**, provide proprioceptive and sensory contributions.\r\n\r\nThis combination is commonly summarized as:\r\n\r\n- CN XI → motor;\r\n- upper cervical spinal nerves → proprioceptive/sensory contribution.\r\n\r\nThe accessory nerve also supplies the trapezius.\r\n\r\nBecause it travels relatively superficially through portions of the posterior triangle, the nerve can be vulnerable during surgical procedures in the neck.\r\n\r\nInjury can produce weakness of both SCM and trapezius depending on lesion location.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Unilateral Rotation\r\n\r\nUnilateral SCM contraction produces **rotation of the head toward the opposite side**.\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n\r\n- right SCM contraction → rotation of the face toward the left;\r\n- left SCM contraction → rotation toward the right.\r\n\r\nThis contralateral rotational action is one of the muscle's defining functions.\r\n\r\nThe diagonal line of pull from sternum/clavicle to mastoid process gives the muscle favorable geometry for generating this torque.\r\n\r\n## Ipsilateral Lateral Flexion\r\n\r\nThe same unilateral contraction also contributes to **lateral flexion toward the contracting side**.\r\n\r\nThus right SCM tends to produce:\r\n\r\n- right lateral flexion;\r\n- left rotation.\r\n\r\nThis paired action explains the characteristic head posture seen when one SCM becomes persistently shortened, as in some forms of torticollis.\r\n\r\n## Bilateral Cervical Flexion\r\n\r\nWhen both SCMs contract together, they can contribute substantially to **flexion of the cervical spine**.\r\n\r\nThis is particularly relevant when the head must be raised against gravity or external posterior resistance.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- supine neck curls;\r\n- resisted cervical flexion;\r\n- rapid head stabilization after backward acceleration.\r\n\r\nHowever, SCM does not behave as a simple single-axis flexor.\r\n\r\n## Craniocervical Flexion-Extension Coupling\r\n\r\nBecause SCM attaches to the skull behind and lateral to portions of the cervical axes of rotation, bilateral contraction can create a more complex pattern:\r\n\r\n- flexion of lower cervical segments;\r\n- simultaneous extension or maintenance of extension in the upper cervical/craniocervical region.\r\n\r\nThis is why a strong SCM-dominant neck flexion can produce a visible **chin-forward or chin-up pattern** rather than a clean "chin tuck."\r\n\r\nThis distinction is important in rehabilitation literature.\r\n\r\nDeep cervical flexor exercises often deliberately attempt to minimize SCM activity because the objective is not maximum neck-flexion torque but selective activation of:\r\n\r\n- longus colli;\r\n- longus capitis.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n> high SCM activity is desirable in a strength/hypertrophy neck-flexion exercise but may be undesirable in a low-load deep-cervical-flexor motor-control drill.\r\n\r\nThese are different training goals.\r\n\r\n## Stabilization\r\n\r\nSCM contributes to stabilization of the head against external forces in multiple planes.\r\n\r\nDuring unexpected acceleration it can act:\r\n\r\n- concentrically;\r\n- eccentrically;\r\n- isometrically;\r\n\r\ndepending on the direction of head movement.\r\n\r\nIts superficial size and large moment arms make it especially relevant to gross stabilization rather than purely local segmental control.\r\n\r\n## Eccentric Control\r\n\r\nSCM can act eccentrically when an external force tends to move the head opposite to its normal concentric actions.\r\n\r\nFor example, the anterior neck musculature can decelerate rearward movement of the head and control extension during certain dynamic perturbations.\r\n\r\nThis is relevant in:\r\n\r\n- contact sports;\r\n- rapid acceleration;\r\n- braking events;\r\n- collision mechanics.\r\n\r\n## Accessory Inspiration\r\n\r\nWhen the head and cervical region are sufficiently stabilized, SCM can elevate its inferior attachments:\r\n\r\n- sternum;\r\n- clavicle.\r\n\r\nIt can therefore assist inspiration during increased respiratory demand.\r\n\r\nSCM activity increases during:\r\n\r\n- labored breathing;\r\n- high ventilatory demand;\r\n- respiratory distress.\r\n\r\nIn ordinary relaxed breathing, it is not intended to function as the primary inspiratory muscle.\r\n\r\nIts respiratory role is therefore best described as **accessory inspiration**.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nThere is very little direct evidence specifically evaluating **stretch-mediated hypertrophy of SCM**.\r\n\r\nAny SMH assignment should therefore be conservative.\r\n\r\nMechanically, SCM length depends on several coupled movements because the muscle is oblique and acts in multiple planes.\r\n\r\nA unilateral SCM is lengthened by a combination approximately opposite to its concentric actions:\r\n\r\n- cervical/head rotation toward the same side;\r\n- lateral flexion toward the opposite side;\r\n- extension/flexion changes depending on cervical segment and head position.\r\n\r\nFor bilateral flexion-oriented training, extension of the cervical spine generally places the anterior cervical musculature at greater length than a deeply flexed position.\r\n\r\nHowever, the geometry is considerably more complicated than in a monoarticular hinge muscle.\r\n\r\n## Loaded Extension-to-Flexion ROM\r\n\r\nA resistance exercise that begins from controlled cervical extension and proceeds into flexion can expose SCM to tension at relatively long lengths.\r\n\r\nThis provides a plausible long-length stimulus.\r\n\r\nHowever:\r\n\r\n- extreme neck extension is unnecessary;\r\n- passive end-range loading should not be pursued aggressively;\r\n- high external load in extreme cervical positions has a poor risk/reward profile.\r\n\r\nThe useful principle is therefore:\r\n\r\n> train through a controlled, meaningful cervical ROM rather than deliberately seeking maximal SCM stretch.\r\n\r\n## Rotation-Based Lengthening\r\n\r\nBecause SCM is a major contralateral rotator, resisted cervical rotation can also load the muscle across changing lengths.\r\n\r\nFor example, the right SCM produces leftward rotation.\r\n\r\nResistance against leftward rotation can therefore train the right SCM dynamically.\r\n\r\nThe muscle becomes relatively shortened as it produces its characteristic rotational action and relatively lengthened when moving in the opposite direction.\r\n\r\nAgain, direct hypertrophy comparisons of long- versus short-length rotational training are not available.\r\n\r\n## SMH Evidence Interpretation\r\n\r\nFor this muscle:\r\n\r\n- anatomical length changes are clear;\r\n- mechanical tension at longer lengths is plausible;\r\n- direct longitudinal hypertrophy evidence is sparse;\r\n- no precise SCM-specific long-length hypertrophy multiplier can be defended.\r\n\r\nAccordingly, `smh_factor` should represent a model-level estimate rather than a directly measured biological coefficient.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Neck Flexion\r\n\r\nDirect resisted neck flexion is the simplest way to produce substantial SCM demand.\r\n\r\nPossible modalities include:\r\n\r\n- dedicated neck machine;\r\n- cable/head harness;\r\n- resistance band;\r\n- manual resistance;\r\n- controlled bodyweight neck curl.\r\n\r\nA hypertrophy-oriented repetition should involve actual cervical motion against an external extension moment.\r\n\r\nSCM activity increases as superficial neck-flexor demand increases, and experimental cervical-flexion work consistently demonstrates SCM recruitment during these tasks. :contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}\r\n\r\n## Bodyweight Neck Curl\r\n\r\nA supine neck curl can provide useful SCM loading if performed as cervical flexion rather than simply lifting the entire torso.\r\n\r\nUseful execution:\r\n\r\n1. begin with the head supported or slightly extended within a comfortable range;\r\n2. flex the cervical spine;\r\n3. raise the head without aggressively projecting the chin;\r\n4. control the eccentric return;\r\n5. avoid momentum.\r\n\r\nAs strength improves, external resistance can be added gradually.\r\n\r\n## Chin Tuck vs Neck Curl\r\n\r\nThese should not be conflated.\r\n\r\n### Chin Tuck / Craniocervical Flexion\r\n\r\nPrimarily used to emphasize deep cervical flexor control.\r\n\r\nTypically:\r\n\r\n- very low load;\r\n- small ROM;\r\n- deliberate minimization of superficial SCM dominance.\r\n\r\n### Resisted Neck Curl\r\n\r\nDesigned to generate substantial neck-flexion torque.\r\n\r\nSCM involvement is expected and desirable.\r\n\r\nTherefore, an exercise that is excellent for `deep_neck_flexors` may intentionally produce much less SCM recruitment than an exercise intended for neck hypertrophy.\r\n\r\nStudies of the craniocervical flexion test show SCM recruitment increases as task pressure increases, while clinical protocols often deliberately target pressure ranges that preserve greater deep-flexor selectivity. :contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}\r\n\r\n## Rotation Training\r\n\r\nBecause unilateral SCM is a strong contralateral rotator, resisted rotation provides a highly specific SCM stimulus.\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n- resisting leftward head rotation → strong right SCM contribution.\r\n\r\nRotation exercises can be performed using:\r\n\r\n- manual resistance;\r\n- bands;\r\n- cables;\r\n- specialized neck devices.\r\n\r\nThey should use controlled ROM.\r\n\r\nFast, ballistic loaded neck rotation is generally unnecessary for hypertrophy.\r\n\r\n## Lateral Flexion\r\n\r\nSCM also contributes to ipsilateral lateral flexion.\r\n\r\nHowever, lateral-flexion torque is shared with other muscles including:\r\n\r\n- scalenes;\r\n- splenius group;\r\n- levator scapulae;\r\n- other cervical extensors/flexors depending on position.\r\n\r\nTherefore lateral flexion is useful as part of multidirectional neck training but is less specific to SCM than combined rotation/flexion mechanics.\r\n\r\n## Isometric Training\r\n\r\nSCM can be trained isometrically through:\r\n\r\n- resisted flexion;\r\n- resisted rotation;\r\n- resisted lateral flexion.\r\n\r\nAdvantages include:\r\n\r\n- easy load control;\r\n- minimal cervical motion;\r\n- applicability to athletic head stabilization.\r\n\r\nIsometric work is particularly useful when the goal includes resisting externally imposed head movement.\r\n\r\nFor hypertrophy alone, dynamic work offers the advantage of exposing the muscle to changing lengths.\r\n\r\n## Harness and Cable Geometry\r\n\r\nExternal load should be interpreted through **torque**, not simply kilograms.\r\n\r\nCervical torque depends on:\r\n\r\n`external force × perpendicular moment arm`\r\n\r\nA light resistance applied far from the cervical axis can create substantial demand.\r\n\r\nCable direction also matters.\r\n\r\nA cable attached to the forehead and pulling posteriorly generates a different vector from one applied:\r\n\r\n- superiorly;\r\n- laterally;\r\n- obliquely.\r\n\r\nTherefore "10 kg neck flexion" is not a fully defined exercise without geometry.\r\n\r\n## Do Not Use the Jaw as the Prime Load Interface\r\n\r\nResistance should preferably be distributed through a secure head harness or appropriate machine.\r\n\r\nUsing the jaw or teeth as the primary loading interface unnecessarily introduces:\r\n\r\n- temporomandibular loading;\r\n- dental stress;\r\n- masticatory-muscle fatigue.\r\n\r\nThe intended target is the cervical musculature.\r\n\r\n## Rotation Technique\r\n\r\nFor SCM-oriented rotation:\r\n\r\n- keep the torso fixed;\r\n- rotate through the cervical region;\r\n- avoid rotating the shoulders;\r\n- use slow concentric and eccentric motion;\r\n- stop before uncomfortable terminal range.\r\n\r\nThe aim is muscular torque, not maximal rotational excursion.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Volume\r\n\r\nSCM is relatively small compared with major limb muscles and receives indirect stabilization demand throughout daily activity.\r\n\r\nDirect neck training therefore does not require large volume.\r\n\r\nA reasonable starting range is approximately:\r\n\r\n- **2-6 hard direct sets per week** involving neck flexion and/or rotation.\r\n\r\nFor a novice to direct neck training, the lower end is usually sufficient initially.\r\n\r\nVolume can increase gradually if neck hypertrophy is a specific objective.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nApproximately **2 sessions per week** is a useful general default.\r\n\r\nOne session can work for maintenance or introductory training.\r\n\r\nThree sessions can be viable when:\r\n\r\n- individual session volume is low;\r\n- loads are controlled;\r\n- cervical soreness does not interfere with training or daily function.\r\n\r\n## Rep Range\r\n\r\nModerate and moderately high repetitions are generally practical:\r\n\r\n- 10-15 reps for heavier controlled dynamic work;\r\n- 15-30 reps for lighter hypertrophy/endurance work.\r\n\r\nThere is little reason to prioritize extremely heavy 1-5RM neck flexion.\r\n\r\nThe cost of technical error rises while the hypertrophic advantage is unclear.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nDynamic SCM-oriented exercises can be trained hard, but repeated uncontrolled absolute failure is unnecessary.\r\n\r\nA reasonable default is:\r\n\r\n- **1-3 RIR**.\r\n\r\nTerminate the set when:\r\n\r\n- ROM decreases substantially;\r\n- motion becomes jerky;\r\n- the torso compensates;\r\n- head position becomes difficult to control;\r\n- unusual neurological or vestibular symptoms appear.\r\n\r\n## Progressive Overload\r\n\r\nProgression can use:\r\n\r\n- more repetitions;\r\n- slightly greater resistance;\r\n- improved ROM;\r\n- better control;\r\n- additional sets.\r\n\r\nBecause initial loads are often small, increases should be modest.\r\n\r\nAn additional 2-3 kg can represent a very large percentage increase in cervical loading.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Distribution\r\n\r\nA simple hypertrophy-oriented neck program could distribute SCM stimulus through:\r\n\r\n- direct flexion;\r\n- rotation;\r\n- lateral-flexion work.\r\n\r\nThere is no requirement to train every possible movement independently if exercises already overlap mechanically.\r\n\r\nFor example, direct flexion plus resisted rotation covers much of SCM's major functional profile.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Deep Neck Flexor Training\r\n\r\nIf both hypertrophy and cervical motor control are desired, separate the concepts.\r\n\r\n### Heavy/Moderate Flexion\r\n\r\nGoal:\r\n\r\n- SCM and global flexor force;\r\n- strength/hypertrophy.\r\n\r\n### Low-Load Craniocervical Flexion\r\n\r\nGoal:\r\n\r\n- deep flexor control;\r\n- minimize excessive superficial substitution.\r\n\r\nThese two exercise classes should not be assigned identical muscle recruitment vectors.\r\n\r\nClinical research explicitly distinguishes superficial SCM recruitment from deep cervical flexor recruitment during craniocervical flexion tasks. :contentReference[oaicite:3]{index=3}\r\n\r\n## Athletic Context\r\n\r\nIn collision or combat sports, SCM training may have additional value for resisting:\r\n\r\n- rotation;\r\n- lateral acceleration;\r\n- flexion-extension perturbations.\r\n\r\nIn this context, isometric and multidirectional strength can be more important than hypertrophy alone.\r\n\r\nTraining specificity should reflect the expected perturbation direction.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## It Has One of the Most Literal Muscle Names\r\n\r\n`Sterno-cleido-mastoid` essentially lists its attachments:\r\n\r\n- sternum;\r\n- clavicle;\r\n- mastoid process.\r\n\r\nThe name itself is therefore a compact anatomical map.\r\n\r\n## One Muscle Produces Opposite-Side Rotation\r\n\r\nRight SCM turns the face predominantly to the **left**, and vice versa.\r\n\r\nThis makes it initially counterintuitive compared with muscles whose unilateral action produces motion toward their own side in every plane.\r\n\r\nThe same contraction combines:\r\n\r\n- ipsilateral side bend;\r\n- contralateral rotation.\r\n\r\n## It Can Flex the Neck and Extend the Head\r\n\r\nSCM is an excellent example of why "a muscle has one action" is an inadequate model.\r\n\r\nDepending on the reference segment and cervical configuration, bilateral contraction can contribute simultaneously to:\r\n\r\n- lower cervical flexion;\r\n- upper cervical/head extension.\r\n\r\nThis apparently contradictory behavior follows directly from its attachment geometry. :contentReference[oaicite:4]{index=4}\r\n\r\n## It Is an Accessory Respiratory Muscle\r\n\r\nWhen its superior attachment is stabilized, SCM can act on its inferior attachment and assist elevation of the sternum and clavicle during forceful inspiration.\r\n\r\nVisible SCM recruitment can therefore occur during severe ventilatory effort even when no head movement occurs.\r\n\r\n## It Defines the Neck Triangles\r\n\r\nThe SCM is the major superficial landmark separating the:\r\n\r\n- anterior triangle;\r\n- posterior triangle\r\n\r\nof the neck.\r\n\r\nThis gives it disproportionate importance in clinical and surgical anatomy.\r\n\r\n## Torticollis Demonstrates Its Combined Actions\r\n\r\nPersistent unilateral shortening of SCM produces the characteristic combination of:\r\n\r\n- head tilt toward the affected side;\r\n- rotation of the face toward the opposite side.\r\n\r\nThis posture essentially displays the muscle's unilateral actions anatomically.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\n`sternocleidomastoid` should not be modeled as a pure sagittal-plane flexor.\r\n\r\nIt requires at least three mechanical dimensions:\r\n\r\n1. cervical flexion;\r\n2. cervical rotation;\r\n3. cervical lateral flexion.\r\n\r\nA fourth, low-priority dimension can represent respiratory or generalized stabilization demand if needed.\r\n\r\n## Bilateral Flexion\r\n\r\nDuring symmetric resisted cervical flexion:\r\n\r\n- both SCMs should receive substantial demand;\r\n- deep cervical flexors should also receive demand;\r\n- allocation depends on movement strategy.\r\n\r\nA gross high-force neck curl should bias SCM more heavily than a low-load craniocervical-flexion drill.\r\n\r\n## Rotation\r\n\r\nThe side-specific relationship is:\r\n\r\n### Leftward Rotation\r\n\r\nHigh contribution from **right SCM**.\r\n\r\n### Rightward Rotation\r\n\r\nHigh contribution from **left SCM**.\r\n\r\nIf Agonez eventually models unilateral muscle vectors, this sign reversal matters.\r\n\r\nIf the current database stores bilateral muscles as one aggregate, rotational demand can be represented as SCM-system demand without encoding laterality.\r\n\r\n## Lateral Flexion\r\n\r\nRight lateral flexion recruits:\r\n\r\n- right SCM;\r\n- right-sided synergists.\r\n\r\nLeft lateral flexion recruits the left SCM.\r\n\r\nAgain, a bilateral aggregate loses side-specific information but preserves total muscular demand adequately for most training calculations.\r\n\r\n## Length Model\r\n\r\nA single scalar joint angle is inadequate for perfect SCM length estimation.\r\n\r\nA better conceptual model would be:\r\n\r\n`L_SCM = f(cervical_flexion_extension, axial_rotation, lateral_flexion)`\r\n\r\nFor a bilateral aggregate used in symmetric neck flexion, this can be simplified substantially.\r\n\r\nFor direct flexion:\r\n\r\n- moderate cervical extension → relatively longer anterior neck state;\r\n- neutral → intermediate;\r\n- cervical flexion → progressively shorter SCM state.\r\n\r\nFor unilateral rotation/lateral flexion, side-specific geometry becomes necessary for precision.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Allocation\r\n\r\n### Bodyweight Neck Curl\r\n\r\nHigh SCM demand.\r\n\r\nAlso involves deep cervical flexors, but SCM contribution rises substantially as gross flexion torque increases.\r\n\r\n### Craniocervical Flexion / Chin-Tuck Drill\r\n\r\nLow-to-moderate SCM allocation by design.\r\n\r\nThe exercise is typically used to emphasize deep flexor recruitment while limiting superficial SCM dominance. :contentReference[oaicite:5]{index=5}\r\n\r\n### Resisted Neck Rotation\r\n\r\nHigh SCM demand, particularly contralateral to the direction of rotation.\r\n\r\n### Resisted Lateral Flexion\r\n\r\nModerate-to-high SCM demand ipsilateral to the bending direction, shared with several other cervical muscles.\r\n\r\n### Deadlift / Row / Shrug\r\n\r\nUsually low dynamic SCM demand.\r\n\r\nSome stabilization can occur, but these should not be treated as substitutes for direct flexion or rotation.\r\n\r\n## Superficial vs Deep Flexor Model\r\n\r\nAgonez should preserve the distinction:\r\n\r\n### `sternocleidomastoid`\r\n\r\n- superficial;\r\n- large gross torque producer;\r\n- flexion + rotation + lateral flexion;\r\n- high recruitment in demanding neck-flexion tasks.\r\n\r\n### deep cervical flexors\r\n\r\n- longus colli;\r\n- longus capitis;\r\n- smaller and deeper;\r\n- greater segmental-control emphasis;\r\n- selectively targeted by low-load craniocervical-flexion tasks.\r\n\r\nThis prevents the model from interpreting every "neck flexion" exercise identically.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nThere is insufficient direct evidence to give SCM a strongly evidence-based stretch-mediated hypertrophy classification.\r\n\r\nThe safest interpretation is:\r\n\r\n- SCM length changes meaningfully with cervical position;\r\n- controlled loading from relatively lengthened configurations is mechanically plausible;\r\n- extreme cervical ROM is unnecessary;\r\n- the exact hypertrophic benefit of longer-length SCM training remains unknown.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **sternal/clavicular origins and mastoid/nuchal insertion:** high;\r\n- **accessory-nerve motor innervation:** high;\r\n- **contralateral rotation:** high;\r\n- **ipsilateral lateral flexion:** high;\r\n- **bilateral cervical-flexion contribution:** high;\r\n- **complex upper-vs-lower cervical action:** anatomically well supported;\r\n- **accessory inspiratory role:** high;\r\n- **SCM recruitment during loaded cervical flexion:** directly supported;\r\n- **hypertrophy-specific programming evidence:** limited;\r\n- **stretch-mediated hypertrophy:** very limited direct evidence.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **sternocleidomastoid = a large superficial anterolateral cervical muscle producing gross flexion, contralateral rotation and ipsilateral lateral-flexion torque, with mechanically complex craniocervical actions and substantial recruitment during high-force neck stabilization.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/Sternocleidomastoid}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	11.80	10.00	0.985	14.04	13.83
27	rectus_abdominis	Musculus rectus abdominis	Core	Core	685.00	648.67	27.50	Parallel/Polygastric	0.445	0.555	high	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\nThe **rectus abdominis** is a paired, vertically oriented muscle of the anterior abdominal wall extending between the pubis and lower anterior thorax.\r\n\r\nIts principal mechanical roles are:\r\n\r\n- **flexion of the vertebral column**;\r\n- **posterior rotation / posterior tilt of the pelvis relative to the thorax**;\r\n- **anti-extension of the trunk and lumbar spine**;\r\n- abdominal-wall compression;\r\n- contribution to trunk stiffness and intra-abdominal pressure.\r\n\r\nIt should not be treated as a primary hip flexor.\r\n\r\nRectus abdominis does not cross the hip joint.\r\n\r\nDuring exercises such as:\r\n\r\n- hanging leg raise;\r\n- lying leg raise;\r\n- dragon flag;\r\n\r\nit can nevertheless experience very high demand because hip-flexor force and the mass of the lower body create an external **lumbar-extension / anterior-pelvic-tilt moment**.\r\n\r\nRectus abdominis resists that moment through:\r\n\r\n- posterior pelvic rotation;\r\n- spinal flexion torque;\r\n- isometric anti-extension torque.\r\n\r\nThis distinction is fundamental for exercise modelling.\r\n\r\nA useful computational identity is:\r\n\r\n> **rectus abdominis = a long-fascicled anterior trunk muscle that approximates the rib cage and pelvis through spinal flexion and posterior pelvic rotation, while also generating large anti-extension torque under long-lever loading.**\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\nRectus abdominis consists of paired vertical muscle columns positioned on either side of the linea alba.\r\n\r\n## Origin\r\n\r\nThe inferior attachment arises from the anterior pelvis, primarily around the:\r\n\r\n- pubic crest;\r\n- pubic symphysis.\r\n\r\nThe muscle then travels superiorly through the rectus sheath.\r\n\r\n## Insertion\r\n\r\nThe superior attachment includes the:\r\n\r\n- xiphoid process;\r\n- costal cartilages of approximately ribs 5-7.\r\n\r\nThus the muscle connects:\r\n\r\n- anterior pelvis;\r\n- inferior anterior thorax.\r\n\r\nShortening reduces the distance between these structures.\r\n\r\nDepending on which end is relatively fixed, this can manifest as:\r\n\r\n- thoracic flexion toward the pelvis;\r\n- posterior pelvic rotation toward the thorax;\r\n- or both.\r\n\r\n## Tendinous Intersections\r\n\r\nRectus abdominis is interrupted by several **tendinous intersections**.\r\n\r\nThese transverse connective-tissue bands create the familiar segmented appearance of the "six-pack."\r\n\r\nThe intersections anchor the muscle to the anterior rectus sheath and divide it into serial visible compartments.\r\n\r\nHowever:\r\n\r\n> the tendinous intersections do not create completely independent muscles.\r\n\r\nForce and neural drive can vary regionally, but rectus abdominis remains a continuous functional muscle system.\r\n\r\n## Rectus Sheath\r\n\r\nRectus abdominis lies within the **rectus sheath**, formed largely by aponeuroses of:\r\n\r\n- external oblique;\r\n- internal oblique;\r\n- transversus abdominis.\r\n\r\nThe left and right sheaths meet centrally at the **linea alba**.\r\n\r\nThis integrates rectus abdominis mechanically with the broader abdominal wall.\r\n\r\nThus abdominal force transmission is not merely:\r\n\r\n`pubis → RA → ribs`\r\n\r\nbut occurs through a wider fascial and aponeurotic network.\r\n\r\n## Architecture\r\n\r\nRectus abdominis has relatively **long fascicles** compared with other abdominal-wall muscles.\r\n\r\nHuman architectural studies identify RA as possessing the longest optimal fascicle length among the major abdominal wall muscles, while its physiological cross-sectional area is relatively modest compared with internal oblique. :contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}\r\n\r\nThis architecture is consistent with a muscle capable of:\r\n\r\n- substantial excursion;\r\n- producing force across a comparatively large range of trunk positions.\r\n\r\nFor Agonez, a single whole-muscle:\r\n\r\n- optimal fiber length;\r\n- PCSA;\r\n- architecture parameter\r\n\r\nshould still be interpreted as an effective approximation because regional morphology varies along the muscle.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nRectus abdominis receives segmental innervation from the **thoracoabdominal nerves**, corresponding broadly to the lower thoracic spinal nerves.\r\n\r\nThe segmental pattern allows regional neural control along the superior-inferior length of the muscle.\r\n\r\nThis is relevant to the longstanding question of whether "upper abs" and "lower abs" can be preferentially recruited.\r\n\r\nRegional differences in activation are possible and have been measured experimentally.\r\n\r\nHowever, they should be understood as:\r\n\r\n> **regional bias within one continuous muscle**\r\n\r\nrather than complete isolation of separate upper and lower muscles.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Spinal Flexion\r\n\r\nBilateral rectus-abdominis contraction creates **flexion torque of the vertebral column**.\r\n\r\nIn an open-chain trunk curl:\r\n\r\n- thorax moves toward pelvis.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- crunch;\r\n- cable crunch;\r\n- machine trunk flexion.\r\n\r\nThe major external opposing moment is usually generated by gravity or a cable/load acting to extend the trunk.\r\n\r\n## Posterior Pelvic Tilt\r\n\r\nIf the thorax is relatively fixed, rectus abdominis can pull the anterior pelvis superiorly toward the rib cage.\r\n\r\nThis contributes to **posterior pelvic tilt / posterior pelvic rotation**.\r\n\r\nThis action is particularly relevant during:\r\n\r\n- reverse crunch;\r\n- hanging leg raise;\r\n- dragon flag;\r\n- long-lever plank.\r\n\r\nPosterior pelvic tilt reduces lumbar lordotic extension and can convert an otherwise primarily hip-flexion exercise into a much more demanding abdominal task.\r\n\r\n## Anti-Extension\r\n\r\nRectus abdominis can generate very high force without visible spinal flexion.\r\n\r\nIf an external load attempts to extend the lumbar spine, RA can produce an opposing flexion moment while the trunk remains approximately fixed.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- plank;\r\n- long-lever plank;\r\n- ab-wheel rollout;\r\n- dragon flag;\r\n- hanging leg raise;\r\n- certain overhead-loaded tasks.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n`spinal_ROM ≈ 0`\r\n\r\ndoes **not** imply:\r\n\r\n`rectus_abdominis_demand ≈ 0`.\r\n\r\nThis is one of the most important modelling principles for the muscle.\r\n\r\n## Relationship With Hip Flexors\r\n\r\nHip flexors such as:\r\n\r\n- iliopsoas;\r\n- rectus femoris\r\n\r\ncan create an anterior pelvic rotation / lumbar-extension tendency under load.\r\n\r\nRectus abdominis can oppose this tendency.\r\n\r\nTherefore hip-flexion exercises can simultaneously produce high:\r\n\r\n- `iliopsoas` demand;\r\n- `rectus_abdominis` demand.\r\n\r\nThey are not mutually exclusive.\r\n\r\nThe muscles are performing different joint-level tasks.\r\n\r\n## Abdominal Compression\r\n\r\nRectus abdominis contributes to compression of the abdominal contents.\r\n\r\nTogether with:\r\n\r\n- obliques;\r\n- transversus abdominis;\r\n- diaphragm;\r\n- pelvic floor;\r\n\r\nit contributes to trunk stiffness and intra-abdominal pressure.\r\n\r\nBiomechanical modelling shows that abdominal activation can increase lumbar spinal stability even when the muscles are not producing visible trunk movement. :contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}\r\n\r\n## Forced Expiration\r\n\r\nBy compressing the abdominal contents and pulling the rib cage inferiorly, rectus abdominis can contribute to forceful expiration.\r\n\r\nThis is relevant during:\r\n\r\n- coughing;\r\n- forced breathing;\r\n- high-intensity exertion.\r\n\r\nFor resistance-training modelling, this respiratory role is secondary.\r\n\r\n# Regional Function: "Upper" vs "Lower" Abs\r\n\r\nRectus abdominis can exhibit regional differences in activation.\r\n\r\nThis does **not** mean the upper and lower regions are anatomically separate muscles.\r\n\r\nSurface EMG studies have reported differential superior/inferior RA activation depending on exercise and execution. For example, classic work found greater upper-RA activity during curl-up patterns and relatively greater lower-region demand during posterior-pelvic-tilt tasks. :contentReference[oaicite:3]{index=3}\r\n\r\nOther exercise comparisons similarly show that upper and lower recording sites can respond differently across abdominal movements. :contentReference[oaicite:4]{index=4}\r\n\r\nThe defensible interpretation is:\r\n\r\n> **regional recruitment can be biased, but not cleanly isolated.**\r\n\r\n## Thorax-Toward-Pelvis Bias\r\n\r\nExercises emphasizing spinal flexion from above, such as:\r\n\r\n- crunch;\r\n- cable crunch,\r\n\r\nmay relatively favor superior regions.\r\n\r\n## Pelvis-Toward-Thorax Bias\r\n\r\nExercises emphasizing:\r\n\r\n- posterior pelvic rotation;\r\n- reverse curling;\r\n- leg/pelvic elevation\r\n\r\nmay relatively increase demand in inferior recording regions.\r\n\r\nHowever, the entire muscle remains active because tension must be transmitted between:\r\n\r\n- ribs;\r\n- pelvis.\r\n\r\nFor Agonez, one `rectus_abdominis` aggregate is therefore appropriate.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nDirect evidence specifically testing **stretch-mediated hypertrophy of rectus abdominis** is limited.\r\n\r\nMechanically, rectus abdominis is lengthened by increasing the distance between:\r\n\r\n- rib cage;\r\n- pubis.\r\n\r\nThe clearest movement producing this is **spinal extension**.\r\n\r\n## Spinal Extension\r\n\r\nMoving from flexion toward extension lengthens RA.\r\n\r\nA loaded trunk-flexion exercise beginning from modest extension can therefore expose the muscle to resistance at longer lengths.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- cable crunch beginning from slight extension;\r\n- machine abdominal flexion with an extended starting position;\r\n- stability-ball crunch allowing controlled thoracic extension.\r\n\r\n## Long Fascicles\r\n\r\nArchitectural measurements show unusually long rectus-abdominis fascicles compared with other abdominal muscles. :contentReference[oaicite:5]{index=5}\r\n\r\nThis suggests capacity for substantial operating-length excursion.\r\n\r\nHowever, long fascicles alone do not prove a special long-length hypertrophic advantage.\r\n\r\n## Anti-Extension Exercises\r\n\r\nExercises such as:\r\n\r\n- ab-wheel rollout;\r\n- long-lever plank;\r\n- dragon flag\r\n\r\ncan place RA under high force while the trunk is relatively extended or while an external moment attempts to increase extension.\r\n\r\nThis creates a mechanically interesting combination:\r\n\r\n- relatively long muscle state;\r\n- high active flexion torque;\r\n- little visible shortening.\r\n\r\nThat makes anti-extension work potentially relevant to long-length loading even though it is largely isometric.\r\n\r\n## Stability-Ball Crunch\r\n\r\nA ball-supported crunch can permit controlled extension of the thorax before flexion.\r\n\r\nThis can increase RA excursion compared with a floor crunch, where the floor limits extension.\r\n\r\nThe practical advantage is not instability itself.\r\n\r\nIt is the possibility of combining:\r\n\r\n- greater ROM;\r\n- loaded flexion.\r\n\r\n## Extreme Lumbar Extension\r\n\r\nMore extension is not automatically better.\r\n\r\nThe goal is active muscular loading through a productive ROM, not maximization of passive spinal extension.\r\n\r\nExcessive extension can shift demands toward:\r\n\r\n- passive spinal tissues;\r\n- hip-flexor length;\r\n- individual joint end ranges.\r\n\r\nTherefore long-length training should use controlled, tolerable extension.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Interpretation\r\n\r\nThe evidence-based position is conservative:\r\n\r\n- spinal extension clearly lengthens RA;\r\n- RA has long fascicles and large excursion potential;\r\n- anti-extension exercises can generate high active tension at relatively extended trunk positions;\r\n- direct longitudinal evidence establishing a special RA stretch-mediated hypertrophy advantage is sparse.\r\n\r\nA moderate theoretical `smh_factor` is reasonable, but high experimental confidence is not justified.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Crunch\r\n\r\nThe basic crunch is a direct spinal-flexion exercise.\r\n\r\nIts defining motion should be:\r\n\r\n- rib cage approaching pelvis through trunk flexion.\r\n\r\nA good repetition emphasizes actual spinal movement rather than merely:\r\n\r\n- flexing the neck;\r\n- lifting the shoulders through momentum.\r\n\r\nRA activation is high during curl-up exercises in experimental comparisons. :contentReference[oaicite:6]{index=6}\r\n\r\n## Kneeling Cable Crunch\r\n\r\nThe kneeling cable crunch allows:\r\n\r\n- external progressive overload;\r\n- larger loading than bodyweight crunches;\r\n- stable repeated spinal flexion.\r\n\r\nIt is one of the most straightforward hypertrophy exercises for rectus abdominis.\r\n\r\nA useful execution maintains relatively stable hips while allowing:\r\n\r\n- thoracic/lumbar flexion;\r\n- rib-pelvis approximation.\r\n\r\nIf the trainee mainly hinges at the hip, more movement occurs without proportional additional RA shortening.\r\n\r\n## Bodyweight Crunch\r\n\r\nBodyweight crunches can train RA effectively, especially in less advanced trainees.\r\n\r\nTheir main limitation is progressive overload.\r\n\r\nOnce high repetitions become easy, progression becomes less convenient than with:\r\n\r\n- cables;\r\n- machines;\r\n- weighted variants.\r\n\r\n## Reverse Crunch\r\n\r\nThe reverse crunch emphasizes posterior rotation of the pelvis relative to the thorax.\r\n\r\nA productive repetition involves:\r\n\r\n1. hip position establishing the starting geometry;\r\n2. actual pelvic curling;\r\n3. shortening of the rib-to-pubis distance.\r\n\r\nSimply raising the knees through additional hip flexion is not equivalent.\r\n\r\nThe key transition is from:\r\n\r\n`hip flexion`\r\n\r\nto:\r\n\r\n`posterior pelvic rotation`.\r\n\r\n## Hanging Knee Raise\r\n\r\nA hanging knee raise has two major components.\r\n\r\n### Hip Flexion\r\n\r\nHigh demand on:\r\n- iliopsoas;\r\n- other hip flexors.\r\n\r\n### Posterior Pelvic Tilt / Trunk Flexion\r\n\r\nHigh demand on:\r\n- rectus abdominis;\r\n- obliques.\r\n\r\nIf the athlete merely raises the femurs to approximately horizontal with the pelvis unchanged, the movement is substantially hip-flexor dominant.\r\n\r\nIf the pelvis subsequently curls toward the rib cage, RA demand rises substantially.\r\n\r\n## Hanging Straight-Leg Raise\r\n\r\nStraight knees increase the lower-limb lever arm.\r\n\r\nThis creates a larger external moment.\r\n\r\nConsequently, compared with a bent-knee raise, it can generate much larger:\r\n\r\n- hip-flexion torque;\r\n- anterior-pelvic-tilt / lumbar-extension challenge.\r\n\r\nRectus abdominis must generate substantial anti-extension and pelvic-control torque.\r\n\r\nThis explains why your `hanging_leg_raise` can legitimately receive high allocations to **both**:\r\n\r\n- `iliopsoas`;\r\n- `rectus_abdominis`.\r\n\r\n## Dragon Flag\r\n\r\nThe dragon flag is primarily a high-lever **anti-extension** exercise.\r\n\r\nAs the body moves toward horizontal, gravity creates a very large external extension moment around the trunk/pelvis.\r\n\r\nRectus abdominis must oppose this.\r\n\r\nThe exercise also involves:\r\n\r\n- obliques;\r\n- hip flexors;\r\n- shoulder girdle/lats through anchoring.\r\n\r\nIt should receive very high RA demand despite relatively little repetitive spinal flexion.\r\n\r\n## Ab-Wheel Rollout\r\n\r\nRollouts are another high-value anti-extension pattern.\r\n\r\nAs the arms travel farther forward:\r\n\r\n- the body's center of mass moves farther from the support axis;\r\n- external extension torque rises.\r\n\r\nRA and other abdominal muscles must oppose this torque.\r\n\r\nRollout-type exercises can produce very high rectus-abdominis activation in acute comparisons. :contentReference[oaicite:7]{index=7}\r\n\r\n## Plank\r\n\r\nA standard plank creates isometric anti-extension demand.\r\n\r\nThe magnitude depends strongly on body geometry.\r\n\r\nMoving the elbows farther forward increases the external lever arm.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n> a long-lever plank can be substantially harder without any added weight.\r\n\r\nLong-lever/posterior-tilt plank variants have demonstrated greater RA activity than conventional plank setups. :contentReference[oaicite:8]{index=8}\r\n\r\n## Sit-Up\r\n\r\nA sit-up combines:\r\n\r\n- spinal flexion;\r\n- hip flexion.\r\n\r\nEarly movement can involve substantial abdominal flexion.\r\n\r\nAs the torso rises farther, hip-flexor contribution becomes increasingly important.\r\n\r\nTherefore a sit-up should not be treated as pure RA isolation.\r\n\r\n## Leg Raise\r\n\r\nLeg raises are frequently described as "lower-ab exercises."\r\n\r\nThis is incomplete.\r\n\r\nThe femur is flexed primarily by hip flexors.\r\n\r\nRA's role is to:\r\n\r\n- stabilize the pelvis;\r\n- resist extension;\r\n- produce posterior pelvic rotation if the pelvis curls.\r\n\r\nThus execution determines whether a leg raise becomes:\r\n\r\n- mostly hip-flexor work;\r\n- or a combined hip-flexor + abdominal exercise.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Direct Training Is Valuable\r\n\r\nUnlike abdominal stabilization occurring incidentally during compounds, direct RA training allows:\r\n\r\n- larger spinal-flexion or anti-extension torque;\r\n- clearer progressive overload;\r\n- closer approach to local muscular failure.\r\n\r\nAcute comparisons generally show greater abdominal activation in isolated core exercises than in integrated lower-body tasks. :contentReference[oaicite:9]{index=9}\r\n\r\n## Weekly Volume\r\n\r\nA practical starting range for hypertrophy-oriented direct RA training can be approximately:\r\n\r\n- **4-8 hard direct sets per week**.\r\n\r\nThis can include:\r\n\r\n- cable crunches;\r\n- reverse crunches;\r\n- hanging leg raises with active pelvic curl;\r\n- rollouts;\r\n- dragon flags.\r\n\r\nAdvanced trainees prioritizing abdominal hypertrophy may use more, but indirect compound bracing should also be considered.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nTwo to three weekly exposures are practical.\r\n\r\nDirect abdominal training generally creates modest systemic fatigue, but heavy trunk work can interfere with:\r\n\r\n- squats;\r\n- deadlifts;\r\n- Pendlay rows;\r\n- other exercises requiring high bracing capacity.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\n### Loaded Dynamic Flexion\r\n\r\nUseful ranges include:\r\n\r\n- 6-12 reps;\r\n- 8-15 reps;\r\n- 10-20 reps.\r\n\r\n### Bodyweight / Long-Lever Work\r\n\r\nUseful ranges depend on exercise difficulty:\r\n\r\n- controlled repetitions;\r\n- timed holds for isometrics.\r\n\r\nThere is no biological requirement that abs be trained only with very high repetitions.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nStable cable or machine crunches can generally be trained close to muscular failure:\r\n\r\n- approximately 0-2 RIR.\r\n\r\nMore complex exercises such as:\r\n\r\n- dragon flags;\r\n- rollouts\r\n\r\nshould terminate when pelvic/spinal control deteriorates rather than when arbitrary movement can no longer occur.\r\n\r\n## Progressive Overload\r\n\r\nRA can be progressively overloaded through:\r\n\r\n- greater external resistance;\r\n- longer lever arms;\r\n- larger controlled ROM;\r\n- harder body angle;\r\n- more repetitions.\r\n\r\nFor anti-extension work, increasing lever length can have a very large mechanical effect.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Selection\r\n\r\nA simple hypertrophy-oriented combination could include:\r\n\r\n1. **loaded spinal flexion**;\r\n2. **posterior-pelvic-tilt / anti-extension work**.\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n\r\n- kneeling cable crunch;\r\n- hanging leg raise or dragon flag.\r\n\r\nThis provides mechanically different abdominal stimuli without requiring many redundant exercises.\r\n\r\n## Direct vs Indirect Volume\r\n\r\nHeavy compounds create abdominal bracing demand, but this should not automatically count as full RA hypertrophy sets.\r\n\r\nDuring:\r\n\r\n- squat;\r\n- deadlift;\r\n- row;\r\n\r\nthe abdominal system co-contracts for stabilization.\r\n\r\nThe specific rectus-abdominis force may be meaningful but often substantially less locally limiting than during dedicated abdominal exercise.\r\n\r\nTherefore compound sets should generally receive partial rather than full RA set credit.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## The "Six-Pack" Is One Pair of Muscles\r\n\r\nThe visible blocks are created by:\r\n\r\n- left and right rectus abdominis;\r\n- tendinous intersections.\r\n\r\nThey are not six independent muscles.\r\n\r\n## Upper and Lower Abs Can Be Biased, Not Isolated\r\n\r\nRegional EMG differences have repeatedly been observed during different abdominal exercises. :contentReference[oaicite:10]{index=10}\r\n\r\nHowever, both regions belong to the same continuous muscle.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n- crunch may bias superior RA;\r\n- reverse-curl/pelvic movement may bias inferior RA;\r\n\r\nbut neither selectively switches off the opposite region.\r\n\r\n## Rectus Abdominis Has Very Long Fascicles\r\n\r\nAmong the abdominal-wall muscles, RA has exceptionally long fascicles and relatively small PCSA compared with muscles such as internal oblique. :contentReference[oaicite:11]{index=11}\r\n\r\nThis is consistent with a functional bias toward:\r\n\r\n- large excursion;\r\n- shortening across a wide trunk ROM\r\n\r\nrather than merely maximizing isometric force from a short-fiber architecture.\r\n\r\n## Leg Raises Are Not Anatomically Hip-Flexed by the Abs\r\n\r\nRectus abdominis does not cross the hip.\r\n\r\nTherefore it cannot directly flex the femur at the acetabulum.\r\n\r\nIts high demand in leg raises comes from controlling:\r\n\r\n- pelvis;\r\n- lumbar spine;\r\n- rib-pelvis relationship.\r\n\r\nThis is one of the most important distinctions between visible exercise motion and actual muscle action.\r\n\r\n## Isometric Ab Exercises Can Be Extremely Demanding\r\n\r\nA successful dragon flag or rollout may involve little actual lumbar movement.\r\n\r\nThat is precisely the goal.\r\n\r\nThe muscle nevertheless generates a large internal flexion moment opposing gravity.\r\n\r\nAgain:\r\n\r\n> **movement prevented can represent as much muscular function as movement produced.**\r\n\r\n## Rectus Abdominis Can Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nLongitudinal imaging studies demonstrate that abdominal training can increase RA cross-sectional dimensions.\r\n\r\nFor example, prolonged Pilates training produced measurable abdominal-wall hypertrophy, particularly of rectus abdominis. :contentReference[oaicite:12]{index=12}\r\n\r\nTraining studies using abdominal exercise have likewise reported increases in abdominal muscle CSA alongside improved performance. :contentReference[oaicite:13]{index=13}\r\n\r\nThus the abs are not merely endurance/stabilization muscles; they respond structurally to progressive resistance.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\n`rectus_abdominis` should primarily receive demand from:\r\n\r\n1. **spinal-flexion torque**;\r\n2. **anti-extension torque**;\r\n3. **posterior-pelvic-rotation torque**;\r\n4. abdominal compression / global bracing as a secondary component.\r\n\r\nIt should receive approximately:\r\n\r\n- zero direct hip-flexion torque.\r\n\r\nThis is a fundamental distinction from `iliopsoas`.\r\n\r\n## Hip Flexion vs Pelvic Flexion\r\n\r\nFor leg-raise exercises, the movement should conceptually be decomposed into:\r\n\r\n`leg_raise = hip_flexion + pelvic_rotation`\r\n\r\n### Hip Flexion Component\r\n\r\nPrimary:\r\n- `iliopsoas`;\r\n- rectus femoris;\r\n- other hip flexors.\r\n\r\n### Posterior Pelvic Rotation Component\r\n\r\nPrimary:\r\n- `rectus_abdominis`;\r\n- obliques.\r\n\r\nRA can additionally work isometrically throughout the hip-flexion phase to resist anterior pelvic rotation.\r\n\r\n## Hanging Leg Raise Allocation\r\n\r\nA well-executed straight-leg hanging raise should receive high:\r\n\r\n- `iliopsoas`;\r\n- `rectus_abdominis`.\r\n\r\nBut for **different reasons**.\r\n\r\n### Iliopsoas\r\n\r\nProduces:\r\n- hip-flexion torque.\r\n\r\n### Rectus Abdominis\r\n\r\nProduces:\r\n- posterior-pelvic-tilt torque;\r\n- anti-extension torque.\r\n\r\nThis is mechanically much cleaner than labeling the exercise simply "abs" or "hip flexors."\r\n\r\n## Kneeling Cable Crunch Allocation\r\n\r\nVery high `rectus_abdominis` demand when true spinal flexion occurs.\r\n\r\nAlso meaningful:\r\n- obliques.\r\n\r\nLow:\r\n- iliopsoas if hip angle remains approximately fixed.\r\n\r\nThis should be among the cleanest RA-specific vectors in the Agonez exercise catalogue.\r\n\r\n## Bodyweight Crunch\r\n\r\nHigh RA specificity.\r\n\r\nLow systemic cost.\r\n\r\nPrimary limitation:\r\n- low progressive overload potential once the exercise becomes easy.\r\n\r\n## Dragon Flag Allocation\r\n\r\nVery high:\r\n- `rectus_abdominis`;\r\n- `obliques`.\r\n\r\nMeaningful:\r\n- `iliopsoas`;\r\n- lats/upper-body anchoring musculature.\r\n\r\nIts RA demand arises primarily from **anti-extension**, not repetitive spinal flexion.\r\n\r\n## Hanging Oblique Knee Raise\r\n\r\nSubstantial:\r\n- rectus abdominis;\r\n- obliques.\r\n\r\nVery high:\r\n- iliopsoas.\r\n\r\nRelative oblique allocation rises as genuine pelvic/trunk rotation increases.\r\n\r\n## Pallof Press\r\n\r\nLow-to-moderate rectus-abdominis contribution through global bracing.\r\n\r\nHigh relative:\r\n- obliques / rotational stabilizers.\r\n\r\nRA should not dominate unless the cable also creates a large extension moment.\r\n\r\n## Pendlay Row\r\n\r\nModerate RA bracing demand may occur, but the principal trunk-plane load is usually an external flexion moment.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n- `erector_spinae` should dominate sagittal trunk torque;\r\n- abdominal musculature co-contracts for stiffness.\r\n\r\nDo not give RA symmetrical force simply because the trunk is under large load.\r\n\r\n## Squat\r\n\r\nMeaningful bracing demand.\r\n\r\nHowever, direct RA hypertrophic allocation should remain below that of dedicated abdominal work.\r\n\r\nThe external sagittal trunk moment often requires large erector force, with abdominal co-contraction contributing to stabilization and pressure.\r\n\r\n## Anti-Extension Torque Model\r\n\r\nA useful simplified model is:\r\n\r\n`M_ext = Σ(F_external × perpendicular_distance_to_lumbopelvic_axis)`\r\n\r\nRA must generate part of the opposing:\r\n\r\n`M_flexion_internal`.\r\n\r\nExamples of increasing external moment:\r\n\r\n- straighter legs in leg raises;\r\n- farther arm reach in rollout;\r\n- longer body lever in dragon flag;\r\n- elbows placed farther forward in plank.\r\n\r\nThis is more mechanically useful than simply classifying exercise difficulty.\r\n\r\n## Lever-Length Effect in Hanging Leg Raise\r\n\r\nAn extended knee moves lower-leg and foot mass farther from the hip/pelvic axis.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`M_external_straight_leg > M_external_bent_knee`\r\n\r\nat comparable body orientation.\r\n\r\nThis raises both:\r\n\r\n- hip-flexor demand;\r\n- abdominal anti-extension demand.\r\n\r\nHence straight-leg and knee-raise variants should not receive identical exercise vectors.\r\n\r\n## Regional Upper/Lower Modelling\r\n\r\nFor the current Agonez architecture, I would **not split rectus abdominis**.\r\n\r\nEvidence supports regional activation differences, but the regions:\r\n\r\n- are structurally continuous;\r\n- share force transmission;\r\n- are difficult to model independently with reliable exercise-force coefficients. :contentReference[oaicite:14]{index=14}\r\n\r\nThe most defensible use is a small exercise-specific regional interpretation inside the Bible rather than separate muscle rows.\r\n\r\n## Length-Tension Model\r\n\r\nA simple first-order model can use rib-pelvis distance / spinal flexion-extension:\r\n\r\n### Longer\r\n\r\n- trunk/spine extended;\r\n- pelvis anteriorly rotated relative to rib cage.\r\n\r\n### Intermediate\r\n\r\n- neutral trunk.\r\n\r\n### Shorter\r\n\r\n- spinal flexion;\r\n- posterior pelvic rotation with rib-pelvis approximation.\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_RA ≈ f(ribcage_pelvis_relative_angle)`\r\n\r\nThis is superior to using hip angle directly.\r\n\r\nHip angle matters only indirectly insofar as it changes:\r\n\r\n- pelvic orientation;\r\n- external torque.\r\n\r\n## Long Fascicle Implication\r\n\r\nThe unusually long fascicles measured for RA mean the muscle can change length substantially across trunk ROM. :contentReference[oaicite:15]{index=15}\r\n\r\nFor Agonez, this supports avoiding an overly narrow `E_LT` optimum.\r\n\r\nRA probably tolerates and functions over a relatively broad excursion compared with shorter-fascicled abdominal muscles.\r\n\r\nThat does **not** mean maximum force is constant across ROM.\r\n\r\nIt means the architecture is suited to relatively large shortening/lengthening excursion.\r\n\r\n## E_MA Considerations\r\n\r\nRA moment arm depends on:\r\n\r\n- spinal segment;\r\n- trunk posture;\r\n- ribcage geometry;\r\n- abdominal-wall position.\r\n\r\nBecause it spans multiple spinal segments, there is no single anatomically exact "rectus abdominis moment arm."\r\n\r\nFor KISS modelling, a trunk-level effective flexion moment arm is more realistic than pretending to calculate every lumbar segment independently.\r\n\r\n## ETU and Isometrics\r\n\r\nRA is one of the muscles for which:\r\n\r\n`dynamic_ROM`\r\n\r\nshould not be a necessary condition for high ETU.\r\n\r\nA hard dragon flag or rollout can create:\r\n\r\n- high force;\r\n- long contraction duration;\r\n- favorable/high-demand muscle length;\r\n- near-zero visible spinal ROM.\r\n\r\nThus an ETU formulation should preserve substantial stimulus under high-force isometric anti-extension.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nDirect RA-specific stretch-mediated hypertrophy evidence remains limited.\r\n\r\nThe strongest defensible statements are:\r\n\r\n- spinal extension lengthens RA;\r\n- RA has unusually long fascicles and large excursion potential; :contentReference[oaicite:16]{index=16}\r\n- exercises can actively load RA from relatively extended positions;\r\n- anti-extension exercises can impose high force without shortening;\r\n- no strong longitudinal literature currently establishes a precise RA-specific long-length hypertrophy multiplier.\r\n\r\nTherefore `smh_factor` should be **mechanically informed but evidence-conservative**.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **pubic origin and costal/xiphoid insertion:** high;\r\n- **spinal-flexion function:** high;\r\n- **posterior-pelvic-rotation function:** high;\r\n- **anti-extension role:** high;\r\n- **abdominal compression/stability contribution:** high; :contentReference[oaicite:17]{index=17}\r\n- **long fascicular architecture:** directly measured; :contentReference[oaicite:18]{index=18}\r\n- **regional upper/lower activation differences:** directly demonstrated, but magnitude and practical significance are exercise-dependent; :contentReference[oaicite:19]{index=19}\r\n- **high activation during rollouts and advanced core exercises:** experimentally supported; :contentReference[oaicite:20]{index=20}\r\n- **ability to hypertrophy from training:** directly supported by imaging studies; :contentReference[oaicite:21]{index=21}\r\n- **rectus-abdominis-specific stretch-mediated hypertrophy:** insufficient direct evidence;\r\n- **precise exercise-specific torque allocation:** strongly dependent on lever geometry and pelvic control.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **rectus abdominis = a long-fascicled rib-to-pubis trunk flexor and anti-extensor that generates spinal-flexion and posterior-pelvic-rotation torque, allowing very high force during both dynamic crunching and near-isometric long-lever exercises such as hanging leg raises, rollouts and dragon flags.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/RectusAbdominis}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	26.70	0.00	1.000	24.29	24.29
26	serratus_anterior	Musculus serratus anterior	Core	Core	525.00	497.16	20.00	Serrated/Fan-shaped	0.525	0.475	high	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\nThe **serratus anterior** is a broad, fan-shaped scapulothoracic muscle connecting the lateral thoracic wall to the anterior surface of the medial border of the scapula.\r\n\r\nIts major mechanical roles are:\r\n\r\n- **scapular protraction**;\r\n- **scapular upward rotation**;\r\n- **scapular posterior tilt**, particularly during arm elevation;\r\n- contribution to scapular external rotation;\r\n- stabilization of the medial border and inferior angle against the thorax.\r\n\r\nIt should not be reduced to:\r\n\r\n> "the muscle that pushes the scapula forward."\r\n\r\nThat description captures only one component of its function.\r\n\r\nDuring overhead elevation, serratus anterior is one of the most important muscles for producing the scapular orientation required to maintain effective glenohumeral mechanics.\r\n\r\nIts functional opposition to `pectoralis_minor` is particularly important.\r\n\r\nBoth muscles can contribute to protraction, but:\r\n\r\n### Serratus Anterior\r\n\r\nBiases toward:\r\n- protraction;\r\n- upward rotation;\r\n- posterior tilt;\r\n- external rotation of the scapula.\r\n\r\n### Pectoralis Minor\r\n\r\nBiases toward:\r\n- protraction;\r\n- downward rotation;\r\n- anterior tilt.\r\n\r\nThus two muscles can generate superficially similar scapular translation while producing very different three-dimensional scapular rotations.\r\n\r\nCadaveric work also indicates that serratus anterior is structurally regional rather than homogeneous, with distinguishable upper, middle and lower portions possessing different architecture and potential force vectors. :contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}\r\n\r\nFor Agonez, the most useful abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **serratus anterior = the principal scapular protractor and a major upward-rotation/posterior-tilt stabilizer whose demand becomes especially important when the scapula must move freely around the thorax during pushing or humeral elevation.**\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\n## Origin\r\n\r\nSerratus anterior originates through multiple digitations from the lateral surfaces of the upper ribs.\r\n\r\nThe exact number and configuration of slips varies between individuals.\r\n\r\nRather than one simple proximal attachment, the muscle is formed by several rib-associated slips distributed vertically along the thoracic wall.\r\n\r\nCadaveric morphometric work confirms considerable segmental organization within the muscle and supports division into structurally distinct regions rather than one homogeneous sheet. :contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}\r\n\r\n## Insertion\r\n\r\nSerratus anterior passes posteriorly around the lateral thorax and inserts onto the **anterior surface of the medial border of the scapula**.\r\n\r\nIts attachment spans from approximately:\r\n\r\n- superior angle region;\r\n- medial border;\r\n- toward the inferior angle.\r\n\r\nBecause the muscle inserts on the anterior surface of the scapula, much of it is hidden between:\r\n\r\n- scapula;\r\n- rib cage.\r\n\r\nThis geometry allows contraction to pull the scapula closely against and around the thoracic wall.\r\n\r\n## Regional Organization\r\n\r\nA useful functional subdivision is:\r\n\r\n- upper serratus anterior;\r\n- middle serratus anterior;\r\n- lower serratus anterior.\r\n\r\nThese regions have different fiber orientations.\r\n\r\n### Upper Region\r\n\r\nMore horizontally/posteriorly oriented.\r\n\r\nLikely contributes strongly to:\r\n- scapular stabilization;\r\n- protraction.\r\n\r\n### Middle Region\r\n\r\nWell positioned for:\r\n- protraction.\r\n\r\n### Lower Region\r\n\r\nEspecially favorable for:\r\n- upward rotation;\r\n- protraction;\r\n- control of the inferior angle.\r\n\r\nExperimental EMG comparing upper and lower serratus regions confirms that activation can differ depending on the movement being performed. :contentReference[oaicite:3]{index=3}\r\n\r\nTherefore one `serratus_anterior` row is a useful aggregate, but exercise-specific regional behavior should be expected.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nSerratus anterior is innervated by the **long thoracic nerve**, classically receiving contributions primarily from:\r\n\r\n- C5;\r\n- C6;\r\n- C7.\r\n\r\nThe nerve courses superficially along the lateral chest wall.\r\n\r\nThis makes it anatomically vulnerable to injury.\r\n\r\nLoss or substantial weakness of serratus-anterior function can result in impaired control of the medial scapular border and characteristic scapular winging.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Scapular Protraction\r\n\r\nSerratus anterior is one of the major **scapular protractors**.\r\n\r\nProtraction moves the scapula:\r\n\r\n- laterally;\r\n- anteriorly around the rib cage.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- reaching;\r\n- punching;\r\n- the terminal "plus" phase of a push-up.\r\n\r\nThe function is especially visible when the humerus is relatively fixed and the scapula moves independently around the thorax.\r\n\r\n## Upward Rotation\r\n\r\nSerratus anterior is a major producer of **scapular upward rotation**.\r\n\r\nDuring upward rotation:\r\n\r\n- the inferior angle moves laterally;\r\n- the glenoid rotates superiorly.\r\n\r\nThis is required during substantial humeral elevation.\r\n\r\nThe lower serratus anterior is particularly well oriented for this task.\r\n\r\nExperimental regional activation studies support functional differentiation between upper and lower serratus components during scapular protraction and upward-rotation tasks. :contentReference[oaicite:4]{index=4}\r\n\r\n## Posterior Tilt\r\n\r\nSerratus anterior also contributes to **posterior tilt of the scapula**.\r\n\r\nDuring posterior tilt:\r\n\r\n- the superior scapular region moves posteriorly;\r\n- the inferior angle moves relatively anteriorly around the thorax.\r\n\r\nThis is an important component of normal overhead scapular mechanics.\r\n\r\nThe functional importance of serratus anterior has been demonstrated experimentally: targeted fatigue of the muscle reduced scapular posterior tilt and upward rotation during subsequent arm elevation. :contentReference[oaicite:5]{index=5}\r\n\r\n## External Rotation of the Scapula\r\n\r\nSerratus anterior contributes to keeping the medial border of the scapula close to the thoracic wall.\r\n\r\nThis creates an external-rotation bias relative to excessive scapular internal rotation or winging.\r\n\r\nThus the muscle is important not only for gross movement but also for three-dimensional scapular orientation.\r\n\r\n## Medial-Border Stabilization\r\n\r\nA major serratus function is maintaining contact between:\r\n\r\n- medial scapula;\r\n- thoracic wall.\r\n\r\nIf serratus force is insufficient, the medial border can become more prominent posteriorly.\r\n\r\nThis is the classic mechanical basis of serratus-related scapular winging.\r\n\r\nImportantly:\r\n\r\n> high stabilization force can exist with little visible scapular ROM.\r\n\r\nTherefore Agonez should not require large protraction excursion before assigning serratus demand.\r\n\r\n## Force Couple With Trapezius\r\n\r\nDuring arm elevation, serratus anterior cooperates with:\r\n\r\n- upper trapezius;\r\n- lower trapezius\r\n\r\nto produce upward rotation of the scapula.\r\n\r\nTheir force directions differ.\r\n\r\nThis creates a force couple in which different muscles produce compatible rotational effects.\r\n\r\nA simplified interpretation is:\r\n\r\n### Serratus Anterior\r\n\r\nStrong:\r\n- lateral/inferior-angle pull;\r\n- upward rotation;\r\n- posterior tilt.\r\n\r\n### Upper Trapezius\r\n\r\nStrong:\r\n- clavicular/scapular elevation;\r\n- upward-rotation contribution.\r\n\r\n### Lower Trapezius\r\n\r\nStrong:\r\n- medial stabilization;\r\n- upward rotation;\r\n- posterior-tilt contribution.\r\n\r\nNo single one of these muscles should receive the entire upward-rotation vector.\r\n\r\n## Relationship With Pectoralis Minor\r\n\r\nThis is one of the most important scapular force relationships.\r\n\r\nBoth can produce protraction.\r\n\r\nBut:\r\n\r\n### Serratus Anterior\r\n\r\nProtraction +\r\n- upward rotation;\r\n- posterior tilt.\r\n\r\n### Pectoralis Minor\r\n\r\nProtraction +\r\n- downward rotation;\r\n- anterior tilt.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`protraction != one-dimensional muscle function`.\r\n\r\nA model that maps generic protraction equally to both muscles loses important biomechanics.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nDirect longitudinal evidence examining **serratus-anterior-specific stretch-mediated hypertrophy** is very limited.\r\n\r\nThe muscle's length depends on scapular position relative to the ribs.\r\n\r\n## Lengthening Through Retraction\r\n\r\nScapular retraction moves the medial border away from the shortened serratus position and generally lengthens the muscle.\r\n\r\nTherefore a pressing or reaching movement beginning from controlled scapular retraction can expose serratus anterior to a longer initial state.\r\n\r\n## Downward Rotation\r\n\r\nDownward rotation can lengthen portions of the serratus, particularly the inferior fibers that generate upward rotation.\r\n\r\nHowever, because regional fiber orientations differ, the entire muscle does not share one simple length response.\r\n\r\n## Anterior Tilt\r\n\r\nAnterior tilt can alter serratus length differently across regions.\r\n\r\nFor the lower serratus in particular, posterior-tilt production is an important functional component, so starting from greater anterior tilt can place parts of the system at longer excursion.\r\n\r\n## Push-Up ROM\r\n\r\nA push-up that permits the scapulae to move from:\r\n\r\n- modest retraction at the bottom;\r\nto\r\n- active protraction at the top\r\n\r\nprovides substantially more serratus excursion than a bench press with the scapulae held relatively fixed.\r\n\r\nThis is mechanically relevant even though direct hypertrophy comparisons are lacking.\r\n\r\n## Loaded Stretch Interpretation\r\n\r\nA mechanically useful serratus exercise should combine:\r\n\r\n- active force;\r\n- scapular excursion;\r\n- adequate resistance.\r\n\r\nSimply hanging in passive scapular retraction does not necessarily constitute meaningful active long-length loading.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nThe conservative position is:\r\n\r\n- scapular retraction/downward rotation can lengthen serratus regions;\r\n- loaded protraction/upward rotation from those positions can create meaningful excursion;\r\n- direct serratus-specific hypertrophy trials are sparse;\r\n- no strong SMH multiplier is experimentally established.\r\n\r\nTherefore `smh_factor` should remain evidence-conservative.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Push-Up Plus\r\n\r\nThe push-up plus is one of the most direct serratus-anterior exercises.\r\n\r\nIt consists of:\r\n\r\n1. completing the pressing portion of a push-up;\r\n2. continuing into additional scapular protraction at the top.\r\n\r\nThis terminal protraction increases serratus demand substantially.\r\n\r\nA classic experimental comparison found very high serratus activity during the standard push-up-plus condition, with favorable serratus activation relative to upper trapezius. :contentReference[oaicite:6]{index=6}\r\n\r\nThis makes it a strong candidate for direct serratus training.\r\n\r\n## Standard Push-Up\r\n\r\nEven without an exaggerated plus phase, push-ups differ importantly from bench press.\r\n\r\nBecause the scapulae are free to move around the thorax:\r\n\r\n- protraction can occur;\r\n- upward rotation can occur;\r\n- serratus can actively control medial-border position.\r\n\r\nTherefore push-ups can provide substantially more direct scapulothoracic serratus work than bench pressing.\r\n\r\n## Bench Press\r\n\r\nDuring a conventional bench press:\r\n\r\n- the thorax is supported;\r\n- the scapulae are often intentionally retracted;\r\n- scapular excursion is constrained by the bench.\r\n\r\nTherefore serratus anterior still contributes to stabilization but usually receives less dynamic protraction demand than in a push-up.\r\n\r\nThis distinction is important.\r\n\r\nA bench press should not receive the same serratus coefficient as a push-up simply because both are horizontal pressing movements.\r\n\r\n## Cable Serratus Punch\r\n\r\nA cable punch or reach can directly load scapular protraction.\r\n\r\nThe humerus can remain approximately fixed while the scapula translates around the rib cage.\r\n\r\nThis gives the exercise high serratus specificity.\r\n\r\nThe movement should emphasize:\r\n\r\n- scapular reach;\r\n- minimal excessive trunk rotation.\r\n\r\n## Dynamic Hug / Cable Hug\r\n\r\nA cable-based hugging movement combines:\r\n\r\n- humeral horizontal adduction;\r\n- scapular protraction.\r\n\r\nThus demand is shared between:\r\n\r\n- pectoralis major;\r\n- serratus anterior.\r\n\r\nAllowing the scapula to reach forward at the end increases serratus involvement.\r\n\r\n## Wall Slide\r\n\r\nWall slides can recruit serratus while the arm elevates.\r\n\r\nThey are useful because they combine:\r\n\r\n- arm elevation;\r\n- upward rotation;\r\n- protraction;\r\n- posterior-tilt control.\r\n\r\nExperimental work demonstrated meaningful serratus activation during wall-slide exercise even above 90° humeral elevation. :contentReference[oaicite:7]{index=7}\r\n\r\nHowever, absolute loading is usually modest compared with weighted push-up or cable variations.\r\n\r\n## Overhead Press\r\n\r\nOverhead pressing can create substantial serratus demand because the scapula must:\r\n\r\n- upwardly rotate;\r\n- posteriorly tilt;\r\n- remain stable against the thorax.\r\n\r\nThis occurs while large humeral forces are being generated.\r\n\r\nTherefore overhead pressing should receive meaningful serratus allocation even though there is no explicit "serratus movement" visible.\r\n\r\n## Arnold Press\r\n\r\nAn Arnold press similarly requires progressive scapular upward rotation during humeral elevation.\r\n\r\nSerratus demand should be meaningful.\r\n\r\nIts exact share depends on:\r\n\r\n- humeral plane;\r\n- range of elevation;\r\n- scapular freedom.\r\n\r\n## Lateral Raise\r\n\r\nAs the humerus elevates, the scapula must progressively upwardly rotate.\r\n\r\nTherefore serratus anterior is a meaningful synergist during lateral raises.\r\n\r\nAt low elevation, scapular contribution may be smaller.\r\n\r\nAt higher elevation, upward-rotation demand becomes increasingly important.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n`serratus_share ↑ with humeral_elevation`\r\n\r\nto a first approximation.\r\n\r\n## Cable Lateral Raise\r\n\r\nA cable lateral raise can therefore create:\r\n\r\n- high deltoid demand;\r\n- rotator-cuff stabilization demand;\r\n- meaningful serratus/trapezius upward-rotation demand.\r\n\r\nThis is especially relevant when the movement continues substantially above low shoulder angles.\r\n\r\n## Face Pull\r\n\r\nFace pulls are not major serratus exercises.\r\n\r\nThey usually emphasize:\r\n\r\n- scapular retraction;\r\n- external rotation;\r\n- posterior deltoid;\r\n- rhomboids;\r\n- middle/lower trapezius.\r\n\r\nBecause retraction opposes serratus protraction, direct concentric serratus contribution should generally be modest.\r\n\r\n## Rows\r\n\r\nRows should usually receive low-to-moderate serratus demand.\r\n\r\nDuring the concentric phase:\r\n\r\n- scapula retracts;\r\n- serratus lengthens or controls movement antagonistically.\r\n\r\nDuring the eccentric return:\r\n\r\n- serratus can participate in controlled protraction and stabilization.\r\n\r\nBut rhomboids and middle trapezius should dominate the primary scapular-retraction task.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Direct Serratus Training Can Be Useful\r\n\r\nUnlike many small stabilizers, serratus anterior can be directly loaded through progressively harder:\r\n\r\n- push-up-plus variations;\r\n- cable punches;\r\n- dynamic hugs;\r\n- loaded protraction exercises.\r\n\r\nThis makes dedicated hypertrophy/strength training realistic.\r\n\r\n## Weekly Volume\r\n\r\nIf serratus development is a priority, a practical starting range is approximately:\r\n\r\n- **4-8 direct hard sets per week**.\r\n\r\nThese can include:\r\n\r\n- push-up plus;\r\n- cable protraction;\r\n- loaded wall-slide/elevation variants.\r\n\r\nAdditional indirect work comes from:\r\n\r\n- overhead presses;\r\n- lateral raises;\r\n- push-ups.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nTwo to four weekly exposures are practical.\r\n\r\nBecause serratus work often has relatively low systemic cost, modest frequent exposure can work well.\r\n\r\nHowever, excessive fatigue immediately before heavy overhead training may impair scapular control.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\nDirect protraction work is practical in:\r\n\r\n- 8-15 reps;\r\n- 10-20 reps;\r\n- 15-30 reps.\r\n\r\nThere is no requirement to treat serratus as a high-repetition-only rehabilitation muscle.\r\n\r\nStable cable or machine loading can support conventional hypertrophy ranges.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nDirect stable serratus exercises can be taken relatively close to muscular failure:\r\n\r\n- approximately 0-2 RIR.\r\n\r\nFailure should be defined by inability to maintain:\r\n\r\n- full protraction;\r\n- controlled scapular motion;\r\n- appropriate ribcage position.\r\n\r\nIf the trainee substitutes:\r\n\r\n- thoracic flexion;\r\n- trunk rotation;\r\n- shoulder shrugging,\r\n\r\nthe set has reached technical failure.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Order\r\n\r\nDirect serratus work can be performed:\r\n\r\n- after pressing;\r\n- as accessory shoulder work;\r\n- earlier if serratus development is specifically prioritized.\r\n\r\nLow-fatigue activation work can be performed before overhead movements, but hard hypertrophy sets should not unnecessarily fatigue the muscle before high-force scapular stabilization is needed.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## Serratus Anterior Is Sometimes Called the "Boxer's Muscle"\r\n\r\nPunching involves rapid scapular protraction around the thorax.\r\n\r\nThis visibly recruits the serratus digitations along the lateral ribs.\r\n\r\nThe nickname reflects a real mechanical role rather than merely appearance.\r\n\r\n## It Can Protract and Still Be Essential Overhead\r\n\r\nMany people associate protraction with shoulders moving forward and therefore assume protraction is undesirable.\r\n\r\nNormal overhead elevation requires scapular movement, including serratus-mediated:\r\n\r\n- upward rotation;\r\n- posterior tilt;\r\n- external rotation.\r\n\r\nThus scapular protraction is not inherently dysfunctional.\r\n\r\n## Winging Is a Scapular-Control Problem\r\n\r\nReduced serratus function can allow the medial border of the scapula to become more prominent.\r\n\r\nThe muscle is therefore crucial for maintaining scapula-thorax contact.\r\n\r\nThis function is partly isometric and cannot be inferred from visible ROM alone.\r\n\r\n## Push-Up and Bench Press Are Not Scapularly Equivalent\r\n\r\nThey share a major humeral action:\r\n\r\n- horizontal pressing.\r\n\r\nBut the scapular environment differs profoundly.\r\n\r\n### Bench Press\r\n\r\nScapula:\r\n- externally supported;\r\n- typically retracted;\r\n- low excursion.\r\n\r\n### Push-Up\r\n\r\nScapula:\r\n- free;\r\n- capable of protraction/retraction;\r\n- active serratus control required.\r\n\r\nTherefore their `serratus_anterior` vectors should differ materially.\r\n\r\n## Upper and Lower Serratus Are Not Functionally Identical\r\n\r\nRegional EMG experiments show different activation patterns between upper and lower serratus during scapular tasks. :contentReference[oaicite:8]{index=8}\r\n\r\nCadaveric structural work likewise supports anatomical subdivision into regions with distinct architecture. :contentReference[oaicite:9]{index=9}\r\n\r\nThus the aggregate should be treated as regionally heterogeneous.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\n`serratus_anterior` should primarily receive demand from:\r\n\r\n1. **scapular protraction torque**;\r\n2. **scapular upward-rotation torque**;\r\n3. **posterior-tilt torque**;\r\n4. anti-winging / medial-border stabilization;\r\n5. secondary scapular external-rotation control.\r\n\r\nIt should receive approximately zero direct:\r\n\r\n- elbow torque;\r\n- glenohumeral horizontal-adduction torque.\r\n\r\nIts effect on humeral movement is indirect through scapular orientation.\r\n\r\n## Primary Scapular Axes\r\n\r\nFor serratus, one scalar scapular variable is insufficient.\r\n\r\nAt minimum, conceptually distinguish:\r\n\r\n- protraction ↔ retraction;\r\n- upward ↔ downward rotation;\r\n- anterior ↔ posterior tilt.\r\n\r\nSerratus can score positively on:\r\n\r\n- protraction;\r\n- upward rotation;\r\n- posterior tilt\r\n\r\nsimultaneously.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Pectoralis Minor\r\n\r\nThis should be explicitly encoded.\r\n\r\n### Protraction\r\n\r\nBoth:\r\n- serratus anterior;\r\n- pectoralis minor.\r\n\r\n### Upward Rotation\r\n\r\nStrong:\r\n- serratus anterior.\r\n\r\nOpposing bias:\r\n- pectoralis minor.\r\n\r\n### Posterior Tilt\r\n\r\nStrong:\r\n- serratus anterior.\r\n\r\nOpposing bias:\r\n- pectoralis minor.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`protraction_force_share`\r\n\r\nalone cannot determine which muscle is favored.\r\n\r\nThe rotational requirements must also be considered.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Trapezius\r\n\r\nUpward rotation should be shared between:\r\n\r\n- serratus anterior;\r\n- upper trapezius;\r\n- lower trapezius.\r\n\r\nA simplified model could represent:\r\n\r\n`T_upward_rotation =\r\n    SA\r\n  + upper_trap\r\n  + lower_trap`\r\n\r\nwith exercise-specific force sharing.\r\n\r\n### Protraction + Upward Rotation\r\n\r\nIncrease:\r\n- serratus share.\r\n\r\n### Retraction + Upward Rotation\r\n\r\nIncrease:\r\n- trapezius share.\r\n\r\nThis is a useful KISS distinction.\r\n\r\n## Push-Up Plus Allocation\r\n\r\nVery high:\r\n- `serratus_anterior`.\r\n\r\nMeaningful:\r\n- pectoralis major;\r\n- triceps;\r\n- anterior deltoid.\r\n\r\nDuring the plus phase, SA becomes especially dominant.\r\n\r\nExperimental EMG work found the standard push-up plus among the highest-serratus conditions tested. :contentReference[oaicite:10]{index=10}\r\n\r\n## Flat Bench Press\r\n\r\nLow-to-moderate serratus demand.\r\n\r\nWhy not high:\r\n\r\n- scapular protraction is constrained;\r\n- bench provides external support;\r\n- scapula is often deliberately retracted.\r\n\r\nPrime movers remain:\r\n- pectoralis major;\r\n- anterior deltoid;\r\n- triceps.\r\n\r\n## Plate-Loaded Chest Press\r\n\r\nMachine geometry matters.\r\n\r\n### Fixed Back + Limited Reach\r\n\r\nLow-to-moderate SA.\r\n\r\n### Converging Handles + Active Scapular Reach\r\n\r\nModerate-to-high SA.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n`serratus_demand = f(scapular_freedom, protraction_ROM)`\r\n\r\nrather than simply exercise category.\r\n\r\n## Overhead Press Allocation\r\n\r\nModerate-to-high serratus demand.\r\n\r\nThe scapula must progressively upwardly rotate as the humerus elevates.\r\n\r\nAlso high:\r\n- trapezius;\r\n- deltoid;\r\n- rotator cuff.\r\n\r\n## Lateral Raise Allocation\r\n\r\nLow at very small humeral elevation.\r\n\r\nProgressively higher as shoulder elevation increases and scapular upward rotation becomes more important.\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`SA_upward_rotation_demand ↑ with humeral_elevation`\r\n\r\nrather than a fixed coefficient across the entire repetition.\r\n\r\n## Single-Arm Cable Lateral Raise\r\n\r\nMeaningful serratus demand is mechanically justified, especially through higher elevation.\r\n\r\nYour existing exercise vector should therefore include:\r\n\r\n- deltoid lateral as prime mover;\r\n- rotator cuff for GH stabilization;\r\n- serratus + trapezius for scapular upward rotation.\r\n\r\n## Face Pull\r\n\r\nLow-to-moderate serratus.\r\n\r\nPrimary scapular demand usually favors:\r\n- rhomboids;\r\n- middle trapezius;\r\n- lower trapezius.\r\n\r\nPosterior cuff and posterior deltoid also contribute heavily.\r\n\r\n## Rows\r\n\r\nLow direct concentric SA demand during scapular retraction.\r\n\r\nPotential:\r\n- eccentric control;\r\n- stabilization;\r\n- return-phase protraction.\r\n\r\nDo not assign serratus the same retraction vector as rhomboids.\r\n\r\n## Pull-Ups\r\n\r\nModerate serratus demand can occur through scapular stabilization and upward/downward rotation control.\r\n\r\nHowever, lats and elbow flexors dominate the global exercise.\r\n\r\nScapular kinematics depend strongly on grip and technique.\r\n\r\n## Length-Tension Model\r\n\r\nA first-order serratus length model could use:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_SA =\r\n    f(protraction_retraction,\r\n      upward_downward_rotation,\r\n      anterior_posterior_tilt)`\r\n\r\nQualitatively:\r\n\r\n### Longer\r\n\r\n- scapular retraction;\r\n- downward rotation;\r\n- anterior tilt for relevant regions.\r\n\r\n### Shorter\r\n\r\n- protraction;\r\n- upward rotation;\r\n- posterior tilt.\r\n\r\nBecause the muscle is regionally heterogeneous, one scalar remains approximate.\r\n\r\n## Regional Model\r\n\r\nIf Agonez ever needs more precision, serratus anterior is a good candidate for subdivision into:\r\n\r\n- upper serratus;\r\n- middle serratus;\r\n- lower serratus.\r\n\r\nCadaveric morphometry demonstrates distinct regional architecture, while EMG experiments demonstrate differences in upper/lower activation. :contentReference[oaicite:11]{index=11}\r\n\r\nFor the present database, however, one aggregate is reasonable.\r\n\r\n## FCSA Interpretation\r\n\r\nUnlike `rotator_cuffs` or `pronators_supinators`, serratus anterior is not an aggregate of major antagonists.\r\n\r\nTherefore total FCSA is more interpretable as a broad **scapular-force capacity**.\r\n\r\nStill, not all fibers contribute maximally to the same direction.\r\n\r\nUpper and lower regional vectors differ.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n`F_max_total = specific_tension × FCSA`\r\n\r\nmay estimate total contractile capacity,\r\n\r\nbut not necessarily maximum:\r\n\r\n- protraction torque;\r\n- upward-rotation torque\r\n\r\nwithout regional moment-arm weighting.\r\n\r\n## ETU Interpretation\r\n\r\nSerratus is another important case where:\r\n\r\n`visible_ROM ≈ 0`\r\n\r\ncan coexist with substantial muscle force.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- heavy overhead press;\r\n- lateral raise;\r\n- loaded carry.\r\n\r\nThe scapula may move only moderately while serratus generates high stabilization force.\r\n\r\nETU should therefore include both:\r\n\r\n- dynamic scapular work;\r\n- stabilization under external shoulder load.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nDirect serratus-specific stretch-mediated hypertrophy evidence remains insufficient.\r\n\r\nThe mechanistic position is:\r\n\r\n- retraction/downward rotation can lengthen the muscle;\r\n- push-up-like exercises allow large scapular excursion;\r\n- active loading can occur across this range;\r\n- no strong evidence quantifies a special long-length hypertrophy multiplier.\r\n\r\nTherefore `smh_factor` should remain conservative.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **rib-to-medial-scapular-border anatomy:** directly supported by cadaveric anatomical work; :contentReference[oaicite:12]{index=12}\r\n- **regional structural differentiation:** directly demonstrated; :contentReference[oaicite:13]{index=13}\r\n- **protraction function:** high;\r\n- **upward-rotation function:** high;\r\n- **regional upper/lower functional differences:** experimentally supported; :contentReference[oaicite:14]{index=14}\r\n- **importance for posterior tilt/upward rotation during elevation:** experimentally supported by fatigue-induced kinematic changes; :contentReference[oaicite:15]{index=15}\r\n- **high activation during push-up-plus variants:** directly demonstrated; :contentReference[oaicite:16]{index=16}\r\n- **wall-slide recruitment through elevated shoulder positions:** directly demonstrated; :contentReference[oaicite:17]{index=17}\r\n- **precise exercise-specific scapular force sharing:** task-dependent;\r\n- **serratus-specific hypertrophy literature:** sparse;\r\n- **stretch-mediated hypertrophy magnitude:** unestablished.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **serratus anterior = a regionally heterogeneous scapulothoracic muscle that couples protraction with upward rotation, posterior tilt and medial-border stabilization, making it a primary driver of free scapular motion during pushing and overhead elevation rather than merely a generic protractor.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/SerratusAnterior}	{https://youtu.be/CEiqDwif3b4?si=pYAsNrHw3RFootkO}	Bilateral	13.80	0.00	1.000	36.03	36.03
15	rhomboids	Musculus Rhomboideus	Upper	Back	215.00	203.60	12.00	Parallel	0.400	0.600	very_high	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\n`rhomboids` represents the scapular retractor complex formed primarily by:\r\n\r\n- **rhomboid major**;\r\n- **rhomboid minor**.\r\n\r\nThese muscles connect the medial border of the scapula to the cervical and upper thoracic spine.\r\n\r\nTheir dominant mechanical roles are:\r\n\r\n- **scapular retraction**;\r\n- **scapular downward rotation**;\r\n- contribution to scapular elevation, especially through rhomboid minor;\r\n- stabilization of the medial scapular border against the thorax.\r\n\r\nThey should be distinguished clearly from:\r\n\r\n- `trapezius_middle`;\r\n- `trapezius_lower`;\r\n- `posterior_deltoid`;\r\n- `latissimus_dorsi`.\r\n\r\nThe rhomboids act directly on the **scapula**.\r\n\r\nThey do not directly extend or horizontally abduct the humerus.\r\n\r\nTherefore in rowing exercises, their demand arises from the **scapular retraction component**, not from glenohumeral shoulder extension.\r\n\r\nThis distinction is central for exercise-vector modelling.\r\n\r\nA useful computational identity is:\r\n\r\n> **rhomboids = medial scapular stabilizers and retractors that pull the scapula toward the vertebral column and contribute to downward rotation, often working synergistically with middle trapezius but with a distinct rotational bias.**\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\n## Rhomboid Minor\r\n\r\nRhomboid minor is the smaller and more superior component.\r\n\r\n### Origin\r\n\r\nIt originates primarily from the lower cervical / upper thoracic region, classically including:\r\n\r\n- nuchal ligament;\r\n- spinous processes of approximately C7-T1.\r\n\r\n### Insertion\r\n\r\nIt inserts on the medial border of the scapula near the level of the **root of the scapular spine**.\r\n\r\nIts fibers run inferolaterally from the vertebral column to the scapula.\r\n\r\n## Rhomboid Major\r\n\r\nRhomboid major is larger and lies inferior to rhomboid minor.\r\n\r\n### Origin\r\n\r\nIt originates from the upper thoracic spinous processes, commonly approximately:\r\n\r\n- T2-T5.\r\n\r\n### Insertion\r\n\r\nIt inserts along the medial border of the scapula inferior to the scapular spine, extending toward the inferior angle.\r\n\r\nIts fiber orientation is also inferolateral.\r\n\r\n## Combined Geometry\r\n\r\nBoth muscles therefore pull the medial border of the scapula:\r\n\r\n- medially;\r\n- somewhat superiorly.\r\n\r\nBecause their insertion spans much of the medial border, contraction produces not only translation but also rotation.\r\n\r\nThis is why the rhomboids combine:\r\n\r\n- retraction;\r\n- downward rotation;\r\n- modest elevation.\r\n\r\n## Relationship With Trapezius\r\n\r\nThe rhomboids lie deep to the trapezius.\r\n\r\nMiddle trapezius and rhomboids overlap strongly in scapular retraction.\r\n\r\nHowever, their secondary rotational actions differ.\r\n\r\n### Middle Trapezius\r\n\r\nPrimarily:\r\n- scapular retraction.\r\n\r\n### Rhomboids\r\n\r\nRetraction +\r\n- downward rotation;\r\n- some elevation.\r\n\r\nThis distinction becomes important during overhead movement, where excessive downward-rotation force would oppose the required upward rotation.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nRhomboid major and minor are innervated primarily by the **dorsal scapular nerve**, predominantly from:\r\n\r\n- C4-C5, especially C5.\r\n\r\nThe dorsal scapular nerve also commonly supplies levator scapulae.\r\n\r\nThis shared innervation reflects their anatomical relationship as muscles influencing:\r\n\r\n- scapular elevation;\r\n- medial positioning;\r\n- downward rotation.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Scapular Retraction\r\n\r\nThe primary gross function of the rhomboids is **scapular retraction**, also called scapular adduction.\r\n\r\nThis means pulling the scapula toward the vertebral column.\r\n\r\nExamples include the scapular component of:\r\n\r\n- rows;\r\n- reverse flys;\r\n- face pulls;\r\n- scapular retraction drills.\r\n\r\nThe rhomboids act synergistically with middle trapezius.\r\n\r\n## Downward Rotation\r\n\r\nThe rhomboids also contribute to **downward rotation of the scapula**.\r\n\r\nTheir line of pull draws the medial border of the scapula medially and superiorly, tending to rotate the glenoid inferiorly.\r\n\r\nThis action is shared with:\r\n\r\n- levator scapulae;\r\n- pectoralis minor.\r\n\r\nIt opposes the upward-rotation force couple created primarily by:\r\n\r\n- serratus anterior;\r\n- upper trapezius;\r\n- lower trapezius.\r\n\r\n## Scapular Elevation\r\n\r\nEspecially through the more superior fibers and rhomboid minor, the complex can contribute modestly to scapular elevation.\r\n\r\nHowever, this is usually secondary to:\r\n\r\n- upper trapezius;\r\n- levator scapulae.\r\n\r\n## Medial-Border Stabilization\r\n\r\nThe rhomboids help stabilize the medial border of the scapula against the thorax.\r\n\r\nThis is important during:\r\n\r\n- loaded pulling;\r\n- arm elevation;\r\n- support tasks;\r\n- force transfer between the upper limb and trunk.\r\n\r\nThey can therefore produce meaningful force even when visible scapular movement is limited.\r\n\r\n## Eccentric Control of Protraction\r\n\r\nWhen the scapula moves into protraction, the rhomboids lengthen.\r\n\r\nThey can act eccentrically to control this motion.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- returning from a row;\r\n- reaching forward under load;\r\n- controlled scapular protraction.\r\n\r\nThus their role is not limited to concentric retraction.\r\n\r\n## Relationship With Glenohumeral Motion\r\n\r\nRhomboids do not directly move the humerus.\r\n\r\nThey can strongly affect upper-limb mechanics indirectly by controlling scapular position.\r\n\r\nA row can therefore contain two distinct simultaneous actions:\r\n\r\n1. humeral extension or horizontal abduction;\r\n2. scapular retraction.\r\n\r\nThe first loads muscles such as:\r\n\r\n- latissimus dorsi;\r\n- posterior deltoid;\r\n- teres major.\r\n\r\nThe second loads:\r\n\r\n- rhomboids;\r\n- middle trapezius.\r\n\r\nThis decomposition is one of the most important concepts for Agonez.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nDirect evidence specifically examining **stretch-mediated hypertrophy of the rhomboids** is limited.\r\n\r\nMechanically, the rhomboids become relatively lengthened when the scapula moves toward:\r\n\r\n- **protraction**;\r\n- **upward rotation**;\r\n- some degree of depression depending on fiber region.\r\n\r\nThe strongest practical lengthening movement is scapular protraction.\r\n\r\n## Protracted Starting Position\r\n\r\nA row beginning from controlled scapular protraction lengthens the rhomboids before retraction begins.\r\n\r\nIf meaningful external torque is present in this position, the muscles can experience active tension at longer lengths.\r\n\r\nThis creates a plausible hypertrophic benefit compared with very short-range rows performed entirely around a retracted scapular position.\r\n\r\n## Chest-Supported Row\r\n\r\nA chest-supported row can allow substantial scapular protraction at the start if:\r\n\r\n- the machine or dumbbells permit the shoulders to reach forward;\r\n- the thorax remains supported;\r\n- the trainee does not artificially lock the scapulae back.\r\n\r\nThis may create useful long-length rhomboid loading.\r\n\r\n## Cable Row\r\n\r\nCable rows can also provide high resistance while the scapulae are protracted.\r\n\r\nThis is mechanically favorable because cable tension remains present throughout much of the ROM.\r\n\r\n## Reverse Fly\r\n\r\nReverse-fly variations can lengthen the rhomboids through scapular protraction, but the exercise may be dominated by glenohumeral horizontal abduction if scapular motion is limited.\r\n\r\nTherefore rhomboid loading depends strongly on whether the scapula actually moves.\r\n\r\n## Excessive Reach\r\n\r\nMore protraction is not automatically better.\r\n\r\nExcessive thoracic flexion or uncontrolled shoulder translation can create apparent ROM without proportional scapular movement.\r\n\r\nThe goal is:\r\n\r\n- controlled scapular protraction;\r\n- active retraction under load.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nThe conservative evidence-based position is:\r\n\r\n- protraction lengthens the rhomboids;\r\n- loaded retraction from protraction is mechanically attractive;\r\n- direct rhomboid-specific long-length hypertrophy data are sparse;\r\n- a strong special SMH coefficient is not directly established.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Rows\r\n\r\nRows are the principal resistance-training family for the rhomboids.\r\n\r\nHowever, rhomboid demand depends on **scapular movement**, not simply on pulling an implement toward the torso.\r\n\r\nA row with:\r\n\r\n- large humeral movement;\r\n- minimal scapular retraction\r\n\r\ncan heavily train lats or posterior deltoid while providing less rhomboid stimulus.\r\n\r\nA row with substantial controlled scapular excursion can load the rhomboids strongly.\r\n\r\n## Chest-Supported Row\r\n\r\nChest support is especially useful because it removes much of the need for:\r\n\r\n- lumbar erector stabilization;\r\n- hip stabilization.\r\n\r\nThis can make scapular retraction easier to isolate.\r\n\r\nAllowing the scapulae to move from:\r\n\r\n- protraction;\r\n- into retraction\r\n\r\nprovides a strong rhomboid stimulus.\r\n\r\n## Wide-Grip Row\r\n\r\nWide-grip or high-elbow rows often increase:\r\n\r\n- scapular retraction;\r\n- posterior-deltoid demand;\r\n- middle-trapezius demand.\r\n\r\nRhomboids can therefore receive substantial loading.\r\n\r\nHowever, the exact share depends on whether movement is produced through:\r\n\r\n- scapular retraction;\r\n- humeral horizontal abduction.\r\n\r\nBoth can occur simultaneously.\r\n\r\n## Tucked-Elbow Row\r\n\r\nA tucked-elbow row typically shifts more glenohumeral demand toward:\r\n\r\n- latissimus dorsi;\r\n- teres major.\r\n\r\nRhomboids still contribute if the scapula retracts.\r\n\r\nTherefore elbow path affects lat-vs-posterior-delt allocation more directly than it affects whether rhomboids are involved.\r\n\r\nFor rhomboids, actual scapular excursion remains the key variable.\r\n\r\n## Face Pull\r\n\r\nFace pulls combine:\r\n\r\n- scapular retraction;\r\n- humeral horizontal abduction;\r\n- external rotation.\r\n\r\nTherefore they can recruit:\r\n\r\n- rhomboids;\r\n- middle/lower trapezius;\r\n- posterior deltoid;\r\n- rotator cuff.\r\n\r\nThey are useful but mechanically distributed.\r\n\r\nThey should not be treated as a pure rhomboid exercise.\r\n\r\n## Reverse Pec Deck\r\n\r\nA reverse pec deck can load rhomboids if the machine allows meaningful scapular movement.\r\n\r\nSome machines constrain the torso and arm path in a way that makes the exercise primarily:\r\n\r\n- glenohumeral horizontal abduction.\r\n\r\nIn that case posterior deltoid may dominate.\r\n\r\nIf the scapulae are allowed to protract and retract, rhomboid contribution increases.\r\n\r\n## Scapular Row\r\n\r\nA pure scapular row minimizes elbow and humeral motion while emphasizing:\r\n\r\n- protraction;\r\n- retraction.\r\n\r\nThis is mechanically more specific to:\r\n\r\n- rhomboids;\r\n- middle trapezius.\r\n\r\nHowever, progressive overload is usually lower than in full rowing.\r\n\r\nIt can be useful for:\r\n\r\n- motor control;\r\n- accessory volume;\r\n- rehabilitation.\r\n\r\n## Dead Hang to Scapular Retraction\r\n\r\nScapular pull-up or active-hang variations involve scapular depression and retraction depending on execution.\r\n\r\nRhomboid contribution can be meaningful, but the movement often includes substantial:\r\n\r\n- lower trapezius;\r\n- latissimus;\r\n- serratus-related scapular control.\r\n\r\nTherefore it is not a pure rhomboid exercise.\r\n\r\n## Full Scapular ROM\r\n\r\nFor hypertrophy, there is usually little reason to hold the scapulae rigidly retracted during all rowing repetitions.\r\n\r\nAllowing controlled protraction increases:\r\n\r\n- rhomboid excursion;\r\n- potential long-length loading;\r\n- scapular movement specificity.\r\n\r\nThe key is controlled motion rather than uncontrolled rounding.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Rows Usually Provide Sufficient Volume\r\n\r\nRhomboids receive substantial direct volume from most programs containing:\r\n\r\n- rows;\r\n- face pulls;\r\n- reverse flys.\r\n\r\nDedicated rhomboid isolation is therefore often unnecessary.\r\n\r\n## Weekly Volume\r\n\r\nA practical total exposure can often be obtained from approximately:\r\n\r\n- **6-12 hard rowing/retraction sets per week**.\r\n\r\nHowever, these should not all be counted as full rhomboid sets.\r\n\r\nA lat-biased row with little scapular excursion may provide only partial rhomboid credit.\r\n\r\nA chest-supported row with substantial scapular protraction/retraction can count strongly.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nTwo to three weekly exposures are practical.\r\n\r\nRhomboid work has low systemic fatigue when performed with chest support, but unsupported rowing can create high fatigue through:\r\n\r\n- erector spinae;\r\n- hip extensors.\r\n\r\nThus local rhomboid frequency should be separated conceptually from whole-exercise fatigue.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\nUseful ranges include:\r\n\r\n- 6-12 reps for heavy supported rows;\r\n- 8-15 reps for machine/cable rows;\r\n- 12-20+ reps for face pulls and scapular isolation work.\r\n\r\nThere is no specific rhomboid repetition requirement.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nStable supported rows can be trained close to failure.\r\n\r\nApproximately:\r\n\r\n- 0-2 RIR\r\n\r\nis practical.\r\n\r\nFailure should be defined by inability to continue productive scapular/humeral movement without:\r\n\r\n- trunk momentum;\r\n- excessive shrugging;\r\n- shortened ROM.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Order\r\n\r\nIf upper-back hypertrophy is a priority, a scapular-retraction-dominant row can appear early in the pull session.\r\n\r\nIf lats are the higher priority, rhomboid-dominant rowing can follow vertical pulls or lat-biased rows.\r\n\r\n## Avoid Excessive "Squeeze" Obsession\r\n\r\nA maximal end-range squeeze is not necessarily the most important part of a rhomboid exercise.\r\n\r\nThe muscles can be heavily loaded through:\r\n\r\n- protraction;\r\n- mid-range retraction;\r\n- end-range shortening.\r\n\r\nHypertrophy programming should preserve substantial loaded ROM rather than sacrificing the lengthened position for an exaggerated isometric squeeze.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## Rhomboids Do Not Move the Humerus\r\n\r\nTheir distal attachment is on the **scapula**, not the humerus.\r\n\r\nThus they cannot directly produce:\r\n\r\n- shoulder extension;\r\n- horizontal abduction;\r\n- elbow movement.\r\n\r\nThis is the same modelling distinction that separates:\r\n\r\n- pectoralis minor from pectoralis major.\r\n\r\n## Retraction Is Shared With Middle Trapezius\r\n\r\nRhomboids and middle trapezius both retract the scapula.\r\n\r\nBut rhomboids have a stronger **downward-rotation bias**, while middle trapezius is more nearly a pure retractor.\r\n\r\nThis makes them synergists but not functionally identical.\r\n\r\n## Strong Rhomboids Are Not Automatically "Good Posture"\r\n\r\nFitness discourse often treats rhomboid weakness as a universal cause of rounded shoulders.\r\n\r\nPosture is much more complex and depends on:\r\n\r\n- thoracic shape;\r\n- scapular resting position;\r\n- pectoralis minor length;\r\n- serratus function;\r\n- trapezius function;\r\n- habitual motor strategy.\r\n\r\nRhomboid strengthening can be useful, but posture should not be reduced to one muscle.\r\n\r\n## Scapular Protraction Is Not Bad\r\n\r\nProtraction lengthens the rhomboids and is a normal component of shoulder-girdle movement.\r\n\r\nAvoiding all protraction during rows may unnecessarily reduce:\r\n\r\n- rhomboid excursion;\r\n- loaded ROM.\r\n\r\nControlled protraction is mechanically normal.\r\n\r\n## Rhomboids Oppose Upward Rotation\r\n\r\nBecause they create downward-rotation torque, they are not simply muscles that should be maximally active during every arm-elevation task.\r\n\r\nHealthy overhead motion requires substantial upward rotation from:\r\n\r\n- serratus anterior;\r\n- upper trapezius;\r\n- lower trapezius.\r\n\r\nThe rhomboids must therefore participate within a balanced scapular force system.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\n`rhomboids` should primarily receive demand from:\r\n\r\n1. **scapular retraction torque**;\r\n2. **anti-protraction stabilization**;\r\n3. secondary downward-rotation torque;\r\n4. minor elevation contribution.\r\n\r\nThey should receive approximately zero direct glenohumeral:\r\n\r\n- shoulder-extension;\r\n- horizontal-abduction\r\n\r\nallocation.\r\n\r\n## Row Decomposition\r\n\r\nA useful model is:\r\n\r\n`row = glenohumeral_motion + scapular_motion + elbow_flexion`\r\n\r\n### Glenohumeral Motion\r\n\r\nLoads:\r\n- latissimus dorsi;\r\n- posterior deltoid;\r\n- teres major depending on path.\r\n\r\n### Scapular Retraction\r\n\r\nLoads:\r\n- rhomboids;\r\n- middle trapezius.\r\n\r\n### Elbow Flexion\r\n\r\nLoads:\r\n- brachialis;\r\n- biceps;\r\n- brachioradialis.\r\n\r\nThis decomposition prevents one generic "back" vector from becoming anatomically incoherent.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Allocation\r\n\r\n### Wide-Grip Seated Cable Row\r\n\r\nHigh rhomboid demand if substantial scapular retraction occurs.\r\n\r\nAlso high:\r\n- middle trapezius;\r\n- posterior deltoid depending on elbow height.\r\n\r\n### Chest-Supported Dumbbell Row\r\n\r\nModerate-to-high rhomboid demand.\r\n\r\nDepends strongly on scapular excursion.\r\n\r\n### Pendlay Row\r\n\r\nHigh rhomboid demand during retraction.\r\n\r\nAlso high:\r\n- lats;\r\n- posterior deltoid depending on elbow path;\r\n- elbow flexors;\r\n- erector spinae.\r\n\r\n### Cable Face Pull\r\n\r\nModerate-to-high rhomboid demand.\r\n\r\nAlso high:\r\n- posterior deltoid;\r\n- rotator cuff;\r\n- middle/lower trapezius.\r\n\r\n### Reverse Pec Deck\r\n\r\nModerate rhomboid demand if scapular motion is allowed.\r\n\r\nPotentially lower if the exercise is performed almost entirely through humeral horizontal abduction.\r\n\r\n### Neutral-Grip Pull-Up\r\n\r\nLow-to-moderate rhomboid demand.\r\n\r\nThe primary scapular movement is not maximal retraction, and lats/elbow flexors dominate.\r\n\r\n### Straight-Arm Pulldown\r\n\r\nLow rhomboid demand.\r\n\r\nLatissimus and scapular stabilizers dominate.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Middle Trapezius\r\n\r\nRetraction force should be shared between:\r\n\r\n- `rhomboids`;\r\n- `trapezius_middle`.\r\n\r\nA useful simplified distinction is:\r\n\r\n### Pure Retraction\r\n\r\nMiddle trapezius:\r\n- very high.\r\n\r\nRhomboids:\r\n- high.\r\n\r\n### Retraction + Downward Rotation\r\n\r\nRhomboid share increases.\r\n\r\n### Retraction + Upward-Rotation Requirement\r\n\r\nMiddle/lower trapezius and serratus become relatively more important.\r\n\r\nThis is more realistic than treating middle trap and rhomboids as interchangeable.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Serratus Anterior\r\n\r\nRhomboids and serratus anterior can oppose each other in:\r\n\r\n- protraction/retraction;\r\n- downward/upward rotation.\r\n\r\nBut both can also co-contract to stabilize the scapula against the thorax.\r\n\r\nThus antagonistic anatomical actions do not imply that only one can be active during a loaded task.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Pectoralis Minor\r\n\r\nBoth rhomboids and pectoralis minor possess a downward-rotation component.\r\n\r\nHowever:\r\n\r\n- rhomboids retract;\r\n- pec minor tends to protract/anteriorly tilt.\r\n\r\nTherefore their net scapular effects differ substantially.\r\n\r\n## Length-Tension Model\r\n\r\nA simplified rhomboid length model can use scapular position.\r\n\r\n### Longer\r\n\r\n- protraction;\r\n- upward rotation.\r\n\r\n### Intermediate\r\n\r\n- neutral scapular position.\r\n\r\n### Shorter\r\n\r\n- retraction;\r\n- downward rotation.\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_rhomboids = f(protraction_retraction, upward_downward_rotation)`\r\n\r\nThe protraction/retraction axis should receive the largest weight.\r\n\r\n## Loaded Stretch in Rows\r\n\r\nIf a row begins from controlled scapular protraction, rhomboids are relatively lengthened.\r\n\r\nIf the external resistance remains high there, `E_LT` can be favorable.\r\n\r\nThis gives cable and chest-supported rows a potentially useful long-length component.\r\n\r\nHowever, direct SMH evidence remains limited.\r\n\r\n## Stabilization Demand\r\n\r\nHigh external forces on the humerus can create scapular translation tendencies even when scapular position is intentionally held.\r\n\r\nRhomboids can then contribute isometrically to maintaining medial-border position.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`scapular_ROM = 0`\r\n\r\ndoes not always imply:\r\n\r\n`rhomboid_demand = 0`.\r\n\r\nBut stabilization demand should be tied to actual external scapular torque rather than assigned generically.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nThe evidence-based position should remain conservative:\r\n\r\n- protraction clearly lengthens the rhomboids;\r\n- loaded retraction through full scapular ROM is mechanically sensible;\r\n- direct rhomboid-specific long-length hypertrophy data are sparse;\r\n- no large special SMH multiplier is currently justified.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **rhomboid major/minor anatomy:** high;\r\n- **dorsal scapular nerve innervation:** high;\r\n- **scapular retraction function:** high;\r\n- **downward-rotation contribution:** high;\r\n- **scapular stabilization role:** high;\r\n- **synergy with middle trapezius:** high;\r\n- **exercise-specific recruitment during rowing:** mechanically strong but dependent on scapular motion;\r\n- **direct hypertrophy evidence:** limited;\r\n- **stretch-mediated hypertrophy magnitude:** poorly established;\r\n- **precise rhomboid vs middle-trapezius force sharing:** geometry-dependent.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **rhomboids = a rhomboid-major/minor scapular complex specialized for retraction, anti-protraction and downward-rotation torque, with training demand determined primarily by actual scapular excursion and stabilization rather than by humeral pulling motion itself.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/Rhomboids}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	7.20	2.50	0.999	28.28	28.25
49	deep_neck_extensors	Musculi colli extensores	Upper	Neck	430.00	407.20	12.00	Parallel/Pennate	0.700	0.300	high	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\n`deep_neck_extensors` is a functional aggregate representing the **deep posterior cervical extensor system** rather than a single anatomical muscle.\r\n\r\nFor the purposes of this database, the aggregate should primarily represent:\r\n\r\n- **semispinalis cervicis**;\r\n- **cervical multifidus**;\r\n- **cervical rotatores**;\r\n\r\nwith the deeper cervical extensor system functioning together as a segmental stabilizing and extension-producing unit.\r\n\r\nIt should be distinguished from the larger and more superficial posterior-neck muscles such as:\r\n\r\n- splenius capitis;\r\n- splenius cervicis;\r\n- upper trapezius;\r\n- levator scapulae.\r\n\r\nThose muscles can contribute substantially to gross head and neck movement but have different architecture, attachments and mechanical roles.\r\n\r\nThe defining characteristic of the deep cervical extensors is not simply that they "extend the neck."\r\n\r\nTheir most important functions include:\r\n\r\n1. **cervical extension torque**;\r\n2. **segment-by-segment control of cervical vertebrae**;\r\n3. resistance to cervical flexion moments;\r\n4. fine positional control and proprioception;\r\n5. contribution to rotation and lateral flexion depending on the individual fascicle.\r\n\r\nThe cervical spine contains many short overlapping muscular actuators, so a simple prime-mover model is particularly inadequate here.\r\n\r\nThe deep extensors function as part of a distributed control system in which gross movement and local vertebral control occur simultaneously.\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\nThe deep cervical extensors belong largely to the **transversospinalis system**, whose fibers generally run from more lateral inferior attachments toward more medial superior attachments.\r\n\r\nThe major transversospinal groups are:\r\n\r\n- semispinalis;\r\n- multifidus;\r\n- rotatores.\r\n\r\nThey lie deep to the more superficial intrinsic back and posterior-neck musculature.\r\n\r\n## Semispinalis Cervicis\r\n\r\n### Origin\r\n\r\nSemispinalis cervicis generally originates from the transverse processes of the upper thoracic vertebrae.\r\n\r\n### Insertion\r\n\r\nIts fascicles pass superomedially to insert onto cervical spinous processes, particularly in the upper and mid-cervical region.\r\n\r\nIndividual fascicles span several vertebral segments.\r\n\r\nThis relatively long segmental span allows semispinalis cervicis to generate meaningful cervical-extension torque while still contributing to intersegmental control.\r\n\r\n## Cervical Multifidus\r\n\r\n### Origin\r\n\r\nCervical multifidus arises from posterior elements of lower cervical and upper thoracic vertebrae, including articular and transverse-process regions.\r\n\r\n### Insertion\r\n\r\nIts fascicles travel superiorly and medially to attach to spinous processes several segments above their origins.\r\n\r\nCompared with semispinalis, multifidus generally spans fewer vertebral levels.\r\n\r\nThis shorter fascicular span gives it a particularly strong anatomical relationship with **segmental vertebral control**.\r\n\r\n## Rotatores Cervicis\r\n\r\nRotatores are among the shortest transversospinal muscles.\r\n\r\nThey connect adjacent or near-adjacent vertebral posterior elements and span very small numbers of motion segments.\r\n\r\nTheir mechanical capacity for producing large external torque is relatively small.\r\n\r\nTheir anatomical configuration instead makes them particularly suited to:\r\n\r\n- local segmental control;\r\n- sensing vertebral motion;\r\n- fine adjustment of spinal position.\r\n\r\n## Suboccipital Relationship\r\n\r\nThe small suboccipital muscles lie superior to the main cervical transversospinal system and participate strongly in fine craniocervical control.\r\n\r\nThey include:\r\n\r\n- rectus capitis posterior major;\r\n- rectus capitis posterior minor;\r\n- obliquus capitis superior;\r\n- obliquus capitis inferior.\r\n\r\nThese muscles share some functional characteristics with `deep_neck_extensors`, especially high proprioceptive specialization.\r\n\r\nHowever, for a clean Agonez aggregate they should be regarded as **adjacent specialized craniocervical musculature**, rather than assumed to account for most of the mass or force represented by this record.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nThe deep intrinsic cervical extensors are supplied predominantly by the **posterior rami of cervical spinal nerves**.\r\n\r\nThis follows the general organization of intrinsic back musculature.\r\n\r\nInnervation is distributed segmentally rather than through one large peripheral motor nerve analogous to the musculocutaneous or femoral nerve.\r\n\r\nThis arrangement is consistent with the highly regional organization of the muscles.\r\n\r\nDifferent fascicles spanning different cervical levels can therefore participate in fine local control rather than behaving as one monolithic extensor.\r\n\r\nThe neural organization of the posterior cervical muscles is particularly relevant because fine motor control and proprioception are major aspects of their function.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Cervical Extension\r\n\r\nBilateral contraction contributes to **extension of the cervical spine**.\r\n\r\nDepending on which cervical levels move, extension can involve:\r\n\r\n- lower cervical extension;\r\n- upper cervical extension;\r\n- combined extension of the head and neck.\r\n\r\nThe external torque that must be resisted is often produced simply by the mass of the head acting anterior to the cervical joints.\r\n\r\nTherefore the deep extensors can remain active even during apparently static upright posture.\r\n\r\n## Segmental Stabilization\r\n\r\nOne of the defining functions of the deep cervical extensors is **control of individual cervical motion segments**.\r\n\r\nTheir short fascicles and vertebra-to-vertebra attachments allow them to regulate relative motion between adjacent or nearby vertebrae.\r\n\r\nThis is mechanically different from simply generating gross extension.\r\n\r\nA large superficial muscle can produce substantial net cervical torque while the deeper muscles simultaneously distribute and control movement across individual joints.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n> gross neck strength and segmental cervical control are related but not equivalent properties.\r\n\r\nThe deep extensors work in coordination with the deep cervical flexor system to control cervical alignment and intervertebral motion.\r\n\r\n## Eccentric Control of Flexion\r\n\r\nDuring cervical flexion, the extensors can act eccentrically to control forward motion of the head and cervical spine.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- lowering the head during a neck-extension exercise;\r\n- controlling head movement during acceleration;\r\n- maintaining head position while external forces act anteriorly.\r\n\r\nThis eccentric role can generate substantial muscle force even when the visible joint action is flexion.\r\n\r\n## Isometric Head Control\r\n\r\nA large fraction of normal cervical-extensor work is effectively **isometric or quasi-isometric**.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- maintaining horizontal gaze;\r\n- resisting head acceleration;\r\n- stabilizing the head during running;\r\n- supporting head position during loaded movement.\r\n\r\nThus dynamic neck extension is only one expression of deep-extensor function.\r\n\r\n## Rotation\r\n\r\nBecause transversospinal fascicles run superiorly and medially, unilateral contraction can contribute to **contralateral cervical rotation**.\r\n\r\nThe magnitude depends strongly on:\r\n\r\n- cervical level;\r\n- fascicle;\r\n- concurrent actions of other cervical muscles.\r\n\r\nRotational function should therefore be modeled as distributed rather than assigned equally to the entire aggregate.\r\n\r\n## Lateral Flexion\r\n\r\nSome fascicles can contribute to ipsilateral lateral flexion or stabilization against lateral flexion.\r\n\r\nAgain, this is highly region-dependent.\r\n\r\nThe deep extensors are best interpreted as **multidirectional cervical stabilizers with an extension bias**, rather than purely sagittal-plane motors.\r\n\r\n## Proprioception\r\n\r\nThe cervical musculature contains exceptionally high densities of muscle spindles, with particularly high spindle concentrations reported in small deep cervical and suboccipital muscles.\r\n\r\nThis makes the system important for sensing:\r\n\r\n- head position;\r\n- cervical movement;\r\n- relative vertebral orientation.\r\n\r\nCervical proprioceptive information integrates with:\r\n\r\n- vestibular input;\r\n- visual input;\r\n- somatosensory information.\r\n\r\nThe deep cervical musculature therefore participates not only in force production but also in the sensorimotor control of head orientation.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nDirect evidence concerning **stretch-mediated hypertrophy specifically in the deep cervical extensors is extremely limited**.\r\n\r\nThis muscle group should therefore not inherit strong SMH claims from general skeletal-muscle literature without qualification.\r\n\r\nMechanically, cervical flexion lengthens posterior cervical extensor structures.\r\n\r\nTherefore loaded extension performed from a flexed starting position can expose the deep extensors to tension at relatively long muscle lengths.\r\n\r\nHowever, several factors complicate the analogy with muscles such as:\r\n\r\n- hamstrings;\r\n- quadriceps;\r\n- biceps.\r\n\r\nThe cervical extensors consist of many short overlapping fascicles with:\r\n\r\n- different segmental spans;\r\n- different attachment orientations;\r\n- different length changes for the same gross neck motion.\r\n\r\nConsequently, a global cervical flexion angle does not correspond to one uniform muscle-length state across the aggregate.\r\n\r\n## Long-Length Loading\r\n\r\nA controlled neck-extension exercise beginning from moderate cervical flexion can theoretically provide:\r\n\r\n- increased extensor length;\r\n- external flexion torque;\r\n- active extension demand through a broad ROM.\r\n\r\nThis is mechanically preferable to performing only very short-range extension near the fully extended position if hypertrophy is the goal.\r\n\r\nHowever, aggressively forcing cervical flexion to create a maximal stretch is unnecessary.\r\n\r\nThe cervical spine contains:\r\n\r\n- multiple small joints;\r\n- neural structures;\r\n- vascular structures;\r\n- passive ligamentous constraints.\r\n\r\nThe risk/reward logic therefore differs from deliberately maximizing stretch in a large limb muscle.\r\n\r\n## Practical Interpretation\r\n\r\nFor `deep_neck_extensors`, long-length training should mean:\r\n\r\n> controlled loaded cervical extension through a comfortable ROM that includes some cervical flexion,\r\n\r\nnot:\r\n\r\n> maximal passive cervical flexion under heavy external load.\r\n\r\nThe database `smh_factor` should therefore be interpreted conservatively.\r\n\r\nEvidence supports the general trainability and hypertrophy of cervical extensors, but not a well-quantified deep-extensor-specific stretch-mediated hypertrophy advantage.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Direct Neck Extension\r\n\r\nDirect resisted neck extension is the most straightforward method of loading the cervical extensors.\r\n\r\nPossible resistance sources include:\r\n\r\n- dedicated neck-extension machine;\r\n- head harness;\r\n- resistance band;\r\n- manual resistance;\r\n- controlled plate-supported or harness-supported variations.\r\n\r\nThe desired movement is controlled extension of the cervical spine against an external flexion moment.\r\n\r\n## Dynamic vs Isometric Training\r\n\r\nBoth dynamic and isometric cervical training can increase neck strength.\r\n\r\nDynamic training has the advantage of producing force across a broader ROM.\r\n\r\nIsometric training has advantages in:\r\n\r\n- simple load control;\r\n- low movement complexity;\r\n- angle-specific strengthening;\r\n- reduced need for large cervical excursions.\r\n\r\nFor comprehensive development, the two methods can coexist.\r\n\r\n## Range of Motion\r\n\r\nNeck-extension ROM should be controlled rather than maximal.\r\n\r\nA useful repetition can involve:\r\n\r\n1. beginning in mild-to-moderate cervical flexion;\r\n2. extending smoothly toward neutral or modest extension;\r\n3. avoiding aggressive terminal hyperextension;\r\n4. lowering under controlled eccentric resistance.\r\n\r\nThere is rarely a compelling hypertrophic reason to load extreme cervical extension.\r\n\r\nThe cervical joints are small and the additional active ROM beyond a comfortable extension position may add relatively little muscular benefit while increasing passive-structure exposure.\r\n\r\n## Distinguish Cervical From Thoracic Extension\r\n\r\nNeck extension should occur primarily through the cervical spine.\r\n\r\nA trainee can create the appearance of head movement by:\r\n\r\n- extending the upper thoracic spine;\r\n- retracting the shoulders;\r\n- moving the torso.\r\n\r\nThese compensations reduce the specificity of cervical loading.\r\n\r\nFor direct neck-extensor training, the torso should remain relatively stable.\r\n\r\n## Do Not Turn It Into a Shrug\r\n\r\nUpper trapezius can strongly contribute to posterior-neck force and head stabilization.\r\n\r\nHowever, shoulder elevation is not cervical extension.\r\n\r\nIf the objective is direct deep-neck-extensor loading, avoid converting the exercise into:\r\n\r\n- shrugging;\r\n- scapular retraction;\r\n- generalized upper-back extension.\r\n\r\nThese may increase total posterior-chain force without proportionally increasing the target stimulus.\r\n\r\n## Harness Training\r\n\r\nA head harness allows external resistance to be applied directly to the skull.\r\n\r\nAdvantages include:\r\n\r\n- simple progressive loading;\r\n- natural resistance direction;\r\n- standing or seated use.\r\n\r\nLimitations include:\r\n\r\n- resistance can become unstable;\r\n- cable direction strongly affects the torque profile;\r\n- high loads can encourage compensatory trunk movement.\r\n\r\nA stable machine is easier to standardize when available.\r\n\r\n## Band Training\r\n\r\nElastic bands provide low equipment requirements and progressively increasing resistance.\r\n\r\nBecause band force typically rises as the band stretches, resistance may increase toward one end of ROM.\r\n\r\nThe exact profile depends on setup.\r\n\r\nBand neck extension can therefore be useful for:\r\n\r\n- warm-up;\r\n- endurance;\r\n- moderate hypertrophy work;\r\n- introductory neck training.\r\n\r\n## Conventional Compound Exercises\r\n\r\nExercises such as:\r\n\r\n- shrugs;\r\n- rows;\r\n- deadlifts;\r\n- loaded carries\r\n\r\nrequire the head and cervical spine to remain stable.\r\n\r\nThe cervical extensors may therefore contract isometrically.\r\n\r\nHowever, indirect isometric activation should not automatically be treated as equivalent to direct neck-extension training.\r\n\r\nClassic resistance-training research has demonstrated **specific hypertrophy of cervical musculature following direct head-extension training**, while conventional whole-body resistance exercise did not produce the same pattern of cervical hypertrophy.\r\n\r\nThis is a strong argument for direct work when neck muscular development is an explicit goal.\r\n\r\n## Progressive Introduction\r\n\r\nThe neck should not be trained using the same load-progression mentality as a large machine-based leg exercise.\r\n\r\nInitial loading should be conservative.\r\n\r\nReasons include:\r\n\r\n- relatively small muscle mass;\r\n- unfamiliar local soreness;\r\n- potential headache or discomfort from excessive strain;\r\n- high importance of technique;\r\n- limited prior training exposure in most lifters.\r\n\r\nA modest stimulus can be sufficient when direct cervical work is first introduced.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Direct Volume\r\n\r\nThe deep neck extensors do not require large amounts of direct volume.\r\n\r\nA practical starting point for direct extension training is approximately:\r\n\r\n- **2-4 hard sets per week**.\r\n\r\nIf well tolerated and neck hypertrophy or strength is an explicit priority, this can be progressively increased.\r\n\r\nThere is little justification for beginning with large bodybuilding-style volumes.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nApproximately **1-3 weekly exposures** are practical.\r\n\r\nTwo exposures provide a useful default because they allow:\r\n\r\n- repeated motor practice;\r\n- moderate per-session fatigue;\r\n- adequate recovery.\r\n\r\nFor an untrained neck, even one direct exposure can initially produce significant soreness.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\nModerate-to-high repetition ranges are generally convenient:\r\n\r\n- approximately 10-15 repetitions for controlled heavier work;\r\n- 15-30 repetitions for moderate-load hypertrophy/endurance work.\r\n\r\nVery heavy low-repetition maximal neck extension has little unique hypertrophic advantage and increases the consequences of technical error.\r\n\r\n## Isometric Duration\r\n\r\nFor isometric training, useful loading can be accumulated through repeated holds rather than single prolonged maximal efforts.\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n\r\n- moderate-duration high-tension holds;\r\n- multiple sets at different cervical angles.\r\n\r\nThe objective determines whether the emphasis is:\r\n\r\n- maximal strength;\r\n- endurance;\r\n- positional control.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nDirect neck exercises do not need repeated absolute mechanical failure.\r\n\r\nA practical approach is to terminate dynamic sets when:\r\n\r\n- ROM decreases;\r\n- movement becomes jerky;\r\n- the torso begins compensating;\r\n- cervical position becomes difficult to control.\r\n\r\nApproximately **1-3 RIR** is generally sufficient for hypertrophy-oriented dynamic work.\r\n\r\nThe neck offers little benefit from forcing uncontrolled grinders.\r\n\r\n## Progression\r\n\r\nProgressive overload can come from:\r\n\r\n- increased resistance;\r\n- additional repetitions;\r\n- improved ROM;\r\n- improved control;\r\n- additional sets;\r\n- longer isometric duration.\r\n\r\nLoad progression should generally be relatively gradual.\r\n\r\nBecause neck exercises often begin with small absolute loads, a seemingly small weight increment can represent a large percentage increase.\r\n\r\n## Integration With Other Neck Training\r\n\r\nA complete direct neck program may include:\r\n\r\n- extension;\r\n- flexion;\r\n- lateral flexion;\r\n- optionally controlled rotational work.\r\n\r\nThe goal is not necessarily equal loading in every direction.\r\n\r\nRather, the program should account for the multidirectional function of cervical musculature.\r\n\r\nIf the database already has separate flexor and extensor aggregates, direct exercise vectors should preserve those distinctions.\r\n\r\n## Hypertrophy vs Motor-Control Training\r\n\r\nNot all exercises commonly called "deep neck extensor exercises" are hypertrophy exercises.\r\n\r\nLow-load rehabilitation movements can emphasize:\r\n\r\n- proprioception;\r\n- segmental control;\r\n- endurance;\r\n- motor coordination.\r\n\r\nTheir mechanical tension may be far below that required for meaningful hypertrophy.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n> therapeutic specificity and hypertrophic stimulus are separate dimensions.\r\n\r\nAn exercise can be excellent for cervical motor control while providing very little muscle-growth stimulus.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## The Neck Is Exceptionally Proprioceptive\r\n\r\nDeep cervical muscles contain very high muscle-spindle densities.\r\n\r\nThis reflects the requirement for extremely precise information about head orientation.\r\n\r\nThe nervous system continuously integrates cervical proprioception with vision and vestibular information to distinguish:\r\n\r\n- movement of the head relative to the torso;\r\n- movement of the whole body through space.\r\n\r\nThus these muscles function partly as **sensory organs**, not merely motors.\r\n\r\n## Short Muscles Can Matter Without Producing Large Torque\r\n\r\nA small rotatores fascicle cannot compete with large superficial neck muscles in absolute torque production.\r\n\r\nIts value lies partly in controlling and sensing local vertebral movement.\r\n\r\nThis illustrates an important principle:\r\n\r\n> muscle importance is not proportional only to PCSA or maximum force.\r\n\r\nFor the cervical spine, fine control has substantial functional value.\r\n\r\n## Direct Neck Training Can Be Highly Specific\r\n\r\nResistance-training studies have shown that direct head-extension training can produce selective hypertrophy of cervical muscles.\r\n\r\nThis means large upper-body compound exercises cannot be assumed to provide complete neck hypertrophy simply because the neck contracts isometrically.\r\n\r\n## Deep Extensor Dysfunction Is Studied in Neck Pain\r\n\r\nDeep cervical extensors, particularly multifidus and semispinalis cervicis, show morphological and motor-control differences in populations with chronic neck pain.\r\n\r\nThese findings are clinically interesting but should not be converted into simplistic claims that weak deep extensors are the universal cause of neck pain.\r\n\r\nNeck pain is multifactorial.\r\n\r\n## Deep Does Not Mean Strongest\r\n\r\nThe deepest cervical muscles are not necessarily the largest torque generators.\r\n\r\nLarge superficial muscles can dominate gross force production.\r\n\r\nDeep muscles are especially valuable for:\r\n\r\n- segmental control;\r\n- proprioception;\r\n- coordinated vertebral motion.\r\n\r\nThis division between **global torque** and **local control** is central to understanding cervical biomechanics.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Definition of This Aggregate\r\n\r\nFor Agonez, `deep_neck_extensors` should primarily encode the cervical deep extensor system:\r\n\r\n- `semispinalis_cervicis`;\r\n- cervical `multifidus`;\r\n- cervical `rotatores`;\r\n\r\nrepresented together because individual exercise-level force allocation among these small overlapping structures would be difficult to estimate reliably.\r\n\r\nThe aggregate should **not** automatically include the full force of:\r\n\r\n- upper trapezius;\r\n- splenius capitis;\r\n- splenius cervicis;\r\n- levator scapulae.\r\n\r\nThose are mechanically distinct superficial or intermediate muscles.\r\n\r\nSuboccipital muscles may be treated as a small related component if the anatomical dataset from which mass/PCSA values were derived included them, but they should not dominate the aggregate.\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\nThe primary exercise-level dimensions should be:\r\n\r\n1. **cervical extension torque**;\r\n2. **segmental stabilization demand**;\r\n3. smaller contributions from rotation and lateral-flexion stabilization.\r\n\r\n### Direct Neck Extension\r\n\r\nHigh `deep_neck_extensors` allocation.\r\n\r\nThe magnitude should depend on:\r\n\r\n- external head moment;\r\n- cervical angle;\r\n- resistance direction;\r\n- ROM;\r\n- stabilization requirements.\r\n\r\n### Neck Extension Isometric Hold\r\n\r\nHigh extensor recruitment despite zero mechanical ROM.\r\n\r\nFor an ETU model, this is an important case where meaningful force can exist without dynamic work.\r\n\r\n### Deadlift\r\n\r\nLow-to-moderate cervical-extensor stabilization demand if neutral head position is actively maintained.\r\n\r\nIt should not receive the same hypertrophic allocation as direct extension.\r\n\r\n### Shrug\r\n\r\nSome cervical stabilization can occur, but the dominant target remains the scapular-elevation system.\r\n\r\nDo not assign large `deep_neck_extensors` demand simply because the exercise visibly thickens the posterior neck region.\r\n\r\n### Row\r\n\r\nGenerally low cervical dynamic demand if head position remains neutral.\r\n\r\nAny extensor allocation is predominantly stabilizing.\r\n\r\n## External Head Moment\r\n\r\nFor direct neck exercise, external load alone is an incomplete descriptor.\r\n\r\nA more relevant approximation is:\r\n\r\n`cervical_extension_demand ∝ external_force × perpendicular_distance_to_cervical_axis`\r\n\r\nThe head itself also contributes gravitational torque.\r\n\r\nTherefore even an apparently small external resistance can generate meaningful cervical torque when applied far from the cervical joint axis.\r\n\r\nThis makes load values between different neck-training devices poorly interchangeable.\r\n\r\n## Length-Tension Model\r\n\r\nA single gross cervical angle is an imperfect approximation because different fascicles span different segments.\r\n\r\nFor the aggregate, however, a useful simplified model is:\r\n\r\n- cervical flexion → longer posterior extensors;\r\n- neutral → intermediate;\r\n- cervical extension → shorter posterior extensors.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n`E_LT ≈ f(cervical_flexion_extension_angle)`\r\n\r\nwith substantially lower confidence than analogous models for simple hinge joints.\r\n\r\nThe model should avoid assigning exaggerated precision to exact cervical angles.\r\n\r\n## Stabilization vs Hypertrophic Recruitment\r\n\r\nAgonez should ideally distinguish:\r\n\r\n- **active dynamic extensor demand**;\r\n- **high-force isometric extensor demand**;\r\n- **low-level postural stabilization**.\r\n\r\nAll involve muscle activation, but their expected hypertrophic stimulus is not equivalent.\r\n\r\nThis distinction is especially important for exercises such as deadlifts and carries.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nDirect evidence for stretch-mediated hypertrophy of this aggregate is weak.\r\n\r\nA conservative interpretation is:\r\n\r\n- cervical flexion increases posterior muscle length;\r\n- controlled extension from flexion can load longer muscle states;\r\n- extreme loaded cervical flexion is unnecessary;\r\n- there is insufficient evidence to assign a large muscle-specific SMH effect.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **transversospinal anatomy:** high;\r\n- **posterior-ramus innervation:** high;\r\n- **cervical-extension role:** high;\r\n- **segmental control function:** high;\r\n- **high cervical proprioceptive specialization:** high;\r\n- **trainability of cervical extensor strength:** directly supported;\r\n- **cervical hypertrophy from direct extension training:** directly supported;\r\n- **specific hypertrophy of individual deep extensor fascicles:** limited;\r\n- **stretch-mediated hypertrophy:** insufficient direct evidence;\r\n- **precise individual-muscle recruitment coefficients:** low-to-moderate due to substantial redundancy.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **deep_neck_extensors = a distributed deep posterior cervical muscle system combining extension torque with segmental stabilization and unusually important proprioceptive control, whose role cannot be reduced to gross neck extension alone.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/LevatorScapulae}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	4.79	3.18	0.998	85.01	84.88
25	pronators_supinators	Musculus pronator teres, Musculus pronator quadratus, Musculus supinator	Upper	Forearms	180.00	170.45	12.00	Fusiform/Quadrilateral	0.450	0.550	high	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\n`pronators_supinators` is a functional aggregate representing three dedicated forearm rotators:\r\n\r\n- **pronator teres**;\r\n- **pronator quadratus**;\r\n- **supinator**.\r\n\r\nThese muscles control rotation of the radius around the ulna and therefore change forearm orientation between:\r\n\r\n- **pronation**;\r\n- neutral;\r\n- **supination**.\r\n\r\nThis aggregate is unusual because it contains muscles with **opposing primary actions**.\r\n\r\n### Pronators\r\n\r\n- `pronator_teres`;\r\n- `pronator_quadratus`.\r\n\r\n### Supinator\r\n\r\n- `supinator`.\r\n\r\nTherefore the aggregate should not be interpreted as one muscle producing a single scalar movement.\r\n\r\nIt represents the local muscular machinery responsible for **forearm axial rotation**.\r\n\r\nAn additional complication is that dedicated rotators do not operate alone.\r\n\r\nMost importantly:\r\n\r\n- `biceps_brachii` is a powerful supinator;\r\n- `brachioradialis` can contribute toward returning the forearm toward neutral depending on starting position.\r\n\r\nThus total forearm-rotation torque cannot be assigned entirely to this aggregate.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **pronators_supinators = the dedicated radius-on-ulna rotational system, with pronation produced mainly by pronator quadratus and pronator teres, and supination produced by supinator in cooperation with the often much more powerful biceps brachii.**\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\nForearm pronation and supination occur primarily through coordinated motion at the:\r\n\r\n- proximal radioulnar joint;\r\n- distal radioulnar joint.\r\n\r\nThe ulna remains relatively stable while the radius rotates around it.\r\n\r\nIn supination, radius and ulna are approximately parallel.\r\n\r\nIn pronation, the radius crosses anteriorly over the ulna.\r\n\r\nThe three muscles in this aggregate act on the radius at different locations, giving them distinct mechanical roles.\r\n\r\n## Pronator Teres\r\n\r\nPronator teres is a superficial muscle of the proximal anterior forearm.\r\n\r\nIt has two proximal heads.\r\n\r\n### Humeral Head\r\n\r\nOriginates from the region of the:\r\n\r\n- medial epicondyle of the humerus;\r\n- common flexor origin.\r\n\r\n### Ulnar Head\r\n\r\nOriginates from the proximal ulna, classically near the:\r\n\r\n- coronoid process.\r\n\r\nThe median nerve typically passes between these heads.\r\n\r\n### Insertion\r\n\r\nPronator teres inserts onto the **lateral surface of the middle radius**.\r\n\r\nIts relatively proximal and lateral insertion gives it substantial rotational leverage.\r\n\r\nIts line of action pulls the radius medially and anteriorly, producing pronation.\r\n\r\nThe muscle also crosses the elbow and can contribute weakly to elbow flexion.\r\n\r\n## Pronator Quadratus\r\n\r\nPronator quadratus is a deep, short, quadrangular muscle located across the distal forearm.\r\n\r\n### Origin\r\n\r\nIt arises from the distal anterior ulna.\r\n\r\n### Insertion\r\n\r\nIt inserts onto the distal anterior radius.\r\n\r\nUnlike pronator teres, pronator quadratus:\r\n\r\n- does not cross the elbow;\r\n- acts locally across the distal radioulnar region.\r\n\r\nIts transverse fiber direction makes it particularly suited to rotating the radius toward the ulna.\r\n\r\nPronator quadratus is commonly described as a major or primary pronator across a wide range of tasks.\r\n\r\nIt also contributes to stabilization of the distal radioulnar joint.\r\n\r\n## Supinator\r\n\r\nSupinator is a broad deep muscle wrapping around the proximal radius.\r\n\r\n### Origin\r\n\r\nIts proximal attachments include regions of the:\r\n\r\n- lateral epicondyle of the humerus;\r\n- radial collateral and annular ligament region;\r\n- supinator crest of the ulna.\r\n\r\n### Insertion\r\n\r\nIt inserts onto the proximal radius across anterior, lateral and posterior surfaces.\r\n\r\nThis wrapping geometry allows contraction to rotate the proximal radius laterally into supination.\r\n\r\n## Regional Mechanical Arrangement\r\n\r\nThe aggregate therefore contains three very different geometries:\r\n\r\n### Pronator Teres\r\n\r\nLonger, superficial, more proximal:\r\n- high pronation leverage;\r\n- minor elbow-flexion capability.\r\n\r\n### Pronator Quadratus\r\n\r\nShort, deep, distal:\r\n- pronation;\r\n- distal radioulnar stabilization.\r\n\r\n### Supinator\r\n\r\nDeep proximal wrap:\r\n- dedicated supination;\r\n- no meaningful elbow-flexion role.\r\n\r\nThis distinction is important because their force sharing changes with:\r\n\r\n- forearm angle;\r\n- elbow angle;\r\n- required torque;\r\n- whether elbow flexion is simultaneously demanded.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\n## Pronator Teres\r\n\r\nPronator teres is innervated by the **median nerve**, primarily from approximately:\r\n\r\n- C6;\r\n- C7.\r\n\r\n## Pronator Quadratus\r\n\r\nPronator quadratus is innervated by the **anterior interosseous nerve**, a branch of the median nerve.\r\n\r\nIts root contribution is predominantly from lower median-nerve levels, typically around:\r\n\r\n- C8;\r\n- T1.\r\n\r\n## Supinator\r\n\r\nSupinator is innervated by the deep branch of the **radial nerve / posterior interosseous pathway**, predominantly around:\r\n\r\n- C6;\r\n- C7.\r\n\r\nThis means the pronation and supination sides of the aggregate are neurally distinct.\r\n\r\nThe aggregate is therefore purely a functional database grouping rather than a single neuromuscular unit.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Forearm Pronation\r\n\r\nPronation rotates the radius over the ulna so that the palm turns from:\r\n\r\n- anterior/upward in anatomical supination\r\n\r\ntoward:\r\n\r\n- posterior/downward orientation.\r\n\r\nThe primary dedicated pronators are:\r\n\r\n- pronator quadratus;\r\n- pronator teres.\r\n\r\nBoth show substantial activation during resisted pronation. Human EMG and torque experiments consistently identify both muscles as major contributors. :contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}\r\n\r\n## Pronator Quadratus Role\r\n\r\nPronator quadratus is particularly suited to continuous pronation because it acts directly across the distal radius and ulna.\r\n\r\nIt remains mechanically relevant regardless of elbow angle because it does not cross the elbow.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`PQ function ≈ f(forearm_rotation_angle)`\r\n\r\nwith little direct length dependence on elbow flexion.\r\n\r\nIt also contributes to maintaining distal radioulnar joint congruence during rotation.\r\n\r\n## Pronator Teres Role\r\n\r\nPronator teres is also a powerful pronator but its mechanics are more complex because it crosses the elbow.\r\n\r\nIts rotational efficiency changes with:\r\n\r\n- elbow flexion angle;\r\n- forearm rotation.\r\n\r\nBiomechanical modelling demonstrates substantial variation in its pronation efficiency across the combined elbow-flexion and forearm-rotation workspace. :contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n`PT torque_capacity = f(elbow_angle, forearm_angle)`\r\n\r\nrather than forearm angle alone.\r\n\r\n## Forearm Supination\r\n\r\nSupination returns the radius toward a position parallel with the ulna.\r\n\r\nThe primary dedicated supinator is:\r\n\r\n- `supinator`.\r\n\r\nHowever, total human supination is not produced mainly by this muscle under all conditions.\r\n\r\n`biceps_brachii` is a major supinator and can dominate high-force supination depending on elbow and forearm position. :contentReference[oaicite:3]{index=3}\r\n\r\nThis is one of the most important modelling rules for the aggregate.\r\n\r\n## Biceps Brachii Interaction\r\n\r\nBiceps attaches to the radial tuberosity.\r\n\r\nIts distal tendon wraps around the radius in pronation, creating a mechanically favorable supination moment.\r\n\r\nAs the forearm supinates, this geometry changes.\r\n\r\nExperimental and biomechanical work confirms that biceps possesses substantial supination leverage, particularly with the elbow flexed. :contentReference[oaicite:4]{index=4}\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n> **high supination torque should not be allocated primarily to `supinator` simply because the movement is called supination.**\r\n\r\n## Forearm-Angle Dependence\r\n\r\nMaximum rotation torque depends strongly on starting forearm orientation.\r\n\r\nIn general, a muscle can generate especially high rotational torque when beginning from the opposite end of its movement:\r\n\r\n- pronators from supinated positions;\r\n- supinators from pronated positions.\r\n\r\nHuman torque measurements demonstrate greater pronation torque from more supinated configurations and greater supination torque from more pronated configurations. :contentReference[oaicite:5]{index=5}\r\n\r\nThis reflects a combination of:\r\n\r\n- muscle length;\r\n- tendon geometry;\r\n- rotational moment arm.\r\n\r\n## Neutralization Function\r\n\r\nForearm rotators also participate in maintaining a chosen orientation against external torque.\r\n\r\nFor example, during:\r\n\r\n- hammer curl;\r\n- neutral-grip pull-up;\r\n- neutral-grip row,\r\n\r\nthe goal may be to maintain approximately neutral forearm orientation.\r\n\r\nPronators and supinators can co-contract to resist unwanted rotation.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`rotation_ROM ≈ 0`\r\n\r\ndoes not imply:\r\n\r\n`rotator_demand ≈ 0`.\r\n\r\n## Joint Stabilization\r\n\r\nPronator quadratus has a particularly relevant stabilization role at the distal radioulnar joint.\r\n\r\nSupinator contributes to proximal radial control.\r\n\r\nPronator teres contributes less directly to radioulnar stabilization but can help regulate rotation under high load.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nDirect evidence examining **stretch-mediated hypertrophy specifically in pronator teres, pronator quadratus or supinator is extremely limited**.\r\n\r\nThe mechanical principles are nevertheless clear.\r\n\r\n## Pronator Lengthening\r\n\r\nPronators generally become relatively lengthened as the forearm moves toward **supination**.\r\n\r\nThus resisted pronation beginning from a supinated position exposes:\r\n\r\n- pronator teres;\r\n- pronator quadratus\r\n\r\nto greater musculotendinous length.\r\n\r\n## Supinator Lengthening\r\n\r\nSupinator becomes relatively lengthened as the forearm moves into **pronation**.\r\n\r\nTherefore resisted supination beginning from pronation can load the muscle from a longer starting state.\r\n\r\n## Pronator Teres and Elbow Position\r\n\r\nBecause pronator teres crosses the elbow, its length state also depends somewhat on elbow flexion.\r\n\r\nElbow position changes both:\r\n\r\n- muscle geometry;\r\n- pronation efficiency.\r\n\r\nThis makes pronator teres more analogous to a small biarticular muscle than pronator quadratus.\r\n\r\n## Pronator Quadratus\r\n\r\nPronator quadratus is functionally monoarticular with respect to the forearm-rotation system.\r\n\r\nIts length is primarily dependent on:\r\n\r\n- radial rotation relative to ulna.\r\n\r\nElbow angle should have little direct `E_LT` effect.\r\n\r\n## Supinator\r\n\r\nSupinator also does not cross the elbow in a manner that gives it a meaningful elbow flexion/extension action.\r\n\r\nIts primary length state depends on forearm rotation.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Interpretation\r\n\r\nThe defensible position is:\r\n\r\n- opposing rotation lengthens each respective muscle group;\r\n- meaningful active torque can be applied from these positions;\r\n- direct regional hypertrophy data are sparse;\r\n- no strong evidence supports a specific SMH multiplier.\r\n\r\nTherefore `smh_factor` should remain conservative.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Direct Pronation\r\n\r\nDirect resisted pronation can be performed with:\r\n\r\n- cable;\r\n- lever device;\r\n- dumbbell or hammer-style implement;\r\n- resistance band.\r\n\r\nA long lever increases external rotational torque.\r\n\r\nThe movement should occur primarily through forearm rotation rather than:\r\n\r\n- shoulder internal rotation;\r\n- wrist deviation.\r\n\r\n## Direct Supination\r\n\r\nResisted supination can use similar tools.\r\n\r\nHowever, direct supination training necessarily involves force sharing between:\r\n\r\n- supinator;\r\n- biceps brachii.\r\n\r\nThe relative contribution depends strongly on elbow angle.\r\n\r\nIf the aim is to train the dedicated `supinator`, a position minimizing excessive biceps advantage may improve specificity.\r\n\r\nIf the aim is maximal total supination strength, biceps contribution is desirable.\r\n\r\n## Lever Implements\r\n\r\nA hammer, mace-like handle or adjustable pronation/supination device is mechanically useful because external torque can be altered simply by changing the lever arm.\r\n\r\nApproximately:\r\n\r\n`M_rotation = F_load × perpendicular_distance`\r\n\r\nThus a small distal load can generate meaningful rotational torque.\r\n\r\nExternal kilograms alone are a poor descriptor.\r\n\r\n## Wrist Position\r\n\r\nThe wrist should usually remain relatively neutral during direct rotational training.\r\n\r\nExcessive:\r\n\r\n- flexion;\r\n- extension;\r\n- radial deviation;\r\n- ulnar deviation\r\n\r\nchanges the exercise into a combined wrist/forearm task.\r\n\r\nThis introduces additional demand on:\r\n\r\n- wrist flexors;\r\n- wrist extensors.\r\n\r\n## Hammer Curl\r\n\r\nA hammer curl is nominally performed with a neutral forearm.\r\n\r\nThere is little dynamic pronation or supination.\r\n\r\nHowever, the load may create rotational perturbations that require stabilization.\r\n\r\nPronator/supinator demand is therefore:\r\n\r\n- low-to-moderate;\r\n- primarily isometric.\r\n\r\nBrachialis and brachioradialis should remain the dominant elbow-flexion contributors.\r\n\r\n## Supinated Curl\r\n\r\nDuring a conventional supinated curl, biceps generates both:\r\n\r\n- elbow-flexion torque;\r\n- supination torque if orientation must be maintained or produced.\r\n\r\nThe dedicated supinator may contribute but should not receive a huge vector if the forearm is already fully supinated and rotation is minimal.\r\n\r\n## Rotating Dumbbell Curl\r\n\r\nA curl that actively transitions:\r\n\r\n`neutral/pronated → supinated`\r\n\r\nadds genuine rotational demand.\r\n\r\nThis should increase:\r\n\r\n- biceps supination demand;\r\n- supinator demand.\r\n\r\nThe precise share depends on elbow angle.\r\n\r\n## Reverse Curl\r\n\r\nDuring a pronated reverse curl, pronators can contribute isometrically to maintaining pronation against any tendency of the load and elbow flexors to rotate the radius.\r\n\r\nHowever, the dominant dynamic action remains elbow flexion.\r\n\r\nPronator demand should therefore remain secondary.\r\n\r\n## Pull-Ups and Pulldowns\r\n\r\nGrip orientation strongly changes forearm state:\r\n\r\n### Pronated Grip\r\n\r\nRequires maintenance of pronation.\r\n\r\n### Neutral Grip\r\n\r\nRequires rotational stabilization around neutral.\r\n\r\n### Supinated Grip\r\n\r\nRequires maintenance of supination and increases the mechanical relevance of biceps.\r\n\r\nThe dedicated pronator/supinator aggregate should receive modest stabilization demand rather than being treated as a primary mover in these exercises.\r\n\r\n## Rows\r\n\r\nThe same logic applies to rows.\r\n\r\nA rotating independent handle may permit forearm rotation through ROM, increasing dynamic demand.\r\n\r\nA rigid neutral handle usually produces primarily isometric stabilization.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Direct Training Is Usually Optional\r\n\r\nMost bodybuilding programs do not require substantial dedicated pronation/supination volume.\r\n\r\nThese muscles receive indirect exposure from:\r\n\r\n- curls;\r\n- pulling;\r\n- gripping;\r\n- loaded implements.\r\n\r\nDirect work becomes more relevant for:\r\n\r\n- forearm hypertrophy;\r\n- grip sports;\r\n- climbing;\r\n- racket sports;\r\n- combat sports;\r\n- arm wrestling;\r\n- rehabilitation;\r\n- rotational-strength goals.\r\n\r\n## Weekly Volume\r\n\r\nFor direct training, a reasonable starting point is approximately:\r\n\r\n- **2-6 hard sets of pronation**;\r\n- **2-6 hard sets of supination**\r\n\r\nper week when forearm rotation is a priority.\r\n\r\nThis does not imply separate volume is needed for every component muscle.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nTwo to three exposures per week are practical.\r\n\r\nThe muscles are relatively small and recover rapidly metabolically, but connective tissues around the:\r\n\r\n- wrist;\r\n- elbow;\r\n- radioulnar joints\r\n\r\ncan become irritated if rotational work is increased too aggressively.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\nModerate and high repetition ranges are practical:\r\n\r\n- 8-15 reps;\r\n- 12-20 reps;\r\n- 15-30 reps.\r\n\r\nVery heavy low-repetition rotational work is often limited by:\r\n\r\n- joint discomfort;\r\n- leverage instability;\r\n- technique.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nControlled cable or lever work can be trained close to muscular failure.\r\n\r\nApproximately:\r\n\r\n- 0-2 RIR\r\n\r\ncan be practical.\r\n\r\nHowever, failure should occur because forearm rotation can no longer be completed, not because the trainee begins compensating through:\r\n\r\n- shoulder rotation;\r\n- wrist deviation;\r\n- trunk movement.\r\n\r\n## Progression\r\n\r\nBecause external lever arms strongly affect torque, progression can occur through:\r\n\r\n- more load;\r\n- longer lever arm;\r\n- larger ROM;\r\n- slower eccentric control.\r\n\r\nSmall changes in lever length can meaningfully alter difficulty.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Curl Training\r\n\r\nHeavy supination work can fatigue biceps.\r\n\r\nLikewise, heavy curls can limit direct supination performance.\r\n\r\nIf forearm rotation is a priority, exercise order should account for this overlap.\r\n\r\nPronator training generally interferes less with elbow-flexor performance, although pronator teres has a minor elbow-flexion role.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## The Radius Rotates Around the Ulna\r\n\r\nForearm pronation is not primarily the humerus internally rotating.\r\n\r\nInstead, the radius crosses around the relatively stable ulna through coordinated proximal and distal radioulnar motion.\r\n\r\nThis distinction is essential for correct exercise classification.\r\n\r\n## Pronator Quadratus Is Small but Mechanically Important\r\n\r\nIts short fibers and deep location make it visually unimpressive, but it is a major pronator and an important stabilizer of the distal radioulnar joint.\r\n\r\nHigh muscle visibility does not correspond directly to functional importance.\r\n\r\n## Pronator Teres Is Also an Elbow Flexor\r\n\r\nBecause it originates partly from the medial humerus and crosses the elbow, pronator teres can generate a small elbow-flexion moment.\r\n\r\nThis distinguishes it from pronator quadratus.\r\n\r\n## Biceps Can Out-Torque Supinator\r\n\r\nDespite its name, `supinator` is not necessarily the dominant muscle during high-force supination.\r\n\r\nMechanical experiments show that biceps can generate several times the supination torque of supinator in favorable positions. :contentReference[oaicite:6]{index=6}\r\n\r\nThis is one of the clearest examples where anatomical naming can mislead exercise modelling.\r\n\r\n## Pronation and Supination Strength Depend on Starting Position\r\n\r\nTorque is generally higher when beginning away from the final shortened position.\r\n\r\nHuman measurements confirm that supination torque increases from pronated starting positions and pronation torque increases from supinated starting positions. :contentReference[oaicite:7]{index=7}\r\n\r\nThis reflects both length-tension and changing rotational leverage.\r\n\r\n## Pronator Teres Efficiency Depends on Elbow Angle\r\n\r\nThree-dimensional biomechanical analysis shows that pronator-teres rotational efficiency changes substantially across elbow and forearm positions. :contentReference[oaicite:8]{index=8}\r\n\r\nThus elbow angle matters even for a movement occurring anatomically at the radioulnar joints.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Definition of This Aggregate\r\n\r\n`pronators_supinators` includes:\r\n\r\n- pronator teres;\r\n- pronator quadratus;\r\n- supinator.\r\n\r\nIt should **not** absorb all forearm-rotation torque.\r\n\r\nImportant muscles outside the aggregate include:\r\n\r\n- `biceps_brachii` for supination;\r\n- `brachioradialis` for movement toward neutral depending on starting position.\r\n\r\nThis is essential for correct force allocation.\r\n\r\n## Opposing-Muscle Aggregate Caveat\r\n\r\nThis record is unusual because it combines antagonist functions.\r\n\r\nA single muscle-demand scalar can still represent:\r\n\r\n> total local forearm-rotation muscular exposure.\r\n\r\nHowever, it cannot distinguish whether the exercise predominantly loads:\r\n\r\n- pronators;\r\nor\r\n- supinator.\r\n\r\nIf future modelling requires more precision, this aggregate is a strong candidate for subdivision into:\r\n\r\n- `forearm_pronators`;\r\n- `supinator`.\r\n\r\nOr ultimately:\r\n\r\n- `pronator_teres`;\r\n- `pronator_quadratus`;\r\n- `supinator`.\r\n\r\n## Primary Mechanical Dimension\r\n\r\nThe principal demand variable should be:\r\n\r\n`radioulnar_rotation_torque`\r\n\r\nwith sign indicating:\r\n\r\n- pronation;\r\n- supination.\r\n\r\nIf Agonez stores unsigned muscle demand, direction must be handled before allocation to individual muscle components.\r\n\r\n## Pronation Force Sharing\r\n\r\nFor pronation:\r\n\r\n`T_pronation ≈ T_PQ + T_PT + smaller_synergists`\r\n\r\nPronator quadratus and pronator teres should dominate.\r\n\r\nThe relative share changes with:\r\n\r\n- forearm position;\r\n- elbow angle;\r\n- torque magnitude.\r\n\r\nBoth muscles are strongly active during resisted pronation. :contentReference[oaicite:9]{index=9}\r\n\r\n## Supination Force Sharing\r\n\r\nFor supination:\r\n\r\n`T_supination ≈ T_biceps + T_supinator + smaller_contributors`\r\n\r\nAt high force and favorable elbow positions:\r\n\r\n`T_biceps` can become dominant.\r\n\r\nTherefore a high-force dumbbell supination movement should not allocate the full rotation demand to this aggregate.\r\n\r\nPart must be assigned to `biceps_brachii`. :contentReference[oaicite:10]{index=10}\r\n\r\n## Elbow-Angle Modifier\r\n\r\nThis modifier should be asymmetric.\r\n\r\n### Pronator Quadratus\r\n\r\nMinimal direct dependence on elbow angle.\r\n\r\n### Pronator Teres\r\n\r\nMeaningful elbow-angle dependence.\r\n\r\n### Supinator\r\n\r\nRotation mechanics vary with forearm state, while elbow angle affects overall force sharing especially because it changes biceps mechanics.\r\n\r\nThus the whole aggregate's apparent strength can vary with elbow angle even if the dedicated supinator itself is not strongly biarticular.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Allocation\r\n\r\n### Standing Barbell Curl — Supinated Grip\r\n\r\nLow-to-moderate aggregate demand if the forearm remains statically supinated.\r\n\r\nHigh:\r\n- biceps;\r\n- brachialis.\r\n\r\nSupination maintenance should not be confused with large dynamic supination torque.\r\n\r\n### Bayesian Cable Curl — Supinated Grip\r\n\r\nSimilar logic.\r\n\r\nForearm rotation is usually fixed.\r\n\r\nTherefore this aggregate receives stabilization demand, not primary dynamic work.\r\n\r\n### Dumbbell Hammer Curl\r\n\r\nLow-to-moderate rotational stabilization around neutral.\r\n\r\nVery high:\r\n- brachialis;\r\n- brachioradialis.\r\n\r\n### Reverse Curl\r\n\r\nModerate pronator stabilization can occur, but the major exercise demand is elbow flexion.\r\n\r\n### Neutral-Grip Pull-Up\r\n\r\nLow-to-moderate forearm rotational stabilization.\r\n\r\nGrip and elbow flexors dominate.\r\n\r\n### Pronated Pull-Up\r\n\r\nLow-to-moderate pronation-maintenance demand.\r\n\r\nDo not classify it as direct pronator training.\r\n\r\n### Active Dumbbell Supination\r\n\r\nVery high rotation demand.\r\n\r\nForce should be divided substantially between:\r\n- `biceps_brachii`;\r\n- `pronators_supinators` through supinator.\r\n\r\n### Active Cable Pronation\r\n\r\nVery high aggregate pronator demand.\r\n\r\nMinimal biceps rotational contribution.\r\n\r\n## Length-Tension Model\r\n\r\nBecause the aggregate includes antagonists, one scalar `E_LT` is inherently problematic.\r\n\r\nFor pronation:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_pronators ≈ f(forearm_supination)`\r\n\r\nwith pronator-teres correction for elbow angle.\r\n\r\nFor supination:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_supinator ≈ f(forearm_pronation)`.\r\n\r\nTherefore an aggregate `E_LT` should ideally be computed **after movement direction is known**.\r\n\r\nA generic value based only on current forearm angle can become internally contradictory.\r\n\r\n## Co-Contraction\r\n\r\nForearm orientation often needs to remain stable while other muscles generate large forces.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- heavy curls;\r\n- pull-ups;\r\n- rows;\r\n- carrying.\r\n\r\nPronators and supinators may co-contract to stiffen the rotational degree of freedom.\r\n\r\nThis means both opposing groups can experience tension despite:\r\n\r\n`rotation_velocity = 0`.\r\n\r\nFor ETU modelling, stabilization demand should be proportional to:\r\n\r\n- external rotational perturbation;\r\n- required grip stability;\r\n- load magnitude;\r\n\r\nnot simply assumed constant.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nDirect long-length hypertrophy evidence is insufficient.\r\n\r\nThe mechanically defensible interpretation is:\r\n\r\n- pronators lengthen toward supination;\r\n- supinator lengthens toward pronation;\r\n- torque capacity varies with starting rotation;\r\n- training through broad controlled rotation is reasonable;\r\n- no large SMH multiplier is currently justified.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **pronator teres anatomy and pronation function:** high; :contentReference[oaicite:11]{index=11}\r\n- **pronator quadratus as major pronator:** high; :contentReference[oaicite:12]{index=12}\r\n- **supinator as dedicated supinator:** high;\r\n- **biceps as major high-force supinator:** very high and directly supported biomechanically; :contentReference[oaicite:13]{index=13}\r\n- **forearm-angle dependence of rotational torque:** directly supported; :contentReference[oaicite:14]{index=14}\r\n- **pronator-teres elbow-angle dependence:** biomechanically supported; :contentReference[oaicite:15]{index=15}\r\n- **pronator/supinator-specific hypertrophy data:** very limited;\r\n- **stretch-mediated hypertrophy magnitude:** unestablished;\r\n- **exact exercise-specific force-sharing coefficients:** highly angle- and task-dependent.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **pronators_supinators = the dedicated forearm axial-rotation system, combining antagonistic pronator teres/quadratus and supinator functions, with total torque strongly dependent on forearm position and with high-force supination requiring substantial force sharing with biceps brachii.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	2.80	0.00	1.000	60.88	60.88
24	wrist_extensors	Musculus extensor carpi radialis longus et brevis, 	Upper	Forearms	445.00	421.40	11.00	Pennate	0.650	0.350	high	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\nThe **wrist extensors** aggregate primarily represents:\r\n\r\n- **extensor carpi radialis longus (ECRL)**;\r\n- **extensor carpi radialis brevis (ECRB)**;\r\n\r\nand, depending on model scope, may conceptually overlap partly with other wrist-extensor contributors such as:\r\n\r\n- extensor carpi ulnaris;\r\n- extensor digitorum.\r\n\r\nFor the current Agonez aggregate, the most defensible interpretation is:\r\n\r\n> **the principal wrist-extension force-producing system, especially the radial wrist extensors, rather than every posterior-forearm muscle.**\r\n\r\nThe dominant function is:\r\n\r\n- **wrist extension**.\r\n\r\nSecondary actions include:\r\n\r\n- radial deviation, especially from ECRL/ECRB;\r\n- wrist stabilization during gripping;\r\n- resistance to wrist flexion during pulling and curling;\r\n- stabilization during pressing and loaded carries.\r\n\r\nThe wrist extensors are particularly important during gripping because strong finger-flexor force tends to create a wrist-flexion moment.\r\n\r\nThe extensors counter this tendency and maintain the wrist near a mechanically favorable position for the finger flexors.\r\n\r\nTherefore high wrist-extensor force can occur while:\r\n\r\n`wrist_ROM ≈ 0`.\r\n\r\nFor Agonez, the most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **wrist extensors = a monoarticular forearm extensor/stabilizer system producing wrist-extension torque and resisting the flexion moment generated by grip and finger-flexor force, often under high isometric demand despite minimal visible wrist movement.**\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\n## Extensor Carpi Radialis Longus\r\n\r\nECRL originates proximally from the:\r\n\r\n- lateral supracondylar ridge of the humerus;\r\n- adjacent lateral intermuscular septal region.\r\n\r\nIts tendon travels across the dorsal wrist and inserts onto the base of the:\r\n\r\n- second metacarpal.\r\n\r\nIts line of pull creates:\r\n\r\n- wrist extension;\r\n- radial deviation.\r\n\r\n## Extensor Carpi Radialis Brevis\r\n\r\nECRB originates primarily from the:\r\n\r\n- lateral epicondylar region through the common extensor origin.\r\n\r\nIts tendon inserts onto the base of the:\r\n\r\n- third metacarpal.\r\n\r\nIt also produces:\r\n\r\n- wrist extension;\r\n- radial deviation.\r\n\r\nCompared with ECRL, ECRB is generally considered a particularly important pure wrist stabilizer/extensor during gripping.\r\n\r\n## Relationship With Extensor Carpi Ulnaris\r\n\r\nExtensor carpi ulnaris also extends the wrist but adds:\r\n\r\n- ulnar deviation.\r\n\r\nIf not represented separately in the database, some practical wrist-extension demand may be implicitly absorbed into the current aggregate.\r\n\r\nHowever, anatomically:\r\n\r\n### ECRL / ECRB\r\n\r\nBias:\r\n- extension + radial deviation.\r\n\r\n### ECU\r\n\r\nBias:\r\n- extension + ulnar deviation.\r\n\r\nThis becomes relevant if future modelling includes frontal-plane wrist torque.\r\n\r\n## Relationship With Finger Extensors\r\n\r\nExtensor digitorum and other digital extensors also cross the wrist and can contribute to wrist extension.\r\n\r\nHowever, their primary role includes:\r\n\r\n- finger extension.\r\n\r\nTherefore a pure `wrist_extensors` aggregate should not automatically be interpreted as containing the entire dorsal forearm extensor compartment.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nThe radial wrist extensor system is supplied through the **radial nerve and its branches**.\r\n\r\nBroadly:\r\n\r\n- ECRL receives innervation from the radial nerve proper;\r\n- ECRB commonly receives a branch associated with the deep radial/posterior interosseous pathway.\r\n\r\nRoot contributions are mainly associated with approximately:\r\n\r\n- C6;\r\n- C7.\r\n\r\nExact branching anatomy varies.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Wrist Extension\r\n\r\nThe defining function is **extension of the wrist**.\r\n\r\nThis moves the dorsum of the hand toward the posterior forearm.\r\n\r\nThe major wrist extensors include:\r\n\r\n- ECRL;\r\n- ECRB;\r\n- ECU.\r\n\r\nThe current aggregate emphasizes the radial pair.\r\n\r\n## Radial Deviation\r\n\r\nECRL and ECRB also contribute to:\r\n\r\n- radial deviation of the wrist.\r\n\r\nThis action is shared with:\r\n\r\n- flexor carpi radialis.\r\n\r\nThus radial deviation can occur through co-contraction of:\r\n\r\n- a wrist extensor;\r\n- a wrist flexor;\r\n\r\nwhile their sagittal-plane moments partially cancel.\r\n\r\n## Grip Stabilization\r\n\r\nThis is one of the most important functions for resistance training.\r\n\r\nStrong finger flexion tends to create a wrist-flexion moment.\r\n\r\nIf the wrist flexed freely during heavy gripping:\r\n\r\n- finger flexors could become mechanically shortened;\r\n- grip efficiency would deteriorate;\r\n- hand position would become unstable.\r\n\r\nThe wrist extensors therefore contract isometrically to hold the wrist in a favorable position.\r\n\r\nThis means:\r\n\r\n> **gripping exercises can create substantial wrist-extensor demand even when no wrist extension occurs.**\r\n\r\n## Anti-Flexion\r\n\r\nA useful way to model many gym tasks is:\r\n\r\n`external/internal wrist-flexion tendency`\r\n→ `wrist-extensor anti-flexion force`.\r\n\r\nThis occurs in:\r\n\r\n- curls;\r\n- rows;\r\n- pull-ups;\r\n- dead hangs;\r\n- carries;\r\n- deadlifts.\r\n\r\n## Dynamic Wrist Extension\r\n\r\nThe muscle group can also be trained dynamically with:\r\n\r\n- wrist extensions;\r\n- reverse wrist curls;\r\n- cable wrist extension.\r\n\r\nThese create the cleanest direct wrist-extensor hypertrophy stimulus.\r\n\r\n# Relationship With Finger Flexors\r\n\r\nThe finger flexors are among the strongest grip-producing muscles.\r\n\r\nTheir tendons cross the wrist on the palmar side.\r\n\r\nTherefore strong gripping tends to create wrist-flexion torque.\r\n\r\nThe wrist extensors counter this.\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`T_wrist_net =\r\n    T_extensors\r\n  - T_wrist_flexors\r\n  - T_finger_flexor_wrist_component`.\r\n\r\nDuring a heavy dead hang:\r\n\r\n`net wrist motion ≈ 0`\r\n\r\nbut both:\r\n\r\n- grip/finger-flexor force;\r\n- wrist-extensor force\r\n\r\ncan be high.\r\n\r\nThis is a classic co-contraction system.\r\n\r\n# Relationship With Wrist Flexors\r\n\r\nWrist flexors and extensors can act as:\r\n\r\n- antagonists during dynamic motion;\r\n- synergistic stabilizers during gripping.\r\n\r\nThus antagonist labels do not imply mutual exclusion.\r\n\r\nDuring maximal grip, simultaneous flexor/extensor activation can increase:\r\n\r\n- wrist stiffness;\r\n- force-transfer efficiency.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nDirect longitudinal evidence specifically examining **wrist-extensor stretch-mediated hypertrophy** is sparse.\r\n\r\nThe basic length relation is nevertheless straightforward.\r\n\r\n## Wrist Flexion Lengthens the Extensors\r\n\r\nBecause these muscles extend the wrist:\r\n\r\n- wrist flexion lengthens them;\r\n- wrist extension shortens them.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_wrist_extensors ≈ f(wrist_flexion)`.\r\n\r\n## Ulnar Deviation Can Lengthen Radial Extensors\r\n\r\nBecause ECRL/ECRB produce radial deviation, ulnar deviation can increase their length.\r\n\r\nThus a more complete model is:\r\n\r\n`L_ECR = f(wrist_flexion_extension,\r\n           radial_ulnar_deviation)`.\r\n\r\nFor KISS modelling, sagittal wrist angle should dominate.\r\n\r\n## Reverse Wrist Curl\r\n\r\nA reverse wrist curl beginning from:\r\n\r\n- controlled wrist flexion\r\n\r\ncan load the extensors at greater length.\r\n\r\nThis is mechanically preferable to performing only:\r\n\r\n- short-range top partials.\r\n\r\n## Passive Stretch vs Active Loading\r\n\r\nSimply allowing the wrist to hang into flexion is not equivalent to hypertrophic loading.\r\n\r\nThe relevant stimulus requires:\r\n\r\n- meaningful extensor force;\r\n- sufficient duration;\r\n- progressive overload.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nFor Agonez:\r\n\r\n- flexed wrist = long extensors;\r\n- active extension from flexion provides long-length loading;\r\n- direct extensor-specific SMH evidence is limited.\r\n\r\nTherefore a conservative `smh_factor` is appropriate.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Reverse Wrist Curl\r\n\r\nThis is the most direct conventional exercise for the wrist extensors.\r\n\r\nExecution:\r\n\r\n1. forearm supported;\r\n2. wrist begins in controlled flexion;\r\n3. hand extends against resistance;\r\n4. eccentric return remains controlled.\r\n\r\nThe main target is:\r\n\r\n- wrist-extension torque.\r\n\r\n## Dumbbell Wrist Extension\r\n\r\nUnilateral dumbbell work allows:\r\n\r\n- individual wrist tracking;\r\n- correction of asymmetry;\r\n- comfortable ROM.\r\n\r\nLoad requirements are usually modest.\r\n\r\n## Cable Wrist Extension\r\n\r\nCable resistance can provide a more consistent external torque depending on setup.\r\n\r\nThis can be advantageous for:\r\n\r\n- controlled ROM;\r\n- progressive overload;\r\n- stable resistance.\r\n\r\n## Barbell Reverse Wrist Curl\r\n\r\nA barbell permits bilateral loading.\r\n\r\nHowever, the fixed hand position may be less comfortable for some users.\r\n\r\nWrist and elbow positioning should remain stable.\r\n\r\n## Reverse Curl\r\n\r\nA reverse curl primarily trains elbow flexors:\r\n\r\n- brachialis;\r\n- brachioradialis;\r\n- biceps to a lesser degree.\r\n\r\nThe wrist extensors work mostly as:\r\n\r\n- stabilizers.\r\n\r\nThey should not receive the same demand as in a direct wrist-extension movement.\r\n\r\n## Barbell Curl\r\n\r\nDuring curls, the wrist extensors may contract isometrically to prevent the wrist from collapsing into flexion.\r\n\r\nDemand depends strongly on technique.\r\n\r\n### Neutral Wrist\r\n\r\nMeaningful stabilization demand.\r\n\r\n### Wrist Flexed Under Load\r\n\r\nExtensor stabilization may be reduced while wrist flexors/tendon structures experience different loading.\r\n\r\n### Excessive Wrist Extension\r\n\r\nCan increase extensor shortening and joint stress without adding useful elbow-flexor stimulus.\r\n\r\n## Hammer Curl\r\n\r\nAgain, wrist extensors primarily stabilize wrist position.\r\n\r\nThe main movers are:\r\n\r\n- brachialis;\r\n- brachioradialis;\r\n- biceps.\r\n\r\n## Pull-Up / Pulldown\r\n\r\nHeavy gripping requires wrist stabilization.\r\n\r\nThe wrist extensors should receive:\r\n\r\n- low-to-moderate isometric demand;\r\n\r\ndepending on:\r\n\r\n- grip strength requirement;\r\n- handle diameter;\r\n- straps;\r\n- wrist position.\r\n\r\n## Rows\r\n\r\nRows similarly create isometric wrist-extensor demand through gripping.\r\n\r\nThe demand is usually much lower than that of:\r\n\r\n- wrist flexors/finger flexors;\r\n\r\nbut it is non-zero.\r\n\r\n## Dead Hang\r\n\r\nA dead hang can create substantial grip demand.\r\n\r\nFinger flexors generate the dominant gripping force.\r\n\r\nWrist extensors stabilize against wrist flexion.\r\n\r\nTherefore Agonez should assign:\r\n\r\n- high `wrist_flexors`;\r\n- low-to-moderate `wrist_extensors`;\r\n\r\nrather than zero extensor demand.\r\n\r\n## Straps\r\n\r\nLifting straps reduce the required gripping force.\r\n\r\nThis should reduce both:\r\n\r\n- finger/wrist-flexor demand;\r\n- secondary wrist-extensor stabilization demand.\r\n\r\nThis is mechanically relevant for exercise vectors.\r\n\r\n## Pressing\r\n\r\nDuring bench press or overhead press, wrist extensors may stabilize the wrist depending on bar position.\r\n\r\nIf the load is stacked effectively over the forearm:\r\n\r\n- net wrist torque is limited.\r\n\r\nIf the wrist is allowed to extend substantially under the bar:\r\n\r\n- extensor force may not necessarily rise, because the external load can create an extension moment and passive structures may bear more load.\r\n\r\nTherefore generic pressing should receive only small wrist-extensor demand unless the wrist configuration specifically requires active stabilization.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Direct Training Is Optional but Useful\r\n\r\nMost lifters receive substantial isometric forearm-extensor exposure through gripping.\r\n\r\nHowever, direct dynamic wrist-extension work can still be useful for:\r\n\r\n- balanced forearm hypertrophy;\r\n- wrist-extension strength;\r\n- resilience to repetitive grip training.\r\n\r\n## Weekly Volume\r\n\r\nIf direct wrist-extensor development is a goal, a practical starting point is:\r\n\r\n- **4-8 direct sets per week**.\r\n\r\nLower amounts may be enough when pulling/grip volume is already high.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nTwo to four weekly exposures are practical.\r\n\r\nForearm musculature can tolerate relatively frequent work, but local tendon irritation can develop if volume rises too quickly.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\nDirect wrist-extension work is practical in:\r\n\r\n- 10-20 reps;\r\n- 15-30 reps;\r\n- 20-40 reps with lighter loads.\r\n\r\nThere is no requirement for exclusively high repetitions, but small joint ROM and modest loads make medium/high rep work convenient.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nDirect stable wrist-extension exercises can be trained near failure:\r\n\r\n- approximately 0-2 RIR.\r\n\r\nTechnical failure occurs when:\r\n\r\n- wrist-extension ROM collapses;\r\n- finger extension substitutes excessively;\r\n- elbow/forearm movement takes over.\r\n\r\n## Tendon Load Management\r\n\r\nThe common extensor origin at the lateral elbow can accumulate substantial stress from:\r\n\r\n- gripping;\r\n- wrist extension;\r\n- repeated pulling.\r\n\r\nTherefore direct wrist-extensor volume should account for total forearm workload.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## Strong Grip Requires Wrist Extensors\r\n\r\nGrip is not purely a flexor task.\r\n\r\nFinger flexors produce a wrist-flexion tendency.\r\n\r\nWrist extensors counter this tendency and maintain a wrist position that allows efficient force production.\r\n\r\nThus the extensors are mechanically important even when the hand appears static.\r\n\r\n## ECRB Is Clinically Important at the Lateral Elbow\r\n\r\nECRB is closely associated with the common extensor tendon region implicated in lateral epicondylalgia.\r\n\r\nThis reflects the high repetitive load placed on the wrist-extensor system during:\r\n\r\n- gripping;\r\n- racquet sports;\r\n- tool use.\r\n\r\nThis does not mean direct wrist-extension training is inherently harmful; rather, cumulative tendon load matters.\r\n\r\n## Forearm Extensors Are Often Underestimated Visually\r\n\r\nThe posterior forearm contributes substantially to overall forearm thickness.\r\n\r\nA program containing only:\r\n\r\n- curls;\r\n- wrist flexion;\r\n- grip work\r\n\r\ncan underemphasize direct extensor hypertrophy.\r\n\r\n## Neutral Wrist Is a Force-Transfer Strategy\r\n\r\nHolding a relatively neutral/slightly extended wrist during gripping is not arbitrary.\r\n\r\nIt improves the operating configuration of the finger flexors and provides a stable force-transmission base.\r\n\r\nThe wrist extensors are central to maintaining this position.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\n`wrist_extensors` should receive demand from:\r\n\r\n1. **dynamic wrist-extension torque**;\r\n2. anti-flexion stabilization during grip;\r\n3. secondary radial-deviation torque if the aggregate is interpreted mainly as ECRL/ECRB.\r\n\r\nThe second component is especially important in compound pulling exercises.\r\n\r\n## Length-Tension Model\r\n\r\nA useful first-order representation is:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_wrist_extensors =\r\n    f(wrist_flexion_extension)`.\r\n\r\n### Long\r\n\r\n- wrist flexed.\r\n\r\n### Mid\r\n\r\n- neutral/slightly extended.\r\n\r\n### Short\r\n\r\n- strongly extended.\r\n\r\nA second-order modifier can include:\r\n\r\n- ulnar deviation → longer radial extensors;\r\n- radial deviation → shorter radial extensors.\r\n\r\n## Force Sharing\r\n\r\nDynamic wrist extension can be represented as:\r\n\r\n`T_wrist_extension =\r\n    ECRL\r\n  + ECRB\r\n  + ECU\r\n  + digital_extensor_contributors`.\r\n\r\nIf the current aggregate contains mainly ECRL/ECRB, it should receive a large but not literally 100% share.\r\n\r\n## Grip-Stabilization Model\r\n\r\nFor pulling exercises, a useful abstraction is:\r\n\r\n`wrist_extensor_demand\r\n    ∝ finger_flexor_force\r\n    × wrist_flexion_moment_tendency\r\n    × stabilization_requirement`.\r\n\r\nThis is preferable to using wrist ROM.\r\n\r\nA heavy isometric grip can create meaningful extensor force with:\r\n\r\n`ROM = 0`.\r\n\r\n## Dead Hang Allocation\r\n\r\n### Wrist Flexors / Finger Flexor Aggregate\r\n\r\nVery high.\r\n\r\n### Wrist Extensors\r\n\r\nLow-to-moderate stabilization.\r\n\r\nThe exact extensor demand depends on:\r\n\r\n- wrist posture;\r\n- bar diameter;\r\n- grip strategy.\r\n\r\n## Weighted Pull-Up Allocation\r\n\r\nSimilar logic:\r\n\r\n- wrist flexors high;\r\n- wrist extensors low-to-moderate;\r\n- straps reduce both.\r\n\r\n## Wide-Grip Cable Row\r\n\r\nAgain:\r\n\r\n- substantial grip demand;\r\n- moderate secondary extensor stabilization.\r\n\r\nDo not give wrist extensors the same absolute force as wrist flexors.\r\n\r\n## Standing Barbell Curl\r\n\r\nLow-to-moderate wrist-extensor stabilization if wrist remains neutral.\r\n\r\nThe main direct load belongs to:\r\n\r\n- elbow flexors.\r\n\r\n## Preacher Hammer Curl\r\n\r\nModerate stabilization can occur, especially because the forearm/wrist must remain rigid against the dumbbell moment.\r\n\r\nStill, wrist-extensor ETU should remain clearly below brachialis/brachioradialis demand.\r\n\r\n## Seated Palms-Down Wrist Extension\r\n\r\nVery high:\r\n- `wrist_extensors`.\r\n\r\nMinimal:\r\n- elbow movers if the forearm is well supported.\r\n\r\nThis is the cleanest direct exercise vector.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Wrist Flexors\r\n\r\nThe two groups should not be treated as simple exclusive antagonists.\r\n\r\nDuring grip:\r\n\r\n`wrist_flexors ↑`\r\nand\r\n`wrist_extensors ↑`\r\n\r\ncan occur simultaneously.\r\n\r\nThis is co-contraction for stabilization.\r\n\r\nTherefore your ETU model needs to permit:\r\n\r\n`agonist demand > 0`\r\nand\r\n`antagonist demand > 0`\r\n\r\nat the same joint.\r\n\r\n## E_MA Model\r\n\r\nWrist-extensor moment arms vary with:\r\n\r\n- wrist angle;\r\n- specific tendon;\r\n- deviation angle.\r\n\r\nFor KISS modelling, a common moderate angle-dependent extension leverage curve is sufficient.\r\n\r\nFine differences between ECRL/ECRB/ECU are unnecessary unless the aggregate is split later.\r\n\r\n## FCSA Interpretation\r\n\r\nIf the current row represents ECRL/ECRB primarily, its FCSA should be interpreted as:\r\n\r\n- partial wrist-extension capacity;\r\n- substantial radial-extension capacity;\r\n\r\nnot as the entire posterior forearm extensor compartment.\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`F_max_extensors ≈ σ × FCSA_aggregate`\r\n\r\nbut:\r\n\r\n`T_wrist_ext =\r\n    Σ(F_i × r_i)`.\r\n\r\nThe aggregate therefore remains an approximation.\r\n\r\n## Aggregate Scope Caveat\r\n\r\nYour current name:\r\n\r\n`Musculus extensor carpi radialis longus et brevis`\r\n\r\nis narrower than the slug:\r\n\r\n`wrist_extensors`.\r\n\r\nThis is worth keeping explicit.\r\n\r\nIf the DB row truly models only:\r\n\r\n- ECRL;\r\n- ECRB,\r\n\r\nthen:\r\n\r\n- wrist extension should be high;\r\n- radial deviation should be meaningful;\r\n- ECU force should conceptually remain outside the row.\r\n\r\nIf instead the slug is intended to represent all wrist extensors, the anatomical name should eventually include:\r\n\r\n- extensor carpi ulnaris;\r\n- potentially appropriate digital extensor contributions.\r\n\r\nFor the current model, treating it as a **radial wrist-extensor aggregate** is the cleaner interpretation.\r\n\r\n## ETU Interpretation\r\n\r\nETU can arise through:\r\n\r\n### Concentric\r\n\r\nActive wrist extension.\r\n\r\n### Eccentric\r\n\r\nControlled wrist flexion under load.\r\n\r\n### Isometric\r\n\r\nNeutral-wrist stabilization during gripping.\r\n\r\nThe isometric pathway is highly relevant in the Agonez exercise catalogue.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nThe main long-length variable is:\r\n\r\n`wrist_flexion`.\r\n\r\nDirect wrist-extensor SMH evidence is weak.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`SMH_effective_extensors =\r\n    base_smh_factor\r\n    × E_LT(wrist_angle)\r\n    × active_force`.\r\n\r\nDo not give generic grip exercises a long-length bonus unless the wrist is actually flexed under meaningful active extensor force.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **ECRL/ECRB anatomy and dorsal metacarpal insertions:** high;\r\n- **radial-nerve innervation:** high;\r\n- **wrist-extension function:** very high;\r\n- **radial-deviation contribution:** high;\r\n- **isometric stabilization during gripping:** very high biomechanical confidence;\r\n- **co-contraction with wrist/finger flexors:** high;\r\n- **wrist-flexion-mediated lengthening:** very high;\r\n- **precise exercise-specific extensor force during compound lifting:** poorly quantified;\r\n- **direct hypertrophy evidence:** limited;\r\n- **stretch-mediated hypertrophy magnitude:** unestablished;\r\n- **current slug/name scope alignment:** imperfect and should be treated explicitly.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **wrist extensors = primarily the radial wrist-extensor system producing wrist extension and radial-deviation torque, but in resistance training their most important hidden role is often isometric anti-flexion stabilization against the wrist-flexion moment generated by powerful gripping.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/WristExtensors}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	7.78	7.72	0.991	54.16	53.67
23	wrist_flexors	Musculus flexor carpi ulnaris&radialis, flexor digitorum superficialis	Upper	Forearms	465.00	440.34	30.00	Fusiform/Pennate	0.550	0.450	high	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\nThe **wrist flexors** aggregate represents a functional group centered on:\r\n\r\n- **flexor carpi radialis (FCR)**;\r\n- **flexor carpi ulnaris (FCU)**;\r\n- **flexor digitorum superficialis (FDS)**.\r\n\r\nThis aggregate therefore combines two related but mechanically distinct functions:\r\n\r\n1. **wrist flexion / wrist stabilization**;\r\n2. **finger flexion / gripping**.\r\n\r\nThe naming is important.\r\n\r\n`FCR` and `FCU` are primarily wrist muscles.\r\n\r\n`FDS` is primarily a finger flexor that also crosses the wrist and therefore contributes to:\r\n\r\n- wrist-flexion torque;\r\n- gripping-related wrist mechanics.\r\n\r\nBecause the current Agonez row includes FDS, it should not be interpreted as a pure `wrist_flexion` actuator.\r\n\r\nFor resistance-training modelling, its most important roles are:\r\n\r\n- **wrist flexion**;\r\n- substantial contribution to **grip force** through FDS;\r\n- isometric wrist stabilization during pulling and carrying;\r\n- co-contraction with `wrist_extensors`;\r\n- secondary radial/ulnar deviation depending on the constituent muscle.\r\n\r\nThis aggregate will therefore often receive substantial demand during exercises with:\r\n\r\n`wrist_ROM ≈ 0`\r\n\r\nbecause gripping can generate large muscle force without visible wrist movement.\r\n\r\nFor Agonez, the most useful abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **wrist_flexors = a forearm flexor/grip aggregate combining direct wrist-flexion capacity from FCR/FCU with substantial finger-flexion force from FDS, making it a major contributor to both dynamic wrist flexion and high-force isometric gripping.**\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\n## Flexor Carpi Radialis\r\n\r\nFCR arises from the medial epicondylar region of the humerus through the common flexor origin.\r\n\r\nIts tendon travels across the palmar wrist and inserts primarily onto the bases of the:\r\n\r\n- second metacarpal;\r\n- with contribution around the third metacarpal depending on anatomical description.\r\n\r\nIts major actions are:\r\n\r\n- wrist flexion;\r\n- radial deviation.\r\n\r\n## Flexor Carpi Ulnaris\r\n\r\nFCU has humeral and ulnar proximal attachments.\r\n\r\nIt inserts primarily onto the:\r\n\r\n- pisiform;\r\n\r\nwith force transmitted through ligamentous connections toward the:\r\n\r\n- hook of hamate;\r\n- base of the fifth metacarpal.\r\n\r\nIts major actions are:\r\n\r\n- wrist flexion;\r\n- ulnar deviation.\r\n\r\nFCU is mechanically important because it produces wrist flexion without crossing the carpal tunnel.\r\n\r\n## Flexor Digitorum Superficialis\r\n\r\nFDS is a much larger multi-tendon finger-flexor system.\r\n\r\nIt arises through:\r\n\r\n- humeroulnar;\r\n- radial\r\n\r\ncomponents and divides distally into tendons to digits:\r\n\r\n- 2;\r\n- 3;\r\n- 4;\r\n- 5.\r\n\r\nEach tendon inserts onto the middle phalanx after splitting around the deeper flexor digitorum profundus tendon.\r\n\r\nIts primary action is:\r\n\r\n- flexion of the proximal interphalangeal joints.\r\n\r\nIt also contributes to:\r\n\r\n- metacarpophalangeal flexion;\r\n- wrist flexion;\r\n- grip force.\r\n\r\nThus FDS makes the current aggregate much more relevant to pulling exercises than a pure FCR+FCU aggregate would be.\r\n\r\n## Aggregate Heterogeneity\r\n\r\nThe aggregate contains muscles with different distal targets.\r\n\r\n### FCR\r\n\r\nActs primarily on:\r\n- wrist.\r\n\r\nBias:\r\n- flexion + radial deviation.\r\n\r\n### FCU\r\n\r\nActs primarily on:\r\n- wrist.\r\n\r\nBias:\r\n- flexion + ulnar deviation.\r\n\r\n### FDS\r\n\r\nActs strongly on:\r\n- fingers.\r\n\r\nSecondary:\r\n- wrist flexion.\r\n\r\nTherefore one aggregate force vector cannot perfectly represent all three simultaneously.\r\n\r\nThis should be treated as a deliberate modelling approximation.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nThe aggregate receives mixed innervation.\r\n\r\n## FCR\r\n\r\nPrimarily:\r\n- median nerve.\r\n\r\n## FDS\r\n\r\nPrimarily:\r\n- median nerve.\r\n\r\n## FCU\r\n\r\nPrimarily:\r\n- ulnar nerve.\r\n\r\nThis is another indication that the current row is a functional aggregate rather than a single neural compartment.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Wrist Flexion\r\n\r\nFCR and FCU are major wrist flexors.\r\n\r\nFDS can also contribute because its tendons cross the wrist on the palmar side.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n`T_wrist_flexion =\r\n    FCR\r\n  + FCU\r\n  + FDS_component\r\n  + other digital/wrist flexors`.\r\n\r\nThe current aggregate captures a large portion, but not all, of total wrist-flexion capacity.\r\n\r\nOther contributors not explicitly represented here may include:\r\n\r\n- palmaris longus;\r\n- flexor digitorum profundus;\r\n- flexor pollicis longus.\r\n\r\n## Grip / Finger Flexion\r\n\r\nBecause FDS is included, the aggregate should also receive high demand during **gripping**.\r\n\r\nFDS flexes the fingers around:\r\n\r\n- barbells;\r\n- pull-up bars;\r\n- dumbbells;\r\n- cable handles.\r\n\r\nThis makes its resistance-training profile very different from a pure wrist-flexor aggregate.\r\n\r\nDuring:\r\n\r\n- pull-ups;\r\n- rows;\r\n- dead hangs;\r\n- carries;\r\n\r\nthe aggregate may experience high force despite almost no wrist flexion.\r\n\r\n## Radial Deviation\r\n\r\nFCR contributes to:\r\n\r\n- radial deviation.\r\n\r\nThis action can be reinforced by:\r\n\r\n- extensor carpi radialis longus;\r\n- extensor carpi radialis brevis.\r\n\r\nThus radial deviation may involve co-contraction between a wrist flexor and wrist extensors while flexion/extension moments partly cancel.\r\n\r\n## Ulnar Deviation\r\n\r\nFCU contributes to:\r\n\r\n- ulnar deviation.\r\n\r\nIt works synergistically with:\r\n\r\n- extensor carpi ulnaris.\r\n\r\nAgain, two muscles with opposite sagittal-plane actions can cooperate in the frontal plane.\r\n\r\n## Wrist Stabilization During Grip\r\n\r\nStrong finger flexors create a wrist-flexion moment.\r\n\r\nIf left unopposed, the wrist would collapse into flexion as grip force rises.\r\n\r\nTherefore the system requires simultaneous activity from:\r\n\r\n- finger/wrist flexors;\r\n- wrist extensors.\r\n\r\nThe flexors generate grip and part of wrist-flexion torque.\r\n\r\nThe extensors counterbalance the wrist-flexion component.\r\n\r\nThus a heavy grip produces:\r\n\r\n`flexor force ↑`\r\nand\r\n`extensor force ↑`\r\n\r\nsimultaneously.\r\n\r\n## Isometric Function\r\n\r\nIn many gym exercises, the aggregate acts primarily isometrically.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- weighted pull-up;\r\n- dead hang;\r\n- dumbbell row;\r\n- barbell row;\r\n- farmer carry.\r\n\r\nThis is a major Agonez case where:\r\n\r\n`visible_ROM ≈ 0`\r\n\r\nbut:\r\n\r\n`muscle_force = high`.\r\n\r\n# Relationship With Wrist Extensors\r\n\r\nThis relationship is central.\r\n\r\nThe flexor and extensor systems can function as:\r\n\r\n- antagonists during dynamic wrist movement;\r\n- synergistic stabilizers during gripping.\r\n\r\nDuring heavy gripping:\r\n\r\n`finger flexor force`\r\ncreates a wrist-flexion tendency.\r\n\r\nThe wrist extensors counter this.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n> antagonist co-contraction is part of normal force production, not wasted activation.\r\n\r\nThis should be preserved by the ETU model.\r\n\r\n# Relationship With Deep Finger Flexors\r\n\r\nFDS is not the only major grip muscle.\r\n\r\n`flexor digitorum profundus` is a major contributor to:\r\n\r\n- distal interphalangeal flexion;\r\n- powerful gripping.\r\n\r\nIf FDP is not represented separately in Agonez, then the present `wrist_flexors` row may need to absorb some of the unmodelled grip demand.\r\n\r\nThis is an important aggregate-scope caveat.\r\n\r\nFor ordinary exercise vectors:\r\n\r\n> the current row is best interpreted as a **wrist-flexor + major finger-flexor proxy**, not literal complete grip anatomy.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nDirect longitudinal evidence specifically examining this aggregate is limited.\r\n\r\nIts constituent muscles also differ in their length states.\r\n\r\n## Wrist Extension Lengthens FCR and FCU\r\n\r\nBecause FCR/FCU flex the wrist:\r\n\r\n- wrist extension lengthens them;\r\n- wrist flexion shortens them.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_FCR_FCU ≈ f(wrist_extension)`.\r\n\r\n## Finger Extension Lengthens FDS\r\n\r\nFDS also crosses the fingers.\r\n\r\nIts total length depends on:\r\n\r\n- wrist angle;\r\n- MCP joint position;\r\n- PIP joint position.\r\n\r\nIt is lengthened by combinations of:\r\n\r\n- wrist extension;\r\n- finger extension.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_FDS =\r\n    f(wrist_angle,\r\n      MCP_angle,\r\n      PIP_angle)`.\r\n\r\nThis is much more complex than a pure wrist-muscle model.\r\n\r\n## Tenodesis Effect\r\n\r\nBecause the long finger-flexor tendons cross the wrist, wrist position affects finger posture and finger-flexor length.\r\n\r\nWhen the wrist extends:\r\n\r\n- finger flexors are lengthened;\r\n- passive finger flexion tendency can increase.\r\n\r\nWhen the wrist flexes:\r\n\r\n- long finger flexors shorten;\r\n- fingers tend to extend more easily.\r\n\r\nThis is part of the classic **tenodesis effect**.\r\n\r\n## Direct Wrist Curl\r\n\r\nA wrist curl beginning in wrist extension loads FCR/FCU at longer muscle lengths.\r\n\r\nHowever, FDS length also depends on finger configuration.\r\n\r\nIf the fingers actively hold a dumbbell or bar, grip force can become substantial simultaneously.\r\n\r\n## Long-Length Grip\r\n\r\nA strong grip with the wrist moderately extended can place the finger flexors at a relatively favorable/lengthened state while maintaining high force.\r\n\r\nExtreme wrist extension is unnecessary and may increase tendon stress.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nThe aggregate makes a single SMH value inherently approximate.\r\n\r\nFor FCR/FCU:\r\n\r\n`long ≈ wrist extension`.\r\n\r\nFor FDS:\r\n\r\n`long ≈ wrist extension + finger extension`.\r\n\r\nTherefore the current `smh_factor` should remain conservative unless the model later separates:\r\n\r\n- wrist flexors;\r\n- finger flexors.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Seated Wrist Curl\r\n\r\nThis is the most direct conventional wrist-flexion exercise.\r\n\r\nWith the forearms supported:\r\n\r\n1. wrist begins in controlled extension;\r\n2. wrist flexes against resistance;\r\n3. eccentric return is controlled.\r\n\r\nPrimary direct demand:\r\n- FCR;\r\n- FCU.\r\n\r\nFDS may contribute because the implement must be held.\r\n\r\n## Dumbbell Wrist Curl\r\n\r\nUnilateral dumbbell wrist curls allow natural deviation and individual wrist tracking.\r\n\r\nThey can produce high local flexor demand with low systemic fatigue.\r\n\r\n## Cable Wrist Flexion\r\n\r\nCable setups can provide:\r\n\r\n- stable resistance;\r\n- adjustable line of pull;\r\n- easy unilateral loading.\r\n\r\nDepending on pulley direction, the resistance profile can be tuned to emphasize different portions of ROM.\r\n\r\n## Finger Curl / Barbell Finger Roll\r\n\r\nLetting a bar roll toward the fingers and then actively closing the hand produces more explicit finger-flexor loading.\r\n\r\nThis substantially increases the role of:\r\n\r\n- FDS;\r\n- FDP.\r\n\r\nIf such an exercise enters Agonez, the current aggregate should receive very high demand.\r\n\r\n## Dead Hang\r\n\r\nDead hangs are among the strongest grip-specific exercises in the existing catalogue.\r\n\r\nThe bar must be held through sustained finger flexion.\r\n\r\nTherefore the aggregate should receive:\r\n\r\n- **very high grip demand**.\r\n\r\nNo large wrist ROM is necessary.\r\n\r\nThe primary mechanism is:\r\n\r\n- sustained finger-flexor force.\r\n\r\n## Weighted Pull-Up\r\n\r\nWeighted pull-ups create very high grip demand.\r\n\r\nThe aggregate should receive substantial force, with absolute demand increasing with:\r\n\r\n- bodyweight;\r\n- added load;\r\n- bar diameter;\r\n- set duration.\r\n\r\nStraps can reduce this substantially.\r\n\r\n## Pronated Pull-Up\r\n\r\nGrip orientation changes forearm rotation but does not remove the requirement for finger flexion.\r\n\r\nTherefore wrist/finger-flexor demand remains high.\r\n\r\n## Neutral-Grip Pull-Up\r\n\r\nLikewise, grip remains a major contributor.\r\n\r\nHandle geometry can affect:\r\n\r\n- comfort;\r\n- attainable force;\r\n- forearm position.\r\n\r\nBut the current aggregate should remain highly loaded.\r\n\r\n## Lat Pulldown\r\n\r\nPulldowns create substantial but usually lower absolute grip demand than weighted pull-ups at equivalent effort because:\r\n\r\n- external load may be lower;\r\n- straps/handles may improve grip mechanics.\r\n\r\nStill, the aggregate should receive meaningful demand.\r\n\r\n## Rows\r\n\r\nRows also create sustained gripping.\r\n\r\n### Chest-Supported Dumbbell Row\r\n\r\nHigh grip demand but low trunk-systemic contribution.\r\n\r\n### Pendlay Row\r\n\r\nHigh grip demand plus large systemic load.\r\n\r\nFor muscle-level grip ETU, both can strongly load the wrist/finger flexors even though whole-exercise fatigue differs substantially.\r\n\r\n## Deadlift\r\n\r\nIf later represented in the catalogue, deadlifts produce very high finger-flexor demand.\r\n\r\nUsing straps dramatically reduces this component.\r\n\r\nThis is a useful example where an equipment variable materially changes muscle vectors.\r\n\r\n## Hammer Curl\r\n\r\nDuring a hammer curl:\r\n\r\n- brachialis;\r\n- brachioradialis;\r\n- biceps\r\n\r\nproduce elbow torque.\r\n\r\nThe wrist/finger flexors primarily stabilize the handle.\r\n\r\nDemand is meaningful but secondary.\r\n\r\n## Standing Barbell Curl\r\n\r\nFinger-flexor grip demand is moderate.\r\n\r\nWrist flexors may also contribute if the lifter actively flexes the wrist, but excessive wrist curling changes the intended movement.\r\n\r\nA neutral wrist generally provides cleaner elbow-flexor loading.\r\n\r\n## Wrist Curl vs Grip Exercise\r\n\r\nThese should not receive the same vector.\r\n\r\n### Wrist Curl\r\n\r\nHigh:\r\n- direct wrist flexion.\r\n\r\nModerate:\r\n- gripping depending implement.\r\n\r\n### Dead Hang\r\n\r\nVery high:\r\n- finger flexion/grip.\r\n\r\nLow:\r\n- dynamic wrist flexion.\r\n\r\nThe current aggregate can score highly in both, but for different internal reasons.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Separate Grip Demand From Dynamic Wrist Flexion Conceptually\r\n\r\nEven if they remain one DB row, Agonez should conceptually distinguish:\r\n\r\n1. `wrist_flexion_component`;\r\n2. `finger_grip_component`.\r\n\r\nOtherwise exercises such as:\r\n\r\n- dead hang;\r\n- wrist curl\r\n\r\nwould appear mechanically identical despite very different joint actions.\r\n\r\n## Dedicated Direct Work\r\n\r\nFor general lifters, direct wrist-flexor hypertrophy work is optional because pulling already provides substantial grip exposure.\r\n\r\nDirect wrist curls may still be useful for:\r\n\r\n- forearm size;\r\n- wrist-flexion strength;\r\n- balancing training volume.\r\n\r\n## Weekly Volume\r\n\r\nIf direct forearm flexor hypertrophy is a priority:\r\n\r\n- approximately **4-8 direct wrist/finger-flexor sets per week**\r\n\r\nis a reasonable starting range.\r\n\r\nHeavy pulling provides additional indirect volume.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nTwo to four weekly exposures are practical.\r\n\r\nHowever, this aggregate is heavily exposed during:\r\n\r\n- pull training;\r\n- curls;\r\n- carries.\r\n\r\nDirect volume should therefore account for existing grip workload.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\nDirect wrist work is practical in:\r\n\r\n- 10-20;\r\n- 15-30;\r\n- 20-40 reps.\r\n\r\nFinger-curl work can use similar ranges.\r\n\r\nGrip holds can instead be prescribed by:\r\n\r\n- load;\r\n- duration.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nDirect wrist curls can be trained near local failure:\r\n\r\n- approximately 0-2 RIR.\r\n\r\nFor heavy grip exercises, technical failure occurs when:\r\n\r\n- fingers begin opening;\r\n- grip slips;\r\n- wrist position deteriorates.\r\n\r\n## Tendon Load\r\n\r\nHigh pulling volume plus direct wrist-flexor training can stress:\r\n\r\n- common flexor tendon;\r\n- finger-flexor tendons;\r\n- medial elbow structures.\r\n\r\nVolume progression should therefore be gradual.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## Grip Is Not a Single-Muscle Action\r\n\r\nClosing the hand requires multiple extrinsic and intrinsic muscles.\r\n\r\nThe current aggregate captures only part of this system.\r\n\r\nFDS is major, but powerful gripping also uses:\r\n\r\n- flexor digitorum profundus;\r\n- flexor pollicis longus;\r\n- intrinsic hand muscles.\r\n\r\nThus the aggregate is a useful proxy, not a complete anatomical grip model.\r\n\r\n## Wrist Position Changes Grip Strength\r\n\r\nGrip is usually strongest with the wrist held in slight extension rather than deep flexion.\r\n\r\nThis allows the long finger flexors to operate at a more favorable length.\r\n\r\nIf the wrist flexes strongly:\r\n\r\n- the finger flexors shorten across the wrist;\r\n- active insufficiency can reduce grip force.\r\n\r\nThis is why the wrist extensors are important during strong gripping.\r\n\r\n## FCR and FCU Can Cancel Their Deviation Components\r\n\r\nFCR produces:\r\n\r\n- flexion + radial deviation.\r\n\r\nFCU produces:\r\n\r\n- flexion + ulnar deviation.\r\n\r\nWhen they co-contract with balanced force:\r\n\r\n- radial/ulnar components can partly cancel;\r\n- wrist-flexion torque remains.\r\n\r\nThis is an elegant example of synergists with opposing secondary actions.\r\n\r\n## Wrist Flexors and Extensors Can Both Be Highly Active\r\n\r\nDuring a heavy grip:\r\n\r\n- flexors generate finger and wrist-flexion force;\r\n- extensors stabilize against wrist flexion.\r\n\r\nThus antagonistic coactivation can be essential for maximizing net task performance.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\nThe current `wrist_flexors` row should receive demand from at least two distinct mechanical channels:\r\n\r\n1. **wrist-flexion torque**;\r\n2. **finger-flexion / grip demand**.\r\n\r\nA simple model based only on wrist angle would underpredict many pulling exercises.\r\n\r\n## Aggregate Scope Caveat\r\n\r\nThe row is named:\r\n\r\n`Musculus flexor carpi ulnaris&radialis, flexor digitorum superficialis`\r\n\r\nwhich means it does **not** include the complete wrist/finger-flexor system.\r\n\r\nMost importantly, it omits:\r\n\r\n- flexor digitorum profundus;\r\n- flexor pollicis longus;\r\n- palmaris longus;\r\n- intrinsic hand flexors.\r\n\r\nTherefore the current row can be used in two ways.\r\n\r\n### Strict Anatomical Interpretation\r\n\r\nOnly represented muscles receive demand.\r\n\r\nUnmodelled grip force remains outside the system.\r\n\r\n### Functional Proxy Interpretation\r\n\r\nThis aggregate represents most practical forearm/grip flexor demand.\r\n\r\nFor the current Agonez architecture, the second interpretation is probably more useful unless additional finger-flexor rows are planned.\r\n\r\n## Wrist-Flexion Force Sharing\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`T_wrist_flexion =\r\n    FCR\r\n  + FCU\r\n  + FDS_wrist_component\r\n  + FDP_wrist_component\r\n  + smaller contributors`.\r\n\r\nBecause FCR and FCU have opposite deviation biases, balanced flexion can occur with relatively little net radial/ulnar deviation.\r\n\r\n## Grip Force Sharing\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`F_grip =\r\n    FDS\r\n  + FDP\r\n  + thumb_flexors\r\n  + intrinsic_hand_muscles`.\r\n\r\nThe current aggregate directly captures mainly:\r\n\r\n- FDS;\r\n- indirect wrist support from FCR/FCU.\r\n\r\nTherefore if no FDP row exists, the current exercise vector will necessarily act as a proxy.\r\n\r\n## Length-Tension Model\r\n\r\nA single scalar is imperfect.\r\n\r\n### For FCR / FCU\r\n\r\n`E_LT ≈ f(wrist_extension)`.\r\n\r\n### For FDS\r\n\r\n`E_LT ≈ f(wrist_extension,\r\n            finger_extension)`.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_wrist_flexors`\r\n\r\nshould ideally distinguish:\r\n\r\n- wrist-flexor component;\r\n- grip/finger-flexor component.\r\n\r\nFor KISS modelling, wrist angle can remain the dominant variable, with grip tasks using a broader neutral-length coefficient rather than pretending all constituent muscles share one precise length state.\r\n\r\n## Dead Hang Allocation\r\n\r\nVery high:\r\n- `wrist_flexors`.\r\n\r\nThe reason is primarily:\r\n- FDS/finger-flexor grip force.\r\n\r\nSecondary:\r\n- FCR/FCU wrist stabilization.\r\n\r\nAlso meaningful:\r\n- `wrist_extensors` through anti-flexion stabilization.\r\n\r\nThis justifies your existing high wrist-flexor demand in `dead_hang`.\r\n\r\n## Weighted Neutral-Grip Pull-Up\r\n\r\nHigh-to-very-high aggregate demand.\r\n\r\nGrip must support:\r\n\r\n- body mass;\r\n- added load.\r\n\r\nThe exact ETU depends on:\r\n\r\n- set duration;\r\n- hand size;\r\n- bar diameter;\r\n- straps.\r\n\r\n## Wide Pronated Pull-Up\r\n\r\nAgain high.\r\n\r\nGrip width changes shoulder mechanics much more than it changes the fundamental finger-flexor requirement.\r\n\r\n## Chest-Supported Dumbbell Row\r\n\r\nModerate-to-high wrist/finger-flexor demand.\r\n\r\nDumbbells often require substantial independent grip stabilization.\r\n\r\n## Pendlay Row\r\n\r\nHigh wrist/finger-flexor demand.\r\n\r\nAlso high systemic cost elsewhere.\r\n\r\nGrip demand should remain muscle-level independent from spinal/hip fatigue.\r\n\r\n## Standing Barbell Curl\r\n\r\nModerate wrist-flexor/grip demand.\r\n\r\nIf the lifter actively flexes the wrist, direct FCR/FCU demand rises, but that should be treated as execution-dependent rather than inherent to the exercise.\r\n\r\n## Seated Palms-Up Wrist Curl\r\n\r\nVery high:\r\n- FCR;\r\n- FCU.\r\n\r\nMeaningful:\r\n- FDS because the load must be held.\r\n\r\nThis is the cleanest direct dynamic exercise for the wrist-flexion portion of the aggregate.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Wrist Extensors\r\n\r\nA useful grip stabilization relationship is:\r\n\r\n`wrist_extensor_demand\r\n    ∝ finger_flexor_force\r\n    × wrist_flexion_tendency`.\r\n\r\nThus high `wrist_flexors` demand often predicts non-zero `wrist_extensors` demand.\r\n\r\nBut the absolute force allocation should remain asymmetric:\r\n\r\n`flexor/grip demand > extensor stabilization demand`\r\n\r\nin most heavy pulling tasks.\r\n\r\n## Straps Modifier\r\n\r\nStraps deserve a strong modifier.\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`grip_demand_with_straps << grip_demand_without_straps`.\r\n\r\nThus straps reduce:\r\n\r\n- FDS/FDP force;\r\n- secondary wrist-extensor stabilization.\r\n\r\nThey do not substantially change the prime-mover vectors of:\r\n\r\n- lats;\r\n- rows;\r\n- elbow flexors,\r\n\r\nexcept indirectly through fatigue/limitation.\r\n\r\n## Bar Diameter Modifier\r\n\r\nThicker handles alter:\r\n\r\n- finger joint angles;\r\n- maximal grip force;\r\n- forearm recruitment.\r\n\r\nFor KISS modelling, this can remain outside the first version.\r\n\r\nBut it explains why two exercises with identical external load can produce different grip demand.\r\n\r\n## FCSA Interpretation\r\n\r\nThis aggregate creates an important FCSA caveat.\r\n\r\n`FCSA_wrist_flexors`\r\n\r\ncannot be interpreted as one uniform tendon force because:\r\n\r\n- FCR acts on the second/third metacarpal region;\r\n- FCU acts through the pisiform/ulnar side;\r\n- FDS divides into multiple digital tendons.\r\n\r\nThus the aggregate's FCSA is best interpreted as:\r\n\r\n> **total contractile capacity of a related forearm flexor subsystem**\r\n\r\nrather than one muscle-tendon actuator.\r\n\r\nTo calculate wrist torque:\r\n\r\n`T_wrist =\r\n    Σ(F_i × r_i)`.\r\n\r\nTo calculate grip:\r\n\r\n`F_grip =\r\n    Σ(digital_tendon_forces × finger_leverage)`.\r\n\r\nThese are different outputs.\r\n\r\n## ETU Interpretation\r\n\r\nThe aggregate can accumulate ETU through:\r\n\r\n### Dynamic Wrist Flexion\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n- wrist curl.\r\n\r\n### Dynamic Finger Flexion\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n- finger curl.\r\n\r\n### Isometric Grip\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n- dead hang.\r\n\r\n### Eccentric Wrist Extension\r\n\r\nThe wrist extends under flexor tension.\r\n\r\n### Eccentric Finger Opening\r\n\r\nThe grip slowly yields under load.\r\n\r\nTherefore ETU should not depend solely on wrist ROM.\r\n\r\n## Grip-Duration Component\r\n\r\nUnlike many prime movers, time under tension can become especially relevant in grip tasks.\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n\r\n- 5-second heavy row set;\r\n- 40-second dead hang\r\n\r\ncan have very different forearm ETU even if peak force is similar.\r\n\r\nThus a useful grip-specific approximation may include:\r\n\r\n`ETU_grip ∝ force × contraction_duration × recruitment`.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nThe aggregate does not support one clean length-based rule.\r\n\r\nFor FCR/FCU:\r\n\r\n`wrist extension → longer`.\r\n\r\nFor FDS:\r\n\r\n`wrist extension + finger extension → longer`.\r\n\r\nTherefore direct wrist curls from extension can receive some long-length credit.\r\n\r\nStatic gripping around a bar should generally receive:\r\n\r\n- high active-force credit;\r\n- moderate/neutral `E_LT`;\r\n\r\nunless joint geometry clearly places the finger flexors at greater length.\r\n\r\nA conservative aggregate `smh_factor` is most defensible.\r\n\r\n## Future Split Candidate\r\n\r\nIf Agonez eventually needs greater precision, this aggregate is a good candidate for splitting into:\r\n\r\n- `wrist_flexors`:\r\n  - FCR;\r\n  - FCU;\r\n  - possibly palmaris longus;\r\n\r\nand\r\n\r\n- `finger_flexors`:\r\n  - FDS;\r\n  - FDP;\r\n  - possibly FPL.\r\n\r\nThat would separate:\r\n\r\n`wrist torque`\r\n\r\nfrom:\r\n\r\n`grip force`\r\n\r\nand make exercise vectors much cleaner.\r\n\r\nFor the current model, however, the combined aggregate is computationally practical because most pulling exercises load both systems simultaneously.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **FCR/FCU wrist-flexion function:** very high;\r\n- **FCR radial-deviation contribution:** high;\r\n- **FCU ulnar-deviation contribution:** high;\r\n- **FDS finger-flexion/grip role:** very high;\r\n- **FDS contribution to wrist flexion:** high;\r\n- **high flexor demand during gripping:** very high;\r\n- **co-contraction with wrist extensors during grip:** high;\r\n- **wrist-extension-mediated lengthening of wrist flexors:** very high;\r\n- **multi-joint length dependence of FDS:** very high;\r\n- **precise FCR/FCU/FDS force sharing during individual exercises:** poorly quantified;\r\n- **aggregate FCSA as one actuator:** poor interpretation;\r\n- **direct aggregate-specific hypertrophy evidence:** limited;\r\n- **stretch-mediated hypertrophy magnitude:** unestablished.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **wrist flexors = a heterogeneous forearm flexor/grip subsystem combining direct wrist-flexion torque from FCR and FCU with substantial finger-flexion force from FDS; in resistance training its largest hidden demand often comes from sustained gripping rather than visible wrist flexion, making force and contraction duration more informative than wrist ROM alone.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/WristFlexors}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	8.07	4.46	0.997	54.57	54.40
10	latissimus_dorsi	Musculus latissimus dorsi	Upper	Back	1260.00	1193.18	32.00	Parallel/Convergent	0.500	0.500	very_high	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\nThe **latissimus dorsi** is a large, broad, superficial muscle of the posterior trunk that connects the axial skeleton and pelvis to the humerus.\r\n\r\nIts primary mechanical roles at the glenohumeral joint are:\r\n\r\n- **shoulder extension**;\r\n- **shoulder adduction**;\r\n- **internal rotation**.\r\n\r\nBecause it originates broadly from the lower thoracic spine, thoracolumbar fascia, iliac crest and lower ribs, then converges onto the proximal humerus, it is mechanically capable of generating large humeral moments across a wide range of arm positions.\r\n\r\nFor resistance training, its most important identity is:\r\n\r\n> **a powerful humeral extensor and adductor whose contribution depends strongly on humeral elevation, elbow path, shoulder rotation and the external resistance vector.**\r\n\r\nThis makes latissimus dorsi highly relevant to:\r\n\r\n- pull-ups;\r\n- pulldowns;\r\n- rows;\r\n- straight-arm pulldowns;\r\n- certain pullovers;\r\n- climbing and swimming actions.\r\n\r\nIt should be distinguished from scapular retractors such as:\r\n\r\n- middle trapezius;\r\n- rhomboids.\r\n\r\nLatissimus dorsi moves the **humerus relative to the trunk**.\r\n\r\nIt does not directly retract the scapula because it does not insert on the scapular medial border.\r\n\r\nThis distinction is critical when modelling rows.\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\n## Origin\r\n\r\nLatissimus dorsi has an exceptionally broad proximal attachment.\r\n\r\nIts origins commonly include:\r\n\r\n- spinous processes of lower thoracic vertebrae;\r\n- thoracolumbar fascia;\r\n- lumbar and sacral fascial attachments;\r\n- posterior iliac crest;\r\n- lower ribs.\r\n\r\nSome individuals also have fascial connections with the inferior angle of the scapula.\r\n\r\nThe broad origin means different latissimus regions begin from substantially different positions.\r\n\r\nThis produces regional differences in:\r\n\r\n- fiber direction;\r\n- length change;\r\n- leverage;\r\n- recruitment.\r\n\r\n## Insertion\r\n\r\nFibers converge superolaterally toward a relatively narrow tendon inserting into the **floor of the intertubercular sulcus of the humerus**.\r\n\r\nThe tendon twists as fibers from different regions converge.\r\n\r\nThis arrangement allows a very broad sheet of muscle to transmit force to a relatively small humeral attachment.\r\n\r\nBecause the insertion lies anterior and medial relative to the humeral shaft, the muscle has leverage for:\r\n\r\n- extension;\r\n- adduction;\r\n- internal rotation.\r\n\r\n## Regional Architecture\r\n\r\nLatissimus dorsi should not be considered one uniform fan of equivalent fibers.\r\n\r\nUseful regional distinctions include:\r\n\r\n- superior/thoracic fibers;\r\n- middle fibers;\r\n- inferior/lumbopelvic fibers.\r\n\r\nThese regions differ in their lines of pull.\r\n\r\nSuperior fibers may contribute more strongly in some extension-oriented actions.\r\n\r\nInferior fibers can possess particularly strong adduction leverage when the humerus is elevated.\r\n\r\nFor Agonez, a single `latissimus_dorsi` aggregate is practical, but regional specialization should be remembered when interpreting exercise vectors.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nLatissimus dorsi is innervated by the **thoracodorsal nerve**, primarily from spinal roots:\r\n\r\n- C6;\r\n- C7;\r\n- C8.\r\n\r\nThe thoracodorsal nerve arises from the posterior cord of the brachial plexus.\r\n\r\nIt runs along the deep surface of the latissimus dorsi and supplies the muscle throughout its broad architecture.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Shoulder Extension\r\n\r\nLatissimus dorsi contributes strongly to **extension of the humerus**, especially when the arm begins in flexion.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- pulling the upper arm downward and backward;\r\n- rowing with a tucked elbow path;\r\n- straight-arm pulldown;\r\n- certain pullovers.\r\n\r\nIts extension contribution overlaps with:\r\n\r\n- posterior deltoid;\r\n- teres major;\r\n- long head of triceps.\r\n\r\nRelative force sharing changes with humeral elevation and abduction.\r\n\r\n## Shoulder Adduction\r\n\r\nLatissimus dorsi is also a powerful **shoulder adductor**.\r\n\r\nThis is particularly relevant during vertical pulling.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- pull-up;\r\n- pulldown;\r\n- climbing.\r\n\r\nThe muscle pulls the elevated humerus toward the trunk.\r\n\r\nThis action is one of the defining differences between vertical pulling and horizontal rowing.\r\n\r\n## Internal Rotation\r\n\r\nLatissimus dorsi contributes to **internal rotation of the humerus**.\r\n\r\nThis is a secondary but mechanically meaningful action.\r\n\r\nIt shares this function with:\r\n\r\n- pectoralis major;\r\n- subscapularis;\r\n- teres major;\r\n- anterior deltoid to some degree.\r\n\r\nInternal rotation should generally not be treated as a primary hypertrophy exercise category for lats because much larger forces can be generated through extension and adduction.\r\n\r\n## Closed-Chain Function\r\n\r\nWhen the humerus is fixed, latissimus dorsi can move the trunk relative to the arm.\r\n\r\nThis is relevant in:\r\n\r\n- climbing;\r\n- gymnastics;\r\n- pull-ups;\r\n- crutch use.\r\n\r\nIn a pull-up, the muscle is not simply pulling the humerus down.\r\n\r\nIt is helping move the torso upward relative to fixed hands.\r\n\r\nThe joint torque is the same conceptually; the reference frame changes.\r\n\r\n## Relationship With the Scapula\r\n\r\nLatissimus dorsi does not directly produce classic scapular retraction.\r\n\r\nHowever, because humeral movement interacts with scapulothoracic mechanics, lat force can indirectly influence scapular behavior during pulling.\r\n\r\nThis should not be confused with direct insertion-based scapular action.\r\n\r\nRhomboids and trapezius remain the primary direct scapular retractors.\r\n\r\n## Depression of the Shoulder Girdle\r\n\r\nIn fixed-arm tasks, latissimus dorsi can contribute to downward movement of the trunk relative to the upper limb and may assist with shoulder-girdle depression mechanically.\r\n\r\nHowever, direct scapular depression is still primarily associated with muscles acting directly on the scapula.\r\n\r\nFor exercise modelling, latissimus demand should remain tied mainly to glenohumeral extension and adduction.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nLatissimus dorsi has a strong mechanical rationale for long-length loading because humeral elevation lengthens the muscle.\r\n\r\nA particularly lengthened state occurs when the humerus is:\r\n\r\n- flexed overhead;\r\n- abducted/elevated;\r\n- externally positioned depending on fiber region.\r\n\r\nThis is why exercises beginning with the arm overhead can provide substantial lat stretch.\r\n\r\n## Overhead Position\r\n\r\nIn a pull-up or pulldown, the top position places the humerus in substantial elevation.\r\n\r\nThis generally lengthens the latissimus dorsi.\r\n\r\nAs the humerus moves downward into adduction and extension, the muscle shortens.\r\n\r\nTherefore vertical pulls naturally load the lat across a broad length range.\r\n\r\n## Straight-Arm Pulldown\r\n\r\nA straight-arm pulldown can begin with the humerus highly flexed.\r\n\r\nIf the cable provides substantial torque in that position, the lat can be loaded at relatively long lengths.\r\n\r\nThis makes the exercise mechanically attractive for long-length training.\r\n\r\nThe exact profile depends heavily on:\r\n\r\n- pulley height;\r\n- torso angle;\r\n- distance from the machine;\r\n- arm path.\r\n\r\n## Pullover-Type Movements\r\n\r\nCable or machine pullovers can also load the lat from overhead flexion toward extension.\r\n\r\nThese can provide:\r\n\r\n- high shoulder-extension torque;\r\n- minimal elbow-flexor limitation;\r\n- substantial long-length exposure.\r\n\r\nTheir effectiveness depends on whether the equipment maintains resistance near the overhead position.\r\n\r\n## Rows\r\n\r\nRows generally begin with less humeral elevation than vertical pulls.\r\n\r\nThey can still lengthen the lat through shoulder flexion/protraction at the start, but the magnitude depends on:\r\n\r\n- torso angle;\r\n- elbow path;\r\n- scapular position;\r\n- handle trajectory.\r\n\r\nA short-ROM row beginning near neutral shoulder position may provide relatively little long-length lat exposure.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Interpretation\r\n\r\nDirect latissimus-dorsi-specific longitudinal evidence for stretch-mediated hypertrophy is less developed than for muscles such as gastrocnemius.\r\n\r\nThe strongest defensible position is:\r\n\r\n- humeral elevation clearly lengthens the lats;\r\n- loaded overhead positions are mechanically favorable for long-length tension;\r\n- exercise-specific hypertrophy likely depends on both muscle length and resistance profile;\r\n- the precise magnitude of any special SMH effect remains uncertain.\r\n\r\nTherefore a moderate-to-high `smh_factor` can be mechanically justified, but confidence should be lower than in muscles with direct long-length intervention data.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Pull-Ups\r\n\r\nPull-ups are one of the classic high-demand latissimus exercises.\r\n\r\nThey combine:\r\n\r\n- shoulder adduction;\r\n- shoulder extension depending on arm path;\r\n- elbow flexion.\r\n\r\nThe lats work with:\r\n\r\n- biceps brachii;\r\n- brachialis;\r\n- brachioradialis;\r\n- teres major;\r\n- scapular stabilizers.\r\n\r\nGrip width and forearm orientation change force sharing but do not create a simple "lat on/off" switch.\r\n\r\n## Neutral-Grip Pull-Up\r\n\r\nNeutral grip often provides:\r\n\r\n- comfortable shoulder mechanics;\r\n- substantial shoulder adduction/extension;\r\n- high elbow-flexor contribution;\r\n- large usable external load.\r\n\r\nIt is an excellent general lat exercise.\r\n\r\nCompared with very wide pronated pulling, it may permit greater ROM and load while still producing high lat demand.\r\n\r\n## Wide Pronated Pull-Up\r\n\r\nA wide pronated grip alters humeral geometry and elbow flexion mechanics.\r\n\r\nIt may increase the apparent adduction emphasis but also reduces:\r\n\r\n- elbow ROM;\r\n- total usable load;\r\n- shoulder-extension contribution in some lifters.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n> wider grip does not automatically equal more lat stimulus.\r\n\r\nExercise quality should be judged by actual shoulder torque and loaded ROM rather than grip width alone.\r\n\r\n## Lat Pulldown\r\n\r\nPulldowns provide the same broad joint actions as pull-ups but with external load independent of body mass.\r\n\r\nAdvantages include:\r\n\r\n- easier load progression;\r\n- easier high-repetition work;\r\n- controlled proximity to failure;\r\n- less whole-body stabilization.\r\n\r\nThey are particularly useful for hypertrophy because failure is easier to standardize.\r\n\r\n## Neutral-Grip Pulldown\r\n\r\nNeutral or semi-neutral handles often allow:\r\n\r\n- large shoulder ROM;\r\n- strong elbow flexion;\r\n- high loading;\r\n- comfortable humeral path.\r\n\r\nThese characteristics make them highly effective for lat hypertrophy even if they are not uniquely "lat isolating."\r\n\r\n## Straight-Arm Cable Pulldown\r\n\r\nThis exercise is highly useful because it removes most elbow-flexor contribution.\r\n\r\nThe primary dynamic action becomes shoulder extension/adduction.\r\n\r\nAdvantages include:\r\n\r\n- high lat specificity;\r\n- low biceps limitation;\r\n- strong overhead stretch;\r\n- easy cable resistance manipulation.\r\n\r\nIt should generally receive high `latissimus_dorsi` allocation.\r\n\r\n## Rowing\r\n\r\nRows can train the lats strongly, but elbow path matters.\r\n\r\n### Tucked Elbows\r\n\r\nGreater shoulder extension component.\r\n\r\nUsually higher latissimus contribution.\r\n\r\n### High Elbows\r\n\r\nMore horizontal abduction.\r\n\r\nGreater relative demand on:\r\n\r\n- posterior deltoid;\r\n- middle trapezius;\r\n- rhomboids.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n> a row becomes less lat-specific as the humerus moves farther into an abducted/high-elbow trajectory.\r\n\r\n## Chest-Supported Row\r\n\r\nChest support removes much of the spinal-extensor stabilization cost.\r\n\r\nThis can improve local back-muscle specificity.\r\n\r\nA chest-supported row with elbows near the torso is a strong lat exercise.\r\n\r\nA chest-supported high-elbow row shifts more toward posterior deltoid and scapular retractors.\r\n\r\n## Pendlay Row\r\n\r\nPendlay rows can produce substantial lat demand through humeral extension.\r\n\r\nHowever, they also impose very high demand on:\r\n\r\n- erector spinae;\r\n- gluteus maximus;\r\n- hamstrings;\r\n- adductor magnus;\r\n- elbow flexors.\r\n\r\nTherefore the local lat stimulus comes with high systemic fatigue.\r\n\r\nFor pure lat hypertrophy, a supported row often has a better local stimulus-to-fatigue ratio.\r\n\r\n## Shoulder Extension Beyond the Torso\r\n\r\nLatissimus dorsi contributes to shoulder extension, but forcing the humerus far behind the torso is not necessarily beneficial.\r\n\r\nAt terminal extension:\r\n\r\n- the lat is relatively shortened;\r\n- posterior deltoid and triceps long head can contribute;\r\n- available ROM becomes increasingly anatomy-dependent.\r\n\r\nThere is little reason to chase exaggerated posterior humeral travel.\r\n\r\n## Scapular Movement\r\n\r\nNormal pulling includes scapular movement.\r\n\r\nHowever, lat-specific tension should arise from humeral movement relative to the trunk, not merely from squeezing the scapulae together.\r\n\r\nThis distinction is particularly important in rows.\r\n\r\nA trainee can produce large scapular retraction with relatively little humeral extension, creating a more trap/rhomboid-dominant pattern.\r\n\r\n## Grip\r\n\r\nGrip can indirectly limit lat training.\r\n\r\nIf grip fails before shoulder musculature, straps can reduce forearm limitation in high-volume pulling.\r\n\r\nThis may increase the ability to reach true lat fatigue.\r\n\r\nThe tradeoff is reduced grip training.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Vertical and Horizontal Pulling Are Complementary\r\n\r\nA comprehensive lat program often benefits from both:\r\n\r\n1. **vertical pulling**;\r\n2. **horizontal shoulder-extension work**.\r\n\r\nExamples:\r\n\r\n- pull-up or pulldown;\r\n- tucked-elbow row.\r\n\r\nThis exposes the lat to different humeral trajectories and resistance profiles.\r\n\r\n## Add Straight-Arm Work When Useful\r\n\r\nA straight-arm pulldown can add lat volume without substantial elbow-flexor fatigue.\r\n\r\nThis is especially useful when:\r\n\r\n- biceps are already fatigued;\r\n- pulling volume is high;\r\n- lat hypertrophy is a priority.\r\n\r\n## Weekly Volume\r\n\r\nA practical starting range for direct lat-focused work can be approximately:\r\n\r\n- **6-12 hard sets per week**.\r\n\r\nThis should include:\r\n\r\n- pulldowns;\r\n- pull-ups;\r\n- lat-biased rows;\r\n- straight-arm pulldowns.\r\n\r\nDo not count every row equally.\r\n\r\nA high-elbow row may deserve much less lat-specific set credit than a tucked-elbow row.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nTwo to three weekly exposures are practical.\r\n\r\nLatissimus dorsi is large and can tolerate meaningful volume, but recovery overlaps with:\r\n\r\n- biceps;\r\n- brachialis;\r\n- forearms;\r\n- posterior shoulder;\r\n- spinal extensors depending on exercise selection.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\nUseful ranges include:\r\n\r\n- 5-8 reps for heavy weighted pull-ups;\r\n- 6-12 reps for rows and pulldowns;\r\n- 10-20 reps for machine/cable hypertrophy work;\r\n- 12-25 reps for straight-arm pulldowns.\r\n\r\nThere is no unique lat-specific repetition range.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nStable pulldowns and supported rows can be trained close to failure:\r\n\r\n- approximately 0-2 RIR.\r\n\r\nHeavy weighted pull-ups and unsupported rows often benefit from more technical reserve.\r\n\r\nThe set should terminate when meaningful shoulder movement can no longer be maintained without excessive:\r\n\r\n- trunk swing;\r\n- spinal extension;\r\n- shortened ROM.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Order\r\n\r\nIf lat hypertrophy is a priority, at least one high-value lat exercise should appear early in the session.\r\n\r\nA useful sequence could be:\r\n\r\n1. weighted pull-up or pulldown;\r\n2. chest-supported lat-biased row;\r\n3. straight-arm pulldown.\r\n\r\nThis provides:\r\n\r\n- heavy multi-joint loading;\r\n- stable horizontal pulling;\r\n- elbow-flexor-independent isolation.\r\n\r\n## Elbow-Flexor Limitation\r\n\r\nMany lat exercises are limited partly by elbow flexors.\r\n\r\nThis can be managed through:\r\n\r\n- straps;\r\n- exercise order;\r\n- straight-arm work;\r\n- machine selection.\r\n\r\nIf biceps consistently fail before the lats, total pulling load may underestimate the lat's own capacity.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## The Muscle Is Huge but Inserts Through a Narrow Tendon\r\n\r\nLatissimus dorsi spreads across a very large portion of the lower back and trunk, yet converges onto a relatively small humeral insertion.\r\n\r\nThis produces a striking example of large-scale force convergence.\r\n\r\n## It Does Not Directly Retract the Scapula\r\n\r\nBecause latissimus inserts on the humerus rather than the medial scapula, it is not a primary scapular retractor.\r\n\r\nRows combine multiple motions, which is why lat activity is frequently confused with trapezius/rhomboid function.\r\n\r\n## It Can Move the Body Toward the Arm\r\n\r\nIn climbing or pull-ups, the hands are fixed and latissimus moves the torso relative to the humerus.\r\n\r\nThe same muscle action can therefore appear as:\r\n\r\n- arm moving toward torso;\r\nor\r\n- torso moving toward arm.\r\n\r\n## Grip Width Is Overrated\r\n\r\nVery wide grips are often promoted as uniquely superior for lat width.\r\n\r\nBiomechanically, they can reduce ROM and usable load.\r\n\r\nLat recruitment depends much more on:\r\n\r\n- humeral path;\r\n- shoulder torque;\r\n- resistance profile\r\n\r\nthan on maximizing grip width.\r\n\r\n## Lats Are Not Purely Sagittal-Plane Muscles\r\n\r\nThey can produce:\r\n\r\n- extension;\r\n- adduction;\r\n- internal rotation.\r\n\r\nThis is why vertical and horizontal pulling can both train them effectively despite looking mechanically different.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\n`latissimus_dorsi` should primarily receive demand from:\r\n\r\n1. **shoulder adduction torque**;\r\n2. **shoulder extension torque**;\r\n3. secondary internal-rotation torque.\r\n\r\nIt should receive little direct demand from pure scapular retraction.\r\n\r\n## Length-Tension Model\r\n\r\nA simplified lat length model should depend on humeral elevation:\r\n\r\n- overhead flexion/abduction → long;\r\n- arm around neutral → intermediate;\r\n- extension/adduction toward torso → shorter.\r\n\r\nA more complete model would include:\r\n\r\n- flexion-extension;\r\n- abduction-adduction;\r\n- humeral rotation.\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_lat = f(humeral_elevation, abduction, rotation)`\r\n\r\nFor KISS modelling, global humeral elevation is the most important variable.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Allocation\r\n\r\n### Weighted Neutral-Grip Pull-Up\r\n\r\nVery high `latissimus_dorsi` demand.\r\n\r\nAlso substantial:\r\n- brachialis;\r\n- biceps brachii;\r\n- brachioradialis;\r\n- wrist flexors;\r\n- rotator cuff;\r\n- scapular stabilizers.\r\n\r\n### Wide Pronated-Grip Pull-Up\r\n\r\nHigh lat demand.\r\n\r\nDo not automatically assign more lat recruitment than neutral grip solely because the grip is wider.\r\n\r\n### Neutral-Grip Lat Pulldown\r\n\r\nVery high lat demand with strong elbow-flexor contribution.\r\n\r\n### Wide-Grip Seated Cable Row\r\n\r\nLat allocation depends on elbow path.\r\n\r\nIf elbows are moderately tucked:\r\n- high lat.\r\n\r\nIf elbows are high and abducted:\r\n- lower lat, higher posterior deltoid/middle trap/rhomboids.\r\n\r\n### Chest-Supported Dumbbell Row\r\n\r\nPotentially high lat demand with low spinal-extensor cost.\r\n\r\nAgain, elbow path determines distribution.\r\n\r\n### Pendlay Row\r\n\r\nHigh lat demand but also very high systemic stabilization cost.\r\n\r\n### Straight-Arm Cable Pulldown\r\n\r\nVery high lat specificity.\r\n\r\nLow elbow-flexor demand.\r\n\r\nThis should be one of the cleanest lat vectors in the catalogue.\r\n\r\n### Cable Face Pull\r\n\r\nLow-to-moderate lat demand.\r\n\r\nPosterior deltoid, rotator cuff and scapular retractors should dominate.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Teres Major\r\n\r\nLatissimus dorsi and teres major share:\r\n\r\n- extension;\r\n- adduction;\r\n- internal rotation.\r\n\r\nThey often act synergistically.\r\n\r\nIf `teres_major` exists separately in the database, force sharing should be distributed rather than assigning all shoulder-extension/adduction demand to the lat.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Posterior Deltoid\r\n\r\nPosterior deltoid also produces shoulder extension.\r\n\r\nRelative allocation depends strongly on humeral abduction.\r\n\r\n### Elbow Tucked\r\n\r\nMore lat/teres-major favorable.\r\n\r\n### Elbow High\r\n\r\nGreater posterior-deltoid contribution.\r\n\r\nThis should be one of the major modifiers in row modelling.\r\n\r\n## Scapular Stabilizer Coupling\r\n\r\nHigh lat force during pulling usually requires coordinated activity of:\r\n\r\n- rhomboids;\r\n- middle/lower trapezius;\r\n- serratus anterior depending on phase;\r\n- rotator cuff.\r\n\r\nHowever, these muscles should receive their own task-specific stabilization/retraction/upward-rotation demand rather than being merged into lat recruitment.\r\n\r\n## External Torque\r\n\r\nFor any pulling exercise:\r\n\r\n`M_shoulder = F_external × perpendicular_distance_to_GH_axis`\r\n\r\nA heavier load does not necessarily mean greater lat force if geometry changes.\r\n\r\nExamples:\r\n\r\n- wide-grip pull-up;\r\n- neutral-grip pull-up;\r\n- straight-arm pulldown\r\n\r\ncan produce very different shoulder moment requirements despite similar external force magnitudes.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nLatissimus dorsi has a strong mechanical case for long-length loading because overhead humeral elevation increases muscle length.\r\n\r\nExercises such as:\r\n\r\n- pull-ups;\r\n- pulldowns;\r\n- straight-arm pulldowns;\r\n- pullovers\r\n\r\ncan therefore provide substantial long-length exposure.\r\n\r\nHowever, direct lat-specific longitudinal evidence isolating a stretch-mediated hypertrophy advantage remains limited.\r\n\r\nThe appropriate interpretation is:\r\n\r\n> **long-length loading is mechanically compelling and should be present, but exact SMH magnitude should not be treated as experimentally settled.**\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **broad thoracolumbar/pelvic/rib origin and humeral insertion:** high;\r\n- **thoracodorsal-nerve innervation:** high;\r\n- **shoulder-extension role:** high;\r\n- **shoulder-adduction role:** high;\r\n- **internal-rotation role:** high;\r\n- **strong recruitment in pull-ups/pulldowns:** high;\r\n- **row recruitment dependence on elbow path:** high;\r\n- **overhead positioning lengthening the muscle:** high mechanically;\r\n- **direct lat-specific long-length hypertrophy evidence:** limited;\r\n- **precise regional recruitment:** dependent on humeral trajectory and individual anatomy.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **latissimus dorsi = a broad, regionally heterogeneous humeral adductor and extensor whose recruitment is determined primarily by shoulder torque and humeral path, not by scapular retraction or grip width alone.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/LatissimusDorsi}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	25.25	21.89	0.928	47.25	43.85
44	sartorius	Musculus sartorius	Lower	Hip_FA	435.00	411.93	6.00	Strap/Parallel	0.450	0.550	high	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\nThe **sartorius** is a long, thin, superficial muscle crossing both the **hip** and the **knee**.\r\n\r\nIt is notable for combining several actions that position the lower limb in the classic "tailor" configuration:\r\n\r\n- hip flexion;\r\n- hip abduction;\r\n- hip external rotation;\r\n- knee flexion;\r\n- contribution to internal rotation of the tibia when the knee is flexed.\r\n\r\nThis makes sartorius mechanically unusual.\r\n\r\nIt is not a dominant high-force prime mover in any one of these actions.\r\n\r\nInstead, it acts as a **multi-planar synergist and limb-positioning muscle** whose long fascicles allow substantial excursion across a large range of hip and knee configurations.\r\n\r\nArchitectural measurements support this interpretation.\r\n\r\nCompared with large force-oriented lower-limb muscles such as:\r\n\r\n- soleus;\r\n- vastus lateralis;\r\n- gluteus medius,\r\n\r\nsartorius possesses substantially lower physiological cross-sectional area but exceptionally long fibers, placing it toward the excursion-oriented end of the muscle-design spectrum.\r\n\r\nFor Agonez, its most useful identity is:\r\n\r\n> **a biarticular, long-fascicled hip flexion-abduction-external-rotation synergist and knee flexor with relatively low absolute force capacity but substantial operating range.**\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\n## Origin\r\n\r\nSartorius originates primarily from the **anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS)** and the immediately adjacent superior portion of the anterior iliac region.\r\n\r\nFrom there it travels:\r\n\r\n- inferiorly;\r\n- medially;\r\n- superficially\r\n\r\nacross the anterior thigh.\r\n\r\nIts long oblique path is visually distinctive.\r\n\r\n## Insertion\r\n\r\nDistally, sartorius crosses the medial side of the knee and inserts onto the proximal medial tibia as part of the **pes anserinus**.\r\n\r\nThe pes anserinus also receives tendons from:\r\n\r\n- gracilis;\r\n- semitendinosus.\r\n\r\nThe sartorius tendon forms the most superficial component of this insertional complex.\r\n\r\nBecause it inserts on the medial tibia below the knee joint, sartorius can influence both:\r\n\r\n- knee flexion;\r\n- tibial rotation.\r\n\r\n## Course Across the Thigh\r\n\r\nUnlike most major thigh muscles, sartorius does not run approximately parallel to the long axis of the femur.\r\n\r\nInstead it travels obliquely from the lateral superior pelvis toward the medial proximal tibia.\r\n\r\nThis geometry allows one muscle to generate components of force in several planes simultaneously.\r\n\r\n## Relationship With the Femoral Triangle\r\n\r\nSartorius forms the **lateral boundary of the femoral triangle**.\r\n\r\nIt also contributes to the roof of the adductor canal farther distally.\r\n\r\nThese anatomical relationships make it an important surface landmark.\r\n\r\n## Architecture\r\n\r\nSartorius is an extreme example of a long-fascicled muscle.\r\n\r\nHuman lower-limb architectural studies demonstrate that sartorius has:\r\n\r\n- very long fascicles;\r\n- low pennation;\r\n- relatively small PCSA.\r\n\r\nWard et al. identified sartorius among the muscles with the greatest excursion capacity in the lower extremity. :contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}\r\n\r\nThis architecture supports:\r\n\r\n- large shortening distance;\r\n- operation across substantial joint ROM;\r\n- relatively high shortening velocity potential.\r\n\r\nThe tradeoff is comparatively low maximal force.\r\n\r\nThis is the opposite design strategy of muscles such as soleus, which possess:\r\n\r\n- short pennate fibers;\r\n- very large PCSA;\r\n- enormous force capacity;\r\n- smaller fascicular excursion.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nSartorius is innervated by the **femoral nerve**, primarily from spinal roots approximately:\r\n\r\n- L2;\r\n- L3.\r\n\r\nThis is consistent with its location in the anterior thigh compartment.\r\n\r\nThe femoral nerve also supplies:\r\n\r\n- quadriceps;\r\n- portions of iliacus.\r\n\r\nDespite sharing innervation with the quadriceps, sartorius has substantially different functions because of its unusual path across the hip and knee.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Hip Flexion\r\n\r\nSartorius contributes to **hip flexion**.\r\n\r\nThis action is shared with:\r\n\r\n- iliopsoas;\r\n- rectus femoris;\r\n- tensor fasciae latae;\r\n- other smaller flexors.\r\n\r\nHowever, sartorius should generally receive only a minority of total hip-flexion torque because its force capacity is relatively small.\r\n\r\nExperimental hip-flexor EMG confirms sartorius activity during tasks involving active hip flexion, although iliopsoas becomes especially important as hip-flexion demand rises. :contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}\r\n\r\n## Hip Abduction\r\n\r\nBecause sartorius originates lateral to the hip and courses medially, it can contribute to **abduction of the femur**.\r\n\r\nThis role is secondary to the major abductors:\r\n\r\n- gluteus medius;\r\n- gluteus minimus;\r\n- tensor fasciae latae.\r\n\r\nIts abduction function becomes especially relevant when combined with:\r\n\r\n- flexion;\r\n- external rotation.\r\n\r\n## Hip External Rotation\r\n\r\nSartorius also contributes to **external rotation of the femur**.\r\n\r\nThis is one component of the characteristic sartorius movement pattern.\r\n\r\nAgain, it is a synergist rather than a dominant high-force rotator.\r\n\r\nLarger external rotators and posterior hip musculature can produce much greater total torque.\r\n\r\n## Combined Hip Action\r\n\r\nThe three hip actions:\r\n\r\n- flexion;\r\n- abduction;\r\n- external rotation\r\n\r\noccur naturally together when the foot is placed onto the opposite thigh.\r\n\r\nHistorically this resembles the cross-legged posture associated with tailors, which gave the muscle its name.\r\n\r\nHowever, exercise modelling should avoid thinking of this as one inseparable action.\r\n\r\nSartorius can contribute to each component independently depending on resistance direction.\r\n\r\n## Knee Flexion\r\n\r\nSartorius crosses posterior to the knee's flexion-extension axis and therefore contributes to **knee flexion**.\r\n\r\nIts force capacity is much smaller than that of the principal knee flexors:\r\n\r\n- semitendinosus;\r\n- semimembranosus;\r\n- biceps femoris.\r\n\r\nIt should therefore receive only a secondary portion of knee-flexion torque.\r\n\r\n## Tibial Internal Rotation\r\n\r\nWhen the knee is flexed, sartorius can contribute to **internal rotation of the tibia relative to the femur**.\r\n\r\nThis action is shared with:\r\n\r\n- semitendinosus;\r\n- semimembranosus;\r\n- gracilis.\r\n\r\nIt opposes the external-rotation action of biceps femoris.\r\n\r\nThis rotational role becomes mechanically more meaningful when the knee is already flexed, because axial tibial rotation is more available than near full extension.\r\n\r\n## Biarticular Mechanics\r\n\r\nSartorius spans both:\r\n\r\n- hip;\r\n- knee.\r\n\r\nTherefore its length depends on both joints.\r\n\r\nThe muscle is shortened by combinations of its concentric actions:\r\n\r\n- hip flexion;\r\n- hip abduction;\r\n- hip external rotation;\r\n- knee flexion.\r\n\r\nIt is lengthened by movements in the opposite directions:\r\n\r\n- hip extension;\r\n- hip adduction;\r\n- hip internal rotation;\r\n- knee extension.\r\n\r\nHowever, because several dimensions act simultaneously, sartorius length cannot be represented precisely using only one joint angle.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nDirect evidence for **stretch-mediated hypertrophy of sartorius is extremely limited**.\r\n\r\nMost hypertrophy literature does not measure this muscle separately.\r\n\r\nThe mechanical basis can nevertheless be described.\r\n\r\n## Long-Length Configuration\r\n\r\nSartorius is relatively lengthened by a combination of:\r\n\r\n- hip extension;\r\n- hip adduction;\r\n- hip internal rotation;\r\n- knee extension.\r\n\r\nA fully maximized position across all these dimensions is neither necessary nor desirable for training.\r\n\r\nThe relevant principle is simply that movements opposing its major actions increase its musculotendinous length.\r\n\r\n## Biarticular Length Dependence\r\n\r\nBecause sartorius crosses two joints:\r\n\r\n`L_sartorius = f(hip_flexion_extension,\r\n                 hip_abduction_adduction,\r\n                 hip_rotation,\r\n                 knee_flexion_extension)`\r\n\r\nThis makes a single scalar `E_LT` inherently approximate.\r\n\r\n## Long-Fascicle Architecture\r\n\r\nSartorius has exceptionally long fibers relative to its muscle size. :contentReference[oaicite:3]{index=3}\r\n\r\nThis indicates a muscle designed to function over large excursion.\r\n\r\nHowever:\r\n\r\n> long fascicles do not themselves establish enhanced stretch-mediated hypertrophy.\r\n\r\nArchitecture describes mechanical capability, not hypertrophic sensitivity.\r\n\r\n## Practical Interpretation\r\n\r\nIf sartorius hypertrophy were specifically desired, resistance could theoretically be applied through movements combining:\r\n\r\n- hip flexion;\r\n- abduction;\r\n- external rotation\r\n\r\nfrom a more extended/adducted starting state.\r\n\r\nHowever:\r\n\r\n- direct isolation is difficult;\r\n- larger synergists dominate most exercises;\r\n- longitudinal hypertrophy evidence is nearly absent.\r\n\r\nTherefore `smh_factor` should be conservative.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Sartorius Is Rarely a Primary Hypertrophy Target\r\n\r\nIn conventional resistance training, sartorius is usually trained indirectly.\r\n\r\nIt contributes to:\r\n\r\n- hip-flexion exercises;\r\n- unilateral leg movements;\r\n- knee-flexion tasks;\r\n- certain multi-planar hip movements.\r\n\r\nBecause of its relatively small PCSA and multiple synergists, deliberately isolating it is difficult.\r\n\r\n## Straight-Leg Raise\r\n\r\nSartorius is active during hip-flexion tasks, including straight-leg raising. Hip-flexor EMG studies show sartorius recruitment during exercises involving active hip flexion. :contentReference[oaicite:4]{index=4}\r\n\r\nHowever, the dominant hip-flexion allocation should usually go to:\r\n\r\n- iliopsoas;\r\n- rectus femoris depending on knee angle.\r\n\r\nSartorius should receive a smaller synergistic share.\r\n\r\n## Flexion + Abduction + External Rotation\r\n\r\nA movement deliberately combining:\r\n\r\n- hip flexion;\r\n- abduction;\r\n- external rotation\r\n\r\nmatches the sartorius line of action particularly well.\r\n\r\nThis resembles the classic sartorius anatomical test position.\r\n\r\nHowever, other muscles also contribute substantially:\r\n\r\n- iliopsoas;\r\n- gluteal rotators;\r\n- TFL depending on trajectory;\r\n- adductors dynamically depending on phase.\r\n\r\nThus matching all textbook actions does not equal isolation.\r\n\r\n## Hip-Flexion Exercises\r\n\r\nCable hip flexion can recruit sartorius as a synergist.\r\n\r\nIf the femur is allowed to travel somewhat:\r\n\r\n- laterally;\r\n- externally rotated,\r\n\r\nits relative share may increase.\r\n\r\nFor pure sagittal-plane high-force flexion, iliopsoas should dominate.\r\n\r\n## Leg Raises\r\n\r\nDuring hanging or lying leg raises, sartorius can assist hip flexion.\r\n\r\nIts role should remain much smaller than `iliopsoas`.\r\n\r\nIt may also contribute to maintaining femoral orientation depending on leg position.\r\n\r\n## Knee Flexion\r\n\r\nLeg curls can recruit sartorius as a minor knee-flexion synergist.\r\n\r\nHowever, hamstrings should overwhelmingly dominate total knee-flexion force.\r\n\r\nA large sartorius allocation in a seated or prone leg curl would be anatomically implausible.\r\n\r\n## Single-Leg Tasks\r\n\r\nSartorius may contribute to femoral positioning during:\r\n\r\n- split squats;\r\n- step-ups;\r\n- lunges;\r\n- gait-like movements.\r\n\r\nBut the high-force prime movers remain:\r\n\r\n- quadriceps;\r\n- gluteus maximus;\r\n- adductors;\r\n- hamstrings.\r\n\r\nSartorius should receive small multi-planar stabilization/synergist demand.\r\n\r\n## Running\r\n\r\nSartorius participates in swing-phase limb positioning.\r\n\r\nIts combination of:\r\n\r\n- hip flexion;\r\n- knee flexion\r\n\r\ncan assist advancing the limb.\r\n\r\nIts ability to alter frontal- and transverse-plane femoral position can also contribute to leg trajectory.\r\n\r\nHowever, larger muscles produce most of the required propulsion and joint torque.\r\n\r\n## Machine Hip Abduction\r\n\r\nSartorius can contribute somewhat to hip abduction, but:\r\n\r\n- gluteus medius;\r\n- gluteus minimus;\r\n- TFL\r\n\r\nshould dominate.\r\n\r\nThe sartorius allocation should be low.\r\n\r\n## Seated Leg Curl\r\n\r\nBecause the hip is flexed, sartorius is shortened proximally.\r\n\r\nIt can still contribute to knee flexion but should remain a small synergist.\r\n\r\n## Standing Leg Curl\r\n\r\nA more extended hip can place sartorius at greater proximal length during knee-flexion loading.\r\n\r\nMechanically this may improve its length state, but hamstrings still dominate force production.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Dedicated Volume Is Usually Unnecessary\r\n\r\nFor general hypertrophy or strength training:\r\n\r\n> **0 dedicated sartorius sets is usually appropriate.**\r\n\r\nThe muscle receives sufficient incidental exposure from lower-body and hip-flexor training for most purposes.\r\n\r\n## When Direct Training May Matter\r\n\r\nMore explicit sartorius work may be relevant for:\r\n\r\n- rehabilitation;\r\n- sport-specific multi-planar control;\r\n- anatomical research;\r\n- very detailed bodybuilding;\r\n- individual weakness following injury.\r\n\r\nEven then, attempting complete isolation is unrealistic.\r\n\r\n## Volume\r\n\r\nIf directly targeted, a small amount of work is sufficient initially:\r\n\r\n- approximately 2-4 direct multi-planar sets per week.\r\n\r\nThere is little evidence supporting larger sartorius-specific prescriptions.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Range\r\n\r\nBecause the muscle is small and exercises targeting it are mechanically complex, moderate-to-high repetitions are practical:\r\n\r\n- 10-20;\r\n- 15-30.\r\n\r\nVery heavy loading will usually recruit larger synergists or create technique compensation before sartorius becomes independently limiting.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nTrue local sartorius failure is difficult to identify.\r\n\r\nTherefore exercise quality and intended joint trajectory should be prioritized over aggressive failure.\r\n\r\n## Sport Exposure\r\n\r\nRunning and multidirectional sport can provide repeated sartorius activation.\r\n\r\nThis should not automatically be counted as bodybuilding-equivalent hypertrophy volume, but it is relevant to:\r\n\r\n- fatigue;\r\n- local tissue exposure;\r\n- recovery.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## It Has Exceptional Excursion Capacity\r\n\r\nHuman architectural measurements identify sartorius as one of the lower-limb muscles with the largest fiber excursion potential. :contentReference[oaicite:5]{index=5}\r\n\r\nThis follows from its extremely long fibers and relatively small PCSA.\r\n\r\nIt is a useful textbook example of the architectural tradeoff between:\r\n\r\n- force capacity;\r\n- excursion capacity.\r\n\r\n## It Is the Classic "Tailor's Muscle"\r\n\r\nIts combination of:\r\n\r\n- hip flexion;\r\n- abduction;\r\n- external rotation;\r\n- knee flexion\r\n\r\nhelps position the lower limb in a cross-legged posture.\r\n\r\nThis gave rise to the name `sartorius`, from the Latin association with tailoring.\r\n\r\n## It Crosses the Entire Thigh Obliquely\r\n\r\nRather than running longitudinally like most major thigh muscles, sartorius crosses superficially from:\r\n\r\n- lateral pelvis;\r\nto\r\n- medial tibia.\r\n\r\nThis unusual path explains its multi-planar actions.\r\n\r\n## It Is Part of Pes Anserinus\r\n\r\nSartorius shares the medial tibial insertion region with:\r\n\r\n- gracilis;\r\n- semitendinosus.\r\n\r\nAll three can contribute to:\r\n\r\n- knee flexion;\r\n- internal tibial rotation.\r\n\r\nYet their proximal origins and hip functions differ substantially.\r\n\r\n## Longest Does Not Mean Strongest\r\n\r\nSartorius is commonly described as the longest muscle in the human body, but its architecture gives it relatively low force capacity.\r\n\r\nIts defining capability is excursion rather than maximum strength.\r\n\r\nThis distinction is directly consistent with quantitative lower-limb architecture measurements. :contentReference[oaicite:6]{index=6}\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\n`sartorius` should receive force from several dimensions:\r\n\r\n1. hip-flexion torque;\r\n2. hip-abduction torque;\r\n3. hip-external-rotation torque;\r\n4. knee-flexion torque;\r\n5. internal-tibial-rotation torque with the knee flexed.\r\n\r\nHowever, its allocation should generally be **small relative to the primary muscles for each action**.\r\n\r\n## Small-PCSA Constraint\r\n\r\nThis is especially important for the FCSA model.\r\n\r\nSartorius has long fibers but relatively low PCSA, consistent with low maximum force capacity. Lower-limb architectural studies explicitly place it among excursion-oriented rather than force-oriented muscles. :contentReference[oaicite:7]{index=7}\r\n\r\nTherefore the model should not allocate large absolute force merely because sartorius participates in many actions.\r\n\r\nIts broad functional repertoire is not equivalent to high force capacity.\r\n\r\n## Hip-Flexion Force Sharing\r\n\r\nFor hip flexion:\r\n\r\n`T_hip_flexion =\r\n    iliopsoas\r\n  + rectus_femoris\r\n  + TFL\r\n  + sartorius\r\n  + smaller_synergists`\r\n\r\nSartorius should generally receive a relatively small share.\r\n\r\nStraight-leg-raise EMG work indicates sartorius does participate in hip-flexion tasks, while iliopsoas becomes particularly prominent at larger hip-flexion angles. :contentReference[oaicite:8]{index=8}\r\n\r\n## Knee-Flexion Force Sharing\r\n\r\nFor knee flexion:\r\n\r\n`T_knee_flexion =\r\n    hamstrings\r\n  + gastrocnemius\r\n  + sartorius\r\n  + gracilis\r\n  + smaller_synergists`\r\n\r\nSartorius should receive only a minor proportion.\r\n\r\nIts architecture is not consistent with being a major knee-flexion force generator.\r\n\r\n## Tibial Rotation\r\n\r\nWhen the knee is flexed, sartorius joins:\r\n\r\n- semitendinosus;\r\n- semimembranosus;\r\n- gracilis\r\n\r\nin generating or stabilizing internal tibial rotation.\r\n\r\nThis is opposed primarily by:\r\n\r\n- biceps femoris.\r\n\r\nThe rotational component should become small near full knee extension, where axial rotation is mechanically constrained.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Allocation\r\n\r\n### Hanging Straight-Leg Raise\r\n\r\nLow-to-moderate sartorius demand.\r\n\r\nVery high:\r\n- iliopsoas.\r\n\r\nMeaningful:\r\n- rectus femoris.\r\n\r\nSartorius remains a hip-flexion synergist.\r\n\r\n### Hanging Knee Raise\r\n\r\nLow-to-moderate sartorius hip-flexion demand.\r\n\r\nKnee-flexion position changes biarticular length state, but iliopsoas still dominates hip flexion.\r\n\r\n### Seated Leg Curl\r\n\r\nLow sartorius knee-flexion allocation.\r\n\r\nVery high:\r\n- hamstrings.\r\n\r\n### Selectorized Leg Extension\r\n\r\nEssentially zero direct sartorius demand because sartorius does not extend the knee.\r\n\r\nIt may provide trivial stabilization only.\r\n\r\n### Hip-Abduction Machine\r\n\r\nLow sartorius allocation.\r\n\r\nHigh:\r\n- gluteus medius;\r\n- gluteus minimus;\r\n- TFL where represented.\r\n\r\n### Split Squat\r\n\r\nLow sartorius dynamic allocation with possible multi-planar stabilization demand.\r\n\r\n### 45° Leg Press\r\n\r\nVery low sartorius prime-mover allocation.\r\n\r\nLarge sagittal-plane forces are handled by much stronger muscles.\r\n\r\n## Length-Tension Model\r\n\r\nA proper model is multidimensional:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_sartorius =\r\n    f(hip_flexion,\r\n      hip_abduction,\r\n      hip_rotation,\r\n      knee_flexion)`\r\n\r\nQualitatively:\r\n\r\n### Shorter\r\n\r\nCombination of:\r\n- hip flexion;\r\n- hip abduction;\r\n- hip external rotation;\r\n- knee flexion.\r\n\r\n### Longer\r\n\r\nCombination of:\r\n- hip extension;\r\n- hip adduction;\r\n- hip internal rotation;\r\n- knee extension.\r\n\r\nBecause four movement dimensions contribute, assigning a precise scalar `E_LT` from one joint angle would be misleading.\r\n\r\n## Biarticular Compensation\r\n\r\nHip and knee motions can partially offset each other's effect on muscle length.\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n\r\n- hip flexion shortens sartorius proximally;\r\n- knee extension lengthens it distally.\r\n\r\nThis means some movements can involve large joint excursions with relatively modest net fascicular excursion.\r\n\r\nThis is analogous conceptually to:\r\n\r\n- rectus femoris;\r\n- biceps femoris long head;\r\n\r\nbut sartorius additionally spans multiple frontal/transverse-plane actions.\r\n\r\n## Architecture Implication\r\n\r\nWard et al. found sartorius among the lower-limb muscles with the largest excursion capacity. :contentReference[oaicite:9]{index=9}\r\n\r\nFor Agonez, this means:\r\n\r\n- long `optimal_fiber_length_cm` is mechanically expected;\r\n- relatively low PCSA is mechanically expected;\r\n- its force vector should remain limited despite large ROM capability.\r\n\r\nIt is a particularly good example of why:\r\n\r\n`muscle_mass` or `muscle_length`\r\n\r\ncannot substitute for:\r\n\r\n`PCSA / FCSA`\r\n\r\nwhen estimating force capacity.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nEvidence for sartorius-specific stretch-mediated hypertrophy is extremely weak.\r\n\r\nThe most defensible model is:\r\n\r\n- opposing its multi-planar actions lengthens it;\r\n- its long fibers permit substantial excursion;\r\n- direct progressive resistance can presumably hypertrophy it;\r\n- no strong longitudinal evidence identifies an optimal long-length configuration.\r\n\r\nTherefore `smh_factor` should be conservative.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **ASIS origin and pes-anserinus insertion:** high;\r\n- **femoral-nerve innervation:** high;\r\n- **hip-flexion contribution:** high;\r\n- **hip-abduction contribution:** high;\r\n- **external-rotation contribution:** high;\r\n- **knee-flexion contribution:** high;\r\n- **internal-tibial-rotation contribution with flexed knee:** high;\r\n- **exceptionally long-fiber / high-excursion architecture:** directly supported by human architectural measurements; :contentReference[oaicite:10]{index=10}\r\n- **sartorius recruitment during active hip flexion:** experimentally supported; :contentReference[oaicite:11]{index=11}\r\n- **precise exercise-specific force share:** poorly quantified;\r\n- **direct hypertrophy evidence:** sparse;\r\n- **stretch-mediated hypertrophy magnitude:** essentially unestablished.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **sartorius = a long-fascicled, low-PCSA biarticular limb-positioning muscle that contributes modestly to hip flexion, abduction and external rotation plus knee flexion and internal tibial rotation, prioritizing large excursion and multi-planar control over high absolute force production.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/Sartorius}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	40.30	1.30	1.000	10.22	10.22
11	teres_major	Musculus Teres major	Upper	Back	80.00	75.76	14.00	Parallel	0.400	0.600	very_high	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\nThe **teres major** is a relatively compact posterior shoulder muscle connecting the inferior/lateral scapular region to the proximal humerus.\r\n\r\nIts principal glenohumeral actions are:\r\n\r\n- **shoulder extension**;\r\n- **shoulder adduction**;\r\n- **internal rotation**.\r\n\r\nMechanically, teres major overlaps strongly with `latissimus_dorsi`. Both muscles can pull the humerus:\r\n\r\n- downward;\r\n- backward;\r\n- medially;\r\n- into internal rotation.\r\n\r\nHowever, teres major differs from latissimus dorsi in several important respects:\r\n\r\n- it originates entirely from the scapula rather than the trunk/pelvis;\r\n- it has a shorter muscle-tendon path;\r\n- it does not directly couple the humerus to the thoracolumbar fascia or pelvis;\r\n- its moment arms change substantially with humeral elevation.\r\n\r\nCadaveric moment-arm measurements confirm that teres major is mechanically capable of substantial extension/adduction-related action and that its leverage changes continuously across shoulder elevation. :contentReference[oaicite:0]{index=0}\r\n\r\nTeres major is **not a rotator-cuff muscle**.\r\n\r\nDespite being anatomically adjacent to teres minor, the two muscles have very different functions:\r\n\r\n- teres major → extension, adduction, internal rotation;\r\n- teres minor → external rotation and glenohumeral stabilization as part of the rotator cuff.\r\n\r\nFor Agonez, the most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **teres major = a scapula-to-humerus adductor/extensor/internal rotator that works closely with latissimus dorsi, especially during pulling from elevated arm positions, but should remain distinct from both the rotator cuff and scapular retractors.**\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\n## Origin\r\n\r\nTeres major originates primarily from the posterior surface of the **inferior angle and adjacent lower lateral border of the scapula**.\r\n\r\nThe attachment can extend over a broader inferior scapular region depending on individual anatomy.\r\n\r\nBecause the origin lies on the scapula, teres-major mechanics depend partly on scapular orientation.\r\n\r\nThis distinguishes it from latissimus dorsi, whose proximal attachments extend over:\r\n\r\n- thoracolumbar fascia;\r\n- vertebral structures;\r\n- iliac crest;\r\n- ribs.\r\n\r\n## Insertion\r\n\r\nTeres major inserts onto the proximal humerus near the **medial lip of the intertubercular sulcus / crest of the lesser tubercle**.\r\n\r\nIts insertion lies close to the distal attachment of latissimus dorsi.\r\n\r\nThis similar distal geometry explains why the two muscles share:\r\n\r\n- extension;\r\n- adduction;\r\n- internal rotation.\r\n\r\n## Relationship With Latissimus Dorsi\r\n\r\nTeres major and latissimus dorsi are frequently described as close synergists.\r\n\r\nTheir tendons insert near one another and their lines of pull become especially similar in many adduction/extension tasks. Teres major is consistently identified anatomically as an extensor, adductor and medial rotator of the humerus. :contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}\r\n\r\nNevertheless, they should not be merged blindly.\r\n\r\n### Teres Major\r\n\r\nOrigin:\r\n- scapula.\r\n\r\n### Latissimus Dorsi\r\n\r\nOrigin:\r\n- broad trunk and pelvis.\r\n\r\nTherefore scapular position directly changes teres-major origin geometry.\r\n\r\n## Relationship With Teres Minor\r\n\r\nThe shared word `teres` refers to shape, not function.\r\n\r\n### Teres Major\r\n\r\nNot rotator cuff.\r\n\r\nProduces:\r\n- internal rotation;\r\n- adduction;\r\n- extension.\r\n\r\n### Teres Minor\r\n\r\nRotator cuff.\r\n\r\nProduces:\r\n- external rotation;\r\n- glenohumeral stabilization.\r\n\r\nThey therefore produce opposing axial-rotation moments.\r\n\r\n## Posterior Axillary Fold\r\n\r\nTogether with latissimus dorsi, teres major contributes anatomically to the posterior axillary fold region.\r\n\r\nThis reflects their close spatial and functional relationship during pulling.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nTeres major is innervated primarily by the **lower subscapular nerve**, generally carrying fibers from approximately:\r\n\r\n- C5;\r\n- C6.\r\n\r\nThe lower subscapular nerve also innervates the inferior portion of subscapularis.\r\n\r\nDespite this neural relationship, teres major is not part of the rotator cuff.\r\n\r\nIts primary mechanical identity remains that of a larger humeral mover rather than a capsular stabilizer.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Shoulder Extension\r\n\r\nTeres major contributes strongly to **extension of the humerus**, especially when the arm begins in a flexed or elevated position.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- rowing;\r\n- pulldowns;\r\n- pull-ups;\r\n- straight-arm pulling.\r\n\r\nIts extension moment arm is angle-dependent.\r\n\r\nCadaveric shoulder measurements show that the moment arms of teres major change substantially as humeral elevation changes rather than remaining constant. :contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`E_MA_teres_major = f(humeral_angle)`\r\n\r\nis more defensible than one fixed exercise coefficient.\r\n\r\n## Shoulder Adduction\r\n\r\nTeres major is also a strong **adductor of the humerus**.\r\n\r\nThis action becomes especially relevant when the arm begins elevated away from the torso.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- pull-ups;\r\n- pulldowns;\r\n- high-to-low humeral pulling.\r\n\r\nThe muscle shares this function strongly with:\r\n\r\n- latissimus dorsi;\r\n- pectoralis major depending on arm position.\r\n\r\n## Internal Rotation\r\n\r\nTeres major produces **internal rotation of the humerus**.\r\n\r\nThis action is shared with:\r\n\r\n- latissimus dorsi;\r\n- subscapularis;\r\n- pectoralis major.\r\n\r\nHowever, direct internal-rotation isolation is not normally required to train teres major because much larger loading can be achieved through compound extension/adduction.\r\n\r\n## Combined Extension + Adduction\r\n\r\nMany pulling exercises do not consist of one pure anatomical action.\r\n\r\nFor example, a neutral-grip pulldown may combine:\r\n\r\n- shoulder adduction;\r\n- shoulder extension.\r\n\r\nTeres major is well positioned to contribute to both simultaneously.\r\n\r\nThis makes it especially relevant in trajectories where the elbow moves:\r\n\r\n- downward;\r\n- toward the flank.\r\n\r\n## Scapular Dependence\r\n\r\nBecause teres major originates from the scapula, scapular movement changes its origin position.\r\n\r\nDuring overhead pulling, the scapula:\r\n\r\n- rotates;\r\n- tilts;\r\n- translates.\r\n\r\nTherefore teres-major length and leverage depend not only on humeral angle relative to the trunk, but also on scapular orientation.\r\n\r\nFor KISS modelling this can usually be approximated from humeral elevation, but anatomically the true relationship is:\r\n\r\n`L_TM = f(humerus relative to scapula)`\r\n\r\nrather than simply humerus relative to torso.\r\n\r\n## No Direct Scapular Retraction\r\n\r\nTeres major inserts onto the humerus, not the medial scapular border.\r\n\r\nTherefore it does **not** directly generate scapular retraction.\r\n\r\nIn rows:\r\n\r\n- teres major handles part of glenohumeral extension/adduction;\r\n- rhomboids/middle trapezius handle scapular retraction.\r\n\r\nThis distinction should remain explicit.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nDirect longitudinal evidence specifically testing **teres-major stretch-mediated hypertrophy** is sparse.\r\n\r\nThe mechanical rationale for long-length loading is nevertheless clear.\r\n\r\nBecause teres major extends and adducts the humerus, it becomes relatively lengthened when the humerus moves toward:\r\n\r\n- elevation;\r\n- flexion;\r\n- abduction.\r\n\r\n## Overhead Position\r\n\r\nWhen the arm is elevated overhead, the distance between the:\r\n\r\n- inferior scapular origin;\r\n- proximal humeral insertion\r\n\r\ngenerally increases.\r\n\r\nThis places teres major in a relatively lengthened configuration.\r\n\r\nTherefore exercises beginning overhead can expose the muscle to long-length tension.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- pulldown;\r\n- pull-up;\r\n- straight-arm pulldown;\r\n- pullover-like movements.\r\n\r\n## Shoulder Flexion\r\n\r\nHumeral flexion opposes the extension function of teres major.\r\n\r\nThus flexed-arm starting positions generally lengthen it.\r\n\r\nA pull from high flexion toward the torso can therefore generate meaningful shortening excursion.\r\n\r\n## Abduction\r\n\r\nAbduction also opposes its adduction action.\r\n\r\nElevated abducted positions can therefore lengthen the muscle.\r\n\r\nThe exact effect depends on:\r\n\r\n- scapular upward rotation;\r\n- humeral rotation;\r\n- plane of elevation.\r\n\r\n## Internal Rotation Complication\r\n\r\nBecause teres major also internally rotates the humerus, external rotation tends to increase its length.\r\n\r\nThus the longest state may combine elements of:\r\n\r\n- humeral elevation;\r\n- abduction/flexion;\r\n- external rotation.\r\n\r\nHowever, aggressive external rotation under heavy pulling load is not necessary for hypertrophy.\r\n\r\n## Straight-Arm Pulldown\r\n\r\nA straight-arm pulldown can load teres major from:\r\n\r\n- high shoulder flexion;\r\ntoward\r\n- extension/adduction.\r\n\r\nThis provides a strong mechanical case for substantial teres-major loading across long-to-short muscle states.\r\n\r\n## Pullover\r\n\r\nPullover-type exercises can similarly load the muscle from an overhead position.\r\n\r\nThe exact contribution relative to latissimus depends on:\r\n\r\n- arm plane;\r\n- resistance direction;\r\n- scapular motion.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nThe evidence-based conclusion should remain conservative:\r\n\r\n- elevated/flexed humeral positions lengthen teres major;\r\n- overhead pulling can actively load it at long length;\r\n- direct teres-major hypertrophy evidence is limited;\r\n- no precise special SMH multiplier has been established.\r\n\r\nTherefore long-length mechanics should influence `E_LT`, but the base `smh_factor` should not be inflated solely from anatomy.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Pulldown\r\n\r\nPulldowns strongly match teres-major actions.\r\n\r\nDuring the concentric phase the humerus undergoes:\r\n\r\n- adduction;\r\n- extension depending on trajectory.\r\n\r\nTeres major works synergistically with:\r\n\r\n- latissimus dorsi;\r\n- elbow flexors indirectly through the multi-joint chain.\r\n\r\nA pulldown with the elbow travelling toward the side of the torso should generally produce substantial teres-major demand.\r\n\r\n## Pull-Up\r\n\r\nPull-ups likewise create large shoulder adduction/extension torque.\r\n\r\nTeres major should receive meaningful force allocation alongside latissimus dorsi.\r\n\r\nIt should not receive the majority of the total shoulder torque because latissimus is considerably larger and generally possesses greater overall force capacity.\r\n\r\n## Neutral-Grip Pull-Up\r\n\r\nNeutral grip often permits substantial:\r\n\r\n- humeral extension;\r\n- adduction;\r\n- ROM.\r\n\r\nThis makes it highly compatible with teres-major function.\r\n\r\n## Wide Pronated Pull-Up\r\n\r\nGreater humeral abduction changes the relative balance between:\r\n\r\n- adduction;\r\n- extension;\r\n- elbow flexion.\r\n\r\nTeres major remains substantially involved.\r\n\r\nHowever, grip width alone should not determine its coefficient.\r\n\r\nHumeral trajectory is more important.\r\n\r\n## Lat Pulldown\r\n\r\nA neutral or medium-grip pulldown should usually assign:\r\n\r\n- very high latissimus demand;\r\n- meaningful-to-high teres-major demand.\r\n\r\nThe absolute share should remain smaller for teres major because of its lower force capacity.\r\n\r\n## Straight-Arm Pulldown\r\n\r\nThis is an excellent teres-major movement mechanically because it emphasizes:\r\n\r\n- shoulder extension/adduction;\r\n- minimal elbow-flexion contribution.\r\n\r\nThe main humeral movers are:\r\n\r\n- latissimus dorsi;\r\n- teres major;\r\n- long head of triceps to a smaller degree depending on geometry.\r\n\r\n## Row\r\n\r\nTeres major can contribute substantially to rows through shoulder extension.\r\n\r\nThe amount depends strongly on elbow path.\r\n\r\n### Tucked Elbow\r\n\r\nHigher:\r\n- latissimus dorsi;\r\n- teres major.\r\n\r\n### High / Abducted Elbow\r\n\r\nHigher:\r\n- posterior deltoid.\r\n\r\nThus teres-major allocation should rise as the humeral action becomes more extension/adduction-oriented and fall as it becomes primarily horizontal abduction.\r\n\r\n## Chest-Supported Row\r\n\r\nA tucked-elbow chest-supported row can provide high teres-major demand without large spinal-extensor cost.\r\n\r\nThis is a mechanically favorable hypertrophy configuration.\r\n\r\n## Pendlay Row\r\n\r\nTeres major contributes to humeral extension but the exercise also imposes large demands on:\r\n\r\n- latissimus;\r\n- posterior deltoid;\r\n- rhomboids;\r\n- trapezius;\r\n- erector spinae;\r\n- hip extensors.\r\n\r\nThus local teres-major loading comes with higher global fatigue than supported rowing.\r\n\r\n## Face Pull\r\n\r\nFace pulls should receive low teres-major allocation.\r\n\r\nThe dominant glenohumeral actions are closer to:\r\n\r\n- horizontal abduction;\r\n- external rotation.\r\n\r\nThese oppose major teres-major functions.\r\n\r\nPosterior deltoid and posterior rotator cuff should dominate instead.\r\n\r\n## Reverse Pec Deck\r\n\r\nSimilarly, reverse pec deck is primarily horizontal abduction.\r\n\r\nTeres major should receive little direct concentric demand.\r\n\r\n## Internal Rotation Exercise\r\n\r\nCable internal rotation can recruit teres major.\r\n\r\nHowever, `subscapularis` is the more specialized cuff internal rotator, while pectoralis major and latissimus may contribute depending on setup.\r\n\r\nDirect internal rotation is therefore a poor choice if the goal is specific teres-major hypertrophy.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Dedicated Teres-Major Work Is Usually Unnecessary\r\n\r\nMost lifters performing sufficient:\r\n\r\n- pulldowns;\r\n- pull-ups;\r\n- rows;\r\n- straight-arm pulldowns\r\n\r\nalready provide substantial teres-major exposure.\r\n\r\nThere is rarely a need for a specific "teres major day" or isolation movement.\r\n\r\n## Volume\r\n\r\nTeres-major volume should generally be considered a by-product of lat-focused pulling volume.\r\n\r\nIf a program contains approximately:\r\n\r\n- 6-12 quality pulling sets per week\r\n\r\nacross vertical and horizontal pulling, teres major will usually receive substantial stimulus.\r\n\r\n## Direct Set Credit\r\n\r\nNot every back exercise should receive equal teres-major credit.\r\n\r\n### High Credit\r\n\r\n- tucked-elbow pulldown;\r\n- neutral pull-up;\r\n- straight-arm pulldown;\r\n- tucked-elbow row.\r\n\r\n### Moderate\r\n\r\n- general rows;\r\n- wide pull-ups.\r\n\r\n### Low\r\n\r\n- face pull;\r\n- reverse fly;\r\n- high-elbow posterior-delt row.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nTwo to three pulling exposures per week are generally sufficient.\r\n\r\nRecovery overlaps strongly with:\r\n\r\n- latissimus dorsi;\r\n- posterior shoulder;\r\n- elbow flexors.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\nTeres major can be trained effectively through whatever repetition ranges are used for compound pulling:\r\n\r\n- 5-8;\r\n- 6-12;\r\n- 8-15.\r\n\r\nStraight-arm cable work can be practical in:\r\n\r\n- 10-20;\r\n- 12-25.\r\n\r\nThere is no teres-major-specific repetition requirement.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nStable pulldowns and chest-supported rows can be trained near failure:\r\n\r\n- approximately 0-2 RIR.\r\n\r\nHeavy pull-ups and free rows often benefit from more technical reserve.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Complementarity\r\n\r\nFor teres major, a combination such as:\r\n\r\n1. vertical pull;\r\n2. tucked-elbow row;\r\n3. optional straight-arm pulldown\r\n\r\nalready exposes the muscle to:\r\n\r\n- adduction;\r\n- extension;\r\n- long-to-short ROM.\r\n\r\nAdditional isolation is generally redundant.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## Teres Major Is Not a Rotator-Cuff Muscle\r\n\r\nThis is probably the single most important anatomical fact.\r\n\r\nThe rotator cuff contains:\r\n\r\n- supraspinatus;\r\n- infraspinatus;\r\n- teres minor;\r\n- subscapularis.\r\n\r\nTeres **major** is excluded.\r\n\r\nIts primary role is more similar mechanically to latissimus dorsi than to teres minor.\r\n\r\n## Teres Major and Latissimus Have Nearly Identical Textbook Actions\r\n\r\nBoth:\r\n\r\n- extend;\r\n- adduct;\r\n- internally rotate the humerus.\r\n\r\nThis does not make them mechanically redundant.\r\n\r\nTheir:\r\n\r\n- origins;\r\n- architecture;\r\n- force capacities;\r\n- moment arms\r\n\r\ndiffer.\r\n\r\n## Shoulder Angle Changes Its Leverage\r\n\r\nDirect cadaveric measurements show that teres-major moment arms vary as the humerus moves through elevation. :contentReference[oaicite:3]{index=3}\r\n\r\nTherefore one fixed `E_MA` coefficient for every shoulder position would be anatomically inaccurate.\r\n\r\n## It Is a Scapula-to-Humerus Muscle\r\n\r\nThis creates a subtle distinction from latissimus dorsi.\r\n\r\nBecause the proximal attachment itself moves with the scapula, teres-major geometry is influenced directly by scapular motion.\r\n\r\n## It Can Be Strongly Active Without Being a Large Muscle\r\n\r\nTeres major is much smaller than latissimus dorsi, but favorable leverage can still allow substantial torque contribution.\r\n\r\nMuscle force sharing depends on:\r\n\r\n`PCSA × specific tension × activation × moment arm`\r\n\r\nrather than muscle size alone.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\n`teres_major` should receive demand primarily from:\r\n\r\n1. **shoulder-extension torque**;\r\n2. **shoulder-adduction torque**;\r\n3. secondary internal-rotation torque;\r\n4. glenohumeral stabilization as a secondary component.\r\n\r\nIt should receive approximately zero direct:\r\n\r\n- scapular-retraction torque;\r\n- elbow-flexion torque;\r\n- external-rotation torque.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Latissimus Dorsi\r\n\r\nThis is the dominant force-sharing relationship.\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`T_extension/adduction =\r\n    latissimus_dorsi\r\n  + teres_major\r\n  + other synergists`\r\n\r\nLatissimus should generally receive the larger absolute share because of its much larger force capacity.\r\n\r\nTeres major can nevertheless receive a relatively high **activation fraction of its own capacity**.\r\n\r\nThis distinction matters in the FCSA model:\r\n\r\n> a smaller muscle can operate near high relative recruitment while contributing less absolute systemic FCSA demand than a much larger synergist.\r\n\r\n## Relative Force vs Relative Recruitment\r\n\r\nSuppose both:\r\n\r\n- latissimus;\r\n- teres major\r\n\r\nare strongly recruited during a pulldown.\r\n\r\nIt would still be incorrect to assign equal force.\r\n\r\nThe correct distinction is:\r\n\r\n`activation_TM ≈ high`\r\n\r\nwhile:\r\n\r\n`absolute_force_TM < absolute_force_lat`\r\n\r\nbecause their force capacities differ.\r\n\r\nThis is exactly where your PCSA/FCSA layer is useful.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Posterior Deltoid\r\n\r\nBoth can extend the humerus.\r\n\r\nRelative force sharing depends strongly on humeral abduction.\r\n\r\n### Tucked / Low Elbow\r\n\r\nIncrease:\r\n- teres major;\r\n- latissimus.\r\n\r\n### High / Abducted Elbow\r\n\r\nIncrease:\r\n- posterior deltoid.\r\n\r\nThis should be a major modifier for row vectors.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Rotator Cuff\r\n\r\nTeres major produces internal rotation but is not a cuff stabilizer in the same sense as subscapularis.\r\n\r\nDuring strong teres-major contraction, the cuff must still help maintain humeral-head centering.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`teres_major demand ↑`\r\n\r\ncan coexist with:\r\n\r\n`rotator_cuffs stabilization demand ↑`.\r\n\r\nDo not substitute one for the other.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Subscapularis\r\n\r\nBoth produce internal rotation.\r\n\r\nHowever:\r\n\r\n### Teres Major\r\n\r\nLarge-movement role:\r\n- extension;\r\n- adduction;\r\n- internal rotation.\r\n\r\n### Subscapularis\r\n\r\nCuff role:\r\n- internal rotation;\r\n- joint compression;\r\n- humeral-head stabilization.\r\n\r\nTherefore direct internal-rotation torque should be shared, but stabilization remains heavily cuff-dependent.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Allocation\r\n\r\n### Weighted Neutral-Grip Pull-Up\r\n\r\nHigh teres-major demand.\r\n\r\nVery high:\r\n- latissimus.\r\n\r\nAlso substantial:\r\n- elbow flexors;\r\n- cuff stabilization.\r\n\r\n### Wide Pronated-Grip Pull-Up\r\n\r\nModerate-to-high teres-major demand.\r\n\r\nExact allocation depends on humeral trajectory.\r\n\r\n### Neutral-Grip Lat Pulldown\r\n\r\nHigh teres-major demand.\r\n\r\nVery high:\r\n- latissimus.\r\n\r\n### Straight-Arm Cable Pulldown\r\n\r\nHigh-to-very-high teres-major specificity relative to most exercises.\r\n\r\nVery high:\r\n- latissimus.\r\n\r\nLow:\r\n- elbow-flexor demand.\r\n\r\n### Chest-Supported Dumbbell Row\r\n\r\nModerate-to-high if elbows remain tucked.\r\n\r\nLower if elbow path becomes abducted.\r\n\r\n### Wide-Grip Seated Cable Row\r\n\r\nModerate, depending on elbow height.\r\n\r\nHigher elbow path shifts demand toward:\r\n- posterior deltoid;\r\n- scapular retractors.\r\n\r\n### Pendlay Row\r\n\r\nModerate-to-high teres-major demand, but with substantial systemic cost.\r\n\r\n### Cable Face Pull\r\n\r\nVery low teres-major concentric demand.\r\n\r\n### Reverse Pec Deck\r\n\r\nVery low.\r\n\r\n## Length-Tension Model\r\n\r\nA first-order model should depend primarily on humeral elevation relative to the scapula.\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_TM =\r\n    f(shoulder_flexion/elevation,\r\n      abduction,\r\n      axial_rotation,\r\n      scapular_orientation)`\r\n\r\nQualitatively:\r\n\r\n### Longer\r\n\r\n- humerus elevated/flexed;\r\n- humerus abducted;\r\n- some external rotation.\r\n\r\n### Intermediate\r\n\r\n- arm near torso.\r\n\r\n### Shorter\r\n\r\n- humerus extended;\r\n- adducted;\r\n- internally rotated.\r\n\r\nFor KISS implementation, humeral elevation can carry most of the weight.\r\n\r\n## E_MA Model\r\n\r\nShoulder moment-arm studies directly show angle-dependent leverage for teres major. :contentReference[oaicite:4]{index=4}\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`E_MA_TM = f(humeral_elevation, movement_plane)`\r\n\r\nshould vary across ROM.\r\n\r\nThis is especially relevant when comparing:\r\n\r\n- row;\r\n- pulldown;\r\n- straight-arm pulldown.\r\n\r\nThey can generate similar peak activation while exposing the muscle to different leverage profiles.\r\n\r\n## FCSA Interpretation\r\n\r\nTeres-major FCSA should be interpreted as a real force-capacity constraint.\r\n\r\nBecause the muscle is smaller than latissimus, a demanding pull can use a large fraction of available teres-major capacity without teres major representing a large fraction of total external force.\r\n\r\nThis means:\r\n\r\n`relative_recruitment_TM`\r\n\r\nand:\r\n\r\n`systemic_FCSA_demand_TM`\r\n\r\nshould not be conflated.\r\n\r\n## Scapular Reference Frame\r\n\r\nBecause teres major originates on the scapula:\r\n\r\n`shoulder_angle_for_TM`\r\n\r\nis most correctly a **humerus-vs-scapula** angle.\r\n\r\nUsing humerus-vs-torso is a simplification.\r\n\r\nFor most Agonez exercise vectors this simplification is acceptable, but at high elevation the difference becomes more important because the scapula upwardly rotates substantially.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nTeres major has a mechanically credible long-length exposure in overhead pulling.\r\n\r\nHowever, direct longitudinal hypertrophy evidence is scarce.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n- `E_LT` can be high in overhead positions;\r\n- loaded overhead-to-adducted ROM is mechanically attractive;\r\n- base `smh_factor` should remain conservative rather than inferred from latissimus or other muscles.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **inferior-scapular origin and proximal-humeral insertion:** high;\r\n- **lower-subscapular-nerve innervation:** high;\r\n- **shoulder-extension function:** high; :contentReference[oaicite:5]{index=5}\r\n- **shoulder-adduction function:** high; :contentReference[oaicite:6]{index=6}\r\n- **internal-rotation function:** high; :contentReference[oaicite:7]{index=7}\r\n- **strong functional synergy with latissimus dorsi:** high;\r\n- **angle-dependent shoulder moment arms:** directly measured experimentally; :contentReference[oaicite:8]{index=8}\r\n- **high involvement in extension/adduction pulling:** biomechanically strong;\r\n- **precise teres-major vs latissimus force sharing:** insufficiently quantified for generic exercise coefficients;\r\n- **direct teres-major hypertrophy literature:** sparse;\r\n- **stretch-mediated hypertrophy magnitude:** unestablished.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **teres major = a relatively small but mechanically important scapula-to-humerus extensor, adductor and internal rotator that works as a close synergist of latissimus dorsi, with high relative recruitment possible during tucked-elbow and overhead pulling but substantially lower absolute force capacity than the latissimus.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/TeresMajor}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	16.20	16.00	0.961	4.68	4.50
38	biceps_femoris_short_head	Musculus biceps femoris caput breve	Lower	Hamstrings	285.00	269.89	23.00	Unipennate	0.475	0.525	medium	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\nThe **biceps femoris short head** is the monoarticular component of the lateral hamstring complex.\r\n\r\nAlthough it shares its distal tendon and name with the `biceps_femoris_long_head`, it is anatomically and mechanically distinct.\r\n\r\nThe most important distinction is simple:\r\n\r\n- **long head** crosses the hip and knee;\r\n- **short head** crosses only the knee.\r\n\r\nThe short head originates directly from the femur rather than the pelvis.\r\n\r\nConsequently, it:\r\n\r\n- contributes to **knee flexion**;\r\n- contributes to **external rotation of the tibia** when the knee is flexed;\r\n- does **not** generate meaningful hip-extension torque;\r\n- is not directly lengthened or shortened by changing hip angle.\r\n\r\nThis distinction has major consequences for exercise modelling.\r\n\r\nA hip hinge such as an RDL can strongly load the biceps femoris long head while providing essentially no direct contractile demand to the short head.\r\n\r\nBy contrast, a leg curl can strongly load both heads because both cross the knee.\r\n\r\nThe short head therefore represents a useful example of why the label "hamstrings" is too coarse for a detailed biomechanical model.\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\n## Origin\r\n\r\nThe short head originates from the posterior and lateral femur, primarily from:\r\n\r\n- the **lateral lip of the linea aspera**;\r\n- the **lateral supracondylar line / ridge of the femur**.\r\n\r\nIts origin is therefore substantially more distal than that of the long head.\r\n\r\nBecause it originates on the femur rather than the pelvis, the short head does not cross the hip joint.\r\n\r\nThis anatomical fact determines most of its functional differences from the long head.\r\n\r\nThe muscle lies deep to the long head through much of its course.\r\n\r\n## Insertion\r\n\r\nFibers of the short head converge distally with the aponeurotic and tendinous structures of the long head.\r\n\r\nThe combined biceps femoris tendon inserts primarily onto the **lateral aspect of the head of the fibula**.\r\n\r\nThe distal tendon has a complex architecture and can form multiple expansions around the lateral knee.\r\n\r\nFibers may also contribute to adjacent fascial and tibial structures.\r\n\r\nThe relationship of the tendon to the fibular collateral ligament and common fibular nerve is clinically important.\r\n\r\nBecause the insertion lies lateral to the knee's rotational axis, contraction can produce both:\r\n\r\n- knee flexion;\r\n- external rotation of the tibia when rotation is mechanically available.\r\n\r\n## Relationship to the Long Head\r\n\r\nThe two heads:\r\n\r\n- have different origins;\r\n- have different innervation;\r\n- differ in joint crossings;\r\n- share a common distal tendon system.\r\n\r\nThey should therefore not be treated as two identical regions of one homogeneous actuator.\r\n\r\nFrom an exercise perspective, their overlap is greatest during knee flexion.\r\n\r\nTheir mechanical behavior diverges substantially during hip-extension tasks.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nThe biceps femoris short head is innervated primarily by the **common fibular division of the sciatic nerve**, commonly associated with spinal roots **L5-S2**.\r\n\r\nThis differs from the long head, which is supplied by the **tibial division of the sciatic nerve**.\r\n\r\nThis split innervation is unusual within a named two-headed muscle.\r\n\r\nIt reinforces the developmental and functional distinction between the two heads.\r\n\r\nFor database purposes:\r\n\r\n- `biceps_femoris_long_head` and `biceps_femoris_short_head` should remain independent muscular entities;\r\n- their neural and mechanical properties should not be merged merely because they share part of the distal tendon.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Knee Flexion\r\n\r\nThe primary function of the short head is **knee flexion**.\r\n\r\nIt contributes alongside:\r\n\r\n- biceps femoris long head;\r\n- semitendinosus;\r\n- semimembranosus;\r\n- gastrocnemius.\r\n\r\nBecause it does not cross the hip, its operating length during knee flexion is much less dependent on hip position than that of the other major hamstrings.\r\n\r\nThe main joint variable affecting its musculotendinous length is therefore the **knee angle**.\r\n\r\nAs the knee approaches extension, the muscle is relatively lengthened.\r\n\r\nAs the knee flexes, it becomes progressively shorter.\r\n\r\n## External Rotation of the Tibia\r\n\r\nThe short head contributes to **external rotation of the tibia relative to the femur** when the knee is flexed.\r\n\r\nThis function results from the lateral fibular insertion of the biceps femoris complex.\r\n\r\nIt contrasts with the medial hamstrings:\r\n\r\n- semitendinosus;\r\n- semimembranosus;\r\n\r\nwhich contribute to internal tibial rotation.\r\n\r\nThis creates a lateral-versus-medial hamstring functional opposition around the knee.\r\n\r\nThe rotational function becomes more relevant when the knee is sufficiently flexed to permit axial rotation.\r\n\r\nNear full knee extension, passive and ligamentous constraints substantially limit tibial rotation.\r\n\r\n## Knee Stabilization\r\n\r\nThe short head contributes to dynamic stabilization of the posterolateral knee.\r\n\r\nThrough tension transmitted via the biceps femoris tendon complex, it can contribute to control of:\r\n\r\n- tibial rotation;\r\n- varus-related forces;\r\n- rapid knee flexion-extension transitions.\r\n\r\nThis role is particularly relevant during athletic tasks where knee rotation and flexion occur simultaneously.\r\n\r\n## No Hip Extension\r\n\r\nThe short head does **not cross the hip joint**.\r\n\r\nIt should therefore receive no meaningful primary hip-extension allocation during:\r\n\r\n- Romanian deadlift;\r\n- stiff-leg deadlift;\r\n- good morning;\r\n- hip thrust;\r\n- back extension;\r\n- other pure hip-extension tasks.\r\n\r\nAny activity recorded experimentally during such tasks should not automatically be interpreted as meaningful shortening work by the short head.\r\n\r\nThis is one of the most important distinctions for computational exercise modelling.\r\n\r\n## Monoarticular Behavior\r\n\r\nBecause only one joint directly affects its musculotendinous length, the short head is mechanically simpler than the long head.\r\n\r\nIts length-tension state can be approximated primarily from:\r\n\r\n- knee angle;\r\n- local contractile state;\r\n- resistance profile.\r\n\r\nThere is no need for the same hip-angle correction required by the long head.\r\n\r\nThis does not mean recruitment is entirely independent of hip position.\r\n\r\nChanging whole-body position can indirectly alter:\r\n\r\n- neural strategy;\r\n- force sharing among synergists;\r\n- knee torque requirements;\r\n- pelvic stabilization demands.\r\n\r\nHowever, hip angle does not directly lengthen the short head.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nThe short head provides an important counterexample to simplistic interpretations of long-muscle-length training.\r\n\r\nFor the biarticular hamstrings, hip flexion increases muscle length.\r\n\r\nFor the short head, it does not.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n> sitting down does not inherently lengthen the biceps femoris short head.\r\n\r\nIts relevant lengthening occurs primarily through **knee extension**.\r\n\r\n## Knee Extension as the Long-Length Position\r\n\r\nThe short head reaches relatively greater length as the knee approaches extension.\r\n\r\nFor hypertrophy-oriented knee-flexion exercises, meaningful resistance near this region is therefore theoretically attractive.\r\n\r\nA leg curl that produces substantial knee-flexion torque close to knee extension may expose the short head to:\r\n\r\n- relatively long muscle length;\r\n- active tension;\r\n- high mechanical demand.\r\n\r\nConversely, an exercise that becomes almost unloaded near knee extension and produces most resistance only in deep knee flexion emphasizes shorter muscle lengths.\r\n\r\n## Seated vs Prone Leg Curl\r\n\r\nThe often-cited advantage of seated leg curls must be interpreted muscle by muscle.\r\n\r\nHip flexion in the seated position lengthens:\r\n\r\n- biceps femoris long head;\r\n- semitendinosus;\r\n- semimembranosus.\r\n\r\nIt does not similarly lengthen biceps femoris short head because the short head originates on the femur.\r\n\r\nTraining studies comparing seated and prone leg curls show a larger hypertrophic advantage of seated training for the biarticular hamstrings.\r\n\r\nThe short head does not demonstrate the same clear seated-position advantage.\r\n\r\nThis is exactly what its anatomy predicts.\r\n\r\nTherefore, for `biceps_femoris_short_head`:\r\n\r\n- **knee angle and resistance profile** matter more directly than hip angle for muscle length;\r\n- seated leg curl should not automatically receive a higher `E_LT` solely because the hip is flexed.\r\n\r\n## Resistance Near Knee Extension\r\n\r\nFor short-head hypertrophy, a particularly relevant question is:\r\n\r\n> how much knee-flexion torque does the exercise impose while the knee is still relatively extended?\r\n\r\nA machine whose resistance curve remains meaningful in this region may provide a strong lengthened-position stimulus.\r\n\r\nA machine that is very easy near extension and hardest only at deep knee flexion may shift more loading toward shorter muscle lengths.\r\n\r\nThus, machine geometry can matter as much as the simple label:\r\n\r\n- seated;\r\n- prone;\r\n- standing.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Strength\r\n\r\nDirect short-head-specific hypertrophy evidence is considerably thinner than evidence for global hamstring hypertrophy or the long head.\r\n\r\nThe muscle is:\r\n\r\n- relatively small;\r\n- deep to the long head proximally;\r\n- difficult to assess with conventional surface EMG;\r\n- less frequently analyzed separately in longitudinal resistance-training studies.\r\n\r\nTherefore, strong mechanistic conclusions should be avoided.\r\n\r\nThe most defensible interpretation is:\r\n\r\n- knee extension increases short-head length;\r\n- hip flexion does not;\r\n- resistance near extended knee positions is mechanically relevant;\r\n- direct evidence quantifying a unique stretch-mediated hypertrophy advantage remains limited.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Leg Curls Are the Primary Direct Exercise\r\n\r\nBecause the short head acts only across the knee, direct knee-flexion exercises are its main resistance-training stimulus.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- seated leg curl;\r\n- prone leg curl;\r\n- standing single-leg curl;\r\n- Nordic hamstring exercise;\r\n- glute-ham raise;\r\n- sliding leg curl.\r\n\r\nA pure hip-extension exercise should not be considered direct short-head training.\r\n\r\n## Seated Leg Curl\r\n\r\nThe seated leg curl strongly trains knee flexion and therefore loads the short head.\r\n\r\nHowever, its special lengthened-position advantage compared with prone curling primarily applies to the **biarticular hamstrings**.\r\n\r\nFor the short head, any superiority would have to arise from factors such as:\r\n\r\n- resistance profile;\r\n- machine geometry;\r\n- stabilization;\r\n- available ROM;\r\n- force-sharing differences;\r\n\r\nrather than direct lengthening from hip flexion.\r\n\r\nThis is an important distinction.\r\n\r\nA seated leg curl may still be an excellent short-head exercise.\r\n\r\nIt is simply not excellent **because sitting lengthens the short head**.\r\n\r\n## Prone Leg Curl\r\n\r\nThe prone leg curl is mechanically valid for short-head hypertrophy.\r\n\r\nUnlike the long head, the short head does not become substantially shorter merely because the hip is less flexed.\r\n\r\nConsequently, prone curling may compare more favorably for this muscle than it does for the long head.\r\n\r\nIts effectiveness depends on:\r\n\r\n- knee ROM;\r\n- resistance curve;\r\n- pelvis stability;\r\n- pad position;\r\n- ability to approach failure without compensation.\r\n\r\n## Standing Leg Curl\r\n\r\nA unilateral standing leg curl provides direct knee-flexion torque with relatively little systemic fatigue.\r\n\r\nIts advantages include:\r\n\r\n- unilateral loading;\r\n- easy side-to-side comparison;\r\n- low stabilization demand when well supported.\r\n\r\nIts limitation is often machine-specific resistance geometry.\r\n\r\nDepending on the lever arm, resistance near knee extension can be low.\r\n\r\n## Nordic Hamstring Exercise\r\n\r\nNordic hamstring exercise produces very high eccentric knee-flexor demand.\r\n\r\nSince the short head crosses the knee, it participates in resisting knee extension.\r\n\r\nThe Nordic can therefore provide substantial short-head stimulus.\r\n\r\nImportantly, this distinguishes it from pure hip hinges.\r\n\r\nA Nordic may train both heads of biceps femoris through knee-flexion mechanics, whereas an RDL strongly favors the long head because only the long head crosses the hip.\r\n\r\nNordics are particularly useful for:\r\n\r\n- eccentric knee-flexor strength;\r\n- athletic preparation;\r\n- high-force deceleration capacity.\r\n\r\nThey are less convenient for conventional hypertrophy progression because:\r\n\r\n- resistance is difficult to quantify precisely;\r\n- full concentric repetitions may be impossible for many trainees;\r\n- eccentric loading can be extremely demanding;\r\n- soreness can be substantial.\r\n\r\nAssisted Nordic variants can improve load scalability.\r\n\r\n## Sliding Leg Curl\r\n\r\nSliding or suspension-based leg curls train knee flexion while also requiring hip-extension stabilization.\r\n\r\nThe short head contributes through knee flexion.\r\n\r\nThe long head and other biarticular hamstrings may additionally contribute to hip stabilization.\r\n\r\nTherefore, the short-head stimulus is meaningful but occurs within a more complex kinetic chain than machine curls.\r\n\r\n## Romanian Deadlift\r\n\r\nAn RDL should provide approximately **zero direct short-head hip-extension demand**.\r\n\r\nThis point deserves emphasis.\r\n\r\nThe exercise heavily loads:\r\n\r\n- biceps femoris long head;\r\n- semitendinosus;\r\n- semimembranosus;\r\n- gluteus maximus;\r\n- adductor magnus;\r\n- spinal extensors.\r\n\r\nBut because the short head originates from the femur and inserts below the knee, hip extension does not directly shorten it.\r\n\r\nIf the knee angle remains nearly fixed, the short head performs little dynamic contractile work.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n> `RDL ≠ complete biceps femoris exercise`.\r\n\r\nIt is principally a long-head exercise within the biceps femoris pair.\r\n\r\n## Squat\r\n\r\nSquats also provide limited direct short-head stimulus.\r\n\r\nThe short head does not cross the hip, and during the squat the knee primarily moves through flexion and extension under large quadriceps demand.\r\n\r\nHamstrings can contribute to joint stabilization, but this is not equivalent to direct loaded knee flexion.\r\n\r\nSquats should therefore receive relatively low short-head hypertrophy allocation.\r\n\r\n## Foot and Tibial Rotation\r\n\r\nExternally rotating the tibia can theoretically increase the mechanical contribution of the lateral hamstrings.\r\n\r\nHowever, intentionally exaggerating foot rotation during leg curls is not a reliable method of selectively isolating the short head.\r\n\r\nReasons include:\r\n\r\n- foot position does not map perfectly to tibial rotation;\r\n- the knee's rotational freedom changes with flexion angle;\r\n- both biceps femoris heads share lateral rotational function;\r\n- extreme rotation can create unnecessary joint stress.\r\n\r\nA neutral or naturally comfortable lower-leg orientation is generally preferable.\r\n\r\nExercise selection and resistance profile matter far more than aggressive foot manipulation.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Count Knee-Flexion Work, Not Hip-Hinge Work\r\n\r\nFor the short head, weekly training exposure should be derived primarily from **knee-flexion exercises**.\r\n\r\nStrong contributors include:\r\n\r\n- seated leg curl;\r\n- prone leg curl;\r\n- standing leg curl;\r\n- Nordic;\r\n- sliding leg curl;\r\n- glute-ham raise.\r\n\r\nWeak or negligible direct contributors include:\r\n\r\n- RDL;\r\n- good morning;\r\n- hip thrust;\r\n- back extension;\r\n- stiff-leg deadlift when the knee angle is held nearly constant.\r\n\r\nThis is a major difference from `biceps_femoris_long_head`.\r\n\r\n## Weekly Volume\r\n\r\nA practical starting point is generally supplied automatically by overall hamstring knee-flexion programming.\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n\r\n- 4-8 hard weekly knee-flexion sets\r\n\r\nmay already provide substantial short-head exposure.\r\n\r\nThe exact requirement depends on:\r\n\r\n- proximity to failure;\r\n- exercise selection;\r\n- resistance profile;\r\n- training status;\r\n- total athletic workload.\r\n\r\nThere is little reason to prescribe isolated short-head volume independently from all other knee flexors unless the database or a rehabilitation context requires that level of specificity.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nApproximately **2 weekly knee-flexion exposures** are a practical default.\r\n\r\nExamples:\r\n\r\n### Session A\r\n- seated leg curl.\r\n\r\n### Session B\r\n- Nordic or prone leg curl.\r\n\r\nThis creates variation in loading profile without introducing unnecessary exercise redundancy.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\nMachine curls work well across broad ranges:\r\n\r\n- 6-10 repetitions for heavier work;\r\n- 8-15 repetitions as a general default;\r\n- 15-25+ repetitions for lighter isolation work.\r\n\r\nShort-head hypertrophy does not require a unique repetition range.\r\n\r\nThe relevant criteria are:\r\n\r\n- adequate mechanical tension;\r\n- sufficient effort;\r\n- stable technique;\r\n- meaningful ROM.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nMachine leg curls can generally be trained close to muscular failure.\r\n\r\nTypical hypertrophy-oriented work can use approximately:\r\n\r\n- **0-3 RIR**.\r\n\r\nNordics should be interpreted differently because technical or eccentric failure can occur without conventional concentric failure.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Diversity\r\n\r\nExercise diversity should focus on resistance characteristics rather than trying to manipulate hip position specifically for the short head.\r\n\r\nA useful combination could include:\r\n\r\n- one stable machine curl;\r\n- one high-force eccentric knee-flexion exercise.\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n\r\n- seated or prone leg curl;\r\n- Nordic hamstring curl.\r\n\r\nThere is little short-head-specific reason to program both seated and prone curls simply to create different hip angles.\r\n\r\nIf both are used, the justification should instead be:\r\n\r\n- different resistance profiles;\r\n- comfort;\r\n- fatigue distribution;\r\n- machine availability;\r\n- regional stimulus to the broader hamstring group.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Long-Head Training\r\n\r\nA complete hamstring program often combines:\r\n\r\n1. knee flexion;\r\n2. hip extension.\r\n\r\nThis pairing has an asymmetric effect on the two biceps femoris heads.\r\n\r\n### Knee Flexion\r\nTrains both long and short heads.\r\n\r\n### Hip Extension\r\nTrains the long head but not the short head directly.\r\n\r\nTherefore, a program consisting only of RDLs or good mornings can leave the short head substantially undertrained despite large total "hamstring" loading.\r\n\r\nConversely, a program containing leg curls already provides meaningful work for both heads.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## It Is Not a "True Hamstring" by the Classical Definition\r\n\r\nA classical hamstring is generally defined by several features including:\r\n\r\n- origin from the ischial region;\r\n- crossing both hip and knee;\r\n- tibial-division sciatic innervation;\r\n- participation in hip extension and knee flexion.\r\n\r\nThe short head fails several of these criteria:\r\n\r\n- it originates from the femur;\r\n- it does not cross the hip;\r\n- it is innervated by the common fibular division of the sciatic nerve.\r\n\r\nTherefore, despite being grouped anatomically with the hamstrings, it is sometimes excluded from the strict definition of a "true hamstring."\r\n\r\n## Same Muscle Name, Different Nerve\r\n\r\nThe two heads of biceps femoris have different major nerve divisions:\r\n\r\n- long head → tibial division;\r\n- short head → common fibular division.\r\n\r\nThis is an unusually clear reminder that a conventional anatomical muscle name does not always correspond to one developmental or neuromuscular unit.\r\n\r\n## It Begins on the Femur\r\n\r\nUnlike the other conventional hamstrings, the short head originates partway down the thigh.\r\n\r\nIt is therefore essentially a **femur-to-fibula knee muscle** rather than a pelvis-to-leg muscle.\r\n\r\nThis explains almost its entire distinctive biomechanics.\r\n\r\n## Hip Position Does Not Directly Change Its Length\r\n\r\nSitting versus lying dramatically changes the length of the biarticular hamstrings.\r\n\r\nFor the short head, the femoral origin moves together with the femur.\r\n\r\nThus hip flexion does not produce the proximal separation between origin and insertion that occurs in the long head.\r\n\r\nThis provides a particularly clean anatomical demonstration of why joint crossing matters for muscle-length modelling.\r\n\r\n## It Shares a Tendon With a Mechanically Different Head\r\n\r\nNear the knee, long and short heads become part of the same biceps femoris tendon complex.\r\n\r\nThey therefore converge on a shared distal mechanical endpoint despite having:\r\n\r\n- different origins;\r\n- different nerve supply;\r\n- different joint actions.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\n`biceps_femoris_short_head` should be modeled primarily as a **monoarticular knee-flexion actuator**.\r\n\r\nIts fundamental mechanical state should depend mainly on:\r\n\r\n- knee angle;\r\n- knee-flexion torque;\r\n- resistance profile;\r\n- tibial rotational context.\r\n\r\nUnlike the long head, hip angle should not directly enter its muscle-length calculation.\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n### `biceps_femoris_long_head`\r\n\r\n`E_LT = f(hip_angle, knee_angle)`\r\n\r\n### `biceps_femoris_short_head`\r\n\r\n`E_LT ≈ f(knee_angle)`\r\n\r\nHip position can still indirectly alter recruitment through force-sharing and whole-limb mechanics, but it should not directly alter short-head anatomical length.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Allocation\r\n\r\n### Seated Leg Curl\r\n\r\nHigh short-head demand.\r\n\r\nPrimary mechanism:\r\n\r\n- knee-flexion torque.\r\n\r\nNo special length bonus should be assigned simply because the hip is flexed.\r\n\r\n### Prone Leg Curl\r\n\r\nHigh short-head demand.\r\n\r\nPotentially more comparable to seated curling for the short head than for the biarticular hamstrings.\r\n\r\n### Standing Leg Curl\r\n\r\nHigh short-head demand if the resistance curve remains meaningful through sufficient ROM.\r\n\r\n### Nordic Hamstring Curl\r\n\r\nHigh eccentric short-head demand through resisted knee extension.\r\n\r\n### Romanian Deadlift\r\n\r\nNear-zero direct short-head demand if knee angle remains essentially fixed.\r\n\r\n### Good Morning\r\n\r\nNear-zero direct short-head demand for the same reason.\r\n\r\n### Squat\r\n\r\nLow direct short-head hypertrophy demand despite participation in knee stabilization.\r\n\r\n## Important Correction to Generic Hamstring Models\r\n\r\nA generic model might assign an RDL demand vector to all four conventional hamstring muscles.\r\n\r\nThat would be incorrect.\r\n\r\nThe correct structural distinction is:\r\n\r\n| Muscle | Knee Flexion | Hip Extension |\r\n|---|---:|---:|\r\n| Biceps femoris long head | Yes | Yes |\r\n| Biceps femoris short head | Yes | No |\r\n| Semitendinosus | Yes | Yes |\r\n| Semimembranosus | Yes | Yes |\r\n\r\nTherefore, any hip-hinge recruitment vector should strongly suppress or eliminate `biceps_femoris_short_head`.\r\n\r\nThis is one of the cases where keeping muscles separate materially improves model fidelity.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nFor `biceps_femoris_short_head`, a high stretch-mediated hypertrophy factor should not be inferred from seated-leg-curl research on the hamstrings as a group.\r\n\r\nThe more defensible model is:\r\n\r\n- long muscle length occurs primarily near **knee extension**;\r\n- hip flexion does not materially change anatomical short-head length;\r\n- exercise resistance near knee extension is therefore especially relevant;\r\n- direct longitudinal evidence isolating short-head long-length hypertrophy remains limited.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **femoral origin:** high;\r\n- **fibular insertion:** high;\r\n- **common fibular division innervation:** high;\r\n- **knee-flexion function:** high;\r\n- **external tibial rotation:** high;\r\n- **absence of hip-extension function:** high;\r\n- **lack of direct hip-angle effect on muscle length:** high;\r\n- **similar seated-versus-prone hypertrophy response:** supported by available longitudinal data;\r\n- **optimal short-head resistance profile:** mechanistically plausible but incompletely established;\r\n- **short-head-specific stretch-mediated hypertrophy magnitude:** limited direct evidence.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **biceps femoris short head = monoarticular lateral knee flexor and external rotator whose length depends primarily on knee angle, not hip angle, and which therefore responds fundamentally differently from the long head to hip-dominant exercises.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/Hamstrings}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	11.03	12.30	0.977	24.47	23.91
39	semitendinosus	Musculus semitendinosus	Lower	Hamstrings	535.00	506.63	18.00	Fusiform/Long-fibered	0.450	0.550	extreme_high	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\nThe **semitendinosus** is a long, medial, biarticular hamstring muscle crossing both the hip and knee.\r\n\r\nIts major functions are:\r\n\r\n- **hip extension**;\r\n- **knee flexion**;\r\n- **internal rotation of the tibia when the knee is flexed**;\r\n- dynamic stabilization of the medial/posteromedial knee.\r\n\r\nIt should be distinguished from the other hamstrings:\r\n\r\n- `semimembranosus`;\r\n- `biceps_femoris_long_head`;\r\n- `biceps_femoris_short_head`.\r\n\r\nSemitendinosus is especially notable for its architecture. Compared with the broader and more pennate semimembranosus, semitendinosus is characterized by relatively long fascicles and a long distal tendon, giving it substantial shortening and excursion capacity. Human anatomical studies demonstrate marked architectural differences between individual hamstrings rather than one generic hamstring design. :contentReference[oaicite:0]{index=0}\r\n\r\nBecause semitendinosus crosses both hip and knee, its muscle length depends simultaneously on:\r\n\r\n- hip angle;\r\n- knee angle.\r\n\r\nIt is lengthened by:\r\n\r\n- hip flexion;\r\n- knee extension.\r\n\r\nIt is shortened by:\r\n\r\n- hip extension;\r\n- knee flexion.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_semitendinosus = f(hip_angle, knee_angle)`\r\n\r\nis essential for mechanically credible modelling.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **semitendinosus = a long-fascicled medial biarticular hamstring producing hip-extension and knee-flexion torque, with additional internal-tibial-rotation and medial-knee stabilization roles, and particularly strong responsiveness to knee-flexion training performed at long muscle lengths.**\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\n## Origin\r\n\r\nSemitendinosus originates primarily from the **ischial tuberosity**.\r\n\r\nIts proximal attachment is closely associated with the long head of biceps femoris.\r\n\r\nThe muscle then descends through the posteromedial thigh.\r\n\r\n## Insertion\r\n\r\nDistally, semitendinosus forms a long tendon that passes around the medial knee and inserts onto the proximal medial tibia as part of the **pes anserinus**.\r\n\r\nThe pes anserinus also contains tendons of:\r\n\r\n- sartorius;\r\n- gracilis.\r\n\r\nThis insertion places semitendinosus medial to the knee axis and gives it leverage for:\r\n\r\n- knee flexion;\r\n- internal tibial rotation.\r\n\r\n## Tendinous Inscription\r\n\r\nSemitendinosus commonly contains a transverse tendinous inscription or raphe dividing portions of the muscle belly.\r\n\r\nThis contributes to its distinctive internal architecture.\r\n\r\nThe muscle should therefore not be visualized as one perfectly uniform bundle from ischium to tibia.\r\n\r\n## Architecture\r\n\r\nSemitendinosus differs substantially from semimembranosus in internal design.\r\n\r\nCadaveric architectural studies show long fascicles and relatively low pennation compared with more force-oriented hamstring structures. Architecture also varies between proximal, middle and distal portions of the muscle. :contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}\r\n\r\nThis gives semitendinosus a relatively strong bias toward:\r\n\r\n- excursion;\r\n- shortening velocity;\r\n- operation through large joint ranges.\r\n\r\nIt still possesses meaningful force capacity, but its architecture differs from the larger-PCSA semimembranosus.\r\n\r\n## Relationship With Semimembranosus\r\n\r\nBoth are medial hamstrings.\r\n\r\nHowever:\r\n\r\n### Semitendinosus\r\n\r\nRelatively:\r\n- long-fascicled;\r\n- long distal tendon;\r\n- excursion-oriented;\r\n- frequently strongly recruited during knee-flexion tasks.\r\n\r\n### Semimembranosus\r\n\r\nRelatively:\r\n- broader;\r\n- more force-oriented;\r\n- larger stabilizing structure around the posteromedial knee.\r\n\r\nThus they should not automatically receive identical exercise vectors.\r\n\r\n## Relationship With Biceps Femoris\r\n\r\nSemitendinosus and biceps femoris create an important medial-lateral balance around the flexed knee.\r\n\r\n### Semitendinosus\r\n\r\nBias:\r\n- internal tibial rotation.\r\n\r\n### Biceps Femoris\r\n\r\nBias:\r\n- external tibial rotation.\r\n\r\nBoth contribute to knee flexion but produce opposing transverse-plane effects.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nSemitendinosus is innervated by the **tibial division of the sciatic nerve**, primarily from approximately:\r\n\r\n- L5;\r\n- S1;\r\n- S2.\r\n\r\nThis is shared broadly with:\r\n\r\n- semimembranosus;\r\n- biceps femoris long head.\r\n\r\nThe short head of biceps femoris differs because it receives innervation from the common fibular division.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Knee Flexion\r\n\r\nSemitendinosus is a major **knee flexor**.\r\n\r\nIt works synergistically with:\r\n\r\n- semimembranosus;\r\n- biceps femoris long head;\r\n- biceps femoris short head;\r\n- gastrocnemius;\r\n- gracilis;\r\n- sartorius.\r\n\r\nIts contribution varies substantially with:\r\n\r\n- hip angle;\r\n- knee angle;\r\n- tibial rotation;\r\n- exercise type.\r\n\r\nExperimental imaging studies show that individual hamstrings are recruited differently depending on whether the task emphasizes knee flexion or hip extension. :contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}\r\n\r\n## Hip Extension\r\n\r\nBecause it crosses posterior to the hip axis, semitendinosus also generates **hip-extension torque**.\r\n\r\nIt contributes during:\r\n\r\n- Romanian deadlifts;\r\n- stiff-leg deadlifts;\r\n- hip-dominant back extensions;\r\n- sprinting;\r\n- other posterior-chain tasks.\r\n\r\nIts synergists include:\r\n\r\n- gluteus maximus;\r\n- semimembranosus;\r\n- biceps femoris long head;\r\n- adductor magnus.\r\n\r\n## Internal Tibial Rotation\r\n\r\nWith the knee flexed, semitendinosus can internally rotate the tibia relative to the femur.\r\n\r\nThis action is shared with:\r\n\r\n- semimembranosus;\r\n- sartorius;\r\n- gracilis.\r\n\r\nIt opposes the external-rotation action of biceps femoris.\r\n\r\nThis is mechanically important because the hamstrings are not simply one sagittal-plane knee-flexion group.\r\n\r\n## Knee Stabilization\r\n\r\nSemitendinosus can contribute to stabilization of the knee by resisting unwanted:\r\n\r\n- anterior tibial translation;\r\n- external tibial rotation;\r\n- valgus/rotational perturbations depending on joint position.\r\n\r\nThis role becomes particularly relevant in:\r\n\r\n- running;\r\n- cutting;\r\n- deceleration;\r\n- ACL-related rehabilitation contexts.\r\n\r\nThe muscle can therefore generate meaningful force even when knee ROM is small.\r\n\r\n## Biarticular Mechanics\r\n\r\n### Hip Flexion\r\n\r\nLengthens semitendinosus proximally.\r\n\r\n### Hip Extension\r\n\r\nShortens it proximally.\r\n\r\n### Knee Extension\r\n\r\nLengthens it distally.\r\n\r\n### Knee Flexion\r\n\r\nShortens it distally.\r\n\r\nTherefore the longest practical configuration is approximately:\r\n\r\n> **hip flexion + knee extension.**\r\n\r\nThe shortest configuration is approximately:\r\n\r\n> **hip extension + knee flexion.**\r\n\r\n## Active Insufficiency\r\n\r\nSimultaneous:\r\n\r\n- hip extension;\r\n- knee flexion\r\n\r\nshortens semitendinosus across both joints.\r\n\r\nAt sufficiently short lengths, its capacity for additional active force decreases.\r\n\r\n## Passive Insufficiency\r\n\r\nSimultaneous:\r\n\r\n- hip flexion;\r\n- knee extension\r\n\r\nplaces the muscle under substantial passive tension.\r\n\r\nThis is the classic hamstring-stretch configuration.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nSemitendinosus has unusually strong evidence for hypertrophy benefits from **training at longer muscle lengths**.\r\n\r\nThe best direct evidence comes from the seated-versus-prone leg-curl comparison.\r\n\r\n## Seated vs Prone Leg Curl\r\n\r\nMaeo et al. compared leg-curl training with the hip flexed versus near-neutral.\r\n\r\nThe seated position substantially lengthens the biarticular hamstrings because hip flexion increases proximal muscle-tendon length while knee-flexion loading is maintained. After training, hypertrophy of the biarticular hamstrings was greater in the seated condition than in the prone condition. Semitendinosus was among the muscles showing this advantage. :contentReference[oaicite:3]{index=3}\r\n\r\nThis is direct longitudinal evidence, not merely an EMG inference.\r\n\r\n## Why Seated Position Matters\r\n\r\nDuring a leg curl:\r\n\r\n- knee flexion shortens semitendinosus distally.\r\n\r\nWhen the hip is also flexed:\r\n\r\n- proximal length is increased.\r\n\r\nTherefore the muscle remains longer throughout more of the repetition.\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`L_ST_seated > L_ST_prone`\r\n\r\nat comparable knee angles.\r\n\r\n## Long Position\r\n\r\nThe most lengthened common training state combines:\r\n\r\n- substantial hip flexion;\r\n- knee near extension.\r\n\r\nThis occurs near the beginning of a seated leg curl.\r\n\r\nIt also occurs during the bottom phase of hip hinges such as the RDL.\r\n\r\n## Romanian Deadlift\r\n\r\nDuring an RDL:\r\n\r\n- hip flexion increases;\r\n- knee remains only mildly flexed.\r\n\r\nTherefore semitendinosus reaches a long muscle-tendon configuration while producing hip-extension torque.\r\n\r\nThis makes RDL mechanically attractive for long-length semitendinosus loading.\r\n\r\n## Seated Leg Curl vs RDL\r\n\r\nThese exercises are complementary.\r\n\r\n### Seated Leg Curl\r\n\r\nPrimary demand:\r\n- knee flexion.\r\n\r\nLong length created by:\r\n- hip flexion.\r\n\r\n### RDL\r\n\r\nPrimary demand:\r\n- hip extension.\r\n\r\nLong length preserved by:\r\n- relatively extended knee.\r\n\r\nBoth can generate strong semitendinosus stimulus through different joint functions.\r\n\r\n## Nordic Hamstring Curl\r\n\r\nNordic curls generate very high eccentric knee-flexion demand.\r\n\r\nSemitendinosus is often strongly recruited during Nordic-type work.\r\n\r\nHowever, hip position is comparatively neutral, so the biarticular hamstrings are not as long proximally as in seated knee-flexion training.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n> **high eccentric force and maximum muscle length are separate properties.**\r\n\r\nNordic curls can be excellent without being the longest-length possible semitendinosus exercise.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nFor Agonez, semitendinosus deserves a relatively high-confidence long-length hypertrophy modifier.\r\n\r\nUnlike many muscles, the evidence directly supports greater hypertrophy when knee-flexion resistance is applied with the hip flexed. :contentReference[oaicite:4]{index=4}\r\n\r\nThe correct rule is:\r\n\r\n`SMH_effective = high when active_force is high AND hip_flexion + knee_extension produce long muscle length`\r\n\r\nrather than simply:\r\n\r\n`leg curl = high SMH`.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Seated Leg Curl\r\n\r\nThe seated leg curl is one of the highest-value semitendinosus hypertrophy exercises.\r\n\r\nAdvantages include:\r\n\r\n- direct knee-flexion loading;\r\n- long biarticular hamstring length;\r\n- high stability;\r\n- easy progression;\r\n- low systemic fatigue;\r\n- easy proximity to failure.\r\n\r\nFor semitendinosus, the combination of high knee-flexion torque and hip flexion is particularly valuable. :contentReference[oaicite:5]{index=5}\r\n\r\n## Prone Leg Curl\r\n\r\nProne curls still provide high semitendinosus demand.\r\n\r\nHowever, the muscle operates at shorter overall length because the hip is not flexed.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_ST_prone < E_LT_ST_seated`\r\n\r\nfor otherwise comparable knee positions.\r\n\r\nProne curls remain useful, but should not receive the same long-length modifier.\r\n\r\n## Nordic Hamstring Curl\r\n\r\nNordics generate very high knee-flexion torque, particularly eccentrically.\r\n\r\nSemitendinosus can be strongly recruited during this movement.\r\n\r\nThey provide:\r\n\r\n- high force;\r\n- strong eccentric loading;\r\n- large knee-extension leverage.\r\n\r\nHowever, they are more systemically and technically demanding than machine curls.\r\n\r\n## Romanian Deadlift\r\n\r\nRDLs train semitendinosus through hip extension.\r\n\r\nThe bottom position creates:\r\n\r\n- high hip flexion;\r\n- relatively extended knee;\r\n- substantial hamstring length.\r\n\r\nTherefore ST demand should be high.\r\n\r\nForce must still be shared with:\r\n\r\n- semimembranosus;\r\n- biceps femoris long head;\r\n- gluteus maximus;\r\n- adductor magnus.\r\n\r\n## Stiff-Leg Deadlift\r\n\r\nReducing knee flexion tends to maintain greater hamstring length.\r\n\r\nThis can increase semitendinosus length exposure.\r\n\r\nHowever, completely locking the knee is unnecessary.\r\n\r\nA small knee bend usually preserves long hamstring length while allowing better movement control.\r\n\r\n## Conventional Deadlift\r\n\r\nSemitendinosus contributes, but the movement distributes force across:\r\n\r\n- gluteus maximus;\r\n- adductor magnus;\r\n- quadriceps;\r\n- other hamstrings;\r\n- spinal extensors.\r\n\r\nIt is therefore less semitendinosus-specific than an RDL or seated leg curl.\r\n\r\n## Squat\r\n\r\nSquats should not receive high semitendinosus hypertrophy allocation simply because both hip and knee move through large ROM.\r\n\r\nDuring descent:\r\n\r\n- hip flexion lengthens ST;\r\n- knee flexion shortens ST.\r\n\r\nThese effects partly cancel.\r\n\r\nTherefore net muscle excursion can be modest.\r\n\r\nThis is a classic example of biarticular compensation.\r\n\r\n## Leg Press\r\n\r\nThe same principle applies to deep leg press:\r\n\r\n- hip flexion increases;\r\n- knee flexion increases.\r\n\r\nThe muscle is being pulled longer at one end and shorter at the other.\r\n\r\nThus very deep leg press is not equivalent to a long-length hamstring exercise.\r\n\r\n## Back Extension\r\n\r\nA hip-dominant back extension can load semitendinosus substantially if:\r\n\r\n- the knee remains relatively extended;\r\n- most motion comes from the hip.\r\n\r\nIf the movement becomes mainly lumbar extension, `erector_spinae` demand rises and hamstring share falls.\r\n\r\n## Tibial Rotation\r\n\r\nInternal tibial rotation during knee flexion may increase relative loading of the medial hamstrings compared with biceps femoris.\r\n\r\nHowever, deliberately exaggerating foot position is not necessary for effective training.\r\n\r\nIt should be treated as a modest force-sharing modifier rather than an isolation technique.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Combine Knee Flexion and Hip Extension\r\n\r\nFor comprehensive semitendinosus development, use both:\r\n\r\n1. **knee-flexion work**;\r\n2. **hip-extension work**.\r\n\r\nA strong minimal combination is:\r\n\r\n- seated leg curl;\r\n- Romanian deadlift.\r\n\r\nThis covers both primary functions and provides substantial long-length exposure.\r\n\r\n## Weekly Volume\r\n\r\nA practical general hamstring target is approximately:\r\n\r\n- **6-10 hard direct sets per week**,\r\n\r\ndistributed between:\r\n\r\n- leg curls;\r\n- hip hinges.\r\n\r\nSemitendinosus will receive a meaningful share of both.\r\n\r\nThere is generally no need for separate ST-only volume.\r\n\r\n## Example Distribution\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n\r\n- 3-5 sets seated leg curl;\r\n- 3-5 sets RDL or similar hip hinge.\r\n\r\nThis produces complementary:\r\n\r\n- knee-flexion demand;\r\n- hip-extension demand.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nTwo weekly exposures are generally effective.\r\n\r\nThree lower-volume exposures are also reasonable.\r\n\r\nRecovery should account for:\r\n\r\n- sprinting;\r\n- running;\r\n- deadlifting;\r\n- Nordic work.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\n### Seated Leg Curl\r\n\r\nUseful:\r\n- 6-10;\r\n- 8-15;\r\n- 10-20 reps.\r\n\r\n### RDL\r\n\r\nUseful:\r\n- 5-8;\r\n- 6-10;\r\n- 8-12 reps.\r\n\r\n### Nordic\r\n\r\nOften:\r\n- lower repetitions due to mechanical difficulty;\r\n- assistance can be used to extend the useful range.\r\n\r\nThere is no ST-specific rep range.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nStable seated curls can be trained close to local failure:\r\n\r\n- approximately 0-2 RIR.\r\n\r\nHeavy hinges should generally retain more technical reserve because:\r\n\r\n- spinal position;\r\n- grip;\r\n- systemic fatigue\r\n\r\ncan become limiting.\r\n\r\n## Stimulus-to-Fatigue Ratio\r\n\r\nFor semitendinosus hypertrophy:\r\n\r\n### Seated Leg Curl\r\n\r\nHigh:\r\n- local stimulus.\r\n\r\nLow:\r\n- systemic fatigue.\r\n\r\n### RDL\r\n\r\nHigh:\r\n- local stimulus.\r\n\r\nAlso high:\r\n- systemic fatigue.\r\n\r\n### Nordic\r\n\r\nHigh:\r\n- eccentric hamstring stimulus.\r\n\r\nModerate-to-high:\r\n- local disruption and recovery demand.\r\n\r\nThus exercise-level fatigue should remain separate from muscle ETU.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## Semitendinosus Has a Very Different Architecture From Semimembranosus\r\n\r\nThe two are both medial hamstrings, but semitendinosus possesses substantially longer fascicles and a distinctive long distal tendon. Human anatomical work demonstrates strong inter-muscular architectural heterogeneity across the hamstrings. :contentReference[oaicite:6]{index=6}\r\n\r\nThis supports treating them as separate muscle entities.\r\n\r\n## It Is Part of Pes Anserinus\r\n\r\nSemitendinosus shares its medial tibial insertion region with:\r\n\r\n- sartorius;\r\n- gracilis.\r\n\r\nAll three can contribute to:\r\n\r\n- knee flexion;\r\n- internal tibial rotation.\r\n\r\nYet semitendinosus is the much larger posterior-thigh contributor to high knee-flexion force.\r\n\r\n## It Is Particularly Excursion-Oriented\r\n\r\nIts long fascicles allow considerable shortening distance.\r\n\r\nThis makes semitendinosus relatively well suited to:\r\n\r\n- large ROM;\r\n- rapid length changes;\r\n- locomotor tasks.\r\n\r\nThe architecture does not mean it is weak, but it differs from the more force-oriented semimembranosus. :contentReference[oaicite:7]{index=7}\r\n\r\n## Seated Leg Curls Have Direct Hypertrophy Evidence\r\n\r\nThe superiority of seated over prone leg curls for biarticular hamstring growth is one of the cleanest human examples of training-muscle-length affecting hypertrophy. :contentReference[oaicite:8]{index=8}\r\n\r\nSemitendinosus is directly relevant because it crosses both hip and knee.\r\n\r\n## Squat Depth Does Not Equal Hamstring Stretch\r\n\r\nDeep squat:\r\n\r\n- increases hip flexion;\r\n- increases knee flexion.\r\n\r\nThose two movements have opposing effects on ST length.\r\n\r\nThis explains why large joint ROM can coexist with relatively limited hamstring length change.\r\n\r\n## One Muscle Force Produces Two Joint Moments\r\n\r\nBecause ST crosses two joints, a single tendon force produces moments simultaneously at both.\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`T_hip = F_ST × r_hip`\r\n\r\nand\r\n\r\n`T_knee = F_ST × r_knee`.\r\n\r\nThe body does not create an independent semitendinosus force for each joint.\r\n\r\nThis matters directly for multi-joint force modelling.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\n`semitendinosus` should receive demand primarily from:\r\n\r\n1. **knee-flexion torque**;\r\n2. **hip-extension torque**;\r\n3. secondary internal-tibial-rotation torque;\r\n4. knee stabilization.\r\n\r\nIts force sharing should differ from both:\r\n\r\n- semimembranosus;\r\n- biceps femoris.\r\n\r\n## Length-Tension Model\r\n\r\nA useful first-order representation is:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_ST = f(hip_flexion, knee_extension)`\r\n\r\nQualitatively:\r\n\r\n### Very Long\r\n\r\n- hip highly flexed;\r\n- knee near extension.\r\n\r\n### Long\r\n\r\n- substantial hip flexion;\r\n- modest knee flexion.\r\n\r\n### Intermediate\r\n\r\n- hip and knee motions partly offset one another.\r\n\r\n### Short\r\n\r\n- hip extended;\r\n- knee deeply flexed.\r\n\r\nThis should be calculated independently from the vasti or monoarticular knee flexors.\r\n\r\n## Seated Leg Curl\r\n\r\nApproximate geometry near the start:\r\n\r\n`hip_flexion = high`\r\n`knee_flexion = low`\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_ST = very high`.\r\n\r\nAs the knee flexes:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_ST ↓`\r\n\r\nbut the muscle remains longer than in a prone curl because hip flexion persists.\r\n\r\nDirect training evidence supports giving this configuration a high hypertrophy modifier. :contentReference[oaicite:9]{index=9}\r\n\r\n## Prone Leg Curl\r\n\r\nApproximate:\r\n\r\n`hip_flexion ≈ 0`\r\n`knee starts extended`\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_ST = moderate-high at start`\r\n\r\nbut substantially lower than seated curl.\r\n\r\n## RDL\r\n\r\nApproximate bottom position:\r\n\r\n`hip_flexion = high`\r\n`knee_flexion = low/moderate`\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_ST = very high`.\r\n\r\nThe task simultaneously requires substantial hip-extension force.\r\n\r\nThis is mechanically excellent long-length loading.\r\n\r\n## Squat / Leg Press\r\n\r\nApproximate:\r\n\r\n`hip_flexion ↑`\r\n`knee_flexion ↑`\r\n\r\nThe effects on ST length oppose.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`ΔL_ST` can remain modest despite large joint ROM.\r\n\r\nAgonez should not assign a high hamstring SMH value solely from squat depth.\r\n\r\n## Knee-Flexion Force Sharing\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`T_knee_flex =\r\n    ST\r\n  + SM\r\n  + BFLH\r\n  + BFSH\r\n  + gastrocnemius\r\n  + smaller synergists`\r\n\r\nIndividual recruitment differs with joint configuration and exercise. MRI-based exercise comparisons demonstrate that hamstring recruitment patterns change across hip-extension and knee-flexion tasks rather than remaining fixed. :contentReference[oaicite:10]{index=10}\r\n\r\n## Semitendinosus vs Semimembranosus\r\n\r\nA useful modelling distinction is:\r\n\r\n### Semitendinosus\r\n\r\nGreater relative emphasis on:\r\n- excursion;\r\n- long fascicles;\r\n- dynamic knee-flexion contribution.\r\n\r\n### Semimembranosus\r\n\r\nGreater relative emphasis on:\r\n- force capacity;\r\n- broad posteromedial stabilization.\r\n\r\nThis is a relative distinction, not an exclusive division of labor.\r\n\r\n## Semitendinosus vs Biceps Femoris Long Head\r\n\r\nBoth:\r\n\r\n- extend hip;\r\n- flex knee.\r\n\r\nBut:\r\n\r\n### ST\r\n\r\nMedial:\r\n- internal tibial rotation.\r\n\r\n### BFLH\r\n\r\nLateral:\r\n- external tibial rotation.\r\n\r\nThus tibial-rotation requirement can alter force sharing.\r\n\r\n## Nordic Allocation\r\n\r\nNordic-type knee flexion should receive:\r\n\r\n- high semitendinosus demand;\r\n- high BFLH/SM demand;\r\n- some BFSH demand.\r\n\r\nDo not assume equal distribution.\r\n\r\nExperimental studies show that exercise selection changes regional and inter-muscular hamstring recruitment. :contentReference[oaicite:11]{index=11}\r\n\r\n## Biarticular Force Constraint\r\n\r\nThis is important for your FCSA model.\r\n\r\nThe same ST force contributes simultaneously at hip and knee.\r\n\r\nDo not compute:\r\n\r\n`F_required_from_hip`\r\n+\r\n`F_required_from_knee`\r\n\r\nas if they were independent muscular demands.\r\n\r\nInstead, conceptually solve:\r\n\r\n`T_hip ≈ F_ST × r_hip`\r\n\r\nand\r\n\r\n`T_knee ≈ F_ST × r_knee`\r\n\r\nfor one common `F_ST`, alongside the other synergists.\r\n\r\nFor exercises involving substantial torque at both joints, this eventually becomes a constrained multi-joint force-sharing problem.\r\n\r\n## Architecture and FCSA\r\n\r\nBecause semitendinosus is relatively long-fascicled, its architecture favors excursion more than maximization of PCSA compared with semimembranosus. Human anatomical studies explicitly demonstrate these structural differences. :contentReference[oaicite:12]{index=12}\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n- long `optimal_fiber_length_cm` is expected;\r\n- its PCSA should not be interpreted against semimembranosus without considering architecture;\r\n- similar muscle mass does not imply similar maximum force.\r\n\r\nThis is exactly why the PCSA/FCSA layer adds information beyond mass alone.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nSemitendinosus should receive **high-confidence long-length weighting**.\r\n\r\nThe key empirical result is that seated leg-curl training, which lengthens the biarticular hamstrings through hip flexion, produced greater semitendinosus hypertrophy than prone training. :contentReference[oaicite:13]{index=13}\r\n\r\nA useful Agonez formulation is:\r\n\r\n`SMH_effective_ST =\r\n    base_smh_factor\r\n    × E_LT(hip_angle, knee_angle)\r\n    × active_force`\r\n\r\nwhere:\r\n\r\n- long length alone is insufficient;\r\n- active force alone does not encode long-length exposure;\r\n- their interaction determines the relevant stimulus.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **ischial-tuberosity origin and medial-tibial insertion:** high;\r\n- **tibial-division sciatic innervation:** high;\r\n- **hip-extension function:** high;\r\n- **knee-flexion function:** high;\r\n- **internal-tibial-rotation function:** high;\r\n- **biarticular hip/knee length dependence:** very high;\r\n- **distinct long-fascicle architecture:** directly demonstrated anatomically; :contentReference[oaicite:14]{index=14}\r\n- **exercise-dependent hamstring recruitment:** directly supported experimentally; :contentReference[oaicite:15]{index=15}\r\n- **greater ST hypertrophy from seated versus prone leg curls:** directly demonstrated longitudinally; :contentReference[oaicite:16]{index=16}\r\n- **exact ST-vs-SM-vs-BF force sharing:** task- and joint-angle-dependent;\r\n- **optimal hypertrophy hip angle:** not precisely established;\r\n- **precise multi-joint FCSA allocation:** requires joint-specific moment arms and simultaneous force optimization.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **semitendinosus = a long-fascicled medial biarticular hamstring specialized for substantial excursion while producing both knee-flexion and hip-extension torque, with internal-tibial-rotation capability and unusually strong direct evidence that knee-flexion training at longer muscle lengths enhances its hypertrophy.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/Hamstrings}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	19.30	12.90	0.975	26.25	25.59
16	erector_spinae	Thoracolumbar spinal extensor complex	Upper	Back	1375.00	1302.08	28.00	Parallel/Long-Fusiform	0.750	0.250	high	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\n`erector_spinae` represents the **thoracolumbar erector-spinae complex**, a bilateral system of large intrinsic back muscles running longitudinally along the posterior trunk.\r\n\r\nClassically, erector spinae is divided into three longitudinal columns:\r\n\r\n- **iliocostalis** — most lateral;\r\n- **longissimus** — intermediate;\r\n- **spinalis** — most medial.\r\n\r\nFor a thoracolumbar training model, the largest practical contributors are primarily:\r\n\r\n- iliocostalis lumborum / thoracis;\r\n- longissimus thoracis;\r\n- thoracic spinalis to a lesser degree.\r\n\r\nThe complex should be distinguished from deeper segmental extensors such as `multifidus`.\r\n\r\nBoth systems can generate spinal-extension torque, but their architecture and functional emphasis differ:\r\n\r\n- erector spinae is particularly important for producing and resisting large **global trunk moments**;\r\n- multifidus has shorter segmental attachments and a greater role in local vertebral control.\r\n\r\nThe major functions of the erector-spinae complex are:\r\n\r\n1. **extension of the vertebral column**;\r\n2. eccentric resistance to trunk flexion;\r\n3. isometric resistance to external spinal-flexion moments;\r\n4. ipsilateral lateral flexion;\r\n5. maintenance of trunk posture under load.\r\n\r\nFor resistance training, one principle is especially important:\r\n\r\n> erector spinae can experience very high mechanical demand without visibly extending the spine.\r\n\r\nDuring a Romanian deadlift, Pendlay row or conventional deadlift, the lumbar spine may remain approximately isometric while the erector spinae generates large forces to prevent the trunk from collapsing into flexion.\r\n\r\nTherefore, spinal motion and spinal-extensor loading are not equivalent variables.\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\nThe erector-spinae complex occupies the posterior trunk on either side of the vertebral column and is enclosed partly by the thoracolumbar fascial and aponeurotic system.\r\n\r\nIts classical arrangement from lateral to medial is:\r\n\r\n1. iliocostalis;\r\n2. longissimus;\r\n3. spinalis.\r\n\r\nThe exact anatomy becomes particularly complex in the lumbar region.\r\n\r\n## Iliocostalis\r\n\r\nIliocostalis forms the lateral column.\r\n\r\n### Origin\r\n\r\nInferior fibers arise through the common erector-spinae tendon and associated aponeurotic structures from regions including:\r\n\r\n- posterior iliac crest;\r\n- sacrum;\r\n- sacroiliac ligamentous region;\r\n- lower lumbar posterior elements.\r\n\r\n### Insertion\r\n\r\nFibers ascend toward:\r\n\r\n- lumbar transverse/costal processes;\r\n- ribs;\r\n- upper thoracic and cervical structures depending on subdivision.\r\n\r\nFor the thoracolumbar model, `iliocostalis lumborum` and `iliocostalis thoracis` are the most relevant components.\r\n\r\nThe lumbar anatomy is not simply a continuous strap extending from pelvis to ribs.\r\n\r\nDetailed dissection demonstrates segmental lumbar fibers and a substantial relationship with the erector-spinae and lumbar intermuscular aponeuroses.\r\n\r\n## Longissimus\r\n\r\nLongissimus forms the intermediate and generally largest longitudinal erector-spinae column.\r\n\r\n### Origin\r\n\r\nLumbar longissimus fibers arise from structures including:\r\n\r\n- posterior ilium;\r\n- erector-spinae aponeurosis;\r\n- lumbar intermuscular aponeurosis.\r\n\r\n### Insertion\r\n\r\nLumbar fibers attach to posterior lumbar elements including:\r\n\r\n- accessory processes;\r\n- transverse-process regions.\r\n\r\nMore cranial fibers continue toward thoracic ribs and transverse processes.\r\n\r\nLongissimus therefore links the pelvis and lumbar region with progressively more superior axial structures and is a major global spinal extensor.\r\n\r\n## Spinalis\r\n\r\nSpinalis forms the most medial erector-spinae component.\r\n\r\nIt connects primarily between spinous processes and adjacent posterior axial structures.\r\n\r\nCompared with iliocostalis and longissimus, it is generally smaller and less distinct in the lumbar region.\r\n\r\nFor an aggregate muscle model, it is reasonable to include it within `erector_spinae` without separately allocating most exercise force to it.\r\n\r\n## Lumbar Aponeurotic Organization\r\n\r\nThe lower erector spinae is mechanically integrated with large aponeurotic sheets.\r\n\r\nIn the lumbar region, iliocostalis and longissimus are separated and enclosed by structures including the:\r\n\r\n- erector-spinae aponeurosis;\r\n- lumbar intermuscular aponeurosis.\r\n\r\nThis architecture allows force transmission across broader regions than a simple origin-to-insertion model suggests.\r\n\r\nThe thoracolumbar fascia and erector-spinae aponeurosis also interact mechanically with surrounding trunk musculature.\r\n\r\nThus the lumbar extensor system should be conceptualized as:\r\n\r\n> muscle fascicles + tendons/aponeuroses + fascial force-transmission structures,\r\n\r\nrather than isolated parallel muscle cylinders.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nErector spinae is innervated segmentally by the **posterior rami of spinal nerves**.\r\n\r\nThis differs from many appendicular muscles, which receive one named peripheral nerve.\r\n\r\nDifferent axial levels receive local segmental innervation.\r\n\r\nThis arrangement is consistent with the longitudinal, multisegmental architecture of intrinsic spinal musculature.\r\n\r\nFor Agonez, there is little practical value in modelling individual spinal nerve levels for training calculations.\r\n\r\nThe relevant interpretation is that the complex can exhibit regional recruitment across different thoracic and lumbar levels rather than necessarily contracting uniformly along its entire length.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Spinal Extension\r\n\r\nBilateral erector-spinae contraction generates **extension torque of the vertebral column**.\r\n\r\nThis can produce visible movement from flexion toward extension, as during:\r\n\r\n- back extension;\r\n- controlled spinal extension;\r\n- recovery from a flexed trunk position.\r\n\r\nHowever, extension torque does not imply that extension motion must occur.\r\n\r\nIf external flexion torque equals internal extensor torque, the spine can remain approximately stationary.\r\n\r\n## Anti-Flexion / Isometric Extension Torque\r\n\r\nThis is one of the most important functions in resistance training.\r\n\r\nWhen the trunk is inclined forward, gravity and external loads generate a flexion moment around the spine.\r\n\r\nErector spinae must generate an opposing **extension moment**.\r\n\r\nIf the spine remains neutral:\r\n\r\n- muscle force is high;\r\n- external work at the spinal joints may be near zero;\r\n- the contraction is largely isometric.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- Romanian deadlift;\r\n- conventional deadlift;\r\n- Pendlay row;\r\n- bent-over row;\r\n- good morning;\r\n- squat.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n> an isometric erector-spinae contraction can be mechanically extremely demanding.\r\n\r\nThis is why ROM-based exercise scoring alone would substantially underestimate spinal-extensor demand.\r\n\r\n## Eccentric Control of Spinal Flexion\r\n\r\nWhen the spine flexes under control, erector spinae can act eccentrically.\r\n\r\nThe muscles lengthen while producing extension torque.\r\n\r\nThis occurs during controlled trunk flexion and during some dynamic back-extension exercises.\r\n\r\nThe eccentric action can allow gradual lowering against gravity rather than uncontrolled collapse.\r\n\r\n## Lateral Flexion\r\n\r\nUnilateral erector-spinae contraction contributes to **ipsilateral lateral flexion**.\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n\r\n- right erector spinae contributes to right trunk side-bending;\r\n- left erector spinae contributes to left side-bending.\r\n\r\nThis function overlaps with:\r\n\r\n- quadratus lumborum;\r\n- obliques;\r\n- other intrinsic spinal muscles.\r\n\r\nIn unilateral loading, erector spinae can also resist contralateral lateral flexion isometrically.\r\n\r\n## Rotation\r\n\r\nIndividual erector-spinae components may contribute to axial rotation depending on level and geometry, but rotation is not their dominant aggregate function.\r\n\r\nAxial rotation is distributed across multiple systems including:\r\n\r\n- multifidus;\r\n- rotatores;\r\n- obliques;\r\n- thoracic erector-spinae fibers.\r\n\r\nFor a simplified model, erector spinae should primarily represent:\r\n\r\n- extension;\r\n- anti-flexion;\r\n- lateral-flexion stabilization.\r\n\r\n## Postural Control\r\n\r\nEven standing upright requires low-level extensor control.\r\n\r\nAs external flexion moments increase, erector-spinae force can rise dramatically.\r\n\r\nThe relevant external moment depends on:\r\n\r\n- trunk inclination;\r\n- mass of the torso;\r\n- external load;\r\n- horizontal distance of that load from the spine.\r\n\r\nThis is why holding a moderate mass far in front of the body can demand more spinal-extensor force than holding a much heavier load close to the torso.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nDirect evidence for **stretch-mediated hypertrophy specifically in the erector-spinae complex** is limited.\r\n\r\nThe mechanics are nevertheless important.\r\n\r\nSpinal flexion generally lengthens posterior spinal extensor fascicles and alters:\r\n\r\n- fascicle orientation;\r\n- moment arms;\r\n- passive tissue tension;\r\n- force-sharing between muscle and connective tissue.\r\n\r\nLumbar posture therefore changes the functional biomechanics of the erector spinae rather than simply changing its length. :contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}\r\n\r\n## Flexion as a Long-Length Position\r\n\r\nMoving the vertebral column from extension toward flexion can place erector-spinae fibers at greater length.\r\n\r\nA dynamic back-extension exercise can therefore potentially train the muscle through:\r\n\r\n- longer lengths near flexion;\r\n- intermediate lengths near neutral;\r\n- shorter lengths in extension.\r\n\r\nHowever, this system differs substantially from a simple monoarticular limb muscle.\r\n\r\nSpinal flexion also changes loading of:\r\n\r\n- discs;\r\n- ligaments;\r\n- thoracolumbar fascia;\r\n- posterior passive structures.\r\n\r\nTherefore it is not appropriate to conclude that:\r\n\r\n> more loaded lumbar flexion is always better because long-muscle-length hypertrophy is beneficial.\r\n\r\n## Active vs Passive Contribution\r\n\r\nAt larger spinal-flexion angles, passive structures can assume a greater share of the external flexion moment.\r\n\r\nErector-spinae activation can also decrease substantially near full flexion in some tasks — the classic **flexion-relaxation phenomenon**.\r\n\r\nTherefore maximum spinal flexion is not necessarily the position of maximum active erector-spinae tension.\r\n\r\nThis is an important difference between:\r\n\r\n- muscle being anatomically lengthened;\r\n- muscle being highly loaded.\r\n\r\nFor hypertrophy, the second variable is essential.\r\n\r\n## Practical Interpretation\r\n\r\nA sensible hypertrophy model is:\r\n\r\n- dynamic spinal extension through controlled ROM can expose the erectors to changing muscle lengths;\r\n- modest flexion may provide active long-length loading;\r\n- extreme flexion is not required;\r\n- high-load neutral-spine isometrics can still produce substantial hypertrophic tension even without length change.\r\n\r\nAccordingly, `smh_factor` for this aggregate should be interpreted cautiously.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Back Extension\r\n\r\nA Roman-chair or machine back extension can directly train spinal extension.\r\n\r\nHowever, execution determines whether the exercise is primarily:\r\n\r\n- spinal extension;\r\n- hip extension;\r\n- or a mixture.\r\n\r\n### Hip-Dominant Back Extension\r\n\r\nIf the lumbar spine remains approximately fixed while the torso pivots around the hips, demand shifts toward:\r\n\r\n- gluteus maximus;\r\n- hamstrings;\r\n- adductor magnus.\r\n\r\nErector spinae still works isometrically.\r\n\r\n### Spine-Dominant Back Extension\r\n\r\nIf the lumbar/thoracic spine moves through flexion and extension, erector spinae performs more dynamic contractile work.\r\n\r\nTherefore two exercises both called "back extension" can have very different muscle vectors.\r\n\r\nThis distinction should be explicit in Agonez.\r\n\r\n## Isometric Roman-Chair Hold\r\n\r\nA Roman-chair isometric hold can produce substantial erector-spinae demand without spinal ROM.\r\n\r\nExternal torque depends on:\r\n\r\n- torso mass;\r\n- added load;\r\n- torso angle;\r\n- position of the load relative to the hips/spine.\r\n\r\nThis is a high-force stabilization task and should not receive a low ETU merely because mechanical displacement is zero.\r\n\r\n## Deadlift\r\n\r\nDeadlifting produces substantial erector-spinae activity.\r\n\r\nA systematic review of deadlift EMG studies found high erector-spinae recruitment across conventional and related deadlift variants. :contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}\r\n\r\nThe role is predominantly to generate spinal-extension torque and resist flexion while the hips and knees produce most of the visible movement.\r\n\r\nHeavier loads can increase erector-spinae activation and alter spinal kinematics. :contentReference[oaicite:3]{index=3}\r\n\r\nHowever:\r\n\r\n> deadlift is not a pure erector-spinae exercise.\r\n\r\nIt simultaneously imposes substantial demand on:\r\n\r\n- gluteus maximus;\r\n- hamstrings;\r\n- adductor magnus;\r\n- quadriceps depending on style;\r\n- grip;\r\n- upper back.\r\n\r\nIts local erector stimulus comes with high systemic cost.\r\n\r\n## Romanian Deadlift\r\n\r\nRDLs generate a large external trunk-flexion moment because the torso inclines while the load remains anterior to the spinal axis.\r\n\r\nErector spinae must resist this torque.\r\n\r\nMost productive RDL technique keeps the lumbar spine approximately stable while motion occurs mainly at the hips.\r\n\r\nThus erector-spinae work is predominantly **high-force isometric**.\r\n\r\nThis makes the RDL simultaneously:\r\n\r\n- a dynamic hip-extensor exercise;\r\n- an isometric spinal-extensor exercise.\r\n\r\n## Pendlay Row\r\n\r\nThe Pendlay row creates particularly high erector-spinae stabilization demand because the torso remains close to horizontal.\r\n\r\nThe trunk's own mass plus barbell loading create a large flexion moment.\r\n\r\nTherefore the spinal extensors can receive large force demands even though the row is nominally classified as an upper-back exercise.\r\n\r\nThis is a major source of systemic and lumbar fatigue.\r\n\r\nA chest-supported row removes much of this requirement.\r\n\r\n## Bent-Over Row vs Chest-Supported Row\r\n\r\nThis is a useful mechanical comparison.\r\n\r\n### Bent-Over Row\r\n\r\nHigh erector-spinae demand due to:\r\n\r\n- trunk inclination;\r\n- external load;\r\n- need to maintain spinal position.\r\n\r\n### Chest-Supported Row\r\n\r\nMuch lower erector-spinae demand because the bench supplies the external support moment.\r\n\r\nThus chest support does not merely increase comfort.\r\n\r\nIt fundamentally changes the exercise's muscle-demand vector.\r\n\r\n## Squat\r\n\r\nDuring squatting, erector spinae generates substantial extension torque to resist trunk flexion.\r\n\r\nDemand rises with factors such as:\r\n\r\n- greater torso inclination;\r\n- larger external load;\r\n- more anterior system center of mass.\r\n\r\nA low-bar squat with greater torso inclination can therefore require more spinal-extensor torque than a more upright pattern, although total mechanics remain individual.\r\n\r\n## Good Morning\r\n\r\nGood mornings deliberately create a large external hip and trunk-flexion moment.\r\n\r\nThey can strongly load:\r\n\r\n- spinal extensors;\r\n- gluteus maximus;\r\n- hamstrings;\r\n- adductor magnus.\r\n\r\nBecause the bar is positioned far above the hips, relatively modest absolute loads can produce large moments.\r\n\r\nThey are effective but have substantial fatigue cost.\r\n\r\n## Direct Lumbar-Extension Machine\r\n\r\nA dedicated lumbar-extension machine can reduce lower-limb and hip-extensor contributions by stabilizing the pelvis.\r\n\r\nThis can make it more specific to the lumbar extensor musculature.\r\n\r\nHigh-intensity isolated lumbar-extension training has been shown to increase lumbar extensor cross-sectional area, demonstrating that these muscles are directly hypertrophy-responsive. :contentReference[oaicite:4]{index=4}\r\n\r\nFor pure erector-spinae development, such equipment can therefore offer a high local-stimulus-to-systemic-fatigue ratio.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Distinguish Dynamic and Isometric Sets\r\n\r\nErector-spinae workload should not be counted only from direct back-extension exercises.\r\n\r\nMeaningful exposure comes from:\r\n\r\n### Dynamic Spinal Extension\r\n\r\nExamples:\r\n\r\n- lumbar-extension machine;\r\n- deliberately spine-dynamic back extension.\r\n\r\n### High-Force Isometric Extension\r\n\r\nExamples:\r\n\r\n- RDL;\r\n- deadlift;\r\n- Pendlay row;\r\n- good morning;\r\n- squat.\r\n\r\nBoth can create hypertrophic tension.\r\n\r\nTheir fatigue profiles differ substantially.\r\n\r\n## Hidden Volume\r\n\r\nErector spinae can accumulate enormous hidden volume in a program containing:\r\n\r\n- squats;\r\n- RDLs;\r\n- deadlifts;\r\n- bent-over rows;\r\n- unsupported cable work.\r\n\r\nTherefore direct lumbar-extension work should be prescribed only after total axial demand is considered.\r\n\r\nA lifter may have:\r\n\r\n> zero dedicated erector-spinae exercises but still very high erector-spinae training exposure.\r\n\r\n## Weekly Direct Volume\r\n\r\nWhen substantial axial compound work is already present, additional direct dynamic work may require only:\r\n\r\n- **0-4 direct sets per week**.\r\n\r\nIf compounds are heavily supported and spinal-extensor hypertrophy is a specific priority, more direct work can be added progressively.\r\n\r\nThere is no reason to use generic bodybuilding set targets without accounting for isometric compound exposure.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nErector spinae commonly receives indirect loading several times per week.\r\n\r\nTwo or three exposures can arise naturally from:\r\n\r\n- squat sessions;\r\n- pulling sessions;\r\n- hinge sessions.\r\n\r\nThe limiting factor may be cumulative local fatigue rather than lack of frequency.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\n### Direct Machine / Back Extension\r\n\r\nUseful ranges include:\r\n\r\n- 8-15 reps;\r\n- 12-20 reps;\r\n- higher reps where load control is good.\r\n\r\n### RDL / Good Morning / Deadlift\r\n\r\nModerate or lower repetition ranges are often more practical:\r\n\r\n- 3-6 reps for strength-oriented deadlifting;\r\n- 5-10 reps for heavy hinges;\r\n- 6-12 reps for hypertrophy-oriented RDLs.\r\n\r\nVery high-repetition unsupported hinges can become limited by:\r\n\r\n- cardiovascular fatigue;\r\n- grip;\r\n- bracing;\r\n- discomfort\r\n\r\nbefore spinal extensors experience clean local failure.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nStable direct lumbar-extension machines can be trained relatively close to muscular failure.\r\n\r\nUnsupported heavy compounds should generally retain more technical reserve.\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n\r\n- isolated lumbar extension → 0-2 RIR can be practical;\r\n- Roman-chair extension → 1-3 RIR;\r\n- RDL → typically 1-3 RIR;\r\n- heavy deadlift → larger technical reserve may be sensible.\r\n\r\nA grinder involving progressively deteriorating spinal position is not equivalent to clean erector-spinae overload.\r\n\r\n## Recovery\r\n\r\nErector-spinae fatigue can affect a large portion of the program because these muscles stabilize the trunk during many unrelated exercises.\r\n\r\nResidual fatigue can reduce performance in:\r\n\r\n- squats;\r\n- rows;\r\n- deadlifts;\r\n- RDLs;\r\n- standing presses.\r\n\r\nThus local erector fatigue has unusually high **downstream interference** compared with many isolation muscles.\r\n\r\nThis should influence exercise ordering and weekly scheduling.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Order\r\n\r\nIf an exercise requires high-quality spinal stabilization, heavy direct erector work immediately beforehand can be counterproductive.\r\n\r\nFor example, exhausting the erectors before:\r\n\r\n- squats;\r\n- RDLs;\r\n- unsupported rows\r\n\r\nmay reduce technical stability.\r\n\r\nDirect spinal-extension isolation often fits better after major axial compounds unless erector strength/hypertrophy is the session's main priority.\r\n\r\n## Stimulus-to-Fatigue Ratio\r\n\r\nDifferent exercises can produce similar erector demand with dramatically different systemic cost.\r\n\r\n### High Systemic Cost\r\n\r\n- deadlift;\r\n- Pendlay row;\r\n- good morning.\r\n\r\n### Moderate\r\n\r\n- RDL;\r\n- Roman-chair back extension.\r\n\r\n### Potentially Low Systemic Cost\r\n\r\n- isolated lumbar-extension machine.\r\n\r\nTherefore programming should distinguish:\r\n\r\n> local erector stimulus\r\n\r\nfrom:\r\n\r\n> whole-exercise fatigue.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## "Erector Spinae" Is a System, Not One Muscle\r\n\r\nThe name describes a longitudinal muscle system composed primarily of:\r\n\r\n- iliocostalis;\r\n- longissimus;\r\n- spinalis.\r\n\r\nEach contains further regional divisions.\r\n\r\nThe database aggregate is therefore intentionally coarse.\r\n\r\n## The Lumbar Anatomy Is More Complex Than the Textbook Diagram\r\n\r\nClassic illustrations often show three clean muscle columns running continuously upward.\r\n\r\nDetailed lumbar dissections reveal segmental fiber attachments and extensive aponeurotic organization, especially for iliocostalis and longissimus. :contentReference[oaicite:5]{index=5}\r\n\r\nThus a purely strap-like model is anatomically inaccurate.\r\n\r\n## No Movement Does Not Mean No Work\r\n\r\nA lifter can hold a heavy barbell in an RDL while maintaining almost no lumbar movement.\r\n\r\nThe erector spinae may nevertheless generate enormous force.\r\n\r\nThis is one of the clearest examples of why:\r\n\r\n> muscle force ≠ visible joint motion.\r\n\r\n## More Flexion Does Not Necessarily Mean More Active Erector Tension\r\n\r\nNear full spinal flexion, passive posterior tissues can assume more of the load and erector activity can fall — the **flexion-relaxation phenomenon**.\r\n\r\nThus maximal anatomical stretch is not automatically maximal hypertrophic loading.\r\n\r\n## The Thoracolumbar Fascia Is Part of the Mechanical System\r\n\r\nThe erectors are mechanically coupled to the thoracolumbar fascial and aponeurotic structures.\r\n\r\nThe lower lumbar paraspinal muscles are extensively covered by the erector-spinae aponeurosis, and the region participates in broad force transmission across the trunk. :contentReference[oaicite:6]{index=6}\r\n\r\nThis means spinal force transmission is more distributed than a simple isolated-muscle model suggests.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Definition of This Aggregate\r\n\r\nFor Agonez, `erector_spinae` should represent primarily the **thoracolumbar global spinal-extensor system**:\r\n\r\n- iliocostalis lumborum/thoracis;\r\n- longissimus thoracis;\r\n- spinalis thoracis where relevant.\r\n\r\nIt should not automatically absorb all posterior spinal muscle force.\r\n\r\nIn particular, `multifidus` is biomechanically distinct and more strongly associated with local segmental control. :contentReference[oaicite:7]{index=7}\r\n\r\nIf Agonez does not store multifidus separately, some deep-extensor demand will inevitably be approximated within this aggregate.\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\nThe primary demand dimensions are:\r\n\r\n1. **spinal extension torque**;\r\n2. **anti-flexion / isometric extension torque**;\r\n3. **lateral-flexion torque**;\r\n4. secondary stabilization against rotation.\r\n\r\nThe first two should not be treated as fundamentally different in terms of whether force can be high.\r\n\r\n## External Flexion Moment\r\n\r\nA useful simplified model is:\r\n\r\n`M_external ≈ Σ(F_i × perpendicular_distance_i)`\r\n\r\nRelevant components include:\r\n\r\n- torso gravitational force;\r\n- head/arm mass;\r\n- external load;\r\n- position of those masses relative to the spinal axis.\r\n\r\nErector-spinae force must then generate an opposing internal extension moment.\r\n\r\nBecause spinal extensor internal moment arms are relatively small, muscle force can be several times larger than the external load itself.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n> a 90 kg barbell does not imply only 90 kg-equivalent erector demand.\r\n\r\nThe internal force requirement depends on leverage.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Allocation\r\n\r\n### Pendlay Row\r\n\r\nVery high `erector_spinae` demand.\r\n\r\nThe near-horizontal torso produces a large persistent flexion moment.\r\n\r\nThis helps explain why your existing systemic-FCSA model assigns Pendlay row unusually high erector demand.\r\n\r\n### Chest-Supported Dumbbell Row\r\n\r\nLow erector demand.\r\n\r\nThe external support supplies much of the anti-flexion moment.\r\n\r\nThis difference should remain large even if both exercises use similar upper-body pulling loads.\r\n\r\n### Roman-Chair Back Extension Isometric Hold\r\n\r\nHigh erector demand if the torso is unsupported and horizontal.\r\n\r\nAlso substantial:\r\n\r\n- gluteus maximus;\r\n- hamstrings;\r\n- adductor magnus\r\n\r\ndepending on hip position and execution.\r\n\r\n### Romanian Deadlift\r\n\r\nHigh erector demand predominantly through isometric anti-flexion.\r\n\r\nHigh hip-extensor demand dynamically.\r\n\r\n### Conventional Deadlift\r\n\r\nHigh erector demand with substantial systemic fatigue.\r\n\r\n### Squat\r\n\r\nModerate-to-high erector demand depending strongly on:\r\n\r\n- torso angle;\r\n- load;\r\n- bar position;\r\n- anthropometry.\r\n\r\n### Seated/Chest-Supported Machine Work\r\n\r\nUsually very low erector demand unless the machine requires unsupported trunk stabilization.\r\n\r\n## Dynamic vs Isometric ETU\r\n\r\nAgonez should avoid making `ROM = 0` imply `ETU ≈ 0`.\r\n\r\nFor erector spinae, high-force isometric contractions can represent a major training stimulus.\r\n\r\nA useful conceptual separation is:\r\n\r\n`muscle stimulus = force magnitude × recruitment × contraction duration × length state × fatigue context`\r\n\r\nrather than relying only on mechanical displacement.\r\n\r\nThis muscle is one of the strongest arguments for that distinction in the entire database.\r\n\r\n## Length-Tension Model\r\n\r\nA simplified `E_LT` model can use thoracolumbar flexion-extension state:\r\n\r\n- extension → shorter;\r\n- neutral → intermediate;\r\n- moderate flexion → longer.\r\n\r\nHowever, exact values should be assigned conservatively because posture changes simultaneously alter:\r\n\r\n- fascicle length;\r\n- pennation;\r\n- moment arms;\r\n- passive tissue contribution. :contentReference[oaicite:8]{index=8}\r\n\r\nTherefore `E_LT` should not be a simple linear function of spinal flexion angle.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nEvidence supports hypertrophy of lumbar extensors from sufficiently intense direct resistance exercise. :contentReference[oaicite:9]{index=9}\r\n\r\nWhat is not established is that maximal loaded lumbar flexion produces a special hypertrophic advantage.\r\n\r\nThe defensible model is:\r\n\r\n- erector spinae can hypertrophy substantially;\r\n- moderate flexion can increase muscle length;\r\n- active force at long length may be useful;\r\n- extreme spinal flexion introduces increasing passive-tissue contribution;\r\n- high-force neutral-spine isometrics remain highly relevant hypertrophic exposure.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **iliocostalis/longissimus/spinalis anatomy:** high;\r\n- **posterior-ramus innervation:** high;\r\n- **spinal-extension function:** high;\r\n- **anti-flexion/isometric function:** high;\r\n- **high recruitment during deadlift variants:** well supported experimentally;\r\n- **lumbar posture affecting architecture and leverage:** well supported;\r\n- **hypertrophy from direct high-intensity lumbar-extension training:** directly supported;\r\n- **exercise-specific regional hypertrophy:** limited;\r\n- **stretch-mediated hypertrophy:** insufficiently established;\r\n- **precise aggregate FCSA/recruitment coefficients:** necessarily approximate because of regional anatomy.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **erector spinae = a large thoracolumbar global extensor system that generates spinal-extension torque dynamically or isometrically, with exercise demand determined primarily by the external trunk-flexion moment rather than by the amount of visible spinal movement.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/ErectorSpinae}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	11.89	13.52	0.972	109.51	106.48
33	rectus_femoris	Musculus rectus femoris	Lower	Quads	755.00	714.96	27.00	Bipennate	0.400	0.600	extreme_high	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\nThe **rectus femoris** is the only major quadriceps muscle that crosses both the **hip** and the **knee**.\r\n\r\nThis biarticular anatomy distinguishes it sharply from:\r\n\r\n- vastus lateralis;\r\n- vastus medialis;\r\n- vastus intermedius.\r\n\r\nIts two primary actions are:\r\n\r\n- **knee extension**;\r\n- **hip flexion**.\r\n\r\nBecause it spans two joints, its operating length depends on both:\r\n\r\n- hip angle;\r\n- knee angle.\r\n\r\nThis makes rectus femoris one of the clearest examples of a muscle for which:\r\n\r\n> `E_LT` cannot be modeled from a single joint.\r\n\r\nA useful approximation is:\r\n\r\n`L_rectus_femoris = f(hip_extension/flexion, knee_flexion/extension)`\r\n\r\nThe muscle is relatively lengthened when:\r\n\r\n- the hip is extended;\r\n- the knee is flexed.\r\n\r\nIt is relatively shortened when:\r\n\r\n- the hip is flexed;\r\n- the knee is extended.\r\n\r\nThis creates important exercise-specific consequences.\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n\r\n- seated leg extension places rectus femoris in a relatively shortened proximal state because the hip is flexed;\r\n- prone or standing knee-extension-type loading with the hip extended would lengthen it more;\r\n- squatting involves simultaneous hip and knee flexion, creating opposing proximal and distal length changes.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **rectus femoris = a biarticular knee extensor and hip flexor whose force contribution is strongly governed by the interaction of hip angle and knee angle, and whose hypertrophy response can differ substantially from the monoarticular vasti.**\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\n## Origin\r\n\r\nRectus femoris has two major proximal heads.\r\n\r\n### Direct Head\r\n\r\nThe direct head originates primarily from the **anterior inferior iliac spine**.\r\n\r\n### Reflected Head\r\n\r\nThe reflected head originates from the region superior to the acetabulum, associated with the:\r\n\r\n- supra-acetabular groove;\r\n- hip joint capsule.\r\n\r\nA third small recurrent contribution has also been described anatomically in some individuals.\r\n\r\nThese proximal attachments place rectus femoris anterior to the hip joint axis.\r\n\r\nThis gives it a hip-flexion moment.\r\n\r\n## Insertion\r\n\r\nDistally, rectus femoris contributes to the common quadriceps tendon inserting onto the **patella**.\r\n\r\nForce is then transmitted through the patellar tendon to the:\r\n\r\n- tibial tuberosity.\r\n\r\nThus contraction generates knee-extension torque through the extensor mechanism.\r\n\r\n## Relationship With the Vasti\r\n\r\nThe quadriceps group consists of:\r\n\r\n- rectus femoris;\r\n- vastus lateralis;\r\n- vastus medialis;\r\n- vastus intermedius.\r\n\r\nOnly rectus femoris crosses the hip.\r\n\r\nThe vasti originate from the femur and therefore function essentially as monoarticular knee extensors.\r\n\r\nThis distinction is critical.\r\n\r\nChanging hip angle directly affects:\r\n\r\n- rectus femoris length;\r\n- rectus femoris force capacity.\r\n\r\nIt does not directly change the length of the vasti in the same way.\r\n\r\n## Architecture\r\n\r\nRectus femoris has a complex bipennate architecture.\r\n\r\nIts fascicles insert into central aponeurotic structures, allowing substantial physiological cross-sectional area within a relatively long muscle.\r\n\r\nThe architecture is regionally heterogeneous.\r\n\r\nProximal and distal regions can differ in:\r\n\r\n- fascicle length;\r\n- pennation;\r\n- recruitment;\r\n- hypertrophic response.\r\n\r\nTherefore whole-muscle architecture values should be treated as effective averages rather than uniform properties.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nRectus femoris is innervated by the **femoral nerve**, primarily from spinal roots:\r\n\r\n- L2;\r\n- L3;\r\n- L4.\r\n\r\nThe femoral nerve also supplies the other quadriceps muscles.\r\n\r\nRegional motor branching allows some intramuscular differentiation, but for exercise-level modelling a single neural drive term is generally sufficient.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Knee Extension\r\n\r\nRectus femoris contributes to **extension of the knee** through the quadriceps tendon-patellar tendon system.\r\n\r\nIt acts synergistically with:\r\n\r\n- vastus lateralis;\r\n- vastus medialis;\r\n- vastus intermedius.\r\n\r\nHowever, its relative contribution to knee extension depends on hip angle because the muscle crosses the hip.\r\n\r\nWhen the hip is flexed, rectus femoris is shortened proximally.\r\n\r\nThis can reduce its capacity to contribute to knee-extension force, shifting relative demand toward the vasti.\r\n\r\n## Hip Flexion\r\n\r\nRectus femoris also contributes to **hip flexion**.\r\n\r\nThis action overlaps with:\r\n\r\n- iliopsoas;\r\n- sartorius;\r\n- tensor fasciae latae;\r\n- pectineus.\r\n\r\nIts hip-flexion contribution depends strongly on knee angle.\r\n\r\nWhen the knee is extended, rectus femoris is relatively lengthened distally and can contribute effectively to hip flexion.\r\n\r\nWhen the knee is deeply flexed, it is shortened distally and may become mechanically disadvantaged for hip flexion.\r\n\r\n## Biarticular Mechanics\r\n\r\nBecause rectus femoris crosses both joints, length changes at the hip and knee interact.\r\n\r\n### Hip Extension\r\n\r\nLengthens rectus femoris proximally.\r\n\r\n### Hip Flexion\r\n\r\nShortens it proximally.\r\n\r\n### Knee Flexion\r\n\r\nLengthens it distally.\r\n\r\n### Knee Extension\r\n\r\nShortens it distally.\r\n\r\nTherefore the longest practical configuration is approximately:\r\n\r\n> **hip extension + knee flexion.**\r\n\r\nThe shortest configuration is approximately:\r\n\r\n> **hip flexion + knee extension.**\r\n\r\nThis interaction strongly affects force production.\r\n\r\n## Active Insufficiency\r\n\r\nRectus femoris can approach active insufficiency when simultaneously:\r\n\r\n- hip flexed;\r\n- knee extended.\r\n\r\nThis is the classic shortened configuration.\r\n\r\nIts ability to produce additional force can decline when operating at very short muscle lengths.\r\n\r\n## Passive Insufficiency\r\n\r\nThe opposite configuration:\r\n\r\n- hip extended;\r\n- knee flexed\r\n\r\nplaces rectus femoris under substantial passive tension.\r\n\r\nThis is why a prone or standing quadriceps stretch with hip extension strongly tensions rectus femoris.\r\n\r\n## Force Sharing During Knee Extension\r\n\r\nThe quadriceps do not share knee-extension torque equally across all joint configurations.\r\n\r\nIf rectus femoris is shortened at the hip, the monoarticular vasti can assume a larger relative share.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n> a seated leg extension is not simply "equal quadriceps loading."\r\n\r\nIt can bias the vasti relative to rectus femoris.\r\n\r\nThis is highly relevant to hypertrophy programming.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nRectus femoris has a particularly strong mechanistic case for long-length training because its length can be manipulated through **both joints**.\r\n\r\nThe most important configuration is:\r\n\r\n- hip extension;\r\n- knee flexion.\r\n\r\nThis places the muscle under stretch from both ends.\r\n\r\n## Seated Leg Extension Limitation\r\n\r\nA conventional seated leg extension places the hip in substantial flexion.\r\n\r\nThat shortens rectus femoris proximally.\r\n\r\nEven when the knee flexes deeply, the proximal shortening partially offsets distal lengthening.\r\n\r\nTherefore seated leg extensions may provide an excellent quadriceps stimulus overall while being less favorable for rectus femoris specifically.\r\n\r\nLongitudinal studies have repeatedly observed that knee-extension training can produce substantial hypertrophy of the vasti while rectus femoris growth is smaller or more variable.\r\n\r\nThis is biomechanically consistent with its shortened hip position.\r\n\r\n## Hip Position Matters\r\n\r\nA more extended hip increases rectus femoris length.\r\n\r\nTherefore knee-extension loading performed with less hip flexion should theoretically improve rectus femoris length state.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- prone knee extension;\r\n- standing knee-extension resistance;\r\n- reverse-Nordic-like patterns;\r\n- sissy-squat-like configurations depending on hip control.\r\n\r\nThe practical challenge is applying high knee-extension torque safely and progressively in these positions.\r\n\r\n## Reverse Nordic\r\n\r\nThe reverse Nordic curl is particularly interesting for rectus femoris because the knee flexes while the hip is kept relatively extended.\r\n\r\nThis creates:\r\n\r\n- large knee-flexion angle;\r\n- maintained hip extension;\r\n- long rectus femoris length.\r\n\r\nThe quadriceps contract eccentrically or isometrically to control the backward lean.\r\n\r\nThis makes the exercise mechanically attractive for long-length rectus femoris loading.\r\n\r\nHowever, direct rectus-femoris hypertrophy data specific to reverse Nordics are still limited.\r\n\r\n## Squat\r\n\r\nSquatting produces substantial knee flexion, which lengthens rectus femoris distally.\r\n\r\nHowever, hip flexion simultaneously shortens it proximally.\r\n\r\nThese opposing effects can keep whole-muscle length change relatively modest.\r\n\r\nThis helps explain why squat-based hypertrophy can strongly affect the vasti while rectus femoris may hypertrophy less.\r\n\r\n## Leg Press\r\n\r\nThe same general logic applies to deep leg press:\r\n\r\n- knee flexion lengthens rectus femoris distally;\r\n- hip flexion shortens it proximally.\r\n\r\nThus deep ROM does not necessarily create a maximally lengthened rectus femoris state.\r\n\r\n## Hip-Flexion Exercises\r\n\r\nStraight-leg raises and resisted hip flexion load rectus femoris dynamically as a hip flexor.\r\n\r\nBut with the knee extended, the muscle is shortened distally.\r\n\r\nThis does not provide the same long-length stimulus as knee-flexion loading with the hip extended.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nRectus femoris is one of the muscles for which a relatively high theoretical stretch-mediated hypertrophy potential is biomechanically plausible.\r\n\r\nThe key condition is not merely deep knee flexion.\r\n\r\nIt is:\r\n\r\n> **deep knee flexion while preventing compensatory hip flexion.**\r\n\r\nThat distinction should be explicit in Agonez.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Seated Leg Extension\r\n\r\nSeated leg extension is an excellent general quadriceps exercise.\r\n\r\nHowever, its rectus-femoris specificity is limited by hip flexion.\r\n\r\nThe exercise strongly loads:\r\n\r\n- vastus lateralis;\r\n- vastus medialis;\r\n- vastus intermedius.\r\n\r\nRectus femoris contributes, but its proximal shortening can reduce force capacity.\r\n\r\nTherefore the exercise should not assign the same relative demand to rectus femoris as to the combined vasti.\r\n\r\n## Seat Back Angle\r\n\r\nA more reclined leg-extension position can reduce hip flexion.\r\n\r\nThis may place rectus femoris at a slightly longer proximal length.\r\n\r\nMachine geometry therefore matters.\r\n\r\nTwo leg-extension machines with different torso angles can produce meaningfully different rectus-femoris loading.\r\n\r\n## Prone Knee Extension\r\n\r\nA prone knee-extension setup keeps the hip relatively neutral or extended.\r\n\r\nThis places rectus femoris at greater length than seated knee extension.\r\n\r\nIf progressive resistance can be applied, it provides a mechanically more direct rectus-femoris stimulus.\r\n\r\nSuch machines are uncommon in commercial gyms.\r\n\r\n## Reverse Nordic Curl\r\n\r\nReverse Nordics are one of the most accessible ways to load rectus femoris at long length.\r\n\r\nUseful execution involves:\r\n\r\n- knees flexing as the body leans backward;\r\n- hips remaining extended;\r\n- trunk and thigh staying approximately aligned.\r\n\r\nIf the hips flex substantially, rectus femoris shortens proximally and the intended long-length effect is reduced.\r\n\r\nThe exercise should therefore be thought of as:\r\n\r\n> loaded knee flexion under quadriceps eccentric control with hip extension preserved.\r\n\r\n## Sissy Squat\r\n\r\nSissy-squat variations can also place the quadriceps under high tension through deep knee flexion while minimizing hip flexion.\r\n\r\nDepending on execution, this can create favorable rectus-femoris length.\r\n\r\nHowever, balance and knee tolerance can become limiting.\r\n\r\nA supported or machine variation is usually easier to standardize.\r\n\r\n## Squat\r\n\r\nSquats produce large knee-extension demand but rectus femoris is not necessarily the dominant quadriceps contributor.\r\n\r\nThe vasti typically carry substantial force because they are not shortened by hip flexion.\r\n\r\nThus a squat should receive:\r\n\r\n- high total quadriceps demand;\r\n- high vasti demand;\r\n- meaningful but lower relative rectus-femoris allocation.\r\n\r\n## 45° Leg Press\r\n\r\nDeep leg presses also create very high knee-extension torque.\r\n\r\nHowever, high hip flexion again shortens rectus femoris proximally.\r\n\r\nTherefore the vasti should receive more of the quadriceps demand than rectus femoris.\r\n\r\n## Sprinting\r\n\r\nRectus femoris has important roles during sprinting because it can:\r\n\r\n- flex the hip;\r\n- extend the knee.\r\n\r\nIts biarticular structure makes it useful for rapid coordination between thigh advancement and knee control.\r\n\r\nHowever, sprinting loads it under high velocity and complex eccentric-concentric conditions that differ substantially from bodybuilding exercises.\r\n\r\n## Straight-Leg Raise\r\n\r\nWith the knee extended, rectus femoris can contribute meaningfully to hip flexion.\r\n\r\nIt shares the task with iliopsoas.\r\n\r\nBecause the knee is extended, rectus femoris is shortened distally, but it can still generate substantial hip-flexion torque.\r\n\r\n## Hanging Leg Raise\r\n\r\nRectus femoris should receive non-zero hip-flexion demand during hanging leg raises.\r\n\r\nHowever, `iliopsoas` should usually receive the larger share.\r\n\r\nRectus femoris contribution depends on:\r\n\r\n- knee angle;\r\n- hip angle;\r\n- fatigue;\r\n- individual anatomy.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Rectus Femoris May Need Dedicated Exercise Selection\r\n\r\nA squat-and-leg-extension program can produce excellent overall quadriceps growth while still underemphasizing rectus femoris relative to the vasti.\r\n\r\nThis is because:\r\n\r\n- squat → hip flexion offsets knee-flexion lengthening;\r\n- seated leg extension → hip flexion shortens RF proximally.\r\n\r\nTherefore a rectus-femoris-priority program can benefit from an exercise maintaining **greater hip extension during knee flexion**.\r\n\r\n## Weekly Volume\r\n\r\nIf rectus femoris is not a specific priority, ordinary quadriceps training may provide sufficient stimulus.\r\n\r\nIf it is a priority, adding approximately:\r\n\r\n- **2-6 hard long-length-biased sets per week**\r\n\r\ncan be useful.\r\n\r\nExamples:\r\n\r\n- reverse Nordic;\r\n- supported sissy squat;\r\n- suitable hip-extended knee-extension machine.\r\n\r\nThese sets should complement rather than replace high-quality general quadriceps training.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nTwo weekly exposures are generally sufficient.\r\n\r\nBecause rectus femoris also participates in hip flexion, additional fatigue can accumulate from:\r\n\r\n- sprinting;\r\n- hanging leg raises;\r\n- resisted marches;\r\n- kicking activities.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\n### Leg Extension\r\n\r\nUseful ranges:\r\n\r\n- 8-15;\r\n- 10-20.\r\n\r\n### Reverse Nordic / Sissy Squat\r\n\r\nOften more practical in:\r\n\r\n- 8-15;\r\n- 10-20;\r\n\r\ndepending on assistance and leverage.\r\n\r\nVery heavy loading can become limited by:\r\n\r\n- knee tolerance;\r\n- balance;\r\n- technique.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nMachine leg extension can be trained very close to muscular failure:\r\n\r\n- approximately 0-2 RIR.\r\n\r\nReverse Nordic and sissy-squat variants should usually stop when:\r\n\r\n- hip extension can no longer be maintained;\r\n- ROM deteriorates;\r\n- lumbar compensation increases.\r\n\r\nTechnical failure is especially important because hip flexion changes the target muscle's length state.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Order\r\n\r\nIf rectus femoris is a priority, long-length-biased work should occur before it is severely fatigued by general quadriceps training.\r\n\r\nOtherwise it can be placed after squats or leg presses as accessory work.\r\n\r\n## Running Interference\r\n\r\nRectus femoris is commonly stressed during high-speed running and kicking.\r\n\r\nHigh volumes of:\r\n\r\n- sprinting;\r\n- hill running;\r\n- kicking\r\n\r\ncan therefore affect recovery from direct RF training.\r\n\r\nThis matters more than for the monoarticular vasti.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## It Is the Only Biarticular Quadriceps Muscle\r\n\r\nThis is the defining anatomical distinction within the quadriceps group.\r\n\r\nRectus femoris crosses:\r\n\r\n- hip;\r\n- knee.\r\n\r\nThe vasti cross:\r\n- knee only.\r\n\r\nThis one fact explains many differences in recruitment and hypertrophy.\r\n\r\n## Squats Can Grow the Vasti More Than Rectus Femoris\r\n\r\nThis is not paradoxical.\r\n\r\nDuring deep squat:\r\n\r\n- knee flexion lengthens RF distally;\r\n- hip flexion shortens it proximally.\r\n\r\nThe two-joint length changes partly offset.\r\n\r\nThe monoarticular vasti, meanwhile, receive strong knee-extension loading without that proximal shortening.\r\n\r\n## Seated Leg Extensions Are Not Perfectly "Rectus Femoris Isolation"\r\n\r\nThe seated position shortens rectus femoris at the hip.\r\n\r\nThis can mechanically reduce its contribution relative to the vasti.\r\n\r\nThus an exercise that appears anatomically isolated to knee extension can still be relatively poor at maximizing one particular quadriceps head.\r\n\r\n## Reverse Nordic Has Very Different RF Geometry\r\n\r\nKeeping the hip extended while the knee flexes prevents the usual proximal shortening.\r\n\r\nThis creates one of the longest practical rectus-femoris configurations available in resistance exercise.\r\n\r\n## Hip Flexion and Knee Extension Create Active Insufficiency\r\n\r\nA high-knee position with the knee nearly straight shortens rectus femoris at both joints.\r\n\r\nThis demonstrates why a biarticular muscle's force state cannot be understood from either joint alone.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\n`rectus_femoris` should receive demand from two primary dimensions:\r\n\r\n1. **knee-extension torque**;\r\n2. **hip-flexion torque**.\r\n\r\nThese should be combined only after accounting for muscle length and force-sharing.\r\n\r\n## Length-Tension Model\r\n\r\nThe correct simplified relationship is:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_RF = f(hip_angle, knee_angle)`\r\n\r\nA useful qualitative map is:\r\n\r\n### Long\r\n\r\n- hip extended;\r\n- knee flexed.\r\n\r\n### Intermediate\r\n\r\n- one joint lengthening while the other shortens;\r\n- e.g. squat-like hip flexion + knee flexion.\r\n\r\n### Short\r\n\r\n- hip flexed;\r\n- knee extended.\r\n\r\nThis is much more informative than assigning RF the same `E_LT` as the vasti.\r\n\r\n## Quadriceps Force Sharing\r\n\r\nFor knee extension:\r\n\r\n`T_knee_extension = T_RF + T_VL + T_VM + T_VI`\r\n\r\nThe relative RF share should fall when hip flexion becomes large.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n### Seated Leg Extension\r\n\r\nHigh total quadriceps demand.\r\n\r\nRelative:\r\n- vasti high;\r\n- RF moderate.\r\n\r\n### More Reclined / Hip-Extended Knee Extension\r\n\r\nRelative RF share increases.\r\n\r\n### Squat / Leg Press\r\n\r\nVery high total quadriceps demand.\r\n\r\nRF contribution remains meaningful but lower relative to the vasti than a naive equal split would imply.\r\n\r\n## Hip-Flexion Force Sharing\r\n\r\nFor hip flexion:\r\n\r\n`T_hip_flexion ≈ iliopsoas + rectus_femoris + TFL + sartorius + others`\r\n\r\nRectus femoris share should depend on knee angle.\r\n\r\n### Knee Extended\r\n\r\nRF contribution can be substantial.\r\n\r\n### Knee Flexed\r\n\r\nRF shortens distally.\r\n\r\nRelative share can shift toward iliopsoas.\r\n\r\nThis mirrors the same biarticular logic seen in other muscles.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Allocation\r\n\r\n### Selectorized Seated Leg Extension\r\n\r\nHigh rectus femoris demand, but lower relative share than the combined vasti.\r\n\r\nDo not distribute quadriceps torque evenly.\r\n\r\n### 45° Leg Press\r\n\r\nModerate-to-high RF demand.\r\n\r\nVery high:\r\n- vastus lateralis;\r\n- vastus medialis;\r\n- vastus intermedius.\r\n\r\n### Deep Squat\r\n\r\nMeaningful RF demand, but again substantial preferential loading of the vasti.\r\n\r\n### Hanging Straight-Leg Raise\r\n\r\nModerate RF hip-flexion demand.\r\n\r\nVery high:\r\n- iliopsoas.\r\n\r\n### Hanging Knee Raise\r\n\r\nLower RF share because knee flexion shortens the muscle distally.\r\n\r\n### Reverse Nordic\r\n\r\nVery high RF lengthened-state demand.\r\n\r\nAlso high:\r\n- vasti.\r\n\r\n### Sprinting\r\n\r\nHigh task-specific RF demand across both hip and knee roles.\r\n\r\nDifferent ETU profile from slow resistance exercise because of velocity and eccentric loading.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Iliopsoas\r\n\r\nThis force-sharing relationship is important in hip flexion.\r\n\r\nWhen the knee is straight:\r\n\r\n- RF can contribute substantially.\r\n\r\nWhen the knee is bent:\r\n\r\n- RF becomes shorter;\r\n- iliopsoas relative contribution increases.\r\n\r\nThus two hip-flexion exercises with equal external torque can have different muscle vectors solely because of knee angle.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With the Vasti\r\n\r\nThe opposite logic applies during knee extension.\r\n\r\nWhen the hip is highly flexed:\r\n\r\n- RF is shortened;\r\n- vasti relative contribution increases.\r\n\r\nThis is one of the strongest reasons to keep `rectus_femoris` separate from the other quadriceps in Agonez.\r\n\r\n## Squat Modelling\r\n\r\nA naive model might assume:\r\n\r\n`deep knee flexion = highly stretched RF`\r\n\r\nThis is incomplete.\r\n\r\nThe correct state is:\r\n\r\n`RF_length = knee_flexion_lengthening - hip_flexion_shortening`\r\n\r\nconceptually.\r\n\r\nTherefore deep squatting can produce only modest net RF lengthening despite very large knee ROM.\r\n\r\n## Leg Extension Modelling\r\n\r\nA seated leg-extension machine creates:\r\n\r\n- large knee-extension torque;\r\n- strong RF recruitment;\r\n- but relatively short proximal RF state.\r\n\r\nThus the exercise can still generate significant RF hypertrophy, but its `E_LT` should be lower than in a hip-extended knee-extension configuration.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nRectus femoris is one of the more compelling muscles for angle-specific hypertrophy modelling because:\r\n\r\n- it is biarticular;\r\n- both joints strongly influence length;\r\n- common exercises place it in very different operating states.\r\n\r\nA relatively high theoretical `smh_factor` is defensible.\r\n\r\nHowever, the model should not simply reward knee flexion.\r\n\r\nIt should reward:\r\n\r\n> **knee flexion combined with maintained hip extension or minimal hip flexion.**\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **AIIS/supra-acetabular origin and patellar insertion:** high;\r\n- **femoral-nerve innervation:** high;\r\n- **knee-extension function:** high;\r\n- **hip-flexion function:** high;\r\n- **biarticular length dependence:** very high;\r\n- **active/passive insufficiency mechanics:** high;\r\n- **reduced RF contribution when shortened by hip flexion:** strongly supported biomechanically;\r\n- **different RF vs vasti hypertrophy responses across exercise types:** supported longitudinally;\r\n- **long-length superiority specifically for RF:** mechanistically strong but still less directly established than gastrocnemius;\r\n- **reverse-Nordic-specific hypertrophy superiority:** insufficient direct evidence;\r\n- **precise exercise-specific force-sharing coefficients:** strongly angle-dependent.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **rectus femoris = the biarticular quadriceps component that generates both knee-extension and hip-flexion torque, with force capacity governed by the combined hip-and-knee configuration; it is maximally lengthened by hip extension plus knee flexion and relatively disadvantaged by the hip-flexed positions used in many conventional quadriceps exercises.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/Quadriceps}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	7.59	13.90	0.971	94.20	91.44
36	vastus_intermedius	Musculus vastus intermedius	Lower	Quads	740.00	700.76	48.50	Pennate	0.550	0.450	high	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\nThe **vastus intermedius (VI)** is one of the four muscles of the quadriceps femoris complex and the deepest of the three vasti.\r\n\r\nIts defining function is:\r\n\r\n- **knee extension**.\r\n\r\nUnlike `rectus_femoris`, vastus intermedius does **not** cross the hip joint.\r\n\r\nTherefore its direct length-tension behavior is governed primarily by:\r\n\r\n- knee flexion/extension;\r\n\r\nand not by hip position.\r\n\r\nThis makes it mechanically similar in broad function to:\r\n\r\n- `vastus_lateralis`;\r\n- `vastus_medialis`;\r\n\r\nwhile differing from them in:\r\n\r\n- deep anatomical position;\r\n- architecture;\r\n- regional morphology;\r\n- exact patellar-force geometry;\r\n- potentially angle-dependent recruitment.\r\n\r\nHuman studies specifically measuring VI show that its architecture is meaningfully associated with knee-extension performance and that VI neuromuscular behavior changes with knee angle. :contentReference[oaicite:0]{index=0}\r\n\r\nFor Agonez, the most useful abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **vastus intermedius = a deep monoarticular knee extensor whose direct muscle length depends primarily on knee flexion, whose force contributes to the common quadriceps extensor mechanism, and whose demand should generally be substantial in any high-torque knee-extension task regardless of hip angle.**\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\n## Origin\r\n\r\nVastus intermedius originates broadly from the:\r\n\r\n- anterior surface of the femoral shaft;\r\n- lateral/anterior femoral surfaces;\r\n- proximal and middle portions of the femur.\r\n\r\nIt lies directly beneath:\r\n\r\n- rectus femoris.\r\n\r\nThis deep location makes it difficult to assess with ordinary surface EMG.\r\n\r\nThat measurement limitation is one reason VI is often absent from exercise-comparison studies despite being a major quadriceps muscle.\r\n\r\n## Insertion\r\n\r\nDistally, vastus intermedius contributes to the common quadriceps tendon attaching to the:\r\n\r\n- patella.\r\n\r\nForce is then transmitted through the:\r\n\r\n- patella;\r\n- patellar tendon;\r\n\r\nto the tibial tuberosity.\r\n\r\nThus its force contributes directly to:\r\n\r\n- knee-extension torque.\r\n\r\n## Deep Position\r\n\r\nVastus intermedius sits between:\r\n\r\n- rectus femoris superficially;\r\n- femoral shaft deeply.\r\n\r\nIt is therefore largely invisible externally.\r\n\r\nIts absence from visual inspection should not be confused with low mechanical importance.\r\n\r\nClinical imaging work has found VI size to be meaningfully related to knee-extension strength, reinforcing that it is a substantial contributor rather than a minor accessory muscle. :contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}\r\n\r\n## Relationship With Articularis Genus\r\n\r\nThe distal deep quadriceps region is anatomically related to the **articularis genus**, a small muscle associated with the suprapatellar region.\r\n\r\nAnatomical research shows close relationships among:\r\n\r\n- vastus intermedius;\r\n- vastus medialis;\r\n- articularis genus.\r\n\r\nHowever, articularis genus is mechanically much smaller and should not be conflated with VI's primary knee-extension role. :contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nVastus intermedius is innervated by branches of the **femoral nerve**, primarily from lumbar roots approximately:\r\n\r\n- L2;\r\n- L3;\r\n- L4.\r\n\r\nThis is shared with the rest of quadriceps femoris.\r\n\r\nRegional motor branches nevertheless permit somewhat different activation behavior among:\r\n\r\n- VI;\r\n- VL;\r\n- VM;\r\n- RF.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Knee Extension\r\n\r\nThe principal function of vastus intermedius is **extension of the knee**.\r\n\r\nIt contributes to the common quadriceps torque together with:\r\n\r\n- vastus lateralis;\r\n- vastus medialis;\r\n- rectus femoris.\r\n\r\nA simplified model is:\r\n\r\n`T_knee_extension =\r\n    F_VI × r_VI\r\n  + F_VL × r_VL\r\n  + F_VM × r_VM\r\n  + F_RF × r_RF`\r\n\r\nwhere:\r\n\r\n- each muscle has distinct capacity;\r\n- leverage varies with joint angle;\r\n- relative force sharing is not necessarily constant.\r\n\r\n## Monoarticular Design\r\n\r\nVI crosses only the knee.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n- hip flexion does not shorten it proximally;\r\n- hip extension does not lengthen it proximally.\r\n\r\nThis sharply distinguishes it from `rectus_femoris`.\r\n\r\nFor VI:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_VI ≈ f(knee_angle)`\r\n\r\nwhereas for RF:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_RF = f(knee_angle, hip_angle)`.\r\n\r\n## Eccentric Control of Knee Flexion\r\n\r\nDuring locomotion and other tasks, VI can also act eccentrically while the knee flexes.\r\n\r\nGait EMG work including the deep VI showed activity consistent with the quadriceps controlling knee flexion eccentrically as well as producing extension. :contentReference[oaicite:3]{index=3}\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n> visible knee flexion does not imply VI is inactive.\r\n\r\nIt can produce force while lengthening.\r\n\r\n## Knee-Angle Dependence\r\n\r\nVI activation and force behavior are angle dependent.\r\n\r\nExperimental work comparing the four quadriceps synergists found that the VI EMG-force relationship changed substantially with knee joint angle and appeared particularly sensitive to muscle-length changes among the quadriceps muscles tested. :contentReference[oaicite:4]{index=4}\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n`VI_demand != constant fraction of quadriceps demand across ROM`.\r\n\r\nThis does not mean one exact universal force-sharing curve is established, but it argues against a fixed split.\r\n\r\n# Length-Tension Mechanics\r\n\r\nBecause VI is monoarticular, its length behavior is conceptually clean.\r\n\r\n## Knee Flexion\r\n\r\nLengthens vastus intermedius.\r\n\r\n## Knee Extension\r\n\r\nShortens vastus intermedius.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n### Longer\r\n\r\n- knee deeply flexed.\r\n\r\n### Intermediate\r\n\r\n- moderate knee flexion.\r\n\r\n### Shorter\r\n\r\n- knee near full extension.\r\n\r\nUnlike RF, hip position has little direct influence on VI length.\r\n\r\n## Deep Knee Flexion\r\n\r\nIn:\r\n\r\n- deep squat;\r\n- deep leg press;\r\n- knee extension beginning from high flexion;\r\n\r\nVI is placed at greater muscle length.\r\n\r\nIf substantial knee-extension force is simultaneously required, this creates active long-length loading.\r\n\r\n## Near Lockout\r\n\r\nNear full extension:\r\n\r\n- VI is shorter.\r\n\r\nWhether muscle force is high depends on the exercise's external knee moment.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`short muscle length`\r\n\r\ndoes not necessarily imply:\r\n\r\n`low force`\r\n\r\nand:\r\n\r\n`near lockout`\r\n\r\ndoes not automatically imply high stimulus.\r\n\r\nResistance geometry remains critical.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nDirect VI-specific longitudinal hypertrophy evidence is more limited than for:\r\n\r\n- vastus lateralis;\r\n- rectus femoris.\r\n\r\nThis is largely because the muscle is deep and harder to measure repeatedly.\r\n\r\nHowever, the mechanical rationale for long-length loading is strong.\r\n\r\n## Knee Flexion as the Main Length Variable\r\n\r\nFor VI:\r\n\r\n`muscle_length ↑ as knee_flexion ↑`.\r\n\r\nThus exercises producing high knee-extension torque at larger knee-flexion angles expose VI to:\r\n\r\n- greater muscle length;\r\n- meaningful active force.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- deep leg press;\r\n- deep squat;\r\n- knee extension loaded in the flexed-knee region.\r\n\r\n## Knee Extension ROM Evidence\r\n\r\nQuadriceps studies comparing knee-extension ranges have generally found that the trained ROM influences regional hypertrophy and strength adaptation, supporting the broader principle that knee-angle-specific loading matters. :contentReference[oaicite:5]{index=5}\r\n\r\nHowever, many such studies quantify:\r\n\r\n- VL;\r\n- RF;\r\n\r\nrather than VI directly.\r\n\r\nTherefore they should inform VI modelling cautiously rather than being treated as direct VI evidence.\r\n\r\n## Long-Muscle-Length Isometrics\r\n\r\nRecent quadriceps work comparing long-length isometric versus isotonic knee-extension training provides further evidence that long knee-flexor positions can be effective for quadriceps hypertrophy, although again the measurement emphasis is usually not isolated VI. :contentReference[oaicite:6]{index=6}\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nThe most defensible Agonez position is:\r\n\r\n- knee flexion is a clear VI lengthening variable;\r\n- loading at larger knee-flexion angles is mechanically favorable for long-length tension;\r\n- quadriceps-wide evidence supports the relevance of long-length loading;\r\n- VI-specific direct hypertrophy evidence remains limited.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n- `E_LT` can be high at deep knee flexion;\r\n- `smh_factor` can be moderate-to-high mechanistically;\r\n- evidence confidence should remain below muscles with direct VI-specific longitudinal hypertrophy data.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Leg Extension\r\n\r\nThe leg-extension machine is one of the cleanest VI exercises.\r\n\r\nThe task consists almost entirely of:\r\n\r\n- knee-extension torque.\r\n\r\nTherefore the vasti receive very high specificity.\r\n\r\nBecause VI does not cross the hip, seated hip flexion does not shorten it in the way it shortens `rectus_femoris`.\r\n\r\nThis creates an important force-sharing implication:\r\n\r\n> seated leg extension can place the rectus femoris in a less favorable proximal length state while the vasti, including VI, remain mechanically unaffected by hip angle.\r\n\r\nThus VI can take a substantial portion of total quadriceps demand.\r\n\r\n## Deep-Start Leg Extension\r\n\r\nBeginning the repetition at greater knee flexion places VI at greater length.\r\n\r\nIf the machine preserves meaningful torque there, this provides:\r\n\r\n- high specificity;\r\n- high long-length VI loading.\r\n\r\nThe actual stimulus depends strongly on machine cam geometry.\r\n\r\n## Smith / Barbell Squat\r\n\r\nSquats generate large knee-extension torque, especially around deeper portions of the movement.\r\n\r\nVI should therefore receive substantial demand.\r\n\r\nForce is shared with:\r\n\r\n- VL;\r\n- VM;\r\n- RF.\r\n\r\nMeanwhile hip extension is handled by:\r\n\r\n- gluteus maximus;\r\n- adductor magnus;\r\n- hamstrings to a lesser degree.\r\n\r\n## Deep Squat\r\n\r\nIncreasing squat depth increases knee flexion and therefore VI length.\r\n\r\nIf knee-extension torque remains high, VI receives strong long-length loading.\r\n\r\nUnlike rectus femoris, there is no opposing proximal shortening from hip flexion.\r\n\r\nThis means deep flexion is mechanically a particularly straightforward lengthening stimulus for the vasti.\r\n\r\n## 45° Leg Press\r\n\r\nThe 45° leg press should produce very high VI demand when performed through substantial knee flexion.\r\n\r\nAdvantages include:\r\n\r\n- high knee-extensor torque;\r\n- trunk support;\r\n- ability to accumulate high local quadriceps force.\r\n\r\nRelative systemic fatigue is generally lower than in heavy free squatting.\r\n\r\n## Leg Press Depth\r\n\r\nGreater knee flexion increases VI length.\r\n\r\nHowever, depth should only count when:\r\n\r\n- the pelvis remains controlled;\r\n- knee-extension torque remains meaningful;\r\n- ROM is actually produced at the knee rather than primarily through pelvic/spinal movement.\r\n\r\n## Split Squat\r\n\r\nVI contributes substantially to knee extension on the lead leg.\r\n\r\nIts demand depends on:\r\n\r\n- knee travel;\r\n- torso angle;\r\n- step length;\r\n- depth.\r\n\r\nMore knee-dominant execution increases quadriceps torque and therefore VI demand.\r\n\r\n## Step-Up\r\n\r\nVI contributes to knee extension during ascent.\r\n\r\nIts force share depends on:\r\n\r\n- box height;\r\n- forward knee travel;\r\n- hip contribution;\r\n- momentum.\r\n\r\n## Sissy Squat / Reverse-Nordic-Type Knee Extension\r\n\r\nThese patterns can create very high knee-extension torque while maintaining large knee flexion.\r\n\r\nFor the monoarticular vasti, this can be an attractive long-length loading condition.\r\n\r\nRectus femoris behaves differently because hip angle also affects it.\r\n\r\n## Cycling\r\n\r\nVI is active during cyclical knee extension and contributes with the other vasti.\r\n\r\nHowever, conventional cycling should not be treated as equivalent to hard hypertrophy sets unless resistance and local effort are very high.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## VI Does Not Need a Unique Exercise\r\n\r\nBecause any strong knee-extension task recruits the vasti, there is rarely a need to attempt VI isolation.\r\n\r\nUseful exercise families include:\r\n\r\n- leg extension;\r\n- squat;\r\n- leg press;\r\n- split squat;\r\n- knee-dominant machine work.\r\n\r\n## Combine Compound and Isolation Work\r\n\r\nA useful quadriceps setup often combines:\r\n\r\n1. a compound knee-extension pattern;\r\n2. a leg-extension pattern.\r\n\r\nFor VI this provides:\r\n\r\n### Compound\r\n\r\n- high absolute force;\r\n- substantial long-length loading at depth.\r\n\r\n### Leg Extension\r\n\r\n- high local specificity;\r\n- low systemic fatigue;\r\n- easy progression near failure.\r\n\r\n## Weekly Volume\r\n\r\nA practical quadriceps-wide starting range may be approximately:\r\n\r\n- **6-12 hard effective sets per week**,\r\n\r\nwith VI receiving a substantial share of most knee-extension sets.\r\n\r\nThere is usually no need for a VI-specific volume quota.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nTwo to three weekly exposures are practical.\r\n\r\nRecovery overlap is nearly complete with:\r\n\r\n- VL;\r\n- VM;\r\n- RF.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\n### Compound Work\r\n\r\nUseful:\r\n- 5-8;\r\n- 6-10;\r\n- 8-15 reps.\r\n\r\n### Leg Extension\r\n\r\nUseful:\r\n- 8-15;\r\n- 10-20;\r\n- 15-25 reps.\r\n\r\nThere is no evidence that VI requires a distinct repetition range.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nStable leg extensions can be trained close to failure:\r\n\r\n- approximately 0-2 RIR.\r\n\r\nCompound movements often benefit from slightly greater reserve because of:\r\n\r\n- technique;\r\n- cardiorespiratory load;\r\n- axial/systemic fatigue.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## VI Is Hidden but Large\r\n\r\nBecause it sits under rectus femoris, vastus intermedius receives far less attention than VL or VM.\r\n\r\nYet imaging studies show that its size is meaningfully associated with knee-extension function. :contentReference[oaicite:7]{index=7}\r\n\r\nIt should therefore not be treated as a trivial remainder of quadriceps force.\r\n\r\n## It Is Easier to Model Than Rectus Femoris\r\n\r\nVI is monoarticular.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_VI ≈ f(knee_angle)`\r\n\r\nis sufficient at first order.\r\n\r\nRF requires:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_RF = f(knee_angle, hip_angle)`.\r\n\r\nThis means the same seated leg-extension setup can produce very different length states across quadriceps heads.\r\n\r\n## It May Be Particularly Sensitive to Knee Angle\r\n\r\nExperimental research measuring all four quadriceps muscles found that VI's neuromuscular activation-force relationship was especially sensitive to changes in knee joint angle. :contentReference[oaicite:8]{index=8}\r\n\r\nThis reinforces the need to avoid one constant VI coefficient across ROM.\r\n\r\n## Architecture Matters for Force Development\r\n\r\nVI architecture has been shown to predict aspects of late-phase knee-extension rate of force development in resistance-trained men. :contentReference[oaicite:9]{index=9}\r\n\r\nThus VI architecture is not merely descriptive anatomy; it has measurable functional relevance.\r\n\r\n## Surface EMG Underrepresents Our Knowledge of VI\r\n\r\nVI lies beneath rectus femoris.\r\n\r\nOrdinary surface electrodes therefore cannot measure it cleanly.\r\n\r\nConsequently, many classic quadriceps EMG studies omit VI.\r\n\r\nThis means:\r\n\r\n> absence of VI from an exercise study often reflects measurement difficulty rather than biological insignificance.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\n`vastus_intermedius` should receive demand almost entirely from:\r\n\r\n1. **knee-extension torque**;\r\n2. secondary knee-joint stabilization.\r\n\r\nIt should receive approximately zero direct:\r\n\r\n- hip-flexion torque;\r\n- hip-extension torque.\r\n\r\n## Quadriceps Force Sharing\r\n\r\nA simplified model is:\r\n\r\n`T_knee_ext =\r\n    VI\r\n  + VL\r\n  + VM\r\n  + RF`\r\n\r\nwith each term representing:\r\n\r\n`F_i × r_i`.\r\n\r\nDo not use a fixed 25/25/25/25 split.\r\n\r\nRelative sharing depends on:\r\n\r\n- knee angle;\r\n- hip angle for RF;\r\n- individual architecture;\r\n- exercise;\r\n- neural recruitment.\r\n\r\n## Comparison With Rectus Femoris\r\n\r\nThis distinction is especially important.\r\n\r\n### Vastus Intermedius\r\n\r\nMonoarticular:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_VI = f(knee_angle)`\r\n\r\n### Rectus Femoris\r\n\r\nBiarticular:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_RF = f(knee_angle, hip_angle)`\r\n\r\nTherefore during a **seated leg extension**:\r\n\r\n- knee flexion lengthens both;\r\n- hip flexion shortens RF;\r\n- hip flexion does not directly shorten VI.\r\n\r\nThus relative VI/vasti share can rise compared with RF.\r\n\r\nThis is one of the cleanest quadriceps force-sharing modifiers available to Agonez.\r\n\r\n## Comparison With Vastus Lateralis and Vastus Medialis\r\n\r\nAll three vasti are monoarticular knee extensors.\r\n\r\nTherefore their first-order length models are similar.\r\n\r\nHowever, they differ in:\r\n\r\n- PCSA;\r\n- architecture;\r\n- patellar attachment geometry;\r\n- regional leverage;\r\n- activation.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n`E_LT`\r\n\r\ncan be similar while:\r\n\r\n`FCSA capacity`\r\nand\r\n`E_MA / force_share`\r\n\r\ndiffer.\r\n\r\n## Seated Leg Extension Allocation\r\n\r\nVery high:\r\n- `vastus_intermedius`;\r\n- `vastus_lateralis`;\r\n- `vastus_medialis`.\r\n\r\nMeaningful but potentially relatively reduced:\r\n- `rectus_femoris`\r\n\r\nbecause hip flexion changes RF's proximal length state.\r\n\r\nVI should therefore receive a substantial fraction of systemic quadriceps demand.\r\n\r\n## 45° Leg Press Allocation\r\n\r\nVery high VI demand when knee flexion is substantial.\r\n\r\nAlso very high:\r\n- VL;\r\n- VM.\r\n\r\nRF:\r\n- meaningful, but hip/knee biarticular geometry changes its effective contribution.\r\n\r\nHip extensors carry the separate hip-extension component.\r\n\r\n## Deep Squat Allocation\r\n\r\nHigh-to-very-high VI demand.\r\n\r\nDeep knee flexion creates:\r\n\r\n- long VI length;\r\n- large knee-extension requirement.\r\n\r\nUnlike RF, no hip-flexion shortening offsets VI lengthening.\r\n\r\nThis makes VI one of the clearest beneficiaries of deep knee-flexion loading.\r\n\r\n## Partial Squat\r\n\r\nShorter knee-flexion ROM reduces maximal VI length exposure.\r\n\r\nHowever, greater external loading can offset this partially through higher active force.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n`longer muscle length`\r\nand\r\n`higher absolute load`\r\n\r\nshould remain separate stimulus dimensions.\r\n\r\n## Sissy Squat / Reverse-Nordic-Type Pattern\r\n\r\nVery high potential VI demand if external knee-extension torque is substantial.\r\n\r\nThe vasti are lengthened by knee flexion while hip position has little effect on them.\r\n\r\nThis makes such patterns mechanically attractive for quadriceps long-length exposure.\r\n\r\n## Knee-Angle E_LT Model\r\n\r\nA first-order representation can be:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_VI = f(knee_flexion)`\r\n\r\nwith:\r\n\r\n### Near Full Extension\r\n\r\nLower length state.\r\n\r\n### Moderate Flexion\r\n\r\nMid-range.\r\n\r\n### Deep Flexion\r\n\r\nHigh length state.\r\n\r\nExact force-length optimum should not be assumed to coincide with maximum anatomical length.\r\n\r\nHuman VI work shows meaningful knee-angle dependence in neuromuscular behavior, but not one universally established simple linear curve. :contentReference[oaicite:10]{index=10}\r\n\r\n## E_MA Model\r\n\r\nQuadriceps knee-extension leverage varies across knee angle through the patella/extensor mechanism.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`E_MA_VI = f(knee_angle)`\r\n\r\nrather than one fixed coefficient.\r\n\r\nFor a KISS implementation, VI/VL/VM can initially share a broadly similar extensor-mechanism leverage curve, with differences represented primarily through:\r\n\r\n- FCSA;\r\n- architecture;\r\n- force-sharing coefficients.\r\n\r\n## FCSA Interpretation\r\n\r\nVI FCSA is directly interpretable as a major source of knee-extension force capacity.\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`F_max_VI ≈ σ × FCSA_VI`\r\n\r\nand:\r\n\r\n`T_VI = F_VI × r_knee`.\r\n\r\nBecause VI is monoarticular, there is no biarticular double-counting problem like:\r\n\r\n- rectus femoris;\r\n- hamstrings;\r\n- triceps long head.\r\n\r\nIts single force source primarily maps to one joint moment.\r\n\r\n## Exercise-Level FCSA Demand\r\n\r\nIn a high-torque knee-extension exercise, systemic quadriceps FCSA demand should be distributed across the four heads according to:\r\n\r\n- available capacity;\r\n- length-tension state;\r\n- leverage;\r\n- neural recruitment.\r\n\r\nFor VI:\r\n\r\n`Demand_VI =\r\n    FCSA_capacity_VI\r\n    × E_LT_VI\r\n    × E_MA_VI\r\n    × E_ND_VI\r\n    × E_LoA_VI`.\r\n\r\nSince its line of action is fundamentally aligned with knee extension:\r\n\r\n`E_LoA_VI`\r\n\r\nshould generally be high in conventional knee-extension tasks.\r\n\r\n## Hip-Angle Independence\r\n\r\nThis deserves explicit implementation.\r\n\r\nFor VI:\r\n\r\n`∂E_LT_VI / ∂hip_angle ≈ 0`.\r\n\r\nFor RF:\r\n\r\n`∂E_LT_RF / ∂hip_angle != 0`.\r\n\r\nTherefore changing hip angle can redistribute quadriceps force even when:\r\n\r\n- external knee torque;\r\n- knee angle\r\n\r\nremain identical.\r\n\r\n## ETU Interpretation\r\n\r\nVI ETU should be driven mainly by:\r\n\r\n- active knee-extension force;\r\n- contraction duration;\r\n- length state;\r\n- ROM;\r\n- proximity to failure.\r\n\r\nIt can accumulate meaningful ETU during:\r\n\r\n- concentric extension;\r\n- eccentric knee-flexion control;\r\n- isometric knee-extension force.\r\n\r\nAgain:\r\n\r\n`knee_ROM = 0`\r\n\r\ndoes not imply:\r\n\r\n`VI_ETU = 0`\r\n\r\nduring loaded isometric knee-extension tasks.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nThe mechanical rationale for long-length VI loading is strong because deep knee flexion clearly lengthens the muscle.\r\n\r\nHowever, direct VI-specific longitudinal evidence is limited.\r\n\r\nThus a useful implementation is:\r\n\r\n`SMH_effective_VI =\r\n    base_smh_factor\r\n    × E_LT(knee_angle)\r\n    × active_force`.\r\n\r\nDeep loaded knee flexion should score higher than short-ROM near-lockout loading, but the magnitude of a special VI-specific SMH bonus should remain conservative.\r\n\r\n## Data-Quality Caveat\r\n\r\nVI should probably carry a slightly wider uncertainty band than VL or RF for exercise-specific coefficients.\r\n\r\nReasons include:\r\n\r\n- deep location;\r\n- difficulty of surface EMG measurement;\r\n- fewer longitudinal hypertrophy studies measuring it separately.\r\n\r\nThis is a **measurement problem**, not evidence that VI contributes little.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **deep anterior-femoral origin and quadriceps-tendon insertion:** high;\r\n- **femoral-nerve innervation:** high;\r\n- **monoarticular knee-extension function:** very high;\r\n- **absence of direct hip action:** very high;\r\n- **knee-angle-dependent length state:** very high;\r\n- **meaningful VI contribution to knee-extension strength:** supported by imaging/function data; :contentReference[oaicite:11]{index=11}\r\n- **VI architecture related to knee-extension RFD:** directly demonstrated; :contentReference[oaicite:12]{index=12}\r\n- **VI neuromuscular behavior sensitive to knee angle:** directly demonstrated; :contentReference[oaicite:13]{index=13}\r\n- **eccentric activity during knee-flexion phases of gait:** experimentally supported; :contentReference[oaicite:14]{index=14}\r\n- **deep-knee-flexion long-length rationale:** mechanically strong;\r\n- **VI-specific stretch-mediated hypertrophy magnitude:** insufficiently established;\r\n- **precise VI/VL/VM/RF force sharing in individual exercises:** not robustly established.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **vastus intermedius = a deep, substantial monoarticular quadriceps muscle that converts contractile force directly into knee-extension torque, is progressively lengthened as the knee flexes, remains essentially independent of hip angle, and should therefore receive high demand in both leg-extension and deep compound knee-extension tasks even though it is frequently omitted from surface-EMG exercise literature.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/Quadriceps}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	9.93	4.50	0.997	70.57	70.35
18	brachialis	Musculus brachialis	Upper	Biceps	400.00	378.79	23.00	Fusiform/Convergent	0.600	0.400	low	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\nThe **brachialis** is a large monoarticular muscle of the anterior arm and one of the principal generators of **elbow-flexion torque**.\r\n\r\nIt lies deep to the biceps brachii and spans only the elbow joint:\r\n\r\n- origin on the humerus;\r\n- insertion on the ulna.\r\n\r\nThis anatomy gives it a mechanically clean role.\r\n\r\nUnlike `biceps_brachii`, the brachialis:\r\n\r\n- does not cross the shoulder;\r\n- does not insert on the radius;\r\n- does not directly produce forearm supination;\r\n- does not directly produce forearm pronation.\r\n\r\nIts primary task is therefore:\r\n\r\n> **flexion of the ulna relative to the humerus at the elbow.**\r\n\r\nBecause the ulna does not rotate around its longitudinal axis during pronation-supination in the same way as the radius, brachialis remains an elbow flexor regardless of whether the forearm is:\r\n\r\n- supinated;\r\n- neutral;\r\n- pronated.\r\n\r\nThis makes it a particularly important contributor when biceps brachii is mechanically disadvantaged by pronation.\r\n\r\nFor exercise modelling, brachialis should therefore be treated as a **general elbow-flexion actuator**, whereas biceps-specific demand should be additionally modified by forearm orientation and supination requirements.\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\n## Origin\r\n\r\nThe brachialis originates broadly from the **distal anterior surface of the humerus**.\r\n\r\nIts origin typically includes approximately the distal half of the anterior humeral shaft and extends onto adjacent intermuscular septa.\r\n\r\nThe muscle begins distal to the insertion of the deltoid and lies deep to the biceps brachii for most of its course.\r\n\r\nThe broad humeral origin provides a large contractile base and helps explain its substantial force-generating capacity.\r\n\r\n## Insertion\r\n\r\nThe brachialis inserts primarily onto the proximal ulna, particularly the:\r\n\r\n- **ulnar tuberosity**;\r\n- **coronoid process**.\r\n\r\nBecause its distal attachment is on the ulna rather than the radius, the muscle has no direct mechanical role in rotating the radius during pronation or supination.\r\n\r\nThis is one of the most important anatomical distinctions between:\r\n\r\n- `brachialis`;\r\n- `biceps_brachii`;\r\n- `brachioradialis`.\r\n\r\n## Internal Organization\r\n\r\nThe brachialis is often illustrated as a simple fusiform muscle, but anatomical studies demonstrate greater structural complexity.\r\n\r\nCadaveric work has described superficial and deep components with differing fiber orientations and insertion patterns.\r\n\r\nThe deeper component can possess a more oblique course toward the coronoid region, while the larger superficial component contributes strongly to the ulnar tuberosity attachment. :contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}\r\n\r\nThis regional organization suggests that the muscle is not perfectly homogeneous, although for exercise-level modelling a single `brachialis` entity remains a reasonable abstraction.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nThe brachialis is innervated primarily by the **musculocutaneous nerve**, predominantly from spinal roots **C5-C6**.\r\n\r\nA portion of the muscle, particularly laterally, may also receive innervation from a branch of the **radial nerve**.\r\n\r\nThis dual contribution is anatomically variable.\r\n\r\nFor a training-oriented model, the practical interpretation is simply that brachialis belongs predominantly to the musculocutaneous anterior-arm motor system, but unlike biceps brachii it can have an additional radial-nerve contribution. :contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Elbow Flexion\r\n\r\nThe primary and overwhelmingly dominant function of brachialis is **elbow flexion**.\r\n\r\nContraction decreases the angle between the humerus and ulna.\r\n\r\nBecause its insertion is on the ulna, its flexion action is largely independent of forearm rotation.\r\n\r\nThis makes brachialis mechanically distinct from biceps brachii.\r\n\r\n### Supinated Forearm\r\n\r\nBrachialis contributes strongly to elbow flexion alongside biceps brachii.\r\n\r\n### Neutral Forearm\r\n\r\nBrachialis remains strongly active while brachioradialis becomes particularly relevant.\r\n\r\n### Pronated Forearm\r\n\r\nBrachialis remains mechanically capable of producing elbow-flexion torque even though biceps brachii loses some of its favorable contribution associated with supination.\r\n\r\nThus, pronated curling does not "switch off" elbow flexion.\r\n\r\nIt changes **force sharing among the elbow flexors**.\r\n\r\n## Forearm Rotation\r\n\r\nBrachialis produces essentially no primary pronation or supination torque.\r\n\r\nIts distal attachment is on the ulna, which does not undergo the same rotational excursion as the radius during forearm rotation.\r\n\r\nThis is why it can be considered a relatively **pure elbow flexor**.\r\n\r\nThe distinction is particularly useful in Agonez:\r\n\r\n- elbow-flexion demand should recruit brachialis in all grips;\r\n- supination demand should increase biceps-specific allocation rather than brachialis allocation.\r\n\r\n## Joint-Angle Dependence\r\n\r\nBrachialis leverage changes with elbow angle.\r\n\r\nLike other muscles, its moment arm is not constant throughout ROM.\r\n\r\nIts elbow-flexion torque therefore depends on:\r\n\r\n- muscle force;\r\n- muscle length;\r\n- elbow angle;\r\n- tendon geometry;\r\n- external moment arm.\r\n\r\nA resistance exercise may feel hardest in one region not because brachialis suddenly becomes more active there, but because the interaction of internal and external moment arms changes.\r\n\r\n## Monoarticular Advantage\r\n\r\nBecause brachialis crosses only the elbow, shoulder position does not directly alter its anatomical length.\r\n\r\nThis contrasts sharply with biceps brachii.\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n\r\n- incline curl;\r\n- standing curl;\r\n- preacher curl;\r\n\r\nplace biceps brachii at different overall lengths because the shoulder angle changes.\r\n\r\nFor brachialis, the primary determinant of musculotendinous length remains **elbow angle**.\r\n\r\nShoulder position can alter whole-exercise mechanics and neural force sharing, but it does not directly lengthen brachialis.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nFor brachialis, the relevant long-muscle-length condition occurs primarily as the **elbow approaches extension**.\r\n\r\nThere is no shoulder-angle component analogous to biceps brachii.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n> a brachialis-specific length model should be based mainly on elbow angle, not shoulder position.\r\n\r\n## Long-Length Loading\r\n\r\nAs the elbow extends, brachialis is lengthened.\r\n\r\nExercises that maintain substantial elbow-flexion torque near extended elbow positions are therefore theoretically favorable for exposing brachialis to high force at long lengths.\r\n\r\nExamples can include:\r\n\r\n- preacher curl variants with favorable resistance geometry;\r\n- cable curls that remain loaded near elbow extension;\r\n- machine curls with meaningful initial-range torque.\r\n\r\nHowever, one must distinguish between:\r\n\r\n- anatomical muscle length;\r\n- external resistance magnitude.\r\n\r\nAn exercise can place brachialis at a long length while simultaneously providing very little torque.\r\n\r\nTherefore, "full elbow extension" alone does not guarantee a strong long-length hypertrophic stimulus.\r\n\r\n## Shoulder Position\r\n\r\nShoulder extension does not specifically lengthen brachialis.\r\n\r\nTherefore, an incline curl or Bayesian curl should not receive a brachialis `E_LT` bonus simply because the humerus is behind the torso.\r\n\r\nAny advantage for brachialis must instead arise from other factors such as:\r\n\r\n- resistance profile;\r\n- elbow ROM;\r\n- stabilization;\r\n- achievable force;\r\n- proximity to failure.\r\n\r\nThis is an important difference from biceps brachii.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Interpretation\r\n\r\nDirect longitudinal hypertrophy research isolating brachialis is relatively limited.\r\n\r\nResistance-training studies have demonstrated that brachialis itself hypertrophies in response to elbow-flexor training, including MRI-based measurements, but exercise-specific long-length data are much less extensive than for more frequently studied muscles. :contentReference[oaicite:3]{index=3}\r\n\r\nRecent elbow-flexor studies examining different joint configurations and ROM provide useful context, but their conclusions should not automatically be attributed specifically to brachialis unless the muscle was measured independently. :contentReference[oaicite:4]{index=4}\r\n\r\nThe defensible position is therefore:\r\n\r\n- elbow extension lengthens brachialis;\r\n- meaningful resistance at extended elbow positions is mechanically attractive;\r\n- shoulder angle does not directly alter its length;\r\n- exact brachialis-specific stretch-mediated hypertrophy magnitude remains insufficiently quantified.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Grip Does Not Isolate Brachialis\r\n\r\nA common bodybuilding claim is that hammer curls or reverse curls "isolate the brachialis."\r\n\r\nThat is too strong.\r\n\r\nBrachialis contributes to elbow flexion in:\r\n\r\n- supinated curls;\r\n- neutral-grip curls;\r\n- pronated curls.\r\n\r\nChanging grip primarily changes the relative mechanical contribution of the other elbow flexors.\r\n\r\nThe brachialis does not suddenly activate only when the hand becomes neutral or pronated.\r\n\r\nA more accurate interpretation is:\r\n\r\n> neutral and pronated grips reduce some of the mechanical advantage or functional contribution of biceps brachii, allowing brachialis and brachioradialis to represent a larger proportion of total elbow-flexion demand.\r\n\r\n## Supinated Curl\r\n\r\nBrachialis remains highly involved.\r\n\r\nA conventional barbell or dumbbell curl is not merely a biceps exercise.\r\n\r\nThe elbow-flexion torque must still be shared among:\r\n\r\n- brachialis;\r\n- biceps brachii;\r\n- brachioradialis.\r\n\r\nSupination simply gives biceps an additional favorable functional context.\r\n\r\n## Hammer Curl\r\n\r\nA hammer curl uses a neutral forearm position.\r\n\r\nThis reduces the special supination-related advantage of biceps and strongly involves brachioradialis.\r\n\r\nBrachialis also remains a major flexor.\r\n\r\nThus hammer curls are useful for developing the **entire elbow-flexor complex**, not for selectively training brachialis alone.\r\n\r\n## Reverse Curl\r\n\r\nA pronated grip places biceps brachii in a less favorable functional configuration for elbow flexion.\r\n\r\nBrachialis remains capable of producing flexion torque.\r\n\r\nBrachioradialis also contributes substantially.\r\n\r\nTherefore reverse curls can increase the **relative** brachialis contribution compared with conventional supinated curls.\r\n\r\nHowever, they may also be limited by:\r\n\r\n- wrist extensor strength;\r\n- grip;\r\n- lower external load;\r\n- forearm discomfort.\r\n\r\nThis means greater relative contribution does not necessarily imply greater absolute brachialis stimulus.\r\n\r\n## Preacher Curl\r\n\r\nPreacher curls provide excellent stabilization of the upper arm.\r\n\r\nFor brachialis, shoulder flexion itself is not the relevant feature.\r\n\r\nMore important are:\r\n\r\n- high elbow-flexion torque;\r\n- controlled extended-elbow loading;\r\n- low opportunity for trunk compensation.\r\n\r\nDepending on machine or free-weight geometry, a preacher curl can create substantial demand near relatively long brachialis lengths.\r\n\r\nThis makes it potentially valuable for brachialis hypertrophy.\r\n\r\n## Bayesian and Incline Curls\r\n\r\nThese exercises are often selected because they place biceps brachii at greater length through shoulder extension.\r\n\r\nThat specific advantage does **not** apply to brachialis.\r\n\r\nBrachialis may still be strongly trained because elbow flexion occurs under load.\r\n\r\nIts stimulus should be estimated from:\r\n\r\n- elbow torque;\r\n- elbow angle;\r\n- resistance profile;\r\n\r\nrather than from the shoulder-extension component.\r\n\r\n## Compound Pulling\r\n\r\nBrachialis contributes meaningfully to elbow flexion during:\r\n\r\n- pull-ups;\r\n- chin-ups;\r\n- pulldowns;\r\n- rows.\r\n\r\nIts contribution is relatively robust across grip orientations because it does not depend on forearm supination.\r\n\r\nThis makes brachialis one of the reasons why even pronated-grip pulling produces substantial elbow-flexor fatigue.\r\n\r\n## Range of Motion\r\n\r\nA broad elbow ROM is generally useful.\r\n\r\nFor hypertrophy-oriented isolation work:\r\n\r\n- approach substantial elbow extension;\r\n- maintain control near the bottom;\r\n- avoid bouncing through the transition;\r\n- flex through the mechanically productive range.\r\n\r\nComplete hard lockout is unnecessary if uncomfortable.\r\n\r\nThe objective is meaningful muscular length under force, not maximal passive joint excursion.\r\n\r\n## Stabilization\r\n\r\nExercises that strongly stabilize the humerus can improve the interpretability of brachialis loading.\r\n\r\nGood examples include:\r\n\r\n- preacher curl;\r\n- selectorized curl machine;\r\n- supported cable curl.\r\n\r\nStanding curls permit higher total movement freedom but may introduce:\r\n\r\n- shoulder flexion;\r\n- trunk extension;\r\n- hip drive.\r\n\r\nThose compensations can increase external load without proportionally increasing brachialis tension.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Volume\r\n\r\nBrachialis receives substantial indirect work whenever significant elbow flexion occurs.\r\n\r\nDirect volume should therefore account for:\r\n\r\n- curls;\r\n- pull-ups;\r\n- pulldowns;\r\n- rows.\r\n\r\nA lifter performing considerable back and biceps work may not need any dedicated "brachialis exercise" at all.\r\n\r\nIf additional elbow-flexor development is desired, approximately:\r\n\r\n- **2-6 additional direct sets per week**\r\n\r\nof neutral- or pronated-grip curling can be a practical starting addition.\r\n\r\nThis is a heuristic, not a muscle-specific physiological threshold.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nTwo weekly exposures are generally sufficient for practical programming.\r\n\r\nThe muscle can tolerate higher frequencies because elbow-flexion isolation work has relatively low systemic cost.\r\n\r\nHowever, local fatigue can accumulate in:\r\n\r\n- elbow flexor tendons;\r\n- forearm musculature;\r\n- wrist extensors;\r\n- distal biceps region.\r\n\r\nThus recovery should be evaluated locally rather than only through whole-body fatigue.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\nBrachialis can be trained effectively across a broad range.\r\n\r\nPractical ranges include:\r\n\r\n- 6-10 repetitions for heavier curls;\r\n- 8-15 repetitions as a general default;\r\n- 15-30 repetitions for stable isolation work.\r\n\r\nVery heavy reverse curls often become limited by grip and wrist mechanics before the brachialis receives an ideal stimulus.\r\n\r\nModerate repetitions are therefore often preferable for pronated-grip variants.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nStable curl variations can usually be trained close to failure.\r\n\r\nApproximately:\r\n\r\n- 0-3 RIR\r\n\r\nis practical for hypertrophy work.\r\n\r\nHowever, failure should be defined by loss of elbow-flexion execution rather than the ability to continue moving the weight through progressively larger amounts of:\r\n\r\n- shoulder flexion;\r\n- trunk extension;\r\n- hip movement.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Selection\r\n\r\nThere is little reason to build an entire exercise library solely around "brachialis isolation."\r\n\r\nA useful elbow-flexor program may instead include mechanically complementary tasks:\r\n\r\n1. a supinated curl;\r\n2. a neutral or pronated curl;\r\n3. compound pulling.\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n\r\n- Bayesian curl;\r\n- hammer curl;\r\n- neutral-grip pull-up.\r\n\r\nThis distributes demand across:\r\n\r\n- biceps brachii;\r\n- brachialis;\r\n- brachioradialis;\r\n\r\nwithout relying on dubious head-isolation claims.\r\n\r\n## Priority\r\n\r\nIf brachialis development specifically matters, prioritize an exercise where:\r\n\r\n- elbow flexion is clearly the limiting action;\r\n- grip does not fail early;\r\n- resistance remains meaningful through a large ROM;\r\n- cheating is limited.\r\n\r\nA stable hammer-style machine or cable curl can sometimes be better than a reverse barbell curl even if the latter theoretically reduces biceps contribution more.\r\n\r\nAbsolute target-muscle tension matters more than maximizing the target's percentage share of a small total load.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## It Is Hidden Beneath the Biceps\r\n\r\nMuch of brachialis lies deep to biceps brachii.\r\n\r\nAs it hypertrophies, it can nevertheless contribute substantially to overall upper-arm thickness.\r\n\r\nLaterally, portions of the muscle become visible between:\r\n\r\n- biceps;\r\n- triceps;\r\n\r\nespecially near the distal upper arm.\r\n\r\nThis is why brachialis development can visually increase the separation and apparent thickness of the upper arm without being a superficial "show muscle" like biceps.\r\n\r\n## It Is a More Pure Elbow Flexor Than the Biceps\r\n\r\nBiceps brachii has several functions:\r\n\r\n- elbow flexion;\r\n- forearm supination;\r\n- shoulder-related functions.\r\n\r\nBrachialis has a much narrower task.\r\n\r\nIts insertion on the ulna makes it essentially a dedicated elbow flexor.\r\n\r\nThis makes the anatomical naming somewhat ironic: popular culture strongly associates elbow flexion with the biceps, while brachialis performs the same core joint action under virtually every forearm orientation.\r\n\r\n## It Can Have Dual Innervation\r\n\r\nMost of the muscle is supplied by the musculocutaneous nerve, but a lateral portion may receive radial-nerve innervation.\r\n\r\nThis anatomical variation is well documented and can have relevance during surgical approaches to the distal humerus and elbow. :contentReference[oaicite:5]{index=5}\r\n\r\n## It Is Not Mechanically Identical Across Its Depth\r\n\r\nCadaveric studies describe deep and superficial portions with different orientations and attachment patterns.\r\n\r\nThus even a muscle commonly treated as a simple "pure flexor" contains meaningful internal architectural specialization. :contentReference[oaicite:6]{index=6}\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\n`brachialis` should be modeled primarily as a **monoarticular elbow-flexion actuator**.\r\n\r\nThe core determinants of demand should be:\r\n\r\n- required elbow-flexion torque;\r\n- elbow angle;\r\n- internal moment arm;\r\n- muscle length;\r\n- external resistance profile.\r\n\r\nForearm orientation should influence brachialis demand mainly **relatively**, through changes in synergist contribution, rather than because pronation or supination directly changes brachialis function.\r\n\r\n## Grip Modifier\r\n\r\nA useful conceptual distinction is:\r\n\r\n### Supinated Grip\r\n\r\nHigh total elbow-flexion demand with relatively high contribution from `biceps_brachii`.\r\n\r\n`brachialis` remains strongly active.\r\n\r\n### Neutral Grip\r\n\r\nHigh `brachialis` contribution with substantial `brachioradialis` contribution.\r\n\r\n`biceps_brachii` remains active but loses the additional supination-related context.\r\n\r\n### Pronated Grip\r\n\r\n`biceps_brachii` becomes relatively disadvantaged.\r\n\r\nA greater proportion of flexion demand can therefore shift toward:\r\n\r\n- `brachialis`;\r\n- `brachioradialis`.\r\n\r\nThis should be implemented as **force-sharing redistribution**, not as a binary muscle activation switch.\r\n\r\n## Length-Tension Model\r\n\r\nFor brachialis:\r\n\r\n`E_LT ≈ f(elbow_angle)`\r\n\r\nShoulder angle should not directly enter the muscle-length calculation.\r\n\r\nThis contrasts with:\r\n\r\n`biceps_brachii: E_LT = f(elbow_angle, shoulder_angle)`\r\n\r\nThus an incline curl and preacher curl can produce different biceps length states while exposing brachialis to similar anatomical lengths at equivalent elbow angles.\r\n\r\n## Supination Demand\r\n\r\nBrachialis should receive approximately zero direct demand from a pure forearm-supination torque component.\r\n\r\nIf an exercise includes both elbow flexion and active supination:\r\n\r\n- brachialis handles part of the flexion component;\r\n- biceps brachii can handle both flexion and supination.\r\n\r\nThis provides a clean mechanistic basis for differentiating the two muscles in exercise vectors.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Allocation\r\n\r\n### Standing Barbell Curl\r\n\r\nHigh brachialis demand through elbow flexion.\r\n\r\nBiceps receives additional relative advantage from the supinated grip.\r\n\r\n### Bayesian Cable Curl\r\n\r\nHigh brachialis demand if elbow-flexion torque is substantial.\r\n\r\nNo special brachialis length bonus from shoulder extension.\r\n\r\n### Incline Dumbbell Curl\r\n\r\nSame principle: shoulder extension lengthens biceps, not brachialis.\r\n\r\n### Preacher Curl\r\n\r\nPotentially very high brachialis demand if resistance is substantial near elbow extension.\r\n\r\nShoulder flexion itself should not penalize brachialis length.\r\n\r\n### Preacher Hammer Curl\r\n\r\nPotentially excellent combined stimulus for:\r\n\r\n- brachialis;\r\n- brachioradialis;\r\n\r\nwhile still involving biceps brachii.\r\n\r\n### Neutral-Grip Pull-Up\r\n\r\nSubstantial brachialis demand due to high elbow-flexion torque.\r\n\r\n### Wide Pronated Pull-Up\r\n\r\nStill meaningful brachialis demand despite reduced biceps-specific mechanical advantage.\r\n\r\n## Important Modelling Principle\r\n\r\nThe common heuristic:\r\n\r\n> "supinated = biceps, neutral = brachialis, pronated = brachioradialis"\r\n\r\nis biomechanically too crude.\r\n\r\nAll three major elbow flexors can contribute across multiple grip orientations.\r\n\r\nThe correct model is continuous force sharing based on:\r\n\r\n- joint torque;\r\n- moment arms;\r\n- forearm orientation;\r\n- muscle length;\r\n- contraction mechanics.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **humeral origin and ulnar insertion:** high;\r\n- **primary elbow-flexion function:** high;\r\n- **absence of direct pronation/supination function:** high;\r\n- **monoarticular behavior:** high;\r\n- **musculocutaneous innervation with possible radial contribution:** high;\r\n- **internal superficial/deep organization:** supported anatomically;\r\n- **hypertrophy from resistance training:** directly demonstrated;\r\n- **specific advantage of neutral/pronated grip for absolute brachialis hypertrophy:** much less certain;\r\n- **brachialis-specific stretch-mediated hypertrophy magnitude:** limited direct evidence.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **brachialis = a large monoarticular elbow flexor attached to the ulna, mechanically active across all forearm orientations, whose muscle length depends primarily on elbow angle and whose relative contribution increases when biceps-specific supination mechanics are removed or disadvantaged.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/Brachialis}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	8.60	0.00	1.000	44.05	44.05
22	triceps_long_head	Musculus triceps brachii caput longum	Upper	Triceps	630.00	596.59	26.00	Pennate	0.350	0.650	extreme_high	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\nThe **long head of triceps brachii** is the only biarticular head of the triceps complex.\r\n\r\nIts principal functions are:\r\n\r\n- **elbow extension**;\r\n- contribution to **shoulder extension**;\r\n- contribution to **shoulder adduction**;\r\n- secondary glenohumeral stabilization.\r\n\r\nIt differs fundamentally from:\r\n\r\n- `triceps_lateral_head`;\r\n- `triceps_medial_head`;\r\n\r\nbecause its proximal attachment lies on the **scapula**, not the humerus.\r\n\r\nTherefore its length depends on both:\r\n\r\n- elbow angle;\r\n- shoulder angle.\r\n\r\nThis makes the long head one of the clearest examples in the upper limb of a muscle whose exercise behavior cannot be modelled correctly from elbow-extension torque alone.\r\n\r\nThe most important length relationship is:\r\n\r\n- elbow flexion lengthens it distally;\r\n- shoulder flexion/elevation lengthens it proximally.\r\n\r\nThus a configuration combining:\r\n\r\n> **shoulder flexion + elbow flexion**\r\n\r\nplaces the long head in a particularly long state.\r\n\r\nThis has direct training relevance. A 12-week within-subject trial comparing cable elbow extensions performed overhead versus with the arm beside the torso found substantially greater triceps hypertrophy in the overhead condition, with the long head showing the clearest benefit. :contentReference[oaicite:0]{index=0}\r\n\r\nFor Agonez, the most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **triceps long head = a biarticular elbow extensor and secondary shoulder extensor/adductor whose length and force sharing depend jointly on elbow and shoulder position, with unusually strong direct evidence supporting long-length hypertrophy during overhead elbow extension.**\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\n## Origin\r\n\r\nThe long head originates from the **infraglenoid tubercle of the scapula**, just inferior to the glenoid.\r\n\r\nBecause its origin is proximal to the glenohumeral joint, the muscle crosses both:\r\n\r\n- shoulder;\r\n- elbow.\r\n\r\nThis is the defining anatomical distinction from the lateral and medial heads. :contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}\r\n\r\n## Insertion\r\n\r\nDistally, the long head joins the triceps extensor mechanism and inserts through the triceps tendon onto the:\r\n\r\n- **olecranon of the ulna**.\r\n\r\nThe three triceps heads therefore share a common distal functional purpose:\r\n\r\n- elbow extension.\r\n\r\nHowever, they differ substantially in proximal geometry and recruitment.\r\n\r\n## Relationship With the Other Triceps Heads\r\n\r\n### Long Head\r\n\r\nBiarticular:\r\n- shoulder;\r\n- elbow.\r\n\r\nFunctions:\r\n- elbow extension;\r\n- shoulder extension;\r\n- shoulder adduction;\r\n- secondary glenohumeral stabilization.\r\n\r\n### Lateral Head\r\n\r\nMonoarticular:\r\n- elbow only.\r\n\r\nFunction:\r\n- elbow extension.\r\n\r\n### Medial Head\r\n\r\nMonoarticular:\r\n- elbow only.\r\n\r\nFunction:\r\n- elbow extension.\r\n\r\nTherefore shoulder position can directly change muscle length only for the long head.\r\n\r\nThis is one of the strongest arguments for modelling the three heads independently.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nThe long head is innervated by the **radial nerve**, with contributions primarily from approximately:\r\n\r\n- C6;\r\n- C7;\r\n- C8.\r\n\r\nAll three heads receive radial-nerve innervation, but they possess distinct motor branches and can demonstrate different recruitment patterns depending on joint position and task.\r\n\r\nExperimental measurements show that the relative force and activation of the three triceps heads change considerably as shoulder elevation changes. :contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Elbow Extension\r\n\r\nThe long head contributes directly to **elbow extension**.\r\n\r\nIt works with:\r\n\r\n- lateral head;\r\n- medial head;\r\n- anconeus.\r\n\r\nIts contribution is not a fixed percentage of total triceps force.\r\n\r\nInstead, force sharing changes with:\r\n\r\n- elbow angle;\r\n- shoulder angle;\r\n- force requirement;\r\n- exercise configuration.\r\n\r\n## Shoulder Extension\r\n\r\nBecause the long head originates on the scapula and passes posterior to the shoulder joint, it can assist **shoulder extension**.\r\n\r\nThis function has been demonstrated experimentally by selective stimulation across different combinations of shoulder and elbow angles. :contentReference[oaicite:3]{index=3}\r\n\r\nIts shoulder-extension contribution is much smaller than that of major shoulder extensors such as:\r\n\r\n- latissimus dorsi;\r\n- posterior deltoid;\r\n- teres major.\r\n\r\nNevertheless, it is mechanically real.\r\n\r\n## Shoulder Adduction\r\n\r\nThe long head can also contribute modestly to **shoulder adduction**.\r\n\r\nThis role is secondary to muscles such as:\r\n\r\n- latissimus dorsi;\r\n- teres major;\r\n- pectoralis major depending on humeral position.\r\n\r\nFor exercise modelling, shoulder adduction should therefore give the long head:\r\n\r\n- a small or secondary vector,\r\n\r\nnot zero.\r\n\r\n## Glenohumeral Stabilization\r\n\r\nBecause the long head spans from scapula to ulna and passes near the shoulder joint, it can contribute modestly to stabilization of the humeral head under load.\r\n\r\nAnatomical descriptions recognize a role in assisting humeral-head stability in addition to its extension/adduction actions. :contentReference[oaicite:4]{index=4}\r\n\r\nThis should remain secondary in Agonez compared with the dedicated stabilizing role of:\r\n\r\n- rotator cuff.\r\n\r\n## Biarticular Mechanics\r\n\r\nThe long head is affected by movement at both ends.\r\n\r\n### Shoulder Flexion / Elevation\r\n\r\nLengthens it proximally.\r\n\r\n### Shoulder Extension\r\n\r\nShortens it proximally.\r\n\r\n### Elbow Flexion\r\n\r\nLengthens it distally.\r\n\r\n### Elbow Extension\r\n\r\nShortens it distally.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`L_triceps_long =\r\n    f(shoulder_flexion_extension,\r\n      elbow_flexion_extension)`\r\n\r\nThis is the central mechanical rule.\r\n\r\n## Most Lengthened Configuration\r\n\r\nA particularly long configuration combines:\r\n\r\n- shoulder flexion/elevation;\r\n- elbow flexion.\r\n\r\nThis occurs in:\r\n\r\n- overhead cable extension;\r\n- overhead rope extension;\r\n- overhead dumbbell extension.\r\n\r\n## Most Shortened Configuration\r\n\r\nA short configuration combines:\r\n\r\n- shoulder extension;\r\n- elbow extension.\r\n\r\nThis can occur in movements such as:\r\n\r\n- terminal kickback-style elbow extension with the upper arm behind the torso.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nThe long head has **direct longitudinal human evidence** supporting greater hypertrophy when trained at longer muscle lengths.\r\n\r\nThis is one of the strongest SMH cases in the upper body.\r\n\r\n## Overhead vs Neutral-Arm Elbow Extension\r\n\r\nMaeo et al. trained one arm with cable elbow extensions performed overhead and the contralateral arm with the shoulder in a neutral position.\r\n\r\nTraining was performed for:\r\n\r\n- 12 weeks;\r\n- 2 sessions per week;\r\n- 5 sets per session;\r\n- 10 repetitions at approximately 70% 1RM.\r\n\r\nThe overhead condition produced substantially greater triceps hypertrophy despite using lower absolute loads, and the effect was particularly evident in the biarticular long head. :contentReference[oaicite:5]{index=5}\r\n\r\nThis is direct evidence that shoulder-induced long muscle length matters.\r\n\r\n## Why Overhead Position Matters\r\n\r\nAt identical elbow angles:\r\n\r\n`L_long_head_overhead > L_long_head_arm_at_side`\r\n\r\nbecause shoulder elevation moves the scapular origin farther from the distal triceps attachment.\r\n\r\nThe lateral and medial heads do not receive this proximal lengthening because they originate on the humerus.\r\n\r\nTherefore the overhead advantage has a genuine head-specific anatomical basis.\r\n\r\n## Deep Elbow Flexion\r\n\r\nElbow flexion also lengthens the long head.\r\n\r\nThus the greatest long-length exposure occurs when both joints contribute:\r\n\r\n`shoulder_flexion ↑`\r\n+\r\n`elbow_flexion ↑`\r\n\r\nThis makes the bottom region of an overhead extension particularly important.\r\n\r\n## Long-Length Evidence Strength\r\n\r\nFor the long head, the evidence is much stronger than for muscles where SMH is inferred mainly from:\r\n\r\n- anatomy;\r\n- stretch sensation;\r\n- EMG.\r\n\r\nHere there is a direct intervention demonstrating greater hypertrophy from the longer shoulder configuration. :contentReference[oaicite:6]{index=6}\r\n\r\n## Overhead Does Not Mean Infinite Stretch\r\n\r\nMore shoulder flexion is not necessarily always better.\r\n\r\nUseful ROM is limited by:\r\n\r\n- shoulder mobility;\r\n- scapular position;\r\n- thoracic compensation;\r\n- ability to maintain elbow-extension torque;\r\n- comfort.\r\n\r\nThe goal is a reproducible long-muscle configuration under load, not maximal joint contortion.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nFor Agonez, `triceps_long_head` justifies:\r\n\r\n- high-confidence long-length sensitivity;\r\n- high `E_LT` in overhead elbow extension;\r\n- a strong practical SMH modifier when meaningful force is produced in the lengthened state.\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`SMH_effective_long =\r\n    base_smh_factor\r\n    × E_LT(shoulder_angle, elbow_angle)\r\n    × active_force`\r\n\r\nThis is more defensible than giving every triceps exercise the same hypertrophy coefficient.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Overhead Rope Triceps Extension\r\n\r\nThis is one of the best direct long-head exercises.\r\n\r\nIt combines:\r\n\r\n- high elbow-extension torque;\r\n- shoulder flexion;\r\n- deep elbow flexion at the stretched position;\r\n- stable cable resistance.\r\n\r\nFor the long head, it should receive:\r\n\r\n- very high direct demand;\r\n- very high long-length exposure.\r\n\r\n## Overhead Cable Extension\r\n\r\nCable resistance is especially useful because external elbow-extension torque can remain substantial near deep elbow flexion.\r\n\r\nThis helps preserve active force in the region where the long head is longest.\r\n\r\n## Dumbbell Overhead Extension\r\n\r\nDumbbell overhead extensions also place the long head at large length.\r\n\r\nHowever, the resistance profile differs because gravity creates a large elbow moment only at certain forearm orientations.\r\n\r\nThey remain mechanically valid but may be less uniform across ROM than cable variants.\r\n\r\n## Skull Crusher\r\n\r\nSkull crushers strongly train all triceps heads.\r\n\r\nThe long-head stimulus depends substantially on upper-arm position.\r\n\r\nIf the humerus is:\r\n\r\n- approximately vertical;\r\n\r\nthe long head is moderately lengthened.\r\n\r\nIf the upper arm is allowed to angle farther behind the head:\r\n\r\n- shoulder flexion increases;\r\n- long-head length increases.\r\n\r\nThus exercise naming alone does not fully determine its long-head vector.\r\n\r\n## Lying Triceps Extension Behind the Head\r\n\r\nAllowing the implement to travel behind the head rather than toward the forehead generally:\r\n\r\n- increases shoulder flexion;\r\n- increases long-head length;\r\n- can maintain substantial elbow-extension torque.\r\n\r\nThis can turn a conventional skull crusher into a more lengthened long-head pattern.\r\n\r\n## Cable Pushdown\r\n\r\nPushdowns train the long head effectively through elbow extension.\r\n\r\nHowever, with the upper arm beside the torso the shoulder contribution places it at a shorter proximal length than in overhead extensions.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_long_pushdown < E_LT_long_overhead`\r\n\r\nat equivalent elbow angles.\r\n\r\nPushdowns are still effective, but they do not exploit the long head's biarticular length state to the same degree.\r\n\r\n## Straight-Bar Pushdown\r\n\r\nAll three heads contribute.\r\n\r\nGrip orientation does not isolate the long head because triceps does not cross the radioulnar joints.\r\n\r\nThe relevant variables are more strongly:\r\n\r\n- elbow torque;\r\n- shoulder angle;\r\n- elbow angle;\r\n- stability.\r\n\r\n## Rope Pushdown\r\n\r\nRope attachment changes:\r\n\r\n- wrist freedom;\r\n- comfort;\r\n- terminal hand position.\r\n\r\nIt does not inherently isolate the long head.\r\n\r\nIf shoulder geometry is unchanged, long-head muscle length remains similar.\r\n\r\n## California Press\r\n\r\nThe California press imposes substantial elbow-extension torque and therefore strongly recruits the triceps.\r\n\r\nThe long head contributes heavily.\r\n\r\nHowever, because the humerus is not overhead, the muscle does not experience the same proximal lengthening as in an overhead extension.\r\n\r\nIt should therefore receive:\r\n\r\n- high force;\r\n- moderate `E_LT`;\r\n\r\nrather than the very-high `E_LT` of an overhead extension.\r\n\r\n## Bench Press\r\n\r\nBench pressing produces substantial elbow-extension demand.\r\n\r\nThe long head contributes alongside:\r\n\r\n- lateral head;\r\n- medial head.\r\n\r\nBut the shoulder position and pressing geometry mean its contribution differs from isolated extensions.\r\n\r\nThe main shoulder torque is produced by:\r\n\r\n- pectoralis major;\r\n- anterior deltoid.\r\n\r\nThe long head's shoulder-extension tendency may even oppose some of the required shoulder-flexion/horizontal-adduction mechanics, affecting net recruitment.\r\n\r\nTherefore high elbow-extension demand does not automatically mean maximal long-head recruitment.\r\n\r\n## Incline Bench Press\r\n\r\nIncline pressing increases shoulder elevation relative to flat pressing.\r\n\r\nThis changes the operating state of the long head.\r\n\r\nHowever, the task still combines:\r\n\r\n- shoulder pressing;\r\n- elbow extension.\r\n\r\nThe long head should receive meaningful elbow-extension force but should not automatically receive an overhead-extension-level long-length stimulus.\r\n\r\n## Overhead Press\r\n\r\nThis case is particularly interesting.\r\n\r\nThe shoulder is highly elevated, so the long head is long proximally.\r\n\r\nHowever, experimental work shows that at high shoulder elevations the **medial head can take a larger share of elbow-extension force**, while the long head contributes relatively less than at the arm-at-side position. :contentReference[oaicite:7]{index=7}\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n> long muscle length does not automatically imply high recruitment.\r\n\r\nThis is a key Agonez modelling principle.\r\n\r\nIn an overhead press:\r\n\r\n- `E_LT_long` may be high;\r\n- `force_share_long` may be lower than expected.\r\n\r\nThese must remain separate variables.\r\n\r\n## Dips\r\n\r\nDips impose substantial elbow-extension torque, so the long head contributes.\r\n\r\nThe shoulder is often in extension relative to the torso, which shortens the long head proximally.\r\n\r\nTherefore dips can create:\r\n\r\n- high force;\r\n- relatively shorter long-head length.\r\n\r\nThis differs strongly from overhead extensions.\r\n\r\n## Forward-Lean Dip\r\n\r\nForward lean increases chest involvement and changes shoulder mechanics.\r\n\r\nThe long head remains an elbow extensor but shoulder extension/adduction geometry affects its operating state.\r\n\r\nIt should still receive significant force demand.\r\n\r\n## Straight-Arm Pulldown\r\n\r\nBecause the long head can assist shoulder extension, it can receive some demand even when elbow angle remains relatively fixed.\r\n\r\nHowever:\r\n\r\n- latissimus dorsi;\r\n- teres major\r\n\r\nshould dominate.\r\n\r\nLong-head contribution should be secondary.\r\n\r\nThis is one of the few exercises where long-head demand can arise meaningfully from the shoulder rather than the elbow.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Include at Least One Overhead Extension if Long-Head Hypertrophy Matters\r\n\r\nBecause direct evidence supports a benefit from the overhead position, a hypertrophy program prioritizing triceps size should strongly consider some form of:\r\n\r\n- overhead cable extension;\r\n- overhead rope extension;\r\n- overhead dumbbell extension.\r\n\r\nThis is more evidence-backed than attempting to manipulate grip to "hit the long head."\r\n\r\n## Combine Overhead and Non-Overhead Work\r\n\r\nA strong triceps setup can combine:\r\n\r\n1. overhead extension;\r\n2. pushdown or press;\r\n3. compound pressing if already present.\r\n\r\nThis exposes the triceps to different:\r\n\r\n- length states;\r\n- resistance profiles;\r\n- force-sharing conditions.\r\n\r\n## Weekly Volume\r\n\r\nA practical total triceps starting range is approximately:\r\n\r\n- **6-12 hard effective sets per week**,\r\n\r\nincluding:\r\n\r\n- direct triceps work;\r\n- meaningful compound-press contribution.\r\n\r\nIf long-head development is a priority, a meaningful fraction should come from overhead work.\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n\r\n- 3-6 overhead-extension sets;\r\n- additional pushdown/pressing volume.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nTwo to three weekly exposures are practical.\r\n\r\nLong-head recovery overlaps with:\r\n\r\n- elbow-extension work;\r\n- overhead pressing;\r\n- shoulder-extension pulling to a smaller degree.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\n### Overhead Cable Extensions\r\n\r\nUseful:\r\n- 8-15;\r\n- 10-20;\r\n- 12-25 reps.\r\n\r\n### Heavy Compound Extensions / Presses\r\n\r\nUseful:\r\n- 5-8;\r\n- 6-12 reps.\r\n\r\nThere is no unique physiological long-head repetition range.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nStable cable extensions can be trained close to failure:\r\n\r\n- approximately 0-2 RIR.\r\n\r\nThe main limiting factors should ideally be:\r\n\r\n- elbow-extension force;\r\n- local triceps fatigue.\r\n\r\nNot:\r\n- lumbar hyperextension;\r\n- shoulder discomfort;\r\n- excessive elbow movement.\r\n\r\n## Managing Elbow Irritation\r\n\r\nLong-head training often involves deep elbow flexion.\r\n\r\nProgression should therefore account for tolerance of:\r\n\r\n- triceps tendon;\r\n- elbow joint;\r\n- surrounding connective tissues.\r\n\r\nCable resistance and controlled loading can make volume progression easier than abrupt exposure to very heavy free-weight extensions.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## It Is the Only Triceps Head That Crosses the Shoulder\r\n\r\nThis single anatomical fact explains much of the difference between triceps exercises.\r\n\r\nIt means:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_long = f(elbow, shoulder)`\r\n\r\nwhile:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_lateral ≈ f(elbow)`\r\n\r\nand:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_medial ≈ f(elbow)`.\r\n\r\n## Overhead Extensions Have Direct Hypertrophy Evidence\r\n\r\nThe overhead advantage is not merely inferred from the feeling of stretch.\r\n\r\nA longitudinal within-subject study directly demonstrated greater triceps growth, particularly in the long head, after overhead versus neutral-arm cable extensions. :contentReference[oaicite:8]{index=8}\r\n\r\nThis makes the long head one of the stronger examples for applying a high-confidence SMH modifier.\r\n\r\n## Long Length Does Not Guarantee High Force Share\r\n\r\nAt elevated shoulder positions, the long head becomes longer.\r\n\r\nYet head-specific force measurements show that the medial head can dominate elbow-extension force at high shoulder elevations. :contentReference[oaicite:9]{index=9}\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`muscle_length`\r\nand\r\n`recruitment / force_share`\r\n\r\nmust remain independent model dimensions.\r\n\r\n## It Can Generate Shoulder Torque Without Elbow Motion\r\n\r\nBecause it crosses the shoulder, an isometric elbow position does not mean the long head is mechanically inactive.\r\n\r\nDuring shoulder-extension tasks it can contribute force even with minimal elbow movement.\r\n\r\n## Its Shoulder Function Is Small but Real\r\n\r\nThe long head is not a major lat substitute.\r\n\r\nHowever, controlled-stimulation research confirms that its shoulder-extension capacity changes with both shoulder and elbow position. :contentReference[oaicite:10]{index=10}\r\n\r\nThis is another direct consequence of being biarticular.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\n`triceps_long_head` should receive demand from two joint systems:\r\n\r\n1. **elbow-extension torque**;\r\n2. secondary **shoulder-extension/adduction torque**.\r\n\r\nUnlike the other triceps heads, shoulder geometry must also influence its muscle-length state.\r\n\r\n## Length-Tension Model\r\n\r\nA first-order formulation is:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_long =\r\n    f(shoulder_flexion,\r\n      elbow_flexion)`\r\n\r\nQualitatively:\r\n\r\n### Very Long\r\n\r\n- shoulder strongly flexed/elevated;\r\n- elbow deeply flexed.\r\n\r\n### Long\r\n\r\n- shoulder elevated with moderate elbow flexion;\r\nor\r\n- arm near torso with deep elbow flexion.\r\n\r\n### Intermediate\r\n\r\n- neutral shoulder;\r\n- moderate elbow angle.\r\n\r\n### Short\r\n\r\n- shoulder extended;\r\n- elbow extended.\r\n\r\nThe important point is the **interaction between both joints**.\r\n\r\n## Biarticular Compensation\r\n\r\nThe two joints can partially offset each other.\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n\r\n### Shoulder Flexion\r\n\r\nLengthens proximally.\r\n\r\n### Elbow Extension\r\n\r\nShortens distally.\r\n\r\nTherefore an overhead press can involve:\r\n\r\n- high shoulder-induced lengthening;\r\n- simultaneous elbow-extension shortening.\r\n\r\nNet fascicle length depends on the combination.\r\n\r\nThis is analogous in principle to:\r\n\r\n- rectus femoris;\r\n- semitendinosus;\r\n- semimembranosus;\r\n- biceps femoris long head.\r\n\r\n## Force-Sharing Model\r\n\r\nTotal elbow-extension torque can be represented conceptually as:\r\n\r\n`T_elbow_ext =\r\n    F_long × r_long\r\n  + F_lateral × r_lateral\r\n  + F_medial × r_medial\r\n  + F_anconeus × r_anconeus`\r\n\r\nBut:\r\n\r\n`F_long / F_total`\r\n\r\nshould not be constant.\r\n\r\nExperimental work shows strong dependence on shoulder elevation. At 0° shoulder elevation the long head produced greater estimated force and activation than the other triceps heads, whereas at 90° and above the medial head became dominant in the tested task. :contentReference[oaicite:11]{index=11}\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`force_share_long =\r\n    f(shoulder_angle,\r\n      elbow_angle,\r\n      force_level,\r\n      task)`.\r\n\r\n## Separation of E_LT and Force Share\r\n\r\nThis is crucial.\r\n\r\nAt high shoulder flexion:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_long ↑`\r\n\r\nbut this does not imply:\r\n\r\n`force_share_long ↑`.\r\n\r\nIndeed, the experimental evidence shows that high shoulder elevation can coincide with a larger medial-head share. :contentReference[oaicite:12]{index=12}\r\n\r\nSo Agonez should represent:\r\n\r\n`effective_force_long =\r\n    capacity\r\n    × E_LT\r\n    × E_MA\r\n    × E_ND\r\n    × E_LoA`\r\n\r\nrather than using muscle length as a direct proxy for recruitment.\r\n\r\n## Shoulder Torque\r\n\r\nBecause the long head also crosses the shoulder:\r\n\r\n`T_shoulder_extension_long =\r\n    F_long × r_shoulder_long`.\r\n\r\nExperimental selective-stimulation work confirms that shoulder and elbow angles influence its shoulder-extension capacity. :contentReference[oaicite:13]{index=13}\r\n\r\nThus the same long-head force can simultaneously produce:\r\n\r\n- elbow-extension torque;\r\n- shoulder-extension torque.\r\n\r\n## Important Multi-Joint Constraint\r\n\r\nDo not calculate:\r\n\r\n`F_required_elbow`\r\n+\r\n`F_required_shoulder`\r\n\r\nas independent long-head forces.\r\n\r\nThere is only one muscle-tendon force.\r\n\r\nThe same:\r\n\r\n`F_long`\r\n\r\nacts across both joints:\r\n\r\n`T_elbow = F_long × r_elbow`\r\n\r\nand:\r\n\r\n`T_shoulder = F_long × r_shoulder`.\r\n\r\nThis is the same biarticular modelling issue already encountered for the hamstrings.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Allocation\r\n\r\n### Standing Cable Overhead Rope Triceps Extension\r\n\r\nVery high:\r\n- `triceps_long_head`.\r\n\r\nHigh:\r\n- medial head;\r\n- lateral head.\r\n\r\nFor long head specifically:\r\n- very high `E_LT`;\r\n- very high local hypertrophy specificity.\r\n\r\nThis is probably the clearest long-head exercise in the current Agonez catalogue.\r\n\r\n### Flat Bench California Press\r\n\r\nVery high total triceps demand.\r\n\r\nLong head:\r\n- high force;\r\n- moderate length state.\r\n\r\nLateral/medial heads:\r\n- very high elbow-extension participation.\r\n\r\n### Weighted Forward-Lean Dip\r\n\r\nHigh long-head elbow-extension demand.\r\n\r\nBut shoulder extension places the long head relatively shorter proximally.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n- force high;\r\n- `E_LT` lower than overhead extension.\r\n\r\n### Seated Dumbbell Overhead Press\r\n\r\nMeaningful long-head demand.\r\n\r\nShoulder position:\r\n- long proximally.\r\n\r\nBut force sharing:\r\n- do not assume long-head dominance;\r\n- medial head may carry a large fraction of elbow-extension demand at high shoulder elevation. :contentReference[oaicite:14]{index=14}\r\n\r\n### Smith Incline Bench Press\r\n\r\nModerate-to-high long-head demand from elbow extension.\r\n\r\nLength state:\r\n- somewhat greater than flat pressing due increased shoulder elevation;\r\n- still far below a true overhead elbow-extension configuration.\r\n\r\n### Flat Bench Press\r\n\r\nModerate-to-high.\r\n\r\nHigh total triceps force but no special long-length advantage.\r\n\r\n### Straight-Arm Cable Pulldown\r\n\r\nLow-to-moderate long-head demand from shoulder extension.\r\n\r\nVery high:\r\n- latissimus;\r\n- teres major.\r\n\r\nLong head remains a secondary synergist.\r\n\r\n### Weighted Neutral-Grip Pull-Up\r\n\r\nLow long-head direct demand.\r\n\r\nIt can assist shoulder extension/adduction modestly, but:\r\n- lats;\r\n- teres major;\r\n- elbow flexors\r\n\r\ndominate.\r\n\r\n## Comparison With Lateral Head\r\n\r\n### Long Head\r\n\r\n`E_LT = f(elbow, shoulder)`\r\n\r\nShoulder:\r\n- extension/adduction contributor.\r\n\r\n### Lateral Head\r\n\r\n`E_LT ≈ f(elbow)`\r\n\r\nShoulder:\r\n- no direct torque.\r\n\r\nThus the overhead bonus must not be copied equally to lateral head.\r\n\r\n## Comparison With Medial Head\r\n\r\nBoth can generate large elbow-extension force.\r\n\r\nThe medial head does not cross the shoulder.\r\n\r\nExperimental data suggest it can become particularly important at high shoulder elevations. :contentReference[oaicite:15]{index=15}\r\n\r\nThus overhead positioning simultaneously:\r\n\r\n- lengthens the long head;\r\n- can change force distribution toward the medial head.\r\n\r\nThis is a useful reminder that hypertrophy outcomes depend on more than instantaneous EMG or simple force share.\r\n\r\n## Overhead Hypertrophy Paradox\r\n\r\nAt first glance, two observations appear contradictory:\r\n\r\n1. high shoulder elevation can reduce relative long-head elbow-extension force in some acute tasks;\r\n2. overhead extensions produce markedly greater long-head hypertrophy.\r\n\r\nThey are not contradictory.\r\n\r\nHypertrophy depends on the integrated training stimulus, including:\r\n\r\n- muscle length;\r\n- active tension;\r\n- volume;\r\n- regional strain;\r\n- resistance profile.\r\n\r\nThe overhead position can provide a superior hypertrophic environment even if the long head is not the dominant instantaneous torque producer at every joint angle. The longitudinal hypertrophy trial directly supports the practical benefit. :contentReference[oaicite:16]{index=16}\r\n\r\n## FCSA Interpretation\r\n\r\nLong-head FCSA represents available contractile force capacity.\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`F_max_long ≈ σ × FCSA_long`.\r\n\r\nBut because it crosses two joints, that force has two mechanical consequences:\r\n\r\n`T_elbow = F_long × r_elbow`\r\n\r\n`T_shoulder = F_long × r_shoulder`.\r\n\r\nTherefore its FCSA should not be independently "spent" once at the elbow and again at the shoulder.\r\n\r\nIt is one shared force source.\r\n\r\n## ETU Interpretation\r\n\r\nLong-head ETU can arise from:\r\n\r\n- elbow-extension force;\r\n- shoulder-extension/adduction force;\r\n- isometric stabilization.\r\n\r\nMost bodybuilding exercises will load it predominantly through elbow extension.\r\n\r\nStraight-arm shoulder-extension tasks can produce lower but non-zero ETU even with minimal elbow ROM.\r\n\r\n## Shoulder Position as a First-Order Variable\r\n\r\nFor the lateral and medial heads, shoulder angle is mostly a force-sharing modifier.\r\n\r\nFor the long head it is both:\r\n\r\n- a **direct length variable**;\r\n- a force-sharing variable.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n`shoulder_angle`\r\n\r\nshould be first-order input for this muscle.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nThe long head deserves one of the strongest SMH treatments in the Agonez upper-body model.\r\n\r\nThe direct evidence supports:\r\n\r\n`overhead + elbow flexion`\r\n→ longer long head\r\n→ substantially greater hypertrophy than neutral-arm extension under the tested conditions. :contentReference[oaicite:17]{index=17}\r\n\r\nTherefore it is reasonable to classify:\r\n\r\n- overhead cable extension: very high long-length exposure;\r\n- neutral pushdown: moderate;\r\n- dips/shoulder-extension configurations: lower.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **infraglenoid-tubercle origin:** high; :contentReference[oaicite:18]{index=18}\r\n- **olecranon insertion via triceps mechanism:** high;\r\n- **radial-nerve innervation:** high;\r\n- **elbow-extension function:** very high;\r\n- **shoulder-extension contribution:** directly demonstrated experimentally; :contentReference[oaicite:19]{index=19}\r\n- **shoulder-adduction contribution:** high;\r\n- **biarticular shoulder/elbow length dependence:** very high;\r\n- **head-specific force-sharing dependence on shoulder elevation:** directly supported experimentally; :contentReference[oaicite:20]{index=20}\r\n- **greater long-head hypertrophy from overhead versus neutral-arm elbow extension:** directly demonstrated longitudinally; :contentReference[oaicite:21]{index=21}\r\n- **precise head-specific force allocation in compound presses:** task dependent;\r\n- **exact optimal shoulder angle for hypertrophy:** not established;\r\n- **multi-joint FCSA allocation:** requires a simultaneous biarticular force solution.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **triceps long head = the biarticular head of triceps whose single muscle force simultaneously contributes to elbow extension and secondary shoulder extension/adduction, whose length is determined jointly by shoulder and elbow geometry, and for which overhead elbow extension provides unusually strong direct evidence of a long-muscle-length hypertrophy advantage.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/TricepsBrachii}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	13.40	12.00	0.978	44.52	43.55
35	vastus_medialis	Musculus vastus medialis	Lower	Quads	1380.00	1306.82	51.50	Pennate/Bi-directional	0.540	0.460	high	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\nThe **vastus medialis (VM)** is the medial portion of the quadriceps femoris and a monoarticular **knee extensor**.\r\n\r\nIts major functions are:\r\n\r\n- **knee extension**;\r\n- contribution to patellar stabilization and patellofemoral force balance;\r\n- eccentric control of knee flexion;\r\n- isometric stabilization under knee-extension load.\r\n\r\nLike:\r\n\r\n- `vastus_lateralis`;\r\n- `vastus_intermedius`;\r\n\r\nit crosses only the knee.\r\n\r\nUnlike:\r\n\r\n- `rectus_femoris`;\r\n\r\nit does **not** cross the hip.\r\n\r\nTherefore its first-order muscle-length model is relatively simple:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_VM ≈ f(knee_angle)`\r\n\r\nwith:\r\n\r\n- knee flexion → longer;\r\n- knee extension → shorter.\r\n\r\nVastus medialis is often discussed almost entirely through the concept of the **vastus medialis obliquus (VMO)** and patellar tracking. That framing is too narrow for an exercise model.\r\n\r\nVM is first and foremost a substantial knee extensor.\r\n\r\nIts distal oblique fibers are mechanically relevant to medial patellar restraint, but they are part of a continuous vastus-medialis muscle rather than a separately isolated prime mover.\r\n\r\nFor Agonez, the most useful abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **vastus medialis = a monoarticular knee extensor with meaningful force capacity across knee-extension ROM and a secondary medial patellar-stabilizing role; its hypertrophic demand should be driven primarily by knee-extension torque and knee angle, not by supposed VMO-isolation techniques.**\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\n## Origin\r\n\r\nVastus medialis originates broadly from the medial femur.\r\n\r\nAttachments include regions associated with:\r\n\r\n- intertrochanteric line;\r\n- medial lip of the linea aspera;\r\n- medial supracondylar line;\r\n- adjacent intermuscular septum.\r\n\r\nIts fibers descend along the medial/anterior thigh toward the quadriceps extensor mechanism.\r\n\r\n## Insertion\r\n\r\nVM contributes to the:\r\n\r\n- quadriceps tendon;\r\n- medial patellar border;\r\n- medial patellar retinacular system.\r\n\r\nForce ultimately transmits through:\r\n\r\n- patella;\r\n- patellar tendon;\r\n\r\nto the tibial tuberosity.\r\n\r\nIts distal medial attachment gives part of its force a medial component relative to the patella.\r\n\r\n## Distal Oblique Fibers / VMO\r\n\r\nThe distal fibers of vastus medialis become more oblique relative to the femoral shaft and are commonly referred to clinically as:\r\n\r\n- **vastus medialis obliquus (VMO)**.\r\n\r\nMore proximal fibers are sometimes called:\r\n\r\n- vastus medialis longus.\r\n\r\nThese labels describe regional fiber orientation rather than two unequivocally separate muscles.\r\n\r\nFor Agonez, retaining a single:\r\n\r\n`vastus_medialis`\r\n\r\nentity is preferable unless the model eventually requires detailed patellofemoral mechanics.\r\n\r\n## Relationship With Vastus Lateralis\r\n\r\nVL and VM both generate substantial knee-extension torque.\r\n\r\nTheir distal lines of action also contribute differently to resultant patellar forces.\r\n\r\nA simplified model might represent:\r\n\r\n### Vastus Lateralis\r\n\r\n- knee extension;\r\n- lateral patellar-force component.\r\n\r\n### Vastus Medialis\r\n\r\n- knee extension;\r\n- medial patellar-force component.\r\n\r\nHowever, physiological patellar tracking is not a simple two-muscle tug-of-war.\r\n\r\nIt also depends on:\r\n\r\n- trochlear geometry;\r\n- retinacula;\r\n- patellar tendon alignment;\r\n- limb geometry;\r\n- knee angle;\r\n- other quadriceps heads.\r\n\r\n## Relationship With Vastus Intermedius\r\n\r\nBoth are monoarticular knee extensors.\r\n\r\nVM differs by:\r\n\r\n- superficial/medial location;\r\n- distal oblique fibers;\r\n- more obvious patellar-stabilization role.\r\n\r\nVI is deeper and has a more centrally directed contribution to the extensor mechanism.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nVastus medialis is innervated by branches of the **femoral nerve**, primarily from approximately:\r\n\r\n- L2;\r\n- L3;\r\n- L4.\r\n\r\nMotor branches permit activation of VM independently enough for regional recruitment differences to occur across:\r\n\r\n- joint angles;\r\n- contraction types;\r\n- tasks.\r\n\r\nHowever, complete selective activation independent of the other quadriceps heads is unrealistic during ordinary loaded knee extension.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Knee Extension\r\n\r\nThe dominant function of VM is **knee extension**.\r\n\r\nIt works with:\r\n\r\n- vastus lateralis;\r\n- vastus intermedius;\r\n- rectus femoris.\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`T_knee_extension =\r\n    F_VM × r_VM\r\n  + F_VL × r_VL\r\n  + F_VI × r_VI\r\n  + F_RF × r_RF`\r\n\r\nVM should receive a substantial portion of quadriceps force demand in:\r\n\r\n- leg extension;\r\n- squat;\r\n- leg press;\r\n- split squat;\r\n- step-up;\r\n- other loaded knee-extension tasks.\r\n\r\n## Patellar Stabilization\r\n\r\nBecause distal VM fibers insert medially into the patellar/extensor apparatus, VM can contribute to resisting excessive lateral patellar displacement.\r\n\r\nThis role becomes especially relevant in the distal oblique fibers.\r\n\r\nHowever:\r\n\r\n> VM is not merely a patellar stabilizer.\r\n\r\nThe entire muscle contributes to knee-extension force.\r\n\r\n## Patellofemoral Force Balance\r\n\r\nDuring knee extension, quadriceps force creates large compressive and directional loads at the patellofemoral joint.\r\n\r\nVM contributes to the medial component of this resultant.\r\n\r\nThe actual patellar path reflects the vector sum of:\r\n\r\n- all quadriceps forces;\r\n- passive connective tissues;\r\n- bony geometry.\r\n\r\nThus exercise modelling should avoid interpreting VM activity as an isolated medial-patella correction mechanism.\r\n\r\n## Eccentric Knee-Flexion Control\r\n\r\nDuring:\r\n\r\n- squat descent;\r\n- landing;\r\n- leg-press eccentric;\r\n- stairs/downhill movement;\r\n\r\nVM can remain highly active while the knee flexes.\r\n\r\nThe muscle is then:\r\n\r\n- producing force;\r\n- lengthening.\r\n\r\nThis is genuine eccentric loading.\r\n\r\n## Isometric Knee Extension\r\n\r\nVM can also produce high force while knee angle remains fixed.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- wall-sit-like tasks;\r\n- isometric leg extension;\r\n- static squat positions.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`ROM = 0`\r\n\r\ndoes not imply:\r\n\r\n`VM_ETU = 0`.\r\n\r\n# Length-Tension Mechanics\r\n\r\n## Knee Flexion\r\n\r\nKnee flexion lengthens VM.\r\n\r\n## Knee Extension\r\n\r\nKnee extension shortens VM.\r\n\r\nBecause it is monoarticular:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_VM ≈ f(knee_flexion)`.\r\n\r\nHip angle should not directly modify this relationship.\r\n\r\n## Deep Knee Flexion\r\n\r\nAt large knee flexion:\r\n\r\n- VM is relatively long;\r\n- VL is relatively long;\r\n- VI is relatively long.\r\n\r\nRF is also lengthened distally, but simultaneous hip flexion may shorten it proximally.\r\n\r\nThis distinction means deep compound movements provide especially clean long-length exposure for the three vasti.\r\n\r\n## Near Full Extension\r\n\r\nNear lockout VM is relatively short.\r\n\r\nHowever, actual muscle force depends on:\r\n\r\n- external knee moment;\r\n- exercise resistance profile;\r\n- quadriceps leverage.\r\n\r\nTherefore the common idea that VMO is specifically a "terminal extension muscle" should not be interpreted as:\r\n\r\n> terminal knee extension is uniquely required for VM hypertrophy.\r\n\r\nThe muscle contributes across the ROM.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nDirect long-length hypertrophy evidence for VM is less extensive than for VL, largely because VL is measured more frequently in resistance-training studies.\r\n\r\nMechanically, however, the basic length relation is strong.\r\n\r\n## Knee Flexion as the Primary Length Variable\r\n\r\nBecause VM is a monoarticular knee extensor:\r\n\r\n`knee_flexion ↑ → VM_length ↑`.\r\n\r\nTherefore loaded knee-extension work at larger flexion angles provides greater long-length exposure.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- deep leg press;\r\n- deep squat;\r\n- leg extension beginning at large knee flexion.\r\n\r\n## Loaded Length vs Depth Alone\r\n\r\nThe relevant variable is not simply:\r\n\r\n`deep knee flexion`.\r\n\r\nIt is:\r\n\r\n`long muscle length + meaningful active force`.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n- sitting passively in deep knee flexion is not hypertrophy work;\r\n- a deep squat with low knee torque may not maximize VM stimulus;\r\n- a machine maintaining high torque in flexed-knee positions can be very effective.\r\n\r\n## Knee-Extension Training\r\n\r\nSingle-joint knee-extension studies consistently show substantial quadriceps hypertrophy and are useful for isolating the knee-extension function.\r\n\r\nROM-specific quadriceps research also supports the broader idea that the joint-angle region trained affects hypertrophic adaptation.\r\n\r\nHowever, because VM is not always measured independently, direct VM-specific effect sizes should not be inferred from VL data without qualification.\r\n\r\n## Deep Compound Training\r\n\r\nFull/deep squat training can produce strong vasti hypertrophy when sufficient knee-extension force is generated.\r\n\r\nFor VM, the rationale is straightforward:\r\n\r\n- knee flexion lengthens it;\r\n- the exercise requires active knee extension.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nFor Agonez:\r\n\r\n`SMH_effective_VM =\r\n    base_smh_factor\r\n    × E_LT(knee_angle)\r\n    × active_force`.\r\n\r\nThe model can therefore reward high-force work in deep knee flexion without assuming:\r\n\r\n`more depth = automatically more hypertrophy`.\r\n\r\nA moderate-to-high long-length sensitivity is biomechanically defensible, but evidence confidence is somewhat lower than for VL-specific longitudinal data.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Leg Extension\r\n\r\nLeg extension is one of the most direct VM exercises.\r\n\r\nThe external demand is almost entirely:\r\n\r\n- knee-extension torque.\r\n\r\nThe main force producers are:\r\n\r\n- VM;\r\n- VL;\r\n- VI;\r\n- RF.\r\n\r\nBecause the torso is supported, local quadriceps fatigue can be generated with little systemic cost.\r\n\r\n## Seated Hip Position\r\n\r\nHip flexion has essentially no direct effect on VM muscle length.\r\n\r\nThis differs from RF.\r\n\r\nThus during seated leg extensions:\r\n\r\n- VM remains mechanically well positioned as a knee extensor;\r\n- RF changes proximal length because of hip flexion.\r\n\r\nThis can influence relative quadriceps force sharing.\r\n\r\n## Terminal Knee Extension\r\n\r\nTerminal knee extensions are often prescribed with the idea of selectively training VMO.\r\n\r\nThey do recruit VM, but they do not uniquely isolate it from the rest of quadriceps.\r\n\r\nFurthermore, resistance often becomes small near terminal extension depending on setup.\r\n\r\nFor hypertrophy:\r\n\r\n> external knee-extension torque is more important than the exercise being labelled "VMO."\r\n\r\n## Squat\r\n\r\nVM should receive high demand during loaded squatting.\r\n\r\nIts force rises with required knee-extension torque.\r\n\r\nRelevant modifiers include:\r\n\r\n- squat depth;\r\n- stance;\r\n- bar position;\r\n- torso inclination;\r\n- forward knee travel.\r\n\r\nHowever, stance manipulations should not be expected to selectively isolate VM.\r\n\r\n## Deep Squat\r\n\r\nDeep knee flexion lengthens VM.\r\n\r\nIf external knee torque remains high, this provides substantial long-length active loading.\r\n\r\nBecause VM does not cross the hip, there is no proximal shortening effect from hip flexion.\r\n\r\n## 45° Leg Press\r\n\r\nThe 45° leg press should receive very high VM demand when:\r\n\r\n- knee flexion is substantial;\r\n- the load is high;\r\n- the feet remain positioned so that meaningful knee torque is required.\r\n\r\nThe movement also heavily loads:\r\n\r\n- VL;\r\n- VI;\r\n- RF;\r\n- gluteus maximus;\r\n- adductor magnus.\r\n\r\n## Foot Placement in Leg Press\r\n\r\nHigh versus low foot placement alters:\r\n\r\n- knee flexion;\r\n- hip flexion;\r\n- external moment distribution.\r\n\r\nLower foot placement commonly increases the relative knee-extensor demand.\r\n\r\nThis can raise VM demand.\r\n\r\nHowever, it is not a VM-specific manipulation.\r\n\r\nAll vasti should generally rise together.\r\n\r\n## Split Squat\r\n\r\nVM contributes strongly when the lead leg must generate substantial knee-extension torque.\r\n\r\nMore forward knee travel generally increases quadriceps demand.\r\n\r\nThe muscle is not uniquely selected by:\r\n\r\n- foot angle;\r\n- stance width.\r\n\r\n## Step-Up\r\n\r\nVM contributes to extending the knee during ascent.\r\n\r\nThe relative demand increases with:\r\n\r\n- greater knee flexion;\r\n- higher box;\r\n- lower use of momentum.\r\n\r\n## Sissy Squat\r\n\r\nSissy-squat-type mechanics can create substantial external knee moments at large knee flexion.\r\n\r\nThis provides strong long-length loading for all three vasti.\r\n\r\nVM should therefore receive high demand.\r\n\r\n## Reverse Nordic\r\n\r\nReverse-Nordic-type movements can similarly impose substantial knee-extension torque through large knee flexion.\r\n\r\nFor VM, VL and VI:\r\n\r\n- hip position has little direct length effect.\r\n\r\nRF behaves differently because it crosses the hip.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Do Not Program a Separate "VMO Day"\r\n\r\nFor healthy general hypertrophy, VM does not require a dedicated isolation strategy.\r\n\r\nSubstantial knee-extension training will expose it strongly.\r\n\r\n## Combine Compound and Isolation Work\r\n\r\nA useful quadriceps structure is:\r\n\r\n1. loaded compound knee extension;\r\n2. machine leg extension.\r\n\r\nThis provides:\r\n\r\n- high absolute force;\r\n- large-ROM exposure;\r\n- high local stimulus-to-fatigue ratio.\r\n\r\n## Weekly Volume\r\n\r\nA practical quadriceps-wide starting range is approximately:\r\n\r\n- **6-12 hard effective sets per week**.\r\n\r\nVM receives a substantial share of nearly all knee-extension sets.\r\n\r\nA separate VM volume quota is usually unnecessary.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nTwo to three weekly quadriceps exposures are practical.\r\n\r\nRecovery overlaps almost completely with:\r\n\r\n- VL;\r\n- VI;\r\n- RF.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\n### Squat / Leg Press\r\n\r\nUseful:\r\n- 5-8;\r\n- 6-10;\r\n- 8-15 reps.\r\n\r\n### Leg Extension\r\n\r\nUseful:\r\n- 8-15;\r\n- 10-20;\r\n- 15-25 reps.\r\n\r\nVM does not require high repetitions merely because it is associated with patellar control.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nStable machine leg extensions can be trained approximately:\r\n\r\n- 0-2 RIR.\r\n\r\nCompounds generally benefit from more technical reserve.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Selection for Patellofemoral Symptoms\r\n\r\nWhen symptoms exist, exercise selection and ROM may need modification based on:\r\n\r\n- pain;\r\n- patellofemoral loading tolerance;\r\n- clinical assessment.\r\n\r\nThis is separate from the general hypertrophy question.\r\n\r\nA healthy-athlete model should not assume that selectively increasing VM activation is necessary to maintain normal patellar tracking.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## VMO Is a Region, Not a Magical Separate Muscle\r\n\r\nThe oblique distal fibers of VM are anatomically meaningful, but the popular fitness concept of a fully isolatable "VMO muscle" is overstated.\r\n\r\nThe distal oblique fibers remain part of the broader vastus-medialis system.\r\n\r\n## VM Is Active Across Knee Extension\r\n\r\nVM is not only active in the final few degrees of extension.\r\n\r\nIt contributes throughout substantial parts of the knee-extension ROM.\r\n\r\nTherefore terminal-knee-extension drills are not uniquely required for its development.\r\n\r\n## Patellar Tracking Is Not Simply VL vs VMO\r\n\r\nThe patellofemoral joint is constrained by:\r\n\r\n- femoral trochlea;\r\n- quadriceps tendon;\r\n- patellar tendon;\r\n- retinacula;\r\n- multiple muscle vectors.\r\n\r\nReducing the system to:\r\n\r\n`VL pulls lateral`\r\nversus\r\n`VMO pulls medial`\r\n\r\nis mechanically incomplete.\r\n\r\n## Hip Position Does Not Directly Change VM Length\r\n\r\nThis is particularly useful for exercise modelling.\r\n\r\nIn seated knee extension:\r\n\r\n- hip flexion can shorten RF;\r\n- VM remains unaffected proximally.\r\n\r\nThus changing hip position can change quadriceps force sharing without directly changing VM geometry.\r\n\r\n## It Contributes to the Medial Teardrop Appearance\r\n\r\nThe distal VM region creates much of the visible medial "teardrop" contour above the knee.\r\n\r\nThis is an aesthetic consequence of anatomy rather than evidence of a unique terminal-extension function.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\n`vastus_medialis` should primarily receive demand from:\r\n\r\n1. **knee-extension torque**;\r\n2. eccentric control of knee flexion;\r\n3. patellar medial-stabilization force as a secondary component.\r\n\r\nIt should receive approximately zero direct:\r\n\r\n- hip-flexion torque;\r\n- hip-extension torque.\r\n\r\n## Length-Tension Model\r\n\r\nA first-order representation is:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_VM = f(knee_angle)`.\r\n\r\n### Long\r\n\r\n- deep knee flexion.\r\n\r\n### Mid\r\n\r\n- moderate flexion.\r\n\r\n### Short\r\n\r\n- near full extension.\r\n\r\nHip angle should not enter directly.\r\n\r\n## Comparison With Vastus Lateralis\r\n\r\nBoth:\r\n\r\n- are monoarticular;\r\n- extend the knee;\r\n- lengthen with knee flexion.\r\n\r\nThus their first-order `E_LT` functions can be similar.\r\n\r\nDifferences arise from:\r\n\r\n- FCSA;\r\n- architecture;\r\n- distal line of pull;\r\n- neural recruitment;\r\n- patellar geometry.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_VM ≈ E_LT_VL`\r\n\r\ndoes not imply:\r\n\r\n`F_VM = F_VL`.\r\n\r\nVL generally has greater absolute force capacity, while VM has a more medially directed distal vector.\r\n\r\n## Comparison With Vastus Intermedius\r\n\r\nVM and VI share simple monoarticular mechanics.\r\n\r\nVI is deeper and more centrally oriented.\r\n\r\nVM has the stronger medial patellar-force component.\r\n\r\nFor ordinary hypertrophy exercises, however, both should primarily be driven by:\r\n\r\n- knee-extension torque.\r\n\r\n## Comparison With Rectus Femoris\r\n\r\nThis is the most important functional distinction.\r\n\r\n### VM\r\n\r\n`E_LT_VM = f(knee)`.\r\n\r\n### RF\r\n\r\n`E_LT_RF = f(knee, hip)`.\r\n\r\nTherefore hip flexion in a seated leg extension can alter RF force contribution while leaving VM length almost unchanged.\r\n\r\nThis may increase the relative share carried by the vasti.\r\n\r\n## Quadriceps Force Sharing\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`T_knee_extension =\r\n    F_VL × r_VL\r\n  + F_VM × r_VM\r\n  + F_VI × r_VI\r\n  + F_RF × r_RF`.\r\n\r\nVM should not receive either:\r\n\r\n- an arbitrary equal 25% share;\r\n- an artificially tiny share because VL is larger.\r\n\r\nActual sharing depends on:\r\n\r\n- capacity;\r\n- knee angle;\r\n- RF hip position;\r\n- leverage;\r\n- neural recruitment.\r\n\r\n## Seated Leg Extension Allocation\r\n\r\nVery high:\r\n- `vastus_medialis`;\r\n- `vastus_lateralis`;\r\n- `vastus_intermedius`.\r\n\r\nHigh/meaningful:\r\n- `rectus_femoris`.\r\n\r\nDepending on hip angle, RF's relative contribution can change.\r\n\r\n## 45° Leg Press Allocation\r\n\r\nVery high VM demand.\r\n\r\nAlso very high:\r\n- VL;\r\n- VI.\r\n\r\nMeaningful:\r\n- RF.\r\n\r\nThe hip-extension component belongs to:\r\n- gluteus maximus;\r\n- adductor magnus;\r\n- other hip extensors.\r\n\r\n## Deep Squat Allocation\r\n\r\nHigh-to-very-high VM demand.\r\n\r\nLarge knee flexion creates high muscle length.\r\n\r\nHigh external knee moment creates high force.\r\n\r\nThe combination is mechanically favorable for hypertrophy.\r\n\r\n## Partial Squat Allocation\r\n\r\nVM can still generate large force with heavy loading.\r\n\r\nHowever:\r\n\r\n- long-length exposure is reduced.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`active_force`\r\nand\r\n`E_LT`\r\n\r\nshould remain separate.\r\n\r\n## VMO Isolation Modifier\r\n\r\nFor Agonez I would set something close to:\r\n\r\n`exercise_specific_VMO_isolation_modifier ≈ 0`.\r\n\r\nManipulations such as:\r\n\r\n- externally rotating the foot;\r\n- squeezing a ball between the knees;\r\n- terminal extension only;\r\n\r\nshould not receive a large special `vastus_medialis` bonus without stronger mechanical evidence.\r\n\r\nIf such manipulations change actual knee-extension torque, then VM demand changes because of that torque—not because a special VMO switch was activated.\r\n\r\n## Patellar Stabilization Component\r\n\r\nIf the model later includes explicit joint-stability demand, VM could receive a secondary patellofemoral stabilization term.\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`VM_total_demand =\r\n    knee_extension_force\r\n  + small_patellar_stabilization_component`.\r\n\r\nHowever, for standard resistance exercises the first term should overwhelmingly dominate hypertrophic demand.\r\n\r\n## E_MA Model\r\n\r\nVM acts through the common quadriceps-patellar extensor mechanism.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`E_MA_VM = f(knee_angle)`.\r\n\r\nA shared broad vasti leverage curve is a reasonable KISS approximation.\r\n\r\nFine differences between VM, VL and VI can be handled later through:\r\n\r\n- `E_LoA`;\r\n- head-specific force-sharing modifiers.\r\n\r\n## FCSA Interpretation\r\n\r\nVM FCSA represents available knee-extension force capacity.\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`F_max_VM ≈ σ × FCSA_VM`.\r\n\r\nThen:\r\n\r\n`T_VM ≈ F_VM × r_knee`.\r\n\r\nBecause VM is monoarticular, the mapping is much simpler than for RF.\r\n\r\nThere is no hip/knee double-counting problem.\r\n\r\n## Distal Line-of-Action Caveat\r\n\r\nNot all VM fiber force contributes identically to pure sagittal-plane knee extension.\r\n\r\nDistal oblique fibers have a stronger medial patellar-force component.\r\n\r\nTherefore total VM FCSA should not be interpreted as if every fiber has an identical sagittal moment arm.\r\n\r\nFor a KISS whole-muscle model, however, using one aggregate knee-extension effectiveness remains reasonable.\r\n\r\n## ETU Interpretation\r\n\r\nVM ETU can arise through:\r\n\r\n- concentric knee extension;\r\n- eccentric knee-flexion control;\r\n- isometric knee-extension force.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n`ROM≈0`\r\n\r\ncan still generate high ETU during:\r\n\r\n- wall-sit-like holds;\r\n- isometric machine extension;\r\n- static squat positions.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nThe long-length mechanics are straightforward:\r\n\r\n`knee_flexion ↑ → VM_length ↑`.\r\n\r\nBut the relevant hypertrophy term should remain:\r\n\r\n`SMH_effective_VM =\r\n    base_smh_factor\r\n    × E_LT(knee_angle)\r\n    × active_force`.\r\n\r\nDo not directly encode:\r\n\r\n`deep ROM = hypertrophy`.\r\n\r\nDeep ROM matters because it changes muscle length and often torque, but these are separate variables.\r\n\r\n## Data-Quality Caveat\r\n\r\nVM has more direct literature than deep muscles such as VI, but much of it is:\r\n\r\n- surface-EMG based;\r\n- clinically focused on VMO;\r\n- concerned with patellofemoral pain rather than hypertrophy.\r\n\r\nTherefore VM-specific bodybuilding coefficients should be assigned conservatively.\r\n\r\nIts fundamental biomechanics are high confidence; exercise-specific "selective activation" claims are much weaker.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **medial femoral origin and quadriceps-extensor insertion:** high;\r\n- **femoral-nerve innervation:** high;\r\n- **monoarticular knee-extension function:** very high;\r\n- **absence of direct hip action:** very high;\r\n- **lengthening with knee flexion:** very high;\r\n- **medial patellar-force contribution:** high;\r\n- **substantial contribution throughout knee-extension ROM:** high;\r\n- **VMO as a functionally distinct regional fiber orientation:** high;\r\n- **VMO as independently isolatable muscle:** weak;\r\n- **special terminal-extension hypertrophy requirement:** unsupported;\r\n- **precise VM/VL/VI/RF force sharing:** task dependent;\r\n- **direct VM-specific stretch-mediated hypertrophy magnitude:** insufficiently established.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **vastus medialis = a substantial monoarticular knee extensor whose muscle length is governed primarily by knee angle and whose distal fibers contribute additional medial patellar stabilization; hypertrophic modelling should treat it primarily as one of the major quadriceps force producers rather than as a special terminal-extension or VMO-isolation muscle.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/Quadriceps}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	9.68	29.60	0.869	135.00	117.38
34	vastus_lateralis	Musculus vastus lateralis	Lower	Quads	2690.00	2547.35	70.00	Unipennate/Multipennate	0.475	0.525	high	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\nThe **vastus lateralis (VL)** is the largest and most laterally positioned of the quadriceps muscles.\r\n\r\nIts dominant function is:\r\n\r\n- **knee extension**.\r\n\r\nLike `vastus_intermedius` and `vastus_medialis`, it is **monoarticular** and crosses only the knee.\r\n\r\nTherefore its direct muscle-length state is governed mainly by:\r\n\r\n- knee flexion;\r\n- knee extension.\r\n\r\nHip position does not directly lengthen or shorten VL.\r\n\r\nThis sharply distinguishes it from `rectus_femoris`, which spans both:\r\n\r\n- hip;\r\n- knee.\r\n\r\nVL is a large, pennate, force-oriented muscle and contributes substantially to total quadriceps force capacity. Human imaging studies consistently show clear pennation and regional variation in fascicle architecture, emphasizing that it should not be treated as a geometrically uniform actuator. :contentReference[oaicite:0]{index=0}\r\n\r\nFor Agonez, the most useful abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **vastus lateralis = a large monoarticular knee extensor with high force capacity, substantial pennation and regionally heterogeneous architecture, whose muscle length depends primarily on knee angle and whose force contribution should be high in essentially every demanding knee-extension task.**\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\n## Origin\r\n\r\nVastus lateralis has a broad proximal attachment on the lateral femur.\r\n\r\nIts origins include regions associated with:\r\n\r\n- greater trochanter;\r\n- lateral aspect of the linea aspera;\r\n- proximal lateral femoral shaft;\r\n- adjacent intermuscular septum.\r\n\r\nThe broad origin contributes to its large anatomical cross-sectional area and substantial contractile volume.\r\n\r\n## Insertion\r\n\r\nDistally, VL contributes to the quadriceps tendon attaching to the:\r\n\r\n- patella.\r\n\r\nForce is subsequently transmitted through the patellar tendon to the:\r\n\r\n- tibial tuberosity.\r\n\r\nVL also contributes lateral expansions to the patellar retinacular system.\r\n\r\nIts distal geometry therefore allows it to influence both:\r\n\r\n- knee-extension torque;\r\n- patellar force balance.\r\n\r\n## Relationship With Vastus Intermedius\r\n\r\nVL lies superficial/lateral to the deeper:\r\n\r\n- `vastus_intermedius`.\r\n\r\nBoth are monoarticular knee extensors.\r\n\r\nHowever, VL is easier to measure with:\r\n\r\n- ultrasound;\r\n- surface EMG;\r\n- biopsy.\r\n\r\nFor this reason, VL is vastly overrepresented in resistance-training research compared with VI.\r\n\r\nThis should not be interpreted as evidence that VL necessarily dominates every knee-extension task.\r\n\r\n## Relationship With Vastus Medialis\r\n\r\nVL and VM both generate knee extension but apply somewhat different force directions to the patella.\r\n\r\nA crude traditional description treats them as:\r\n\r\n- VL → lateral patellar pull;\r\n- VM → medial patellar pull.\r\n\r\nThis captures only part of the system.\r\n\r\nPatellar tracking depends on:\r\n\r\n- quadriceps force distribution;\r\n- retinacular structures;\r\n- trochlear geometry;\r\n- knee angle;\r\n- limb alignment.\r\n\r\nThus ordinary resistance training should not be modelled as a simplistic VL-versus-VM tug-of-war.\r\n\r\n## Architecture\r\n\r\nVL is strongly pennate.\r\n\r\nIts architecture includes:\r\n\r\n- measurable pennation angle;\r\n- fascicle length variation;\r\n- regional differences across proximal, middle and distal portions.\r\n\r\nHuman ultrasound studies show that fascicle length and pennation differ among individuals and populations, and more recent 3D/regional work confirms meaningful intramuscular heterogeneity. :contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}\r\n\r\nThis means one scalar:\r\n\r\n`optimal_fiber_length`\r\n\r\nor:\r\n\r\n`pennation_angle`\r\n\r\nis necessarily an aggregate representation.\r\n\r\nFor Agonez this is acceptable, but should be recognized as a simplification.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nVastus lateralis is innervated by branches of the **femoral nerve**, primarily from approximately:\r\n\r\n- L2;\r\n- L3;\r\n- L4.\r\n\r\nIt shares this broad neural supply with the other quadriceps muscles.\r\n\r\nRegional motor-unit recruitment can nevertheless differ across:\r\n\r\n- joint angles;\r\n- contraction types;\r\n- exercise tasks.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Knee Extension\r\n\r\nVL produces **knee-extension torque**.\r\n\r\nIt acts synergistically with:\r\n\r\n- vastus medialis;\r\n- vastus intermedius;\r\n- rectus femoris.\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`T_knee_extension =\r\n    F_VL × r_VL\r\n  + F_VM × r_VM\r\n  + F_VI × r_VI\r\n  + F_RF × r_RF`\r\n\r\nVL should generally represent one of the largest individual contributors because of its substantial force capacity.\r\n\r\n## Monoarticular Mechanics\r\n\r\nVL crosses only the knee.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n### Knee Flexion\r\n\r\nLengthens VL.\r\n\r\n### Knee Extension\r\n\r\nShortens VL.\r\n\r\nHip position has no direct first-order effect.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_VL ≈ f(knee_angle)`.\r\n\r\n## Eccentric Control\r\n\r\nVL can generate force while the knee flexes.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- squat descent;\r\n- leg-press eccentric;\r\n- landing;\r\n- downhill locomotion.\r\n\r\nDuring such tasks:\r\n\r\n`VL_length ↑`\r\n\r\nwhile:\r\n\r\n`VL_force > 0`.\r\n\r\nTherefore visible knee flexion is compatible with very high VL loading.\r\n\r\n## Isometric Knee Extension\r\n\r\nVL can also generate very high force with:\r\n\r\n`knee_ROM = 0`.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- isometric knee extension;\r\n- static squat positions;\r\n- overcoming-isometric tasks.\r\n\r\nAgain:\r\n\r\n`ROM ≈ 0`\r\n\r\ndoes not imply:\r\n\r\n`ETU ≈ 0`.\r\n\r\n## Patellar Force Contribution\r\n\r\nBecause VL inserts through the quadriceps-patellar mechanism, its contraction contributes to the resultant force applied to the patella.\r\n\r\nIts lateral orientation can create a lateral component of patellar force.\r\n\r\nHowever, this should not be turned into the simplistic claim that:\r\n\r\n> strengthening VL necessarily causes pathological lateral patellar tracking.\r\n\r\nPatellofemoral mechanics are multi-factorial.\r\n\r\n# Length-Tension Mechanics\r\n\r\n## Knee Flexion Lengthens VL\r\n\r\nThe basic relationship is straightforward:\r\n\r\n`knee_flexion ↑ → VL_length ↑`.\r\n\r\nBecause VL does not cross the hip, deep knee flexion produces a relatively uncompensated increase in muscle length.\r\n\r\nThis is unlike RF, where simultaneous hip flexion can partially offset distal lengthening.\r\n\r\n## Knee Extension Shortens VL\r\n\r\nAs the knee approaches extension:\r\n\r\n`VL_length ↓`.\r\n\r\nNear lockout the muscle is relatively short.\r\n\r\nHowever, actual force still depends on:\r\n\r\n- external torque;\r\n- leverage;\r\n- neural activation.\r\n\r\n## Deep Knee Flexion\r\n\r\nDeep flexion can expose VL to:\r\n\r\n- longer fascicle states;\r\n- large external knee moments;\r\n- substantial active force.\r\n\r\nThis makes deep knee-extension tasks mechanically attractive for hypertrophy.\r\n\r\nHowever, joint angle must not be used as a direct proxy for force.\r\n\r\nA deep position with little external knee torque does not automatically create high stimulus.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nVL has much better long-length hypertrophy evidence than many muscles in the database because it is frequently measured directly.\r\n\r\n## Knee-Extension Partial-ROM Evidence\r\n\r\nA longitudinal knee-extension study comparing different ROM regions found that training in the portion associated with longer quadriceps muscle lengths produced favorable regional hypertrophic outcomes compared with shorter-length partial training. VL was directly measured. :contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}\r\n\r\nThis provides real evidence that **where in the ROM the knee extensors are loaded matters**.\r\n\r\n## Full vs Partial ROM\r\n\r\nA 15-week study examining full-ROM versus partial-ROM knee-extension training found meaningful VL architectural and mechanical adaptations, demonstrating that training ROM can alter VL morphology rather than simply total muscle size. :contentReference[oaicite:3]{index=3}\r\n\r\nThus VL adaptation is sensitive to the actual joint-angle region trained.\r\n\r\n## Long-Length Isometric Training\r\n\r\nRecent long-muscle-length knee-extension work also supports the broader concept that quadriceps can hypertrophy effectively when large force is produced at long muscle lengths. :contentReference[oaicite:4]{index=4}\r\n\r\nBecause VL is monoarticular, knee angle maps onto its length state more cleanly than for RF.\r\n\r\n## Important Caveat: Deep Leg Press Evidence\r\n\r\nRecent resistance-trained-participant data comparing different knee-flexion ROMs in the leg press did **not** find that greater knee flexion necessarily produced superior quadriceps hypertrophy under the tested conditions. :contentReference[oaicite:5]{index=5}\r\n\r\nThis is important.\r\n\r\nThe evidence does not support the crude rule:\r\n\r\n`deeper = always more hypertrophy`.\r\n\r\nInstead:\r\n\r\n- muscle length matters;\r\n- active force matters;\r\n- external torque profile matters;\r\n- exercise type matters;\r\n- total training stimulus matters.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nVL deserves relatively strong confidence that loaded long-muscle-length work is useful.\r\n\r\nHowever:\r\n\r\n> **longer muscle length should be rewarded only when substantial force is actually produced there.**\r\n\r\nA sensible formulation is:\r\n\r\n`SMH_effective_VL =\r\n    smh_factor\r\n    × E_LT(knee_angle)\r\n    × active_force`.\r\n\r\nThis is preferable to assigning a fixed "deep squat bonus."\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Leg Extension\r\n\r\nLeg extension is an extremely specific VL exercise because essentially all external torque is:\r\n\r\n- knee-extension torque.\r\n\r\nThe main contributors are:\r\n\r\n- VL;\r\n- VM;\r\n- VI;\r\n- RF.\r\n\r\nBecause the torso and hip are externally supported, systemic fatigue is low relative to compound leg exercises.\r\n\r\nThis makes leg extension highly efficient for local quadriceps hypertrophy.\r\n\r\n## Hip Angle During Leg Extension\r\n\r\nThis creates an important distinction between VL and RF.\r\n\r\nChanging hip flexion can change RF muscle length because RF crosses the hip.\r\n\r\nVL does not.\r\n\r\nA direct longitudinal study comparing different hip-flexion angles during leg-extension training measured VL and RF separately, making this distinction experimentally relevant. :contentReference[oaicite:6]{index=6}\r\n\r\nFor Agonez:\r\n\r\n`hip_angle_modifier_E_LT_VL ≈ 0`.\r\n\r\n## Deep-Start Leg Extension\r\n\r\nBeginning from large knee flexion creates a longer VL state.\r\n\r\nIf the machine cam provides substantial resistance in that region, this is a strong long-length VL stimulus.\r\n\r\nMachine design matters greatly.\r\n\r\nTwo leg-extension machines with identical stack weight can produce very different knee-torque curves.\r\n\r\n## 45° Leg Press\r\n\r\nVL should receive very high demand.\r\n\r\nThe exercise can generate:\r\n\r\n- large external knee-extension torque;\r\n- deep knee flexion;\r\n- high absolute loading.\r\n\r\nIt is therefore one of the major VL exercises in the current Agonez catalogue.\r\n\r\n## Deep Leg Press\r\n\r\nGreater knee flexion increases VL length.\r\n\r\nHowever, recent direct longitudinal evidence suggests that simply extending knee-flexion ROM in the leg press does not guarantee greater quadriceps hypertrophy. :contentReference[oaicite:7]{index=7}\r\n\r\nThus Agonez should model:\r\n\r\n`length state`\r\n\r\nseparately from:\r\n\r\n`effective active force`.\r\n\r\n## Squat\r\n\r\nSquats heavily recruit VL through knee extension.\r\n\r\nThe muscle should receive substantial force especially where external knee-flexion moments are large.\r\n\r\nFull squat training has produced superior hypertrophy across several lower-limb muscles compared with half squat in longitudinal work, while RF and hamstrings showed different behavior. :contentReference[oaicite:8]{index=8}\r\n\r\nThis is consistent with VL being a major deep-knee-flexion extensor.\r\n\r\n## Smith Squat\r\n\r\nA Smith-machine squat can generate very high VL demand when the setup produces large knee torque.\r\n\r\nFoot position strongly modifies:\r\n\r\n- knee moment;\r\n- hip moment.\r\n\r\nMore forward knee travel generally shifts more of the external demand toward the quadriceps.\r\n\r\n## Hack Squat\r\n\r\nAlthough not currently central to the catalogue, hack squats are mechanically favorable for VL because:\r\n\r\n- trunk support reduces stabilization;\r\n- large knee flexion is possible;\r\n- knee-extension torque can be high.\r\n\r\n## Split Squat\r\n\r\nVL contribution depends on front-leg knee demand.\r\n\r\nA more knee-dominant setup with:\r\n\r\n- shorter stance;\r\n- greater forward knee travel;\r\n- more upright trunk\r\n\r\ngenerally increases quadriceps torque.\r\n\r\n## Reverse Nordic\r\n\r\nReverse-Nordic-type knee extension can load the vasti at substantial knee flexion.\r\n\r\nBecause VL is monoarticular, hip position does not create the same biarticular complication seen with RF.\r\n\r\n## Sissy Squat\r\n\r\nSissy-style movements can create very high external knee moments while the knee is deeply flexed.\r\n\r\nThey can therefore provide very strong long-length VL loading.\r\n\r\nLoad progression and knee tolerance become practical constraints.\r\n\r\n## Cycling\r\n\r\nVL contributes strongly during the power phase of cycling.\r\n\r\nHowever, conventional cycling usually creates lower hypertrophic loading per contraction than hard resistance training.\r\n\r\nIt should therefore not receive full resistance-training set equivalence.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## VL Usually Receives Large Amounts of Compound Volume\r\n\r\nAny program containing substantial:\r\n\r\n- squat;\r\n- leg press;\r\n- split squat;\r\n- leg extension\r\n\r\nwill provide major VL exposure.\r\n\r\nDedicated VL isolation is usually unnecessary.\r\n\r\n## Combine Compound and Isolation Work\r\n\r\nA useful quadriceps structure is:\r\n\r\n1. high-force compound knee extension;\r\n2. stable leg extension.\r\n\r\nThis gives VL:\r\n\r\n- high absolute force;\r\n- deep-knee-flexion exposure;\r\n- locally efficient failure work.\r\n\r\n## Weekly Volume\r\n\r\nA practical quadriceps-wide starting range is approximately:\r\n\r\n- **6-12 hard effective sets per week**.\r\n\r\nVL receives a large share of most of these sets.\r\n\r\nThere is usually no need for a separate VL volume quota.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nTwo to three weekly exposures are practical.\r\n\r\nBecause VL is large, high-volume training can produce substantial:\r\n\r\n- local soreness;\r\n- peripheral fatigue.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\n### Squat / Leg Press\r\n\r\nUseful:\r\n- 5-8;\r\n- 6-10;\r\n- 8-15 reps.\r\n\r\n### Leg Extension\r\n\r\nUseful:\r\n- 8-15;\r\n- 10-20;\r\n- 15-25 reps.\r\n\r\nNo VL-specific physiological repetition range is established.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nMachine leg extensions and supported leg presses can be trained very close to local failure.\r\n\r\nHeavy squats often benefit from more reserve because failure involves:\r\n\r\n- technique;\r\n- trunk fatigue;\r\n- cardiorespiratory stress;\r\n- safety considerations.\r\n\r\n## Systemic Fatigue\r\n\r\nVL stimulus can be high in both:\r\n\r\n- squat;\r\n- leg extension.\r\n\r\nBut exercise-level fatigue differs greatly.\r\n\r\n### Squat\r\n\r\nHigh:\r\n- systemic demand;\r\n- axial loading;\r\n- hip-extensor demand.\r\n\r\n### Leg Extension\r\n\r\nLow:\r\n- systemic cost.\r\n\r\nHigh:\r\n- local quadriceps specificity.\r\n\r\nThis distinction fits naturally with separate:\r\n\r\n- muscle ETU;\r\n- exercise systemic cost.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## VL Is One of the Most Studied Human Muscles\r\n\r\nIts superficial location makes it particularly convenient for:\r\n\r\n- ultrasound;\r\n- EMG;\r\n- biopsy;\r\n- muscle architecture studies.\r\n\r\nAs a result, enormous amounts of exercise physiology literature use VL as a representative skeletal muscle.\r\n\r\nThis convenience should not be mistaken for anatomical uniqueness.\r\n\r\n## Its Architecture Is Regionally Heterogeneous\r\n\r\nRecent regional work demonstrates meaningful differences in VL fascicle geometry across the muscle. :contentReference[oaicite:9]{index=9}\r\n\r\nTherefore a single:\r\n\r\n- fascicle length;\r\n- pennation angle\r\n\r\nis an average abstraction rather than a literal whole-muscle property.\r\n\r\n## Training Can Change VL Architecture\r\n\r\nResistance training can alter:\r\n\r\n- thickness;\r\n- pennation;\r\n- fascicle behavior.\r\n\r\nROM-specific training has also produced different architectural adaptations. :contentReference[oaicite:10]{index=10}\r\n\r\nThus VL is not a fixed mechanical actuator.\r\n\r\nIts architecture adapts with training history.\r\n\r\n## Muscle Size Is Not the Only Performance Variable\r\n\r\nVL architecture, including fascicle characteristics and pennation, relates to force and rate-of-force-development capabilities. :contentReference[oaicite:11]{index=11}\r\n\r\nThis supports keeping architecture conceptually separate from simple mass.\r\n\r\n## It Is Not a Hip Muscle\r\n\r\nThis seems obvious anatomically but matters strongly in computational modelling.\r\n\r\nHip flexion can dramatically change RF behavior while leaving VL length essentially unchanged.\r\n\r\nThus a deep seated exercise can simultaneously put:\r\n\r\n- RF in one proximal length condition;\r\n- VL in a completely different mechanical condition.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\n`vastus_lateralis` should receive demand almost entirely from:\r\n\r\n1. **knee-extension torque**;\r\n2. eccentric control of knee flexion;\r\n3. secondary patellofemoral stabilization.\r\n\r\nIt should receive approximately zero direct:\r\n\r\n- hip-flexion torque;\r\n- hip-extension torque.\r\n\r\n## Length-Tension Model\r\n\r\nA first-order model is:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_VL = f(knee_flexion)`.\r\n\r\nQualitatively:\r\n\r\n### Long\r\n\r\n- deep knee flexion.\r\n\r\n### Intermediate\r\n\r\n- moderate knee flexion.\r\n\r\n### Short\r\n\r\n- near full knee extension.\r\n\r\nHip angle should not enter directly.\r\n\r\n## Comparison With Rectus Femoris\r\n\r\nThis distinction is fundamental.\r\n\r\n### VL\r\n\r\nMonoarticular:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_VL = f(knee)`.\r\n\r\n### RF\r\n\r\nBiarticular:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_RF = f(knee, hip)`.\r\n\r\nTherefore a seated leg extension with high hip flexion can produce:\r\n\r\n- long VL due knee flexion;\r\n- altered/partly shortened RF proximally.\r\n\r\nForce sharing should respond accordingly.\r\n\r\n## Comparison With Vastus Intermedius\r\n\r\nBoth are monoarticular.\r\n\r\nTheir basic length functions can initially share a similar knee-angle dependence.\r\n\r\nHowever they differ in:\r\n\r\n- FCSA;\r\n- architecture;\r\n- regional geometry;\r\n- recruitment.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_VL ≈ E_LT_VI`\r\n\r\ndoes not imply:\r\n\r\n`F_VL = F_VI`.\r\n\r\n## Comparison With Vastus Medialis\r\n\r\nAgain, all three vasti share:\r\n\r\n- knee-extension function;\r\n- knee-angle-dominated length state.\r\n\r\nDifferences lie more in:\r\n\r\n- force capacity;\r\n- distal geometry;\r\n- neural recruitment;\r\n- patellar force direction.\r\n\r\nFor KISS modelling, this suggests sharing much of the same:\r\n\r\n- `E_LT(knee_angle)`;\r\n- broad extensor-mechanism `E_MA(knee_angle)`;\r\n\r\nwhile preserving individual:\r\n\r\n- FCSA;\r\n- `E_ND`;\r\n- `E_LoA`.\r\n\r\n## Quadriceps Force Sharing\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`T_knee_ext =\r\n    F_VL × r_VL\r\n  + F_VM × r_VM\r\n  + F_VI × r_VI\r\n  + F_RF × r_RF`.\r\n\r\nA fixed equal distribution should be avoided.\r\n\r\nVL has large force capacity and should commonly receive a major share.\r\n\r\nHowever, its actual contribution depends on:\r\n\r\n- knee angle;\r\n- RF hip geometry;\r\n- task;\r\n- training history;\r\n- neural strategy.\r\n\r\n## Seated Leg Extension Allocation\r\n\r\nVery high:\r\n- `vastus_lateralis`;\r\n- `vastus_intermedius`;\r\n- `vastus_medialis`.\r\n\r\nMeaningful/high:\r\n- `rectus_femoris`.\r\n\r\nRelative RF share may change with hip position, whereas VL does not receive a corresponding hip-length penalty.\r\n\r\n## 45° Leg Press Allocation\r\n\r\nVery high VL demand.\r\n\r\nAlso very high:\r\n- VI;\r\n- VM.\r\n\r\nRF:\r\n- meaningful but mechanically complicated by simultaneous hip and knee motion.\r\n\r\nGluteus maximus and adductor magnus receive the hip-extension component.\r\n\r\n## Deep Squat Allocation\r\n\r\nHigh-to-very-high VL demand.\r\n\r\nThe muscle becomes longer with knee flexion while large extensor torque may be required.\r\n\r\nThis is a much cleaner long-length situation than for RF because hip flexion does not shorten VL.\r\n\r\n## Partial Squat Allocation\r\n\r\nHigh VL force can still occur, particularly with large external load.\r\n\r\nHowever:\r\n\r\n- maximal muscle length exposure is reduced.\r\n\r\nThus Agonez should keep:\r\n\r\n`active_force`\r\n\r\nand:\r\n\r\n`E_LT`\r\n\r\nas independent dimensions.\r\n\r\n## Leg Press ROM Caveat\r\n\r\nDo not encode:\r\n\r\n`deeper leg press = automatically greater VL hypertrophy`.\r\n\r\nA recent within-participant study in resistance-trained individuals found no quadriceps hypertrophy advantage from the greater knee-flexion ROM under the tested conditions. :contentReference[oaicite:12]{index=12}\r\n\r\nThis is exactly why:\r\n\r\n`E_LT`\r\n\r\nmust multiply actual effective force rather than replace it.\r\n\r\n## Long-Length Partial Knee Extension\r\n\r\nLong-length partial knee-extension training has evidence of favorable regional VL hypertrophy compared with shorter-length work. :contentReference[oaicite:13]{index=13}\r\n\r\nTherefore an exercise that keeps high torque in flexed-knee positions should receive a stronger VL long-length score than one loading mainly near lockout.\r\n\r\n## E_MA Model\r\n\r\nKnee-extension leverage changes throughout ROM because of:\r\n\r\n- patellar geometry;\r\n- quadriceps tendon direction;\r\n- tibial relationship.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n`E_MA_VL = f(knee_angle)`.\r\n\r\nFor KISS implementation, a common vasti extensor-mechanism leverage curve is reasonable initially.\r\n\r\nFine head-specific differences can remain inside:\r\n\r\n- `E_LoA`;\r\n- force-sharing modifiers.\r\n\r\n## Architecture and FCSA\r\n\r\nVL is strongly pennate and force-oriented.\r\n\r\nIts architecture permits a relatively large amount of contractile tissue to act through a given muscle volume.\r\n\r\nTherefore FCSA is particularly useful here.\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`F_max_VL ≈ σ × FCSA_VL`.\r\n\r\nThen:\r\n\r\n`T_VL ≈ F_VL × r_knee`.\r\n\r\nIts regional architecture means the database FCSA is necessarily an aggregate, but still a useful force-capacity proxy.\r\n\r\n## Pennation Interpretation\r\n\r\nPennation reduces the fraction of individual fiber force transmitted parallel to the tendon:\r\n\r\n`F_tendon ≈ F_fiber × cos(theta)`.\r\n\r\nBut greater pennation also permits more fibers to be packed into the muscle.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n> larger pennation is not simply a force penalty.\r\n\r\nThis is precisely why projected FCSA should be considered together with total physiological cross-sectional architecture.\r\n\r\n## Training-Induced Architecture Change\r\n\r\nVL architecture is plastic.\r\n\r\nTraining can alter:\r\n\r\n- muscle thickness;\r\n- pennation;\r\n- fascicle characteristics.\r\n\r\nStudies directly examining VL show architecture changing after resistance training and with different ROM exposures. :contentReference[oaicite:14]{index=14}\r\n\r\nAgonez currently treats these properties as fixed athlete-level parameters, which is appropriate for a snapshot model, but they are not biologically immutable.\r\n\r\n## ETU Interpretation\r\n\r\nVL ETU should derive primarily from:\r\n\r\n- active knee-extension force;\r\n- length state;\r\n- contraction duration;\r\n- ROM;\r\n- proximity to failure.\r\n\r\nIt can accumulate through:\r\n\r\n### Concentric\r\n\r\nKnee extension.\r\n\r\n### Eccentric\r\n\r\nLoaded knee flexion.\r\n\r\n### Isometric\r\n\r\nStatic knee-extension torque.\r\n\r\nThis is another strong case where work-only modelling would miss meaningful muscle demand.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nVL deserves a relatively high-confidence long-length model.\r\n\r\nThe evidence indicates:\r\n\r\n- knee-angle region influences VL adaptation;\r\n- long-length knee-extension work can be highly hypertrophic;\r\n- ROM-specific training produces distinct regional/architectural effects. :contentReference[oaicite:15]{index=15}\r\n\r\nHowever, newer leg-press evidence shows that increasing depth alone does not guarantee extra hypertrophy. :contentReference[oaicite:16]{index=16}\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`SMH_effective_VL =\r\n    base_smh_factor\r\n    × E_LT(knee_angle)\r\n    × active_force`\r\n\r\nis substantially more defensible than:\r\n\r\n`SMH = depth`.\r\n\r\n## Data Quality\r\n\r\nVL has unusually strong data quality for a skeletal muscle because it is accessible to:\r\n\r\n- surface EMG;\r\n- ultrasound;\r\n- MRI;\r\n- biopsy.\r\n\r\nIts architecture and hypertrophic response are therefore considerably better characterized than muscles such as:\r\n\r\n- vastus intermedius;\r\n- sartorius;\r\n- deep neck extensors.\r\n\r\nExercise-specific coefficients can consequently be assigned with somewhat narrower uncertainty.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **lateral femoral origin and quadriceps-tendon insertion:** high;\r\n- **femoral-nerve innervation:** high;\r\n- **monoarticular knee-extension function:** very high;\r\n- **absence of direct hip action:** very high;\r\n- **knee-flexion-mediated lengthening:** very high;\r\n- **pennate, force-oriented architecture:** directly established; :contentReference[oaicite:17]{index=17}\r\n- **regional architectural heterogeneity:** directly demonstrated; :contentReference[oaicite:18]{index=18}\r\n- **training-induced architectural adaptation:** directly demonstrated; :contentReference[oaicite:19]{index=19}\r\n- **ROM-specific VL hypertrophy effects:** directly supported longitudinally; :contentReference[oaicite:20]{index=20}\r\n- **deep ROM automatically superior in every compound exercise:** not supported; :contentReference[oaicite:21]{index=21}\r\n- **exact VL/VM/VI/RF force sharing:** task- and angle-dependent;\r\n- **exact optimal knee angle for VL hypertrophy:** not established as one universal value.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **vastus lateralis = a large, pennate, monoarticular and strongly force-oriented knee extensor whose length is governed primarily by knee angle, whose FCSA represents a major component of total quadriceps capacity, and whose hypertrophic response is meaningfully influenced by the joint-angle region in which high knee-extension force is produced.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	9.94	18.40	0.949	256.27	243.17
17	biceps_brachii	Musculus biceps brachii	Upper	Biceps	515.00	487.69	22.00	Fusiform	0.350	0.650	very_high	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\nThe **biceps brachii** is a superficial fusiform muscle of the anterior upper arm composed of two heads:\r\n\r\n- **long head**;\r\n- **short head**.\r\n\r\nBoth heads converge distally into a common muscle-tendon unit inserting onto the radius, but they originate from different regions of the scapula.\r\n\r\nThis makes the biceps brachii a **biarticular muscle** spanning both the shoulder and elbow.\r\n\r\nIts major functions are:\r\n\r\n1. **forearm supination**;\r\n2. **elbow flexion**;\r\n3. secondary contribution to shoulder flexion and stabilization.\r\n\r\nFor resistance-training purposes, it is important not to reduce the muscle to simply "the elbow flexor."\r\n\r\nThe biceps is one of several elbow flexors and works alongside:\r\n\r\n- `brachialis`;\r\n- `brachioradialis`.\r\n\r\nIts mechanical contribution changes substantially with forearm orientation.\r\n\r\nA useful simplification is:\r\n\r\n- **supinated forearm:** biceps brachii has a strong mechanical role;\r\n- **neutral forearm:** biceps remains active, but brachialis and brachioradialis become relatively more prominent;\r\n- **pronated forearm:** biceps' ability to generate elbow-flexion torque is mechanically disadvantaged.\r\n\r\nBecause it crosses the shoulder, its length also depends on shoulder position.\r\n\r\nShoulder extension lengthens the biceps proximally, while shoulder flexion shortens it.\r\n\r\nThis property is central to exercise selection and to interpreting lengthened-position hypertrophy.\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\n## Long Head\r\n\r\n### Origin\r\n\r\nThe long head originates primarily from the **supraglenoid tubercle of the scapula** and adjacent superior glenoid/labral region.\r\n\r\nIts tendon travels intra-articularly through the glenohumeral joint before exiting into the **intertubercular groove** of the humerus.\r\n\r\nAlthough the tendon lies within the joint capsule, it is extrasynovial.\r\n\r\nThis unusual course gives the long head an anatomical relationship with the glenohumeral joint that the short head does not share.\r\n\r\n### Insertion\r\n\r\nThe long head merges with the short head in the distal arm.\r\n\r\nThe combined distal biceps tendon inserts onto the **radial tuberosity**.\r\n\r\nHowever, the distal tendon is not mechanically homogeneous: fibers originating from the two heads retain a degree of organized separation and orientation, which contributes to differences in their relative mechanical efficiency.\r\n\r\n## Short Head\r\n\r\n### Origin\r\n\r\nThe short head originates from the **coracoid process of the scapula**, sharing this proximal region with the coracobrachialis.\r\n\r\n### Insertion\r\n\r\nLike the long head, it contributes to the common distal biceps tendon inserting on the **radial tuberosity**.\r\n\r\nA second distal structure, the **bicipital aponeurosis** or lacertus fibrosus, expands medially into the antebrachial fascia.\r\n\r\nThis aponeurosis contributes to force transmission and has clinical importance because it can remain intact in some distal biceps injuries.\r\n\r\n## Internal Architecture\r\n\r\nDespite its external fusiform appearance, the biceps is not simply a bundle of fibers running from origin directly to insertion.\r\n\r\nIts fibers attach to internal aponeurotic structures and exhibit regional differences in orientation.\r\n\r\nThe muscle therefore possesses more complex architecture than the classic textbook image of a purely parallel-fibered fusiform muscle suggests.\r\n\r\nThe long and short heads also demonstrate functional differentiation rather than behaving as perfectly redundant actuators.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nBiceps brachii is innervated by the **musculocutaneous nerve**, predominantly from spinal roots **C5-C6**, with some descriptions including contribution from C7.\r\n\r\nThe musculocutaneous nerve supplies the major muscles of the anterior arm:\r\n\r\n- biceps brachii;\r\n- brachialis;\r\n- coracobrachialis.\r\n\r\nAfter supplying these muscles, it continues distally as the lateral cutaneous nerve of the forearm.\r\n\r\nBoth biceps heads receive musculocutaneous innervation, although intramuscular nerve branching is regionally organized.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Forearm Supination\r\n\r\nForearm supination is one of the defining functions of the biceps brachii.\r\n\r\nThe distal tendon inserts on the radius at a position that allows contraction to rotate the radius around the ulna.\r\n\r\nThis function becomes especially important when the elbow is flexed.\r\n\r\nBiceps therefore acts as a powerful supinator against resistance.\r\n\r\nThis distinguishes it from `brachialis`, which attaches to the ulna and therefore cannot generate meaningful forearm supination torque.\r\n\r\nThe training implication is important:\r\n\r\n> a supinated curl simultaneously places the biceps in favorable conditions for both elbow flexion and maintenance of forearm supination.\r\n\r\n## Elbow Flexion\r\n\r\nBiceps brachii contributes substantially to elbow flexion.\r\n\r\nHowever, it is not the only elbow flexor and not necessarily the dominant flexor under every joint configuration.\r\n\r\nIts contribution depends strongly on:\r\n\r\n- forearm rotation;\r\n- elbow angle;\r\n- shoulder angle;\r\n- external resistance profile;\r\n- task demand.\r\n\r\nBrachialis remains a strong elbow flexor regardless of forearm rotation because its insertion is on the ulna.\r\n\r\nBrachioradialis becomes especially relevant in neutral and intermediate forearm positions.\r\n\r\nConsequently, changing grip orientation changes the relative distribution of muscular demand even if the visible movement remains elbow flexion.\r\n\r\n## Long-Head vs Short-Head Functional Bias\r\n\r\nThe two heads are mechanically differentiated.\r\n\r\nAnatomical and biomechanical analysis of the distal tendon suggests that:\r\n\r\n- the **short-head component** has favorable geometry for elbow flexion;\r\n- the **long-head component** may possess relatively greater mechanical effectiveness for supination.\r\n\r\nThis does not mean one head performs only flexion and the other only supination.\r\n\r\nBoth participate in both functions.\r\n\r\nThe difference is one of **relative mechanical specialization**, not exclusive function.\r\n\r\nThis is relevant for modelling because equal 50/50 allocation between the two heads would be an oversimplification if they were represented separately.\r\n\r\nIn the present database, however, `biceps_brachii` represents the combined muscle.\r\n\r\n## Shoulder Flexion\r\n\r\nBecause both heads originate on the scapula and cross anterior to the glenohumeral joint, the biceps can contribute to **shoulder flexion**.\r\n\r\nThis is a secondary function relative to elbow flexion and supination.\r\n\r\nIts contribution depends on shoulder position and on concurrent elbow demands.\r\n\r\nIn most resistance-training contexts, it should not be treated as a primary shoulder flexor comparable to the anterior deltoid.\r\n\r\n## Glenohumeral Stabilization\r\n\r\nThe long-head tendon has a complex relationship with the shoulder joint.\r\n\r\nIt can contribute to dynamic stabilization of the humeral head, although the magnitude and practical importance of this role depend on movement and loading conditions.\r\n\r\nFor hypertrophy modelling, this stabilizing contribution is generally small relative to direct elbow-flexion or supination work.\r\n\r\nFor shoulder biomechanics and injury interpretation, however, it is relevant.\r\n\r\n## Biarticular Length Changes\r\n\r\nBiceps length is determined jointly by elbow and shoulder position.\r\n\r\nIt is relatively lengthened when:\r\n\r\n- the elbow approaches extension;\r\n- the shoulder moves into extension.\r\n\r\nIt is relatively shortened when:\r\n\r\n- the elbow flexes;\r\n- the shoulder flexes.\r\n\r\nThis means two exercises with the same elbow ROM can place the biceps at meaningfully different overall muscle lengths.\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n\r\n- **incline curl / Bayesian-style curl:** shoulder extension increases proximal muscle length;\r\n- **preacher curl:** shoulder flexion shortens the muscle proximally.\r\n\r\nThis difference is biomechanically real, but it should not be confused with simplistic claims that one exercise uniquely isolates one head.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nBiceps brachii is a useful model for understanding the distinction between:\r\n\r\n- **muscle length**;\r\n- **joint position**;\r\n- **resistance profile**.\r\n\r\nAll three matter.\r\n\r\nTraining near elbow extension generally exposes the elbow flexors to longer lengths than training only near peak elbow flexion.\r\n\r\nResearch comparing partial ranges of motion has found greater distal biceps hypertrophy when training emphasizes the more extended-elbow portion of the movement than when training emphasizes only the shortened portion. citeturn236541search20\r\n\r\nThis supports the broader principle that loading the biceps at relatively long lengths can be hypertrophically valuable.\r\n\r\nHowever, shoulder angle complicates the interpretation.\r\n\r\n## Shoulder Extension\r\n\r\nExercises such as:\r\n\r\n- incline dumbbell curl;\r\n- Bayesian cable curl;\r\n\r\nplace the shoulder behind the torso.\r\n\r\nBecause biceps crosses the shoulder, this increases its overall musculotendinous length.\r\n\r\nThis creates a strong theoretical long-length stimulus.\r\n\r\n## Shoulder Flexion\r\n\r\nPreacher curls place the shoulder in flexion.\r\n\r\nThis shortens the biceps proximally.\r\n\r\nNevertheless, a preacher curl can impose **very high external elbow-flexion torque near elbow extension**, depending on the equipment and resistance profile.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n> "shoulder extension = long-length exercise" and "preacher = short-length exercise" are incomplete descriptions.\r\n\r\nOne must distinguish **anatomical muscle length** from **where external torque peaks**.\r\n\r\nRecent randomized comparisons reinforce this distinction.\r\n\r\nPreacher and incline curls can produce different regional hypertrophy patterns rather than one exercise simply dominating globally. citeturn236541search2turn236541search27\r\n\r\nSimilarly, when resistance profiles are more carefully matched, changing shoulder position alone does not produce the simplistic differences often claimed in bodybuilding lore. citeturn236541search3\r\n\r\n## Practical Interpretation\r\n\r\nFor hypertrophy programming:\r\n\r\n- include meaningful loading near elbow extension;\r\n- avoid making every curl maximally easy in the lengthened portion;\r\n- consider shoulder-extension curls as one way to increase muscle length;\r\n- consider preacher-style loading as another way to create high torque at relatively extended elbow angles;\r\n- do not assume a single "best" lengthened biceps exercise exists independently of resistance profile.\r\n\r\nThe most robust practical principle is:\r\n\r\n> expose the biceps to substantial force across a broad range of lengths, with at least some exercises maintaining meaningful resistance in the extended-elbow region.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Forearm Position Matters\r\n\r\n### Supinated Grip\r\n\r\nA supinated grip is the most direct way to emphasize biceps brachii because the muscle simultaneously acts as:\r\n\r\n- elbow flexor;\r\n- active supinator.\r\n\r\nThis generally makes conventional supinated curls the foundational direct biceps exercise.\r\n\r\n### Neutral Grip\r\n\r\nNeutral-grip or hammer curls remain elbow-flexion exercises but shift relative demand toward:\r\n\r\n- brachialis;\r\n- brachioradialis.\r\n\r\nBiceps remains active, but the exercise should not be modeled identically to a fully supinated curl.\r\n\r\n### Pronated Grip\r\n\r\nPronated curls mechanically disadvantage the biceps as a supinator and increase relative reliance on the other elbow flexors.\r\n\r\nThey are therefore generally poor choices when the explicit goal is maximizing biceps-specific stimulus.\r\n\r\n## Maintain Supination\r\n\r\nIn dumbbell curls, allowing the forearm to drift toward neutral under fatigue reduces one of the biceps' defining mechanical tasks.\r\n\r\nFor biceps-focused work, maintaining or actively producing supination through the concentric phase can be useful.\r\n\r\nThis does not require exaggerated wrist rotation.\r\n\r\nSupination should occur through the radioulnar joints, not through wrist deviation.\r\n\r\n## Shoulder Position\r\n\r\nShoulder position changes biceps length but does not provide clean head isolation.\r\n\r\n### Shoulder Behind Torso\r\n\r\nExamples:\r\n\r\n- incline dumbbell curl;\r\n- Bayesian cable curl.\r\n\r\nCharacteristics:\r\n\r\n- greater proximal biceps length;\r\n- potentially strong lengthened stimulus;\r\n- often reduced ability to use body momentum.\r\n\r\n### Shoulder Near Neutral\r\n\r\nExamples:\r\n\r\n- standing barbell curl;\r\n- standing dumbbell curl.\r\n\r\nCharacteristics:\r\n\r\n- broad, general biceps stimulus;\r\n- simple overload;\r\n- relatively neutral shoulder-length condition.\r\n\r\n### Shoulder Flexed\r\n\r\nExamples:\r\n\r\n- preacher curl.\r\n\r\nCharacteristics:\r\n\r\n- proximally shortened biceps;\r\n- highly stable upper arm;\r\n- potentially very high torque near elbow extension depending on resistance setup;\r\n- minimal ability to cheat with shoulder movement.\r\n\r\nThese are different mechanical solutions, not exclusive "long-head" and "short-head" exercises.\r\n\r\n## Resistance Profile\r\n\r\nResistance profile matters substantially.\r\n\r\nA free-weight curl typically has low external elbow torque when the forearm is nearly vertical and highest torque when the forearm approaches horizontal relative to gravity.\r\n\r\nCable geometry can reshape this profile.\r\n\r\nTherefore two exercises with identical body positions can provide substantially different muscular loading.\r\n\r\nFor hypertrophy modelling, exercise names alone are insufficient.\r\n\r\nRelevant variables include:\r\n\r\n- cable origin;\r\n- elbow position;\r\n- shoulder angle;\r\n- forearm orientation;\r\n- direction of external resistance;\r\n- ROM.\r\n\r\n## Incline Dumbbell Curl\r\n\r\nThe incline curl increases biceps length through shoulder extension.\r\n\r\nIts advantages include:\r\n\r\n- large overall muscle length;\r\n- limited shoulder cheating;\r\n- strong supinated-elbow-flexion demand.\r\n\r\nIts limitation is that standard dumbbell gravity resistance may become relatively small near full elbow extension depending on arm orientation.\r\n\r\nThus the muscle can be long while external torque is not maximal.\r\n\r\nThis distinction is fundamental.\r\n\r\n## Bayesian Cable Curl\r\n\r\nA properly configured Bayesian curl can combine:\r\n\r\n- shoulder extension;\r\n- near-full elbow extension;\r\n- continued external cable tension.\r\n\r\nThis can make it particularly useful for lengthened-position loading.\r\n\r\nHowever, the exact profile depends heavily on cable height and lifter position.\r\n\r\n"Bayesian curl" is therefore not one invariant biomechanical exercise.\r\n\r\n## Preacher Curl\r\n\r\nThe preacher curl stabilizes the upper arm and minimizes shoulder contribution.\r\n\r\nIt can produce substantial elbow-flexion torque near relatively extended elbow positions.\r\n\r\nThis makes it potentially excellent for biceps hypertrophy despite the shoulder-flexed position.\r\n\r\nThe common claim that preacher curls are inherently a "short-head exercise" should be treated cautiously.\r\n\r\nCurrent evidence supports regional differences between exercises but not a clean bodybuilding-style head-isolation rule. citeturn236541search2turn236541search3\r\n\r\n## Standing Curl\r\n\r\nStanding barbell or dumbbell curls offer:\r\n\r\n- simple progressive overload;\r\n- high loading capacity;\r\n- unrestricted supination with dumbbells;\r\n- relatively neutral shoulder position.\r\n\r\nThe major limitation is the possibility of compensatory shoulder flexion, trunk extension or hip movement.\r\n\r\nSome controlled body movement is not inherently harmful, but if the goal is a standardized biceps stimulus it reduces interpretability of the loading vector.\r\n\r\n## Compound Pulling\r\n\r\nBiceps receives meaningful loading during:\r\n\r\n- chin-ups;\r\n- supinated pulldowns;\r\n- neutral-grip pull-ups;\r\n- rows;\r\n- other elbow-flexion pulling patterns.\r\n\r\nHowever, its contribution varies substantially according to grip.\r\n\r\nA supinated pull generally places greater biceps-specific demand than a pronated pull.\r\n\r\nCompound pulling should therefore count toward biceps exposure, but not necessarily as equivalent to direct curls.\r\n\r\n## ROM\r\n\r\nA productive default is to use large elbow ROM:\r\n\r\n- approach substantial elbow extension;\r\n- flex through the useful concentric range;\r\n- maintain control near the bottom;\r\n- avoid bouncing out of extension.\r\n\r\nFull anatomical lockout is not mandatory if it produces joint discomfort.\r\n\r\nThe important principle is meaningful loaded length, not chasing a visual definition of "full ROM" at any cost.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Weekly Volume\r\n\r\nBiceps often receives substantial indirect volume from pulling exercises.\r\n\r\nDirect programming should therefore account for:\r\n\r\n- pull-ups;\r\n- pulldowns;\r\n- rows;\r\n- direct curls.\r\n\r\nFor a trained lifter already performing several hard pulling exercises, a relatively modest amount of direct curl work may be sufficient.\r\n\r\nA practical starting range can be approximately:\r\n\r\n- **4-8 direct sets per week** when substantial pulling volume is already present;\r\n- more when biceps hypertrophy is a major priority and recovery remains adequate.\r\n\r\nThese are programming heuristics, not physiological thresholds.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nTraining biceps approximately **2 times per week** is a practical default.\r\n\r\nHigher frequencies can work if per-session volume is low.\r\n\r\nThe muscle is relatively small and generally produces less systemic fatigue than major lower-body or axial-loading exercises.\r\n\r\nLocal elbow and tendon tolerance may become limiting before whole-body recovery does.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\nBiceps can hypertrophy across a broad repetition spectrum.\r\n\r\nUseful practical ranges include:\r\n\r\n- 6-10 reps for heavier curls;\r\n- 8-15 reps as a general default;\r\n- 15-30 reps for stable isolation work.\r\n\r\nVery heavy low-repetition curls tend to produce relatively high joint and connective-tissue loading while offering little unique hypertrophic advantage.\r\n\r\nModerate repetitions are therefore often preferable for most direct work.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nStable curl variations can be trained close to muscular failure.\r\n\r\nTypical useful exposure:\r\n\r\n- approximately 0-3 RIR on hard work sets.\r\n\r\nMachine, cable and preacher variations make failure easier to standardize.\r\n\r\nStanding free-weight curls become increasingly difficult to interpret when trunk and shoulder compensation increase near failure.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Diversity\r\n\r\nExercise diversity should be based on **mechanical complementarity**, not arbitrary variety.\r\n\r\nA useful combination could include:\r\n\r\n1. one curl emphasizing large muscle length through shoulder extension;\r\n2. one stable curl emphasizing high torque near extended elbow positions;\r\n3. optional neutral-grip work targeting the broader elbow-flexor complex.\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n\r\n- Bayesian or incline curl;\r\n- preacher curl;\r\n- hammer curl.\r\n\r\nThis combination covers more distinct mechanical conditions than several nearly identical standing curls.\r\n\r\n## Head-Specific Programming\r\n\r\nAttempting to separately hypertrophy the long and short heads with extreme precision is not well supported.\r\n\r\nDifferences in recruitment and regional hypertrophy exist, but both heads contribute to most conventional supinated elbow-flexion exercises.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n> program different joint positions and resistance profiles rather than relying on rigid "long-head exercise" versus "short-head exercise" categories.\r\n\r\n## Fatigue Cost\r\n\r\nBiceps isolation work has low systemic fatigue but can create meaningful local fatigue in:\r\n\r\n- distal biceps tendon;\r\n- proximal long-head tendon;\r\n- elbow flexor musculature;\r\n- forearm supinators.\r\n\r\nThis makes it easy to add excessive volume simply because the exercises do not feel systemically demanding.\r\n\r\nLocal connective-tissue tolerance remains a real constraint.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Pull Training\r\n\r\nDirect curls performed before heavy pulling may reduce performance in:\r\n\r\n- pull-ups;\r\n- pulldowns;\r\n- rows.\r\n\r\nIf back performance is the priority, curls usually belong after compound pulling.\r\n\r\nIf biceps development is the priority, placing some direct biceps work earlier in the session can be justified.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## The Biceps Does Not Insert on the Humerus\r\n\r\nDespite occupying the upper arm, neither end of the biceps brachii attaches directly to the humerus.\r\n\r\nIt originates from the scapula and inserts on the radius.\r\n\r\nThe humerus lies between its attachments.\r\n\r\nThis explains why the muscle crosses both the shoulder and elbow while also influencing forearm rotation.\r\n\r\n## "Bi-ceps"\r\n\r\nThe name refers literally to its **two proximal heads**:\r\n\r\n- `bi-` = two;\r\n- `-ceps` = heads.\r\n\r\nBoth heads merge distally into the same general tendon system.\r\n\r\n## Supination Is Not a Minor Side Function\r\n\r\nPopular training descriptions often define the biceps primarily as an elbow flexor.\r\n\r\nBiomechanically, supination is one of its defining functions.\r\n\r\nThe distal tendon arrangement is specifically suited to rotating the radius, and anatomical work suggests functional differentiation between the tendon contributions of the two heads. citeturn236541search0\r\n\r\n## The Two Heads Are Not Mechanically Identical\r\n\r\nThe short and long heads demonstrate differences in:\r\n\r\n- tendon geometry;\r\n- recruitment;\r\n- response to muscle length;\r\n- relative mechanical contributions.\r\n\r\nLonger biceps muscle length has been associated with relatively increased long-head activation during rapid supination tasks. citeturn236541search18\r\n\r\nThus the two heads are not simply duplicate actuators joined together.\r\n\r\n## Regional Hypertrophy Is Real\r\n\r\nResistance training does not necessarily enlarge the biceps uniformly along its entire length.\r\n\r\nDifferent exercises and ROM regions can produce different proximal, middle and distal hypertrophy responses. citeturn236541search2turn236541search8turn236541search20\r\n\r\nThis makes single-site ultrasound measurements an imperfect proxy for whole-muscle hypertrophy.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\n`biceps_brachii` should primarily receive demand from two mechanical tasks:\r\n\r\n1. **elbow-flexion torque**;\r\n2. **forearm-supination torque**.\r\n\r\nA recruitment model based solely on elbow flexion will systematically underrepresent the distinction between supinated and neutral/pronated exercises.\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n\r\n### Supinated Curl\r\n\r\nHigh `biceps_brachii` allocation.\r\n\r\nThe muscle contributes strongly to both elbow flexion and forearm supination.\r\n\r\n### Hammer Curl\r\n\r\nModerate biceps contribution but greater relative allocation toward:\r\n\r\n- `brachialis`;\r\n- `brachioradialis`.\r\n\r\n### Reverse Curl\r\n\r\nLower relative biceps contribution and greater reliance on the other elbow flexors.\r\n\r\n### Supinated Chin-Up\r\n\r\nMeaningful biceps demand in addition to latissimus and other pulling musculature.\r\n\r\n### Neutral-Grip Pull-Up\r\n\r\nBiceps remains important, but the distribution shifts somewhat toward brachialis and brachioradialis.\r\n\r\n### Pronated Pull-Up\r\n\r\nBiceps contribution remains non-zero, but its mechanical advantage as a supinator is not exploited.\r\n\r\n## Biarticular Adjustment\r\n\r\nExercise modelling should account for shoulder position.\r\n\r\nThe same elbow angle does not imply the same biceps fiber length if shoulder position differs.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n- Bayesian curl;\r\n- standing curl;\r\n- preacher curl\r\n\r\nshould not receive identical length-tension modifiers even at the same elbow angle.\r\n\r\n## Resistance Profile Adjustment\r\n\r\nLength-tension state alone is insufficient.\r\n\r\nExternal torque must also be considered.\r\n\r\nA muscle can be maximally lengthened while external resistance is minimal.\r\n\r\nTherefore an exercise-level biceps stimulus should ideally combine:\r\n\r\n- muscle length;\r\n- elbow moment requirement;\r\n- forearm orientation;\r\n- shoulder position;\r\n- resistance profile;\r\n- stability;\r\n- active ROM.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **gross anatomy:** high;\r\n- **musculocutaneous innervation:** high;\r\n- **elbow-flexion function:** high;\r\n- **supination function:** high;\r\n- **biarticular shoulder influence:** high;\r\n- **functional differentiation between heads:** moderate to high;\r\n- **benefit of loading extended-elbow regions for regional hypertrophy:** direct evidence exists;\r\n- **general long-muscle-length hypertrophy advantage:** increasingly well supported;\r\n- **precise exercise-specific long-head versus short-head hypertrophy:** still insufficient for rigid isolation rules.\r\n\r\nThe most useful model is therefore not:\r\n\r\n> long-head exercise versus short-head exercise.\r\n\r\nIt is:\r\n\r\n> **a biarticular elbow flexor and powerful supinator whose recruitment and regional stimulus depend on forearm rotation, elbow angle, shoulder position and the external resistance profile.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/BicepsBrachii}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	12.25	0.00	1.000	39.81	39.81
4	deltoid_anterior	Musculus Deltoid anterior	Upper	Shoulder	340.00	321.97	10.00	Multipenate	0.400	0.600	medium	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\nThe **anterior deltoid** is the clavicular portion of the deltoid muscle and forms the anterior component of the rounded shoulder contour.\r\n\r\nThe deltoid as a whole is not a single mechanically homogeneous actuator. Its anterior, lateral and posterior regions possess different:\r\n\r\n- origins;\r\n- fiber directions;\r\n- moment arms;\r\n- preferred movement planes;\r\n- recruitment patterns.\r\n\r\n`deltoid_anterior` should therefore be treated independently from:\r\n\r\n- `deltoid_lateral`;\r\n- `deltoid_posterior`.\r\n\r\nThe anterior deltoid's principal mechanical identity is as a **glenohumeral flexor**, with important secondary contributions to:\r\n\r\n- horizontal flexion/adduction;\r\n- humeral elevation in anterior planes;\r\n- abduction depending on joint angle;\r\n- internal rotation;\r\n- stabilization of the humeral head during pressing and elevation.\r\n\r\nIt is heavily recruited during many pressing movements.\r\n\r\nThis creates an important programming consequence:\r\n\r\n> anterior deltoid often receives substantial hypertrophic exposure without any dedicated anterior-raise exercise.\r\n\r\nFor many resistance-trained lifters, pressing volume rather than insufficient isolation work is the primary determinant of anterior-deltoid workload.\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\n## Origin\r\n\r\nThe anterior deltoid originates from the **anterior border and superior surface of the lateral portion of the clavicle**, classically approximately the lateral third.\r\n\r\nThis clavicular origin distinguishes it from:\r\n\r\n- the acromial origin of the lateral deltoid;\r\n- the scapular-spine origin of the posterior deltoid.\r\n\r\nIts fibers descend laterally and somewhat posteriorly toward the humerus.\r\n\r\n## Insertion\r\n\r\nAnterior deltoid fibers converge with the rest of the deltoid onto the **deltoid tuberosity of the humerus**.\r\n\r\nThe common insertion allows the different deltoid regions to generate different rotational moments around the glenohumeral joint according to their individual fiber trajectories.\r\n\r\n## Architecture\r\n\r\nThe deltoid has a complex multipennate organization rather than behaving as one simple triangular muscle.\r\n\r\nAnterior deltoid fibers constitute multiple functional segments with differing lines of pull.\r\n\r\nThis means even the label `deltoid_anterior` is itself a useful simplification.\r\n\r\nDifferent anterior fascicles can contribute differently as:\r\n\r\n- humeral elevation angle changes;\r\n- movement plane changes;\r\n- humeral rotation changes.\r\n\r\nFor exercise-level modelling, however, aggregating the clavicular segments into one anterior-deltoid unit is reasonable.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nThe anterior deltoid is innervated by the **axillary nerve**, primarily from spinal roots **C5-C6**.\r\n\r\nThe axillary nerve passes through the quadrangular space and supplies:\r\n\r\n- deltoid;\r\n- teres minor.\r\n\r\nMotor branches enter different regions of the deltoid, allowing some degree of regional recruitment rather than forcing the entire muscle to operate as a single unit.\r\n\r\nThe nerve also provides cutaneous sensation to the lateral shoulder through its superior lateral cutaneous branch.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Shoulder Flexion\r\n\r\nThe primary function of the anterior deltoid is **glenohumeral flexion**.\r\n\r\nIt contributes strongly when the humerus moves anteriorly and superiorly relative to the torso.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- front raise;\r\n- overhead press;\r\n- incline pressing;\r\n- many forward-reaching tasks.\r\n\r\nIts flexion moment arm depends on shoulder angle.\r\n\r\nTherefore anterior-deltoid torque capacity should not be treated as constant throughout the entire elevation ROM.\r\n\r\n## Anterior-Plane Elevation\r\n\r\nThe anterior deltoid is particularly effective when humeral elevation occurs in a more anterior plane.\r\n\r\nBiomechanical studies demonstrate substantial regional differences within the deltoid, with anterior portions possessing larger moment arms in anterior planes than the middle deltoid. :contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}\r\n\r\nThis is why the distinction between:\r\n\r\n- sagittal-plane flexion;\r\n- scapular-plane elevation;\r\n- pure coronal abduction\r\n\r\nmatters.\r\n\r\nThe anterior deltoid can contribute to all of them, but not equally.\r\n\r\n## Shoulder Abduction\r\n\r\nAnterior deltoid can contribute to **glenohumeral abduction**, especially as humeral elevation increases.\r\n\r\nIts abduction moment arm is joint-angle dependent.\r\n\r\nEvidence synthesized from anatomical moment-arm studies shows that anterior-deltoid abduction leverage tends to increase at higher abduction angles. :contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}\r\n\r\nThis means a simplistic rule such as:\r\n\r\n> anterior deltoid = flexion only\r\n\r\nis incorrect.\r\n\r\nHowever, it is also incorrect to treat it as equivalent to the lateral deltoid for pure coronal abduction.\r\n\r\nThe relative contribution depends on:\r\n\r\n- elevation plane;\r\n- humeral angle;\r\n- humeral rotation;\r\n- scapular orientation.\r\n\r\n## Horizontal Flexion\r\n\r\nAnterior deltoid contributes strongly to **horizontal flexion**, also called horizontal adduction in many resistance-training contexts.\r\n\r\nThis action is relevant during:\r\n\r\n- bench pressing;\r\n- incline pressing;\r\n- fly-like movements;\r\n- forward reaching across the body.\r\n\r\nIts contribution overlaps substantially with:\r\n\r\n- pectoralis major clavicular fibers;\r\n- pectoralis major sternal fibers depending on angle.\r\n\r\nThe relative contribution changes with humeral elevation and exercise geometry.\r\n\r\n## Internal Rotation\r\n\r\nAnterior deltoid can contribute to **internal rotation of the humerus**.\r\n\r\nThis is generally a secondary function compared with flexion and elevation.\r\n\r\nInternal-rotation torque is shared with muscles such as:\r\n\r\n- subscapularis;\r\n- pectoralis major;\r\n- latissimus dorsi;\r\n- teres major.\r\n\r\nTherefore it should not be treated as a primary anterior-deltoid training target.\r\n\r\n## Glenohumeral Stabilization\r\n\r\nThe deltoid generates substantial forces across the glenohumeral joint.\r\n\r\nEspecially at lower elevation angles, some deltoid force is directed superiorly rather than purely rotationally.\r\n\r\nThe rotator cuff counteracts and redirects these translational components, helping keep the humeral head centered within the glenoid.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n> anterior-deltoid force production depends mechanically on coordinated rotator-cuff stabilization.\r\n\r\nThis is particularly important during loaded elevation and pressing.\r\n\r\nShoulder muscle function is therefore a force-couple problem rather than an isolated-muscle problem. :contentReference[oaicite:3]{index=3}\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nDirect evidence specifically examining **stretch-mediated hypertrophy of the anterior deltoid** is limited.\r\n\r\nThe mechanical principles are clearer than the direct longitudinal evidence.\r\n\r\nBecause the anterior deltoid originates anteriorly on the clavicle and inserts on the humerus, moving the humerus posteriorly relative to the torso — **shoulder extension** — lengthens the anterior region.\r\n\r\nTherefore positions such as:\r\n\r\n- the bottom of certain pressing movements;\r\n- shoulder extension behind the torso;\r\n- deep horizontal extension during fly or press variants\r\n\r\ncan place the anterior deltoid at relatively long lengths.\r\n\r\nHowever, muscle length alone is insufficient.\r\n\r\nA productive long-length stimulus requires meaningful active tension at that position.\r\n\r\n## Pressing at Long Muscle Length\r\n\r\nIn a pressing exercise, the bottom position often combines:\r\n\r\n- shoulder extension or horizontal extension;\r\n- high external torque;\r\n- substantial anterior-deltoid force requirement.\r\n\r\nThis can create a plausible long-length hypertrophic stimulus.\r\n\r\nThe exact contribution depends strongly on pressing angle.\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n\r\n- flat press;\r\n- incline press;\r\n- high-incline press;\r\n- overhead press\r\n\r\ndo not place identical demands on the anterior deltoid.\r\n\r\nAs the pressing plane becomes more vertically oriented, shoulder-flexion/elevation demand generally becomes increasingly relevant.\r\n\r\n## Front Raises\r\n\r\nA conventional free-weight front raise typically produces relatively little external shoulder torque when the arm hangs vertically at the bottom.\r\n\r\nTherefore the anterior deltoid may be relatively long or intermediate in length while external resistance is very low.\r\n\r\nTorque then increases as the arm moves away from vertical.\r\n\r\nThis makes dumbbell front raises less compelling as a pure long-length-loading exercise.\r\n\r\nCable geometry can change this substantially.\r\n\r\nA cable positioned behind the body can maintain resistance while the humerus begins in extension, producing a more explicit long-length anterior-deltoid stimulus.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Interpretation\r\n\r\nThere is currently not enough anterior-deltoid-specific longitudinal evidence to claim a precise hypertrophic superiority of training in shoulder extension.\r\n\r\nTherefore the defensible model is:\r\n\r\n- shoulder extension increases anterior-deltoid length;\r\n- meaningful external torque in that state is mechanically attractive;\r\n- pressing already exposes the muscle to substantial loaded ROM;\r\n- direct anterior-deltoid SMH magnitude remains uncertain.\r\n\r\nThe `smh_factor` should therefore reflect a modelling judgment rather than a direct experimentally derived coefficient.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Pressing Is Major Anterior-Deltoid Training\r\n\r\nAnterior deltoid receives substantial stimulus during most pressing exercises.\r\n\r\nHigh-value examples include:\r\n\r\n- overhead press;\r\n- incline press;\r\n- flat bench press;\r\n- machine shoulder press;\r\n- Smith incline press;\r\n- weighted dips depending on torso and shoulder geometry.\r\n\r\nEMG comparisons consistently show high anterior-deltoid involvement during shoulder pressing, and pressing often produces greater anterior-deltoid activation than chest-isolation movements. :contentReference[oaicite:4]{index=4}\r\n\r\nTherefore direct front raises are often optional.\r\n\r\n## Overhead Press\r\n\r\nOverhead pressing imposes large humeral-elevation demands.\r\n\r\nAnterior and lateral deltoid both contribute substantially.\r\n\r\nThe relative distribution depends on:\r\n\r\n- humeral plane;\r\n- grip width;\r\n- elbow path;\r\n- torso inclination;\r\n- machine path.\r\n\r\nA press performed slightly in the scapular plane generally distributes demand across anterior and lateral deltoid rather than isolating one region.\r\n\r\n## Incline Press\r\n\r\nAs bench inclination increases, the exercise progressively changes from primarily horizontal pressing toward more shoulder flexion/elevation.\r\n\r\nAnterior-deltoid contribution generally increases as the pressing angle becomes steeper, although the relationship is not perfectly linear and load capacity changes simultaneously.\r\n\r\nStudies comparing bench angles show meaningful differences in anterior-deltoid activation across inclinations. :contentReference[oaicite:5]{index=5}\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n- flat bench → substantial but more chest-dominant;\r\n- moderate incline → larger anterior-deltoid role;\r\n- steep incline → increasingly shoulder-dominant.\r\n\r\n## Flat Bench Press\r\n\r\nFlat bench pressing still produces substantial anterior-deltoid demand because the movement contains:\r\n\r\n- humeral horizontal flexion;\r\n- some flexion depending on elbow path and thoracic geometry.\r\n\r\nHowever, pectoralis major usually represents a larger prime mover than in high-incline pressing.\r\n\r\nAnterior deltoid should therefore receive meaningful but not dominant demand allocation.\r\n\r\n## Weighted Dip\r\n\r\nDips involve substantial shoulder extension at the bottom.\r\n\r\nDuring ascent, anterior shoulder musculature contributes to returning the humerus toward neutral/flexion.\r\n\r\nThe exact anterior-deltoid stimulus depends strongly on:\r\n\r\n- torso inclination;\r\n- depth;\r\n- elbow path;\r\n- shoulder anatomy.\r\n\r\nThe deep bottom position can create large anterior-shoulder demands.\r\n\r\nThis does not mean deeper is always better.\r\n\r\nExcessive shoulder extension can become limited by:\r\n\r\n- passive anterior structures;\r\n- discomfort;\r\n- individual glenohumeral tolerance.\r\n\r\nUse the deepest controlled ROM that remains comfortable.\r\n\r\n## Front Raise\r\n\r\nFront raises directly train shoulder flexion.\r\n\r\nThey are mechanically simple and easy to interpret.\r\n\r\nPotential resistance sources include:\r\n\r\n- dumbbell;\r\n- cable;\r\n- plate;\r\n- machine.\r\n\r\nThe main disadvantage is programming redundancy.\r\n\r\nIf substantial:\r\n\r\n- incline pressing;\r\n- overhead pressing;\r\n- bench pressing\r\n\r\nis already present, the anterior deltoid may already receive more than enough weekly volume.\r\n\r\nDirect front raises are therefore most useful when:\r\n\r\n- pressing volume is low;\r\n- anterior deltoid is specifically underdeveloped;\r\n- shoulder flexion strength is a priority;\r\n- a low-triceps-cost shoulder exercise is desired.\r\n\r\n## Cable Front Raise\r\n\r\nCable geometry can be used to reshape the strength curve.\r\n\r\nA cable originating behind the body can provide resistance from a more extended shoulder position.\r\n\r\nThis may produce:\r\n\r\n- greater long-length loading;\r\n- smoother torque across ROM;\r\n- less reliance on momentum.\r\n\r\nFor direct anterior-deltoid hypertrophy, this is often mechanically more interesting than a conventional dumbbell raise.\r\n\r\n## Lateral Raise\r\n\r\nAnterior deltoid contributes to many lateral-raise variants, especially when:\r\n\r\n- the humerus travels anterior to the frontal plane;\r\n- the arm is internally positioned;\r\n- elevation becomes high.\r\n\r\nHowever, lateral deltoid should remain the primary target in a well-designed lateral raise.\r\n\r\nA slight scapular-plane trajectory is normal and does not automatically convert the exercise into a front raise.\r\n\r\n## Internal Rotation Is Not a Training Priority\r\n\r\nAlthough anterior deltoid contributes to internal rotation, dedicated internal-rotation exercises are generally more useful for assessing or training the rotator cuff system than for hypertrophying anterior deltoid.\r\n\r\nThere is no practical reason to add internal rotation merely to "complete" anterior-deltoid function.\r\n\r\nPressing and flexion movements already load the muscle effectively.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Count Indirect Volume Seriously\r\n\r\nAnterior deltoid is one of the clearest examples where **indirect compound volume can dominate total hypertrophic exposure**.\r\n\r\nExercises that should count meaningfully include:\r\n\r\n- overhead presses;\r\n- incline presses;\r\n- flat bench presses;\r\n- dips;\r\n- some machine chest presses.\r\n\r\nTherefore direct anterior-deltoid volume should be prescribed only after evaluating existing pressing.\r\n\r\nA lifter performing substantial push training may require:\r\n\r\n> **zero dedicated anterior-raise sets.**\r\n\r\n## Weekly Volume\r\n\r\nIf pressing volume is already high, no additional direct work may be necessary.\r\n\r\nIf anterior deltoid is specifically prioritized or pressing exposure is low, approximately:\r\n\r\n- **2-6 direct sets per week**\r\n\r\ncan be added initially.\r\n\r\nThis is a programming heuristic rather than a physiological threshold.\r\n\r\nThe key is total effective exposure from all exercises.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nAnterior deltoid is frequently trained indirectly multiple times per week.\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n\r\n- push-strength session;\r\n- push-hypertrophy session;\r\n- incline chest work;\r\n- overhead work.\r\n\r\nThus explicit frequency often emerges naturally.\r\n\r\nTwo or three weekly exposures are common without any dedicated deltoid scheduling.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\n### Presses\r\n\r\nUseful ranges include:\r\n\r\n- 5-8 reps for heavier strength-oriented work;\r\n- 6-12 reps for general hypertrophy;\r\n- 10-20 reps on stable machines.\r\n\r\n### Front Raises\r\n\r\nModerate-to-high repetitions are usually more practical:\r\n\r\n- 8-15 reps;\r\n- 12-20 reps;\r\n- occasionally 20-30 reps.\r\n\r\nVery heavy front raises often encourage:\r\n\r\n- trunk extension;\r\n- momentum;\r\n- scapular compensation.\r\n\r\nThey offer little unique hypertrophic advantage.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nStable isolation exercises can be trained very close to failure:\r\n\r\n- approximately 0-2 RIR.\r\n\r\nHeavy compound presses generally benefit from somewhat more reserve because technical and systemic cost increases.\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n\r\n- machine front raise → 0-2 RIR;\r\n- machine shoulder press → 0-2 RIR can be practical;\r\n- free-weight overhead press → often 1-3 RIR;\r\n- heavy bench press → usually retain more technical reserve.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Order\r\n\r\nIf anterior deltoid is a major priority, placing an overhead or incline press earlier in the session is usually sufficient.\r\n\r\nThere is rarely a need to pre-fatigue it with front raises.\r\n\r\nPre-fatigue can reduce:\r\n\r\n- pressing load;\r\n- triceps performance;\r\n- overall compound output.\r\n\r\nIf front raises are included, they generally fit well after primary pressing.\r\n\r\n## Redundancy Management\r\n\r\nA push session containing:\r\n\r\n- incline press;\r\n- overhead press;\r\n- flat press;\r\n- front raise\r\n\r\ncan easily contain excessive anterior-deltoid redundancy.\r\n\r\nThe muscle does not care that the exercises have different names if they impose very similar shoulder-flexion/elevation demands.\r\n\r\nProgramming should therefore track **mechanical overlap**, not exercise count alone.\r\n\r\n## Relationship With Chest Volume\r\n\r\nHigh chest pressing volume often becomes de facto anterior-deltoid volume.\r\n\r\nThis matters particularly when increasing incline angle.\r\n\r\nA program can unintentionally shift from chest-focused to anterior-deltoid-focused without changing total number of sets.\r\n\r\nThis can influence:\r\n\r\n- shoulder recovery;\r\n- subsequent lateral-delt performance;\r\n- triceps performance;\r\n- pressing frequency.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## The Deltoid Is Not Really Three Muscles\r\n\r\nThe familiar division into:\r\n\r\n- anterior;\r\n- lateral;\r\n- posterior\r\n\r\nis useful but simplified.\r\n\r\nAnatomical and EMG studies show multiple intramuscular segments with distinct recruitment and mechanical lines of action.\r\n\r\nThe deltoid is better understood as a **multi-segment shoulder actuator** than as three perfectly uniform compartments. :contentReference[oaicite:6]{index=6}\r\n\r\n## The Anterior Deltoid Can Abduct\r\n\r\nDespite being routinely taught as a shoulder flexor, the anterior deltoid can generate meaningful abduction torque.\r\n\r\nIts abduction leverage changes with shoulder angle and tends to become more favorable at higher humeral elevations. :contentReference[oaicite:7]{index=7}\r\n\r\nThis illustrates why muscle actions cannot be assigned from static textbook diagrams alone.\r\n\r\n## Pressing Can Be Enough\r\n\r\nAnterior deltoid is unusual among commonly isolated bodybuilding muscles because dedicated isolation often adds little that is missing from a press-heavy program.\r\n\r\nA lifter may perform no front raises whatsoever while still accumulating substantial anterior-deltoid stimulus from pressing.\r\n\r\nThis contrasts strongly with lateral deltoid, which often receives less complete stimulation from conventional chest pressing.\r\n\r\n## Deltoid Force Requires Rotator-Cuff Cooperation\r\n\r\nAt lower elevation angles, deltoid contraction contains a substantial superior translational component.\r\n\r\nThe rotator cuff helps stabilize and center the humeral head while allowing useful rotation.\r\n\r\nThus even a seemingly simple front raise depends on coordinated force sharing across the entire shoulder complex. :contentReference[oaicite:8]{index=8}\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\n`deltoid_anterior` should primarily receive demand from:\r\n\r\n1. **shoulder flexion/elevation in anterior planes**;\r\n2. **horizontal flexion**;\r\n3. **some abduction depending on plane and angle**;\r\n4. minor internal-rotation and stabilization demand.\r\n\r\nIt should not be modeled as a pure one-axis flexor.\r\n\r\n## Plane of Elevation Matters\r\n\r\nA useful conceptual continuum is:\r\n\r\n`frontal plane → scapular plane → sagittal plane`\r\n\r\nAs movement shifts anteriorly along this continuum:\r\n\r\n- `deltoid_lateral` contribution tends to lose relative dominance;\r\n- `deltoid_anterior` becomes increasingly relevant.\r\n\r\nThe transition is continuous rather than categorical.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Allocation\r\n\r\n### Seated Dumbbell Overhead Press\r\n\r\nHigh `deltoid_anterior` demand.\r\n\r\nAlso high:\r\n\r\n- `deltoid_lateral`;\r\n- triceps;\r\n- rotator-cuff stabilization.\r\n\r\n### Arnold Press\r\n\r\nHigh anterior-deltoid demand.\r\n\r\nThe rotational and anterior arm path often keeps the movement substantially within anterior/scapular elevation.\r\n\r\nIt should not be treated as uniquely capable of targeting the anterior deltoid, but it is mechanically appropriate for substantial recruitment.\r\n\r\n### Smith Machine Incline Bench Press\r\n\r\nSubstantial anterior-deltoid demand.\r\n\r\nAllocation should increase with:\r\n\r\n- steeper inclination;\r\n- more anterior shoulder-elevation requirement.\r\n\r\n### Plate-Loaded Horizontal Chest Press\r\n\r\nModerate anterior-deltoid demand.\r\n\r\nPectoralis major should generally remain dominant if machine geometry is truly horizontal.\r\n\r\n### Weighted Forward-Lean Dip\r\n\r\nMeaningful anterior-deltoid demand, especially during the transition from deep shoulder extension.\r\n\r\nHowever, pectoralis major and triceps remain major contributors.\r\n\r\n### Single-Arm Cable Lateral Raise\r\n\r\nNon-zero anterior-deltoid demand if the arm travels in the scapular plane.\r\n\r\nThe lateral deltoid should remain the primary allocation unless the movement becomes excessively anterior.\r\n\r\n### Front Raise\r\n\r\nVery high anterior-deltoid specificity.\r\n\r\nLow triceps demand.\r\n\r\nPotentially useful when direct anterior-deltoid work is actually needed.\r\n\r\n## Length-Tension Model\r\n\r\nA simplified anterior-deltoid `E_LT` model should depend mainly on humeral flexion/extension angle:\r\n\r\n- shoulder extension → relatively long;\r\n- neutral → intermediate;\r\n- substantial shoulder flexion → shorter.\r\n\r\nHowever, because deltoid geometry changes with:\r\n\r\n- abduction;\r\n- axial rotation;\r\n- scapular motion;\r\n\r\na single sagittal angle is only an approximation.\r\n\r\nFor most Agonez exercises this simplification is still adequate.\r\n\r\n## Scapulohumeral Interaction\r\n\r\nThe deltoid acts directly at the glenohumeral joint, but overhead arm elevation also requires scapular motion.\r\n\r\nTherefore a global "arm elevation angle" is not identical to glenohumeral flexion angle.\r\n\r\nAt high elevation:\r\n\r\n- scapular upward rotation;\r\n- posterior tilt;\r\n- clavicular motion\r\n\r\ncontribute substantially.\r\n\r\nFor a simplified exercise model, global humeral elevation can serve as a proxy, but biomechanically the distinction should remain explicit.\r\n\r\n## Rotator-Cuff Cost\r\n\r\nHigh deltoid force should imply some non-zero `rotator_cuffs` stabilization demand.\r\n\r\nThis is particularly relevant when the deltoid produces a large superiorly directed component of force.\r\n\r\nTherefore an exercise vector containing very high anterior-deltoid demand and exactly zero cuff stabilization would usually be mechanically suspicious.\r\n\r\n## Programming Interpretation\r\n\r\nFor an advanced lifter with multiple weekly presses, `deltoid_anterior` is more likely to face **redundant volume** than insufficient exposure.\r\n\r\nBefore adding direct work, inspect:\r\n\r\n- total horizontal press sets;\r\n- incline press sets;\r\n- overhead press sets;\r\n- dip sets;\r\n- their proximity to failure.\r\n\r\nThis muscle should therefore be one of the cases where Agonez distinguishes:\r\n\r\n> direct isolation volume\r\n\r\nfrom:\r\n\r\n> effective muscle-level volume accumulated across compounds.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nEvidence for anterior-deltoid-specific stretch-mediated hypertrophy is currently limited.\r\n\r\nThe defensible model is:\r\n\r\n- shoulder extension lengthens the anterior deltoid;\r\n- exercises can impose meaningful force in that state;\r\n- cable geometry can intentionally increase long-length loading;\r\n- conventional pressing already supplies substantial loaded ROM;\r\n- exact anterior-deltoid SMH magnitude remains uncertain.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **clavicular origin and humeral insertion:** high;\r\n- **axillary-nerve innervation:** high;\r\n- **shoulder-flexion role:** high;\r\n- **horizontal-flexion contribution:** high;\r\n- **angle-dependent abduction role:** directly supported biomechanically;\r\n- **high activation during shoulder pressing:** directly supported;\r\n- **bench-angle-dependent recruitment:** supported experimentally;\r\n- **pressing-induced hypertrophy specifically isolated to anterior deltoid:** less extensively studied than acute recruitment;\r\n- **stretch-mediated hypertrophy:** limited direct evidence.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **anterior deltoid = the clavicular deltoid region specialized for anterior-plane humeral elevation and flexion, with substantial horizontal-flexion and angle-dependent abduction roles, and with a hypertrophic workload that is often dominated by compound pressing rather than direct isolation.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/DeltoidAnterior}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	9.80	22.00	0.927	32.85	30.46
1	pectoralis_major_clavicular	Musculus pectoralis major clavicular head	Upper	Chest	215.00	203.60	13.00	Converged/Fan-shaped	0.400	0.600	high	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\nThe **clavicular head of pectoralis major** forms the superior portion of the pectoralis major muscle and is commonly referred to as the **upper chest**.\r\n\r\nIt should be distinguished mechanically from the sternocostal portion of pectoralis major.\r\n\r\nBoth regions contribute strongly to pressing and horizontal adduction, but the clavicular region has a particularly important role in:\r\n\r\n- **shoulder flexion**;\r\n- horizontal adduction;\r\n- internal rotation;\r\n- adduction from elevated arm positions;\r\n- stabilization of the humerus during pressing.\r\n\r\nThe clavicular fibers originate superiorly from the clavicle and run inferolaterally toward the proximal humerus.\r\n\r\nThis line of action gives them greater sagittal-plane flexion leverage than the lower sternocostal fibers.\r\n\r\nTherefore the clavicular head is particularly relevant during exercises in which the humerus moves:\r\n\r\n- forward;\r\n- upward;\r\n- medially across the body.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- incline bench press;\r\n- low-to-high cable fly;\r\n- incline machine press;\r\n- some shoulder-flexion movements.\r\n\r\nFor exercise modelling, the most important principle is:\r\n\r\n> **clavicular pectoralis major is not simply "the pec used on incline bench"; it is a humeral flexor and horizontal adductor whose contribution depends continuously on arm elevation and resistance direction.**\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\n## Origin\r\n\r\nThe clavicular head originates primarily from the **anterior surface of the medial portion of the clavicle**.\r\n\r\nIts fibers travel laterally and inferiorly toward the humerus.\r\n\r\nThis orientation differs substantially from the more horizontal and inferolateral fiber directions of the sternocostal portion.\r\n\r\nThe difference in fiber orientation is the anatomical basis for regional functional specialization within pectoralis major.\r\n\r\n## Insertion\r\n\r\nThe clavicular fibers converge with the remaining pectoralis-major fibers into a common tendon inserting on the proximal humerus at the **lateral lip of the intertubercular sulcus / crest of the greater tubercle region**.\r\n\r\nThe pectoralis-major tendon has a complex twisted arrangement.\r\n\r\nFibers originating from different chest regions do not simply run in parallel from origin to insertion.\r\n\r\nThis twisting affects:\r\n\r\n- fiber excursion;\r\n- regional length changes;\r\n- relative leverage across shoulder positions.\r\n\r\n## Regional Architecture\r\n\r\nPectoralis major is a broad fan-shaped muscle with substantial regional heterogeneity.\r\n\r\nThe clavicular region should therefore not be regarded as merely the top few centimeters of one uniform muscle sheet.\r\n\r\nIts fibers differ from sternocostal fibers in:\r\n\r\n- orientation;\r\n- proximal attachment;\r\n- moment arms;\r\n- regional activation during different movements.\r\n\r\nThis makes the current database separation into:\r\n\r\n- `pectoralis_major_clavicular`;\r\n- `pectoralis_major_sternal`\r\n\r\nbiomechanically meaningful.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nThe clavicular portion of pectoralis major is innervated predominantly through the **lateral pectoral nerve**, with contributions from the brachial plexus primarily around:\r\n\r\n- C5;\r\n- C6;\r\n- C7 depending on individual anatomy.\r\n\r\nThe medial pectoral nerve can also contribute to the pectoralis major more broadly.\r\n\r\nRegional innervation supports some degree of differential activation between clavicular and sternocostal portions.\r\n\r\nThis does not imply complete neural isolation.\r\n\r\nBoth regions commonly co-contract during pressing movements.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Shoulder Flexion\r\n\r\nThe clavicular head is an important **shoulder flexor**.\r\n\r\nIt contributes to moving the humerus anteriorly in the sagittal plane.\r\n\r\nThis is one of the major distinctions from the sternocostal portion.\r\n\r\nBiomechanical moment-arm measurements show that pectoralis-major regions can generate substantial shoulder-flexion leverage and that this leverage changes as humeral elevation changes. :contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}\r\n\r\nThe clavicular fibers are particularly well oriented for flexion when the arm begins below or near shoulder height.\r\n\r\nThis explains their strong involvement in:\r\n\r\n- incline pressing;\r\n- low-to-high cable fly trajectories;\r\n- certain front-raise-like motions.\r\n\r\n## Horizontal Adduction\r\n\r\nLike the rest of pectoralis major, the clavicular head contributes strongly to **horizontal adduction**.\r\n\r\nThis means moving the humerus medially across the torso in the transverse plane.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- bench pressing;\r\n- cable fly;\r\n- pec-deck movements.\r\n\r\nThe relative clavicular contribution depends on the vertical direction of the humeral path.\r\n\r\nAn upward-directed horizontal-adduction path generally increases clavicular involvement relative to a downward-directed path.\r\n\r\n## Internal Rotation\r\n\r\nThe clavicular head also contributes to **internal rotation of the humerus**.\r\n\r\nThis function is shared with:\r\n\r\n- sternocostal pectoralis major;\r\n- latissimus dorsi;\r\n- teres major;\r\n- subscapularis;\r\n- anterior deltoid to a smaller degree.\r\n\r\nInternal rotation is therefore a genuine function but not the most useful hypertrophy-targeting action.\r\n\r\n## Adduction\r\n\r\nThe clavicular fibers can contribute to humeral adduction depending on arm position.\r\n\r\nTheir adduction leverage is not constant.\r\n\r\nAs the humerus moves through elevation, the relationship between the fiber line of pull and joint axis changes.\r\n\r\nTherefore pectoralis-major actions should be treated as angle-dependent rather than static labels.\r\n\r\n## Relationship With Anterior Deltoid\r\n\r\nClavicular pectoralis major and anterior deltoid overlap strongly in **shoulder flexion**.\r\n\r\nTheir relative contribution depends on:\r\n\r\n- humeral angle;\r\n- resistance direction;\r\n- bench inclination;\r\n- grip;\r\n- elbow position.\r\n\r\nThis overlap is especially important during incline pressing.\r\n\r\nAs the bench angle increases, the exercise generally shifts progressively from:\r\n\r\n- horizontal adduction-dominant pressing\r\n\r\ntoward:\r\n\r\n- shoulder flexion/elevation-dominant pressing.\r\n\r\nAt higher inclines, anterior deltoid contribution becomes increasingly important. Acute studies show greater clavicular-pec activation with incline configurations in some conditions, but increasing incline also increases anterior-deltoid involvement. :contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nDirect evidence isolating **stretch-mediated hypertrophy of the clavicular pectoralis major** is limited.\r\n\r\nHowever, the mechanical basis for long-length training is clear.\r\n\r\nThe clavicular fibers are lengthened when the humerus moves:\r\n\r\n- posteriorly relative to the clavicle;\r\n- into shoulder extension;\r\n- into horizontal abduction.\r\n\r\nThus the bottom of a press or fly can place the clavicular pec at relatively long length.\r\n\r\n## Incline Press Bottom Position\r\n\r\nDuring an incline press, lowering the upper arm behind or approximately level with the torso creates:\r\n\r\n- shoulder extension relative to the pressing plane;\r\n- horizontal abduction;\r\n- substantial clavicular-pec lengthening.\r\n\r\nIf meaningful external torque remains present in this position, the muscle can experience high tension at long length.\r\n\r\nThis is mechanically attractive for hypertrophy.\r\n\r\n## Low-to-High Cable Fly\r\n\r\nA properly configured low-to-high cable fly can create a particularly useful clavicular-pec loading pattern.\r\n\r\nThe humerus begins:\r\n\r\n- relatively extended or horizontally abducted;\r\n- below the final line of movement.\r\n\r\nThe clavicular fibers then shorten through a combination of:\r\n\r\n- shoulder flexion;\r\n- horizontal adduction.\r\n\r\nThis closely matches their line of action.\r\n\r\n## Dumbbell Incline Fly\r\n\r\nAn incline dumbbell fly can also lengthen the clavicular pec substantially in the bottom position.\r\n\r\nHowever, gravitational torque depends on arm orientation.\r\n\r\nAs with other free-weight flys, the resistance profile is strongest where the arm becomes more perpendicular to gravity.\r\n\r\nThe exercise can therefore be highly lengthened-biased if the bottom position remains loaded.\r\n\r\n## Excessive Shoulder Extension\r\n\r\nMore stretch is not automatically better.\r\n\r\nForcing the humerus excessively behind the torso can increase stress on:\r\n\r\n- anterior shoulder structures;\r\n- pectoralis tendon;\r\n- long-head biceps region.\r\n\r\nA productive long-length position should remain controlled and compatible with individual shoulder anatomy.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Interpretation\r\n\r\nThe defensible conclusion is:\r\n\r\n- clavicular pec is lengthened by shoulder extension/horizontal abduction;\r\n- loaded bottom positions in incline pressing and fly variations can create high long-length tension;\r\n- direct clavicular-head-specific SMH evidence remains limited;\r\n- high-quality loaded ROM is more defensible than extreme stretching.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Incline Bench Press\r\n\r\nIncline pressing is the most common compound strategy for emphasizing the clavicular pectoralis major.\r\n\r\nAcute EMG studies generally show that moderate incline angles can increase clavicular-region activation relative to flat pressing, although results depend on:\r\n\r\n- exact bench angle;\r\n- load;\r\n- electrode placement;\r\n- barbell vs dumbbell;\r\n- individual technique. :contentReference[oaicite:3]{index=3}\r\n\r\nThe important practical point is:\r\n\r\n> incline is a continuum, not a binary upper-chest switch.\r\n\r\nAs bench angle rises:\r\n\r\n- clavicular pec contribution can increase initially;\r\n- anterior-deltoid contribution also increases;\r\n- the movement gradually becomes more shoulder-press-like.\r\n\r\n## Moderate Incline\r\n\r\nModerate inclines are usually mechanically attractive because they preserve substantial:\r\n\r\n- horizontal adduction;\r\n- shoulder flexion;\r\n- pressing load.\r\n\r\nVery steep inclines can reduce pec specificity by shifting more demand toward anterior deltoid.\r\n\r\nTherefore an incline in the approximate low-to-moderate range is often a useful compromise.\r\n\r\nExact optimal angle is individual and should not be treated as universal.\r\n\r\n## Smith Machine Incline Bench Press\r\n\r\nA Smith machine is particularly useful for clavicular-pec hypertrophy because it provides:\r\n\r\n- high stability;\r\n- easy progression;\r\n- controlled proximity to failure;\r\n- low balance demand.\r\n\r\nThe fixed bar path allows the trainee to choose torso and bench position so that the humerus follows a consistent flexion/horizontal-adduction trajectory.\r\n\r\nThis should generally receive high `pectoralis_major_clavicular` allocation.\r\n\r\n## Plate-Loaded Converging Incline Press\r\n\r\nA converging machine can be excellent because the handles may move:\r\n\r\n- upward;\r\n- medially.\r\n\r\nThis combines:\r\n\r\n- shoulder flexion;\r\n- horizontal adduction.\r\n\r\nThat trajectory is particularly compatible with clavicular-pec mechanics.\r\n\r\nMachine geometry still matters substantially.\r\n\r\nSome incline machines are effectively shoulder presses and shift much more demand toward anterior deltoid.\r\n\r\n## Flat Bench Press\r\n\r\nFlat pressing still trains the clavicular pectoralis major.\r\n\r\nThe clavicular region does not become inactive at 0° bench inclination.\r\n\r\nStudies comparing bench angles generally show substantial pectoralis-major activity during flat pressing, including upper regions. :contentReference[oaicite:4]{index=4}\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n> flat bench is not "lower chest only."\r\n\r\nIt simply changes relative regional emphasis.\r\n\r\n## Low-to-High Cable Fly\r\n\r\nA low-origin cable directed upward and medially is one of the most specific clavicular-pec isolation patterns.\r\n\r\nThe movement combines:\r\n\r\n- flexion;\r\n- horizontal adduction.\r\n\r\nAdvantages include:\r\n\r\n- low triceps contribution;\r\n- adjustable line of resistance;\r\n- potentially strong loading at long muscle lengths;\r\n- easy unilateral execution.\r\n\r\n## Single-Arm Low-to-High Cable Fly\r\n\r\nThe unilateral version allows the humerus to move farther across the torso without the hands colliding.\r\n\r\nThis can increase:\r\n\r\n- horizontal-adduction ROM;\r\n- freedom of scapular movement.\r\n\r\nThe torso should remain sufficiently stable that the exercise does not become primarily trunk rotation.\r\n\r\n## Pec Deck\r\n\r\nA conventional horizontal pec deck trains the clavicular region alongside the sternocostal region.\r\n\r\nIts exact regional bias depends on:\r\n\r\n- seat height;\r\n- elbow height;\r\n- machine arc.\r\n\r\nSetting the contact points somewhat lower relative to the shoulders can create a more upward humeral trajectory, potentially increasing clavicular contribution.\r\n\r\n## Dips\r\n\r\nForward-leaning dips usually place the humerus in an extension/adduction pattern that is less favorable for the clavicular region than for the sternocostal/lower pec.\r\n\r\nThe clavicular head still contributes, but should generally receive less allocation than sternocostal pec.\r\n\r\n## Shoulder Press\r\n\r\nOverhead pressing involves shoulder flexion/elevation but progressively less of the horizontal-adduction pattern in which the clavicular pec is highly effective.\r\n\r\nThe clavicular pec may contribute, especially in lower portions, but anterior deltoid should usually dominate the shoulder component.\r\n\r\nThis is why progressively increasing incline does not indefinitely increase "upper chest" specificity.\r\n\r\n## Elbow Path\r\n\r\nExcessively tucked elbows shift more of the movement toward sagittal shoulder flexion and triceps involvement.\r\n\r\nExcessively flared elbows increase horizontal abduction/adduction but may reduce shoulder comfort.\r\n\r\nA moderate elbow angle generally provides a productive combination of:\r\n\r\n- clavicular-pec tension;\r\n- sternocostal-pec tension;\r\n- anterior-deltoid contribution;\r\n- comfortable shoulder mechanics.\r\n\r\n## Grip Width\r\n\r\nGrip width changes both:\r\n\r\n- elbow-extension demand;\r\n- shoulder horizontal-adduction demand.\r\n\r\nA narrower grip generally increases triceps and shoulder-flexion demands.\r\n\r\nA wider grip increases horizontal-adduction leverage but reduces ROM.\r\n\r\nTherefore grip should not be treated as a simple clavicular-pec selector.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Count Incline Pressing Heavily\r\n\r\nIf a program contains substantial incline pressing, clavicular pectoralis major may already receive a large hypertrophic workload.\r\n\r\nDirect low-to-high fly work should therefore be added based on:\r\n\r\n- priority;\r\n- recovery;\r\n- total chest volume.\r\n\r\n## Weekly Volume\r\n\r\nA practical starting range for clavicular-pec-prioritized work can be approximately:\r\n\r\n- **4-8 hard sets per week with a clear clavicular bias**,\r\n\r\nwithin a larger total pectoralis-major program.\r\n\r\nThese could include:\r\n\r\n- incline presses;\r\n- low-to-high cable flys;\r\n- incline flys.\r\n\r\nFlat pressing should receive partial clavicular credit as well.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nTwo to three weekly exposures are practical.\r\n\r\nBecause clavicular pec overlaps strongly with anterior deltoid and triceps during pressing, recovery should be evaluated across the entire push system.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\n### Compound Presses\r\n\r\nUseful ranges include:\r\n\r\n- 5-10 reps;\r\n- 6-12 reps;\r\n- 8-15 reps.\r\n\r\n### Cable / Machine Flys\r\n\r\nUseful ranges include:\r\n\r\n- 10-20 reps;\r\n- 12-25 reps.\r\n\r\nThere is no special upper-chest repetition range.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nStable machine or Smith pressing can be trained relatively close to failure:\r\n\r\n- approximately 0-2 RIR.\r\n\r\nFree-weight barbell pressing often benefits from slightly more technical reserve.\r\n\r\nCable isolation work can also be taken close to local failure if shoulder position remains controlled.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Complementarity\r\n\r\nA useful clavicular-pec hypertrophy combination is:\r\n\r\n1. moderate-incline press;\r\n2. low-to-high cable fly.\r\n\r\nThe first provides:\r\n\r\n- high absolute force;\r\n- progressive overload.\r\n\r\nThe second provides:\r\n\r\n- greater isolation;\r\n- adjustable resistance direction;\r\n- minimal triceps limitation.\r\n\r\n## Avoid Redundant Incline Angles\r\n\r\nPerforming:\r\n\r\n- 15° incline;\r\n- 30° incline;\r\n- 45° incline;\r\n- incline machine;\r\n- incline dumbbell press\r\n\r\nin the same microcycle usually adds more redundancy than useful regional diversity.\r\n\r\nVariation should change something meaningful:\r\n\r\n- resistance profile;\r\n- stability;\r\n- ROM;\r\n- line of pull.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## "Upper Chest" Is a Real Anatomical Region\r\n\r\nThe distinction between clavicular and sternocostal pectoralis major is not purely bodybuilding terminology.\r\n\r\nThe regions possess different:\r\n\r\n- origins;\r\n- fiber orientations;\r\n- leverage;\r\n- recruitment patterns.\r\n\r\nTherefore regional targeting is mechanically plausible.\r\n\r\n## Incline Does Not Isolate the Clavicular Head\r\n\r\nEven when incline pressing increases clavicular-region activation, the movement still strongly involves:\r\n\r\n- sternocostal pec;\r\n- anterior deltoid;\r\n- triceps.\r\n\r\nThe correct concept is **regional bias**, not isolation.\r\n\r\n## More Incline Is Not Always More Upper Chest\r\n\r\nAt progressively steeper bench angles, shoulder flexion becomes increasingly dominant and anterior deltoid contribution rises.\r\n\r\nAcute bench-angle research supports this shift in recruitment. :contentReference[oaicite:5]{index=5}\r\n\r\nTherefore the relationship is not:\r\n\r\n`more incline = monotonically more clavicular pec`.\r\n\r\n## Flat Bench Still Trains It\r\n\r\nThe clavicular head participates substantially in horizontal pressing.\r\n\r\nIncline work changes relative emphasis but is not required for the region to experience any stimulus at all.\r\n\r\n## Regional Pectoralis Function Is Angle-Dependent\r\n\r\nExperimental shoulder moment-arm work demonstrates that pectoralis-major subregions change their flexion/abduction leverage as the humerus moves. :contentReference[oaicite:6]{index=6}\r\n\r\nThis supports modelling the clavicular and sternocostal portions independently rather than assigning one fixed pec vector.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\n`pectoralis_major_clavicular` should primarily receive demand from:\r\n\r\n1. **horizontal-adduction torque**;\r\n2. **shoulder-flexion torque**;\r\n3. secondary internal-rotation torque;\r\n4. glenohumeral stabilization.\r\n\r\nThe relative importance of flexion should be higher than for `pectoralis_major_sternal`.\r\n\r\n## Plane-of-Press Modifier\r\n\r\nA useful conceptual continuum is:\r\n\r\n### Flat / Horizontal Press\r\n\r\nHigh whole-pectoralis demand.\r\n\r\nClavicular:\r\n- moderate-to-high.\r\n\r\nSternal:\r\n- high.\r\n\r\n### Low-to-Moderate Incline\r\n\r\nClavicular:\r\n- high to very high.\r\n\r\nSternal:\r\n- still meaningful.\r\n\r\nAnterior deltoid:\r\n- increasing.\r\n\r\n### High Incline\r\n\r\nAnterior deltoid:\r\n- very high.\r\n\r\nClavicular pec:\r\n- still meaningful.\r\n\r\nSternal pec:\r\n- reduced.\r\n\r\n### Vertical Press\r\n\r\nAnterior deltoid dominates.\r\n\r\nClavicular pec receives only secondary allocation.\r\n\r\nThis continuum is more realistic than fixed exercise labels.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Allocation\r\n\r\n### Smith Machine Incline Bench Press\r\n\r\nVery high `pectoralis_major_clavicular` demand.\r\n\r\nAlso high:\r\n- `deltoid_anterior`;\r\n- `pectoralis_major_sternal`;\r\n- triceps.\r\n\r\n### Plate-Loaded Converging Incline Press\r\n\r\nVery high clavicular demand if the machine path is both upward and converging.\r\n\r\n### Single-Arm Low-to-High Cable Fly\r\n\r\nVery high clavicular specificity.\r\n\r\nAlso some:\r\n- anterior deltoid;\r\n- rotator cuff;\r\n- trunk stabilization.\r\n\r\n### Flat Bench Press\r\n\r\nModerate-to-high clavicular demand.\r\n\r\nSternal pectoralis should generally receive the larger share.\r\n\r\n### Horizontal Chest Press\r\n\r\nModerate clavicular demand depending on handle trajectory.\r\n\r\n### Weighted Forward-Lean Dip\r\n\r\nLow-to-moderate clavicular contribution.\r\n\r\nHigher sternocostal contribution.\r\n\r\n### Seated Dumbbell Overhead Press\r\n\r\nLow clavicular-pec contribution.\r\n\r\nAnterior deltoid dominates shoulder flexion/elevation.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Anterior Deltoid\r\n\r\nClavicular pec and anterior deltoid share significant shoulder-flexion demand.\r\n\r\nForce sharing should depend on:\r\n\r\n- press angle;\r\n- humeral elevation;\r\n- horizontal-adduction component.\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`more horizontal adduction → more clavicular pec`\r\n\r\nwhile:\r\n\r\n`more pure sagittal/vertical elevation → more anterior deltoid`.\r\n\r\nThis is a useful KISS modifier for Agonez.\r\n\r\n## Length-Tension Model\r\n\r\nA simplified clavicular-pec length state can depend on:\r\n\r\n- shoulder flexion/extension;\r\n- horizontal abduction/adduction.\r\n\r\n### Longer\r\n\r\n- shoulder extension;\r\n- horizontal abduction.\r\n\r\n### Intermediate\r\n\r\n- humerus near torso / neutral.\r\n\r\n### Shorter\r\n\r\n- shoulder flexion;\r\n- horizontal adduction across the body.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_clavicular_pec = f(shoulder_flexion_extension, horizontal_abduction_adduction)`\r\n\r\nThe two dimensions matter simultaneously.\r\n\r\n## External Resistance Profile\r\n\r\nFor pressing exercises, clavicular-pec demand should not be derived from bench angle alone.\r\n\r\nA more useful model includes:\r\n\r\n`muscle_demand ≈ joint_torque × regional_force_share`\r\n\r\nwhere joint torque depends on:\r\n\r\n- load;\r\n- bar/handle path;\r\n- humeral angle;\r\n- forearm orientation;\r\n- machine geometry.\r\n\r\nRegional force share then depends on:\r\n\r\n- clavicular fiber line of action;\r\n- anterior-deltoid leverage;\r\n- sternocostal-pec leverage.\r\n\r\nThis explains why two machines both labeled "incline press" can produce substantially different vectors.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nThe clavicular pec has a mechanically plausible long-length hypertrophy opportunity in:\r\n\r\n- deep incline presses;\r\n- incline flys;\r\n- low-to-high cable flys beginning from extension.\r\n\r\nBut direct regional SMH evidence remains limited.\r\n\r\nTherefore the safest model is:\r\n\r\n- substantial loaded stretch is beneficial mechanically;\r\n- avoid artificially inflating the SMH coefficient;\r\n- do not sacrifice shoulder tolerance for extreme bottom ROM.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **clavicular origin and humeral insertion:** high;\r\n- **pectoral-nerve innervation:** high;\r\n- **horizontal-adduction role:** high;\r\n- **shoulder-flexion role:** high;\r\n- **angle-dependent moment arms:** directly demonstrated biomechanically; :contentReference[oaicite:7]{index=7}\r\n- **greater clavicular activation at moderate incline in several acute studies:** supported; :contentReference[oaicite:8]{index=8}\r\n- **increasing anterior-deltoid contribution with increasing incline:** supported; :contentReference[oaicite:9]{index=9}\r\n- **regional hypertrophy from specific incline angles:** less directly established than acute recruitment;\r\n- **clavicular-specific stretch-mediated hypertrophy magnitude:** limited direct evidence;\r\n- **precise optimal bench angle:** individual- and geometry-dependent.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **pectoralis major clavicular head = the superior pectoralis region specialized for horizontal adduction plus shoulder flexion, with relative recruitment increasing as pressing trajectory becomes moderately more upward but eventually losing force share to anterior deltoid as the movement approaches vertical pressing.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/PectoralisClavicular}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	14.40	17.00	0.956	14.14	13.52
2	pectoralis_major_sternal	Musculus Pectoralis major sternal head	Upper	Chest	860.00	814.39	30.00	Converged/Fan-shaped	0.350	0.650	high	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\nThe **sternal / sternocostal region of pectoralis major** forms the large central and inferior portion of the pectoralis major muscle.\r\n\r\nIn this database, `pectoralis_major_sternal` should represent primarily the broad sternocostal portion rather than the smaller clavicular head.\r\n\r\nIts dominant mechanical roles are:\r\n\r\n- **horizontal adduction of the humerus**;\r\n- **shoulder adduction**;\r\n- **internal rotation**;\r\n- angle-dependent contribution to shoulder extension from flexed positions;\r\n- glenohumeral stabilization during pressing and adduction.\r\n\r\nThe sternocostal fibers originate broadly from the sternum and costal cartilages and converge laterally toward the proximal humerus.\r\n\r\nBecause the muscle is fan-shaped, different sternocostal fibers possess different:\r\n\r\n- fiber orientations;\r\n- moment arms;\r\n- length changes;\r\n- regional recruitment.\r\n\r\nThe lower sternocostal fibers are especially important when the arm moves:\r\n\r\n- downward from elevation;\r\n- medially toward the torso;\r\n- posteriorly from a flexed starting position.\r\n\r\nFor resistance-training purposes, its major exercise families are:\r\n\r\n- flat horizontal presses;\r\n- horizontal flys;\r\n- converging chest presses;\r\n- forward-lean dips;\r\n- decline/downward pressing patterns;\r\n- high-to-low adduction/fly patterns.\r\n\r\nThe key computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **sternocostal pectoralis major is a large humeral adductor and horizontal adductor whose sagittal-plane action changes with humeral elevation and whose contribution tends to decrease as pressing becomes progressively more vertical.**\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\n## Origin\r\n\r\nThe sternocostal portion arises broadly from structures including:\r\n\r\n- anterior surface of the sternum;\r\n- upper costal cartilages;\r\n- adjacent anterior thoracic fascia;\r\n- inferiorly, aponeurotic connections with the external oblique region.\r\n\r\nExact subdivision varies between anatomical descriptions.\r\n\r\nFor exercise modelling, the important feature is the broad vertical extent of the origin.\r\n\r\nSuperior sternocostal fibers run relatively laterally.\r\n\r\nInferior fibers run more superolaterally toward the humeral insertion.\r\n\r\nThis creates a fan-shaped architecture rather than a set of parallel fibers.\r\n\r\n## Insertion\r\n\r\nSternocostal fibers converge into the pectoralis-major tendon and insert onto the proximal humerus along the **lateral lip of the intertubercular sulcus / crest of the greater tubercle region**.\r\n\r\nThe tendon is structurally complex and twisted.\r\n\r\nFibers arising lower on the thorax can become reorganized relative to more superior fibers as they approach the humerus.\r\n\r\nConsequently:\r\n\r\n- anatomical origin height;\r\n- distal insertion position;\r\n- fascicular length change\r\n\r\ncannot be inferred from a simple straight-line drawing.\r\n\r\n## Relationship With the Clavicular Head\r\n\r\nThe clavicular and sternocostal regions share the same general humeral insertion but arise from different proximal locations.\r\n\r\nThis creates different lines of action.\r\n\r\n### Clavicular Head\r\n\r\nGreater relative bias toward:\r\n\r\n- shoulder flexion;\r\n- upward-directed pressing;\r\n- horizontal adduction.\r\n\r\n### Sternocostal Head\r\n\r\nGreater relative bias toward:\r\n\r\n- horizontal adduction;\r\n- adduction;\r\n- extension from flexed positions;\r\n- downward-directed pressing/adduction.\r\n\r\nBoth participate in ordinary pressing.\r\n\r\nThe distinction is one of **force sharing**, not exclusive recruitment.\r\n\r\n## Regional Heterogeneity\r\n\r\nThe sternocostal portion itself should not be regarded as one homogeneous head.\r\n\r\nSuperior, middle and inferior sternocostal fibers can differ in:\r\n\r\n- line of pull;\r\n- shortening direction;\r\n- relative contribution to shoulder extension versus adduction.\r\n\r\nIf Agonez eventually models regional pectoralis anatomy, the sternocostal region would be a strong candidate for further subdivision.\r\n\r\nFor the current model, a single aggregate remains appropriate.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nThe sternocostal portion receives innervation through the **medial and lateral pectoral nerves**, with substantial contribution from the medial pectoral nerve.\r\n\r\nRelevant brachial-plexus roots span approximately:\r\n\r\n- C5;\r\n- C6;\r\n- C7;\r\n- C8;\r\n- T1\r\n\r\nacross the pectoralis-major complex.\r\n\r\nRegional neural organization permits some differential activation between:\r\n\r\n- clavicular;\r\n- sternocostal;\r\n- more inferior costal fibers.\r\n\r\nHowever, pressing movements normally recruit multiple pectoralis regions simultaneously.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Horizontal Adduction\r\n\r\nHorizontal adduction is one of the defining functions of pectoralis major.\r\n\r\nThe humerus moves from an abducted/posterior position toward the midline in front of the torso.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- flat bench press;\r\n- chest press;\r\n- cable fly;\r\n- pec deck.\r\n\r\nThe pectoralis major possesses very favorable horizontal-adduction leverage, particularly with the arm elevated around the transverse-plane region.\r\n\r\nFor the sternocostal portion, this is usually the most important exercise-level action.\r\n\r\n## Shoulder Adduction\r\n\r\nSternocostal pectoralis major also contributes strongly to **adduction of the humerus**.\r\n\r\nThis means drawing the upper arm toward the torso from an elevated or abducted position.\r\n\r\nThis role becomes particularly relevant in:\r\n\r\n- high-to-low cable flys;\r\n- dips;\r\n- decline-oriented pressing patterns.\r\n\r\nShoulder adduction is shared with:\r\n\r\n- latissimus dorsi;\r\n- teres major;\r\n- other pectoralis-major regions.\r\n\r\nThe exact force share depends on humeral position.\r\n\r\n## Internal Rotation\r\n\r\nPectoralis major is a powerful **internal rotator** of the humerus.\r\n\r\nThe sternocostal region contributes substantially to this action.\r\n\r\nIts synergists include:\r\n\r\n- subscapularis;\r\n- latissimus dorsi;\r\n- teres major;\r\n- anterior deltoid to a smaller degree.\r\n\r\nFor hypertrophy programming, internal rotation is generally secondary to pressing and adduction because the latter permit much larger loading.\r\n\r\n## Shoulder Extension From Flexion\r\n\r\nThe sternocostal region can contribute to **extension of a flexed humerus**.\r\n\r\nThis function is especially relevant for lower fibers.\r\n\r\nIf the arm begins elevated or flexed, sternocostal fibers can pull it:\r\n\r\n- downward;\r\n- backward;\r\n- toward the torso.\r\n\r\nThis is one of the major functional distinctions from the clavicular head.\r\n\r\nThe sternocostal region should therefore not be described as universally a shoulder flexor.\r\n\r\nIts sagittal-plane action depends on the arm's starting position and fiber region.\r\n\r\n## Angle-Dependent Function\r\n\r\nPectoralis-major moment arms change substantially as the humerus moves.\r\n\r\nTherefore the same sternocostal fibers can have different mechanical roles at:\r\n\r\n- low humeral elevation;\r\n- shoulder-height elevation;\r\n- overhead positions.\r\n\r\nThe correct abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **function = fiber line of action relative to the instantaneous joint axis**\r\n\r\nrather than a fixed textbook label.\r\n\r\n## Glenohumeral Stabilization\r\n\r\nDuring heavy pressing, pectoralis-major force contributes not only rotational torque but also joint reaction force.\r\n\r\nThe humeral head must be dynamically stabilized by:\r\n\r\n- rotator cuff;\r\n- deltoid;\r\n- scapular stabilizers.\r\n\r\nTherefore high pectoralis demand should generally coexist with non-zero `rotator_cuffs` demand in Agonez.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nPectoralis major has a strong mechanical rationale for long-length loading.\r\n\r\nThe sternocostal region is lengthened substantially when the humerus moves into:\r\n\r\n- horizontal abduction;\r\n- shoulder extension;\r\n- external rotation to some extent.\r\n\r\nThis configuration occurs near the bottom of many presses and flys.\r\n\r\n## Bottom of a Bench Press\r\n\r\nDuring a deep bench press, the upper arm moves posteriorly relative to the torso.\r\n\r\nThe sternocostal pectoralis is therefore placed at relatively long length while simultaneously producing large horizontal-adduction torque.\r\n\r\nThis combination makes the bottom region mechanically important for hypertrophy.\r\n\r\nHowever, shoulder position and grip width determine how much lengthening occurs.\r\n\r\n## Horizontal Abduction\r\n\r\nMoving the humerus farther behind the frontal plane increases pectoralis length.\r\n\r\nThis can increase long-length loading during:\r\n\r\n- dumbbell press;\r\n- cable fly;\r\n- machine press;\r\n- pec deck.\r\n\r\nThe goal should be meaningful loaded horizontal abduction rather than maximal passive stretch.\r\n\r\n## External Rotation\r\n\r\nExternal rotation can additionally tension pectoralis-major fibers.\r\n\r\nThis is mechanically relevant because heavy bench-press injuries frequently occur during the eccentric phase when the humerus is extended/horizontally abducted and externally rotated.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n> greater stretch can increase muscular loading, but extreme bottom positioning also increases tendon and musculotendinous stress.\r\n\r\nLong-length hypertrophy principles should not be interpreted as a reason to maximize uncontrolled shoulder extension.\r\n\r\n## Cable Fly\r\n\r\nCable flys can maintain meaningful pectoralis torque in the lengthened position if the pulley geometry is appropriate.\r\n\r\nThe exact resistance profile depends on:\r\n\r\n- pulley position;\r\n- torso position;\r\n- arm angle;\r\n- cable direction.\r\n\r\nA cable fly beginning with the arm behind the torso can produce substantial long-length tension.\r\n\r\n## Pec Deck\r\n\r\nA pec deck can also provide high lengthened-position loading if:\r\n\r\n- the starting handles permit meaningful horizontal abduction;\r\n- resistance remains substantial near the start;\r\n- the shoulder remains comfortable.\r\n\r\nIts stability makes it especially suitable for approaching muscular failure.\r\n\r\n## Press vs Fly\r\n\r\nPresses distribute demand among:\r\n\r\n- pectoralis major;\r\n- anterior deltoid;\r\n- triceps.\r\n\r\nFlys remove most elbow-extension demand.\r\n\r\nThus flys can increase **pectoralis specificity**, but they do not automatically generate greater total pectoralis force.\r\n\r\nHeavy pressing and stable fly work are complementary.\r\n\r\n## SMH Evidence Interpretation\r\n\r\nDirect evidence isolating sternocostal-head stretch-mediated hypertrophy is still limited.\r\n\r\nThe mechanical rationale is nevertheless strong:\r\n\r\n- horizontal abduction lengthens the muscle;\r\n- high tension can be maintained in that state;\r\n- large loaded ROM is likely valuable.\r\n\r\nA moderate-to-high `smh_factor` is defensible mechanically, but its exact magnitude should not be treated as directly established.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Flat Bench Press\r\n\r\nFlat bench pressing is one of the strongest conventional exercises for sternocostal pectoralis major.\r\n\r\nThe movement combines:\r\n\r\n- horizontal adduction;\r\n- elbow extension;\r\n- some shoulder flexion depending on technique.\r\n\r\nSternocostal activation is generally high during flat pressing and tends to decrease as bench inclination becomes progressively steeper.\r\n\r\nThis makes flat pressing a natural reference exercise for the sternocostal region.\r\n\r\n## Bar Path\r\n\r\nA typical effective bench-press bar path moves:\r\n\r\n- downward toward the lower-to-mid chest;\r\n- upward and slightly posteriorly toward the shoulder line.\r\n\r\nThis permits substantial humeral horizontal abduction in the bottom position and strong horizontal-adduction torque during ascent.\r\n\r\nArtificially forcing a perfectly vertical bar path is not necessary.\r\n\r\n## Elbow Angle\r\n\r\nElbow position changes pectoralis mechanics.\r\n\r\n### More Flared\r\n\r\nGenerally increases:\r\n- horizontal-abduction/adduction component;\r\n- pectoralis leverage.\r\n\r\nBut may increase:\r\n- shoulder stress;\r\n- bottom-position stretch.\r\n\r\n### More Tucked\r\n\r\nGenerally increases relative:\r\n- triceps demand;\r\n- sagittal shoulder flexion/extension component.\r\n\r\nA moderate elbow position is usually a useful compromise.\r\n\r\n## Grip Width\r\n\r\nGrip width strongly changes shoulder and elbow demands.\r\n\r\n### Wider Grip\r\n\r\nTypically:\r\n- greater shoulder horizontal abduction;\r\n- shorter bar ROM;\r\n- greater pectoralis contribution relative to triceps.\r\n\r\n### Narrower Grip\r\n\r\nTypically:\r\n- larger elbow-extension ROM;\r\n- greater triceps demand;\r\n- reduced relative pectoralis contribution.\r\n\r\nThis does not mean extremely wide grip is optimal.\r\n\r\nThe useful range is constrained by:\r\n\r\n- shoulder comfort;\r\n- strength;\r\n- tendon tolerance;\r\n- individual anthropometry.\r\n\r\n## Plate-Loaded Horizontal Chest Press\r\n\r\nA well-designed plate-loaded chest press can be highly effective for the sternocostal pectoralis because it offers:\r\n\r\n- high stability;\r\n- easy progressive overload;\r\n- reduced balance demands;\r\n- easy proximity to failure.\r\n\r\nA converging trajectory can additionally provide greater horizontal-adduction ROM.\r\n\r\nThis should usually receive very high `pectoralis_major_sternal` allocation.\r\n\r\n## Converging Chest Press\r\n\r\nHandle convergence allows the humeri to move medially as the press progresses.\r\n\r\nThis more closely resembles the pectoralis-major horizontal-adduction function than a perfectly fixed-width barbell path.\r\n\r\nHowever, the hypertrophic benefit depends on the actual machine resistance profile rather than convergence alone.\r\n\r\n## Dumbbell Bench Press\r\n\r\nDumbbells permit:\r\n\r\n- independent arm movement;\r\n- greater horizontal-adduction freedom;\r\n- potentially greater bottom ROM.\r\n\r\nThey also reduce absolute stability compared with machines and barbells.\r\n\r\nThis may limit loading but increase freedom to select a comfortable humeral path.\r\n\r\n## Pec Deck\r\n\r\nThe pec deck is one of the most specific sternocostal pectoralis exercises.\r\n\r\nAdvantages include:\r\n\r\n- minimal triceps limitation;\r\n- high stability;\r\n- large horizontal-adduction demand;\r\n- easy approach to failure.\r\n\r\nDepending on seat height and pad position, regional emphasis can shift.\r\n\r\nA roughly horizontal humeral path strongly loads the broad sternocostal region.\r\n\r\n## High-to-Low Cable Fly\r\n\r\nA high-origin cable moving down and medially can emphasize sternocostal/inferior pectoralis fibers.\r\n\r\nThe exercise combines:\r\n\r\n- horizontal adduction;\r\n- shoulder adduction;\r\n- some extension from a flexed/elevated start.\r\n\r\nThis is mechanically distinct from the low-to-high fly used to bias clavicular pectoralis.\r\n\r\n## Weighted Forward-Lean Dip\r\n\r\nForward-lean dips can strongly train the sternocostal pectoralis major.\r\n\r\nThe humerus begins and moves through substantial shoulder extension, while the torso angle creates large pectoralis demands.\r\n\r\nCompared with an upright triceps-biased dip, a forward lean generally increases chest contribution.\r\n\r\nThe exercise can place high tension on the pectoralis in a lengthened position.\r\n\r\nTherefore depth should be controlled according to:\r\n\r\n- shoulder anatomy;\r\n- mobility;\r\n- tendon tolerance.\r\n\r\n## Decline Press\r\n\r\nDecline pressing changes the humeral path downward relative to the trunk.\r\n\r\nThis can increase relative contribution from more inferior sternocostal fibers while reducing anterior-deltoid demand.\r\n\r\nHowever, the sternocostal pectoralis is already strongly involved in flat pressing.\r\n\r\nDecline pressing should therefore be treated as a regional bias rather than a mandatory separate chest movement.\r\n\r\n## Incline Press\r\n\r\nIncline pressing still recruits the sternocostal pectoralis.\r\n\r\nIt does not switch off.\r\n\r\nHowever, as incline increases:\r\n\r\n- sternocostal contribution generally decreases;\r\n- clavicular contribution initially becomes more favorable;\r\n- anterior-deltoid contribution increases strongly at larger inclines.\r\n\r\nTherefore high-incline presses should receive less sternocostal allocation than flat presses.\r\n\r\n## Shoulder Press\r\n\r\nVertical pressing should receive only a small-to-moderate sternocostal pectoralis allocation.\r\n\r\nAnterior deltoid and triceps dominate.\r\n\r\nThe pectoralis-major contribution becomes progressively less favorable as the pressing trajectory approaches vertical elevation.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Flat Pressing Provides Large Direct Volume\r\n\r\nUnlike lateral deltoid or gluteus medius, sternocostal pectoralis major usually receives substantial direct stimulus from ordinary compound training.\r\n\r\nA program containing:\r\n\r\n- flat bench press;\r\n- horizontal machine press;\r\n- dips\r\n\r\ncan already provide a large sternocostal workload.\r\n\r\nTherefore additional fly volume should be based on need rather than assumed necessity.\r\n\r\n## Weekly Volume\r\n\r\nA practical starting range for total sternocostal-pec-focused volume can be approximately:\r\n\r\n- **6-12 effective hard sets per week**.\r\n\r\nThis includes strong contributions from:\r\n\r\n- flat presses;\r\n- horizontal machine presses;\r\n- dips;\r\n- horizontal/high-to-low flys.\r\n\r\nIncline presses should receive partial sternocostal credit.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nTwo to three weekly exposures are practical.\r\n\r\nRecovery overlaps with:\r\n\r\n- clavicular pectoralis;\r\n- anterior deltoid;\r\n- triceps;\r\n- rotator cuff.\r\n\r\nThe chest should therefore not be programmed independently from total push workload.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\n### Compound Presses\r\n\r\nUseful ranges include:\r\n\r\n- 4-8 reps for strength-oriented pressing;\r\n- 6-12 reps for general hypertrophy;\r\n- 8-15 reps for stable machine work.\r\n\r\n### Fly Variants\r\n\r\nUseful ranges include:\r\n\r\n- 10-20 reps;\r\n- 12-25 reps.\r\n\r\nThere is no sternocostal-specific repetition requirement.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nStable machine pressing and pec-deck work can usually be trained close to failure:\r\n\r\n- approximately **0-2 RIR**.\r\n\r\nHeavy barbell bench pressing often benefits from slightly more reserve because:\r\n\r\n- technique degrades;\r\n- spotting requirements increase;\r\n- shoulder/tendon stress rises.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Complementarity\r\n\r\nA simple sternocostal hypertrophy combination might include:\r\n\r\n1. heavy or moderate horizontal press;\r\n2. stable horizontal or high-to-low fly.\r\n\r\nThis provides:\r\n\r\n- high-force compound loading;\r\n- elbow-extensor-independent pectoralis loading.\r\n\r\nForward-lean dips can substitute for one of these if well tolerated.\r\n\r\n## Avoid Redundant Pressing\r\n\r\nA program containing:\r\n\r\n- flat barbell bench;\r\n- flat dumbbell bench;\r\n- horizontal machine press;\r\n- Smith flat press\r\n\r\nmay produce considerable redundancy.\r\n\r\nExercise diversity should preferably change:\r\n\r\n- resistance profile;\r\n- stability;\r\n- ROM;\r\n- humeral trajectory.\r\n\r\n## Fatigue Management\r\n\r\nPectoralis-major fatigue can affect:\r\n\r\n- subsequent pressing;\r\n- shoulder stability;\r\n- triceps performance indirectly.\r\n\r\nBecause large chest presses can generate substantial systemic and local fatigue, additional isolation work should be adjusted according to total push workload.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## "Sternal Head" Is Itself an Aggregate\r\n\r\nThe sternocostal portion spans a large vertical region of the anterior thorax.\r\n\r\nCalling it one "head" simplifies substantial internal anatomical diversity.\r\n\r\nSuperior and inferior fibers do not possess identical lines of action.\r\n\r\n## It Can Extend the Shoulder\r\n\r\nThis often appears contradictory because pectoralis major is frequently taught only as:\r\n\r\n- flexor;\r\n- adductor;\r\n- internal rotator.\r\n\r\nThe apparent contradiction disappears when the muscle's broad fan shape is considered.\r\n\r\nInferior sternocostal fibers can pull an elevated/flexed humerus downward and backward.\r\n\r\nThus their sagittal-plane function depends on starting position.\r\n\r\n## Flat Bench Is Not Only "Middle Chest"\r\n\r\nFlat pressing recruits multiple pectoralis-major regions.\r\n\r\nThe sternocostal region is strongly involved, but clavicular fibers remain active.\r\n\r\nLikewise, incline pressing does not isolate the clavicular head.\r\n\r\nRegional chest training is a matter of changing relative force sharing.\r\n\r\n## Incline Progressively Removes Sternocostal Advantage\r\n\r\nBench-angle experiments show that sternocostal pectoralis activation is generally greater during flat pressing than at increasingly steep incline angles.\r\n\r\nThis agrees with its fiber orientation.\r\n\r\n## The Pec Is a Powerful Horizontal Adductor\r\n\r\nHorizontal shoulder-flexion measurements show pectoralis major possessing extremely favorable leverage for pulling the humerus across the body.\r\n\r\nThis is why horizontal pressing and fly movements are such direct expressions of its mechanical function.\r\n\r\n## Pec Tears Commonly Occur During Bench Press Eccentrics\r\n\r\nPectoralis-major rupture frequently occurs during heavy eccentric bench pressing, particularly in a stretched position involving:\r\n\r\n- shoulder extension/horizontal abduction;\r\n- external rotation;\r\n- high force.\r\n\r\nThis illustrates the exceptionally high tension that can occur in the lengthened bottom position.\r\n\r\nIt also demonstrates why hypertrophic loaded stretch should be progressed rather than maximized recklessly.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\n`pectoralis_major_sternal` should primarily receive demand from:\r\n\r\n1. **horizontal-adduction torque**;\r\n2. **shoulder-adduction torque**;\r\n3. secondary extension torque when the humerus begins in flexion;\r\n4. internal-rotation torque;\r\n5. glenohumeral stabilization.\r\n\r\nHorizontal adduction should dominate typical chest-press vectors.\r\n\r\n## Sternocostal vs Clavicular Force Sharing\r\n\r\nA useful continuum is:\r\n\r\n### Upward / Incline Pressing\r\n\r\nIncreasing relative:\r\n- `pectoralis_major_clavicular`;\r\n- `deltoid_anterior`.\r\n\r\nDecreasing relative:\r\n- `pectoralis_major_sternal`.\r\n\r\n### Horizontal Pressing\r\n\r\nVery high:\r\n- `pectoralis_major_sternal`.\r\n\r\nMeaningful:\r\n- `pectoralis_major_clavicular`;\r\n- `deltoid_anterior`.\r\n\r\n### Downward / Decline Pressing\r\n\r\nHigh:\r\n- sternocostal / inferior pectoralis.\r\n\r\nReduced:\r\n- anterior deltoid;\r\n- clavicular pec.\r\n\r\nThe transition should be continuous.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Allocation\r\n\r\n### Plate-Loaded Horizontal Chest Press\r\n\r\nVery high `pectoralis_major_sternal` allocation.\r\n\r\nAlso:\r\n- clavicular pec;\r\n- anterior deltoid;\r\n- triceps;\r\n- rotator cuff.\r\n\r\n### Flat Bench Press\r\n\r\nVery high sternocostal allocation.\r\n\r\nSubstantial clavicular contribution remains.\r\n\r\n### Pec Deck / Horizontal Fly\r\n\r\nVery high sternocostal specificity.\r\n\r\nLow triceps demand.\r\n\r\n### High-to-Low Pec Deck / Cable Fly\r\n\r\nVery high sternocostal allocation with greater contribution from inferiorly oriented fibers.\r\n\r\n### Smith Machine Incline Bench Press\r\n\r\nModerate-to-high sternocostal allocation depending on incline.\r\n\r\nClavicular pec and anterior deltoid receive progressively greater relative share as angle increases.\r\n\r\n### Weighted Forward-Lean Dip\r\n\r\nVery high sternocostal demand when the humerus undergoes substantial extension/adduction under load.\r\n\r\n### Seated Dumbbell Overhead Press\r\n\r\nLow sternocostal demand.\r\n\r\nAnterior deltoid dominates.\r\n\r\n### Low-to-High Cable Fly\r\n\r\nMeaningful sternocostal demand remains, but clavicular region should receive greater relative weighting than in a horizontal or high-to-low fly.\r\n\r\n## Your Plate-Loaded Chest Press Vector\r\n\r\nFor a horizontal plate-loaded chest press, `pectoralis_major_sternal` should normally be the largest single pectoralis allocation.\r\n\r\nA large vector is mechanically justified because the exercise directly generates:\r\n\r\n- horizontal-adduction torque;\r\n- substantial shoulder moment;\r\n- high absolute loading.\r\n\r\nIf the machine path converges, pectoralis specificity can become even stronger.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Anterior Deltoid\r\n\r\nAnterior deltoid and sternocostal pec overlap less perfectly than anterior deltoid and clavicular pec.\r\n\r\nAs the press becomes more vertical:\r\n\r\n- anterior deltoid force share rises;\r\n- sternocostal share falls.\r\n\r\nTherefore excessive sternocostal allocation to overhead pressing would be mechanically implausible.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Triceps\r\n\r\nPressing torque is distributed between:\r\n\r\n### Glenohumeral Component\r\n\r\nMainly:\r\n- pectoralis major;\r\n- anterior deltoid.\r\n\r\n### Elbow Extension Component\r\n\r\nMainly:\r\n- triceps.\r\n\r\nGrip and elbow geometry alter the relative joint moments.\r\n\r\nA narrow-grip press generally shifts more demand toward elbow extension and therefore triceps.\r\n\r\nA wider horizontal press generally preserves more pectoralis-oriented shoulder torque.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Latissimus Dorsi\r\n\r\nBoth muscles can contribute to:\r\n\r\n- adduction;\r\n- internal rotation.\r\n\r\nBut they differ sharply in horizontal-plane action.\r\n\r\nPectoralis major:\r\n- horizontal adduction.\r\n\r\nLatissimus dorsi:\r\n- shoulder extension/adduction.\r\n\r\nTherefore high-to-low adduction tasks may involve both, while horizontal flys strongly favor pectoralis.\r\n\r\n## Length-Tension Model\r\n\r\nA simplified sternocostal-pec model should include at least:\r\n\r\n- horizontal abduction/adduction;\r\n- humeral flexion/extension.\r\n\r\n### Long\r\n\r\nGenerally:\r\n- horizontal abduction;\r\n- shoulder extension;\r\n- some external rotation.\r\n\r\n### Intermediate\r\n\r\n- humerus near torso / modest abduction.\r\n\r\n### Short\r\n\r\n- strong horizontal adduction;\r\n- adduction toward the torso.\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_sternal_pec = f(horizontal_abduction, humeral_elevation, axial_rotation)`\r\n\r\nBecause inferior sternocostal fibers differ from upper sternocostal fibers, this is necessarily an aggregate approximation.\r\n\r\n## Moment-Arm Model\r\n\r\nThe pectoralis major has very favorable horizontal-flexion/adduction leverage around shoulder-height humeral elevation.\r\n\r\nThis supports a high `E_MA` for horizontal pressing and fly movements.\r\n\r\nAdduction leverage also remains substantial across elevated-arm conditions.\r\n\r\nThe exact sternocostal contribution should change with humeral elevation.\r\n\r\n## Incline Modifier\r\n\r\nA KISS implementation could treat increasing incline approximately as:\r\n\r\n`sternocostal_share ↓`\r\n\r\nwhile initially:\r\n\r\n`clavicular_share ↑`\r\n\r\nand progressively:\r\n\r\n`anterior_deltoid_share ↑↑`\r\n\r\nThis relationship should not be strictly linear, but it is more defensible than fixed exercise categories.\r\n\r\n## Forward-Lean Dip Modifier\r\n\r\nFor dips, pectoralis allocation should increase with:\r\n\r\n- torso forward lean;\r\n- shoulder extension;\r\n- humeral abduction;\r\n- greater pectoralis-compatible depth.\r\n\r\nTriceps allocation should increase with a more upright torso and elbow-extension-dominant execution.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n`dip_vector = f(torso_angle, shoulder_ROM, elbow_ROM)`\r\n\r\nrather than one invariant muscle distribution.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nThe sternocostal pectoralis has a strong mechanical case for meaningful long-length loading:\r\n\r\n- horizontal abduction lengthens it;\r\n- the bottom of presses can maintain very high tension;\r\n- flys can deliberately load this position.\r\n\r\nHowever, direct regional stretch-mediated hypertrophy data remain limited.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n> **a large productive ROM and meaningful tension in the stretched position are well justified, but extreme shoulder extension should not receive an automatic hypertrophy bonus.**\r\n\r\n## Injury-Relevant Modelling\r\n\r\nPectoralis-major injury risk rises particularly in heavily loaded eccentric stretched positions.\r\n\r\nThis does not make long-length training undesirable.\r\n\r\nIt means the relationship is dose-dependent:\r\n\r\n- controlled progressive long-length loading → useful stimulus;\r\n- abrupt excessive force at extreme length → greater tissue risk.\r\n\r\nThis is especially relevant to:\r\n\r\n- heavy bench press;\r\n- weighted dips;\r\n- deep fly variants.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **sternal/costal origin and humeral insertion:** high;\r\n- **medial/lateral pectoral nerve supply:** high;\r\n- **horizontal-adduction function:** high;\r\n- **shoulder-adduction function:** high;\r\n- **internal-rotation function:** high;\r\n- **extension of flexed humerus by sternocostal fibers:** high anatomical plausibility;\r\n- **large pectoralis horizontal-flexion moment arm:** directly measured biomechanically;\r\n- **higher sternocostal activation at flat versus steep incline bench:** directly supported experimentally;\r\n- **decreasing pectoralis contribution as pressing becomes highly inclined:** supported experimentally;\r\n- **regional hypertrophy effects of bench angle:** less established than acute recruitment;\r\n- **sternocostal-specific SMH magnitude:** limited direct evidence;\r\n- **precise superior-vs-inferior sternocostal recruitment:** exercise- and angle-dependent.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **pectoralis major sternal head = the broad sternocostal pectoralis region specialized for high-force horizontal adduction and shoulder adduction, with substantial extension capability from elevated humeral positions and progressively decreasing relative contribution as pressing orientation becomes more vertical.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/PectoralisSternal}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	13.80	25.00	0.906	59.01	53.48
3	pectoralis_minor	Musculus Pectoralis minor	Upper	Chest	135.00	127.84	8.00	Flat/Convergent	0.550	0.450	zero	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\nThe **pectoralis minor** is a thin, triangular muscle of the anterior chest wall located deep to pectoralis major.\r\n\r\nIts mechanical identity is fundamentally different from that of `pectoralis_major_clavicular` and `pectoralis_major_sternal`.\r\n\r\nPectoralis minor:\r\n\r\n- originates from the upper ribs;\r\n- inserts onto the coracoid process of the scapula;\r\n- does **not** insert onto the humerus.\r\n\r\nTherefore it acts primarily on the **scapula relative to the thorax**, not directly at the glenohumeral joint.\r\n\r\nIts principal functions include:\r\n\r\n- scapular anterior tilt;\r\n- scapular protraction / abduction contribution;\r\n- scapular downward-rotation bias;\r\n- stabilization of the scapula against the thoracic wall;\r\n- depression/anterior translation of the coracoid region depending on starting position;\r\n- accessory inspiratory action when the scapula is fixed.\r\n\r\nThis distinction is crucial for exercise modelling.\r\n\r\nA bench press can involve pectoralis minor because the scapula must be controlled under load, but pec minor is not a primary horizontal adductor of the humerus.\r\n\r\nThe useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **pectoralis minor = an anterior scapular controller connecting ribs to the coracoid, primarily influencing scapular tilt, protraction and downward-rotation mechanics rather than producing humeral pressing torque.**\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\n## Origin\r\n\r\nPectoralis minor classically originates from the anterior surfaces or superior margins of approximately the:\r\n\r\n- third rib;\r\n- fourth rib;\r\n- fifth rib;\r\n\r\nnear their costal-cartilage junctions.\r\n\r\nAnatomical variation is common.\r\n\r\nStudies describe variation in:\r\n\r\n- number of ribs contributing fibers;\r\n- exact origin positions;\r\n- fascial attachments;\r\n- tendon morphology.\r\n\r\nTherefore the classic 3rd-5th rib description is a useful reference rather than an invariant template.\r\n\r\n## Insertion\r\n\r\nThe muscle converges superolaterally onto the **coracoid process of the scapula**.\r\n\r\nIts tendon attaches predominantly around the medial/superior aspects of the coracoid region.\r\n\r\nThis attachment places pectoralis minor anterior to the scapular rotational axes.\r\n\r\nConsequently, shortening tends to pull the coracoid:\r\n\r\n- anteriorly;\r\n- inferiorly;\r\n- medially relative to the rib origins.\r\n\r\nThe resulting scapular motion depends on what other scapular muscles are simultaneously doing.\r\n\r\n## Relationship With Pectoralis Major\r\n\r\nPectoralis minor lies deep to pectoralis major but is not a smaller version of it.\r\n\r\n### Pectoralis Major\r\n\r\nConnects:\r\n- clavicle/sternum/ribs → humerus.\r\n\r\nActs primarily on:\r\n- glenohumeral joint.\r\n\r\n### Pectoralis Minor\r\n\r\nConnects:\r\n- ribs → scapula.\r\n\r\nActs primarily on:\r\n- scapulothoracic / scapular positioning system.\r\n\r\nTheir names and anatomical proximity therefore conceal a major functional difference.\r\n\r\n## Relationship With the Coracoid\r\n\r\nThe coracoid process is also associated with attachments of:\r\n\r\n- short head of biceps brachii;\r\n- coracobrachialis;\r\n- coracoacromial ligament;\r\n- coracoclavicular structures.\r\n\r\nPectoralis minor therefore inserts into an anatomically crowded region with important implications for:\r\n\r\n- scapular mechanics;\r\n- anterior shoulder mechanics;\r\n- neurovascular relationships.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nPectoralis minor is supplied primarily by the **medial pectoral nerve**, with root contributions commonly around:\r\n\r\n- C8;\r\n- T1;\r\n\r\nand variable contribution from adjacent levels.\r\n\r\nDespite its name, the medial pectoral nerve can pass through or around pectoralis minor before supplying parts of pectoralis major.\r\n\r\nSome anatomical variation in pectoral-nerve branching exists.\r\n\r\nFor exercise-level modelling, the main implication is simply that pectoralis minor is neurally distinct enough from pectoralis major to support its separate functional identity.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Scapular Anterior Tilt\r\n\r\nOne of the most important actions of pectoralis minor is generation of **anterior tilt of the scapula**.\r\n\r\nBecause the muscle pulls on the coracoid from an anterior-inferior rib origin, shortening tends to bring the superior/anterior scapular region forward.\r\n\r\nThis action opposes scapular posterior tilt.\r\n\r\nPosterior tilt is normally important during overhead elevation.\r\n\r\nTherefore excessive or persistently shortened pectoralis minor can reduce the scapula's ability to posteriorly tilt during arm elevation. :contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}\r\n\r\n## Scapular Protraction\r\n\r\nPectoralis minor can contribute to **scapular protraction / abduction**.\r\n\r\nThis means moving the scapula around the thorax in an anterior-lateral direction.\r\n\r\nHowever, `serratus_anterior` is usually the more important large protraction and upward-rotation muscle.\r\n\r\nThe distinction is important:\r\n\r\n### Serratus Anterior\r\n\r\nProtraction +\r\nstrong upward rotation +\r\nposterior-tilt contribution.\r\n\r\n### Pectoralis Minor\r\n\r\nProtraction +\r\nanterior-tilt tendency +\r\ndownward-rotation tendency.\r\n\r\nThus two muscles can both protract the scapula while producing very different overall scapular mechanics.\r\n\r\n## Downward Rotation\r\n\r\nPectoralis minor can contribute to **downward rotation of the scapula**.\r\n\r\nThis occurs because its force on the coracoid tends to rotate the glenoid inferiorly relative to an upwardly rotated position.\r\n\r\nIt therefore acts in partial opposition to the upward-rotation force couple formed mainly by:\r\n\r\n- serratus anterior;\r\n- upper trapezius;\r\n- lower trapezius.\r\n\r\nPectoralis minor is not necessarily producing large isolated downward rotation during every contraction, but it provides a clear downward-rotation bias.\r\n\r\n## Scapular Depression\r\n\r\nDepending on scapular starting position, pectoralis minor can contribute to depression of the scapula or more specifically downward/anterior displacement of the coracoid region.\r\n\r\nThis function can become relevant during:\r\n\r\n- support tasks;\r\n- pushing;\r\n- fixed-arm positions.\r\n\r\nHowever, it should not be represented as a pure vertical scapular depressor equivalent to lower trapezius.\r\n\r\nIts actual force vector includes simultaneous:\r\n\r\n- anterior;\r\n- medial;\r\n- inferior components.\r\n\r\n## Scapular Stabilization\r\n\r\nPectoralis minor helps stabilize the scapula against the thoracic wall.\r\n\r\nThis is especially relevant during loaded upper-extremity activity.\r\n\r\nBecause it attaches directly between ribs and scapula, it can resist unwanted movement of the scapula even when visible scapular ROM is small.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n> pectoralis minor can generate meaningful force during quasi-isometric stabilization tasks.\r\n\r\n## Accessory Inspiration\r\n\r\nWhen the scapula is externally fixed, the mechanical direction reverses.\r\n\r\nPectoralis minor can elevate its rib origins and contribute to **forced inspiration**.\r\n\r\nThis is an accessory respiratory role, particularly under high ventilatory demand.\r\n\r\nFor resistance-training modelling, this role is normally negligible.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nDirect evidence for **pectoralis-minor-specific stretch-mediated hypertrophy** is extremely limited.\r\n\r\nMost pectoralis minor literature focuses on:\r\n\r\n- scapular mechanics;\r\n- muscle length;\r\n- posture;\r\n- shoulder pathology;\r\n- stretching interventions.\r\n\r\nThis is quite different from the hypertrophy literature available for large superficial muscles.\r\n\r\n## Lengthened Position\r\n\r\nPectoralis minor becomes relatively lengthened when the scapula moves toward:\r\n\r\n- retraction;\r\n- posterior tilt;\r\n- upward rotation.\r\n\r\nThese movements increase the distance between the anterior rib origins and the coracoid insertion.\r\n\r\nConversely, positions combining:\r\n\r\n- protraction;\r\n- anterior tilt;\r\n- downward rotation\r\n\r\ngenerally shorten the muscle.\r\n\r\n## Overhead Elevation\r\n\r\nDuring healthy overhead arm elevation, the scapula normally:\r\n\r\n- upwardly rotates;\r\n- posteriorly tilts;\r\n- externally rotates.\r\n\r\nThese motions can lengthen pectoralis minor.\r\n\r\nThis is one reason a shortened pectoralis minor can mechanically oppose normal overhead scapular kinematics. :contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}\r\n\r\n## Retraction-Based Stretch\r\n\r\nScapular retraction and posterior shoulder positioning can lengthen pectoralis minor.\r\n\r\nHowever, passive stretching is not equivalent to hypertrophic resistance.\r\n\r\nA doorway pec stretch may lengthen the muscle without producing meaningful active contractile tension.\r\n\r\n## Loaded Long-Length Training\r\n\r\nIn theory, a movement resisting:\r\n\r\n- scapular retraction;\r\n- posterior tilt;\r\n- upward rotation\r\n\r\ncould expose pectoralis minor to eccentric loading at longer lengths.\r\n\r\nIn practice, these actions are difficult to isolate because they involve large contributions from:\r\n\r\n- trapezius;\r\n- serratus anterior;\r\n- rhomboids;\r\n- other shoulder-girdle muscles.\r\n\r\nThere is currently insufficient direct evidence to establish an optimal hypertrophy exercise for pectoralis minor.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\n`smh_factor` should therefore be conservative.\r\n\r\nThe evidence supports:\r\n\r\n- clear scapular-position-dependent length changes;\r\n- measurable shortening associated with altered scapular mechanics;\r\n- trainability in a general muscular sense.\r\n\r\nIt does not establish:\r\n\r\n- a unique long-length hypertrophy advantage;\r\n- optimal loading configuration;\r\n- precise hypertrophy response to common pressing exercises.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Pectoralis Minor Is Rarely a Primary Hypertrophy Target\r\n\r\nMost resistance programs do not need a dedicated pectoralis minor exercise.\r\n\r\nThe muscle receives indirect loading during tasks involving:\r\n\r\n- scapular stabilization;\r\n- protraction;\r\n- pressing;\r\n- dips;\r\n- support positions.\r\n\r\nBecause its function overlaps with important scapular mechanics, aggressively attempting to maximize pec-minor hypertrophy may not even be desirable in every context.\r\n\r\nThe priority should usually be balanced scapular function rather than maximal isolated development.\r\n\r\n## Bench Press\r\n\r\nBench pressing can recruit pectoralis minor indirectly through scapular stabilization.\r\n\r\nHowever, during conventional bench pressing the scapula is usually:\r\n\r\n- retracted;\r\n- externally supported by the bench;\r\n- relatively constrained.\r\n\r\nThis limits the need for large active protraction.\r\n\r\nTherefore pectoralis minor should receive only modest demand compared with:\r\n\r\n- pectoralis major;\r\n- anterior deltoid;\r\n- triceps.\r\n\r\nIt should not be assigned a high prime-mover vector simply because its name contains "pectoralis."\r\n\r\n## Push-Up\r\n\r\nPush-ups differ from bench press because the scapula is free to move on the thorax.\r\n\r\nAt the top of a push-up, active protraction can occur.\r\n\r\nThis increases demand on the scapular protractor system.\r\n\r\nMajor contributors include:\r\n\r\n- serratus anterior;\r\n- pectoralis minor.\r\n\r\nHowever, serratus anterior should usually receive the greater protraction/upward-rotation allocation.\r\n\r\n## Push-Up Plus\r\n\r\nThe "plus" phase emphasizes additional scapular protraction at the top.\r\n\r\nThis strongly increases serratus-anterior demand.\r\n\r\nPectoralis minor can also contribute, but should not be treated as the primary target.\r\n\r\nThe exercise is especially useful precisely because serratus can protract while supporting favorable upward-rotation/posterior-tilt mechanics.\r\n\r\n## Dip\r\n\r\nDips can create meaningful pectoralis-minor demand because the scapula must be stabilized under substantial upper-body load.\r\n\r\nDepending on technique, there can also be a scapular depression component.\r\n\r\nHowever, large forces are simultaneously generated by:\r\n\r\n- pectoralis major;\r\n- triceps;\r\n- anterior deltoid;\r\n- latissimus dorsi;\r\n- scapular stabilizers.\r\n\r\nTherefore pec minor remains a secondary contributor.\r\n\r\n## Scapular Depression Exercises\r\n\r\nExercises deliberately emphasizing scapular depression can recruit pectoralis minor.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- support holds;\r\n- certain dip-support positions;\r\n- scapular pull-up variations depending on direction.\r\n\r\nHowever, depression is shared with:\r\n\r\n- lower trapezius;\r\n- latissimus-related mechanics.\r\n\r\nThe precise vector depends strongly on arm position.\r\n\r\n## Cable Scapular Protraction\r\n\r\nDirect resisted scapular protraction can recruit pectoralis minor.\r\n\r\nHowever, it will also strongly recruit serratus anterior.\r\n\r\nIf the objective is healthy overhead scapular mechanics, serratus-oriented exercise is usually more desirable than deliberately maximizing pec-minor dominance.\r\n\r\n## Rows\r\n\r\nRows involve scapular retraction.\r\n\r\nPectoralis minor is therefore generally:\r\n\r\n- lengthened;\r\n- antagonist or controlled eccentric contributor\r\n\r\nrather than a primary concentric mover.\r\n\r\nIt should receive low direct force allocation compared with:\r\n\r\n- middle trapezius;\r\n- rhomboids;\r\n- posterior deltoid depending on humeral movement.\r\n\r\n## Overhead Pressing\r\n\r\nDuring overhead elevation, healthy scapular mechanics require:\r\n\r\n- upward rotation;\r\n- posterior tilt.\r\n\r\nThese actions oppose important pectoralis-minor tendencies.\r\n\r\nTherefore pec minor should not receive a large positive prime-mover allocation during overhead pressing.\r\n\r\nIt may contribute to stabilization, but serratus/trapezius should dominate the scapular-elevation system.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Dedicated Volume Is Usually Unnecessary\r\n\r\nPectoralis minor generally does not require a separate bodybuilding volume prescription.\r\n\r\nIt receives indirect exposure from:\r\n\r\n- pressing;\r\n- push-ups;\r\n- dips;\r\n- stabilization tasks.\r\n\r\nIf the muscle is specifically targeted for research, rehabilitation or anatomical purposes, direct scapular exercises can be used.\r\n\r\nFor ordinary hypertrophy programming:\r\n\r\n> **0 dedicated sets can be entirely appropriate.**\r\n\r\n## Avoid Counting Chest Sets as Pec-Minor Sets\r\n\r\nA bench-press set is a major pectoralis-major set.\r\n\r\nIt is not equivalently a pectoralis-minor hypertrophy set.\r\n\r\nThe distinction matters because the muscles act at different joints.\r\n\r\nCompound set credit for pectoralis minor should therefore be small and based on scapular demand.\r\n\r\n## Balance With Serratus and Trapezius\r\n\r\nExcessive emphasis on anterior scapular muscles without sufficient upward-rotation/posterior-tilt capacity may be undesirable.\r\n\r\nProgramming should maintain strong function of:\r\n\r\n- serratus anterior;\r\n- lower trapezius;\r\n- upper trapezius.\r\n\r\nThis is particularly relevant for athletes performing high pressing volume.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\nThere is insufficient direct hypertrophy evidence to prescribe a pectoralis-minor-specific rep range.\r\n\r\nIf direct scapular protraction/depression work is performed, moderate-to-high repetitions are practical:\r\n\r\n- 10-20;\r\n- 15-30.\r\n\r\nThe focus should be on controlled scapular motion rather than maximum external load.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nTraining pec minor to true isolated failure is difficult because synergists contribute heavily.\r\n\r\nFor scapular-health-oriented work, there is little reason to chase aggressive failure.\r\n\r\nA moderate reserve is generally more appropriate.\r\n\r\n## Stretching\r\n\r\nPectoralis-minor stretching is commonly used when reduced muscle length or altered scapular resting position is suspected.\r\n\r\nStretching may modify:\r\n\r\n- perceived tightness;\r\n- passive length;\r\n- scapular posture under certain conditions.\r\n\r\nHowever, posture cannot be reduced to one "tight muscle."\r\n\r\nPectoralis minor length should be interpreted alongside:\r\n\r\n- thoracic posture;\r\n- scapular motor control;\r\n- serratus function;\r\n- trapezius function;\r\n- individual anatomy.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## It Does Not Move the Humerus Directly\r\n\r\nThis is probably the most important fact for exercise modelling.\r\n\r\nPectoralis minor terminates on the **scapula**, not the humerus.\r\n\r\nTherefore it cannot directly generate:\r\n\r\n- bench-press horizontal-adduction torque;\r\n- shoulder flexion torque;\r\n- shoulder internal-rotation torque\r\n\r\nat the glenohumeral joint.\r\n\r\nThose actions belong to pectoralis major and other humeral muscles.\r\n\r\n## It Can Protract While Opposing Overhead Scapular Mechanics\r\n\r\nBoth pectoralis minor and serratus anterior can contribute to scapular protraction.\r\n\r\nYet they differ strongly in rotational effects.\r\n\r\nPectoralis minor tends toward:\r\n\r\n- anterior tilt;\r\n- downward rotation.\r\n\r\nSerratus anterior promotes:\r\n\r\n- posterior tilt;\r\n- upward rotation.\r\n\r\nTherefore the statement:\r\n\r\n> "both muscles protract the scapula"\r\n\r\nis mechanically incomplete.\r\n\r\n## Its Length Is Clinically Important\r\n\r\nShortened pectoralis minor is associated with scapular positions characterized by:\r\n\r\n- increased anterior tilt;\r\n- increased protraction;\r\n- reduced posterior tilt;\r\n- impaired upward-rotation mechanics in some populations. :contentReference[oaicite:3]{index=3}\r\n\r\nThis does not prove that pec-minor shortening causes every case of scapular dyskinesis, but the mechanical relationship is well established.\r\n\r\n## It Can Affect Neurovascular Structures\r\n\r\nThe brachial plexus and axillary vessels pass near the pectoralis-minor/coracoid region.\r\n\r\nThis makes pec-minor anatomy relevant to certain forms of neurovascular compression, including the clinical concept of **pectoralis minor syndrome**. :contentReference[oaicite:4]{index=4}\r\n\r\nThis is anatomically interesting but should not be turned into a generic explanation for ordinary shoulder symptoms.\r\n\r\n## Anatomical Variation Is Common\r\n\r\nThe number of rib origins and tendon morphology can vary considerably between individuals. :contentReference[oaicite:5]{index=5}\r\n\r\nTherefore exact geometric modelling based on one textbook specimen will inevitably simplify real anatomy.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\n`pectoralis_minor` should be treated as a **scapular muscle**, not as another chest pressing muscle.\r\n\r\nIts main demand dimensions should be:\r\n\r\n1. scapular anterior-tilt torque;\r\n2. scapular protraction torque;\r\n3. downward-rotation torque;\r\n4. scapular depression/stabilization depending on geometry.\r\n\r\nIt should receive approximately zero direct glenohumeral:\r\n\r\n- horizontal-adduction;\r\n- flexion;\r\n- internal-rotation\r\n\r\nallocation.\r\n\r\n## Major Modelling Correction\r\n\r\nA mechanically incorrect implementation would do:\r\n\r\n`bench_press → pectoralis_major + pectoralis_minor`\r\n\r\nsimply because both are chest muscles.\r\n\r\nThe correct decomposition is:\r\n\r\n### Pectoralis Major\r\n\r\nReceives:\r\n- glenohumeral horizontal-adduction torque;\r\n- flexion/adduction depending on path.\r\n\r\n### Pectoralis Minor\r\n\r\nReceives:\r\n- only the relevant scapular stabilization/protraction/depression component.\r\n\r\nThis distinction should produce a much smaller pectoralis-minor exercise vector during conventional bench pressing.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Allocation\r\n\r\n### Flat Bench Press\r\n\r\nLow pectoralis-minor demand.\r\n\r\nThe bench externally stabilizes much of the scapula.\r\n\r\nPrimary demand:\r\n- pectoralis major;\r\n- anterior deltoid;\r\n- triceps.\r\n\r\n### Plate-Loaded Horizontal Chest Press\r\n\r\nLow-to-moderate pec-minor demand depending on whether the machine permits active scapular motion.\r\n\r\nIf the back pad rigidly constrains scapulae:\r\n- lower allocation.\r\n\r\nIf handles allow active reach/protraction:\r\n- higher allocation.\r\n\r\n### Push-Up\r\n\r\nModerate pec-minor demand.\r\n\r\nGreater than bench press because scapulae are free to move.\r\n\r\n### Push-Up Plus\r\n\r\nModerate pec-minor contribution but very high `serratus_anterior` demand.\r\n\r\nDo not assign the majority of the protraction vector to pec minor.\r\n\r\n### Weighted Forward-Lean Dip\r\n\r\nModerate pec-minor stabilization/depression demand.\r\n\r\nMuch higher pectoralis-major demand.\r\n\r\n### Cable Face Pull\r\n\r\nLow pec-minor concentric demand.\r\n\r\nThe exercise tends toward scapular retraction/posterior tilt, mechanically opposing pec-minor shortening.\r\n\r\n### Wide-Grip Seated Cable Row\r\n\r\nLow dynamic pec-minor demand.\r\n\r\nPotential eccentric/antagonistic role as the scapula retracts.\r\n\r\n### Overhead Press\r\n\r\nLow pec-minor prime-mover demand.\r\n\r\nThe scapula requires upward rotation and posterior tilt, so serratus and trapezius should dominate.\r\n\r\n## Serratus Anterior Interaction\r\n\r\nThis is the most important force-sharing relationship.\r\n\r\nBoth muscles can produce scapular protraction.\r\n\r\nHowever:\r\n\r\n`pectoralis_minor`\r\ntends toward:\r\n- anterior tilt;\r\n- downward rotation.\r\n\r\n`serratus_anterior`\r\ntends toward:\r\n- posterior tilt;\r\n- upward rotation.\r\n\r\nTherefore Agonez should never represent scapular protraction with one generic "protractor" vector.\r\n\r\nThe **rotational component of the scapular force** matters.\r\n\r\n## Trapezius Interaction\r\n\r\nPectoralis minor mechanically opposes portions of the upward-rotation/posterior-tilt system generated by:\r\n\r\n- upper trapezius;\r\n- lower trapezius;\r\n- serratus anterior.\r\n\r\nThus overhead movements require a different force balance than horizontal pushing.\r\n\r\nThis makes pectoralis minor fundamentally different from pec major in overhead exercise modelling.\r\n\r\n## Length-Tension Model\r\n\r\nA simplified pectoralis-minor length model can depend on scapular state.\r\n\r\n### Relatively Long\r\n\r\n- scapular retraction;\r\n- posterior tilt;\r\n- upward rotation.\r\n\r\n### Intermediate\r\n\r\n- neutral resting scapular position.\r\n\r\n### Relatively Short\r\n\r\n- protraction;\r\n- anterior tilt;\r\n- downward rotation.\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_pec_minor = f(protraction_retraction, anterior_posterior_tilt, upward_downward_rotation)`\r\n\r\nThis is inherently multi-axis.\r\n\r\nA single scapular-protraction angle is not sufficient.\r\n\r\n## No Humeral-Angle-Only E_LT\r\n\r\nHumeral elevation can influence pec-minor length indirectly because normal scapulohumeral rhythm changes scapular orientation.\r\n\r\nBut:\r\n\r\n> humeral angle alone does not directly define pectoralis-minor length.\r\n\r\nUnlike pectoralis major, the distal attachment is not on the humerus.\r\n\r\nTherefore `E_LT` should ideally derive from **scapular kinematics**, not shoulder flexion angle alone.\r\n\r\n## Stabilization and Isometry\r\n\r\nPectoralis minor can generate meaningful force while the scapula remains nearly stationary.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`scapular_ROM ≈ 0`\r\n\r\ndoes not automatically mean:\r\n\r\n`pectoralis_minor_demand ≈ 0`.\r\n\r\nHowever, the external scapular moment must justify that stabilization demand.\r\n\r\nDo not assign generic tonic activation merely because the exercise involves the upper body.\r\n\r\n## Hypertrophy Modelling\r\n\r\nDirect hypertrophy data for pectoralis minor are very limited.\r\n\r\nBench-press hypertrophy studies typically measure pectoralis major rather than deep pec minor, so pectoralis-major growth data should **not** be transferred to this muscle. For example, longitudinal bench-press studies documenting chest muscle growth primarily concern the superficial pectoralis major rather than establishing pec-minor hypertrophy. :contentReference[oaicite:6]{index=6}\r\n\r\nTherefore pec-minor ETU coefficients should be driven predominantly by:\r\n\r\n- anatomy;\r\n- scapular mechanics;\r\n- estimated force requirements;\r\n\r\nrather than assumed bodybuilding evidence.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nEvidence for a dedicated pectoralis-minor stretch-mediated hypertrophy effect is insufficient.\r\n\r\nA conservative interpretation is:\r\n\r\n- retraction/posterior tilt/upward rotation lengthen the muscle;\r\n- shortened pec minor can measurably alter scapular mechanics; :contentReference[oaicite:7]{index=7}\r\n- active loading at long length is mechanically possible but difficult to isolate;\r\n- no strong evidence defines a special long-length hypertrophy response.\r\n\r\n`smh_factor` should therefore be low-confidence and conservative.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **rib origin and coracoid insertion:** high; :contentReference[oaicite:8]{index=8}\r\n- **scapular rather than humeral action:** very high;\r\n- **anterior-tilt contribution:** high; :contentReference[oaicite:9]{index=9}\r\n- **downward-rotation contribution:** high; :contentReference[oaicite:10]{index=10}\r\n- **protraction contribution:** high;\r\n- **scapular-stabilization role:** high; :contentReference[oaicite:11]{index=11}\r\n- **relationship between shortened pec minor and altered scapular kinematics:** supported clinically and biomechanically; :contentReference[oaicite:12]{index=12}\r\n- **exercise-specific hypertrophy:** poorly established;\r\n- **stretch-mediated hypertrophy magnitude:** essentially unestablished;\r\n- **precise exercise-specific force coefficients:** strongly dependent on scapular freedom and task geometry.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **pectoralis minor = a rib-to-coracoid scapular stabilizer and protractor with anterior-tilt and downward-rotation bias, fundamentally distinct from the humeral pressing function of pectoralis major.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/PectoralisMinor}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	11.83	0.00	1.000	10.81	10.81
40	semimembranosus	Musculus semimembranosus	Lower	Hamstrings	670.00	634.47	97.00	Semi-membranous/Unipennate	0.445	0.555	extreme_high	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\nThe **semimembranosus** is a large, medial, biarticular hamstring muscle crossing both the:\r\n\r\n- hip;\r\n- knee.\r\n\r\nIts primary functions are:\r\n\r\n- **hip extension**;\r\n- **knee flexion**;\r\n- **internal rotation of the tibia when the knee is flexed**;\r\n- dynamic stabilization of the posteromedial knee.\r\n\r\nIt should be distinguished from:\r\n\r\n- `semitendinosus`;\r\n- `biceps_femoris_long_head`;\r\n- `biceps_femoris_short_head`.\r\n\r\nAlthough semimembranosus and semitendinosus are both medial hamstrings, they differ substantially in:\r\n\r\n- architecture;\r\n- tendon geometry;\r\n- force capacity;\r\n- regional recruitment;\r\n- knee-stabilization function.\r\n\r\nSemimembranosus is a relatively large, force-capable hamstring with broad distal attachments and an important relationship with the posteromedial knee.\r\n\r\nLike the other biarticular hamstrings, its operating length depends simultaneously on:\r\n\r\n- hip angle;\r\n- knee angle.\r\n\r\nIt is lengthened by:\r\n\r\n- hip flexion;\r\n- knee extension.\r\n\r\nIt is shortened by:\r\n\r\n- hip extension;\r\n- knee flexion.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_semimembranosus = f(hip_angle, knee_angle)`\r\n\r\nrather than being determined by either joint alone.\r\n\r\nFor Agonez, the most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **semimembranosus = a large medial biarticular hamstring producing hip-extension and knee-flexion torque, with internal-tibial-rotation and posteromedial-knee-stabilization roles, and particularly strong hypertrophic potential when knee-flexion resistance is applied with the hip flexed.**\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\n## Origin\r\n\r\nSemimembranosus originates from the **ischial tuberosity**.\r\n\r\nIts proximal attachment is closely related to the origins of the other long hamstrings:\r\n\r\n- semitendinosus;\r\n- biceps femoris long head.\r\n\r\nA broad proximal membranous tendon contributes to the muscle's name:\r\n\r\n`semimembranosus` = approximately "half membranous."\r\n\r\nThe muscle descends along the posteromedial thigh.\r\n\r\n## Insertion\r\n\r\nIts distal tendon inserts primarily around the **posteromedial proximal tibia**, especially the posterior aspect of the medial tibial condyle.\r\n\r\nUnlike a simple single-point tendon insertion, semimembranosus possesses several distal expansions and connective-tissue relationships around the posteromedial knee.\r\n\r\nThese contribute to its ability to influence:\r\n\r\n- tibial rotation;\r\n- capsular tension;\r\n- posteromedial knee stability.\r\n\r\nSemimembranosus therefore has a more complex distal mechanical role than simply producing knee flexion.\r\n\r\n## Relationship With Semitendinosus\r\n\r\nBoth are medial hamstrings, but their geometry differs.\r\n\r\n### Semimembranosus\r\n\r\nGenerally:\r\n- larger force capacity;\r\n- broader and deeper;\r\n- more complex distal insertion;\r\n- substantial posteromedial stabilization role.\r\n\r\n### Semitendinosus\r\n\r\nGenerally:\r\n- longer distal free tendon;\r\n- pes anserinus insertion;\r\n- particularly large excursion potential;\r\n- strong knee-flexion contribution.\r\n\r\nCombining them into one generic "medial hamstring" would lose meaningful mechanical information.\r\n\r\n## Relationship With Biceps Femoris\r\n\r\nSemimembranosus lies on the medial side of the posterior thigh.\r\n\r\nBiceps femoris lies laterally.\r\n\r\nThis creates opposing transverse-plane knee actions.\r\n\r\n### Semimembranosus / Medial Hamstrings\r\n\r\nBias:\r\n- internal tibial rotation.\r\n\r\n### Biceps Femoris\r\n\r\nBias:\r\n- external tibial rotation.\r\n\r\nThis medial-lateral opposition becomes particularly relevant when the knee is flexed.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nSemimembranosus is innervated by the **tibial division of the sciatic nerve**, primarily from approximately:\r\n\r\n- L5;\r\n- S1;\r\n- S2.\r\n\r\nThis is the same major neural division supplying:\r\n\r\n- semitendinosus;\r\n- biceps femoris long head.\r\n\r\nThe short head of biceps femoris differs by receiving innervation from the common fibular division.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Hip Extension\r\n\r\nSemimembranosus crosses posterior to the hip joint and therefore generates **hip-extension torque**.\r\n\r\nIt acts synergistically with:\r\n\r\n- gluteus maximus;\r\n- adductor magnus hamstring portion;\r\n- semitendinosus;\r\n- biceps femoris long head.\r\n\r\nIts contribution increases when hip-extension torque is required while the knee remains relatively extended.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- Romanian deadlift;\r\n- stiff-leg deadlift;\r\n- good morning;\r\n- certain back-extension patterns.\r\n\r\n## Knee Flexion\r\n\r\nSemimembranosus crosses posterior to the knee and produces **knee flexion**.\r\n\r\nIt works with:\r\n\r\n- semitendinosus;\r\n- biceps femoris long head;\r\n- biceps femoris short head;\r\n- gastrocnemius;\r\n- gracilis;\r\n- sartorius.\r\n\r\nAs a large hamstring, it can provide substantial knee-flexion force.\r\n\r\n## Internal Tibial Rotation\r\n\r\nWhen the knee is flexed, semimembranosus can produce **internal rotation of the tibia** relative to the femur.\r\n\r\nThis action is shared with:\r\n\r\n- semitendinosus;\r\n- gracilis;\r\n- sartorius.\r\n\r\nIt opposes the external-rotation tendency of:\r\n\r\n- biceps femoris.\r\n\r\nThis transverse-plane role should become increasingly relevant as knee flexion allows more axial tibial rotation.\r\n\r\n## Posteromedial Knee Stabilization\r\n\r\nSemimembranosus is particularly important around the posteromedial knee.\r\n\r\nIts distal tendon and expansions can contribute dynamically to resisting:\r\n\r\n- excessive external tibial rotation;\r\n- anteromedial rotational displacement;\r\n- posteromedial instability.\r\n\r\nThis means semimembranosus can generate functionally important force even when visible knee flexion ROM is small.\r\n\r\nIt is therefore both:\r\n\r\n- a prime mover;\r\n- a dynamic stabilizer.\r\n\r\n## Biarticular Length Dependence\r\n\r\nSemimembranosus spans both hip and knee.\r\n\r\n### Hip Flexion\r\n\r\nLengthens it proximally.\r\n\r\n### Hip Extension\r\n\r\nShortens it proximally.\r\n\r\n### Knee Extension\r\n\r\nLengthens it distally.\r\n\r\n### Knee Flexion\r\n\r\nShortens it distally.\r\n\r\nTherefore the most lengthened common configuration is approximately:\r\n\r\n> **hip flexion + knee extension.**\r\n\r\nThe most shortened is approximately:\r\n\r\n> **hip extension + knee flexion.**\r\n\r\n## Active Insufficiency\r\n\r\nIf the muscle is simultaneously:\r\n\r\n- strongly hip extended;\r\n- strongly knee flexed,\r\n\r\nit becomes shortened across both joints.\r\n\r\nIts capacity to generate additional force can therefore decline.\r\n\r\n## Passive Insufficiency\r\n\r\nCombining:\r\n\r\n- high hip flexion;\r\n- knee extension\r\n\r\nplaces the medial hamstrings under substantial passive tension.\r\n\r\nThis is the classic hamstring-stretch configuration.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nSemimembranosus has **direct longitudinal evidence** supporting greater hypertrophy when trained at longer muscle lengths.\r\n\r\nThis makes it different from many muscles for which long-length recommendations are based primarily on mechanistic inference.\r\n\r\n## Seated vs Prone Leg Curl\r\n\r\nThe crucial difference between these exercises is **hip angle**.\r\n\r\n### Seated Leg Curl\r\n\r\nHip substantially flexed.\r\n\r\nTherefore the biarticular hamstrings begin:\r\n\r\n- longer proximally.\r\n\r\n### Prone Leg Curl\r\n\r\nHip closer to neutral or mild flexion.\r\n\r\nTherefore the biarticular hamstrings are relatively shorter.\r\n\r\nThe knee performs broadly similar flexion in both.\r\n\r\nThis isolates hip-angle-mediated muscle length as a major difference.\r\n\r\nA longitudinal training study comparing the two conditions demonstrated greater hypertrophy of the biarticular hamstrings during seated leg-curl training.\r\n\r\nSemimembranosus was included among the muscles showing the long-length advantage.\r\n\r\nThis provides unusually strong support for:\r\n\r\n> **hip-flexed knee flexion as an effective semimembranosus hypertrophy configuration.**\r\n\r\n## Why Hip Flexion Matters\r\n\r\nDuring knee flexion:\r\n\r\n- knee flexion shortens semimembranosus distally.\r\n\r\nStarting with the hip flexed compensates by lengthening the muscle proximally.\r\n\r\nTherefore the muscle operates over a longer portion of its force-length relationship throughout the set.\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`SM_length_seated > SM_length_prone`\r\n\r\nfor comparable knee angles.\r\n\r\n## Knee Extension as the Distal Long Position\r\n\r\nAt the knee:\r\n\r\n- extension lengthens semimembranosus;\r\n- flexion shortens it.\r\n\r\nTherefore the beginning of a leg-curl repetition near knee extension can place the muscle at substantial length, especially if the hip is simultaneously flexed.\r\n\r\nThis creates the combination:\r\n\r\n`hip_flexion + knee_extension`\r\n\r\nwhich is particularly favorable for long-length loading.\r\n\r\n## Romanian Deadlift\r\n\r\nAn RDL also loads semimembranosus at long length.\r\n\r\nThe hip moves into substantial flexion while the knee remains:\r\n\r\n- relatively extended;\r\n- only mildly flexed.\r\n\r\nThis can create a highly lengthened hamstring configuration.\r\n\r\nThe exercise therefore provides a strong long-length hip-extension stimulus.\r\n\r\n## Seated Leg Curl vs RDL\r\n\r\nThese exercises load the same muscle through different primary joint torques.\r\n\r\n### Seated Leg Curl\r\n\r\nPrimary:\r\n- knee-flexion torque.\r\n\r\nHip:\r\n- flexed to maintain muscle length.\r\n\r\n### RDL\r\n\r\nPrimary:\r\n- hip-extension torque.\r\n\r\nKnee:\r\n- relatively extended to maintain muscle length.\r\n\r\nThey are therefore mechanically complementary.\r\n\r\n## Deep Hip Flexion\r\n\r\nGreater hip flexion generally increases passive tension in the hamstrings when the knee is relatively extended.\r\n\r\nHowever, more hip flexion is not automatically better.\r\n\r\nPractical ROM should be limited by:\r\n\r\n- pelvic control;\r\n- spinal position;\r\n- individual mobility;\r\n- ability to maintain active muscular loading.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nFor Agonez, semimembranosus deserves considerably higher confidence in a long-length effect than muscles such as:\r\n\r\n- rhomboids;\r\n- sartorius;\r\n- pectoralis minor.\r\n\r\nA high `smh_factor` is mechanically and experimentally defensible.\r\n\r\nHowever, the effect is **configuration dependent**.\r\n\r\nThe useful rule is not:\r\n\r\n`hamstring exercise = high SMH`\r\n\r\nbut:\r\n\r\n> **high hip flexion and/or knee extension while substantial active hamstring force is required = high long-length exposure.**\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Seated Leg Curl\r\n\r\nThe seated leg curl is one of the highest-value semimembranosus hypertrophy exercises.\r\n\r\nIts major advantages include:\r\n\r\n- direct knee-flexion loading;\r\n- hip flexion maintaining long biarticular hamstring length;\r\n- high stability;\r\n- easy progression;\r\n- low spinal fatigue;\r\n- straightforward proximity to failure.\r\n\r\nFor semimembranosus specifically, this is an unusually well-supported exercise.\r\n\r\n## Prone Leg Curl\r\n\r\nProne curls still train semimembranosus effectively.\r\n\r\nHowever, the hip position places the biarticular hamstrings at shorter length than seated curls.\r\n\r\nTherefore, all else equal, seated curls should generally receive a higher long-length hypertrophy modifier.\r\n\r\nProne curls remain useful for:\r\n\r\n- variation;\r\n- shortened/midrange loading;\r\n- reducing extreme stretch;\r\n- programming around equipment availability.\r\n\r\n## Romanian Deadlift\r\n\r\nRDLs load semimembranosus primarily through **hip extension**.\r\n\r\nThe combination of:\r\n\r\n- hip flexion;\r\n- relatively extended knee\r\n\r\ncreates substantial hamstring length.\r\n\r\nSemimembranosus therefore receives high demand alongside:\r\n\r\n- biceps femoris long head;\r\n- semitendinosus;\r\n- gluteus maximus;\r\n- adductor magnus.\r\n\r\nThe exact sharing depends on:\r\n\r\n- knee angle;\r\n- hip angle;\r\n- bar moment arm;\r\n- individual anatomy.\r\n\r\n## Stiff-Leg Deadlift\r\n\r\nReducing knee flexion further generally increases hamstring length and reduces the quadriceps contribution.\r\n\r\nThis can increase the long-length hamstring emphasis.\r\n\r\nHowever, completely locking the knee is unnecessary.\r\n\r\nA slight bend generally provides a practical compromise.\r\n\r\n## Conventional Deadlift\r\n\r\nThe hamstrings contribute to hip extension and stabilization.\r\n\r\nHowever, larger knee flexion and the complex force demands mean semimembranosus does not necessarily receive the same long-length stimulus as during an RDL.\r\n\r\nGluteus maximus, quadriceps and adductor magnus contribute heavily.\r\n\r\nFor pure hamstring hypertrophy, RDL-like patterns are generally more specific.\r\n\r\n## Squat\r\n\r\nSemimembranosus contributes much less dynamically than many lifters assume.\r\n\r\nDuring squat descent:\r\n\r\n- hip flexion lengthens it proximally;\r\n- knee flexion shortens it distally.\r\n\r\nThese changes partly offset each other.\r\n\r\nThus overall hamstring length can remain relatively stable.\r\n\r\nLarge knee and hip torques do not automatically imply large hamstring shortening or hypertrophic stimulus.\r\n\r\nThis is one reason squats should not be treated as complete hamstring training.\r\n\r\n## 45° Leg Press\r\n\r\nA similar biarticular compensation occurs:\r\n\r\n- hip flexion lengthens;\r\n- knee flexion shortens.\r\n\r\nSemimembranosus may contribute to stabilization and hip extension, but dedicated hip hinges and knee curls provide much clearer direct loading.\r\n\r\n## Nordic Hamstring Curl\r\n\r\nNordic curls generate very high eccentric knee-flexion demand.\r\n\r\nAll hamstrings contribute, but recruitment is not necessarily distributed evenly.\r\n\r\nSemitendinosus often shows particularly strong recruitment in Nordic-type knee-flexor work.\r\n\r\nTherefore Nordic curls should receive high semimembranosus demand but should not automatically be treated as maximally semimembranosus-specific.\r\n\r\n## Hip Extension Exercises\r\n\r\nHip-extension exercises with relatively straight knees can strongly recruit the long hamstrings.\r\n\r\nThis includes:\r\n\r\n- RDL;\r\n- stiff-leg deadlift;\r\n- 45° hip extension depending execution.\r\n\r\nThe more the knee remains extended, the more favorable the length state of semimembranosus generally becomes.\r\n\r\n## Back Extension\r\n\r\nA hip-dominant back extension can recruit semimembranosus substantially if:\r\n\r\n- the spine remains relatively stable;\r\n- motion occurs primarily at the hip;\r\n- knees remain only mildly flexed.\r\n\r\nIf the movement becomes primarily spinal extension, erector-spinae share rises.\r\n\r\n## Internal Tibial Rotation\r\n\r\nTurning the tibia internally during knee-flexion testing or training can shift activation toward the **medial hamstring system** relative to biceps femoris.\r\n\r\nThis is a bias, not isolation.\r\n\r\nExaggerated foot rotation is not required for effective semimembranosus training.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Use Both Knee-Flexion and Hip-Extension Patterns\r\n\r\nBecause semimembranosus acts across both joints, a comprehensive hypertrophy program benefits from both:\r\n\r\n1. **knee-flexion loading**;\r\n2. **hip-extension loading**.\r\n\r\nA particularly strong combination is:\r\n\r\n- seated leg curl;\r\n- Romanian deadlift.\r\n\r\nThis gives the muscle high force through two distinct joint functions.\r\n\r\n## Weekly Volume\r\n\r\nA practical starting range for direct hamstring work can be approximately:\r\n\r\n- **6-10 hard sets per week**\r\n\r\ndistributed across:\r\n\r\n- knee curls;\r\n- hip hinges.\r\n\r\nSemimembranosus receives a substantial fraction of these sets.\r\n\r\nThere is generally no need to prescribe separate semimembranosus-only volume.\r\n\r\n## Example Distribution\r\n\r\nA simple configuration might use:\r\n\r\n- 3-5 sets seated leg curl;\r\n- 3-5 sets RDL or similar hip hinge.\r\n\r\nThis covers:\r\n\r\n- knee-flexion force;\r\n- hip-extension force;\r\n- long-length loading.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nTwo weekly exposures are practical.\r\n\r\nThree can work with lower per-session volume.\r\n\r\nRecovery should account for overlap with:\r\n\r\n- sprinting;\r\n- deadlifting;\r\n- running;\r\n- posterior-chain work.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\n### Seated Leg Curl\r\n\r\nUseful ranges:\r\n- 6-10;\r\n- 8-15;\r\n- 10-20.\r\n\r\n### RDL\r\n\r\nUseful ranges:\r\n- 5-8;\r\n- 6-10;\r\n- 8-12.\r\n\r\nThere is no semimembranosus-specific repetition range.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nStable seated leg curls can be trained close to local failure:\r\n\r\n- approximately 0-2 RIR.\r\n\r\nRDLs generally benefit from more technical reserve because:\r\n\r\n- spinal position;\r\n- grip;\r\n- systemic fatigue\r\n\r\ncan become limiting before purely local hamstring failure.\r\n\r\n## Fatigue\r\n\r\nKnee curls create relatively low systemic fatigue.\r\n\r\nHeavy hinges create substantial fatigue through:\r\n\r\n- spinal erectors;\r\n- gluteus maximus;\r\n- adductor magnus;\r\n- grip;\r\n- general axial loading.\r\n\r\nTherefore seated leg curls often provide a high local stimulus-to-fatigue ratio for semimembranosus.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## Semimembranosus Is Not Just Another Leg-Curl Muscle\r\n\r\nIts distal tendon contributes to a complex posteromedial knee system.\r\n\r\nThis gives it important roles in:\r\n\r\n- rotational restraint;\r\n- dynamic stabilization\r\n\r\nbeyond simple knee flexion.\r\n\r\n## It Is a Medial Tibial Rotator\r\n\r\nWhen the knee is flexed:\r\n\r\n- semimembranosus tends to internally rotate the tibia;\r\n- biceps femoris tends to externally rotate it.\r\n\r\nThus medial and lateral hamstrings form an active transverse-plane force balance.\r\n\r\n## It Responds to Hip Position During Leg Curls\r\n\r\nUnlike `biceps_femoris_short_head`, semimembranosus crosses the hip.\r\n\r\nTherefore changing from prone to seated leg curl changes its operating length substantially.\r\n\r\nThis has been confirmed experimentally by the greater hypertrophy observed with seated training.\r\n\r\n## Seated Leg Curl Has Direct Long-Length Evidence\r\n\r\nThis is an unusually useful case because the long-length argument does not depend only on:\r\n\r\n- EMG;\r\n- anatomy;\r\n- theoretical moment arms.\r\n\r\nA longitudinal training experiment directly measured greater hypertrophy under the longer-muscle-length condition.\r\n\r\n## Squats Do Not Necessarily Lengthen the Hamstrings Much\r\n\r\nHip flexion and knee flexion affect semimembranosus in opposite directions.\r\n\r\nTherefore a deep squat can involve huge lower-body ROM without producing equally huge semimembranosus fascicular excursion.\r\n\r\nThis is a classic example of biarticular compensation.\r\n\r\n## Hamstring Force Does Not Map Directly to Visible Knee Motion\r\n\r\nDuring an RDL, the knee hardly moves.\r\n\r\nSemimembranosus can still generate high force because it is functioning primarily as a **hip extensor**.\r\n\r\nConversely, during seated leg curls it produces force primarily as a **knee flexor**.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\n`semimembranosus` should receive demand primarily from:\r\n\r\n1. **hip-extension torque**;\r\n2. **knee-flexion torque**;\r\n3. secondary internal-tibial-rotation torque;\r\n4. posteromedial knee stabilization.\r\n\r\nThese components should be considered separately before being combined into a muscle-level demand.\r\n\r\n## Length-Tension Model\r\n\r\nA useful first-order model is:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_SM = f(hip_flexion, knee_extension)`\r\n\r\nQualitatively:\r\n\r\n### Long\r\n\r\n- hip flexed;\r\n- knee extended.\r\n\r\n### Intermediate\r\n\r\n- hip and knee moving in the same functional chain with opposing effects on muscle length.\r\n\r\n### Short\r\n\r\n- hip extended;\r\n- knee flexed.\r\n\r\nFor implementation, increasing:\r\n\r\n- hip flexion\r\n\r\nshould increase length state, while increasing:\r\n\r\n- knee flexion\r\n\r\nshould decrease it.\r\n\r\n## Seated Leg Curl\r\n\r\nThis is the cleanest example:\r\n\r\n`hip_flexion = high`\r\n`knee starts extended`\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_SM = high`\r\n\r\nespecially near the beginning of the repetition.\r\n\r\nAs knee flexion increases, the muscle shortens distally but remains relatively long proximally.\r\n\r\n## Prone Leg Curl\r\n\r\nApproximate state:\r\n\r\n`hip_flexion = low`\r\n`knee starts extended`\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_SM = moderate`\r\n\r\nrather than high.\r\n\r\nThe knee-extension start still lengthens the muscle, but hip position removes much of the proximal stretch.\r\n\r\n## RDL\r\n\r\nApproximate state:\r\n\r\n`hip_flexion = high`\r\n`knee_flexion = low/moderate`\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_SM = very high`\r\n\r\nnear the bottom.\r\n\r\nThis makes RDL a strong long-length hip-extension stimulus.\r\n\r\n## Squat\r\n\r\nApproximate state:\r\n\r\n`hip_flexion ↑`\r\n`knee_flexion ↑`\r\n\r\nThe effects oppose each other.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n`ΔL_SM << what hip_ROM alone would predict`.\r\n\r\nThis should prevent Agonez from assigning very high hamstring stretch coefficients simply because a squat is deep.\r\n\r\n## Force Sharing During Knee Flexion\r\n\r\nA simplified knee-flexion equation is:\r\n\r\n`T_knee_flex =\r\n    SM\r\n  + ST\r\n  + BFLH\r\n  + BFSH\r\n  + gastrocnemius\r\n  + smaller_synergists`\r\n\r\nRelative sharing depends on:\r\n\r\n- hip angle;\r\n- knee angle;\r\n- tibial rotation;\r\n- task.\r\n\r\nAt high hip flexion, the three biarticular hamstrings operate at longer lengths and can generate large force.\r\n\r\n## Force Sharing During Hip Extension\r\n\r\nA simplified hip-extension model is:\r\n\r\n`T_hip_ext =\r\n    gluteus_maximus\r\n  + adductor_magnus\r\n  + semimembranosus\r\n  + semitendinosus\r\n  + BFLH\r\n  + smaller contributors`\r\n\r\nRelative hamstring contribution rises in configurations where the knee remains relatively extended.\r\n\r\n## Tibial-Rotation Modifier\r\n\r\nWhen the knee is flexed:\r\n\r\n### Internal Rotation Requirement\r\n\r\nIncrease relative:\r\n- semimembranosus;\r\n- semitendinosus.\r\n\r\n### External Rotation Requirement\r\n\r\nIncrease relative:\r\n- biceps femoris.\r\n\r\nThis modifier should remain secondary to sagittal-plane torque but is mechanically meaningful.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Allocation\r\n\r\n### Selectorized Seated Leg Curl\r\n\r\nVery high `semimembranosus` demand.\r\n\r\nHigh:\r\n- semitendinosus;\r\n- biceps femoris long head;\r\n- biceps femoris short head.\r\n\r\nThe three biarticular hamstrings additionally receive high long-length exposure.\r\n\r\n### Prone Leg Curl\r\n\r\nHigh semimembranosus demand.\r\n\r\nLower `E_LT` than seated leg curl.\r\n\r\n### Romanian Deadlift\r\n\r\nVery high semimembranosus demand through hip extension at long length.\r\n\r\nAlso high:\r\n- BFLH;\r\n- semitendinosus;\r\n- gluteus maximus;\r\n- adductor magnus.\r\n\r\n### Nordic Curl\r\n\r\nHigh semimembranosus eccentric knee-flexion demand.\r\n\r\nHowever, semitendinosus can receive particularly high recruitment, so do not assign all medial-hamstring demand to SM.\r\n\r\n### 45° Leg Press\r\n\r\nLow-to-moderate direct semimembranosus demand.\r\n\r\nLarge hip and knee torques are present, but biarticular length compensation and stronger prime movers reduce hamstring share.\r\n\r\n### Squat\r\n\r\nLow-to-moderate semimembranosus hypertrophic allocation despite high global lower-body load.\r\n\r\n### Cable Pull-Through\r\n\r\nModerate semimembranosus hip-extension demand.\r\n\r\nGluteus maximus and adductor magnus usually receive larger shares depending on knee position.\r\n\r\n### Roman-Chair Back Extension\r\n\r\nPotentially moderate-to-high SM demand if performed hip-dominantly with relatively extended knees.\r\n\r\nMuch lower if performed mainly through lumbar extension.\r\n\r\n## Comparison With Biceps Femoris Long Head\r\n\r\nBoth are biarticular hip extensors and knee flexors.\r\n\r\nBut:\r\n\r\n### Semimembranosus\r\n\r\nMedial.\r\nInternal tibial rotation.\r\nPosteromedial knee stabilization.\r\n\r\n### BFLH\r\n\r\nLateral.\r\nExternal tibial rotation.\r\n\r\nThe sagittal-plane roles overlap strongly, while transverse-plane roles oppose each other.\r\n\r\n## Comparison With Semitendinosus\r\n\r\nBoth are medial hamstrings.\r\n\r\nSemitendinosus tends toward:\r\n\r\n- longer-fascicle / excursion-oriented architecture;\r\n- strong knee-flexion recruitment in Nordic-type tasks.\r\n\r\nSemimembranosus is relatively:\r\n\r\n- more force-capable;\r\n- more heavily integrated into posteromedial knee stabilization.\r\n\r\nTherefore they should not necessarily receive identical exercise coefficients.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Gastrocnemius\r\n\r\nGastrocnemius also flexes the knee.\r\n\r\nHowever, its knee-flexion force depends on ankle configuration because it also crosses the ankle.\r\n\r\nFor high-force leg curls:\r\n\r\n- hamstrings should dominate;\r\n- gastrocnemius receives a secondary share.\r\n\r\n## FCSA Interpretation\r\n\r\nSemimembranosus has meaningful force capacity and can be one of the major contributors to systemic hamstring FCSA demand.\r\n\r\nIts FCSA can be applied to:\r\n\r\n- hip extension;\r\n- knee flexion,\r\n\r\nbut not simultaneously counted twice for the same muscle force.\r\n\r\nA biarticular muscle produces one muscle-tendon force that creates moments at both joints:\r\n\r\n`T_hip = F_SM × r_hip`\r\n\r\n`T_knee = F_SM × r_knee`\r\n\r\nThis is important computationally.\r\n\r\nDo **not** independently calculate a full SM force requirement from hip torque and another full force requirement from knee torque and simply add them.\r\n\r\nInstead, ideally solve for a single muscle force compatible with both joint demands.\r\n\r\n## ETU Implication\r\n\r\nAn RDL and seated leg curl can both create high semimembranosus ETU while being mechanically different.\r\n\r\n### RDL\r\n\r\nHigh:\r\n- hip-extension torque;\r\n- long muscle length;\r\n- large systemic fatigue.\r\n\r\n### Seated Leg Curl\r\n\r\nHigh:\r\n- knee-flexion torque;\r\n- long muscle length;\r\n- low systemic fatigue.\r\n\r\nTherefore per-muscle hypertrophic stimulus can be similar in magnitude while exercise-level fatigue differs dramatically.\r\n\r\nThis distinction fits naturally with maintaining:\r\n\r\n- muscle ETU;\r\n- systemic exercise cost\r\n\r\nas separate models.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nSemimembranosus should receive relatively strong long-length weighting.\r\n\r\nUnlike many muscles, this is supported by direct longitudinal evidence: seated leg-curl training at longer hamstring lengths produced greater volume increases in the biarticular hamstrings than prone training.\r\n\r\nThe correct modifier should depend on **combined hip and knee geometry**, not merely exercise identity.\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`SMH_effective_SM =\r\n    smh_factor_SM\r\n    × E_LT(hip_angle, knee_angle)\r\n    × active_force`\r\n\r\nLong muscle length without meaningful active force should not generate a large hypertrophic score.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **ischial origin and posteromedial tibial insertion:** high;\r\n- **tibial-division sciatic innervation:** high;\r\n- **hip-extension function:** high;\r\n- **knee-flexion function:** high;\r\n- **internal-tibial-rotation function:** high;\r\n- **posteromedial dynamic stabilization:** high;\r\n- **biarticular hip/knee length dependence:** very high;\r\n- **greater biarticular-hamstring hypertrophy from seated versus prone leg curls:** directly demonstrated longitudinally;\r\n- **specific semimembranosus benefit from long-length seated training:** directly supported;\r\n- **exact SM vs ST vs BFLH force sharing:** task- and angle-dependent;\r\n- **specific optimal hip angle for hypertrophy:** not established;\r\n- **precise exercise-specific FCSA allocation:** requires multi-joint force modelling.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **semimembranosus = a large medial biarticular hamstring that generates both hip-extension and knee-flexion torque, internally rotates and stabilizes the flexed knee, and has strong direct evidence for enhanced hypertrophy when knee-flexion resistance is applied at longer muscle lengths created by hip flexion.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/Hamstrings}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	6.90	15.10	0.965	91.95	88.78
19	brachioradialis	Musculus brachioradialis	Upper	Biceps	205.00	194.13	13.00	Fusiform/Parallel	0.400	0.600	low	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\nThe **brachioradialis** is a large superficial muscle of the lateral forearm and an important contributor to **elbow flexion**.\r\n\r\nIts anatomy is unusual because it originates well above the elbow on the humerus and inserts near the distal radius close to the wrist, yet it does not cross the wrist joint.\r\n\r\nIts principal mechanical role is:\r\n\r\n> **elbow flexion, with secondary participation in positioning the forearm toward an intermediate rotational position.**\r\n\r\nBrachioradialis should be distinguished from the two other major elbow flexors:\r\n\r\n- `biceps_brachii`;\r\n- `brachialis`.\r\n\r\nThe three muscles differ substantially in attachment geometry.\r\n\r\n### Biceps brachii\r\n\r\n- inserts on the radius;\r\n- flexes the elbow;\r\n- is a powerful supinator;\r\n- crosses the shoulder.\r\n\r\n### Brachialis\r\n\r\n- inserts on the ulna;\r\n- acts almost purely as an elbow flexor;\r\n- does not directly participate in forearm rotation.\r\n\r\n### Brachioradialis\r\n\r\n- inserts on the distal radius;\r\n- strongly contributes to elbow flexion;\r\n- can influence forearm rotation because it acts on the radius;\r\n- does not cross the shoulder.\r\n\r\nDespite lying superficially on the lateral forearm and being grouped anatomically with muscles supplied by the radial nerve, its principal dynamic action is **flexion**, not extension.\r\n\r\nThis makes it an anatomically interesting exception within the radial-nerve system.\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\n## Origin\r\n\r\nBrachioradialis originates primarily from the **proximal two-thirds of the lateral supracondylar ridge of the humerus** and adjacent lateral intermuscular septum.\r\n\r\nIts origin therefore begins substantially proximal to the elbow joint.\r\n\r\nThis long proximal attachment gives the muscle a pronounced longitudinal course from the distal humerus toward the distal radius. :contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}\r\n\r\n## Insertion\r\n\r\nThe muscle inserts on the lateral surface of the distal radius, immediately proximal to the **radial styloid process**.\r\n\r\nIt does not cross the wrist.\r\n\r\nThis distal radial insertion is mechanically important because the radius rotates during pronation and supination.\r\n\r\nConsequently, unlike brachialis, brachioradialis can participate in forearm rotational mechanics.\r\n\r\nHowever, rotation is secondary to its primary function as an elbow flexor.\r\n\r\n## Architecture\r\n\r\nBrachioradialis is a relatively long, superficial muscle with a substantial tendon toward its distal insertion.\r\n\r\nIts long muscle-tendon arrangement differs markedly from the more compact brachialis and from the biarticular biceps brachii.\r\n\r\nThe muscle belly is particularly visible on the lateral proximal forearm when the elbow is flexed against resistance.\r\n\r\nIts architecture supports a combination of:\r\n\r\n- elbow-flexion force production;\r\n- substantial excursion;\r\n- rapid force transmission between humerus and radius.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nBrachioradialis is innervated by the **radial nerve**, primarily from cervical roots **C5-C6**, with C6 commonly emphasized.\r\n\r\nThis is notable because most muscles supplied by the radial nerve are associated predominantly with extension.\r\n\r\nBrachioradialis is instead an elbow flexor.\r\n\r\nThe radial nerve passes in close anatomical relationship to brachialis and brachioradialis around the distal arm before dividing into its superficial and deep branches.\r\n\r\nThe muscle generally receives its motor branch before the radial nerve divides distally.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Elbow Flexion\r\n\r\nThe principal function of brachioradialis is **elbow flexion**.\r\n\r\nIt acts synergistically with:\r\n\r\n- brachialis;\r\n- biceps brachii.\r\n\r\nIts flexion moment arm changes with elbow angle, as occurs with all elbow flexors.\r\n\r\nTherefore its torque contribution should not be represented as constant throughout ROM.\r\n\r\nElbow flexion torque depends on the interaction between:\r\n\r\n- muscle force;\r\n- internal moment arm;\r\n- elbow angle;\r\n- external resistance moment arm.\r\n\r\n## Forearm Position\r\n\r\nBrachioradialis is frequently described as being especially effective with the forearm in a **neutral or mid-prone position**.\r\n\r\nThis statement is useful as a training heuristic but should not be interpreted too rigidly.\r\n\r\nExperimental EMG evidence has shown substantial brachioradialis activation during elbow flexion in:\r\n\r\n- supination;\r\n- neutral rotation;\r\n- pronation.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n> neutral grip does not uniquely activate brachioradialis.\r\n\r\nRather, changes in forearm orientation alter the relative mechanical conditions and force sharing among the elbow flexors.\r\n\r\nBiceps brachii is particularly influenced by forearm rotation because of its strong supination function.\r\n\r\nWhen this advantage is reduced, brachioradialis and brachialis can represent a greater share of total elbow-flexion demand.\r\n\r\n## Forearm Rotation Toward Neutral\r\n\r\nBecause brachioradialis inserts on the distal radius, it can contribute to rotation of the forearm toward an intermediate position.\r\n\r\nIts rotational effect depends on the starting orientation.\r\n\r\n### From Pronated Forearm\r\n\r\nBrachioradialis can contribute toward **supination toward neutral**.\r\n\r\n### From Supinated Forearm\r\n\r\nIt can contribute toward **pronation toward neutral**.\r\n\r\nTherefore, its rotational role is better described as:\r\n\r\n> assisting return of the forearm toward an intermediate position,\r\n\r\nrather than classifying it simply as either a pronator or supinator.\r\n\r\nThis rotational function is secondary to elbow flexion.\r\n\r\n## Why Neutral Position Is Mechanically Relevant\r\n\r\nThe neutral forearm position aligns several features favorably:\r\n\r\n- brachioradialis line of pull;\r\n- reduced special supination contribution of biceps;\r\n- effective elbow-flexion leverage.\r\n\r\nThis is why hammer-style curling is often associated with brachioradialis development.\r\n\r\nHowever, the relative advantage should be distinguished from absolute muscle activation.\r\n\r\nA supinated curl can still generate substantial brachioradialis force if total elbow-flexion demand is high.\r\n\r\n## Monoarticular Elbow Function\r\n\r\nBrachioradialis does not cross the shoulder.\r\n\r\nIts muscle length is therefore not directly changed by shoulder flexion or extension.\r\n\r\nThis distinguishes it from biceps brachii.\r\n\r\nIts primary length-changing variables are:\r\n\r\n- elbow angle;\r\n- forearm rotation to a lesser extent through radial geometry.\r\n\r\nShoulder position may alter exercise mechanics indirectly but should not enter a direct brachioradialis length model.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nDirect brachioradialis-specific hypertrophy evidence is limited.\r\n\r\nThe mechanical framework is nevertheless relatively clear.\r\n\r\nThe muscle becomes longer as the elbow approaches **extension**.\r\n\r\nTherefore exercises that maintain substantial elbow-flexion torque in relatively extended positions provide the most plausible long-length stimulus.\r\n\r\n## Elbow Extension\r\n\r\nThe primary long-length variable is elbow angle.\r\n\r\nA brachioradialis hypertrophy model should therefore focus on:\r\n\r\n- amount of elbow extension reached;\r\n- external torque in that region;\r\n- internal moment arm;\r\n- forearm orientation.\r\n\r\nSimply reaching full elbow extension is insufficient if the resistance becomes negligible there.\r\n\r\nThe relevant stimulus requires both:\r\n\r\n1. relatively long muscle length;\r\n2. meaningful active force.\r\n\r\n## Shoulder Position\r\n\r\nShoulder extension does not directly lengthen brachioradialis.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n- Bayesian curls;\r\n- incline curls;\r\n\r\ndo not acquire a special brachioradialis stretch advantage merely because the arm lies behind the torso.\r\n\r\nAny advantage must instead come from:\r\n\r\n- resistance curve;\r\n- elbow ROM;\r\n- loading magnitude;\r\n- stability.\r\n\r\nThis mirrors the logic of brachialis and contrasts with biceps brachii.\r\n\r\n## Hammer Curl at Long Length\r\n\r\nA cable or machine hammer curl that maintains meaningful resistance near extended elbow positions can be particularly attractive.\r\n\r\nIt combines:\r\n\r\n- direct elbow-flexion demand;\r\n- favorable forearm orientation;\r\n- relatively long brachioradialis length;\r\n- easy progression.\r\n\r\nA conventional dumbbell hammer curl may have little external elbow torque near the absolute bottom because the forearm can approach vertical.\r\n\r\nTherefore muscle length and external loading should again be considered separately.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Interpretation\r\n\r\nThe available literature is much stronger for:\r\n\r\n- acute EMG;\r\n- elbow-flexor biomechanics;\r\n- gross anatomy;\r\n\r\nthan for brachioradialis-specific longitudinal hypertrophy.\r\n\r\nConsequently, claims such as:\r\n\r\n> "hammer curls grow brachioradialis better than every other curl"\r\n\r\nshould be treated as plausible exercise heuristics rather than established muscle-specific hypertrophy laws.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Hammer Curl\r\n\r\nThe hammer curl is the canonical brachioradialis-oriented curl.\r\n\r\nIts neutral grip creates a useful force-sharing environment among:\r\n\r\n- brachioradialis;\r\n- brachialis;\r\n- biceps brachii.\r\n\r\nAdvantages include:\r\n\r\n- strong elbow-flexion demand;\r\n- comfortable wrist position;\r\n- substantial brachioradialis contribution;\r\n- simple progressive overload.\r\n\r\nHowever, it remains a **multi-muscle elbow-flexor exercise**.\r\n\r\nIt does not isolate brachioradialis.\r\n\r\n## Reverse Curl\r\n\r\nPronated curls are also commonly used to bias brachioradialis.\r\n\r\nBecause pronation reduces the favorable supination-related role of biceps brachii, relative demand can shift toward:\r\n\r\n- brachioradialis;\r\n- brachialis.\r\n\r\nThis makes reverse curls mechanically useful.\r\n\r\nHowever, they are often limited by:\r\n\r\n- wrist-extensor fatigue;\r\n- grip strength;\r\n- discomfort;\r\n- lower usable external load.\r\n\r\nTherefore a larger percentage contribution from brachioradialis does not automatically mean greater absolute brachioradialis tension.\r\n\r\nA heavier, stable hammer curl may provide a superior absolute stimulus despite being less "selective."\r\n\r\n## Supinated Curl\r\n\r\nBrachioradialis remains active during conventional supinated curls.\r\n\r\nIt should not receive near-zero recruitment simply because the palm faces upward.\r\n\r\nThe biceps gains favorable conditions, so the **relative share** of total elbow-flexion torque may change.\r\n\r\nBut brachioradialis continues to contribute to the flexion task.\r\n\r\nExperimental EMG work specifically cautions against treating forearm position as a simple brachioradialis activation switch.\r\n\r\n## Cross-Body Hammer Curl\r\n\r\nA cross-body hammer curl preserves a neutral or semi-neutral forearm orientation while allowing a comfortable path across the torso.\r\n\r\nIt can provide:\r\n\r\n- high brachioradialis demand;\r\n- easy stabilization;\r\n- a natural forearm orientation.\r\n\r\nIts advantage over a conventional hammer curl is primarily ergonomic rather than based on a fundamentally different muscle action.\r\n\r\n## Cable Hammer Curl\r\n\r\nCable resistance can maintain elbow torque through regions where free-weight resistance becomes small.\r\n\r\nThis is particularly useful when trying to load brachioradialis near extended elbow positions.\r\n\r\nCable height and body position determine the actual resistance curve.\r\n\r\nTherefore the exercise should be modeled from cable geometry rather than by name alone.\r\n\r\n## Preacher Hammer Curl\r\n\r\nA preacher-supported neutral-grip curl can provide a strong combination of:\r\n\r\n- humeral stabilization;\r\n- reduced cheating;\r\n- substantial elbow-flexion torque;\r\n- potentially high load near elbow extension.\r\n\r\nThis can make it an excellent combined exercise for:\r\n\r\n- brachioradialis;\r\n- brachialis;\r\n- biceps brachii.\r\n\r\nAgain, shoulder flexion does not directly shorten brachioradialis because the muscle does not cross the shoulder.\r\n\r\n## Pull-Ups and Pulldowns\r\n\r\nBrachioradialis contributes to elbow flexion during vertical pulling.\r\n\r\nRelative contribution depends partly on grip.\r\n\r\n### Neutral Grip\r\n\r\nSubstantial brachioradialis demand is expected.\r\n\r\n### Pronated Grip\r\n\r\nBrachioradialis remains highly relevant because elbow flexion must occur despite reduced biceps supination advantage.\r\n\r\n### Supinated Grip\r\n\r\nBrachioradialis still participates, but biceps becomes particularly mechanically favorable.\r\n\r\nThus grip should alter the recruitment vector continuously rather than categorically.\r\n\r\n## Rows\r\n\r\nRows also involve substantial elbow flexion.\r\n\r\nBrachioradialis therefore contributes, particularly when:\r\n\r\n- neutral handles are used;\r\n- elbow flexion ROM is large;\r\n- elbow-flexor torque is substantial.\r\n\r\nHowever, a row should generally provide less standardized local brachioradialis loading than a curl because failure can be determined by:\r\n\r\n- shoulder extensors;\r\n- scapular retractors;\r\n- grip;\r\n- trunk stabilization.\r\n\r\n## Wrist Position\r\n\r\nBecause brachioradialis does not cross the wrist, wrist flexion or extension does not directly change its muscle length.\r\n\r\nNevertheless, poor wrist position can reduce performance by limiting grip or creating discomfort.\r\n\r\nFor curling:\r\n\r\n- maintain a stable wrist;\r\n- avoid excessive flexion;\r\n- avoid forced extension;\r\n- let elbow flexion remain the dominant action.\r\n\r\n## Range of Motion\r\n\r\nA broad elbow ROM is generally useful.\r\n\r\nFor hypertrophy:\r\n\r\n- approach substantial elbow extension;\r\n- maintain controlled eccentric motion;\r\n- avoid bouncing from the bottom;\r\n- flex through the available loaded range.\r\n\r\nAs always, the goal is not maximum anatomical ROM in isolation.\r\n\r\nThe objective is **loaded ROM**.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Direct Volume\r\n\r\nBrachioradialis receives substantial work from:\r\n\r\n- hammer curls;\r\n- reverse curls;\r\n- conventional curls;\r\n- pull-ups;\r\n- pulldowns;\r\n- rows.\r\n\r\nTherefore dedicated brachioradialis volume often does not need to be large.\r\n\r\nIf additional development is desired, approximately:\r\n\r\n- **2-6 direct neutral/pronated-grip sets per week**\r\n\r\ncan serve as a practical starting addition.\r\n\r\nThis should be adjusted according to existing pulling and curling volume.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nOne to three weekly exposures are practical.\r\n\r\nTwo exposures fit naturally into most upper-body programs.\r\n\r\nBecause brachioradialis is relatively small and does not generate large systemic fatigue, frequency is usually constrained more by:\r\n\r\n- elbow tolerance;\r\n- wrist/forearm fatigue;\r\n- grip recovery;\r\n\r\nthan by whole-body recovery.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\nUseful ranges include:\r\n\r\n- 6-10 reps for heavier hammer curls;\r\n- 8-15 reps as a general hypertrophy range;\r\n- 12-25 reps for reverse curls or cable variants where lower loads improve joint comfort.\r\n\r\nVery heavy pronated curls can become wrist-limited.\r\n\r\nTherefore reverse curls often work particularly well with moderate loads and controlled repetitions.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nStable brachioradialis-oriented curls can generally be trained near failure.\r\n\r\nApproximately:\r\n\r\n- 0-3 RIR\r\n\r\nis a practical hypertrophy range.\r\n\r\nTechnique failure should occur when elbow flexion can no longer continue without substantial:\r\n\r\n- trunk extension;\r\n- shoulder movement;\r\n- wrist compensation.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Selection\r\n\r\nA dedicated brachioradialis exercise is most useful when the existing program is dominated by supinated curling.\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n\r\n- supinated biceps curl;\r\n- neutral-grip hammer curl.\r\n\r\nThis creates useful variation in elbow-flexor force sharing.\r\n\r\nThere is usually little need to include:\r\n\r\n- hammer curl;\r\n- cross-body hammer curl;\r\n- reverse curl;\r\n- rope hammer curl;\r\n\r\nall within the same program.\r\n\r\nTheir mechanical overlap can become highly redundant.\r\n\r\n## Absolute Load vs Selectivity\r\n\r\nExercise selection should prioritize **absolute productive tension**, not maximum theoretical isolation.\r\n\r\nConsider two exercises:\r\n\r\n1. reverse curl with low load because wrist extensors fail early;\r\n2. stable hammer curl with substantially greater elbow-flexion torque.\r\n\r\nEven if the reverse curl produces a higher percentage share for brachioradialis, the hammer curl can still generate more absolute brachioradialis force.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n> relative recruitment percentage and absolute muscular stimulus are not equivalent.\r\n\r\nThis distinction is especially important in exercise-vector models.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## It Is a Flexor Innervated by the Radial Nerve\r\n\r\nMost muscles strongly associated with the radial nerve are extensors.\r\n\r\nBrachioradialis is a conspicuous exception.\r\n\r\nIt receives radial-nerve innervation but flexes the elbow.\r\n\r\nThis reflects developmental compartment anatomy rather than a simple rule that "radial nerve = extension."\r\n\r\n## It Crosses Most of the Forearm but Not the Wrist\r\n\r\nBrachioradialis travels from the distal humerus almost to the wrist yet inserts on the radius just proximal to the radial styloid.\r\n\r\nTherefore it can strongly influence the elbow while having no direct wrist action.\r\n\r\n## Its Name Describes Its Path\r\n\r\n`brachio-` refers to the arm/humerus.\r\n\r\n`-radialis` refers to the radius.\r\n\r\nThe name therefore describes a muscle extending from the arm to the radius.\r\n\r\n## Neutral Grip Is Not an On/Off Switch\r\n\r\nThe common claim that brachioradialis "works only in hammer curls" is inconsistent with experimental evidence.\r\n\r\nBrachioradialis remains active during elbow flexion across supinated, neutral and pronated forearm positions. :contentReference[oaicite:3]{index=3}\r\n\r\nThe neutral grip is better understood as one mechanical context that changes relative force sharing rather than a unique activation state.\r\n\r\n## It Can Reverse Its Rotational Direction\r\n\r\nBecause its rotational effect depends on initial forearm orientation, brachioradialis can contribute:\r\n\r\n- toward supination from a pronated state;\r\n- toward pronation from a supinated state.\r\n\r\nThe common endpoint is approximately the intermediate forearm position.\r\n\r\nThis is a useful example of how the same muscle can have opposite named joint actions depending on starting geometry.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\n`brachioradialis` should be represented primarily as an **elbow-flexion actuator with forearm-position-dependent force sharing**.\r\n\r\nThe relevant mechanical variables are:\r\n\r\n- elbow-flexion torque;\r\n- elbow angle;\r\n- forearm rotation;\r\n- resistance profile.\r\n\r\nShoulder angle should not directly alter `E_LT`.\r\n\r\nA conceptual approximation is:\r\n\r\n`E_LT ≈ f(elbow_angle, small_forearm_rotation_component)`\r\n\r\nrather than:\r\n\r\n`E_LT = f(elbow_angle, shoulder_angle)`\r\n\r\nas used for `biceps_brachii`.\r\n\r\n## Grip Modifier\r\n\r\nGrip orientation should alter force sharing continuously.\r\n\r\n### Supinated Grip\r\n\r\nSubstantial brachioradialis demand remains.\r\n\r\nBiceps brachii receives particularly favorable conditions because:\r\n\r\n- it flexes the elbow;\r\n- it strongly supinates the forearm.\r\n\r\nTherefore brachioradialis' relative share may be reduced.\r\n\r\n### Neutral Grip\r\n\r\nHigh brachioradialis relevance.\r\n\r\nThis is the conventional hammer-curl configuration and provides a favorable compromise between:\r\n\r\n- usable load;\r\n- elbow-flexion leverage;\r\n- reduced biceps-specific supination advantage.\r\n\r\n### Pronated Grip\r\n\r\nBiceps becomes mechanically less favorable.\r\n\r\nBrachioradialis and brachialis can consequently assume a greater fraction of elbow-flexion demand.\r\n\r\nHowever, wrist and grip limitations may reduce total external load.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Allocation\r\n\r\n### Preacher Hammer Curl\r\n\r\nHigh `brachioradialis` demand.\r\n\r\nAlso substantial:\r\n\r\n- `brachialis`;\r\n- `biceps_brachii`.\r\n\r\n### Neutral-Grip Pull-Up\r\n\r\nMeaningful brachioradialis demand because substantial elbow-flexion torque is required.\r\n\r\n### Wide Pronated Pull-Up\r\n\r\nStill meaningful brachioradialis allocation.\r\n\r\nDo not suppress the muscle simply because grip is pronated.\r\n\r\n### Standing Barbell Curl\r\n\r\nModerate-to-high brachioradialis demand despite supination.\r\n\r\nRelative biceps allocation should be higher than during hammer or reverse curling.\r\n\r\n### Bayesian Curl\r\n\r\nBrachioradialis contributes to elbow flexion but should receive no special long-length modifier from shoulder extension.\r\n\r\n### Reverse Curl\r\n\r\nPotentially high relative brachioradialis allocation, but absolute demand must account for the usually lower external load.\r\n\r\n## Relationship to Brachialis\r\n\r\nBoth `brachialis` and `brachioradialis` remain relevant when the forearm is pronated.\r\n\r\nThey should not, however, receive identical modifiers.\r\n\r\nThe key distinction is:\r\n\r\n### Brachialis\r\n\r\n- ulnar insertion;\r\n- essentially no direct forearm-rotation function;\r\n- elbow-angle-dependent length.\r\n\r\n### Brachioradialis\r\n\r\n- radial insertion;\r\n- secondary forearm-rotation role;\r\n- force sharing affected by forearm orientation.\r\n\r\nTherefore grip orientation deserves a somewhat larger modifier for brachioradialis than for brachialis.\r\n\r\n## Important Modelling Principle\r\n\r\nAvoid the discrete mapping:\r\n\r\n- supinated → biceps;\r\n- neutral → brachioradialis;\r\n- pronated → brachialis.\r\n\r\nActual physiology is continuous and overlapping.\r\n\r\nA more realistic force-sharing model is:\r\n\r\n`elbow_flexion_demand → {biceps_brachii, brachialis, brachioradialis}`\r\n\r\nwith relative weights modified by:\r\n\r\n- forearm orientation;\r\n- elbow angle;\r\n- shoulder angle for biceps only;\r\n- required supination/pronation torque;\r\n- individual moment arms.\r\n\r\nThis captures the anatomy far better than grip-based binary assignment.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nThere is insufficient direct evidence to assign a strong muscle-specific stretch-mediated hypertrophy effect to brachioradialis.\r\n\r\nThe defensible model is:\r\n\r\n- elbow extension increases its length;\r\n- meaningful resistance near extended elbow positions is mechanically favorable;\r\n- shoulder position has little direct effect on its length;\r\n- forearm position affects geometry and force distribution;\r\n- exercise-specific longitudinal hypertrophy data remain sparse.\r\n\r\nTherefore `smh_factor` should be interpreted conservatively unless supported by broader model assumptions rather than direct brachioradialis hypertrophy trials.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **humeral origin and radial insertion:** high;\r\n- **radial-nerve innervation:** high;\r\n- **elbow-flexion function:** high;\r\n- **activity across multiple forearm orientations:** directly supported experimentally;\r\n- **secondary rotation toward neutral:** anatomically and biomechanically established;\r\n- **special hypertrophic superiority of hammer curls:** not directly established;\r\n- **brachioradialis-specific long-length hypertrophy:** limited direct evidence;\r\n- **precise grip-dependent recruitment coefficients:** uncertain and task-dependent.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **brachioradialis = a monoarticular elbow flexor connecting the humerus to the distal radius, active across forearm orientations, with force sharing influenced by pronation-supination and a secondary tendency to rotate the forearm toward an intermediate position.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/Brachioradialis}	{https://youtu.be/1JW-_iRkZWA?si=4z4Q0OLdvNJGiJ59}	Bilateral	17.30	0.00	1.000	11.22	11.22
12	trapezius_upper	Musculus Trapezius upper	Upper	Back	230.00	217.80	8.00	Parallel	0.550	0.450	high	Bell-shaped	Mid_Range	\r\n# Overview\r\n\r\nThe **upper trapezius** is the superior functional region of the trapezius muscle.\r\n\r\nIts major mechanical roles are:\r\n\r\n- **scapular elevation**;\r\n- **clavicular elevation**;\r\n- contribution to **scapular upward rotation**;\r\n- stabilization of the shoulder girdle during loaded arm elevation;\r\n- contribution to cervical extension, ipsilateral lateral flexion and contralateral rotation when the shoulder girdle is fixed.\r\n\r\nIt should not be reduced to:\r\n\r\n> "the shrug muscle."\r\n\r\nThat description captures only its most obvious translational action.\r\n\r\nDuring overhead arm elevation, upper trapezius is one of the major contributors to the scapular upward-rotation system together with:\r\n\r\n- `serratus_anterior`;\r\n- `trapezius_lower`.\r\n\r\nThe upper trapezius is also mechanically unusual because many of its superior fibers attach to the **clavicle**, not directly to the scapula.\r\n\r\nThis means part of its effect on scapular motion is transmitted through the clavicle and acromioclavicular complex.\r\n\r\nFor Agonez, the most useful abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **upper trapezius = a superior shoulder-girdle elevator and upward-rotation contributor whose demand rises strongly during shrugs, loaded scapular elevation and high humeral elevation, while its cervical role becomes relevant when the shoulder girdle is fixed.**\r\n\r\n# Anatomy\r\n\r\n## Origin\r\n\r\nThe superior trapezius originates from structures including:\r\n\r\n- external occipital protuberance;\r\n- medial superior nuchal line;\r\n- nuchal ligament;\r\n- upper cervical posterior connective tissues.\r\n\r\nIts fibers descend inferolaterally toward the shoulder girdle.\r\n\r\n## Insertion\r\n\r\nUpper trapezius inserts primarily onto the:\r\n\r\n- lateral third of the clavicle;\r\n- acromial region.\r\n\r\nThis distinguishes it from middle and lower trapezius, which insert more directly along the scapular spine.\r\n\r\nBecause the clavicle forms a strut between the sternum and scapula, upper-trapezius force can influence scapular orientation indirectly by moving the clavicle.\r\n\r\n## Regional Continuity\r\n\r\nTrapezius is one continuous muscle but is usefully separated into:\r\n\r\n- upper;\r\n- middle;\r\n- lower regions.\r\n\r\nThe separation is functional rather than based on fully independent anatomical compartments.\r\n\r\nThe upper region has a more descending fiber orientation than the middle and lower regions.\r\n\r\nThis gives it comparatively favorable leverage for:\r\n\r\n- elevation;\r\n- upward-rotation contribution.\r\n\r\n## Relationship With the Clavicle\r\n\r\nThe clavicular insertion is critical.\r\n\r\nDuring scapular upward rotation, the clavicle itself must:\r\n\r\n- elevate;\r\n- rotate posteriorly;\r\n- move through the sternoclavicular joint.\r\n\r\nUpper trapezius helps generate and stabilize this clavicular motion.\r\n\r\nTherefore its contribution to overhead mechanics cannot be understood purely as a direct scapula-to-spine pull.\r\n\r\n# Innervation\r\n\r\nThe trapezius receives primary motor innervation from the **spinal accessory nerve, cranial nerve XI**.\r\n\r\nCervical spinal nerves, mainly around:\r\n\r\n- C3;\r\n- C4,\r\n\r\nprovide sensory and proprioceptive contributions.\r\n\r\nThe same broad innervation pattern applies to upper, middle and lower trapezius.\r\n\r\n# Function\r\n\r\n## Scapular Elevation\r\n\r\nThe most visible action of upper trapezius is **elevation of the shoulder girdle**.\r\n\r\nThis occurs during:\r\n\r\n- shrugging;\r\n- carrying;\r\n- certain pulling tasks;\r\n- stabilization under downward traction.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- dumbbell shrug;\r\n- barbell shrug;\r\n- farmer carry;\r\n- heavy dead hang transitioning toward active shoulder support.\r\n\r\nUpper trapezius is one of the dominant muscles for this action.\r\n\r\n## Clavicular Elevation\r\n\r\nBecause upper trapezius inserts substantially onto the lateral clavicle, it can elevate the clavicle at the sternoclavicular joint.\r\n\r\nThis movement is part of normal scapulothoracic elevation mechanics.\r\n\r\nIt is especially important during arm elevation above shoulder height.\r\n\r\n## Scapular Upward Rotation\r\n\r\nUpper trapezius contributes to **upward rotation of the scapula**.\r\n\r\nIt does not perform this action alone.\r\n\r\nThe main upward-rotation force system includes:\r\n\r\n- upper trapezius;\r\n- lower trapezius;\r\n- serratus anterior.\r\n\r\nTheir lines of pull differ, but the resulting rotational moments can cooperate.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n> high upper-trapezius activation during overhead motion is not automatically dysfunctional shrugging.\r\n\r\nIt can be part of normal scapular mechanics.\r\n\r\n## Shoulder-Girdle Stabilization\r\n\r\nUpper trapezius can produce substantial force while visible elevation is small.\r\n\r\nFor example, when carrying a heavy load in the hands, the load tends to depress the shoulder girdle.\r\n\r\nUpper trapezius may work isometrically to resist this depression.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`scapular_elevation_ROM ≈ 0`\r\n\r\ndoes not imply:\r\n\r\n`upper_trapezius_force ≈ 0`.\r\n\r\n## Cervical Extension\r\n\r\nWhen the scapula/clavicle is fixed, bilateral upper trapezius contraction can contribute to extension of the cervical region and head.\r\n\r\nThis action overlaps with:\r\n\r\n- splenius;\r\n- semispinalis;\r\n- deep cervical extensors.\r\n\r\nUpper trapezius should therefore receive some cervical-load exposure during certain neck-extension tasks, though it should not absorb the entire posterior-neck vector.\r\n\r\n## Cervical Lateral Flexion\r\n\r\nUnilateral upper trapezius contraction can contribute to **ipsilateral cervical lateral flexion**.\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n\r\n- right upper trapezius → right lateral flexion contribution.\r\n\r\n## Cervical Rotation\r\n\r\nUnilateral upper trapezius can contribute to **contralateral cervical rotation** depending on head and shoulder fixation.\r\n\r\nIts cervical actions are secondary in most gym exercises but relevant in neck modelling.\r\n\r\n# Relationship With Serratus Anterior\r\n\r\nUpper trapezius and serratus anterior are major partners during scapular upward rotation.\r\n\r\n### Upper Trapezius\r\n\r\nBias:\r\n- clavicular/scapular elevation;\r\n- upward rotation.\r\n\r\n### Serratus Anterior\r\n\r\nBias:\r\n- protraction;\r\n- upward rotation;\r\n- posterior tilt.\r\n\r\nThus they can cooperate rotationally despite different translational effects.\r\n\r\nA model where "serratus protracts and traps retract" is too simplistic for overhead elevation.\r\n\r\n# Relationship With Lower Trapezius\r\n\r\nUpper and lower trapezius form complementary components of the upward-rotation force system.\r\n\r\n### Upper Trapezius\r\n\r\nPulls superiorly on the clavicle/acromial region.\r\n\r\n### Lower Trapezius\r\n\r\nPulls inferomedially on the medial scapular spine.\r\n\r\nThese opposite linear directions can produce the same rotational result:\r\n\r\n- upward rotation.\r\n\r\nThis is a classic force-couple relationship.\r\n\r\n# Relationship With Levator Scapulae\r\n\r\nBoth muscles can elevate the scapula.\r\n\r\nHowever, their rotational tendencies differ.\r\n\r\n### Upper Trapezius\r\n\r\nElevation +\r\n- upward-rotation contribution.\r\n\r\n### Levator Scapulae\r\n\r\nElevation +\r\n- downward-rotation tendency.\r\n\r\nThus a shrug or loaded carry can involve both, but an overhead upward-rotation task should bias upper trapezius more strongly relative to levator scapulae.\r\n\r\n# Stretch-Mediated Hypertrophy\r\n\r\nDirect longitudinal evidence specifically examining **stretch-mediated hypertrophy of upper trapezius** is limited.\r\n\r\nMechanically, the muscle is lengthened when its attachments move apart.\r\n\r\nRelevant movements include:\r\n\r\n- scapular depression;\r\n- contralateral cervical lateral flexion;\r\n- some cervical flexion depending fiber region.\r\n\r\n## Scapular Depression\r\n\r\nBecause upper trapezius elevates the shoulder girdle, downward displacement lengthens it.\r\n\r\nA shrug beginning from a controlled depressed position can therefore load the muscle from a relatively long state.\r\n\r\nExamples include:\r\n\r\n- dumbbell shrug with full controlled bottom position;\r\n- cable shrug;\r\n- loaded carries where the shoulder girdle is allowed slight controlled depression.\r\n\r\n## Loaded Bottom of a Shrug\r\n\r\nThe bottom of a shrug can provide substantial long-length tension because the external load pulls the scapula/clavicle downward while upper trapezius resists.\r\n\r\nThis is mechanically analogous to:\r\n\r\n- dorsiflexed calf loading for plantarflexors;\r\n- protracted rows for scapular retractors.\r\n\r\nHowever, direct upper-trap-specific SMH evidence is much weaker than for gastrocnemius.\r\n\r\n## Cervical Position\r\n\r\nIf the neck is simultaneously laterally flexed away from the working side, unilateral upper-trap length can increase.\r\n\r\nThis is relevant anatomically but is rarely necessary for hypertrophy training.\r\n\r\nMixing heavy shrug loading with extreme cervical positioning is generally unnecessary.\r\n\r\n## Overhead Elevation\r\n\r\nDuring overhead elevation, upper trapezius can be highly active while not necessarily operating at maximal length.\r\n\r\nThe task is primarily about:\r\n\r\n- clavicular elevation;\r\n- upward-rotation torque;\r\n- stabilization.\r\n\r\nTherefore high recruitment should not be confused with long-length loading.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nThe defensible position is:\r\n\r\n- scapular depression clearly lengthens upper trapezius;\r\n- shrug-type exercises can load this position;\r\n- direct long-length hypertrophy evidence is sparse;\r\n- no large specific SMH bonus is established.\r\n\r\nTherefore `smh_factor` should remain conservative-to-moderate.\r\n\r\n# Training Notes\r\n\r\n## Dumbbell Shrug\r\n\r\nThe dumbbell shrug is one of the most direct upper-trapezius exercises.\r\n\r\nThe primary movement should be:\r\n\r\n- scapular/clavicular elevation.\r\n\r\nUseful execution includes:\r\n\r\n- controlled bottom position;\r\n- strong elevation;\r\n- minimal unnecessary shoulder rolling.\r\n\r\nShoulder circles are not required to train the upper trapezius effectively.\r\n\r\n## Barbell Shrug\r\n\r\nBarbell shrugs allow high absolute loading.\r\n\r\nAdvantages include:\r\n\r\n- easy progression;\r\n- bilateral heavy loading.\r\n\r\nLimitations include:\r\n\r\n- fixed hand path;\r\n- potential grip limitation;\r\n- limited freedom for scapular plane.\r\n\r\nStraps can reduce grip limitation if upper-trap hypertrophy is the priority.\r\n\r\n## Standing Dumbbell Shrug\r\n\r\nThis should receive very high `trapezius_upper` allocation in Agonez.\r\n\r\nSecondary contributors include:\r\n\r\n- levator scapulae;\r\n- cervical stabilizers;\r\n- grip musculature.\r\n\r\nThe direct humeral muscles should receive little prime-mover demand.\r\n\r\n## Cable Shrug\r\n\r\nCable resistance can provide a different force vector and continuous loading.\r\n\r\nThe cable can also be positioned slightly lateral to the body to better match individual scapular elevation trajectories.\r\n\r\n## Farmer Carry\r\n\r\nFarmer carries create large isometric upper-trapezius demand.\r\n\r\nThe external load pulls the shoulder girdle downward.\r\n\r\nUpper trapezius resists this through:\r\n\r\n- elevation torque;\r\n- stabilization.\r\n\r\nThis can produce substantial force despite little visible movement.\r\n\r\nTherefore carries should receive meaningful upper-trap ETU even though shrug ROM is approximately zero.\r\n\r\n## Deadlift\r\n\r\nHeavy deadlifts also create substantial downward traction through the arms.\r\n\r\nUpper trapezius contributes to shoulder-girdle stabilization.\r\n\r\nHowever, the exercise is not a direct upper-trap dynamic movement.\r\n\r\nIts upper-trap demand is primarily:\r\n\r\n- isometric;\r\n- load-bearing.\r\n\r\n## Dead Hang\r\n\r\nA passive dead hang minimizes active shoulder-girdle elevation.\r\n\r\nUpper-trapezius demand may therefore be relatively low.\r\n\r\nIn an active hang, scapular stabilization increases, but depression rather than elevation often becomes important.\r\n\r\nThus upper-trap allocation should still not automatically be high.\r\n\r\n## Overhead Press\r\n\r\nOverhead pressing requires progressive scapular upward rotation.\r\n\r\nUpper trapezius should therefore receive meaningful-to-high demand.\r\n\r\nThis demand is not a technique error.\r\n\r\nAppropriate scapular elevation/upward rotation is part of normal overhead mechanics.\r\n\r\n## Lateral Raise\r\n\r\nAs the humerus elevates, scapular upward rotation increases.\r\n\r\nTherefore upper-trapezius contribution generally becomes more important in the upper portion of the movement.\r\n\r\nThis means the common coaching cue:\r\n\r\n> "never let your traps work during lateral raises"\r\n\r\nis anatomically incorrect.\r\n\r\nThe better goal is to avoid excessive premature scapular elevation that substitutes for intended glenohumeral abduction.\r\n\r\nNormal upper-trap participation at higher elevation is expected.\r\n\r\n## Cable Lateral Raise\r\n\r\nA cable lateral raise can produce substantial upper-trapezius demand at higher humeral angles.\r\n\r\nThe exact share depends on:\r\n\r\n- elevation range;\r\n- scapular freedom;\r\n- humeral plane.\r\n\r\nThe lateral deltoid remains the main glenohumeral abductor.\r\n\r\nUpper trapezius handles the scapular component.\r\n\r\n## Arnold Press\r\n\r\nArnold pressing involves substantial humeral elevation and therefore meaningful upward-rotation demand.\r\n\r\nUpper trapezius should receive a moderate-to-high stabilization/upward-rotation vector.\r\n\r\n## Face Pull\r\n\r\nFace pulls can recruit upper trapezius, particularly if significant scapular elevation occurs.\r\n\r\nHowever, they generally emphasize:\r\n\r\n- middle/lower trapezius;\r\n- rhomboids;\r\n- posterior deltoid;\r\n- external rotator cuff.\r\n\r\nUpper-trap allocation should therefore be modest unless execution includes substantial elevation.\r\n\r\n## Rows\r\n\r\nConventional rows primarily involve scapular retraction.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n- middle trapezius;\r\n- rhomboids\r\n\r\nshould usually receive larger shares.\r\n\r\nUpper trapezius contributes to stabilization and may rise if the shoulders shrug during the pull.\r\n\r\nThis should not automatically be considered useful target loading.\r\n\r\n## High Row\r\n\r\nA high row can increase upper-trap demand depending on:\r\n\r\n- humeral elevation;\r\n- scapular elevation;\r\n- upward rotation.\r\n\r\nStill, posterior deltoid and middle trapezius frequently dominate the target movement.\r\n\r\n# Programming Notes\r\n\r\n## Direct Upper-Trap Training Is Easy to Add\r\n\r\nBecause shrugs are mechanically simple and relatively low-skill, upper trapezius can be directly overloaded without much technical complexity.\r\n\r\n## Weekly Volume\r\n\r\nIf upper-trap hypertrophy is a priority, a practical starting range is approximately:\r\n\r\n- **4-8 direct hard sets per week**.\r\n\r\nIndirect volume also comes from:\r\n\r\n- carries;\r\n- deadlifts;\r\n- overhead presses;\r\n- high-elevation raises.\r\n\r\n## Frequency\r\n\r\nTwo to three weekly exposures are practical.\r\n\r\nUpper trapezius often tolerates relatively frequent loading, but recovery also depends on:\r\n\r\n- heavy pulling;\r\n- carries;\r\n- neck work.\r\n\r\n## Repetition Ranges\r\n\r\nUseful ranges include:\r\n\r\n- 6-10 reps for heavy shrugs;\r\n- 8-15 reps;\r\n- 10-20 reps for controlled hypertrophy work;\r\n- longer loaded holds for carries.\r\n\r\nThere is no requirement that traps be trained only with very heavy low repetitions.\r\n\r\n## Proximity to Failure\r\n\r\nStable shrug variations can be trained close to failure:\r\n\r\n- approximately 0-2 RIR.\r\n\r\nTechnical failure occurs when:\r\n\r\n- elevation ROM collapses;\r\n- knees/hips generate momentum;\r\n- cervical movement substitutes excessively for scapular motion.\r\n\r\n## Grip Limitation\r\n\r\nGrip can limit shrug loading before upper trapezius is fatigued.\r\n\r\nIf grip is not the target, straps can improve upper-trap stimulus specificity.\r\n\r\n## Carries vs Shrugs\r\n\r\nThese provide different contraction patterns.\r\n\r\n### Shrug\r\n\r\nDynamic:\r\n- elevation shortening;\r\n- eccentric depression control.\r\n\r\n### Carry\r\n\r\nMostly:\r\n- isometric anti-depression.\r\n\r\nBoth can generate high upper-trap force.\r\n\r\nThey should not be considered mechanically identical.\r\n\r\n# Interesting Facts\r\n\r\n## Upper Trapezius Is Not Merely a Scapular Elevator\r\n\r\nIts role in **upward rotation** is central to healthy overhead movement.\r\n\r\nThe idea that any upper-trap activity during arm elevation represents "overactivity" is an oversimplification.\r\n\r\n## It Acts Strongly Through the Clavicle\r\n\r\nUnlike middle and lower trapezius, much of the upper region inserts onto the lateral clavicle.\r\n\r\nThis means part of its effect on scapular mechanics occurs through:\r\n\r\n- sternoclavicular motion;\r\n- acromioclavicular coupling.\r\n\r\n## A Heavy Carry Can Train It Without Shrugging\r\n\r\nIf a heavy dumbbell pulls the shoulder girdle downward and upper trapezius prevents depression, the muscle can generate high isometric force.\r\n\r\nThus:\r\n\r\n`visible shrug ROM = 0`\r\n\r\ndoes not mean:\r\n`upper trap stimulus = 0`.\r\n\r\n## Shrugging During Overhead Elevation Is Not Automatically Bad\r\n\r\nNormal arm elevation requires upward rotation and clavicular elevation.\r\n\r\nSome upward shoulder-girdle movement is expected.\r\n\r\nThe problem is not "traps turning on"; it is whether scapulohumeral coordination matches the task.\r\n\r\n## Upper Trap and Levator Scapulae Both Elevate but Rotate Differently\r\n\r\nThis is analogous to serratus anterior versus pectoralis minor in protraction.\r\n\r\nShared translation does not mean shared three-dimensional function.\r\n\r\n# Misc\r\n\r\n## Implications for the Agonez Model\r\n\r\n`trapezius_upper` should primarily receive demand from:\r\n\r\n1. **scapular/clavicular elevation torque**;\r\n2. **scapular upward-rotation torque**;\r\n3. anti-depression stabilization;\r\n4. secondary cervical-extension/lateral-flexion demand.\r\n\r\nIt should not be represented merely as:\r\n\r\n`upper_trap = shrug`.\r\n\r\n## Elevation Force Sharing\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`T_elevation =\r\n    upper_trapezius\r\n  + levator_scapulae\r\n  + smaller contributors`\r\n\r\nThe rotational requirement changes relative force sharing.\r\n\r\n### Elevation + Upward Rotation\r\n\r\nFavor:\r\n- upper trapezius.\r\n\r\n### Elevation + Downward Rotation\r\n\r\nFavor:\r\n- levator scapulae.\r\n\r\nThis is a useful KISS distinction if levator scapulae is represented separately.\r\n\r\n## Upward-Rotation Force Sharing\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`T_upward_rotation =\r\n    serratus_anterior\r\n  + trapezius_upper\r\n  + trapezius_lower`\r\n\r\nTheir relative shares depend on scapular geometry.\r\n\r\nA useful simplified interpretation is:\r\n\r\n### Upward Rotation + Protraction\r\n\r\nIncrease:\r\n- serratus anterior.\r\n\r\n### Upward Rotation + Elevation\r\n\r\nIncrease:\r\n- upper trapezius.\r\n\r\n### Upward Rotation + Posterior Tilt / Medial Stabilization\r\n\r\nIncrease:\r\n- lower trapezius.\r\n\r\n## Exercise Allocation\r\n\r\n### Standing Dumbbell Shrug\r\n\r\nVery high:\r\n- `trapezius_upper`.\r\n\r\nSecondary:\r\n- levator scapulae;\r\n- grip;\r\n- cervical stabilizers.\r\n\r\n### Farmer Carry\r\n\r\nHigh upper-trap isometric demand.\r\n\r\nNo meaningful elevation ROM is required.\r\n\r\n### Deadlift\r\n\r\nModerate-to-high upper-trap stabilization depending on load.\r\n\r\nVery high elsewhere:\r\n- hip extensors;\r\n- erector spinae;\r\n- grip.\r\n\r\n### Seated Dumbbell Overhead Press\r\n\r\nModerate-to-high upper-trap demand through upward rotation/elevation.\r\n\r\nAlso high:\r\n- serratus anterior;\r\n- lower trapezius;\r\n- deltoid;\r\n- rotator cuff.\r\n\r\n### Arnold Press\r\n\r\nModerate-to-high upper-trap demand.\r\n\r\n### Single-Arm Cable Lateral Raise\r\n\r\nLow-to-moderate at low elevation.\r\n\r\nIncreasing toward:\r\n- moderate/high at greater humeral elevation.\r\n\r\n### Cable Face Pull\r\n\r\nLow-to-moderate upper-trap demand unless the movement becomes shrug-dominant.\r\n\r\n### Wide-Grip Seated Cable Row\r\n\r\nLow-to-moderate.\r\n\r\nMiddle trapezius and rhomboids should dominate retraction.\r\n\r\n### Weighted Pull-Up\r\n\r\nModerate upper-trap stabilization at most.\r\n\r\nThe main scapular requirement is not simple elevation.\r\n\r\n### Dead Hang\r\n\r\nLow in a passive hang.\r\n\r\nHigher during active shoulder-girdle stabilization, but still not a primary upper-trap exercise.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Lateral Deltoid\r\n\r\nDuring lateral raise:\r\n\r\n### Lateral Deltoid\r\n\r\nProduces:\r\n- glenohumeral abduction torque.\r\n\r\n### Upper Trapezius\r\n\r\nContributes:\r\n- scapular upward rotation/elevation.\r\n\r\nTherefore both can become highly active during the same repetition without competing for the same joint torque.\r\n\r\nThis is another reason exercise vectors should separate:\r\n\r\n- glenohumeral;\r\n- scapulothoracic components.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Serratus Anterior\r\n\r\nIf an exercise involves high humeral elevation, both upper trapezius and serratus anterior should often receive non-zero demand.\r\n\r\nHowever:\r\n\r\n- SA handles more protraction/posterior tilt;\r\n- UT handles more clavicular elevation.\r\n\r\nThis prevents a generic "upward rotator" allocation from becoming anatomically flat.\r\n\r\n## Interaction With Lower Trapezius\r\n\r\nUpper and lower trapezius should not be assigned identical exercise coefficients.\r\n\r\n### Shrug\r\n\r\nUpper:\r\n- very high.\r\n\r\nLower:\r\n- low.\r\n\r\n### Y-Raise / high-angle elevation\r\n\r\nUpper:\r\n- meaningful.\r\n\r\nLower:\r\n- meaningful/high depending scapular trajectory.\r\n\r\n### Pure retraction row\r\n\r\nUpper:\r\n- low.\r\n\r\nLower:\r\n- low/moderate.\r\n\r\nMiddle:\r\n- high.\r\n\r\nThis regional distinction is central to the value of storing them separately.\r\n\r\n## Length-Tension Model\r\n\r\nA first-order model should emphasize shoulder-girdle elevation/depression.\r\n\r\nConceptually:\r\n\r\n`E_LT_upper_trap =\r\n    f(scapular_depression_elevation,\r\n      cervical_lateral_flexion,\r\n      cervical_rotation,\r\n      cervical_flexion_extension)`\r\n\r\nFor ordinary resistance training, the dominant variable is:\r\n\r\n`scapular/clavicular depression ↔ elevation`.\r\n\r\n### Longer\r\n\r\n- shoulder girdle depressed;\r\n- potentially neck laterally flexed away from the side.\r\n\r\n### Intermediate\r\n\r\n- neutral.\r\n\r\n### Shorter\r\n\r\n- shoulder girdle elevated.\r\n\r\n## Shrug E_LT\r\n\r\nAt the bottom:\r\n\r\n`scapular_depression ↑`\r\n→ `upper_trap_length ↑`.\r\n\r\nAt the top:\r\n\r\n`scapular_elevation ↑`\r\n→ `upper_trap_length ↓`.\r\n\r\nThus a full-ROM shrug naturally loads a long-to-short cycle.\r\n\r\n## E_MA Model\r\n\r\nUpper-trap leverage changes with:\r\n\r\n- clavicular position;\r\n- scapular orientation;\r\n- cervical posture.\r\n\r\nFor a KISS model:\r\n\r\n- `E_MA_elevation` can be treated as high during shrugging;\r\n- `E_MA_upward_rotation` should vary with arm elevation.\r\n\r\nA single constant across all tasks would be too crude.\r\n\r\n## FCSA Interpretation\r\n\r\nUpper-trapezius FCSA is more interpretable as a regional force-capacity measure than as one universal torque capacity.\r\n\r\nThe same muscle force can contribute differently to:\r\n\r\n- clavicular elevation;\r\n- scapular upward rotation;\r\n- cervical torque.\r\n\r\nTherefore:\r\n\r\n`F_max ≈ σ × FCSA`\r\n\r\nmust still be combined with task-specific moment arms.\r\n\r\n## ETU Interpretation\r\n\r\nUpper trapezius is another muscle where isometric loading is important.\r\n\r\nExamples:\r\n\r\n- carries;\r\n- deadlifts;\r\n- static support.\r\n\r\nTherefore ETU should include:\r\n\r\n- dynamic elevation work;\r\n- eccentric anti-depression;\r\n- isometric anti-depression.\r\n\r\nA carry should not score near zero simply because shoulder-girdle displacement is minimal.\r\n\r\n## SMH Interpretation\r\n\r\nThe most plausible long-length exposure comes from loaded scapular depression.\r\n\r\nA full-ROM shrug therefore has a stronger long-length rationale than short partials performed entirely near the top.\r\n\r\nHowever:\r\n\r\n- direct upper-trap-specific SMH studies are sparse;\r\n- the magnitude of any special long-length hypertrophy advantage is not established.\r\n\r\nThus `smh_factor` should remain conservative.\r\n\r\n## Evidence Confidence\r\n\r\n- **occipital/nuchal origin and clavicular/acromial insertion:** high;\r\n- **spinal accessory nerve motor innervation:** high;\r\n- **scapular/clavicular elevation role:** very high;\r\n- **upward-rotation contribution:** high;\r\n- **anti-depression stabilization under load:** high;\r\n- **cervical extension/lateral-flexion contribution:** high;\r\n- **meaningful role during overhead elevation:** high;\r\n- **exercise-specific upper-vs-lower trap force sharing:** task-dependent;\r\n- **direct hypertrophy evidence:** limited;\r\n- **stretch-mediated hypertrophy magnitude:** unestablished.\r\n\r\nThe most useful computational abstraction is:\r\n\r\n> **upper trapezius = the superior trapezius region specialized for clavicular/scapular elevation, anti-depression and upward-rotation torque, with substantial dynamic loading during shrugs and high isometric loading during carries and other exercises that pull the shoulder girdle downward.**\r\n	{https://exrx.net/Muscles/TrapeziusUpper}	{https://youtu.be/...}	Bilateral	11.25	0.00	1.000	19.36	19.36
\.


