ntal antagonist braking at very low tension and without productive extension ROM represents distinctly low-quality hypertrophic exposure.", "normalized_etu": 0.0113, "contraction_mode": ["isometric", "eccentric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 106.48, "smh_interpretation": "The extensors provide low-level antagonist control while lengthening during spinal flexion, but they are not directly overloaded through an extension movement.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.50, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mixed"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "brachialis", "etu_cm2": 0.85, "rationale": "The exposure is mechanically meaningful for rope positioning but remains low-level, isometric, and accessory.", "normalized_etu": 0.0193, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "smh_interpretation": "The elbow remains flexed at a nearly fixed angle, so the muscle maintains arm position without productive elbow-flexion excursion.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.00, "resistance_profile_alignment": "moderate", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mixed"}], "modifier_distribution": {"mode": 0.8500, "median": 0.8500, "maximum": 1.0500, "minimum": 0.8000, "unweighted_mean": 0.8857, "tension_weighted_mean": 0.9886}, "hypertrophic_quality_ratio": 0.9886, "active_tension_exposure_sum_cm2": 54.59}	{"obliques": 1.0000, "iliopsoas": 0.9500, "brachialis": 0.9500, "wrist_flexors": 0.9500, "erector_spinae": 0.9500, "rectus_abdominis": 1.0500, "transverse_abdominis": 0.9500}	{"slug": "kneeling_cable_crunch", "notes": ["The single above-reference mechanism is the cable maintaining tension as the trunk returns into extension, giving rectus abdominis mild loaded lengthening that bodyweight crunching does not produce.", "Obliques were held at neutral rather than matched to rectus abdominis, since their contribution is more compressive and anti-rotational and they are less exposed to end-range loaded lengthening.", "Elbow, grip and hip involvement is low-level positional isometry with full inter-repetition unloading, hence a uniform 0.95 rather than any isometric perfusion penalty.", "The intense acute burning typical of loaded abdominal work was excluded as a target variable; it resolves quickly and does not represent persistent restoration of force-producing readiness."], "reconciliation": "PASS", "muscle_evaluation": [{"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "Contributes to trunk flexion through a controlled dynamic range with a resisted eccentric back to the extended position. Its role is partly compressive and anti-rotational rather than the primary spinal flexor, so it experiences less of the loaded lengthening at end-range extension than rectus abdominis, and a moderate tension fraction with no other distinguishing mechanism leaves the neutral reference appropriate.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "tension_fraction": 0.3264, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "moderate", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 26.67, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "mild"}, {"R_m": 1.0500, "muscle": "rectus_abdominis", "rationale": "Unlike bodyweight crunching, the cable maintains meaningful resistance as the trunk returns into extension, so active force persists while the muscle is lengthened at the top of the movement. This mild high-force lengthening, combined with a tension fraction of 0.78 that adds a small nonlinear residual cost, justifies a modest elevation. It stays at 1.05 because the achievable spinal extension range is anatomically limited and the absolute cable force is modest, so this is not the severe loaded stretch that would warrant more.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.29, "tension_fraction": 0.7789, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 18.92, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["loaded_eccentric_into_trunk_extension", "high_relative_tension_nonlinear_residual"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "mild"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "transverse_abdominis", "rationale": "Generates intra-abdominal pressure isometrically at low force with no contractile lengthening under load and full release between repetitions, leaving marginally less persistent local deficit than the dynamic reference.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 21.97, "tension_fraction": 0.1366, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_bracing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "iliopsoas", "rationale": "The kneeling position fixes the hip angle, so hip flexor involvement is low-force pelvic stabilization rather than dynamic or lengthening work; its fatigue is largely transient and its persistent cost per unit tension sits just below reference.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 102.23, "tension_fraction": 0.0196, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_stabilizing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "erector_spinae", "rationale": "Acts only as a low-force antagonist controlling segmental spinal position; the tension is intermittent, submaximal and not applied at a lengthened position under meaningful load, so it leaves little persistent force deficit.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 106.48, "tension_fraction": 0.0141, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.50, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_antagonist_co_contraction"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "wrist_flexors", "rationale": "Light grip on the rope produces submaximal isometric tension with no lengthening and no perfusion-limiting force; the resulting fatigue resolves rapidly and contributes little persistent deficit.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "tension_fraction": 0.0276, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.50, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_gripping_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "brachialis", "rationale": "Holds elbow angle constant at very low force to keep the rope fixed against the head; there is no dynamic excursion and no loaded lengthening, so persistent recovery cost per unit tension falls slightly below the dynamic reference.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "tension_fraction": 0.0227, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_e