ance direction, so stabilization demand is secondary to direct moment and internal-force loading.", "load_exposure": 0.7600, "loaded_joint_position": "demanding", "primary_loading_modes": ["net_joint_moment", "compression", "shear", "muscle_force_transmission"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "high", "stabilization_requirement": "low", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "moderate", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "high"}, {"joint": "hip_joint", "rationale": "The hips remain substantially flexed in the kneeling position and contribute to maintaining pelvic and trunk geometry, producing limited muscular force transmission and stabilization demand. However, proper cable-crunch execution emphasizes spinal flexion rather than large dynamic hip flexion, so the hip experiences only secondary mechanical exposure.", "load_exposure": 0.1200, "loaded_joint_position": "demanding", "primary_loading_modes": ["stabilization", "muscle_force_transmission"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "low", "stabilization_requirement": "low", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "minimal", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "low"}], "maximum_exposure_joint": "lumbar_spine", "standardized_repetition": {"tempo": "controlled conventional tempo", "range_of_motion": "full intended ROM", "proximity_to_failure": "effective repetition"}}
