etition.", "Rectus femoris and sartorius were held at or below reference because knee flexion offsets hip extension on the descent, preventing loaded lengthening at long length."], "reconciliation": "PASS", "muscle_evaluation": [{"R_m": 1.0500, "muscle": "iliopsoas", "rationale": "Free hanging means the limb weight is never set down, so the hip flexors carry meaningful tension continuously through the lowering phase and into the hanging position where they approach full hip extension — mild loaded lengthening rather than a mid-range return against a stop. The alternating pattern also extends the duration over which substantial tension is maintained relative to a conventional supported repetition. Together these give a small elevation; neither mechanism alone is severe enough to warrant more.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 102.23, "tension_fraction": 0.4842, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "substantial", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 49.50, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "prolonged", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["controlled_eccentric_return_toward_full_hip_extension", "prolonged_meaningful_tension_across_the_repetition"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "mild"}, {"R_m": 1.0500, "muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "The alternating oblique pattern requires resisted eccentric control of trunk rotation and lateral flexion while hanging elongates the torso, so meaningful active force coincides with a mildly lengthened trunk position rather than resolving in mid-range. Tension also remains substantial across the whole repetition because bodyweight never unloads. These combine into one modest structural mechanism justifying 1.05.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "tension_fraction": 0.3529, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "moderate", "active_tensi