ain high force at a lengthened hip-abducted position with materially restricted perfusion and no unloading phase, making a residual post-exercise force deficit plausible rather than purely intra-set fatigue.", "Sustained tension and lengthened-position loading were combined into one mechanism for the adductors rather than counted as two full penalties, which is why the value stops at 1.10.", "Obliques were placed at the neutral reference rather than elevated: their hold is continuous but near neutral muscle length and at moderate force, so it does not meet the threshold for a persistent-deficit mechanism.", "All remaining shoulder, scapular, trunk and hip stabilizers operate at low force relative to their available capacity, which the specification treats as having little effect on R_m; they received 0.95 despite the continuous duty cycle.", "The exercise's reputation for adductor strain prevention and its high perceived difficulty were excluded; only local persistent recovery cost per unit tension was estimated."], "reconciliation": "PASS", "muscle_evaluation": [{"R_m": 1.1000, "muscle": "adductor_magnus", "rationale": "The bench-supported position holds the hip abducted, so the adductors sustain high force while already substantially lengthened, and unlike ordinary postural isometry there is no unloading phase whatsoever within the effort. The combination of sustained near-continuous tension, materially restricted local perfusion, and the lengthened position under load makes a residual post-exercise force deficit plausible rather than merely severe intra-set fatigue, which is what justifies the elevation. It is capped at 1.10 because much isometric fatigue does resolve on release and the sustained-tension and lengthened-position factors are treated as one combined mechanism rather than two full penalties.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 105.22, "tension_fraction": 0.5455, "perfusion_constraint": "high", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "substantial", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 57.40, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "continuous", "sustained_isometric_tension": "sustained", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["sustained_high_force_isometry_at_lengthened_adductor_position", "perfusion_constrained_tension_with_no_unloading_phase"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "moderate"}, {"R_m": 1.1000, "muscle": "adductor_longus_brevis", "rationale": "Shares the defining mechanism: the short adductors are held near end-range abduction while bearing the supported limb and trunk load continuously, with no phase in which tension releases. A tension fraction of 0.65 adds a small nonlinear residual cost of operating close to available capacity. The persistent component of this exposure exceeds ordinary dynamic tension, warranting the same clearly elevated value as adductor magnus.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 66.55, "tension_fraction": 0.6546, "perfusion_constraint": "high", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 43.56, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "continuous", "sustained_isometric_tension": "sustained", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["sustained_high_force_isometry_at_lengthened_adductor_position", "perfusion_constrained_tension_with_no_unloading_phase", "high_relative_tension_nonlinear_residual"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "moderate"}, {"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "Holds the trunk against lateral collapse continuously with no unloading phase, which lifts it above the sub-reference value given to intermittent stabilizing tension. However, the trunk is held near neutral length rather than in a loaded lengthened position, and moderate-force isometric fatigue of this type recovers substantially once tension is released, so the neutral reference rather than an elevated value is the appropriate estimate.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "tension_fraction": 0.3740, "perfusion_constraint": "moderate", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "moderate", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 30.56, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "continuous", "sustained_isometric_tension": "sustained", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["sustained_lateral_anti_gravity_isometry"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "gluteus_medius", "rationale": "Contributes frontal-plane hip control on the supported side at low force relative to its large available capacity; the tension is not applied at a lengthened position and generates fatigue that resolves largely on release, so persistent cost sits just below the dynamic reference despite the continuous duty cycle.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 113.75, "tension_fraction": 0.0352, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 4.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "continuous", "sustained_isometric_tension": "sustained", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_sustained_stabilizing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "transverse_abdominis", "rationale": "Maintains intra-abdominal pressure at low force throughout the hold with no contractile lengthening under load; such low-level bracing produces predominantly transient fatigue and leaves little persistent local deficit.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 21.97, "tension_fraction": 0.1366, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "continuous", "sustained_isometric_tension": "sustained", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_bracing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Stabilises the down-side glenohumeral joint at very low force relative to its available capacity, without lengthening under meaningful load; the resulting fatigue resolves on release rather than persisting.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "tension_fraction": 0.0146, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.50, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "continuous", "sustained_isometric_tension": "sustained", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_sustained_stabilizing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "rectus_abdominis", "rationale": "Provides low-force anti-extension bracing in the side-lying position; the muscle is neither lengthened under load nor highly loaded, so its persistent recovery component sits marginally below the dynamic reference.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.29, "tension_fraction": 0.1029, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.50, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "continuous", "sustained_isometric_tension": "sustained", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_bracing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "serratus_anterior", "rationale": "Holds the scapula against the thorax on the supporting side at low force with no loaded lengthening; sustained tension of this magnitude leaves little persistent force deficit relative to dynamic loading.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 36.03, "tension_fraction": 0.0555, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "continuous", "sustained_isometric_tension": "sustained", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_sustained_scapular_stabilizing_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "erector_spinae", "rationale": "Maintains segmental spinal alignment at very low force relative to its capacity; per the isometry guidance, low-level postural stabilization should have little effect and its fatigue is transient rather than persistent.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 106.48, "tension_fraction": 0.0141, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.50, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "continuous", "sustained_isometric_tension": "sustained", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_postural_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "deltoid_lateral", "rationale": "Assists shoulder position on the supporting arm at low force with no dynamic excursion and no lengthened-position loading, giving predominantly transient fatigue.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 45.73, "tension_fraction": 0.0328, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.50, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "continuous", "sustained_isometric_tension": "sustained", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_sustained_stabilizing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "gluteus_minimus", "rationale": "Contributes minor femoral stabilization at very low force without loaded lengthening; the persistent component of this tension is smaller than that of conventional dynamic contraction.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.82, "tension_fraction": 0.0483, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.20, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "continuous", "sustained_isometric_tension": "sustained", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_sustained_stabilizing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "triceps_lateral_head", "rationale": "Holds the supporting elbow angle constant at very low force with no excursion and no lengthened-position loading, so its fatigue is transient and persistent cost falls just below reference.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 36.10, "tension_fraction": 0.0277, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "continuous", "sustained_isometric_tension": "sustained", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_sustained_stabilizing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}], "modifier_distribution": {"mode": 0.9500, "median": 0.9500, "maximum": 1.1000, "minimum": 0.9500, "unweighted_mean": 0.9917, "tension_weighted_mean": 1.0803}, "standardized_repetition": {"tempo": "controlled conventional tempo", "range_of_motion": "full intended ROM", "proximity_to_failure": "effective repetition"}, "active_tension_exposure_sum_cm2": 150.72}	{"hip_joint": 0.6600, "elbow_joint": 0.0800, "lumbar_spine": 0.4200, "glenohumeral_joint": 0.1000, "tibiofemoral_joint": 0.1800, "scapulothoracic_articulation": 0.1200}	{"slug": "copenhagen_plank", "notes": ["The defining mechanical demand is high isometric hip-adduction force generated against a long body lever.", "Lumbar loading is driven primarily by anti-lateral-flexion and anti-rotation stabilization rather than dynamic spinal movement.", "Knee and upper-limb exposures are secondary support demands rather than primary movement demands."], "loaded_joints": ["hip_joint", "lumbar_spine", "tibiofemoral_joint", "scapulothoracic_articulation", "glenohumeral_joint", "elbow_joint"], "reconciliation": "PASS", "joint_evaluation": [{"joint": "hip_joint", "rationale": "The hip joint is the primary loaded articulation. Supporting the body through the upper leg on the bench creates a substantial frontal-plane external abduction moment that must be opposed by the hip adductors. High adductor force generates meaningful hip-joint reaction and compression, while the unsupported pelvis and lower limb require strong frontal- and transverse-plane stabilization. Relative to conventional bodyweight hip-adduction exercises, the long body lever makes the Copenhagen plank a substantial hip-loading pattern.", "load_exposure": 0.6600, "loaded_joint_position": "demanding", "primary_loading_modes": ["net_joint_moment", "compression", "muscle_force_transmission", "stabilization"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "high", "stabilization_requirement": "high", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "moderate", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "high"}, {"joint": "lumbar_spine", "rationale": "The lumbar spine must resist lateral flexion and axial rotation while the body is suspended in a side-plank position. Oblique, quadratus lumborum, and other trunk stabilizers generate meaningful co-contraction and shear-control demand to maintain a rigid trunk. The spine remains near neutral, so exposure is moderate rather than high despite substantial stabilization requirements.", "load_exposure": 0.4200, "loaded_joint_position": "favorable", "primary_loading_modes": ["stabilization", "co_contraction", "rotation", "shear"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "moderate", "stabilization_requirement": "high", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "moderate", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "moderate"}, {"joint": "tibiofemoral_joint", "rationale": "The supported leg transmits substantial force through the knee while the adductors and surrounding musculature maintain limb alignment. Depending on bench contact position, the knee may also experience modest bending and rotational stabilization. However, direct knee flexion-extension moment remains limited, keeping tibiofemoral exposure low.", "load_exposure": 0.1800, "loaded_joint_position": "mid_range", "primary_loading_modes": ["compression", "muscle_force_transmission", "stabilization"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "low", "stabilization_requirement": "moderate", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "low", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "moderate"}, {"joint": "scapulothoracic_articulation", "rationale": "The supporting upper limb requires scapular fixation against the thorax to maintain the side-plank position. However, upper-body loading is only a secondary component of the exercise and scapular motion is minimal.", "load_exposure": 0.1200, "loaded_joint_position": "mid_range", "primary_loading_modes": ["stabilization", "muscle_force_transmission"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "low", "stabilization_requirement": "moderate", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "minimal", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "low"}, {"joint": "glenohumeral_joint", "rationale": "The supporting shoulder transmits part of bodyweight and stabilizes the trunk, but the body is also supported through the bench-contact leg. This limits glenohumeral reaction force relative to a conventional side plank or push-up-support position, leaving shoulder exposure low.", "load_exposure": 0.1000, "loaded_joint_position": "favorable", "primary_loading_modes": ["stabilization", "muscle_force_transmission"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "low", "stabilization_requirement": "moderate", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "minimal", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "low"}, {"joint": "elbow_joint", "rationale": "The supporting elbow mainly transmits force from the forearm into the upper arm while remaining fixed. Direct flexion-extension moment and dynamic elbow loading are small.", "load_exposure": 0.0800, "loaded_joint_position": "favorable", "primary_loading_modes": ["muscle_force_transmission"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "minimal", "stabilization_requirement": "low", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "minimal", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "low"}], "maximum_exposure_joint": "hip_joint", "standardized_repetition": {"tempo": "controlled conventional tempo", "range_of_motion": "full intended ROM", "proximity_to_failure": "effective repetition"}}
single_arm_bayesian_cable_curl	30.00	85.10	{"brachialis": 31.18, "wrist_flexors": 5.44, "biceps_brachii": 32.34, "brachioradialis": 6.57, "deltoid_anterior": 6.22, "pronators_supinators": 3.35}	{"obliques": 3.50, "rhomboids": 3.00, "brachialis": 31.18, "rotator_cuffs": 5.00, "wrist_flexors": 5.44, "biceps_brachii": 32.34, "erector_spinae": 3.00, "brachioradialis": 6.57, "trapezius_lower": 2.00, "deltoid_anterior": 6.22, "rectus_abdominis": 1.50, "trapezius_middle": 3.00, "pronators_supinators": 3.35, "transverse_abdominis": 2.00, "pectoralis_major_clavicular": 3.00}	{"slug": "single_arm_bayesian_cable_curl", "notes": ["Supplied vector sums to 85.10, matching systemic_propulsive_fcsa_demand exactly.", "deltoid_anterior appeared in the supplied vector but cannot be propulsive here: the shoulder angle is deliberately held constant behind the torso and produces no task-relevant flexion excursion. Its value was retained as the mandated lower bound and reclassified as isometric fixation, consistent with the treatment of the elbow flexors in the reverse fly row.", "Tension expansion ratio of 1.3055 is the highest in this series so far, which is expected: a standing unilateral cable exercise externalizes almost no stabilization, unlike the seated machines previously evaluated.", "Nine muscles were added along three distinct pathways: glenohumeral centring and scapular fixation against a posterior line of pull acting on an extended humerus, and standing anti-rotation trunk bracing against a unilateral load.", "Triceps were considered as antagonists and excluded; their activity during a controlled cable curl is incidental co-contraction with no specific mechanical requirement, which does not meet the evidence standard.", "Latissimus dorsi and posterior deltoid were excluded because the cable pulls the humerus in the same direction they would act, so no restraining tension is required from them.", "Coracobrachialis, a genuine contributor to resisting shoulder extension in this position, is absent from MUSCLE_FCSA and was not invented or folded into a broader group.", "load_capacity_kg of 30 is read under the bilateral systemic convention as the combined figure for this single-arm exercise. It was not used to recalculate any mechanical value."], "added_muscles": ["rotator_cuffs", "obliques", "erector_spinae", "pectoralis_major_clavicular", "rhomboids", "trapezius_middle", "transverse_abdominis", "trapezius_lower", "rectus_abdominis"], "reconciliation": "PASS", "muscle_evaluation": [{"muscle": "biceps_brachii", "rationale": "Prime mover for elbow flexion while simultaneously resisting the further shoulder extension the posterior cable line imposes. Both the flexion torque and the shoulder-level restraint are expressed by one force state, so the supplied value is retained rather than increased.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "mechanical_roles": ["propulsive", "multi_joint_control", "eccentric_control"], "tension_fraction": 0.8123, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 32.34, "contraction_character": ["dynamic_concentric", "dynamic_eccentric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 32.34, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_propulsive"}, {"muscle": "brachialis", "rationale": "Monoarticular elbow flexor carrying a large share of the flexion torque irrespective of shoulder position. Supplied value retained; no secondary mechanical demand exists for this muscle.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "mechanical_roles": ["propulsive", "eccentric_control"], "tension_fraction": 0.7078, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 31.18, "contraction_character": ["dynamic_concentric", "dynamic_eccentric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 31.18, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_propulsive"}, {"muscle": "brachioradialis", "rationale": "Contributes elbow flexion torque with a long moment arm, most effective in the mid-range of the arc. Supplied value retained.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 11.22, "mechanical_roles": ["propulsive", "eccentric_control"], "tension_fraction": 0.5856, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 6.57, "contraction_character": ["dynamic_concentric", "dynamic_eccentric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 6.57, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_propulsive"}, {"muscle": "deltoid_anterior", "rationale": "The defining feature of this variation is that the cable originates behind the athlete, so it applies a continuous shoulder extension moment. Anterior deltoid tension holds the humerus at its fixed extended angle and prevents the arm drifting further back. This is positional fixation rather than propulsion, since no shoulder flexion excursion is produced; the supplied value is retained and reclassified.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 30.46, "mechanical_roles": ["isometric_fixation", "limb_position_maintenance"], "tension_fraction": 0.2042, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 6.22, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 6.22, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "wrist_flexors", "rationale": "Transmits the entire cable tension from handle to forearm and maintains a neutral wrist against the extension moment the handle applies. Supplied value already represents this demand and is retained.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "mechanical_roles": ["grip_maintenance", "load_transmission"], "tension_fraction": 0.1000, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 5.44, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 5.44, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Added because the posterior cable line applies a distracting force to the glenohumeral joint with the humerus already behind the torso, a position of reduced passive stability. The cuff centres the humeral head against that distraction throughout the set.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "mechanical_roles": ["joint_stabilization", "isometric_fixation"], "tension_fraction": 0.0293, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 5.00, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "Added because the unilateral cable acts posteriorly on one side only, creating a trunk rotation and lateral flexion moment toward the working arm. Resisted isometrically. Magnitude modest relative to a horizontal single-arm pull, since the load here is small and the athlete is free to set a stable split stance.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "mechanical_roles": ["trunk_bracing", "isometric_fixation"], "tension_fraction": 0.0428, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.50, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "pronators_supinators", "rationale": "Maintains forearm supination against the biceps supination tendency and the handle's rotational moment, preserving the intended grip orientation through the arc. Retained at the supplied value.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 60.88, "mechanical_roles": ["limb_position_maintenance", "isometric_fixation"], "tension_fraction": 0.0550, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 3.35, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.35, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "erector_spinae", "rationale": "Added to hold trunk position against the posteriorly directed unilateral pull, which would otherwise draw the torso backward and into rotation. Standing exercise with no external trunk support, but a light load.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 106.48, "mechanical_roles": ["trunk_bracing", "isometric_fixation"], "tension_fraction": 0.0282, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.00, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "pectoralis_major_clavicular", "rationale": "Added as the synergist to anterior deltoid in resisting the shoulder extension moment. Its relatively high tension fraction reflects only its small maximum FCSA, not a large absolute contribution.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 13.52, "mechanical_roles": ["isometric_fixation", "limb_position_maintenance"], "tension_fraction": 0.2219, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.00, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "rhomboids", "rationale": "Added because cable tension applied through a humerus held behind the torso tends to draw the scapula into protraction and anterior tilt. Retractor tension fixes the scapula so the elbow flexors act against a stable proximal base.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 28.25, "mechanical_roles": ["scapular_control", "isometric_fixation"], "tension_fraction": 0.1062, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.00, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "trapezius_middle", "rationale": "Shares the anti-protraction scapular fixation demand with the rhomboids. Purely positional; no retraction excursion occurs.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 26.18, "mechanical_roles": ["scapular_control", "isometric_fixation"], "tension_fraction": 0.1146, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.00, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "transverse_abdominis", "rationale": "Added for intra-abdominal pressure supporting the anti-rotation demand carried by the obliques. Low-level and sustained.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 21.97, "mechanical_roles": ["trunk_bracing"], "tension_fraction": 0.0910, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.00, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "trapezius_lower", "rationale": "Added because holding the humerus behind the torso invites compensatory scapular elevation and anterior tilt. Lower trapezius tension opposes both, completing the scapular fixation couple.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 14.98, "mechanical_roles": ["scapular_control", "isometric_fixation"], "tension_fraction": 0.1335, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.00, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "rectus_abdominis", "rationale": "Added as the anterior counterpart to erector spinae in resisting the backward trunk lean the posterior cable encourages. Deliberately small.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.29, "mechanical_roles": ["trunk_bracing"], "tension_fraction": 0.0618, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.50, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}], "saturated_muscles": [], "standardized_repetition": {"tempo": "controlled conventional tempo", "range_of_motion": "full intended ROM", "proximity_to_failure": "effective repetition"}, "tension_expansion_ratio": 1.3055, "propulsive_vector_sum_cm2": 85.10, "propulsive_fcsa_demand_cm2": 85.10, "active_tension_exposure_sum_cm2": 111.10}	{"obliques": 2.98, "rhomboids": 2.55, "brachialis": 32.74, "rotator_cuffs": 4.25, "wrist_flexors": 4.62, "biceps_brachii": 38.81, "erector_spinae": 2.55, "brachioradialis": 6.57, "trapezius_lower": 1.70, "deltoid_anterior": 5.29, "rectus_abdominis": 1.28, "trapezius_middle": 2.55, "pronators_supinators": 2.85, "transverse_abdominis": 1.70, "pectoralis_major_clavicular": 2.55}	{"slug": "single_arm_bayesian_cable_curl", "notes": ["The 1.20 assigned to biceps_brachii is the highest modifier issued in this series and rests on a single specific mechanism: sustained high cable tension on a biarticular muscle held lengthened at the shoulder while the elbow reaches full extension. It is not a reward for prime-mover status, which is already carried by a tension fraction of 0.8123.", "The three-step separation between biceps at 1.20 and brachialis at 1.05 is the analytically important result of this row. Both experience identical elbow mechanics; only the biceps crosses the shoulder, so only the biceps can benefit from the cable position that defines this variation.", "Normalized ETU of 0.9749 for biceps_brachii approaches one full reference effective-repetition equivalent, the highest value produced in this series. It arises from the combination of a high tension fraction and a strong quality modifier, both independently justified.", "Hypertrophic quality ratio of 1.0170 sits just above the global target despite twelve of fifteen muscles being at 0.85, because the two lengthened-loaded elbow flexors carry 57 percent of total tension.", "No muscle received the 0.80 floor. Every stabilizer here has a specific mechanical requirement and sustained tension rather than incidental activity.", "Anterior deltoid and clavicular pectoralis were held at 0.85 despite genuinely lengthened positions, since excursion-free fixation at modest tension does not meet the conditions for a lengthened-position bonus."], "etu_sum_cm2": 112.99, "reconciliation": "PASS", "muscle_evaluation": [{"H_m": 1.2000, "muscle": "biceps_brachii", "etu_cm2": 38.81, "rationale": "The entire purpose of the behind-the-body cable position is to keep the biarticular biceps lengthened at the shoulder while the elbow extends fully, and the horizontal cable line maintains high resistance torque precisely at that stretched bottom position rather than unloading it as a free-weight curl does. High tension therefore coincides with the muscle's longest attainable length across a broad arc. Set at 1.20 for a well-supported and pronounced lengthened-tension advantage; withheld from 1.25 because no eccentric overload is present and the top of the range is conventionally loaded.", "normalized_etu": 0.9749, "contraction_mode": ["concentric", "eccentric"], "high_tension_rom": "broad", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "smh_interpretation": "No SMH factor supplied, but this is among the strongest anatomical cases available: the long head crosses the shoulder, and sustained shoulder extension holds it at a length unreachable in any curl performed with the humerus at the side.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 32.34, "resistance_profile_alignment": "very_good", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "substantially_lengthened"}, {"H_m": 1.0500, "muscle": "brachialis", "etu_cm2": 32.74, "rationale": "Benefits from the cable's retained tension at full elbow extension, which loads the lengthened position that a free-weight curl leaves slack, but gains none of the shoulder-mediated advantage that drives the biceps value. Deliberately separated from the biceps by three calibration steps to reflect that the shoulder position is the specific mechanism at work.", "normalized_etu": 0.7433, "contraction_mode": ["concentric", "eccentric"], "high_tension_rom": "broad", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "smh_interpretation": "Monoarticular, so shoulder position confers nothing; its length depends solely on elbow angle, which does reach full extension under load here.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 31.18, "resistance_profile_alignment": "good", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "moderately_lengthened"}, {"H_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "brachioradialis", "etu_cm2": 6.57, "rationale": "Conventional dynamic elbow-flexion tension with controlled phases and no muscle-specific length or resistance-profile feature in either direction. The neutral reference value.", "normalized_etu": 0.5856, "contraction_mode": ["concentric", "eccentric"], "high_tension_rom": "substantial", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 11.22, "smh_interpretation": "Its effective moment arm and highest contribution fall in the mid-range, away from the lengthened bottom position that benefits the biceps.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 6.57, "resistance_profile_alignment": "moderate", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mid_range"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "deltoid_anterior", "etu_cm2": 5.29, "rationale": "Sustained isometric fixation at a fixed shoulder angle. The lengthened position lifts it clear of the incidental co-contraction floor but does not justify approaching the reference, since no excursion occurs and the tension is a fifth of capacity.", "normalized_etu": 0.1737, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 30.46, "smh_interpretation": "The shoulder-extended position does hold it lengthened, but with zero excursion and modest tension the lengthened position alone cannot produce a reference-quality stimulus.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 6.22, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "moderately_lengthened"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "wrist_flexors", "etu_cm2": 4.62, "rationale": "Meaningful sustained isometric grip and wrist fixation with no excursion. 0.85.", "normalized_etu": 0.0849, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "smh_interpretation": "Static grip with no length change through the repetition.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 5.44, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mid_range"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "etu_cm2": 4.25, "rationale": "Unlike the reverse fly, where the cuff contributed genuine dynamic external rotation, here it performs only humeral head centring with no excursion. 0.85 rather than 0.95.", "normalized_etu": 0.0249, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "smh_interpretation": "Purely centring isometry at a fixed humeral position; individual cuff muscles sit at differing lengths with no net advantage.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 5.00, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mixed"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "obliques", "etu_cm2": 2.98, "rationale": "Meaningful sustained isometric anti-rotation without excursion. 0.85.", "normalized_etu": 0.0365, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "smh_interpretation": "Anti-rotation bracing holds a fixed mid-range length.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.50, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mid_range"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "pronators_supinators", "etu_cm2": 2.85, "rationale": "Sustained low-ROM isometric control of forearm orientation. 0.85.", "normalized_etu": 0.0468, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 60.88, "smh_interpretation": "Forearm rotation is held constant, so no length change occurs.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.35, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mid_range"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "erector_spinae", "etu_cm2": 2.55, "rationale": "Sustained postural isometry with no excursion. 0.85.", "normalized_etu": 0.0239, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 106.48, "smh_interpretation": "Static standing trunk position; no lengthened loading.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.00, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mid_range"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "pectoralis_major_clavicular", "etu_cm2": 2.55, "rationale": "Same lengthened but excursion-free fixation profile as anterior deltoid, and given the same modifier for consistency. 0.85.", "normalized_etu": 0.1886, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 13.52, "smh_interpretation": "Shoulder extension holds the clavicular fibres lengthened, but the tension is low-level fixation rather than loaded work.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.00, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "moderately_lengthened"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "rhomboids", "etu_cm2": 2.55, "rationale": "Purely positional scapular fixation, in contrast to the reverse fly where the same muscle produced retraction excursion. 0.85.", "normalized_etu": 0.0903, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 28.25, "smh_interpretation": "Scapular position is held constant, so no length change occurs.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.00, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mid_range"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "trapezius_middle", "etu_cm2": 2.55, "rationale": "Isometric anti-protraction fixation without excursion. 0.85.", "normalized_etu": 0.0974, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 26.18, "smh_interpretation": "No scapular excursion, hence no length variation.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.00, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mid_range"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "transverse_abdominis", "etu_cm2": 1.70, "rationale": "Sustained bracing isometry without excursion. 0.85.", "normalized_etu": 0.0774, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 21.97, "smh_interpretation": "Pressure generation involves negligible length change.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.00, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mid_range"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "trapezius_lower", "etu_cm2": 1.70, "rationale": "Isometric opposition to scapular elevation and anterior tilt, with no excursion. 0.85.", "normalized_etu": 0.1135, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 14.98, "smh_interpretation": "Fixed scapular position throughout.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.00, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mid_range"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "rectus_abdominis", "etu_cm2": 1.28, "rationale": "Low-ROM isometric bracing. 0.85.", "normalized_etu": 0.0527, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.29, "smh_interpretation": "Static trunk position; no length variation.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.50, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mid_range"}], "modifier_distribution": {"mode": 0.8500, "median": 0.8500, "maximum": 1.2000, "minimum": 0.8500, "unweighted_mean": 0.8967, "tension_weighted_mean": 1.0170}, "hypertrophic_quality_ratio": 1.0170, "active_tension_exposure_sum_cm2": 111.10}	{"obliques": 0.9500, "rhomboids": 0.9500, "brachialis": 1.0000, "rotator_cuffs": 0.9500, "wrist_flexors": 0.9500, "biceps_brachii": 1.1000, "erector_spinae": 0.9500, "brachioradialis": 1.0000, "trapezius_lower": 0.9500, "deltoid_anterior": 0.9500, "rectus_abdominis": 0.9500, "trapezius_middle": 0.9500, "pronators_supinators": 0.9500, "transverse_abdominis": 0.9500, "pectoralis_major_clavicular": 0.9500}	{"slug": "single_arm_bayesian_cable_curl", "notes": ["The Bayesian setup differs from a standing barbell curl in two respects that both matter here: the humerus is held behind the torso, adding shoulder-driven lengthening to the biceps, and the cable maintains full resistance at the bottom rather than unloading as gravitational torque does at full elbow extension.", "This is why the biceps receives 1.10 against the 1.05 assigned to the same muscle in a standing barbell curl — the mechanism is the coincidence of peak force with near-maximal length, not merely the presence of a stretched position.", "Brachialis and brachioradialis were held at the neutral reference precisely because they are monoarticular at the elbow and gain nothing from the shoulder position, which is the discriminating anatomical fact in this exercise.", "Shoulder-girdle and trunk involvement is positional isometry at low force with the relevant muscles near neutral length, hence a uniform 0.95 despite the unilateral anti-rotation demand.", "No stretch-mediated hypertrophy reasoning was applied; the elevation reflects restoration of local force-producing readiness after lengthened-position loading, and the exercise's reputation as a stretch-biased curl variant was excluded."], "reconciliation": "PASS", "muscle_evaluation": [{"R_m": 1.1000, "muscle": "biceps_brachii", "rationale": "This is the defining feature of the Bayesian variant: standing forward of the low pulley holds the humerus behind the torso, so the biceps is lengthened at the shoulder while the elbow also extends, and the cable — unlike a free weight — maintains full resistance precisely in that stretched bottom position rather than unloading there. High active force therefore coincides with near-maximal muscle length during the eccentric, a genuine persistent-deficit mechanism. A tension fraction of 0.81 adds a small nonlinear residual. Eccentric stress and lengthened-position loading are treated as one combined mechanism, capping the value at 1.10.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "tension_fraction": 0.8124, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "very_high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 32.34, "eccentric_structural_stress": 