y mechanism.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 4.50, "tension_fraction": 0.3333, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "moderate", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.50, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_stabilizing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}], "modifier_distribution": {"mode": 0.9500, "median": 0.9500, "maximum": 1.0000, "minimum": 0.9500, "unweighted_mean": 0.9722, "tension_weighted_mean": 0.9789}, "standardized_repetition": {"tempo": "controlled conventional tempo", "range_of_motion": "full intended ROM", "proximity_to_failure": "effective repetition"}, "active_tension_exposure_sum_cm2": 132.67}	{"elbow_joint": 0.0800, "radiocarpal_joint": 0.0600, "glenohumeral_joint": 0.6200, "acromioclavicular_joint": 0.2600, "scapulothoracic_articulation": 0.4800}	{"slug": "side_facing_reverse_pec_deck", "notes": ["The exercise primarily loads glenohumeral horizontal abduction with substantial accompanying scapular retraction.", "The side-facing single-arm setup increases unilateral shoulder-girdle control demand relative to a fully symmetric bilateral reverse pec deck, while the selectorized machine still constrains the resistance path.", "Distal upper-limb joints remain only minimally loaded because the elbow angle and wrist position are largely fixed."], "loaded_joints": ["glenohumeral_joint", "scapulothoracic_articulation", "acromioclavicular_joint", "elbow_joint", "radiocarpal_joint"], "reconciliation": "PASS", "joint_evaluation": [{"joint": "glenohumeral_joint", "rationale": "The glenohumeral joint is the primary loaded articulation. The machine resistance creates a substantial horizontal-adduction moment that must be opposed by posterior-deltoid and other horizontal-abduction forces. These muscles generate meaningful glenohumeral compression and stabilization while the humerus moves through a relatively abducted and horizontally extended position. The guided machine path limits instability compared with a free-weight reverse fly, but the single-arm side-facing configuration still requires meaningful humeral-head control and produces substantial shoulder loading.", "load_exposure": 0.6200, "loaded_joint_position": "demanding", "primary_loading_modes": ["net_joint_moment", "compression", "translation", "muscle_force_transmission", "stabilization"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "high", "stabilization_requirement": "moderate", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "moderate", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "high"}, {"joint": "scapulothoracic_articulation", "rationale": "Horizontal abduction of the humerus is accompanied by scapular retraction and controlled positioning against the thorax. Middle trapezius, rhomboid, and other scapular musculature must transmit force between the upper limb and trunk, creating substantial functional scapulothoracic exposure. Machine guidance reduces perturbation but does not remove the meaningful retraction and fixation demand.", "load_exposure": 0.4800, "loaded_joint_position": "mid_range", "primary_loading_modes": ["muscle_force_transmission", "stabilization", "net_joint_moment"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "moderate", "stabilization_requirement": "moderate", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "low", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "high"}, {"joint": "acromioclavicular_joint", "rationale": "Scapular retraction and humeral horizontal-abduction loading require force transmission through the clavicle-scapula interface. This produces meaningful AC-joint mechanical exposure, particularly as the shoulder girdle is loaded in a retracted configuration, but the forces remain clearly below those of heavy pressing or overhead-loading exercises.", "load_exposure": 0.2600, "loaded_joint_position": "demanding", "primary_loading_modes": ["muscle_force_transmission", "stabilization"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "low", "stabilization_requirement": "moderate", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "low", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "moderate"}, {"joint": "elbow_joint", "rationale": "The elbow remains approximately fixed in slight flexion and mainly serves as a rigid link between the handle and upper arm. Only minor isometric stabilization and force transmission occur, with little direct flexion or extension moment.", "load_exposure": 0.0800, "loaded_joint_position": "favorable", "primary_loading_modes": ["stabilization", "muscle_force_transmission"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "minimal", "stabilization_requirement": "low", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "minimal", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "low"}, {"joint": "radiocarpal_joint", "rationale": "The wrist mainly maintains a neutral grip while transmitting relatively modest handle force. Direct wrist moment is small, so radiocarpal exposure is only incidental.", "load_exposure": 0.0600, "loaded_joint_position": "favorable", "primary_loading_modes": ["muscle_force_transmission"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "minimal", "stabilization_requirement": "low", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "minimal", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "low"}], "maximum_exposure_joint": "glenohumeral_joint", "standardized_repetition": {"tempo": "controlled conventional tempo", "range_of_motion": "full intended ROM", "proximity_to_failure": "effective repetition"}}
