e"}, {"R_m": 1.0500, "muscle": "triceps_long_head", "rationale": "As a biarticular shoulder extensor, the long head experiences meaningful tension while becoming lengthened as the humerus returns overhead. The effect is modest because relative tension is only moderate and eccentric loading remains conventional.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.55, "tension_fraction": 0.3513, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "moderate", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 15.30, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["loaded_lengthening_at_the_shoulder", "conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "mild"}, {"R_m": 1.0500, "muscle": "deltoid_posterior", "rationale": "The posterior deltoid bears substantial relative tension during shoulder extension and remains active during the controlled return toward flexion. The resulting loaded lengthening is meaningful but not severe enough to justify more than a mild elevation.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.65, "tension_fraction": 0.5497, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "substantial", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 13.55, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["substantial_relative_tension", "mild_loaded_lengthening", "conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "mild"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Rotator-cuff tension is predominantly low-relative-load stabilization without severe lengthening, eccentric overload, or prolonged high-force isometry. Persistent recovery cost per unit tension is therefore slightly below the dynamic reference.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "tension_fraction": 0.0615, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 10.50, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["glenohumeral_stabilization"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 1.0500, "muscle": "pectoralis_major_sternal", "rationale": "The sternal pectoralis contributes to extension from a flexed shoulder and is exposed to some active loading near a relatively lengthened position during the eccentric return. Absolute and relative involvement are low, so only a mild per-unit recovery premium is warranted.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 53.48, "tension_fraction": 0.1554, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 8.31, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["loaded_lengthening_from_flexed_shoulder_position", "conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "mild"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "rectus_abdominis", "rationale": "The rectus abdominis maintains trunk position largely isometrically. Although tension can be continuous, the force level is not sufficient to imply a major persistent post-exercise deficit, so its per-unit recovery cost remains slightly below dynamic muscle work.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.29, "tension_fraction": 0.2882, "perfusion_constraint": "moderate", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "moderate", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 7.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "continuous", "sustained_isometric_tension": "moderate", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["trunk_anti_extension_isometry"], "high_force_lengt