ral and scapular stabilization requirements relative to a selectorized or fixed-path overhead press.", "Bench support keeps lumbar exposure low while preserving high local shoulder-complex and elbow loading."], "loaded_joints": ["glenohumeral_joint", "elbow_joint", "scapulothoracic_articulation", "acromioclavicular_joint", "radiocarpal_joint", "lumbar_spine"], "reconciliation": "PASS", "joint_evaluation": [{"joint": "glenohumeral_joint", "rationale": "The glenohumeral joint is the principal loaded articulation. The dumbbells create a large external shoulder-adduction and extension moment through much of the press that must be opposed by deltoid and associated shoulder musculature. High deltoid and rotator-cuff forces generate substantial glenohumeral compression and humeral-head stabilization, while the free and independently moving dumbbells increase translational control demands. Meaningful force is applied through elevated and abducted humeral positions, placing this exercise among the more mechanically demanding conventional shoulder-loading movements.", "load_exposure": 0.8200, "loaded_joint_position": "demanding", "primary_loading_modes": ["net_joint_moment", "compression", "translation", "muscle_force_transmission", "stabilization"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "very_high", "stabilization_requirement": "high", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "moderate", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "very_high"}, {"joint": "elbow_joint", "rationale": "Elbow extension is required to elevate the dumbbells and creates a substantial external flexion moment that is opposed by the triceps. High triceps force produces meaningful elbow-joint reaction and compression. The elbow shares the task with the shoulder and does not reach the same relative exposure as direct heavy elbow-extension exercises, but it remains a major loaded joint throughout the press.", "load_exposure": 0.7000, "loaded_joint_position": "mid_range", "primary_loading_modes": ["net_joint_moment", "compression", "muscle_force_transmission"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "high", "stabilization_requirement": "moderate", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "low", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "high"}, {"joint": "scapulothoracic_articulation", "rationale": "Overhead pressing requires substantial scapular upward rotation, elevation control, and fixation against the thorax. Serratus anterior and trapezial forces must coordinate scapular motion while transmitting the dumbbell loads from the upper limbs to the trunk. Independent dumbbells increase the requirement for bilateral scapular control, making scapulothoracic mechanical exposure substantial.", "load_exposure": 0.6200, "loaded_joint_position": "demanding", "primary_loading_modes": ["net_joint_moment", "muscle_force_transmission", "stabilization"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "high", "stabilization_requirement": "high", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "low", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "high"}, {"joint": "acromioclavicular_joint", "rationale": "The AC joint transmits substantial force between the clavicle and scapula while the scapula upwardly rotates and elevates under overhead load. Heavy shoulder-girdle force transmission and scapular rotation create meaningful compression and rotational demand, although the AC joint is not subjected to the same direct moment magnitude as the glenohumeral joint.", "load_exposure": 0.5000, "loaded_joint_position": "demanding", "primary_loading_modes": ["compression", "rotation", "muscle_force_transmission", "stabilization"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "moderate", "stabilization_requirement": "moderate", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "moderate", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "high"}, {"joint": "radiocarpal_joint", "rationale": "The wrists support and stabilize the dumbbells while transmitting substantial external force through the hands. With conventional technique they remain near neutral, so direct flexion or extension moment is limited. Grip and stabilization forces nevertheless create low but meaningful radiocarpal exposure.", "load_exposure": 0.1800, "loaded_joint_position": "favorable", "primary_loading_modes": ["muscle_force_transmission", "stabilization"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "low", "stabilization_requirement": "moderate", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "low", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "moderate"}, {"joint": "lumbar_spine", "rationale": "The seated configuration and bench support substantially reduce trunk moment demand. The lumbar spine still contributes minor isometric bracing and force transmission while the athlete presses overhead, but direct spinal loading is small when the torso remains supported and technically stable.", "load_exposure": 0.1000, "loaded_joint_position": "favorable", "primary_loading_modes": ["stabilization", "compression"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "minimal", "stabilization_requirement": "low", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "minimal", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "low"}], "maximum_exposure_joint": "glenohumeral_joint", "standardized_repetition": {"tempo": "controlled conventional tempo", "range_of_motion": "full intended ROM", "proximity_to_failure": "effective repetition"}}
