 "The exposure is low-level, positional, and predominantly isometric.", "normalized_etu": 0.0167, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 30.46, "smh_interpretation": "The anterior deltoid provides minor humeral-position stability without meaningful loaded shoulder-flexion excursion.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 0.60, "resistance_profile_alignment": "moderate", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mixed"}], "modifier_distribution": {"mode": 0.8500, "median": 0.8500, "maximum": 1.0500, "minimum": 0.8000, "unweighted_mean": 0.9000, "tension_weighted_mean": 0.9898}, "hypertrophic_quality_ratio": 0.9898, "active_tension_exposure_sum_cm2": 51.21}	{"brachialis": 1.1000, "rotator_cuffs": 0.9500, "wrist_flexors": 0.9500, "biceps_brachii": 1.0500, "brachioradialis": 1.1000, "wrist_extensors": 0.9500, "deltoid_anterior": 0.9500, "pronators_supinators": 0.9500}	{"slug": "preacher_dumbbell_hammer_curl", "notes": ["The preacher pad's angle is the identifiable mechanism: peak gravitational resistance torque occurs at or near full elbow extension, so unlike a standing curl the load does not fall away where the elbow flexors are longest.", "The monoarticular elbow flexors (brachialis, brachioradialis) receive 1.10 while the biceps receives only 1.05, because the pad holds the humerus flexed and thereby shortens the biceps proximally — the inverse of the Bayesian curl arrangement, where the shoulder position amplified biceps lengthening instead.", "The neutral hammer grip shifts load toward brachialis and brachioradialis and away from the biceps, which is reflected in the supplied tension exposure rather than in R_m.", "Eccentric structural stress and lengthened-position loading were combined into one mechanism per muscle rather than counted as two full penalties, which caps the values at 1.10.", "The pronounced acute discomfort at the stretched bottom position of preacher work was excluded as a target variable; the elevation reflects persistent restoration of local force-producing capacity, not soreness or perceived difficulty."], "reconciliation": "PASS", "muscle_evaluation": [{"R_m": 1.1000, "muscle": "brachialis", "rationale": "The preacher pad is the defining feature: with the humerus fixed on an inclined pad, the forearm approaches horizontal at full elbow extension, so gravitational resistance torque is at its maximum precisely where the elbow flexors are longest. This is the opposite of a standing curl, where load falls away at the bottom, and it means high active force coincides with near-maximal muscle length throughout the terminal eccentric — a genuine persistent-deficit mechanism. Eccentric stress and lengthened-position loading are treated as one combined mechanism, capping the value at 1.10.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "tension_fraction": 0.5818, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "substantial", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 25.63, "eccentric_structural_stress": "emphasized", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["high_force_active_lengthening_at_full_elbow_extension", "peak_resistance_torque_coinciding_with_longest_muscle_length"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "pronounced"}, {"R_m": 1.0500, "muscle": "biceps_brachii", "rationale": "It shares the preacher pad's loaded end-range elbow extension, but the pad holds the humerus in flexion, which shortens the biceps proximally and partially offsets the distal lengthening — the opposite arrangement to a Bayesian curl. The neutral hammer grip further reduces its contribution. A smaller elevation than the monoarticular elbow flexors is therefore warranted.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "tension_fraction": 0.2831, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "moderate", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 11.27, "eccentric_structural_stress": "emphasized", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["high_force_active_lengthening_at_full_elbow_extension", "shoulder_flexed_position_reducing_proximal_lengthening"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "moderate"}, {"R_m": 1.1000, "muscle": "brachioradialis", "rationale": "The neutral hammer grip places it in its strongest position, so it carries a substantial share of the load, and being monoarticular at the elbow it experiences the preacher pad's peak-torque-at-full-extension profile without any proximal offset. A tension fraction of 0.67 adds a small nonlinear residual to this lengthened-position loading mechanism.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 11.22, "tension_fraction": 0.6729, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 7.55, "eccentric_structural_stress": "emphasized", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["high_force_active_lengthening_at_full_elbow_extension", "high_relative_tension_nonlinear_residual"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "pronounced"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "pronators_supinators", "rationale": "Holds the neutral forearm position against the dumbbell at low force with no dynamic rotation under load and no lengthening, so the fatigue generated is transient rather than persistent.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 60.88, "tension_fraction": 0.0470, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.86, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_stabilizing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "wrist_flexors", "rationale": "Grips the dumbbell at submaximal isometric force with no lengthening under load and no meaningful perfusion restriction; such fatigue resolves rapidly after the set.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "tension_fraction": 0.0276, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.50, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_gripping_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "wrist_extensors", "rationale": "Stabilises wrist position as a grip antagonist at very low force with no excursion and no loaded lengthening, leaving minimal persistent local deficit.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 53.67, "tension_fraction": 0.0186, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_stabilizing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "The preacher pad supports the humerus, so cuff involvement is reduced to negligible glenohumeral centring at very low force relative to its large capacity, with no loaded lengthening.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "tension_fraction": 0.0047, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 0.80, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_stabilizing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "deltoid_anterior", "rationale": "With the arm resting on the pad it contributes only minimal positional tension; there is no dynamic excursion and no lengthening under load, so its fatigue is transient.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 30.46, "tension_fraction": 0.0197, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 0.60, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_stabilizing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}], "modifier_distribution": {"mode": 0.9500, "median": 0.9500, "maximum": 1.1000, "minimum": 0.9500, "unweighted_mean": 1.0000, "tension_weighted_mean": 1.0723}, "standardized_repetition": {"tempo": "controlled conventional tempo", "range_of_motion": "full intended ROM", "proximity_to_failure": "effective repetition"}, "active_tension_exposure_sum_cm2": 51.21}	{"elbow_joint": 0.7200, "radiocarpal_joint": 0.1800, "glenohumeral_joint": 0.1000}	{"slug": "preacher_dumbbell_hammer_curl", "notes": ["The 16 kg load is interpreted according to the database bilateral convention despite the exercise being performed one arm at a time.", "The neutral grip shifts elbow-flexor demand toward brachialis and brachioradialis but does not fundamentally change the elbow-joint load class relative to other preacher-curl variants.", "External upper-arm support keeps shoulder exposure low and makes the exercise predominantly a localized elbow-flexion loading pattern."], "loaded_joints": ["elbow_joint", "radiocarpal_joint", "glenohumeral_joint"], "reconciliation": "PASS", "joint_evaluation": [{"joint": "elbow_joint", "rationale": "The elbow is the primary loaded articulation. The dumbbell creates a large external elbow-extension moment through the forearm that must be opposed by elbow-flexor force, with brachialis and brachioradialis strongly contributing in the neutral-grip configuration. High flexor force acting through short internal moment arms produces substantial elbow-joint reaction and compression. The preacher bench fixes the upper arm and places meaningful resistance on the elbow through a large portion of the intended ROM, including relatively extended elbow positions, while substantially reducing stabilization demand.", "load_exposure": 0.7200, "loaded_joint_position": "demanding", "primary_loading_modes": ["net_joint_moment", "compression", "muscle_force_transmission"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "high", "stabilization_requirement": "low", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "low", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "high"}, {"joint": "radiocarpal_joint", "rationale": "The wrist must maintain a neutral hammer-grip position while transmitting the dumbbell load through the hand. Grip and forearm stabilization generate meaningful radiocarpal force transmission, but there is little intended wrist flexion, extension, or deviation, keeping exposure clearly secondary to the elbow.", "load_exposure": 0.1800, "loaded_joint_position": "favorable", "primary_loading_modes": ["muscle_force_transmission", "stabilization"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "low", "stabilization_requirement": "moderate", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "low", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "moderate"}, {"joint": "glenohumeral_joint", "rationale": "The preacher bench externally supports the upper arm and largely removes the need for dynamic shoulder motion or substantial glenohumeral stabilization. Some force is transmitted proximally through muscles crossing or attaching near the shoulder, but direct shoulder loading remains low.", "load_exposure": 0.1000, "loaded_joint_position": "mid_range", "primary_loading_modes": ["muscle_force_transmission"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "minimal", "stabilization_requirement": "minimal", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "minimal", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "low"}], "maximum_exposure_joint": "elbow_joint", "standardized_repetition": {"tempo": "controlled conventional tempo", "range_of_motion": "full intended ROM", "proximity_to_failure": "effective repetition"}}
neutral_grip_lat_pulldown	75.00	191.51	{"rhomboids": 15.63, "brachialis": 32.78, "teres_major": 3.51, "rotator_cuffs": 8.55, "wrist_flexors": 27.75, "biceps_brachii": 24.32, "brachioradialis": 7.34, "trapezius_lower": 10.87, "latissimus_dorsi": 34.50, "trapezius_middle": 14.49, "deltoid_posterior": 8.31, "pectoralis_major_sternal": 3.46}	{"obliques": 3.00, "iliopsoas": 4.00, "rhomboids": 15.63, "brachialis": 32.78, "teres_major": 3.51, "rotator_cuffs": 8.55, "wrist_flexors": 27.75, "biceps_brachii": 24.32, "erector_spinae": 6.00, "brachioradialis": 7.34, "trapezius_lower": 10.87, "trapezius_upper": 2.00, "latissimus_dorsi": 34.50, "pectoralis_minor": 2.00, "rectus_abdominis": 2.50, "trapezius_middle": 14.49, "deltoid_posterior": 8.31, "serratus_anterior": 3.00, "triceps_long_head": 4.00, "pronators_supinators": 3.00, "transverse_abdominis": 2.00, "pectoralis_major_sternal": 3.46}	{"slug": "neutral_grip_lat_pulldown", "notes": ["Supplied vector sums to 191.51, matching systemic_propulsive_fcsa_demand exactly. No supplied value required adjustment.", "wrist_flexors at 27.75 is by far the largest grip contribution in this series and appears in the propulsive vector despite producing no task torque. It was retained in full and reclassified as high-force load transmission. This is a genuine feature of pulling exercises rather than a modelling error, since grip is the limiting link in the chain.", "Tension expansion ratio of 1.1645 is among the lowest in this series, which is expected: the supplied vector was unusually complete, already including grip, scapular, and cuff contributions that had to be added in other rows.", "The three scapular muscles are treated as propulsive rather than stabilizing, since scapular depression and downward rotation are required components of a pulldown, not fixation. This mirrors the treatment of the upward rotators in the overhead press.", "trapezius_lower shows a 0.7256 tension fraction, the third highest in this row, which correctly reflects that depression is a primary movement rather than a support function here.", "Ten muscles were added, mostly small, covering the trunk and hip anchoring chain against the upward stack pull, grip orientation control, and scapular co-contraction.", "Deltoid anterior and clavicular pectoralis were excluded. They oppose the movement direction and no restraining requirement falls on them, unlike posterior deltoid which assists it."], "added_muscles": ["erector_spinae", "iliopsoas", "triceps_long_head", "serratus_anterior", "obliques", "pronators_supinators", "rectus_abdominis", "pectoralis_minor", "transverse_abdominis", "trapezius_upper"], "reconciliation": "PASS", "muscle_evaluation": [{"muscle": "latissimus_dorsi", "rationale": "Principal generator of shoulder extension and adduction torque against the bar. Supplied value retained; the eccentric return uses the same force state.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.85, "mechanical_roles": ["propulsive", "eccentric_control"], "tension_fraction": 0.7867, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 34.50, "contraction_character": ["dynamic_concentric", "dynamic_eccentric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 34.50, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_propulsive"}, {"muscle": "brachialis", "rationale": "The neutral grip places the elbow flexors in a mechanically strong position, and elbow flexion is a genuine torque-producing component of the pull rather than incidental. Supplied value retained.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "mechanical_roles": ["propulsive", "eccentric_control"], "tension_fraction": 0.7442, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 32.78, "contraction_character": ["dynamic_concentric", "dynamic_eccentric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 32.78, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_propulsive"}, {"muscle": "wrist_flexors", "rationale": "The single largest grip demand in this series. The entire stack load passes through the hands, and unlike a pressing exercise the load actively tries to open the grip. This is high-force isometric load transmission rather than propulsion, since no wrist torque contributes to the movement, but the magnitude is mechanically justified and is retained.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "mechanical_roles": ["grip_maintenance", "load_transmission"], "tension_fraction": 0.5101, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 27.75, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 27.75, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "biceps_brachii", "rationale": "Contributes elbow flexion torque while also spanning the shoulder, where it assists flexion against the extension the lats produce. Both roles are one force state; supplied value retained.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "mechanical_roles": ["propulsive", "multi_joint_control"], "tension_fraction": 0.6109, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 24.32, "contraction_character": ["dynamic_concentric", "dynamic_eccentric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 24.32, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_propulsive"}, {"muscle": "rhomboids", "rationale": "Produces scapular downward rotation and retraction, which is a required component of the pulldown rather than fixation. The scapula genuinely rotates through a substantial excursion here, so this is dynamic propulsive work. Supplied value retained.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 28.25, "mechanical_roles": ["propulsive", "scapular_control"], "tension_fraction": 0.5533, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 15.63, "contraction_character": ["dynamic_concentric", "dynamic_eccentric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 15.63, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_propulsive"}, {"muscle": "trapezius_middle", "rationale": "Retraction torque accompanying the pull, with real scapular excursion. Supplied value retained.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 26.18, "mechanical_roles": ["propulsive", "scapular_control"], "tension_fraction": 0.5535, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 14.49, "contraction_character": ["dynamic_concentric", "dynamic_eccentric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 14.49, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_propulsive"}, {"muscle": "trapezius_lower", "rationale": "Scapular depression is a primary movement component of a pulldown, not a stabilizing function, which explains the high tension fraction relative to its small maximum FCSA. Supplied value retained.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 14.98, "mechanical_roles": ["propulsive", "scapular_control"], "tension_fraction": 0.7256, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 10.87, "contraction_character": ["dynamic_concentric", "dynamic_eccentric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 10.87, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_propulsive"}, {"muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Teres minor and infraspinatus assist humeral extension while the whole cuff centres the head against the distraction the overhead cable applies at the top. Supplied value retained; the seat and thigh pads mean this demand is lower than in a free-weight overhead exercise.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "mechanical_roles": ["joint_stabilization", "propulsive", "isometric_fixation"], "tension_fraction": 0.0501, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 8.55, "contraction_character": ["isometric", "dynamic_concentric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 8.55, "input_contribution_classification": "mixed_propulsive_and_stabilizing"}, {"muscle": "deltoid_posterior", "rationale": "Assists shoulder extension throughout the pull, with a larger role in the neutral-grip variation than in a wide pronated pulldown because the humeral path is closer to the sagittal plane. Supplied value retained.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.65, "mechanical_roles": ["propulsive", "eccentric_control"], "tension_fraction": 0.3371, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 8.31, "contraction_character": ["dynamic_concentric", "dynamic_eccentric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 8.31, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_propulsive"}, {"muscle": "brachioradialis", "rationale": "The neutral grip is its most advantageous forearm position for elbow flexion, which accounts for its comparatively high tension fraction. Supplied value retained.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 11.22, "mechanical_roles": ["propulsive", "eccentric_control"], "tension_fraction": 0.6542, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 7.34, "contraction_character": ["dynamic_concentric", "dynamic_eccentric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 7.34, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_propulsive"}, {"muscle": "erector_spinae", "rationale": "Added because the overhead cable pulls the trunk into flexion, and the slight backward torso lean of a pulldown is held actively rather than supported by any backrest. Isometric throughout.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 106.48, "mechanical_roles": ["trunk_bracing", "isometric_fixation"], "tension_fraction": 0.0564, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 6.00, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "iliopsoas", "rationale": "Added because the entire load path anchoring the athlete runs from the trunk through the hips to the thigh pads. Isometric hip flexion holds the pelvis and trunk down against the upward pull of the stack.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 102.23, "mechanical_roles": ["isometric_fixation", "load_transmission", "trunk_bracing"], "tension_fraction": 0.0391, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 4.00, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "triceps_long_head", "rationale": "Added on a specific biarticular pathway: it assists shoulder extension alongside the lats while simultaneously acting as antagonist to the elbow flexion occurring at the same time. Kept low, since excessive tension would oppose the elbow component of the movement.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.55, "mechanical_roles": ["multi_joint_control", "antagonist_co_contraction"], "tension_fraction": 0.0919, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["co_contractile"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 4.00, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "teres_major", "rationale": "Acts with the latissimus in humeral extension and adduction across an essentially identical line of pull. High tension fraction reflects only its very small maximum FCSA. Supplied value retained.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 4.50, "mechanical_roles": ["propulsive", "eccentric_control"], "tension_fraction": 0.7800, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 3.51, "contraction_character": ["dynamic_concentric", "dynamic_eccentric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.51, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_propulsive"}, {"muscle": "pectoralis_major_sternal", "rationale": "From a fully elevated humeral position the sternal fibres contribute to shoulder extension and adduction through the upper portion of the pull, before their moment arm reverses. The small supplied value is consistent with that limited window and is retained.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 53.48, "mechanical_roles": ["propulsive"], "tension_fraction": 0.0647, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 3.46, "contraction_character": ["dynamic_concentric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.46, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_propulsive"}, {"muscle": "serratus_anterior", "rationale": "Added because it holds the scapula against the ribcage while the retractors and depressors act, and modulates the rate of downward rotation as an antagonist. Deliberately small, since strong protraction tension would oppose the movement.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 36.03, "mechanical_roles": ["scapular_control", "antagonist_co_contraction"], "tension_fraction": 0.0833, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["co_contractile"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.00, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "Added for trunk rigidity linking the ribcage to the pelvis so the pull acts against a stable base. Kept modest because the bar loads both sides symmetrically.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "mechanical_roles": ["trunk_bracing"], "tension_fraction": 0.0367, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.00, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "pronators_supinators", "rationale": "Added because the neutral grip must be actively maintained against the rotational moment the handle applies and against the supination tendency of the biceps under high flexion demand.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 60.88, "mechanical_roles": ["limb_position_maintenance", "isometric_fixation"], "tension_fraction": 0.0493, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.00, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "rectus_abdominis", "rationale": "Added as part of the anterior chain holding the ribcage down and the trunk fixed against the upward and forward pull of the cable.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.29, "mechanical_roles": ["trunk_bracing"], "tension_fraction": 0.1029, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.50, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "pectoralis_minor", "rationale": "Added because its coracoid attachment makes it a scapular depressor and downward rotator, both of which occur here; it produces no humeral torque so its contribution is stabilizing rather than propulsive.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 10.81, "mechanical_roles": ["scapular_control"], "tension_fraction": 0.1850, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.00, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "transverse_abdominis", "rationale": "Added for intra-abdominal pressure supporting the trunk against the vertical load path. Low-level and sustained.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 21.97, "mechanical_roles": ["trunk_bracing"], "tension_fraction": 0.0910, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.00, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "trapezius_upper", "rationale": "Added because the eccentric phase requires controlled scapular elevation rather than passive collapse; the upper fibres pay out that motion under load. Kept small, as they are largely antagonistic to the concentric depression.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 19.36, "mechanical_roles": ["scapular_control", "eccentric_control"], "tension_fraction": 0.1033, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric", "dynamic_eccentric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.00, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}], "saturated_muscles": [], "standardized_repetition": {"tempo": "controlled conventional tempo", "range_of_motion": "full intended ROM", "proximity_to_failure": "effective repetition"}, "tension_expansion_ratio": 1.1645, "propulsive_vector_sum_cm2": 191.51, "propulsive_fcsa_demand_cm2": 191.51, "active_tension_exposure_sum_cm2": 223.01}	{"obliques": 2.55, "iliopsoas": 3.40, "rhomboids": 14.85, "brachialis": 32.78, "teres_major": 3.69, "rotator_cuffs": 7.70, "wrist_flexors": 24.98, "biceps_brachii": 24.32, "erector_spinae": 5.10, "brachioradialis": 7.34, "trapezius_lower": 10.33, "trapezius_upper": 1.70, "latissimus_dorsi": 37.95, "pectoralis_minor": 1.70, "rectus_abdominis": 2.13, "trapezius_middle": 13.77, "deltoid_posterior": 8.31, "serratus_anterior": 2.55, "triceps_long_head": 3.40, "pronators_supinators": 2.55, "transverse_abdominis": 1.70, "pectoralis_major_sternal": 3.46}	{"slug": "neutral_grip_lat_pulldown", "notes": ["Hypertrophic quality ratio of 0.9697 sits modestly below the global target. The bonus muscles are outweighed by an unusually heavy shortened-biased scapular contribution and a very large excursion-free grip component.", "latissimus_dorsi at 1.10 rests on the specific mechanism of a cable holding undiminished tension at full overhead elevation, the muscle's longest position. Its normalized ETU of 0.8655 is the second highest produced in this series.", "wrist_flexors received 0.90 rather than the 0.85 used for grip in every other row. This is the one deliberate exception in the series, justified by a tension fraction above 0.50, which meets the specification's allowance for high-force isometry approaching the reference. Grip in a dumbbell curl or bench press does not approach this magnitude.", "biceps_brachii at 1.00 completes a three-point contrast across this series: 1.20 in the Bayesian curl where the shoulder was extended, 1.05 in the preacher curl where it was flexed against a pad, and 1.00 here where the overhead position shortens the muscle proximally exactly as the extended elbow lengthens it distally.", "All three scapular movers sit at 0.95, above the fixation value used for the same muscles in the bench press, because a pulldown produces genuine scapular excursion whereas bench technique holds the scapula still.", "teres_major shows the highest normalized ETU relative to its size at 0.8200, but its absolute ETU of 3.69 is small; the figure is an artefact of a 4.50 maximum FCSA and should not be read as a large stimulus.", "No muscle received the 0.80 floor."], "etu_sum_cm2": 216.26, "reconciliation": "PASS", "muscle_evaluation": [{"H_m": 1.1000, "muscle": "latissimus_dorsi", "etu_cm2": 37.95, "rationale": "The stack applies undiminished tension at the fully stretched overhead start, a position the muscle cannot escape, and the shoulder extension moment arm remains useful through the upper two thirds of the pull. Set at 1.10 for clearly favourable lengthened loading. Withheld from 1.15 because tension falls off in the final portion as the elbow approaches the torso and the lat shortens.", "normalized_etu": 0.8655, "contraction_mode": ["concentric", "eccentric"], "high_tension_rom": "substantial", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.85, "smh_interpretation": "No SMH factor supplied. Full overhead elevation with scapular upward rotation places the lat at its longest attainable position, and the cable holds constant tension there rather than unloading it.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 34.50, "resistance_profile_alignment": "good", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "substantially_lengthened"}, {"H_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "brachialis", "etu_cm2": 32.78, "rationale": "Conventional dynamic elbow flexion with high recruitment and controlled phases, but no length or resistance-profile feature in either direction. Notably not given the preacher curl bonus, since nothing here concentrates load at the extended elbow position. The neutral reference value.", "normalized_etu": 0.7442, "contraction_mode": ["concentric", "eccentric"], "high_tension_rom": "substantial", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "smh_interpretation": "Monoarticular; elbow angle alone sets length, and the greatest flexion demand falls in the mid-range rather than at full extension.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 32.78, "resistance_profile_alignment": "moderate", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mid_range"}, {"H_m": 0.9000, "muscle": "wrist_flexors", "etu_cm2": 24.98, "rationale": "Deliberately separated from the 0.85 given to grip in every other row in this series. The specification allows high-force isometry at a productive muscle length to approach the reference, and at a 0.51 tension fraction this is a substantially higher grip demand than holding a dumbbell. Held below reference because the complete absence of excursion still limits stimulus quality.", "normalized_etu": 0.4592, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "smh_interpretation": "Grip position is held constant, so no excursion occurs, but the tension is high-force rather than low-level positional holding.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 27.75, "resistance_profile_alignment": "moderate", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mid_range"}, {"H_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "biceps_brachii", "etu_cm2": 24.32, "rationale": "Conventional dynamic flexion tension with no net length advantage. This sits between the 1.20 of the Bayesian curl and the 1.05 of the preacher curl, and the mechanism is explicit: neither shoulder position here works in the muscle's favour across the arc. The neutral reference value.", "normalized_etu": 0.6109, "contraction_mode": ["concentric", "eccentric"], "high_tension_rom": "substantial", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "smh_interpretation": "The biarticular case cancels here. Overhead shoulder flexion at the start shortens the long head proximally at the same moment full elbow extension lengthens it distally.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 24.32, "resistance_profile_alignment": "moderate", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mixed"}, {"H_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "rhomboids", "etu_cm2": 14.85, "rationale": "Genuine dynamic work through a real scapular excursion, which keeps it near reference, but peak demand coincides with the shortest position. One step below reference for that shortened bias, matching the treatment of the same muscle in the reverse fly.", "normalized_etu": 0.5257, "contraction_mode": ["concentric", "eccentric"], "high_tension_rom": "limited", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 28.25, "smh_interpretation": "Retraction and downward rotation both shorten it, and demand is highest at the finish where it is shortest.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 15.63, "resistance_profile_alignment": "moderate", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "shortened"}, {"H_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "trapezius_middle", "etu_cm2": 13.77, "rationale": "Same shortened-at-peak profile as the rhomboids, with genuine excursion. 0.95.", "normalized_etu": 0.5260, "contraction_mode": ["concentric", "eccentric"], "high_tension_rom": "limited", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 26.18, "smh_interpretation": "Shortest at the finish where retraction demand peaks.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 14.49, "resistance_profile_alignment": "moderate", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "shortened"}, {"H_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "trapezius_lower", "etu_cm2": 10.33, "rationale": "Dynamic depression with real excursion but a shortened bias at peak demand. Matched to the other two scapular movers at 0.95.", "normalized_etu": 0.6896, "contraction_mode": ["concentric", "eccentric"], "high_tension_rom": "limited", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 14.98, "smh_interpretation": "Depression progressively shortens it while the load remains constant.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 10.87, "resistance_profile_alignment": "moderate", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "shortened"}, {"H_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "deltoid_posterior", "etu_cm2": 8.31, "rationale": "Conventional dynamic shoulder extension across a substantial arc. The lengthened start does not earn a bonus because its peak contribution falls later in the pull, once the humerus has descended. The neutral reference value.", "normalized_etu": 0.3371, "contraction_mode": ["concentric", "eccentric"], "high_tension_rom": "substantial", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.65, "smh_interpretation": "The overhead start lengthens it, but its extension moment arm is modest at full elevation, so the lengthened position is not where it contributes most.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 8.31, "resistance_profile_alignment": "moderate", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "moderately_lengthened"}, {"H_m": 0.9000, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "etu_cm2": 7.70, "rationale": "Some genuine external rotation excursion accompanies humeral extension, but centring against cable distraction dominates. Matched to the bench press and overhead press treatment at 0.90.", "normalized_etu": 0.0451, "contraction_mode": ["isometric", "concentric"], "high_tension_rom": "limited", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "smh_interpretation": "Individual cuff muscles occupy opposing lengths through the arc, leaving no net advantage for the group.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 8.55, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mixed"}, {"H_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "brachioradialis", "etu_cm2": 7.34, "rationale": "Conventional dynamic flexion tension. The favourable grip is already expressed in its high tension exposure rather than in tension quality, so no bonus is applied. The neutral reference value.", "normalized_etu": 0.6542, "contraction_mode": ["concentric", "eccentric"], "high_tension_rom": "substantial", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 11.22, "smh_interpretation": "The neutral grip places it in its optimal line of action, and its peak moment arm falls in the mid-range where pulldown demand is high.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 7.34, "resistance_profile_alignment": "good", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mid_range"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "erector_spinae", "etu_cm2": 5.10, "rationale": "Sustained isometric trunk positioning without excursion. 0.85.", "normalized_etu": 0.0479, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 106.48, "smh_interpretation": "Static trunk lean held throughout; no length variation.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 6.00, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mid_range"}, {"H_m": 1.0500, "muscle": "teres_major", "etu_cm2": 3.69, "rationale": "Benefits from the same loaded overhead stretch as the latissimus. Placed one step lower because its shorter fibre excursion and purely humeral attachment mean it gains less from the scapular upward rotation component of the stretch.", "normalized_etu": 0.8200, "contraction_mode": ["concentric", "eccentric"], "high_tension_rom": "substantial", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 4.50, "smh_interpretation": "Shares the lat's line of pull and is likewise held at maximum length by full overhead elevation under load.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.51, "resistance_profile_alignment": "good", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "substantially_lengthened"}, {"H_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "pectoralis_major_sternal", "etu_cm2": 3.46, "rationale": "The lengthened position at the start is genuine and would support a bonus, but it is offset by a brief contributing window and a very low tension magnitude. The two considerations cancel at the reference value.", "normalized_etu": 0.0647, "contraction_mode": ["concentric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 53.48, "smh_interpretation": "Full overhead elevation does lengthen it considerably, but its contributing window closes almost immediately as the humerus descends.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.46, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "brief", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "substantially_lengthened"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "iliopsoas", "etu_cm2": 3.40, "rationale": "Sustained isometric anchoring in a shortened position. 0.85.", "normalized_etu": 0.0333, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 102.23, "smh_interpretation": "Held shortened by the seated hip angle throughout, with no excursion.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 4.00, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "shortened"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "triceps_long_head", "etu_cm2": 3.40, "rationale": "Low-level co-contractile and multi-joint tension at a small fraction of capacity. 0.85.", "normalized_etu": 0.0781, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.55, "smh_interpretation": "Elbow flexion lengthens it distally while shoulder extension shortens it proximally, giving no net advantage.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 4.00, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mixed"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "serratus_anterior", "etu_cm2": 2.55, "rationale": "Low-level antagonist co-contraction without productive excursion. 0.85.", "normalized_etu": 0.0708, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 36.03, "smh_interpretation": "Downward rotation shortens it while it co-contracts at very low tension.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.00, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "shortened"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "obliques", "etu_cm2": 2.55, "rationale": "Sustained isometric trunk bracing without excursion. 0.85.", "normalized_etu": 0.0312, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "smh_interpretation": "Static bracing length maintained throughout.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.00, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mid_range"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "pronators_supinators", "etu_cm2": 2.55, "rationale": "Sustained low-ROM control of grip orientation. 0.85.", "normalized_etu": 0.0419, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 60.88, "smh_interpretation": "Forearm orientation is held constant, so no length change occurs.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.00, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mid_range"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "rectus_abdominis", "etu_cm2": 2.13, "rationale": "Low-ROM isometric bracing. 0.85.", "normalized_etu": 0.0877, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.29, "smh_interpretation": "Static trunk position; no lengthened loading.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.50, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mid_range"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "pectoralis_minor", "etu_cm2": 1.70, "rationale": "Low-level scapular stabilization in a shortening position. 0.85.", "normalized_etu": 0.1573, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 10.81, "smh_interpretation": "Shortened by scapular depression and downward rotation, with minimal excursion.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.00, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "shortened"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "transverse_abdominis", "etu_cm2": 1.70, "rationale": "Sustained bracing isometry without excursion. 0.85.", "normalized_etu": 0.0774, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 21.97, "smh_interpretation": "Pressure generation involves negligible length change.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.00, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mid_range"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "trapezius_upper", "etu_cm2": 1.70, "rationale": "Low-magnitude eccentric and co-contractile control at a small fraction of capacity. 0.85.", "normalized_etu": 0.0878, "contraction_mode": ["eccentric", "isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 19.36, "smh_interpretation": "Scapular depression lengthens it, but the tension is low-level paying out of the eccentric rather than loaded work.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.00, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "moderately_lengthened"}], "modifier_distribution": {"mode": 0.8500, "median": 0.9000, "maximum": 1.1000, "minimum": 0.8500, "unweighted_mean": 0.9227, "tension_weighted_mean": 0.9697}, "hypertrophic_quality_ratio": 0.9697, "active_tension_exposure_sum_cm2": 223.01}	{"obliques": 0.9500, "iliopsoas": 0.9500, "rhomboids": 1.0000, "brachialis": 1.0000, "teres_major": 1.0000, "rotator_cuffs": 1.0000, "wrist_flexors": 0.9500, "biceps_brachii": 1.0500, "erector_spinae": 0.9500, "brachioradialis": 1.0000, "trapezius_lower": 1.0000, "trapezius_upper": 0.9500, "latissimus_dorsi": 1.0500, "pectoralis_minor": 0.9500, "rectus_abdominis": 0.9500, "trapezius_middle": 1.0000, "deltoid_posterior": 1.0000, "serratus_anterior": 0.9500, "triceps_long_head": 0.9500, "pronators_supinators": 0.9500, "transverse_abdominis": 0.9500, "pectoralis_major_sternal": 0.9500}	{"slug": "neutral_grip_lat_pulldown", "notes": ["The only above-reference mechanism is the stack maintaining full resistance in the fully overhead position, so the lat and the biarticular biceps resist meaningful force while lengthened — a cable-specific feature absent from gravitational pulling patterns where load falls away at the stretched position.", "Biceps was separated upward from brachialis and brachioradialis on the same biarticular logic used for curling variants: it is lengthened at both elbow and shoulder in the top position, while the monoarticular elbow flexors are governed by elbow angle alone.", "Grip received 0.95 despite a substantial tension fraction and continuous duty cycle, because it is pure isometric force transmission with no contractile excursion; per the specification such fatigue is intra-set rather than persistent, and the magnitude is already carried by T_m.", "Scapular retractors, depressors, posterior deltoid and cuff were placed at the reference rather than 0.95 because in a pulldown they act as dynamic agonists moving through a loaded range, not as positional stabilizers.", "Teres major's very high tension fraction is an artefact of its small FCSA and was deliberately not converted into a modifier."], "reconciliation": "PASS", "muscle_evaluation": [{"R_m": 1.0500, "muscle": "latissimus_dorsi", "rationale": "The weight stack maintains full resistance as the arms return overhead, so the lat resists meaningful force while lengthened at maximal shoulder elevation with scapular upward rotation — the load does not fall away at the stretched position as gravitational resistance would. This mild high-force lengthening, combined with a tension fraction of 0.79 contributing a small nonlinear residual, justifies a modest elevation. It stays at 1.05 rather than higher because the guided cable path removes deceleration demand and the eccentric is controlled rather than overloaded.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.85, "tension_fraction": 0.7868, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 34.50, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["loaded_eccentric_into_full_overhead_shoulder_elevation", "high_relative_tension_nonlinear_residual"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "mild"}, {"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "brachialis", "rationale": "Monoarticular elbow flexion through a conventional range with a controlled eccentric; the overhead position that lengthens the biceps has no effect on it, and it is never loaded at an unusually lengthened elbow position. The high tension fraction reflects load magnitude already carried by T_m.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "tension_fraction": 0.7442, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 32.78, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "wrist_flexors", "rationale": "Grip tension is continuous and substantial here, but it is purely isometric force transmission with no lengthening and no contractile excursion under load. Per the isometry guidance, such fatigue is predominantly intra-set and resolves once the handle is released, so it does not warrant a persistent-recovery penalty despite the tension magnitude, which is already carried by T_m.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "tension_fraction": 0.5101, "perfusion_constraint": "moderate", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "substantial", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 27.75, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "continuous", "sustained_isometric_tension": "sustained", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["sustained_gripping_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 1.0500, "muscle": "biceps_brachii", "rationale": "Being biarticular, it is lengthened at both ends in the top position — elbow extended and shoulder fully elevated — while the stack still applies meaningful resistance. This combined two-joint lengthening under load is the specific mechanism separating it from the monoarticular elbow flexors, warranting a small elevation.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "tension_fraction": 0.6109, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 24.32, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["biarticular_lengthening_at_overhead_elbow_extension", "conventional