cute fatigue, but much of that deficit is rapidly reversible after the dumbbells are released, so persistent recovery cost remains at the neutral reference.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "tension_fraction": 0.6066, "perfusion_constraint": "moderate", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 33.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "continuous", "sustained_isometric_tension": "sustained", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["high_force_grip_isometry", "local_perfusion_constraint"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 1.1000, "muscle": "trapezius_upper", "rationale": "The upper trapezius operates at near-maximal relative active tension and remains loaded during the controlled descent as the scapula moves from elevation toward depression, lengthening the muscle under load. The interaction of very high relative tension and meaningful eccentric lengthening supports a clear but non-overload recovery premium.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 19.36, "tension_fraction": 0.9401, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "very_high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 18.20, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["very_high_relative_tension", "loaded_eccentric_scapular_depression", "conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "moderate"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "rhomboids", "rationale": "Rhomboid exposure is substantial relative to available FCSA but is predominantly stabilizing rather than strongly dynamic. The shrug does not impose pronounced loaded scapular protraction or another structural lengthening mechanism, so persistent cost per unit tension is slightly below conventional dynamic work.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 28.25, "tension_fraction": 0.5239, "p