 "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_postural_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "trapezius_lower", "rationale": "Contributes low-force scapular depression to maintain set position; tension is intermittent, submaximal and non-lengthening, so the persistent component of its fatigue is slightly smaller than for conventional dynamic loading.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 14.98, "tension_fraction": 0.1669, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.50, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_scapular_stabilizing_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "Trunk bracing tension is minimal in a seated supported press, is applied isometrically at low force and releases fully between repetitions, leaving slightly less persistent local deficit than the dynamic reference.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "tension_fraction": 0.0245, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_bracing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "latissimus_dorsi", "rationale": "Acts only as a low-level humeral and torso stabilizer here; the tension is submaximal isometric with no meaningful force applied at a lengthened position, so it carries marginally lower persistent recovery cost per unit than dynamic work.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.85, "tension_fraction": 0.0456, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_stabilizing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "transverse_abdominis", "rationale": "Intra-abdominal bracing is low-force isometric tension without contractile lengthening under load; its persistent recovery component is slightly below the conventional dynamic reference.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 21.97, "tension_fraction": 0.0683, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.50, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_bracing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "pectoralis_minor", "rationale": "Assists scapular protraction at very low force with no loaded lengthening and no sustained high-tension holding, so the fatigue it accrues is largely transient and its persistent cost per unit tension sits just below reference.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 10.81, "tension_fraction": 0.0888, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 0.96, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_scapular_stabilizing_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}], "modifier_distribution": {"mode": 0.9500, "median": 0.9500, "maximum": 1.0500, "minimum": 0.9500, "unweighted_mean": 0.9750, "tension_weighted_mean": 1.0069}, "standardized_repetition": {"tempo": "controlled conventional tempo", "range_of_motion": "full intended ROM", "proximity_to_failure": "effective repetition"}, "active_tension_exposure_sum_cm2": 166.36}	{"elbow_joint": 0.6400, "radiocarpal_joint": 0.1200, "glenohumeral_joint": 0.7800, "acromioclavicular_joint": 0.4400, "scapulothoracic_articulation": 0.5000}	{"slug": "plate_loaded_converging_incline_chest_press", "notes": ["The converging incline press retains high local shoulder and elbow loading while reducing stabilization demand relative to free dumbbells.", "Compared with a horizontal plate-loaded chest press, the incline path shifts more of the shoulder demand toward flexion and clavicular-pectoral loading and modestly increases shoulder-girdle elevation and AC-joint involvement.", "The 70 kg working load is interpreted according to the machine's bilateral database convention rather than as a direct estimate of force at the handles."], "loaded_joints": ["glenohumeral_joint", "elbow_joint", "scapulothoracic_articulation", "acromioclavicular_joint", "radiocarpal_joint"], "reconciliation": "PASS", "joint_evaluation": [{"joint": "glenohumeral_joint", "rationale": "The glenohumeral joint is the primary loaded articulation. The incline pressing path creates a large external shoulder-extension and horizontal-abduction moment that must be opposed by the clavicular and sternal pectoralis major, anterior deltoid, and associated stabilizers. High agonist and rotator-cuff forces generate substantial glenohumeral compression and translational control demand. The converging machine path reduces free-weight instability but still allows substantial humeral motion, and meaningful force is applied in the deeper position where the shoulder is extended and horizontally abducted. Relative to conventional pressing exercises, this places glenohumeral exposure in the high range.", "load_exposure": 0.7800, "loaded_joint_position": "demanding", "primary_loading_modes": ["net_joint_moment", "compression", "translation", "muscle_force_transmission", "stabilization"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "high", "stabilization_requirement": "moderate", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "moderate", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "very_high"}, {"joint": "elbow_joint", "rationale": "The elbows undergo substantial extension against the machine resistance. Triceps force opposes the external elbow-flexion moment and generates meaningful elbow-joint reaction and compression. Exposure is substantial but remains below direct heavy elbow-extension work because the pressing task is shared strongly with the shoulder complex.", "load_exposure": 0.6400, "loaded_joint_position": "mid_range", "primary_loading_modes": ["net_joint_moment", "compression", "muscle_force_transmission"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "high", "stabilization_requirement": "low", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "low", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "high"}, {"joint": "scapulothoracic_articulation", "rationale": "The inclined and converging press requires coordinated scapular control as the humeri move from the deeper pressing position toward flexion and horizontal adduction. Serratus anterior, trapezial musculature, and other scapular stabilizers transmit substantial upper-limb force into the thorax. The supported torso and guided lever arms reduce whole-body stabilization, but scapulothoracic force transmission and controlled rotation remain mechanically meaningful.", "load_exposure": 0.5000, "loaded_joint_position": "demanding", "primary_loading_modes": ["muscle_force_transmission", "rotation", "stabilization"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "moderate", "stabilization_requirement": "moderate", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "low", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "high"}, {"joint": "acromioclavicular_joint", "rationale": "The AC joint transmits substantial force between the clavicle and scapula while the shoulder girdle supports an inclined pressing pattern with meaningful scapular rotation and protraction. Incline geometry increases the elevation component compared with a horizontal chest press, producing somewhat greater AC-joint rotational and force-transmission demand while remaining below heavy vertical overhead pressing.", "load_exposure": 0.4400, "loaded_joint_position": "demanding", "primary_loading_modes": ["compression", "rotation", "muscle_force_transmission", "stabilization"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "moderate", "stabilization_requirement": "low", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "low", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "high"}, {"joint": "radiocarpal_joint", "rationale": "The wrist mainly transmits handle force while remaining close to neutral. The machine handles constrain hand orientation and minimize direct flexion-extension or deviation torque, so radiocarpal exposure remains low.", "load_exposure": 0.1200, "loaded_joint_position": "favorable", "primary_loading_modes": ["muscle_force_transmission"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "low", "stabilization_requirement": "low", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "minimal", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "low"}], "maximum_exposure_joint": "glenohumeral_joint", "standardized_repetition": {"tempo": "controlled conventional tempo", "range_of_motion": "full intended ROM", "proximity_to_failure": "effective repetition"}}
