9500, "deltoid_anterior": 1.0500, "latissimus_dorsi": 1.0000, "pectoralis_minor": 0.9500, "trapezius_middle": 0.9500, "serratus_anterior": 0.9500, "transverse_abdominis": 0.9500, "pectoralis_major_sternal": 1.1000, "pectoralis_major_clavicular": 1.0000}	{"slug": "high_to_low_pec_deck_fly", "notes": ["The fly's single-joint resistance profile is the identifiable mechanism: peak moment arm coincides with maximal horizontal abduction, so the pectoralis resists high force at near-maximal length during the eccentric — materially more pronounced than in pressing, hence 1.10 versus the 1.05 assigned to machine chest pressing.", "Eccentric structural stress and lengthened-position loading were combined into one mechanism for the pectoralis rather than penalised twice.", "Latissimus dorsi and teres major were held at or below reference because the high-to-low arc loads them through a shortening range, the opposite of the pectoral lengthening profile.", "Teres major's high tension fraction is an artefact of its very small FCSA and was not converted into a modifier, since no structural mechanism accompanies it.", "All grip, elbow, scapular, cuff and trunk involvement is low-level intermittent isometry with complete inter-repetition unloading, hence a uniform 0.95.", "No stretch-mediated hypertrophy reasoning was applied; the elevation reflects restoration of local force-producing readiness after lengthened-position loading, not hypertrophic quality."], "reconciliation": "PASS", "muscle_evaluation": [{"R_m": 1.1000, "muscle": "pectoralis_major_sternal", "rationale": "A single-joint fly places the resistance moment arm at its longest precisely when the shoulder is maximally horizontally abducted, so peak active force coincides with the pectoralis at near-maximal length during the eccentric — this is the exercise's defining persistent-recovery mechanism and is substantially more pronounced than in pressing patterns, where load falls as leverage improves in the stretched position. A tension fraction of 0.77 adds a small nonlinear residual cost. Eccentric stress and lengthened-position loading are treated as one combined structural mechanism, capping the value at 1.10.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 53.48, "tension_fraction": 0.7687, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 41.11, "eccentric_structural_stress": "emphasized", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["high_force_active_lengthening_at_maximally_abducted_shoulder", "high_relative_tension_nonlinear_residual"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "pronounced"}, {"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "latissimus_dorsi", "rationale": "The high-to-low path recruits the lat as a shoulder extensor and adductor through a shortening range, so it is not loaded at a lengthened position — it is shortest, not longest, at the point of peak resistance. Conventional dynamic tension at a moderate fraction leaves the neutral reference appropriate.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.85, "tension_fraction": 0.3521, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "moderate", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 15.44, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 1.0500, "muscle": "deltoid_anterior", "rationale": "As a horizontal flexion synergist it shares the fly's loaded end-range position, resisting force while lengthened at maximal horizontal abduction, though at a smaller share of the total moment than the pectoralis. This mild high-force lengthening justifies a small elevation only.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 30.46, "tension_fraction": 0.4370, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "substantial", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 13.31, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["loaded_eccentric_at_horizontally_abducted_shoulder", "conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "mild"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Maintains glenohumeral centring at low force relative to its large available capacity on a machine-fixed arc that removes path-control demands; the tension unloads between repetitions and leaves less persistent deficit than dynamic loading.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "tension_fraction": 0.0527, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 9.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_stabilizing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "serratus_anterior", "rationale": "Controls scapular position against the back pad at submaximal force with no meaningful loaded lengthening; brief, intermittent tension of this kind produces predominantly transient fatigue.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 36.03, "tension_fraction": 0.1721, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 6.20, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_scapular_stabilizing_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "pectoralis_major_clavicular", "rationale": "The descending high-to-low arc shortens rather than favours the clavicular fibres, so their contribution is modest and their lengthened-position exposure is limited compared with the sternal head. No mechanism distinguishes this from conventional dynamic tension.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 13.52, "tension_fraction": 0.2478, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "moderate", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.35, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "mild"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "teres_major", "rationale": "Assists shoulder adduction and extension through a shortening range on the descending arc, so it is never loaded at a lengthened position. Its absolute force contribution is small and the tension releases fully between repetitions, giving slightly less persistent local deficit than the dynamic reference despite the high tension fraction, which reflects only its very small FCSA.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 4.50, "tension_fraction": 0.7089, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.19, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["conventional_synergist_shortening_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "brachialis", "rationale": "Holds a fixed elbow angle against the pads at low force with no excursion and no loaded lengthening, so the fatigue generated is largely transient.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "tension_fraction": 0.0602, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.65, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_stabilizing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "pectoralis_minor", "rationale": "Contributes scapular protraction and depression at low force without loaded lengthening or sustained high-tension holding, leaving persistent cost marginally below the dynamic reference.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 10.81, "tension_fraction": 0.2405, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "moderate", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.60, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_scapular_stabilizing_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "biceps_brachii", "rationale": "Stabilises elbow and shoulder position at very low force; no dynamic excursion and no loaded lengthening occur, so persistent recovery cost per unit tension falls just below reference.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "tension_fraction": 0.0419, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.67, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_stabilizing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "wrist_flexors", "rationale": "Light grip on the handles produces submaximal isometric tension with no lengthening and no perfusion restriction; such fatigue resolves rapidly.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "tension_fraction": 0.0276, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.50, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_gripping_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "trapezius_lower", "rationale": "Maintains scapular depression against the pad at low force with full inter-repetition unloading and no loaded lengthening, leaving little persistent local deficit.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 14.98, "tension_fraction": 0.0801, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.20, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_scapular_stabilizing_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "wrist_extensors", "rationale": "Stabilises wrist position as a grip antagonist at very low force without excursion or loaded lengthening, so its fatigue is transient.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 53.67, "tension_fraction": 0.0186, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_stabilizing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "trapezius_middle", "rationale": "Holds scapular position against the back pad at very low force; submaximal intermittent tension with no lengthened-position loading leaves persistent cost just below reference.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 26.18, "tension_fraction": 0.0306, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 0.80, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_scapular_stabilizing_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "transverse_abdominis", "rationale": "Provides minimal bracing in a seated supported position with no contractile lengthening under load; its persistent recovery component sits slightly below the dynamic reference.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 21.97, "tension_fraction": 0.0364, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 0.80, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_bracing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}], "modifier_distribution": {"mode": 0.9500, "median": 0.9500, "maximum": 1.1000, "minimum": 0.9500, "unweighted_mean": 0.9767, "tension_weighted_mean": 1.0322}, "standardized_repetition": {"tempo": "controlled conventional tempo", "range_of_motion": "full intended ROM", "proximity_to_failure": "effective repetition"}, "active_tension_exposure_sum_cm2": 103.82}	{"elbow_joint": 0.0800, "radiocarpal_joint": 0.0600, "glenohumeral_joint": 0.6800, "acromioclavicular_joint": 0.3000, "scapulothoracic_articulation": 0.4400}	{"slug": "high_to_low_pec_deck_fly", "notes": ["The exercise is dominated by glenohumeral horizontal adduction with an oblique high-to-low component.", "The high starting arm position creates somewhat greater shoulder-girdle rotational involvement than a strictly horizontal pec-deck fly.", "Machine guidance keeps elbow, wrist, and stabilization-related exposure low while preserving substantial direct shoulder loading."], "loaded_joints": ["glenohumeral_joint", "scapulothoracic_articulation", "acromioclavicular_joint", "elbow_joint", "radiocarpal_joint"], "reconciliation": "PASS", "joint_evaluation": [{"joint": "glenohumeral_joint", "rationale": "The glenohumeral joint is the primary loaded articulation. The selectorized resistance creates a substantial external horizontal-abduction and elevation-related moment that must be opposed by the pectoralis major and associated anterior shoulder musculature as the arms move through a high-to-low adduction path. High agonist and rotator-cuff forces generate meaningful glenohumeral compression and humeral-head control, particularly in the stretched starting position where the humerus is abducted and horizontally opened. Machine guidance reduces instability relative to free-cable or dumbbell fly variants, but direct shoulder loading remains substantial.", "load_exposure": 0.6800, "loaded_joint_position": "demanding", "primary_loading_modes": ["net_joint_moment", "compression", "translation", "muscle_force_transmission", "stabilization"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "high", "stabilization_requirement": "moderate", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "moderate", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "high"}, {"joint": "scapulothoracic_articulation", "rationale": "The scapulae must remain controlled against the thorax as the humeri move from a more elevated and horizontally abducted position toward lower horizontal adduction. Serratus anterior, trapezial musculature, and other scapular stabilizers provide meaningful fixation and coordinated scapular motion while transmitting force between the upper limbs and trunk. The guided pec-deck path limits stabilization demand but does not eliminate functional scapulothoracic loading.", "load_exposure": 0.4400, "loaded_joint_position": "mid_range", "primary_loading_modes": ["muscle_force_transmission", "stabilization", "rotation"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "moderate", "stabilization_requirement": "moderate", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "low", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "moderate"}, {"joint": "acromioclavicular_joint", "rationale": "The AC joint transmits force between the clavicle and scapula while the shoulder girdle changes orientation during the high-to-low fly path. The elevated starting position and subsequent scapular motion create meaningful force transmission and rotational demand, but exposure remains clearly secondary to the glenohumeral joint.", "load_exposure": 0.3000, "loaded_joint_position": "demanding", "primary_loading_modes": ["muscle_force_transmission", "rotation", "stabilization"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "low", "stabilization_requirement": "low", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "low", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "moderate"}, {"joint": "elbow_joint", "rationale": "The elbow remains slightly flexed and largely fixed throughout the fly. It primarily acts as a force-transmission link between the machine contact point and upper arm, with little direct flexion-extension moment.", "load_exposure": 0.0800, "loaded_joint_position": "favorable", "primary_loading_modes": ["stabilization", "muscle_force_transmission"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "minimal", "stabilization_requirement": "low", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "minimal", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "low"}, {"joint": "radiocarpal_joint", "rationale": "The wrist mainly maintains handle position and transmits modest force while remaining mechanically neutral. Direct wrist moment is negligible, so radiocarpal exposure is incidental.", "load_exposure": 0.0600, "loaded_joint_position": "favorable", "primary_loading_modes": ["muscle_force_transmission"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "minimal", "stabilization_requirement": "low", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "minimal", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "low"}], "maximum_exposure_joint": "glenohumeral_joint", "standardized_repetition": {"tempo": "controlled conventional tempo", "range_of_motion": "full intended ROM", "proximity_to_failure": "effective repetition"}}
standing_barbell_curl	40.00	87.35	{"brachialis": 32.98, "wrist_flexors": 4.35, "biceps_brachii": 27.87, "erector_spinae": 6.29, "brachioradialis": 6.85, "deltoid_anterior": 6.06, "rectus_abdominis": 1.32, "transverse_abdominis": 1.63}	{"soleus": 1.00, "obliques": 1.50, "brachialis": 32.98, "rotator_cuffs": 1.20, "wrist_flexors": 4.35, "biceps_brachii": 27.87, "erector_spinae": 6.29, "gluteus_medius": 1.20, "brachioradialis": 6.85, "wrist_extensors": 2.00, "deltoid_anterior": 6.06, "rectus_abdominis": 1.32, "deep_neck_extensors": 0.60, "pronators_supinators": 1.50, "transverse_abdominis": 1.63}	{"slug": "standing_barbell_curl", "notes": ["The supplied contribution vector sums exactly to the systemic propulsive FCSA demand.", "The supplied erector-spinae, deltoid-anterior, wrist-flexor, rectus-abdominis, and transverse-abdominis entries were interpreted as mixed stabilization, grip, and load-transmission exposure rather than purely elbow-flexion propulsion.", "The brachialis, biceps brachii, and brachioradialis already capture the principal dynamic elbow-flexion demand and were not expanded mechanically a second time.", "Additional exposure was limited to antagonist wrist fixation, forearm-orientation control, shoulder stabilization, trunk anti-rotation, and low-level standing postural control.", "The exercise is modeled as a strict bilateral curl without deliberate hip extension, lumbar extension, knee drive, or shoulder swing."], "added_muscles": ["wrist_extensors", "pronators_supinators", "obliques", "gluteus_medius", "rotator_cuffs", "soleus", "deep_neck_extensors"], "reconciliation": "PASS", "muscle_evaluation": [{"muscle": "brachialis", "rationale": "Produces a dominant share of elbow-flexion torque independently of forearm orientation and eccentrically controls elbow extension during the lowering phase. The supplied contribution already represents its complete dynamic force state.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "mechanical_roles": ["propulsive", "eccentric_control"], "tension_fraction": 0.7487, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 32.98, "contraction_character": ["dynamic_concentric", "dynamic_eccentric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 32.98, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_propulsive"}, {"muscle": "biceps_brachii", "rationale": "Produces substantial elbow-flexion and supination-related tension while controlling the eccentric return. The supplied value already includes its biarticular shoulder-stabilization component.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "mechanical_roles": ["propulsive", "eccentric_control", "multi_joint_control"], "tension_fraction": 0.7001, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 27.87, "contraction_character": ["dynamic_concentric", "dynamic_eccentric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 27.87, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_propulsive"}, {"muscle": "brachioradialis", "rationale": "Assists elbow flexion and stabilizes the forearm as the bar rises and lowers. The supplied contribution is retained as the complete dynamic and positional demand.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 11.22, "mechanical_roles": ["propulsive", "eccentric_control", "limb_position_maintenance"], "tension_fraction": 0.6105, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 6.85, "contraction_character": ["dynamic_concentric", "dynamic_eccentric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 6.85, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_propulsive"}, {"muscle": "erector_spinae", "rationale": "Maintains upright spinal alignment against the barbell's anterior moment and prevents trunk flexion during strict execution. The supplied value is treated as the complete spinal-stabilization exposure.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 106.48, "mechanical_roles": ["trunk_bracing", "isometric_fixation", "load_transmission", "antagonist_co_contraction"], "tension_fraction": 0.0591, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 6.29, "contraction_character": ["isometric", "co_contractile"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 6.29, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "deltoid_anterior", "rationale": "Maintains humeral position and may contribute minor shoulder flexion as the bar approaches the upper range. The supplied value already captures this mixed positional and dynamic force state.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 30.46, "mechanical_roles": ["joint_stabilization", "limb_position_maintenance", "isometric_fixation", "multi_joint_control"], "tension_fraction": 0.1989, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 6.06, "contraction_character": ["isometric", "co_contractile", "dynamic_concentric", "dynamic_eccentric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 6.06, "input_contribution_classification": "mixed_propulsive_and_stabilizing"}, {"muscle": "wrist_flexors", "rationale": "Maintains the barbell grip and resists unwanted wrist extension while elbow-flexion force is transmitted through the hands. The supplied value already represents this gripping demand.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "mechanical_roles": ["grip_maintenance", "isometric_fixation", "load_transmission"], "tension_fraction": 0.0800, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 4.35, "contraction_character": ["isometric", "co_contractile"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 4.35, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "wrist_extensors", "rationale": "Co-contracts with the wrist flexors to preserve neutral wrist alignment beneath the barbell.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 53.67, "mechanical_roles": ["grip_maintenance", "antagonist_co_contraction", "isometric_fixation"], "tension_fraction": 0.0373, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric", "co_contractile"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.00, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "transverse_abdominis", "rationale": "Provides circumferential abdominal stiffness while barbell force is transmitted through the standing torso. The supplied value is retained unchanged.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 21.97, "mechanical_roles": ["trunk_bracing", "isometric_fixation", "load_transmission"], "tension_fraction": 0.0742, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 1.63, "contraction_character": ["isometric", "co_contractile"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.63, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "pronators_supinators", "rationale": "Maintains the supinated forearm and bar orientation against rotational disturbances throughout the curl.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 60.88, "mechanical_roles": ["grip_maintenance", "limb_position_maintenance", "isometric_fixation"], "tension_fraction": 0.0246, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric", "co_contractile"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.50, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "Resists rotation and lateral deviation while the bilateral barbell load is held anterior to the torso.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "mechanical_roles": ["trunk_bracing", "antagonist_co_contraction", "isometric_fixation"], "tension_fraction": 0.0184, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric", "co_contractile"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.50, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "rectus_abdominis", "rationale": "Co-contracts with the erector spinae to maintain ribcage-to-pelvis alignment and prevent lumbar extension as the bar approaches the upper range.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.29, "mechanical_roles": ["trunk_bracing", "antagonist_co_contraction", "isometric_fixation"], "tension_fraction": 0.0543, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 1.32, "contraction_character": ["isometric", "co_contractile"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.32, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "gluteus_medius", "rationale": "Maintains frontal-plane pelvic stability during the standing bilateral movement.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 113.75, "mechanical_roles": ["joint_stabilization", "limb