ension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mixed"}, {"H_m": 0.8000, "muscle": "deep_neck_extensors", "etu_cm2": 0.48, "rationale": "Low-level cervical-position maintenance with negligible productive ROM warrants the lower calibration anchor.", "normalized_etu": 0.0057, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 84.88, "smh_interpretation": "The cervical spine remains neutral without loaded extension excursion.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 0.60, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mixed"}], "modifier_distribution": {"mode": 0.8500, "median": 0.8500, "maximum": 1.0000, "minimum": 0.8000, "unweighted_mean": 0.8667, "tension_weighted_mean": 0.9532}, "hypertrophic_quality_ratio": 0.9532, "active_tension_exposure_sum_cm2": 96.35}	{"soleus": 0.9500, "obliques": 0.9500, "brachialis": 1.0000, "rotator_cuffs": 0.9500, "wrist_flexors": 0.9500, "biceps_brachii": 1.0500, "erector_spinae": 0.9500, "gluteus_medius": 0.9500, "brachioradialis": 1.0000, "wrist_extensors": 0.9500, "deltoid_anterior": 0.9500, "rectus_abdominis": 0.9500, "deep_neck_extensors": 0.9500, "pronators_supinators": 0.9500, "transverse_abdominis": 0.9500}	{"slug": "standing_barbell_curl", "notes": ["Only the biceps brachii received an elevation, and the mechanism is specifically biarticular: it lengthens across both elbow and shoulder at the bottom of the eccentric, whereas the monoarticular brachialis and brachioradialis are lengthened at the elbow only.", "The elevation was kept small because the standing barbell curl's resistance torque falls away substantially at full elbow extension, so the lengthened position is not where peak force occurs — unlike incline or preacher variants.", "High tension fractions in brachialis and brachioradialis were deliberately not converted into modifiers, since that magnitude is already represented in Active Tension Exposure and no structural mechanism accompanies it.", "Standing barbell curls are commonly performed with body English, but the standardized effective repetition excludes momentum and technical breakdown, so no rapid force-development factor was applied.", "The pronounced acute pump characteristic of arm training was excluded as a target variable; it resolves quickly and does not represent persistent restoration of force-producing readiness."], "reconciliation": "PASS", "muscle_evaluation": [{"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "brachialis", "rationale": "Monoarticular elbow flexion through a conventional range with a controlled eccentric. Standing curls have their weakest leverage in the mid-range, and resistance torque falls off substantially at the bottom where the arm is extended, so the brachialis is not exposed to high force at long length. The high tension fraction reflects load magnitude already carried by T_m and no accompanying structural mechanism exists, so the neutral reference applies.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "tension_fraction": 0.7487, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 32.98, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "mild"}, {"R_m": 1.0500, "muscle": "biceps_brachii", "rationale": "Unlike the monoarticular elbow flexors, the biceps crosses the shoulder as well, so the terminal eccentric position — full elbow extension with the humerus hanging neutral or slightly behind — places it at a genuinely long length while meaningful force is still resisted. This mild high-force lengthening, combined with a tension fraction of 0.70 contributing a small nonlinear residual, justifies a modest elevation. It stays at 1.05 because standing barbell curls unload substantially at full extension and the shoulder is not held in extension as it would be in an incline or drag variant.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "tension_fraction": 0.7001, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 27.87, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["biarticular_lengthening_across_elbow_and_shoulder_during_eccentric", "high_relative_tension_nonlinear_residual"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "mild"}, {"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "brachioradialis", "rationale": "Assists elbow flexion through a conventional dynamic range with a controlled eccentric and no lengthened-position loading; the supinated barbell grip reduces its mechanical advantage but does not alter its recovery profile. Nothing distinguishes this exposure from the reference condition.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 11.22, "tension_fraction": 0.6106, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 6.85, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "erector_spinae", "rationale": "Resists the forward-shifting load moment at low force relative to its large capacity, without dynamic excursion or loaded lengthening; per the isometry guidance, low-level postural stabilization leaves little persistent deficit.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 106.48, "tension_fraction": 0.0591, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 6.29, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_postural_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "deltoid_anterior", "rationale": "Holds the humerus in position and contributes minor shoulder flexion at low force; the tension is submaximal, non-lengthening under load and unloads between repetitions, so its fatigue is largely transient.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 30.46, "tension_fraction": 0.1990, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 6.06, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_stabilizing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "wrist_flexors", "rationale": "Grips the bar and holds wrist position at submaximal force without lengthening or meaningful perfusion restriction; such fatigue resolves rapidly after the set.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "tension_fraction": 0.0800, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 4.35, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_gripping_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "wrist_extensors", "rationale": "Stabilises wrist position as a grip antagonist at very low force with no excursion and no loaded lengthening, leaving minimal persistent local deficit.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 53.67, "tension_fraction": 0.0373, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.00, "ecc