rried, and there is no free-bar deceleration or reversal demand. This is the mild version of the pressing lengthening mechanism, warranting a small elevation rather than the 1.10 assigned to a free barbell bench press where the bar descends to torso contact under full load.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 53.48, "tension_fraction": 0.7880, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 42.14, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["loaded_eccentric_at_moderately_lengthened_position", "high_relative_tension_nonlinear_residual"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "mild"}, {"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "triceps_lateral_head", "rationale": "Conventional dynamic elbow extension through a machine-guided mid-range path; the monoarticular lateral head never reaches an unusually lengthened position during pressing. The very high tension fraction reflects load magnitude already carried by T_m with no accompanying structural mechanism.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 36.10, "tension_fraction": 0.8042, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "very_high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 29.03, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "triceps_long_head", "rationale": "Elbow flexion at the bottom lengthens it distally while the accompanying shoulder extension shortens it proximally, so the two joint actions offset rather than combine into a lengthened-position load. Conventional dynamic tension applies.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.55, "tension_fraction": 0.6331, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_d